KANT_TR['reflm/guyer'] = {"reflm:3489":["expresses *propositiones tavtologicas*.",["*propositiones tavtologicas*: “tautological propositions”, in Kant's spelling."]],"reflm:3490":["Boring pastime is *contradictio in adiecto*.\nAmusing pastime is tautology.",["The wordplay is lost: langweilig (“boring”, literally “long-while”) and kurzweilig (“amusing”, literally “short-while”) against Zeitvertreib (“pastime”, literally “driving away time”)."]],"reflm:3491":["visible darkness",[]],"reflm:3492":["Above and \n*pia fraus*",["The editorial location note “Über und” is cut off in the source.","*pia fraus*: “pious fraud”."]],"reflm:3493":["In the right margin \nApparent contradictions in the government of the world. In the mysteries. |\nIn the riddles. In the witty notions. Which are resolved. A book would be shorter etc. etc. In the writing of a great man the contradictions are held to be apparent.",["“In the mysteries … In the riddles … In the witty notions” render bare datives (Den Geheimnissen, Den Rätzeln, Den witzigen Einfällen), which seem to continue “in der Regirung”.","“apparent contradictions” renders Scheinwidersprüche; “apparent” at the end renders scheinbar."]],"reflm:3494":["*identitas et contradictio explicita et implicita*.\nOn M §. 13 »patens«:\n*indemonstrata*.\nOn M §. 13 »latens«:\n*demonstrata*.",["*identitas et contradictio explicita et implicita*: “explicit and implicit identity and contradiction”; *indemonstrata* / *demonstrata*: “undemonstrated” / “demonstrated”."]],"reflm:3495":["*pia fraus*.",["*pia fraus*: “pious fraud”."]],"reflm:3496":["*latens* is *pia fraus*.",["*latens*: “latent”; *pia fraus*: “pious fraud”."]],"reflm:3497":["Also tautology.",[]],"reflm:3498":["The ground is something other than the consequence.\nNothing contains the ground in itself.\nPrior, posterior. Antecedent and consequent.",["“ground” renders ratio, “consequence” rationatum.","“the ground in itself” renders rationem in se ipso, which can also be read “its own ground in itself”."]],"reflm:3499":["Opposition logical, real.",["Latin: Oppositio Logica, realis."]],"reflm:3500":["In the right margin \nThe real ground of actuality is the efficient cause.",["“real ground” renders realgrund; “actuality” Wirklichkeit; “efficient cause” Wirkende Ursache."]],"reflm:3501":["Connected are those of which, one being posited, another is posited.",["“Connected” renders connexa."]],"reflm:3502":["That which not being posited, another is not posited, is a condition.",[]],"reflm:3503":["the consequence being posited, the ground is not posited, but required.",["“consequence” renders rationatum, “ground” ratio."]],"reflm:3504":["ground either analytic (logical) or synthetic (real ground).",["The words in parentheses are Kant's German in a Latin line: logisch, Realgrund."]],"reflm:3505":["that which being posited another is necessarily posited, is a ground.",[]],"reflm:3506":["the consequence not being posited, the ground is not posited.",["The Latin non rationato posito may also be read “a non-consequence being posited”."]],"reflm:3507":["That which being posited (if I posit it) another is posited (s determinately) according to a rule, is a ground.\nfrom the consequence to the ground the inference is not valid.\ncondition: that which not being posited another is not posited.",["“(if I posit it)” renders Kant's German gloss wenn ich es setze.","“inference” renders consequentia."]],"reflm:3508":["ideal and real.",[]],"reflm:3509":["*nexus* is also of coordinated things, not always of subordinated things.",["“coordinated things”, “subordinated things” render coordinatorum, subordinatorum."]],"reflm:3510":["Something, Nothing, no thing.",["“no thing” renders kein Ding."]],"reflm:3511":["In the left margin \nOf possibility. Of the something. Of the thing.",[]],"reflm:3512":["in certain, in all regard (*respectu*).",["“regard” renders Betracht."]],"reflm:3513":["connected (g mutually) either through agreement or opposition.",["“connected” renders connexa; “mutually” inuicem; “agreement” consensum."]],"reflm:3514":["In a series (g of coordinated things and) of subordinated things.",[]],"reflm:3515":["Primitive and subaltern ground.",["“ground” renders ratio."]],"reflm:3516":["primitive or subaltern ground.",[]],"reflm:3517":["a ground which is itself a consequence.",["“ground” renders ratio, “consequence” rationatum."]],"reflm:3518":["Of condition in general — limiting.",["“limiting” renders einschränkende."]],"reflm:3519":["Above and \nthe ground either suffices for the consequence or [sibim] also for itself; if the former: it is *secundum quid* sufficient, if the latter: it is *simpliciter*.",["The editorial location note “Über und” is cut off in the source; [sibim] is a deleted, unfinished sibimet.","*secundum quid*: “in a certain respect”; *simpliciter*: “absolutely”."]],"reflm:3520":["logical predicate. Determines:",[]],"reflm:3521":["He who is a human being is determined with regard to learnedness, but not as human being. I.e. not the logical *subiect* human being, but the real one, namely the one who is thought through the concept of humanity.",["“human being” renders Mensch, “humanity” Menschheit; “learnedness” Gelehrsamkeit."]],"reflm:3522":["that in which there is the relation of ground to consequence is called determining.\nwhat is of itself undetermined is determined by another.",["“relation” renders respectus."]],"reflm:3523":["logical predicate or determination.",["Latin and German mixed: praedicatum logicum oder determination."]],"reflm:3524":["See p.12.\nOn M §. 36 »realitas — negatio«:\nof itself, not relative to another concept, which is logical, not metaphysical or transcendental.",["“transcendental” renders transscendentalis."]],"reflm:3525":["Absolute and respective concepts.\nas those of the ground and of the consequences. are respective. Only the respective ones can be made distinct.",["“consequences” renders folgen."]],"reflm:3526":["A book in the library\n*deest* — negation: is missing. What is not in its place. (g *abest*: what is not in its time.)\n*deficit* — when there is missing what ought to be, i.e. negation opposed to perfection (g is lacking).\n*abest* — when [it] one knows where it otherwise is (g is not present).",["“is missing”, “is lacking”, “is not present” render Kant's German fehlt, Mangelt, ist nicht zugegen.","*deest*, *deficit*, *abest*: “is wanting”, “is deficient”, “is absent”."]],"reflm:3527":["This is a logical *respectus*.\n(s *realis*.)",["*respectus*: “relation”; *realis*: “real”."]],"reflm:3528":["The essence of a thing (adjectivally).\nA being: *ens*.",["“essence” and “being” both render Wesen."]],"reflm:3529":["proper\nFurther, from σ-ψ:\nare those whose concepts can be reciprocated with the subjects.",["“proper” renders eigenthümliche (also “peculiar”); “subjects” renders Subiecten."]],"reflm:3530":["From the assertoric to the problematic, but not conversely.",[]],"reflm:3531":["ψ−χ?? M 16.\nFrom the negation of the assertoric (g antecedent) not to the negation of the problematic.",[]],"reflm:3532":["*nullo*\nBetween §. 59 and §. 60 stand the words:\nfor if the upper proposition is true, then from a particular nothing at all can follow.",["“upper proposition” renders obere Satz, i.e. Obersatz (major premise); “true” renders war (wahr)."]],"reflm:3533":["i.e. from the concept neither of thoroughgoing determination nor of a reality can one not infer to existence.",["The double negative (weder … noch … nicht) is kept as in the German.","“existence” renders Daseyn."]],"reflm:3534":["»*omni, non tollitur possibilitas*« one cannot say; what does not exist at all must also not be possible in every respect.",["*omni, non tollitur possibilitas*: “by all, possibility is not removed”.","muß auch nicht can be read “must not” or “need not”; “in every respect” renders in aller Absicht."]],"reflm:3535":["that which contains reality",[]],"reflm:3536":["Thing and its predicates",[]],"reflm:3537":["chimera, fantasy, ideal.",[]],"reflm:3538":["determination of a being, insofar as what is posited or how many times something is posited is considered.",["“being” renders ens."]],"reflm:3539":["The difference of one thing from another through the plurality of the homogeneous that is contained in [it] both is magnitude. The difference of the heterogeneous is quality.",["“plurality” renders Vielheit; “magnitude” Größe."]],"reflm:3540":["that which is possible through one thing posited several times is a *qvantum*.",[]],"reflm:3541":["better *quanta*; that something is a *Qvantum* can be cognized absolutely, but how great (*qvantitas*), only relatively.",["“cognized” renders erkennen."]],"reflm:3542":["*quantum* and *quantitas*.",[]],"reflm:3543":["In the right margin, \nTaken substantively.\nThe adjectival is hypothetical.\nFurther, likewise in the right margin, above line 3, beside M §. 74 »Quicquid — est«, probably also on »unitas categorica«:\nquantitative and qualitative",[]],"reflm:3544":["That of which there are not several of the same kind is unique.",["“kind” renders Art; “unique” einzig."]],"reflm:3545":["Nonsense in which there is method.",[]],"reflm:3546":["On »Sectio VII. Perfectum.«, or \nthe perfection of a being in general is the totality of reality.",["The editorial note “bzw.” (“or”) is cut off in the source.","“totality” renders omnitudo."]],"reflm:3547":["they agree either logically, insofar as they do not contradict, or really, insofar as they are not really opposed to one another.",[]],"reflm:3548":["On »Sectio VII«:\nperfection taken adjectivally or substantively. likewise unity, truth, possibility.",[]],"reflm:3549":["In every way contingent",["Latin: Omnimode contingens."]],"reflm:3550":["Something is contingent either to a thing (i.e. it is not necessary to it), e.g. composition to the elements, or breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3551":["There are contingent essences, namely the essences of all contingent things, e.g. motions.",["“essences” renders Wesen, which can also be read “beings”."]],"reflm:3552":["Unconditioned necessity is both with regard to the external and internal det breaks off.",["“det” renders the unfinished Best (Bestimmung, “determination”)."]],"reflm:3553":["Thoroughly necessary.",["“Thoroughly” renders Durchaus (also “absolutely”)."]],"reflm:3554":["Either with regard to one determination or to all: 1. the opposite is internally or absolutely not thinkable, i.e. there cannot be more necessary beings.\nFurther:\ni.e. under the condition under which something necessarily belongs to a thing, this thing cannot be determined in another way; but it can be subjected to another condition. e.g. A bell cannot be otherwise than [int] internally hollow and open; but the same metal can nevertheless be melted into a bar.",["“[int]” renders the deleted, unfinished [inn].","“more necessary beings” can be read “further necessary beings” or “more than one necessary being”."]],"reflm:3555":["After the opening word of M §. 125 Kant wants inserted:\n*perdurabilis*",["*perdurabilis*: “perdurable”."]],"reflm:3556":["the subject is altered, the determinations are varied, the state breaks off.",["“state” renders status."]],"reflm:3557":["Beside and \nWhether matter, motion is reality.\nWhether vice is *realitas* or *negatio*.\nnegations in objects are limitations.\n*realitas affecta negationibus*.\nPlurality of reality and degree.\nSpace and time have no degree.\nExtensive, protensive and intensive magnitude.",["The editorial location note “Neben und” is cut off in the source.","“objects” renders Obiekten; “limitations” Schranken; *realitas affecta negationibus*: “reality affected with negations”."]],"reflm:3558":["*opposito realis*",["*opposito* is probably a slip for oppositio: “real opposition”."]],"reflm:3559":["In the lower margin, \nlimitation.",["“limitation” renders limitatio."]],"reflm:3560":["Occamists (g Thomists) nominalists. Scotists realists.",[]],"reflm:3561":["nominalists and realists.",[]],"reflm:3562":["not 2/3 + 3/4 = 1. Of the 21 quarters of the city of Paris.",["“quarters” renders viertheilen, “fourth parts”, which also means city quarters."]],"reflm:3563":["Kant alters the definition at the beginning of M §. 157 so that it reads:\nA composite altogether the same as its parts is a whole.",[]],"reflm:3564":["That which cannot exist except either as part or as consequence or as accident.",["“consequence” renders rationatum."]],"reflm:3565":["totality either of a *quantum* or of a composite as such. Part of a whole either of a *quantum* or of a composite.",["“as such” renders qva talis."]],"reflm:3566":["*qvantum* of *qvantitas* — — indeterminate.\ndeterminate is *qvantitas*.\n[assignable parts are]\na givable *qvantum* is that which [whole] as complete can be actual. assignable parts are those which can in themselves be clearly cognized. [whose parts] that in which the multitude of assignable parts is indeterminate (whose parts cannot be given in every way except through the whole), is continuous.",["“givable” renders dabile; qvantum dabile est can also be read “a qvantum is givable”.","“indeterminate” renders indeterminatae, which agrees with qvantitatis."]],"reflm:3567":["It is false; for that which is greater than nothing, of it a part is equal to nothing; therefore nothing is part of something, which is contradictory.",["“is contradictory” renders repugnat."]],"reflm:3568":["Absolutely (i.e. in a certain respect) possible.\nIn a certain respect also possible.",["The gloss “h.e. respectu qvodam” after Absolute is kept as it stands."]],"reflm:3569":["determines strictly, broadly.\nlatitudinarian, who admits exceptions.",[]],"reflm:3570":["the more it approaches universality and lacks restrictions.",[]],"reflm:3571":["The intensive magnitude of laws rests on necessity, i.e. the lack of exceptions. No rule without exception, but a law is without exception.",["The second sentence is Latin: Nulla regula sine exceptione, sed lex est absque exceptione."]],"reflm:3572":["*substratum*, stable.",[]],"reflm:3573":["The real subject is substance.",[]],"reflm:3574":["an accident can be a logical subject.",[]],"reflm:3575":["*Phaenomenon substantiatum*",["“substantiated phenomenon”."]],"reflm:3576":["If these were true, the state of no being would be immutable.",[]],"reflm:3577":["On the heading: »Sectio VIII. Status«:\nWhether it is all the same to say: a thing has altered, or: its state (if need be inner) has altered.",["“has altered” renders habe sich verändert; “if need be” allenfalls."]],"reflm:3578":["Properly it is not a *modus* but the substance that is altered. For that which is altered remains; alteration is only the change of its determinations.",["“alteration” renders Veranderung, “change” Wechsel."]],"reflm:3579":["On the last two sentences of M §. 210:\nThe [human] body (s the lifeless,) works; the living (g makes) acts (s *operatur*); the free does (s *facit*).",["“[human]” renders the deleted, unfinished [Menschl].","“works” renders wirkt, “makes” machet, “acts” handelt, “does” thut."]],"reflm:3580":["substance actuating substance (g producing) creates*.\n(g as cause of duration conserves.)\nsubstance adding a complement to an acting substance concurs.\n*(s as cause of the origin of a substance creates.)",["“acting” renders actrici; “origin” ortus."]],"reflm:3581":["If all undergoing of the substance is properly the determination of the activity of the undergoing subject, then no substance can be determined internally even by God to other and greater *accidentibus* than are possible from its nature; otherwise another fundamental force and substantiality would be produced in it, and thus it would be recreated, and not be the identity of the subject.",["“undergoing” renders Leiden and leidenden (passio, also “suffering”); “fundamental force” Grundkraft; “recreated” umgeschaffen."]],"reflm:3582":["The inner possibility of a force (s of acting) is the faculty. e.g. Someone can have a great faculty for science, but has as yet no force of acuity etc. etc.",["“force” renders Kraft, “faculty” Vermögen, “acuity” Scharfsinnigkeit."]],"reflm:3583":["The internally sufficient ground for it is force; if it is also externally sufficient, then it is living force; if it is externally insufficient, then it is dead force. Dead force on account of the hindrance is *conatus*.",[]],"reflm:3584":["Faculty and force,\n*potentia* — *actus*.\nForce acts, the faculty does not.",[]],"reflm:3585":["The inner principle of the possibility of acting is the faculty*, e.g. machine**.\n*(g The inner determining ground of the faculty for acting is striving (*conatus*). The faculty with a striving to act is force. Force, insofar as it is internally sufficient for the actuality of the action, but without effect, is dead force; this always proves a hindrance. (s Force sufficient internally as well as externally is living.))\n**(s The faculty together with a determining ground of it (e.g. an applied lever) is force. Force (thus faculty and determining ground of it together) [is brings] is for itself sufficient for the actuality of the action. Thus *posita vi* (g *per se*) *ponitur actio*. But another can conflict with the determining ground [of the] in the force.[This] The state of a force in this conflict is striving (*conatus*) and that which contains the ground of the action's not arising, the hindrance. Every *conatus* is an action, against which, however, something counteracts. Hence it is dead force. Living force is not greater than the *conatus*, but brings about a desired effect.\nNB. For the *conatus* a time is required, if it is to become living force. For if the support were taken from a weight and gravity were not to act further, then the one impression would give no motion.)",["“determining ground” renders Bestimmungsgrund, “striving” Bestrebung, “lever” Hebebaum, “impression” Eindruk.","*posita vi … ponitur actio*: “the force being posited … the action is posited”.","“proves a hindrance” renders beweiset … Hindernis, which can also be read “proves (the presence of) a hindrance”."]],"reflm:3586":["To act means: to contain the sufficient ground of the *accidentis*.\nThe possibility of the action is the faculty.\nThe internally sufficient ground of the action is force.\nIt is dead if [externally] a ground of the opposite is there.\nThe opposed ground is *impedimentum*; if it is another substance: *resistentia*.",["*impedimentum*: “hindrance”; *resistentia*: “resistance”."]],"reflm:3587":["Concordant forces: Of these one adds to the other something homogeneous, e.g. solar attraction to the moon. uncombined: there the accession is 0. Opposed: –a; take away just as much, thus the difference of the adding from the taking away is double the amount of the *accessorii*. Hence the danger of diminution is always greater than the gain of the addition.",["“uncombined” renders nicht verbundene; “accession” Hinzukunft; “adding” Hinzuthuung."]],"reflm:3588":["The substance, insofar as it contains the sufficient ground of a determinate *accidentis*, acts; insofar as it contains the ground of accidents in general, has a force. This is either internally sufficient or insufficient; in the latter case it is a mere faculty, in the former it is either externally insufficient, i.e. a *conatus*, or externally sufficient: *vis viva*.\nWith every dead force another opposed one is also dead.",["“accidents” renders accidentzen; *vis viva*: “living force”."]],"reflm:3589":["Where there is force, there is also action (in lifeless beings), but not therefore at once effect, because an inner or outer resistance [a] is the counteraction of a force which cancels the effect of the former. In living beings faculty and force are distinguished; in lifeless ones e.g. a rope has as much faculty to bear a load held on it as it exercises actual force of cohesion.",["“cohesion” renders Zusammenhang (elsewhere “interconnection”); “cancels” aufhebt."]],"reflm:3590":["The real relation of a substance as cause to [its] the [there] through it possible effect is called force. This before the effect is called *conatus*; without it, because a resistance occurs, *vis mortua*.",["“[there]” renders the deleted [da], apparently the start of dadurch.","*vis mortua*: “dead force”; “without it” (ohne dieselbe) may refer to the effect or to the force."]],"reflm:3591":["At the very top of the page:\nBecause all *nexus* is logical or real. so is breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3592":["origin is existence which follows non-existence.",["“origin” renders ortus."]],"reflm:3593":["Arising and perishing, beginning and ceasing. The latter goes to accidents.",["“Arising and perishing” renders Entstehen und vergehen, “beginning and ceasing” anheben und aufhören."]],"reflm:3594":["The substance *phaenomenon* is perdurable.",["Latin: Substantia phaenomenon est perdurabile."]],"reflm:3595":["Above the final word of M §. 233:\nComposite said quantitatively, qualitatively.",[]],"reflm:3596":["simile of the bow.",[]],"reflm:3597":["tensed.",["“tensed” renders angespannt (also “drawn”, “strained”)."]],"reflm:3598":["Space and time have boundaries.",["“boundaries” renders terminos."]],"reflm:3599":["Sameness and difference belong to the relation of comparison.",["“Sameness” renders Einerleyheit, “difference” Verschiedenheit."]],"reflm:3600":["Generically, specifically and numerically identical.",[]],"reflm:3601":["totally the same —\nnumerically the same\n(g congruent) (g as to internal things —\nin different relations)\nas to quantity —\nare also as to quality, because only homogeneous things can be equal,\nas to quality —\ncan be also as to quantity, and then would be congruent.",[]],"reflm:3602":["the boundary of space is distinguished from limits.\nA spherical surface is properly not bounded anywhere. but is bounding.",["“boundary” renders terminus, “limits” limitibus; “bounded”, “bounding” terminata, terminans."]],"reflm:3603":["Because the boundary is a ground of the limitations, it is no part, in that it adds nothing to the magnitude, but rather cuts off; the cutting off, however, belongs to both that is cut off; thus the boundary is common.",["“boundary” renders Grentze / Grenze, “limitations” Schranken."]],"reflm:3604":["The quality of the boundary of the extended is the figure.",[]],"reflm:3605":["The community of boundaries. Two points do not touch, therefore *distant*.",["*distant* is Latin: “they are distant”."]],"reflm:3606":["Two points lie always in one line, three points [in a plane and 4 in a corporeal space.], through which no common straight line can be drawn, lie in a plane, and four points, of which through three no straight line can go, lie in corporeal space. Yet from these *data* two bodies are possible, namely according to two opposed regions.\nThis means as much as this. If one point is given, then from a given distance another point can be given in infinitely many positions (g i.e. it lies in a spherical surface). If two points are given, then the third point lies in a circle*. If three are given, then the fourth point lies in two positions on opposite sides**. All position of the points can be determined geometrically from certain *data*, but the last position of the fourth point only with regard to our body.\nOne must therefore be acquainted with the whole corporeal space in order to draw from a given point with a given distance a line; for this can be drawn only to a determinate point in the surface.\n*(g the third point is determined, but not its position.)\n**(g for then no rotation is possible.)",["\"regions\": Gegenden; \"position\": Stelle; \"position\" in \"All position\": Lage; \"distance\": Weite.","\"plane\": Fläche; \"surface\": Oberfläche; \"must be acquainted with\": kennen."]],"reflm:3607":["*Continua* have not only limitations, but also boundaries.",["\"limitations\": Schranken; \"boundaries\": Grentzen."]],"reflm:3608":["The mathematical concepts are unity, plurality, number, infinity, (the relations) [equality, inequality], composition of one magnitude out of another one or several times or out of its parts, or also out of many magnitudes *per synthesin vel positivam vel negativam* (through synthesis either positive or negative); hence the idea of equality, inequality and relations. The objects of mathematics are space, time, motion or any other ground either as a *totum* or as a ground estimated, the former through the many that is in it, the latter through that which is posited by it.",["\"objects\": obiecte (the Latinate word).","\"estimated\": geschätzt; \"relations\": Verhältnisse."]],"reflm:3609":["If [magnitudes of] the quality of a magnitude is of such a kind that the action [the *coordinatio*] of raising one of them, which has a certain extensive magnitude, intensively to the second degree is a multiplication, then there are no more than 3 *dimensiones*. But where the magnitude |\ngrows not intensively, *multiplicando*, but extensively, *addendo*, there is only one *dimensionem*. Of things that are at the same time one can represent the generation of the multitude out of unity as in flux.",["The German construction \"die Handlung ..., die eine derselben ... zu erheben\" is irregular (a relative \"die\" followed by an infinitive); rendered as \"of raising\".","\"multitude\": Menge; \"generation\": Erzeugung."]],"reflm:3610":["It is remarkable that with the point, line and plane, when they move (g perpendicularly), the motions of the elements do not coincide with one another, but they do with the body. It is also not necessary that it happen perpendicularly, if only it is not in the direction in which the motion of the previous elements would meet. The question is: why then do they meet with the fourth dimension, namely that after the generation of a body. Does the ground of this not perhaps lie in the combinations in general?",["\"coincide with one another\": in einander fallen; \"meet\": zusammentreffen.","\"plane\": Fläche."]],"reflm:3611":["as if rest were an accident.",["Latin: qvasi qvies esset accidens."]],"reflm:3612":["Only in *phaenomenis*.",["Latin ablative plural: \"in appearances\"."]],"reflm:3613":["as quantity\nforgot, however, to strike out the second word of the text, »continuatio«, which with the new definition must of course drop away.",["Latin: ut quantitas.","The second line is the AA editor's remark; its subject (Kant) is unexpressed in the German."]],"reflm:3614":["in all parts of time",["Latin: omnibus partibus temporis (ablative without preposition)."]],"reflm:3615":["*ens a se* is not changed. For it is in no determination through others, hence not to something else.",["*ens a se*: a being from itself. Latin \"non mutatur\" is passive.","\"determination\": Bestimmung."]],"reflm:3616":["*respectus* and relation; the former: of comparison, the latter: of influences.",["\"relation\": relation (the Latinate German word), set against Latin *respectus*."]],"reflm:3617":["An accident, insofar as it is a cause, is not called efficient.\nA substance, insofar as its accident is a cause, is likewise not efficient.",[]],"reflm:3618":["either efficient or a *conditio sine qva non*",["*conditio sine qva non*: condition without which not."]],"reflm:3619":["Büffon seeks *nexus effectivos*, others final ones. Epicurus the former in the organization.",["Latin: nexus effectivos (effective connections); \"final ones\" renders \"finales\"."]],"reflm:3620":["On the concluding sentence of M §. 332:\nThe causality in the cause is not nobler than the effect.",["Latin: non est nobilior effectu.","\"causality\": causalitaet."]],"reflm:3621":["Estimable (worthy of estimation)\nInestimable (not capable of estimation),\njust as \"incorrigible\" has a twofold sense.",["\"Estimable\": Schätzbar; \"Inestimable\": Unschätzbar, which can mean both \"priceless\" and \"not capable of being estimated\".","\"incorrigible\": unverbesserlich, which can mean both \"incorrigible\" and \"not capable of improvement\"."]],"reflm:3622":["objective",["German: obiectiv."]],"reflm:3623":["Idea. Archetype. Model. Example. Similarity, analogy are different.",["\"Archetype\": Urbild; \"Model\": Muster; \"Example\": Beyspiel."]],"reflm:3624":["absolute universality of the concept.\nabsolute universality of the whole, i.e. the metaphysical universe of things. this would prove that there is only one single world, that the cause is the highest.",["Latin: universitas (\"universe\", also \"totality\") beside universalitas (\"universality\").","\"that the cause is the highest\": causam esse summam."]],"reflm:3625":["Nature and freedom.",[]],"reflm:3626":["*casus* respective. absolute.",["Latin *casus*: chance, or case; respectivus, absolutus."]],"reflm:3627":["Nature is here taken only adjectivally.",[]],"reflm:3628":["On the heading »Mundus optimus«:\nThe world as *summum bonum derivativum*.",["*summum bonum derivativum*: derivative highest good."]],"reflm:3629":["[All coercion is either a] All necessitation (g is either a necessitation) unwillingly (g or willingly,) either aesthetic or physical, a necessitation of the will itself breaks off?",["\"coercion\": Zwang; \"necessitation\": Nöthigung.","\"unwillingly\": wiederwillen; \"willingly\": Mit Willen.","\"breaks off\" (bricht ab) is the AA's note that the Reflexion stops here."]],"reflm:3630":[") M 277'.\nThe action, insofar as it arises from the faculty of desire in such a way that I can become conscious that I am not determined to it breaks off.",["\"faculty of desire\": Begehrungsvermögen; \"am not determined\": sey ... nicht bestimmt (subjunctive).","\"breaks off\" (bricht ab) is the AA's note that the Reflexion stops here."]],"reflm:3631":["Separation of the soul from the body.",[]],"reflm:3632":["removal of the *nexus* either virtual or local.",["Latin: remotio nexus vel virtualis vel localis; \"virtual\": virtualis, pertaining to force (vis)."]],"reflm:3633":["The separation of the soul from the body is either local or virtual.",[]],"reflm:3634":["Beside the second-to-last sentence of M §. 784:\nMetempsychosis of Michel Angelo, Gallilaei and Newton. (by Nelli in Florence.)",["\"by Nelli\": von Nelli, which could also be read \"from Nelli\"."]],"reflm:3635":["Internal predicate of God\nexternal\n{\nindependence dependence of all things on him",["Latin: Dei praedicatum internum / externum; independentia, dependentia omnium ab illo. The brace is the AA's layout."]],"reflm:3636":["logical perfection (*sufficientia*) or practical.",[]],"reflm:3637":["*nexus* with contingent things",["Latin: nexus cum contingentibus."]],"reflm:3638":["He is not changeable.\nBeing existence is not changeable; persistent.",["\"Seyn Daseyn\": \"Seyn\" can be read as the noun \"being\" or as the possessive \"his\" (sein); it may be a first word left uncorrected.","\"existence\": Daseyn; \"persistent\": beharrlich."]],"reflm:3639":["Because things subsist through God, it seems as if they subsisted in him.\nHe is not the soul of the world.",["Latin: Non est anima Mundi; the subject (\"he\" or \"it\") is unexpressed."]],"reflm:3640":["*Causatum maximum*. The highest created good.",["*Causatum maximum*: the greatest thing caused."]],"reflm:3641":["as active being he has nature.",["\"active being\": thatig Wesen."]],"reflm:3642":["On the word »theologia«, which he underlined\nectypal: from worldly affections. accommodated through reduction: analogical.",["Latin: ectypa: e mundanis affectionibus. per reductionem accomodata: analogica."]],"reflm:3643":["The cognition of the good (g of that which is good in the whole (in every actual relation)) from the idea of the whole as a ground of choice is wisdom.\nWhat is to be good in every respect must be derived from the idea of the most perfect whole.\n(g Prudence is the knowledge of the means to ends that are not completely in our power. Thus with regard to the opinion and freedom of other human beings.)\nThe wisdom of the human being consists in his doing without the ends that are not in his power.",["\"in every respect\": in aller absicht; \"relation\": Beziehung.","\"Prudence\": Klugheit; \"knowledge\": Kentnis; \"choice\": Wahl.","\"doing without\": entbehre."]],"reflm:3644":["Wisdom is directed to the good.\nPrudence to the useful.",["\"is directed to\": geht auf."]],"reflm:3645":["The faculty of cognizing the relation of all things* to the highest good** is wisdom; to the (g good in appearance) particular: prudence (not of God.).\n(s The adequacy of cognition to the highest good and its possibility is either merely theoretical — science, or moral: wisdom.)\n*(s The faculty of the cognition of the [means] best means to it is art-wisdom; the faculty of the cognition of the end together with the willing of it: moral wisdom.)\n**(s either to it itself or to the derived highest good. The former is the ground of the latter, but only in the divine understanding.)",["\"relation\": Verhaltnis; \"adequacy\": Angemessenheit; \"art-wisdom\": Kunstweisheit.","\"the willing of it\": dem Willen desselben, also \"the will for it\".","\"to it itself\": ihm selbst, which may refer to the highest good or to God (\"to him himself\")."]],"reflm:3646":[") M 359.\nWisdom in general is the perfection of cognition insofar as it is adequate to the whole of all ends (either to the physical or moral whole).",["\"is adequate\": angemessen ist."]],"reflm:3647":["Wisdom is the (g subjective) *principium* of the unity of all ends [a pr] according to reason, consequently happiness united with morality.",["\"happiness\": Glükseeligkeit; \"morality\": Sittlichkeit.","The deleted \"[a pr]\" is left as the AA prints it (probably \"a priori\" begun)."]],"reflm:3648":["The highest good has two parts. Which is the condition, which is the conditioned. Morality or happiness?",["\"Morality\": Sittlichkeit; \"happiness\": Glükseeligkeit."]],"reflm:3649":["Whether we can fathom the ends of the divine wisdom in nature and whether that is physicotheology? — He is the only Holy — Blessed — Wise.",["\"fathom\": ergründen; \"Blessed\": Seelige."]],"reflm:3650":["The divine understanding derives all cognition of the particular from the whole of all possibility and every particular end from the whole of all ends. Everything from itself.",[]],"reflm:3651":["M359':\n[Kl] Skill is the faculty of reason with regard to means to arbitrary ends, prudence with regard to the sum of all subjective ends.\nWisdom: the faculty of determining every particular end through the whole of all objective ends. |\nM 359: |\nHence »a human being« and »wise« is a contradiction, except in morality as a model.",["The deleted \"[Kl]\" is the beginning of \"Klugheit\" (prudence).","\"Skill\": Geschiklichkeit; \"arbitrary\": beliebigen (ends one pleases); \"sum\": Summe."]],"reflm:3652":["It is all the same whether many or few means lead to the end, if only the end is attained; superfluous means are in the human being only blameworthy because they hinder his other ends. Yet because the sufficient ground of the means is only in the end, nothing more can be in the former than what is required for the latter.",["\"bey den Menschen\" can be singular or plural; \"seine\" (his) suggests the singular.","\"only blameworthy because\": the \"nur\" may attach to \"tadelshaft\" or to the \"weil\" clause."]],"reflm:3653":["As means in and for itself (g in the execution) there can surely never be more than in the end, i.e. there is not more in the cause than in the effect. but as means in the intention there can be more than what is required for the end, from error. But there can be much present besides what fulfills the whole end, and the law of parsimony is not for God.",["\"in and for itself\": an und vor sich selbst; \"vor Gott\" can be read \"for God\" (vor = für) or \"before God\".","\"parsimony\": Sparsamkeit; \"be present\": daseyn."]],"reflm:3654":["The measuredness in means.",["\"measuredness\": Abgemessenheit (proportioning, exact measure)."]],"reflm:3655":["Not everything is ends or means, at least not everything good in the sequels of certain causes.",["\"sequels\": Folgen."]],"reflm:3656":["The divine wisdom is the ground of the agreement of his choice with itself without exceptions and collision from one plan.",["\"agreement\": Einstimung; \"choice\": Wahl."]],"reflm:3657":["The parsimony of the divine decree in the choice [of the] of that alone which is useful as a means to the highest represented good is no property of the divine wisdom. It is the property of the impotent one, who, if he does more in one case than what contributes something to some use, neglects other use.",["\"parsimony\": Sparsamkeit; \"decree\": Rathschluß; \"property\": Eigenschaft.","\"use\": Nutzen (also \"benefit\"); \"impotent one\": ohnmächtigen."]],"reflm:3658":["The objectively antecedent will is that whose [object] motive is what is universal in the objects.\nThe objectively consequent will is that whose motive is what is individual in the objects.",["Latin: voluntas obiective antecedens / conseqvens; \"motive\": motivum."]],"reflm:3659":["The best will determines [perfection] the particular volition in the totality, i.e. according to the human manner of speaking: in the universal, insofar as it is restricted through particular conditions.",["\"totality\": omnitudine; \"volition\": volitionem."]],"reflm:3660":["The divine will, insofar as it is directed to that which the object has in common with others that [not ge] he does not will, is called *antecedens*; insofar as it is directed to that whereby it distinguishes itself from all others, is called *conseqvens*.",["The deleted \"[not ge]\" breaks off inside a word (\"nicht ge...\"); its end is left untranslated.","\"it distinguishes itself\": \"er\" may refer to the will or to the object (Gegenstand)."]],"reflm:3661":["*voluntas antecedens* is the one that is directed to a universal concept of an object (as undetermined). *conseqvens*: to the determinate concept of the object.",["\"the one that\": der, masculine, agreeing with \"Wille\" rather than Latin voluntas.","\"object\": obiect."]],"reflm:3662":["The object of the highest intelligence is the highest good according to cognition and as author according to will.\nThe highest good in the whole and also the relation of each individual to it:\n1. the relation to the universal concept of the good. *voluntas antecedens*.\n2. to the thoroughly determined cognition of this world. *voluntas conseqvens*.",["\"object\": Gegenstand; \"relation\": relation.","\"thoroughly determined\": durchgangig bestimmten."]],"reflm:3663":["One could still ask whether reward is a matter of justice or of benevolence.",["\"benevolence\": Gütigkeit."]],"reflm:3664":["On M §. 907 Kant has made a sign after the words »bonum morale«, which he underlined, to which in the right margin the same sign before the word\n*meritum*\ncorresponds, which is evidently to take the place of the two words of the text. For Kant adds:\nFor whether *bonum morale* in general is merely [wisdo] wise goodness.",["All but the last line is the AA editor's description.","The last line is syntactically incomplete in the German (\"Denn ob ... ist\").","\"[wisdo]\" renders the deleted fragment \"[Weishei]\"."]],"reflm:3665":["Rewards are either gifts: gratuitous rewards\n—\n—\n—\nwages: — — owed.\nmoral: of wisdom,\npragmatic: of prudence.",["Latin: praemia, munera, mercedes, debita; \"owed\": debita (due)."]],"reflm:3666":["Punitive justice is distinct from vindictive justice; the former is exercised by him whose right, the other by him whose person has been injured.",["\"Punitive justice\": Strafgerechtigkeit; \"vindictive justice\": Rachgerechtigkeit (justice of revenge)."]],"reflm:3667":["God does not punish the offense to his person (*laesae maiestatis*), but, because the human being cognizes God only according to the analogy with human beings, the malice that the human being expresses, that he would be capable [it] of it in relation to other human beings. [Hence] Otherwise we would become confused in the infinity of the crime.",["\"[it] of it\": the deleted accusative \"sie\" is replaced by the genitive \"ihrer\", referring to Bosheit (malice).","*laesae maiestatis*: of injured majesty."]],"reflm:3668":["Punitive justice belongs to wisdom.",[]],"reflm:3669":["On the opening word of M §. 910:\ndistributive or vindicative",["Latin: distributiva vel vindicativa."]],"reflm:3670":["distributive justice is not instrumental, but final.",["Latin: iustitia distributiva non est instrumentalis, sed finalis."]],"reflm:3671":["Kant alters the first sentence of M §. 910 so that it reads:\nThe justice of God is not remunerative, but punitive; it is either pragmatic: of the ruler, or moral: of the judge, because it is sin.",["\"because it is sin\": quia peccatum est, which can also be read \"because sin has been committed\".","\"of the ruler\": des Regenten; \"of the judge\": des Richters (German in the Latin sentence)."]],"reflm:3672":["Whether there is a crime of injured divine majesty.",["Latin: Crimen laesae majestatis divinae."]],"reflm:3673":["All these properties are the moral [which], chiefly on the ground that God demands the interior of the human heart [conformable] in agreement with him and his presence.",["\"das Gott\" stands for daß Gott; \"die moralische\" lacks the plural ending.","\"conformable\": Gemaß (deleted); \"in agreement\": einstimmig; \"interior\": das innere."]],"reflm:3674":["He demands long-suffering among human beings in punishing.\nThe benevolence that postpones the punishment of the offender for his improvement.",["\"long-suffering\": Langmuth; \"benevolence\": Gütigkeit."]],"reflm:3675":["He punishes according to the order of nature in the whole only after a long time.\nHe punishes only in conformity with the infinite *progressus*.",[]],"reflm:3676":["He [wills] loves impartiality in the judgment of human beings.\nHe has no favorites. e.g. the Jewish people.",["\"in the judgment of human beings\": im Urtheil der Menschen, either judgment passed by or on human beings."]],"reflm:3677":["The human being cannot always derive the particular good that God distributes from the universal provision; hence he is entitled to regard it as a particular provision and to thank God for it, yet also to believe that in a way unknown to him this is nevertheless universal.",["\"provision\": Vorsorge (not Vorsehung, providence)."]],"reflm:3678":["He administers equity, i.e. before his court a transgression of equity is a violation of strict right.",["\"equity\": Billigkeit; \"strict right\": strengen rechts."]],"reflm:3679":["He judges from the standpoint of a human being, who is a limited being. The human being can regard many a thing as a preference that God gives him over others.",["\"preference\": Vorzug (also advantage); \"over others\": für andere, also \"for others\"."]],"reflm:3680":["He demands sincerity, which with regard to God is commonly very much lacking, in that one affects praises and devotions that one does not feel, out of fear pretends to believe much and applies oneself to flattery.\n(s God is open in his revelations necessary to us, not hidden, not enigmatic and ambiguous. He does not lead us into temptation.)",["\"sincerity\": Aufrichtigkeit; \"devotions\": Ergebenheiten."]],"reflm:3681":["He wills truthfulness among human beings.\n(s one must not say of God what is completely identical with the predicates through which we think his moral perfections.)",["\"truthfulness\": warhaftigkeit."]],"reflm:3682":["*beatus*. Self-sufficiency in well-being.",["\"Self-sufficiency\": Selbstgnugsamkeit; \"well-being\": Wohlseyn."]],"reflm:3683":["He is above the [nature] world as the author (nature) and as the lawgiver (freedom).",[]],"reflm:3684":["end without motives.",["Latin: finis absqve motivis."]],"reflm:3685":["actuation of the world and government.",["Latin: actuatio mundi et regimen."]],"reflm:3686":["On the last two sentences of M §. 950:\nThe preservation of its form conforming to the final end in time is called gubernation: administration.",["\"final end\": Endzwek; \"administration\": Verwaltung; \"its\": derselben, which may also be \"their\"."]],"reflm:3687":["not influx, which is [action] modification, not actuation of substance.",["Latin: non influxus, qvi est [actio] modificatio, non actuatio substantiae."]],"reflm:3688":["A continued beginning is a contradiction.",[]],"reflm:3689":["*Concursus* to nature or freedom.\nProvidence with respect to laws (determining them) is general, with respect to events is special.",["Latin: Providentia respectu legum (illas determinans) est generalis, eventuum est specialis."]],"reflm:3690":["He does not concur in the formal element of actions. He is not the author of evil according to the ordinary direction.",["Latin: Non concurrit ad formale actionum; the subject is unexpressed."]],"reflm:3691":["Permission. Allowing is not permission.",["\"Permission\": Erlaubnis; \"Allowing\": Zulassung."]],"reflm:3692":["Lawgiver at the beginning,\nRuler in the duration,\nJudge at the end. properly in the infinity of the progression.",["\"progression\": Fortganges."]],"reflm:3693":["who is not subjected to the power of another.",[]],"reflm:3694":["397. E I 689.\nIs he who [s] binds the creature in conformity with an absolute will, without * taking their use under the laws into consideration. Thus there are despotic and patriotic laws.\n*(g taking into consideration the agreement of their own will governed through reason, i.e. as machines.)",["\"binds\": verbindet (also \"obligates\").","\"their\": ihren, plural, though \"das Geschöpf\" is singular."]],"reflm:3695":["The will that, in regard to the general grounds [of the same] of willing, is determined to a certain action is *antecedens*. That which is determined in regard to the *individui*: *consequens*.",["*antecedens* / *consequens*: the antecedent and consequent will (voluntas antecedens, consequens), Kant's Latin kept.","*individui* is a Latin genitive (\"of the individual\"), kept as Kant wrote it after \"in Ansehung des\"."]],"reflm:3696":["Here God is represented as if he resolved upon everything possible, which part he would take out for his choice.",["\"he would\" is not in the German, which has only \"welchen theil ... herausnehme\"; the clause depends loosely on \"beschlösse\" (resolved, decided)."]],"reflm:3697":["A decree is consequent will, hence with respect to an individual.\nAntecedent will is that whose impelling cause is only something general, which is common to all objects of choice.\nThe decree [none] of founding this world is a decree of an individual, hence it is not universal. There is no universal decree. but a decree entire or total, different not from a [partial] particular one, but from a partial one.",["\"decree\" renders decretum; \"consequent/antecedent will\" voluntas consequens/antecedens; \"impelling cause\" caussa impulsiva; \"objects of choice\" obiecta arbitrii.","\"entire or total\" renders universum sive totale, as against universale (universal)."]],"reflm:3698":["is the highest good of the world, insofar as it is opposed to the highest good of an individual.",["\"highest good\" renders summum bonum; the subject is missing in the source."]],"reflm:3699":["Unconditioned without qualification the divine choice never is, but perhaps unconditioned in regard to the moral quality of the person, as despotism; for God's aim could after all be [and d] directed to the world's best in the whole, and this be the condition.* God cannot sacrifice a (g rational) creature to the world-whole, but may well permit that it sacrifice itself.\n*(g Here it is assumed that God uses the rational creature merely as means to the perfection of the whole. But there is no moral worth in the world where rational beings are used merely as means.)",["\"choice\" renders Willkühr (arbitrium); \"world's best\" renders weltbeste; \"world-whole\" Weltganzen.","\"as despotism\" (als despotism) may attach to \"unconditioned\" or to \"choice\"; left as ambiguous as the German."]],"reflm:3700":["Whether the divine will is in itself determined by motives, or is merely thought by us according to this analogy?",["\"motives\" renders Kant's foreign word motiven, not Bewegungsgrund."]],"reflm:3701":["Fate.",["\"Fate\" renders Verhängnis (fatum); it can also mean dispensation, doom."]],"reflm:3702":["as the end of the will",["tanquam fine voluntatis: fine is ablative (\"as by/with the end\"); the construction it depended on is missing."]],"reflm:3703":["You ask: [is] who is happier in the world, the virtuous or the vicious? if one investigates it, then among the advantages of the [virtuous] wicked there will always be something mixed in that the [vicious] virtuous does not desire and on account of which he would not want to exchange his state for that of the other; thus he is in fact more content with himself than one thinks. The ills that befall the virtuous do not properly befall virtue, but are common to all. If the general laws were to be restricted only to the relation of the pious and the godless, then say: which, then, are the pious. Will not the one take this one for deserving of punishment, but the other another |\nfor such, and would God's justice then be cognized by all. Bread is the reward not of virtue but of industry. If, finally, you give the human being everything good, say: are you then content, do you not always desire more, and will God well be able to find an end to your wishes. The true prize of virtue is the inner stillness of the soul, the other goods overthrow or corrupt it. Learning, posthumous fame, wealth: all do not have the true good with them. Thus virtue alone makes the true happiness, which in abundance as well as in want, in weeping as well as in gladness finds something that satisfies it. Since virtue thus finds no want, wishing counts for nothing.\nSelf-love, which combines itself with the love of God and of one's neighbor, constitutes the happiness of human beings. The greater the love, the further extended: the greater is the happiness. God begins with love at the whole and extends it to the parts, but human love begins from itself and spreads little by little over the whole. On such a one the earth smiles from all sides, and the godhead itself sees its image in his soul.",["\"the vicious\" renders Lasterhafte, \"the wicked\" Boshaften; \"ills\" Übel; \"prize\" Preis (also price).","\"overthrow or corrupt it\": sie may be the stillness or the soul (Seelen, old singular); the ambiguity is kept.","\"Thus virtue alone makes\": nur may also qualify \"makes\" or \"the true happiness\"."]],"reflm:3704":["S. I:\nOutline of optimism.\nOptimism is that doctrinal system [which from the presup|position\nof a supremely benevolent, wise and almighty original being] the [presence] ills of the world [with the concept] from the presupposition of an infinitely perfect, benevolent and almighty original being to [unite] justify, in that one convinces oneself that [of such nothing but that] notwithstanding all apparent contradictions, what has been chosen by this infinitely perfect being must nevertheless be the best among everything possible, and ascribes the presence of evil not to the choice of the divine good pleasure, but to the unavoidable necessity of the essential defects of finite things, [which] that, in that they have been brought along into the plan of creation without its fault through the decree of permission, are nevertheless through its wisdom and goodness so turned to the best of the whole that the displeasure which [they them before] the sight of them in particular can arouse is, through the compensation that the divine goodness knows how to arrange, perfectly made good in the whole, since thus this world is, among all that were possible through the divine power, the best, and that which piecemeal could be better, [the without] it might not through the bricht ab.\n[Herr von Leibnitz] One will, if one examines the outline that Leibniz gives of his principles [with] precisely, find that this idea expresses his opinion as precisely as possible. One need only consider this division into the necessary or metaphysical and into the contingent or hypothetically physical and hypothetically moral ills. One must consider his distinction between the antecedent and consequent will, of which the first [everything without] strives to exclude all evil, the second [through the] includes it in its plan as unavoidable consequence of the [finitude] eternal nature of things, then one will be convinced of the |\n[correctness] truthfulness of our portrayal. Leibniz represents the goodness of God, spreading itself without measure, as the pull of a stream that drives everything found in its sum total to motion with equal force, but carries along the heavy cargo ships, which possess more natural force of inertia than those of less mass, more slowly than these.\nS. II:\nHe represents God as he is ready to reveal himself with [his] the whole infinity of all properties in the works of creation, [but] and how he has a true displeasure at the ills, the sight of which so greatly disquiet the mind of the well-disposed. but he lets them hope, from the goodness, wisdom and power of this Lord, sufficiently cognized from other undeniable grounds, that the defects will be compensated in interconnection with advantages and that, [so much] though the ills may in the end also disfigure the [plan] best plan, [it] they could nevertheless in the whole [not] without still greater deviation not have been prevented. To be sure, freely acting beings could have avoided many an evil action, to the great good pleasure of God; but it was [not in relation to] an unavoidable necessity, among two ills, of which one is the lack of freedom, the other of the morally best, to choose the least, and there were also in the best plan other hindrances that could move God, out of apprehension of still greater absurdities, [to] to omit the arrangement of certain moving causes that could prevent some kinds of ill. In short. It was not possible otherwise, there had to be evil. Thanks be to the eternal wisdom, that he permitted the least and has carried everything in the whole out most gloriously to his praise. It is true, it seems to me, we do not yet see where the compensation will properly turn out that is to indemnify the whole for its |\npartial defects. but [getra] are you not assured that God is (g so), (g as he) must be in order to be able to expect everything good from him. In this case you can expect nothing other than, in the sequel, a complete satisfaction, at the least a complete justification of the divine justice and goodness.\nS. III:\nComparison of the doctrinal concept of Pope with optimism, and advantage of the former.\nLeibniz admitted that the deviations and defects which, as true ills, put the well-disposed in distress, were so in fact; but he reserved for himself to excuse the highest wisdom [of] (g which he cognized from other grounds, on account of) the permission. [The best] Thus the properties of God were put in safety for the benefit of those who have insight and docility enough to give assent to the metaphysical proofs of the divine existence. The remaining part of those who [on account of] at the sight of the world would gladly recognize the trace of God. remains in distress. Pope chose a way which, in order to make the beautiful proof of God [to] perceptible to all human beings, the most apt [is] among all possible is, and which, what precisely constitutes the perfection of his system, (g even) subjects all possibility to the dominion of an all-sufficient original being, under which things can have no other properties, not even such as one calls essentially necessary, that do not agree together perfectly for the ex|pression\nof its perfection. He goes through creation piecemeal, chiefly there where it seems most to lack agreement; yet he shows that every thing which we would gladly wish away out of the plan of the greatest perfection is also, considered by itself, good, and that one need not (g beforehand) have an advantageous prejudice about the wisdom of the ordering being in order [it as] to win assent for it. The essential and necessary determinations of things, the general laws, which are put in relation to one another through no [foreign, extorted from them] (g forced) union into a harmonizing plan, will adapt themselves as it were of themselves to the maintenance of perfect ends. Self-love, which has only one's own enjoyment as its aim and which evidently seems to be the cause of the moral disorder that we observe, is the origin of that beautiful [property] agreement that we admire. [Wealth can itself] Everything that benefits itself finds itself in the necessity of being at the same time useful to others. The general bonds which connect the whole together in an unexplored |\nS. IV: |\nway make it that the single advantages always relate to the advantage of the other things, and indeed through a natural sequence. Thus a general law of nature sets that love [fast through the drives] which preserves the whole fast, and indeed through such moving causes as naturally also produce that ill whose sources we would gladly see annihilated.\nCan one well, when one sees the essential constitutions in so general a natural harmony, suppose that what so adapts itself to an excellent whole is at the same time the cause of such evil bricht ab.\nOf the general perfection of the world-constitution in the physical and moral sense.\nThe foremost rule of the perfection of the world is that it [so] be complete in the highest degree, that everything be there that is possible and that neither in the chain of beings nor in the manifoldness of their variation anything be lacking that is only capable of existence; for there is for the world in general no greater infirmity than the nothing in any one of its parts. Hence the [revelation] field of the revelation of the divine power comprehends all genera of finite things and [through] with a kind of richness it extends itself even to the defects and loses itself not otherwise than through all stages of diminution from the highest degrees of perfection down to the nothing.\nLet us, upon this presupposition, hear with contempt the complaints of those to whom heaven, in their opinion, has allotted no pleasing lot of perfections. I would gladly, says one, have less [over] darkness in the understanding and less vehemence in the sensible inclinations, if I were only so fortunate as to have no [inclination to sin] other inclination than that for virtue. If most considered that, when they wish themselves such properties as presuppose an alteration of their nature, it means just as much as to desire that they not be at all and that in their place another being of the desired constitution might be present, |\nthen one would go into oneself, for self-love has an abhorrence of annihilation and is rather content with the state, as it may be allotted to it, than that it bricht ab mit der Seite.",["\"ills\" renders Übel, \"evil\" das Böse; \"defects\" Mängel; \"moving causes\" Bewegungsursachen; \"properties\" Eigenschaften, \"constitution(s)\" Beschaffenheit(en).","\"bricht ab\" and \"bricht ab mit der Seite\" (breaks off with the page) are the AA's notes, kept in German.","\"One need only consider\" renders \"Man darf ... erwegen\" (darf = may, or need); \"sets ... fast\" keeps the split verb setzt ... fest around the deletion."]],"reflm:3705":["S. I:\nDefects of optimism.\nLeibniz was right that he named his doctrinal edifice a theodicy or defense of the good cause of God. For in fact it is nothing other than a justification of God against the imputation that he might perhaps be the author of evil, through the assurance that, so far as it lies with him, everything is good and that it is at least not his fault if [everything] not every thing turns out as perfectly as it ought to turn out according to the wish of the upright.\nThe errors of this doctrinal concept are far too important for one to be able to stop at it. Leibniz [represents] represents the rules of (g having as their end) perfection as they conflict with one another in their exercise. He regards the exceptions as necessary defects and perceives the action befitting the highest wisdom in this, that it makes the choice on the side of the best. just as a seafarer throws a part of his wares overboard in order to save the ship and the rest. It is true, the wisdom and goodness of God triumph here over all reproaches. But what will one say of infinity (g and) of independence. From what causes may the essential determinations of things have that discord which makes the perfection, each of which in particular would increase the satisfaction of God, incompatible in one combination. What sort of [strange] inscrutable conflict is it between the universal will of God, which aims solely at the good, and the (g metaphysical) necessity[of the |\nmetaphysical properties], which will not adapt itself to it with complete agreement (g without exceptions). If the evils, through a fatality necessary in I know not what way, extort from God their permission, without having aroused [on their account] satisfaction in him, then they put this most blessed being in a certain kind of displeasure, which can indeed be mitigated to some degree by the justification of innocence on his side, but not quelled. If everything was good in the whole or is still good in the parts, then unfailingly the sight from all sides is the source of a true gratification. Why then must it be so arranged that everything in the parts |\nS. II: |\nbe unpleasant, only in order to arouse satisfaction in the whole. If God abhors vices and torments, [why] if he does not desire them, but only permits them: why then was it necessary that they must be present, [since they the] granted even that they cannot be excluded, in order not to make room for still greater defects; this excuse serves, to be sure, to acquit God of the fault, but it will never resolve the important doubt why the [necessary propert] essential necessity has something which [God against his] conflicts with the universal will of God and extorts from him the permission, without having gained his satisfaction. The whole error rests in this, Leibniz places the plan of the best world partly in a kind of independence, partly in a dependence on the will of God. [All] All possibility is spread out before God. God sees, reflects on, examines it; he is steered to one side by the determinations inherent in them according to the measure of their (g particular) perfections and to the other side according to that which they make up in combination; this comparison occasions his decree. [The world is really not so because God wants to have it so, but because it cannot be done, to make it |\notherwise. The independence of the eternal natures comes first. The dependence consists only in the plan, in which God strives to order them as well according to the rules of the best as their essential determinations permit.]\nThe world's essence [good] is not [merely good because] solely so because God wants to have it so [and], but because it was not possible otherwise, only ei breaks off, but still leaves room for 7 lines.\nS. III:\nThe second chief error of [doctr] optimism is that the evils and absurdities which are perceived in the world are excused only from the presupposition of the existence of God. and that one must therefore (g beforehand) believe that there is an infinitely benevolent and infinitely perfect being, before one can assure oneself that the world, which is assumed to be his work, is beautiful and regular, instead of the universal agreement [and] of the arrangements of the world, if they can be cognized in and for themselves, offering the most beautiful proof of the existence of God and of the universal dependence of all things on him. The surest and easiest proof, therefore, of the actuality of an all-sufficient, infinite[ being] benevolent and infinitely wise being, which is cognized from the consideration of the excellent arrangements that the world shows everywhere, is invalidated by the doctrinal edifice of Herr v. Leibniz. It seems to me that an Epicurus would answer him who built on this proof: If the agreement that you perceive in the world seems to you to prove [to] an ordering wisdom as its author, then you must admit that [that part] the world for the greatest part does not depend on it, because everywhere for more than half it contains absurdities and repugnant deviations within itself. I do not accept your subterfuge |\nthat of that wisdom which has perhaps ordered some parts [with] to prudent ends it is to be presumed that it has likewise hidden in the rest proofs of its supervision. I would rather, therefore, conclude: if[you] the wise higher cause has not been able to bring all things into a plan of harmonizing beauty, then at least not all things were, according to their properties, subject to its satisfaction, and [that] the eternal fate, which limits the [intention] power [of this deity] of the capable cause [only room to in] so greatly and extorts from it consent to gross evils, thereby robs it of all-sufficiency |\nS. IV: |\nand [in that it weighs it with the rest, sub] subjects it to that very same necessity.",["\"in die Schantze Schlagt\" (in die Schanze schlagen): to put at stake, sacrifice; rendered \"throws overboard\". Wohlgefallen = satisfaction, Vergnügen = gratification; Lehrgebäude = doctrinal edifice, Lehrbegriff = doctrinal concept.","\"unendlich[en Wesens] gütigen\": with the deleted ending the text read \"infinite being\"; with it struck it reads \"infinitely benevolent\".","\"nur ei bricht ab, läßt aber noch Raum für 7 Zeilen übrig\": \"ei\" is Kant's broken-off word; the rest reads as the editor's remark, translated as it stands. \"Der Welt wesen\": Wesen = essence or being."]],"reflm:3706":["S. I:\nIf, among the various predicates that may belong to a thing, existence could (g also) be counted as one of them, then certainly no proof could be demanded that would be more cogent and at the same time more comprehensible, in order to establish the existence of God, than the Cartesian one. For among all possible things there is one in which [all possible (g the highest) reality that is to be met with in one] all realities are to be met with that can be together in one being.[Among these] To these realities, i.e. truly positive predicates, existence also belongs; consequently existence belongs [some] to the most real of all beings on account of its inner possibility. One also objects in vain against this that [all reality only in thought with one another] such a possible thing includes existence within itself only in the understanding, i.e. is posited only as the thing itself is in thought, but not [in the real understanding] outside of thought, for in such a way we would have to say of all predicates that belong to a [thing] possible thing: they did not belong to it in fact, but were only posited in it in thought. This latter does indeed take place when one arbitrarily combines something with a concept that is not necessarily posited through it; e.g., thus wings are posited in thought on a horse in order to make a Pegasus, hence [the winged horse] wings belong to some horse only in thought. By contrast, where the connection |\nof a predicate |\nS. II: |\nwith a thing is not arbitrary, but is combined through the essence of the things themselves, there it does not belong to it because we think it in it, but it is necessary to think it in it, because it belongs to it in itself. For which reason I cannot say that the equality of the angles with two right ones belongs to a triangle only in thought, but rather in itself. Nor does it hinder this that I merely think such a possible thing; for [I] it is in itself always something, although no one thinks it, and the predicate belongs to it in itself, although no one combines it with it. So it is also with existence, if it could be regarded (g as) a predicate of things. For it would necessarily belong, among everything possible, to that [i.e. that thing will necessarily exist] in which all reality is, i.e. [to a] a most real being will necessarily exist, and its possibility would include actuality. Also, if without my thoughts or those of every thing existence [no predicate of the] did not belong to the most perfect being of all, the thought of this being would be false in general. For if it is correct, then it can represent no other predicates than those which also take place in the thing without this thought.\nS. III:\nthe question here is not whether more is posited (g in general through an actual thing) than through a non-actual one, but whether more predicates are posited in it; now [in a benevolent good one as such insofar as he is considered as existing] in the goodness of God, insofar as it is considered as existing, no more is posited than in the goodness that lies in his possibility; but the difference consists only in this, that in the first case the |\nthing itself with what is contained in it, but in the second the *respectus* of this manifold toward one another are posited.\nThe most essential difficulty is presumably this, that our linguistic usage, insofar as it seems to express our most precise idea [and], always designates existence like a predicate, which has the look of arising from the immediate constitution of our concept. I say by an existing thing: existence belongs to a thing [I] etc: I consider the existence of things separately and distinguish it from possibility. This constitution of our judgments deserves very much to be weighed. That is not called philosophizing, when one merely seeks to establish that something is a delusion, a deception of the understanding, but one must also learn to have insight into how such a deception would be possible. This semblance is itself a [tru] actual appearance in the nature of our mind, and I must either be able to explain it, or I have cause to fear that my judgment, which passes off [this] one or another opinions as a delusion, might itself be such a semblance.\nS. IV:\nI now ask: if existence is not a predicate of things, how then I can make use of the expression 'existence' at all; for this distinguishes something in the thing from the thing itself, which can be ascribed to it. e.g. [This] To a certain thing actuality belongs. Because one and the very same thing can be posited in different ways, this positing itself seems to be a mark of the difference, which one could ascribe to a thing as a predicate or separate from it. But it is certain that this difference depends merely on how this thing is posited with everything belonging to it, and not: what is posited in it. According to the difference of the way in which the thing is now posited, something different is also posited thereby: namely through its predicates in relation to the subject merely relations and |\nthe possibility, [through the] through the thing, insofar as it is posited *absolute*, the thing itself. [But because we that through which we] We therefore do not distinguish the possible from the actual through a predicate that belongs to the one more than to the other.\nThe concept of an existing thing can never be changed into a judgment of which the thing would be the subject and existence the predicate.\nRather, [the] something existing must be the subject and everything that belongs to it its predicates.",["\"existence\" renders both Daseyn and the Latinate Existentz; Wirklichkeit = actuality; allerrealestes / allervollkommenstes Wesen = most real / most perfect being of all.","\"überhaupt falsch\": either \"false in general\" or \"false at all\". \"eines jeden Dinges Gedancken\": literally \"the thoughts of every thing\".","Blendwerk = semblance; Täuschung = deception; Wahn = delusion; \"das ansehen hat\" = has the look (Ansehen, not Erscheinung)."]],"reflm:3707":["S. I:\nPreparation\nOn the certainty and uncertainty of cognition in general.\nUncertainty is either an uncertainty of the things or of insight. Although the objects of cognition are in themselves certainly that which they are, so [so] one can nevertheless ascribe uncertainty to these things, insofar as from that [Din] which one cognizes in them and which is thus given, nothing is settled about the remainder, which one seeks, one may [dasje] cognize these *data* as perfectly as one ever will. If in a mixture of three different metals (g whose specific gravity is given, in addition) the weight of the whole lump and its hydrostatic relation to water is given, then [is] one may cognize these *data* as one will, [so] it always remains uncertain here in what relation they are [among] mixed with one another, and indeed for this reason, because the cognized pieces |\nare in themselves of such a kind that from them this relation remains undetermined. This kind of uncertainty, which one could call the objective, must be encountered in the cognition of every cognition which is limited. He who does not cognize everything, from his pieces of cognition something must (g as to its constitution) remain unsettled, he may also make comparisons with this cognition as he ever will. [Es kommt nur d]\nNow if one cognizes [diese Er] that from certain *data* which one knows certain other [Best] pieces are undetermined, then [is] insofar no error can take place in our cognition, and the same is therefore not (g objectively) uncertain. If the apparent diameter of a star is known, but the distance unknown, then [is] it remains uncertain which is the true magnitude of the star, although [woferne man nur nicht weiter ge] from this uncertainty alone no error can arise. Likewise if in the observation |\nS. II: |\nof the angles it is once known that an error of two seconds could not be noticed, then all that which depends more or less on such a magnitude is undetermined from the observation, and, if one cognizes this as such, then to that extent no error can take place.\nSince uncertainty consists in the possibility of erring, i.e., of passing a judgment which is false, so all grounds of this possibility will be either negative or positive are, namely: they consist either in this, that grounds for a (g certain) true [cognition] judgment are lacking, or, that there are positive grounds to judge, which judgments are nevertheless not in conformity with the constitution of the things. The former ground in itself is not sufficient to understand the possibility of error thereby. For from this, because I do not know certain things, it does not yet follow that I could pass a false judgment. For presupposing that I cognize that I would have to know these pieces in order to judge, then I would, [since] when I find I do not know them, not want to judge and be safeguarded against all error. (g With) To the astronomer, as long as he knows of a comet the apparent diameter, to be sure, but not the [distance] parallax, it is insofar objectively uncertain how far and how large it is, but it is impossible that he can err in this, as long as |\nhe is conscious that he could not judge. Also this [Ungewishei] ignorance of certain given pieces is not even then a ground of the possibility of erring in itself, when the human being is not even conscious of this indeterminacy; for as long as there is no ground which occasions him to judge [so] in a thing about which he does not know enough, so long is he safeguarded against all judgment. Hence the common man is safeguarded against many errors; for, since he is unconcerned with regard to most things after which the learned strive so eagerly, even if he hears the questions that are raised, and most questions never come into his thoughts, there is nothing that should occasion him to judge. |\nS. III: |\nAccordingly one sees that uncertainty in the subjective sense (g also) presupposes a certain ground [is] for passing judgments. If to this is added uncertainty in the objective sense, of which, however, one is not conscious.\nHence with all the drive to pass a judgment, if consciousness of objective uncertainty is added to it, error is impossible, as one can see in geometry. The greater the desire to judge or the habit of judging is. We have adduced objective uncertainty only insofar as they are taken together with the [subjective] inner grounds for judging, in order to make the subjective possibility of erring comprehensible. But even if this desire is the same, then [is] the grounds of distinction of the true from the false are nevertheless either hidden and, relative to the agreements which a false cognition has with a true one, small, or not. (Since we infer from analogy, many agreements can be a ground of errors, e.g., reason of animals). In the case where one has many but not all grounds of distinction, error is possible only through an ignorance of the indeterminacy of the cognition from these grounds and the inclination to judge.\nCertainty must be just as possible in philosophical cognition as in mathematical and, as concerns the degree for conviction, be adequate to it. One cannot say that one certainty is greater |\nthan the other; nothing is ever more certain than that which is certain. But as concerns the kind, it concerns not certainty but clarity.\n(s All uncertainty is a possibility of erring. To err means to pass a false judgment. The possibility of erring is either under a potential or an actual condition. The first is understood thus, that one could err if one wanted to judge; the second: that there is also, from actual grounds, a conditioned possibility of judging, where it is nevertheless possible to err. The potential possibility of erring rests on the constitution of the things: namely where from that which one cognizes of them it is not determined what belongs to them or not; the actual condition of uncertainty is the ground for judging in which, after all, a potential uncertainty is lodged. This ground for judging [lies either in the] is lodged in the [Mang] apparent similarity of the procedure in a false cognition with that in a true one. As, first: Where I do not notice that something has been forgotten in the marks of the thing, there nothing has been forgotten. 2. Things that are similar in certain pieces are similar in others. 3. What belongs to many belongs to all.\nIn the manner of designating: where signs of [the same] similar things are regarded as the same.)\n(s The uncertainty of that of which various causes are possible. Existence of bodies.)\nOn S. I and II Kant had left free beneath the text a margin of about 4 cm across the whole width of the page. On S. I he subsequently filled this free space with additions as well, and indeed, as the content proves, still within phase γ. The additions show small differences in hand and ink. They are printed in the probable order of their origin.\n(s Means of mathematics: to take the *medium* from many.)\n(s Provisional judgments.)\n(s On philosophical opinions and hypotheses,\non noting the grounds why an opinion is uncertain.)\n(s Uncertainty is of a twofold kind: either where one does not find sufficient grounds of assent in the thing itself, or where, notwithstanding all of one's conviction, one is apprehensive, from the expectation of similar cases, of erring in calculating.)\n(s Uncertainty is either a possibility of erring under the condition that one wanted to judge of that for which there are no *data*, or a conditioned possibility of judging in that for which one has no *data*.)\n(s Immediately certain propositions: before one makes a concept distinct, whereby, however, one begins this.)\n(s Immediately certain proposition: many thinking ones cannot bring forth one thought.)\n(s Immediately certain proposition. can only be when without the predicate the subject cannot be thought, not when it seems one could not deny it.)",["\"thing\" renders both Sache and Ding; \"pieces\" renders Stücke; \"in the subjective/objective sense\" renders \"im subjektivischen/Objektiven Verstande\", Verstand here in its idiomatic sense.","\"of every cognition which is limited\" (eines jeden erkenntnisses) as it stands; a word for the cognizer may be expected. \"all grounds ... will be either negative or positive are\" keeps the broken construction (werden ... sind).","Deleted fragments that break off ([Din], [dasje], [Es kommt nur d], [diese Er], [Best], [woferne man nur nicht weiter ge], [Ungewishei], [Mang]) are left in the German."]],"reflm:3708":["Only rational concepts can be defined, empirical ones never. The concept of the latter serves only to designate the *subject* of synthetic judgments, but never for *analysis*.",["\"defined\" renders erklären (cf. Erklärung = definition)."]],"reflm:3709":["*Prolegomena Metaphysicorum.*\nIn our collective cognition some cognitions must, to others, |\nlie at the ground. At the ground of many concepts lie other concepts, and of many judgments other judgments. At the ground of the commonest concept of a clock lies the concept of time, of a motion, and of measurement. Whoever names the word \"friendship\" supports himself on the concepts of love, of honesty etc. etc. It is just so with judgments. Whoever says that envy is a vice grounds himself secretly on many hidden judgments: that love of one's neighbor is a duty, that that which is opposed to a duty is a vice, that envy is a disfavor and opposed to love etc. etc. One can call the fundamental concepts *notiones fundamentales*, the fundamental judgments *judicia fundamentalia*. Those fundamental concepts which[themselves] do not in turn presuppose others are called *notiones primitivae* (first fundamental concepts) and the judgments of such a kind *judicia primitiva* (first fundamental judgments). But something can be regarded as a first either absolutely or relatively to something else. Something is a *cognitio absolute primitiva* insofar as no other at all lies at its ground; but it is *respective primitiva* insofar as it [einer gewißen vernünftigen Absicht nicht ge] either is not within the powers of a subject to cognize its ground, or is not in conformity with a certain rational aim. At the ground of the concept of the good and evil, in agreement, lies much of various kinds, which the philosophers also clearly cognize, but the common man surely cannot penetrate so far into his concepts, and for him they are *notiones primitivae*. It is just so in common cognition with the concepts of space and of time. Some of these fundamental concepts can be *notiones primitivae* with regard to the collective human understanding, although *in sensu absoluto* they may be only *derivativae*, e.g. at the same time, being after one another etc. These one can call *notiones* [*absolute*] *primitivas in sensu subjectivo*. whether they are so *in sensu objectivo* as well, the human being cannot settle. Just so are *judicia in sensu subjectivo prima* with regard to some human beings. e.g. proverbs; or with regard to all, e.g. *principium contradictionis*.\nIf we now call all cognitions that lie at the ground of others *principia*, then there are *respectus* with regard to breaks off.",["\"lie at the ground\" renders zum Grunde liegen; \"fundamental concept/judgment\" renders Grundbegriff/Grundurteil; \"philosophers\" renders Weltweise.","\"in agreement\" renders \"überein\", which is doubtful as it stands.","\"breaks off\" renders the closing \"bricht ab\"; the deleted fragment that breaks off is left in the German."]],"reflm:3710":["All (g first) principles are either formal or material. The first contain the ground of how the concepts in the judgment are to be considered in relation. The second contain the *medium terminum* by means of which they are to be considered with one another in this relation.\n1. The way in which the judgments are to be compared is either through affirmation or negation. An affirmative judgment represents that a predicate is identical with the subject (is its mark). A negative one, that the predicate contradicts the subject (is an opposite of its mark). The universal formal principle of the former can thus only be this one, which indicates the essence of affirmation: *cuilibet subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi identicum*, and of the second the one which contains the essence of a negation: [*cuilibet subjecto*] *nulli subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum*. The first is the proposition of identity, the second of contradiction. From these principles alone nothing at all can be proved. For to prove means to judge mediately or to infer. Every syllogism, however, is a judgment by means of an intermediate mark (*nota intermedia, medius terminus,*). But the named propositions contain no *medium terminum* to others. All propositions that stand immediately under these two are indemonstrable; all that stand mediately under them are demonstrable.\n2. Universal judgments that contain the *medium terminum* to others are their material grounds; and, if they stand immediately under the two highest *principiis formalibus*, they are material first principles. e.g. A body is composite, stands immediately under the proposition of identity and contains the *medius terminus* to the proposition: a body is alterable; e.g. Everything composite is alterable, a body is composite, therefore it is etc. etc. By contrast the proposition: no space is without places, stands immediately under the proposition of contradiction etc:",["\"principle\" renders Grundsatz, \"proposition\" Satz, \"syllogism\" Vernunftschluß, \"relation\" Verhältnis; \"indemonstrable/demonstrable\" render unerweislich/erweislich.","cuilibet subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi identicum: to every subject belongs a predicate identical to it; nulli subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum: to no subject belongs a predicate opposed to it."]],"reflm:3711":["*Nihil negativum* is entirely nothing. *non A* is also nothing and in itself not thinkable, but everything else can be thought. Now if the *Nihil negativum* were merely the nothing of a certain concept, then it would be A–A\n=0, thus the same as a *privation*; thus the *nihil negativum* is P–P = 0×P.",[]],"reflm:3712":["The mark of the absolutely necessary being cannot be the contingent in the existence of the other things, for then this necessity is only *hypothetica antecedentis*; thus not in this, that it is regarded as an ultimate ground of everything that exists, but that it is a ground of everything in general, both what exists and what is possible; for since possibility in general is certainly necessary, then that which contains the ground is also so.",["\"exists\" renders \"da ist\"; \"existence\" renders Daseyn; \"being\" renders Wesen."]],"reflm:3713":["On and \nThe determination of a being (s of something), insofar as what has been posited can be regarded, is quality.\nThe determination of a being (s of something), insofar as how many times something has been posited can be regarded, is quantity.\nA one in which there is quantity is a quantum.\nA positing of the same thing made several times is multitude.\n(s absolute unity is not a quantum.)\nIn any quantum whatever there is multitude, and that in which there is multitude is a quantum.\nTo cognize a multitude distinctly by successively adding one to one is to count.\nA multitude distinctly cognized by counting is number.\nA quantum in which how many times one has been posited is in itself undetermined (s which happens in that which is nothing but a mere possibility of coordination) is continuous.\nA quantum in which how many times one has been posited is in itself determined is discrete.",["\"Zuund\": the § numbers after \"Zu\" (on) and \"und\" (and) are missing from the source line.","\"quid sit positum\" (what has been posited) and \"quoties sit positum\" (how many times it has been posited) are the contrast behind quality and quantity.","\"numerare\" = to count; \"numerus\" = number; \"multitudo\" = multitude."]],"reflm:3714":["Every *caussatum* is *in se contingens*; the question is whether every *absolute contingens* is the *caussatum alterius*. The answer is this: *contingentia* can be discerned only through this, that the *existentia* of a thing, without being a *caussatum*, is undetermined.",["*caussatum* = something caused; *in se contingens* = contingent in itself; *caussatum alterius* = caused by another.","\"eingesehen\" (einsehen), rendered \"discerned\", i.e. have insight into."]],"reflm:3715":["*arbitrium* is either *liberum* or *brutum*. in the former one is conscious of the *contingentiae internae* and *externae* of the action.",["*arbitrium liberum* / *brutum* = free / brute choice; *contingentia interna* / *externa* = internal / external contingency."]],"reflm:3716":["S. I:\nA cognition of reason that has no other *principia* than empirical concepts can only be a critique; one can understand the universal (e.g. the moving) only from the particular, and the universal rules can only be abstracted from the particular ones of practice. it is the same with the doctrine of the probable.\nTruth has objective marks; but the certainty that in each case these objective ones are to be met with can have only subjective ones, i.e. the agreement of cognition with itself.\nA universal concept is empirical if it cannot otherwise [than] be understood than if one cognizes its *concretum*. e.g. One cannot get a concept of water through all definitions, [still] than if one has seen it. above all, all concepts of beauty, of simplicity, of the ridiculous are comprehensible only in their *concreto*. Hence the rules here too cannot occasion practice, but rather this makes the rules.\n––––––––––\nWith regard to the judging of the moral the question is not: whether such is agreeable, but: good. We do indeed cognize therein a con|nection\naccording to possibility with happiness, but it does not rest on it. The principles of moral judging are not to be grounds of the inclination to the good, [i.e.] for these belong to feeling. The most vicious human being, however small the moral inclination to the good may be proportionally, nevertheless holds it to be the best. The principles of this judgment are to be established.\n––––––––––\nAll sciences and arts relate either to the culture of healthy reason or not. in the latter case there also prevails a complete lack of taste. The age of healthy reason and of the arts and sciences is only the age of honor; this is found only in republics and monarchies, thus not in the feudal constitution, where an all too great inequality prevails.\n(g The *principium* of *identitaet* and of *contradiction* are for reason what space and time are for the senses. In general: the *principium* of agreement (be it of sameness or of difference). The *principium* of the highest reality is parallel to the sum total of all sensations.)\nPerfection is: what constitutes the worth of the person, happiness: what constitutes the worth of the state.\n(The proposition of sufficient ground is grounded on this, that one can cancel everything in and for itself, thus, in order to posit something, something else must be posited, with which this cancelling conflicts. This connection, however, is either according to laws of reason or of the senses.)\nThis proposition, however, means only that nothing can be cognized according to reason without a ground.\n––––––––––\n(g If we want to posit something through reason, we need a ground. I.e. positing *a priori* is through a ground, unless it were intuition.)\nIn the proposition: everything that is, has a ground, there lie the two: 1. something else, 2. through which the sequel is determined, the former *dependens*, the latter *contingens*.\nS. II:\nAll ideas of metaphysics are analytic except of space, time and force.\nAnalysis rests on the propositions of *identitaet* and of contradiction. All these and other propositions, however, are grounded on this one, that the understanding posits nothing *absolute*, but only insofar as it is necessitated to it by a *condition* either analytically or synthetically.\nThe idea of the possible. The idea of all or of a whole.\nMetaphysics is not a philosophy about the *obiecten*, for these can only be given through the senses, but about the *subiect*, namely its laws of reason.\nWe have laws for the use of our reason *a posteriori*; these cannot be *applicirt* to concepts, but only to objects of experience.\nMetaphysics thus treats only the *subiect* dogmatically, but the *obiect*, with regard to synthetic judgments, problematically.\nAll judgments are either logical, which *enunciiren* *respective* being, or real, which *enunciiren* that of the *absolute*. All being in [itself] itself (*position*) is an existence either *respective* (g *hypothetice*) or *categorice*.\nUniversality is understood either synthetically and then holds only of the cases of experience of the synthesis, or analytically and holds according to reason of concepts.\nTo determining the boundaries of reason there belongs something positive: first to show the domain of the cognitions of reason, and something negative: namely the limitations, finally also the quality of the boundaries, as it were the figure.\nCognitions are of a twofold kind: either those which go to objects that are given, or: to the concepts of the form, how reason considers every object. The latter are merely subjective and also alone can have a universality of reason.\nIn synthetic judgments I represent to myself an *identitaet* for the sake of the predicate, but not the predicate for the sake of the *identitaet*.\nThe fundamental concepts of analysis are: possibility, impossibility, necessity, contingency, unity etc etc.; of synthesis: space, time and force.\nNot all *conceptus superiores* are *abstracti*, but only those which arise analytically *a posteriori*; those *a priori* are not *abstracti*. These *abstracti* are empirical or rational.",["\"gesunde Vernunft\" = healthy reason; \"Beurtheilung\" = judging; \"Lehnsverfassung\" = feudal constitution.","\"an sich [sich] selbst\": the bracketed \"sich\" stands between \"an sich\" and \"selbst\"; rendered \"in [itself] itself\".","\"Obiect\" and \"Gegenstand\" are both \"object\"; the Latinate forms are kept as *obiect(en)*."]],"reflm:3717":["27:\nS. I:\nThe *principium* of the form of all experiences are space and time.\nThe *principium* of the form of all judgments of pure reason: *identitaet* and contradiction.\nThe *principium* of the form of all judgments of reason *a posteriori*: ground and force.\nComparison, derivation, taking-together.\nPossible is *contradistinguirt*: 1. to the [confl] whose concept contains a conflict. 2. To the determined or true. 3. To the actual.\nIn all empirical judgments the basic formula is: [everything] In every thing that which contains the ground of one sensation is also the same *subiect* that contains the ground of the other. The synthesis is one of taking-together or of derivation.\nAll necessity and contingency that we can represent to ourselves is conditioned. The unconditioned is thought problematically. Absolutely contingent (e.g. free action) and absolutely necessary can both not be thought.\nAll relation is either of combination or of comparison or of taking-together.\nAll combination (in the real sense): space, time and force.\nAll definitions are grounded on the concept of essence; hence we can define nothing but pure concepts of reason and arbitrary concepts.\nAll abstract cognitions have first grounds *a posteriori*.\nThe universal judgment that has not arisen through abstraction *a posteriori* and thus empirically is not abstract, but a pure judgment of reason.\nIn metaphysics the grounds of cognition must be taken *a priori*, although the *obiecte* *a posteriori*.\nIf the grounds [from that] about that which must be are taken from that |\nwhich happens, then they are *a posteriori*. The synthesis is twofold: of the coordinated and of the subordinated.\nThe synthesis of reason or experience.\nS. II:\nBesides the proposition of sufficient ground this one also holds: all analysis [is posits a synt] includes conversely the possibility of a synthesis. Accordingly in every series of subordinated things there must be a first, because otherwise no complete synthesis and thus also no analysis would take place.\nThe synthetic principle of the understanding is bricht ab.\nMathematical analysis is always a philosophical synthesis, only as I think the whole before the parts; but if I think the parts before the whole, then it is a mathematical synthesis[in the]. all synthesis rests on coordination and happens through understanding. The *analysis philosophica*, however, on subordination and happens through reason. The ground is no part of the sequel, nor conversely.\nIn all images of things I coordinate nothing but immediate marks and partial representations.\nab represents [a co] the action of coordination, ac however of subordination.\nThe soul has three dimensions. sense and its clarity, the understanding and its form as well as matter (i.e. extensiveness) of coordination, and reason, that\nis height of subordination.\nWhether it is not also thus with the relations to feeling?\nBecause all negations serve only to prevent errors and to demonstrate the not-knowing: metaphysics is a very useful science, not insofar as it extends knowledge, but prevents errors. One learns that which Socrates knew.\nTaste goes to appearances and not to sensation, consequently to the constitution of the object insofar as it is an *obiect* of the senses, and is thus, as a cognition, subject to universally valid rules.\nThe sensible is a perfection, and confusion is not essential to it, but rather distinctness of coordination can take place.\n–––––––––––\nMathematics shows the greatest dignity of human reason, metaphysics however the limitations and its peculiar vocation.\nIt belongs throughout to everything beautiful that I need for it only understanding and as little reason as possible; for it is to please in appearance. Hence the coordination.\n–––––––––––\nRemark: that the rules of the beautiful are given through experience, for the reason that one cannot think a beautiful shape of a rational being without a human figure.",["\"bricht ab\" (breaks off) is the AA's editorial note, kept in German; \"[Wiederst]\" is a struck fragment of \"Wiederstreit\" (conflict), rendered \"[confl]\".","\"nur da ich\": \"da\" may be temporal (when) or causal (since); rendered \"as\" to keep both.","\"daß\" before \"ist Hohe\" reads as \"das\" (that); doubtful reading, rendered \"that\"."]],"reflm:3718":["After »dunckele« (l. 32) Kant inserts:\nclear ones, to be sure, but still in part confused.",["\"dunckele\" = obscure; \"klare\" = clear; \"verworrene\" = confused."]],"reflm:3719":["ε2? ι2? κ3? (µ? ρ2? ψ?)\nBeside »ebenso nothwendig — doch ich muß« (ll. 15-18):\nIf the real sequel were contained in the real ground and thereby posited according to the rule of *identitaet*, then it would at all times be simultaneous with it. All alterations are thus possible only through the real relation of the grounds to their sequels, and the logical grounds are thus not through the limitations of my cognition, but in themselves, distinguished from the real ground.",["\"realfolge\" / \"realgrund\" = real sequel / real ground (Folge = sequel); \"Veränderungen\" = alterations."]],"reflm:3720":["ε2? ι2? κ3? (µ? ρ2? ψ?)\nAfter »widersprechen« (l. 38) Kant inserts:\nbut indeed conflict: namely that the one cancels the sequel of the other. Contradiction is the taking-together of two *contradictorie*, i.e. *logice* opposed predicates in one *subiect*, and its result is *nihil negativum*: a and non a at once. Real conflict is the combination of two real grounds in one subject, of which one cancels the sequel of the other, and its result is\n=0, which is no *nihil negativum*.",["\"widersprechen\" = contradict; \"widerstreiten\" / \"Widerstreit\" = conflict.","\"aufheben\" = cancel; \"zusammennehmung\" = taking-together; \"Subiect\" and \"subiect\" are both \"subject\"."]],"reflm:3721":["ε2? ι2? κ3? (µ? ρ2? ψ?)\nBeside ll. 25-29 (»widerstreiten — princip.«) Kant has made a brace ({) and added to the left of »zwar — entweder« (ll. 26-30):\nThat both positings can be removed does not yet constitute any [contradiction] conflict, and a contradiction is not here at all, but rather the conflict is possible only through this, that besides the sequel of a=x and the sequel of b=y, when both are taken together, a third, namely 0, is still possible.",["\"positionen removirt\" = positings (*positiones*) removed; \"[Wiederspruch aus]\" (contradiction) is struck in favor of \"Wiederstreit\" (conflict)."]],"reflm:3722":["Not possible =0P. Impossible –P. With the former I need only not think something, as *blitri*; with the latter I must cancel the possibility. For that an actual thought is required. but were it only the cancelling of a possibility, then it would again be 0.",["\"darf ich nur nicht etwas denken als blitri\": \"als\" may mean \"as\" (thinking something as *blitri*) or \"other than\"; kept ambiguous.","\"wirklicher Gedanke\" = actual thought; \"aufheben\" = cancel."]],"reflm:3723":["One must distinguish between the possible, the not possible and the impossible. If A is thought, it is possible; if nothing besides non A (which happens in the signification of an empty term), it is not possible; if the possibility is cancelled, it is impossible. Impossibility is negative possibility.",["\"terminus inanis\" = empty term; \"tollitur\" = is cancelled."]],"reflm:3724":["On and \nPossibility is respective positing conformable to the principle of contradiction. Truth (affirmative) is respective positing according to the principle of identity. Existence is absolute positing. From respective positing |\nto absolute the consequence is not valid. But from respective positing taken generally to absolute the consequence is valid, since, unless something were absolutely posited, no respect at all would be possible.",["\"Zuund\": the § numbers after \"Zu\" (on) and \"und\" (and) are missing from the source line.","\"positio respectiva\" / \"absoluta\" = respective / absolute positing; \"respectus\" = respect (relation); \"consequentia\" = consequence (inference)."]],"reflm:3725":["Absolute necessity is either logical: on account of the principle of contradiction, or real: not on account of the principle of contradiction.\nThe former is the necessity of judgments. or the necessity of the relation of predicate and subject.\nThe latter is the necessity of beings. 1. God is omnipotent. 2. God is. the latter through the contradiction of the opposite (g in itself) cannot be cognized. The opposite of existing is not-being. But not-being alone does not contradict itself. Existence is not a predicate, therefore its opposite is not a predicate opposed to anything.",["\"1. Gott ist allmächtig. 2. Gott ist.\" are in German in the Latin note.","\"Verum\" may be the conjunction \"but\" or the adjective \"true\" (\"true not-being\"); read as \"but\"."]],"reflm:3726":["On the heading »Simplex et compositum«:\nThere is no simple accident, since there is no simple action, but it is always a quantum.",["\"Simplex et compositum\" = the simple and the composite."]],"reflm:3727":["On the heading »Simplex et compositum« and \nQuantity is either of a composite or intensively such, i.e. degree. A greater degree is not the whole of the lesser ones, but the ground, i.e. a double degree, which can be the cause of two equal degrees; but in order that in the same |\nsubject there reside grounds of an effect which equals the effects of two substances taken together, there is required 1. a ground for any effect whatever, 2. a principle of uniting these grounds. E.g. an understanding understanding twice as much.\nWhatever is composite, since before composition whatever is given is simple, necessarily consists of simples, but not on that account is every *quantum* composite and a whole.",["The opening line is the AA's editorial indication of where the note stands.","ratio = ground; intellectus = understanding; principium = principle.","qvantitas … compositi vel intensive talis: 'of a composite' (genitive) as printed."]],"reflm:3728":["We can just as little think (g total) absolute contingency as absolute necessity through reason, but experience shows it in the case of that which happens.",["geschieht = happens; 'es' (it) is left without a stated referent, as in the German."]],"reflm:3729":["There is among the substances of the universe a commerce through established harmony; this is either an internally established harmony (physical influence) or an externally established one; the latter is either an externally pre-established harmony or an externally occasionally established one.",["commercium = commerce; stabilita = established; praestabilita = pre-established.","physical influence = influxus physicus."]],"reflm:3730":["If each of the existing substances were necessary, there would be no connection among them, [if] i.e. if there were no God at all, the variety of all substances would not constitute a world. If [plur] of some substances a common God, of others another, were the founder, there would be several [would be] possible worlds, not a single one. If there is one author of all, all that he has caused necessarily constitutes a single whole.\nThe system through which, for the reason that several substances exist, their relation and commerce seems necessarily to follow, is physical influence.\nThe system through which, for the reason that all depend on one, commerce and universal harmony is necessary, is pre-established harmony. [In this every influence]\nThe system through which, for the reason that all depend on one, [not yet necessar] commerce is only possible and is in itself contingent, so that each mutual dependence is established arbitrarily, is occasionalism.\nHere, however, it is to be noted that the pre-established harmony of Leibniz is a system that imagines for itself the contingency of the commerce of substances, even with one author posited, and a correspondence of one with another instituted arbitrarily. In true harmony, however, all harmonic acts happen according to a rule; in pre-established harmony and occasionalism [su] however [are] the divine act inducing the harmony is nothing but singular, and the difference of these consists only in time.",["colligatio = connection; commercium = commerce; influxus physicus = physical influence.","[plur], [nondum necessari] and [su] are broken-off deletions, rendered as fragments.","fingens = imagines (also 'feigns'); arbitrario = arbitrarily."]],"reflm:3731":["The question: why something is, presupposes the thought that it is possible that something also is not. This thought, however, [is grounded] |\ngoes [either] either to the existence of the substances or of their relations. As concerns the latter, that kind of composition which [itself] could not be understood from the necessary existence of the substances is a proof of the [existence] contingent existence [of them] of this composition. But as concerns the existence of the substance itself, the question why it is is grounded on the inner possibility of thinking its not-being.\nThere are only three kinds of concepts of things: first an absolute one, from the inner determinations, second a respective one, from the relation to possibility, third a relative one, from that to the actuality of other things. From the first concept no absolute necessity can be proved, for each thing with all its inner predicates can be cancelled without contradiction. From the third not either, because this is only a *necessitas hypothetica conseqventiae*. thus there remains the second, that possibility itself stands in such *relation* to actuality that it includes it.\nThe absolute necessity of a thing must be able to be cognized without any determinate condition. [This condition is]\nHow do we cognize absolute possibility? *Data* must always be given to us for it.\nIf I say: a certain *praedicat* is impossible for a thing, then I say: it [contra] does not belong to it, i.e. it contradicts it, although not what I think in it, but what belongs to it. (The question is whether I can say: it contradicts the rest of a thing, apart from its opposite.) What is thus impossible contradicts something or other; what is absolutely impossible contradicts everything, namely the contradiction rests on no particularly determined *condition*.",["Verhältnis = relation; relation in the Latin-type word is kept as *relation*; Wirklichkeit = actuality; aufheben = cancel.","[contra] renders the broken-off deletion [wiede], the beginning of wiederspricht.","Daseyn/Dasein = existence; Art = kind."]],"reflm:3732":["A concept concerning whose possibility nothing is established, remaining when the condition is removed under which alone it is permitted to judge its possibility, is pro|blematic.\nThus the absolute necessity of a being, the simple element of a body, an action determined by no ground.",["problematicus = problematic; ratio = ground; ens = being."]],"reflm:3733":["(g The thing,) What contains the ground of the inner possibility of things is absolutely necessary. Conversely (g the thing), what is absolutely necessary contains the ground of the (g inner) possibility of things; (g for) if even without it things were inwardly necessary, then there would be no impossibility at all in its not being. (g Or because [a thing] the opposite of a thing cannot contradict itself, it must conflict with some other.)\nWhat contains the ground of one inner possibility contains the ground of all. For let us posit that something possible still remained over, then this would not agree with something possible or other, that is: it would contradict something possible; but what contradicts something possible is impossible. (g The necessary being thus contains the ground of all possibility.)\nIn all possibility the formal and the material are distinguished. The ground of the first is the *principium contradictionis*; of the latter some existence or other which contains the ground of reality.\nAccordingly the most real being contains the ground of all possibility (s not a particular ground of the inner possibility of things and another one not.) and is absolutely necessary.\nThe necessary being is all-sufficient (s the most perfect *transscendentaliter*), i.e. the most real as a ground.\nThe necessary being is a single one. For if we posit several, then each of them will be able to be cancelled, yet in such a way that all the others remain; but because, if something necessary |\nis cancelled, the impossible arises, it would be possible that something possible were impossible. Moreover each of them would be a sequel of the others: *regressus in infinitum curvilineus*.\nThe necessary being is a simple one. For if a whole is necessary, then the parts must be so too; but then there would be many necessary beings.\nThe world is thus not a necessary being.\nThe necessary being is the highest good, for among all possibles *bonitaet* is a reality without which all existence is, in respect of worth, equal to nothing. Thus it contains the ground of all good. But the good consists in the relation of things (g to beings) which have cognitions and feeling, [the] and a being which for that reason contains the ground of things.\nThe necessary being has the most perfect understanding and will.\nThe necessary being is thus a person which contains the ground of all existence through understanding and will. I.e. it is a God.",["Wesen = being; allerrealeste = most real; allgnugsam = all-sufficient; aufheben = cancel.","Folge = sequel; bonitaet (Latinate, 'goodness') kept in italics.","wiederstreiten = conflict with, beside wiedersprechen = contradict."]],"reflm:3734":["Uniqueness follows either from the highest reality or from absolute necessity.\nEither from the notion of the most real necessary existence is concluded or from the absolute necessity of existing the greatest reality.\n[Greatest] Each principle is either drawn from experience or from mere notions. the former obtains if 1. the great perfection and then the absolute necessity of the author of the world is concluded, 2. if there is concluded breaks off?\n. . . . . .\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . infers\nEither from contingent existence to the necessary or from perfect existence to the most perfect.",["'breaks off?' renders the AA's bricht ab?; the dotted lines are the AA's, marking illegible or lost text.","Vnicitas = uniqueness; notio = notion; concluditur = is concluded; schließt = infers."]],"reflm:3735":["logical necessity,\nreal necessity.\n(s Impossibility.\nin the logical sense,\nin the real sense.)",["im logischen Verstande, im realverstande: Verstand here in the sense of 'sense, meaning', not understanding."]],"reflm:3736":["On the running title of M 333: »Exsistentia Dei«:\nFirst of all the question offers itself to investigation: what might be the foundation of absolutely necessary existence, or in what way the opposite of the existence of something can be absolutely necessary.\n1. the existence of contingents as things caused does not constitute the principle of absolute Necessity; rather, this is the ultimate ground of the possibility of the existence of several contingents.\n2. No other plain existence contains the ground of absolute necessity, for of contingents it has already been said, of the condition of necessary existence there is already the question.\n3. Therefore the proper mark of necessary existence will have to be found in its respect to possibility, which likewise is necessary.\n4. Impossibility consists in contradiction; [will be] it is namely properly so if you [den] posit what was to be denied, or vice versa. e.g. Impossible is a triangle [parallelo] quadrilateral. Which also sounds thus: a triangle is not a quadrilateral, which expresses a contradiction. For let |\nA be not B; then A, which is B, would be B not B, which is impossible and contradictory.\n5. The opposite of no existence contradicts itself. Contradiction is between subject and predicate, existence is not a predicate, therefore non-existence contradicts no subject in itself.",["The opening line is the AA's editorial indication of where the note stands.","'the opposite … can be absolutely necessary' and 'plain' (plana) are translated as printed; both readings are doubtful.","nota = mark; respectus = respect; ratio = ground; [nege] rendered [den], [parallelo] left as the broken-off word."]],"reflm:3737":["It is the question whether the power of God is greater than his decree and, since the former extends to all [*in nexu* possible] things, whether the latter does not also extend to them; in this case everything possible is actual. But if it seems that much that is possible is not actual, then we represent that to ourselves as possible whose *conditionen* are nevertheless impossible.",["Ratschlus = decree (also 'counsel'); gehet auf = extends to; wirklich = actual."]],"reflm:3738":["All analytic judgments teach what is thought in the concepts, but confusedly; the synthetic, what is to be thought as combined with the concept. In all judgments the concept of the subject is something (g a) that I think in the object x, and the predicate is regarded as a mark of a in the [synthetic] analytic judgments or of x in the synthetic.\n|–a—|—x——|\nall analytic judgments are rational and conversely. all synthetic judgments are empirical and conversely. first rational principles [either] of matter [or prior of form] are [judg] elementary principles; synthetic principles, if they were at the same time rational, would be called axioms; but, since such are not given, analogues of the rational ones in mathematics are so called. In philosophy there are no synthetic principles except *a posteriori*, i.e. empirically, and analytic principles *a priori*, i.e. elementary propositions, both material.\nI can compare notions by thinking their relation either according to the rule of the understanding: empirically (g and synthetically), or of reason: rationally and analytically, or according to the rules of the analogue of reason, i.e. |\nof imagination and wit. To this crusius owes many of his principles.\nLocke seems to have scented the distinction of synthetic and analytic judgments in his inquiry into man.",["object = Objekt; Merkmal = mark; verbunden = combined.","In the Latin: intellectus = understanding, ratio = reason, ingenium = wit, analogon rationis = analogue of reason.","accepta fert = owes, credits to."]],"reflm:3739":["If I deny something of a subject, then this happens in accordance with the principle of contradiction. This negation together with the affirmation is impossible.",["satz des Wiederspruchs = principle (Satz) of contradiction; Bejahung = affirmation."]],"reflm:3740":["Something opposite of nothing\n{\nnegative privative or negative.",["The brace is the AA's layout; how negativo, privativo and negativo attach to nihili is left as open as the Latin."]],"reflm:3741":["The material principles of judgments are notions\nthe formal\nthe principle of identity\nor of contradiction\nthe material principles of ratiocinations are notions\nthe formal\nthe *dictum de*\n*omni et nullo*\n(s The logical principle of judgments. The real principle of concepts.)",["ratiocinia = ratiocinations (syllogisms); notiones = notions; Begriffe = concepts."]],"reflm:3742":["(g There are material, there are also formal basic concepts.)\nAnalytic, synthetic principles.",["Grundbegriffe = basic concepts; the second line is Latin: Principia analytica, synthetica."]],"reflm:3743":["All mathematical concepts are synthetic.",[]],"reflm:3744":["There are synthetic propositions from experience, thus *principia prima synthetica*; of this kind are also (g the definitions of mathematics) the *axiomata* of mathematics concerning space.\n*Principia rationalia* cannot be synthetic at all.\nAll empirical propositions are synthetic and conversely.\nAll rational propositions are analytic.",["Sätze = propositions.","können gar nicht synthetisch sind: sind for seyn as written; rendered by the sense of the infinitive."]],"reflm:3745":["From the principle of identity and of contradiction there follow not only propositions, but necessary ones.",["Satz stands for both 'principle' (dem Satze der identitaet) and 'propositions' (Sätze)."]],"reflm:3746":["The *principia formalia* are only the first grounds of analytic or rational judgments.",[]],"reflm:3747":["All *principia* of human cognition are *vel formalia vel materialia*. The former contain merely the relation of concepts in judgments: either the logical or metaphysical. The latter of things, and are synthetic. (s Either of the analytic, and are called logical and hold for every understanding, are objective (only not conversely); or of the synthetic, and are called real, and, because they are stated universally without senses, they are either principles of the form of the understanding or of sensibility [and hold in both ca]; in the first case they are merely subjective laws. In the second they are objective only under the hypothesis of sensibility; but if the hypothesis already lies in the subject, they are objective.)",["Grundsätze = principles; Verhältnis = relation; ausgesagt = stated.","[und gelten in beyden fä] breaks off in fä(llen), rendered [and hold in both ca].","'Jene … Diese' rendered 'The former … The latter'; what 'of the analytic' and 'of the synthetic' qualify is left as open as the German."]],"reflm:3748":["Metaphysical basic concepts. Elementary concepts. Systematic basic concepts.",["Grundbegriffe = basic concepts; Elementarbegriffe = elementary concepts."]],"reflm:3749":["One can connect concepts with one another in order thus to make out of them a greater concept (synthetic); or one can think concepts as connected with one another in order to cognize that which is in them.\nThe concepts of causes are synthetic and thus empirical.",["verknüpfen = connect; erkennen = cognize."]],"reflm:3750":["All *principia primitiva* are either elementary propositions and ana|lytic\nor *axiomata* and are synthetic. Difference of an analytic and synthetic proposition in general. The rational are analytic, the empirical synthetic, likewise mathematical ones.",["Satz = proposition; elementarsätze = elementary propositions."]],"reflm:3751":["Not positing and cancelling is different.\nTo posit both of contradictorily opposed predicates at once is impossible through the principle of contradiction.\nTo cancel both of contradictorily opposed predicates is impossible through the principle of the excluded middle.\nTo think neither of contradictorily opposed predicates of something is possible: rule of abstract notions, which are indeterminate; that the latter is possible is evident from this, that, if they are not material, there is no contradiction.",["The first line can be read as 'not-positing and cancelling are different' or as 'not positing is different from cancelling'; the ambiguity is kept.","setzen/ponere = posit; aufheben/tollere = cancel; utrumque = both (each)."]],"reflm:3752":["[*respectus*]\nWhoever thinks a ground also thinks the sequel, and conversely: it is a concept of *respectus*. Since no simpler *respectus* can be thought, the concept of it is also inexplicable. One can nevertheless prevent through a judgment that ground and sequels are not regarded as entirely one and the same: so that it is said: *posita ratione ponitur aliud*; *sive: ratio et rationatum sunt diversa*.",["Folge = sequel; unerklärlich = inexplicable (Erklärung = explanation; definition).","verhindern, daß … nicht: the pleonastic nicht is kept.","posita ratione ponitur aliud = when the ground is posited, something else is posited; sive: ratio et rationatum sunt diversa = or: ground and grounded are different."]],"reflm:3753":["A ground is either of positing or of canceling.\n(g The relation is either logical: of concepts (of identity),\nor real: of things (*realiter diversi*).)\nThe relation of a positing ground is connection, of a canceling one is opposition.\nThe relation of a logical ground, either of positing or of canceling, is analytic — rational;\nthe relation of a real ground, either of positing or of canceling, is synthetic — empirical.",["“ground” is *ratio*, “relation” *respectus*, “connection” *nexus*, “canceling” *tollendi*.","*realiter diversi*: “really diverse”; the parenthetical lines mix German (der Begriffe, der Sachen) into the Latin."]],"reflm:3754":["The relation is either of connection or of opposition,\neach either logical or real.\nThe former on account of identity [post] or contradiction, the latter not through them.\n(s The logical ground and consequence are always simultaneous, the real ones after one another.)\n(In logical opposition, when one is posited, the other is canceled.)\n(In real opposition, when one is posited, the consequence of the other is canceled.)\n(s In the former the connection is cognizable analytically, in the latter synthetically. The former rationally, the latter empirically.)\nin the former one or the other is a negation;\nin the latter both are positive.\nin the former (s both being posited) the consequence is a negative nothing (s or impossible);\nin the latter (s both being posited) the consequence is a privative nothing =0.",["“relation” is *respectus*, “connection” *nexus*, “ground” *ratio*, “consequence” *rationatum*.","The Latin sets *nexus* (nominative) beside *oppositionis* (genitive): “either connection or of opposition”; the construction is left as uneven as it stands.","*nihil negativum*, *nihil privativum*: “negative nothing”, “privative nothing”."]],"reflm:3755":["The relation of the logical ground to the consequence is a judgment.\nThe relation of the real ground is a concept.\nVarious [Grundb] primitive concepts of real grounds.\nThe possibility of a real connection in primitive grounds cannot be rationally seen into.",["“consequence” is Folge, “relation” Verhältnis, “real connection” realverknüpfung.","“seen into”: eingesehen (einsehen), to have insight into.","[Grundb] is a deleted beginning, presumably of “Grundbegriffe”."]],"reflm:3756":["The logical of possibility is contradistinguished from truth, the real of possibility from actuality.\nThe relation of identity and contradiction thought negatively, i.e., that one concept is not identical with the other, and also does not conflict with it, is the logical or formal of mere possibility, the real of possibility is at the same time the material of it, and our concept of it extends as far as the simple of our sensations, likewise as the primitive relations (*respectus reales*) which we come to know through experience.\nThe logical of possibility is a relation of comparison, the logical of truth a relation of connection.\nThe cognition of the logical relation is rational, the [zweyte] real one empirical.\nGround and consequence are at all times different, but either (g only) logically, in that the consequence is only a partial concept of the ground (consequently where it is entirely the same as it, it is also no consequence), or really, in that it is thought not as a partial concept belonging to the ground, but as entirely different from it.\nAll connection of the logical ground with the consequence is an affirmative judgment, in which the predicate is the consequence and the subject the ground. In real connection this is never so; although the consequence can be a predicate of the cause, yet there is none of the ground in it. The logical connection and conflict can be cognized analytically and thus rationally, the real not otherwise than empirically. Since everything empirical teaches only what is customarily wont to be with or after one another (except in the properties of space), the question arises whether we |\nin this counter-relation do not distinguish the ground from the consequence through time.",["“relation” stands for both Verhältnis and Beziehung (“logischen Beziehung”); “consequence” is Folge, “conflict” wiederstreiten / Wiederstreit.","“the logical of”, “the real of”: das Logische der, das reale der, substantival adjectives left without a noun.","“yet there is none of the ground in it” (so ist doch keine des Grundes in derselben): it is unclear what “keine” (none) refers to, the consequence or the predicate."]],"reflm:3757":["That whose position is necessarily (according to universal laws) [etwa] combined with the position of something else is called its ground.",["“position” is Kant's loanword position, i.e. positing; “combined” is verbunden."]],"reflm:3758":["The possible, insofar as it is a consequence, is connected *a priori*; insofar as it is a ground, it is connected *a posteriori*. What is neither ground nor consequence is entirely unconnected. The series of all possibles subordinated to one another has a terminus *a priori* and *a posteriori*, and cannot [esse] again be connected with others.",["“consequence” is *rationatum*, “ground” *ratio*.","The text reads “connexum osse”; “osse” is presumably a slip for “esse”, rendered “be”."]],"reflm:3759":["Several connected things make a series. A series of [connexorum] coordinated things can be contained under the idea of all, a series of subordinated ones not so. The little word *simul* comprises everything.\nThe first grounds in the series of the coordinated parts of a body are simple things.",["*Vocula simul* is taken as the word “simul” (at once, together) being mentioned; it could also be read “the little word at once comprises everything”.","“simple things”: *simplicia* (neuter plural)."]],"reflm:3760":["reality and negation are distinctions between what is absolutely or meta|physically\nposited or negative and that which is called such only logically, in which there is only a relation either of position or of negation.",["“relation” is *respectus*; “position” *positionis*."]],"reflm:3761":["A universal and merely possible concept is not *omnimode* determined. But a singular concept is so and can yet be merely possible.\nExistence can be no predicate, for otherwise a thing would be cognized as existing only through a judgment and by means of the understanding. But as it is, we cognize the existence of things through sensation.",["*omnimode determinirt*: “determined in every respect”, i.e. thoroughly determined.","“existence” stands for both existentz and Daseyn.","“singular” is einzelner."]],"reflm:3762":["The respective positing of the predicates of a being toward one another in conformity with the principle of contradiction contains less in itself than the absolute positing of the same at once; therefore from the former to the latter the inference does not hold.",["“the inference does not hold”: *non valet consequentia*.","*simul* (“at once”) could attach to “positing” or to “absolute”."]],"reflm:3763":["Fictions can be possible [in sensu absolu]; but if one occupies oneself merely with their possibility, then an ideal way of thinking comes of it.",["“Fictions” is Erdichtungen; “way of thinking” Denkungsart.","[in sensu absolu] is a deleted, unfinished Latin phrase (“in the absolute sense”), left as it stands."]],"reflm:3764":["(s That in which there is quantity is a *quantum*.)\nQuantity is the positing of the same, made some number of times. It is pure: in which what has been posited is not asked at all, but it is something in general, as in Arithmetic. Quantity *in concreto* is: in which what has been posited is determined, i.e., in which a certain quality is given as one.",["“made some number of times”: *aliqvoties facta*.","The text spells *Arithemetica*.","“as one”: *pro uno*, also “for a unit”."]],"reflm:3765":["(s Unity is conditioned or unconditioned.)\nBy the 4 sections one has perhaps only wanted to say that the concepts of the one, the true, the perfect can be applied to all things in general.\nEach thing is not many: signifies the transcendental unity.\nEach thing contains much that is yet one and the same with one*: the metaphysical (g can be greater or lesser unity).\nEach thing is true, i.e.: it cannot be denied under all possible concepts and agrees with other things: transcendental.\nEach thing contains truths: metaphysical, the *principium* of the elementary propositions. (g some things do contain possibility, but not enough metaphysical truth, i.e., conditioned possibility.)\nEach thing is perfect, what it is: transcendental.\nEach has reality: metaphysical, it is comparatively perfect.\n[Das] The absolute unity, truth and perfection consists in the possibility which comprises all-sufficiency in itself, the possibility in every *respectu* and all reality.\n*(s in the circle there is more unity than in the polygon. The unity of the conic sections.)",["“all-sufficiency” is Allgnugsamkeit; “elementary propositions” elementarsätze.","“polygon”: the text reads “Vielek”, for Vieleck.","“one and the same with one” (einerley mit einem): “einem” may be “one thing” or the numeral."]],"reflm:3766":["Unity is either absolute (g categorical) (s numerical), which is opposed to multitude, or hypothetical, insofar as it belongs to several things joined together\n|\nunity either by coordinating a whole or by subordinating: unity of the ground and also unity of the consequence (in machines, in the aim).",["“ground” is *ratio*, “consequence” *rationatum*, “aim” *scopo*.","*coordinando totum vel subordinando*: “totum” may be the object of both gerunds or of the first only."]],"reflm:3767":["All necessity is either logical or real. The former is cognized rationally, the latter empirically. The opposite of the latter is called impossible because it never happens; the opposite of the former never happens because it is impossible.",[]],"reflm:3768":["That whose opposite is possible cannot for that reason be called contingent, if by the opposite is understood something else that is non A. For [loco] besides the substance whose determination is A, there can be another which is determined by non A; e.g., thought is not for that reason contingent to a human being because another can also be without thought; [aut] at least this contingency is only logical, not real, because in this human being it is perhaps necessary. Nor is a substance contingent whose determinations can succeed one another.",["“thought” is *cogitatio*; “human being” *homo*."]],"reflm:3769":["Opposite the number of M § 109:\nPossibility without existence of the same thing is not one and the same with the possibility of its non-being. I can represent to myself comets that are not; but to represent to oneself that the sun is not could be hypothetically impossible.",["The first line is the AA's note on where the Reflexion stands in Kant's copy.","“existence” is Daseyn, “non-being” Nichtseyn."]],"reflm:3770":["The (g absolute) possibility can be cognized only from the *nexu* with everything possible. e.g. whether in a triangle the square of one side can be equal to that of the two others cannot be cognized from a triangle |\nin general, but from its concept connected with the right angle.",["“to that of the two others” (dem der beyden andern): singular as in the German.","“connected” is verknüpftem."]],"reflm:3771":["A thing exists necessarily the opposite of whose existence is impossible. Yet for all that the opposite of its determinations is possible. After all, one says of alterable things that the same things successively have all sorts of determinations. If the opposite of the determinations were at the same time the opposite of the thing, then no thing would be alterable. Indeed, even (g in) an altered thing these alterations themselves can be necessary, for the logical opposite of a determination consists in this, that instead of A non A obtains at the same time; but if [statt] non A follows upon A, then this is [potentialiter] *disparatum*, *non oppositum*.\nThe change of a thing or alteration is what [mit] agrees very well together and where both follow necessarily upon one another. Only the thing must not cease to be, because then there is also no succession, consequently the real opposite.",["“change” is Wechsel, “alteration” Veränderung; “thing” stands for both Ding and Sache.","“at the same time” renders both zugleich and in derselben Zeit.","*disparatum, non oppositum*: “disparate, not opposed”."]],"reflm:3772":["Reality is (g that whose concept is) το *esse*;\nNegation is\nτο *non esse*.",["το *esse*, το *non esse*: Greek article with Latin infinitive, “the being”, “the not-being”."]],"reflm:3773":["Negation is a predicate. A being is a subject. A merely negative being would be something in which there is nothing.",["“being” is *ens*."]],"reflm:3774":["Opposite and \nReality is either relatively positing or relatively canceling. The diversity is only from the relation.\nThe negation of one of the opposites is the positing of the other.",["The AA's note on the Reflexion's position (“Opposite and”) is incomplete in the source.","“relation” renders *relatione*; “canceling” *tollens*."]],"reflm:3775":["The (s transcendentally) maximal being is the most real, and generally, in which there is not synthetic or extensive quantity, but intensive; for in the former the synthesis is never terminated. (s every minimum or maximum is only relatively such. e.g. diameter, periphery with respect to the area.) (s Comparative quantity of the universe: *universitas*.)",["“maximal being” is *ens maximum*, “most real” *realissimum*.","*et generaliter, in qvo*: the subject of “in which” is left unexpressed, as in the Latin.","*universitas* (spelled vniversitas) is left in Latin: “universality” or “the whole”."]],"reflm:3776":["Opposite and \nThe concepts of the (g absolutely) greatest and least in mathematical (g and ideal) synthesis are deceiving concepts; but in the real synthesis of qualities they are possible and are equal to totality, but in repeating the same there is no absolute totality.",["The AA's note on the Reflexion's position (“Opposite and”) is incomplete in the source.","“totality” is *omnitudo*; “deceiving concepts” *conceptus deceptores*.","*absolute* (“absolutely”) may qualify “greatest and least” or “concepts”."]],"reflm:3777":["several things in a being are either to be counted or to be weighed.\nthe minimum of a ground is a moment.",["“ground” is *rationis*; “moment” *momentum*."]],"reflm:3778":["negative determinations are not accidents.",[]],"reflm:3779":["The empirical concept of substance is identity in all varieties.",["“in all” renders bey allen, also “among all”."]],"reflm:3780":["inherence is not an accident.",[]],"reflm:3781":["In any accident there is regarded first the relation of real dependence on the [subiecto] substance, second the logical relation as of a predicate toward the subject. If there is only the former, the consequence does not therefore inhere in the ground, as spinoza wrongly held about the world in relation to God. if there is only the latter, the subject will be only logical, not a substance, and the predicate a determination, not an accident.",["“relation” renders both *respectus* and *relatione*; “consequence” is *rationatum*, “ground” *ratio*."]],"reflm:3782":["All properties which the poets personify are *phaenomena substantiata*.",["“properties” is Eigenschaften; *phaenomena substantiata*: “substantialized phenomena”."]],"reflm:3783":["The *accidentia* are not particular things that inhere in the subject, but predicates of a subject, i.e., ways in which the subject exists. The concept of inherence is a logical auxiliary means, because there one can represent all things in the form of predicates.",["“things” stands for both Dinge and sachen; “ways” is arten.","“there” (daselbst) may refer to the concept of inherence or to logic."]],"reflm:3784":["Whether [s] an accident is something accessory to the substance (g as color) and properly inheres in it, or only a form of the substance itself, positive and its actual determination? e.g. Motion inhering in a body.",["“and” renders “ct”, presumably a misreading of *et*."]],"reflm:3785":["three things are to be thought: substance, accident, the relation of both. This, however, is either of the accident and is inherence; or of the substance and [est] this again either of a potential ground and is force, or of an actuating one and is action.",["“three things” renders the distributive *trina*; “relation” is *respectus*, “ground” *rationis*, “force” *vis*."]],"reflm:3786":["Just as in logical matters subject, predicate and relation are distinguished, and therefore [praedicatum] the relation is not a predicate, so also in real matters. force is not an accident, but the relation of the substance as of a ground [resp] toward the accidents.",["“relation” is *respectus*, “force” *vis*; “in logical matters”, “in real matters”: *in logicis*, *in realibus*."]],"reflm:3787":["The *respectus* of a *partis* and of its *complementi ad totum* must be [mutuis] *mutuus* and *homogeneus*; thus a consequence cannot be a part of the ground and belong with the ground, as a part, to the same whole. The thought is not a part of the human being, but its consequence.",["*respectus partis et complementi ad totum*: “the relation of a part and of its complement to the whole”, Latin inflections kept as Kant wrote them.","“consequence” is Folge; “thought” Gedanke."]],"reflm:3788":["The mode of composition is not the essence of the composite, but of the composition.\nComposition is the form. The parts, the matter.",[]],"reflm:3789":["The question is whether (g in) a substantial composite there is no substance, but only substances, and whether only the plural of it is possible. A *totum syntheticum* is that whose [The] composition is grounded, as regards its possibility, on the parts, which can also be thought without [diese] all composition. A *totum analyticum* is that whose parts, as regards their possibility, already presuppose the composition in the whole. *Spatium* and *tempus* are *tota analytica*, bodies *synthetica*. A composite of substances is a *totum syntheticum*. A *totum analyticum* is neither composed of substances nor of accidents, but a whole of possible relations.",["*totum syntheticum*, *totum analyticum*: “synthetic whole”, “analytic whole”; *Spatium* and *tempus*: “space” and “time”.","“composition” is Zusammensetzung, and in the Latin *compositum*.","[The] is a deleted beginning, perhaps of “Theile”."]],"reflm:3790":["Either space contains the ground of the possibility of the copresence of many substances and their relations, or these contain the ground of the possibility of space.",["“copresence” is compraesentz.","“these” may refer to the substances or to their relations."]],"reflm:3791":["division is either logical, insofar as various things are regarded as contained under some higher notion. Mathematical, insofar as several things are distinguished as parts of the same whole (ideal division). Physical, insofar as it proceeds into parts in such a way that the real nexus through which they constitute the same whole is canceled (real division).\n(s A monad is not mathematically divisible either, since the several things discernible in it are not parts, but consequences.\nlogical division is distinguished from analysis; the latter is division of the notion [pr] and metaphysical; the former division of the sphere of the notion, and the less the notion is metaphysically indivisible, the greater the sphere.)",["“notion” is *notio*, “consequences” *rationata*, “canceled” *tollatur*.","“it proceeds into parts”: impersonal *agitur in partes*."]],"reflm:3792":["A moment is an element of a ground. Therefore the moment of free descent on an inclined plane is different. Moments *in facto*.",["“ground” is *rationis*; *in facto*: “in the fact” or “in the deed”."]],"reflm:3793":["(s The quantity of one thing as a whole is regarded extensively.\nThe quantity of one thing as a ground is regarded intensively.)\n[Die W] There is in a kettle full of boiling water more heat than in a spoon of the same, but not greater. Two donkeys pull a wagon with more speed, but not with greater.\nIf many individual things cannot be so combined that they give a greater degree — — breaks off.\nMore virtue, greater virtue; more welfare, greater welfare.",["“ground” is *rationis*; “heat” Wärme, “combined” verbunden.","“breaks off” (bricht ab) is the AA's notice that the sentence is unfinished.","[Die W] is a deleted beginning, presumably of “Die Wärme”."]],"reflm:3794":["Infinity relates to the absence of an end (*limitis*), either in adding: and then it is the greatest, or in |\ntaking away: and then it is not for that reason the greatest. The former is as it were like a line to which none can be added. The second like one from which a certain line can never be taken away in such a way that it reaches the end.",["“it reaches the end” (sie das Ende treffe): “sie” may be the line taken away or the line it is taken from."]],"reflm:3795":["it is not yet established whether the greatest reality is compossible in a simultaneous being or in a successive series.",["*in ente compossibilis sit simultaneo*: “simultaneo” is taken with *ente* (“in a simultaneous being”); it could also be read “compossible with what is simultaneous”."]],"reflm:3796":["The impenetrable inert extended is matter.",["“the extended” is *extensum*; “inert” *iners*."]],"reflm:3797":["Opposite and \nThe existence of a being which all duration succeeds is the *Initium* (beginning).\nThe existence of a being which succeeds all duration is the *Finis* (end).\nBeginning and end are the termini of duration, the one *a parte ante*, the other *a parte post*. Beginning and end are *Instantia* (instants).",["'Gegenüber und' is a locator remark, cut off in the source.","'cui succedit omnis duratio': literally 'to which all duration succeeds', i.e. what all duration follows upon."]],"reflm:3798":["Useful is what is mediately agreeable, i.e. is a |\nmeans to happiness; for the gratifications are the elements of happiness, perfection consists of others.",["Vergnügen = gratification(s).","'besteht aus andern': 'of other ones' (other elements) or 'of others' (other things); left open."]],"reflm:3799":["If the concept of the world signified the whole of all possible things, namely those which are possible in interconnection with the all-sufficient ground, then it would be more fruitful.\nOf cosmology. 1. *commercium* of substances.",["Zusammenhang = interconnection; allgnugsam = all-sufficient."]],"reflm:3800":["The question is twofold: whether for some given world another* could be surrogated, i.e. substituted, or whether to a given world another could be superadded. The former is permitted on account of contingency (unless it is supposed that it must have a totality that is such *simpliciter*); the latter is not permitted for this reason, that they would be joined in time, either in the same [or] part of it or in different ones.\n*(s *alius* (another) is either *per oppositionem* or *per additionem*.)",["'vel eadem [vel] ipsius parte vel diversis': the deleted 'vel' leaves the construction doubled; rendered as it stands."]],"reflm:3801":["121. E II 1736.\nM 121':\nThe mathematical law of continuity is: Space and time are continua.\nThe physical law of continuity is: just as between any points posited outside one another in a simultaneous series [is space] there lies a continuous series of points, so also between two states succeeding one another there lies a continuous series of states,[as] also in diversity a continuous series of degrees of diversity. e.g. Of a motion the direction is not changed except through the continuous interposition of degrees of intervening directions. So perhaps neither the state of the mind.\nThe cosmological law of continuity is. Between any diverse species lie all possible intermediate degrees, and there is a continuous series of degrees of diversity.\nThe logical law of continuity.\n(s No sequence of two states is immediate, otherwise an instant would be common to them: *simultaneorum oppositorum contradictio* (contradiction of simultaneous opposites).)",["'Motus non mutatur directio' is irregular as it stands (Motus nominative beside directio); rendered literally.","'est.' after 'cosmologica' stands with a period before its content; kept.","mens = mind; Folge = sequence; Zustand = state."]],"reflm:3802":["Cause and caused do not belong to the same whole, because then the cause would suffer externally from itself; therefore the cause of the world is an extramundane being.",["'pateretur' = would suffer (be acted upon)."]],"reflm:3803":["Every Spinozist is an egoist. It is asked whether every egoist is necessarily a Spinozist.",[]],"reflm:3804":["The absolute perfection of the world does not consist in the multitude, order, variety of substances, but in the greatest [sense] and purest sense of pleasure. Hence the rules of perfection thus so-called physico-mechanical do not collide with the pneumatic ones, since in the former there is no perfection.",["'ita sic dictae': 'ita' and 'sic' doubled in the source; both kept.","voluptas = pleasure; pnevmaticis = pneumatic (of spirits)."]],"reflm:3805":["Rules of the perfection of the world of spirits and of the natural never stand in collision, for the latter has no absolute perfection.",["Geisterwelt = world of spirits."]],"reflm:3806":[") (ι1?) M 147'. E II 768.\nThe difference between the *influxu ideali* and *reali* makes no distinct system, but is merely a correctly determined concept of *influxus* in general. But because all *influxus* is ideal, it can be concluded from this that it becomes possible in no other way to think a *harmonia* of substances in their self-activity than insofar as they depend on one.*\nSpace and time are the first relations which all things receive thereby, and the first grounds of the possibility of a world-whole.\n(g The theory of the *influxus idealis* is not a [ground of a] particular system, but a ground of the abolition of the *influxus physici*. Then [still the possibility] must be shown how a *harmonia sub conditione influxus realis* (harmony under the condition of real influence) is possible. Then the possibility [of an *influxus*] of a *harmoniae praestabilitae* or rather its necessity must be demonstrated.)",["The asterisk after 'einem' has no corresponding note in the passage.","'ein [Grund eines] besonderes System': the article and adjective do not agree after the deletion; rendered as it stands.","gedenken = think; Weltganzes = world-whole; besonder = particular."]],"reflm:3807":["Rational beings in other worlds (s theurgy). *spiritus astrales*. *angeli*. Influence into the spirit of the human being. Citation of departed souls or of those still living, haunting.\nPoltergeists. (s idol. Fetish. Talisman. Amulet. sympathy.)\nRiebezahl. (g kobold.) *incubi*, *succubi*. delrio.\nChangeling. Vampire. (s Subterraneans.)\nWater-nixies. Mountain-mannikins. Ghost-seeing.\nCitation. *Pactum*. *Magia*. *obsessio*. *Exorcismus*. Ventriloquists.",["Citation = summoning (of spirits); kept as 'citation'.","Geist = spirit; mind. 'spücken' = spuken (to haunt).","'Riebezahl' (Rübezahl) and 'delrio' (Del Rio) kept in Kant's spelling."]],"reflm:3808":["The original being (The [first] independent one), highest, supreme If. (Ground.) The being of all beings (of all). The supreme ground of everything.\nThat it is no sequel, hence a first,\nThat it is a (g first) ground,\nThat it is a ground of everything.",["'Wenn' (If) is doubtful; it may be a slip, but is kept as it stands.","'er' (masculine) is rendered 'it'; it may refer to Grund or to the being as God.","Urwesen = original being; Folge = sequel."]],"reflm:3809":["The possibility of a thing is twofold: either 1, that what is there is combined in a certain way according to laws which already obtain in them by virtue of their own properties. e.g. If wood is there, then a house of wood is possible, or also that from forces an effect flows. 2, The possibility where nothing is there; because then there is no matter there for anything at all, so also the concept of no thing can arise. Thus the concept of possibility is indeed *logice* before actuality according to given concepts, but *realiter* it does not go before everything actual. Thus [that] a being which contains the matter for all possibility and in which possibility is to be distinguished from actuality is just as absurd as, when one takes space for an *obiect*, one cannot say it is possible.",["Wirklichkeit = actuality; Materie = matter; Eigenschaft = property.","The last sentence is irregular in construction (after the deletion of 'dasienige'); rendered as it stands."]],"reflm:3810":["Possibility is logically discerned only through [pr] judgments in which there is no connection, also no conflict.",["einsehen = discern (have insight into); Verknüpfung = connection; Wiederstreit = conflict."]],"reflm:3811":["All *negationes* are limitations. Limitations in general are possible only through the unlimited. Accordingly the unlimited is that where|by\neverything else is possible. The possibility of things *ascendendo* happens through comparison, *descendendo* it is discerned through derivation.",["Schranken = limitations; 'wo|durch' split across the line in the source."]],"reflm:3812":["In the Wolffian proof one can indeed cognize the independence, but not the (g inner) necessity; for its existence is necessary only for the sake of the world.\nFrom the necessity he infers the *omnitudinem realitatum*. — — If that is correct, [that] then he had no need to substruct an experience.\nIt is strange that one wants to prove the existence of an absolutely necessary being from something other than from the necessary. For if one understands what absolutely necessary signifies, and cognizes that the concept is something, then the proof has been done. If one does not know this breaks off.",["'absolutely' renders both schlechterdings and schlechthin.","'sein Daseyn': 'its' or 'his' existence; left open.","substruiren = substruct (lay underneath)."]],"reflm:3813":["It is to be proved, 1. that an absolutely necessary being is possible. 2. That it is *realissimum*. The absolute necessity of the thing must be derived from concepts [and not from] and not from the relation with other existing things.\nIt does not consist in this, that the opposite contradicts itself.\nNo opposite of existence contradicts itself; only of propositions.\nIt must thus be necessary in connection with the possible. Not as a sequel, but as a ground.\nPossibility is a *respective* position. Much can indeed be posited according to possibility, although it is not posited *absolute*; but without that nothing at all exists, there is no possibility.\n(g All simple possibilities contain the material ground of possibility; their possibility does not rest on the principle of contradiction.)",["'ohne das gar nichts existirt': either 'without that (anything existing), nothing at all exists' or 'without (the fact) that nothing at all exists'; the ambiguity is kept.","Satz des Wiederspruchs = principle of contradiction; setzen = posit."]],"reflm:3814":["Opposite the numeral of M § 815:\nAll judgments are logical or real. The latter are of existence and cannot, if they concern absolute necessity, be cognized through the principle of contradiction.",[]],"reflm:3815":["God contains the material ground of all possibility, and herein lies at the same time, according to the *principio contradictionis*, the formal ground of it.\nThat God contains the ground of the essence of things is no *perfectio operativa*, but *qviescens*.\nThe divine all-sufficiency is either *omnisufficientia interna vel externa*.",["'die Materialen Grund': article and noun do not agree (plural 'grounds' is possible); rendered singular.","Wesen der Dinge = essence of things; Allgnugsamkeit = all-sufficiency."]],"reflm:3816":["God is [firstly] represented as the being of the highest perfection, as a ground, namely as a ground of all perfections in the ontological, secondly in the cosmotheological sense.",["Verstand here = sense (meaning), not 'understanding'."]],"reflm:3817":["According to the analogy of empirical concepts breaks off?",[]],"reflm:3818":["analogy of the one born blind with regard to colors and tones. The cognition of the properties of God with regard to the moral is dogmatic and positive, with regard to the theoretical is [analo] critical and negative. That one not err is the foremost thing. Thus the theoretical cognition that we need is very simple.",["einfältig = simple (plain)."]],"reflm:3819":["Moral properties. The goodness of the creator, The holiness of the lawgiver, The justice of the judge. } }\n}All-sufficiency as to duration, as to power, as to [omniscience] cognition. Eternity, omnipotence, omniscience.",["German 'der Dauer, der Macht, der [Allwissenheit nach] Erkentnis nach': 'nach' (as to) is placed after the nouns; the deleted words include it.","The braces are carried over as they stand."]],"reflm:3820":["That there is an original being.\nwhether there are more original beings?",[]],"reflm:3821":["That there can be only one most perfect being is clear.",[]],"reflm:3822":["The supreme cause is the author of the world through freedom.\nthe world is a being from another, but not from several, but from one; not through nature, but through freedom; therefore the cause of the world is author and God; but that God is at the same time all-sufficient and unique cannot be concluded from this.",["Urheber / auctor = author; omnisufficiens = all-sufficient."]],"reflm:3823":["Whether there are comparative gods, whose right is absolute with respect to human beings, and the subjection of human beings natural. It is denied. for there is no |\nnatural subjection, except under the will of him who cannot be obligated, therefore not under a creature.",[]],"reflm:3824":["Independence and omnisufficiency are the two *principia cognoscitiva* for determining the properties of God.",["'Independentz' and 'omnisufficientz' are Germanized Latin in the source."]],"reflm:3825":["*Cognitio prototypa* (prototypal cognition): cause of things.\n*Cognitio ectypa* (ectypal cognition): the caused.",[]],"reflm:3826":["God cognizes everything in that he cognizes himself.\nThe human being cognizes himself in that he cognizes other things.",[]],"reflm:3827":["One could ask whether all will also presupposes a representation of the good in the *obiect* and whether he who wills the highest good could not will still more than is good; the last will. If the ground of willing does not need a *causam moventem* in the *obiect*, but rests in the subject's nature, then breaks off.",["'der letzte wille': 'the last will' or 'the latter will'; left open."]],"reflm:3828":["The motive of God is only analogically related to ours; no ground of his action is in the *obiect*; but, because he |\nconsiders himself as the cause and as the most perfect cause, he is the author of the best.",["Bewegungsgrund = motive; betrachten = consider."]],"reflm:3829":["The concepts of substances, insofar as they are abstracted, are only respective: hence bodies are substances insofar as they are the subjects of the inherence of their accidents and inhere in no other known subject; but they would not be called substances insofar as they in turn [be] have another subject, of which they are merely the effects.",["'respectiv' = respective (relative)."]],"reflm:3830":["That will with which a smaller (g cognized) good agrees more than a greater is a worse will. The proof is conducted here *a posteriori* that no better world is possible. In the case [that] of the infinite series of better worlds no best will would have to be possible. Indeed, every possible will would be infinitely evil. The proof of *optimismus* under the hypothesis: there is a God, is more evident than even the proof of the divine existence. indeed, it is still more necessary, or rather one needs the idea of God only for this. Only the idea of the *maximum* can teach me what I have to expect from God.\nIt is asked whether not even only a single world is possible.\n(s One can represent to oneself no existence at all (in agreement with reason) which would be equally possible with the not-being. This is also to be regarded as a difficulty for the rational concept of freedom.)",["'welches mit dem nicht seyn gleich moglich sey': 'equally possible with (its) not-being', or, reading 'dem' as a pronoun, 'which would not be equally possible with that'; kept ambiguous.","Source has 'maximi' (genitive of maximum)."]],"reflm:3831":["Whether it is possible to create a spontaneous substance or one of a given degree of spontaneity.",[]],"reflm:3832":["At the very top of the page:\nOne will sooner say: infinite space is in God, than: God is in infinite space.",[]],"reflm:3833":["Naturalism is either of method or dogmatic naturalism; the latter is either of conservation or of origin, but this one is atheism. Naturalism of method is either of a propaedeutic or of a universal one; the former is Epicurus's and quite rational, the latter is atheism negatively such.",["'propaedeuticae vel vniversalis' are genitive, apparently with 'methodi' understood."]],"reflm:3834":["Synthesis is either philosophical or mathematical, and likewise also analysis; philosophical synthesis is combination through a ground.",["Verbindung = combination."]],"reflm:3835":["Nothing according to the concept or according to existence.",[]],"reflm:3836":["On the inner margin \nMaterials and form belong to impossibility, not to the not-possible.",["Unmöglichkeit (impossibility) is distinguished from Nichtmögliches (the not-possible)."]],"reflm:3837":["It is precisely the opposite, when a definition includes a circle, of that when it involves a *contradictio*.\nIn the case of what is necessary we do not ask why it is. Why a triangle has 3 sides. but rather: why three sides are.",["Erklärung = definition."]],"reflm:3838":["Contingent is that whose not-being is possible. Being is taken here without regard to time, whether it be the present, past or future. Not-being is the complete cancellation and thus from all time. The alterations themselves can be necessary; and if they are contingent, then the persistence of the thing is possible.\nThe opposite of a predicate at another time [signifies] proves that the subject in and for itself is undetermined with regard to it. It does not prove that it is possible that the determination had not been in the previous time. The alterations themselves, if they are necessary, since none of the successive determinations takes place without the opposite of the previous one being at the same time, prove not so much the contingency as the restrictions, i.e. the nothing which follows the something with equal steps.",["'in Ansehung derselben': 'with regard to it' (the predicate) or 'to that (time)'; left open.","Aufhebung = cancellation; Einschränkungen = restrictions; Beharrlichkeit = persistence."]],"reflm:3839":["That magnitude which can be the ground of another not otherwise than through diminution or separation is *extensiva*. That which is a ground without diminution is *intensiva*.\nThe magnitude of matter and form.\nThe degrees of possibility.",[]],"reflm:3840":["The elapsed infinite which has a beginning is impos|sible.\nIf everything future is a merely mathematical repetition, then future infinity is to be regarded as possible. But if the future is determined from the previous or otherwise, then it makes up a whole which is thoroughly determined through some ground or other, and its infinity is no more graspable than that of past and present time.",["durchgängig bestimmt = thoroughly determined; faßlich = graspable."]],"reflm:3841":["There is no necessary completeness of possible coordination, hence also no (g necessary) bounds of a whole in which the quality of the unities is undetermined. (g all bounds are conditions of a whole of contingent magnitude.) Hence every whole is only contingently determined with respect to the bounds, and the possibility of still more parts is the void. Hence because the elapsed time constitutes a whole, it has bounds, before which a time precedes. The future, which constitutes no whole, has no bounds. The (g multitude of) things in the present time constitutes a whole which has bounds, and the world is thus in absolute empty space. On the other hand every series of subordinated things necessarily has *a priori* a bound, and the *universitas* itself extends to all possible, not merely actual members, and the first of things can be seen into through reason.",["'bounds': Grentzen (Grenzen).","'unities': Einheiten, which can also be read as 'units'.","'be seen into': eingesehen werden (einsehen, to have insight into)."]],"reflm:3842":["In the concept of all together there must be a particular ground of the taking-together of all in the thing itself. If now each member is thought synthetically to the others, then the present past time is that which takes them all together, because it is itself a whole. [In the analytic] If the parts are given analytically from the whole, then the *completudo* of the *analysis* is with the breaks off.",["'thing': Sache.","'present past time': Gegenwärtig Vergangene Zeit; Gegenwärtig is uninflected and may be adverbial ('the presently past time').","'breaks off' renders the AA's notice 'bricht ab'; the sentence is cut off after 'with the'."]],"reflm:3843":["The relation of the cause to the effect is no relation of *identity*; consequently there is also neither similarity nor equality between cause and effect, but rather *conformity*. From the effect the cause is to be cognized and also to be named. *talis est notio causae qva talis, qvalis oritur e notione causati*.\nWe know every thing of the world only as cause, but in the cause only the *causality* of the effect, thus only the effects, and thus not the thing itself and its determinations, through which [they] it produces the effect.",["The Latin: 'the concept of the cause as such is such as arises from the concept of what is caused'.","'identity', 'conformity', 'causality': Kant's Latinate identitaet, conformitaet, caussalitaet.","'know': kennen."]],"reflm:3844":["*Effectus plenus* is measured not *in serie subordinatorum, sed coordinatorum*, that is not through the series of the effects of the effects, e.g. of a ball's force through the series of the balls which it can mediately move. From this one sees that it is always only the same effect which continues in time, but in different conditions; thus the effect which arose immediately always continues and neither increases nor diminishes.",["The Latin: 'the full effect' is measured not 'in the series of things subordinated, but of things coordinated'.","'continues': fortdauern, twice.","'ball': Kugel, also 'sphere'."]],"reflm:3845":["102'. E II 719.\nTo the closing word of M § 333 Kant adds: *aliqva*, and then continues on p. 102':\nbut the question is: what do we cognize first. Do we cognize first that something is an effect, and thus has a cause, or that something is a cause and thus has an effect. The former. For that |\nsomething is an effect, what happens, is posited by reason only *per aliud*; we cognize this *nexum* only *a posteriori*. were we to cognize causes beforehand, then the *nexus* would be cognized *a priori*.",["The second line is the AA editor's note, not Kant's text.","'what happens' (was geschieht) may stand in apposition to 'something' or be the subject of 'is an effect'; the German leaves it open.","'first': eher (sooner); 'posited': gesetzt; per aliud: 'through another'."]],"reflm:3846":["The immediate goodness is for the understanding the greatest, chiefly because it is necessary and universal; the mediate usefulness is smaller, because it pleases only contingently and under circumstances.",["'goodness': Kant's Latinate bonitaet (bonitas)."]],"reflm:3847":["If many causes agree together to one effect, then they are connected among one another *a posteriori*; from this one infers that they are so also *a priori*, i.e. that there is a common cause of them. Are breaks off.",["'connected': verbunden.","The last sentence breaks off after its first word, Sind ('Are', probably opening a conditional 'If they are'); 'breaks off' renders the AA's 'bricht ab'."]],"reflm:3848":["For reason not the *data*, but form are what is essential.",["The plural verb sind agrees with data, not with form, as in the German; 'what is essential': das Wesentliche."]],"reflm:3849":["The concept of matter also extends to something passive, hence the substantial forms are active forces: entelechies. Spirits in their republic cannot be called the matter of the whole, because they are not passive, but are combined self-actively and constitute their whole.",["'passive': leidend (literally 'suffering'); 'self-actively': selbstthätig.","'Spirits': Geister; 'combined': verbunden."]],"reflm:3850":["*forma dat esse rei*. For what is essential in the thing can be cognized only through reason; but now all matter of cognition must be given through senses; thus the essence of things is [merely], insofar as they are cognized through reason, the form.",["The Latin: 'form gives being to the thing'.","'what is essential in the thing': das Wesentliche der Sache; 'essence': Wesen."]],"reflm:3851":["Matter is at all times regarded as passive, and form depends on activity, likewise all activity extends to nothing further than form. Meanwhile corporeal matter as substance is according to Leibniz an *entelechia*, i.e. something active. Our cognition is merely passive as to the *data*, and the form rests on self-activity.",["'passive': leidend; 'self-activity': Selbstthätigkeit."]],"reflm:3852":["Form gives being to the thing. for through the understanding we cognize only form. Matter contains the data, from which various things can arise by determining in diverse ways; and therefore the determinate being of any thing depends on the form. Form is the specific difference.",["'understanding': intellectus; 'cognize': cognoscere.","'the determinate being': determinatum esse, which can also be read as 'being determined'."]],"reflm:3853":["On the 1st proposition of M § 381 (10718f.):\nFor otherwise all members of the series would be *caussata* of another member of the series. [Thus wou] Thus there would be a member of the series which would be something other than all members of the same.",["The first line is the AA editor's note; (10718f.) is page 107, lines 18f.","caussata: 'things caused'."]],"reflm:3854":["If the present (our) world is the [most per] only existing one, then it is also the most perfect whole from a single original being. But if there are several worlds, then one must surely be the most perfect among all possible ones, but perhaps not ours.\nThe idea of the most perfect world is as needful to us as of the most perfect cause; indeed the latter is properly needful to assume for the sake of the former.",["[most per] renders the deleted fragment allervo(llkommenste).","'needful': nöthig, kept apart from notwendig ('necessary').","'original being': Urwesen."]],"reflm:3855":["Life is the faculty of beginning a state (its own or that of another) from an inner *principium*. The former is not a complete life, inasmuch as that whose state is alterable itself requires something external as cause. Bodies do have an inner *principium* of acting in one another (e.g. interconnection), also [something] of preserving an externally imparted state, but not of beginning of themselves. Thus all alteration, all origin proves a first beginning and consequently freedom. [Thus] But because the beginning can be *comparative* the first, namely according to mechanical laws. e.g. When a carcass smells to the dog, then motion begins in it, which, because it was not produced according to mechanical laws by the smell, but was caused through the stirring up of desire. In animals, however, this is just as much an external necessitation as in machines; hence they are called *automata spiritualia*. but in the human being the chain of determining causes is in every case cut off, and hence one also distinguishes |\nthe immaterial as a *principium* of life from the material. In the human being the spirit is free and wills the good; the animal is *automaton*; were this spirit always to be effective only in the animal and not to alternate with its forces, then we would find more proofs of freedom.\n(s *libertas est independentia a necessitatione externa*.\n*libertas est vel originaria vel derivativa [haec vel]. Arbitrium autem est vel sensitivum vel intellectuale; illud est vel brutum vel liberum.*)",["'interconnection': Zusammenhang, here possibly physical cohesion; 'necessitation': Nothigung.","'smells to': anricht, a doubtful form, probably for anriecht; the sentences beginning 'But because' and 'which, because' are incomplete in the German.","The Latin: 'freedom is independence from external necessitation. freedom is either original or derivative [this either]. Choice, however, is either sensitive or intellectual; the former is either brute or free.'"]],"reflm:3856":["To be determined in freedom is indeed admissible, but not passively, neither through the way objects affect, nor through a supreme producing cause. I can say: in this moment I am free (g *liber aut devinctus*) and unbound to do what pleases me; but it is nevertheless unavoidably necessary that I act thus. It is a law of self-activity which makes the opposite impossible. Can one also with respect to the (g moral) evil be determined just so from free resolve. No! one can be determined to it only passively or not at all, because the pure will nevertheless always remains and thus cannot be bound at all, but does not always *exsert* its activity.",["liber aut devinctus: 'free or bound'.","'is indeed admissible': geht schon an; 'resolve': Vorsatz.","'exsert': exseriren (exserere, to put forth)."]],"reflm:3857":["The substance acts freely which, not externally determined, produces something which previously was not, and this freedom is opposed to inner or outer natural necessity. It acts from free choice insofar as the *causality* of the action lies in the good pleasure, which is not passive. The difficulties concern only the first idea of |\nfreedom, and it is incomprehensible in the necessary being as well as in the contingent. but for different reasons, because the former can begin nothing, but the latter can begin nothing first. The first degree of (g independence is) self-activity [is]: that of a substance in general; the second degree: independence in acting from all external determining causes; the third degree: independence from its own nature.\nThus the negative is properly incomprehensible, the positive of the motives is comprehensible.",["'choice': Willkühr; 'good pleasure': Belieben.","'incomprehensible' / 'comprehensible': unbegreiflich / begreiflich; 'motives': motiven, not Bewegungsgründe."]],"reflm:3858":["In all actions the ground, the *causality*, lies (g as to matter) in nature; but as to form either merely in nature, e.g. attraction, or in another faculty, which inwardly directs the forces of nature. The latter is freedom.",[]],"reflm:3859":["Freedom is (g a) practically necessary fundamental concept.\nThe first necessary is not to be comprehended, because there is no ground of it; the first contingent is also not [of] to be comprehended, because a ground making necessary must be there. The former, because it is necessary, although it has no ground, the latter, because it is contingent, although it has one.",["'fundamental concept': Grundbegrif; 'comprehended': begreifen.","'a ground making necessary': ein nothwendig machender Grund."]],"reflm:3860":["For freedom 1. *spontaneitas simpliciter talis* (*automaton*) (g *independentia a causis subiective necessitantibus (stimulis)*) is needful, so that [the arb what from the subject] the determinations can be attributed to the subject as its action. 2. The faculty of the *arbitrii*\n|\n*intellectualis*, so that it can be imputed to it as a *factum*. Because everything that happens presupposes, according to laws of the faculty of desire, a satisfaction, the *complacentia* which is independent of the subjective *necessitation* must be intellectual, and thus the former presupposes the latter.\nThe greatest difficulty lies in this: how a subjectively unconditioned choice can be thought (g *est obiective hypotheticum*) in (g according to) the *nexu causarum efficientium sive determinantium* or, if one departs from that, how the *imputability* of actions is possible.",["The Latin: 'spontaneity simply as such'; 'independence from causes subjectively necessitating (stimuli)'; 'of intellectual choice'; 'is objectively hypothetical'; 'the nexus of efficient or determining causes'.","'satisfaction': wohlgefallen; 'choice': Willkühr; 'faculty of desire': Begehrungsvermögen.","'needful': nothig."]],"reflm:3861":["On aversation.\nThe natural and practical possibility [has] is the same breaks off.\nThe first question is: how one is to comprehend the *causality* [of a] of freedom, whether it breaks off.\nIn a contingent (g acting) being the causality can be either necessitated or dependent.\nWhether the generation (g *genesis*) of the action is determined *aliunde* or *spontanea*.",["'breaks off' renders the AA's 'bricht ab', twice.","'is the same': ist die einerley.","aliunde: 'from elsewhere'; spontanea: 'spontaneous'."]],"reflm:3862":["One often says: The human being has talents, he also has no evil heart. He could become useful. It lies only in willing. One would think this circumstance can easily be changed; but nevertheless it does not happen (g Negligent). If he now in addition has an evil heart: will it then still be able to happen?\nIs, in evil human beings, the will evil, or is it good, but weak. The means of making the will good are moral; of making it strong, pragmatic. If one seeks to practice resoluteness, steadfastness in resolve, resignation. In some it seems not possible to produce a good will (although correct power of judgment, i.e. provisional will), in others not a strong will.",["'Negligent': Nachlaßig.","'resolve': Vorsatz; 'power of judgment': Urtheilskraft."]],"reflm:3863":["(g sensuality)\nFreedom from brutality (*spontaneitas practice talis*)\nFreedom from fatality (*transscendentalis*)\n*actus arbitrii vel originarii vel derivativi*.",["The Latin: 'spontaneity practically as such'; 'transcendental'; 'act of choice either original or derivative'.","'sensuality', 'brutality', 'fatality': Kant's Latinate sensualitaet, brutalitaet, fatalitaet."]],"reflm:3864":["Good pleasure is a desiring without any necessity of an efficient cause; consequently a pure good pleasure can be necessary for the sake of the goodness of the thing and is nevertheless free from inclination and breaks off.",["'Good pleasure': Belieben; 'goodness': bonitaet; 'thing': Sache.","'breaks off' renders the AA's 'bricht ab'."]],"reflm:3865":["Freedom is properly a faculty of subordinating all voluntary actions to the motives of reason.",["'voluntary': willkührliche (from Willkühr, choice); 'motives': Bewegungsgründe."]],"reflm:3866":["The *stimuli* [move] do not necessitate our choice *objective*, i.e. that which one ought to do is independent of all sensible stimuli.",["'sensible stimuli': sinnlichen Reizen; the first 'stimuli' is Kant's Latin stimuli.","objective: 'objectively'; 'choice': Willkühr."]],"reflm:3867":["No one counts as part of freedom the faculty of being able to desire that which is worthy of abhorrence (evil).\nWe thus have sensible cognitions. sensible pleasure and displeasure and sensible desires. The faculty according to motives of reason is freedom. The possibility of willing with consciousness that which is disapproved by reason is the weak will; the [Inc] aptitude for willing evil is the evil will.",["[Inc] renders the deleted fragment Nei(gung), 'inclination'.","'aptitude': Fertigkeit; 'motives': Bewegungsgründe."]],"reflm:3868":["The faculty of actively willing the cognized good, which is in our power, is freedom; but [not] there does not belong to it just as necessarily the faculty of willing the cognized evil, whose prevention is in our power. This is also not properly a faculty, but a possibility of suffering. Evil actions do stand under freedom, but do not happen through it.",["'suffering': leiden, also 'being passive'."]],"reflm:3869":["The true ends with respect to things are subordinated to the idea of the good; but with respect to the person the idea of that which is personally good is subordinated to the end, not which the person has, but to which the nature of the person is arranged and which one knows through observation.\nOf all things there is a twofold ground of their nature: the cause and the end. The end seems to be just as much a ground of existence, namely of the worth of existence, as the efficient cause.",["'existence' stands for both Daseyn and the Latinate existentz.","'end': Zwek (Zweck); 'efficient cause': Wirkende Ursache."]],"reflm:3870":["M 283':\n(s pathological is that which, as to the determination of the force, is in conformity with impressions.)\n(s Practical is everything that belongs to action according to rules, or everything that is connected with determining rules of actions. *Necessitatio est vel pathologica vel practica*; |\nM 283: |\nthe latter can be objective, the former not.)\nM 283':\nSomething is considered practically in general insofar as it is weighed according to the laws of free choice. If it happens according to the rules of good choice, then it is moral, hence practically and morally |\npossible or necessary. To practical philosophy there belongs only understanding and a physical feeling, to the moral understanding and a moral feeling. A human race would be possible even without the latter.",["The Latin: 'Necessitation is either pathological or practical'.","'choice': Willkühr; 'connected': verknüpft; 'impressions': Eindrüke."]],"reflm:3871":["Opposite M § 723 (13638-1371), probably \nAs concerns the inner necessity [of the distinction] in the faculty of desire, we see ourselves necessitated not only through feeling (*stimuli*) to desire something; but we see ourselves also necessitated [our these our desires] to put ourselves in the place of a being which is subject to no such desires or feeling, and to regard our actions themselves from merely universal concepts of satisfaction, and we feel ourselves necessitated to regard ourselves with abhorrence or satisfaction. Now because this abhorrence itself goes to the desire, it furnishes a more excellent law than the desire.\nWhy are we necessitated, the abhorrence vo bricht ab.",["The opening line is the AA's locus note; 'wohl' (probably) stands without its complement in the source.","Wohlgefallen = satisfaction; Abscheu = abhorrence; 'ein vorzüglicher Gesetz' is ungrammatical in the German (for 'vorzüglicheres').","'vo' is a broken-off word, left as it stands; 'bricht ab' (breaks off) is the editor's."]],"reflm:3872":["The free will is as it were isolated. Nothing external determines it; it is active without suffering. The *motiva* are only objects that harmonize with the inner law of its activity.* The good moves only a good will, i.e., it is only the effect [according to] that is possible according to laws of the same. Whether it be determined by moving causes or by *causis efficientibus* (the Godhead), the *subiect* is hung onto a foreign cause by means of a chain, and its actions are only derived, the good as well as the evil.\nFreedom consists in the faculty of being able to act independently of external determining grounds according to intellectual choice. [Of it] Under this stands all sensibility. Hence |\nwe think even precipitancies or a series of obscure representations, which are the causes of errors, as subject to our choice. The actions that happen according to mere rules of sensibility. We must distinguish in the human being the animal, i.e., him according to the laws of sensibility, and the spirit: according to laws of reason. His choice as animal is actually always determined by *stimulos*; but his will is nevertheless free, insofar as his reason is capable of altering these determinations of choice. Why it is that reason sometimes does not do this and the human being does not act according to the intellectual will is unknown. But precisely from this it happens that the human being, considered as spirit, censures himself as animal, is in contradiction and strife with himself, wishes himself other inclinations, but often loses himself in sensibility. The one spiritual use is to order the animal sensations and to direct means according to animal ends. The other is to consider the ends themselves *intellectualiter*.\nHow is it possible that one can censure oneself, if the self is not, so to speak, twofold? For otherwise one can indeed pass no other judgment on oneself than what is taken from oneself and also agrees with oneself.\n*(s Not the objective but the subjective grounds necessitate *actualiter*.)",["Willkühr = choice; Wille = will; Bewegursachen = moving causes; Geist = spirit (here opposed to Thier, animal).","'The actions that happen according to mere rules of sensibility' is a sentence without a main verb in the German.","'necessitiren' is a Germanized Latin verb, rendered 'necessitate'."]],"reflm:3873":["The (g contradictory) opposite of the [things] thing is to be distinguished from the opposite of its relation; the latter is the opposite of the judgment.\ne.g. a divisible body; its opposite is: *Corpus* ×0 + *divisibile* ×0 =0 *nihil privativum*. This opposite never contradicts itself.",["The deleted word is 'Sachen', the word kept 'Dinges' (thing)."]],"reflm:3874":["Space and time are mere possibilities, whose opposite, i.e., no space and no time, is impossible. (There is no space, there is no time). Now space and time are grounded on something actual, therefore something actual is absolutely necessary.",["wirklich = actual."]],"reflm:3875":["The absolute necessity of something being cognized, its existence is cognized.\nAbsolute necessity is not cognizable externally through things caused, but internally from the idea of it itself.\nAn existent with whose cancellation all possibility is cancelled is absolutely necessary.\nsome possibility (g internal) being cancelled, all possibility is cancelled; for that being cancelled, something absolutely impossible is thought; but if there were still some things thinkable, it would be possible that something is impossible. Otherwise: If some existent were absolutely necessary, another existent likewise, and so |\non: one of the existents could be cancelled with the existence of the others preserved, and therefore possibility would extend only as far as some existence reaches.\non the notion of possibility in which abstraction can be made from existence, in relation to the possibility in which abstraction cannot be made from it, e.g. space, time.\nImpossibility is either of repugnance or of negation; the former always obtains if the relation of certain given notions is impossible; the latter insofar as there is not anything that can be given for thinking, i.e., possible.",["tollere / sublatum = cancel / cancelled; causata = things caused.","'ipsius' may refer to the necessity or to the thing whose necessity it is.","The sentence after 'Aliter' is elliptical in the Latin and is left so."]],"reflm:3876":["That the possibility of something does not contain the ground of its existence itself. Nor existence that of its possibility. But that in the necessary, possibility and existence are the same. That nothing contains the ground of itself.",["Accusative-with-infinitive throughout, rendered 'That ...'; ratio = ground."]],"reflm:3877":["The cause of a substance through necessity of nature is emanative (*systema emanationis*).\nThe cause of a substance through freedom is creator.\nabsolute necessity of the substances of the universe with contingency and dependence of state bricht ab?",["'bricht ab?' (breaks off?) is the editor's, with his query."]],"reflm:3878":["Benignity is the divine will in relation to the state (g the possible happiness) of the rational creature. Holiness is the divine will as a ground of the rules of the human. Justice is the (g wise) goodness of the divine will in relation to the prescription of holiness, *virtualis*.",["Gütigkeit = benignity, Güte = goodness.","'des Menschlichen' may be read as 'of the human [will]' or 'of what is human'."]],"reflm:3879":["The world cannot be compared as regards time with any being outside the world. It has a beginning, but not in regard to God an origin, but rather a dependence. I can compare the duration of the world with its parts as an hour with minutes, but not with any thing that is distinct from the world. Each thing in the world could indeed well have been earlier, but not the world itself.",["'Eher konte wohl ein iedes Ding in der Welt gewesen seyn' can also be read 'each thing could well have been in the world earlier'."]],"reflm:3880":["The world, according to that which in it is a work of God, is the best among all possible ones. For this is the [practical] representation of the greatest practical perfection of God. The representation of the greatest perfection of the world on this side includes in itself all hopes that I can make for myself from the existence of God, as an identical proposition. And hence this proposition is also a principal proposition. But from the side of freely acting beings one cannot assert it in just the same way. If one wanted to say: God has the power to make the free beings do what pleases him, then this would be contrary to their freedom; or: he foresaw what they would do in all circumstances, and chose the best conditions, then this foreseeing would prove the necessary connection of the actions with these conditions, which are posited by God.",["'conditions' renders both the Latinate 'conditionen' and 'Bedingungen'.","Hauptsatz = principal proposition; Verknüpfung = connection."]],"reflm:3881":["Insofar as the occurrences of the world are supposed to arise from its nature, they all happen according to [communal] universal laws, because nature [with] (unless a miracle happens) always remains the same and thus the alterations are in accordance with a common *principio*. Now all things of the world, each of which has its particular |\nnature, are on one another [dependent] and one state dependent on the other; thus no occurrence happens merely in relation to one state, but to all states of the thing, namely to its nature, and at the same time according to the laws of the nature of other connected things. e.g. A human being perishes at sea according to laws of the nature of the water, the winds and the shore, and not merely in relation to his moral constitution. Now it is impossible that the occurrences as individually intended [by] with all things of the world should by chance accord, e.g. the human being's vicious deed and winds, each of which happens according to the order of the nature of the *subiect*; hence they could then not follow from nature and according to one and the same laws.\nOne is accustomed to answer the opinion of some [of the], that God directs his direction not[before] at *individua* but at the universal, by saying that the universal is thought only through separation from the *individuis*. Here it is to be noted that the universal is either the logically universal ([or] the common mark of many) or the *realiter* universal. [That one] e.g. [posits] suppose: a machine is designed for many individual effects [e.g] such as the duck of Vaucanson, then I can say: [its] the provision of the artist is directed at the universal of the animal actions, namely at the logically universal or at everything; but if the arrangement of the machine is so made that it is designed for no individual ones at all, but indeterminately for one ground of all of them, then the preparation is a universal real ground. e.g. thus a barometer is a universal air-gauge, and the feet of a leaper are arranged not for the individual dances, but for walking and dancing in general.",["'gemeinschaftlich' = common (deleted '[communal]' renders the same word); Merkmal = mark.","'ohn' and 'vor' are broken-off deleted words; the source writes 'ei' for 'ein' before 'Barometer'.","Springer = leaper (one who jumps or dances); Zurichtung = preparation."]],"reflm:3882":["It is something different: to concede a particular providence, and to have insight into its marks in a particular case or also to understand the particular rules of the application of the goodness and wisdom of God. He who pretends to recognize a particular divine aim in an occurrence asserts that he has insight into the grounds of the divine decrees through which an occurrence necessarily had to turn out so and not otherwise. But then just this same insight would also have to suffice to predict this occurrence from the known rules of the divine decrees: Just as he has no insight into the causes of eclipses [who them not] insofar as they are determined to a certain point in time, who cannot announce them in advance.",["einsehen / Einsicht = have insight into / insight; Rathschlüsse = decrees.","Finsternisse = eclipses."]],"reflm:3883":["A ground is that through the concept of which (i.e., a universal one) (g not according to the laws of phenomena) something else is posited, and hence by which something else is universally determined; from the grounded (g predicate) to the ground (g subject) the consequence is not valid, except indeterminately or particularly.",["ratio = ground, rationatum = the grounded; conseqventia = consequence."]],"reflm:3884":["The fundamental concepts are not determinations, hence also not the essential pieces.\nThe logical essence is hypothetical or absolute; the former, what expresses the whole (g primitive) concept that we have of the thing, the second, what expresses the possible concept.\nThe real essence signifies the *subiect*, which is unaltered and determinable. Everything that is there has a real essence, which always remains, and the rest are determinations.",["'die wesentlichen Stüke' = the essential pieces (Baumgarten's essentialia).","Wesen = essence; realwesen = real essence."]],"reflm:3885":["from being to being possible the consequence is valid, which is empirical. Being possible, concluded [being possible] through the principle of contradiction, is rationally cognized. The possibility of a real nexus we cognize empirically.\nfrom not being to not being possible the consequence is not valid, but it is nevertheless an imperfect empirical conclusion.",["esse / posse = being / being possible; nexus realis = real nexus.","'qvae est empirica' may refer to the consequence or to the being."]],"reflm:3886":["Whether magnitude is not something that is in itself relative in relation to that which is the greatest and contains the data for everything possible, e.g. the triangle in infinite space. [Since] This is the absolute magnitude, which we cognize by the fact that something can be still greater, and not by the fact that it can be diminished, i.e., contains parts. The latter is the relative magnitude.",["The deleted 'Da' may be 'since' or 'there'.","'Ob nicht ...' is an indirect question without a main clause."]],"reflm:3887":["Perfections simply are [necessary agreements], what harmonizes together toward the inner worth of things. Hence only rational beings can have perfections; e.g. understanding is distinguished from beauty and happiness.\n*in sensu respectivo* [either the perfection of the], where to one nothing is lacking either *in respectu* to something positive in general or *in respectu* to a determinate concept: form of perfection.\n(g the [inner] worth itself is the *bonitaet*, comes down to the matter of perfection.)",["schlechthin = simply; Werth = worth; zusammenstimmen = harmonize together.","'einem' may be 'to one' (a thing) or 'to someone'."]],"reflm:3888":["The [schl] internally necessary is simply necessary (*omnimode*); but we cannot have insight into the inner necessity because of the omission of the determinate conditions. But our concept of necessity also permits that of a ground, and indeed of a universal ground, and this is also understood in the idea of thoroughgoing necessity; but this is only a relational concept in the subordination of ideas and logically conditional, and also serves only to distinguish the *omnimode* necessary from that which is *restrictive tale*.",["'[schl]' is a deleted beginning of 'schlechthin', left as it stands.","'conditions' renders the Latinate 'conditionen'; durchgängig = thoroughgoing."]],"reflm:3889":["*Realitas in sensu substantivo* is a perfection in the metaphysical sense.\nThe highest reality consists not in everything being in it, but through it as a ground; for the *maximum* of reality is not possible synthetically or through *coordination*, but lesser degrees are possible only through limitation of the greatest. Now the highest reality is that which cannot be limited; thus that which is the measure of all things and wherein the reality of all things lies is only the sequel of the *ente summo*.",["Einschränkung / eingeschränkt = limitation / limited; Folge = sequel."]],"reflm:3890":["Every universal concept contains that which is common to many things |\n(g in which many are one and the same), and is undetermined with regard to the differences.\n[Through it one can think several things, and it contains many under itself]\nA particular concept is more determined, namely with regard to the differences. (g Of the thoroughly determined.)\nIt contains [many] the particular ones under itself and is *latior*; the particular ones are *conceptus angustiores*. It is called *superior* with regard to the particular ones, which are *inferiores*.\nIt [holds less in itself] is contained in the lower ones and contains less in itself.\nlogical *subordination*. *Conceptus summus* (g *minime determinatus*), *infimus*. *maxime determinatus* (g *singularis*). *Sphaera conceptus*. *Logica divisio*.\n*Progressus per synthesin, regressus per analysin*.\nIn the series of logical *subordination* there is a member which has higher ones over itself and lower ones in itself: *conceptus communis* [*subord:*] (s *subalternus*); if it is considered only in its *superiori*, then it is represented *in abstracto*; in its *inferiori*: *in concreto*; *maxime in abstracto* or *in individuo*.\n*conceptus communis* [*inferior*] (s *subalternus*) is with regard to its *inferioris* *genus*, with regard to its *superioris* *species*. A *genus* which with regard to its *superioris* is *species* is *subalternum*; one which is not: *summum*. A *species* which contains only *individua* under itself, *species infima*. *Continuitas formarum logicarum*. Between two *conceptibus* logically subordinated to one another there is always a middle one.\n*Subordinatio realis*. *Omnitudo realis*. In place of the determinations of the universal concept, limitation of the *sphaera* of the highest ground, *negationes*. In logical divisions one limits the *sphaeram*, in real determinations one limits the reality. The *conceptus* (g *logice*) *inferior* contains fewer determinations, the *realiter inferior* fewer *positiones* in itself, until *conceptus infimus*, |\n*realitas evanescens* is or *negatio pura*. Nevertheless the *subordinatio realis* is also only according to possibility and thus *Metaphysica*. from which the *caussalis* in existence is called *physica*.\n*Omnitudo realis* [signif] is opposed to the *particulari negationi*.",["einerley: one and the same; Bestimmungen: determinations; Einschränkung, einschränken: limitation, limit.","realitaet (Latinate spelling) rendered reality; [bedeut] is a deleted beginning of bedeutet, signifies.","von der die caussalis im Daseyn physica heißt: von der may mean from which, or as distinguished from which."]],"reflm:3891":["The origin is an instant upon which the whole existence follows; the passing away (end) the instant which follows upon the whole existence. Thereby it is shown first that the origin is an existence, second a *terminus* of duration.\nA whole arises out of something if the existence of the parts precedes the form, and out of nothing if nothing of the parts is prior to the whole.",["Ursprung: origin; Vergehen: passing away; Augenblik: instant; entspringen: arise."]],"reflm:3892":["Whether there is a *spatium absolutum* or *tempus absolutum* would mean as much as whether between two things in space one could annihilate everything [other] lying between and yet the determinate empty gap would remain, and whether, if [between the beginning] for a whole year motions and alterations in general ceased, the following could not begin, so that an empty intervening year would have elapsed. We do not resolve these difficulties, but answer our opponents by *retorsion*, because their conception has precisely these difficulties.\nThe ground or principle of a state is time, and indeed of the conjunction of all things which belong to the same state.",["Veränderungen: alterations; Auffassung: conception (also apprehension).","retorsion: Kant's Latinate term (retorsio), turning an argument back on the opponent.","Latin last sentence: ratio = ground, principium = principle, coniunctio = conjunction."]],"reflm:3893":["It is absolutely impossible that a thing be in many places at the same time, but not that it be in them one after another.\nIt is only conditionally [necessary] impossible that many substances be in one and the same place, namely those which are impenetrable.",["schlechthin: absolutely; bedingt: conditionally; einerley Orte: one and the same place."]],"reflm:3894":["Cosmology is either empirical (physics and psychology) or rational.\nThis considers either the material, i.e. [subject] the constitutive things, (matter and the immaterial), or the form. The latter is treated here.\n[Empirical] Rational cosmology belongs to metaphysics.",["Latin: materiale (singular) and constitutiva (neuter plural) stand in apposition; [subiect] is a deleted beginning of a word, perhaps subiectiva.","Haec, this, may refer to cosmology or to rational cosmology."]],"reflm:3895":["Whether the *ens realissimum* is at the same time *absolute perfectum* (g *summum bonum*), is the question; for *respectiv sive externe perfectissimum* it is certainly. The *interna perfectio* (*bonitas*) presupposes the feeling of pleasure.",["den read as denn, for.","respectiv: German-Latin form of respective, relatively."]],"reflm:3896":["Nothing is either in the logical sense opposed to something; in the metaphysical sense it is opposed to the thinkable. The former is nothing relatively such (none of those things which are contained under some notion). The latter is the opposite of those things which are contained under any notion at all.",["Latin: the first vel has no second vel corresponding to it; the second member follows without it.","notio rendered notion; cogitabile: the thinkable."]],"reflm:3897":["*ratio* is either among coordinates (s *ratio completa*) or subordinate sufficient.\nWith the former one no longer asks the »why« of the sequel, with the latter of the ground.\n( s The latter stands under no higher ground, the former is no part of a greater ground. The *terminus* of the subordinate grounds is *ratio primitiva*. The *terminus* of the coordinate grounds is *completudo*. )\n(s The whole of the series or of the ground.)",["ratio: ground; Folge: sequel (the consequence of a ground).","subordinata sufficiens: whether sufficiens qualifies ratio or subordinata is left open, as in the Latin."]],"reflm:3898":["In complex concepts the consequence *a posse ad esse* is not valid, but in simple ones.\nFrom the form of possibility one cannot infer to actuality, but indeed from the matter.",["conseqventia: consequence, the inference from being possible to being; schließen: infer; Wirklichkeit: actuality."]],"reflm:3899":["All unity is either of comparison or of connection. The first is, insofar as something is one and the same with much else. The second, insofar as many are combined in one ground.\nThe unity of taking together (*addition*): is mathematical, or of derivation: this is philosophical.",["Verknüpfung: connection; verbunden: combined; Zusammennehmung: taking together; Ableitung: derivation."]],"reflm:3900":["Everything is necessary. either conditionally (g i.e. through a ground) or unconditionally.\n[Everything] Among all grounds there must be a first ground, this is absolutely necessary.",["The period after nothwendig and the lower-case entweder are kept; the two may be read as one sentence.","bedingt / unbedingt: conditionally / unconditionally; schlechthin: absolutely."]],"reflm:3901":["The *synthesis* of reality consists merely in the separation from the |\nsingle greatest through negations, because negations presuppose reality; consequently therein lies the difference of the [gan] composite and simple.",["einigen read as einzigen, single; Absonderung: separation.","[gan] is a deleted beginning of gantz, whole, left untranslated."]],"reflm:3902":["Besides substance and *accidente* one can think yet something positive, namely the *respectum*. All *respectus* (g of these) is threefold: that of substance (empirical): space, that of the *accidentium*: time, that of substance to the *accidentibus*: force.",["respectus: relation; Kant keeps the Latin case forms (accidente, respectum, accidentium, accidentibus).","Kraft: force."]],"reflm:3903":["Substance alone is a thing or [something] object.",["Gegenstand: object; Ding: thing."]],"reflm:3904":["A state is the coexistence of the substantial with the accidents.\nThe substantial is immutable; for since it contains the first ground of the accidents and hence of the alterations, if it were altered, this alteration would be an accident, not the substantial. But the first ground is immutable for this reason, because, since there is no other ground of the alteration, it would alter itself, i.e. a and not a would be determined at the same time in the same being.",["Latin: status = state, ratio = ground, mutatio = alteration, ens = being."]],"reflm:3905":["On M 369:\nThe whole of the world must not be confused |\nwith the whole state of the same. For the accidents are not parts of the world. Consequently I can say: the world arises little by little, in that one part after the other is created.",["ZuM 369: on § 369 of the Metaphysica (M is the AA's siglum for it).","Das ganze der Welt: the whole of the world; Zustand: state; entspringt: arises."]],"reflm:3906":["( s Necessity and contingency in the world. Nature (s with ground) and freedom and the perfection of both: happiness and morality. Blind necessity, blind contingency. Hazard. Supreme *principium* of the rationally necessary, natural necessity [of the rationally contingent] and freedom; both are contained in the supreme being. )( s Nature and freedom fate and chance. { Connection. *saltus*. Order. *)\n*( (g this concerns occurrences, consequently what happens in the world; for the origin of the same is no occurrence.) )\nAll occurrences of the world happen either through blind necessity:\n*bruta necessitas*;\n*Fatum* or through fate — destiny — decree of the supreme cause;\n*per libertatem* or through freedom of the acting beings in the world;\n*casus* or not through fate or freedom;\n*fortuitum* or from no cause at all.\nBlind necessity (s — Rational)\nPredetermined. Destiny.\nThrough freedom (s Contingency.)\nwithout will — — — chance\nblind hazard. (s Natural chance.)\nNothing happens through a leap, chance and fate.\n( s In the series of everything contingent the first is the free action. In the series of everything necessary the first the absolute necessity. Boundaries. )\n(s quality of the world; the world does not consist of a void.)\n(s Every transition is through all possible intermediate members.)\n(s The in itself contingent, which nevertheless necessarily [is] happens without ground, is blind necessity.\nThe in itself dependent, which there [is] happens without any[ground] necessity, is blind chance.)\n(s The necessity in the world without any ground is the blind one.\nThe contingency in the world without any ground is the blind one.)",["Zufall: chance; Ohngefehr: hazard; Zufälligkeit: contingency; Schicksal: fate; Verhängniß: destiny; Rathschlus: decree.","Begebenheiten: occurrences; Grentzen: boundaries. The *) and *( are Kant's reference marks, kept.","Latin lines translated: Nihil per saltum, casum et fatum accidit; qvalitas mundi; mundus non constat vacuo."]],"reflm:3907":["( s (g negative: One does not speak of the divine reason.) Understanding. No sensibility. (*non affectus*.) intuitive, not discursive: goes to the possible and the actual equally strongly (g sees everything in itself). No difficulty on account of the future time. (g *Perfectiones qviescentes et operativae*.) [Will] Pleasure and displeasure From itself, blessedness. Will. Freedom. Independence from impulses (g *analogon* of motive); yet no *absolutum decretum*, but conformable to the goodness of the object. Moral motive: justice. Benevolent aim. )\nAll great properties which I state of God from the arbitrary idea of him are only expositions of the hypothesis which I assume. But those which I draw from the work (these, however, are only practically perfect) apply to a real being, and one given through actual documents.\n1. Existence [of God] of the original being: against the atheists (s dogmatic and skeptical). Existence [of God] of the supreme intelligence: against the deists.\n(s real condition of all possibility. Necessity. immutability. impassibility. Immateriality. Infinity.)\n[2. Original against the Epicureans] (Existence of the supreme person: against the fatalists.)\n3. Immutable and impassible: against the anthropomorphists (g that his concept is purely intellectual).\n4. Substantiality: against Spinoza.\n(s Not the sole substance (g Simple): against Spinoza (so that the world would inhere in him as an accident); not the sole substance (g composite) as the complex of all things, i.e. το παν [as if God were an accident inhering in the world]. God is an extramundane being.)\n5. that he is an extramundane being: against the Stoics, because they maintained a soul of the world.\n6. Uniqueness: against the polytheists.\n7. Supreme perfection (s Highest good): against the Manichaeans.\n8. Simplicity. (s Infinity: against the anthropomorphists.)\n9. Eternity. (s 10. Causality with respect to the world.)\n10. Omnipresence. (s Extramundane being (not *anima mundi*): against the Stoics.)\n11. Omnipotence. (s Intelligence: against the deists.)\na. Personality: against the deists through intellectual concepts of the consciousness of oneself, not insofar as we are affected, but act. (s The transcendental, natural and moral theology. God as a holy one.)",["Bewegungsgrund: motive; Antriebe: impulses; Absicht: aim; Willkührlich: arbitrary; Seeligkeit: blessedness.","conform der bonitaet des obiects: Kant's Latinate words (conform, bonitaet, obiect) rendered conformable, goodness, object.","The Latin heads are translated; treffen rendered apply to (literally hit upon)."]],"reflm:3908":["(s a)\na: Against the Manichaeans.\n(s a 2,)\n3. All-sufficiency. As cause of the universe, even of the possibles.\n4. Immutability.\n5. Impassibility (s Spirituality: against the deists, theism, but not anthropomorphism.)\n(s c)\n6. Unity. (s Holiness: against the heathens.)\n(s d)\n7. Simplicity.\n8. Immateriality.\n9. Intelligence. Living God.\n(s a 3. Infinity [ads] internal — relative, all-sufficiency [with respect]. the conditions are 9 and 10.)",["[ads] is a deleted fragment, left untranslated; [respectu] rendered [with respect].","omnisufficientia: all-sufficiency; ethnici: heathens."]],"reflm:3909":["transcendental concept of God.\nGod as the highest being. (s Defense against atheism.)\nmetaphysical Psychological:*\nGod as the highest intelligence. (s Defense against deism. Which properly attacks anthropomorphism.)\nmoral:\nGod as the highest good. (s Defense against Epicureanism.)\n(s He can be no [comp] rightful lawgiver of morals if he is not at the same time that of nature, and indeed in its whole completeness. For if he does not have fate wholly in his power, then he cannot command absolutely. But for that he must be creator; this is necessary to speculation, but not to religion.)\nα. Morality against the Epicureans. (s Moral theology, theological morals.)\n*(s we can cognize God only psychologically, according to the analogy with the forces of nature, which contain the complete causality of perfection. For we have no *data* of God, but only *data* of his relation to the world, thus we cognize in him only s breaks off.)",["Sitten: morals; Moral: morals; erkennen: cognize; Kräfte: forces; Verhältnis: relation.","[comp] is a deleted fragment, left untranslated; theologischeMoral is run together in the source.","s bricht ab: the AA's note that the s addition breaks off."]],"reflm:3910":["I know of God only the condition of his sufficient relation to the world, and indeed this relation is similar to that of the things of the world, because he is the *principium* of the series; but these properties are not similar to the properties of the things of the world. We can quite well ignore that which has no such relation. For it does not belong to us.",["kennen: know; hinlänglich: sufficient (as zureichend); Verhältnis: relation; Eigenschaften: properties."]],"reflm:3911":["God is perfect through his nature, but he is good through his will. The *ideal* of a good will is realized in God or through God.\nThat which is good, its existence has a worth. Perfection contains the *completudinem* of the *datorum* for something, from whose worth we abstract.",["Werth: worth; completudo datorum: completeness of the data, in Kant's Latin case forms."]],"reflm:3912":["The world has a beginning, i.e. a state which is no sequel from another state: *terminum a priori*; *non est aeternus a parte ante*. For if every state of the world were a sequel from another state of the world, then all states of the world would have another state of the world before them; thus a state of the world would be distinct from all states. which *contradictorium*.\nFrom the beginning of the world up to the present time no eternity has elapsed: *mundus non creatus est ab aeterno*.\nThe world could not have been created earlier; but we could well have existed later.\nWe cannot distinctly represent how the causality of a necessary cause begins. For we always think that it was not before. But we must indeed think at the beginning of all things a *relation* of it *a parte post*, but none *a parte ante*, thus no relation of the world to a previously elapsed time.",["Folge: sequel; Zustand: state; unterschieden: distinct.","relation (Kant's Latinate word) and Verhaltnis are both rendered relation.","es in daß es vorher nicht gewesen sey may refer to the causality or be impersonal."]],"reflm:3913":["[*consensus vel opposit*]\nAll combination is either one of combination or of comparison. The former is either of agreement or of conflict.\nThat of combination is either analytic (of the part with the whole and thus also with itself) and is logical; or synthetic: of the parts among one another, of the [parts] combined unities among one another.",["Verbindung: combination, repeated as in the source; Einstimmung: agreement; Wiederstreit: conflict.","The deleted Latin heading [consensus vel opposit] breaks off and is left in Latin."]],"reflm:3914":["There is nothing in the [human soul] metaphysics empirical except what concerns the universal laws of thinking in the human soul.\nHence also the synthetic principles hold not as rational but as subjectively universal; and it is not possible that the understanding should begin from the concepts and merely compare the concepts analytically, if some rules of *synthesis* do not lie at the ground.",["'principles': Grundsätze.","'hold ... as subjectively universal': subiectiv allgemein gelten; 'subjectively' may qualify 'universal' or the verb 'hold'."]],"reflm:3915":["[The relation of magnitudes is the ground of an ideal combination, in that I from a]\nThere are comparisons which are not connections, e.g. of identity, diversity. These are ideal connections or conflicts.\nComparisons of magnitude.",["'combination': Verbindung; 'connections': Verknüpfungen; 'conflicts': wiederstreite (Widerstreit).","'identity, diversity': identitaet, diversitaet, Latinate forms in the source.","The deleted opening sentence breaks off after 'aus ein'."]],"reflm:3916":["The comparison of concepts according to the law of the imagination is not wholly to be rejected. Things that are always encountered combined with one another give presumption that they are combined according to the law of the understanding. Hence the inference from induction. According to the law of wit the inference from analogy.",["'presumption': vermuthung (also 'conjecture').","'wit': Witz; 'inference': Schluß."]],"reflm:3917":["(s All cognition has two *termini*, *a priori et a posteriori*; hence *principia prima a priori* or *a posteriori*. [If ab] The *principia absolute prima a priori* are all indemonstrable; thus metaphysics contains only *principia comparative prima*.)\n(s All pure ideas of reason are ideas of *reflexion* (*discursivae* and not *intuitus*, as Plato asserted). Hence through them objects are also not represented, but only laws for comparing the concepts which have been given to us through senses.)\nAll cognition has grounds and among these the first. The first are either those that naturally proceed in the arising, or those [from which] in which it finally lets itself be resolved and out of which again be composed: *a priori* or *posteriori*. Grammatical: empirical, rational.\n(s empirical or intellectual principles)\nThe first grounds from behind of cognition are experiences.\nThe science of arriving at the supreme grounds *a priori* is metaphysics. This science is resolving. Fundamental concepts, fundamental judgments.\nMethod of nature.\nConditioned indispensability of this science in higher judgments of reason.\nHistory. Names.\nOrder of the disciplines.\n( g Grounds of one's own insights\nothers' [Ins] testimonies Proverbs )",["'it ... lets itself be resolved': sie ... sich auflösen ... lassen; 'sie' may refer to the cognition or to the grounds.","'Grammatical': Grammatic, perhaps 'grammar'; the sense of the abbreviation is uncertain.","'resolving': auflösend; 'judgments of reason': Vernunfturtheilen."]],"reflm:3918":["The *territorium* of metaphysics. Parts.\nThe function of it determines its worth. Is not the satisfac|tion\nof the desires for knowledge, but to determine the boundaries, partly positively, partly negatively.\nIt is a science of the ends of our cognition. Comparison with mathematics.\n[history. Aristotle.]",["'desires for knowledge': Wisbegierden (plural).","'boundaries': Grenzen; 'ends': Zweke."]],"reflm:3919":["(s The understanding connects concepts because one includes the other; the *imagination*, because one accompanies the other; wit, because one is similar to the other. With negative judgments it is just the same. *e.g.* Because we combine contact with the immediate effect, we deny attraction.)\nThe law of the understanding is not brought into exercise otherwise than with a great intermixture with the law of the *imagination*.\nIt is not enough to have grounds for a cognition; one must also slowly accustom oneself to it. (Friction). It is not enough to cancel grounds; one must also for a time resist habit, gradually to hold back the same former judgment even without grounds.",["'connects': verknüpft; 'combine': verbinden; 'cancel': aufheben.","The final infinitive clause may state the purpose of resisting habit or what the habit does; the German leaves it open."]],"reflm:3920":["With all judgments of the understanding the case is as follows. (If (g any) something x [which] through a representation a cognized [is, is compared with another concept b, either that it the representation b] can be, then a is a mark of something x, [but] the cognition, however, of x through a is a concept. Thus extension, motion, ignorance etc etc. is a mark of something x). If any something x, which is cognized through a representation a, is compared with another concept (g b), either that it include this or exclude it, |\nthen this relation is in the judgment. This judgment is (g thus) either the cognition of agreement or of conflict, so that in the [concept of] thing x, which I know through the concept a, either b is contained as a partial concept and thus x [also], which is cognized through a, can also be cognized through b, or that [through] x cancels the concept of b.\nIn all judgments the matter and the form are to be considered. That the first are the concepts of the subject (y+a)\n= x. and of the predicate b. secondly the form, which among the logicians is called the concept of combination (*copula*). (One can represent every concept in relation with all others; those that it includes stand toward it in the relation of affirmation, those that it excludes, in negation.) x is called the possible concept of a thing, a the representation through which I think it. All form of judgments is either affirmation or negation. The first represents the relation where the concept of the thing y+a includes the mark b and thus is identical with it as to its part. Negation consists in this, that the concept of the thing y+a is the opposite of the mark b, and in the representation of the cancellation consists negation.",["The German word order of the first conditional is kept so that the deletion stands where it does; the final text reads 'if something x can be cognized through a representation a'.","'That the first': Daß erstere, as printed, perhaps for 'Das erstere' ('the first').","'know': kenne; 'partial concept': Theilbegriff; 'concept of combination': Verbindungsbegriff; 'cancels', 'cancellation': aufhebt, Aufhebung."]],"reflm:3921":["The predicate is no part(g concept) of the subject, but a representation of the [whole] subject through a partial concept. The understanding cognizes something always through a clear or obscure judgment, in that it resolves something into its predicates. All [signs] our concepts are marks drawn from sensation. Sensation itself is no object of the understanding, but its marks; hence e.g. the concept of the human being is nothing other than the representation of something which has the predicates into which we can resolve the concept of a human being. Hence in every judgment the subject is in general something =x, which, cognized under the mark a, is compared with another mark. Hence it is also no wonder that we cognize no subject |\nprior to all predicates except merely the I, which nevertheless is no concept but an [sensation] intuition. Hence we cognize through the understanding in bodies not the real subjects, but the predicates of extension, solidity, rest, motion etc.. The cause is: through our senses only the relations of things can reveal themselves, and we can represent the absolute or subject only from ourselves. The idea of substance comes properly from the *repraesentatione sui ipsius*, insofar as we represent that something is distinct from us, and predicates cannot be thought without subject and without ultimate subject, [in addition c] the constant predicates are then together called the subject.\nThrough a predicate I do not represent to myself a part of the thing or have a concept of the part, but represent to myself the object itself and have of it a partial concept, hence also designation through mathematical signs is impossible. Let y+s be the thing itself which is represented under the concept s, and its predicate p. then y+s–p =0 would be, consequently y+s=p.",["'object': obiect (Latinate) in both places.","'thing': Ding, and in the last paragraph also sache ('a part of the thing').","'real subjects': eigentliche subiecten (also 'proper subjects'); 'constant': bestandigen."]],"reflm:3922":["M III:\nMaterial principles seem to be: what [arises] happens must have a ground. Every successive series has a beginning*. Such propositions implicate themselves; for because the beginning is an arising [is] or happening, there would have to be, to that extent, again a ground of it. The idea of freedom indicates an arising without a preceding determining ground. The nature of our understanding brings it with it that according to its rule nothing (g contingent) is thinkable without connection with grounds, and that a sequence (according to time) without ground and a happening of something without in connection with its ground cannot be thought, because then the understanding would be entirely unusable.\nM IV:\n*(g and every series of subordinated things has a first. †)\n†(s It is difficult to think in general, in the series of all things, a beginning which everything else succeeds, as creation. Just so it is impossible to think an elapsed infinite series. To use the idea All of an infinite series seems to be false, and yet on this is grounded the necessity of the *causa prima*, because otherwise everything would be *causatum*.)\nM III:\nAnother synthetic principle is: what thinks is only a simple subject. Every thing must (not *absolute*, but *respective* to another) be at some time, either simultaneously or one after another.\nIt does not follow that what must be judged according to the laws of our understanding is true, if it concerns such things about which our understanding is not determined, according to the arrangement of its use, to judge.\n[There would be no philosophy] We borrow from bodily appearances the law of sufficient ground; but if we want to make it universal [then] and apply it to things that are elevated above the idea of our understanding, then we mix the idea of the absolute unintelligibility for us with inner impossibility.",["'sequence': Folge; 'arising': entstehen; 'happening': geschehen.","'without in connection with its ground': ohne in Verknüpfung mit seinem Grunde, as the German has it.","'unintelligibility': Unverstandlichkeit (from verstehen)."]],"reflm:3923":["Some principles are analytic and concern the formal element of distinctness in our cognition. Some are synthetic and concern the material, such as the arithmetical, geometrical and chronological ones. likewise the empirical ones. But there are still principles which concern the use of reason in *synthesi* in general. The nature of our reason, however, has this law, that it cognizes things not immediately but mediately; hence it can expect everything that happens only according to a ground, and what is determined by no other ground is irrational to it.\nMatter is lifeless, is a rational and not an empirical judgment, [it] because one also [assumes, but] perceives much life in matter, but distinguishes this from the material properties. On the other hand: matter has attractive forces, is empirical.",["'the formal element', 'the material': das formale, das materiale, substantival adjectives in the German.","'perceives': warnimt; 'assumes' in the deletion: annimt."]],"reflm:3924":["From the imperfect idea of substance it follows that one can with difficulty represent that which is and acts merely through alien force, with difficulty, as a distinct substance. Spinozism.",["'with difficulty' is doubled in the German (schweerlich ... schwerlich).","'alien force': fremde Kraft (also 'another's force')."]],"reflm:3925":["The *synthesis* is either one after another,[or] i.e. of the subordinated, or next to one another: of the coordinated. from the latter arise the judgments of the whole and its parts.",[]],"reflm:3926":["How do empirical and synthetic judgments become universal.\nDo we not perhaps have, besides the *principiis formalibus* of rational propositions, also *formalia* of the synthetic and empirical. Likewise does one not have just so *principia formalia* of real connection as of the logical.",["'propositions': sätze; 'real connection': realverknüpfung."]],"reflm:3927":["Of the empirical fundamental concepts through abstraction there are innumerable ones, which cannot be further resolved into their elements, although one can indeed show the determination in their cause. The rational fundamental concepts, in which solely and alone the sensed properties of things can be explained, are, in the case of outer objects: space, time, motion. In the case of the [out] inner: A 1. immediate representation of the present, of the past, of the future. 2, comparison, distinction and sameness. 3. relation (logical) of connection and of conflict. 4. consciousness, judgments, inferences. B 1. feeling. pleasure, displeasure. 2. in relation to the judgment of the understanding or of the senses. C. desire etc etc. [Fundamental concepts which are common to both sensations] Through the nature of the understanding, not *abstrahendo* but *iudicando*, fundamental concepts of *synthesis* arise. Existence, possibility, unity, substance, *accidens*, relation, [the lo] *respectus realis*, *logicus*, necessary, contingent. Whole, a part. Simple, composite, ground, sequel, force, cause.",["'fundamental concepts': Grundbegriffe; 'existence': Daseyn; 'sameness': einerleyheit.","'sequel': Folge; 'relation': Verhältnis in the list A 3, relation in the list of concepts of synthesis."]],"reflm:3928":["The *principia* of the form of all analytic judgments (g of the existential judgments of things *absolute* or of the predicates *relative*) are the proposition of identity and of contradiction, and [the subjective] the proofs are conducted in that through the *analysin* of the given concept the identity or opposition with the predicate is shown. The *principia* of the form of synthetic judgments are: whatever is at all times combined with [the] a known part of the possible concept of a thing belongs along with it, as a part, to this concept etc etc. The proposition: every body is impenetrable, is analytic, because [itself the b] not only can the body not be thought without impenetrability, but because it can be thought solely through impenetrability; this mark belongs as *pars* to the notion of the body. But: every body is inert, is a synthetic proposition; for inertia is *compars* with the concept [of the b] of that which is thought under the expression body, to a whole concept, which is necessarily combined with those partial concepts that belong in the notion of the body. If one had the whole concept of which the notions of the subject and predicate are *compartes*, then the synthetic judgments would transform themselves into analytic ones. The question is how far there is something arbitrary here.\nSo much for the *nexu logico*. As concerns the *nexum realem*, its *principia materialia* are the experiences, the formal *principia* are: everything that happens has a determining ground, and secondly: everything has a first ground. These *principia* are both synthetic, the former for the use of our reason, the latter for the *termino* of this use. For according to the former we always look, in the series of mutually determining causes, to the higher grounds, and according to the latter we admit that this series is bounded. But it is just as impossible to represent to oneself a series of subordinated grounds that has no |\nbeginning, as to conceive how it begins. Meanwhile the proposition: everything that happens has a determining ground, this proposition, which makes an infinite series necessary, is the *principium* of the form of all our judgments of reason about real connection. But the proposition: every series of subordinated things and every successive series has a first, is a synthetic proposition, which is abstracted more from the bounds of our understanding than from the object of cognition.\nThe first in the series of causes is always free choice. That this has no determining ground is an empirical proposition, but to that extent uncertain; but its certainty depends on this, that otherwise there could be no first at all.\nOf the possibility of alteration, i.e. of coming to be and ceasing in general.",["Satz der identitaet und des wiederspruchs: singular Satz with two genitives, rendered 'the proposition of identity and of contradiction'.","Struck '[sich der K]' and '[des K]' are cut off; 'K' presumably Körper, rendered 'b(ody)'.","Latin case forms (analysin, nexu logico, nexum realem, termino) are kept as Kant wrote them; Willkühr is rendered 'choice', Veränderung 'alteration'."]],"reflm:3929":["Properly the representation of all things is the representation of our own state and the relation of one representation to the other according to our inner laws. The impossibility of separating concepts, or the combination of them without any laws of our understanding, are merely subjective; likewise also the possibility. We cognize the possibility of free actions only empirically; in a rational sense it conflicts with the laws of using our understanding. *Analysis sine termino* [is] makes the *synthesin* impossible. Hence according to the laws in the use of our understanding we posit a ground in everything that happens, but [according to] precisely for that reason the synthesis is impossible (this very thing applied to the *analysin* of the body *sine termino*, where no synthesis is impossible). Of the first relations according to laws of our understanding no further ground can be given. (g The *analysis completa* led [in all actions] from the many to unity, *in serie successivorum* to the *principium*, *in simultaneis* to the *monas*.)\nThe *axiomata* (*synthetica*) of philosophy pertain solely to the relation which can be cognized only subjectively according to laws of our understanding.",["'wo keine synthesis unmoglich ist' (where no synthesis is impossible) is rendered as it stands; the double negative may be a slip.","Verhältnis is rendered 'relation' throughout; 'relation' in the first sentence is Kant's own Latinate word.","'die Verbindung derselben ohne alle Gesetze': 'ohne alle' may attach to Verbindung or to the whole clause; left ambiguous."]],"reflm:3930":["Some concepts are abstracted from the sensations, others merely from the law of the understanding to compare, [and] to combine or to separate the abstracted concepts. The origin of the latter is in the understanding, of the former in the senses. All concepts of such a kind are called pure concepts of the understanding, *conceptus intellectus puri*. To be sure, we can set these activities of the understanding in motion only on the occasion of the sensible sensations, and become conscious of certain concepts of the universal relations of abstracted ideas according to laws of the understanding; and so here too Locke's rule holds, that without sensible sensation no idea becomes clear in us; but the *notiones rationales* arise, to be sure, by means of the sensations, and can also be thought only in application to the ideas abstracted from them, but they do not lie in them and are not abstracted from them. Just as in [mathem] geometry we do not borrow the idea of space from the sensation of extended beings, although we can make [it cl] this concept clear only on the occasion of the [bodily] sensation of bodily things. Hence the idea of space is *notio intellectus puri*, which can be applied to the abstracted idea of mountains and of barrels.\nThe [scienc] philosophy about [all that] the concepts of the *intellectus puri* is metaphysics. it is related to the rest of philosophy as the *mathesis pura* to the *mathesis applicata*. The concepts of existence (g reality), of possibility, of necessity, of ground, of unity and plurality, of the whole and the parts (g all, none), of the composite and simple, of space, of time, of alteration, (g of motion,) of substance and of accident, of force and of action, and everything that belongs to ontology proper, is in relation to the rest of metaphysics as the (g universal) arithmetic in the *mathesi pura*.",["Struck fragments '[Mathem]', '[ihn gl]', '[Wissensch]' are cut off; rendered '[mathem]', '[it cl]', '[scienc]'.","Daseyn is rendered 'existence', Vielheit 'plurality', Veränderung 'alteration'.","The final verb 'ist' is singular after a plural list of subjects, kept as 'is'."]],"reflm:3931":["The first *Principia* of empirical philosophy are propositions of experience which are universal, from which one descends to the lower ones. Those of the rational are pure grounds of reason. In empirical philosophy one proceeds analytically, in the rational synthetically.\nWe can give grounds of synthetic principles *in sensu subiectivo*, but not *in sensu obiectivo*. Only of abstracted concepts can we give grounds through the *analysin obiectivam*, but of rational ones only through *analysin subiectivam*. Or rather: of rational propositions we can give grounds only through *Analysin subiectivam*. *e.g.* Of the proposition that a being exists necessarily, namely because our thoughts of a possibility without any actuality are [contradictory] null, inasmuch as all possibility must be borrowed from something actual.\nMetaphysics proper or pure philosophy consists of ontology.",["Grundsatz is rendered 'principle', Satz 'proposition', Wirklichkeit 'actuality'.","'nichtig' rendered 'null' (void, empty)."]],"reflm:3932":["The ideas and rules of reason are also used in the relation of empirical concepts, and this is their natural and correct use; but they are then grounded on *iudicia empirica primitiva*, which are universal only through induction. but precisely these judgments of reason, insofar as they are pure, are supposed to be universal in themselves. The *philosophia pura* is not, like the *mathesis pura*, certain in itself.",[]],"reflm:3933":["In all identity of concepts [which not] two concepts agree in one, i.e. [one concept is common in both. In every judgment] one [mark] concept belongs to two; in every judgment two concepts belong to one thing. The thing that I think through the concept A, the very same |\nI think also through the concept B. is a judgment (g of connection). The concept that I think in A, that I think also in B: is a judgment of comparison.\nIn identity I compare two subjects which have one and the same predicate.\nIn unity I compare two predicates or concepts which have one subject.",["Struck '[die nich]' and '[Merkm]' are cut off; rendered '[which not]' and '[mark]'.","Verknüpfung is rendered 'connection', Vergleichung 'comparison'; einerley 'one and the same'."]],"reflm:3934":["To the unthinkability of divine properties belongs not only his presence in all places of space at once, but also in all points of time at once. i.e. that he can represent an eternity to himself wholly.",["Undenklichkeit rendered 'unthinkability'; it could also mean what is beyond (our) thinking, inconceivability.","'gantz' may attach to 'eine Ewigkeit' (a whole eternity) or to the act of representing (wholly)."]],"reflm:3935":["The proposition: all occurrences have sequels (each one), and all together in turn have sequels, are not one and the same.\nAll rational synthetic propositions are subjective, and conversely only the analytic ones are objective. *principia identitatis et contradictionis*.\nThe synthesis of reason (g rational) or of experience (g empirical).\nThe first is either of coordination: whole and parts, number and unity, or of subordination: ground and sequel.\n[Empirical] The second of coordination according to space and time.",["Singular 'Der Satz' with plural 'sind' is kept: 'The proposition ... are'.","Begebnisse rendered 'occurrences', Folge 'sequel'."]],"reflm:3936":["Everything is considered either *distributive* or *collective*. The former indicates a universal concept insofar as it is a common mark of the lower ones; the second indicates the taking together of |\nmuch. Now since the collection of everything cannot always happen, the *omnitudo collectiva* is sometimes imagined.\nThe *principia subiectiva*, when they are weighed objectively, contradict each other. E.g. Everything has a ground, contradicts this proposition: nothing is necessary through itself etc etc.",["Zusammennehmung rendered 'taking together'; 'vielem' may be 'much' or 'many'.","eingebildet: 'imagined' or 'imaginary'."]],"reflm:3937":["One is supposed to think something absolutely necessary, because everything that is, is necessary, but everything cannot be hypothetically necessary. But one can think nothing absolutely necessary. One must think the world as bounded, but one can also not think the bounds.\nThose synthetic propositions which by their nature do not [through] cancel the same conditions of comprehensibility which they posit hold objectively, as of the *commercio substantiarum*.",["schlechthin rendered 'absolutely'; aufheben 'cancel'; Begreiflichkeit 'comprehensibility'.","'als vom commercio substantiarum' may mean 'as that (proposition) of' or 'for example of'; left ambiguous."]],"reflm:3938":["Besides those determinations without which objects cannot be, there are in our reason also conditions without which we cannot think certain objects through reason, even though these conditions are not determinations of the objects themselves. These *conditiones* are therefore subjective, and their concepts signify nothing in the object. All synthetic judgments of pure reason are accordingly subjective, and the concepts themselves signify relations of the actions of reason to themselves.",["'objects' renders obiecte in the first clause and Gegenstände thereafter.","'zu sich selbst' may refer to the actions of reason or to reason itself."]],"reflm:3939":["Logic treats the objective laws of reason, that is, how it ought to proceed. Metaphysics the subjective ones of pure reason, how it proceeds. In both, reason is the object, for the (g rational) form is in every science of philosophy and mathematics. That reason, according to its laws, wants to treat the laws according to which it ought to think, brings it about that it makes use of the rules which it first wants to discover. Hence come the tautologies and rules that are without practice.",["'object' renders Obiect, not Gegenstand.","'Praxis' rendered 'practice'."]],"reflm:3940":["In all cognition of reason only relations are to be considered, and these are either given (sensibly or through the nature of reason) or invented. But we can invent no relations (g of) whose possibility we can be convinced, except as to magnitude, through repetition in the science of number. To invent with regard to the quality of relations, and thus to invent (g first) concepts of reason, is just as impossible as first sensations of the senses.",["dichten / gedichtet rendered 'invent' / 'invented' (also: fabricate, feign).","The added '(g von)' leaves the relative clause grammatically unsettled, kept as it stands."]],"reflm:3941":["Metaphysical concepts pertain 1. merely to the relation of coordination: (g *absolutum et relativum*, whole, part, *continuum*, *discretum*) (g many) some, all (g the first, last, a single one); 2. or of subordination in the logical sense: universal or particular; 3. to subordination in the real sense: ground, sequel, cause, effect. From this arises the concept of the first cause, of the last sequel, of the cause of everything, of some. 4. to existence: necessary, contingent (g possible); 5. substance. (g subject, predicate) simple, composite, *actio*, *passio* (g *vis*, *receptivitas*), *spontanea*, [*coacta*] *iners*. (g whole of substances. world.)\nTime and space.\n| Duration, moment, eternity, beginning, end. Alteration, persistence. | Place, extension, point. | (g Space and time:) Motion, rest. Omnipresence.\nEffect in space: filling, force, mass.\nEffect in time: alteration, creation, annihilation.",["Erfüllung rendered 'filling' (of space); Beharrlichkeit 'persistence'.","Verstande in 'im logischen / realverstande' rendered 'sense'."]],"reflm:3942":["True is a cognition which agrees with the constitution of the object. Since through the idea of space the representation of outer objects is only possible, all axioms of space, and what is derived from them, agree with the object, likewise all relations of concepts according to the rule of identity. For the ideas then agree among one another. But since the metaphysical concepts of ground, substance etc. are not properly representations of objects, inasmuch as even the most perfect sense cannot sense these in any thing, and without these relations things altogether, although not through our reason, can be represented: so these concepts are not objective; thus in their axioms everything is subjective. Hence, if they are falsely regarded (g as) objective, neither truth nor falsehood holds of them. In general, if one wants to find the concept of cause, then apart from the relations of connection according to ideas of time one will find no definition that would not include a circle, and it seems nothing else.\nThe surest proof that they are not objective is that they stand in evident contradiction.",["'nur Möglich' may mean 'possible only through the idea of space' or 'merely possible'; left ambiguous.","erklärung rendered 'definition' (also: explanation).","'und sie scheint nichts anderes' is elliptical and possibly incomplete; rendered as it stands."]],"reflm:3943":["(g Metaphysics is the ground of the form of all philosophy.)\nThe utility of metaphysics [first in th] can best be seen in the actual (g natural) use of it.\nThis utility is positive, to increase insights;\nnegative, to prevent false metaphysics;\nin both respects the insight is objective, through which one seeks to acquire the various objects of cognition, or subjective, where one seeks to know the limitations of cognition.\nIt is noble. The subjective can be certain, although the objective is uncertain.",["Struck '[erstlich in de]' is cut off; rendered '[first in th]'.","Schranken rendered 'limitations'; kennen 'know'."]],"reflm:3944":["The logical form of our cognitions is to be distinguished from the metaphysical; the first is analysis, the second synthesis.",[]],"reflm:3945":["About all objects of outer and inner senses we judge rationally, in that we [the laws of the] through universal laws of appearances indicate the ground of them. The first possible laws of appearances cannot be established through experience, but rather the first grounds of them rest on pure reason; *physica pura et pnevmatologia*.",["The deletion \"[die Gesetze der]\" leaves the sentence as Kant left it; \"Grund\" (ground) stands without an article."]],"reflm:3946":["M XXXXIII:\nAll pure philosophy is either logical or metaphysical. The former [judges] contains only the subordination of concepts under the *sphaeram* of the others, either immediately: in judgments, or mediately: in inferences. [The latter] It leaves, however, the concepts themselves, which can be subordinated to one another, undetermined and does not settle which predicates belong to things according to laws of pure reason. Hence to cognize the first predicates of things through pure reason is a matter of metaphysics, consequently to find the first fundamental concepts with which we judge through pure reason, and the principles.\nM XXXXIV:\n(s Metaphysics goes to cognitions solely through reason, logic to all, even empirical ones. All concepts of reason are universal; logic shows only the relation of the universal to the particular in general. Metaphysics, however, the origin of universal concepts, to which all cognition must be traced back if the appearances are to be transformed into concepts.)\nM XXXXIII:\nMetaphysics is thus a science of the fundamental concepts and principles of human reason, and not of human cognition in general, in which there is much that is empirical and sensible; logic a science of the relations of universal concepts and propositions in general. Logic borrows concepts and propositions from metaphysics or from some empirical cognition or other and teaches how to make use of them.\nLogic contains the concept of the universal, metaphysics universal concepts of reason (s the former deals with the relation of universal concepts to one another, the second indicates the universal |\nconcepts under which [other things] the concepts of reason of things are contained; that is logical theory, for logical *praxis* is tautological if the cognition of the subject is not presupposed); logic propositions which express the relation of the universal to the particular without predicate and subject, metaphysics, however, universal propositions. It contains the elements of which all cognition of reason is composed.\nM XXXXIV:\nLogic leaves the particular nature of human reason undetermined and is valid for every reason; metaphysics shows the universal concepts that flow from the nature of human reason, and their particular laws.",["\"fundamental concepts\" = Grundbegriffe; \"concepts of reason\" = Vernunftbegriffe; \"cognition of reason\" = Vernunfterkentnis.","\"[The latter] It\": Kant struck \"Diese\" and wrote \"Sie\", which can refer to logic or to pure philosophy.","\"It contains the elements\": \"Sie\" may refer to metaphysics or to logic."]],"reflm:3947":["All rational cognition contains either the first grounds of that which is an object of merely pure cognition, or the first grounds of appearance. Dogmatic and objective are the cognitions which are valid for everyone and rest on mere concepts.",[]],"reflm:3948":["If the question were whether in empirical sciences new discoveries are still to be hoped for, then there is no doubting it, because more experiences give more and new cognitions. If the question is whether in the science of pure reason [more] new discoveries are to be expected, then it is clear that in mathematics, logic and pure morals many are possible, because according to the rule of identity from universal concepts according to all sorts of arbitrarily stipulated combinations sequences [will] can be inferred. But with metaphysics it is entirely otherwise. Here it is not asked what in arbitrarily assumed |\nrelations according to the rule of identity flows from the universal to the particular, but rather which relations really lie at the first ground of universal rules. Now since these relations are given neither through the senses in experience, nor through the understanding in an intuiting and singular concept, nothing further can happen than that one analyzes one's confused ideas. From this, however, only a science of the subject can arise. Since no object is given here, we would also be able to discover nothing about it through this cutting and analyzing of our ideas.",["\"folgen [werden] geschlossen werden können\": before the deletion \"folgen werden\" reads \"will follow\"; after it \"folgen\" reads as the noun Folgen (sequences).","\"discoveries\"/\"discover\" = Erfindungen/erfinden, also \"inventions\"/\"invent\"; \"analyze\" = zergliedern."]],"reflm:3949":["Under the logical rules stand mathematics and morals (*purae*), under the metaphysical the pure cognition of that which falls into outer and inner senses, consequently the first grounds of reason of outer and inner appearances. In both one must consider that which is the most universal of the outer and inner senses according to its first grounds of reason.\nThus there is no philosophy of pure reason except logic and *metaphysica*.\nThe fundamental concept of logic is that of the universal, insofar as it contains something under itself or not; according to this relation things are thought, but in it they are not represented. The concept of the species or genus is not given, but rather is only the relation according to which *conceptus dabiles* can be compared.\nLogic gives no fundamental concepts of reason at all, but rather of the laws according to which we [to ourselves] in general make cognitions distinct through one another. Thus it contains the rules without which we can obtain no distinct cognition of things; metaphysics, however, the rules without which objects cannot be cognized by us at all. The logical propositions are [arbitr] rules which we use arbitrarily as means in order to make cognitions distinct through comparison. Metaphysics shows the marks which are given through the nature of reason (logic only the use of marks in general).",["\"grounds of reason\" = Vernunftgründe; \"things\" stands for Dinge and, in \"cognition of things\", for Sachen.","\"[arbitr]\" renders the broken-off deletion \"[willkü]\"."]],"reflm:3950":["Synthetic [propositions] concepts are space, time and number. [and are objective.]\nIf the concepts also are synthetic, but the propositions are analytic, i.e., according to the rule of identity, then they are objective.\nBut if concepts are subjective, i.e., of no object at all, neither of a given condition of the representation of objects: space and time, nor [of] arbitrary concepts of quantity, then the judgments are nevertheless objective and universally valid, i.e., either universally true or false, [but are] if their form is analytic. But if [then] their form is synthetic, then they are subjective, and objectively they are valid only as rules of appearances, consequently as empirical judgments.",["\"If the concepts also are synthetic\" (Wenn die Begriffe auch synthetisch seyn) may also be read \"Even if the concepts are synthetic\".","\"condition\" here is the Latinate Condition, not Bedingung."]],"reflm:3951":["Dogmatic *philosophia pura* contains two disciplines.\n1. The rules of the universally valid use of the understanding;\n2. the rules of the universally valid use of the free will.\nIn both one judges according to the *nexu logico* from no *datis* of experience; but in the *physiologia transscendentali* the *data* from experience are given according to the relations of space and of time in such a way that the most universal concept of the object of all outer and inner sensations is taken and the ground of it is sought.",["Kant's Latin case forms (*nexu logico*, *datis*, *physiologia transscendentali*) are kept as written."]],"reflm:3952":["Metaphysics is a science of the laws of pure human reason and therefore subjective. The objective pure philosophy |\nhas either analytic *principia* without any axioms of experience or synthetic ones. The former [consists] rests on universal judgments according to the rule of identity and on the subordination of the particular under the universal. Thus the universally valid rules of the understanding and will, consequently *Logica et Moralis pura*. The second has as its object the most universal of outer and inner sense and thus contains the pure grounds of reason to which all natural sciences of outer and inner sense can be brought.",["\"axioms of experience\" = Erfahrungsaxiomen; \"grounds of reason\" = Vernunftgründe."]],"reflm:3953":["The principles of space are objective; but whether a thing is in space or not is subjective, because the concept of space is not objective.",[]],"reflm:3954":["A chief error arises from this, when one [compares] confuses the *genus* with all *individuis* and regards as a sequence according to concepts that which is only a lawful appearance for immediate intuiting. Hence the ground is nothing objective. What we call a (g real) ground is only a concomitant appearance for the intuitive concept. And that which we call reason, likewise all its pure concepts, since we infer from the universal to the particular, have their reality, chiefly in regard to their fundamental concepts, only through the fact that they lie in things and can be separated from them. Accordingly our reason is only a supplementing of the lack of *intuitus*.\nIf I therefore say: a principle is subjective, i.e., it contains the *conditiones* under which alone we can judge through our reason according to laws of experience, then this does not signify that our reason must assume this law in the objects; for it does not concern them at all; one can thus say neither that it is false nor true.",["\"sequence\" = Folge; \"separated\" = abgesondert, also \"abstracted\".","\"since\" (da) may also be read temporally or relatively (\"where we infer\")."]],"reflm:3955":["All cognitions are either empirical, insofar as they presuppose sensations, or pure cognitions, insofar as they have no sensation as their ground. The latter, namely the pure cognitions, are either [singular] *conceptus singulares* and are called *intuitus puri*, or universal [concepts] and are pure concepts of reason. The empirical cognitions are sensation, appearance and empirical concept; from the first the matter of all cognition is to be derived, the second adds the form of intuition to it, the third brings both under a universal concept.",[]],"reflm:3956":["Immediate judgments of experience according to relations of space and time must be distinguished from reflected ones according to relations of reason.",["\"relations of reason\" = Vernunftverhältnissen."]],"reflm:3957":["M XXXXVII:\nAll human cognitions can be divided into two chief genera: 1. those which arise from the senses [or] and are called empirical; 2. those which are acquired not at all through the senses [into the], but rather have their ground in the constant nature of the [cognitive power] thinking power of the soul, and can be called pure representations. Since all materials for thinking must necessarily be given through our senses, the matter of our entire cognition is empirical. For just that reason all pure concepts must concern merely the form of cognitions. Now we have a twofold form of cognitions: the intuitive and rational form. The first takes place only in the immediate cognition of singular things, the second in universal representations; the first I will call intuiting, the second concepts of reason. Now in all empirical cognition one can look firstly |\nmerely to the matter, and this consists in sensation; secondly to the form of appearance [and this consists]; thirdly to the form of reason in concepts. The form of appearances rests solely on space and time, and these concepts arise through no senses or sensation, but rather rest on the nature of the mind, according to which the various sensations can be represented under such relations. Hence, when all sensation of the senses is set aside, then that of space and time is a pure concept of intuition, and because in it lies everything that the understanding can cognize in experiences, it is a concept of the understanding; and, although the appearances are empirical, it is nevertheless intellectual. In the same way sensations and appearances made universal are not pure but empirical concepts of reason; but if one leaves out every effect of the senses, then the concepts are pure concepts of reason, such as: possible, substance etc. Hence all pure concepts are intellectual and intuitive, or rational and [discursive] reflecting concepts. |\nM XXXXVIII: |\nFurther, all cognitions are either given or fabricated. The matter of cognition cannot be fabricated, thus only the form, and in the form only repetition; thus all fabrication of reason concerns only mathematics; on the other hand the form which is given in geometry is space.\nM XXXXVII:\n(g Because one can describe only the form of experience, it belongs to the understanding.)\nM XXXXVIII:\nA science of pure reason is possible and also necessary. It is, however, either philosophy or mathematics; the former considers: what there is, and reason solely: which *respectus* there is; the latter: how many times one and the same is. Space is an object both for philosophy and for mathematics. The judgments about space which are intuitive are not yet mathematical, nor philosophical either. Both assess space not\n|\n*a priori* [through the rea] from principles of space which reason cognizes, but rather from principles *a posteriori*, i.e., intuitively and singularly, although not empirically. The philosophy of pure reason is either dogmatic, and then its object is given through that which all senses have most in common, and as it were through the *genus* of the senses: thus firstly the power of representation and the faculty of desire as the objects of inner sense, 2. space and time, consequently universal mechanics, as the most universal of outer senses; hence the dogmatic pure philosophy is theoretical logic, theoretical morals and universal natural science. Secondly it is critical, consequently subjective. It is zetetic, skeptical, problematic.",["\"fabricated\"/\"fabrication\" = gedichtet/Erdichtung; \"power of representation\" = Vorstellungskraft; \"faculty of desire\" = Begehrungsvermögen.","\"the first I will call intuiting\": \"anschauende\" stands without a noun in the German.","\"[through the rea]\" renders the broken-off deletion \"[durch die Ver]\"."]],"reflm:3958":["All cognitions [from experience are either] from experience (g empirical ones) belong either to sensation and contain the matter of empirical cognition, or to appearance and [comprehend] contain at the same time the form, or to the concept and contain the [what to many objects] universal of different sensations or appearances. Sensation represents individual objects insofar as they stir the senses, e.g. red, black, sweet, hard, warm etc., [the] consequently only the matter of empirical cognition. The form of objects is thought according to space and time. The form of empirical cognition is that of *coordination*, that of rational cognition is that of *subordination*.\nLet one leave out all matter of cognition, consequently everything that stirs the senses, then there still remains the empirical form of appearances; let one leave this out too, then the rational form remains; |\nand cognitions of the first kind are pure concepts of intuitions, of the second pure concepts of reason.",["'matter' renders materie; 'stir' renders rühren.","The opening sentence has no main verb for its first subject after the deletion; left as it stands."]],"reflm:3959":["Metaphysics contains nothing but ontology*, which is falsely considered as a science of things in general *qvoad praedicata vniversalia et disiunctiva*.\n(g for there is no matter, consequently a something in general, the object.)",["The asterisk after 'ontology' is Kant's reference mark for the addition in parentheses.","*qvoad praedicata vniversalia et disiunctiva*: with respect to universal and disjunctive predicates.","'object' renders obiect."]],"reflm:3960":["All cognitions are either sensible cognitions or cognitions of reason. The former are either of sensation bricht ab.",["'bricht ab' (breaks off) is the AA's note that the sentence is unfinished.","'cognitions of reason' renders Vernunfterkentnisse."]],"reflm:3961":["All (g cognitions from) experience are called empirical and are either [representations of sensation or of appearances senses or] sensations or appearances or concepts. In the former everything is given through sense [in the second] and merely the matter for cognition; the second contains in itself the sensations at the same time according to the form of space and time; the third [just] the sensations or appearances made universal through reason.\nIf one leaves out the two last actions, then sensation remains. If one leaves this out, then there remain pure concepts 1. of the understanding: of *coordination*, 2. of reason: *subordination*.",["'empirical' renders Kant's spelling Empyrisch.","'actions' renders Handlungen."]],"reflm:3962":["All concepts become universal through abstraction, but they do not all ori|ginate\nfrom it; *non subtrahendo a sensibus oriuntur, sed abstrahendo*.",["The | falls inside entspringen ('originate').","*non subtrahendo a sensibus oriuntur, sed abstrahendo*: they arise not by subtracting from the senses but by abstracting."]],"reflm:3963":["Cognitions of reason are either given in the senses as to their matter and have only the form of reason, e.g. universal concepts, or they express the form of reason itself; the former are empirical, the latter *notiones purae*.",["*notiones purae*: pure notions."]],"reflm:3964":["Metaphysics is a critique of pure reason and no doctrine. Logic is the doctrine 1. of pure, 2. of mixed reason. The application of pure reason to objects that are given through experience is only in the case of empirical concepts, where one can infer from the universal to the particular.\nThe science of a cognition considered *subiective* is critique, of it *obiective* is doctrine.\nThere are certain universal concepts which are given through the nature of reason, according to which others (g and their relation) are thought. [But there are others] E.g. subject and predicate. These are metaphysical. There are representations which are given through the senses and are made universal through reason.",["'critique' renders Critik; 'doctrine' renders doctrin, Kant's Latinate word, not Lehre.","'objects' renders obiecte; 'relation' renders Verhältnis.","'of it' (von der) presumably refers back to the cognition; the construction is elliptical."]],"reflm:3965":["There are pure concepts of intuitions, arbitrary ones of fiction, and universal ones of reason.\nA concept that cannot be regarded as an impression of the senses is pure.\nPrinciples:\n[1. What we do not through intuitions] 1. We cognize everything through judgments that we do not cognize through intuitions.\n2. All judgments of our reason are mediate.",["'arbitrary' renders willkührlich; 'fiction' renders Erdichtung.","'Principles' renders Grundsätze."]],"reflm:3966":["The logical essence is the subjective fundamental concept and does not hold for all, is also changeable; the real essence is objective; the former concerns the meaning of a word, which admittedly is gradually worn down and through use becomes unanimous.",["'essence' renders Wesen; 'fundamental concept' renders Grundbegrif.","'is worn down' renders sich abschleift; 'unanimous' renders einstimig (agreeing in voice)."]],"reflm:3967":["Reason feels the need of a real ground and yet cannot think it according to its own laws; from this it is to be seen that this concept is not objective.",["'real ground' renders Realgrund."]],"reflm:3968":["All connection is either of *subordination* or *coordination* [the former is either of]. The unity, when to many grounds one consequence is subordinated, is the unity *a priori*, e.g. in the case of the utility of things. The second: when something is the ground of much, or when many consequences arise from one ground.\nIt is peculiar that, when many grounds agree together toward one consequence, one also thinks a common ground for it, e.g. the many things for the utility of the human being, many limbs on one animal.",["'connection' renders Verknüpfung; 'consequence' renders Folge.","'peculiar' renders besonders."]],"reflm:3969":["Our reason contains nothing but *relationes*. Now if these are not given through the relations according to space and time in experience, nor through the repetition and the composition of the one out of many in pure mathematics, then they are not *relationes* which refer to objects, but only relations of our concepts according to laws of our reason.",["'relations' renders Verhältnisse, beside Kant's Latin *relationes*.","'refer to objects' renders auf obiecte gehen."]],"reflm:3970":["Intuiting concepts of the senses, concepts of the understanding of *abstraction*, concepts of reason of [relation] relation, which universal cognitions have.\nThe relations of the senses are space and time, those of the understanding: the universal and particular, consequently also all judgments and the rules of all syllogisms. Those of reason: the ground and the consequence, namely *obiective*: the ground of things (real ground).\nThe relation of sameness and difference is logical (g likewise the relation of the universal and particular), [all] that of repetition is mathematical, [that] the remaining are metaphysical — namely the real relations.\nMetaphysics is the critique of human reason, logic is the universal doctrine of the same; the first is subjective and problematic, the second objective and dogmatic.",["'Intuiting' renders Anschauende; 'things' in 'ground of things' renders Sachen, not Dinge.","The deleted and the retained word are both Verhältnis, in two spellings; 'sameness' renders Einerleyheit.","'which universal cognitions have' (welches allgemeine Erkentnisse haben): welches may refer to the relation or be read as 'which [relation] universal cognitions have'."]],"reflm:3971":["True is that which holds for every cognition, and thus not merely under the condition of reason, which judges mediately. Accordingly that is true whose opposite contradicts itself, because the opposite cannot even once fall into the senses. But that something should be without ground indicates only a subjective condition, and the object could also fall into the senses without this.",["'object' renders obiect.","'without this' (ohne diese) may refer to the subjective condition."]],"reflm:3972":["The concept of the ground (g of the consequence) contains not only that something which is there is accompanied by something else, but moreover that this relation is universal and necessary; for, where such a thing (g b) is, there is such a ground a, and where a [ground] a is, there is the consequence b. Now all real grounds and even the possibility of them are knowable only *a posteriori*; these, however, do show a constant |\naccompaniment, but no universality of the connection, consequently the concept ground is not objective.",["'relation' here renders Beziehung, elsewhere Verhältnis.","'thing' renders Sache; 'knowable' renders kennbar; 'connection' renders Verknüpfung."]],"reflm:3973":["No other merely arbitrary concepts of pure reason can arise in us than those through repetition, consequently of numbers and magnitude. All [other] concepts of reason that contain another relation require that this relation be given through experience or the nature of the understanding and reason; empirical relations, however, presuppose a fundamental relation-concept. The absolute [can] and simple one cannot feign.",["'fundamental relation-concept' renders fundamentalVerhältnisbegrif.","'feign' renders erdichten; the last sentence has the absolute and simple as object of 'feign'."]],"reflm:3974":["In the use of pure reason there arises from the mixing up of these 2 concepts a particular confusion. Everything (*singuli*) has a ground; but everything taken together cannot have a ground, thus something is without ground. The first is a usable rule of reason for the explanation of appearances; the second a consequence of the *synthesi completa*, which is impossible for our understanding. Just so: everything that is to be posited through reason is necessary; but everything is necessary under its *hypothesi*.\nAll concepts are either sensible concepts or concepts of reason. The first are either of sensation or of appearance; these have as ground of the form space and time. The second can be found through no *analysin* of experience, although all experience is coordinated with them, and are pure concepts of reason if through them no object of experience is thought; if however it is the latter, then they are empirical concepts. E.g. a *genus* is a pure concept, but a stone in general or the *genus* of stone an empirical one.\nThe science of reason of the rules for judging objectively, i.e. of all [concepts] judgments and inferences insofar as they arise *per analysin*, is logic. The science of reason of [analytic] synthetic cognitions and judgments is metaphysics.\nSpace is no concept of reason, but metaphysics seeks the concept of reason of it.",["'mixing up' renders Vermengung; 'confusion' renders Verwirrung; 'explanation' renders Erklärung.","'these have as ground of the form space and time' (diese haben zum Grunde der Form Raum und Zeit): 'of the form' may go with 'ground' or with 'space and time'.","'science of reason' renders Vernunftwissenschaft and Vernunft Wissenschaft; 'inferences' renders schlüsse."]],"reflm:3975":["Because through reason, i.e. through the cognition that is no sensation, only concepts arise through which it remains undetermined whether the thing is posited or not, there belongs something to it which necessitates us to posit something. Thus the concept of a ground has a subjective origin, and the word: something is posited through another thing or for the sake of another, signifies *obiective* only: things accompany each other or follow upon one another.\nThe idea of the ground (g cause) arises from experiences.\nTo cognize through reason that something is not or that something is not possible is one and the same; that something is or that something is necessary is one and the same. On the other hand everything appears as contingent which I indeed cognize through senses, but not through reason, that it is.\nThe only concepts of pure reason through whose relation according to the rule of identity truths can be discovered are the magnitudes; but these are also arbitrary fictions.",["'there belongs something to it' renders so gehöret etwas dazu.","'identity' renders identitaet; 'fictions' renders Erdichtungen."]],"reflm:3976":["It is necessary according to the subjective law of reason to assume a first action through which all the rest follows; but it is just as necessary to assume a ground in general of every action and thus no first.\nThere are pure fundamental concepts of intuition or of reflection; the first are the principles of appearance, the second of insight; the first show *coordination*, the second *subordination*. Because everything is represented in time, all our concepts of reason are nevertheless always at the same time thought under the condition of the *phaenomeni*; the conditions of the |\nsecond do not agree with the first. In time no first is possible, and in the ground there is supposed to be one.\nIf the comprehensibility of an [act] thing were to be complete, then we would have to have a first ground; but we also cannot posit a first ground through reason, and thus it follows from this that the comprehensibility of no absolute position is complete through human reason.\nThe simple concepts of reason, indeed all simple concepts, are subjective; the objective ones consist in the agreement of cognition with itself and are thus composite. Hence the concept of the first is subjective, for it contains the concept of everything.\nAll real relations (space and time excepted) are given through experience (g according to the relations of space and time) and thus can serve for no propositions of pure reason.",["'reflection' renders reflexion; 'principles' renders principien, not Grundsätze; 'position' renders position.","'[act]' renders the deleted, unfinished Handl, the start of Handlung.","'thing' in 'comprehensibility of a thing' renders Sache; 'comprehensibility' renders begreiflichkeit."]],"reflm:3977":["All truth consists in the necessary agreement of a cognition with itself. If the cognition that is to agree with itself constitutes the form of appearance itself, e.g. space and time, then every judgment is [subjec] objective and either true or false. But if the cognition concerns solely a law of human reason through which we compare concepts, then it is not objective at all, hence neither true nor false. Hence ground and consequence is no property at all of things, which is given through mere reason, but is given only through experience. It is, however, a law of reason to look to this relation; all universal rules of reason about cause and effect have no validity at all of objects.",["'[subjec]' renders the deleted, unfinished subiec.","'which is given' (die ... gegeben ... ist) may refer to the property or to the things; the singular verb favors the property.","'property' renders Eigenschaft; 'validity of objects' renders Gültigkeit von Obiecten."]],"reflm:3978":["Every objective concept is either an original image (*archetypon*) or |\ncopy (*Ectypon*). The former happens when the concept of the object is thought arbitrarily or yet without it no concept at all of the objects [mo] and no experience is possible. The latter happens when the object is given through the senses. The pure concepts of reason, which are given through the nature of reason, are [are] not copies, secondly also not original images, for they are not thought arbitrarily, but contain the ground of the possibility of all arbitrary ideas of reason. They are also universal concepts; consequently they do not contain the form of experiences, and without them [representations] immediate ideas of experience are always possible; consequently they are only concepts of condition, without which a cognition through our reason is impossible, hence not objective.\nThe logical form all cognitions of reason have, but the metaphysical form only those [in] which presuppose real fundamental concepts or principles of pure reason.",["'original image' renders Urbild, 'copy' Nachbild.","'[mo]' is the deleted, unfinished start of möglich ('possible'), left as it stands.","'without it' (ohne denselben) may refer to the concept or to the object; 'principles' renders Grundsätze."]],"reflm:3979":["M XXXXI:\nA dogmatic science of pure reason has arbitrary ideas as its ground, and their relations it thinks to itself in logical form. But no relations can be thought arbitrarily other than those of repetition, i.e. of numbers, and consequently the science is mathematics; but one can also seek the rule of choice itself, insofar as it is supposed to be universally valid, and this is morals.\nWhere the concepts are given neither through experience nor through the nature of reason, there choice is the ground of them, either as to form, if no matter at all (g object) is given (but the arbitrary form of concepts is only the mathematical one), |\nM XXXXII: |\nor (g pure) choice (g in general) is itself the matter (g the |\nobject) and form for the concept, and the judgments are passed in both according to logical form. In mathematics it says: I will think these relations to myself, which rules follow from them according to the logical form. In morals: I will think choice itself in relations, which rules hold for it in general. Thus mathematics is the bricht ab.",["'choice' renders Willkühr; 'arbitrary' and 'arbitrarily' render willkührlich, its adjective.","'object' renders obiekt and Obiekt; M XXXXI and M XXXXII are the pages of Kant's copy.","'bricht ab' (breaks off) is the AA's note that the sentence is unfinished."]],"reflm:3980":["If I want to understand something in nature, then I must not go outside nature with my explanation. But if I want to understand the whole of nature, then I must be outside its bounds.",["'explanation' renders Erklärung; 'whole' renders gesamte."]],"reflm:3981":["The pure concepts of reason have no *exemplaria*, but are themselves the archetypes; but the concepts of our pure reason have as their archetype this reason itself, are therefore subjective and not objective.",["“archetypes”: Urbilder; “concepts of reason”: Vernunftbegriffe.","“subjective”, “objective”: subiectiv, obiectiv."]],"reflm:3982":["The logical laws of reason contain the rules according to which concepts are subordinated one to the *sphaera* of the other, which is called logical subordination. The metaphysical laws are, according to which the concepts are subordinated to one another *realiter*.",["The second sentence is elliptical in the German (“Die metaphysischen Gesetze sind, nach welchen …”), with no antecedent for “according to which”; kept so."]],"reflm:3983":["All concepts are either archetypes, which [conc] are grounds of the determinations that belong to the objects and through which the object itself is determined among all possible ones, or they are ectypes, which |\nare consequences of the determinations of things. Since through space and time all concepts of experience are possible, so bricht ab.",["“archetypes”: Urbilder; “ectypes”: Nachbilder (copies).","“consequences”: Folgen; “[conc]”: [Begr], an abbreviation of Begriffe.","“bricht ab” (breaks off) is the AA's note, kept as it stands."]],"reflm:3984":["Everything that happens, i.e. that one is necessitated [through the] prior to experience to cognize as having happened, has a ground; but what one wills, that it shall happen, has no further ground. For every object that is determined through choice has no further ground why it is thought thus and not otherwise than this choice. Since through the will something first arises, so it can just as little be asked in the case of arbitrary ideas, which are a ground of actions, as in the case of arbitrary speculative ideas bricht ab.",["“prior to experience”: vor der Erfahrung; vor may be the eighteenth-century spelling of für, “for experience”.","“choice”: Willkühr; “arbitrary”: willkührlich.","“bricht ab” (breaks off) is the AA's note, kept as it stands."]],"reflm:3985":["We can posit nothing through reason without subordinating it to another. We can think no subordination without the ground being thought as *antecedens*, hence the whole series being thought *per synthesin a priori completam*.\nNo *synthesis* is *completa* except only that which is completed *successive progrediendo* in a time. If, therefore, a whole is given, then we posit that *regrediendo* a *terminus* is found from which the *synthesis completa* begins, consequently that a whole is neither divisible to infinity nor composed to infinity. The *synthesis infinita*, however (eternity), never gives a whole.",["*per synthesin a priori completam*: through a complete synthesis a priori; *successive progrediendo*: by progressing successively; *regrediendo*: by regressing.","“to infinity”: ins unendliche."]],"reflm:3986":["One can assume that the motion of a body is only a successive presence of a great efficacy of impenetrability in space |\nwhere not the substance changes its place, but this effect of impenetrability succeeds gradually in different places, as with sound the waves of air. One can also assume that in space there are no substances at all, but a greater or lesser efficacy of a single supreme cause in different places of space. From this it would follow that matter would be infinitely divisible.",["“efficacy”: Wirksamkeit; “effect”: Wirkung.","“place” renders both platz (first) and Ort, Örter (afterwards)."]],"reflm:3987":["The concept of God is the only idea that is thoroughly determined through reason and is also given as the supreme ground of all determination.",[]],"reflm:3988":["[In our judgments we attend either only to the idea of a thing alone and to that through which we think it to ourselves, or to its relation to others.]\nWe have two kinds of concepts: those that can arise in us through the presence of the thing; or those [which] through which the understanding represents to itself the relation of these concepts to the laws of its own thinking. To the latter belongs the concept of ground, |\nof possibility, of existence. Hence the principles about the former are objective, those about the latter subjective.\nMetaphysics is a science of having insight into the relation of human reason to the first properties of things.\nAll rational fundamental concepts are concepts of form, the empirical ones are *principia* of matter. the former are merely subjective. i.e. abstracted from the [action] laws of our thinking. the latter objective. from the representation itself, through which the object is represented. The understanding is applied to experiences only according to laws of the understanding, but the separated idea of the relation of the representation of sensation in general according to laws of the understanding makes up the pure rational concept. The understanding proceeds according to a natural law when it thinks one and many. This understanding, [abstr] applied to the sensation of a body, abstracts the idea of a whole not from it, but from itself.\nThe *philosophia pura* about the rational concepts is only subjective and never synthetic; the *principia* of the *philosophiae applicatae domestica* are the first laws of experience in combination with the *principiis rationalibus*, insofar as these refer to the *empirica*. The proposition: nothing comes from nothing, and the proposition: everything has a ground, have the same fault, that they are expressed rationally, although they are valid only empirically.\nThe idea of an undetermined freedom cannot be thought at all according to laws of our understanding; but it is not false on that account.\nBy what does one cognize the plurality and unity of substances. The representation of the I is actually a unity; but is the diversity of places a proof of plurality?\nIf one only observes bricht ab.",["“have insight into”: einzusehen; “properties”: Eigenschaften; “representation of sensation”: Empfindungsvorstellung; “rational concept”: rationalbegriff.","*philosophiae applicatae domestica*: the Latin is doubtful as it stands; domestica (native, proper) may go with *principia* (“the proper principles of applied philosophy”).","“bricht ab” (breaks off) is the AA's note, kept as it stands."]],"reflm:3989":["A–A=0 signifies an empty representation, for negating is not |\nempty; but whether an empty representation signifies as much as something impossible, is a question.",[]],"reflm:3990":["If I first posit something and afterwards cancel it, then it is 0 (g *rei*), if I at the same time posit and cancel, then [0 *cogitationis*\n. . . 0] is impossible. The impossible does not signify merely *conceptum inanem*, in which nothing at all is thought; for it is possible to think nothing at all.",["“cancel”: aufheben. *rei*: of the thing; *cogitationis*: of thought; *conceptum inanem*: an empty concept.","The bracketed passage is defective in the AA; the construction of “then … is impossible” is accordingly uncertain."]],"reflm:3991":["Through the *principium contradictionis* is cognized not the possibility of things, but of the predicates of the same or of their relation.",[]],"reflm:3992":["Affirmative judgments serve for cognition of the identity of a thing with others, negative ones for distinction. Hence tautological propositions are ridiculous.\nReason contains nothing but *respectivae notiones*; the senses, since they cognize without reflection, must give something absolute.\nWe must here pay very careful attention that reason judges about itself according to its own laws, consequently also not always objectively.",["*respectivae notiones*: relative notions.","“reflection”: reflexion."]],"reflm:3993":["From the nature of our understanding flows the idea of boundaries, i.e. of the first and last.",["“boundaries”: Grentzen."]],"reflm:3994":["There are 2 concepts which are not properly predicates of things, but through which the things themselves are thought with all their predicates.",[]],"reflm:3995":["In metaphysics, just as in optics, one must pay attention that the combinations or separations of concepts whose ground is subjective are not taken for objective.",["“taken for”: vor … gehalten (= für … gehalten)."]],"reflm:3996":["There are two principles of the objective laws of reason, which are analytic; two principles which are subjective, and are synthetic. The latter are principles of every [use] real use of reason: the rules how we are to use reason in all real relations, even in the case of the senses. These are falsely regarded as principles of pure reason, which are objective.",["“principles”: Grundsätze; “use” (n.): Gebrauch; “to use”: brauchen."]],"reflm:3997":["On and \n*Conceptus ponens*: A; *tollens*: non A; [*deficiens*\n= A×0] *deficiens*\n=0; *conceptus inanis*: to which no object corresponds, e.g. a certain fictitious force. *Conceptus implicans* =A non A (non A + non A). *Ens ponens*: A; *ens tollens*: –A; *ens deficiens*: A–A=0. the *realiter* opposed not contradictory to itself, and the impossible is different from the not possible.",["“On and”: Über und; the heading looks incomplete.","*ponens*: positing; *tollens*: cancelling; *deficiens*: lacking; *inanis*: empty; *implicans* (sc. contradictionem): self-contradictory.","The last sentence has no verb in its first clause (realiter oppositum sibi non contradictorium); kept so."]],"reflm:3998":["The proposition can be immediately certain; but its immediacy can be uncertain (g be hidden), as: a whole is greater than a part.\nThere are concepts which are problematic for reason, others which are dogmatic.",["The German doubles the verb around the addition: “ungewiß (g verborgen seyn) seyn”.","“for reason”: vor die Vernunft (= für)."]],"reflm:3999":["Since we form rational concepts only through judgments, but in these the thing is represented through predicates, we represent to ourselves an object of thoughts and its being or non-being separately as a predicate; if we abstract from this [and the] there still always remains an object of thought, which, insofar as it does not cancel itself, is something possible.\nThe possibility of judgments is contradistinguished from truth, and the possibility of things from actuality.\nThe contradictions of pure reason arise from this, that it does not, like empirical philosophy, draw the universal propositions from particular ones, but judges universally from (g pure) concepts, but nevertheless needs a *terminum* [a priori] (g of subordination or coordination), which contradicts universality.\nWe can through reason cognize only the possibility of judgments, but not of things wholly, because to these belongs the presupposition of the possibility of matter, which must be given through the senses and thus a posteriori. As regards matter the understanding cannot even invent something impossible. The falsity of the form in the *synthesi* can be established through nothing but through the conflict of the consequences with other certain cognitions.\nPossibility is only a form, but the possible also contains matter.",["“object of thoughts”: Gegenstand der Gedanken; “object of thought”: Gegenstand des Gedankens. “separately”: besonders, which can also mean “especially”.","“wholly” (gantz) may go with “cognize” or with “of things”; left as ambiguous as the German.","“conflict”: wiederstreit; “consequences”: folgen."]],"reflm:4000":["Some things are valid *obiective* under an arbitrary condition, others *subiective* under a (g through the understanding) given condition. These given conditions are appearances (space and time lie every|where\nat the ground). Hence although, if the concepts ground and cause were objective, one of two propositions: There is a first cause or: there is none, would have to be true; but now, since neither of the two is objective, both are true at the same time as subjective laws.",["“arbitrary”: willkührlich.","The sentence beginning “Hence although” has no main clause answering “although”; kept so."]],"reflm:4001":["It is difficult to represent to oneself how the consequence is after the ground, if this relation is not merely a *phaenomenon*.",["“consequence”: Folge. “after”: nach, which can also mean “according to”."]],"reflm:4002":["Where there are nothing but real connections, there is no 0, also no alteration. All alterations in the world therefore stem from real oppositions; the last effect of them is therefore 0.",["“real connections”: Realverknüpfungen; “real oppositions”: realoppositionen."]],"reflm:4003":["Impossibility of propositions and impossibility of judgments. If I include the condition of the impossibility in the concept of the thing, then it is absolute impossibility according to concepts or according to experience.",["“propositions”: Sätze; “judgments”: Urtheile."]],"reflm:4004":["Every concept is possible in which there lies a predicate that is considered in the subject and does not contradict it; but not every *synthesis* is possible in which there is no contradiction. i.e. real relations are not seen into through the principle of contradiction.",["“principle of contradiction”: Satz des Wiederspruchs."]],"reflm:4005":["We can through reason cognize only the conditioned possibility. The absolute possibility that we cognize through reason |\nhappens according to the *principio contradictionis* and concerns propositions into which we resolve the concepts.\nAll absolute possibility must otherwise be given through experience; what is there is in itself possible.",["“what is there”: was da ist, i.e. what exists."]],"reflm:4006":["In the case of all elementary representations we can borrow possibility only from actuality, because the former are given for thinking only through the latter.\nIf something under certain conditions always happens in the same way and yet has in these conditions no natural ground, then it must have the ground in an intention or in freedom. e.g. if the game always falls on one side. Thus freedom is a first ground and is not understood along with that which happens.",["“the former … the latter”: jene … diese, with a plural verb, so that “the former” may be the elementary representations rather than possibility.","“intention”: Absicht; “game”: Spiel, perhaps a throw of dice.","“understood along with”: mit verstanden."]],"reflm:4007":["Of everything that is there we must cognize a ground if we want to cognize through reason that it is*; therefore we cannot cognize the absolutely necessary. In everything subordinated we must assume a first, which therefore is necessary in itself**; therefore there is a conflict of subjective laws.\n*(s therefore it is a subjective rule of reason. 2. everything that (g contingently) is an object of experience, (happens), has a ground: is an objective rule of [the senses] experience.)\n**(s principle, which we assume only for this reason, because otherwise we could not see into something a priori.)",["“conflict”: Streit; “principle”: princip."]],"reflm:4008":["Everything that (g comes to be) happens is through something or other, is similar to the proposition:\nEverything that comes to be is out of something or other. From nothing comes nothing, i.e. substance cannot come to be. Which only indicates that our understanding is valid only *in consequentia* and that in all explanations of nature we must assume that substance always is. arising and passing away, coming to be and ceasing are also bound to time and are valid only for phenomena.\nIt is also a law of our reason: The supreme cause (one or many) always is, can neither arise nor pass away. Includes also the relation of time in itself.",["“comes to be”: wird (werden); “arising and passing away”: entstehen und vergehen.","“explanations”: erklärungen."]],"reflm:4009":["In this demonstration 1. the word “nothing” in the logical sense (which signifies as much as the universal negation in a judgment and affects the *copulam* of a universal judgment) is confused with the nothing, namely the remotion of things. e.g. no thing has a ground, Nothing has a ground, and: the nothing is the ground of some things.\nThus the author also did it with the *definitio* of the *minimi*.",["“remotion”: remotion (Latin remotio, removal).","“in the logical sense”: im logischen Verstande."]],"reflm:4010":["Everything has a supreme logical ground\nand last logical consequence.\n(g The former the most universal concept, which under none, the latter the singular, under which none is contained.)",["“consequence”: Folge. “which under none”: the verb is missing in the German (der unter keinem)."]],"reflm:4011":["Is a necessary hypothesis of healthy reason, is therefore valid |\nonly in the world and is also the principle of the use of the same in it. Everything has a first ground, is also such a principle, because it begins a posteriori; but to begin a priori from the first ground and to posit it itself is an action of original reason.",["“healthy reason”: Gesunde Vernunft, i.e. common sense.","“the same” (derselben) may be reason or the world; “to posit it itself” (ihn selber zu setzen) may mean to posit the ground itself or to posit it oneself."]],"reflm:4012":["The *principium rationis*, likewise the *principium rationati*, is a rule of healthy reason and thus restricted to the objects of experience. The boundaries of the sensible world are therefore also the boundaries of its use. If one goes further and wants to make their validity universal, then one errs and brings it into science.",["*principium rationis*: principle of ground; *principium rationati*: principle of the grounded. “sensible world”: Sinnenwelt.","“it” (ihn, masculine) in the last clause most naturally refers to Gebrauch, use; “their” (deren) to the two principles."]],"reflm:4013":["Everything that is, has a ground, is a merely subjective principle of reason and has no relation to the possibility of appearances. What happens, has a ground, signifies that in which I am passive and observe it. But what I do freely is for me not something that happens. (s Is a conditional proposition of the reality of our |\nrepresentations, which is opposed to their nullity, namely to fantasies. If the representation of an object is not to be [given] made by us, but rather given, then it must be posited according to [all] a universal rule under certain conditions, whereby the relation of this object to the representation and the existence of it is valid for others too. It is entirely different when the representation is made by us (through freedom); for then we are immediately conscious of its reality, i.e., a state in us that we ourselves make can be cognized as a true state, even without ground.\nTo cognize something as what, to understand, to have insight, to comprehend. We cognize that which is given to us as what; i.e., we give its representations an object through the function of the understanding.\nIf, where, why. *accidentia* and composition are not things.)",["\"principle\" renders Kant's Germanized \"princip\"; \"object\" renders both Gegenstand and \"obiect\" (Latinate).","\"to cognize something as what\": etwas wovor (= wofür) erkennen; the same wovor recurs in the next sentence.","\"to have insight\": einsehen; \"the existence of it\": derselben, most naturally the representation."]],"reflm:4014":["Everything that arises has a ground;\nEverything that passes away, also a positive ground.",[]],"reflm:4015":["All signifies either the sufficiency (g of the derivation) *a priori* or the completeness of the taking-together *a posteriori*; e.g., We can say something in general of all motion, but not cognize all motion together.",["\"All\": Alles, the word itself is under discussion.","\"taking-together\": Zusammennehmung."]],"reflm:4016":["The cognition of the logical essence is acquired merely analytically, that |\nof the real essence synthetically. The former is the first subjective fundamental concept, the latter the first objective one. *In conceptibus arbitrariis qva talibus* subjective and objective are the same.",["\"essence\": Wesen; \"fundamental concept\": Grundbegriff.","*In conceptibus arbitrariis qva talibus*: \"in arbitrary concepts as such\"."]],"reflm:4017":["*praedicata logica* are concepts [which] through which I can cognize [can] or posit certain things. Accordingly all concepts are predicates; but they signify either things or their position: the former is a real predicate, the second only a logical one.\nConcepts are undetermined with regard to many predicates, but things are not. That is why thoroughgoing determination also adheres to actuality and is more in existence than in possibility. This also flows from the fact that through possibility I posit merely the concept, through actuality the thing. If God takes existence from a thing, then he takes from it no predicate, but rather the thing itself (s but not the possibility or [its] the concept of the thing). Whoever denies existence removes the thing with all its predicates.\nExistence can indeed be a logical, but never a real predicate of a thing.",["\"things\" renders both Dinge and Sachen; Sachen in \"signify either things\", \"but things are not\", \"the thing itself\", \"the concept of the thing\", \"removes the thing\".","\"existence\": Daseyn; \"actuality\": Wirklichkeit; \"removes\": removirt."]],"reflm:4018":["The existential propositions express: how we are to think things, not: what; namely *absolute*, not *respective*.",["*absolute* ... *respective*: Latin adverbs, \"absolutely\" ... \"relatively\"."]],"reflm:4019":["An object is given insofar as it is thoroughly determined. If through its concept it is thoroughly given, then it is necessary.",["\"thoroughly\": durchgängig."]],"reflm:4020":["Existence pertains only to things, actuality also to their actions.",["\"Existence\": Daseyn; \"actuality\": Wirklichkeit."]],"reflm:4021":["We can represent nothing to ourselves as possible except what has been given in the senses both in its *data* and in its real relations; thus our possibilities are merely modifications of that whose universal ground is given through experience.",["\"real relations\": realverhaltnissen."]],"reflm:4022":["The proposition that a possible necessarily exists signifies as much as: *sublata omni actualitate tollitur possibilitas*; whether this latter proposition be true must be investigated.",["*sublata omni actualitate tollitur possibilitas*: \"when all actuality is cancelled, possibility is cancelled\"."]],"reflm:4023":["If each thing is only *in nexu omnimodo* with all determinations of the supreme cause and thus also with its whole effect, but of that being [only] the whole effect is only a single one: then everything that does not actually exist is also not possible *in totum*.",["The first clause has no complete predicate (\"is only in nexu omnimodo\"); kept as in the German.","\"its\": dessen, masculine or neuter, so not grammatically the cause (Ursache); left ambiguous.","*in nexu omnimodo*: \"in an all-round nexus\"; *in totum*: \"as a whole\"."]],"reflm:4024":["Magnitude is cognized either through the composition or through the limitation of a given all. The first always requires time.\nWhy is *mathesis* capable of such evident propositions and of such correctness? Origin of its concepts.\nOur faculty of counting is (g in itself) unlimited; if it is [no] not limited, it is undetermined; for it cannot be taken whole. but in the *realissimum* the *omnitudo* precedes the limitations.",["\"limitation\" renders both Einschränkung and Schranken (\"the limitations\"); \"unlimited\": uneingeschränkt.","\"all\": All (noun), the whole; *omnitudo*: allness."]],"reflm:4025":["transscendentaliter something is considered when it is weighed in relation to its (s own) essence* as the sequel; metaphysice, when the essence is considered with regard to its sequels as a ground.\n*(s if it is considered respective to the essence of things in general, then it is called metaphysical.)",["The asterisks are Kant's reference sign; the Latin words (transscendentaliter, metaphysice, respective) are left without italic asterisks so as not to be confused with it.","\"essence\": Wesen; \"sequel\": Folge; \"weighed\": erwogen."]],"reflm:4026":["transcendental unity.\nMetaphysical unity, truth and perfection. Agreement of one as a ground with the manifold, of the manifold with one as sequel, and of the manifold among one another.",["\"Agreement\": Zusammenstimmung; \"sequel\": Folge."]],"reflm:4027":["The transcendental unity is logical, *respective* to the essence of the thing.\nThe metaphysical unity is real, *respective* to possibility in general.\nThe former is the unity of the taking-together, where something does not contradict itself.\nThe latter: unity of connection, where the many is a ground of One and the unity is not arbitrary.",["\"taking-together\": Zusammennehmung; \"connection\": Verknüpfung.","\"where something does not contradict itself\": wo sich was nicht wiederspricht, also readable as \"where nothing contradicts\" one another."]],"reflm:4028":["All finite things are defective as things in general (not *transscendentaliter*). One indeed supplies the imperfection of the other; but because it is another thing, it replaces the imperfection only in the whole.\nThe perfection of a thing *respective* to a certain concept or [*respective* to] with regard to the concept of a thing in general is the *completudo rationis*, the former the respective, the latter the metaphysical.\nGoodness is the ground of perfection, *unum ad qvod consentitur*.\nThe goodness of human beings is the moral. The relative goodness is that which is only a means to [absol] something else that pleases.",["\"Goodness\": Kant's Germanized \"bonitaet\" (*bonitas*); \"supplies\": supplirt.","*completudo rationis*: completeness of the ground; *unum ad qvod consentitur*: the one with which there is agreement."]],"reflm:4029":["Perfection taken *substantive* or *adiective* [betr].\nIn the latter case it is no quality or quantity or relation, but only the *modus* of a determination; the *perfectio substantive sumta* is a determination in general, insofar as this *modus* belongs to it; with regard to entity, reality is that to which the *modus* of perfection belongs.",["[betr] is the start of a struck word (betrachtet?), left untranslated.","*perfectio substantive sumta*: perfection taken substantively; \"entity\": entitaet."]],"reflm:4030":["Necessity is combined with every categorical position of reason.\nSomething is posited through concepts.\n*Necessarium et contingens* is either absolute or hypothetical.\nLogical or real necessity. The latter the absolute or hypothetical.\nNominal concept through omission of the condition.\n*oppositum entis sibi non contradicit*. *Conceptus terminator*.",["*oppositum entis sibi non contradicit*: \"the opposite of the being does not contradict itself\"; *Conceptus terminator*: terminating concept."]],"reflm:4031":["Law of reason, not of the senses:\nEverything that is, is necessary, either simply or conditioned.\nAll necessity is conditioned, but under condition of the inner determinations of a thing (of that which is inwardly in the thing) or under outer conditions.",["\"simply\": schlechthin; \"conditioned\": Bedingt, here used adverbially."]],"reflm:4032":["Everything that happens is contingent in itself; but because it must nevertheless be necessary in virtue of the above, it is necessary through an alien ground.",["\"alien\": fremden, also \"another's\"."]],"reflm:4033":["All (g absolute) necessity is either of judgments or of things. The first, as the logical, is at all times a conditioned necessity of the predicate. The necessity of things that we can cognize is at all times conditioned; for in itself we can at all times cancel every thing, because, since we affirm nothing, we also contradict nothing through the negation. The concept of the necessary is nevertheless first of all a concept given through reason, because through it alone something is determined. Absolute necessity is a boundary concept, because without it no *completudo* in the series of the contingent would |\nbe. This boundary concept, however, is itself problematic and cannot be cognized *a priori* through reason, because it is a *conceptus terminator*, nor be had insight into *a posteriori*, for that is not called having insight, nor immediately; it is therefore problematic.\nThe supreme *principium* of everything contingent is the absolutely necessary, and the supreme *principium* (g of the possibility) of arising is freedom. It is thought that with regard to a freely acting being every state that is in its power constitutes a *terminum a priori* that is not connected with the preceding ones through a natural link. It is itself no link of a greater chain, at least not as regards activity.",["\"things\": Sachen; \"cancel\": aufheben; \"boundary concept\": Grentzbegrif; \"have insight\": einsehen.","\"in itself\": an sich selbst, which may go with \"we\" or with the thing; \"nothing\": keinem, also \"no one\".","\"link\": Ring, literally \"ring\"."]],"reflm:4034":["The actuality of the *omnimode contingentis* (free action) is just as little to be comprehended as that of the *omnimode necessarii*.[The latter] This, for the reason that, if the *necessitas* is *mediata*, the first necessity is not to be understood; that, because [if the *contingentia* is only *respectiva*] the existence of such a thing is not to be comprehended.",["\"This\" (Dieses) and \"that\" (jenes): the latter and the former respectively.","*omnimode contingens / necessarium*: the in every way contingent / necessary; *necessitas mediata*: mediate necessity."]],"reflm:4035":["All necessity is a necessity of judgments or of things; with the former, if I want to think the opposite, I retain the subject and cancel its identical predicate; with the latter, if I want to think the opposite, I cancel the thing with all its predicates; hence in the former case I can cognize the (g absolute) necessity from the principle of contradiction, but in the latter not.",["\"things\": Sachen; \"cancel\": aufheben; \"principle of contradiction\": Satz des Wiederspruchs."]],"reflm:4036":["Not necessity, but contingency is incomprehensible for rea|son.\nThis idea of contingency is either logical (with universal concepts that are undetermined with regard to particular determinations) or consists in this, that I can cancel the thing; this possibility, however, proves too much.",["\"undetermined\": undeterminirt; \"cancel\": aufheben."]],"reflm:4037":["Inwardly contingent is what, if one abstracts from all conditions, is contingent; absolutely contingent is what (g either has no conditions or) is contingent even with all conditions, because they are the conditions.\nAbsolutely necessary is what even under necessary conditions, and thus outwardly, is necessary. The inner or outer necessity makes no difference in this concept, but only in its conditions.\nNothing is absolutely necessary except what is necessarily connected with something inwardly necessary. Absolutely contingent is what is contingent under all conditions; such a thing one must deny.",["\"absolutely\": schlechterdings throughout, not absolut.","\"because they are the conditions\": weil es die Bedingungen sind; the reference of es is left as open as in the German."]],"reflm:4038":["Above and beside the running head »Necessarium et contingens«:\nAbsolute necessity is more than the hypothetical.\nAbsolute contingency is less than the hypothetical,\nbecause absolute possibility is the least.\n(g The *omnimoda contingentia*)",["The first line is the Academy Edition's statement of where the note stands.","\"the least\": das kleinste, literally \"the smallest\"."]],"reflm:4039":["The concept of an *absolute necessarii* is a *conceptus terminator* (because we [in de] must regard everything contingent as necessary through a ground, and finally the condition must fall away); and since the con|dition\nof intelligibility falls away, it cannot, according to the laws of reason, be had insight into, in that in the series of possible insights it is something in relation to the sequels and borders on nothing with regard to the grounds. The concept of freedom is also a boundary concept of an absolute beginning. (g Likewise the concept of the simple and in general of the first. All laws of the understanding serve for comprehensibility in the series i breaks off.)\nThe concept of substance is also *conceptus terminator*. Likewise the boundary of the world. But who is *conceptus comprehensor*.\nPossibility without actuality rests on the difference between the self-active representation through concept and the passive one through sensation, since the former remains even if the latter has been entirely canceled. In general the possibility of the non-actual is only something that is thought of a certain universal concept.\nAll *conceptus terminatores* are taken from possibility *per synthesin* and always require a first. Hence *a parte inferiori vel posteriori* there is no *conceptus terminator*. The *terminus* of the series is its first member, but the *conceptus terminator* the concept through which a first of the series is possible. Of this concept, which is supposed to be perfectly comprehensible through itself, [can nevertheless] such properties must be thought whereby it can be, in general and without a limitation to be taken from elsewhere, and thus as an unlimited being for itself, the *principium* of the series.\nMorality also has its own *termini a priori* and *a posteriori*: a lawgiver and a reward and punishment according to spiritual laws, thus another world.",["\"i breaks off\": Kant's \"i\" followed by the Academy Edition's \"bricht ab\"; the note is unfinished.","\"be had insight into\": einsehen; \"boundary\": Grentze; \"self-active\": selbstthatigen; \"passive\": leidenden.","*a parte inferiori vel posteriori*: on the lower or later side; \"for itself\": vor sich selbst, also \"by itself\"."]],"reflm:4040":["The two propositions: a state is possible in which a certain de|termination\nis not, and: it is possible that a certain state not be at all, are different. The opposite of a state is therefore possible either as a particular state or as the not-being of the given one. In general the cancellation of that which is, is not the same as non-being.\nTo be necessarily and to endure necessarily are different concepts. The necessary being cannot be compared with time at all; therefore it can in time neither persist with the same *determinationes*, nor change them [nor] or vary with regard to them.\nWhat I call unalterable is nevertheless considered under the hypothesis of time; accordingly the *intellectualia* are neither alterable nor unalterable. The beginning of sensibility contradicts itself because then the sensible is considered as bordering on the *intellectuali*.",["\"cancellation\": Aufhebung; \"not-being\" and \"non-being\": nicht seyn and Nichtseyn.","\"alterable\": Veranderlich; \"contradicts itself\": contradicirt sich."]],"reflm:4041":["Contingent is that whose opposite is possible in its place; [zuf] Alterable: [whose] that which is possible in connection with its opposite.\nIn every alteration [is] there are: 1. *oppositae determinationes, qvatenus eidem competunt*. 2. *successio earundem*. The possibility of mutation is not to be cognized from mere contingency. For because it is possible that instead of one predicate there be another, it is not yet to be cognized from this that the subject has the *opposita* one after another. The relation of the real ground to the sequel seems at the same time to contain the idea of succession; for [in de] the sequel is here too something other than the ground, and both can hardly be distinguished otherwise than through *prius et posterius*[erkann]. Whether a simple substance can alter itself in itself? and how through the *compraesentiam* and |\nthe *nexum* of several the alteration is possible? All alterations can be thought only in time.",["*oppositae determinationes, qvatenus eidem competunt*: opposite determinations, insofar as they belong to the same thing; *successio earundem*: succession of the same.","\"mutation\": mutation, \"contingency\": contingentz, beside Veränderung (\"alteration\"); \"sequel\": Folge.","[zuf] and [erkann] are struck word-beginnings, left untranslated."]],"reflm:4042":["(s Whether a concept has objective reality?)\n*realitas* taken *adiective* is opposed to ideality and signifies [relation] the objective; e.g. realists with regard to bodies or idealists.\nJust so nature taken *adiective* signifies something other than nature simply. (s Magnitudes and the magnitude of a thing.)",["\"[relation]\" renders the struck-out Beziehung.","\"simply\": schlechthin."]],"reflm:4043":["Negation is either the consequent of a cancelling or of a deficient ground*; the former is privation. The latter cannot properly be called a consequent, because nothing is posited in it.\n*(s The latter can still be thought as a vanishing ground and thus as specifically one and the same with the ratione ponente. e.g. point an infinitely small space, rest as vanishing motion.)",["Latin: rationatum rationis tollentis vel deficientis; \"consequent\" renders rationatum.","The asterisk is Kant's reference sign linking the addition to the first line; it is kept, and the Latin ratione ponente (positing ground) is therefore left without italics.","\"specifically one and the same\": specifisch einerley."]],"reflm:4044":["Opposite and \nIn so far as a being is not the most perfect, some defects are necessary in order that certain perfections be obtained. e.g. abstraction for distinctness. A certain degree of cowardice, laziness, ignorance is very necessary to human nature.",["\"Opposite and\" (Gegenüber und) is the AA's locus note; the § numbers are not in the source.","\"defects\": Mängel."]],"reflm:4045":["whether realities are all of one and the same kind and can be measured through more and less.",["\"of one and the same kind\": von einerley Art."]],"reflm:4046":["The *omnitudo collectiva* (g in One) or totality rests on the *positione simultanea*. From the [*omnitudine*] *multitudine distributiva* I can infer to the *unitatem collectivam*, but not from the *omnitudine*, because the progression is infinite and not complete. Time is infinite with regard to potential simultaneity. Hence we represent space to ourselves as *actualiter* infinite.",["\"in One\": in Einem, capitalized in the source; it may mean \"in one thing\" or \"in a unity\".","\"infer\": schließen."]],"reflm:4047":["The whole is either of subordination: series (serial order), or of coordination: aggregate (heap).\nThe whole, insofar as in it the parts are homogeneous, is a magnitude; but they can also be heterogeneous, and to that extent it is not a magnitude.\nThat whole of which no part is an absolute unity is infinitely divisible.",["\"series (serial order)\": Reihe (reihenfolge); \"heap\": Haufen."]],"reflm:4048":["The ground of the unity of coordination: whether it is founded on the unity of subordination [and whether not sel], insofar as they all flow immediately from one ground.\nThe matter of the whole is the *subiect* of the *coniunction* (relation), i.e. the parts. The form of the *coniunctio coordinationis*. The possibility of this form is cognized either *per intellectum*: through space and time, or *per rationem*: through *rationem aut causam communem*.",["\"sel\" in the struck-out phrase is a broken-off word and is left as it stands.","*per intellectum* / *per rationem*: through the understanding / through reason; *rationem aut causam communem*: a common ground or cause."]],"reflm:4049":["The consequences are also combined with the grounds, but not as parts [with] in a whole. The combination of the parts with the whole is [to] according to the rule of identity, but that of the parts among one another is not according to the same.\nOften one has first the idea of the whole or of the *dispertibilitaet*; e.g. universal concept with regard to the classes, space with regard to the parts.\nAn ideal or real whole, the former either logical or arbitrary.",["\"consequences\": Folgen; \"combined\", \"combination\": verbunden, Verbindung.","*dispertibilitaet*: divisibility into separate parts (dispertibilitas), kept in Kant's form.","Last line Latin: Totum ideale aut reale, prius vel logicum vel arbitrarium."]],"reflm:4050":["Extensive or intensive quantity. Degree.\nWhat with respect to some *quantum* cannot be thought except intensively, [is] namely as a ground. e.g. Heat. Wit. God.\n(s A greater degree of heat cannot be composed out of smaller ones. But the calorific force of the rays is infinite and is not limited except by the inertia of matter. Hence by combining out of weak (through rarefaction) rays of light the intensity of heat can increase.)",["The whole passage is Latin; \"ground\": ratio; \"Wit\": Ingenium.","\"by combining out of weak (through rarefaction) rays\" keeps the Latin order: combinando ex debilibus (per rarefactionem) radiis lucis."]],"reflm:4051":["»*Prima matheseos intensorum principia*« (first principles of the mathematics of intensive things).\nMaximum and minimum: deceptive concepts.",["The first line is a title in quotation marks and is kept in Latin; \"deceptive concepts\": conceptus deceptores."]],"reflm:4052":["Because logical *subiecte* can in turn be predicates, we are |\naccording to laws of reason necessitated to think to ourselves a last *subiect*; this is substance.",[]],"reflm:4053":["Are substances *realiter* distinct from *accidentibus*? is it not the substance itself, which I can think through all its predicates, and does even the least remain over, in order to distinguish it from its predicates. Only the succession of something, while something remains one and the same, seems to confirm the distinction.",["\"of something, while something\": von einigem, indem einiges; it may also be read \"of some things, while some things\"."]],"reflm:4054":["The concept of substance has, besides the idea of the *subiect*, also the concept of persistence in that which follows upon one another, and of sameness in this sequence, which one therefore calls alterations of the very same thing, with it. But because [as well] all *accidentia* are variable and the *substantiale* is not known at all, the persistence of the *substantialis* is assumed *precario*. Even consciousness can flow. Above all if everything endures only through the divine force.",["\"sameness\": Einerleyheit; \"alterations\": Veränderungen.","*substantialis* stands in the genitive as written; *precario*: as on sufferance, without right."]],"reflm:4055":["The word \"predicate\" indicates no new distinction of things at all; hence every thing or the concept of it can be predicated of something. e.g. rational beings are spirits. Thus [if] nothing can exist without being a predicate of other things. The human being is a predicate of a rational inhabitant of the earth.\nThis logical *respectus* can be transformed into a real one, if [it] something exists in another, not in the possible concept of another, and indeed as in the real ground. For a part is in the whole from logical grounds, because the whole is only in part something other than its *partes*; otherwise all *partes* would lie in one whole (not in the |\nplace so to speak for all these parts) and the whole would thus still be something other than all the parts together.",["\"predicated of something\": wovon praedicirt werden.","\"place\": Platze, not Ort."]],"reflm:4056":["Every substance has a force, is an identical proposition. For substance is properly the *subiect* which contains the ground of the accidents and of the effects; consequently the concept of substance arises from the necessity of the force of a *subiect*.",["\"force\": Kraft."]],"reflm:4057":["Under and \nThat whose representation properly [is that through which we something] is the predicate of the representation of another thing is its determination; and if it is something positive in itself, then it adheres to others (inherence).",["\"Under and\" (Unter und) is the AA's locus note; the § numbers are not in the source.","\"adheres to\": hängt ... an; \"inherence\": inhaerentz."]],"reflm:4058":["60. E II 1058.\nThe concept of substance depends on inner sense. Just so the *phaenomena* also teach us only the concomitance or consequence, hence not the *compositionem* or *nexum* and the ground, but *phaenomena* of them. Thus the *axiomata phaenomenorum* are not to be regarded as *intellectualia*. E.g. to be sure, everything that happens has a determining ground and follows upon something else according to a determinate law; but that is no rule for the actual actions which we perceive in ourselves.",["\"concomitance or consequence\": concomitantz oder conseqventz."]],"reflm:4059":["All successions are alterations of the same abiding sub|ject.\nThe substances do not flow, but their *status*; so reason demands it, so experience shows it; whence this agreement of experience with reason?",["\"abiding\": bleibenden."]],"reflm:4060":["If the very same *subiect* x is to be after one another a and non, then the *subiect* must not be altered; otherwise x would first be a and then y non a.\nThe *substantiale* is unalterable. For in the succession of the accidents it is always the same, and in *ortu* and *interitu* it is not altered.",["\"a and non\" as written (a und non).","*ortu* and *interitu*: arising and perishing."]],"reflm:4061":["In every composite all composition can be cancelled, but not in every *Qvanto*. In all parts of space parts can be indicated, but not in any one part of space can all parts be indicated.",["\"cancelled\": aufheben; \"indicated\": anzeigen."]],"reflm:4062":["In every whole [parts] several things are coordinated, not subordinated; therefore ground and consequent do not make a whole.",["Latin: ratio et rationatum, \"ground and consequent\"."]],"reflm:4063":["Every whole is composed either of substances or of accidents or of relations; since relations are either of substances or of accidents, the latter will be time, the former space.",[]],"reflm:4064":["That in composition through which it is distinguished from all other is *modus compositionis*.",[]],"reflm:4065":["That every *compositum substantiale* consists of simple parts does not signify that through the analysis of the concept of the *compositi* one arrives at simple parts, but: that according to the law of human cognition the material of the composite, i.e. the simple, precedes the form. It therefore proves nothing objectively.",["\"analysis\": Zergliederung; \"material\": material."]],"reflm:4066":["Every substantial composite consists of simple substances. For since the parts are substances, [possu] their existence is subsistence, and they can exist even if they are not composed with one another; therefore all composition can be abrogated, in such a way that nevertheless all the parts of such a composite remain. But when all composition has been abrogated, the remaining parts are simple.\nThis proof applies only to the *compositum substantiale*, [which] and its possibility can be thought a priori, i.e. *synthetice*, because then *partes non compositae* make up the *principia* of the *synthesis* through which the idea of the *compositi* arises. But a *compositum accidentale* is not always of that kind. Time and space have no parts which could [be] take place even without all composition.",["The first paragraph is Latin; [possu] is a struck-out, broken-off word.","\"abrogated\": abrogari."]],"reflm:4067":["A [unity] plurality we cannot without (g absolute) unity |\nthink, if the [*data* as well] whole plurality is to be thought through reason. But if it is given as a phenomenon, then the analysis can be *absque termino*.",["\"plurality\": Vielheit.","*absque termino*: without end-term."]],"reflm:4068":["Composition is an *accidens*. Because the *substantiale* is thought before all *accidentibus*, the parts are thought before all connection.",["\"connection\": Verknüpfung."]],"reflm:4069":["Opposite and \nThings which exist in the very same time are at the same time; those which exist at different, are after one another.",["\"Opposite and\" (Gegenüber und) is the AA's locus note; the § numbers are not in the source.","\"at different\": zu verschiedener, with \"time\" understood and not supplied.","\"at the same time\" / \"after one another\": zugleich / nach einander."]],"reflm:4070":["Time is everywhere, or the parts of space are at the same time. Newton.\nSpace is at all times, this signifies: that time extends over all concepts, outer as well as inner sensations, but not that the concept of space extends over time.\nThat is not the shortest way which goes through the smallest space, but that which can be completed in the shortest time; just as that book is the shortest through which I can comprehend the object in the smallest time.",["\"completed\": absolvirt; \"comprehend\": begreifen."]],"reflm:4071":["[All] The concept of time is a singular concept. For all [parts of the] different times are after one another, consequently contained in one whole of time. No two years can be at the same time. Time has only one dimension, but existence measured through time has two.\n after one another\nat the same time\nSpace is nothing actual, but a possibility which has its ground in something actual. The possibility of the alterations of things is grounded first on their connection (for one thing alone would not alter), secondly on their dependence on another; for the being which as to its existence does not depend on another would not be able to alter without ceasing.\nOne cannot think any space, e.g. cubic foot, without an outer space which surrounds it, and thus no space without contained in the whole. Likewise no two spaces without a determinate distance and position relative to one another.",["\"singular\": einzelner; \"dimension\": Abmessung.","\"after one another\" / \"at the same time\" stand on their own lines in the source.","\"without contained in the whole\": ohne in dem Ganzen enthalten, as elliptical as the German."]],"reflm:4072":["The concepts of understanding are reflected representations and also have their elements of reflection; space is an intuitive representation.",["\"concepts of understanding\": Verstandesbegriffe."]],"reflm:4073":["Concepts are either intuitive or [rational] reflected.\nThe former either sensitive intuitions or pure intuitions, insofar as either the matter or the sole form of sensitive representation is present. Reflected concepts are either also empirical or pure; the former are universal concepts containing matter given by the senses, the latter only form |\n[conc] containing.[the former The former are intellect] Space and time are concepts of the pure understanding. Metaphysical notions are concepts of pure reason.\nSpace is either determining (possible) or determined (actual).",["The passage is Latin; \"sensitive\": sensitivus; \"understanding\": intellectus.","[conc] and \"intellect\" in [priores Priores sunt intellect] are broken-off words kept as they stand."]],"reflm:4074":["Above, beside and \nThe persistence of the existence of all things in One is the *omnipraesentia*, space.\nThe persistence of all existence of each thing in One is conservation, time.\nIf the whole existence of a thing depends on one, then it is of contingent nature; if only the beginning depends on it, then [sei] the duration of its state is contingent.",["\"Above, beside and\" (Über, neben und) is the AA's locus note; the § numbers are not in the source.","\"persistence\": Beharrung; \"conservation\": Erhaltung."]],"reflm:4075":["If all existence depends on one as well *extensive* as *protensive* breaks off.",["\"breaks off\" (bricht ab) records that the note ends here."]],"reflm:4076":["The whole existence possible at all is called eternity. The [existence] eternal existence [with], which is properly only the [boundary] continuous limitation of eternity, is in time as it were a continuous beginning in the one *respectu* and continuous beginning in the other. Is related as motion through a line to the extension in this line.",["\"limitation\": Begrentzung; \"[boundary]\": Grentze.","\"beginning\" stands twice in the source (Anfang ... Anfang); the reading is doubtful."]],"reflm:4077":["Time and space precede the things: that is quite |\nnatural. Both namely are subjective conditions, [under] under which alone objects can be given to the senses. Taken objectively this would be absurd. Hence the difficulty of the place of the world and of the time before the world. Yet in absolute time no place is determined without actual things, thus absolute time cannot furnish a ground of the explanation of the *phaenomenorum*.",["\"place\": Orte; \"explanation\": Erklarung."]],"reflm:4078":["That space is a mere *phaenomenon* and something subjective, but not a representation of the things, is to be seen from this, because in it relations are represented which yet are no effects, but merely grounds of the possibility of the effects, and these grounds are themselves no things.",["\"things\" here renders Sachen, not Dinge."]],"reflm:4079":["Perhaps the eternity of the world is a consequence of the infinity of it as a *quanti simultanei*; for from a limited number of causes the effects must come to an end, but from an infinite one the combinations and consequences are infinite.\nThe difficulty of representing to oneself a *Quantum simultaneum* as infinite rests on the nature of the human understanding, which can think a *totum* as to its possibility only synthetically, i.e. *successive addendo unum uni*. But the *synthesis* which is to go to infinity is never complete. On the other hand one can very well think an *infinitum successivum*, precisely because the *synthesis* has no *terminos*. But precisely |\nfor that reason it is also impossible according to the nature of our understanding to think this successive series as whole and completed in the idea of a being.",["\"consequence\", \"consequences\": folge, folgen.","*successive addendo unum uni*: by successively adding one to one."]],"reflm:4080":["Opposite the running head »Finitum et infinitum«.\nInfinitely great is what in the *synthesi partium dabilium aeqvalium* has no last. What in the *analysi* has no last is infinitely divisible.",["*synthesi partium dabilium aeqvalium*: synthesis of equal parts that can be given.","The first line is the AA's locus note."]],"reflm:4081":["(g These propositions show how one mixes up the subjective rule with the objective. It follows only that we could not distinguish such things, [but not] or that the [things] concepts many would not belong to them.)\nOn M § 270, first sentence:\nFor they would be distinguished only by places (g and times). thus properly be only just the same at different times and places. For the substance itself must be presupposed before all relation; and if that is the same, then it is one and the same thing.\nThe difference of the [things] places does not constitute a difference of the things themselves, but presupposes it. i.e. [it is the differ] a thing stands in relation to itself if it is in relation with one that is one and the same with it.",["die [Dinge] Begriffe viel nicht zukommen würden: viel (perhaps for viele, many) is doubtful and the clause is left as it stands.","einerley Ding / mit ihm einerley: one and the same (einerley); Verhältnis and relation both rendered relation.","und wenn die dieselbe ist: die taken as demonstrative (that), referring back ambiguously."]],"reflm:4082":["For in that case they would be distinguished only by magnitude, i.e. one would be completely equal and similar to the part of the other, *qvod contra prius* (which is contrary to the preceding).* The following proposition lies at the basis here.\n*\n(g Likewise, since a smaller magnitude can be transformed into a greater, they would, if (g the lack) of sameness rests only on the circumstance, be able to become completely congruent.)",["einerleyheit: sameness; the inserted (g der Mangel) leaves the construction of the clause loose, and it is kept so.","liegt hier zum Grunde: lies at the basis (Grund)."]],"reflm:4083":["For equal things must be *homogenea*, hence *totaliter aeqvalia* be *totaliter homogenea*. (s *totaliter aeqvalia* here means equal in every relation.)",["*homogenea*: homogeneous; *totaliter aeqvalia*: totally equal; *totaliter homogenea*: totally homogeneous.","Beziehung: relation; heissen: literally are called."]],"reflm:4084":["Rest is the enduring presence of a body in the same place.\nIf in a given place a body did not act, change of place would not be a succession of action. A force of change is properly not thinkable [but rather a force], but it is necessary that mutable accidents be thought beforehand, whose actuation or removal requires force. Therefore motive force is the ground on account of which a certain unknown force is present successively in different places. But if the force of presence in any place whatever is again called motive, the same is explained by the same. It is evident therefore that we cognize in bodies nothing but determinations of space and time, but do not cognize intellectually the absolute marks of the subjects.",["The passage is Latin. *perdurabilis*: enduring; *ratio*: ground; *vis motrix*: motive force.","*idem per idem explicatur*: the same is explained by the same.","*cognoscere*: cognize; *notas*: marks."]],"reflm:4085":["The idea of the world is not arbitrary. For just as I must finally think a |\npart that is no further a whole, so I must think a whole that is no part.",["endlich: finally, at last; the German can also be read as finite (think a part as finite)."]],"reflm:4086":["* There exists a world, i.e. a whole that is no part of any actual (possible) whole. From this it can be concluded:\n1. The world is a *compositum substantiale*. Thus does not consist of necessary beings. Its parts also do not hang together through themselves. [After] For this reason, because they are through one cause. Space is the *phaenomenon* of the existence of all things through one. Infinity. The *commercium* of substances is possible thereby.\n2. By its having its boundary (s of subordination and of coordination) *a priori*, it has an origin. whether also a beginning? The beginning of the world is considered only with respect to the world, but not to something outside the world.\n(g 3. On the order of nature. 4. Ladder of beings. texture, figure, mixture.)\nThe whole of the world and of its state.\nIt depends on the supreme cause, but is not *in commercio* with it. There are no more worlds besides this one, although possible instead of this one, because there is only one cause. This holds with respect to the things themselves; but with respect to the supreme cause only one is hypothetically possible.\nWe are thus to infer from a world which, how great and how perfect it may be, is undetermined, to a supreme cause that is infinitely |\ngreater than the ground of proof of its existence. From this it is evident that the existence of this supreme and sole cause and its all-sufficiency must already be known otherwise, and the existence of the world must give proof only of the existence of a necessary one.\nThe composition of a *totius substantiarum* is a proof of contingency;\nThe limitation of each thing is a proof of contingency.\nThe alteration of each thing is a proof of contingency.\nWhether from space as the ground of the possibility of the *commercii* of the *substantiarum* according to laws of sensibility one could not infer to the intuition of a cause valid for all things that can stand *in commercio*. And from infinite time as the ground of the possibility of the duration of things to the eternity and necessity of the cause.\n(g A *compositum* that is no *totum absolute tale* is, *tanqvam pars*, possible only through the limitation of the *totius*; thus everything that holds of a thing only as *parte* presupposes the concept of the whole.)\n*(g In every *composito actuali* no part is *complete* determined but through all the rest together, consequently the thoroughgoing determination goes from all coordinated substances to one, thus it is at all times a part of a *toto absoluto*, i.e. of a world. But in a *composito potentiali* every part is *complete* determined, because the rest are posited in addition to it and have no influence on the existence of the former; thus the sequence [therefore not com] is not necessarily complete. But if in a series of things following upon one another there is a last, then there is also a first.)",["eines nothwendigen: the adjective (necessary) has no noun in the German, and none is supplied.","Beweisgrund: ground of proof; Grentze: boundary; Einschränkung: limitation; Veränderung: alteration.","kein Theil complete determinirt wie durch alle übrige: wie read as other than (but); *complete*: completely. *totum absolute tale*: a whole absolutely as such; *tanqvam pars*: as a part."]],"reflm:4087":["Dependent is a ring in the chain; and that on which others depend |\nand which is itself independent is the uppermost ring. According to laws of gravity that one too must depend, because the lower links are a ground of the sinking of the upper ones. but in itself a thing could be with its consequences without any foreign ground.",["abhängen / abhängend: depend, dependent; the German also means hang (down), which the image of the chain and of gravity turns on.","Folgen: consequences (Grund and Folge, ground and consequence)."]],"reflm:4088":["Relation of the world to the empty intuition outside the world.\nThe world's unity or plurality.\n- -\n- -\nContingency or necessity. Dependence.\n- -\n- -\nFiniteness or infinity.\nConnection (g contingency) and unconnectedness.\n(s Composition.* Community.)\nmetaphysical perfection or imperfection.\n(s Natural and supernatural.)\n(s Egoist)\n(s *Partes materiales et immateriales* (material and immaterial parts).)\n( s *partes repraesentativae* (representative parts): whether simple? whether they fill space? )\nTogether with the sensitive. Of eternity, of immensity, of the beginning, of continuity.\n*(s *Systemata commercii substantiarum* (systems of the community of substances). If the cause of the harmony is external and merely arbitrary, then the state of the one does not necessarily agree with that of the others, but everything comes down to choice; thus there is no necessary ground that would be universally valid for the relation of the things; thus they do not constitute a whole; or the harmony does not follow sufficiently from the things, is thus no *influxus*.)",["contingentz (in the insertion) and Zufälligkeit are both rendered contingency; contingentz is the Latinate word.","Verhaltnis and Beziehung are both rendered relation; Beziehung is the second (of the things).","willkührlich: arbitrary; Willkühr: choice."]],"reflm:4089":["[*Multitudo Complexus*]\nA multitude of subordinated things is a series. A series is of things subordinated either sensitively or intellectually.",["The passage is Latin. *Multitudo Complexus*: multitude, complex (struck through by Kant).","*subordinatorum*: of subordinated things; *sensitive vel intellectualiter*: sensitively or intellectually."]],"reflm:4090":["Over, beside and \nThe impossibility of a *regressus* in a series of the *successionum* (not *causatorum*) comes from this, that the series is regarded as an *absolutum*, completed, and we, who can determine magnitudes only successively, yet can never cognize it [full] *complet*. This, however, does not prove its impossibility.\nBut whether it be a *causatum* of another is not asked at all thereby.",["The first line breaks off in the source after und.","solche / ihre: plural in the German, referring either to the magnitudes or to the series (die Reihe); the English it/its follows the series, and the reference is open.","*successionum*: of successions; *causatorum*: of things caused; *complet*: completely."]],"reflm:4091":["To blind necessity is opposed the absolute (s natural) (rational).\nTo blind chance is opposed freedom (intellectual).\n(s are both contrary to the order which makes cognitions of experience possible, thus impossible in regard to the objects thereof.)",["rationale, intellectuale: Latinate forms in the German (rational, intellectual).","Ohngefehr: chance; Gegenstände: objects."]],"reflm:4092":["In the series of marks subordinated to one another no two are the nearest; all differences are divisible to infinity.",[]],"reflm:4093":["All alteration happens in a time. For between the two |\nmoments of the one state and of the following there is a time, thus the alteration has happened in an infinite multitude of moments between both states. Let us posit that the substance persisted through a time under certain conditions in the same state: then it will persist therein eternally under the same conditions.\nBetween two states are two moments, and between two moments is a time, and in this time the subject is at all times in another state.",["Veränderung: alteration; Augenblik: moment; beharren: persist."]],"reflm:4094":["Egoism can be refuted only by this, that no alteration is possible in a single substance alone.\nThe idealist regards all his outer (g actual) representations as *spontaneas*. The egoist [as if also no outwardly] all outer possible ones.",["*spontaneas*: spontaneous ones; the last sentence has no verb, and the construction of the first is understood.","äußere: outer; wirkliche: actual."]],"reflm:4095":["Essence is the first logical ground of the inner determinations;\nNature is the first real ground of the inner determinations.\nThe first: the ground of that which is [constantly] necessary in a thing;\nThe second: the ground of that which is possible through a thing.\n(s 1. That through which a thing is possible.\n2. What is possible through a thing.\nNature we know through experience.)",["Wesen: essence; kennen: know."]],"reflm:4096":["The real essence is nature.",["realwesen: real essence (Wesen)."]],"reflm:4097":["The necessary of the concept constitutes the essence.\nThe necessary of actuality makes nature. is the permanent.\nNature is the *principium* of contingencies.\nWe cognize through experience nature sooner than essence, e.g. the hardness of steel. Essence and nature are both permanent.\nThe first of that which a thing is, is called essence.\nThe first of that which happens through a thing is called nature. The essence of bodies consists in impenetrable extension; to the nature of bodies belongs *inertia*. The cognition of nature is grounded on experience or at least contingent relation of the thing itself.",["zufallig Verhältnis: the adjective is uninflected in the German; the construction (on contingent relation) is left as loose as it stands.","Wirklichkeit: actuality; Zufälligkeiten: contingencies."]],"reflm:4098":["progression.\nThe infinity of the series as such is possible, but not the infinity of the *aggregat*. The former is an infinite possibility (of additions), the latter an infinite (actual) taking-together.",["Hinzuthuungen: additions; Zusammennehmung: taking-together, comprehension.","*aggregat*: aggregate."]],"reflm:4099":["The greatest of an *aggregat* is impossible; for it would be an *apposition* to which none would be possible.",["*apposition*: a placing-beside, addition; none (keine) refers back to apposition."]],"reflm:4100":["The incompetence of reason to set limitations to sensibility itself under the condition of this intuition, both of expansion and of division, and of both according to time and to space. Reason can set limitations to the empirical with regard to the rational [e.g. if one that] and restrict empiricism, e.g. that everything be extended, that before every series in the world another always precede and there be no first origin; but it cannot restrict sensualism.",["The first sentence has no verb in the German.","Schranken: limitations; einschränken: restrict; Ausbreitung: expansion (not Ausdehnung, extension)."]],"reflm:4101":["In the *harmonia generaliter stabilita* the dancing master, for example, is also apt for walking at the same time instead of dancing; in the *harmonia praestabilita*, however, there is no [ground] general ground at all, but all determination is individual.",["*harmonia generaliter stabilita*: generally established harmony; *harmonia praestabilita*: preestablished harmony."]],"reflm:4102":["Opposite and \n*effectus vel naturae vel libertatis* (effect either of nature or of freedom).\n*actio vel naturalis vel libera* (action either natural or free).",["The first line breaks off in the source after und; Gegenüber: opposite, over against."]],"reflm:4103":["What is in different places admits of no cognition through one [outer] inner sense. hence no selfhood.",["einen: one or an (inner sense); the German does not decide between them."]],"reflm:4104":["*Systema emanationis (e Deo vel parentibus)*; *systema creationis*.\n*Materialismus*: *systema [propagationis] generationis naturalis, epigenesis psychologica*.\n(s Bad habit or naughtiness, e.g. violent heat.)\nWhether everything in the character of the child depends solely on the man or the woman. The system of the *ovulorum* presupposes that, if the woman had had another man, she would nevertheless have begotten the same children; that of the *animalculorum*: if the man had had another woman, he would have gotten the same children. This is very practical; for in the first case a [father] man has to look very much to the constitution of the woman and her race, in the second he has not to look to it at all, but only the woman to the race of the man. According to the *epigenesi* one must look to both: 1. because of the alternative, secondly because of the mixture. With free *praeexistentz* to neither of the two.\nBefore birth was the soul a pure spirit? Did it find itself in the spiritual life? Has it already lived on earth, or does it live in two worlds, the pneumatic and the mechanical, at the same time?",["*Systema emanationis (e Deo vel parentibus)*: system of emanation (from God or the parents); *systema creationis*: system of creation; *systema [propagationis] generationis naturalis*: system [of propagation] of natural generation.","*ovulorum*: of the little eggs; *animalculorum*: of the animalcules; *epigenesi*, *praeexistentz*: epigenesis, preexistence.","Unart oder Ungezogenheit: bad habit or naughtiness (ill-breeding); Hitze: heat, also hot temper."]],"reflm:4105":["The perpetual duration of substances, i.e. the equal age of each with the whole world, cannot so much be proved as that it must lie at the basis in the method of philosophizing.",["nicht so wohl ... als daß: not so much ... as that; the comparison is kept as loose as the German."]],"reflm:4106":["All proofs of the immortality of the soul* must be conducted in such a way that they hold not merely for some but for all (g and not merely for a short existence after death, but for an eternal duration). They can therefore not be inferred from the necessity that the world must at all times have spectators who connect its epochs, because from this it does not yet follow that we, who have once begun to live, must always live.\n1. Not from divine justice. For firstly this would hold only of some, whose virtue and vice have not been sufficiently requited in this world. And 2ndly it would prove only a short existence, which would cease after the requital.\n2. Not from the cognized immortal nature of the soul. Here the grounds of the opposite are preponderant.\n(s not from empirical but from rational grounds.)\n3. Only from the analogy with other things of nature. Human capacities have no correct relation to the post in this world.\n*(s *Imo* they are either derived from the nature of the soul from empirical principles or concepts *a priori* of the thinking being in general. The first is psychological, the second properly metaphysical;\nor *IIdo* they are teleological, either of the analogy of the physical ends or of the moral ones. According to the order of nature.)",["*Imo*, *IIdo*: firstly, secondly.","Principien: principles (the Latinate word, not Grundsatz); Fahigkeiten: capacities; Posten: post, station.","verknüpfen: connect; Vergeltung / vergolten: requital / requited."]],"reflm:4107":["Life cannot derive from the combination with the lifeless; rather, this must diminish a degree of life. Birth can thus be no beginning of life in general, but only of animal life, and death the end of the same.",["Verbindung: combination; herrühren: derive, stem from."]],"reflm:4108":["[Is the soul in general always in this world of sense or]\nThe transition either into another world or another [relations with] region of this one.\nThe 1st question: is the soul after death a pure spirit or still the soul of an animal. The answer is grounded on the decision of the question whether the soul is still, according to the present law of sensibility, in combination with the world, hence also with the corporeal world. Some sensibility or other will probably remain. [compar]\n2nd question. Is the other world another region of the world of sense or is it, as to form, different. Answer: [As to substance] *obiective* there can be only one world, for all substances apart from the supreme cause make up one whole; but [as to form] *subiective*, i.e. as to the way in which the subject represents them, there can be another world. And there it is to be supposed that sensibility diminishes and thus the transition from the *mundo sensibili* into the *intelligibilem* passes over *per approximationem*. The *intuitus* is *comparative* intellectual, the [more dis] more the inner sense grows.\nThe *mundus vere intelligibilis* is *mundus moralis*. The principles of its form hold for everyone, and from it one can infer to God as the *causam mere intelligibilem*; but this *mundus intelligibilis* is no object of intuition, but of *reflexion*. The intuiting of God would at the same time give *intuitum intellectualem* of the world.\nThose who assume an *intuitum mere intellectualem* that begins naturally after death assert that the soul after death sees itself in the other world and does not pass over to it (g departure of the soul), that it belongs at all times to the *mundo immateriali* as the true substance, that the corporeal world is only a certain sens|ible\nappearance of the spirit-world, that the actions here are *symbola* of the real character in the intelligible world, and that the virtuous one does not pass over into heaven, but only sees himself in it.\n3. If the other world is this very same world of sense, although in another relation, then the soul at all times has a body, either an ordinary (according to the laws of (g ordinary) sensation) aspectable body: *Metempsychosis*, or one that is visible only through the choice of the departed soul: *apparitiones*, *spectra*, either through outer or inner influence. *prodigia*.",["“aspectable” renders aspectablen (“that can be looked at”, visible).","“choice” renders Willkühr.","[mehr deu]: the struck word is cut off, probably the start of deu(tlich), “dis(tinct)”."]],"reflm:4109":["Hope or fear of the other world.\nA human being who has the resolve of malice and roguery (g if he does not fear God and another world) I cannot convince of the existence of both, but can only strike this fear into him and thus push into his mind sufficient grounds of possibility and speculative refutations or counter-grounds of the opposite possibility, but cannot bring it about either that he know there is another world or that he believe it. A human being must have an immediate resolve to be honest, if only he did not thereby put his entire happiness at stake. Then he will wish for, hope for and believe in the [Gl] other world and wise government, and indeed unwaveringly and without being shaken.",["“Then” renders Denn, read as dann.","“happiness” renders Glückseeligkeit; [Gl] is a struck, cut-off beginning, perhaps of Gl(ückseligkeit) or Gl(auben)."]],"reflm:4110":["The analogical proof of the hope of the other world is beautiful in this, that it does not concern itself with the difficulties of the speculative explanations, nor engage in theories and kinds of explanation that one can [not] defend only with difficulty, but infers according to the same law according to which one, in physics, where the object conceals itself as to its inner |\nconstitution, infers from what shows itself outwardly to what is not immediately discovered.",["“constitution” renders Beschaffenheit."]],"reflm:4111":["It is excellent that on this earth the course of the world does not harmonize with the moral laws, because otherwise no human being would himself know whether he acts from prudence or morality, and purely moral motives could not be felt.",["“morality” renders sittlichkeit; “motives” Bewegungsgründe."]],"reflm:4112":["Blessedness is a perfect calm gratification, contentment in the consciousness of one's state as a good one. “Saumseelig”, “redseelig” signify an activity prevailing in a calm state of mind.\nFortune is a contingent acquisition of the agreeable. Happiness is contentment with fortune.",["“Blessedness” renders Seeligkeit, “fortune” Glük, “happiness” Glükseeligkeit; “gratification” vergnügen.","saumselig (“dilatory”) and redselig (“talkative”) are mentioned as words formed with -selig and are left in German."]],"reflm:4113":["S. I:\n1. Through speculation.\na. (g The natural) original being. Supreme and eternal cause. *Substratum*. Einos. (g Lower gods, gods of the land.)\n(g c. Freely acting original being. Life alone can make the first beginning.)\nb, The first mover (g the [architect] author). |\nThe supreme spirit. Living God.\nWorld-soul. Spinozism.\n2. From morality. There must be an inwardly present and universal judge.\nThe holy, kind and just being.\n––––––––––\nThe greatest perfection 1. As [a] (g the highest) ideal of all good, like a *principium* of the understanding.\n2. As a *principium* of method, to judge from ends in physics.\n––––––––––\nThe unity of the All of perfection, because we know the manifold of beings only through limitation of the reality that is given to us.\n––––––––––\nThe idea of God is therefore\n1. The necessary (g *principium*) logical ideal,\n2. The necessary hypothesis of the natural order,\n3. —\n— —\n— —\n—\nmoral\n—.\nThe necessary being, the first, the highest, the being of all beings, the most perfect being, the all-sufficient.\nTotality is something determinate in itself. Is something absolute. The greatest is determined only by its being the greatest among the possible things (hence All); otherwise it is something relative.\nThe original magnitude is not thought through *synthesin*. All derived magnitude is only comparative, namely relatively to the measure.\n––––––––––\nTheology is either rational or revealed (it is not empirical), rational either philosophical or of common sense (most to be cultivated), philosophical either transcendental or natural (physicotheology). One. Omnipotent. Eternal. Omnipresent. Simple. Unchangeable. impassible.\nOriginal being. The highest intelligence. God.\nS. II:\nThe concept of God is there once and for all; one must [investigate what one] develop it genetically from its use, by seeking out not the sense |\nthat one actually connects with it, but the aim that lies at the ground of all these concepts.\nIn all cognitions that arise long before science and give occasion for it breaks off.\n1. Fear. Sorcery. Spirits. Priests.\n2. Supreme cause.\n––––––––––\n(s At the ground of all our concepts of things lies the concept of reality. A thing, something. [This] The manifold realities [make the U] must be given to us through senses: reality thus makes up, as it were, the matter of all possibility. The All of reality [makes th] thus comprehends the possibility of things altogether and is [only] (g the concept of) a single one. The differences of things thus rest on the determination of the All of reality. Namely [of the position and of the ex] only in the difference of the limitation of the All of reality. Negations are thus limitations and cannot be thought otherwise than in that in which there are no negations at all. [The concept of the highest reality is thus a fundamental con ground] With regard to the All-concept of reality, the real in a thing is the (g transcendental) matter, the negations the transcendental form. The highest reality is thus the *substratum* [of the] of our cognition of reason of all possibility, In which either or Through which all possibility is given.)",["The Latin sentence on theology is Kant's: Theologia est vel rationalis vel revelata (non est empirica), rationalis philosophica vel sensus communis (maxime colenda), philosophica vel transscendentalis vel naturalis (physicotheologia).","“Einos” is kept as it stands; the reading is doubtful. “limitation” renders both Einschränkung and (in the plural, “limitations”) schranken.","“kind” renders gütige; “All” renders the noun All throughout; “comprehends” begreift; [Grundb Grund] is cut off, probably Grundb(egriff), “fundamental con(cept)”."]],"reflm:4114":["The concept of God is either a mere ideal of reason (g in relation to everything possible) or in relation to the world alone [and] given. in the latter case it is a concept inferred *a posteriori*, in the first a concept given *a priori*. Insofar as it has been inferred in relation to the world, it is either only necessary [according to] as a practical ground or as a ground of theory.",["“inferred” renders geschlossen."]],"reflm:4115":["Changeability in no way proves contingency; the former is *possibilitas statuum diversorum*, the latter *possibilitas oppositorum*; the former is a *determinatio secundum leges sensitivi in cognitione*. We have no marks at all of contingency through reason, just as little as of necessity except the conditioned. The absolute is in all respects unknown.",["*possibilitas statuum diversorum*: “possibility of different states”; *possibilitas oppositorum*: “possibility of opposites”.","*determinatio secundum leges sensitivi in cognitione*: “determination according to the laws of the sensitive in cognition”."]],"reflm:4116":["The proposition that a universal author is necessary says as much as: that it is comprehensible for itself and the opposite absurd in itself, that a being full of universal reason, of the highest power and of the best will should exist, and that it is on the other hand obscure and not graspable that many things which alter among one another, or even no thing at all, should exist.",["“author” renders Urheber; “absurd” ungereimt.","“for itself” renders vor sich selbst (= für sich selbst), which may also be read “by itself”."]],"reflm:4117":["The inference from contingent things to a necessary being says only so much, that the existence of changeable things is not (g completely) comprehensible unless a being is assumed with which the question: why? ceases, [It] i.e. which is necessary for itself. But something is just as little comprehensible without conditions as necessary existence, as [it] without first conditions the subaltern necessity is comprehensible. This shows that the inference from changeable things to a necessary one does not prove that such a being necessarily exists, but that such a concept, if it is possible, is requisite for explanation; and of this being one can in turn say that its necessity cannot be comprehended without a condition under which it is necessary.",["“conditions” renders both the Latinate conditionen and, at the end, Bedingung.","The second sentence is kept as it stands; “as necessary existence” may attach to “something” or be the second term of the comparison."]],"reflm:4118":["The existence of God is [the condi] combined with the highest maxims in the use of our reason both with regard to the speculative and the practical.",["[die Bedin] is cut off, probably die Bedin(gung), “the condi(tion)”.","“combined” renders verbunden."]],"reflm:4119":["Above, beside and\nThe possibility of a thing is only the reality or negation *respective* to a certain particular concept. [Actuality] Reality given absolutely is actuality. All reality must be given absolutely, and so some actuality precedes all possibility, just as space is [before] not something possible, but the ground of all possible figures.",["“Above, beside and” renders the editorial locus note Über, neben und, which breaks off.","“actuality” renders Wirklichkeit, “reality” realitaet; “absolutely” renders schlechthin."]],"reflm:4120":["In the lower margin\nWe see through mere concepts that all realities are there and that the possibilities of things are given through their combination and limitation.\nThis *omnitudo realitatum* is in one single being [because otherwise]. Insofar as this is supposed to be a cause of something, this is contingent; but of something contingent something cannot from itself be the cause otherwise than through the will.",["“We see” renders sehen … ein (einsehen, “have insight into”).","*omnitudo realitatum*: “totality of realities”."]],"reflm:4121":["The moment is existence without any duration. Eternity is an existence with all duration.",[]],"reflm:4122":["Arising, passing away and being altered is only in time. Of what is not in time, none of these can be said.\nAlteration is no intellectual predicate at all. Thus not the things, but their *phaenomena* alter; but this alteration is itself a *phaenomenon*. (g The things in themselves are constant, and the alterations appearances of their limitations.) What corresponds to the alterations *in intellectualibus*, we do not know.\nIt is indeed the question whether the concept of substance, which indicates the constancy of something with the change of determinations, is not only a concept that holds among *phaenomenis*; for *intellectualiter* the substance taken together with its determinations cannot be other than one and the same, and inherence cannot be a particular relation of something in the thing to the subject itself.\nTime is also eternal, i.e. the *finita* are, compared with one another through representations, i.e. insofar as one cognizes the other, all in time.",["“alteration” renders Veränderung; “constancy”, “constant” render Bestandigkeit, beständig; “limitations” schranken.","“appearances” renders Erscheinungen, beside Kant's Latin *phaenomena*."]],"reflm:4123":["The philosophical cognition of magnitudes determines it from the idea of the *omnitudinis* (g *totum absolutum*) [through]*limitando*, consequently the *omnisufficiens* through the *oppositum* of all limitations.\nThe mathematical cognition does not begin from the absolute whole, but from the respective one, and determines from the parts the whole.\nThe *modus cognoscendi* is also different. The *mathematica per constructionem conceptus secundum intuitionem sensitivam*; the *philosophica per conceptus secundum cognitionem discursivam*.\nIn the concept of God the sensible condition must be left out. The concept of his magnitude is: as *conceptus definitor, terminator et comprehensor* to infer from the universal to the particular or from the all to the part. One must leave out the limitations (which are hindrances).",["“it” renders dieselbe, singular, after the plural Größen; it may refer to the cognition or to magnitude.","*modus cognoscendi*: “way of cognizing”; mathematics “through construction of the concept according to sensitive intuition”, philosophy “through concepts according to discursive cognition”.","*conceptus definitor, terminator et comprehensor*: “defining, bounding and comprehending concept”."]],"reflm:4124":["The cognition of God is not ectypal: borrowed from objects, but prototypal: the principle of objects.",["Kant's Latin: Cognitio dei non est ectypa: mutuata ab obiectis, sed prototypa: principium obiectorum."]],"reflm:4125":["A necessary being can be the cause of contingent things only through freedom, for from the necessity of its nature only that would [arise] exist which is in it.\nThe independence of the divine will, hence without sensibility, and thus greatest freedom.\nThe goodness of the same.\nThe all-sufficiency or omnipotence.\nThe inner necessity.",["“goodness” renders the Latinate bonitaet; “all-sufficiency” Allgnugsamkeit."]],"reflm:4126":["Because God represents the highest good as determined from his own (g free) power, he wills.",["“power” renders Kraft; “he wills” renders so will er."]],"reflm:4127":["(g Free) (g Will of reason) (g free)\nHis will is original. All-powerful. Holy. Wise. Kind. Just. independent. Because he is pleased with himself as a (g fruitful) ground, [therefore are the] therefore the greatest perfection is actual through him. He is not satisfied by the things.",["“Will of reason” renders Vernunftwille; “All-powerful” Allgewaltig; “Kind” Gütig."]],"reflm:4128":["No inclination. He needs nothing. His perfection is overflowing with consciousness. That is his willing.\nIn transcendental theology it must be shown that we actually think that through transcendental representations. e.g. That the original being has an understanding on account of the possibility of things through ideas, order. That it has a will on account of the efficient cause according to ideas. That the contingent is not determined through the nature of a being necessary in itself, consequently through freedom, that a first beginning is possible only through freedom.",[]],"reflm:4129":["Everything that is there outside God is there only through his will, nothing through the necessity of his nature except what is in him.\nEverything that is there is there either through physical cause or according to laws of freedom. We can think no beginning without a freedom.",["“is there” renders da ist (Dasein, “existence”)."]],"reflm:4130":["The will of God goes from the whole to the part (s From the highest good, the good in the whole, to each single one).\nThis is the wise will.",[]],"reflm:4131":["Will, whose [motives] are not *stimuli*, but a satisfaction (g acquiescence) of the cause with itself. Motives are subjectively intellectual.",["‘motives’: *motiva*; ‘satisfaction’: *complacentia*; ‘acquiescence’: *acquiescentia*."]],"reflm:4132":["The wise decrees are kind and just.\nThe immediate desire for the happiness of others is kindness.\nInsofar as this is in accordance with worthiness (good conduct), justice.",["‘decrees’: Rathschlüsse; ‘kind’, ‘kindness’: gütig, Gütigkeit (also ‘benevolent’, ‘benevolence’)."]],"reflm:4133":["Because the will of God goes from the universal to the particular, it is at all times just; the human goes from the particular to the particular.\n*Iustitia passiva*: by virtue of which someone does what he owes; *activa*: by virtue of which he necessitates others to do what they owe. The goodness which is exercised in proportion to conduct belongs to the *activa*.",["‘the human’: der menschliche, i.e. the human [will], the noun left unstated as in the German."]],"reflm:4134":["That God is the cause of the world through his nature can be assumed, even though he is not the author.\nThat God is the author of the world, i.e. that it rests on his decree, can be assumed, even though he is not the creator, i.e. has brought it forth in time.\nA beginning in the world can be thought, but not a beginning of the world, because for that an imagined time is required. For the world is not merely to be through another, but to come to be, since it previously was not.\nNor can the *actus* of creation be thought, i.e. where an *actus* begins together with its effect: for in a necessary being nothing begins.\nNor, even if one were to assume this as possible, can any motive of freedom be thought for choosing in absolute time.\nThe world is perpetual, not because it is in the whole absolute time, but because this does not take place at all; [but because] because before the world there is no time, the world is at all times.\nEternity is an unrestricted existence of a thing. [Zuf] Thus only the necessary being is eternal. What is in time is perpetual, but not eternal; for in every time it either has been or still is or yet will be.",["‘author’: Urheber; ‘creator’: Schöpfer; ‘perpetual’: immerwährend.","‘where’: da, which can also be read ‘since’.","[Zuf]: a deleted beginning, probably of zufällig (‘contingent’); left as it stands."]],"reflm:4135":["God has created the world from eternity (g i.e. his decree is eternal *a parte ante et post*, or properly without *ante et post*; but this decree is called with respect to the beginning of the world creation, with respect to the continuation conservation), but has not thereby created an eternal world. (g Eternity belongs in the first case to the divine action (s causality), in the second to the product of it, which cannot have a determination of that kind.) For the eternity of God is his duration outside all condition of time, but the eternity of the world is in time. The duration of the world one can therefore bring into no common measure with the duration [of the world] (s of God). (s The phenomenon of a thing is a product of our sensibility. God is author of the things in themselves.)",["‘decree’: Rathschluß; ‘conservation’: Erhaltung; ‘duration’: Dauer."]],"reflm:4136":["What is formed is mere matter. What forms itself, a growth. What forms other things in the world, animal. One does not call stones created things, because they do not have their form from creation, but plants indeed. Animals one calls *creatures*. Freely acting beings are not called *creatures*.",["‘growth’: Gewächs (‘plant’, ‘vegetable’).","‘created things’: Geschöpfe; ‘creatures’: Creaturen, the Latinate word, distinguished here from Geschöpfe."]],"reflm:4137":["A substance of the world may not be the creator of another, because otherwise it would depend on itself (g as to its whole existence) (*ob commercium*). (g For things that stand *in commercio* can stand *in commercio* only through that which makes their existence possible as externally dependent). The *substantia creatrix est extramundana*.\nThe creator of one substance is at the same time the creator of all, because it puts all the rest in dependence on this one. (g There is no community without a common *principium*.)\nCreation is a unity, i.e. there did not come little by little several substances in addition to the created ones. For otherwise we would have no rule or unity for the use of our understanding. If e.g. in the growth of a tree substances arose in addition; thus the *principium stabile perpetuum* must also be *invariabile* with respect to quantity.",["‘may not’: mag nicht (‘cannot’).","‘it puts’: sie versetzt; sie may refer to the substance or to the creator (Schöpfer is masculine, so more naturally the substance)."]],"reflm:4138":["(g Only in freedom is evil lodged.)\nNature is in the whole good. 2. In every particular order that has its laws, good. e.g. even the faults of the female sex, which are natural.\nBut chance, i.e. the occurrence of nature insofar as it is made deviating from one of the rules of the order through influence of the others, thus insofar as it lies not under a particular rule of predetermination but merely of possibility, can be evil.",["‘chance’: Zufall; ‘predetermination’: Vorherbestimmung."]],"reflm:4139":["If I could assume a most perfect world without a wise author, then everything important in morals could be had insight into without the existence of God.",["‘be had insight into’: eingesehen werden (einsehen)."]],"reflm:4140":["To fear God: to do his commandments from obligation. To love God: to do his commandments gladly.\n}\nVeneration.",["‘obligation’: Schuldigkeit (what one owes)."]],"reflm:4141":["God proves his honor through exercise of his moral properties, i.e. through actions for whose sake he is worthy of veneration and worship, not of high praise. We do not always venerate him whom we love, nor him whom we fear, but at the same time. *adoratio* is love, fear and admiration at once.",["‘honor’: Ehre (also ‘glory’); ‘high praise’: Hochpreisung.","‘but at the same time’: sondern zugleich, elliptical as in the German."]],"reflm:4142":["Creation (beginning) arises from freedom.",[]],"reflm:4143":["The end of creation is at all times a combination of the world with the divine highest pleasure, but not as its ground, but as its consequence. Human beings depend also in moral things in part on their end. but if they could feel their own moral perfection even without its exercise, then the gratification would not arise through the action, but the latter from the former.\nThe highest happiness [from d] under the condition in created beings which lets them be regarded as universally good is the final end. Thus the highest happiness agreeing with morality.\nThus our actions are never directed at the satisfaction of the divine desire. The ends of the actions thus lie not in God but in created beings; thus the honor of God is not the end but the [motive] ground of good actions.",["‘consequence’: Folge; ‘gratification’: Vergnügen; ‘pleasure’: Lust.","‘lets them be regarded’: sie ... betrachten läßt; sie may refer to the created beings or to the happiness.","[motive]: the deleted Bewegungs- of Bewegungsgrund (‘motive’), leaving Grund (‘ground’)."]],"reflm:4144":["*Omnipraesentia* not (g itself) in space, not *localis*, but *virtualis*.",[]],"reflm:4145":["Not (g as) world-soul, which reciprocally also suffers.\nSpace the *sensorium* of divine omnipresence: Newton.\nIntimate presence is represented fanatically.",["Latin: *Spatium sensorium omnipraesentiae divinae*; *Intima praesentia fanatice repraesentatur*.","‘suffers’: leidend (‘passive’, ‘being acted upon’)."]],"reflm:4146":["(s system of pure reason. Not mathematics.)\nMetaphysics is a philosophy of pure reason.\n—\n— —\n— —\n— —\nof form.\n—\n— —\n— —\n— —\nof the subject and not object.\n(s is the critique of pure reason.)\n— — of the [Ges] conditions under which alone\nhuman pure reason can judge.\n(s concepts either empirical or pure)",["[Ges]: a deleted beginning, perhaps of Gesetzen (‘laws’); left as it stands.","‘object’: obiect, the Latinate word; last line Latin: *Conceptus vel empirici vel puri*."]],"reflm:4147":["One can never infer from below upward (for reason infers only from the universal to the particular), but only by means of higher rules, under which many particular cases are subsumed, to a subordinate law.\n(s One must regard the *principia* as heterogeneous (rational).)",["‘infer’: schließen."]],"reflm:4148":["Critique is a science not of the production but of the judging of certain things according to rules of perfection; thus metaphysics a science of judging cognitions from pure reason. Apart from logic, which is an [organon] propaedeutic science of all learning, the [phil:] learning of reason contains a critical part of experience and reason and two dogmatic ones; the first is metaphysics, the second mathematics and morals in pure reason, and physics and psychology in empirical science of reason.",["‘judging’: Beurtheilung, beurtheilen; ‘learning’: Gelehrsamkeit; ‘learning of reason’: VernunftGelehrsamkeit.","[phil:]: deleted abbreviation, presumably of philosophische."]],"reflm:4149":["*Philosophia transscendentalis*.\n*Omnitudo* is (s disjunctive)\nor distributive.\n*Vniversalitas*.*\nor collective.\n*Vniversitas*.\nCollective *omnitudo* is either of coordination or of subordination [and]; the terminus of the former is the world, the terminus of the latter is the first being.\nHence. 1. Ontology 2. Cosmology 3. Theology }\n}\n}\n} transcendental { s\n{\n{\n{pure philosophy applied to nature in general or this nature. }\n}\n}\n}\n*(g distributive is logical. Each one.\ncollective: real. All together.\nEvery [human being is] body is movable; not: all together are movable.\n[Al] Every alteration has an end; not: all together an end.)",["*omnitudo*: totality; *Vniversalitas* (universality) and *Vniversitas* (the collective whole) kept in Latin to preserve the distinction.","[Al]: a deleted beginning, probably of Alle (‘all’)."]],"reflm:4150":["All philosophy is either empirical or *pura* (g *intellectualis*).\nThe first is that whose *principia* are borrowed from experience. Hence *psychologia et physica*.\n*Philosophia pura* is either *theoretica* or *practica*.\nThe *theoretica* has either no object of the senses at all for its object: *transscendentalis*, or it has the universal of all objects of the senses for its object: *metaphysica propria*. The latter consists of *physiologia, mechanica, pnevmatologia, theologia naturalis*.",["‘object’ renders both obiect (‘object of the senses’) and Gegenstand (‘for its object’)."]],"reflm:4151":["Stolpian question: From the attributes which are common to any things whatever constituting this universe (But no others are common, or rather are cognized as common, than that they constitute [this] universe.) to demonstrate that they are not necessary, but created by a necessary being.\n––––––––––\nProf: Pestel, Secretary of the Stolp institute, before 1 Jul. 1771.",["Latin: *Qvaestio Stolpiana*; ‘to demonstrate’: *demonstrare*; ‘are cognized’: *cognoscuntur*."]],"reflm:4152":["That ontology is nothing other than a transcendental logic (g subjective), but applied metaphysics merely negative, and nothing remains but morals, whose data the human will furnishes, and the *principia formalia* are analytic.",["The whole is a dependent daß-clause with no main clause, as in the German."]],"reflm:4153":["The synthetic judgments of pure reason are clear not through themselves, but only in their application *in conseqventia*, and thus cannot be used *in antecedentia*.",[]],"reflm:4154":["Since in the sensible there are matter and form, should there not also be matter and form in the intellectual?\nThe form of sensibility is twofold: space and time.\nThe form of reason is twofold: logical and real.\nuniversal and particular\n| ground and consequence",["‘consequence’: Folge."]],"reflm:4155":["The metaphysical concepts are first absolute. Possibility and existence. Second respective.\na. Unity and plurality. *omnitudo* and particularity. [Infin]\nb. Bounds. The first, the last, *infinitum, finitum*.\nc. Combination of coordination. Whole and part, simple and composite.\nof subordination.\n1. subject and predicate.\n2. ground and consequence.",["‘bounds’: Gräntzen; ‘combination’: Verbindung; ‘consequence’: Folge.","[Infin]: a deleted beginning, presumably of *infinitum*."]],"reflm:4156":["We can think no contingent being that would not be conditionally necessary, and we must finally terminate this subordination at a being that is unconditionally necessary, although we have no insight of reason into this absolute necessity, because otherwise we would cognize the existence of it in itself immediately; and the *ens necessarium* is a concept given through reason and its boundary, but not located within the boundary, hence problematic.\nWe can think nothing that happens without its being contingent, and indeed without thinking an absolute contingency [to th] of the first ground in arising [so that]. The first beginning takes place only through freedom.\nThus according to rules of (g pure) reason nothing is absolutely contingent, and the supreme causality is necessary; but according to rules of reason which has [to] concepts of appearance as its ground, the causality of the first cause of that which happens is contingent. According to rules of morality there must be authors and not mere intermediate causes of occurrences; according to rules of [pure reason metaphysics] causality there is only one author, who, however, is outside the world.",["‘the existence of it in itself’: das Daseyn davon an sich selbst; ‘in itself’ may attach to the existence or to the cognizing.","‘concepts of appearance’: erscheinungsbegriffe; ‘intermediate causes’: Mittelursachen.","‘one author’: ein Urheber, which can also be read ‘an author’."]],"reflm:4157":["The actuality of (g contingent) things demands a necessary *principium*, arising the absolute contingency of causality. Thus [are the concepts laws of appearance to the laws of reason] reason [a pr] cannot be used with the principles which, applied to appearances, are correct, *a priori*.",["‘arising’: das Entstehen; the verb ‘demands’ is to be carried over, as in the German.","‘principles’: Grundsätze."]],"reflm:4158":["One can represent a subject in no other way than through its predicates, and predicates in no other way than in their subject. Hence the necessity of representing substances to oneself, which is [a] more a subjective necessity of the laws of our understanding than an objective one.\nAll judgments arise in that the understanding attends to its own actions or in general to representations. If these are representations of sensation and of their relations, then they are empirical and correct; if they are representations breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4159":["Of arbitrary [and abstracted] concepts many sorts of definitions can be given, i.e. explanations from which [all] entirely the same can follow be derived. and for that very reason [from] of one and the same proposition different proofs. But of self-standing concepts of reason only breaks off.",["‘definitions’: definitionen; ‘explanations’: Erklärungen.","‘can follow be derived’: folgen könen abgeleitet werden, two verbs left side by side, as in the German."]],"reflm:4160":["The adverbs and prepositions, likewise (I, you), further the |\npluperfect etc. are inexplicable; in this consists the philosophy about languages.\nPiccard: *isagoge in lectionem aristotelis*.",["‘inexplicable’: unerklärlich, which can also be read ‘indefinable’ (Erklärung, ‘definition’)."]],"reflm:4161":["If it came down to names of sciences, then we would distinguish *Physicam, Metaphysicam, Hyperphysicam* and *hypophysicam*. Namely 1. the nature of actual things (g objects of the senses) from experience, 2. Of all possible things (g objects) from reason*. 3. That which depends on the cause of nature among actual things. 4. What is actual without having its determining ground in any nature, i.e. chance. (g and *Qvalitas occulta*.) (s and blind necessity.)\nThis division holds for the [under] determination of the sciences with respect to empirical cognition.\n*(g We cognize through reason the things and their relation, or the relation of the concepts. Morals.)",["‘chance’: das Ohngefehr; ‘objects’: Gegenstände."]],"reflm:4162":["All cognition has either empirical or rational principles; the latter are either logical or real. The logical assume the first concepts of things and relations as they are given, |\nand consider only the subordination according to identity and contradiction; the second are first concepts and relations given through the nature of reason.",["‘principles’: principien, the Latinate word, not Grundsätze."]],"reflm:4163":["All sciences of (g pure) reason are either those that through pure reason consider the rules of universal cognition in general, or the particular rules of pure reason itself. *Logica*. *Phaenomologia generalis*, *Noologia generalis* have merely the rules of universal [and not empirical] cognitions, which are given through no experience, as their end. Applied *noologie*, to that which is given through experience, although not through grounds of experience, is theoretical: metaphysics, or practical: morals.",["“universal” renders allgemein throughout.","“Phaenomologia” stands so in the text (for Phaenomenologia)."]],"reflm:4164":["Metaphysics is the *organon* of the pure understanding, first critical.\n[All doubt]\nSkepticism is either dogmatic or critical, the former breaks off.",["“first critical”: primo critica; critica may be read as an adjective agreeing with metaphysica or as the noun “critique”.","“breaks off” (bricht ab) is the AA's note that the Reflexion stops here."]],"reflm:4165":["The *terminus* of coordination is the world, of subordination God; thus *cosmologia* and *theologia*. The *omnitudo logica* is in ontology *realis*.",["*realis* may go with *omnitudo* (the logical totality is real in ontology) or stand as a predicate; the ambiguity is left."]],"reflm:4166":["The first part, of the first grounds of our pure cognition of reason of all things (g supreme *principio cognoscendi*). Ontology.\nThe 2nd part, of the first ground of things: supreme *principio essendi*. *Theologia Naturalis*.\nOf the first principles of reason with regard to the [objects] corporeal nature, i.e. of the possibility of outer appearances *pnevmatologia* or breaks off.",["“cognition of reason”: Vernunfterkenntnis; “principles”: principien.","“breaks off” (bricht ab) is the AA's note that the Reflexion stops here."]],"reflm:4167":["In empirical philosophy it is permitted to feign, since the possibility of the principles is empirically given; in pure philosophy it is not permitted to feign, since the fictions would be of pure reason, i.e. relations given neither from the nature of reason nor by experience, and therefore asserted quite arbitrarily as to possibility.",["“feign”: fingere; “fictions”: fictiones.","“possibilitatis plane arbitrario assertae” is grammatically irregular (arbitrario perhaps for arbitrarie); rendered as it can be construed."]],"reflm:4168":["Metaphysics [is a science] has either mere objects of pure reason as its object or [also] objects of the senses, of which it comes to know the grounds and their nature not through the senses but through pure reason; the former is the general, the latter the particular part (*Metaphysica applicata*) and consists of *pneumatologia* and *somatologia pura*; before both the *physiologia* of inner or outer sense must go first, but not before *metaphysica universali*,\n|\nnamely ontology and *theologia generali*, of which the first considers the supreme *principium* of all cognitions through pure reason, the second the supreme *principium*, to be cognized through pure reason, of all things. In *ontologia* everything is considered *disiunctive*, in *theologia Naturali* and *cosmologia* everything *collective*.",["“objects” renders both obiecten and Gegenstand(e).","“comes to know”: kennen lernt."]],"reflm:4169":["Universality (g *Omnitudo*) is either distributed or comprehensive universality, *distributiva* or *collectiva*. Of this one must first treat in general. (It has its bounds or not.) The *collectiva* is either of subordination or of coordination. That of the latter gives the concept of the world, the former the concept of the original being as the ground of all sequels subordinated to one another, so that no sequel among those that stand under one another is encountered which would not stand under it.\nThe [univers] *omnitudo distributiva* is either *universaliter* or *disiunctive distributiva*. Ontology treats of the predicates of things which hold *universaliter* or *disiunctive* of all. *Cosmologia* of all together that belong as parts to one and the same whole. *Theologia naturalis* of all together that belong as sequels to one ground.",["“distributed or comprehensive”: vertheilte oder zusammenfassende; “original being”: Urwesen.","“Of this” (Von dieser) may refer to the *collectiva* or to universality as a whole."]],"reflm:4170":["An empty concept is one that (g goes to) no object [has]; the negative: one that goes to its negation; the impossible: one that cancels itself.",["“goes to”: geht auf (is directed at); the deleted [hat] and the added auf ... geht stand in the German as: der (g auf) keinen Gegenstand [hat] geht.","“cancels”: aufhebt; “negation”: Verneinung."]],"reflm:4171":["Logical canceling is as much as: not positing; the real, just as much as: removing what is there. *prior negatio, posterior ablatio* (the former negation, the latter removal).",["“canceling”: aufheben; “removing”: removiren."]],"reflm:4172":["Of the principles of pure reason there are two: 1. from the concepts given through pure reason but confused, *per analysin*, e.g. substance, whole, necessary etc.; 2. from the concepts given through reason *per synthesin*. The concepts given through reason before all experience, although abstracted on the occasion of experience from the laws of reason, e.g. that of ground, are forms impressed on the ordering understanding for the use of experiences.",["“Der Grundsätze ... sind zwey” (genitive plural): “Of the principles ... there are two”.","“confused”: verworrenen; “impressed”: eingedrükte."]],"reflm:4173":["What lies in the concept of a necessary being and can be drawn from it *per analysin* is little; what is combined with it *per synthesin*, i.e. what is required for a necessary being, is much, but can only be cognized *per principia synthetica*.",[]],"reflm:4174":["Everything that happens is in the series of successions [of the] and is represented therein. But nothing can be represented in absolute time, rather things are only represented in a sequence insofar as a real connection of them is conceived, whereby one member draws the other after it; thus nothing in a series can be cognized as actual if the transition from what precedes to it is not necessary according to a universal law. I.e. without a ground, although one does not cognize it.\nThe relations of things are in general always represented through real connections, and time is only the form of appearance in which these things connected in such a way are intuited. Time does not bring forth representations of connected things.",["“successions”: successionen; “sequence”: reihenfolge; “conceived”: concipirt.","“real connection(s)”: reale Verknüpfung, realverknüpfungen."]],"reflm:4175":["The thinking of negation is properly a thinking of the removal of true position, which could otherwise creep into the understanding. A visible eclipse of the moon is possible, but not Milton's visible darkness.",["“removal”: wegschaffung; “position”: position.","“Milton's visible darkness”: sichtbare Dunkelheit des Milton."]],"reflm:4176":["With the essence, sufficiency, as concerns coordination, and [subordination what] precision (g measuredness), as concerns subordination, is the aim. *reductio ad minimos terminos*.",["“essence”: Wesen, which may also be read as “being”.","“sufficiency”: zulenglichkeit; “precision”: praecision; “measuredness”: Abgemessenheit; “aim”: absicht."]],"reflm:4177":["Properly only substances exist. Accidents are only predicates of existing substances. The predicate itself is nothing existing.",[]],"reflm:4178":["Necessity is either (g as) of the grounded or as of the ground or of independence;\nNecessity is either absolute, i.e. necessary in every respect,\nor hypothetical: necessary only in a certain respect.\nThoroughgoing contingency is of that whose opposite is possible in any respect whatever, mere chance, the haphazard. Contingency[omnimod] thoroughgoing in an external respect is freedom.\nA first beginning can be thought only through freedom. Motion is by its nature internally contingent, but given a certain external nexus always necessary; freedom is the reverse.",["“grounded” and “ground”: rationati, rationis; “thoroughgoing”: omnimoda.","“mere chance, the haphazard”: German in the Latin, der bloße Zufall, das Ungefehr."]],"reflm:4179":["Two propositions which stand directly opposed to one another: if something is, then it is necessary through something else (*ratio intelligendi in serie data*); if something necessary is, then there is an absolutely necessary being (*ratio complete intelligendi seriem, h.e. terminandi*); according to the first there is nothing absolutely necessary, according to the second there is. The cause is that possibility can be thought without actuality and yet not without all.",["*ratio intelligendi in serie data*: ground of understanding given in the series; *ratio complete intelligendi seriem, h.e. terminandi*: ground of completely understanding the series, i.e. of terminating it.","“not without all” (nicht ohne alle): sc. actuality, left as elliptical as the German."]],"reflm:4180":["The hypothetically necessary, without an absolutely necessary *principium*, permits a long use of reason, if one does not think of the boundary, and is therefore better suited to the understanding than mere chance; but if one denies it (*atheismus assertorius*), this use of reason is nevertheless made useless again, for the whole chain is contingent, because it demands something outside itself which would not hang further on another ring, and this is denied. The concept of the absolutely necessary is thus an essential condition of the use of our reason, but only a presupposition, not however a product of it. For I finally leave out all condition; [I] what remains can no longer be cognized *a priori*, i.e. had insight into. I grasp after a higher hold and am in empty space, which is infinite and through itself makes everything possible and bounds it. But this boundary-concept we can cognize sufficiently relative to the things subordinated to it, namely what would be required in order that it hold all things through itself. So it is also with freedom, the first beginning of arising. We know well what flows from freedom and its presupposition, and [it] also need to presuppose it. but no one can comprehend the arising of a free action, because it is the beginning of all arising. I am a substance, in me the predicates terminate, and I am myself none. This self I know with certainty as the *terminum* of my imputations. But I am in all respects contingent and am not the independent of existence, though of inherence etc. etc. That without [to] presupposing which I cannot use my reason (necessity) is, relative to me, the condition of my reason; but that without knowing which I cannot use my reason completely is merely a boundary for reason.",["“mere chance”: das bloße ohngefehr; “had insight into”: eingesehen; “boundary-concept”: Grentzbegrif.","“arising”: entstehen; “relative to”: respective.","“das unabhängige der existentz, obzwar der inhaerentz” is elliptical: the independent (thing) as regards existence, though (the independent) as regards inherence."]],"reflm:4181":["From succession one cannot yet infer contingency; for the state following upon it is no *contradictorie oppositum* of the previous one, consequently (g from that) it is not to be cognized that its opposite is possible.\n[absolute] (g *interne*, internally) unchangeable means either: what for itself alone can never change; but what can change in no combination either is absolutely unchangeable. The internally unchangeable has no such difficulty as the internally changeable for reason.",["“absolutely”: schlechterdings; the deleted [absolute] is Latin.","Only one member of the “either” (entweder) is given."]],"reflm:4182":["Reality is either comparative or absolute.\nReality *phaenomenon* is not absolute. e.g. impenetrability.\nBetween realities and negations *phaenomena* there is (g not) given a sensual third, but an intellectual one. (s between realities *phaenomena* there is given a third =0.)\n(s Intellectual is that whose concept is a doing.)",["“sensual”: sensuale; “doing”: Thun.","With the added (g non) the sentence reads: there is given not a sensual third but an intellectual one."]],"reflm:4183":["A [every] *qvantum* is not for that reason a *compositum*, nor |\neven every *totum*, although it is *divisibile*; but the *divisio* is rather a distinguishing than a separation; hence it is also not possible through composition of the separated. *qvantum continuum non est divisibile [omne]*. The magnitude of an (g intensive) ground proves no composition out of smaller [ausser], but the magnitude of a whole does.\nWhat can be divided only through limitation is not possible through composition; thus not space.\n(s The earth is not composed of lands, but divided into them.\n*materia*: *partes*,\n*forma*: *conjunctio*. *Coordinatio*, not *subordinatio*.\n*Qvantitas*, *totalitas*.)",["“distinguishing”: Unterscheidung; “separation”: Trennung; “limitation”: Einschränkung.","The deleted [ausser] (“except” or the start of another word) is left untranslated.","*qvantum continuum non est divisibile [omne]*: a continuous quantum is not divisible, with [omne] (“every”) deleted."]],"reflm:4184":["Opposite the column heading:\nIncomprehensible is the first of things (*necessarium*) and the first of states (*initium primum*: *libertas*). Likewise the last, i.e. the outermost of things in the *synthesi*, that breaks off.",["“Opposite the column heading” (Gegenüber der Columnenüberschrift) is the AA's note of where the Reflexion stands.","“breaks off” (bricht ab) is the AA's note that the Reflexion stops here."]],"reflm:4185":["Several things conjoined make a composite. All things conjoined, a whole. Therefore not every composite (e.g. eternity) is a whole, although every whole is a composite. The word “all” is taken either generically or singularly. Of all numbers we enunciate generically in arithmetic, and we think all straight lines in geometry generically, but [if] to think all and each is not permitted.\nIn the rational notion of a composite the terminus of analysis is the simple and the synthetic terminus the whole. The former is the terminus *a priori*, the latter *a posteriori*.\nIn order that all things be thought distributively, it is necessary that unities be posited at the same time. and therefore eternity is not a whole.\nIn any whole whatever, therefore, there are to be thought: matter, form and totality.",["“composite”: compositum; “whole”: totum; “all and each”: omnes et singulas.","The deleted [sin] (“if”) is rendered in place."]],"reflm:4186":["Time is the first condition for the possibility of a whole, for coordination demands simultaneity and subordination succession.",[]],"reflm:4187":["Things that are simultaneous are connected through time equally as (g those) that are after one another. but time is unique; therefore it is a possibility of the coordination of many depending on one. The coordination of contradictory opposites is succession, that of non-opposites is coexistence.\nThe ground through which the existence of many belongs to one, namely that they are simultaneous, lies hidden in this, that [all are through] grounds effect one state of the world; and therefore simultaneity belongs to the unity of the world and testifies to a common cause. (time is everywhere.) That changes are possible (in which the idea of succession in time consists) rests breaks off.",["“ground(s)”: ratio, rationes; “connected”: connexa; “unique”: unicum.","“breaks off” (bricht ab) is the AA's note that the Reflexion stops here, after nititur (“rests”)."]],"reflm:4188":["Space is no concept of experience: l. only through it are experiences possible; 2. it is no object of the senses; 3. the immediate principles of it do not have the contingency and particularity of propositions of experience and are also not appeals to experience. It |\nis also no concept of reason: 1. it is not a universal but a singular concept; hence all spaces parts of a single one and the principles are [not] cognized through immediate intuiting.",["“l.” stands so in the text, for “1.”.","“hence all spaces parts of a single one” has no verb in the German.","“particularity”: particularitaet; “intuiting”: anschauen."]],"reflm:4189":["With time there is the relation of things among one another with regard to existence in general, whether they are simultaneous or after one another, and the magnitude of their duration. They all appear as in one [Dase] whole of existence, yet [with] of vanishing magnitude in becoming and passing away at once. With space there is the relation of things with regard to their community: all as in one whole, which makes all [possible] community possible.\nBecause we cognize intuitively not merely the space of the object that affects our senses, but the whole space, space must not merely arise from the actual affecting of the senses, but precede it. Meanwhile, because it is nevertheless represented as something actual, it will be the effect of the feeling of omnipresence, and time of the dependence of all things in existence on One, just as space the sequel of this upon the community of things.",["“affects” / “affecting”: rührt / rührung; “cognize intuitively”: anschauend erkennen.","The deleted [Dase] is the start of Daseyn (“existence”).","The last clause (so wie der Raum die folge daraus ...) has no verb of its own in the German."]],"reflm:4190":["All existence in time is contingent. For it is a perpetual vanishing and commencing; and from the fact that a thing exists it does not follow that it will exist. Necessary existence is the only complete existence.\nExistence in time is to necessary existence as [the present] in space, with regard to extension, an infinite line is to the motion of a point in it.",["“existence”: Daseyn; “commencing”: Anheben."]],"reflm:4191":["M 72:\nOf space the rules are: 1. that, since it is only the condition of the appearance of outer things, one must not regard it as the condition of the existence of the things themselves, unless the things themselves were to be only appearances; 2. that the predicates of space must be regarded not as predicates of things, but of their appearances, consequently the propositions which express the possibility of things do not hold of space; 3. that space is something wholly true |\nM 72': |\nwith regard to outer appearances, because it is the condition for them.",["“must not regard” (nicht ansehen müße) may also be read as “need not regard”.","“unless the things themselves were to be” (außer die Dinge selbst müsten ... seyn): literally “had to be”."]],"reflm:4192":["Infinity is not an objective determinate concept of a magnitude in relation to others, but subjectively an exceeding of a magnitude over all that are assignable by us, although not assignable by every understanding.",["“assignable” renders angebliche (= angebbare, “that can be given or stated”)."]],"reflm:4193":["Above, beside and \nAmong the things there are the following kinds of the greatest:\n1. the concept that contains everything [under itself] in its sphere (under itself),\n2. that which comprises everything in itself as a whole,\n3. that which comprehends everything under itself as a ground.\nThe widest concept, the greatest whole, the greatest ground. Logical. Mathematical. Metaphysical: all intellectual sciences.",["The editorial location note “Über, neben und” is cut off in the source; “Unter den Dingen” may also be read as continuing it (“below”).","“comprises” renders befasset; “comprehends” begreift.","Logisch. Mathematisch. Metaphysisch can be read as adjectives or adverbs (“logically” etc.)."]],"reflm:4194":["Beside and \nThe potential infinity, actual. Why the past one is also counted toward the actual one.",["The editorial location note “Neben und” is cut off in the source.","“the past one” renders die Vergangene, feminine, agreeing with Unendlichkeit."]],"reflm:4195":["Below and \nIn the infinite the difficulty is to reconcile the totality with the impossibility of a *synthesis completae*. consequently the difficulty is subjective. On the other hand the *potentialiter infinitum* (*infinitum coordinationis potentialis*) is very well comprehensible, but without totality.",["“totality” renders totalitaet; *synthesis completae*: “of a complete synthesis”, Latin genitive after German unmöglichkeit.","*potentialiter infinitum* (*infinitum coordinationis potentialis*): “the potentially infinite (the infinite of potential coordination)”."]],"reflm:4196":["The greatest concept, the greatest intuition, the greatest thing are all given; and through their limitation we generate the logical, the sensible and real possibility of all things.",[]],"reflm:4197":["The mathematically infinite is without end according to laws of sensible estimation, the metaphysically infinite is without end in general, i.e. without limitations.\nA maximum either relatively such, e.g. ideals, or absolutely as regards reality.",["The second line is Latin (Maximum vel respective tale … vel absolute qvoad realitatem) with the German word Ideale; “relatively” renders respective."]],"reflm:4198":["On and \nThe infinite, the greatest *in sensu absoluto*; that which contains everything. The second is the same as the third, if the thing is considered as a ground. The third signifies the most.\nThrough reason the greatest can be discerned only by means of the *consummati*.",["*in sensu absoluto*: “in the absolute sense”.","“discerned” renders eingesehen (einsehen, gain insight into).","*consummati*: genitive of *consummatum*, “the completed”, after German des."]],"reflm:4199":["That two things cannot be in one place is not an objective proposition; rather we call [two] things different only because they are in different places, and we have in one and the same place through outer sense no ground of the distinguishing of different things.\nThe being of a thing in a place can be expressed thus: The place is the ground of something, which signifies as much as: to be in a place is as much as to act externally in a certain relation. Because in absolute space no relation of a thing to others is encountered, a thing can be encountered in no absolute place (without relation of the *actus* to others). but in empty space, which is not filled.",["“act” renders wirken; *actus*: “acts”, Latin.","The last sentence is a fragment, as in the source."]],"reflm:4200":["1. That [no] (g all) relation in space is [one-sided] (g relative and never absolute); e.g. no absolute place, no absolute rest or motion, but at all times motion and rest must refer to some other body;\n2. what follows from this: namely that all motion and rest is reciprocal and equal and that no body, in respect of another which is moved in relation to it, rests relative to it; [that therefore effect and that these motions are opposed]*\n3. That [what the production], if they are thus combined, a common rest results from it, i.e. that effect and countereffect are equal to one another.\n*(g That no motion can be generated without producing just as much in the opposite direction (in the moving one), because a body moves only as much relative to another as this one |\nrelative to it, or another toward itself as itself toward the other, and because they reciprocally move away from one another or approach one another. [The] Both bodies taken together will therefore, in respect of the [place] space relative to which they were considered before the generated motion, remain in one and the same state. I.e. if they were at rest before the communication, their *systema* will rest, and, if they moved, their *systema* will move.)",["“effect and countereffect” renders würkung und gegenwirkung, the usual terms for action and reaction.","“relative to” renders gegen throughout; “communication” renders Mittheilung.","Item 3 is syntactically incomplete in the source: the deleted words leave “Daß … eine gemeinschaftliche Ruhe daraus erfolge” without a construction."]],"reflm:4201":["(s the substantial whole absolutely complete is the world.)\n(g contingency, i.e. relation to a cause, flows from composition.)\nto the (g concept) of the world belong 1. the material: [subjects] plurality of substances. An egoistic world is not a world. 2. The formal or the (g real) nexus of substances, insofar as they constitute a whole (g coordination (not subordination under its cause)). 3. [determination] relation negatively determined: That it not be a part of another whole. or the totality. In every whole there is *totalitas* [*absoluta*] *respectiva*, in the world *totalitas absoluta*. The earth [is] has *totalitatem*; and if one takes all its parts together, then one has *totalitatem respectivam*; but it is itself still a part, thus it has no *totalitatem absolutam*.\nThe *terminus* of coordination *per analysin*, i.e. *a priori*, is the simple; the *terminus* of it *per synthesin*, i.e. *a posteriori*, is the world. The *terminus* of subordination *a priori* is the supreme cause, *a posteriori* the totality of the consequences. Reason cognizes as incomplete its insight which is not enclosed in *terminos*. The necessity of the *terminorum*, however, is not objective. But it also cannot think how a *terminus* should take place, whether of coordination or also of subordination.\nWhat are connected only as [mutual] subordinates do not always admit of totality, unless by computing they can be coordinated logically.\n(s Completeness of a composite as such is totality, and this either absolute or hypothetical; the former either with respect to all possible wholes or with respect to some actual whole.)",["The first three lines, the sentence beginning “What are connected” and the last line are Latin; the rest is German with Latin terms.","*totalitas respectiva* / *absoluta*: “respective / absolute totality”; *terminus*: “term, limit”; *per analysin* / *per synthesin*: “through analysis / synthesis”.","“totality” in the German sentences renders totalitaet; “consequences” renders Folgen."]],"reflm:4202":["Complete is a quantum, is not a part of another.",["Latin: Completum est qvantum, non est pars alterius; no relative pronoun links the two clauses."]],"reflm:4203":["In any (g substantial) whole whatever there is a reciprocal dependence of states among the parts, not a dependence [under] as to the actuality of the substance. For cause and caused are not co-parts of the same whole, because the nexus of subordination differs from the nexus of coordination. The dependence of states, however, consists in this, that the conditions of the inherence of accidents in substances mutually inhere.",["Latin throughout.","substantiis can attach to inhaerentiae (“inherence … in substances”) or to inhaereant (“mutually inhere in substances”); the English keeps both readings."]],"reflm:4204":["the real nexus of coordination (g real) is mutual, of subordination of substances as such is not mutual.",["Latin throughout."]],"reflm:4205":["The supreme and all-sufficient cause is that which is subordinated and coordinated to no other.\n(s *independentia* and *completudo*)",["*independentia* and *completudo*: “independence” and “completeness”."]],"reflm:4206":["The coordination according to which all [Best] *accidentia* of a thing make up a *totum status* is a consequence of the subordination under one, namely the substantial. The coordination according to which all substances make up a whole is the subordination under an *ente primo*.",["[Best] is a deleted, unfinished German word, left as it stands.","*accidentia*: “accidents”; *totum status*: “whole of the state”; *ente primo*: “first being”, Latin dative/ablative after German unter einem.","“consequence” renders folge."]],"reflm:4207":["Opposite and \nSpace and time are formal grounds of the possibility of a world. Space, time and force alone are capable of magnitude in the world.\n1. From the composition of the world the contingency and the beginning or eternity; [2. From space] likewise the magnitude, infinity.\n2. Alteration of the state. Order of nature. Gradation.\nThe supreme ground of combination is also the formal ground of the possibility of the *commercii*. Sensibly expressed it is space. But space is presumably only the sensible intuition, which the immediate consciousness (intellectual intuition) underlies, but cannot be found in it through any dissection.",["The editorial location note “Gegenüber und” is cut off in the source.","*commercii*: genitive of *commercium*, “community, commerce”; “Gradation” renders Stufenfolge.","The subject of “cannot be found” is grammatically the immediate consciousness; “dissection” renders Zergliederung."]],"reflm:4208":["The effects are symbols of the causes, thus space (through which things are nevertheless actually represented as necessarily connected by means of a common ground) a *symbolum* of the divine omnipresence or the *phaenomenon* of the divine [intuitions] causality. In space possibility is not distinguished from actuality.\nAll sensible representation is a *symbolum* of the intellectual. All (g sensible) representation of the relation of the effect to its cause is the *symbolum*.",["“symbols” renders German symbole; *symbolum* and *phaenomenon* are Latin and Greek forms in the source.","“actually” renders wirklich, “actuality” Wirklichkeit."]],"reflm:4209":["An infinite past series of appearances would presuppose an infinite duration of sensibility, hence an appearance whose representation would in turn be appearance.",[]],"reflm:4210":["In time the world is either from eternity (at all time *a parte priori*) or not from eternity, but yet in time (in a part of time *a priori*). Both are false. For the part of time outside or before the world [is] or rather absolute time is indeed a condition of actual, but not of non-actual things, in which their *positus* would be determined in respect of what precedes.\nNo infinite world is possible to represent to oneself. Now, however, the world (*phaenomenon*) and time are only something in representation. If I wanted to say: whether an infinite world is not possible in itself, then the world of sense is nothing in itself. However far I want to go (g back), the world is finite; to cognize the world back into the infinite is impossible; thus the world of sense, as far as I go, is always finite. But is world thus to be measured *a parte priori*?",["*a parte priori*: “on the side of the earlier”; *positus*: “position”, Latin.","The interlinear (g zurük) forms one word with gehen (zurükgehen, “go back”).","“world of sense” renders Sinnenwelt; “determined” renders determinirt."]],"reflm:4211":["M 121':\n*continuum formarum*.\nThere is a continuity among the *speciebus*; that is: there is no *speciem alii proximam* between which others could not be placed, and thus also no *speciem infimam*. We have *species infimas* because we do not know the small differences. E.g. gold, of which we know no *species*. Likewise of rye and plants. Perhaps they were at first very [from one anoth] placed according to the law of continuity and only separated through intermixture.\n*Non datur species infima* can mean either as much as: no *species infima* is possible, or: it is not actual. e.g. the germs that lie in the human being can bring forth a creature that is perfectly similar to an ape.\nM 121:\nDifferent *species* argue a difference of what belongs to the substance; but the difference of the states makes no difference of the things, thus also not of the *specierum*.",["*continuum formarum*: “continuum of forms”; *speciem alii proximam*: “a species next to another”; *species infima*: “lowest species”; *Non datur species infima*: “there is no lowest species”.","*speciebus*, *speciem*, *specierum* keep the Latin case forms of the source.","“know” renders kennen; “things” in the last sentence renders Sachen."]],"reflm:4212":["If everything is there through One, then every thing's thoroughgoing determination is a restriction of the world-whole, just as every measured-off space is a limitation of the infinite space: i.e. the whole does not arise from the composition of the action through which parts are brought forth, for through this a *quantum discretum* comes to be, but rather each part is possible only through the restriction of the power that is in the whole. But one product of nature is combined with another only through the restriction of the universal force which brings them |\nboth forth, thus [also only] because in the universal sufficient force lies the ground for all *speciebus*, which between one product and the other are possible only by means of all the intermediate products, of which there are infinitely many, which are contained between one *specie* and the other.",["“restriction” renders Einschränkung, “limitation” limitation; “power” renders Macht, “force” Kraft.","*quantum discretum*: “discrete quantum”; *speciebus*, *specie*: Latin case forms of *species*.","The construction of the last clause (die zwischen … moglich seyn nur vermittelst …) is loose in the source and is kept so."]],"reflm:4213":["In the metaphysical sense things are of one and the same kind from one of which to the other the transition is possible [only] through the alteration of magnitude, be it in a property or in the relation of two. On the other hand things of different kind are those whose rule is already different.",["“sense” renders Verstande; “kind” renders Art; “property” renders Eigenschaft."]],"reflm:4214":["139. E II 1354. 1355.\nM 139':\nThe first (g [inner]) inner ground of everything that belongs to the actuality of a thing is the nature of the same.\nAn effect has its ground either in the nature or the freedom of the cause. In the first case the opposite is physically, in the second practically impossible.\nThe first ground of the contingent is not in the nature of any thing.\nEverything that happens through the nature of a thing has a pre|\nM 139: |\nceding ground and is not the first. The [Zus] thoroughgoing determination of what belongs to the inner of existence, according to subjective laws: nature, according to objective: freedom.",["[inner] renders the deleted unfinished innerl; [Zus] is a deleted, unfinished German word, left as it stands.","“existence” renders Daseyn."]],"reflm:4215":["space being given, there is given the possibility of the copresence of many, but the copresence of many is not possible except an existing common principle being posited; therefore, space being given, one can infer to some first being. but space is unique; therefore one infers to a unique first cause. likewise it is necessary, insofar as it is mere possibility; therefore one infers to a necessary one.",["Latin throughout; “one can infer” renders concludi potest, “one infers” concluditur.","sed est unicum spatium can also be read “but there is a single space”.","“a necessary one”: necessariam, feminine, presumably agreeing with causam."]],"reflm:4216":["Every harmony is either generally or singularly established; the former requires that something be a substantial whole.\nGenerally established is either externally or internally established; the latter physical influence. singularly established is either occasionally or preestablished.\n[In the universe there is] As regards the special commerce of the mind and the body, the difficulty arises from this, that the substance which we cognize through inner sense is entirely unknown [as to] as an object of outer sense, and therefore no force [could] is given as a general principle of external presence; but to invent it is not permitted. But this trouble is also found [in] in regard to the actions of bodies, whose principle is established only from experience, not from intuition of reason.\nspace is a singular idea; from this it is established that the possibility of the copresence of many rests on the existence of a singular being.",["Latin throughout; “physical influence” renders influxus physicus, “commerce” commercium, “mind” animus.","“occasionally” renders occasionaliter (by occasional causes).","“is established” renders constat (also “is evident”)."]],"reflm:4217":["148. E II 1693. 784.\nM 148':\nThe cause of substance is creative, the cause of form architectonic. Therefore [cause of be] the extramundane being is not only the architect, but the creator of the world.\nThe world [is is not par] is a whole that is not an [possible] actual part of another whole; if it is not also a part of any possible world, it is the greatest whole. The rational cause of the world is the author. The author is not the architect. etc etc. The author of the world is neither coordinated nor subordinated to anyone. God.\nThings that [externally] are not spontaneously harmonious, but agreeing by external and arbitrary artifice, are in apparent, i.e. sympathetic, commerce. [Whose nature] Things that are conjoined from a general principle, so that their common nature is the ground of mutual dependence, agree physically or naturally. Physical influence*, however, is either original or derivative (s subaltern). The former would take place if the formal ground is domestic to the substances; the latter, if it is foreign. But the cause foreign to the world is the extramundane being. The commerce of the substances of the world therefore takes place through derivative influence. or it is natural commerce on account of common dependence.\nThe nature of the universe as such (since universal nature is the nature of all substances) consists in conjunction and completeness (wherefore it is called the universality of things); therefore breaks off.\nM 148:\n*(s originally a substance cannot influence a substance except one which it sustains, because substances do not suffer from others [from which not], except so that the passion is at the same time action.)",["Latin throughout, except the AA's German bricht ab, rendered “breaks off”; “ground” renders ratio.","Causa mundi rationalis can also be read “the cause of the rational world”.","“universality of things” renders rerum universitas; “passion” renders passio."]],"reflm:4218":["(g If there is a power of choice in God, then it is free power of choice.)\nThe divine freedom is very well to be comprehended. The spontaneity and the *necessitas subiectiva per motiva intellectualia* (which never necessitate subjectively, in that they do not consist in the way the subject is affected) are in him the most perfect. Here there is not combination of necessity with contingency. But in human freedom there is 1. a series of determining grounds of each free action necessary for the comprehensibility of its origin, but [at the same time] then the *spontaneitas simpliciter talis* on account of the [supreme determ] external determining grounds in a contingent thing impossible; but on the other side [if one it a first beginning in the] for a first beginning unconditioned contingency [necessary] or independent freedom necessary.",["“power of choice” renders Willkühr throughout.","*necessitas subiectiva per motiva intellectualia*: “subjective necessity through intellectual motives”; *spontaneitas simpliciter talis*: “spontaneity simply as such”.","“spontaneity” renders the Latinate spontaneitaet."]],"reflm:4219":["The difficulty of comprehending human freedom lies in this, that the subject is dependent and yet is supposed to act independently of other beings.\nThe necessary being cannot begin to act, the contingent one no first beginning.",[]],"reflm:4220":["Freedom is properly only the self-activity of which one is conscious. When one lets something occur to oneself, then this is an *actus* of self-activity; but one is here conscious not of one's activity, but of the |\neffect. The expression: I think (this object), already indicates that in regard to the representation I am not passive, that it is to be ascribed to me, that the opposite depends on myself.",["“self-activity” renders Selbstthatigkeit; “passive” renders leidend (literally “suffering”).","“object” renders the Latinate obiect."]],"reflm:4221":["Freedom is the faculty to bring forth and to effect something *originarie*. But how *causalitas originaria et facultas originarie efficiendi* can take place in an *ente derivativo* is not at all to be comprehended.",["*originarie*: “originally”; *causalitas originaria et facultas originarie efficiendi*: “original causality and the faculty of effecting originally”; *ente derivativo*: “derivative being”."]],"reflm:4222":["Our *arbitrium* is not sensitive, but the *actus arbitrii* either sensitive or intellectual. For *arbitrium* is not inclination, but the choice from inclination or reason.",["*arbitrium*: “power of choice”; *actus arbitrii*: “acts of the power of choice”.","“sensitive” renders sensitiv, “choice” Wahl."]],"reflm:4223":["practically that is nothing which I cannot perceive at all*. Now the transcendental necessitation cannot be perceived by me, but I feel nothing but the law, a force sufficient for it, and the pleasure; thus the practical is never breaks off.\n*(g For that does not come into account among the motives. Will I indeed on account of this predetermination be indifferent in regard to the aims of life or proceed otherwise. It is one thing to speculate and to think practically; the former for explaining, the latter for acting.)",["“practically” renders practisch, which can be adverb or adjective here.","“pleasure” renders Belieben (what one is pleased to do).","“breaks off” renders the editor's “bricht ab”: the note is unfinished."]],"reflm:4224":["The pathological necessitation is inner and agrees with spontaneity, but it is nevertheless the condition of a possible external necessitation. The intellectual necessitation is grounded not on passive, but on |\nactive *conditiones*; thus this is not opposed to freedom, because it is an independent, but self-made pleasure. If we have the nearest causes, then we need not know the interconnection with the most remote, nor how we come to this force which we have. The laws of motion are always the same, the cause of them may be what it will.",["“an independent, but self-made pleasure”: the German “ein independentes, sondern selbst gemachtes Belieben” sets sondern without a preceding negation; kept as it stands.","“pleasure” renders Belieben; *conditiones*: “conditions”."]],"reflm:4225":["10\nSince freedom is a complete self-activity of the will, without being determined by *stimuli* or by anything else that affects the subject, with it everything depends only on the certainty of personality: namely that it is conscious (g of itself) that it acts from its own power of choice, [it acts] the will is active and not passive, neither through *stimuli* nor through foreign impressions. Otherwise I would have to say: I am driven or moved to act thus or so, which means as much as: I am not acting, but passive. If God governs the determinations of the power of choice, then he acts; if the charms of things determine them necessarily, then they necessitate; in both cases the action does not spring from me, but I am only the means of another cause.\nIn the sensible world nothing is comprehensible but what is necessitated by preceding grounds. The actions of the free power of choice are *phaenomena*; but their connection with a self-active subject and with (g the faculty of) reason are intellectual; accordingly the determination of the free power of choice cannot be submitted to the *legibus sensitivis*.\nThe question whether freedom is possible is perhaps the same as the one, |\nwhether (g the human being) is a true person and whether the I is possible in a being of external determinations.\nThe I is an inexplicable representation. It is an intuition that is unchangeable.",["“it” in “that it is conscious … it acts” renders sie, which can refer to freedom or to personality.","“charms” renders reitze (stimuli); *legibus sensitivis*: “sensitive laws”.","The opening “10” stands in the source as printed."]],"reflm:4226":["(g Freedom does not consist in this, that the opposite could have pleased us, but only that our pleasure was not *passive* necessitated.)\nThe *arbitrium sensitivum* without consciousness is *brutum*.\nThe *arbitrium* is to an action either actively determined or *passive* determined. In the first case it is nevertheless free, although the *motiva* are *obiective necessitantia*. For it acts [without that] necessarily, not because its subject is affected by the objects according to its passive property, sensibility. The human power of choice is never *passive* determined. The *arbitrium* which is merely *active* determined is a good will. But though (g even) a good will is in itself determined to good actions (g and free), even if the substance is *causatum alterius*: yet the mixture with the *sensitivo* is just as much as if the *rationes ad determinandum* became *incompletae*. Therefore the human will is determined from itself to no kind of actions. Its power of choice is thus a free, to be sure, but undetermined power of choice (the divine is determined). The *arbitrium brutum* is determined *secundum rationes* [*intellectuales*] *sensitivas*, the divine *secundum intellectuales*, the human by neither. His actions could all have happened according to reason. Hence he is free. But is there then not a determining ground, though indeed not in the power of choice of the human being in general, yet in the circumstances and conditions? and if this is not, whence then do the actions actually happen. Answer: All *stimuli* of the sensible power of choice still cannot make the active of the human being into the passive. The higher power of choice after all decides itself; but why it sometimes decides on the side |\nof sensibility, sometimes on that of reason, of that no law can be given, because there is no constant law of both forces.\nIn an active *principium* the state has no influence at all on the subject, neither the past nor the present one, to determine its action. The subject is at all times from itself the source of the actions. But why it acts thus and not otherwise.",["*passive*, *active*: Kant's Latin adverbs, “passively”, “actively”, beside the German thätig.","“But though (g even)”: without the addition, “Ob aber” reads “But whether”; with it, “obgleich”, “although”.","*causatum alterius*: “caused by another”; *rationes ad determinandum … incompletae*: “grounds for determining … incomplete”; *secundum rationes sensitivas / intellectuales*: “according to sensitive / intellectual grounds”."]],"reflm:4227":["If human beings were completely intellectual, then all their actions would be actively determined, but nevertheless free, and would be contingent only in regard to the changeable occasions. These actions could also be imputed to them together with the rewards, although they were creatures of a higher being. For they would be to be regarded as self-active principles and as worthy objects of his goodness. If they were completely sensible, then their actions would be passively determined alone; nothing could be imputed to them, and they would be capable of no rewards and punishments. Now they are partly sensible, partly intellectual, yet so that sensibility certainly cannot make the intellectual passive, But the intellectual also cannot overcome the actions otherwise than through a certain measure of preponderance over sensibility. thus the human being is neither *active* nor *passive* determined; and since sensibility as well as the strength of reason depends on the circumstances, his actions depend partly on the circumstances, partly on the use of his reason, and cannot be wholly imputed to him. He is free, if one takes it most exactly, only the possibility of doing something good, in which freedom properly consists. But whether the action actually springs from this *principium* or from the sensitive one comes down to the conditions. Just as in a game every throw can win, regardless of the preceding and accompanying circumstances.",["“He is free, if one takes it most exactly, only the possibility”: the construction is incomplete in the German; allein can also be read “but”.","“conditions” renders the Latinate conditionen; “depend” renders both abhängt and dependiren."]],"reflm:4228":["We see ourselves through the consciousness of our personality in the intellectual world and find ourselves free. We see ourselves through our dependence on impressions in the sensible world and find ourselves determined. Our intuitions of bodies all belong to the sensible world; accordingly the experiences agree with its laws of determining grounds. but our intellectual intuitions of the free will do not agree with the laws of the *phaenomenorum*.",["*phaenomenorum*: “of phenomena” (Latin genitive plural)."]],"reflm:4229":["Freedom cannot be divided. The human being is either wholly free or not at all, because he either can act from an active *principium* or depends on conditions.",[]],"reflm:4230":["292. E II 1284. I 356.\nM 292':\nThe pure doctrine of the soul, because it presupposes no *principia* (g principles) from experience, can consist of no other than the following pieces:\n1. [Cogn] Absolute consideration of it from transcendental concepts: Substance. Simple (g single). contingent.* (g whether I or another in me effects the *actus* of the power of choice. if I am a contingent being.) finite. (s imperishable.)\n2. Comparison of it with other beings (g transcendental concepts) which we know through experience:\na, bodies in general. immaterial;\nb,** other thinking natures of the world. Which are below or beside or above the human being (*bruta, incolae planetarum, angeli*).\n3. Connection of it with the body:\n(g first difficulty: how merely inwardly acting forces can have outward consequences, how merely outwardly acting forces are supposed to bring forth inner alterations. secondly: how the union of an immaterial being with a material one is possible.)\na. Possibility of it. *commercium animae et corporis*. Seat of the soul (g cannot alter it). *Sensorium commune*. (g *ideae materiales*. *materialismus* [*subli*] *vel subiectivus vel formalis*.) (s *Status animae in diverso statu hominis. Sedes animae.*) (s *principium* of life. Freedom.)\nb. Beginning. Birth. before birth.\nc. End. Death. after death (s *metempsychosis*). (g Separation. Cessation of dependence on the body. whether it is a new dependence? Place.) (g Resurrection. Sleep of the soul, ethereal body. *Metempsychosis*. Place immediately after death: in the grave, on the earth, in heaven and hell. pneumatists.)\n*** *Anima est subiectum cogitans pendens a corpore (sentiens). Animus activum qvidem, sed adstrictum conditionibus corporis. Mens activum in et per se.*\n4. Connection with other spirits. Appearances: inner, outer.\nM 292: |\n*(s [Its] self-activity (s freedom). My state is not necessary through my nature, but I am free. Higher powers of cognition and of the will. Power over oneself, possibility of morality.)\nM 292: |\n**(s Universal condition of inner sense: consciousness. 2, Solely an object of inner sense: spirit.)\nM 292':\n***(g Soul. Mind. Spirit. A pain strikes the soul through sensation, it is in the mind (g springs) through reflection and in the spirit through disapproval of oneself. †)\nM 292: |\n(† s In the one the sensation displeases, in the second the whole state, in the third the person displeases itself. The first finds itself unwell, the second [is] esteems itself unhappy, the third worthy of the unhappiness. (Sometimes before the unhappiness is yet actual.) )\nAbove the Rfl., between 46719-23, 4681-5, or in the left margin, the following s-additions also stand on M 292':\n(s 1. Soul as thinking being: a. Belonging to the sensible world. b. To the intelligible. 2. As dignity.)\n(s *Existentia corporis contra idealismum*.)\n(s NB. I am substance, I am simple etc. etc. The I *in sensu stricto* contains all this.)\n(s I as object of inner sense, as thinking, am not object of outer. Thoughts are not *phaenomena* in space.)\n(s *bruta* without inner sense, consciousness: *Analoga* of Me.\n*spiritus* – – outer – –\nmere animality, mere intelligence. Humanity (*a priori*).)",["“soul”, “mind”, “spirit” render Seele, Gemüth, Geist; “power over oneself” renders Gewalt über sich selbst, “powers” Kräfte.","“As dignity” renders “Als würde”, lower-case in the source; it can also be read as a broken-off “As if [it] would”.","Latin: *bruta, incolae planetarum, angeli* “brutes, inhabitants of the planets, angels”; *Anima est …*: “The soul is a thinking subject depending on the body (sentient). The *animus* active indeed, but bound to the conditions of the body. The *mens* active in and through itself.”"]],"reflm:4231":["In theoretical philosophy the objects are either objects *rationis purae* (s *et etiam principia intellectualia*): *philosophia transscendentalis*, or objects *sensuum* (s *sed principia* (g *vel*) *intellectualia vel* [*sens*] *empirica*): Nature. *physiologia*.\n*Physiologia vel sensus interni*. *Psychologia*,\n*externi*. *Physica*;\n*vtraque vel e principiis rationalibus vel empiricis*.",["*rationis purae* (s *et etiam principia intellectualia*): “of pure reason (and also intellectual principles)”; *sensuum* (s *sed principia vel intellectualia vel empirica*): “of the senses (but principles either intellectual or empirical)”.","*Physiologia vel sensus interni … externi … vtraque vel e principiis rationalibus vel empiricis*: “Physiology either of inner sense: psychology, of outer: physics; each either from rational or from empirical principles”."]],"reflm:4232":["objects of pure reason and empirical principles according to analogy, i.e. the law of a certain philosophical proportion, [or] from the given sensible world into the antecedents to the terminus *a priori* (the principle): god, or into the consequents to the terminus *a posteriori* (the end): the World.\n(g 1. Where the intellectual is the first *principium*,\n2. Where it is the last consequence.)",["The main text is Latin (obiecta rationis purae et principia empirica secundum analogiam …); the addition is German.","*terminus* (ad terminum) can mean “term” or “limit”; “end” renders finem.","“consequence” renders Folge."]],"reflm:4233":["Opposite 14116-18:\nFrom immateriality it flows that soul and body do not act on one another through touchings, indeed by that means cannot even be present to one another; that their community with matter must rest on that in matter which is no object of appearance etc. etc.",["“their community” renders Gemeinschaft derselben, which can refer to soul and body or to the soul alone."]],"reflm:4234":["Opposite 14119-21:\nProperly all proof of the simple nature of the soul comes down to this, that it is an immediate intuition of itself through the absolute unity I, which is the *singularis* of the actions of thinking! For the proof which has been taken from this, that many thinking subjects can make up only one thought, signifies just as |\nmuch as: the I is required for it and thinking is at all times an action *in singulari*.",["“of itself” renders seiner selbst (masculine or neuter, not feminine like Seele); “which” renders welcher, whose antecedent is not certain.","*singularis*, *in singulari*: “the singular”, “in the singular” (the grammatical number)."]],"reflm:4235":["At the very top of the page:\nThe soul is not only simple, but also single in the human being. *anima sentiens, vegetativa, rationalis*.",["“single” renders einzeln; *anima sentiens, vegetativa, rationalis*: “sentient, vegetative, rational soul”."]],"reflm:4236":["The whole difficulty rests on how a body can bring forth thoughts in the soul and the latter through its will motions. Every efficacious subject alters in the other the inner state, and this outer relation is *influxus* or outer action, whose *phaenomenon* the breaks off.",["“breaks off” renders the editor's “bricht ab”: the note is unfinished.","*influxus*: “influence”; “efficacious” renders Wirksame."]],"reflm:4237":["No matter is living, consequently death, i.e. lifelessness, |\nwhich follows upon life, is no occurrence that can befall the human body. The human being has a twofold life: 1. the animal, 2. the spiritual. The latter is personality. not merely taken *potentialiter*, but *actualiter*. In the existence of the human soul there is to be considered 1. the *existentz* of the substance; 2. life in general (animal) as a soul; 3. personality, i.e. life as a human spirit. The question is therefore whether it is possible that the human soul should be a particular person even without a body (at the least it could not then become conscious of its outer state, because for that it is required that one sense oneself through the very same sense with which one perceives outer things). This question does not contain whether certain interruptions of its personality (as sleep) take place, but whether it can ever become conscious of itself without a body.",["\"existence\" renders Daseyn, *existentz* is kept as Kant's Latinate word beside it.","\"spirit\" and \"spiritual\" render Geist and geistig."]],"reflm:4238":["314. E II 1263.\nM 314':\nEither the human being has (g long ago, before he was born) represented something great before his birth, or after the (g his) death he will represent nothing at all. (g If his origin is owed to birth — —) Consider the contingency of births. |\nM 314: |\n(g If one wants to dispute their contingency, then all bad kings are Pharaohs, since God, if not bringing forth the wickedness, yet furthers it a little, in order to prove his power.) |\nM 314': |\nIf he has a spiritual part which thinks even after the complete decay of the body: why should he not have thought rationally before he put it on. If matter has had to help him to thinking, then it will [and] leave him thoughtless when it is separated from him. But how has this spirit been compelled to animate this matter? we do not know the spirit-world, and how matter stands under its command. I do not know how philosophers can be so squeamish about an incomprehensibility.",["\"represent\" renders vorstellen.","\"put it on\" renders anlegte; \"it\" is ihn, presumably the body.","\"be so squeamish about\" renders spröde thun mit."]],"reflm:4239":["315. E II 1306. 1268. 1264.\nM 315':\nIf from the nature of the grown human being one can infer his eternal duration, then the newborn human being too must give hope of just this. So also the embryo, the *ovulum*, the *ovulum* of the *ovulo*. This claim to eternity cannot depend on the contingent combination with the body; for that perfection which cannot arise without combination with bodily things also cannot continue without it. Thus human souls also had a spiritual life before the body; thus the animal life cannot decide about their eternal fate.\nThe continuing life of the soul is distinct from its immortality. The first signifies that it will not die, perhaps for the sake of particular divine arrangements; the second, that it cannot die naturally. The proofs of the first are moral, the second metaphysical. The enduring life of the soul consists not in the continuance of its substance or of the remaining powers, but of its personality. If it can no longer attain to consciousness, then it is as a tree which has died off, which can no longer put forth sap.\nThe physical of this life is of no significance, because it concerns only the contingent combination with the corporeal world, which is not our natural state; but the moral, which can be encountered only in the soul in accordance with its spiritual nature, hangs together with the spiritual life, and because the moral belongs to the inner worth of the person, it is inextinguishable, whereas fortune and misfortune, since it belongs merely to the fleeting state, loses all worth after its short duration*. Hence we must esteem this life little. But we would have cause to esteem it highly if the physical of it were the condition of the beginning of the metaphysical. The [life] physical of life accordingly remains a trifle; birth and death are beginning and end of a scene in which only *moralitaet* is of consequence, and indeed only so that [it] one does not act against it.\nM 315:\n*(g To grieve over pain, especially past pain: is soft or foolish; but over transgressions: decent and praiseworthy. Hence the latter belongs to our person, thus insofar as we are spiritual beings.)",["\"combination\" renders Verbindung.","\"whereas fortune and misfortune ... it belongs ... loses\": the singular verbs after a double subject are Kant's.","\"scene\" renders auftritt, which can also mean an appearance on stage."]],"reflm:4240":["316'. E II 1277. 1265.\nM 315':\nMatter is lifeless. This proposition does not merely attach to the concept of matter, but, even if the parts of it possessed life, then without a common *principium*, which united the many intermediate causes, no unity of life and a determination according to common choice would be possible. The *principium* of life in the human being, the proper *subiect* of his personality, insofar as it animates matter, is called soul, and the life of the human being is an animal life. But if this *principium* of life can live by itself alone, then this life would be a spiritual one. Since the lifeless must be no ground of life, but rather a hindrance to it, the pure spiritual life will be the original and self-sufficient life; the animal life, on the other hand, is derived and limited. Thus birth is not a beginning of life in general, but of the animal life, and a degree of lifelessness, and the complete spiritual life begins after the death of the animal. The other world will represent not other objects, but the very same objects seen otherwise (namely *intellectualiter*) and in other relations to us. And the cognition of things through the divine intuiting, likewise |\nthe feeling of blessedness through him is no longer the world, but heaven.\nBut how has the soul been combined with this body?, is just as much as if one asks: what is the beginning and the cause of sensibility. On this footing the present life is only the appearance and the image of the spiritual, and the vice which we practice here, |\nM 316': |\nwould, if our eyes were opened, present us in our bad character in the spiritual world, and the punishments here would be only weak images of those there; death is nothing other than the end of sensibility. But is\nvice not perhaps itself only an effect of sensibility and must finally cease with the end of all sensibility itself. Nothing can be imputed to a human being but what belongs to his character and to that which is peculiar to him. The vices which one practices before another do not always indicate a more vicious character; most are *delicta fortunae*. take away everything that is not in your power: how much can you ascribe to yourselves. Meanwhile in the state of sensibility the character can be vicious and therefore also punishable which in the intellectual is only [faulty] deficient, as always with a creature which wants to make applications from the part which it knows that fit the whole.",["\"choice\" renders Willkühr; \"intuiting\" renders Anschauen.","\"before another\" renders vor dem Andern, which can mean \"in front of another\" or \"more than another\".","\"die Augen geöfnet würden\" is impersonal in the German: \"if the eyes were opened to us\"."]],"reflm:4241":["The concepts of God (and of the universal subordination under the law of necessity), of freedom (of contingency and independence from the present world) and of the other world (the relation of our freedom and independent nature to God) are the three concepts of reason which have an inner importance, are *connex* among themselves and are the ground of the consequence of the whole of metaphysics.\nAll cognitions of reason have in the end a practical relation. The concepts of freedom are on both sides problems for reason; but we must, with regard to past actions, assume contingency, to present ones necessity, to future ones both at once, if we want to do anything.\n(s We have a natural instinct to direct our care, even without reflection, to that [fut] which will go on in the world after our death, just as the butterfly, before it dies, carefully stores and provides for its eggs. We become avaricious in old age and would like our goods to be well applied. We do not want people to think ill of us. We spare the deceased, just as if they could take harm from it. We miss them in society. One brings food to the dead. The miser would gladly have it (g that) his money stay together after death. Through this prolongation everything in the world hangs together. Funerals of crude nations.)\n(s Civilized human beings take leave of life as of society, just as if they supposed they would see such again one day. They shrink either from dying as *poltrons* or from remaining in memory as base people or also from offending those who remain behind and making them angry at them.)",["\"consequence\" renders Erheblichkeit, \"importance\" Wichtigkeit.","[zuk] is the start of a word Kant struck out, presumably zukünftige (\"future\").","\"angry at them\" renders böse auf sich, where sich refers to the ones dying."]],"reflm:4242":["The concept of God is a concept of completion, either of speculation with regard to things, or of *moralitaet*, and arises from the following basic concepts. 1. A necessary being. 2. The supreme cause. 3. The all-sufficient being (instead of the most perfect of all, because |\nthis does not on that account contain everything). The first concept is sufficient to itself. The second completes the *seriem subordinatorum*, the third *coordinatorum*. One can infer from one to the other. 1. The necessary being is the ground of everything, thus also the first cause. 2. The first cause is necessary, thus also the most perfect being. 3. The most perfect being is necessary and the first cause. The first concept ends all questions about existence.",["\"concept of completion\" renders Vollendungsbegrif.","\"existence\" renders Daseyn."]],"reflm:4243":["The idea of God is occasioned: 1. by a need of the understanding (*vide supra*); 2. By a need of the will. This arises from the lack of the supreme grounds from which to derive one's fates with regard to happiness and one's verdict with regard to morality. The former is the strongest incentive, and herein that of the averting of ills is the mightiest. One needs an object of worship from which, because powers do not suffice, wishes, entreaties and submissions can fetch our happiness. If from this ground of physical needs the concept of a godhead is generated, then it is at the beginning thought as terrible. Beside this a benign *principium* is commonly set.",["\"morality\" renders sittlichkeit; \"incentive\" renders triebfeder.","\"its verdict\" or \"one's verdict\": seinen Urtheilsspruch may be the verdict passed on one or by one."]],"reflm:4244":["On the necessary being, the original being, the supreme and first of all beings.\nEvery thing is in itself thoroughly determined, but not every concept is thoroughly determined. Thus in order to cognize a thing wholly it is not enough to cognize what it is, but also what it is not; for were this left out, then it is undetermined whether |\nbeyond that which is cognized in it, still more does not belong to it. Accordingly a thing's possibility is thoroughly determined only through the collective possibility of everything, and whoever wants to cognize something wholly must cognize everything. Now of all opposed *praedicaten* the negative are thinkable only through the *opposita realia* (what is cleared away there I can know only insofar as I know that which is to be canceled). Accordingly the possibility of all things is given through its being contained in the sum total of infinite *realitaet*, and through limitation of it. There is accordingly a universal *principium reale* of all possibilities, and the possibilities are only *positionen* and *limitationen* made *respective* to a certain concept in the *omnitudine realitatum*, which *limitationen*, since they are thought only *respective* and the *ens illimitatum* is herein laid at the ground *absolute*, so the *ens fundamentale* is not itself something merely possible, but what lies at the ground of everything possible; it is thus posited *absolute*, and in it all *respectus* are contained. Accordingly is [the] Actuality taken in general breaks off.\n(g The proposition: from nothing comes nothing, indicates that possibility cannot be thought simply, but presupposes something that is [which]. But that we can afterwards think every thing as merely possible comes from this, that, once we have a determinate concept, we do not ask: whence?, but, in regarding it as given, cancel the thing itself, but yet not everything in general.)\nI assert: the proof of a single original being from the descent of all possibilities is the one which directs all others in the human understanding and dwells in each. The old proposition: from nothing comes nothing, signifies nothing other than that the possibilities must lie in something and that things, without there being anything at all in connection with which they can be thought through determinations, cannot take place at all. Even in the works of human beings all possibilities are merely *modificationen* |\nof something that is, at least of our concepts given through something or other; and with every possibility we represent to ourselves the agreements with everything else, consequently a certain unity of the thinkable.",["\"bricht ab\" (\"breaks off\") is the editor's note that the sentence breaks off unfinished.","\"know\" renders both kennen and weiß (wissen) in the parenthesis.","\"canceled\" and \"cancel\" render aufheben; \"sum total\" renders Inbegrif."]],"reflm:4245":["All things seem to lie in an infinity; for they can be undetermined with regard to nothing possible, and, since *qvoad praedicata affirmantia* they are a part of this infinite, but *qvoad negantia* are limited: so (g they) are necessarily in a thoroughgoing relation, and the complete representation of each thing must express this determinately or indeterminately. Even with a number, e.g. 8, everything else is excluded from the infinite multitude, notwithstanding that properly in 10 no other possibilities or *realitaeten* are thought in addition, but only the same action is repeated.\nAll finite things are thus, according to their possibility, dependent on a being of all beings, which contains all *realitaet* and is independent of all others; for in their thoroughgoing determination they yet always relate to the highest *realitaet*, which is the [ground] original ground of their possibility.",["\"original ground\" renders Urgrund, \"ground\" the struck-out Grund.","\"thoroughgoing\" renders durchgängig."]],"reflm:4246":["Possibility belongs to things necessarily, thus the possibilities of things have no further ground; but nevertheless a being is necessary through which possibilities are given. In relation to the same every possibility is only a restriction of the highest reality, and thoroughgoing possibility is properly the relation to the highest reality in its determination. The original being itself cannot be regarded as possible, but rather it is the *principium* of possibility. Even the possibility of all fictitious beings rests merely on limitations. The plurality of things rests on the manifoldness of the limitations, and that which they have that is different are limitations, |\nthus relations to an original being. Accordingly there are not many original beings. The necessity in the existence of this being needs no particular proof. For that it is the ground of all possibility and that this is only a derivation from it already signifies as much as: nothing is possible without a *realissimum*; consequently this itself does not belong among the merely possible beings. For otherwise, if one wants to make the possibilities by way of composition, then a being in which there are many realities is not more possible than that in which there are few, and in the most real of all, as the most composite, is the greatest contingency.",["'restriction' renders Beschränkung; 'limitation' renders both Einschränkung and Schranke.","Urwesen = original being; durchgängige Möglichkeit = thoroughgoing possibility."]],"reflm:4247":["Possibility consists precisely in the actuality of the reality wherein through restrictions all things are given; e.g. a triangle is possible through the actuality of space, and [it is] its possibility consists in this, that through restrictions it lies in this space. So possibility is thus a derived concept, not from the actuality of this thing, but from [any] the actuality which contains the first *data* for things in general. In all restricted things possibility (its concept) precedes actuality, because the given reality for things precedes the restriction of it. But not in the case of the original being.\n*conceptus terminator*.",["Wirklichkeit = actuality; realitaet = reality; Beschränkung = restriction."]],"reflm:4248":["The highest reality cannot be limited, because otherwise [it] the ground of the possibility of all things would cease. Just as space does not properly allow itself to be limited, but rather the things in space.\nWe thus cognize the original being and its necessity not from itself, i.e. from the concepts which we have in any way of the inner predicates of a being, in which existence were included, but relatively from the relation of the same to our concepts of the possibility of all things; it is a boundary concept *a priori*. We assume it without contradiction, we need it for the completion of our reason, we can make no grounded objections against it.",["Grentzbegrif = boundary concept; 'relatively' renders relativisch.","'it is a boundary concept' renders er ist ein Grentzbegrif: the masculine er does not agree with the neuter Urwesen."]],"reflm:4249":["The diversity of things rests, according to our concepts, on the limitations of given realities. But it cannot be regarded as the limitations of different things, because this diversity again presupposes limitations; consequently it arises from the limitation of the thing or of its sequels, [through in] whose concept contains all reality. For the realities must be given, because without this one could not think them. The limitation is either of abstraction or of determination.\nThe inferences from this are given from the possibility of cognitions through our reason, not from the things, consequently the *argument ad homines* is valid.",["'For' reads the source's Den as Denn.","The relative clause 'whose concept contains all reality' is as unsettled as the German after the deleted [durch in]."]],"reflm:4250":["This proof from mere concepts is not complete without confirmation through the empirical proof that the principles of reason agree with experience, although they are not borrowed from it. Conversely an empirical argument without this proof of reason is not sufficient for a determinate concept of the original being.",["'proof of reason' renders Vernunftbeweis; 'sufficient' renders hinreichend."]],"reflm:4251":["If an object interests us in acting, then [that constitutio] that judgment about it which we would pass on it according to the natural laws of our reason, [whether] although the *conditiones* of our judgment contain [nothing] no *data* of it, is nevertheless practically certain (e.g. of God and the other world) (g i.e. according to rules of prudence and morality).\n(Morally certain is a mere probability, if it concerns an obligation.)",["'object' renders the Latinate obiect.","The deleted [dieienige Beschaffenhei] breaks off within the word Beschaffenheit (constitution).","Klugheit = prudence; Sittlichkeit = morality; Schuldigkeit = obligation."]],"reflm:4252":["The limited is regarded as contingent because the opposed reality is necessarily presupposed in all possible limitation.",[]],"reflm:4253":["a. The proof: 1. that all possibility must be regarded as a determination of the highest reality and presupposes such a being, is grounded on this, that the *omnimoda determinatio* is possible only through the *omnitudinem* [of the prae] *metaphysicam*, but in this the negative predicates presuppose the *realia*; and since every *ens limitatum* is possible only through presupposition of a *realis*, everything presupposes a *realissimum*; but this is the necessary subordination of concepts according to the law of the human understanding.\n2. The absolute necessity of a thing is a boundary concept. It cannot be discerned through contradiction. That which contains the matter for all possibility is the real ground of it and [its opposite] *realiter* necessary.\nIn itself the necessity of a perfect being is not to be discerned better than of an imperfect one.\nb. The proof *a contingentia* [presupp] infers from concepts: namely that the limited cannot be necessary. Consequently it must |\npresuppose the necessity in the *realissimo*. The cause is this, because the universal concept of a being which has limitations comprehends many kinds of things under it; but the unlimited is *conceptus singularis*./\nc. The physico-theological infers from the incomplete knowledge of the perfection of the world to the highest of God and from there back to the greatest perfection of the world.\nd. The moral from the geometrical, but objective necessity of actions under the moral laws, but also to the necessary assumption of a governor.\nTo [incompl] negative proofs, namely that the world is not from itself, belongs the [that] from the *primo motore*.\nThat the *ens realissimum* or *summum* has understanding.",["'discerned' renders eingesehen (einsehen); 'real ground' renders realgrund.","'moral laws' renders den sittlichen Gesetzen; 'The moral' renders Der moralische.","[presupp], [incompl]: deletions broken off in the source (setzt vo, unvollst)."]],"reflm:4254":["327. E II 1654. 1157.\nM 327':\nThe existence of God can be cognized only through the understanding, because his concept is a highest concept of the understanding, which [no relation to] is not restricted to objects of experience, but whose *omnisufficientia* is intellectual.\nBut it cannot be cognized from an assumed arbitrary concept, and thus not in itself (unless through immediate intuitions).\nThus only in relation to the world, but that which is valid for every world; for this is only intellectual.\nThe intelligible world is: |\nM 327: |\nthat whose concept holds for every world; consequently it contains not physical laws, but objective and moral ones.\nM 327':\nThe intellectual concept of the world is thus the concept of perfection. The world of understanding is thus the moral one, and its laws hold for every world as objective laws of perfection.\nThus one can only infer from the necessity of the moral laws in this world as in every one to the original and universally valid ground of the essential ends of things, hence to the existence of the most perfect being, and his concept is that which makes us perfect, and thus practically certain. *Mundus intelligibilis* is the world of rational beings, considered according to objective laws of freedom.\nThe supreme *principium* of morality is the agreement of free actions with the original and universally valid laws of choice or with that |\nM 327: |\nwhich is essentially connected with the ends in the world. Thus the concept of God is breaks off?",["'his concept' renders sein Begrif, which could also refer to Daseyn (existence).","Verstandesbegrif = concept of the understanding; Verstandeswelt = world of understanding; Willkühr = choice.","'breaks off?' renders bricht ab?"]],"reflm:4255":["The proposition of the existence of God is a *hypothesis originaria* (g *tam naturaliter qvam obiective*) both of the comprehensibility, the possibility and actuality of things through the understanding (*theoretica*) and also as a *principium* of actions (*practica*). The proof proceeds through a *dilemma theoreticum et practicum*.",["Begreiflichkeit = comprehensibility."]],"reflm:4256":["M 327':\nIf I deny the existence of God, then I must regard myself either as a fool, if I want to be (or am) an honest man, or as a villain, if I want to be a prudent man. There are proofs *per deductionem contrarii ad absurditatem* or *turpitudinem*. Moral faith presupposes that the necessitating breaks off.\nM 327:\n(s If one denies God, then the virtuous one is a fool and the prudent man a rogue. Prudence and morality can be combined in the practical only if I assume the existence of God.\nWithout this proposition the [law] moral laws of the *mundi intelligibilis*, which are yet universally commanding, would not hold for the present one, and that which is universally true would be false in the particular case.)\nM 327':\n(g There are proofs *per deductionem contrarii ad absurdum logicum* and also such *per deductionem ad absurdum morale*: that he who does not [den] assume it is a [villain] (g fool, fantast,) deceiver; both are\n|\nM 327: |\nnot genetic, but proofs of the necessity of assuming something.)",["Glaube = faith; 'the necessitating' renders die nöthigende, which breaks off before its noun.","[law], [den]: deletions broken off in the source (Geset, läugn).","Bösewicht = villain; Schelm = rogue; Phantast = fantast; Betrüger = deceiver."]],"reflm:4257":["All *negationes* are limitations; (g for they can only be thought through the *oppositum reale*, just as every line, figure and body is thought only through limitation of space. But why not the numbers. Because the reality for them is regarded not as given, but as arbitrarily invented.) everything limited is thought as circumscribed in the greater, hence everything limited in another. That which is distinguished from everything limited is unlimited; everything limited thus in the infinite, which is therefore *realiter* infinite (without end) and all-sufficient.",["'limitations' renders Schranken; 'limitation' renders einschränkung; 'circumscribed' renders umschränkt.","allgnugsam = all-sufficient."]],"reflm:4258":["Because [we] that [unli] which we can restrict does not have to be necessary in itself, the infinity which as it were contains the |\nmatter for all possibility is itself given through a higher ground. The *ens summum* is thus *realissimum* as a ground.",["[unli]: a deletion broken off in the source (unbe), presumably of unbeschränkt."]],"reflm:4259":["The possibilities seem in general to signify nothing other than the relation through the limitations in the given infinite as the matter.",[]],"reflm:4260":["Because all diversity of things is only a manifoldness of limitations, on account of the relation which each possible has to all reality, the concept of a highest reality, which is single, lies at the ground of everything.",["'single' renders einig, which can also mean 'unified' or 'at one'."]],"reflm:4261":["One can want to prove the existence of God either as a necessary *hypothesin* for the cognition of the existence of other things or as a *dogma a priori*. The latter is impossible.",[]],"reflm:4262":["The cause why we represent to ourselves the (g necessary) existence of a [necessary] most real being as more comprehensible than the necessity of a limited one comes from this, that we can think only the limited things and their negative determinations by limiting the more real concept. Accordingly a greater possibility cannot be composed from smaller ones. And the universal possibility is no aggregate of all particular possibilities, but rather these are sequels of the former. The limited thing is that which has some of |\nall possible reality and is thereby distinguished from others. But something distinguishing from everything possible is only thinkable through everything. Duration and the outer relation (g or figure and magnitude) can only be cognized through restriction of infinite time and of infinite space. e.g. triangle.",["'limiting' renders the Latinate limitiren; 'restriction' renders Beschränkung.","'Einiges aber unterscheidende von allem moglichen' is rendered as it stands; the construction is unsettled."]],"reflm:4263":["In order that an *ens realissimum* be possible, it is not enough that the realities do not contradict one another, but that among all possible subjects there be one which unites them all as a common ground.",["'subjects' renders the Latinate subiecten."]],"reflm:4264":["All predicates of God concern his existence or his properties. The first are possibility, *omnitudo*, necessity, eternity, independence (*relat:*), all-sufficiency, omnipresence etc etc. The second: his omnipotence, understanding, will; the all-powerful, living God. To the understanding belongs: 1. that he is the *prototypon*, that he is independent; 2. that he goes to everything originally.",["Eigenschaften = properties; Allgnugsamkeit = all-sufficiency.","In 1. and 2. er may refer to God or to der Verstand (the understanding)."]],"reflm:4265":["If from mere concepts an existence is to be inferred. then the necessity of existence must be contained in the mere possibility.",[]],"reflm:4266":[") M 332'. E II 1664.\nIf from the properties of a thing its necessary existence is to be inferred, then either existence itself must be considered as |\na property, or the concept of absolute necessity as one that is one and the same with the given properties.",["einerley = one and the same."]],"reflm:4267":["The physicotheological concepts of God have only an idea, related to us, of the magnitude of God, which is indeed practically but not theoretically sufficient. God is wise, but we compare him only through our understanding with ours. God is great: signifies just as much as: how small we are! The absolute concept of d breaks off.",["Größe = magnitude; it also means 'greatness'.","'d' stands so in the source before bricht ab (breaks off)."]],"reflm:4268":["(g we are to infer from the concept of God to the necessity of another world, thus he must not be inferred from this one, because it does not entirely agree with him.)\nThe physicotheological proof gives only an opinion of the existence of God, namely of the existence of an intelligent author a certainty, and then a faith must yet be added in order to represent him to oneself as highest perfect. One infers from experience not to a most perfect world (rather one assumes it to be very imperfect, in order to prove the future world), but only to an intelligent one; of him one believes that whoever can do so much will be able to do everything.\nThe transcendental proof also can only be conducted *respective* to our *hypothesin subiectivam* of reason. why should not the practical one be able to be conducted from the subjective *hypothesi* of the moral laws, because otherwise *leges morales* would be *effectu vacuae* and the virtuous one a fantast, i.e. would not harmonize with this world.",["'intelligent' renders verständig (having understanding); Urheber = author.","'moral laws' renders sittlichen Gesetze; Phantast = fantast."]],"reflm:4269":["Eternity (infinite duration) as a *phaenomenon* is infinite time. [existence in] Time is the measure of the duration |\nof finite things, insofar as they appear. Existence in time is contingency in appearance [for non-being and its]. The existence of that which is eternal is not infinitely limited by non-being. It is like infinite time itself; but contingent existence is in the following point of time cut short by the previous one and [merely se] changes its place in time more than that it enlarges the duration in it. (s than only in foresight. That which one has lived has not enlarged our existence, except only through the present consequences.) It is always the mere boundary of time, which is limited by infinite *negation* of the past and future.",["Daseyn = existence; Zufelligkeit = contingency; Folgen rendered consequences.","um den vorigen abgekürtzt: cut short by the previous one, presumably the previous point of time; the German leaves the referent open.","[blos se]: deleted, the second word broken off."]],"reflm:4270":["God determines the cognition of each thing from (g and in) the cognition of the whole; thus it is intuiting and an idea. We collect it from partial representations; [there] the whole goes *originarie*, as regards possibility, before the part.",["Anschauend: intuiting (a participle), not anschaulich.","theilvorstellungen: partial representations, literally part-representations."]],"reflm:4271":["The world sensibly represented has no first, i.e. no beginning, but it does through reason; i.e. no first of time, but indeed a first of cause. The two have nothing in common with each other.\nLikewise the world, as regards magnitude sensibly represented, has no boundaries through empty space, but indeed limitations of reality.",["Grentzen = boundaries; schranken = limitations; realitaet = reality."]],"reflm:4272":["To *auctoribus coordinatis* a *beneficium divisum* is imputed; to *auctoribus plene subordinatis* the same is imputed *pro indiviso* to the *auctori primario*. Hence I cannot impute my happiness to the creature and to God in different parts, but I can directly ascribe it to nature, to my own industry, and thus to divine providence by means of the order of nature. It is also irreverent to regard God as a mere *causam concurrentem*; he is *solitaria*, but [per remot] *superior*.",["auctores coordinati / plene subordinati: coordinate / fully subordinate authors; beneficium divisum: a divided benefit; pro indiviso: undivided; auctor primarius: primary author.","imputiren rendered impute, zurechnen ascribe.","causa concurrens: concurring cause; solitaria agrees with causa (sole cause); [per remot]: deleted, broken off."]],"reflm:4273":["In every judgment one represents an analytic unity. Among the parts of a whole a synthetic unity. But this analytic unity is not always cognized *per analysin*.\nWhere this unity is not conceived in the identity, there not a judgment but a comparison of two judgments through a common mark is made. e.g. The human being is[vernunf] an animal, the horse is an animal.",["[vernunf]: deleted fragment, presumably of vernünftig (rational); left in German.","Merkmal = mark; concipirt = conceived."]],"reflm:4274":["Disjunctive propositions are comparison of two propositions with one subject and two opposed predicates. If [also] the subjects are different, while the predicates are opposed, then they are propositions of distinction. Propositions of comparison have two kinds of subject and one predicate.",["Unterscheidungssätze: propositions of distinction; Vergleichungssetze: propositions of comparison.","zweyerley Subiekt: two kinds of subject, i.e. a twofold subject."]],"reflm:4275":["Analysis of reason. *principium contradictionis, identitatis*; gives objectively valid propositions.\nSynthesis of reason: various laws (*axiomata subreptitia*), subjectively valid propositions.\nThe conditions of our reason, which 1. cognizes objects only mediately and not through intuition, thus the *conditiones* by means of |\nwhich a cognition of anything is possible for it, and the necessity of positing something *primitive* and without *conditiones*.\nIntuitions of the senses (g as regards sensible form and matter) give synthetic propositions which are objective. Crusius explains the real principles of reason [vor] according to the *systemate praeformationis* (g from subjective *principiis*), Locke according to the *influxu physico* like Aristotle, Plato and Malebranche from the *intuitu intellectuali*, we according to *epigenesis* from the use of the natural laws of reason.\n(g The common human understanding, *sensus veri et falsi*, is *Qvalitas occulta*.)\nAntithesis: A method of reason for discovering the *oppositionem* of the subjective laws, which, if it is taken for objective *per vitium subreptionis*, is *scepticismus* (*in sensu obiectivo*); but if it is only a [critique of the subject] propaedeutic, then it is *methodus sceptica* for the determination of the subjective laws of reason. *Antithesis subiectiva*.",["deren ihr ... moglich ist: ihr refers back to reason (die Vernunft).","wie Aristoteles may attach to Locke (as Aristotle does) or begin a new member with Plato and Malebranche; the punctuation is kept ambiguous.","[vor]: deleted; vor may be the preposition before or Kant's spelling of für; left in German."]],"reflm:4276":["Pure sensibility. Pure reason. Mixed.\n*ubi, quando*.\n*transscendentalis philosophia*.\nAesthetic is the philosophy of sensibility, either of cognition or of feeling.\n*Logica vel generalis vel [tran] speciatim transscendentalis*.\nCategories are the universal actions of reason through which we think an object in general (to the representations, appearances). Aristotle.\nPredicaments are *modi* of the categories.\n3. Categories. Thesis, synthesis,\nhypothesis.*\n*coordinatio subordinatio*\n(g both in mere categorical judgments and in all three kinds of judgments)\n*Praedicamenta*.\nThesis: *Possibile, actuale, necessarium cum oppositis*.\n—\n—\nsynthesis: *Qvantitas*.\n10 categories of Aristotle. Pythagoras. 1. *Substantia, Accidens*, 2. *qvalitas*, 3. *qvantitas*, 4. *relatio*, 5. *actio*, 6. *passio*, 7. *qvando*, 8. *ubi*, 9. *situs*, 10. *habitus*.\n*postpraedicamenta*: *oppositum, prius, simul, motus, habere*.\n*(g The actions of the understanding are either in regard to concepts, wherever they may be given from, in relation to one another through the understanding, even if the concepts and the ground of their comparison is given through senses;\nor in regard to things, where the [Gegen] understanding thinks to itself an object in general and the way of positing something in general and its relations. The two are distinguished only in this, that in the first the representations, in the others through the representations the things are posited.)",["Logica vel generalis vel speciatim transscendentalis: logic either general or specifically transcendental; [tran] deleted.","Gegenstand = object; Sachen = things; [Gegen]: deleted fragment of Gegenstand, left in German.","handlungen = actions; Verhaltnis = relation."]],"reflm:4277":["In this world I can become happy only according to the laws of sensibility, and accordingly I depend on the mechanical laws and on the [thieris] sensible choice of human beings. (The latter could be in agreement, and then I would also be happy according to merit.)",["Willkühr = choice; [thieris]: deleted fragment, presumably of thierisch (animal); left in German."]],"reflm:4278":["Reason is bound to the law of thinking everything mediately: *regrediendo*, and yet in the *progressu* it must think something *originarie*.",["adstringirt: bound, from Latin adstringere."]],"reflm:4279":["All propositions predicate *per thesin* (g actual, possible, necessary) or *per synthesin* or *analysin*.\nThetic propositions have no predicate and can be cognized only in intuitions. or express the immediate [Vorste] intuition of a thing.",["[Vorste]: deleted fragment, presumably of Vorstellung (representation); left in German.","The lower-case oder after the full stop is kept as in the source."]],"reflm:4280":["All particular propositions are synthetic, but not all universal ones are therefore analytic.\nIf a particular proposition is analytic, then the particularity is an empty limitation.",[]],"reflm:4281":["Limitation is distinguished from restriction in this, that the former concerns merely the universality, the latter the necessity of the condition. Reason can limit only through *restrictiones*.",[]],"reflm:4282":["On *synthesis*; that in metaphysics there are no (g free) hypotheses.\nOn the method, one must no longer doubt that which one has once had to concede. In metaphysics one must certainly doubt when difficulties show themselves. The spirit of the whole is distinguished from the spirit of trifles. subtle and great.",["Geist = spirit; subtil und Groß stands unattached in the source."]],"reflm:4283":["Of the principle to ask: 1. Is it synthetic or analytic. Now if it is the first:\n2. Is it borrowed from universal experience or *a priori*.\n3. Is it a principle of sensibility or of reason?\n4. Is it a ground of the possibility of appearances or of real concepts, *principium constitutivum*?\n5. If it is the latter: is it a *principium subiectivum* or *obiectivum*?\n6. If it is the first, then the question is whether a *principium convenientiae* or a *principium concipiendi per se*.",["Von dem Grundsatz fragen: an infinitive heading, literally of (about) the principle to ask.","Grundsatz = principle; principium convenientiae: principle of agreement (fittingness)."]],"reflm:4284":["Metaphysics is not science, not erudition, but merely the understanding that knows itself; consequently it is merely a correction of the healthy understanding and reason.\nErudition and wide reading are a means of making its doctrines practical through examples. [Die Wi andern Wi] It serves other sciences for the demarcation of boundaries and holds the human being to his vocation as regards the use and the limitations of his reason; it is logical self-cognition. It is disconcertingly bitter, because it strikes down vain pride and takes away imagined knowledge; it makes our possessions more secure, but to the detriment of the imagined ones, and prevents books from swelling up.\nThe greatest gain from it is made by religion; this is through it secured in everything in religion that is moral, protected against enthusiasm and unbelief, freed from dependence in regard to scholastic subtlety. It brings it about that our actions can flow from the\n|\nsources of the healthy understanding, without having to consult the uncertain and always changeable scholastic broodings.",["Gesunder Verstand: healthy understanding (common sense); Bestimmung = vocation; Selberkenntnis = self-cognition; Wissen = knowledge.","[Die Wi andern Wi]: deleted fragments, presumably of Wissenschaft(en); left in German.","Sie in the second paragraph may refer to metaphysics or to Gelehrsamkeit; the German is kept as open."]],"reflm:4285":["Object is the name given only to what has a logical quality. e.g. *subiectum* not relatively to other concepts, but in itself. These objects are distinguished from appearances, which can indeed be brought into logical relations, [sonde] but to which this logical quality does not belong absolutely. If parallel to an appearance a logical position, which pertains to the *data* of appearance and not to the relation of one representation to another, then this is objective. e.g. ground, cause.\nOnly thereby can we conceive appearances as corresponding to the things, that they are grounds of a possible and universally valid cognition. But they can be this if they are in accordance with the logical form.",["Obiekt and Gegenstande are both rendered object; Obiekt is the Latinate member.","The conditional clause beginning If parallel lacks a verb in the source; left incomplete.","[sonde]: deleted fragment of sondern (but); left in German."]],"reflm:4286":["1. What an object is?\n2. In what the relation of a determination of the soul to something else consists?\n3. What the ground of the agreement is?\n4. By what we distinguish what relates to the object and what to the subject?",["Beziehung rendered relation; einstimmung = agreement."]],"reflm:4287":["Logical is what consists in comparison; real, what is posited in itself.",[]],"reflm:4288":["Possibility, actuality and, from both, necessity are not concepts of objects nor predicates, but positions of the object.\n1. Of the faculty of invention. The object of invention is possible. The invention that cancels itself is no invention. To every inventing it belongs that something be given. 2. The object of the sense is actual. 3. The positing through reason is necessary. Hence the possibility of the sensible is thought in space and time. Numbers are the purest inventions, and their possibility is thus possible entirely without *data* through understanding, but not the possibility of qualities, except only through composing and cancelling.",["Dichtungskraft, Erdichtung, Dichten: faculty of invention, invention, inventing (also: fiction, fabrication).","stellungen rendered positions; Setzung rendered positing.","aufheben rendered cancel."]],"reflm:4289":["On the method of appealing, regarding the certainty of the metaphysical axioms, 1, to the constant presupposition in all empirical cases and of inferring from this to their abstract universality.\n2. Because with just the same right as one would deny them, one would have to let the certainty of all the rest of cognition vanish.",["schließen rendered infer."]],"reflm:4290":["Whether one cannot invent something through reason according to analogy. *Theologia Naturalis*, because all realities do not contradict one another.\nThe question is why magnitudes are the only thing of which the |\nhuman understanding makes concepts out of itself, whose possibilities it discerns by itself, and in whose relations it can arrive, through the principle of contradiction, at certainty and at infinite expansion.",["weil sich alle realitaeten nicht wiedersprechen: either no realities contradict one another, or not all fail to; the English keeps the order.","dichten = invent; einsieht = discerns (has insight into); Satz des Wiederspruchs = principle of contradiction."]],"reflm:4291":["Metaphysics is not the mother of religion, but its bulwark (g against false sophistry). It proves the existence of God not *apodictice*, but *suppletorie*, in that it replaces through reason the lack of morality in the case of unbelief.",["apodictice: apodictically; suppletorie: by way of supplement."]],"reflm:4292":["The *conditiones* without which objects cannot be given are objective, although according to laws of sensibility. The *conditiones* without which they (even if they have been given) cannot be cognized (understood) are objective. Those without which they cannot be discerned (cognized through reason)*, are merely subjective; but these subjective *conditiones* are objective in regard to the use of reason in experiences [cond] (*leges convenientiae*).\n*(s possibility, necessity)",["eingesehen werden: be discerned (have insight into); verstanden: understood.","[cond]: deleted, broken off; leges convenientiae: laws of agreement (fittingness)."]],"reflm:4293":["Philosophy has this particularity, that the universal thoughts can be recorded first, whereas in mathematics last. Hence in meditation the universal project comes first.",["proiet: project, plan (French projet)."]],"reflm:4294":["All sciences and arts teach skill, philo|sophy\nalone the principles and rules of the use of all skill according to the ultimate relations of the understanding and of the will, in that it fixes the object itself and its relation to the human being.",["Grundsatze = principles; Beziehungen = relations; Verhaltnis = relation."]],"reflm:4295":["How the predicate lies in the subject belongs to metaphysics; how the [unt] subject stands under it, to logic.",["[unt]: deleted fragment, presumably of unter (under); left in German."]],"reflm:4296":["5. E II 1055.\nM 5':\nThe proposition: everything has a ground, is a law of reason; but the law: everything that happens is constantly accompanied by something else, is a |\nM 5: |\nlaw of appearances.",[]],"reflm:4297":["Above and \nExternal possibility is greater than internal.\nHypothetical possibility is smaller than the unconditioned (absolutely, in general, in every respect).\nWhat is possible in every respect is actual.",["Über und: the locus note breaks off in the transcription.","in aller Absicht: in every respect (Absicht, elsewhere aim)."]],"reflm:4298":["Possibility: the agreement (*non repugnantia*) with a rule, actuality: the position simply, necessity: the position according to a rule.\nThe first is thought without being given. The second given without its being thought. The third given by its being thought.",["schlechthin: simply, without qualification."]],"reflm:4299":["The relation (of an object) to perception (*perceptio*) is existence; to thinking: possibility; to thinking insofar as it determines existence: necessity.",["Daseyn = existence."]],"reflm:4300":["A and not-A are contradictorily opposed; A and B disparate. Between contradictory opposites there is no third (which is neither A nor not-A.).",["contradictorie opposita: contradictorily opposed; disparata: disparate."]],"reflm:4301":["*Realitas* is *materia cognitionis**, whether it be used affirmatively or negatively.\n*(g are *constitutiva* of things, negations only of concepts.)\n(s Does the concept of the simple designate reality or negation?)",["materia cognitionis: the matter of cognition; constitutiva: constituents (constitutive elements)."]],"reflm:4302":["Above and \nThe object of the concept is possible, hence not thoroughly determined through this concept, because it is universal. The object of intuition is actual; the object of the intuition which is the ground of the concepts of it in general is necessary.\n(g The intuition itself is not an object. Something can be a logical or a real object.)",["Über und: the locus note breaks off in the transcription.","durchgangig bestimt: thoroughly determined."]],"reflm:4303":["On. M § 54:\nThe *principium contradictionis* is the formal condition, but not *principium* of all possibility.",[]],"reflm:4304":["Since we can think nothing without concept, every object that we are to think must have a relation of agreement with concept in general, i.e., be possible.\nAn object, insofar as it is given, is thoroughly determined, but not insofar as it is thought.",["\"without concept\", \"concept in general\": Kant writes Begrif with no article (ohne Begrif, zum Begriffe überhaupt).","Einstimmung = agreement; durchgängig bestimt = thoroughly determined."]],"reflm:4305":["*Mutabilitas* presupposes the existence of things in time, hence also *immutabilitas*. Therefore, since absolute necessity is a concept of reason, the *ens necessarium* is *nec mutabile nec immutabile*. Hence *mutabilitas* or the *oppositum* belongs only to *sensualibus*.",["mutabilitas / immutabilitas = mutability / immutability; ens necessarium nec mutabile nec immutabile = the necessary being is neither mutable nor immutable.","sensualibus (dative): to sensible things. Vernunftbegriff = concept of reason."]],"reflm:4306":["The inference from mutability to contingency is *metabasis eis allo genos*, for I infer from a *sensitivum* to the *intellectuale*.",["metabasis eis allo genos: Greek, transliterated, \"transition into another genus\".","Schluß / schließe = inference / infer; Veränderlichkeit = mutability; Zufälligkeit = contingency."]],"reflm:4307":["In a stream without banks we can notice no alteration of our position nor of the position of the water itself.",["Veranderung = alteration; stelle = position (kept apart from Ort = place)."]],"reflm:4308":["First, the contingency of the state is not to be confused with the contingency of the thing. The thing remains.\nSecond, the mutability of the state not with the contingency of the state.\nThird, mutability is a proof of a sensible contingency of the state.",["\"thing\" renders Sache here (Ding elsewhere is also \"thing\").","The second sentence lacks its verb in the German; it is left elliptical."]],"reflm:4309":["In the upper margin \nThe whole play of alterations seems to stem from real oppositions. Since a reality drives the thing out of another state, no motion can be generated without producing just as much [in] opposed motion; thus there are two forces that bring about the continuous change of direction.",["realoppositionen = real oppositions; Veranderungen = alterations, Anderung (der Richtung) = change (of direction)."]],"reflm:4310":["Only one being is *metaphysice* perfect, all others have perfections, but are not perfect. Totality is lacking.\nOne does see, though, that realities are considered as grounds toward perfection and negations as defects, not merely as not-being, but defects in the totality.",["metaphysice = metaphysically.","\"totality\" renders both Allheit and the Latinate totalitaet.","Mängel = defects; nicht seyn = not-being."]],"reflm:4311":["What is opposed to totality is limitation.\nWhat is opposed to universality is restriction.",["totalitaet, limitation, restriction: Kant's Latinate loanwords; Allgemeinheit = universality."]],"reflm:4312":["A real whole: in which there is connection.*\nAn ideal whole: in which there is conjunction.\n*(g this, if it is possible through the addition of parts, is a composite.)",["Latin: totum reale / totum ideale; nexus = connection; coniunctio = conjunction; compositum = composite.","The asterisks are Kant's reference mark, not italics."]],"reflm:4313":["The existence of a thing, insofar as it is determined in time, is its state; the state is either of the coexisting or of the determinations following upon one another. An original being is in no state.",["Urwesen = original being; auf einander folgenden = following upon one another (successive).","\"of the coexisting\": the noun Bestimmungen stands only once in the German and governs both members."]],"reflm:4314":["The whole belongs to magnitude, namely to the *omnitudini*. The *compositum* belongs to relation and indeed to that of the *reciprocae coordinationis*. What is possible through the *coniunctionem (mutuam)*. A *qvantum* is in itself no *compositum*, but its concept can arise *per compositionem idealem*. Hence we speak of *compositis realibus*. The *compositum ex accidentibus* is collective, *ex substantiis* is aggregate.",["Kant's Latin case forms are kept: omnitudo = allness; reciproca coordinatio = reciprocal coordination; coniunctio mutua = mutual conjunction.","per compositionem idealem = through ideal composition; composita realia = real composites; ex accidentibus / ex substantiis = of accidents / of substances.","\"What is possible ...\" stands as a fragment in the German."]],"reflm:4315":["One can think of spaces only insofar as one cuts something out of the universal space. Space precedes things; |\nhence it can be no predicate of things, but only a law of sensibility, which as the condition of all possible appearances of course precedes all actual ones.",["allgemeinen Raum: \"universal\" or \"general\" space.","praedicat = predicate; Gesetz der Sinnlichkeit = law of sensibility."]],"reflm:4316":["The predicate of space and of place does not belong to substance, but to the sensible representation. Corporeal parts are comparative substances according to laws of sensibility and *phaenomena substantiata*. The quantity of substance in a body (not of its space) is to be judged from the magnitude of the effect at the same velocity. With regard to this, now, nothing is simple. Now the question is whether with regard to space there is something simple and a determinate assignable number.",["phaenomena substantiata = substantiated appearances.","angebliche: eighteenth-century sense \"assignable\" (angebbar), not \"alleged\".","\"this\" (derselben) may refer to the quantity of substance or to the body."]],"reflm:4317":["All axioms in which the subject has no content of sensibility and which nevertheless universally restrict it through the condition of sensibility are false. They are reduced when [to it] the condition of sensibility is given back to the subject.",["\"it\" (dasselbe) most naturally refers to the subject; the German leaves it open.","\"[to it]\" renders the deleted [ihm], which stands before dem subiect."]],"reflm:4318":["The all-concept of time is no universality.",["Allbegrif = all-concept; Allgemeinheit = universality."]],"reflm:4319":["Of infinite space and time. Time contains the ground of |\nthe limitations of the existence of things, space of the limitations of their presence, the highest being of the limitations of their reality. Hence all of this is infinite. Here the highest being, as it were as the matter for all things, is thought only sensibly.\nTime is what encompasses the existence of all things, wherein all things exist, whereby therefore to each its existence is determined relatively to others, when and how long.",["Schranken = limitations; Gegenwart = presence; befaßt = encompasses.","\"only sensibly\": nur could also attach to the whole clause (\"is only thought sensibly\")."]],"reflm:4320":["Whether space and time empty of sensation, likewise infinite space and time, are *phaenomena*. For *noumena* they are not.",["Empfindungsleere = empty of sensation."]],"reflm:4321":["Time is the condition of infinite apposition, space of infinite aggregation. The two cannot be united, because aggregation, i.e., totality, does not take place in the case of an apposition.\nHere the subjective conditions do not agree with one another.",["apposition / aggregation: Kant's Latinate loanwords."]],"reflm:4322":["75. E II 621.\nM 75':\nThe boundaries [find] (g goal, end) are negations which exclude the greater possible addition [end]. The limitations are negations which [the gre the] from the given greatest [d som the rest] exclude everything except some. Number is a multitude which has boundaries; a determinate magnitude in space and time arises from the boundaries of infinity. The former is always a whole number, the latter a fraction. In the former no diminutions are thought, the latter is possible only through them. The definitions arise analytically through the limitations of still greater concepts and are presented synthetically through the |\nboundaries of the composition of the marks. |\nM 75: |\nThe definition is like a geometrical figure which encloses a space through the number and magnitude of the sides and through the way of their composition. The space is the *definitum*. If the figure does not close, then the definition is *latior suo definito*.\nThe lack of enlargement is the boundary (is opposed to a possible greater).\nThe lack from diminution is the limitation (is opposed to an actual greater).\nThe all [the multitude (determinate)]. Some [Show] are, others not: indicates limitations.",["Grentzen = boundaries; Schranken = limitations; Menge = multitude; Hinzuthuung = addition.","The deleted fragments [die grö das] and [d eini das übrige] are broken words, rendered as far as they go.","latior suo definito = wider than its definitum. \"The former\" (Jenes, neuter) and \"the latter\" (diese, feminine) do not agree in gender with their evident antecedents."]],"reflm:4323":["All alteration is a succession of states; that of the inner state is an alteration of the thing, that of the outer an altered relation.",["Veränderung = alteration; Sache = thing."]],"reflm:4324":["Of the boundaries of a world and of its state according to laws of sensibility and reason.",[]],"reflm:4325":["The connection of a thing with that which is not its cause, consequently which is not a subordination, is at all times a connection of one part with the other into a whole. Accordingly the whole, which is itself no part, is connected with no thing as with its cause, which is thus outside it; also with no finite being, because this, |\nsince it is not grounded in itself, must in turn have the ground of its connection in another.",["Verknüpfung = connection.","\"which is not a subordination\": the relative die refers grammatically to the connection (Verknüpfung)."]],"reflm:4326":["Below and \nThe alterations make up not parts of the world, but of its state.",["The editorial placement note (Unter und) appears cut off in the source."]],"reflm:4327":["Beside the end of M § 358 and the beginning of M § 359:\nThat not merely *multitudo* is a world. That *finitudo* is a consequence of composition.",["multitudo = multitude; finitudo = finitude; folge = consequence (Folge)."]],"reflm:4328":["In the lower margin \nComposition proves contingency (g and dependence), for necessary beings cannot be composite.\nTotality proves dependence on One.\nComposition also proves *finitudinem*.",["finitudinem (accusative): finitude."]],"reflm:4329":["The world is finite, because it is a *totum* of beings that limit one another.",["einschränken = limit; totum = whole."]],"reflm:4330":["The sensible world is only a single one.\nEach *totum* is *contingens* with regard to the parts.",["sinnenwelt = sensible world (literally world of the senses); contingens = contingent."]],"reflm:4331":["In time, i.e., the sensible series or the series of the understanding, signifies a first, even if the series were infinite.",["The German sentence has no evident subject for bedeutet; \"ein Erstes\" may be its subject or its object. Left as it stands."]],"reflm:4332":["Infinity is a concept with regard to sensibility; it is indeed for the understanding, but not for reason. The world (g of the senses) is necessarily infinite, because the senses borrow no *terminos* from themselves, as those which consist precisely in this, that the mind is affected by others.",["vor = für (\"for\"), eighteenth-century usage; termini = terms, bounds.","keine ... als die may mean \"no terminos other than those which consist ...\" or \"none, since they consist ...\"; the ambiguity is kept.","Gemüth = mind; afficirt = affected."]],"reflm:4333":["The will of the human being is free, signifies as much as: reason has a faculty over the will and the other faculties and inclinations. For reason determines itself, and without this all other faculties are determined according to the law of efficient causes and are externally necessary. Reason cannot be determined, i.e., be affected; for then it would be sensibility and not reason.",["Vermögen = faculty; wirkenden Ursachen = efficient causes.","\"without this\" (ohne diese): may refer to reason or to its self-determination."]],"reflm:4334":["Of freedom.\nWe can consider an action of ours either as something |\nthat happens, i.e., as appearance, or as something that ought to happen, i.e., as an intuition of self-activity toward possible effects. In the first case it is [to] subject to the laws of the determining grounds; in the second case it is an intellectual one, in which the subject is not passive; here the *regula rationis determinantis* rules only as a *principium* [*cognosc*] *comprehensionis* or *logicum*, i.e., that one cannot cognize it *a priori* (except in the case of the perfect understanding) otherwise than through determining grounds. The good understanding has determining grounds, but according to laws of the understanding through intellectual *motiva*; the affected one gives itself over to sensibility.\nSomething happens necessarily, i.e., according to the empirical concept of necessity, which nevertheless admits a contingency according to concepts of reason. The understanding must itself excite sensibility, so that it may determine the action; thus it happens according to laws of sensibility and yet of the understanding. We also know only the nearest determining grounds of sensibility, and according to these the action must always be able to be explained.",["regula rationis determinantis = rule of the determining ground; principium comprehensionis = principle of comprehension; [cognosc] is a deleted, unfinished cognoscendi.","Selbstthatigkeit = self-activity; bestimmende Gründe = determining grounds; kenen = know (kennen).","\"so that it may determine the action\": sie refers grammatically to sensibility."]],"reflm:4335":["It is true: all morality must aim at something useful. But not the utility, but rather: the universality of it is what makes it morally good, namely that it is good as a rule alone and without it no universal rule would take place. Even in the case of duties of obligation the action, even if it were harmful, is not to be omitted, and in this precisely consists the obligation.",["Nutze = utility; Schuldigkeit = obligation (what is owed).","\"as a rule alone\": allein may limit \"as a rule\" or mean \"alone is good\"; \"it\" (sie) may refer to morality, the action or universality."]],"reflm:4336":["The actuality of freedom we cannot infer from experience. But we nevertheless have a concept of it only through our intellectual inner intuiting (not the inner sense) of our activity, which can be moved by *motiva intellectualia*, and through which |\npractical laws and rules of the good will are possible even with regard to ourselves. Thus freedom is a necessary practical presupposition. It also does not contradict theoretical reason. For as appearances [one may the] the actions are at all times in the field of experience, as objective *data* they are in the field of reason and are approved and disapproved. Sensibility is here under the laws of the understanding and yields breaks off.",["Wirklichkeit = actuality; Anschauen = intuiting; motiva intellectualia = intellectual motives.","\"breaks off\" (bricht ab) is most likely the editor's note that the text ends there, after weicht (\"yields\")."]],"reflm:4337":["Contingently discretionary actions (freedom of human beings) are those that are determined by no rules. Necessarily discretionary actions (divine freedom) are those that are determined only according to the rule of the good power of choice.",["beliebige = discretionary (done at one's pleasure); Wilkühr = power of choice."]],"reflm:4338":["Freedom from all external necessitation of our power of choice is clear through experience, likewise the moving force of the intellectual grounds of the good; we can on that account shift the blame onto no other beings. We can impute it to ourselves, even the good that God works in us. Thus morals and religion are *in salvo*.\nBut how do things stand with speculative philosophy concerning the possibility of this freedom? The proposition: everything that happens has a determining ground, i.e., something else through which it is necessitated, is the principle of the alterations of all passive substances (g of all appearances or of that which is given *a posteriori*; but the actions of giving something *a priori* are not comprehended under it.), [but] as of the body, also of the soul insofar as it is modified, i.e., in everything that is distinguished from the actions of freedom. To this extent this principle is objective. But as a *principium* of activities it cannot be objective, for there a first beginning must be possible; but in the states of a being as a passive one there is no first (This lies in |\nthe active). Freedom is supposed to be a faculty of first beginning a state. Passive states are nothing but consequences and belong necessarily to what precedes. I can say in the present moment: for me the whole series hitherto is as nothing. I now begin my state as I will. It is thus [only] the difficulty (g not) *secundum* [*rationes*] *possibilitatem fiendi*, but *cognoscendi*. One cannot have insight into the possibility [of a] of freedom, because one can have insight into no first beginning, neither necessity in existence in general, nor freedom in coming to be. For our understanding cognizes existence through experience, but reason has insight into it when it cognizes it *a priori*, i.e., through grounds (namely that which is not necessary according to identity, but which is posited *realiter*); now of the first there are no grounds, thus no insight through reason is possible either. That there must be a first (g *natura*) action, which lies at the ground of everything contingent, lies indeed in reason; but a first *tempore* is not to be comprehended at all, because time itself and what is in it does not depend on reason. Further: that what is determined through another being in its whole existence must terminate in itself the determining ground of its actions is not to be comprehended; but only on this account not, because it is not to be comprehended how it is a substance. Now this is no objection, but a subjective difficulty; for the I proves the endpoint of the grounds of the actions. I do this [or shows not] does not mean: another effects this; and even when I say: I suffer this, it nevertheless signifies the intuition [of my sel] of a subject, [which] which is for itself and suffers.",["leidend / leiden = passive / suffer (undergo); Nöthigung = necessitation; einsehen / Einsicht = have insight into / insight; begreifen = comprehend.","secundum possibilitatem fiendi / cognoscendi = according to the possibility of coming to be / of being cognized; natura / tempore = by nature / in time; realiter = really.","\"only on this account not, because\" (nur darum nicht, weil) keeps the German's construction; \"for itself\" renders vor sich selbst (für sich selbst)."]],"reflm:4339":["Applied philosophy is that whose object is represented through an empirical concept; the empirical concept, however, insofar as through analysis it is the ground of the cognition of the object, has no principles except rational ones.",["Latin throughout: ratio = ground; cognitio = cognition; principia rationalia = rational principles."]],"reflm:4340":["All philosophy (g theoretical) is either empirical (g physiology, psychology or physics) or rational (g transcendental philosophy). Rational either pure or applied.",["Latin throughout: philosophia theoretica, empirica, rationalis (pura vel applicata); Physiologia, Psychologia, Physica; Philosophia transscendentalis."]],"reflm:4341":["All theoretical philosophy is either pure or applied. The former: whose objects are not given except through concepts of the pure understanding; applied: to which objects are given through the senses. Applied is physiology, and this either empirical or rational, each either of inner or of outer sense.",["“the former” renders Prior; “understanding” renders intellectus."]],"reflm:4342":["1. (g transcendental:) That the *principium* of life cannot die. That life does not depend on matter, although the animal one does.\n2. Psychological: That the soul already now acts independently of the body.\n3. *theologia moralis*: Divine ends.\n4. Analogy.",["“although the animal one does” renders obzwar das thierische, with no verb in the German.","*theologia moralis*: “moral theology”."]],"reflm:4343":["The proof from analogy requires no experiences that we cannot have, and, in the absence of these, no arbitrarily assumed powers.\nIn the analogical proof we must seek out all cases where [the] an incentive is in us that is entirely distinct from the senses, and a power, indeed even a calling, to free oneself from them. Where life does not reward the effort or is too short for it, and where we are noble creatures and have insight into our own lowliness. Hence the human being has an ideal of perfection in himself that he does not reach.\nThis proof goes to our nature, hence to all; likewise, because it concerns the higher powers, to gradual natural expansion.",["“powers” renders Kräfte throughout; “incentive” renders triebfeder; “calling” renders Beruf.","“have insight into” renders einsehen."]],"reflm:4344":["According to the laws by which we with healthy reason have to infer the existence of certain things, their properties and laws in the world, an inference [can] must necessarily be made to the existence of a supreme, wise being, thus practically sufficient up to responsibility. But as regards the condition of the comprehensibility of this supreme cause, through which we want to [unab] prove the strength of our reason a priori, and thus according to logical rules of synthetic or dogmatic cognition, this use of reason is precarious. Now no human being can excuse himself that, because he has not been able to have insight into something a priori, he also has not assumed it, just as he [the beginning o] also does not have insight into the attraction of gravity a priori and yet assumes it in his actions.\nDeism is that which recognizes no other theology than the transcendental. God as a spiritual being (g intelligence) as distinguished from the eternal and necessary nature is called the living God.",["“healthy reason” renders gesunde Vernunft; “precarious” renders mislich; “have insight into” renders einsehen.","The deleted [unab] and [den Anfang d] (“the beginning o…”) break off in the source.","“recognizes” renders erkennt."]],"reflm:4345":["The human being has a faculty to ascend in cognitions up to the Godhead, signifies nothing more than: he has a faculty to complete his concepts and to bring forth an idea of the *maximum*.",["*maximum*: maximi, genitive, in the German."]],"reflm:4346":["The understanding of the original being is *intellectus originarius*; consequently [has e] is its understanding the archetype of things, and it is the cause of things only by means of the understanding, not through mere nature.",["“original being”: Urwesen; “archetype”: Urbild; *intellectus originarius*: “original understanding”.","“it is the cause” renders er ist, which can refer to the understanding (der Verstand) or to the original being.","The deleted [hat e] breaks off in the source."]],"reflm:4347":["Not concepts, but ideas (*archetypae*; ours: *ectypae*); i.e. all perfection we cognize only insofar as we represent it as in one being, which thereby becomes the supreme idea. In it everything that nature contains becomes a part of the perfection of the whole. The most perfect is only one. The multiplicities conceal from us the self-subsistent and original of the archetype. The abstract and universal representation arises in this way, from which we leave out much and retain only marks. For us everything is appearance. Platonism. The divine understanding contains every perfection in the archetype, ours in rules.\nThe idea is the unity of cognition, from which the manifold either of the cognition or of the object becomes possible. There the whole of the cognition [or the] precedes the part, the universal the particular; here the cognition precedes the possibility of the |\nthings, as e.g. with order and perfection. Everything intelligible is at the same time incomprehensible.\nThat the original being has understanding. Inferred from the all-sufficiency, because without this the possibility of every kind of relations, e.g. order [not], through which something in general can be an object of the understanding, would not be possible.\n* No discursive cognition. intuition. not sensation, not appearance.\nNo abstraction.\n*(g No sensibility. *materialiter*. none *formaliter*: confusion, obscurity.)\nOur idea is a discursive cognition. Hence it is only *idea informata*.\nThe intuitive is *archetypa*.\nOur cognition of the understanding is discursive. Thereby no immediate representations of objects are given, but such are only brought under rules, or that which gives occasion to a rule.",["*archetypae* / *ectypae*: “archetypal” / “ectypal”; “archetype” renders Urbild.","“appearance” renders both apparentz (“For us everything is apparentz”) and Erscheinung (“not Erscheinung”).","“things” after the line break renders Sachen, not Dinge; “intuition” is Kant's own word, not Anschauung."]],"reflm:4348":["Our understanding can cognize nothing concretely (it cognizes merely the way of sensibility to dispose according to rules) except that which is in it itself. *Now the good is that which necessarily relates to it itself or to the inner of analogous beings, thus it has cognitions a priori of it. But God cognizes objects in themselves a priori. Consequently they must be given through him, his understanding is thus *archetypus*.\n*(g of which it itself is the free author, the understanding can cognize a priori; for only the cause through freedom is so through understanding.)",["“the way of sensibility to dispose according to rules” renders die Art der sinnlichkeit, nach regeln zu disponiren, which can also be read “the way to dispose sensibility according to rules”.","“is so” renders ist es, whose reference is open.","The asterisks are Kant's reference marks."]],"reflm:4349":["Cognition is either sensitive or intellectual; the objects ei|ther\nsensible or intelligible. There [is for] can be given to us no other world than the sensible; thus every *mundus physicus* (g *materialiter*) is *sensibilis*; only the *mundus moralis* (g *formaliter*) is *intelligibilis*. For this reason, because freedom is the only thing that is given a priori and consists in this giving a priori; the rule of freedom a priori in a world in general constitutes the *formam mundi intelligibilis*. This leads according to [laws] grounds of freedom to the presumption of the *intelligibilium*: God and a future world, in which everything (g nature) will be in conformity with the moral laws.\nThe *mundus intelligibilis* as an object of intuition is a mere (g indeterminate) idea; but as an object of the practical relation of our intelligence to intelligences of the world in general and to God as the practical original being thereof, it is a true concept and determinate idea: *Civitas dei*.",["*mundus physicus … sensibilis*: “physical world … sensible”; *formam mundi intelligibilis*: “form of the intelligible world” (accusative); *intelligibilium*: “of the intelligibles”; *Civitas dei*: “city of God”.","“presumption” renders praesumtion.","“thereof” renders derselben, which can refer to the world or to the intelligences."]],"reflm:4350":["The opinion that we must regard all ills as punishments, and indeed those that befall us already in childhood as punishments of the sins of the first parents.\nIs God the [origin of] author of evil.",["“ills” renders Übel, “evil” das Böse.","The second line has no question mark in the source."]],"reflm:4351":["Pains are nothing evil, i.e. they can also subsist together with the highest good in the world without preceding sin. They are means of exercise of freedom and culture.",["“evil” renders Böses; “means of exercise” renders Übungsmittel."]],"reflm:4352":["*inanimata dicuntur res creatae, animata creaturae*. A plant |\nis a divine work, a worm is a creature; for to bring forth life, hence something which is active from itself, is *creatio per eminentiam*.",["*inanimata dicuntur res creatae, animata creaturae*: “inanimate things are called created things, animate ones creatures”.","*creatio per eminentiam*: “creation in the eminent sense”."]],"reflm:4353":["We find in the instincts of animals proofs of the divine wisdom, but not in the reason of human beings. Does this not show that our own reason, which is known to itself, but to which the animals are unknown, here gives the rules of inferring.",["The question has no question mark in the source; “inferring” renders Schließen."]],"reflm:4354":["Above and \nEvery creature stands under the universal and particular divine provision, but not every occurrence befalls it through particular direction, rather here chance often rules. Namely, provision is potential direction or supervision, in which all ends are taken into consideration. but God has not determined the human being's fates through individual aims, but left them to the order of nature. e.g. I have a fine stud: I do not ordain every mating, but let them be, as I know that they will pair according to universal order. Then there is in this universal provision, but not particular direction. For thereby alone is the human being put in the position to steer his fates himself according to universal laws.",["The editorial location note “Über und” is cut off in the source.","“provision”: Vorsorge (not Vorsehung, providence); “fates”: Schiksale.","“Then” renders Denn, apparently for Dann."]],"reflm:4355":["Above and \nProvidence goes either to the particular through the universal or to the universal through the particular. The first is the systematic one.",["The editorial location note “Über und” is cut off in the source.","“Providence”: Vorsehung."]],"reflm:4356":["A universal determination is a fate (e.g. natural death).\nA particular determination is a destiny.\nAn occurrence that is not at all determined beforehand is fortune or misfortune.",["“fate”: Schiksaal; “destiny”: Verhängnis; “fortune or misfortune”: Glük oder Unglük."]],"reflm:4357":["On the diminution of a miracle through mere direction.\nOn the enthusiastic *concursus* to holiness of the will; we must ourselves do good.\nGod and the physician. Ebb and flow. Preservation of empires. Life and death.",["“enthusiastic” renders schwärmerisch; *concursus*: “concurrence”."]],"reflm:4358":["He who will not subject himself to the laws is a free spirit (*libertin*); he who will not subject himself to the statutes, a freethinker.",["“free spirit”: Freygeist; “statutes”: Satzungen; “freethinker”: Freydenker."]],"reflm:4359":["Through the concept of the first one can explain no science. Every whole can be divided into two parts, of which the one is the first *relative* to the other, e.g. the first centuries. The absolutely first is only a single one, the relatively 1st is undetermined.",["“explain” renders erklären, which can also mean “define”."]],"reflm:4360":["*Metaphysica est logica intellectus puri*, but to *philosophia pura* belongs also morals. Because all logic is dogmatic or critical, metaphysics is either dogmatic or critical. and this critique is not merely of the propositions, but of human reason, consequently also of the opposite.",["*Metaphysica est logica intellectus puri*: “metaphysics is the logic of the pure understanding”; *philosophia pura*: “pure philosophy”.","“morals” renders moral."]],"reflm:4361":["division of philosophy into theoretical and practical; theoretical either from empirical or from rational principles, the former physiology.",[]],"reflm:4362":["Metaphysics is theoretical [pure] intellectual philosophy (opposite: empirical), and this either transcendental (g *ens summum*) or applied; the latter either to the nature of inner or of outer sense* or to universal nature (g *summum bonum*).\n*(g The question is: what reason can cognize without all experience. (in mathematics much.) which are the conditions, the objects and the boundaries. Pure reason is dogmatic in morals.)",["*ens summum*: “highest being”; *summum bonum*: “highest good”.","“boundaries” renders Grentzen; the asterisks are Kant's reference marks."]],"reflm:4363":["All our cognition can be distinguished according to kind into sensible and rational cognition.\nAccording to relation (g of subordination): into *principia et principiata*,\nOf coordination: as a part to the entire human cognition.",["“rational cognition” renders Vernunfterkentnis, “cognition of reason”.","*principia et principiata*: “principles and what follows from principles”."]],"reflm:4364":["Theoretical philosophy is either[*pura*] *rationalis* or *empirica*. The latter is *psychologia* and *physica*. The *rationalis* [is] has either also no object at all from experience [or] and is called *transscendentalis*; or indeed from experience, but *principia* from reason, and is called *metaphysica* (*psychologia rationalis* and *physica rationalis*); finally it has an object of reason, insofar as it is cognized through concepts of experience *per analogiam*.",["*pura*, *rationalis*, *empirica*: “pure”, “rational”, “empirical”; *principia*: “principles”.","“or indeed from experience” renders oder zwar aus der Erfahrung, with no noun in the German."]],"reflm:4365":["Distinction of cognition in the order: a priori. a posteriori.\n—\n—\n—\nin kind: empirical or rational.\nThe former are universal ones, the second singular ones.",["“universal ones … singular ones” renders Allgemeine … einzelne."]],"reflm:4366":["Metaphysics is *philosophia pura*. The form of all cognition is merely rational, the matter sensitive; thus metaphysics is the philosophy about the form.",["*philosophia pura*: “pure philosophy”; “matter” renders materie."]],"reflm:4367":["Analysis and Synthesis *negativa* are very different. A *divisio logica* is a *synthesis negativa*: I do not divide the concept, but divide it up (*repartior*).",["*negativa* can attach to Synthesis alone or to both Analysis and Synthesis.","“divide” renders theilen, “divide up” eintheilen; *divisio logica*: “logical division”; *repartior*: “I distribute”."]],"reflm:4368":["Our material concepts can never go to anything other than what we have sensed; and thus also our material principles can be regarded only as laws of experience and never be more universal. But if one extends the way of judging up to the concepts of form, then there comes from it more a law of subjective use.",["“sensed” renders empfunden; “principles” renders Grundsätze.","“more” renders mehr, which can also be read “rather”."]],"reflm:4369":["The question is whether metaphysics deals with the objects that can be cognized through pure reason, or with the subject, namely the principles and laws in the use of pure reason. Because |\nwe can cognize all objects only through our subject, above all those that do not affect us, it is subjective.",["“principles” renders principien, not Grundsätze."]],"reflm:4370":["By what do I know that *canones* of reason are not axioms, i.e. can be used only a posteriori and *descendendo*, but not a priori and *ascendendo*. For the subjective laws of reason are rules of the use of it in application, but the objective ones in explanation.\nAll immediately certain propositions are either 1. fundamental formulas or 2. *axiomata* or 3. *canones* or 4. elementary propositions of analysis or 5. immediately certain propositions of synthesis. The first are the principle of identity and of contradiction. The second: [Only ground] objective principles of synthesis, space and time. The third: objective principles of *synthesis qvalitativae*. The 4th and fifth: material propositions contained immediately under the principles of form [and of the] of synthesis as well as of analysis.\n[*Axiomata*] *Formulae primitivae* hold for every cognition.\nThe synthetic fundamental concepts of reason just like the synthetic fundamental concepts of appearances give occasion to axioms, which however both serve only for the use of reason a posteriori.\nThe use of principles a posteriori, when one applies them a priori, is a cognition of reason of objects *per analogiam*, [either] e.g. of space, of pleasure and displeasure from God.",["“principle” renders Satz in “principle of identity and of contradiction”, elsewhere Grundsatz; Satz is otherwise “proposition”.","*canones*: “canons”; *descendendo* / *ascendendo*: “descending” / “ascending”; *synthesis qvalitativae*: “of qualitative synthesis”; *Formulae primitivae*: “primitive formulas”.","“explanation” renders Erklärung, which can also mean “definition”."]],"reflm:4371":["The logical relation is merely a relation of subordination, i.e. of the *sphaerarum conceptuum*, and the logical rational constitution |\nof concepts consists in their being universal [or] concepts or *intuitus*.\nThe real relation is that of coordinations [either according to rules of the], and the rules of these are either those of reason or of sensibility. To the first belong substance and accident, whereas the logical relation has only subject and predicate. Inherence is a *coordinatio*, agreement a *subordinatio logica*. The concept of ground. That of the whole or rather of the composite. Possibility and existence are *positiones absolutae*, the former of the concept (Something), the latter of the thing.\nThus all synthetic concepts are (*intellectualiter*). Substance, *compositum* and *ratio*.",["*sphaerarum conceptuum*: “of the spheres of concepts”; “rational constitution” renders Vernunftbeschaffenheit, an unusual word and possibly a doubtful reading.","“coordinations” renders Zusammenordnungen; “agreement” renders convenientz; “thing” renders Sache.","*positiones absolutae*: “absolute positings”; *ratio*: “ground”."]],"reflm:4372":["All judgments [are] have a logical *object*, something in the logical sense, it may be possible or impossible, =A.\nIn these judgments there is either no predicate or there is one.\nIn the first case. Either:\nA is — — possible or:\nA is — — actual;\nin the second case.\nA is B.\nIf A contains a *subject* with *predicates*, then the first proposition can be expressed logically. E.g. A figure can be enclosed in three sides, *i.e.* a triangle is possible. But because this presupposes that a figure be possible, otherwise it is only a proposition that expresses the relation of two concepts and not the absolute possibility, one sees well that possibilities must be given. The lack of contradiction proves [sens] not the possibility of the thing and of the *data*, but of the combination. What then does the possibility of the thing itself signify? Logical possibility signifies the possibility of the relation A and B or A and non B. A triangle can be oblique-angled. Logical actuality is called truth.\nMetaphysical possibility signifies something *objective*, an object of thought. Non-possibility, of which the thought is empty. The impossible.\nEvery simple thought contains something possible, for one can invent nothing simple. It must accordingly be given through experience or inner law.",["“sense”: Verstande (im logischen Verstande); “object” in the first line renders obiect, in “object of thought” Gegenstand.","“thing”: Sache throughout; “actuality”: Wirklichkeit; “invent”: erdichten.","[sinnl] is a struck-out abbreviation, probably of sinnlich; rendered “[sens]”."]],"reflm:4373":["All truth consists in the agreement of all (g thoughts with the) laws of thinking and thus among one another. That is the *object* for us, what and insofar as it is given to us mediately or immediately through experience. Independently of all experience there are no objects and also no laws of the understanding. (*e.g.* substance: that this concept be something must be inferred from the experience of the constancy of a certain *subject* in all circumstances.) We have accordingly concepts, 1. in order to explain *phaenomena*, 2. in order to have insight into the grounds of the morally good and evil. Everything that is given through these laws, e.g. cognition of the highest good (of God), is practically true. The cognition of this very same object breaks off.",["“object”: obiect in the second sentence, Gegenstände and Gegenstandes elsewhere.","“have insight into”: einsehen; “inferred”: geschlossen; “constancy”: Beständigkeit.","The last sentence ends without completion in the source."]],"reflm:4374":["Appearances of reason. substance, spirit etc: concepts of reason as to their form: genus, species; as to matter: good and evil; for the universal in the matter of all sensation is pleasure or displeasure or the equilibrium: 0, *indifferentia*.",["“spirit”: Geist; “pleasure or displeasure”: Lust oder Unlust.","“Genus, species” are Latin in the source."]],"reflm:4375":["We have of the objects of the senses only representations that are adequate to our *phaenomenis*, and of the things themselves (through pure reason), as they are adequate to the laws of our understanding. |\nEven the concept of God is possible and needful to us only insofar as it is necessarily in conformity with the laws of the understanding which we necessarily follow in the (g judging of the) world. It is thus nevertheless only appearance, namely of the understanding, which borrows its certainty from the necessity of confirming our moral principles, from which everything must ultimately be derived. [Through] According to the laws of the understanding in physical appearances we come to the *idea* of a necessary cause, according to those in moral ones we come to the *idea* of a perfect author. One acts absurdly if one does not think for every motion a cause and for every combination a common cause; just as absurd is it to set the moral rules still above happiness without God. What are appearances of reason? (*subjectively* determined universal cognitions.) Hence comes the contradiction of the principles, when one judges according to the rule of reason with respect to the use in the world and afterwards wants to judge *in abstracto* from all such relation.",["“adequate”: angemessen; “in conformity with”: gemäß; “author”: Urheber.","The source's “daraus alles sollen zuletzt hergeleitet werden muß” is ungrammatical (sollen and muß both); rendered by the one modal.","“relation” in the last sentence: Beziehung; “judging”: Beurtheilung."]],"reflm:4376":["Besides the appearance, there is also the being or non-being of it to be noted.",[]],"reflm:4377":["The laws of pure reason can contain the ground of the possibility of objects only relatively to the mind, insofar as they concern such objects whose essence consists in the *relation* to the soul, i.e. the morally good.",["“mind”: Gemüth; “essence”: Wesen; “relatively”: relativisch."]],"reflm:4378":["Just as sensibility is a faculty of ordering things according to rela|tions\nof space and time, so also reason is a law of the coordination of things separated from the laws of sensibility.",["“coordination”: Zusammenordnung; “relations”: Verhältnissen; “separated”: abgesondert, which may attach to “law” or to “things”."]],"reflm:4379":["The concepts and propositions of reason which are correct *in concreto* with respect to the objects of the world or of the senses, of the actual as well as the possible, lead us, if one goes back, to boundaries which cannot be comprehensible according to these very rules; for their usefulness holds only within these boundaries. Nevertheless we cannot dispense [of] with the [constitution] cognition of that which sets these boundaries [no other] insofar as it serves, according to rules of our concrete reason, to explain and to confirm what is within the boundaries. The rest is useless. Thus with freedom the first is incomprehensible.",["“boundaries”: Grentzen; “dispense with”: entbrechen (sich … entbrechen, with genitive).","The deletions leave the sentence broken; the struck words are rendered where they stand."]],"reflm:4380":["1. Substance and accident; 2. unity and plurality; 3. ground and consequence (cause, effect, combination and separation).",["“consequence”: Folge; “plurality”: Vielheit."]],"reflm:4381":["M LIII:\nFor a pure concept of reason, *e.g.* substance, the *predicate* impenetrable is too narrow, and for a sensible one (impenetrable) the pure concept substance is too wide. In the former one must either take away sensibility from the |\n*subject* or add it to the *predicate*, in the latter the reverse. *Praedicatum univocum* or *aequivocum*. It is called in the first case: |\nM LIV: |\nA thing in general [stands], thought through the understanding, stands under conditions of sensibility, i.e. is *phaenomenon*, in the second: an appearance is *noumenon*. E.g. the sensible world is an absolute whole. Here the *predicate* would have to be restricted thus: it is a whole that can be sensibly determined; or, the *affection* left out of the *subject*: a world (*noumenon*) is a whole. The proposition: the soul is simple, is transcendent, and I must either say: I, who think the soul, am something simple, or: the soul is something that is not physically separable. In short: to an object as *phaenomeno* an intellectual *predicate* can belong only insofar as it is sensibly determinable.",["The source prints aeqvivocum; normalized to *aequivocum*.","“sensible world”: Sinnenwelt; “transcendent”: transscendent."]],"reflm:4382":["There are empirical concepts, through which an object is first given and which cannot be experimented upon but only analyzed. That they want to think the empirical fundamental concepts (matter) etc etc.",["“analyzed”: zergliedern; “fundamental concepts”: Grundbegriffe.","The second sentence is a fragment; its subject “sie” is unresolved."]],"reflm:4383":["Above, beside and \nWhat is necessary under the (g *subjective*) *conditions* under which things appear to us, that is also necessary to things as appearances.\nWhat is necessary under the hypothesis which I invent arbitrarily is *objectively* necessary everywhere where the case of the hypothesis is given.\nBut what is said of things in general, without regard to the *subject*, and not under an arbitrary hypothesis, cannot be cognized through the agreements of thoughts with themselves.",["“daß ist” in the source is read as “das ist”.","“invent”: erdichte; “arbitrarily”: willkührlich."]],"reflm:4384":["In and \nMathematical *synthesis* is not [not] an *analysis* of what we think in the given concept.",[]],"reflm:4385":["The metaphysical concepts are: 1. possible, 2. being, (g 2b. necessary,) 3. one posited to another (whole), 4. one in another (substance), 5. one through another (ground). The three last are real relations. The unity of the many: a. of the whole, b. the unity of the *predicates* in one *subject* or c. of the consequences through one ground.",["“being”: Seyn; “real relations”: realverhaltnisse; “consequences”: folgen."]],"reflm:4386":["The supreme principle of human reason is either: the one that expresses the *condition* under which we can cognize things through our reason alone, or: under which alone things are possible; *subjective*, *objective*. The principles of acquisition, of *genesis*, or of the mere cognition of possibility.",["“alone” (allein) in the second clause may attach to “things” or to “possible”; left ambiguous."]],"reflm:4387":["1. The matter for a true judgment. Possibility.\n2. The *est*. existence.\n3. The combination through concept. Necessity.",[]],"reflm:4388":["*Lockii simplices ideae: cogitatio, voluptas et taedium, existentia, unitas, potentia, successio.*",["Latin: “Locke's simple ideas: thought, pleasure and weariness, existence, unity, power, succession.”"]],"reflm:4389":["(s Something and nothing are formal concepts in metaphysics and signify merely the *relation*.)\nSomething and nothing in the logical sense is possible and impossible, i.e. that to which no concept at all belongs: not A, also not non A, which there is derived through contradiction, but in metaphysics from comparison with the original being. [Something or nothing in the met] The possible is something or nothing in the metaphysical sense as *realitas* or *negation*. The three absolute concepts, as the simplest, are: possible (comes from undetermined judgments), existence (from affirmations), necessary (from affirmation through concepts.) Categories.",["“original being”: Urwesen; “existence”: Daseyn; “undetermined”: unbestimmten.","“sense”: Verstande."]],"reflm:4390":["Possibility as to matter must be given through the senses |\nWe cognize only hypothetical possibility and the absolute one of space and time.",[]],"reflm:4391":["Above, beside and \nThe lack (of *existence*) is =0A. That something does not belong under possibility signifies an empty concept, i.e. a total lack of the concept; but that it be impossible, as a square circle, signifies the cancelling of possibility in general. For with a square circle not merely the possibility of this figure is cancelled; for an empty concept conflicts with nothing possible, although it is itself nothing; but: to be a circle and also square conflicts with the possibility of a circle and also of every other; hence what conflicts with possibility is impossible. But to conflict with possibility is not of the thing, but of the form; consequently, however ............",["“cancelling”, “cancelled”: Aufhebung, aufgehoben; “conflicts”: wiederstreitet.","The source breaks off with a row of dots."]],"reflm:4392":["Beside and \n*Nihil in sensu logico opponitur contrarie omnibus.* Nothing *logice* means as much as none.",["Latin: “Nothing in the logical sense is contrarily opposed to all things.”","“none”: keines."]],"reflm:4393":["The rational fundamental concepts can be brought to a certain number, but not the elementary concepts. Fundamental concepts of form and fundamental concepts of matter.",["“fundamental concepts”: Grundbegriffe; “elementary concepts”: Elementarbegriffe."]],"reflm:4394":["All simple concepts are possible; impossibility lies only in composition.",[]],"reflm:4395":["There are material and formal fundamental concepts, just as material and formal principles.",["“fundamental concepts”: Grundbegriffe; “principles”: Grundsatze."]],"reflm:4396":["What is posited in itself exists; what is cancelled in itself does not exist; what is neither the one nor the other is undetermined.\nWhat is posited through its *idea* is necessary; what is cancelled thereby is impossible; what is neither the one nor the other is merely possible or in itself contingent.\nPossible is that whose concept is not empty, also not cancelled through itself. That it is not to be empty signifies after all that something outside the thought, thus an actuality, *corresponds* to it.",["“in itself”: an sich selbst; “cancelled”: aufgehoben; “corresponds”: respondire, a Latinate verb."]],"reflm:4397":["In the concept of the understanding, ground signifies that from which the inference to something else holds universally. The possibility of this can indeed be had insight into in logical, but not real grounds. But experience gives no true universality, because it gives no necessity. Now we nevertheless take the application of the concept of real ground merely from experience. Hence the principles can only be empirically universal and also have only an empirical significance, namely that with something something else is always combined as ground.",["“universally” (allgemein) may attach to “holds” or to “inference”.","“have insight into”: einsehen; “significance”: Bedeutung; “combined”: verbunden."]],"reflm:4398":["Inner possibility is the smallest, absolute the greatest.\nAbsolutely possible is *quod absque ulla hypothesi* possible is.",["Latin: “what without any hypothesis”; the source prints qvod.","The German word order of the second line is kept."]],"reflm:4399":["A–A=0 signifies no concept at all, but not no thing at all, i.e. a *remotion* of the thing. For A signifies an affirmative concept, –A a negating one, both consequently none at all. The impossible is thus not a negative concept, but none at all; but if A signifies a thing, –A its real opposite, then 0 = *privativum*. In all concepts something is thought (*reale*), and then the positing or cancelling (*actus intellectus: positio vel negatio*).",["“thing”: Sache; “cancelling”: aufhebung; “real opposite”: reales Gegentheil.","Latin: “act of the understanding: positing or negation”."]],"reflm:4400":["A thought without affirming or negating *object* [the *object*] |\nrepresents something impossible, in that we neither posit nor cancel anything. (g or rather: it is an empty thought.)",[]],"reflm:4401":["In every negating judgment it is thought that the [*predicate*] *subject* is not contained under the *predicate*; whether because of contradiction or not is undecided.",[]],"reflm:4402":["Everything that is considered *transcendentaliter* is weighed *respective* to the essence, but in the metaphysical sense *absolute* and *universaliter*. In the former the essence is the consequence of the *essentialia*, in the latter the essence with its *essentialibus* the ground of consequences *in indefinitum*. Hence the former propositions are tautological, but the latter pregnant. For in the former the *essentialia* are given only through the essence and are in turn constituents of it.",["“essence”: Wesen; “consequence(s)”: Folge, Folgen; “constituents”: bestandtheile.","*in indefinitum*: “into the indefinite”; “pregnant”: praegnant."]],"reflm:4403":["Absolutely necessary is necessary in every regard, but internally necessary is (g it) in itself; consequently such a thing must at the same time include in itself the relation to everything, i.e. be the *hypothesis* to everything.\nImpossibility is restricted possibility. By the more conditions possibility is restricted, the smaller is the domain of this concept, until, if something is [all] in every consideration impossible, then it is in itself (g simply) impossible. Through mere concepts this can be had insight into only when something contradicts itself. [But the M]",["“in every regard”: in aller Absicht; “in every consideration”: in allem Betracht; “simply”: schlechthin.","“relation”: Beziehung; “domain”: Umfang.","The source sentence beginning “bis, wenn” is grammatically broken and kept so."]],"reflm:4404":["All *commercium* and consequently all *compositum* is as such contingent.",["*commercium*: “community, interaction”; *compositum*: “composite”."]],"reflm:4405":["Absolute necessity consists in this, that something is 1. no consequence of another, 2. a ground of everything else.",[]],"reflm:4406":["From alteration logical contingency with respect to concepts can be inferred, likewise inner possibility can be inferred, but not the contingency of the series. The determinations that follow one another are contingent; but the series is necessary; still much more is the thing necessary, because it remains while its determinations change.",["“alteration”: Veränderung; “inferred”: geschlossen."]],"reflm:4407":["In the same sense in which a thing is necessary, it is also unalterable. If a *predicate* belongs to it simply necessarily, then it is also unalterable with respect to that; if conditionally |\nso, then it is unalterable under this condition; but the thing itself is not therefore unalterable, but only with respect to its *predicates*. With respect to existence no thing can be alterable, for not being is no alteration.",["“simply necessarily”: schlechthin nothwendig; “conditionally”: bedingter|weise.","“existence”: Daseyn; “alteration”: Veränderung."]],"reflm:4408":["The agreement of the concept of an *entis omnimode realis* with the synthetic conditions of thinking [has] seems to have no difficulty, because between *realitatibus noumenis* one can comprehend no conflict, but indeed between *realitatibus phaenomenis*. The latter one expresses through effect in opposite direction.",["*entis omnimode realis*: of a being real in every respect; *realitatibus noumenis / phaenomenis*: realities as noumena / as phenomena.","“conflict” renders Wiederstreit; “The latter” (Letzteren, masc.) agrees grammatically with Wiederstreit."]],"reflm:4409":["Opposite and \nEach one, all, the All. I have a penny for each poor person, but not for all poor persons, still less for the All. The latter is *completum*. From this All the rest is derived.",["The opening line is the AA's editorial indication of where the note stands, cut off as printed.","“the All” renders das All; *completum*: complete."]],"reflm:4410":["Of which no totality is possible, that is necessarily incomplete; so is a multitude in general. (s[series.] progressions.)\nThere are three kinds of *respectus*, thus also as many *correlata*, and thus also as many *incompleta*. *rationatum*, *pars*, *praedicatum*.\nThe part is as such incomplete. (All things of the world are incomplete [therefore]; the *ens realissimum* is the only complete one as a ground.) The unity of the whole is nevertheless a ground why something belongs to it as a *complementum*.",["“totality” renders totalitaet, “incomplete” incomplet, “complete” complete, Kant's Latinate forms; “multitude” renders menge.","*respectus*: relation; *rationatum, pars, praedicatum*: consequence, part, predicate."]],"reflm:4411":["The magnitude of a whole is extensive.\nThe magnitude of a ground is intensive or degree.\nThe beginning of a degree (*minimum*) is called moment.\n(s Magnitude of weight and degree of heaviness.\nDegree of extension, of light and of warmth.\nMoment of warming.)\n(s Degree of reality. Generation of a reality from 0, i.e. moment of generation.)\n(s The magnitude of motion depends on time and space. The magnitude of velocity (in an instant) is the magnitude of the moment of motion, through which namely a space can be traversed in a time.)",["“weight” renders Gewicht, “heaviness” Schwere; “generation” renders Erzeugung."]],"reflm:4412":["The difference between a logical and real subject is this, that the former contains the logical ground to posit the predicate, the latter the real ground (something other and positive), and thus is the cause, the |\n*accidens*, however, the effect. This relation of the cause we draw from our own [effect] actions and apply it to that which is constant in the appearances of outer things. But we finally find everything in the object to be *accidentia*. The first subject is thus a something through which the *accidentia* are. There thus arise *iudicia synthetica*. e.g. Something that I know through the constant *accidentia* of impenetrability also contains that of attraction. The reality of the *accidentis* we judge from the sensation of an object, which is distinct from that of the subject.",["“subject”, “predicate”, “object” render subiect, praedicat, obiect; “apply” renders appliciren; “actions” renders Handlungen, the struck “[Wirk]” beginning Wirkungen, effects.","*iudicia synthetica*: synthetic judgments.","“which is distinct from that of the subject”: die may refer to the sensation or to the reality."]],"reflm:4413":["Opposite the end of M § 195 and the paragraph number of M § 196:\nThe unity of the ground, the unity of the whole, the unity of the subject (in the case of the simple).\nThe connection of many (distinct) things in one whole presupposes dependence on one ground and flows from it.",["“connection” renders verknüpfung; “in one whole”, “on one ground”: einem may also be read as the article “a”.","“flows from it” renders fließet daher; daher can also be read “therefore”."]],"reflm:4414":["One distinguishes the substantial from all its predicates. But a thing cannot be distinct from all its predicates.",[]],"reflm:4415":["All (g real) relations are either of connection or of conflict. The first are either one through the other (*ratio et rationatum*) or to the other (*partium ad totum*) or in one another (*accidens et substantia*). The unity in all of this is the greatest connection (*subordinatio, coordinatio, involutio*). The boundaries are the first ground, the *omnitudo* of what is connected and the last *subiectum*. A substance that contains the first ground of everything that is real, thus |\nwithout dependence, without limitation and without another subject, constitutes the supreme *principium* of everything. *Vltimam rerum lineam etc etc.* The logical parallel of the *ente summo* is the *ens entium logicum* (*ens in genere*). It is the supreme subject, gathers all things together in its *sphaera* and is the supreme ground of explanation, which is presupposed in everything as that which expresses its possibility. *Symbola* are the hanging of one part on others ([inherence] *composita in toto*), the depending of one link of the chain on others (dependence), the resting of one thing on the other (*portitor*) (inherence). To the first ground attaches necessity, to the universal infinity, to the [subst] self-subsistent — — breaks off.",["“relations” renders Verhaltnisse, “conflict” Wiederstreit, “limitation” Einschränkung, “ground of explanation” Erklärungsgrund, “self-subsistent” Selbständigen.","*ratio et rationatum*: ground and consequence; *partium ad totum*: of parts to the whole; *Vltimam rerum lineam*: the last line of things; *portitor*: carrier.","“breaks off” is the AA's editorial note (bricht ab)."]],"reflm:4416":["[The G] The *conceptus terminator* of *inhaerentia* is *substantiale*\n*dependentia* *ens primum*\ncomposition *simplex*.",["*conceptus terminator*: terminating concept; the three lines are set out as a table in the AA, *dependentia* and composition standing under *inhaerentia*."]],"reflm:4417":["an aggregate of several things mutually determining one another is a real composite; not every whole is a composite. e.g. a series of subordinated things.\nIn every *composito* there is unity of interaction, which proves community with a third as *principio*.",["“interaction” renders Wechselwirkung, “community” Gemeinschaft."]],"reflm:4418":["All unity is relative; whether an absolute one is possible is a question.",["“is a question” renders frägt sich."]],"reflm:4419":["A *compositum* of each kind of parts consists of parts that are not composed in the same way, but can well consist of parts that are composed of another kind of parts (for the latter is not the *contradictorie oppositum* of the former). E.g. A rod consists of feet that are not further composed of feet, but indeed of inches. If therefore the composition is cancelled (namely that of a certain kind), then parts remain that are not composed in the same way, but not such as would be composed in no way.",["“of each kind of parts” renders aus jeglicher Art Theile; “rod” renders ruthe, a measure of length.","*contradictorie oppositum*: contradictory opposite; “cancelled” renders aufgehoben."]],"reflm:4420":["The question here properly is whether a real composite can be thought as a continuous quantum or as a discrete one; if it were [discrete] continuous, it would be nothing but some force bound in space or time.\nThat whole which is the *totum intelligibile*, whose *phaenomenon* is body, may perhaps consist of simple parts, but not on that account the body, because this whole in space does not exert simplicity and its consequences.\nThe necessity of simple parts rests on this, that they can also exist separated all together. But total separation in space is the complete cancellation of the relations of space. Thus a body consists of simple parts only insofar as I do not consider it in relations of space, thus not as an *extensum*.",["“exert” renders exserirt, from Latin exserere, to put forth; “consequences” renders folgen.","“cancellation” renders Aufhebung; “it” (diesen, masc.) agrees with Korper."]],"reflm:4421":["Body is not a substantial composite, but a substantiated phenomenon. [Nor] and (g as much) composition with respect to space as division are phenomena of conjunction and division. If a composite substance, whose phenomenon is body, [be] is conceived to consist of simple things, its phenomenon does not on that account consist of such. [Nor] Moreover space contains the ground of the possibility of the phenomenon; whatever therefore is so present that its presence is action in space, is bound by the conditions of this.",["“praeseus” is printed, presumably for praesens, present.","“of this” renders huius, which may refer to space or to the action."]],"reflm:4422":["At the very top of the page:\nSince space is a phenomenon, bodies are [action] phenomena of the external presence of several substances. Therefore although all composition in the substantial whole, yet it cannot on that account be removed in the phenomenon of external presence. Since space contains the ground of the possibility of external presence, the [possibility] necessity of division does not reach further in the composite than is admitted through the concept of space. Suppose something simple to be present in an assigned part of space: space would limit it objectively, hence it would be something objective.",["“although all composition in the substantial whole” is elliptical in the Latin (qvanqvam omnis compositio in toto substantiali), with no verb of its own.","“removed” renders tolli; “ground” renders rationem."]],"reflm:4423":["Opposite the end of M § 244:\nAlterations are actual, signifies: we actually represent to ourselves the determinations of things according to our law of sensibility in the sequence of time. Alterations are possible only in time, i.e. their representation presupposes the sensible form of time.",["“alterations” renders Veranderungen; “actual”, “actually” render wirklich; “sequence” renders Folge."]],"reflm:4424":["Every *qvantum* is either a *compositum*, i.e. *syntheticum*, or a *qvantum analyticum*. *Compositum* is that whose parts can be before the |\ncomposition and which comes to be only through composition and combination. Every *compositum* consists of *simplicibus* and is *divisibile*.\n*Qvantum indivisibile* is what cannot arise *per compositionem minorum qvantorum*. The magnitude, force of the understanding is no *compositum*. A greater moral perfection also not. The lesser degrees arise only from the limitation of the greatest, and this is the first, which is given in itself. Every such *qvantum* is *continuum*. A *discretum* is composed.\n(g In all those magnitudes that cannot arise through the composition of the smaller, although these are contained in them, there is a *progressus continuus in infinitum*. For they [they] are themselves there only through a greatest, and they are limited only through others that are simultaneous.)",["“comes to be” renders wird, used absolutely; “combination” renders Verbindung.","*per compositionem minorum qvantorum*: through the composition of smaller quanta; *simplicibus*: simple things.","“The magnitude, force of the understanding”: the comma is printed, so Größe and Kraft may be two subjects or one in apposition."]],"reflm:4425":["Space is a *quantum*, but not a *compositum*. Because space does not arise by the parts being posited, but the parts are only possible through space; just so time. The parts can indeed be thought separately *abstrahendo a caeteris*, but not *removendo caetera*. and they can thus indeed be discerned, but not separated, and the *divisio* is not real, but logical. Because [know whether not] all matter [only as] as regards divisibility seems to depend on the space that it fills, and it is as divisible as this space, the question is whether the divisibility of matter is not merely logical just like that of space.",["*abstrahendo a caeteris*: by abstracting from the rest; *removendo caetera*: by removing the rest.","“discerned”, “separated” render discerniren, separiren; “separately” renders besonders.","The struck “[weiß ob nicht]” is rendered word for word."]],"reflm:4426":["Velocity does not arise through mere addition of smaller degrees, |\nbut merely through the limitation of the greatest force. Hence the Leibnizian law of the square of velocity; for the tortoise A carries itself with one degree of force and velocity, carries the second B with just the same degree of force =2. This, in pushing itself forward, also acts against the tortoise A with one degree, and the latter therefore needs, in the direction AB, in order to resist the counteraction, also one degree of force =3, and B has moreover in the direction AB its own velocity =4. The third C [requires] thus presupposes first these 4, secondly in A yet one degree in order to carry it =5, in B also in order to carry it =6, in A a resistance that arises from this =7, further in B a degree from the resistance, in that C pushes itself forward =8, and finally one in C, with which it goes forward, =9. The forces that [one upon the other] each applies are thus as the odd numbers. A=1. A+B=3. A+B+C=/5=9.",["“tortoise” renders Schildkröte as printed; “counteraction” renders Gegenwirkung, “resistance” Wiederstand.","“A+B+C=/5=9” is kept as printed."]],"reflm:4427":["The velocity that a spring can give to bodies in just the same space of its tension grows to infinity. Hence every lasting force, e.g. spring, that acts externally has infinite force. Every internally acting force is in any case infinite as regards time, because it [not as] (first goes to all bodies however large) can act through every time however long in equal degree without diminution.",["“tension” renders Ausspannung, the spring's stretching; “lasting” renders daurende.","“in any case” renders ohnedem."]],"reflm:4428":["We can represent to ourselves the finite *in concreto* as possible only through the limitation of the infinite. E.g. a *spatium* of a certain figure. Just so, since a thing is thought as complete only [through] by this, that, if it [according to] has all reality, everything is affirmed of it and hence all being is thought, and if it is an *ens limitatum*, that some is posited in it, but all the remaining reality is indeed thought, but negated: so the concept of the finite seems to arise from that of the infinite.",["“complete” renders complet; “being” renders Seyn; “negated” renders verneint.","The struck “[laut]” is rendered “[according to]”; it may also be the start of another word."]],"reflm:4429":["[The infinitely small] In that which is infinitely divisible (*continuum*), there is a *terminus*, but no *finis divisionis*. But what is infinite in the *synthesi* is no *finis* or *terminus* and yet totality. The synthesis has in the case of the *continuo* no first beginning and in the case of the *infinito* no end.",["*terminus*: bound; *finis divisionis*: end of division; “totality” renders totalitaet."]],"reflm:4430":["The real infinite is all-sufficient (in which there is *omnitudo*). The infinite is not mutable; all alterations are limitations of existence.",["The first two sentences are Latin: *Infinitum reale est omnisufficiens (in qvo est omnitudo). Infinitum non est mutabile*.","“limitations” renders einschrankungen, “existence” Daseyn."]],"reflm:4431":["For the concept of matter extension and impenetrability is enough; for mere matter *inertia* is further required; *materia spontanea* (*viva*) is not mere matter. how do we come to this, that we (g presuppose) impenetrable extension could not be self-active apart from a foreign, hence immaterial *principio*. Does that come from this, because we encounter some *impenetrabile iners* and hence in the case of the *vivo* suspect in addition a *principium vitae*?\n(s Resistance takes place only insofar as a body does not have a motion, but rather a striving toward the opposite.)",["*materia spontanea (viva)*: spontaneous (living) matter; *impenetrabile iners*: inert impenetrable thing; *principium vitae*: principle of life.","“apart from” renders ausser, which may also mean “outside”; “self-active” renders selbstthatig.","“striving” renders Bestrebung."]],"reflm:4432":["The *extensum impenetrabile* is according to its substance matter, according to its form body; to the form belongs the outer (figure) and the inner structure, (s thirdly the mixture; the figure is an object of mathematics, the structure of mechanics, the mixture of chemistry and forces of physics.)\nThe [mathematical] cognition of corporeal nature is called, as doctrine, [doctrine of nature] knowledge of nature, as discipline doctrine of nature. The doctrine of nature is either rational (natural science) or empirical, *phaenomena*, (description of nature). Natural science is either mathematical or philosophical. the former considers breaks off.",["“doctrine” renders doctrin, Kant's Latinate word; “doctrine of nature” renders Naturlehre, “knowledge of nature” Naturkunde.","“natural science” renders Naturwissenschaft, “description of nature” Naturbeschreibung.","“breaks off” is the AA's editorial note (bricht ab)."]],"reflm:4433":["If we assume that the difference of all things consists merely in the diversity of magnitude either of the whole or of the parts or also of the motion of these parts, then between all their possible *speciebus* there is a continuous interconnection. Between rest and motion. Between the equilateral, isosceles, scalene triangles, the conic sections. Between a surface that moves\ninfinitely little, and moves through an assignable magnitude and makes bodies.\nThis interconnection can also at least be seen as a law of the understanding from the division into classes always attempted. For there one presupposes that all things can be regarded as variations of one fundamental being. For were this not so, then from one concept, through the closer determination, a necessary (g and determinate) manifoldness of those contained under it could not be inferred. Or one also represents to oneself the unity of the cause, and each product not as immediately subordinated to it, but one by means of the other. Because in the supreme cause everything must be [of a single] homogeneous and the heterogeneity not a limitation of the parts by the supreme cause itself, but of one part by the other. As if from crude matter gradually plants, from these animals and finally from these the human being had arisen with gradual transition; so that the animal capacities, which con|sist in a very special relation to certain circumstances that occur,\nare gradually arranged for more or for the more general and finally entirely for the general and are called reason. One can represent to oneself that either all species of animals arose through the gradual increase of perfection from the mineral on *ascendendo* or through the decrease of a creature that was more perfect than the human being, where in this original animal lay the sources of all possible mechanical formation, which in their products breaks off.",["“assignable” renders Angebliche (angebbar).","“manifoldness” renders Mannigfaltigkeit; “could be inferred” renders können geschlossen werden.","“[of a single]” renders the deleted [einer eintzigen]."]],"reflm:4434":["The *saltus* is either according to degree (from a smaller state suddenly to a greater) or according to quality (where, after the series of grounds has ceased, a new one begins *spontaneo*). There is in the world a continuity of the dynamical series, but not merely as world of sense, but also as intelligible world. The first especially then has *hiatum*. In regard to the receptivity of the mind there is continuity; the *facultas* has spontaneity.",["“world of sense” renders Sinnen-[Welt]; “mind” renders Gemüth."]],"reflm:4435":["Mere matter is *iners* or lifeless; hence what among extended beings lives (animal) [is] has in itself matter and a special *principium* of life: the immaterial. (The inner *principia* of the *phaenomena*, which are cognized only through inner sense.) Hence the immaterial natures and their concept already rest on the difference of inner and outer sense.",["*iners*: inert."]],"reflm:4436":["The supreme cause is also the ground of the possibility of the alterations of one and the same world. I.e. that the subject remains, although it exists otherwise; otherwise the subject would have to perish together with its state and no other arise. The cause of this possibility is thus preservation, i.e. the infinite duration of the supreme cause.",["“alterations” renders Veränderungen; “preservation” renders Erhaltung."]],"reflm:4437":["Nothing is changed except what is sustained by another.\nBecause all things subsist in another, there is space; because all things subsist durably in another, there is time.\nIn alteration there is a unity of various states of one and the same subject; this unity rests on the dependence of substances on One.\nWhat pertains to the same state of the world is simultaneous; what pertains to different or opposite states is successive.\nTime contains in itself the ground of the possible change of contingent states; for from contingency alone mutability does not follow.\nNot contingency, but mutability proves existence from another, and because time is unique, existence from one. Simultaneity also proves existence from one.",["“is changed” and “change” render mutantur and mutatio; “alteration” renders the German Veränderung.","“ground” renders rationem; “proves” renders arguit."]],"reflm:4438":["At the top of the page:\nThe influence of the sustaining substance alone is original.\nNo community of substances (mutual influence) is original; that of all is derivative, insofar as they are sustained through another.",["“influence” renders influxus; “community” renders commercium."]],"reflm:4439":["One cognizes the course of nature through the senses, and through the (s reason the) order of nature; the greater the reason, the more order one discovers. In the lack of reason everything seems chance (s or blind necessity). Nature is at all times a *principium* of order.\nThe order of nature is distinguished from the order according to rules of perfection (i.e. of a good will, how the things would have to be if they were to please). [This is the objective order of the world] The order of nature is throughout necessary for the moral order, and the most perfect world will attain this [ground] perfection according to the order of nature, because only under this condition is it possible to use understanding.\n(s Freedom also stands under the order of nature.)",["“[ground]” renders the deleted, unfinished [Gründ]."]],"reflm:4440":["There are three kinds of [psych] physiological effects of the human soul.\n1. the [sensation] mere representations, 2. the connection, 3. the comparison.\nThree kinds of cognition [i.e.] and objective differences:\n1. sensation, 2. form of appearance, 3. concept.\nThreefold use of reason:\n1. Inner sense or intuition of oneself and one's thoughts. 2. Universal representations and the relation of their spheres (logical use). 3. The form of *thesis* and *synthesis*. The use of pure reason contains either absolute or relational con|cepts.\nThe former either intuitive, i.e. according to matter (good), or discursive: according to mere form (existence).\n(s Animals also have *facultatem diiudicandi* (*iudicium sensitivum*), but not *iudicandi* (*iudicium intellectuale*).",["“[psych]” renders the deleted, unfinished [psych]; “connection” renders Verknüpfung.","“relational concepts” renders Verhältnisbegriffe.","The parenthesis opened before s is not closed in the source."]],"reflm:4441":["One can regard freedom as if it were the influence of a particular cause, namely of intelligence, which however does not alone determine, and in turn of sensibility as a particular cause. The first gives as the ground of explanation the character, the second the natural disposition or temperament. Although in every case, whatever the human being may otherwise have done, yet now the opposite is possible (as in a game, where someone has often hit): yet this freedom is on the whole to be regarded like nature, and the actions are sufficiently (practically) determined from character and temperament.",["“natural disposition” renders Naturel."]],"reflm:4442":["The state after death is either a regeneration or a liberation; the former either an earthly or a supra-earthly one. Either a new animal life of earthly or supra-earthly kind or a spiritual life. And this its relation either in this world or in another world.\nSeat of the soul. Place after death. Separation from the body. Heaven, hell and earth. The former belongs both to the world, but not to the world of sense.",["Jenes gehört beydes: “both” may refer to heaven and hell together, or to the two preceding items; left as ambiguous as the German."]],"reflm:4443":["The cause on account of which we hope for another life is, so that the outcome may agree more with morality. This, however, is brought about by this, that only the means of dissembling disappears [and]; for thereby good human beings will soon associate with one another and expel the evil and produce happiness among themselves. Not past vices, but the character ingrained through them will constitute the punishment of the future world.",["“dissembling” renders sich zu verstellen; “ingrained” renders eingeartete."]],"reflm:4444":["334. E II 1719.\nM 334':\nThat magnitude through which the smaller ones alone are possible, the magnitude of the universal ground, can be compared with the magnitudes of the consequence or in general smaller magnitudes only *per analogiam*; it cannot be regarded as the sum of smaller forces; they are not contained in it, but posited through it; thus the all-sufficient ground is only *per analogiam* a magnitude, i.e. only through the relation to the effect.\n(s Proof that there is an *ens realissimum*, in which all things are given; that this is necessary; that it does not exist in time and in space, but yet eternal and omnipresent, all-sufficient.)\n[Possibility, actuality]\nTranscendental predicates.\nUnity: that the concept of God is a *conceptus singularis* and there also can be no other *conceptum singularem* of a being than that which contains *omnitudinem*. For everything is only once. Some *a* is *b*, other is *non b*. (g Polytheism, Manichaeism.)\nIs proved by this, that all beings that differ do it through their limitations, thus many beings are always considered in the unlimited. This concept is practically necessary.\n[Simp] Substantiality. Spinoza. *Originaria*.\nSimplicity.\nAll-sufficiency.\n( g Unalterability *impassibilitas* )\nMagnitude. Infinity. *illimitatum*. (Eternity, omnipresence.)\nin existence, in all time and in all places. Eternity and omnipresence.\nin power.\n(s This is not the difficulty, how God is in all, but rather in no time, likewise place.)\nMetaphysical properties. In relation to the determinations of the *Cosmologiae generalis*.\n(s Are taken from causality in regard to the things, because the causality of the contingent lies in freedom and in general the causality of an effect outside him.)\n(s *systema Inhaerentiae: Spinozae, emanationis, et creationis*.)\n(s *Substantia omnisufficiens intellectualis est Deus*.)\n(s Independence; *intuitus intellectualis*, *prototypon*; not discursive: intuitive; not reason, not number.)\nIs a spirit, not world-soul. *Anthropomorphismus crassior*.\n1. Understanding. Omniscience. (s Prediction.)\n3. Will. Benevolence. Holiness. Justice. Freedom and independence.\n2. Blessedness (s not well-being). *complacentia relativa non est sensus voluptatis*.\nPhysicotheological predicates (s inner and outer).\nCreation (s causality in regard to) of the world.* Of matter, not merely according to form.\n(s The world is not in time, but the things of the world in it. The world has no beginning, but a cause.)\n(s A time of creation cannot be thought.)\n(s That all things of the world are creatures, but not from one another.)\n(g Conservation) Government and providence. (s Not the cause of evil. *Concursus*.)\nFinal end. (s Ordinary, extraordinary direction.) The two propositions: the most perfect cause and the best world with its *consectario* of the other life are inseparably combined.\nUniversal negative determinations. Analogy.\nM 334:\n*(s signifies *intellectualiter* only the free cause of substances, *sensitive* the beginning of the duration of the world.)",["“[Simp]” renders the deleted, unfinished [Einf].","“how God is in all, but rather in no time” renders wie Gott zu aller, sondern keiner Zeit sey, whose ellipsis is kept.","“Benevolence” renders Gütigkeit; “Conservation” Erhaltung; “propositions” Setze (Sätze)."]],"reflm:4445":["(s Possibility of such a science. In regard to that which is not intuitive.)\nProlegomena. Some sciences contain merely a use of the understanding in regard to various objects of curiosity and of advantage, and logic is the universal *organon* of them, namely the instruction for its use.\nOther sciences have the determination of the understanding itself as their aim, namely as much according to its faculty as according to its supreme ends, or the objects to which the understanding and the will are restricted by the supreme laws of nature. Thus [is] are all other sciences organs of skill, at most of prudence, metaphysics of wisdom.\nThe use of metaphysics in regard to the theoretical is merely negative; it does not open up the cognition of things and is not dogmatic; for where should it take [its] the [propert] cognition of things from without senses. Mathematics makes arbitrary concepts of magnitudes as hypothetical conditions, from which consequences can be drawn [A categorical science], through mere repetitions. With the question, however, what a thing is, we can invent no concepts and also no relations. they must refer to given ones at least as |\ngrounds of their use. Metaphysics only prevents the false use of reason, which steps out of its limitations and regards the *intellectualia* as objects, since they yet serve only for the *modo cognoscendi* of the *sensitive dabilium* and at most for the restriction of these, insofar as it wants to use the *sensitiva* beyond their limitations. Pure reason is dogmatic only in regard to the objects of the will, but in regard to [specu] speculation (merely supervisory) cathartic. In metaphysics there are no hypotheses, because first the possibility of a supreme ground of reason would be assumed entirely without rule and because metaphysics precisely wants to set limitations to reason in its pure use.\nAristippus: our judgments of that which is agreeable (g likewise all that express mere appearances) are at all times true, the objects may be constituted as they will, because the agreeableness signifies something that is in us; but the judgments: this or that object exists, [is] are uncertain, because the objects are distinct from us.",["“objects” renders both Obiecten (Obiecte) and Gegenstände.","“[propert]” renders the deleted, unfinished [Eigensch]; “limitations” renders Schranken, “restricted” eingeschränkt.","“consequences” renders Folgen; “supervisory” renders aufsehend."]],"reflm:4446":["The *intellectualia* are either theoretical (g metaphysics) or practical cognitions (g morals).\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n[dogmatists] Aristotle (g Romans) Scholastics\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\nPlato Ramus\nRoughness of the ...\n(s Critique before doctrine)\nunderstanding.\n((s Ideas in Plato are *intuitus*.) s *methodus Dogmatica — Sceptica Critica*.\nDescartes\nHume )\nMetaphysical *loci* of Aristotle\nhouse of intellectual concepts.\n( s innate ideas; acquired: through the senses, through anticipations. )\n(s Natural or supernatural sources of the intellectual cognitions.)\nLocke. subjective habituation. (s He took the *principia fiendi* for the *essendi*.)\n( s (s instead of analytic) *Physiologia intellectus*, where [its phae] the origin of its concepts is explained. Wolff does not have it at all. )\nCrusius* Method of preestablished cognition.\neither through *epigenesis* or through preformation.\n*(s is not to be used, because we do not know what God has disclosed to us.)\n(s All have treated metaphysics dogmatically, not critically.)\n(s Criterion of truth: the taking to be true.)",["The | lines are the vertical strokes of Kant's diagram, kept as they stand.","“house of intellectual concepts” renders domus conceptuum intellectualium; “Method of preestablished cognition” renders Methodus cognitionis praestabilitae.","“[its phae]” renders the deleted, unfinished [dessen phae]; the source spells decartes and Wolf."]],"reflm:4447":["Plato gave the origin of the concepts of perfection well. But not of the *notionum*. Plato enthusiast, Aristotle analyst. The objective and subjective principles of the D breaks off.",["“D” is the unfinished word at which the note breaks off."]],"reflm:4448":["M VII:\nThe analytic concept of the thing contains what lies in it; the synthetic, what must be attributed to it, insofar as it is to be a certain thing.*\nM VIII:\n(* g *apparentia*. *conceptus*. *notio*. *idea*. The idea that through which *a priori* things are possible according to rules of a perfect will. Images. Concepts. ideas. Plants. Origin: minerals. Ends. )",["*apparentia*. *conceptus*. *notio*. *idea*: “appearance. concept. notion. idea.”","“attributed” renders beygelegt; “Ends” renders Zweke (Zwecke)."]],"reflm:4449":["History of the differences between *sensitivis* and *intellectualibus*.\nEgyptians. Pythagoras. Heraclitus (g Eleatic). Plato (g *ideae innatae*) and Pythagoras made the *intellectualia** into particular objects of possible intuition; his school, the [phi] academic |\nphilosophers, exoterically *sceptice* and esoterically *dogmatice*. *Intuitus intellectualis*, from which everything descends. Aristotle taught the sensitive (g categories), but did not stay with them. Epicurus: a philosopher of sensations. [Ep] Aristippus: merely the feeling of the agreeable.\n*(s *intellectualia vel qvoad obiecta vel formam cognitionis*)\n(s *intellectualia vel mystice vel logice talia*)\n(s the intellectual cognitions, but *circa obiecta sensuum, neqve per notiones connatas*)\n( s *vel per intuitus vel conceptus intellectuales*. The latter either metaphysical\nLocke — Leibniz.\nor physiological\nAristotle and Epicurus. )",["*sensitivis* / *intellectualibus*: “sensitive things” / “intellectual things”; *ideae innatae*: “innate ideas”; *sceptice*, *dogmatice*: “skeptically”, “dogmatically”; *Intuitus intellectualis*: “intellectual intuition”.","The Latin additions: “intellectual things either as regards the objects or the form of cognition”; “intellectual things either mystically or logically such”; “concerning objects of the senses, nor through connate notions”; “either through intellectual intuitions or concepts”.","“his school” (seine Schule): the singular possessive after “Plato … and Pythagoras” is kept; “cognitions” renders cognitionen."]],"reflm:4450":["Aristotle erred in this, that in logic he made a division of the universal concepts through which one can think things: this belongs to metaphysics. Logic has to do with concepts, whatever they may be, and treats only their relation.",["“belongs to” renders gehört vor (= für).","“treats” renders tractirt; “relation” renders Verhältnis."]],"reflm:4451":["theosophical and mystical principles.\nPythagoras, the Eleatic school: Parmenides, Plato treated *intellectualia*; Plato *per ideas connatas et intuitivas*, the others *per discursivas*, but the *sensitiva* only as *apparentias*. Aristotle borrowed everything from the senses, but nevertheless treated *intellectualia*, which he took for *sensitiva*.",["*intellectualia* renders the German plural intellectualien; *per ideas connatas et intuitivas*: “through connate and intuitive ideas”; *per discursivas*: “through discursive ones”; *apparentias*: “appearances”.","“borrowed” renders lehnte (for entlehnte); the reading is doubtful.","“took for” renders hielt … vor, read as für; vor could also mean “before”, i.e. “which he placed before *sensitiva*”."]],"reflm:4452":["The true concepts of reason go only to the relation of things in general. The objects are sensitive; only [their] the use of reason with regard to them takes place according to merely intellectual laws; if the objects are intellectual, then this is enthusiastic.",["“concepts of reason” renders Vernunftbegriffe; “things” renders Sachen; “objects” renders the Latinate obiecten.","“enthusiastic” renders enthusiastisch."]],"reflm:4453":["The *quaestiones* of metaphysics are all raised by common reason and by our most important ends; it is no *organon* of science, but of wisdom, and is useful negatively, to remove the hindrances that oppose themselves to the most important cognition.",["*quaestiones*: “questions”; “oppose themselves” renders sich … opponiren."]],"reflm:4454":["In the critique of metaphysics one can avail oneself of two sorts of methods. The first is: to examine the proofs and to seek out their *paralogismos* or *petitiones principii*. The second: to oppose to one proof another, and indeed one just as convincing, of the opposite. This last method is the best. For since the errors of metaphysical inferences consist chiefly in this, that what holds merely of the conditions of sensible cognition is enunciated of the object, a proof can seem so rigorous that one hardly becomes aware of an error, which one best discovers through a *demonstratio oppositi*.",["*paralogismos* or *petitiones principii*: “paralogisms” or “begging of the question”; *demonstratio oppositi*: “demonstration of the opposite”.","“critique” renders Critik; “inferences” renders Schlüße; “enunciated” renders enunciirt; “object” renders Obiect."]],"reflm:4455":["(g It is discipline of pure reason. Aesthetics: critique of taste.)\nidea of metaphysics: Is it a critique or doctrine: is its procedure zetetic or dogmatic? It is the question: what can one [without] cognize through mere reason without any experience (mathematics, morality)? which are the sources, the conditions and boundaries. Transcendental philosophy is critique of pure reason. study of the subject, confusion of the subjective with the objective, prevention.",["“doctrine” renders doctrin, not Lehre; “zetetic” renders zetetisch, “inquiring”.","“boundaries” renders grentzen; “morality” renders Moral."]],"reflm:4456":["In transcendental philosophy there occur notions, but not ideas.",["“notions” renders notionen, “ideas” Ideen."]],"reflm:4457":["In *metaphysica applicata* much is dogmatic. (g in the transcendental everything critical; can anything indeed be discovered through metaphysics? Yes, with regard to the subject, but not to the object.)\n[However] As critique it has utility. Even if religion and virtue are not grounded on it, but have other sources, it serves to remove the hindrances. Critique of science and *organon** of wisdom (g which depends more on doing without than on acquiring. Socrates).\n[The *quaest*] It is necessary; the *quaestiones* are set it as problems by healthy reason and moral concerns. It is indispensable.\n*(g a propaedeutic of the same; morality is an *organon*.)",["*metaphysica applicata*: “applied metaphysics”; *quaestiones*: “questions”.","“are set it as problems” renders sind ihr … aufgegeben; “healthy reason” renders Gesunde Vernunft.","“which” in the addition (welche) may refer to wisdom or to the critique."]],"reflm:4458":["We have, of metaphysics, as of an unknown land on whose possession we are intent, first diligently investigated the (g situation and) approaches. (It lies in the (g region) hemisphere of pure reason;) we have even drawn its outline, where this island of [cogni] of cognition [to the] hangs together with the land of experience by bridges, or where it is separated from it by a deep sea; we have even sketched its outline and know as it were its geography (g ichnography), but do not yet know what may be encountered in this land, which some have held to be uninhabitable for human beings, others have regarded as their actual settlement. After this general geography of this land of reason we will take into consideration its general history.",["“drawn” renders gezogen, “sketched” gezeichnet; the repeated clause “we have even … outline” is Kant's.","“its” after “geography” renders desselben, which agrees grammatically with Land, not with Insel (“island”).","“ichnography”: ground plan; “land of reason” renders Vernunftlandes."]],"reflm:4459":["M XI:\nUtility.\nWhat is that which gives the deep investigations of metaphysics their supreme motive and in what is the true importance of such a science to be posited.\n1. It is not the immediate desire for knowledge that it satisfies, hence not as science.\n2. Nor as an *organon* of other sciences. e.g. of natural science.\n3. Hence only as a propaedeutic of wisdom. But as such a one, in what consist the foremost questions which it resolves, or the important cognitions to which it is to be the key. They are 2. Is there a God, and is there a future life. The answering of these questions is in tu|rn\nimportant, insofar as it is a ground of our conduct and makes firm the principles of life.\nBut if the principles of life have (g other) [rules] sources independent of it, |\nM XII: |\nIf in order to support them no science, but only practically sufficient cognition is needed, but this, even without [the] strict logical perfection, is given from [the] practical sources themselves: then this science will be able to serve only for the security of the doctrine of wisdom against all incursions of a seeming reason, which service is very great. The belief in God gives the metaphysical inferences *favorem utilitatis*.\n(s Attack not on the things, but on the assertions: speculations.)",["“desire for knowledge” renders Wisbegierde; “doctrine of wisdom” renders Weisheitslehre; “seeming” renders scheinbaren.","“themselves” renders selbst, which may also go with “given” (“is itself given”) or mean “even”.","*favorem utilitatis*: “the favor of utility”. The second “If” clause takes up the broken-off “But if” before the page break."]],"reflm:4460":["[Analytic part Princ]\nGenetic part: sensibility and reason.\nZetetic:\na. Analysis, *Principium Contradictionis*.\nb. Synthesis.\nc. Antithesis. Skeptical part.",["*Principium Contradictionis*: “principle of contradiction”; “Zetetic” renders Zetetischer, “inquiring”."]],"reflm:4461":["There are 2 supreme practical propositions. 1. What we must do in order to become worthy of happiness. 2. What sort of ground we have [to believe] practically sufficiently to believe that one should become happy, insofar as one is worthy of this happiness. Hence a practically sufficient ground of the belief in God and another world. (s All other sciences have a worth only as a means to contingent ends (which to despise would be still more sublime), yet insofar as they cultivate the understanding, |\nwhich is the universal *organon* of all ends, they have a worth which is greater than the end.)",["“propositions” renders Sätze; “happiness” renders Glückseeligkeit; “worth” renders Werth.","“cultivate” renders excoliren (Latin excolere)."]],"reflm:4462":["The desire for happiness expresses the sum of all inclinations, [as is] whatever they may consist in; hence it is the first [desi] or supreme desiring. The matter and condition of everything (subjective).",["“desire” renders Verlangen, “desiring” Begehren; “[desi]” renders the broken-off [Verl].","“it” (sie) may refer to happiness or to the sum."]],"reflm:4463":["The universal desire of human beings is to be happy; but a [limitation and] condition to which reason (not appetite and sensibility) limits this desire is: to be worthy of this happiness.\nNow if the worthiness to be happy is the sole condition of the hope of also becoming so, then the precepts of morality go before those of [prud] prudent self-love. But if these laws are to get their incentives through the hope of becoming happy, then it is necessary that one at least be able to believe that the world stands under a wise government.",["“desire” renders Verlangen, “appetite” Begierde; “limits” renders einschränkt, “[limitation]” Einschränkung.","“morality” renders Sittlichkeit; “incentives” renders triebfedern; “go before” renders gehen … vorher."]],"reflm:4464":["If metaphysics is treated thus, then it is a hindrance to no science of experience through intellectual *fictiones*, guards morality |\nagainst false subtlety and promotes the practical, is advantageous to the beautiful sciences, promotes the inner knowledge of the human being.\n(s It is the demarcation of pure reason and the boundary-guard, to prevent it from, while it strays beyond its boundaries, confusing itself and disquieting religion and morals with its chimeras.)",["*fictiones*: “fictions”; “beautiful sciences” renders Schönen Wissenschaften.","“morality” renders Moral, “morals” Sitten; “knowledge” renders Kenntnis.","“it” in the addition (sie) is pure reason, grammatically; “strays beyond” renders ausschweift."]],"reflm:4465":["In the sciences of pure reason the philosophy of the present time is more critical than dogmatic, [more] an investigation of the subject [than] and thereby of the possibility of thinking an object for oneself.",["“subject” and “object” render the Latinate subiects and obiect."]],"reflm:4466":["The critique of pure reason a preliminary exercise for metaphysics in theoretical philosophy.",["“preliminary exercise” renders Vorübung; “philosophy” renders Weltweisheit."]],"reflm:4467":["[The speculation] The learnedness of reason and wisdom of reason: the first is speculative and [goes to] is a skill to resolve the *quaestiones* of reason; the second a determination of the worth of these exercises of the understanding and of their boundaries. Philosophy contains the former; the philosopher, who contains the end of philosophy in himself, is the latter. He is not an artist of reason, but a legislator of human reason.",["“learnedness of reason” renders Vernunftgelehrsamkeit, “wisdom of reason” Vernunftweisheit; “artist of reason” renders Vernunftkünstler.","*quaestiones*: “questions”; “exercises” renders Ausübungen."]],"reflm:4468":["That reason needs a discipline. That, if it is not trained, but spreads its branches wildly, it brings blossoms without fruits. |\nThat hence a master of discipline is necessary (not a taskmaster), who governs it. That without this discipline it does not agree with religion and morals, leads the great word and, in that it does not know itself, confuses the healthy understanding practiced in experiences.",["Zucht means both “discipline” and “cultivation” (of plants), and gezogen “trained” or “raised”; the play with Meister der Zucht and Zuchtmeister (“taskmaster”, “disciplinarian”) is lost.","“leads the great word” renders das große Wort führe, “talks big”, “holds the floor”.","“governs” renders gouvernirt; “agree” renders zusammenstimme."]],"reflm:4469":["The skeptical method is the best and only one for driving back the objections through retortion. Does a universal doubt then arise from it? No, but the pretensions of pure reason with regard to the conditions of the possibility of all things are driven back through it. Thereby all judgments of healthy reason with regard to the world and the practical get their great standing. Healthy reason, or the practical, will never let itself be persuaded that there is no God, if only the subtle does not strive to win the rank from it.",["“retortion” renders retorsion, turning an argument back on its author.","“standing” renders ansehen; “the practical” and “the subtle” render die practische and die subtile (feminine, sc. reason)."]],"reflm:4470":["The first question is: how concepts can arise in us [of things] which have become known to us through no appearance of the things themselves, or propositions which no experience has taught us.",[]],"reflm:4471":["To philosophy belong many cognitions as means and as [vari] cultures of our insight, but they do not belong to it as constituent parts. Philosophy itself is the science of determining for all this use of reason its ends and its bounds [to] or circle. The former are a piece of learnedness, the latter of wisdom. A different thing, after all, is a wise man and a philosopher. The latter goes only to the cognition and estimation of the essential ends, the former to the exercise, and is practical. The philosophical spirit and the spirit of philosophy.",["“[vari]” renders the broken-off [versch] (verschiedene?); “learnedness” renders Gelehrsamkeit; “estimation” renders Beurtheilung.","“The former … the latter” render Jene … diese; “the latter” in the following sentence renders Der letzte (the philosopher), “the former” der erste (the wise man)."]],"reflm:4472":["Empirical (g ground-) propositions are always synthetic. Universal ones are never empirical. Hence the former are always universal only *in tantum*. One makes new propositions from empirical ones *per analysin*.",["The addition Grund turns Sätze (“propositions”) into Grundsätze (“principles”).","*in tantum*: “to that extent”; *per analysin*: “through analysis”."]],"reflm:4473":["M XIX:\nIt is the question how we can represent to ourselves things completely *a priori*, i.e. independently of all experience (even *implicite*), and how we can grasp principles which are borrowed from no experience, consequently *a priori*; how it [possible i] comes about that to that which is merely a product of our self-isolating mind objects correspond and these objects are subject to those laws which we prescribe to them. That there are such cognitions *a priori*, pure mathematics and metaphysics teach; but it is an investigation of importance to have insight into the ground of their possibility. That a representation which is itself an effect of the object corresponds to it is surely to be [grasped] comprehended. But that something which is merely a birth of my brain should relate to an object as representation is not so clear. Further, that with an impression stemming in me from objects another is combined, consequently we connect one representation with the other in accordance with experience, is also graspable. But that we can, from ourselves*, [connect others with representations can so] connect properties and *praedicate* (g validly) with the represented objects, although no experience has shown them to us connected, is hard to have insight into. To say that a higher being has already wisely placed such concepts and principles in us is to ruin all philosophy. It must be sought in the nature of cognitions in general how a relation and connection is possible where yet only one of the *relatis* is given.\n*(s to be able to say something universally of objects without needing them to disclose it to us;)\nM XX:\nCognitions of experience are not mere impressions. We must ourselves think something in addition to the impressions in order that such arise. Hence there must after all be actions of cognition which go before experience and through which it is possible. Likewise experiences never give truly universal cognitions, because necessity is lacking to them. Now reason does need universal propositions for certain cognition. Hence certain universal judgments must lie in it even before experience.",["“objects” renders both Gegenstände and obiect; “combined” renders verbunden, “connect” verknüpfen, “connection” verknüpfung.","“[possible i]” renders the broken-off [moglich s(ey)]; *implicite*: “implicitly”; *praedicate*: “predicates”; *relatis*: “related terms”.","“have insight into” renders einsehen; “graspable” renders faßlich; “mind” renders Gemüths."]],"reflm:4474":["The experiences on the occasion of which we first form transcendental concepts indicate to us not their origin, but only the empirical condition of their arising. For since they are not drawn from experience, so their source must breaks off.",["“breaks off” (bricht ab) is the AA's note that the Reflexion stops here.","“on the occasion of which” renders bey denen; “indicate” renders zeigen … an."]],"reflm:4475":["Mathematics has no principles at all from experience, even the *applicata*, but only objects of experience.",["*applicata*: “applied” (sc. mathematics); “objects of experience” renders Erfahrungsobiecte."]],"reflm:4476":["M XXV:\nThe category of [Hypothes] synthesis will probably be called: *substantia* — *Causatum* (g *et independens*), *Compositum* (g *et simplex*).\nThe category of analysis: *Totale* (g *perfectum, completum*) *et* [*simplex*] *partiale*. (g [*Pars*] *Finitum et infinitum* [(*qvod non est pars*)].) (g *particularitas est infinita*.) (g *Vnum et plura*.)\nThe [Tes] idea of thesis: *Realitas*.\n(s *qvae, qvalis, qvanta*.)\nof synthesis: *Materia et forma*.\nof analysis: *qvantitas et qvalitas*.\nIn *analysi* [also a composition] the whole is earlier than the part, in *synthesi* the part earlier than the whole.\nM XXVI: |\n*idem et diversum* [(*qvantitas qvalitas*)]: *realitas, negatio consentiens et oppositum* (*forma affirmationis et negationis*) *externum et internum* (*qvantitas, qvalitas*) *absolutum et relativum*\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\nComparison\nCombination\nRelation\n{s {\n{\n{\n{\n{\n{\n{\n{\nIn judgments the relation 1. Of accord, fitting-together.\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}",["[Hypothes] and [Tes] are deleted word-beginnings (of Hypothesis and perhaps Thesis), left untranslated.","“Relation” renders Verhältnis; “combination” renders Verbindung.","The braces and bars reproduce the transcription's bracketing of the three lines Vergleichung, Verbindung, Verhältnis."]],"reflm:4477":["Analytic propositions can be proved from the *principio contradictionis* or *identitatis*, but not synthetic ones; whence then do we arrive at these? 1. Empirically. 2. Through pure intuition. 3. Through subjective conditions of the representation of the understanding.",["“Representation of the understanding” renders Verstandesvorstellung."]],"reflm:4478":["The *principium* of all synthetic judgments of pure reason (not of pure intuition) is: everything that contains the conditions without which an apprehension would be impossible is true. The *principium contradictionis* holds of all cognitions insofar as they are regarded [auf] as merely possible. I.e. what contradicts the concept that I could have of an object is false.",["[auf]: a deleted preposition (“on”, “as”), left in German since it governs nothing in the sentence as it stands.","“Object” renders Gegenstand."]],"reflm:4479":["Formulas are not axioms. The former are logical, the latter metaphysical; the former speak only of predicates and subjects in general, the latter give one.",["“Give one”: eines, neuter, with no noun expressed; what is given is left open."]],"reflm:4480":["To no subject does a predicate belong which contradicts an analytic predicate of the subject. To no subject does a predicate which contradicts the synthetic one of the same belong at the same time with it.\nWith regard to synthetic predicates, therefore, every thing is alterable, and the synthesis can be thought as successive determination. Possibility of concepts rests merely on the proposition of contradiction. That of synthesis on experience.",["“Alterable” renders veränderlich.","“Proposition of contradiction” renders Satz des Wiederspruchs."]],"reflm:4481":["Since all our simple concepts must be given through the senses (g and cannot be invented), hence their object is actual, everything that we think is possible, except that the relation of the concepts can constitute the impossibility. This relation can be logical or real; in the first case it is a contradiction.",["“Invented” renders erdichtet.","“Relation” renders Verhältnis."]],"reflm:4482":["When I negate, I think something — — affirm, — — —\n}\nboth are *logice* something, but the first *realiter* nothing, both\ntogether *logice* nothing; the zero is *logice* something, *realiter* nothing; *transscendentaliter* nothing: where there are no *data* for thinking, hence neither affirmation nor negation. Impossibility, however, is merely in contradiction. Hence it is impossible that the transcendental nothing be possible.",["The dashes and the brace stand in the source, which sets the two openings “Wenn ich verneine … bejahe” side by side."]],"reflm:4483":["In the outer margin, beginning beside the 3rd sentence of M § 18:\nEven to impossibility belongs something given. The first *data* are not cognized *a priori* as possible, but rather constitute the condition of all our judgments of possibility, so that only that is possible which agrees with the conditions [of the] *a priori* of empirical cognition. Logical possibility, *principium contradictionis*, is not objective, but of cognition. We can think up no possibility of intuition, of reality, of real relation, of the necessary, except insofar as [it] the principles thereof have been given in experience.",["“Think up” renders erdenken.","[it] renders the deleted es."]],"reflm:4484":["The possibility of that which one cannot cognize determinately *a priori* must be represented according to the analogy of nature, i.e. of the empirical.",["“Determinately” renders bestimmt, which could also be read with erkennen as “cognize determinately” or as “certainly”."]],"reflm:4485":["Alteration is given through experience and a problem for reason.",["“Alteration” renders Veränderung."]],"reflm:4486":["Opposites one after another do not contradict one another. hence the *succedens* is no *contradictorie oppositum* of the *antecedentis*, hence alterations are no proof of the possibility of the *contradictorie oppositi*, thus also not of contingency.",["“Opposites” renders the Latin opposita; the Latin case endings are kept as Kant wrote them."]],"reflm:4487":["Alterable are either substances or their state, but alteration has in these cases different significance; e.g. the shape, the place is alterable, signifies: the substances are alterable with regard to both.",[]],"reflm:4488":["We infer contingency in the world not so much from alteration as rather from dependence on certain conditions. Afterwards, when we take together everything that is necessary only under condition, we infer the contingency of the whole: *primus motor*.",["“Infer” renders schließen (aus, auf).","*primus motor*: “first mover”."]],"reflm:4489":["Beside and \nOne time contains a state, the other its opposite: is not *repugnans*, but *oppositum potentiale* or *disparatum*.\nFrom merely sensible ideas of succession nothing *intellectuale* can be inferred.",["The editorial location note “Neben und” is cut off in the source.","“Opposite” renders Gegentheil."]],"reflm:4490":["*Omnitudo collectiva est completudo. distributiva vniversalitas.*\n[*Multitudo completa est omnitudo.*] Universality against particularity (g exception).\n*Vnitas in coniunctione plurium completa est totalitas.* Totality.\n(*Vnum completum est totum.*) *Completudo in coniunctione plurium: Totalitas. defectus.*\nWith the whole there is a taking-together: *coniunctio*, with the *composito* a putting-together.\nA number is no *compositum*; hence a body is *totum reale, non ideale*.",["“Totality” renders Die Allheit, Kant's German beside the Latin totalitas.","“Universality” renders Allgemeinheit; “taking-together” Zusammennehmen; “putting-together” zusammensetzen.","Latin: “Collective allness is completeness; distributive, universality.” “Unity complete in the conjunction of many is totality.”"]],"reflm:4491":["The problematic concept of absolute totality, necessity.\nSuch concepts one can indeed think, but not determine and carry out.",["“Totality” renders totalitaet; “carry out” renders ausführen."]],"reflm:4492":["*Conceptus substantiae est terminator non entium, sed statuum.*\nThis lies in our reason; now comes the application of the same to the senses. What is the real subject, namely that which serves the various [prae] accidents and modifications, the former without distinction of time, the latter in the relation of the same, as the very same fixed point?",["Latin: “The concept of substance is the terminator not of beings, but of states.”","“In the relation of the same”: im Verhaltnis derselben; derselben may refer to time or to the accidents.","[prae]: a deleted word-beginning (perhaps of praedicaten), left untranslated."]],"reflm:4493":["58'. E II 585.\nM 58:\nThere are only three kinds of *respectus reales* possible:\n1. of the sequel to the ground, *dependentiae ab una et causalitatis ab altera parte*;\n2. of the part to the [whole composite] whole;\n3. of the *accidens* to the substance.\nIn (g all) three arises unity: of subordination*, of coordination and of inherence (g of many accidents in one subject. The I is the intuition of a substance.)\nAll three have their boundaries:\n1. the *independens* and *absolute necessarium*;**\n2. the *totalitas absoluta* (*completa.*[*Mundus*] *infinitum*), i.e. a synthesis which is not possible [further] greater;\n3. the *substantiale*.\n[Du] The first concept indicates how things belong through one another, the second: to one another, the third: in one another. All three are *termini*. The first [*a priori*] the *necessarium* and its *oppositum*: the absolute or *primum contingens* (*libertas*); the second the *universitatem*: everything taken together, and its *oppositum*: no taking-together, *simplex*; the third substantiality and its *oppositum*: mere relation.\nAll three are not to be had insight into; the first, because the condition of necessity or on the other side all necessity is lacking; the second, because the *terminus* of the synthesis, and in the third the predicates are lacking. |\nM 58': |\nAll these relations are only the realized logical ones of the relation of subject and predicate, of the *antecedente* to the *conseqvente* and of the universality of the concept of the subject. Subject and predicate with *est* appended behind signifies *existere*. The *identitas*: necessity. Or only universality: necessity, particularity: contingency.\nM 58:\n*(g One can also think a subordinated division.)\n**(g It is not yet clear that the first ground (e.g. freedom) is something necessary.)",["The single and double asterisks are Kant's footnote marks, not italics.","“Sequel” renders Folge; “not to be had insight into” renders nicht einzusehen.","[Du] is a deleted word-beginning, left untranslated."]],"reflm:4494":["All outer appearances are *phaenomena substantiata*, because one treats them as substances.",["“Treats” renders tractirt."]],"reflm:4495":["That something is a subject and no predicate (of the impression, *apparentia*) of another, we can only through the active verb »I«, hence through consciousness, cognize; hence *corpora, h.e. externe apparentia, sunt phaenomena substantiata, h.e. permanentia substrata ceteris phaenomenis*.",["Erkennen is capitalized in the source but construed as the infinitive governed by können.","Latin: “bodies, i.e. external appearances, are substantiated phenomena, i.e. persisting substrata for the other phenomena.”"]],"reflm:4496":["Around and \nThree *principia*. 1. [Each] In everything actual there is the relation of a substance to accident (*inhaerentia*); 2. of the ground to the sequel (*dependentia*); 3. of the parts and of the composition (composition).\nThere are thus three presuppositions: of the subject, of the ground and of the parts; and three real *modi*: of insition, of subordination and of composition. Hence also three first *principia*: 1. subject that is no predicate; 2. ground that is no sequel; 3. unity that is not (g in itself) composite.",["The editorial location note “Um und” is cut off in the source.","“Insition” renders insition (Latin insitio, “ingrafting”)."]],"reflm:4497":["The community of the boundary of extension is contact or of the impenetrability of the contact of matter.\nPoints cannot touch one another.",["The syntax of “oder der Undurchdringlichkeit der Berührung der materie” is unclear in the source and is kept so.","“Contact” renders Berührung."]],"reflm:4498":["On the upper margin above the running head:\nAll composition in a body cannot be canceled. |\nFor the body is possible only according to the determinations of space, i.e. [the part] such that every constituent part of it occupies a space. All composition in a *phaenomeno substantiato* is not possible to cancel.",["“Cancel” renders aufheben.","“All … cannot” keeps the German scope (Alle … kan nicht): it may mean “not all” or “none”."]],"reflm:4499":["Above, beside and \nThe substantiality of the parts of a body is only respective; namely each part exists without inhering in the other. In themselves, however, they are not substances, but their *phaenomena*. The *phaenomenon*, however, of each substance, if it consists in the filling of space, is possible only through the condition of space, namely only according to laws of continuity and not through composition out of the simple, but always as a magnitude [which] whose parts are all in turn magnitudes.",["The editorial location note “Über, neben und” is cut off in the source."]],"reflm:4500":["The opinion of Leibniz, that bodies are *phaenomena*, offers nothing other than that the idea of body is sensitive, whose intellectual substratum is unknown, i.e. that it expresses nothing but a concept which arises from the relation of unknown substances to the sensitive mode of cognizing. If this is taken for intellectual, it is called *phaenomenon substantiatum*. But if this is avoided, bodies as *phaenomena* [are in] do not consist of simples, i.e. according to the concept of space, in dividing [not a terminus] the division is without terminus. But, conceived intellectually, it consists of monads. But this thesis has no Influence on the consequences, i.e. it is not a principle of empirical cognition. but only indicates that the nexus of the soul with bodies is not objectively, but only according to the cognitive form, different from the nexus of the soul with simple substances generally. The sensitive form removed, the intellectual remains through |\npre-established harmony. And therefore both generation and death are nothing but different representative states, nor does it demand an explanation of the nexus with heterogeneous substances, but of the heterogeneity of the cognitive form. Space, however, is the formal element of phenomena; if it is taken for the very real conception of the nexus of substances, it is called *phaenomenon intellectuatum*, namely if it is extended further than to the sensitive mode of cognizing.\nIf sensitive cognition should be extended in its species to infinity, it nevertheless remains sensitive, and bodies will never be found empirically to consist of simples; therefore monads are of no use in physics, and in metaphysics are of negative use, so that one may beware lest, by taking *phaenomena* for the real constitution of objects, sensitive axioms become as it were intellectual.",["The source is Latin throughout; “sensitive” renders sensitivus, “cognitive form” forma cognoscitiva.","“Conceived intellectually, it consists”: conceptum is neuter with no noun expressed; the subject is left open as in the Latin.","sentitiva (for sensitiva) and the capital in Influit stand in the source."]],"reflm:4501":["All composition can be canceled: namely as much as has been composed can also be separated. But then there remain simple parts, if the composition has taken place from simple parts, or magnitude-parts, if it has taken place from such. The simple is opposed to magnitude, and the composite to the separated.\nBut as concerns all parts [of the comp] of magnitude, there is of the *continuo* only *omnitudinem collectivam*; every magnitude contains the possibility of a composition, but is not on that account composite.",["[of the comp] renders the deleted des Zus (presumably the start of des Zusammengesetzten).","“Magnitude-parts” renders Größentheile."]],"reflm:4502":["On the upper margin:\nIf the *phaenomena* of different substances (*qvae sunt in locis diversis*) are actually many substances, then all matter consists of sim|ple\nparts. The multitude of these simple parts cannot be infinite, for otherwise they would, by moving away from one another, increase their extension to infinity; and, if between two there is a finite velocity, the velocity of the surface would be infinite. If they are not substances, but only *phaenomena*, then they do not consist of simple parts at all. Accordingly they are divisible to infinity, but no assignable part is actually infinite. If the multitude of the parts makes up a finite number of simple substances breaks off.",["“Assignable” renders angeblicher, apparently in the sense of angebbar (“that can be specified”) rather than “alleged”.","“breaks off” renders bricht ab: the note ends mid-sentence."]],"reflm:4503":["Time and space alone permit boundaries, but not of totality. The first beginning and the outermost boundary of the world are equally incomprehensible. For the first is a being and not-being at once, and both seem to indicate an absolute time and space, i.e. something that bounds there and yet contains nothing.",["“Something that bounds there”: was da begrentzt may also be read “something that is there bounded”."]],"reflm:4504":["Time goes over everything and is the form of inner sense.",[]],"reflm:4505":["The doctrines of space and time have the positive: that the propositions which bear on them with regard to sensibility cannot be reformed by reason, because they are no things; that they are entirely |\ncertain and evident. But the negative: that they restrict the application to the world of sense.",["“They” in “because they are no things” may refer to space and time or to the propositions, as in the German.","“World of sense” renders Sinnenwelt."]],"reflm:4506":["With space: the place, the situation, the position, the region. The place is a point; the situation is the relation of a place to the places of others; the breaks off.",["“Place, situation, position, region” render Ort, Lage, Stellung, Gegend.","The note breaks off after the article die (“the”)."]],"reflm:4507":["Space is neither a thing itself, nor an actual real relation whereby one thing posits something in the other; consequently it is no concept of the understanding; for that does have some object or other, it thus does not go to the object, but the subject, and indeed not sensation, but the form of the senses.",["“Thing” renders both Sache and Ding.","“Object” renders both Gegenstand and the Latinate obiect (the second occurrence).","“It thus does not go”: er may refer to space or to the concept of the understanding."]],"reflm:4508":["The *axiomata* of space and time with regard to sensibility are certain and intuitive. The principles: everything is somewhere and sometime, those of shape and place, are mere conditions of sensibility.",["“Intuitive” renders anschauend; “principles” renders Grundsätze."]],"reflm:4509":["All spaces are at the same time and all times one after another; in space a thing is not in many places [at the same time], only one after another.",["“At the same time” renders zugleich in both places."]],"reflm:4510":["Are not acquired but innate intuitions. (s Are *conceptus originarii*, not *derivativi* (*abstrahendo*).)\nSensation\nAppearance\nConcept\nHardness\nShape\nSubstance\nof the body.\nThe play. Alteration without alterable things.",["“Harte” is printed without umlaut; read as Härte, “hardness”, though “hard” (hart) is also possible.","Veränderung / veränderlich = alteration / alterable."]],"reflm:4511":["Beside and \nIs space before things? Certainly. For the law of coordination is before things and lies at their ground.\nBut is space sensible without things, or can one notice it only through things? Yes, hence empty space as an object of the senses, e.g. between planets, is impossible.",["“sensible” renders empfindbar, “capable of being sensed” (from Empfindung), not sinnlich.","“Yes” may answer either alternative of the preceding double question; left as ambiguous as the German."]],"reflm:4512":["Under and \nPure space is merely the potential relation and is represented before things, but not as something actual. Empty space without filling is possible; but absolute space, as that against which creatures stand in actual relation, is impossible. For no substance is present anywhere without acting, and indeed externally; but in absolute space there are not correlates.",["“relation” renders both relation (Kant's Latinate word) and Verhältnis.","“correlates”: Kant's correlate, the German plural of Correlat."]],"reflm:4513":["Space and time do not yet give anything actual. Only sensation furnishes it. Hence the real understanding is an activity, parallel to sensation.",["“furnishes” renders giebt an die Hand, literally “gives to hand”."]],"reflm:4514":["According to reason time is the *nexus* of the *coordinatio*, but according to sensibility of the *subordinatio*. The first and last is to be specified arbitrarily in space.",[]],"reflm:4515":["Space is the ground of the possibility of relations, indeed even of their necessity. Possibility and actuality is not distinguished in space and time. In both the part is possible only through the whole. Both are so connected that space is at all time, i.e. necessary. Time concerns being in general, space external being. Both are the only given grounds of synthesis without any inferences.",["The singular verb of the German (“ist ... nicht unterschieden”) is kept.","“concerns” renders geht auf, literally “goes to”; “being” renders das Seyn.","“inferences” renders Schlüsse."]],"reflm:4516":["Time and space are what is necessary in intuition. Time expresses the whole existence of things.",["“existence” renders Daseyn."]],"reflm:4517":["We cannot use the [verb] word *est* otherwise than as a verb. “God is” as distinguished from “will be.” The predicates of possible things can only be used *in praesenti*.",["“verb” renders Zeitwort, literally “time-word”, the German school term for the verb.","“predicates”: Kant's praedicate, the Latinate German plural."]],"reflm:4518":["That time is the form of inner sense is to be seen from this, because one can indeed have it in thought, but never intuit it as something external, as with extension. The substances are in space, their state (*accidentia*) in time. All predicates have as copula: *est, fuit, erit*.",["*est, fuit, erit*: “is, was, will be”."]],"reflm:4519":["The synthetic propositions of space do not lie in the universal concept of space, any more than the chemical propositions of experience about |\ngold lie in the universal concept of it, but are drawn from the intuition of it or found in the intuition of it.",["“propositions of experience” renders Erfahrungssätze.","“lie” in the second clause is not repeated in the German; “of it” (desselben) is as ambiguous as the German in each place."]],"reflm:4520":["The first cause of all existence is eternity, and the relation of the existence of contingent things to the all-comprehending existence is time. [The first] Insofar as this cause of all existence is the cause of all things, this is omnipresence, and the relation of the created things to it is space.\nHow does it come about that our sensibility contains determinations in which reason sees its own fundamental concepts. That is because the sensible form is possible through the same grounds which the understanding needs for comprehension.",["“all-comprehending” renders alles begreifende; “comprehension” renders Begreifung.","The question closes with a period in the German."]],"reflm:4521":["The present concept of the world serves rather to put in order the idea of a substantial whole and of an isolated one, than to prove the existence of any determinate cause thereby.",["“an isolated one” renders eines isolirten, which may mean an isolated whole or an isolated substantial whole."]],"reflm:4522":["The world (g that) can be no part of another whole is the world in the transcendental sense. The world that is no part of an actual whole is the world in the physical sense. Because the [possible] possible is distinguished from the actual only in that in the |\nformer the *conditiones* are not considered in thoroughgoing determination, a whole world that has no other *conditiones* outside itself than in the absolutely necessary being must also have no other boundaries of actuality than the inner conditions of possibility. From a world in this sense one could infer to a single cause and its all-sufficiency, further to the oneness of this world. But from the world that is not the absolute universe one can infer no more than from a *composito substantiali* in general. Finally from a mere *multitudine rerum finitarum* no more than from a single *finito*.\nThe concept of completion is the last in increase and arises from the concept of all, both in *synthesi* and *analysi*. The boundary concept is that which remains in the diminution of the *conditionum*: e.g. a ground that is no consequence; the absolutely necessary; the absolutely (regardless of all conditions) contingent, i.e. freedom.",["“sense” renders Verstand in in transscendentalem / physischem Verstande; “single” renders einige, “oneness” Einigkeit.","“consequence” renders Folge; Kant's Latin is kept in its inflected forms.","Vollendungsbegrif / Grentzbegrif = concept of completion / boundary concept."]],"reflm:4523":["Above and below the heading “Notio mundi”:\nPrivate happiness and religion must in the human being be derived either from a universally valid inner or outer principle, if it is to agree.",["The German verbs (muß, soll) are singular, so “it” may take the two together; “principle” renders princip, not Grundsatz.","“agree” renders stimmen, literally “be in tune”."]],"reflm:4524":["From our concept of the world only the concept of the relation of each part of a *compositi substantialis* to the whole is put in order. We can think no effects of a *substantiae* or *compositi* except [under presupposition] according to universal rules of the nature of the substance itself or of its cause. In a whole, because the parts stand *in commercio*, the aim at each part in particular cannot subsist with that at the other, yet in such a way that their community |\nwould agree with it, or they have no community at all. The greater the number of substances is, the harder it is to bring out an accord from individual aims, until finally in general the unity in the accord of the substances must follow from the unity of the whole, and the aim at the whole and the nature put into it for that reason can alone contain the supreme ground of the possibility of the agreement of the aims at the parts. One cannot begin from the aims at each part in order to bring out a clock through their composition. The whole must have a nature which contains universal laws according to which it is only possible that the [parts] aim of the parts becomes concordant. The unity of the aim at the whole can alone become the ground of the harmony of the partial aims. Even in each individual substance the occurrences must flow from it according to universal laws, which is its nature. Only through this is it a substance, that in it itself lies the universal ground of the accidents; otherwise it would always agree with another. Nature is just that with regard to the real determinations and alterations which space is with regard to the *principii* of all possible relations according to laws of sensibility.\n(g *commercium*. Community. *nexus reciprocus*.)",["The sentence “In a whole ... no community at all” is obscure in the German and is kept so; “would agree” renders stimmete.","stimmen / Zusammenstimmung / Übereinstimmung / stimmig = agree / accord / agreement / concordant; Absicht = aim.","“Community” renders Gemeinschaft, Kant's German for *commercium*; Kant's Latin is kept in its inflected forms."]],"reflm:4525":["The world is the absolute whole of possible experience. We can quite well think an absolute world-whole, only not in space and time. The absolute whole in appearance is a contradiction.",[]],"reflm:4526":["At the very top of the page. \nRelation is a determination of a being by which (g or) it can act on another or suffer from it. Reciprocal active relation, by which it can as much act as |\nsuffer. *Nexus* is the relation of what actually acts on what suffers. In any whole whatever there is a *nexus*.",["Latin in the original; “suffer” renders pati, “be acted upon”, and “what suffers” patiens.","*relatio actu agentis*: “actually” may attach to “acts” or to the relation as a whole."]],"reflm:4527":["The universal is not always borrowed from the particular. The [math] geometrical properties are not borrowed from the particular determinations of the figures, but these from universal space. Likewise with the science of number. In the divine will everything is determined according to universal rules. And if in the *substantia* I cancel the universality of the ground, it ceases to be substance. This real universality is the derivation of the partial determinations from the whole as a ground. It is then a standing *principium*. If the things are already given, cognition goes from the particular to the universal. But from the ground through which the things are given, the inference goes from the universal to the particular and, with the whole, from the *universitate* to the *individuo*.",["“science of number” renders Zahlwissenschaft; “cancel” renders aufhebe.","“inference” renders Schluß."]],"reflm:4528":["Series of the understanding. The All of it requires a supreme cause.\nSeries of the senses. The series itself always requires something preceding.\nNothing happens in the world *per fatum, per casum, per saltum*. (g Rules of the) order of nature. (s *non existunt — non fiunt — non connectuntur per saltum*.)",["*per fatum, per casum, per saltum*: “through fate, through chance, through a leap”; *non existunt — non fiunt — non connectuntur per saltum*: “they do not exist — do not come to be — are not connected through a leap”.","“All” renders das All, not Allheit (totality)."]],"reflm:4529":["The boundaries of appearance cannot appear. Hence |\nno finite world can be represented to the senses. If I stretch a hand out of the world, then to that extent the larger world appears; the remaining space does not appear, except insofar as [I it] I put something into it. Thus appearance without an object (the empty) is not possible. But an infinite time *a parte ante* is indeed necessary in appearance, and in the concept of reason mathematical magnitude does not take place at all; but therewith the understanding cannot suffice for the complete exposition of sensibility.",["The deletion [ichs] is “ich es”, “I it”.","“concept of reason” renders Vernunftbegriff; “therewith” renders dabey, which may also mean “in this case”."]],"reflm:4530":["The *saltus spurius* is attraction at a distance. For here the *intermedia* are not necessarily *causae intermediae*; for the *intermedia* are only places.",["*saltus spurius*: “spurious leap”; *intermedia*: “intermediates”; *causae intermediae*: “intermediate causes”."]],"reflm:4531":["Two (g successive) states are never immediately combined with one another. For two states are in two different points of time. But between two moments there is a time. In which states, then, was the thing within this time? [It is the] The states are the *extrema* of a line. There is thus an infinite series of intermediate states. (But whether I cannot already consider two states as two successive series that are immediately connected with one another, just as 2 lines with one another?) All of this cannot be compared with the rational propositions; but the question is also not rational, but only: which are the laws and conditions of appearance. Meanwhile the difficulty is also not from the understanding, but from another law of sensibility, namely to regard a succession as a series whose members can be counted through and which thus could never be infinite.",["verbunden / verknüpft = combined / connected; “question” renders Kant's qvaestion.","“which thus could never be infinite”: the singular könte refers to the series rather than its members."]],"reflm:4532":["Different power and honor\nDistributes, by gradation, the innumerable hosts,\nWhich, unequally sated with the luster of the imparted light,\nStand in long order from God to the desolate nothing.",["Verse. The singular vertheilt leaves open whether “power and honor” are subject and “the hosts” object, or the reverse.","“by gradation” renders nach Stufenart, literally “after the manner of steps”."]],"reflm:4533":["There are negative or positive oppositions; with the latter [is] the combination of both makes a negative opposition, which is logical. There, there is continuity; between magnanimity and avarice to find the *punctum flexus contrarii*. Another object is another direction.",["*punctum flexus contrarii*: “point of contrary flexure”.","“object” renders Kant's obiect, not Gegenstand."]],"reflm:4534":["Around M § 393, \nSimple beings (as such) can not at all be parts of the sensible world. For then they would be parts of the object of the outer senses, i.e. of the extended; but the extended does not consist of simple parts. Hence every principle of life must be counted among the intelligible, consequently also the soul. Of the intelligible, however, one knows nothing but the relation of the appearances, to which it is the substratum, in the sensible world. What it is, therefore, outside of this (after death) is absolutely uncognizable. On this is also grounded our ignorance with regard to all organized beings and beings organizing matter, whose possibility, which rests on a principle of life, cannot be seen into at all. Simple beings have no place in the world.",["“sensible world” renders Sinnenwelt, literally “world of the senses”; “principle of life” renders Lebens princip.","“relation” renders Beziehung; “seen into” renders eingesehen."]],"reflm:4535":["At the very top of the page, opposite the running head and M § 393:\nWhether the world is in empty space or not, (g is no empirical question); for no empirical conditions of the determination are possible whether a space is empty or not (in empty space no outer experience would be possible either). Whether it is possible that all matter would be taken away out of space through annihilation is an intellectual question; whether through motion, is a physical one, which presupposes whether [the impenetrabi] a body that is moved takes all the matter with it that fills the space, or whether one [unent] is not immovable.",["The deleted fragment [unent] is left in German; the word it began cannot be told.","“one” renders eine, which may stand for “matter” (Materie, feminine); ob nicht ... sey may also be read “whether perhaps one is immovable”."]],"reflm:4536":["At the upper margin and beside as well as \nThe question whether the bodies outside me are something actual is answered thus: bodies are outside my sensibility no bodies (*phaenomena*), and so they are only in the power of representation of sensing beings. Whether something outside me corresponds to these appearances of theirs is a question of the cause of this appearance and not of the existence of what appears itself. This existence as of an object is the representation of the interconnection with all appearances according to laws.",["“power of representation” renders Vorstellungskraft; “sensing” renders empfindender.","“what appears itself”: selbst may attach to “what appears” or to “the existence”."]],"reflm:4537":["All *commercium* of substances is* a harmony of their activities. The ground of this harmony therefore cannot be in one alone, but in the common ground of all; the *commercium* breaks off.\n*(s the state of different substances insofar as they flow *mutuo* into one another: *realis* or *idealis*.)",["The first asterisk is Kant's reference mark, not italics; the second line is the note it refers to.","“flow into” renders einfließen; *mutuo*: “mutually”; *realis* or *idealis*: “real or ideal”.","“breaks off” renders bricht ab, probably the editor’s note that the text stops after “the *commercium*”, its angle brackets lost in the transcription."]],"reflm:4538":["*sola non successio plurium existentium non ideo est simultaneitas.*\nThe *commercium substantiarum* is either *absolutum* or *hypotheticum*: the unconditioned community or the conditioned. The former merely through their existing, or by means of a third. The former is *systema arbitrarium*.\nThe hypothetically established *commercium* is [either *naturalis*] at all times *a causa extramundana*; but either [*per*] as *ordo naturae* (*mutuae dependentiae substantiarum secundum regulas generales*) or [as] *supernaturaliter*, so that there actually is no *commercium naturale*. The first is *influxus physicus*, the other *harmonia idealis vel praestabilita vel occasionalis*.",["The Latin first line: “the non-succession alone of several existing things is not on that account simultaneity.”","*ordo naturae (mutuae dependentiae substantiarum secundum regulas generales)*: “order of nature (of the mutual dependence of substances according to general rules)”; *a causa extramundana*: “from an extramundane cause”.","Jene, “the former”, stands twice; the German does not settle whether both refer to the same member."]],"reflm:4539":["Beside and \nThe cruder *influxus* is that according to which a substance influencing another infuses accidents into the other, so that they are not sustained by the patient, but actuated by an alien force. Such a passion, however, could also be devised in the system of occasional causes. But from the impossibility of such a passion, and because accidents do not inhere except through their own force, it can be concluded that substances cannot be in *commercium* except by means of the same sustaining cause.",["Latin in the original; *influxus crassior*: “cruder influx”.","“passion” renders passio, being acted upon; “the patient” renders patiente, what is acted upon.","“could be devised” renders poterat confingi, which may also mean “could be feigned”."]],"reflm:4540":["Intuition is either through inner or outer sense. Pure outer intuition always has the idea of space annexed.",["Latin in the original; “intuition” renders intuitus."]],"reflm:4541":["The general grounds of sensible choice are inclinations.\nThe general grounds of rational choice are principles.\n[The concept of freedom is from a] According to theoretical principles the concept of hypothetically necessary choice, according to practical ones that of absolutely independent choice, is more probable.",["“choice” renders Willkühr throughout.","“principles” renders both Grundsetze and principien."]],"reflm:4542":["The beginning of acting must be met with in the world; everything, therefore, that is not free has no first beginning. whether a being whose *subsistentia* is *derivativa* could not be independent in its actions? *spontaneitas simplex*.",["“acting” renders wirken, which can also be read “effecting”.","*subsistentia derivativa*: “derivative subsistence”; *spontaneitas simplex*: “simple spontaneity”."]],"reflm:4543":["Blind is what one cannot have insight into.\nBlind necessity (fate) (g providence), blind chance.",["“have insight into” renders einsehen; “fate” Schiksal; “chance” Zufall."]],"reflm:4544":["Everything is either in and for itself or externally necessary. Contingency is only respective. But free actions of contingent substances are to have neither of the two necessities.",["“respective” renders respectiv, i.e. relative (Latin respectivus)."]],"reflm:4545":["In the lower margin:\nThe concept of conditionally necessary choice is only a *hypothesis* of theory and must be assumed in order to explain free actions as *phaenomena*. The concept of unconditionally free choice is a *postulatum practicum*, which everyone actually assumes, and where he contradicts himself if he demands of others a use of the understanding.",["*postulatum practicum*: “practical postulate”.","“assumed” / “assumes” render angenomen / annimt."]],"reflm:4546":["In the upper margin:\nfreedom is either of the faculty or of the state (dogs in the kennel); likewise it is either internal or external; the latter is independence from things that compel.\n(s The will cannot be compelled, but choice can; this, however, not simply, but in a certain respect.)",["Latin: libertas … facultatis vel status; independentia a cogentibus; “will” renders voluntas, “choice” arbitrium.","“simply … in a certain respect” renders simpliciter … secundum qvid.","“(dogs in the kennel)” is German in the source: Hunde im Zwinger."]],"reflm:4547":["Beside and \nfreedom therefore is either of choice or of power. freedom of choice is internal and [proper] such through itself. freedom of power is externally such. freedom of choice is practical [or transsce] or transcendental the former is independence from stimuli, the latter from all external necessitation. acts of choice that are simply spontaneous are original, those in a certain respect are derivative. Necessitation of choice is either through stimuli and is pathological, or through motives and is practical.\nSpontaneity of choice simply such in the original being is a necessity of the acts of choice independent of everything external, therefore it is freedom.",["Latin: libertas arbitrii / potestatis; “original” renders originarii (and originario), “derivative” derivativi; “motives” motiva.","“simply … in a certain respect” renders simpliciter … secundum qvid.","No punctuation stands before “the former” (prior) in the source."]],"reflm:4548":["Beside and \nFreedom is twofold: either of choice or of power. The first is internal, the second is merely external.\nThe freedom of choice (g *arbitrium liberum*) is *independentia a stimulis* and is called practical freedom. Opposed to it stands the *arbitrium brutum necessitatum a stimulis*. (The *arbitrium liberum* [is either] has either *spontaneitatem practicam* or *transscendentalem*.)\nThe *necessitatio arbitrii bruti* is pathological, that of the *arbitrii liberi* is [intellectual] practical. The former’s *causae impulsivae* are *stimuli*, the latter’s *motiva*. The *actus arbitrii liberi* are either *originarii* or *derivativi*; the former is the transcendental, the latter the merely practical freedom.",["“choice” renders Willkühr, “power” gewalt.","*arbitrium brutum necessitatum a stimulis*: “animal choice necessitated by stimuli”; *causae impulsivae*: “impelling causes”.","*actus … originarii* or *derivativi*: “original” or “derivative” acts."]],"reflm:4549":["Above and \nFreedom consists in the (g subjective) independence of choice from everything that flows in upon our senses internally and externally. Right is the objective independence of our choice from another’s, i.e. the limitation of every choice through the condition of mutual agreement and the necessity of the actions that flow from these conditions. The wisest and kindest human being, who arranged everything for our best, does not on that account have a right over us.\n(s One also may not transform the title of right into that of kindness and religion. The latter is to serve only for execution.)",["“Right” renders Recht; “agreement” Einstimung; “kindness” Gütigkeit.","“The latter” (Die letztere) can refer to religion alone or to kindness and religion together."]],"reflm:4550":["Beside and. \nThat something preeminently [pleases or] gratifies a human being, he cannot help; but that he lets it please him is to be ascribed to himself.\nHence he who is malicious by natural temperament reproaches himself for it, because the blame does not fall on what is innate, but on the lack of will.\nIt seems that a human being must give account to the spirit that is in him. He must therefore surely have a faculty to obey its command or not. This latter is freedom. The soul senses, the mind judges [a poster] or estimates *a posteriori*, the spirit *a priori*.",["“lets it please him” renders sich … belieben läßt, which can also mean “chooses it”.","“natural temperament” renders Naturel; “reproaches” reprochirt.","“mind” renders Gemüth, “spirit” Geist; “senses” empfindet."]],"reflm:4551":["In the estimation of actions that have happened, [impute] we nevertheless presuppose that they all have their determining (although not (g still by others) determined) grounds, and impute them, although they may flow from the innate character of the human being; indeed even: we impute them all the more, partly because one |\nyet knows that each has a higher choice, under which even this character stands, partly because each yet acts according to his own pleasure and inclination and thus not against his own pleasure, he may do good and evil, and has insight that according to the rule of the higher will the punishments and rewards fit such a character, and he thus cognizes himself as worthy of them, since he can divest himself of himself, hence also of his inclinations.",["“against” renders wieder (wider).","“pleasure” renders Belieben; “divest himself of” sich … entschlagen.","The subject of “has insight” (einsieht) is grammatically “each”; the sentence is not complete in its construction."]],"reflm:4552":["Around M § 770:\nIt is the question whether there is an organically forming nature (*epigenesis*) or merely one that forms mechanically and chemically.\nIt seems: to this there belongs a spirit, on account of the unity of the relation of all parts, as to their generation, to each single one. But is there not also in animals and plants a spiritual animating being. In such a way one would even have to assume in the first chaos animating spirit, in order to explain the different animals, which now can only propagate themselves.\nOf that breaks off.",["“their generation” (ihrer Erzeugung) can refer to the parts or to nature.","“Of that” (Von dem) is all that stands before the editorial “bricht ab”."]],"reflm:4553":["It is not clear that the human soul could not be begotten [through th] from its parents’, although simple, substance. There is no need of materialism.",["“from its parents’ … substance” renders aus der Eltern ihrer … Substanz, an irregular construction.","“[through th]” renders the deleted fragment [durch de]."]],"reflm:4554":["Above and \nFirst the question is: whether the human being or merely [the same] the soul will live after death, and wherein the identity of the substance and |\nof the person consists. [In the hum] Does the future life depend merely on the body or the soul. Resurrection. Second, if the problem concerns only the future life of the human soul: whether it merely lives after death (g contingently) or is immortal (g necessarily) (universality, eternity). The first by means of reason from the ends of God, the second from the nature of the soul: a. from particular observation, b. from the nature of a spirit in general.\nGrounds of the problem are either explanation of the *phaenomenorum* (g speculative) (e.g. appearance of spirits) or the concern of the human being: that of self-love or of morality.\nEpicurus held it necessary for philosophers to decide nothing about it, so that 1. the motives would be purely moral, 2. no superstitious pretense would produce false terrors and hopes. This is grounded on too high an opinion of humanity. [The Stoi] Socrates had considered the future state merely problematically. The Stoics in the beginning also abstracted from it. Without it the order of nature does not agree with the moral one, and well-being and good conduct are separated. Christian law makes it the chief point of view of all motives of this life.",["“identity” renders Einerleyheit; “morality” Sittlichkeit.","“vor philosophen” can be read “for philosophers” or “as philosophers”.","“Without it” (Ohne sie) can refer to the future state or to the future life."]],"reflm:4555":["Life does not belong to mere matter. The life appropriate to a certain nature. Freedom. Bestial (animal.), Spiritual life.",["“Bestial (animal.)” renders Thierisch (animalisch.), a German word and its Latinate equivalent."]],"reflm:4556":["Above, beside and \nLife consists in the inner sufficiency of spontaneity for representations and desires; but under external hindrances this substance can sleep, although it lives. The body does not contain the condition of the possibility of the life of the soul or even of only one of its |\nactivities, but is a hindrance, to the overcoming of which the soul is nevertheless bound. The disposition of the body is a diminution of this hindrance; hence thinking is all the freer breaks off.\n(g since we can institute experiences only in the body, they will prove dependence more than independence.)",["“institute experiences” renders die Erfahrungen … anstellen, which can also mean “conduct experiments”.","“sufficiency” renders Zulänglichkeit."]],"reflm:4557":["Above and \nI cannot say: I know that there is another life; but rather: I believe it. This much I know, that no one could prove that there is no other life. One also cannot say that one believes there is no other life; but rather: one only cannot believe that there is one. This indicates more a still-continuing lack of insight than an insight into the opposite. The hope of another world is a necessary *hypothesis* of reason with regard to ends and a necessary *hypothesis* of the heart with regard to morality. It is thus practically set on secure grounds, but theoretically obscure and uncertain. Beyond this world one can know only what is incumbent on us in this one.",["“know” renders weiß / wissen; “believe” glauben; “insight” Einsicht.","“morality” renders moralitaet."]],"reflm:4558":["He who cognizes morality as important and hence a holy law and a just court together with kind government, is easily convinced of the future. He to whom this seems to have only a conditioned necessity cannot be convinced at all. For why should precisely the enjoyment of a life that comes down to mere enjoyment be continued still after death?",["“court” renders gericht, which can also mean “judgment”."]],"reflm:4559":["That there be a being after death that is conscious of all our determi|nations\nof life and ascribes them to itself as past, does not yet prove that this being is I. Here it seems the consciousness must be interrupted, at least yet be continued through the dull interval at awakening. Inner sense must endure uninterrupted [although] (as regards sensation), although consciousness is interrupted as regards reflection. Thus the constant personality in this life notwithstanding all alterations proves that the soul’s nature survives the body. Now the I cannot be a passive sensation. Consequently it is a self-active one and does not depend on the body.",["“must be interrupted” is as the text stands; the following “at least … continued” suggests a “not” (nicht unterbrochen) may be missing.","“dull” renders stumpfe; “survives” überstehe.","“it is a self-active one” renders sie ist eine selbstthatige: the feminine sie refers to Empfindung, not to das Ich."]],"reflm:4560":["Another world either in the cosmographical or in the cosmological sense, as to region or as to kind. The latter either objective or subjective. The transition either little by little or sudden. Community with this world or not. Ghosts.",["“sense” renders Verstande; “region” Gegend."]],"reflm:4561":["Personality is either enduring (continuous) or interrupted. Sleep of the soul.",["“continuous” renders continuirlich; “Sleep of the soul” Seelenschlaf."]],"reflm:4562":["If in what follows someone became conscious of himself and also became conscious of all such actions as we remember, he would on that account be I myself. Thus the identity of the person rests not on the agreement of apperception, but on the continuation of it, even if it should be under obscure representations.",["The first sentence ends with a period; the sense suggests a question or a missing “not” (würde er darum [nicht] ich selber seyn).","“agreement” renders übereinstimung; “obscure” dunkeln."]],"reflm:4563":["*Mundus* is either *aspectabilis* or *concupiscibilis*. If it is [sens] as *sensibilis* aspectable, then it is to that extent also (g *sensitive*) concupiscible; if it is |\nconsidered as *intelligibilis*, then it is also *intellectualiter concupiscibilis*, not according to appearance and feelings, but concepts. *Mundus moralis* is *intelligibilis* as to perfection and is in union with the *sensibili*, which however is separated through death. With the ceasing of (g present) sensibility we do come out of one *mundo aspectabili* into another, but the *mundus intelligibilis* endures, as well with regard to the substance (*noumenon*), as also with regard to the mutual determination of the intelligences.",["*aspectabilis*: “visible”; *concupiscibilis*: “desirable”; aspectabel and concupiscibel are Kant’s Germanized forms.","“as to perfection” renders the Latin qvoad perfectionem.","“which” (die) refers to the union."]],"reflm:4564":["Theology is either philosophical or revealed; the former is either transcendental or natural, just as religion (practical) is either supernatural or natural. (s Theology is either rational or revealed; the former either philosophical or common. the former either transcendental or natural; he who maintains that there is none but the former is a deist; he who the latter (highest intelligence), is a theist; he who none but the rational, also a naturalist.)\n(s Natural is either metaphysics or physicotheology.)",["The passage is Latin; “maintains” renders contendit, “highest intelligence” summam intelligentiam.","“also” renders et, which may also be read “and”."]],"reflm:4565":["Of a most real being it is not more comprehensible that it exists than of a limited one; but from its presupposition it is easier to derive limited beings. Accordingly there is here a neces|sity\nto assume such a being for the sake of the grounds of speculative reason. It is not easier to have insight into the necessity of a completely holy will[than the] and the connection of it with moral perfection than of a will that is limited; but we need it for morality. In such a way both proofs are valid for the subject, which is also enough for the supreme ground of actions.",["“most real being” renders allerrealsten Wesen; “connection” Verknüpfung; “morality” Sittlichkeit.","“which” (welches) can refer to the subject or to the whole preceding clause."]],"reflm:4566":["Unless a most perfect being is proved, I have no determinate concept of its properties. If not only a single original being is determined, I have no concept of the number of them and their properties. Likewise of the best world as the ground of the judgment of that which lies ahead for the rational creatures in the world.",["“original being” renders Urwesen; “properties” Eigenschaften.","“If not only” (Wird nicht nur) may mean “unless only”."]],"reflm:4567":["We cannot derive from the concept of any thing its necessity, but indeed from the conditions of necessity the concept; for necessity is morality.",["“morality” renders moralitaet."]],"reflm:4568":["329'. E II 1236. 1630.\nM 329:\nThe subjective conditions of human reason are the *postulata* of its use and not *axiomata*.\n(g absolutely necessary is the assumption of a thing in the subjective sense, without which I, according to the law [of my] of universal human reason, can have no concept of the possibilities of things in general.\nThat the sensitive conditions affect only the cognition of the present world serves only so that we do not set dogmatic hindrances against the concepts of reason (which from other grounds are necessary of God).\nM 329':\n(g This necessity from subjective grounds of universal human reason shows itself also in this, that we can on that account more easily derive the origin of the contingent from a will that acts and chooses in conformity with the cognition of various possible things, Because cognition and will are what is truly active in us and it is also everywhere necessary to regard ends as given, so that our actions may be in accord with the world.)",["“von Gott” can attach to the concepts of reason (“concepts of reason of God”) or to nöthig.","“assumption” renders Annehmung; “sense” Verstande; “universal” allgemeinen."]],"reflm:4569":["Everything possible is considered *in respectu* toward everything possible (through either or) and thus lies as a member of the division in a whole that contains all *data* for it. Possibility in every respect (*absolute*, simply possible) requires breaks off.\nThe transcendental proof is a necessary *hypothesis* of pure reason.",["“in every respect” renders in aller Absicht; “simply” schlechthin.","“division” renders Eintheilung."]],"reflm:4570":["All our possibility presupposes *data*, [and the] either for all things with the indeterminacy of each, or a given *principium* of relation with the indeterminacy of a particular one, e.g. In space triangles are possible. The logical relation is the principle of contradiction, that of the *phaenomenorum* space and time, those of things and their real relations are forces. From the former one cannot infer to the latter. The principle of contradiction is usable only negatively, namely to cognize the impossible through the contradiction. positively, however: the possible, it presupposes too much, although it is in this way also true.",["Satz des Wiederspruchs: principle of contradiction; *phaenomenorum*: of phenomena.","Dinge and Sachen are both rendered things; Sachen is the second (of things and their real relations).","zu allen Dingen: for, or to, all things."]],"reflm:4571":["Whether the gospel has thus improved theology?\nThe idea of the highest perfection and of a universal and immediate ruler is needful from need, but not necessary from reason.",["nöthig: needful; nothwendig: necessary; Bedürfnis: need.","so: thus, or so much; the German leaves it open."]],"reflm:4572":["The absolute proposition: if something is God, then it exists, expresses absolute necessity. Hence I cannot say: if something is a sun, then it exists. The absolute proposition that is to express necessary existence must be analytic, hence: if something is absolutely necessary, then it exists.",["existentz / existirt: existence / exists (not Daseyn)."]],"reflm:4573":["We can infer from the *datis* of experience to the existence of no being and its properties which in magnitude is a mere ideal and surpasses all concepts of experience.",["*datis*: data; Eigenschaften: properties; Erfahrungsbegriffe: concepts of experience."]],"reflm:4574":["Everything can be possible only through one. For through many together that is not possible which is through one that unites their forces.\nThe proof from concepts: either from God or from the world in general; the first: from the concept of a most perfect one the necessity, and the 2nd: from this to derive that.",["eines Vollkommensten: of a most perfect (one); the German has no noun.","einen: one, masculine; the noun it stands for is not given."]],"reflm:4575":["The positive concept of God is easy. The negative serves for correction. analogy.",["Berichtigung: correction, rectification."]],"reflm:4576":["Our cognition of God is for the most part negative, i.e. to determine the prac|tical\nas well as theoretical boundaries of our theological cognition, as also to guard against errors, harmful logically as well as practically. Presumption, forwardness. For all our cognition of God is only an investigation of what might belong in the ideal of the highest perfection. Now we cannot derive from our idea of the highest perfection everything that belongs to the thing itself; but the material *principia* must be given through experience, and the form serves only to separate out what contradicts perfection.",["Grentzen: boundaries; Vermessenheit: presumption; Vorwitz: forwardness, impertinent curiosity.","Erkentnis and Erkentnisses are both rendered cognition."]],"reflm:4577":["M330:\n(g transcendental theology.)\nThe concept terminating all things is that of a necessary being; of an original being or first of all beings, of the highest being: insofar as it has no ground above itself, of the all-sufficient or being of all beings: insofar as it has no ground beside itself, also nothing independent outside itself, consequently of a single being. The question is whether of these three concepts one posits all the rest. The two first concepts we need, because we require a *terminum* of subordination. These *qvaestionen* are transcendental and concern merely the subordination of our concepts. The second, of an origin of all things cognized *a priori*, of the constitution of the cause, whether it has understanding or not, is metaphysical and presupposes analogies with known causes. The concept of the original being |\nM 330': |\ngoes to the essences and mere possibilities, that of the cause to actualities, and causality always refers to contingencies.\nM 330:\n(g Natural theology: a perfect spirit as to understanding and will. Namely in relation to nature.)",["Urwesen: original being; einigen Wesens: single, sole being; *terminum*: terminus, end-point.","gehet auf die Wesen: Wesen can be beings or essences; essences is chosen beside possibilities, and the other reading stands.","Die zweyte: the second (question); the construction of the sentence is left as loose as the German."]],"reflm:4578":["Cosmotheology or physicotheology. The latter is empirical.",[]],"reflm:4579":["God as the highest intelligence: fundamental concept of natural theology. Relation to our concerns and ends. *Conceptus terminator [stator], comprehensor, definitor*; that which determines the limitations of all things; *omnium determinator* in subordination, coordination and conception, because reason goes only from the *universali ad particularia*.\n(g in itself only the all is sufficiently determined: *conprehensor*.) What belongs to the concept of God.",["*Conceptus terminator [stator], comprehensor, definitor*: the terminating [establishing], comprehending, defining concept; *omnium determinator*: determiner of all things.","*universali ad particularia*: universal to the particulars.","der: that which; the German does not say whether the concept or God is meant."]],"reflm:4580":["The transcendental concept is needful, not the transcendental proof; likewise that one gain insight: one could assert nothing heterodox with ground. The necessity of the divine existence [either] as of a necessary hypothesis either of the mere concepts of the possible or of experiences in this world, likewise as hypothesis of morality. The absolute necessity cannot be proved.\n(g The proof of the existence of God is not apodictic, but hypothetical *sub hypothesi logica* and [*morali*] *practica*.)\nThe *hypothesis* that is necessary in relation to the laws of pure reason is *originaria*; that which is necessary in relation to experience is *conditionalis sive relativa*. The first is necessary, the second needful; the former *originaria*, the latter *subsidiaria*, e.g. for the explanation of the order in the world *suppletoria*.",["nöthig: needful; nothwendig: necessary; einsehe: gain insight.","*sub hypothesi logica* and [*morali*] *practica*: under a logical and [moral] practical hypothesis.","*originaria*: original; *conditionalis sive relativa*: conditional or relative; *subsidiaria*: subsidiary; *suppletoria*: supplementary."]],"reflm:4581":["Everything possible stands in a thoroughgoing interconnection. To be possible properly means as much as: to agree. All possibility has *data*, but the form of it consists in the agreeing-together. Hence thoroughgoing possibility is properly an agreeing-together with everything.",["Zusammenhang: interconnection; stimmen: agree, accord; Zusammenstimung: agreeing-together, concord."]],"reflm:4582":["M 330:\nThe need of reason to cognize a highest being is that of a necessary *hypothesis* of its use: 1. of pure reason. 2. of empirical, both speculative; 3. of practical. (s Hence 1. transcendental theology; 2. Natural: cosmotheology, physicotheology; 3. Moral theology. All of this: 1. to determine the concept of the highest being, 2. to demonstrate its existence. |\nM 330': |\nAll this cognition is a belief, as at all times when we go back to first causes. Transcendental theology alone is deistic; natural alone is anthropomorphistic; moral alone is not sufficiently secured against encroachments. Transcendental theology secures against them.)",["Glauben: belief; darthun: demonstrate.","derselben: its, referring to reason."]],"reflm:4583":["[On the existence of God toward the end] *conceptus definitor*: that which determines everything through ends; *terminator*: that which begins; *comprehensor*: that which comprises everything.",["*conceptus definitor*: defining concept; *terminator*: terminating; *comprehensor*: comprehending.","der: that which; the German does not say whether the concept or God is meant."]],"reflm:4584":["The practical ground of belief in God can be sufficient; but speculative ones are nevertheless needful *in subsidium*, in order to secure the former against false rationalizing.",["*in subsidium*: in support; vernünfteley: rationalizing (vernünfteln); nöthig: needful."]],"reflm:4585":["1. pathological origin of theology; 2. speculative, rationalizing; 3. moral. So various religion there is also. [The 2nd is deism.] God is in all these cases only a necessary *hypothesis* of the use of reason with regard to its own happiness or to speculation or to morality.",["seiner eigenen Glükseeligkeit: its own or one's own happiness; seiner may refer to reason (Gebrauch der Vernunft) or to the one using it.","Vernünftelnder: rationalizing (vernünfteln)."]],"reflm:4586":["*vid.* p.336. Contingency of nature and *ens supramundanum* make theology necessary.",["*vid.*: see; *ens supramundanum*: supramundane being."]],"reflm:4587":["At the upper margin, \n*ad p:330*. The idea of a highest being is [a] the necessary presupposition for the completion of our cognition of actual things through reason, as well as of a first and necessary, as also for the sake of all relations: as of the being of all beings, of the being that comprises everything in itself, [so that] because only thereby can something determinate be cognized. We infer from the existence of God to the greatest perfection of the world and from this to the existence of God. This comes about thus. We must assume a supreme understanding and will in order to explain the contingencies of the world, and both together with the power and duration in the highest degree and perfect unity, because without such nothing determinate can be cognized.",["Am oberen Rand (at the upper margin) is the AA editor's note on where the entry stands, as the source gives it.","eines ersten und nothwendigen: of a first and necessary (one); no noun in the German.","Macht: power; Bestimtes: something determinate."]],"reflm:4588":["If one wants to prove the existence of God from empirical grounds, then the strange circle arises, that we indeed do not trust ourselves to infer from the perfections that we become aware of in the world that this is among all possible the best, but first: that the cau|se\nof it is among all possible the most perfect, consequently also the effect.\nIf we want to infer from concepts the [existence] properties of God, yet in such a way that we assume some existence through experience, then we infer from this existence to one necessary in itself, just as if this did not have to be clear by itself; and, since this is not clear by itself through the concept that we have of it, we nevertheless infer from this problematic concept [to] to such properties through which it can be only a single one, namely all-sufficiency. Now, however, must, since the necessity of existence can be inferred only from conditions that can belong to one thing alone, so one must also be able to reverse the inference, consequently be able to infer the existence of God from mere concepts without an empirical *principium*.",["Nun aber muß, da ... so muß man: the first muß has no completion in the German, and the broken construction is kept.","vor sich selbst: by itself, or for itself; Schlus: inference; Allgnugsamkeit: all-sufficiency."]],"reflm:4589":["Above and \nWe have need of the concept of God as of a supreme ground of things through freedom. 1. In order to explain the origin of contingent beings, and secondly: to determine the final ends and hopes of rational beings. The former in the series of speculation, the latter of *praxis*. The former serves only on account of the beginning of nature. The natural occurrences themselves must not be explained from it.",["Über und (above and) is the AA editor's note on where the entry stands, cut off in the source.","Naturbegebenheiten: natural occurrences (Begebenheit); letzte Zweke: final ends."]],"reflm:4590":["Above and \nThe infinite reality is the *substratum* of all possibility. The universal foundation. If all negations are limitations, then no thing is possible except only through another which it presupposes, except the *ens realissimum*. [The infinite time of the] The all-comprehending time, the all-containing space, the all-sufficient thing.",["Über und (above and) is the AA editor's note on where the entry stands, cut off in the source.","realitaet: reality; Schranken: limitations; *ens realissimum*: most real being.","alles begreifende: all-comprehending (begreifen); ausser: except."]],"reflm:4591":["At the outer margin \nAtheism.\n1. That there is no original being at all: Epicurus.\n2. That it is the world itself or the world a modification of it.\n3. That it is cause of things according to blind fatality.",["Am Aussenrande (at the outer margin) is the AA editor's note on where the entry stands, as the source gives it.","Urwesen: original being."]],"reflm:4592":["Above, at the outer margin beside and \n* In the *theologia Naturali* the *ens summum* (g determinately) is considered as the cause of nature (g of our world); consequently, since we know causality only through forces that are given to us in experience [so] and these cannot be encountered *proprie* in the highest being (*anthropopathos*), we will be able to think of God only their relation to the effects, which the understanding cognizes, *proprie*, but the way in which they act, *improprie*, and, in that we separate out what has been borrowed here from the nature of the things of the world and think the absolute of them negatively, must think *theoprepos*; consequently we will cognize God in the *theologia naturali* according to the analogy from nature in general. *theologia naturalis vel cosmologia vel physico-theologia*.\nWe infer from this world to another, because it does not agree alone with the greatest perfection; and nevertheless one wants to infer from it to the existence of the most perfect being and afterward from this and its perfections to the other world.\n*(g all proof of the existence of the *entis summi* is to be conducted only from pure concepts of reason, consequently *a priori* (Wolf). This proof breaks off.)",["The first line is the AA editor's note on where the entry stands, cut off in the source; (g bestimmt) may be read as determinately or as determined.","*proprie* / *improprie*: properly / improperly; *anthropopathos*: with human passions; *theoprepos*: as befits God.","*ens summum*, *entis summi*: highest being; *theologia naturalis vel cosmologia vel physico-theologia*: natural theology, either cosmology or physico-theology."]],"reflm:4593":["At the inner margin \nin natural theology God as cause of this world, consequently merely his relation, and he himself according to analogy *per eminentiam*.",["Am Innenrand (at the inner margin) is the AA editor's note on where the entry stands, as the source gives it.","*per eminentiam*: by way of eminence."]],"reflm:4594":["At the inner margin \nCosmotheology is built on the proposition: the first cause of a contingent thing is so from freedom, not from the necessity of its nature; first mover, teleological.",["Am Innenrand (at the inner margin) is the AA editor's note on where the entry stands, as the source gives it.","ist es: is so (literally, is it), i.e. is the first cause."]],"reflm:4595":["Natural theology is either cosmotheology or physicotheology.",[]],"reflm:4596":["It is not so easy to prove theoretically the existence of God, but easy and natural to believe it.",[]],"reflm:4597":["Above and \nTo cosmotheology belongs the relation of God to the world according to causality. 1. [princ] *systema inhaerentiae vel emanationis vel liberae productionis* (system either of inherence or of emanation or of free production); the latter *vel tanqvam architecti vel tanqvam creatoris* (either as of an architect or as of a creator). 2. *praesentia vel commercii vel conservationis* (presence either of community or of conservation). The first is *anima mundi* (world-soul). 3. *duratio non in tempore, qvemadmodum praesentia non in spatio et creatio non in tempore* (duration not in time, just as presence not in space and creation not in time). Moral theology: *sapientia* (g *benignitas*) *et sanctitas* (wisdom (g benevolence) and holiness), *finis* * (end), *summum bonum* (highest good), optimism. *Summa felicitas cum summa dignitate ipsius* (highest happiness with the highest worthiness of it). *Origo mali* (origin of evil). *decreta omnia non absoluta, sed hypothetica* (all decrees not absolute but hypothetical). *Gubernatio et idea gubernationem determinans: providentia* (government and the idea determining government: providence).\n*(g *non est impellens, sed complacentiae immanentis* (is not impelling, but of immanent satisfaction).)",["Über und (above and) is the AA editor's note on where the entry stands, cut off in the source; [princ] is a struck-out beginning of a word.","The asterisk after *finis* is Kant's sign referring to the note at the end.","*dignitate ipsius*: worthiness of it, or of himself; *ipsius* may refer to the happiness or to the person."]],"reflm:4598":["In the doctrine of nature, too, the causes are presented only as necessary |\nhypotheses, but not as absolutely necessary, because yet another cause of the same *phaenomeni* would be possible.",["schlechthin: absolutely, without qualification (not absolut); *phaenomeni*: phenomenon.","Naturlehre: doctrine of nature."]],"reflm:4599":["Prudence and morality cannot hang together except solely through the *hypothesis* of a third being, which is mighty and kind and at the same time holy and just.",["Klugheit: prudence; Sittlichkeit: morality; Gütig: kind, benevolent."]],"reflm:4600":["The causality of the whole series of the contingent, hence of its arising and beginning, is not physical but practical, i.e. from freedom.\n(s Freedom is the supreme condition of the most real being as cause, and the causality in it is through understanding and will.)\n(s *Primus motor ex libertate. intelligentia.* (First mover from freedom. intelligence.))",["bey derselben: in it, referring to freedom or to the most real being; the German leaves it open."]],"reflm:4601":["All composition and limitation is contingent; the cause of it is itself contingent and can consist in nothing other than a choice.",["Wahl: choice (also used for Willkühr elsewhere); Einschrankung: limitation."]],"reflm:4602":["The [empirical] cosmotheological proof has adequacy as *principium* of the empirical use of reason with regard to all possible order in the world and also of the first beginning according to the analogy of experiences.\nThereby, too, the inference is only to an *Analogon* of our freedom.",["zulenglichkeit: adequacy (zulänglich), distinct from zureichend, sufficient.","*Analogon*: analogue."]],"reflm:4603":["Causality (g of things) according to the necessity of nature gives substantial unity of the cause and effect, because the cancelling of the latter would cancel the nature of the former, hence its existence.",["Aufhebung / aufheben: cancelling / cancel, also removal / remove."]],"reflm:4604":["Analogy with the human understanding.\n*via reductionis* to separate out all sensibility and its (g limiting conditions) limitations.\nbut if we leave out the conditions of sensibility, then all cognition *in concreto* falls away; accordingly we can never think God *proprie* through cognition that is borrowed from nature, but only *secundum analogiam*.\nThe idea breaks off. |\nThe two words stand to the right of the end of § 868; beneath them, written in lead pencil, the word: |\nPlato.",["*via reductionis*: by the way of reduction; *proprie*: properly; *secundum analogiam*: according to analogy.","The sentence beginning The two words is the AA editor's note, as the source gives it; Schranken: limitations."]],"reflm:4605":["The original being is not an original nature, but an original intelligence.",["The passage is Latin: *Ens originarium*, *natura originaria*, *intelligentia originaria*."]],"reflm:4606":["transcendental theology;\nnatural theology*: determination through predicates of nature — psychological;\n*(g either from transcendental or from physical principles)\nmoral theology, determination and conclusion from moral predicates and arguments.",[]],"reflm:4607":["At the very top of the page, but \nAcquiescence in oneself is the principle of all causality of the highest being and is blessedness.",["“Acquiescence in oneself”: *acqviescentia in semet ipso*, i.e. contentment with oneself.","The location note before the Reflexion appears incomplete as transmitted (“aber”)."]],"reflm:4608":["Moral theology: God as *summum bonum* (highest good), *legislator et finis* (lawgiver and end).",[]],"reflm:4609":["The principle of the inner possibility of a [Wes] thing is the essence. This essence according to (g the determination of) its causality is nature. The condition of nature as a first cause of the contingent is freedom.",["“essence”: Wesen, which also means “being”; the struck-out [Wes] began the same word before “Dinges” (thing) replaced it.","The later addition makes the phrase read “nach der Bestimmung seiner caussalitaet” (according to the determination of its causality)."]],"reflm:4610":["On the outer margin, beginning above the page number, down to \nEssence: God (g as) the highest possibility.\nNature: God as the highest perfection.\nFreedom:\nthe highest good.\nPerfections are faculties for the highest end;\ngood is the intention of the highest end.\nThe highest good is the union of the highest happiness with the worthiness or objective receptivity for it, which is as great as is practically determinable in universal happiness, i.e., as much as through practical agreement with the universal principles of happiness* one is permitted to be happy.\n*\n(g both in relation to the ends of human nature (g and) of human beings.)\nMoral perfection, insofar as it is the determination of the divine will or is determining with regard to the creatures; i.e. [He is the principle] His will is just, and he wills that human beings be just. Moral theology is the right one, which makes good.\nGod in this theology in threefold person or quality as the (g holy) lawgiver, as the benevolent ruler and the just judge.",["“Essence”: Wesen; the lines beginning “God (g as)” to “permitted to be happy” are Latin in the source; “worthiness”: *dignitas*; “receptivity”: *receptivitas*; “intention”: *intentio*.","The footnote pairs “so wohl … als” across the later additions, hence “both … and”; “Menschen” is rendered “human beings”.","“which makes good” (welche Gut macht) may mean that this theology makes [one] good or that it makes [things] good; the ambiguity is kept."]],"reflm:4611":["Morality is the agreement of the free power of choice with the end of humanity and of human beings in general, namely with necessary conditions of the universal ends of humanity and of human beings.",["“power of choice”: Willkühr; “agreement”: Übereinstimung; “universal”: allgemein."]],"reflm:4612":["Above and \nWe ought to have as much share in the universal happiness as we contribute to it. Morality is itself comprehensible only from the idea of such a will.",["“happiness”: Glük, which also means “fortune”.","The location note appears incomplete as transmitted (“Über und”)."]],"reflm:4613":["Fortune and fate, in a word: the advantages from nature, can never have the worth as those under correct rules.",["“Fortune”: Glük. The comparison “den Werth … als die unter richtigen Regeln” is elliptical as written and is kept so."]],"reflm:4614":["Opposite the concluding sentence of M § 893:\nFreedom of the will — Perfection either natural: omnipotence, wisdom (g [natural] artificial) [Holiness], or moral: Holiness, benevolence, Justice = Wisdom.",["The Reflexion is Latin: *libertas voluntatis*, *perfectio*, *sapientia* (g [*naturalis*] *artificialis*), *sanctitas*, *benignitas*, *justitia*.","“benevolence”: *benignitas*."]],"reflm:4615":["The holiness, benevolence and justice of the divine will. *Summa*: wisdom.",["“benevolence”: Gütigkeit; *Summa*: “in sum”, “the sum”."]],"reflm:4616":["At the very top of the page. \nThe world considered *intellectualiter* (intellectually) has no beginning; not because it has lasted an infinite time, for then it would be considered *sensitive* (sensitively), but [it] because in this respect it is not weighed in time at all.\nThe *sensibilis mundus* (sensible world) has no beginning, because a first beginning is impossible. He who considers the world *sensitive* cognizes in it no boundaries.",["“considered”: betrachtet; “in this respect”: in diesem Betracht; “weighed”: erwogen.","“boundaries”: Grentzen."]],"reflm:4617":["The world, as it begins, cannot be thought according to grounds of reason, and without beginning not according to grounds of sensibility.",["“as it begins” (wie sie Anfangt) can also be read “how it begins”."]],"reflm:4618":["Above and \nIf we look into the world as world of sense, then we will find no beginning in the *regressu* of appearances. But now these appearances are not actual things; thus it does not properly mean: the world is (g in itself) without beginning, but rather: the appearance (in relation to us) has no beginning. In the appearances there is no absolute boundary of [comp] increase or decrease.\nThe world has an origin, but not as appearance, but rather as being in itself.",["“world of sense”: sinnenwelt; *regressu*: Latin ablative of *regressus*, kept as Kant wrote it.","“being in itself”: Wesen an sich selbst; Wesen can also mean “essence”.","The location note appears incomplete as transmitted (“Über und”)."]],"reflm:4619":["Above and \nThe causal relation of the intellectual to the sensitive and the determination of sensibility according to merely intellectual principles or conversely cannot be seen into by us at all. e.g. The first ground of composition, the first action through freedom, the origin and beginning of the world.",["“seen into”: eingesehen (einsehen, to have insight into).","The location note appears incomplete as transmitted (“Über und”)."]],"reflm:4620":["Causality with regard to substance: creation and conservation;\nwith regard to (g the course of the world) the ordering: providence and government.",["“conservation”: Erhaltung; “ordering”: Anordnung; “government”: Regirung."]],"reflm:4621":["On the upper margin and on the outer page margin \nThe physicotheological concept seeks for the contingent the necessary determinately, the moral for the necessary the necessary, in order to give it reality [der]. It is not necessary to go to the first ground in speculation. But it is necessary for the sake of duties to go back to the supreme lawgiver, ruler and judge.\nRational belief, which is necessary, is unavoidable hypothesis of a moral being.\nThe subjective grounds of the need of our pure as well as applied reason increase this obligation.",["“determinately”: bestimmt, which can also be read as a participle, “the necessary [as] determined”.","“Rational belief”: Vernunftglaube; “moral being”: sittlichen wesens (sittlich, where elsewhere “moral” renders moralisch).","“obligation”: Verbindlichkeit."]],"reflm:4622":["The good is the harmony with the order of nature and of the world by means of freedom. Hence God is by means of nature author of the good and at the same time the one who hinders evil.",["“harmony”: Zusammenstimung; “the order of nature and of the world”: der Natur- und Weltordnung."]],"reflm:4623":["The moral *concursus* happens through the removal of the hindrances of our inner morality and not through determination of our actions.",["*concursus*: concurrence."]],"reflm:4624":["At the very top of the page. \nGod is the author of the order of nature, freely acting beings in many respects of the course of nature.",["The second clause is elliptical in the German (“frey handelnde Wesen … von dem Laufe der Natur”); “the author” is to be understood, and is not supplied."]],"reflm:4625":["Whether the Danube flows eastward for this reason, so that the Turks do not flood the Empire, and the Falun mine is placed in the land of Dalecarlia, because this is an industrious people?",["“the Falun mine”: das fahlunsche Bergwerk, the copper mine at Falun in Dalarna (Dalecarlia), Sweden."]],"reflm:4626":["The analysis of pure reason gives nothing but distinctness in the representations that we already have.\nThe synthetic propositions pertain to the conditions of judgments through pure reason and are subjective. Thus pure reason cannot teach us to cognize *obiecte* otherwise than in application [to that which] to senses.",["“pertain to”: gehen auf. “through pure reason” may attach to “judgments” or to “pertain”; the ambiguity is kept.","*obiecte*: Kant's Latinate spelling of Objekte, “objects”."]],"reflm:4627":["Metaphysics has neither the utility in use nor the splendor of invention and strength of insight of reason that mathematics has; but its end is a universally human end, and thereby it takes precedence over all theoretical knowledge.",["“insight of reason”: Vernunfteinsicht; “knowledge”: Kenntnissen (plural)."]],"reflm:4628":["Metaphysics does not serve science except only negatively, to hold off the errors of pure reason; but it is no small service that it the most sublime and dr bricht ab.",["The sentence breaks off; no verb follows “that it” (den sie), and “dr” is an unfinished word. “bricht ab” is the editor's note that the text breaks off."]],"reflm:4629":["The basic concepts of all our cognition are [the quality (what is there) the quantity (how many times)] 1. being in general (g *qvidditaet*), secondly: how something is, thirdly: how many times it is.\nThat through which the things are given is sensation; how they are given, pure intuitions.\nThrough the understanding either things are thought or only their sensible representations. Number requires space and time for its intuitive representation.\nThe logical form is just that for the [concepts] representations of the understanding of a thing which space and time are for the appearances of it: namely the former contain the places, to order them. The representation [sei], through which we assign to an *obiect* its proper logical place, is the real concept of the understanding and pure: e.g. Something that I can always use only as *subiect*; Something from which I must infer hypothetically to a *conseqvens* etc etc.\nThrough the fact that our sensations receive a determinate place* in space and time, they attain a function among the appearances; but the place in space and in time is determined through the neighborhood of other sensations in them; e.g. upon the state of my sensations, which have something in common with the previous ones, the other follows; the sensation of a resistance is at the same time combined in the same space with weight.\nThrough the determination of the logical place the representation receives a function among the concepts. e.g. *Antecedens*, *conseqvens*. Yet the sensitive function is the ground of the intellectual.\n*(g a determinate place is distinguished from the arbitrary.)",["“place”: Stelle throughout; “being in general”: das seyn überhaupt; *qvidditaet*: quiddity.","“representations of the understanding”: Verstandes Vorstellungen; “real concept of the understanding”: reale Verstandesbegrif.","“arbitrary”: willkührlichen."]],"reflm:4630":["Propositions can be cognized *a priori*, but their principles *a posteriori*.",["“Propositions”: Satze; “principles”: Grundsatze."]],"reflm:4631":["(g Space is a *datum*.)\nThe logical actions are *actus* through which we posit and order the *data* to representations of things *respective* toward one another. Thereby the representations receive logical functions. The real function [is] consists in the way in which we posit a representation in and for itself; thus it is an action (*a priori*) which corresponds to each *dato* (*a posteriori*) and through which this becomes a concept. These actions are the sources from which the logical ones are possible. From this arise all cognitions, namely how we grasp *data* and can form for ourselves something that is called cognition. In nature no *data* can come before us except that, if one perceives laws in it, they correspond to the universal ways according to which we posit something, because otherwise no laws would be noticed, or no *obiect* at all, but only confused inner alterations. Since we can thus represent *obiecte* only through our alterations, insofar as they have in themselves something in conformity with our [Ge] rules for positing and cancelling, the real functions are the ground of the possibility of the representation of things and the logical functions the ground of the possibility of judgments, consequently of cognitions. For an *obiect* is called only that, which bricht ab.",["“to representations” (zu Vorstellungen) may mean “into representations” or “for representations”; *respective*: respectively, relatively.","“things”: Dinge in the first sentence, Sachen in “representation of things”; “ways”: arten; “cancelling”: aufzuheben.","The final clause breaks off, and its “welche” does not agree with “das”; “bricht ab” is the editor's note."]],"reflm:4632":["Universality is a continuous action, which can afterwards be divided.",["“continuous”: stetige; “divided”: eingetheilt."]],"reflm:4633":["How can cognitions be generated in us of which the objects have not yet presented themselves to us. Since the *obiecte* must conform not to our cognitions, but these to the *obiecte*, it seems they would have at least (g according to) their basic elements to be beforehand |\ngiven to us, before they can be thought. It is thus the possibility of a (g every) cognition *a priori*, which for itself [its sources and its Best] is constant, without [der Ge] having been drawn from the objects themselves, which constitutes our first and most important question. A question [which even only its importance even only to understand already a] which even only to have raised and well understood already has some merit in itself, namely in a part of philosophy which owes nothing to experience and the senses. There are to be sure in fact [sciences Erkennt] even whole sciences of this kind. Pure mathematics, which flows entirely from the pure sources *a priori*, without taking up anything from experience among its grounds, has in this kind incomparable progress and a rightly admired and at times probably envied good fortune. Yet there is [also] (g on the other hand) no lack of others which, in that they would be of just as pure an origin, find themselves incessantly among contradictions. Hence it is good [to] to investigate the sources of cognition *a priori* in general, without heeding this distinction, and only afterwards to make it comprehensible.",["“objects”: Gegenstände; *obiecte*: Kant's Latinate Objekte, also “objects”.","“basic elements”: Grundstücken; “constant”: beständig; “progress”: Fortgang; “good fortune”: Glük.","“in this kind” (in dieser Art) may also be read “in this way”."]],"reflm:4634":["M XXII:\nWe know every object only through predicates that we say or think of it. Before that, what is encountered of representations in us is to be counted only as materials, but not as cognition. Hence an object is only a something in general, which we think to ourselves through certain predicates that make up its concept. In every judgment there are accordingly two predicates, which we compare with one another. Of which the one, [through which to us the] which makes up the given cognition of the object, is called the logical *subiect*, the second, which is compared with it, the logical *praedicat*. If I say: a body is divisible, then it signifies as much as: Something [which] x, which I know under the predicates that together make|up a concept of body,\nI also think through the predicate of divisibility. x  is the same as xb. Now a as well as b belongs to x. Only |\nM XXIII: |\nin different ways: either that b already lies in that which makes up the concept a, and thus can be found through analysis of it, or that b belongs to the x without being included and comprehended in a. In the first case the judgment is analytic, in the second synthetic. The case cited is of an analytic judgment, but the proposition: Every body is heavy, is a *synthesis*; the one predicate is not involved in the subject, but added. Now all analytic judgments can be seen into a priori; and what can be cognized only a posteriori is synthetic. Hence the properly empirical judgments are synthetic. But there are nevertheless judgments, [which a] whose validity seems to stand firm a priori, which are nonetheless synthetic, e.g. Everything alterable has a cause; whence does one come to these judgments? [through which ha] whence do we take it, to associate with one concept another of the very same object, which no observation and experience shows in it. Nonetheless all proper *axiomata* are such synthetic propositions, *e.g.* between two points there can be only one straight line. On the other hand the proposition: [Every magnitude is itself If one Every part is smal] Every magnitude is equal to itself, is an analytic proposition. The *principium* or the norm of all analytic propositions is the principle of contradiction and of identity. It is (if I take them both together) no *axioma*, but a formula, i.e. a universal model of analytic propositions; for it contains no *medium terminum*.\nWe have accordingly judgments a posteriori which are synthetic, but also judgments a priori which are nevertheless synthetic and for that reason cannot be derived from any experience, because they contain a true universality, hence necessity, as well as comprehend in themselves nothing but concepts which could not have been drawn from experience. [Whence] These concepts may dwell in us from wherever they will: whence do we take this connection of them. Are they revelations, prejudices etc etc.\nIf certain concepts in us contain nothing other than that through which all experiences are possible on our side, then they can be said a priori before experience and yet with complete validity for everything that may ever come before us. They then hold, to be sure, not of things in general, but yet of everything that can ever be given to us through experience, because they contain the conditions through which these experiences are possible. Such propositions will thus contain the condition of the possibility not of things, but of experience. |\nM XXIV: |\nBut things that cannot be given to us through any experience are nothing for us; thus we can very well use such propositions as universal with a practical aim, only not as *principia* of speculation about objects in general.\nNow in order to settle what sort of concepts those are which must necessarily precede all experience and through which this is alone possible, which are thus given a priori and also contain the ground for judgments a priori, we must analyze an experience in general. In every experience there is something through which an object is given to us, and something through which it is thought. If we take the conditions that lie in the activities of the mind through which alone it can be given, then one can cognize something of the objects a priori. If we take that through which alone it can be thought, then one can also cognize something a priori of all possible objects. For through this alone does something become for us an object or a cognition of it.\nWe will investigate the former. That through which an object (g of experience) is given to us is called appearance. The possibility of appearances is, on the side of the human mind, sensibility. In sensibility there is a matter, which is called sensation, and in regard to which and its diversity we are merely passive, and the manifoldness of impressions brings it about that we find nothing a priori in us which we could know from ourselves a priori before the impressions. One cannot ever in thought |\nrepresent to oneself any [new] impression of a new kind. But appearances also have a form, a ground lying in our subject, through which we order either the impressions themselves or that which corresponds to them and give to each part of them its place. This [is] can be nothing other than an activity which is indeed naturally excited by the impressions, but yet can be cognized by itself.\n(When we posit something in space and in time, we act; when we posit it next to and after one another, we connect. These actions are only means to bring about those places; but one can take them separately; when we posit the same thing several times or in the one action at the same time posit the other, this is a kind of actions through which we posit something in accordance with the rule of appearances, whereby this positing must have its particular rules, which are distinguished from the conditions of the form, as they are to be placed with regard to the appearance.)",["“know” renders kennen, “cognize” erkennen, “analysis” Zergliederung; “principle of contradiction” renders Satz des Wiederspruchs.","“x  is the same as xb” is kept as printed: a letter appears to be missing before the double space (perhaps “xa”).","“cannot ever … any” renders the German double negative “keinen … niemals”; “places” (Stellen) and “to be placed” (zu stellen) keep Kant's wordplay."]],"reflm:4635":["Not every object is a thing (Sache). That in the representation through which it has its peculiar function represents [the thing itself] the *Obiect* (real function); its function in the judgment is the respective function, through which it has a manifold validity with regard to other representations. The first function goes to that which lies in the representation; the second, what can be cognized through it.",["“object” renders obiect / Obiect (Kant's Latinate spelling), not Gegenstand; “thing” renders Sache.","“manifold validity” renders vielgültigkeit, literally “many-validity”."]],"reflm:4636":["Cognitions a priori must not go at all to determinate things (because these are not yet given), but to universal representations of |\nthings in general and thus to intuitions (g not sensations, for these are that through which something determinate is given) or to thoughts in general. (One can intuit something without thinking anything thereby or under it.)\n(g All cognitions come about in us through thinking, i.e. through concepts; they are not intuitions.)",["“determinate” renders bestimmt (also “determined”).","“must not go at all” renders müssen gar nicht … gehen, which can also be read “need not go at all”."]],"reflm:4637":["That belonging to our cognitions through which the objects are [given] thought (for space is only the way in which they are given to us) is either the thinking of an object in general: *possibile*[it may be given or not], or how it can be given in general by ourselves: *qvantitas*, or how it must be given to us through experience.",["“in general” after “given” renders überhaupt, which may attach to “can be given” or to “by ourselves”."]],"reflm:4638":["All cognition consists in judgments. The judgments may now be immediate or mediate (syllogisms); [a determinate] Thinking is called judging. Even the concepts are predicates. Accordingly [cognitions] concepts to which no object is given, but which are nevertheless supposed to express the ways of thinking objects in general, will contain in themselves that which in judgments is thought relatively of two concepts to one another.\nCommonly valid concepts have a logical [function] place [the place] among concepts. The place in a judgment is a logical function; it is bricht ab.\nThe determinate logical function of a representation in general is the [real] pure concept of the understanding.",["“syllogisms” renders Vernunftschlüsse; “commonly valid” renders Gemeingültige.","“bricht ab” (breaks off) is the AA's note that the sentence is unfinished, kept as printed."]],"reflm:4639":["That the necessity of mere truths of reason involves a kind of existence is to be judged from the little word “is”. E.g. one cannot say: all triangles will be three-cornered.",["“triangles” renders the Latinate triangel, “three-cornered” the German dreyeckigt; “will be” renders the future werden … werden."]],"reflm:4640":["Cause and effect seems to be for [fantasy] *praevision*, whole and part for fantasy, substance and accident for the senses, possibility for the faculty of invention, actuality for [intuition] sensation, magnitude to intuition, necessity for the w bricht ab.\nIn all these items the constant combination makes the ground of an abstract concept of the understanding. For those powers are combining, and from this arises the possibility of judgments a priori and also of these concepts.\nEverything subjective relates ultimately to sensation, hence every objective to substance.\nSince all form (g of the subjective) is grounded on one determination being combined together with the other, inherence is grounded on intuition, the connection of the effect with the cause on imitation, conversely on anticipation, whole and part on imagination.",["“fantasy” renders phantasie, “faculty of invention” Dichtungsvermögen; “imitation”, “anticipation” and “imagination” render Nachbildung, Vorbildung and Einbildung (not Einbildungskraft).","“magnitude to intuition” keeps Kant's shift from vor to auf; “the w” and “bricht ab” (breaks off, the AA's note) mark the broken-off sentence."]],"reflm:4641":["The distinct representation of that which one\n[sees] intuits or which one thinks.\n*synthesis*\n*analysis*\nOne can still transform a sensible concept or concept of appearance into a concept of reason.\nDistinctness is opposed to indistinctness, order to confusion. [Through the understanding] Order depends on one's giving each partial representation its place, thus it is not merely the consciousness of this partial representation (distinctness), but also the [co-or] co- or subordination. Distinctness of the understanding depends on analysis and receives order through the logical places.",["“analysis” in the last sentence renders Zergliederung; “partial representation” renders Theilvorstellung."]],"reflm:4642":["Everything that a priori makes a law of our representations, [every *actus*] hence the relation of the representations to a (g a priori established) universal law, is the object. That shall be thought under it, represents the thing as it is, i.e. as it holds for all appearances. But for that reason it must be something determinable a priori, because only thereby can we think; for thinking signifies nothing other than determining representations from the universal.\nThe concepts of the understanding express all *actus* of the powers of the mind insofar as representations are possible according to their universal laws, and indeed [that] this their possibility a priori.",["“object” renders obiect; “thing” renders Sache; “powers of the mind” renders Gemüthskräfte.","“That shall be thought under it, represents” keeps an elliptical construction: Das may be demonstrative or stand for “that which”."]],"reflm:4643":["Transcendental is every pure cognition a priori in which, therefore, no sensation is given.\n1. The transcendental aesthetic.\n2. The transcendental logic.\n3. The transcendental critique.\n4. —\n—\n—\n—\nArchitectonic.\nOf that which one knows only through inner experience one cannot say the predicates of space (hence the soul a spirit).",["“knows” renders kennt; “spirit” renders Geist."]],"reflm:4644":["If one uses of a thing in general [what] a priori predicates of space and time, then this is a *petitio phaenomenorum*.\nIf one uses of things that one knows only under the appearance of space universal [concepts] real concepts of the understanding (of the kind through which the things themselves are given to the understanding, and their quality, not the magnitude or possibility), then one commits a *petitionem noumeni*. The former is *synthesis subrepticia*, the latter *analysis subrepticia*.\nQuantity presents no thing itself, but indeed *substantia*, *causa*, *compositum*.",["“thing” renders Ding in the first sentence and Sache/Sachen thereafter.","*petitionem noumeni* is kept in the accusative as Kant wrote it."]],"reflm:4645":["In all cognitions is in the object\n1. the matter and the form of them, i.e. the quality.\n2. The difference, how it is given.\n3. The quantity to be noted.\nIn a judgment the matter is subject and predicate, but the form of each consists precisely in this, that the one is called subject etc etc.\nThe positing is copula: *est*. Quantity concerns both, either that the subject is a universal concept in itself etc etc.\nHere what is weighed is not what is logical in the judgment, but what is real in the concepts. It is the matter of the concepts.\nTo the matter belong objective *realdata*, e.g. cause, inherence, composition.\nMagnitude concerns only the division of the representation, as it can arise from repetition, but not the real composition and the origin from it with antecedent simple.",["“them” (derselben) and “it” (sie) in items 1–2 may refer to the cognitions or to the object; the list hangs on “is … to be noted”, as in the German.","“what is logical … what is real” renders das Logische … das reale; “with antecedent simple” keeps mit vorhergegangenem Einfachen without a noun."]],"reflm:4646":["We would have no concept of magnitude at all if we did not repeat one several times; thus time and its intuiting counter-image, space, are here wholly effective.\nMagnitude is for pure intuition in general without data.\nQuality for the difference of sensation in general.\nThe categories belonging under it relate to sensations of the relations which are posited through the activity of the soul.\nPosition to the faculty of invention, the [Int] sensation and the bricht ab.\n(g Categories of quantity, quality and position.)",["“intuiting counter-image” renders Anschauendes Gegenbild; “faculty of invention” renders Dichtungsvermögen.","“[Int]” renders the struck-out beginning “[Ans]” (Anschauung); “bricht ab” (breaks off) is the AA's note, kept as printed."]],"reflm:4647":["Among the maxims of reason belongs that even that which contains the most distinct relation to ends has nevertheless arisen according to the order of nature.\nMy ontotheological proof, which is correct in this, that according to the order of nature under the same creator we become aware of relations to ends.",["The second sentence has no main verb in the German and is left incomplete."]],"reflm:4648":["Am Innenrand quer:\nMotion is something that happens, thus belongs to the actual appearances and not to the mere sensible form, also presupposes something mo|vable\ni.e. alterable with regard to place, which cannot be cognized a priori, but presupposes empirical concepts, among them also concepts of the understanding.",["“Am Innenrand quer” (on the inner margin, crosswise) is the AA's locus note, kept as printed; “actual” renders wirklichen."]],"reflm:4649":["Am Innenrand quer:\nThe understanding represents things as they are, but not in *intuition*, but in *reflexion*, i.e. not as they are given in general, but as they are thought. That which the appearances of the object for the same sense among themselves bricht ab.",["*intuition* and *reflexion* are Kant's Latin terms; “Am Innenrand quer” (on the inner margin, crosswise) and “bricht ab” (breaks off) are the AA's notes, kept as printed."]],"reflm:4650":["Crosswise on the inner margin:\nM XXV:\nAll *axiomata univoca*, whether of sensibility or of reason, [are] can be correct.\nThe *axiomata aeqvivoca*, where either the subject is intellectual and the predicate sensible or the reverse, are reduced, either through *interpolation* in the first case or through *restriction* in the second.\nM XXVI:\nThe *axiomata* or *postulata rationalia* can become true only through one's not exactly limiting them, but restricting them to the conditions of cognition *a priori* or of *comprehension*, so that there are properly no *axiomata intellectus puri* at all.",["\"limiting\" and \"restricting\" render limitiren and restringiren, which Kant here contrasts.","\"comprehension\" renders the loanword comprehension (comprehensio)."]],"reflm:4651":["Crosswise on the inner margin:\nA learned journal ought by rights to announce the progress of a science. This has until now not been able to hap|pen\nwith metaphysical works. Now it can happen, and indeed as regards completeness, distinctness as well as *precision*. Indeed, the completion of the science too can be announced, and indeed not after a very long time.",["The line break | falls inside ge|schehen (\"happen\"); English word order moves \"with metaphysical works\" after it.","\"progress\" renders Fortgang; \"completion\" renders Vollendung."]],"reflm:4652":["Crosswise on the inner margin:\nI at first doubted whether motion belongs to the transcendental aesthetic. now I see that, since it contains something in space which is moved, hence the alteration of something with respect to relations, it contains not mere sensibility but an intellectual concept.",[]],"reflm:4653":["Crosswise on the inner margin:\nOur understanding does not give materials through which we can cognize a new world, but forms, in order to arrange the present one according to them.",[]],"reflm:4654":["In and \nThe *contrapositum* of the principle of contradiction is a subjective law. Of thinkability, the supreme *criterio veritatis* of Crusius.",["The location heading \"In und\" is incomplete in the source: the references it joined are missing.","\"thinkability\" renders Denklichkeit; *criterio veritatis* is kept in the case Kant gives it."]],"reflm:4655":["In and \n*principium identitatis* and *contradictionis* are both called *contradictionis*. indemonstrable judgments stand immediately under it; material principles, in which the predicate is determined, are indemonstrable.\nIs the *principium* of judgments and of their possibility.\nThe possibility of things requires a real ground.",["The location heading \"In und\" is incomplete in the source.","darunter (\"under it\") may refer to the principle so named or to both *principia*.","The sentence \"Is the *principium* ...\" has no subject in the source."]],"reflm:4656":["In and \nThe most general object is object in general,\nbeing and not being, *aliqvid et nihil*.\n(s Something or nothing according to the concept: possible, impossible; or objectively: positive, negative.)\nThe possibility of predicates or of things.\nEvery object is either possible or impossible. The former affirmative or negative. The — in the logical sense means: not to posit, in the real: to lift out the thing.",["\"object\" renders obiect, except in \"object in general\" (Gegenstand).","ausheben (\"lift out\") is as printed; it may stand for aufheben (\"cancel\").","The location heading \"In und\" is incomplete in the source."]],"reflm:4657":["Simple concepts can be given only through experience; thus the possibility in their relation cannot be seen into through reason. *Possibilitas synthetica*, *Analytica*.",["\"seen into\" renders eingesehen (einsehen, \"have insight into\")."]],"reflm:4658":["A necessary being, which is posited not merely under one condition, but|\nunder every possible condition, must through its existence have a relation to all possibility.\nIf a contingent thing is not, then it is\n=0A. If a necessary thing is not, then it is =0û, i.e. *nihil negativum*; through the *nihil negativum* everything is always cancelled d breaks off.",["The line break | falls inside son|dern (\"but\").","The signs \"=0A\" and \"=0û\" are reproduced as printed; the reading is doubtful.","\"d\" is a broken-off word; \"breaks off\" renders the editorial bricht ab."]],"reflm:4659":["The common proof of the [existence of a] necessary existence of a most perfect being is that, if it did not exist, then it would lack a perfection, namely existence; that it would thus already contain existence in its concept. I answer: if it did not exist, then not to the most perfect being would something be lacking, but rather the most perfect being would be lacking entirely. There can surely be no contradiction if the thing itself is cancelled and nothing remains. The most perfect possible, which does not exist, is the most perfect among all that which is merely possible.",["\"existence\" renders Daseyn throughout; \"the thing itself\" renders die sache selbst (Sache, not Ding)."]],"reflm:4660":["ρ1? φ1?? M 31.\nThe necessity of a *principii* (of the rule) or of a *principiati*. The first is the necessity of *assumtion* according to reason.",["The Latin is kept in the genitive Kant gives it (principium, principiatum).","*assumtion* is Kant's spelling of the loanword (assumption)."]],"reflm:4661":["Next to and \nLet only the possibility of a necessary being be demonstrated, so is its existence also proven. For a necessary being |\nis (necessarily) there, that is its definition. One does not need reality for this. This is only supposed to serve to represent necessity as possible. But that all reality is, is possible; that none is, is also possible.",["The location heading \"Neben und\" is incomplete in the source.","\"Let only ... be demonstrated\" renders Nur ... darf dargethan werden (\"need only be demonstrated\").","\"is ... there\" renders ist ... da (cf. Daseyn, \"existence\")."]],"reflm:4662":["Under and \nSubstances among the appearances are only empirically necessary, i.e. they hang together with the whole of appearances according to time; but they are not absolutely necessary, because they can be cancelled together with all other appearances. Empty space and time.",["The location heading \"Unter und\" is incomplete in the source."]],"reflm:4663":["It is possible that a thing which is at one time not be at another time: expresses alterability.\n(s in the same time signifies contingency.)\nWhat is contingent is not on that account alterable; and what is alterable is not yet contingent.",["\"alterability\" renders Veränderlichkeit (cf. Veränderung, \"alteration\")."]],"reflm:4664":["It is (g a time) possible that something [is at a time] be. It is possible in just the same circumstances of time that it not be; i.e.: It is contingent under just the same hypotheses of time.",[]],"reflm:4665":["That in which there is a multitude determinate in itself is *qvantum discretum*.\nThe representation of a determinate multitude is *qvantitas discreta*.",["\"multitude\" renders Menge; \"determinate\" renders bestimmt in both sentences."]],"reflm:4666":["*De lege continui logica*. Living and dead force (g if it has a different measure) cannot be distinguished through motion and rest, because rest is an infinitely small motion or also infinitely small velocity. If virtue and vice were distinguished only according to degree, then a certain degree, as the boundary of both, would be virtue as well as vice. To be sure, the boundary of a magnitude can be regarded as something and also as nothing — (signifying); but never the ground for positing something however small as a ground for cancelling it, because these according to *quality* |\nare distinct or *disparata*. If heaviness were equal at all distances, then the body would be heavy even in the *centro*.\nIs the boundary of the matter of a body[also] determined through a leap, or does its density decrease imperceptibly up to a certain distance, where it is vanishing? The continuous in alteration and the boundaries, the beginning and end. everything is as it were referred to duration in a motion. All *qvanta discreta* are numbers. Are the magnitudes in nature like numbers? and are numbers something from which the natural magnitudes are possible? or conversely are numbers only divisions of already given magnitudes?\n*Lex continui aesthetica*.\n(s The *lex continui* means to say: two states following upon one another always have something in common, namely their boundary.\nHeaviness cannot be equal at all distances from the center point.)",["unterschiede may be read as \"distinct\" (unterschieden) or as the noun \"distinctions\".","\"heaviness\" renders Schweere, to keep its link with schweer (\"heavy\").","\"(signifying)\" renders bedeutend; its attachment to \"nothing\" is left as in the source."]],"reflm:4667":["All action and reaction of substances distinct from one another is possible only insofar as many make up a unity, without which the state of the one would not concern that of the other at all; and [the] two opposed determinations conflict with one another only insofar as they are found in one; thus if many substances were not combined into unity, then the state of the one would never conflict with the state of the other.",["\"action and reaction\" renders Wirkung und Gegenwirkung (Wirkung elsewhere \"effect\").","\"conflict\" renders widerstreiten (Kant writes both widerstreiten and wiederstreiten)."]],"reflm:4668":["If our representations are not effects of the objects, then they prove their existence not otherwise than through revelation.",["\"objects\" renders obiecten."]],"reflm:4669":["Of short expressions that contain much sense. The white bear. We learn in youth to talk of it. The philosopher talks of the white bear when he talks of the state after death. Clergymen have written large books for and against about the white bear. And the frontispiece of such writings ought always to be the white bear. Further the proposition: do let each one on his hobbyhorse the streets of the city etc etc. That is understanding in the vehicle.\n(g We can let the theologians ride on their hobbyhorses of linguistic research and criticism to their heart's content; but it is not to be endured that they compel the commonwealth to sit up behind.)\n(g It is too much to get heated over the white bear even to the wildness of a bear.)\nM305:\n(g When two people quarrel over the white bear, I look on calmly, do not mix myself in it.)",["The proposition \"do let each one ...\" has no main verb in the source; it is cut off by \"etc etc\".","\"commonwealth\" renders Gemeine wesen; \"criticism\" renders critik."]],"reflm:4670":["From the analogy of nature we prove most beautifully the future life. The ground of proof, however, consists in this, that we develop capacities out of ourselves which exceed our present vocation. This is shown above all in the sciences; thus it is already to be seen from this that [our] the sciences do not harmonize with our present vocation and are premature developments, which nevertheless, by betraying a talent that should have remained hidden, at least have the use of discovering our future vocation. Meanwhile the sciences do indeed give this prospect, but no incentive. They belong to the play of the human race, but not to its ends.",["\"vocation\" renders Bestimmung; \"ground of proof\" renders Beweisgrund; \"incentive\" renders triebfeder."]],"reflm:4671":["Remark of Kant's on the last page of the letter of his brother J.H. Kant of 3 July 1773 (2 X 140f.) in Vol. II of the Dorpat collection of letters to Kant, p. 492:\nAll morality consists in the derivation of actions from the idea of the subject, not from sensation. The idea is universally valid [and contains] both from the end (g *abstrahendo*) and from the relation to all (g *combinando*).\nThe sources of all cognition of experience [lie in] are transcendental. They are (g inner) anticipations. [The]",["\"cognition of experience\" renders Erfahrungserkentnis.","The first paragraph's second sentence stands as printed after the deletion; it has no complement for \"valid\"."]],"reflm:4672":["Remark of Kant's on the back (address page) of the letter from E.T. von Kortum of 18 Nov. 1773 (2 X 142f.) in the III. Dorpat volume of letters, sheet 27ψ.\nCrosswise to the left of the address:\nFirst there must be certain titles of thinking under which appearances in themselves are brought: e.g. whether they are regarded as magnitude or as subject or as ground or as whole or merely as reality (figure is no reality). I will for that reason not regard in appearance what I want as subject or, as I want, either as subject or predicate, but it is determined as subject |\nrespectively as ground. What logical function therefore is properly valid of one appearance in respect of the other, whether that of magnitude or of the subject, thus which function of judgments. For otherwise we can use logical functions at will, without settling, also without perceiving, that the object is more suited to one than to the other. Thus one can think an appearance without bringing it under a title of thinking in general, hence determining an object for it.\nCrosswise to the right of the address:\nIn order that appearances be represented as proper to certain rules or as determined according to them, it is required that they be represented as belonging under one or another function of them. Through this they become in a determinate way objects of thoughts; otherwise there is nothing in their relations (for sensations [lass] are not thoughts) that would make them thinkable before the understanding.",["\"object\" renders obiect throughout.","\"of them\" renders derselben, which may refer to the rules or to the appearances.","\"before the understanding\" renders vor dem Verstande, which can also be read \"for the understanding\"."]],"reflm:4673":["of von Lossow's of 28 Apr. 1774 (2 X 167) in vol. III of the Dorpat collection of letters, leaves 6, 6υ, 7, 7υ.\nP. I. Above the salutation:\n[single. I.e. all representations make in my inner state through the unity of intuition] For as intuition of a single subject [obtain all ob] all objects belong only to one breaks off?\nBetween salutation and letter, as well as between its first four lines:\nAt the same time with the sense, [und] under which they are offered to our mind breaks off?\n1. Time single. [For it is a sub] Which means as much as: I can all objects only in myself and in the representations found in [him this] my |\nsingle subject [cognize insofar only according to the form of inner intuition immediately cognize.] (g intuit, and all possible objects of my intuition stand in relation among one another according to the particular form of this intuition.)\n[2. It is infinite]\n2. It is infinite without first and last. For it is the [*datum*] condition of *coordination* through inner sense, which [through] must be determined and limited not otherwise than through the representations which are [pl] placed according to it. The [mere form of the thi recep] *receptivity* of coordination cannot limit itself.\n3. It is necessary, i.e. it depends on no thing, but lies at the ground of all, is thus [the first *datum* of possibility] the condition of inner intuitions and lies at the ground of the possibility of all intuitions.\n4. In it all things and all states of things have their determinate position. For [only through the re] they have (g must) through the unity of inner sense their determinate relation to all [possible th] other alleged objects of intuition.\n5. It precedes all actual things and can [*a priori*] itself consequently also [the co] be cognized as condition of objects *a priori*.\nBetween X2 16722 and 16725:\n(g The inner determinations are not in space.)\nWith these concepts we cannot come out of the world.\n1. If, then, a *subject* is a thing in general and the *predicate* is space and time or a concept built upon them as condition, then |\nthe judgment is transcendent. (g Everything is somewhere and somewhen.) (g I do not say that it is false; it only does not conclude, *non liquet*. That is [however] namely false, that from this *subjective* something universal and *objective* should follow.)\n2. If the *subject* is given only through (g *predicates* of) inner sensation and the *predicate* contains a condition of outer sensibility, then it is also transcendent.\nP. II:\nRemark.\nSpace is nothing but the intuition of the mere form even without given matter, thus pure intuition. It is a singular representation on account of the unity of the subject (g and of the capacity), in which all representations of outer *objects* can be placed (g beside) one another. It is infinite, because in the capacity to receive there are no boundaries. It is necessary, because it is the first condition of the possibility of outer representations; consequently it is the ground of the capacity for outer representations, and we cannot represent the opposite to ourselves, because otherwise we would have to have yet another [oth] higher capacity. It is something actual, which does not depend on the existence of things; [but] for the capacity to intuit does not depend on the existence of things, can thus be cognized *a priori*.\nSpace is not an object of intuitions (an *object* or its determination), but the intuition itself, which precedes all objects and [through which it] in which (g if) they are |\nplaced, the appearance of them is possible. It is a pure intuition *a priori*. But how is such an intuition possible. [It is] It is nothing other than the consciousness of one's own *receptivity* to receive representations (impressions) of things according to certain relations among one another.\nThe *spatium absolutum*, this riddle of the philosophers, is entirely something correct (g but not real, rather ideal), otherwise one would not be able to say something about it *a priori*, and indeed not through universal concepts, but through properties that can be perceived in it through [inner per] immediate apprehension. But it is nothing external, but the condition of the form of all outer representation subsisting in the mind itself. It is [no] nothing imagined (*ens imaginarium*). For it is the sole actual condition of the representation of actual outer things. The order of things that are beside one another is not space, but space is that which makes such an order or better *coordination* possible according to determinate conditions. If it is a mere universal concept of order, then let one try how much one can derive from it and how one comes *a priori* [the] to the necessity of such an order; for to borrow it *a posteriori* is first against the evidence of the eyes, and then it would have only the consequences of an observation, but not of a [fundamental re] fundamental representation.\nSpace as an outer representation would also have to have something in the object through which it would be effected in the mind. For it would be no representation *a priori*. But where there is as yet nothing at all, there is also no influence, and the mere form cannot be communicated to us through influence.\nIn general, that there are things which *correspond* to sensibility the understanding must cognize; thus the *ideality* of space is nothing further than the distinguishing of sensibility and of that which is posited through it from the understanding and what is thought through it. Through the *ideality* the actuality of bodies |\n(g of certain beings that *correspond* to it) and of certain properties is not denied, indeed not thought of otherwise than merely negatively.\nThe *ideality* of space does not cancel its *reality* with respect to bodies, i.e. all [sens] outer objects of sensibility, and space actually belongs to them, but only distinguishes objects of the senses as such from things in themselves. To a thing in itself no space belongs (g as condition or determination), but every object of the outer senses is thought through the condition of space.\nP. III. Below X2 16733-35:\nThe propositions about [s] the properties of absolute space and time seemed strange; one thus either denied absolute time and made it into an *abstracto* or *empirico*, or one [vorichtete] made time *objective* and made it into a most real ideal, i.e. a *chimera*.\nTo the right of X2 16734f., above and below ll. 12-16:\nWe have no intuitions otherwise than through the senses; thus no other concepts can dwell in the understanding than those which pertain to the *disposition* and the order among these intuitions. These concepts must contain the universal and rules. The faculty of rules *in abstracto*: the learned understanding, *in concreto*: the healthy one. The healthy understanding has [therefore vielfelt] in all cases the advantage where the rule must be abstracted from the cases *a posteriori*; but where it has its origin entirely *a priori*, there it has no place at all.\nP. IV:\nSpace is the condition attaching to our sensibility with respect to all outer appearance, for it is the form of this capacity |\nof the mind of perceiving things as external [to]. Hence bodies are representations that [necessarily in this cond] are possible only under this condition [and are themselves nothing], and to that extent [has] is space certainly something real. [The *ideality*] But through a body there is thought not an object of cognition in general, but a thing as an object of outer sense. Space as predicate thus does not pertain at all to a thing in itself, but only as the object of outer sense. It is the condition not of things, but of the phenomenon [of the] of [a] the things, and indeed [who with respect to the through outer sense] of outer sense. Hereby sensibility is only distinguished from the understanding, through which something is represented not as it is given to us as object of the senses, but is thought independently of that.\nVarious predicates of space, now, which one otherwise regarded as *objective*, can be explained through this concept with respect to their origin. 1. Space is single, because it is the form of representations (g of all possible outer objects) in a single subject. 2. Space is infinite. For the capacity [of the add] to admit more impressions of outer things, or receptivity, has in itself no limitations. 3. Space is necessary; for it is that on which the possibility of the senses itself is grounded.\nThe representation of space is no imagining and one that refers merely to the subject (g all-comprehending), but a [means] condition for representing outer things, and a means of ordering them. The order is in conformity with the inner form.\nThe omnipresence of space and the eternity of time. That it is present everywhere, i.e. it is itself the condition of all presence, for through it presence is cognized.\nThe former signifies: that we can intuit [to ourselves] no thing as present except somewhere in space.\nReason is the faculty of rules *a priori*.\nA rule *a priori* is a rule of reason [and this]. A rule from concepts *in abstracto* is a law. Reason is the faculty of laws. A rule of the *construction* of concepts is called formula. A symbolic formula —\n[Reason considers the uncondi] Concepts of reason are unconditionally valid concepts, thus the all, the first, the [un] transcendent. The unconditioned necessity, the unconditioned *principium* (g the independent *principium*), the unconditioned (g unrestricted) all.\n––––––––––\nSpace and time contain the conditions of the rules of appearance, hence all categories, with respect to their application, lay them at the ground.\n––––––––––\nThe question whether space is something ideal (not imaginary) or real does not interest various sciences at all. It is in mathematics, mechanics and general physics not heeded; although Leibniz as well as Newton (I name them here at the head of the other great names), the latter assumes its subsisting *reality*, the former the adhering one, yet both posit in the application to objects of the world their, as if [it] both, space and time, were [containers] containers of things subsisting for themselves, and even though the *ideality* has been proved by us, it can make nothing different with respect to such investigations. But there, where these answers become transcendent, there it sounds otherwise.\nDifferent things are in different places and conversely: the difference of places proves the difference of things (is already a proposition that concerns the empirical understanding).\nEven if we cannot comprehend many propositions from the concepts of space and time, we must note that they are not representations of reason, but of intuition and [that we the] breaks off.",["“single”: einig, Einig; “singular representation”: einzelne Vorstellung; “capacity”: Fähigkeit; “receptivity”: Empfänglichkeit, and in italics the Latinate receptivitaet; “object” renders both Gegenstand and obiect, the latter in italics.","Doubtful readings: “alleged” (angeblichen) in no. 4; “For it would be” renders “Denn würde”, perhaps for “Dann würde”; “their” renders an unclear “ihr” in the Leibniz–Newton sentence; the struck-out [vorichtete] and [darum vielfelt] are garbled and left as they stand.","The editorial lines are translated: S. = p., Z. = l., Bl. = leaf, υ = verso; X2 is AA X, 2nd ed.; “breaks off” renders the AA's “bricht ab”."]],"reflm:4674":["S. I:\nThe principles of appearance (g in general) are merely of the form, namely time.\nThe *principium* of the exposition of appearances is the ground of exposition in general of that which has been given. The exposition of that which is thought rests merely on consciousness, but of that which is given, if one regards the matter as undetermined, on the ground of all relation and of the concatenation of representations (sensations). The concatenation is grounded (as the appearance is not on mere [influence] sensation, but in inner principles of form) not on the mere appearance, but is a representation of the inner action of the mind of connecting representations, not merely of placing them by one another in intuition, but of making a whole as regards the matter. Here there is thus unity, not [the] by virtue of that: wherein, but: whereby the manifold is brought into one, hence universal validity. Hence it is not forms but functions on which the *relationes* of appearances rest. The exposition of appearances is thus the determination of the ground on which the interconnection of the sensations [the sa] in them rests.\n––––––––––\nBy a universal concept of a sensible *dati*, in which the reality and at the same time its relation to the sensible condition in general is indicated, we understand the action of determining an object sensibly according to such conditions; e.g. what happens signifies the action of determining something in time according to sequence. Now x is this determinable, which contains the conditions of the determination; a, however, signifies only the action of determining in general. It is thus no wonder if in x, beyond the action of deter|mining\nstill more is contained, which is expressed by b (g still more is contained in the concept a, which belongs to it in order to determine it in the mind, i.e. to cognize the way in which it is generated or specified in the mind, or what flows from its specification or is combined with it as condition). e.g. In space, beyond the universal action of constructing a triangle, still the magnitude of the angles, and in inner sense, beyond the designation of that [wha] in general which happens, still the conditions under which alone this happening (g as apprehension) can be determined in the mind. These conditions are found in x in the case of intuition through the construction of a, e.g. triangle, but in a real concept through [the subject] the *concretum* of the subject in which the representation a is posited. Thus the relation which is thought through a is to be determined solely through the real condition of the subject, which consists in the (g function of) relative positing in general and with regard to the *dati* a in particular; because the subjective condition (g x) is to suffice for all these positings, the determination of a, i.e. b, must be a universal action whereby the appearance of a is expounded i.e. breaks off.\n(g Only [with regard to] of the relation do objectively synthetic propositions of appearance hold.)\nIn synthetic propositions there is properly represented not the relation among the concepts immediately [but] (for this happens only in analytic ones), but in the [subject giv] conditions of their concrete representation in the subject, be it intuition or appearance. This subject contains the conditions of the representation of everything of which we have concepts, and in its sensibility [must yet the objects] the objective of them must yet be determined. x always signifies the object of the concept a. But it can be no object except either of pure or of empirical intuiting. As concerns the |\nlatter, the concept a can refer either to a given object of the senses x or to conditions of sensibility under which an object [of the concept of the], insofar as it merely corresponds to the concept x, must be given and under which alone it can be cognized as conforming to a.\nS. II:\nThis is a proof that space is a subjective condition, because, since the propositions about it are synthetic and (g thereby objects) can be cognized *a priori*, this would be impossible if space were not a subjective condition of the representation of these objects.\nOn the other hand the synthetic judgments of experience are cognized *a posteriori*, because they are directed immediately at given objects.\nBut now if of things, not merely as regards the form of appearance, but with regard to their remaining constitution, something is to be cognized *a priori* etc etc.\n––––––––––\n(g The x drops out, for it is to signify the object which is thought through a; but because b is merely compared with the concept a and thereby already determined, the rest in x is indifferent; if a is considered *adiective*, then the proposition is not always universal.)\nb must be a determination of a and no analytic predicate. Analytic predicates are identical and tautological. Of the analytic hypothetical judgments. disjunction, dichotomy. (g categorical ones are the foundation.) The concept substance and *accidens* gives in itself a *synthesin*, likewise cause and effect and multitude in a real unity. That now nature, according to the various relations to inner sense, must stand throughout under one of these *syntheses*.\nx is thus the determinable (object) which I think through the concept a, and b is its determination (g or way of determining it). In mathematics x is the construction of a, in experience the *concretum*, with regard to an inhering representation or thought |\nin general x is the function of thinking in general in the subject, there the (g real) concept a is thus [determined] determined together with everything: 1. through the subject, 2. with regard to succession through the ground, 3. with regard to coexistence through the composition.\n(g [The condition whereby a gets an object x is enunciated in b but] x is the object. This can be given *a priori* in construction, but in exposition (which is something quite other than observation, which has nothing [with] combined *a priori* with a) the conditions can be cognized *a priori* in the subject under which a /in general relates to an object, namely a real one. This object can be represented only according to its relations and is nothing other than the subjective representation (of the subject) itself, but made universal, for I am the original of all objects. It is thus the conjugation as function which makes the exponent of a rule.)\n––––––––––\nThe reality must be given (g in sensation). The magnitude we can construct (g as regards intuition). The [real] real synthesis is not merely given to us in sensation, also cannot be constructed, but yet lies in the appearance neither as intuition nor sensation. For experience does yet give us to cognize substance, effect and cause and whole (although we cannot think the latter to ourselves *a priori*, namely how of many things one reciprocally determines all others and is determined by them, and can take much together only in thought). These three kinds of concepts refer to objects as appearances (possibility etc etc. only as concepts *a priori*); with magnitude I need no sensation, but only time, with the real synthesis both sensation in general and time. (Threefold dimension of synthesis. [We can thus] How then can we represent to ourselves [the] *positiones* of the postulates of synthesis *a priori*. They are the three functions of apperception, which are encountered in [all] the thinking of our state in general |\nand under which all appearance must fit for this reason, because in it no synthesis lies in itself, if the mind does not add such or make it out of its *datis*. The mind is thus to itself the archetype of [the possibility] such a synthesis through original and not derived thinking.\n––––––––––\nConcepts give only the outline of objects, namely that [whereby one] which is a sign of their representation. b is at all times compared with the object x by means of a, but x is not always considered merely in the concept a; in the latter case b refers either to the way in which the object a is given *a priori* (g objectively) in intuition, or *a posteriori* in experience, or *a priori*, but [subjectively] in the subjective perception of apperception. This latter refers only to cases of perception, and indeed to the *synthesin* in it, i.e. to the relation. Apperception is the perception of oneself as a thinking subject in general.\nApperception is the consciousness of thinking, i.e. of representations as they are posited in the mind. Herein there are three exponents: 1. the relation to the subject, 2. the relation of sequence among one another, 3. of taking together. The determination of a in these *momentis* of apperception is the subsumption under one of these *actibus* of thinking; one (g cognizes it as in itself determinable and thus objective, namely) the concept a, if one brings it under one of these universal actions of thinking by means of which it comes under a rule. A proposition of this kind is a *principium* of the rule, thus of the cognition of appearance through the understanding, whereby [it] it is considered as something objective, which is [not] thought in itself independently of the singularity in which it was given.",["'object' renders both Gegenstand and the Latinate obiect/Obiekt; 'determine' renders both bestimmen and the Latinate determiniren; 'principles' renders principien, not Grundsatz.","[wha], [the sa] and [subject giv] render words Kant broke off and struck out: [wa], [derse], [subiekt gege].","'magnitude' reads Die große as Größe; the slash in '/in general' (/überhaupt) and the unclosed parenthesis after 'Threefold dimension' are the transcription's."]],"reflm:4675":["Remarks of Kant on Bertram's letter, folded into two octavo leaves, of 20 May 1775 (2 X 182). R I 21-26.\nS. I (address side):\nThe same being can subsist together with opposed predicates one after another. [The predicates so] Something is posited outside us only insofar as its representation makes up constancy and a particular point of reference.\nIf my representation follows upon something, then the object of it would not yet follow upon that, if its representation were not determined through something as a sequence, which can never happen otherwise than according to a universal law. Or there must be a universal law that all sequence is determined through something preceding, otherwise I would posit no sequence of the objects to the sequence of the representations. For to posit objects to my representations, to this there always belongs that the representation be determined according to a universal law, for in the universally valid point the object precisely consists.\nLikewise I would not represent something as outside me [if] and thus make appearance into experience (*obiectiv*), if the representations did not refer to something that is parallel to my I, whereby I refer them from me to another subject. Likewise if manifold representations did not determine one another according to a universal law. The three relations in the mind thus require three analogies of appearance, in order to transform the [functions of the mind] subjective functions of the mind into objective ones and [its] thereby to make them into concepts of the understanding, which give reality to the appearances.\nEverything that is at the same time in reciprocal relations belongs to one whole: *contra vacuum* [*separans*] *interrumpens* (*vacuum* [*ter*] *terminans*); from this follows the continuity of the breaks off.\nAll this is grounded on conditions of experience; consequently it is not necessary and is also not seen into as such, |\nbut they are *analoga* of axioms, which take place *a priori*, but [only] only as anticipations of all laws of experience in general.\nEverything that happens is *a priori* connected; everything that is at the same time is *comitative* connected; everything that is there is *inhaesive* connected.\nThe axioms have a primitive, the analogies a derivative, the petitions an adopted certainty. [De] The derivative certainty from the nature of our thinking (g in general)[as] not as appearances, but actions of the subject, which must think, [if it] insofar as there is to be an object, in a substance [be], be determined through a ground and be combined with the whole of the power of representation. It is thus derived from the subjective real condition of thinking in general. Everything that belongs to an aggregate (g *obiective*) is in reciprocal determination with one another, for otherwise it is only a subjective (g ideal) whole.\nS. II (containing signature and date):\n(g Intuition\nThinking.\n*a priori*\nSensibility\nUnderstanding\nReason\nThe understanding thus combines both extremes in that it connects the *data a posteriori* with conditions *a priori*, but still only *in concreto*, consequently [in a] to an empirical cognition.)\nThe ideal or realsubject\n—\n—\n—\n—\nSeries\n—\n—\n—\n—\nAggregate.\nThe former are only [*actus*] actions of the mind, the second is something [unte] in the objects in themselves in relation to thinking without distinction of the subject.\nBoth are distinguished through the necessity of the relations, which flows from the universality; the subject, which always lies at the ground, the member of the series, from which something determinate always follows, the manifold, of which each is determined by the remaining ones and in turn determines them.\nThe aggregate considered *obiective* must have a common ground of unity, whereby the manifold depends on one another. (g The sequel of this is: much that agrees together among one another has a common ground.)\n[See image below: successio aggregatio sustentatio]\nContinuity in space and time.\nOn the intellectuation of apprehension.\na and b can in a threefold way by means of the x in relation\nx\n/ \\\nbe: either a:b or a:x:b or a+b=x.\nThe inner necessity of appearance, since namely it is detached from everything subjective and [under a] regarded as determinable through a universal rule (of the appearances), is the objective. The objective is the ground of the agreement of the appearances among one another.\n––––––––––\nIn all three unities necessity prevails. Every aggregate is contingent; hence there must be something whereby its *respectus* become necessary. All happening is contingent, hence its origin must be necessary. Everything that breaks off.\n––––––––––\nThe objective is the ground of the agreement of the appearances. Hence threefold agreement: 1. in the common subject, 2. in the [a] common beginning, 3. in the common whole.\nS. III (letter side). Across the letter:\nThe difference of all our cognitions[of the] is as to matter (content, object) or as to form. As concerns the latter, it is intuition or concept. The former is of the object, insofar as it is |\ngiven, the latter: insofar as it is thought. The faculty of intuition is sensibility, of thinking is understanding (of thinking *a priori*, without the object's being given, reason). The understanding is therefore opposed to sensibility and to reason. The perfection of cognition as to intuition is aesthetic, as to concepts is logical. Intuition is either of the object (*apprehensio*) or of ourselves; the latter (*apperceptio*) goes to all cognitions, also those of the understanding and reason.\nTranscendental logic treats of cognitions of the understanding as to content, but undetermined with regard to the way in which objects are given.\nAbove the letter, parallel to it:\nThe condition of all apperception is the unity of the thinking subject. From this flows the connection (g of the manifold) according to a rule and in a whole, because [as well] the unity of the function must suffice both for subordination and for coordination.\nS. IV:\nKeys. Cupboard.\nInkwell. Pen and knife. Paper. Writings. Books.\nSlippers. Boots. Fur. Cap. Night-trousers.\nNapkins. Tablecloth. Hand towel. Plates. Dish. Knife and fork. Saltcellar.\nBottle. Wine and beer glasses. Bottle of wine.\nTobacco. Pipes. Tea things. Tea. Sugar.\nBrush.\nOf concepts that nevertheless cannot be determined *a priori*, i.e. constructed.\n(g If x, which is the objective condition of the a, is at the same time the subjective one of b, then a synthetic proposition arises which is true only *restrictive*. e.g. All existence belongs to a substance, everything that happens, [into a series] under a member of the series, everything that is at the same time, into a whole ([of wh] whose parts determine one another reciprocally.). x, time, wherein (g insofar as therein) is determined what happens, is the subjective condition to think it [ve] in the concept of the understanding only as [to think substance] sequence from a ground. The subjective condition signifies the condition of the specification of a concept of the understanding corresponding to this relation. Such principles are not axioms. Actual anticipations of appearance there are none. One finds them confirmed through experiences, because [the] laws of experience become possible through them. Other appearances give no laws. They have no evidence, because not the appearances, but experiences become possible through them. Synthesis of thinking and of appearance.)\nThe subjective conditions of appearance which can be cognized *a priori* are space and time: intuitions.\nThe subjective condition of empirical cognition is apprehension in time in general and thus according to conditions of inner sense in general.\nThe subjective condition of rational cognition (g is) construction [in time] through the condition of apprehension in general.\n––––––––––\n[Rules of critique]\n[Laws of intuition. Analogies of nature]\nThe universal relation of sensibility to the understanding and reason is either that whereby they are given *a priori* [or the], thus the (g sensible) condition of intuition, secondly the sensible condition of judgment in general about what is given, finally the sensible condition of the concept *a priori*. The rules (g *a priori*) which enunciate these conditions contain in general the relation of the subjective to the objective. Either of the subjective whereby the objective is given, or of that whereby it is thought as given in general (as object) or is determined *a priori*.\nEverything that is given is thought under the universal conditions of apprehension. Thus the subjectively universal of apprehension is the condition of the objectively universal of intellection. Everything is thought *a priori* under the subjective condition of construction, although the latter is only problematic, i.e. the condition is not given, but yet necessary for construction. To determine *a priori* is to construct.",["“object” renders both Gegenstand and the Latinate Obiect/Obiekt; “refer” renders both beziehen and referiren; “connected” renders the loanword connex; “sequence”, “sequel” render Folge.","In “actions of the subject, which must think, … in a substance [seyn], be determined …”, denken (“think”) and the other infinitives all depend on the closing muß; the sentence is kept as broken as the German. [unte] and [ve] are struck, cut-off words.","“Cupboard” renders Schaff (East Prussian for a cupboard); “evidence” renders evidentz (evidentness, certainty)."]],"reflm:4676":["S. I-II:\n(g In all reality there is the relation of substance to accident, in that which happens: of the ground to the consequence etc. etc.)\nThe principle of identity and of contradiction contains the comparison of two predicates a and b with x, but only [through] so that [x by mea] the |\nconcept a of x is compared with b (*substantive*), thus the x becomes useless. It is a principle of form, not of content, thus merely logical. A principle of analysis, from which nothing is cognized *obiective*. [It can In cat] It can be cognized in categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive form. If I refer both predicates to the x and thereby to one another, then it is synthetic: no x who is learned is without science, for there [it signifies] it requires the restriction of time, namely: at the same time. The (g lack of) science does indeed contradict learnedness, but not the human being x who is learned, except insofar as he is learned. Thus the contradiction is directed either at the concept a which I have of x or at x, to which this concept does not necessarily attach. The synthetic validity of b and non b in regard to the x, which can be thought through the concept a or non a, is called change.\nBut if a cannot be separated from b in x, e.g. no x that is a body is indivisible, then it is to be seen that the x which is thought through a can never be thought through non a, that no [body] being which has the nature of a body can never become incorporeal, and that the a in itself is in regard to the x no predicate, but is a reciprocal concept with it and thus is valid *substantive*.\nDifference of the affirmative and negative propositions and of the principles of affirmation and negation, which are equal according to their contents.\n––––––––––\nBut if a and b are not identical, whether they be used affirmatively or negatively, and x is not wholly (g determined) thought through the concept of a, then a and b are not in a logical, but real relation (g something different) of combination, hence not of [ident] involution. Thus their relation is determined not through [them] their concepts in themselves, but by means of the x, of which a contains the designation. How are such *syntheses* possible. x must be a *datum* of sensibility, [which then through] in which a synthesis, i.e. a relation of coordination, takes place; for [a discursive] this |\ncontains more than is thought through its concept a, and is the representation of a *in concreto*. Now there are three cases where a transcendental subject is sensible and [through conc] furnishes a relation of concepts: either that it is the [construction] intuition of a or the appearance of a or the appearance of a (g or the empirical cognition). In the former case [is] the relation of a:b follows from the construction of the a=x. In the second [and third] from [the R Exp] [example of the] [empirical object of a in d] the sensible condition of the intellection of a, in the third drawn from observation. The two first *syntheses* are *a priori* (all three objective). Because in the second case a [the an experience-c] signifies a universally sensible condition of perception, but x the condition of the subject in general, in which the relation of all perceptions is determinable (for perceptions are not merely objective, but presuppose sensation, which has only subjective validity). So [the Re] a will signify the universal of perception, x the (g sensible) condition of the subject [in which] (*substratum*), in which this perception is supposed to receive its place. Consequently the condition of the disposition. b finally the universal function of the mind, to determine for the a its place in x, thus the exponent of the relation of perceptions [in the mind toward one another], hence to determine their place according to a rule.\nS. III:\nIf something is apprehended, then it is taken up into the function of apperception. I am, I think, thoughts are in me. These are altogether relations, which do not indeed give rules of appearance, but make it that all appearance is represented as contained under rules. The I constitutes the *substratum* for a rule in general, and apprehension relates every appearance to it.\nFor the origination of a rule three pieces are required: 1. x. as the *datum* for a rule (object of sensibility or rather sensible real representation). 2. a. the *aptitudo* for the rule or the condition through which it is related to a rule at all. 3. b. the exponent of the rule.\nNow if a norm for the rule of appearances in general or of experiences is to arise. e.g. Everything existing is in the substance, then x is sensation in general as the specif: of reality. Through its being represented as reality, it becomes the matter of a rule or [it] becomes sensation capable of a rule, and a is only a function of the apprehension of appearance as given in general. Now because everything must be given in time, it thus comprehends everything in itself, then b. is an [e function] *actus* of apperception, namely [the] the consciousness of the subject which apperceives, as of that which is given in the whole of time, is necessarily combined with it, for otherwise the sensation would not be represented as belonging to me.\n––––––––––\ntranscendental Thetic: of the establishment of the principles of pure reason. Antithetic: of the (g natural) use of these principles [in general]. For general logic also treats of the natural |\nuse [of the same] in the common understanding. The basic rules of the latter are abstracted from the common understanding, although not borrowed and derived from it. But the universal rules or principles of thinking in general without determinate objects or determination of cognition from the relation to the objects is at all times dialectical.\nS. IV:\nWe think everything to ourselves through predicates, thus there is at all times a relation to x. But in judgments there is a relation of a:b, which both relate to x. a and b in x, x by means of the a:b, finally a+b=x.\n––––––––––\nThe (g absolute) predicate in general is reality and of what.\nDeterminate predicates (g relation predicates), which are real, concern only relations. Of these there are three. according to the three relations in judging.\nThe relation-predicates are transcendental, the relation of the predicates is logical.\nWhat sort of action does the relation predicate express in the mind, and on what is its relation grounded, on the one side to sensibility, on the other side to the logical, so that through the first it receives reality, through the second the form of thinking.\nIs the x the form of (g inner) sensibility or the real of apprehension?",["“principle” renders Satz (of identity and contradiction), Princip and Grundsatz alike; “relation” renders both Verhältnis and Beziehung.","*substantive*, *obiective*: Kant's Latin adverbs, “substantively”, “objectively”; “change” renders wechsel, “reciprocal concept” wechselbegrif.","“it becomes the matter of a rule”: er, masculine, has no evident antecedent; “specif:” is abbreviated in the source."]],"reflm:4677":["S. I:\nOnly through this, that [that] the relation which is posited according to the conditions of intuition is assumed as determinable according to a rule, [is from the] does the appearance relate to an object; otherwise it is only an inner affection of the mind.\n––––––––––\nEverything that is thought as an object of intuition stands under a rule of construction.\nEverything that is thought as an object of perception stands under a rule of apperception, self-perception.\n(g Experience in general. Either intuition or sensation.)\nThe appearance is made objective through this, that it is brought as contained under a title of self-perception. and thus the original relations of apprehension are the conditions [as well] of the perception of the (g real) relations in the appearance, and just through this, that one says: an appearance belongs under it, is it determined from the universal and is represented objectively, i.e. thought. Through one's representing it not as belonging [to] under the functions of self-sensation, but [as] by means of single perception, it is called mere sensation. We can just as well *a priori* from the functions of perception [determine] these in regard to the objective, i.e. the conditions [of the] which are independent of the single relations of the senses, determine, as in regard to the relations of space and time. The mind must have a faculty to apprehend, and its functions are just as necessary for perception as the receptivity of appearances.\nIf we intuited intellectually, then there would be need of no titles of apprehension in order to represent an object to oneself. It would then not appear at all. Now [is has] the appearance must be subordinated [to a ground] to a function through which the mind disposes over it, and indeed to a universal condition of it, because otherwise nothing universally valid would be met with in it.\n––––––––––\nAll synthetic propositions have a condition of sensibility (an opening one) Either for intuition [or and Per] |\nconstruction exposition\nspecification\n(pure or empirical) or of thinking through the understanding or of insight through reason.\nThe x thus contains at all times the condition.\nEither an objective one of appearance or a subjective one of pure intuitions, both in judgments where the predicate is sensible,\nor an objective one of the understanding in regard to [perception] intellection or a subjective one of reason in regard to conception, both in intellectual predicates.\n(g In an analytic proposition thus the subject is at all times taken *substantive*. The concept of learnedness is contradictory to the concept of the unlearned.)\nS. II:\nAll appearances relate to the concept of an object which is valid for them altogether, e.g. appearances of a quadrangle; they thus stand under rules of judging through which this concept can be determined (optical illusion). Perceptions are not appearances alone, i.e. representations [of that] of appearances, but of the existence of them. e.g. That reality is there, that it is after one another, that it is at the same time (g with) other reality. Perception is the position in inner sense in general and concerns sensation according to relations of the apperception of self-consciousness, according to which we become conscious of our own existence. All perception stands just as well under a rule of judging.\nPresumption is no anticipation, because it does not determine, but only says that something is determinable according to a rule yet to be found according to a certain given exponent. It thus serves to attempt this deter|mination\nand to expound the appearance, and is the *principium* of the judging of it. E.g. What happens has its ground in something or other preceding.",["“for intuition” renders vor die Anschauung (vor for für); “its functions” renders deren functionen, whose antecedent is uncertain.","“judging” renders Beurtheilung; “existence” renders both existentz and Daseyn.","“(pure or empirical)” renders “(reine oderempirische)”, run together in the source."]],"reflm:4678":["S. I:\nThat in the soul there lies a *principium* of disposition just as well as of affection. That the appearances can have no other order and can belong to the unity of the power of representation in no other way than in that they are in accordance with the common *principio* of disposition. For all appearance with its thoroughgoing determination must after all have unity in the mind, consequently be subject to such conditions whereby the unity of representations is possible. Only that which is required for the unity of representations belongs to the objective conditions. The unity of apprehension is necessarily combined with the unity of intuition space and time, for without this the latter would give no real representation.\nThe principles of exposition must on the one hand be determined by the laws of apprehension, on the other hand by the unity of the faculty of understanding. They are the standard of observation and not borrowed from perceptions, but from the ground of them in the whole. (g originally and abstracted from them.)\nPure thinking (*a priori*), however, in relation to experiences, i.e. to objects of the senses, contains principles which contain the origin of all experiences, i.e. of that which is thoroughly determined for experiences.\nWe must expound concepts if we cannot construct them. Appearances we cannot construct, although intuitions. But we must have rules of their exposition. These rules are actually rules of the appearance itself, but insofar as the inner of it is to be discovered in the resolution of it. The |\nrules therefore of the resolution of appearances are properly the conditions of apprehension, insofar as it passes over from one to the other and conjugates them.\n[The perceptions stand under rules of the intuitions the conjugated under rules of the]\nThe principle: everything that is thought stands under a rule, for only by means of the rule is it an object of thinking.\n––––––––––\nThe synthesis (g *principium* of) contains rules of thinking *a priori*, but insofar as it is determined to objects. Thus therein is 1. pure thinking (g a) and the rule of it, 2. the condition of the object, i.e. under which something is given to be thought as object (g x) (or is brought), 3. the determination of the thought from this relation (g b)/.\nThe *principium* of analysis: a rule of thinking in general. The principles of thinking, insofar as it is restricted by the condition of the subject or determined to the subject, are not principles, but restrictions. (1. On the possibility of empirical synthesis in general.) Cognition is determined *a priori* to object if it 1.\nconstruction\ngoes to the condition whereby object is given, and the cognition represents it only through concepts of appearance. 2. if it goes to [conditions of apprehension in general whereby] appearance, insofar as it contains the conditions for making oneself a concept of it, |\nS. II: |\n3. if it goes to apprehension in general, insofar as it contains the condition of the unity of perception as well as of intellection, i.e. of the agreement of the appearances among one another and with the unity of the mind, consequently of exposition.\nThe [first go] second goes to the appearances insofar as they can be brought under titles of thinking, e.g. what always is, is substance; the 1st to appearances among one another *a priori*, e.g. A triangle has three angles; the 3rd to that which constitutes the thoroughgoing determination of the appearance.\nBesides these there are subjective *principia* of thinking and objective ones of thinking or determining according to rules *a priori*.\nOf the synthetic propositions: all objects of the senses are in space and time.\nAll objects of experience are under a rule of sensibility.\nSensation\nIntuition\nAppearance\nConcept\nThe determinability of the object according to the\nsensation: *perceptio positiva*,\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\nintuition: construction,\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\nappearance: disposition,\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\nconcept: comprehension.\nIn an analytic proposition the x drops out entirely, because a taken *substantive* already stands with b in a determinate identical relation.\nBut in synthetic propositions the x is that in which a and through the condition of a b is determined.\nIn the (g Through the) construction x of the concept a (triangle) is at the same time in the triangle the equality of 3 angles etc. determined. Through the specification x of the concept a is at the same time in this a the relation b determined.\nIf I determine arising *specifice* in time, i.e. a reality in the series of time, then time is indeed the condition in which, but the rule the condition through which.\nIf x is the sensible condition under which a is specifically determined, then b is the universal function whereby it is determined therein.",["‘principles’ renders both principien (Latinate) and Grundsätze; in ‘are not principles, but restrictions’ the second word is Grundsätze.","‘power of representation’: Vorstellungskraft; ‘resolution’: Auflösung; ‘conjugates’: coniugirt.","‘although intuitions’: obzwar Anschauungen, elliptical as in the German."]],"reflm:4679":["S. I:\nWe are conscious of ourselves and of our own actions, and of the appearances insofar as we become conscious of the apprehension of them, either whereby we coordinate them with one another or apprehend one sensation through the other.\nWe would thus not become conscious of the appearances at all if we did not breaks off.\nAn object of the senses is only that which acts on my senses, hence acts and thus is substance. Hence the category of substance is principial. Every beginning of a state of representation is always a transition from a preceding one, for otherwise we would not perceive that the former had begun. hence, since the same subject always holds of one object as well as of the other and the boundary is also common to both, the following belongs to the preceding as to that which determines it. In the unity of the mind a whole is possible only through the mind's reciprocally determining from one partial representation the other and all together being comprehended in one action that holds of all.\n––––––––––\nx:a = m:n\nIf a indicates the condition whereby x is given, then the relation of a:b follows according to the principle of contradiction and holds universally without restriction of the ‘at the same time’. e.g. in every reality (it is given only through the action of the subject) there is a relation of substance to *accidens*. x signifies here the subject. a the apprehension of the object. x:a is thus the relation of the original action of the breaks off.\n––––––––––\nMere apprehension already explains that behind the appearance there must be a substance, cause of the composition; but observation and judgment must show which the substance etc. is. Where there is an action, there is substance, e.g. with light warmth; but whether light is substance does not flow from apprehension, but from the exposition of the appearance. That something is represented as having happened is enough to regard it as effect; for in fact the apprehension of it is an effect that occurs in the mind etc.\nThe intellectual functions thus make the beginning in apprehension, but specification gives us the rule of the application of this concept; hence determinate rules of synthesis can be given only through experience, but the universal norm of it *a priori*.\nS. II:\nempirical intuition is appearance.\nAppearance of which one is conscious is perception.\nEvery perception must be brought under a title of the understanding, because otherwise it gives no concept at all and nothing is thought in it. By means of these concepts we make use of the appearances, or rather the concepts indicate the way in which we make use of the appearances as the matter for thinking. 1. of intuition in general for magnitude, 2. of sensation, in order to determine the real relation in the appearance. We say: the stone weighs, the wood falls (g the body moves), i.e. it acts, hence it is substance. the field is prepared, the meadow dried out, the glass broken: these are effects that refer to a cause. The wall is firm, the wax soft, the gold dense: these are connections in the composite. Without such concepts the appearances would altogether be separated and not belong to one another. If they have equal relations in space or time toward one another, these are nevertheless not determined from the objects of the appearances, but only placed next to one another.\nExperience is an understood perception. We understand it, however, when we represent it to ourselves under titles of the understanding. Experience is a specification of the concepts of the understanding through given appearances. Appearances are the matter or the substrate.\nExperiences are thus possible only through its being presupposed that all appearances belong under titles of the understanding, i.e. in all mere intuition there is magnitude, in all appearance substance and *accidens*. In the change of them cause and effect, in the whole of them interaction. Thus these propositions hold of all objects of experience. These very same propositions hold also of the mind with respect to the generation of its own representations and are moments of the genesis. Under the titles, however, of apperception all appearances must be brought, so that they as well according to intuition constructed breaks off.\nThe conditions, however, of subsumption under these concepts are taken from the sensible relation, which stands in analogy with the action of the understanding and belongs to inner sense, of which apperception the breaks off.\nWhence is that which acts regarded as if it were constant and as if only the actions, effects and compositions varied.",["‘acts on’: wirkt; ‘acts’, ‘action’: handelt, Handlung.","‘the at the same time’: des zugleich, the word mentioned rather than used.","‘occurs’: eräugnet (sich ereignen); ‘change’: Wechsel; ‘connections’: Verknüpfungen."]],"reflm:4680":["Everything that happens is, for the sake of the determination of its concept among the appearances, i.e. with a view to the possibility of experience, represented as contained under a rule, of which the relation is expressed through a concept of the understanding. In the appearance x, therefore, in which a is a concept, there must be contained, besides that which is thought through a, conditions of its specification, which make necessary a rule whose function is expressed through b. a cannot be specifically determined in time, when it happens, otherwise than by means of a rule. Thus no experience of a can take place without rule. Thus the principle of sufficient ground is a *principium* of the rule of experience, namely of instituting such.\nThe proposition that everything that in time follows upon something follows upon something else in accordance with a rule, or that with respect to its sequence a rule takes place, does not lie in the specification of the concept a of happening or of contingency, for there only the appearance is meant. (But happening is already an existence according to a rule of time.)\nThe ordering of the appearances according to relation of space and time requires a rule, just as appearance itself a form.",["‘principle of sufficient ground’: Satz des zureichenden Grundes; ‘sequence’: Folge.","‘follows upon something’: worauf folgt, an awkward relative construction kept as it stands.","‘namely of instituting such’: nemlich solche anzustellen; solche may refer to the rule or to experience."]],"reflm:4681":["S. I:\nBefore an occurrence something must always precede (condition of perception).\nBefore an occurrence all sorts of things can precede, but there is one among these upon which it always follows.\nA reality always hangs (g on a point of time and that which determines it) on something accompanying, [which] through which the point of time is determined for it (condition of perception).\nThere is all sorts of accompanying, but among this there is something which is always there.\n[An aggregate is many in reciprocal relation but among this must]\n(g In [the] regard to that which is simultaneous there is always taking-together (condition of perception).)\nAll sorts of things can be taken together (g but with); but [there where the many determines itself reciprocally this combination is objectively a] where something is to be considered as objectively combined together, there is a reciprocal determination of the manifold among one another.\nIf there were not something always, thus something [persist] permanent, *stabile*, then there would be no fixed point or determination of the point of time, thus no perception, i.e. determination of something in time.\nIf something did not constantly (g before an occurrence) precede, then among the many that precedes there would be nothing [which] to which that which happens belongs in a series, it would have no determinate place in the series.\nThrough the rules of perception the [things] objects of the senses are determinable in time, in intuition they are merely given as appearances. According to those rules an entirely different series is found than is the one in which the object was given.\nS. II:\n(g Nothing synthetic can be objectively valid except that which is the condition, as that through which it is given as object or through which something that has been given is thought as object. Object is only thought insofar as it stands under a rule of appearance, and the receptivity of the rule [makes that the ap] is what makes the ap|pearance\nobjective; thus it is not the appearances that stand under a rule, but the objects which lie at their ground. According to this rule they are exposited.)\nWithout [any] such rules of perception no experiences could be made, because these are the titles of appearances, just as the [sensible concepts of the fo] sensible concepts are titles of intuitions.\nRule of the presumptions as estimation of the appearances for judgments to be provisionally determined.\n(g One can indeed see much, but understand nothing of what appears, except when it is brought under concepts of the understanding and by means of them into relation to a rule; this is the acceptance through the understanding.)\n(g The *synthesis* contains the relation of the appearances not in perception, but in the concept. That all relation in [concepts must] perception nevertheless still presupposes a relation in concepts indicates that the mind contains within itself the universal [source] and sufficient source of the *synthesis* and that all appearances are exponible in it.)\n*principia* of perception.\n[Rules] Principles of observation or of the exposition of appearances in general.\nThey are presumptions of experience.\nAnalogies of the understanding.\nAxioms of intuitions, analogies of the understanding, petitions of reason.\nWe perceive something only through being conscious of our apprehension, consequently of existence in our inner sense, [conseq] |\nhence as belonging to one of the three relations in the mind. All observation requires a rule.\nThe intellectual element of perception pertains to the power of inner sense. [The principles of obs] The analogies of observation pertain to thoroughgoing perception or perception determined throughout.\nAll combinations are made by the mind, and the mind combines nothing objectively except what is necessarily determined from its *correlato*; otherwise the representations are indeed put together, but not connected, in perception indeed, but not in the concept.\n––––––––––\nOnly that which is capable of constant principles in the mind do we call object. Thus estimation must precede the objective judgments. For everything else that does not accept such principles is nothing for us and can also not be perceived. Because perception requires a conjugation according to a universal [either] ground.",["'object' renders both obiect/Obiekt and Gegenstand; Begebenheit = occurrence; Zusammennehmung = taking-together.","'combination', 'combines' render Verbindung, verbinden; 'connected' renders verknüpft; Beurtheilung = estimation.","praesumtionen = presumptions; petitionen = petitions; the sentence 'Rule of the presumptions ...' is as unsettled as the German."]],"reflm:4682":["S. I:\nThe concept: what happens, is a determination of sensibility, but through the understanding, in that something is posited in the temporal sequence. Now this cannot happen otherwise than in relation to something preceding. Accordingly the rule that what happens is determined through something preceding says nothing other than (g that all this is determinable in the order of time) that there the determination [of the re of so] of a place of existence in time must happen through the understanding, hence according to a rule.\nReality is that through which something is an object of perception. »In every reality there is a relation of the accident to the substance«, means |\nas much as: The determination of an existence in time in general can happen only through something which is in all time.\nThe analogies of appearance mean as much as: if I did not determine every relation of it through a universal condition of relation in time, then I would assign to no appearance its place.\nThus the concepts substance, ground and whole serve only to assign to every [appearance] reality in appearance its place, in that |\nS. II: |\neach represents a *function* or [potency of the] dimension of time, in which the object that is perceived is to be determined and from the appearance experience is to come to be.",["Zeitfolge = temporal sequence; Obiekt and Gegenstand are both 'object'.","'that all this' reads the source's das as daß."]],"reflm:4683":["S. I:\n1.\nA synthetic proposition that holds of all things in general is false, and in general the one whose subject is a pure concept of the understanding. Unless it is not to hold objectively (g absolutely), but only under the subjective restriction of the use of reason.\nOnly the conditions of sensibility make the *synthesin* possible. 1. of pure, 2. of empirical intuition (of outer and inner sense).\nFurther of the empirical or rational use of my understanding. For only in the sensible condition of a lies something in which someone can cognize, besides the concept of a, still b.\nS. II:\n2.\nAll synthetic propositions [of the] have a homogeneity, although it seems that one concept is intellectual, the other empirical. In the exposition they are [empirical] homogeneous. One need only take instead of the concept its specification [and].\n*Concipere* means to make oneself a concept of something *a priori*. The principles of conception [pertain to subj] are either of thinking in general or of absolute positing or of taking-together *a priori*. Of the first the sensible condition is the[reception] entire sensibility, of the second the entire thinking in regard to a *dati* in general, of the third the whole in itself or totality.\n––––––––––\nThe understanding can determine nothing in sensibility except through a universal action. e.g. Coming to be through a universal condition of sequence. Existence through a subject of all existence. Being-together through a universal unity.",["'principles' renders principien here; elsewhere it renders Grundsatz.","[reception] renders the deleted Empfang; 'sequence' renders Folge."]],"reflm:4684":["S. I:\nHow can one know what, in a thing in general which is not given to the senses at all, is contained still further besides that which one actually thinks through its concept a. But because one time in which something happens is not to be distinguished from another, the sequence [must] can only be determined through a rule of time, and thus we can represent to ourselves in the sensible condition still more than was thought in a, namely in this time as a construction (of the triangle) every time where, if the very same member is, the very same *correlatum* is. We thus represent the object to ourselves through an *analogon* of construction, namely that it can be constructed for inner sense, namely that, just as something follows upon something else, always when something happens, it is something upon which something else follows, or that this representation is one of the universal actions of the determination of appearances, which therefore give a rule |\njust as a triangle is constructed only according to a rule and serves all as rule.\n––––––––––\nIn analytic judgments the predicate properly pertains to the concept a, in synthetic ones to [the condition of the] the object of the concept, because the predicate is not contained in the concept. But the object which corresponds to a concept has certain conditions of the execution of this concept, i.e. of the position *in concreto* (for every concept is a universal action which presupposes a *substratum* in which the [object] representation of the object can be posited). Now the condition of all concepts is [proper] sensible; thus, if the concept is also sensible, but universal, then it must be considered in its *concreto*, e.g. triangle in its construction. If the concept signifies not pure intuition, but empirical, i.e. experience, then the x contains the condition of relative position (a) in space and time. i.e. the condition of determining something in it universally.\nOtherwise appearances are determined through time, but in the *synthesi* time through an appearance, e.g. of that which exists or happens or is together. These are The most universal of appearances, of which [the real the] reality is the matter.\nOf the intellectuation of appearance, e.g.: Something exists, something happens. This is indeed already an *intellectuale*, placed in the form of time. The something is reality (sensation), the happening is existence as sequence. Now through what does the appearance become intellectual?\n––––––––––\nIn a synthetic judgment 2 pure concepts of reason can never stand in relation with one another, but rather a pure concept of the understanding with a concept under sensible condition, be it of appearance or of representation *a priori*. Cause of this.\nThe sum total of the laws of free actions which are naturally determined through the common choice is right. By choice I understand the will determining with force.\n––––––––––\nWhence do the analogies of the understanding have no evidence. They are nevertheless constitutive, only not directly objective.\nS. II:\nRational psychology as to the general predicates of the human soul:\n1. As to the subject. [Sim] Substance. Single in a human being. Simple. Immaterial.\n2. As to the powers. The representative power not single. Spontaneity either practically or transcendentally absolute (Freedom), either original or derivative.\n(g Choice is either brute or free, and freedom either original or derivative; freedom is opposed either to brute necessity or to fatality, the former in the practical sense, the latter in the transcendental sense; in the transcendental sense it is accordingly either original or derivative.)\n3. As to the state either of human life: Commerce*) of body and soul. or of birth**) or of death.\n*(g either ideal or real)\n**(g either Epigenesis or Preformation)\nThe soul after death [is] either [contingently] lives by decree or from its own nature, the latter life is immortality.\nThe argument for immortality is either natural or moral. the former either from rational principles (metaphysical) or empirical ones (physio|logical).\nThe metaphysical argument is concluded from the principle of life joined with inert matter devoid of life, that the principle of life through separation from matter is not deprived of life, but is freed from the impediments of life. And therefore after death animal life ceases and spiritual [or free] begins, while the soul is freed.\nThe physiological argument is either strict or analogical (g this the best of all, from human nature). The former can be given neither for nor against, since we have no experiences of the soul separated from commerce with the body, because we do not think except in this commerce. Nor can one argue against from the dependence of the soul on the body in this life, because we do not see in what the body obstructs the soul, [sim] since at the same time it is an impediment to it, as, if anyone were tied to a cart, he cannot move himself unless he moves it. If the wheels are well greased, the motion still proceeds freely, but they are not the causes of this motion, although, if they are braked by some cause internal to the cart, they render the man unmoved.\nThe moral argument is practically sufficient although not apodictic. It is teleological, drawn from the wisdom, goodness and justice of God, in one word from the laws of providence, and is most suited to common sense.\n(g animal life — spiritual)\nThe state after death is either of regeneration or of liberation, the former either to an earthly life (*metempsychosis*) or a heavenly one (*corpusc.*).\nLife after death either [of the soul] dreaming or waking.\nthe former *Hypnopsychita* (g revival or) with Personality or with the Lethean cup.",["Wilkühr and arbitrium = choice; Recht = right; evidentz = evidence; 'Whence' renders Woher, perhaps for Warum.","commercium = commerce; officiat (obstructs) is rendered as it stands, though the sense seems to call for 'serves'.","[or free] renders [s. libera], s. = seu."]],"reflm:4685":["Inner side I:\nThe real differences of the logical *actus* are not with ....... │\nThe absolute of the actions of the understanding pertains to the object, │ the relative to other representations, e.g. to the logical relation. │\nThe real differences of concepts are essential, the logical │ arbitrary, e.g. substance is no *accidens*, but subject and predicate │ are reciprocally etc. │\nInner side II:\nThe *qvidditas* is reality or existence through │ some predicate or other. And [its] the difference among the same | from others which have the same predicate is quality. │\nGuilielmi Tyrii historia belli sacri | cum continuatione Heroldi. Basel. 1549.",["'Inner side' renders Innenseite, the inside of the binding.","The book title (William of Tyre, history of the holy war, with Herold's continuation) is left in Latin as written."]],"reflm:4686":["S. I:\nAll appearances stand in community, i.e. there is nothing in the void.\n––––––––––\nIn order to determine something we must have something determining, hence the first or complete determination *a priori* is impossible.\nThe dispute between the conditioned and the unconditioned.\nS. II:\nDifference of evidence.\ntopical. Place in the mind, place among one another.\nThe mind can become conscious of itself only through the appearances which correspond to its dynamical functions, and of the appearances only through [that which] its dynamical functions.\nAppearances, if they were seen through in their continuous interconnection, can only to the dynamical functions breaks off, the rest is cut away.",["“mind”: Gemüth; “place”: Stelle; “seen through”: durchschauet.","“breaks off, the rest is cut away” (bricht ab, das Übrige ist weggeschnitten) is the AA's note on the state of the page."]],"reflm:4687":["Objects are given either in appearance or in the idea, and thought either in intuition or in the concept (*conceptus*). In the idea the object is given which is possible only through it. This is perfection. This cannot be borrowed from the things, but the things are perfect insofar as they agree with the idea. Ideas are *repraesentationes archetypae*, sensible concepts *ectypae*, concepts of the understanding are *reflectentes*.\nEither the *conditiones* through which the objects are given are those, that they are thought: [pr] *cognitio prototypa*; or they are only thought through this, that they are given: *ectypae*.",["“things”: Sachen; “agree with”: zusammen stimmen; “concepts of the understanding”: Verstandesbegriffe.","“which is possible only through it” (der dadurch nur Möglich ist): dadurch may refer to the idea or to the being given."]],"reflm:4688":["The absolute is the relation to thoroughgoing determination. Agreement is absolute possibility; position in every respect: actuality; actuality through possibility *in sensu absoluto*: absolute necessity.",["“thoroughgoing”: durchgängigen; “in every respect”: in aller Absicht."]],"reflm:4689":["From a particular *major propositio* one infers only to a possible conclusion, from a singular one to the actual conclusion, which is contained in that one, from a universal one to the necessity of the conclusion.\n(*Qvidditas*) *qvaeitas*, *qvalitas*, *qvantitas*.",["“that one” (jener) may refer to the particular major premise or to the singular one."]],"reflm:4690":["The inner necessity of a being is impossible to think. For in itself, from what belongs to a thing internally, the canceling of it with all predicates is always possible.\nBut the [absolu] relative necessity can well be thought, and necessity in every *respectu* as condition of the possibility of all things is absolute necessity.\nIt is the necessity of a universal *substrati* of the manifoldness of the concepts of things.",["“relative”: respective; “canceling”: Aufhebung; “manifoldness”: Manigfaltigkeit.","The deleted [absolu] is the start of absolute."]],"reflm:4691":["Beside, in and \nFirst, the alteration of the thing is the persistence of the thing. The thing is altered in that its *affectionen* change.",["“thing” renders Ding the first time, Sache the second and third.","“its” (seine) agrees grammatically with Ding, not with Sache."]],"reflm:4692":["Dependence proves inner contingency.",[]],"reflm:4693":["Alteration proves the contingency of the state;\nArising and passing away proves the contingency of the thing.\nBoth only inner contingency, namely in itself; not the conditioned, (g that) under the circumstances and [in] the conditions the opposite of what happens would also be possible.\nBecause we can never experience the contradictory opposite, absolute contingency, i.e. in every *respectu*, also cannot be inferred from experience. *omnimoda contingentia* (g *possibilitas*). Freedom is that whose sequels are absolutely contingent.\nThe possibility of the *contrarie oppositi* does not prove contingency at another time. Rest of a moved body. Not-being and the opposite are not the same.\nThe *contradictorie oppositum*, namely at the same time, (g as possible) is the haphazard.\nThe *oppositum* at another time is *contrarie oppositum*.",["“alteration”: Veränderung; “arising”: Entstehen; “the haphazard”: das Ungefehr."]],"reflm:4694":["The distributive *omnitudo* is universality, the collective: totality, absolute totality: *Vniversitas*.",["*Vniversitas* (the universe as a whole) is left in Latin beside universalitas (“universality”)."]],"reflm:4695":["A composite in which there is completeness (*omnitudo partium*) is a whole, and this completeness is called totality. Taking-together.",["“completeness”: completudo; “Taking-together”: Simul sumtio, literally a taking together at once."]],"reflm:4696":["The bounds of [aggregation] composition: infinitely large, or no bounds (g immeasurable), of decomposition: infinitely small. Extensive, protensive, intensive magnitude.",["“bounds”: Grenzen; “immeasurable”: Unermeßlich."]],"reflm:4697":["A magnitude in which every unity is itself a magnitude is continuous; consequently one which contains no absolute unities in itself, hence constitutes in itself no number [in itself], but multitude in general. Point, instant.\nOf the *maximo*. Of the greatest number. Of the infinite multitude. Of bounds and limitations. Of finite measure.\nThe infinite of increase is possible. But the infinite of taking-together not. *infinitudo progressiva*, *collectiva*.",["“unity”: Einheit, also “unit”; “multitude”: Menge.","“bounds and limitations”: Gräntzen und schranken; “taking-together”: Zusammennehmung."]],"reflm:4698":["At the top of the page:\nTo cognize and to determine the *Quantitas phaenomenon* is what |\nmathematics teaches, the *Qvanta noumena* only philosophy. Totality, unlimitedness. — Reality, degree etc etc.",["“At the top of the page” (Zuoberst auf der Seite) is the AA's note of where the Reflexion stands.","“Totality”: Allheit; “unlimitedness”: Uneingeschranktheit."]],"reflm:4699":["Divisibility is no *accidens*; an *accidens* is always positive, because it is an existence.\nBodies are *phaenomena substantiata*, not because they are composite, but because they are outer appearances, where the first subject is lacking.\nThinking beings I can represent to myself only as substances, because I lend them my I.",[]],"reflm:4700":["At the top of the page, \nIn a judgment the singular proposition expresses unity, the particular plurality and the universal the *omnitudinem*. A universal proposition expresses the combination of the many that stands under the universal concept of a subject through the common predicate, or rather the combination in the *sphaera* of a concept; if the predicate is a *reciprocum* of the subject, then the subject [is] fills the *sphaeram* and designates a *totum*. The conditioned proposition expresses the relation of the ground to the sequel, which (g a) ground is either of subordinated sequels in a *sorites* or of coordinated ones |\nin the *copulativo*. Third, that the predicate is in the subject as an *accidens* expresses the combination of the accidents in a substance.\nAll combination of the many is unity. Hence many things are combined in one substance or through one ground or into one whole.\nThat of two things one can be inferred to the other as its [sequel] predicate or as its sequel, or from two things to the unity of a composite, experience must teach.\nWith regard to quantity just the same relations hold as with regard to quality. Position, *defectus* and privation have the signs +a, 0, and –a.",["“plurality”: Vielheit; “combination”: Verbindung; “sequel(s)”: Folge(n).","“which (g a) ground is”: welcher (g ein) Grund ist; with the addition it reads “which is a ground either of ...”."]],"reflm:4701":["The magnitude of the substance is always cognized and measured through the persistence in its own state under all grounds of alteration. If an air has much elasticity, then the quantity of the substance is to be measured not from this elasticity but from the *inertia* with which it resists the expansion of the same or also every force which [it] moves its mass.",["“of the same” (derselben) may refer to the elasticity or to the air.","“persistence”: beharrlichkeit."]],"reflm:4702":["The proposition: no substance passes away, signifies as much as: all alterations always refer to a subject, hence no alteration or change of subjects can take place, because otherwise they would be only alterations. Hence these are a thread which is always inseparably fastened to certain points. The subjects are also called the standing, with regard to which one can cognize the rest as variable.",["“alteration”: Veranderung; “change”: Wechsel.","“these” (diese) may refer to the alterations or to the subjects."]],"reflm:4703":["Opposite and \nNo substance can pass away of itself. For to that an action is required whereby it is canceled. But it cannot cancel itself. But no substance can annihilate another either (if this does not exist through that one). Because one can only flow into the state of the other and indeed only into that which is common to their states.",["“Opposite and” (Gegenüber und) is the AA's note of where the Reflexion stands.","“flow into”: einfließen; “canceled”: aufgehoben."]],"reflm:4704":["The *principium generale commercii* is: all influence in the world is partly the effect of the agent in the patient, partly counteraction of the latter. Of this counteraction the inner state and determination of the substance is the action whereby the *accidens* of the influence is actuated (apprehension). There is thus [a] unity of the acting forces of different substances in every *commercio*.",["“agent ... patient”: Handlenden ... Leidenden; “counteraction”: gegenwirkung; “actuated”: actuirt.","The genitive Dieser gegenwirkung may go with innerer Zustand (“the inner state of this counteraction”) or stand on its own; the construction is left as it is."]],"reflm:4705":["a composite either substantial or accidental.\na composite of substances and accidents is impossible.",[]],"reflm:4706":["Unity is either of composition or of decomposition; the former is numerical unity, the latter the simple; both unity of mathematical synthesis.",["“numerical unity”: numeraleinheit."]],"reflm:4707":["The question is whether a given magnitude is infinite. The condition is that it be given. Now through the at-the-same-time the being-given is indicated. On the other hand it is not given in apprehension or in determination.",["“the at-the-same-time the being-given”: das zugleich das gegeben seyn."]],"reflm:4708":["(s *Lex isonomiae*.)\nA first beginning is impossible. For beginning can be thought only according to laws of sensibility, consequently only in a time occupied by objects of sensibility. Consequently a first beginning, before which no phenomenon preceded, cannot be thought. Thus all productions are only alterations. But a first cause can well be thought, because this is merely something intellectual. The former can thus not serve for the explication of the appearances: the cause of the appearances must be in *commercio* with the world. from which it follows that nothing can arise without something being canceled against it, so that the sum of reality remains. For otherwise time itself (absolute time) would stand in relation to the alterations and the cause of the alteration be outside time. The *universum* itself cannot move, neither in empty space, still less in an entirely full space. For otherwise in the first case [space] there would be an appearance of which the *correlatum* is no appearance, in the second a motion in no space at all. Hence every cause of motion is in community with the world, and none is possible except that just as much is produced on the opposite side. A spirit that moved a matter absolutely would [determine] effect this motion of the *universi*. That one who |\ncan move only insofar as he remains at rest in the sum is in union with matter.",["“productions” / “produced”: Erzeugungen / erzeugt; “canceled”: aufgehoben; “absolutely”: schlechthin.","“The former” (Jener, masculine) agrees with Anfang (“beginning”).","“That one who ... he” (Derienige, der ...er) agrees grammatically with Geist (“spirit”)."]],"reflm:4709":["Beside and \nIn appearance two things can be distinguished through the mere *relationes*, but not universally through the understanding. (s *diversitas phaenomenon*.)",["“Beside and” (Neben und) is the AA's note of where the Reflexion stands."]],"reflm:4710":["If completely similar things from different places were exchanged, would then everything be just as before.\nAnalytic identity; synthetic, of space and of time.",["The word order (würde denn alles ...) is that of a question, but the text has no question mark."]],"reflm:4711":["The question is whether one could say: a thing is in two places at the same time, if [that] which is in one place is *interne* (g completely) the same as that in the other.\nSpace also makes possible the real outer and, according to the analogy, the inner conflict.",["“the same”: einerley; “conflict”: Wiederstreit."]],"reflm:4712":["The question is whether 2 things can be different merely *numero* or whether they must be thoroughly specifically *plura* in order to be more than one thing.",[]],"reflm:4713":["93'. E II 860. 861. 894.\nEverything that is, is necessary, either absolutely or in a conditioned way*, thus *ut causatum alterius*.\n*(s We thus also cognize in all relations of the manifold in the composite a necessity which must always be derived. Nothing can be originally contingent (g |\nM 93': |\nrather the reverse: every *causatum* is contingent in itself). |\nM 93: |\nThe only question is: what is contingent? and on what does the *principium necessitatis* rest? On this, because otherwise there would be no determining grounds *a priori*.)\nThe *principium rationati* says only: everything that happens is necessary under a condition.\nEverything must be determined objectively *a priori*.\nEverything alterable has a beginning, thus arises and is contingent and is *causatum alterius*.",["The lone asterisks after “conditioned way” and before “(s” are the AA's footnote marks, not italics.","*ut causatum alterius*: as the caused of another; “absolutely”: schlechthin."]],"reflm:4714":["The *completudo logica* is *universalitas*, the *realis* is *universitas*; likewise the *communitas* and *communio*.",[]],"reflm:4715":["We have three [prae] categories and their predicaments: first, that of position (being and not being); 2. that of the *respectus*. 3. That of the *completu|dinis*.\nThe first is whether something is or not; the second, what is there relative to another or not; the third, how much of one thing is together etc. *Qvaeitas*, *Qvalitas*, *qvantitas propositionis*.",["The deleted [prae] is presumably the start of praedicamente."]],"reflm:4716":["The proof of Leibniz, who takes the world *intellectualiter*, from the power of representation of the monads, is not bad, except that it nevertheless proves more than it concludes; it leads to an idealism.",["“it ... it” (er ... er) may refer to the proof or to Leibniz.","“power of representation”: Vorstellungskraft."]],"reflm:4717":["That in the series and the aggregate of sensibility there is no beginning and no totality, i.e. determinability with regard to the *omnitudinis*, comes from this, because the All here must be sufficient for the determination according to conditions of sensibility, consequently for the addition or the progression without bounds. This is however no judgment about the world in itself, in whose concept the All precedes, and from which every part alone is determinable. In this series there are not true things and true causes, but only appearances; and [an] the appearance in general must be without end, because in it alone the undetermined action of sensible cognition can go on without end.",["“addition”: Hinzuthuung; “progression”: fortgang; “bounds”: Grenzen.","“every part alone is determinable” (ieder Theil allein bestimbar ist): allein may qualify “part” or “determinable”."]],"reflm:4718":["The outer senses give me nothing but impressions; only through |\ninner intuition (which however goes only to myself) can I cognize the object that lies at the ground of the impressions. The question now is: is what we call body a particular kind of being, distinct from the thinking ones, or is it only a particular appearance of their reciprocal presence. I have no ground for the latter; but I also cannot be refuted. This would be the mystical world.\n(s The physical or mystical idealist; the former denies objects of appearance altogether; the latter assumes such, but gives them other positive fanatical determinations.)",["“kind of being”: Art wesen; “lies at the ground”: zum Grunde liegt.","“fanatical”: fanatische."]],"reflm:4719":["The transition from reality to negation is continuous, i.e. through infinite degrees of the smaller difference up to vanishing. Thus the decomposition of a body through a continuous motion also continuous, and from the place in the body (outermost monad) it cannot happen immediately to a place outside the body, but through all degrees of the intervening spaces between the two. Just so with every alteration.\nMonads can thus be no objects of an empirical synthesis of outer appearance.\nOf the whole of all (g possible) decomposition and composition no experience can be given.",["“continuous” renders both continuirlich and stetig.","“Thus the decomposition ... also continuous” has no verb in the German."]],"reflm:4720":["Opposite the section heading, as well as M § 406f.:\nSpace has in this something particular over the concept of time, that |\nfirstly the concept of time, consequently the whole of sensibility, can be thought in the determinations of it; secondly that a force which I posit somewhere in space, as the *principium* of appearances, does not remain merely a force in general, but through the relations of space toward everything external has determinate conditions and thus gives a determinate concept of the possibility of the object. Thirdly space is the ground of the possibility (g of the external, consequently) of the object, whereas time concerns only the state and in general pertains [all] to mere existence [ohne d] as the ground of the relations of things in their existence and the measure of their duration. Hence time is no ground of the possibility of things, especially not of substances. Hence positive *principia intellectualia* of physics, but not of [cosmology] psychology, are possible; everything in the latter is negative in relation to the pure doctrine of nature. 1. Lack of parts; 2. no such *commercium* as matter has; 3. no such origin and perishing as the body.",["desselben (of it) refers grammatically to space (der Raum).","[ohne d]: deleted, ohne (without) followed by a broken-off word; left in German.","Naturlehre: doctrine of nature; Untergang: perishing."]],"reflm:4721":["That a force is combined with motion is not established from motion, because in it no *accidens absolutum* is to be met with, but from the sensation which is to be met with in motion, both when we move and when motion strikes us.",["constirt: is established (Latin constat); verbunden = combined.","wenn wir bewegen: transitive verb without object in the source (when we move [something])."]],"reflm:4722":["Nature simply or taken *subiective* is that which corresponds to the necessary being.\nThe object of all empirical cognitions is nature.\nThe contingent all: nature.",["das zufallige all: the contingent All (universe), Kant's noun."]],"reflm:4723":["Appearances are representations insofar as we are affected. The representation of our (g free) self-activity is one in which we are not affected, consequently is not appearance, but [ursp frey] apperception. Now the principle of sufficient ground holds only as *principium* of the *exposition* of appearances, consequently not as exposition of the original intuitions.",["[ursp frey]: deleted, ursp(rünglich) (original) broken off, then frey (free); left in German.","Satz des Zureichenden Grundes: principle of sufficient ground (Satz = principle)."]],"reflm:4724":["We cannot prove freedom *a posteriori*, because the lack of perception of determining grounds yields no proof that none of the kind exist either. We also cannot cognize its possibility *a priori*, since the possibility of the original ground which is not determined by another cannot be comprehended at all. We can thus prove it not theoretically at all, but as a necessary practical *hypothesis*.",["begriffen = comprehended; determinirt = determined."]],"reflm:4725":["The practical concept of freedom is that which suffices to perform actions according to rules of reason, which thus gives to the imperatives of the latter their force; the speculative or rationalizing concept of freedom is that which suffices to explain [Handl] free actions according to reason. The latter is impossible, because it is the original *in derivativo*.",["dieser ihren imperativen: to its (reason's) imperatives; Gewalt rendered force (also authority).","vernünftelnd = rationalizing; [Handl]: deleted fragment of Handlungen (actions); left in German.","in derivativo: in what is derived."]],"reflm:4726":["Above and \nThat of which the ground (and its relation to the sequel) through the senses |\ncan not be given at all, i.e. incomprehensible. Now the ground of the higher desire is not sensible, thus it cannot [be] be discerned, because our reason has use only *a posteriori* upon what has been given to the senses.\nLikewise to the sense can the repeated repetitions of the one in the *actualiter* infinite (namely the past, as regards time, after [and the] one another, and the present at the same time), but indeed the *potentialiter* infinite. The former are given entire, but the generation of them is never entire.",["Über und: the locus note breaks off in the transcription.","The sentence beginning Likewise lacks a main verb (presumably not be given); left incomplete.","der Zeit nach [und dem] einander: the deletion splits nach einander (one after another); actualiter / potentialiter: actually / potentially."]],"reflm:4727":["Whether God is free, although he can do nothing evil?",[]],"reflm:4728":["Spirit is a pure intelligence. (pure is what is separated from everything heterogeneous.) Thus spirit is an intelligence separated from all community with bodies. If in *psychologia rationalis* I abstract from all *commercio* with bodies, then the concept of the soul becomes that of spirit, and *psychologia* becomes *pnevmatologia*. If I leave out the intelligence and take merely soul in community with body, then there remain *animae brutorum*.",["Geist = spirit; Gemeinschaft = community; animae brutorum: souls of brutes."]],"reflm:4729":["Negations in general mean: some realities not. But this posits the opposite, namely all with a limitation. Thus |\nthe concept of all reality is necessary to us as a *substratum* of reason; but we cannot on that account regard a highest reality as necessary in itself. Further, according to the *principio exclusi medii* every thing is considered in *relation* to everything possible, as in a division, consequently in a whole of reality.\nThe existence of a thing can never be proven from mere concepts, because existence is not one of the predicates and because from concepts nothing more than the *respective* affirming or denying, but not the absolute position of the object with its predicates, can be inferred. The concept which lies at the ground is a necessary presupposition and therefore seems to be a concept of the necessary being.",["principium exclusi medii: principle of excluded middle; respective: relatively.","Einschränkung rendered limitation; geschlossen = inferred; Wesen = being."]],"reflm:4730":["At the bottom of the page:\nIn the original being the possibilities of things lie not as parts, but as sequels. Because the original being could otherwise be composed of *limitata*. Boundary concept of magnitude: *infinitum*. Of dynamics: *libertas*. Of existence: necessary.",["Urwesen = original being; Folgen = sequels; limitata: limited things."]],"reflm:4731":["The cognition of God from the first mover grounds bricht ab.",["bricht ab (breaks off) is the Academy Edition's notice that the note ends here; kept as it stands.","gründet: grounds, without object; the sentence is incomplete."]],"reflm:4732":["Around M § 822:\nIn *theologia Naturali* we cognize the original being only in relation to the world, consequently not according to its absolute predicates (for we will surely not want to think the *ens extramundanum* through *praedicata mundana*), but according to predicates of relation with regard to the world; thus only the causality, consequently the *exponent* of the relation to the world. e.g. As a (a work of art) to b (the understanding artist): so c (the world) to x (that which I call understanding |\nin God). Whether I could infer so securely, and this concept not be sufficient for us, indeed even entirely correct?",["dem Verständigen Künstler: the artist possessed of understanding (verständig); Verstand = understanding.","ens extramundanum: extramundane being; praedicata mundana: worldly predicates."]],"reflm:4733":["The objections in regard to the existence of God and his properties [must] are all taken from the condition of sensibility, which one has taken for intellectual, and from the subjective conditions of comprehensibility, which one has taken for objective (existence has a magnitude, this we know from time; this magnitude is called duration; afterwards, however, we separate time from duration, and that is intellectual; from this arises eternity, in which concept there can be no contradiction, except only with sensible conditions). One must not be so zealous and anxious against this play of grounds and counter-grounds. Whoever is in a fortress will not have a battery fired at every swaggering Pandour.\nThe infinite and unique space, the [ground] condition of the possibility of all outer presence of things, as they appear, is indeed not a proof of the existence of a ground- and original being which comprises everything and in which everything is sustained, from which also all unity and relation derive, because it is as it were possible through its place in the all; but it is nevertheless a proof that the human mind can think no combination without a common ground and no determinations without in one which contains everything. Likewise with time, in which all existence lies. This serves to assume the assumption of such a being as subjectively necessary, consequently also as sufficient for *praxi*.",["wieder = wider (against); Gemüth = mind; Verbindung = combination; einige Raum: the single (unique) space.","weil es gleichsam ... möglich ist: es may refer to everything (alles) or to space; left open.","keine Bestimungen ohne in Einem: syntax as in the source (no determinations without [being] in one)."]],"reflm:4734":["If in a being we find the connection with just the same effects, then the inner constitution of the determinations |\nwhich contain the causality may be what it will. We cognize the cause after all only from the causality and the effect. Thus, if that which is in God relates to the misery of human beings just as mercy does, then we can call God merciful, wrathful, jealous etc: but only *per analogiam*, since we must not compare the absolute with ourselves.",["Verknüpfung = connection; Beschaffenheit = constitution."]],"reflm:4735":["God judges not according to kindness (indulgence), but justice; gives laws according to holiness and governs according to kindness. Thus in our faults we will have to [hope] trust not in the goodness of the judge, but of the governor, both to help our impotence and also, in accordance with this worthiness, to give us unmerited blessedness.\nHe cannot hope to come into heaven if he has much [prayers] begged pardon and repented, but if he is a better human being. Unless etc etc. The old testament was not so. Hence Christ prescribes actions and faith, not petitions and observances.\nThat the divine law in regard to us is a holy law we see from our conscience, which notwithstanding our frailty yet does not approve the least transgressions, but condemns them all.",["Gütigkeit = kindness; Güte = goodness; helligkeit read as Heiligkeit (holiness).","dieser Würdigkeit gemäß: the referent of dieser (this worthiness) is unclear in the source.","abgebeten: begged pardon; [gebete] (prayers) deleted."]],"reflm:4736":["The polytheism of subordination is genuine monotheism,\nthe polytheism of coordination manichaeism.",[]],"reflm:4737":["On the natural cognition of God according to the *analogia per viam reductionis et eminentiae*.\nThe absolute we do not cognize.",["analogia per viam reductionis et eminentiae: analogy by way of reduction and of eminence."]],"reflm:4738":["On. M § 890:\nGod is the author through a will: *contra emanationem, Spinozam*.\nGod is the author through free will: *contra fatalistas*.",["contra emanationem, Spinozam: against emanation, Spinoza; contra fatalistas: against the fatalists."]],"reflm:4739":["(g Freedom in God is subjective necessity, but not natural.)\nThe freedom of the divine will consists in the determination of all things according to his highest pleasure, thus according to the necessary rules of the highest pleasure, thus not [üb] in disposing over these rules. His will is holy, his end is kind, and his judgment is just. [Gott k] God is the lawgiver, not in that he gives the laws [freely] at discretion, but in that through his will he subjects creatures to his laws.\nHe cannot dispense from laws. Yet he is kind, but only [in regard] so that he gives *complementa* to *fragilitati humanae*, not indulgently.",["Wohlgefallen = pleasure (satisfaction); Zwek = end; gütig = kind.","[üb], [Gott k]: deleted fragments (über, God c[an]); left in German.","complementa fragilitati humanae: complements to human frailty."]],"reflm:4740":["God's decrees are wise, his will is holy, his end (g government) kind, his [judgment is] judgment is just.",["The deleted [Urtheil ist] and the replacing Gericht are both rendered judgment; Gericht is judgment in the judicial sense.","Rathschlüße: decrees (counsels)."]],"reflm:4741":["At the top of the page:\nPhysicotheology. Chief rule. One must appeal to God as an immediate cause in no single case, but only generally in |\nregard to the [first] last *substrati* in the world (substance), unless it is supposed to be a revelation. For our reason is not a faculty of whose use we can cut short the progression at will. It is a law to itself. It is against reason [oneself] to cut short further investigation and presumptuously [at the last point] to exempt oneself from all further effort, by presuming to judge what God has done immediately. For reason alone can determine what is fitting or not. There is an interval, undetermined for us, between an occurrence or arrangement of nature and God, where we must apply our powers to explain everything according to laws of nature.",["dessen Gebrauch wir nach Belieben den Fortgang abschneiden könen: the construction is irregular in the source; kept as near as English allows.","Fortgang = progression; Begebenheit = occurrence; Krafte = powers (forces).","[sich]: deleted reflexive pronoun."]],"reflm:4742":["At the bottom of the page:\nIn appearances no absolutely first is to be met with. But in the synthesis of the understanding, indeed. Thus there is indeed no first beginning, but indeed a first cause, part [etc], action etc etc. A first as *phaenomenon* would appear as a boundary from nothing. The antinomy of reason is thus nothing other than the difference of the principles of reason, insofar as the *data* are given sensibly, i.e. dependent on objects, or intellectually, i.e. from the mind itself, which indeed agree with regard to (g individual) possible experiences, but not to the whole of them. Hence, considered *a posteriori*, all actions of human beings are empirically determined, *a priori* undetermined and free.",["Vernunftprincipien = principles of reason; Gemüth = mind.","welche (which) may refer to the principles or to the data."]],"reflm:4743":["Cause and beginning are, the former intellectual, the latter sensitive. The beginning is only in the world, but not of the world. Why did God not create the world earlier? God is in no [abs] relation to absolutely empty time.",["[abs]: deleted fragment, presumably of absolut; left in German."]],"reflm:4744":["The world is a proof of divine omnipotence,\nOf divine wisdom,\nOmniscience,\nOf divine goodness,\nOf divine justice.",[]],"reflm:4745":["Providence, which makes each end consonant with the whole of all ends, is wisdom.",["Vorsehung = providence; Einstimig: consonant, in agreement."]],"reflm:4746":["[Es] *Creatio et annihilatio in cursu mundi* is contrary to the principles of the empirical use of reason; rather the divine influence is *conservatio*. Because in regard to him there is no temporal sequence. *Conservatio* is not *influxus*. Not *concursus* (g except in *actionibus liberis*). It is either *providentia* (g in creation) or *gubernatio* in the conservation of the world. The former, either *providentia* only general or also special; the latter upon the state of an individual in general or a singular case, and then it is called *directio extraordinaria*.",["Creatio et annihilatio in cursu mundi: creation and annihilation in the course of the world; [Es] (It) deleted.","Prior ... haec: the former ... the latter; haec may refer to gubernatio or to special providence.","actiones liberae: free actions; directio extraordinaria: extraordinary direction."]],"reflm:4747":["One can ask where the order of nature, not of the whole world, but of a particular kind of things, begins. e.g. the order of the arrangement of the earth, or the production of each animal. Finally one can ask: How does one represent to oneself in general the beginning of the order of nature?",["Art = kind; Einrichtung = arrangement."]],"reflm:4748":["*Concursus* is contradistinguished from *actioni solitariae*. Co-operation or sole operation. The (g forces of the) world are divided into nature and freedom; in regard to nature God alone is cause, in regard to freedom he concurs. The *nexus* of causes subordinated to one another is not *concursus*.",["actio solitaria: sole action; Mitwirkung oder Alleinwirkung: co-operation or sole operation (Wirkung otherwise effect).","Causarum sibi subordinatorum nexus non est concursus: the nexus of causes subordinated to one another is not concurrence."]],"reflm:4749":["How the *intima praesentia* brought Spinoza to regard substances not as effects, but as actions of the deity.",["intima praesentia: innermost presence."]],"reflm:4750":["Inner sense contains a feeling of the divine omnipresence; for merely by our being immediately conscious of the cause of us and of everything are we conscious of our connections with other things.\n(s We cannot become conscious of ourselves as in God; but, were we in him, then God would be conscious of us as his determinations and we not of our selves at all.\nOf the divine omnipresence we cannot (g through sense) become conscious.)",["Verknüpfungen = connections; Gefühl = feeling."]],"reflm:4751":["If God concurs in the creature's action, then the creature also concurs in the actions of God; for *concursus* is reciprocal, although not equal on both sides. It is a mutual share in a third as effect.",[]],"reflm:4752":["Predestination is that subordination of the things of the world under the divine decree whereby all occurrences are regarded as ends and all causes as means. Then there is no order of nature.",["Rathschlus = decree; Begebenheiten = occurrences; Zweke = ends."]],"reflm:4753":["Revelation as a dispensation of the extraordinary direction, according to which someone's insights improve religion; such writings are called imparted by God.\n(s To regard our own restriction of cognition as divine wisdom.)",["“dispensation” renders Fügung; “restriction” renders Einschrankung."]],"reflm:4754":["revelation is for each creature according to its kind of cognition nature.\nThe revelation of God through reason must precede every other; for it gives the first correct concept, according to which I can test every other.\nrevelations of his existence or of his will.\n(s The pure concept of both God can reveal to us merely inwardly through our reason and not outwardly either through works or words.)",[]],"reflm:4755":["Utility of not knowing that there is a God.",["“Utility” renders Nutzen."]],"reflm:4756":["S. I:\nA. Space.\nDialectic of [appearances] sensibility.\n1. Is space something actual (g real). *Substantia, Accidens, relatio*. (g *Idealitas spatii*:) Hobbes: it is the phantasm of an existing thing as of an external one. Descartes takes space for the abstract of the extension of matter. To these Leibniz accedes. Clarke, however, defends the reality of space. Newton: it is the sensorium of the divine omnipresence. Epicurus asserted the subsisting, Wolff the inhering reality of space.\n2. Is there a *vacuum mundanum et extramundanum*. It would be appearance without object that determined the intuition and its places. In the latter case motion of the world-whole. In the former outer intuition without objects. If *vacuum* signifies as much as: what is not filled by matter (the impenetrably extended), then a *vacuum* will be possible.\n3. All parts of space are in turn spaces. The point is no part, but boundary. Continuity.\n4. All given magnitudes of space are parts of a greater. *Infinitudo*.\n5. Unity, consequently pure intuition, and not concept of the understanding.\nB. Time.\nAbsolute space and time or adhering space and time.\n1. Time is nothing real. Clarke held it to be real as pure time, Leibniz to be an empirical concept of succession.\n2. Is there an empty time before the world and in the world. i.e. are two different states separated by time that is not filled by a continuous series of alterations. The moment of time can be filled, but so that no time-series is designated.\n3. All parts of time are in turn times. The moment. Continuity.\n4. All given times are parts of a greater time. Infinity.\n5. Unity of time. All-concept.\nDialectic of the understanding.\ntranscendental doctrine of magnitude.\n(g Continuity of space and of time and of all magnitudes.)\nSpace and time do not consist of simple parts (g their parts are themselves magnitudes), i.e. absolute unities: continuity.\nThe magnitude of a thing in space and time is continuous.\nThe magnitude of a ground is called degree, intensive, extensive, protensive.\nDetermination of magnitude through number and given unit (likewise its magnitude).\nInfinite is greater than all number. (g The totality or totality (the All) is not to be understood in series, not to be comprehended in the aggregate.)\nInfinite of continuation or of taking-together. (g infinitely small of composition or decomposition.) Where the former is the condition, the latter does not take place.\nInfinite space and elapsed infinite time are incomprehensible.\nIn the world the domain, the progression and the division go to infinity.\nWhence is mathematics demonstrative? because it is [rational cog] cognition in intuition *a priori*.\nWhich are the boundaries (g of [demo] mathematical cognition)? That which can be represented *a priori* in intuition, thus space and time and alteration in time.\nS. II:\nThe perfection of a thing (g *in sensu* [*substantivo*] *adiectivo*) is the totality of the manifold which is [to a] requisite to make up a thing. A perfection (*in sensu substantivo, transscendentali*) is reality.\nTranscendental doctrine of appearance.\nReality and negation.\n[Is there a vac]\n( g The sum total of appearances *In mundo non datur*\n*saltus*, — — — —\n*hiatus*, — — —\n*abyssus nihili*. )\n(g all parts of space and time are in turn times. From nothing everything passes into something only in time.)\n(g There is nothing simple in appearance, consequently no immediate transition from a determinate state (not its boundary) into another.)\n(g there is no empty time between two states. Alteration is only the continuation of the progression.)\n(g All appearance consists in turn of appearances, no sensation is simple. [All] Appearance does not, however, consist of nothing and appearance, thus not of the empty and the full.)\nOf the ideality and reality of appearance in general.\nAll spaces and times are parts of a greater.\nAll parts of space and time are themselves spaces.\n––––––––––\nIn the propositions of the infinite [and the infinite] of extension and division philosophy chicanes with mathematics. In those of the empty and full chican breaks off.\nSpace and time belong only to appearances and thus to the world and not outside the world.\nA *hiatus*, gap, is a lack of the interconnection of appearances, where their transition is missing. Appearances become possible through space; the emptiness of space is no gap and belongs to the determination of sensible intuition.\nThe proposition that all alteration is continuous cancels the ceasing of a substance.\nThe proposition that, if an alteration in the whole of appearance ceased, it would never begin again; likewise, that every part of the appearances is implicated with the others and through their alterations is altered along with them, consequently every thing is constantly altered. (g Beginning belongs to appearance, origin to the idea of the understanding.)\n––––––––––\nTranscendental doctrine of experience.\n(g Antithesis: there is not any [substance but all are appearances].)\n1. Something as substance, that is matter, does not arise and does not perish, from nothing comes nothing, i.e. matter is eternal (g *ex nihilo nihil (in mundo) fit*), although dependent.\n2. Every state of the world is a sequel, for with the continuity of alteration everything is in commencing and ceasing, and both have a cause.\nAntithesis. For there would be no first beginning.\n3. All appearances together make up one world and [nothing is isolated. 1st There is one world i.e. a whole of substance] belong to actual objects (against idealism). God as cause does not belong to the world. For only through the agreement of representations with objects do they agree among themselves and receive the unity which perceptions must have that are to be appearances.\nOn no. 2. What happens has a ground, i.e. is through something determined according to a rule. Unity of [simultaneity] succession. For |\nwithout this rule unity of experience would not be possible. From this it follows that in the world the sequence of appearances has no beginning at all. But whether the world itself has an origin, that belongs to intellectual concepts.",["The first “totality” renders Allheit, the second totalitaet; “alteration” renders Veränderung throughout.","In mundo non datur saltus, hiatus, abyssus nihili: in the world there is no leap, no gap, no abyss of nothingness.","“[rational cog]” and “[to a]” render the deleted, unfinished [Vernunfterk] and [zu e]; “chican” is unfinished in the source."]],"reflm:4757":["S. I:\nThe principles of the possibility of experiences (of the distributive unity) are at the same time principles of the possibility of the objects of ex|perience.\n(g appearance) (g of experiences.)\n1. (g Unity) Of intuition, 2. of the giving or existence of appearances.\nImmanent (g space and time are conditions of appearance) or transcendent (g they are not) principles.\nThe former of the [understanding's] empirical, the latter of the pure use of reason. Agreement of reason with itself in the whole.\nThe former needs (g has) no first *a priori*, but *a posteriori* and from there *progressus* or *regressus in infinitum*.\nImmanent principles of the empirical use of the understanding:\n1. There is no boundary of the composition and decomposition of appearances.\n2. There is no first ground or first beginning.\n3. Everything is mutable and variable, thus *empirice* contingent, because time is in itself necessary, but nothing necessary adheres to time.\nTranscendent principles of the pure use of the understanding:\n1. There is a first part. namely the simple as *principium* of composition. and are limitations of all appearance together.\n2. There is an absolute spontaneity, transcendental freedom.\n3. There is something necessary in itself, namely the unity of the highest reality, wherein all manifoldness of possibilities through limitations |\ncan be determined, as in space the shapes and (g whereby) also all existence as in time all state.\nBecause space and time are only conditions of appearance, it must be *principium* of the unity of pure reason, through which cognition is determined irrespective of appearance.\nGround of the antithetic or of the\nseeming antinomy of pure reason.\nThe former [ge] are principles of the exposition of appearances, the latter of the spontaneity of pure reason. Agreements with itself in the whole, thus also of morality.\nS. II:\nWe must have principles of the original unity or of the systematic unity of our cognitions, i.e. of the spontaneity [their] of the same, insofar as we act independently and want to determine the appearances themselves practically or ourselves among the appearances *originarie*.\nThey are principles of the self-determination of reason [of the unity of actions which]\nor of the unity of the whole of our determinations of reason.\nBefore reason all possible cognitions make up a whole, thus the synthesis of absolute unity is the condition of reason.\n––––––––––\n(g The soul is in appearances one substance with the body, it appears only as entelechy of the body. It is not in community, but union with the body and in no relation of place to the body. For it is intelligible, but the body merely *phaenomenon*. There is, however, no relation of the *noumeni* to the *phaenomeno*.\n––––––––––\nDialectic.\nRules.\n1. what does not belong to appearances at all, not to judge according to rules of appearance, e.g. God with space and time.\n2. what does not belong to outer appearance, e.g. spirit, not to subject to its conditions.\n3. What cannot be comprehended (g and what cannot be represented in intuition): the totality of the infinite or of infinite division, not to hold to be impossible. The infinity of the series, the [infinity of the] finitude [without] of the derived without the *substratum originarium*.\nFurther, not to mix up the principles of the absolute unity of reason with those of the empirical unity.\na. Simplicity of the thinking subject.\nb. Freedom as [*principium*] condition of [actions] rational actions.\nc. [Infinite] *Ens* [*infinitum*] *originarium* as *substratum* of all combination of its representations in one whole.\n[d Real unlimited totality of the same not with]\nd. Restriction of the world according to origin and content not to mix up with bounding.\n––––––––––\nPrinciples of reason are those which the conditions of the [determinations] unity of our cognition, insofar as it is *a priori* [under] determinable, consequently only those which contain the completeness of speculative cognition *a priori*, which agrees with the ideas of the practical *a priori*.",["“principles” renders both principien and Grundsätze (Vernunftgrundsätze: “principles of reason”).","“it must be principium” renders so muß es principium ... seyn, which may also be read “there must be a principium”.","The parenthesis opened at (g The soul is not closed in the source; the sentence Vernunftgrundsätze sind die ... has no verb for die Bedingungen."]],"reflm:4758":["S. I:\nThe principles [of the synthetic use] of the synthesis of pure reason [*a priori*] in general are all metaphysical.\nThose of the synthetic use of reason with regard to the intuitions which can be given in experience are prin|ciples\nof the empirical or physical use. Those with regard to the intuitions that cannot be given *a posteriori*, principles of the hyperphysical [use] or transcendent use. (The former are mathematical, the second dynamical.)\nThe principles of the hyperphysical use go to the collective universality of the synthesis, those of the physical to the distributive.\n1. Principle. The principles of the possibility of experiences are also principles of the possibility of the objects of experience. Example.\n2. Principle: In everything that exceeds the boundary of experience, we can assume only principles of the [synthesis] absolute unity of the synthesis *a priori* (g i.e. of the unity of the use of reason *a priori*).\n––––––––––\n1. Mathematical principles *a priori* and their possibility. evidence.\n2. metaphysical principles of mathematical synthesis in general. Infinity of the synthesis of intuition, as regards composition (g progressive) as well as decomposition.\nThere takes place no other synthesis of appearances than with appearances, consequently which is empirically possible, hence not with the empty.\nA. dynamical principles.\nMathematical and dynamical principles of the possibility of experiences*: the former of intuitions, the latter of the relation to apperception, i.e. to existence.\n*(g all appearances stand under rules of a dynamical unity, so that experiences come to be.)\nMathematics treats of nothing but what can be given *a priori* in intuition. Not of reality etc. Not of existence.\nObject and its existence.\nThe transcendent principles are principles of the subjective unity of cognition through reason, i.e. of the agreement of reason with itself.\nObjective principles are principles of a possible empirical use.\nS. II:\nEverything actual must be able to be cognized *a priori* (possible) and must also be given or determined *a priori* (necessary).\n––––––––––\nThere must be two kinds of principles of unity (g *a priori*). Unity of the intellection of appearances *a priori*, insofar as we are determined by them, and unity of the spontaneity of the understanding, insofar as [it itself and] the appearances are determined by it.\nThe unity of the relation to the whole of cognition, hence principles of the totality of the synthesis and of the *termino a priori*, the first, outermost (for what is given in intuition is the *terminus a posteriori*), hence the synthesis of composition and not merely of decomposition, are principles not of the empirical use of reason with regard to appearances, but of the architectonic and pure use. The simple (g of substance). The spontaneity of action, the [original] original being, the universal cause are the cardinal concepts [which] on which the unity of the [whole in] use of reason in the whole rests.\nThe understanding itself (a being that has understanding) is simple. It is substance. It is transcendentally free. It is affected with sensibility (space), with others in community. All its objects make up one (composite), which is called world (unity of space). The All of appearances is immeasurable, but limited and limits. The whole is contingent or dependent. Everything is grounded on an original understanding. which is the all-sufficient ground [of] of the world.\nThe (g necessary) unity of time and space is transformed into the necessary unity of an original being, the immeasurability of the former into the all-sufficiency of the other. The beginning (g of the world) in time into its origin. The divisibility of appearances into the simple. The breaks off.\nUnity of reason. Unity of the self-determination of reason with regard to the manifold of the unity of rules or principles. Not of exposition, i.e. of the analytic unity of appearances, but of determination (comprehension), i.e. of the synthetic, through which |\nthe manifold as given in general (not merely to the senses) necessarily has unity.",["“principles” renders both Grundsätze (Grundsetze) and Kant's Latinate principien.","“original being” renders Urwesen, “original” ursprünglich; “intellection”, “exposition”, “determination (comprehension)” keep Kant's Latinate words; “evidence” renders evidentz.","“and of the *termino a priori*”: the German has the dative dem termino, not the genitive, as printed; “The breaks off.”: the AA's bricht ab follows the article Die."]],"reflm:4759":["S. I:\nOf the principles of empirical [exposition] intellection as distinguished from the principles of comprehension.\nHow one can deceive the dogmatic enemy of religion and the dogmatic religious brooder both through a seeming antithetic and thus feigned skepticism.\nHow one can refute this skepticism itself through the dogmatic principles [of the positive] with regard to the practical use of reason.\nHow one can derive from the principles of the unity of reason with regard to totality principles that conflict with the [conditions] principles of determination in appearances, which must at all times be partial and conditioned, but must be regarded as belonging to cognition in the whole.\nThere is a *synthesin prototypam* and *ectypam*: the former of self-determination *a* [*priori*] *termino a priori, non empirice dato*, from nothing, the latter *a termino a posteriori*, — the former *simpliciter*, the latter *secundum qvid*. I am, I act, I together with all the manifold, I insofar as I exclude the manifold. The principles of [s] absolute synthesis are rational and conditions of practical cognition *a priori*.\n––––––––––\n1. Principles of intuition. [Of] That conditions of intuition are not of the things.\n2. Of the understanding, e.g. *principium rationis*.\n3. Of reason.\n––––––––––\nReason goes from the universal to the particular [or from], the understanding from the particular to the universal. The latter universal is so only *secundum qvid* and belongs to the empirical or physical use of reason. The former is absolute and belongs to the free use or metaphysical. Likewise to the moral.\nS. II:\n(g Exposition and [comprehension] rationality.)\n(g To appearances.) (g Unity of experience and unity of reason.)\nPrinciples of the exposition of appearances presuppose [them] these altogether as conditioned, hence nothing posited absolutely.\n1.\nno absolute totality (g *totalitas secundum qvid*) [of the synthesis] of composition, hence the [*regressus* infin] *progressus* infinite.\n(g unconditioned bounding *in mundo phaenomeno*. *Inter phaenomena non datur universitas absoluta*.)\n2.\nno absolute [division of the] totality of decomposition, hence no unconditionedly simple.\n(g *non simplicitas absoluta*.)\n(g The infinite progression cannot be comprehended and the unconditioned cannot be made intuitive.)\n3.\nno absolute totality of the series of generation, no unconditioned spontaneity.\n(g *non causalitas absoluta*.)\n4.\nno unconditioned necessity. All things can be taken out of time and space.\n(g *non necessitas absoluta*.)\n(g World in the physical sense\nSimple —\n—\n—\n*spontaneitas*\n—\n—\n*necessitas*\n—\n—)\nAll these propositions are as principles of empirical use (g objectively) certain, but contrary to reason.\n(g To things in general.)\nPrinciples of rationality or comprehension (g of the same). From the universal to the particular: absolute synthesis.\n1. Unconditioned All of the (g dependent) whole. Origin of the world.\n(g *in mundo noumeno datur universitas*.)\n2. Unconditionedly simple.\n(g *monas*.)\n3. Unconditioned spontaneity of action.\n(g *libertas transscendentalis*.)\n4. Unconditionedly necessary existence.\n(g *necessitas absoluta originaria*.)\n(g World in the metaphysical sense\nSimple —\n—\n—\n—)\nThese propositions are subjectively necessary as principles of the use of reason in the whole of cognition: unity of the whole of the manifold of the cognition of the understanding. They are practically necessary with regard to the breaks off.\nSubjective conflict of the partiality of sensibility and the totality of reason in the determination of cognitions:\nof the conditions of empirical use in exposition of appearances,\nof the conditions of rational use in comprehension of appearances.\n[The empirical canons] The canon of empirical use goes to universality (g of appearances), that of the rational to *universitatem* of things.\nWhat cannot be determined in appearance is not on that account impossible.\nThere must be principles of the self-determination of reason which are distinguished from those where reason is determined through appearances and their conditions. These are principles of the unity of cognitions in the whole, hence not of partial, but of total unity.",["“religious brooder” renders religionsgrübler; “feigned skepticism” renders verstellte sceptic; “conflict” renders wiederstreit(en).","“universality” renders universalitaet, kept apart from *universitatem* (universitas, the whole); “sense” renders Verstand in “im physischen Verstand”; “[*regressus* infin]” renders the struck “[regressus unend]”.","Latin: *a termino a priori, non empirice dato*: from a terminus a priori, not empirically given; *in mundo phaenomeno / noumeno*: in the world as phenomenon / noumenon; *Inter phaenomena non datur universitas absoluta*: among phenomena there is no absolute universitas."]],"reflm:4760":["S. I:\nThe ground of the antinomy of reason is the dispute: 1. All empirical synthesis is conditioned, the mathematical as well as the dynamical. A. All appearance has parts and is itself a part. B. Everything that happens is a sequel (g is there, is conditioned) and is itself a ground. There is thus no first and last. No simple, no boundary of magnitude, no first ground, no necessary being. I.e. we cannot arrive at it in appearances (g and must not appeal to it). On the other hand [the] 2. the transc. synthesis (g through pure concepts of reason) is unconditioned, but also happens through nothing but intellectual concepts; there is thus actually no antinomy. The world is limited. Consists of the simple. There is freedom. There is a necessary being. [Ground of this synthesis] Ground of these principles. Unity of the entire use of reason, through this, that it has collective unity.\n1. Infinite (of addition (g composition) and of division (g decomposition)). (g Finite.) 2. Infinite of derivation. 3. Infinite of contingency.\n––––––––––\nThe principles of the possibility of experiences go breaks off.\n––––––––––\nThe synthesis of the parts and of the whole [are] is in empirical cognition at all times conditioned, thus also of effects and causes (substantial) and of the contingent. For they go to the unity of appearances, where the manifold is given sooner than the unity. Determinable unity: where the unity rests on the rule of the *regressus*. On the other hand the unity of the pure use of reason (simple, free, necessary) is determining and a *progressus*, which begins from the condition *a priori*, e.g. from freedom in morals. These conditions do not belong in the field of appearances and make synthesis *a priori* in general possible.\nWhat can never become an object of our senses is absolutely [impossible] impossible as appearance, consequently also no empty space. But that something can occur in appearance which is regarded as a sequel of empty space is quite possible.\nS. II:\n(g Only the conditions of empirical synthesis are objective.)\n[Principle of the]\nSkeptical principle of the comprehension of appearances.\nThere is no absolutely first of the synthesis of appearances (because all altogether is conditioned).\n(g No absolute boundary)\n1. No first of the aggregate in space and time. (The absolutely first therein is that whose boundary is determined through nothing. (g The boundary can be determined only among appearances.) Empirical synthesis is at all times conditioned.) I.e. The whole of appearances is *a priori* unbounded. The totality can thus not be determined through successive addition.\n2. No absolutely first of composition (no absolute boundary of division) (nothing simple), no simple part [of that which in the Re] of the extended or of alteration.\n3. No absolute boundary of the subordination [of sequels and grounds. No] of the sequence of actions and [sequels] effects. No first action. No transcendental freedom.\n4. No first cause (no original being).\nFor all appearance is possible only in space and time. But time (g and space) is determinable only through appearance. But it is without a first.\nThese propositions are contradicted merely by the (g which however does not contradict the exposition of appearances) incomprehensibility of the propositions: There is a *regressus in infinitum* [of the] (from the empirical *termino* on) of dimension, of division, of [sub aris] generation and of dependence. We always remain with experiences in the chain of appearances.\nBut insofar as the things lying at the ground of appearances are taken, which consequently are thought only through concepts of the understanding, the unity of their synthesis requires an absolutely first (g of the inner state of reason) (i.e. unconditioned) [of the *synthesi* of totality, of the] of origin, of composition, of action, of existence in general. These are conditions of the (subjective) unity in the use of reason with regard to appearances, as those are principles of the manifold. In the former sameness of the condition, in the latter unconditioned unity.",["“sequel(s)” renders Folge(n), “sequence” Reihenfolge; “addition” renders Hinzuthuung and addition; “original being” renders Urwesen; “sameness” renders Einerleyheit.","“[sub aris]” renders the struck “[sub Entst]”, presumably the beginnings of a Latin word and of Entstehung, arising.","“The absolutely first” renders the printed superlative “absoluterste”; “The breaks off” and “go breaks off” reproduce where the AA's bricht ab stands."]],"reflm:4761":["S. I:\nProof of the future life from the analogy of nature with regard to the purposive determination of its predispositions.\nevolution.\nThe merely moral proof is valid only for some human beings and only for a certain point in time of the future existence, namely those who have transgressed (g children, savages, the stupid) — and only until they are punished |\n—. On the other hand the (g physico-) teleological proof is valid for all for this reason, because in the whole of human nature the predispositions are there and because their development and practical use prepares similar consequences into the infinite.\nEstimation of our cognition. The proof has practical (g positive) sufficiency and the metaphysical one negative with regard to the dogmatic presumption of the opponents.\nOf the metaphysical hypothesis*\nOf the\npneumatic\nlife problematic.\n—\n—\n—\n*commercio*\n—\n— —\n—\nAppearances (g apparitions)\nNot to deny the latter dogmatically, but principles of the reluctance of the understanding against every single narrative. Maxim of reason. Unity. *miracula comparativa* are worse than *rigorosa*. *ortus phaenomenorum univocus — aeqvivocus*. No laws.\n* we do not know whence the whole appearance of the corporeal world and of our own comes, but one cannot regard it as necessary.\nPractical sufficiency is that which is adequate to connect through reason the incentive, namely the *effectus a lege determinatos*, with moral laws.*\n*(g From experience one cannot infer the existence of God in the strict sense.)\n(g Fatalism.\nCosmological proof from the relation of the contingent to a will.)\n––––––––––\n(g *Sum*)\nThe Wolffian proof infers from an existence in general, thus cosmologically, to the existence of an *entis realissimi*; is properly an ontological proof that something necessary must be *realissimum*.\nThe divine will is intellectual. self-sufficient and inde|pendent.\nWithout affect. Remorse, wrath (g *tamen iustitia punitiva*, not itself revenge). Mercy.\nThe antecedent will toward the universal good, the consequent (decree) toward the [conseq] individual, can differ from each other and be without succession.\n(g The *providentia generalis* is natural, the *specialis* supernatural; the latter is directed at *individua*, but the extraordinary direction at single occurrences.)\nDivine blessedness.\nThe free causality of the world as regards substance is creation.\nIn the world there are beginnings, but not a beginning of the world. The world has always been. [God has created an eternal world, but not from E] Empty time before the world. The decree is not in time.\nThe cause\nof the existence of the world is\ncreation,\n—\n—\nof the duration — — —\npreservation.\nNo world can [itself] create something [what to it b]. All the parts of the world are created things. Because otherwise they would depend externally on themselves.\nThe divine will toward the particular in the world,\ninsofar as it belongs to creation, is\nprovidence,\nto preservation\n—\n—\ngovernment.\nS. II:\nNature (g without qualification) or the whole\nof appearances,\n— —\n[chance] of limited things,\n— —\nof contingent things.\n(g World. The essence of a composite and of mutual community is always derivative. derivative being.)\nRefers necessarily to God as original, not derived being. (everything composite [posits] is (g dependent) contingent, derived (g limited).)\n1. According to the mere concept.\n2. According to existence.\nAbsolute completeness [and], unity (g and necessity) of the original being. Being above nature and world.\nCannot properly be cognized through predicates of nature, but according to the analogy.\nTheology either rational or revealed. The former either transcendental or natural. The latter either metaphysical or physicotheology or moral theology.\nTranscendental — deist.\nNatural — theist; denying revelation: naturalist. [God] He who calls the world God is an atheist (g explicitly). The deist is an atheist, covertly such.\nThe concept of a thing, insofar as it is negatively determined, is always a derived concept, because it is congruent only with the part of some other concept different from it. Of different things one must necessarily be negatively determined and thus its concept be derived. There is thus only a single being whose concept is a *conceptus originarius*; the concepts of other things are only limitations of it. One always asks: whence then has this or that precisely these limitations.\nIn it they are all determined as to substance. From it all state originally stems. In it they are all *in commercio*.\n––––––––––\n( g (g *primus*,) *motor*. The highest being, The highest intelligence, The highest good. )\n(g Emanation or creation. Intell: analogy (g not anthropomorphism).\nUnderstanding. Feeling. Will.\nForesight. inmost presence.\nNot *mundi causa bruta (genitor)*, but *libera (autor)*.)\nThe transcendental concept of things, i.e., as beings in general, [has th] has for the cognition of their difference a basic concept of an *entis realissimi*, in which they are all determinable.",["Latin sentences are translated (Voluntas antecedens ... esse; Beatitudo divina; Caussalitas libera mundi qvoad substantiam est creatio; Mundi partes omnes sunt res creatae; Mundus. Essentia compositi ...; Theologia vel rationalis ...; Mundum deum vocans ...); Latin terms inside German sentences are kept.","Anlagen = predispositions; Triebfeder = incentive; Vorsehung = providence, Vorhersehung = foresight; Erhaltung = preservation; Regirung = government. The deleted fragments [was zu ihr s] and [hat ih] break off mid-word.","\"those who have transgressed\": the insertion (g Kinder, Wilde, Dumme) — stands between verbrochen and haben. \"metaphysical\" (Metaphysica) may be the adjective agreeing with theologia or the noun \"metaphysics\"."]],"reflm:4762":["S. I:\n(g 1.) Reality (s partly real, partly negative. negation presupposes reality. Metaphysical good). Negation. Cryptic reality (g apparent).\n2. Unity [or] mathematical (of aggregating) or qualitative (of conceiving) (g of connecting) (s of subordinating). e.g. The unity of the system. Of speech. Of a machine. Commonwealth. Multitude. [Def] Indefinite. Number. Infinite. Quantum (g assignable). Quantity. Greater. Smaller. Equal, Measure. (Continuous, Discrete). Limit. (g terminus.)[Construction] Construction (g of intuition or of magnitude in general). Arithmetic. Geometry. Evidence. (Mathesis. rational science. from the constructions of concepts, which is why it has quantity for its object.) [Limited being Unlimited]. Formal perfection. Most perfect being transc: Limited being, unlimited (s maximum, all-sufficient), infinite, real. (s Possibility, perfection, order, unity.) Quantum (s either givable or) assignable. (s Than every assignable a greater, smaller assignable.)\nGreater, Smaller. (s in space there is not) Maximum, Minimum. Infinite simultaneous, successive, of adding and of comprehending. (s Infinitely small (infinite of division).)\n*Unitas* as the way of determining something: the unity,\n— —determination itself: a unity.\nUnity, perfection, truth, possibility. either predicable or predicamental. Unity either of determining or determined [or the unity of determination and the determining unity]. (s determining unity, determined unity.)\nThere is no *qvantum assignabile infinitum et infinite parvum*.\nSomething can be greater and smaller if besides the smaller it contains still more, although of a different kind.\nIn space there is already a simultaneity; it is thus an *infini|tum*\n*comprehensionis*, but we cannot think it, because our concept is an *infinitum successionis*.\nThe concepts of the greatest and of the infinite are not the same (s the greatest number is impossible, and the greatest multitude — infinite, not so number.)\nIrrational is a [number] magnitude which is congruent with no number, but is either greater or smaller than *qvilibet dabilis*.\nIt is a natural use of our pure reason, but this is dialectical. To set this within limitations there belongs a discipline of pure reason and after this canon. Before that, however, in the transcendental logic the analytic of the basic concepts and of the principles; of cognition *a priori* domain and limitations.\nComposite and simple.\nUnity of taking-together and connection.\nAppearances in space and time are continuous quanta. The appearance in space is the extended, the appearance in time is alteration.\nS. II:\nWhat (g A positing which) is not a quantum is simple (g absolute unity.)\nThat in which there is no composition is simple.\nA positing or canceling cause.\n–––––––––––\n(g Here nothing but real relations are spoken of, consequently the relations in existence.)\n(g If I leave out everything that is called inherence, there remains merely that whose existence is subsistence, i.e., the substantial.)\nCategories of relation (s determination): 1. of the subject, 2. of the principle, 3. of the composite (of real things (g not of logical nor of ideal ones)). [a. subsistence and inherence. b. Of cause and caused. c. of the co-parts and of the whole] (g Inclusion, consequence and accompaniment.) (s Threefold unity:) a. Relation of inherence (g subsistence), b. dependence (g causality), c. of community (g reciprocation).\n(g Whose appearances one another (g whose representation at all times a part of the representation of another) include (g e.g. of the rainbow and of rain), follow one another and accompany one another.)\nPredicables are. (s A. The substantial and accidents (substratum).) A and B. To act. To suffer. Force, faculty, receptivity. A, B, and C. Substantial composite. Co-parts. action and reaction. Simple substance.\nPredicables of appearances. Substance as phenomenon is the perdurable constant, in whose [concept] representation that of the accidents is at all times included. accident is the variable.\nCause as phenomenon, upon which certain other things succeed according to a rule.\nComposite*(g substantial), [which] in which (insofar as it is composite) the states of several are together at any time.\n*(g Community (g interaction). Insofar as [determ] it is determinable only simultaneously. Relation of the parts to one another, of the parts to the whole; whether they are simple, absolute unity.)\nTherefore through these categories appearances (g the mode of composition) are, as regards [existence constant] existence, i.e., of experience, subjected to perpetual laws, just as as regards intuition [to laws] to rules and as regards concepts to *conditores normae*.\nOf alteration.\n––––––––––\nIn all categories the relation is heteronomous, in that of the composite homonomous.\n(g The relation to perception (g given), to thinking and [sensing] determining *a priori*.\nAre not predicates.\nThing, non-thing. Essence, attributes. Necessary being. | Absolute hypothetical.)\n( g analytic principles of possibility synthetic — )\n––––––––––\nFalse definition by Carthes of substance.\n––––––––––\nOf causality. 1. Ground and consequence (real relation). 2. Ground of existence. Cause. 3. Substance as cause. *Causa afficientiae*.\nOf this substance the *respectus* to the *accidentibus* as *principium* is force. The causality of force with regard to the *accidentium*: action. The dependence of the *accidentium* on the force of another: suffering. The possibility of acting: *facultas*, of suffering: *receptivitas*.\nThe *principia* of the exposition of the *phaenomenorum* are *principia* of intellection, not of the perspicience of them: to seek causes, but not to determine them. and in general to determine nothing but intuition.\n––––––––––\nThe ground precedes in the position (the concept of it), although it is simultaneous with the consequence. I first light the candle, thereafter it shines, although, when it burns, it shines simultaneously. The bordering of the ground on the consequence is simultaneous, just as the initial moment is simultaneous with the year, but nevertheless precedes.",["The Latin lists and sentences are translated (Realitas ... Negatio; Vnitas ... Mensura ... Mathesis; Conceptus maximi et infiniti non sunt eadem; Qvod non est qvantum, est simplex; Categorien der relation ... commercii; Praedicabilia ...; Igitur per has categorias ...); Latin terms inside German sentences are kept.","conditores normae (\"founding norms\"?) is obscure and left in Latin; \"after this canon\" (nach diesem Canon) may also mean \"according to this canon\"; \"Absolute hypothetical\" (Absolute hypotheticum) may be a pair, \"absolute, hypothetical\".","Zusammennehmung = taking-together; Leiden / pati = suffering / to suffer (undergo); Wechselwirkung = interaction; Carthes = Kant's spelling of Descartes; Licht = light, here a candle."]],"reflm:4763":["S. II:\nEverything perishable is a contingent thing, but not the mutable.",["Vergangliche = perishable; Veranderliche = mutable."]],"reflm:4764":["S. II:\nThe multitude that is no part (infinite). The unity that is no multitude (simple). Ground that is no consequence.",["Menge = multitude; Folge = consequence."]],"reflm:4765":["S. II:\nOf the principles [a pr] of the pure understanding.\nAll appearances have a determinate magnitude (whose relation to another is assignable).\nThe infinite does not appear as such, likewise not the simple.\nFor appearances are enclosed between two boundaries (points) and are thus themselves determinate magnitudes.\nRationalizing principle (from subjective grounds of reason.) [All possible magnitudes are] No object of intuition (g should read: no appearance) is infinitely large and divisible to infinity, i.e., everything has boundaries of extension and division.\nThe all in the case of the infinite and of the parts of the finite (of the infinitely divisible) is for the human understanding, but not in itself, impossible.\n––––––––––\nAll appearances are real and *negatio*: Rationalizing: All reality must be sensation.\n––––––––––\nEverything that is there (g insofar as it is there) is substance and *accidentia*; insofar as it happens (g follows): a consequence from a ground; insofar as it is simultaneous: composite in a whole and [is] reciprocally determined.\nRationalizing: [All substance is necessary] [Everything mutable is contingent] Everything persistent (g in appearance) is substance. (determined in itself.) (*phaenomenon substantiatum*.)\nEverything that happens is determined in the series. There is no absolutely first beginning.",["Vernünftelnder / Vernünftelnd = rationalizing; Grentzen / Grenzen = boundaries; Eintheilung = division; Beharrliche = persistent.","[a pr] is a deleted, unfinished a priori.","vor = für (\"for\"), eighteenth-century usage."]],"reflm:4766":["Unity (connection) (g harmony), truth (quality), completeness (quantity).",["Verknüpfung = connection; Zusammenstimmung = harmony (agreement); Vollständigkeit = completeness."]],"reflm:4767":["[A The marks] The manifold in a concept can be weighed either in relation to the thing itself or to things in general.\nMetaphysical unity is real (*substantia*, *Ratio*, *Totum*).\nMetaphysical truth: relation to the data. if the conditions are given.\nMetaphysical completeness: *omnitudo realitatis*.",["respectus ad data. si conditiones datae sunt (Latin) is translated; omnitudo realitatis = the allness of reality.","erwogen = weighed (considered); Ratio = ground."]],"reflm:4768":["Necessity is best divided into the conditioned and unconditioned. The former in turn into the internally or externally conditioned. For *hypotheticum* is opposed not to the *interno*, but to the *categorico*.",["hypotheticum / internum / categoricum = the hypothetical / the internal / the categorical (Kant's case forms kept)."]],"reflm:4769":["The necessary without a ground is incomprehensible, but the contingent determination with a ground is so just as well; the former is necessary in every respect, the latter just as contingent.",["unbegreiflich = incomprehensible; in aller absicht = in every respect."]],"reflm:4770":["A concept cannot be altered with regard to what necessarily belongs to it.\nIt is noteworthy that the mark of all action is alteration, either *actu* or *potentia*. of the inner action: the inner, of the outer: motion. One sees that the position in time and the order of it nevertheless constitutes the condition of all concepts of the understanding.",["actu / potentia = in act / in potency; Handlung = action; Veränderung = alteration.","\"the inner\" (die innere) has no noun in the German; it is left elliptical."]],"reflm:4771":["What is absolutely necessary is immutable for this reason, because it is [in a] not subordinated in a series, consequently another state finds no ground.",["unveränderlich = immutable; subordinirt = subordinated."]],"reflm:4772":["At the very top of the page:\nFirst analytic of the categories. without synthetic propositions. Distinction of the pure concepts of the understanding or those applied to *phaenomena*.",["The German (der reinen oder auf phaenomena angewandten Verstandes Begriffe) can be read as one class named two ways or as two classes distinguished."]],"reflm:4773":["An object is possible only insofar as it contains reality; otherwise the *intuitus* is empty, i.e. without object. All objects, insofar as they are thought *absolute*, i.e. through understanding, presuppose reality [and negations]. [What all] The sum total of all reality is only one single object; the manifoldness of objects is therefore possible only through the All of reality and its restriction. An *ens originarium* therefore lies at the ground of the possibility of the manifold.",["“object” renders both obiect/object and Gegenstand.","*intuitus*: “intuition”; *absolute*: Latin adverb, “absolutely”; *ens originarium*: “original being”.","“restriction” renders Einschrankung."]],"reflm:4774":["At the bottom of the page:\nEvery reality borders on nothing on one side. For every sensation fills the [empty] time and is [from] to be regarded as having arisen in empty time as from nothing through continuous position, hence as continuous magnitude. Reason starts from the highest reality.",["“position” renders position (positing)."]],"reflm:4775":["At the bottom of the page:\nTo the anticipation of appearance belongs also that the intensive magnitude of matter can decrease to infinity down to empty space.",[]],"reflm:4776":["What does not [can] exist except as the consequence of another is dependent; what does not exist except as the predicate of another is an accident. What exists even if it is not the consequence of another is independent; what exists even if it is not the predicate of another is substance.",["Latin throughout; “consequence” renders rationatum."]],"reflm:4777":["By confusing these concepts with one another Descartes and Spinoza erred.",["Latin; Descartes is carthesius in the source."]],"reflm:4778":["existence denotes: 1. to be a real accident; 2. not to belong to pos|sibility\nas a logical predicate, therefore not to be posited through identity nor understood through analysis.\nThat which does not exist except another being posited denotes:\n1. on the side of the predicate inherence (in logic there is only competence or agreement),\n2. on the side of the subject force.",["Latin throughout; the infinitives (accidens esse reale, non pertinere …) have no expressed subject.","“competence or agreement” renders competentia sive convenientia; “force” renders vim."]],"reflm:4779":["At the bottom of the page:\n*qvantitas continua* arises from nothing in a time through continuous position.",["*qvantitas continua*: “continuous quantity”; “position” renders position (positing)."]],"reflm:4780":["At the very bottom of the page, \nInfinity is the absolute impossibility of a complete synthesis (not of the completeness of the object) of the composition or decomposition of a given object. The [synthesis of] appearance is infinite, and the division of it goes to infinity. This [synthesis concerns both] infinity concerns both the dynamical and mathematical synthesis. On the other hand in the intellectual the synthesis is complete, [and the] but the condition of cognizing this completeness *in concreto* is sensible (a first or outermost). Reason therefore demands independence from the sensible, but the determination of its concept can only be sensible (antinomy). The *omnisufficientz* of reason regarded as determining is with regard to us an origin of practical laws of our reason, which necessarily presuppose completeness as hypothesis.",["“object” renders obiekt and Gegenstand.","*omnisufficientz*: “all-sufficiency”, Latin with a German ending.","“which” may refer to the practical laws or to reason."]],"reflm:4781":["Cosmology is necessary for this reason, so that, when we take together everything that is composed among one another, everything contingent and everything that is consequence, we may arrive at the absolute, and what has no further *respectum dependentiae*. Thus here also the consideration of the boundaries occurs internally (empty space, empty time). Ontology goes to the complete resolution, cosmology to the complete combination. Now because the world is the whole of the actual things which relate to another as its presupposition, one arrives at the concept of the original being, which contains everything completely and absolutely, relates to no thing *a* (g *parte*) *priori*, but *a parte posteriori* to all.",["*respectum dependentiae*: “relation of dependence”; *a parte priori* / *a parte posteriori*: “on the side of the earlier / the later”.","“resolution” renders auflösung; “original being” renders Urwesen; “boundaries” Grentzen."]],"reflm:4782":["According to the laws of appearance a *regressus in infinitum* is not impossible, but it is in the *subordinatione intellectuali*; the former is subjectively impossible to think as a whole. The totality is impossible.",["*regressus in infinitum*: “regress to infinity”; *subordinatione intellectuali*: “intellectual subordination”.","“the former” renders jene, feminine, which cannot refer to the masculine regressus; it may refer to Erscheinung or to subordinatio.","“totality” renders totalitaet."]],"reflm:4783":["Free actions happen according to a rule* just as natural ones do. But they are not to be determined *a priori* according to it as these are; both are therefore in conformity with reason, whereas blind fate and blind chance are *qvalitates occultae* and contrary to reason.\n*\n(g There are rules contrary to which actions cannot happen, although they are not to be determined according to these.)",["*qvalitates occultae*: “occult qualities”; “chance” renders Ohngefehr.","“in conformity with reason” renders vernunftmaßig, “contrary to reason” vernunftwidrig."]],"reflm:4784":["At the very top of the page and \nIf something happens, then it must have a cause. But the causality of the cause is [als wied] also something that happens; it must therefore in turn have a cause, and so to infinity. The very same holds of the other synthesis.\nThe idea is a concept [de] to which the empirical one, though in conformity with it as with a *principio*, but approximating to infinity, can never be adequate.",["[als wied] and [de] are deleted, unfinished German words, left as they stand.","*principio*: Latin dative of *principium*, “principle”."]],"reflm:4785":["The egoist and materialist are directly opposed to one another: the one denies the existence of something in space and thus of the things outside him, hence of bodies, but rather has merely a monadic existence; the materialist denies everything inner of substances. Egoism is more plausible.",["“more plausible” renders scheinbarer, which can also mean “more specious”."]],"reflm:4786":["All life is derived and conditioned. There is only one original and unconditioned life. All other life depends on the determining cause in time.\n*Primus motor* (s God as living being), i.e. that which gives to the series of alterable states [first] the first beginning, is not a mere [blind] necessary nature, but freely acting intelligence.\nThe life of creatures is the series of alterations from an inner *principio*. This must have a beginning. God begins all life.\n[Life in tr] Motion in the transcendental sense is life.",["*Primus motor*: “first mover”; *principio*: Latin ablative of *principium*.","“sense” renders Verstande."]],"reflm:4787":["Opposite the heading »Voluntas Dei«:\n*contra fatalismum*.\nHis freedom can most easily be comprehended, but not the freedom of his creatures.",["*Voluntas Dei*: “the will of God”; *contra fatalismum*: “against fatalism”."]],"reflm:4788":["At the bottom of the page:\nWe can very well understand and gain insight into divine freedom, but not human freedom. If the human being were merely intellectual, then we would be able to gain insight into his choice through reason; if he were a *brutum*: likewise. But as a sensible and rational being not, because his action afterwards is a *phaenomenon*, but beforehand, under practical laws, *noumenon*.\n(s The will is the property of a being, in that it is through its understanding cause of the objects — faculty of desire only through its representations.)",["“gain insight into” renders einsehen; “choice” renders Willkühr; *brutum*: “brute”.","“faculty of desire” renders Begehrungsvermögen; “property” renders Eigenschaft."]],"reflm:4789":["When did God create the world? — Not in time, hence not from eternity. — Yet we must distinguish the *actuatio mundi in initio* |\nfrom the *actuatio durationis creatio*. The universal is — it exists through God.",["“When” renders Wenn (= wann).","*actuatio mundi in initio*: “actualization of the world in the beginning”; *actuatio durationis creatio*: “actualization of duration” with creatio, “creation”, standing loose; the reading may be corrupt."]],"reflm:4790":["If matter were originally existing, then (s matter would preserve itself) preservation would be directed only at the form, and God could not be regarded completely as the author of all form, because matter cannot be merely passive, hence a part of the guilt and of the merit must always be ascribed to it. Also not all form could have been originally laid into it, it would have to be produced in it successively; thus providence would not alone be enough, but also governance, i.e. extraordinary *concursus* for [Sch] providence.",["“preservation” renders Erhaltung; “providence” providentz; “governance” gubernation; *concursus*: “concurrence”.","[Sch] is a deleted, unfinished German word, left as it stands."]],"reflm:4791":["The actions of freedom are (g externally) *phaenomena* (g internally *noumena*) and to that extent not sufficiently determined among the *phaenomenis*. On the whole all actions are good (in the intellectual world), although they are determined beforehand by God.",["*phaenomenis*: Latin dative plural after German unter den."]],"reflm:4792":["(g Preservation. Omnipresence.)\nCreation is the bringing forth of the world.\nProvidence is the arrangement [of the same of the grounds to the ends] (g in creation), through which the states (g following) upon one another become in accord with the divine ends.\nProvision is providence insofar as it is directed at the well-being of the creature; providence is also directed at punishments.\nGovernment: The preservation (g according to universal laws) of nature, in conformity with the rule of providence.\nSteering (g guidance), *direction* is the [Ang] action of making the occurrences of the world appropriate to his particular ends.\nThe *praedestination* (predetermination): the pre-ordaining of the determining grounds to individual ends, chiefly free actions.\nCooperation: the immediate supplementation of the natural faculties for the fulfillment of individual ends.\nAs creation relates to preservation *in nexu causali*: so providence to government *in nexu finali*.",["“Providence” renders Vorsehung, “Provision” Vorsorge, “Government” Regirung, “Steering” Lenkung, “guidance” Leitung, “Cooperation” Mitwirkung, “pre-ordaining” Vorherverordnung.","[Ang] is a deleted, unfinished German word, left as it stands.","*in nexu causali* / *in nexu finali*: “in the causal / final nexus”."]],"reflm:4793":["Providence is the [Besti] establishment of the order of nature.\nGovernment is the cause of the course of nature,\n*direction*: of individual occurrences,\n(g freedom)\nNot the cause of evil.",["[Besti] is a deleted, unfinished German word, left as it stands; “establishment” renders Einrichtung."]],"reflm:4794":["The ground determines the consequence. The consequence testifies to a ground, namely leads to this, to seek some ground or other.",["“consequence” renders folge; “determines” renders determinirt."]],"reflm:4795":["Logical predicates are\naffirmations or negations,\nreal ones\n—\ndeterminations —\n—",[]],"reflm:4796":["Logical position: affirmation,\ntranscendental position: reality.\nThe opposition of realities against one another is real.\nThe opposition of realities against negations is logical.",["“position” renders position (positing)."]],"reflm:4797":["essence is the internal principle of all determinations adhering to the concept of a being.",["Latin throughout."]],"reflm:4798":["Through experience we indeed cognize, empirically, how the object is given to us; but not the *principium* of these determinations through reason.",["“through reason” can attach to *principium* or to “cognize”."]],"reflm:4799":["The first complete ground of the logical predicates or of the determinations.\nThe essence of things: substance, or of the determination of them.",["“essence” renders Wesen."]],"reflm:4800":["The complete fundamental concept of a thing is the essence.\nFundamental concept is the completion of the subordination of the inner determinations.\nComplete is the completion of the coordination of the inner determinations.\nWe seek the essence for this reason, because we like to complete everything and because through this everything receives unity, when from one ground and in One whole, in one subject, the manifold is cognized.",["“fundamental concept” renders Grundbegrif (literally “ground-concept”); “essence” renders Wesen; “completion” renders Vollendung."]],"reflm:4801":["Above, beside and \nPossibility is agreement with the conditions of thinking in general, impossible: what conflicts with them. What agrees with the analytic conditions of thinking is logically possible; what with the synthetic, is really possible. Logical possibility without real is the empty concept without content, i.e. relation to object.",["The editorial location note “Über, neben und” is cut off in the source."]],"reflm:4802":["Concept,\nJudgment (truth) and\nInference.\nPossibility,\nActuality,\nNecessity.\nUnderstanding,\nSense,\nReason.",["“Inference” renders Schlus; “Actuality” renders Wirklichkeit."]],"reflm:4803":["Understanding,\nIntuition,\nReason\nThought,\ngiven*,\ngiven through this, that it is thought.\n*(g whose cognition depends on the object. The object of empirical cognition.)",[]],"reflm:4804":["Unity, truth and perfection can be taken *formaliter* or *materialiter*. In the first case only *singularis*, in that the manifold fits only into one form, in the second *pluralis* is possible. A *constitutivum* of the multitude is taken *materialiter*, e.g. One part of something insofar is perfect; but is the form, is no part.",["*formaliter* / *materialiter*: “formally / materially”; *singularis* / *pluralis*: “singular / plural”; *constitutivum*: “constituent”.","The last sentence is obscure in the source and is kept so."]],"reflm:4805":["Essential unity (transcendental) (g In concepts, not in the relation of concepts) is: 1. Unity of the *principii* of the manifold (the essence is the *principium*); 2. Unity of the agreement among one another: *essentiae et attributorum*; 3. Unity of the connection and composition into one being.",["*principii*: genitive of *principium*; *essentiae et attributorum*: “of the essence and of the attributes”.","“agreement” renders Zusammenstimmung; “essence” renders Wesen in the parenthesis, “being” in the last clause."]],"reflm:4806":["Thoroughgoing interconnection of that which belongs to the essence.\n*unitas*\n*Consensus*\n*unicitas*.\nUnity\nUnanimity\nOneness\ntranscendental unity: derivation (g of the manifold) from one concept. Essence.\ntranscendental truth: derivation of the manifold from one another. attributes.\ntranscendental perfection: derivation of the concept from the manifold.\n(g Unity (g etc etc.) is taken here *adiective*, i.e. *formaliter*, not as the concept of an object, but as the agreement with the possibility of a concept of the understanding in general. This threefold kind of interconnection and of formal unity does not belong under the categories, but (s to) the unity of understanding through the same.)",["*unitas*, *Consensus*, *unicitas*: “unity”, “consensus”, “uniqueness”; “Unanimity” renders Einhelligkeit, “Oneness” Einigkeit.","*adiective*: “adjectivally”; *formaliter*: “formally”.","“the same” renders dieselbe, singular, which may refer to the threefold kind or to formal unity."]],"reflm:4807":["Unity is the form of the understanding.\nCombination of the manifold with One. Unity: understanding.\nCombination of the manifold among one another. Truth: power of judgment.\nCombination of the manifold into a whole. Perfection: reason.\nThe thought of this Refl. Kant subsequently turned somewhat |\ndifferently in the four following lines, which date from about the same time, and connected the addition by a sign with the words with One (and thereby with the whole. Refl.):\nthrough derivation (g determination) (g of the manifold through one) from One,\nthrough derivation from one another (in One),\nthrough derivation of the One from the manifold.",["The paragraph beginning “The thought of this Refl.” is the AA editor's note, not Kant's.","“and thereby with the whole. Refl.” follows the source's punctuation (mit der ganzen. Rfl.), which looks garbled."]],"reflm:4808":["The rule is not the proposition itself, but is only expressed through it.",[]],"reflm:4809":["The rule must be distinguished from the formula of it. The former is only a universal ground of the use of a faculty, through which the manifold of the same becomes concordant. Through a rule the manifold in the cognition is brought under unity, precisely thereby comprehended, and has something constant.",["“of the same” renders desselben, which may refer to the faculty or to its use; “concordant” renders Einstimmig."]],"reflm:4810":["There are rules of the generation of representations through universal action or of the taking together of them.",["“taking together” renders zusammenfassung."]],"reflm:4811":["The formative force requires a form, the reflecting one a rule, the former for intuiting, the latter for thinking.\nThe rule is the validity of a concept, to bring the manifold to unity in a universal way. The understanding gives rules, reason laws.",["“formative force” renders bildende Kraft."]],"reflm:4812":["Rules *a priori* are laws; *a posteriori* are never without exception.",[]],"reflm:4813":["The unconditioned is either unconditioned necessity or contingency: fate and chance in regard to the occurrences and series without a first among the things.",["“chance” renders Zufall."]],"reflm:4814":["Above and \nThe determinations of an alterable thing [are] can (g hypothetically) be necessary (g and are so too.). [They are not] (A state is not the contradictory opposite.) Another state is not the *oppositum* of the thing.\nBut the alterable thing itself cannot (g in itself) exist necessarily, because its existence would otherwise be determinable only from concepts and thus only in one single way.",["*oppositum*: “opposite”; “existence” renders Daseyn."]],"reflm:4815":["By itself alone nothing alters; pleasure does not transform itself into displeasure.",["“pleasure” and “displeasure” render Lust and Unlust."]],"reflm:4816":["Alterability of the concepts or of the things in regard to them;\nAlterability of the things in existence.\nBesides contingency something more belongs to alterability. The succession of states is [not] alteration. Continuance of the thing. Arising and passing away is no alteration.",["“things” renders Dinge in the first line, Sachen in the second; “thing” renders Sache.","“Continuance” renders Fortdauer."]],"reflm:4817":["*Realitas phaenomenon* or *noumenon*. The former is what in the appearance corresponds to perception (g sensation) or to the lack of perception. *Noumenon* is what in the object in itself is position.\nEmpty sensible intuition would be *phaenomenon absqve realitate*.",["*Realitas phaenomenon*: “reality [as] phenomenon”; *phaenomenon absqve realitate*: “phenomenon without reality”.","“position” renders Kant's position, not Setzung."]],"reflm:4818":["We go through the categories analytically.",[]],"reflm:4819":["Distinction of quantity and quality in general.\nThe word “determination” (g “predicate”) is logical. Likewise the concept of thing (object), of possible and impossible merely logical. universal negation [or affirmation] a priori.",["“determination” renders Kant's determination, not Bestimmung; “predicate”: praedicat; “object”: obiect."]],"reflm:4820":["Extensive magnitude in that which is at the same time; protensive in that which is after one another; intensive breaks off.",["“breaks off” renders bricht ab, the editors' note that the text stops."]],"reflm:4821":["With the *continuum* every part is itself magnitude. No simple part.",[]],"reflm:4822":["Above and \nAll magnitude is either an accumulated (g *aggregatum*) (*discretum*) or continuous magnitude (*continuum*). The latter is the magnitude of every unity, and without it there can also be no accumulated magnitudes, because in all magnitudes the whole and the part must always be homogeneous. The whole must always have the term of the parts. e.g. a *qvantum* of money, but not a magnitude of ducats*.\n*(s a *Qvantum* which is thought before the composition (g as such a one) is *continuum*; that which is thought only through the composition, where therefore the parts precede, is *discretum*.)",["The editorial location note “Über und” is cut off in the source.","“accumulated” renders gehäufte, “continuous” stetige; “term” renders Benenung.","The asterisks are Kant's reference marks."]],"reflm:4823":["The subject of inherence and the ground of inherence.",[]],"reflm:4824":["We cognize the substantial only through the persistent in all sorts of determinations.\nEverything in substances that we cognize is force. *vis primitiva*.",["*vis primitiva*: “primitive force”."]],"reflm:4825":["The duty of the philosopher is to treat all force as derivative, nevertheless not to deny that there are primitive ones.",[]],"reflm:4826":["Beside and \nCombination with the category of magnitude.\nSubstance is either absolute unity or in itself plurality: *compositum substantiale*.",["The editorial location note “Neben und” is cut off in the source.","*compositum substantiale*: “substantial composite”; “plurality” renders Vielheit."]],"reflm:4827":["The reciprocally (g determining) relation of simultaneity is composition; they have a point of time in common, consequently [also a gr] in the sense, insofar as they are composed, also a ground in common; hence simultaneity is the condition of community.",["“in the sense” renders in dem Verstande, literally “in the understanding”.","The deleted [auch einen Gr] breaks off in the source."]],"reflm:4828":["Several things coordinated, i.e. determining one another in one, make a composite.",["“composite”: compositum; “i.e.”: h.e."]],"reflm:4829":["The relation of the manifold in a *compositum* is *actio atque reactio sive actio reciproca et passio reciproca*.",["*actio atque reactio sive actio reciproca et passio reciproca*: “action and reaction, or reciprocal action and reciprocal passion”."]],"reflm:4830":["In the bottom margin. \nFrom the *phaenomenis* one can indeed arrive at the *substantiam comparativam* and *phaenomena substantiata*, but not at the substances themselves. That many monads lie at the ground of bodies cannot be inferred at all.",["*phaenomenis*: “phenomena”; *substantiam comparativam*: “comparative substance”; *phaenomena substantiata*: “substantiated phenomena”."]],"reflm:4831":["Beside and \nWhether there is a subject first for the senses, a ground first for the senses and a first part. The *phaenomena* do not on that account have a first. The universe consists of simple parts, but not on that account exactly the bodies.",["The editorial location note “Neben und” is cut off in the source.","The German has ein only once: “Ob ein vor die sinne erstes subiekt, vor die Sinne erster Grund und erster Theil sey”."]],"reflm:4832":["physical division is the dislocation of the manifold: displacement. Space is not physically divisible.",["“dislocation” renders dislocation, “displacement” Versetzung."]],"reflm:4833":["progressive infinity is possible, collective not likewise. Totality.",["Latin: infinitudo progressiva … collectiva; Totalitas."]],"reflm:4834":["In and \nWe find no difficulty with the infinity of the form of all magnitudes, namely space and time.",["The editorial location note “In und” is cut off in the source."]],"reflm:4835":["Under and \nThe infinite whole is distinguished from the infinite series.",["The editorial location note “Unter und” is cut off in the source."]],"reflm:4836":["At the very top of the page. \nA *quantum* is *infinitum* in which an infinite *progressus* is possible. — Thus [infinite] multitude can be regarded as infinite. *infinitum actuale* would be that where the *progressus* were total.",["*infinitum*: “infinite”; *infinitum actuale*: “actual infinite”; “multitude” renders Menge."]],"reflm:4837":["Among all sensations there are only two which are the same in all and [therefore each also to all the rest] intelligible to everyone: pleasure and displeasure in the inner and motion or moving force and rest in the outer. Both serve also for metaphysics, the latter for that of nature, the former of morals, as the only empirical thing that can be laid at the ground and to which the rest of all [laws must reduc] *phaenomenorum* or principles must let itself be related.",["“the same” renders einerley; “morals” renders Sitten.","*phaenomenorum*: “of the phenomena”; the deleted [Gesetze muß reduc] breaks off in the source."]],"reflm:4838":["Rest is natural to every body, motion made. Everything, |\ninsofar as it is natural, is determined according to rules. All things have a nature, and things of the world belong to one all-encompassing nature.",["“made” renders gemacht, also “artificial”."]],"reflm:4839":["The first inner *principium* of everything (g according to a rule) which belongs to the possibility of something is the essence; what to the actuality, is the nature.",["“essence” renders Wesen; “of something” renders wovon."]],"reflm:4840":["157 E II 569.\nM 157':\nThe inner (s universal) *principium* of the accidents* (g determinations) according to universal laws is nature. (s Nature and freedom.)\nM 157:\n*(s The first inner principle [of a] of everything that belongs to the existence of a thing. The principle of that which belongs to existence is called law. Thus existence under laws.\nTo existence belong inner and outer determinations. The principles are substance, cause and *commercium*.\nNature taken *substantive* (g *materialiter*).)",["“accidents” renders accidentium; “principle” renders Princip; “existence” renders Daseyn.","*commercium*: “community, interaction”; *substantive*: “as a substantive”.","The asterisks are Kant's reference marks."]],"reflm:4841":["Transcendental theology serves as the norm of the natural one; for because [we] in the latter the concepts are empirical, but the object not an object of experience, we shall have to distinguish |\nwhich empirical concepts, and with what limitation or amplification, are fitting for the original being, highest being. First: from outer experiences these concepts cannot be abstracted, because these do not give what the things are, but how they appear. thus only from inner ones. These predicates will be ascribed to god *per reductionem*, *per eminentiam*, and the rest *per analogiam*. Transcendental theology is thus the norm of the natural one, indeed also its origin.",["“limitation” renders Einschränkung, “amplification” Erweiterung; “are fitting for” renders sich … schicken.","*per reductionem*, *per eminentiam*, *per analogiam*: “by reduction”, “by eminence”, “by analogy”."]],"reflm:4842":["There is dispute over whether the ancient peoples had just as correct concepts of morality of the Godhead and of the future life. These concepts are natural and everywhere correct, although not refined. But the religions of these have two faults: first, that they set [the moralit] the moral law and their worthiness so indulgently that [they] religion could be rhymed together with their weaknesses; second: that they used something other than morality, namely natural or prescribed adorations, as means of religion. In respect of the first chiefly the Greeks and Romans, in respect of the second Christians and others have erred. Thus theology is indeed easy, but the concept of religion difficult. The heathens worshipped subordinate deities to whom the government of the world was entrusted; the Christians the supreme Godhead. That was a theological, but no moral difference at all, except only contingently or *indirecte*.\nSocrates and Christ are heaven-wide distinguished from each other in morals (g According to human or divine demand). The first demanded it heavenly (valid for heaven), the other earthly (for human beings); yet with Socrates the universal principle is good; only the determination of the particular actions which are contained under the moral |\nprinciple was dependent on each one's private judgment and not precise or pure.\nThe ancients had no concept of holiness, i.e. of the moral purity of the heart; rather their [virtue] morality was virtue, i.e. strength, hence rough.\nChrist wanted to improve not theology but morality in respect of it, whereby then theology itself is also purified.",["“of morality of the Godhead and of the future life” renders von moralitaet der Gottheit und dem künftigen Leben, which can also be read as a list: morality, the Godhead and the future life.","“heaven-wide” renders himmelweit (“worlds apart”), keeping its echo in himmlisch; “rhymed together” renders zusammen reimen (“reconciled”).","“in respect of it” renders in Ansehung ihrer, which can refer to theology or to morality, or be read “of them”."]],"reflm:4843":["Wisdom is the relation of one's choice to the whole (g union of one's aim with the whole) of all ends; consequently wisdom is a moral perfection, not mere natural perfection.\nWisdom is in itself good. Omniscience not immediately.\nJustice [reward] is not rewarding, but punishing; thus goodness is limited by wisdom. Goodness in proportion with morality. Not vindictive justice, although avenging. [*justitia*] *poenae vel vindicativae vel correctivae (hae vel animadversiones vel poenae exemplares)*.",["“vindictive justice” renders rachgerechtigkeit, “avenging” rächend.","*justitia*: “justice”; *poenae vel vindicativae vel correctivae (hae vel animadversiones vel poenae exemplares)*: “punishments either vindicative or corrective (these either reprimands or exemplary punishments)”."]],"reflm:4844":["Moral theology. 1. (g practical) Proof of the existence of God, 2. Properties (g as many as belong to religion (g Divine morality)).\nThe all is good (g *universitas*) — best world, but not on that account everything good.\nEvil is also positive from freedom, but together with the punishments it is in turn a part of the good.",["“Properties” renders Eigenschaften; *universitas*: “the universe, the whole”; “Evil” renders Das Böse."]],"reflm:4845":["The good is also from us; but because it rests on the agreement with the order of nature, it is also from God. Chiefly because the true moral good rests merely on faith in God.\nNot all utilities are at the same time ends, except those that ensue according to the order of nature and not freedom.\nUnconditioned decree in respect of morality.\nDestiny (*praedestination*) in respect of the occurrences of nature.\nEvil is not from God, because it rests in freedom, hence not on the divine determination. (g It is contrary to the order of nature, hence to God.)\nOnly good conduct can entitle one to the hope that God will supplement extraordinarily.",["“agreement” renders Einstimung; “moral” in “moral good” renders sittlich; “decree” renders Rathschlus.","“Destiny” renders Verhängnis; *praedestination*: “predestination”."]],"reflm:4846":["God is not at all the (g determining) *auctor* of our actions, but indeed the occasioning one of the good ones.",["*auctor*: “author”."]],"reflm:4847":["Tetens: Philosophische Versuche über die menschliche Natur und ihre Entwickelung vol. I (Leipzig 1777) p. 19\nRight margin, addition to »Search«, line 2 from the top:\nTucker.",["The first two lines are the editors' description of where the note stands in Kant's copy of Tetens."]],"reflm:4848":["4847. p. 131.\nRight margin, beside the words »man diess letzte« to the end of the paragraph »genennet werden soll«:\nthe leaving out of the determinations of an action can, before all comparing, make a universal representation.",["“before all comparing” renders vor allem Vergleichen, which can also be read “above all, comparing”.","The first two lines are the editors' description of where the note stands in Kant's copy of Tetens; the quoted words are Tetens'."]],"reflm:4849":["p. I [also: XXIII 471,3-473,7]:\nThe end of metaphysics: [the restrin] 1. to make out the origin of synthetic cognition a priori. 2. to have insight into the restricting conditions of the empirical use of our reason. 3. To show the independence of our reason from these conditions, hence the possibility of the absolute use. 4. Thereby |\nto extend our use of reason beyond the bounds of the sensible world, although only negatively, i.e. to remove the hindrance which reason itself (from principles of its empirical use [makes]) makes. 5. To show [chief] the condition of the absolute unity of it, so that it can be a [princ] complete *principium* of practical unity, i.e. harmony with the sum of all ends.\n(g The same principles of amplification are in turn negative in respect of the empirical use, where nothing but nature holds.)\nThe dogmatic use of our reason beyond the bound of (g possible) experiences cannot be objectively determining, and no new synthesis takes place, but is only a harmony of the theoretical with the practical unity, since the practical use beyond the bounds of the pragmatic, hence also beyond the present world, is led according to the analogy of the empirical use, but [with] in relation to conditions of a complete unity, [is] and thereby our business of reason is completed *a parte priori* and *posteriori*.\n(g Liberation of the unity of reason from the limitations of its empirical use makes the transcendental one possible.)\nBecause the amplification of reason here is merely negative, yet the absolute unity of the cognition of objects in general and of all its ends [must] (free from all restriction of sensibility) is required for the absolute spontaneity of reason, the amplification is practically necessary.\nReason is the faculty of the absolute unity of our cognitions.\np. II:\nThe principles of the completion of our cognition, i.e. of the (g of the absolute unity of the use of reason) absolute whole of it, are synthesis of reason.\nThey contain conditions of wisdom, i.e. of harmony with the sum of all our ends.\nWe complete only through the independent, thus not through sensibility.\nThe determination of all objects through mere reason is thus the completion of our cognition of the understanding (s *in progressu* of my existence).\n1. In respect of the self-cognition of reason. Completion *in progressu*.\na. I belong in a universe.\nb. am simple.\nc. Free. intelligence.\nd. My existence is externally not dependent (g on the body) nor contingent.\n(s among empirical principles belongs this one: The manner of existence of all things of the world is contingent, only the *ens originarium* exists in every sense in a necessary manner.)\nHere regard myself not as soul, but intelligence. The synthesis is here merely negative, namely to separate the conditions of sensibility from me as intelligence.\nAnd the ground of this synthesis is the freedom of reason from the restricting conditions of sensibility, which is a negative *principium* of morality, thus of wisdom.\n2. Completion *in regressu* from the conditioned to the unconditioned.\nThere is an *ens originarium*,\na. which is all-sufficient and unique,\nb. Simple,\nc. free cause (intelligence),\nd. Necessary according to its nature.\nThese are the conditions of the complete unity of all objects and hence cognitions. This unity, however, is the condition of the harmony of everything practical.\nThese cognitions are not dogmatic, but only a liberation of the (g absolute) unity of the use of reason in the theoretical and practical from the conditions of the empirical use, in order to establish [according to its analogy] principles of the pure practical use.\n––––––––––\nReason is free from the conditions of sensibility and must be it in the practical. The continuation of the function of reason |\nup to complete unity beyond the limiting conditions of sensibility.\n––––––––––\n(s The concepts of the unity of reason, e.g. of the absolute whole — ground, cannot be represented *in concreto* according to conditions of empirical cognition. But they also do not go to the sensible world, for that is no object of pure reason, but to the world of understanding, which lies at the ground of the former.)",["“amplification” renders Erweiterung, “extend” ausdehnen; “harmony” renders Zusammenstimmung; “bound(s)” renders both Grenze and Grentze.","“principles” renders principien, but Grundsätze in “empirical principles”; “in every sense” renders in allem Verstande, literally “in every understanding”.","“Here regard myself” renders Hie betrachte mich, which has no subject; the deleted “[is]” renders [wird], whose verb returns at the end of the sentence."]],"reflm:4850":["p. I [also: XXIII 488,19-25]:\nThat which makes space into an object of outer sense is matter. That which makes time into an object of inner sense is sensation and perception combined with consciousness. Just as the moving forces of matter striving toward the center of a cosmic body, so likewise they act toward the periphery, striving up from it toward the circumference, in that they do not cancel each other, but reciprocally penetrate.",["The first clause (Sowie … hinstrebend) has no finite verb.","“cosmic body” renders Weltkörper; “periphery” renders Umfang, elsewhere “domain”; “circumference” renders Umkreis."]],"reflm:4851":["22. p. I, II:\np. I:\n( g Whether the concepts are merely *educta* or *producta*.* preformation and epigenesis )\n*(g *producta* either through physical (g empirical) influence or through [gel] consciousness of the formal constitution of our sensibility and understanding on the occasion of experience, hence *producta a priori* after all, not *a posteriori*.)\nThe doctrine of *ideis connatis* leads to enthusiasm. *acqvisitae* are *a priori* or *a posteriori acqvisitae*, the former are not always intellectual. Thus the division of cognition into sensitive and intellectual is not the first, but rather into that *a priori* or *a posteriori*. The former either sensible or intellectual.\n(g The study of the object is dogmatic or skeptical, thus that of the subject either physiological or critical. Critique separates 1. the |\npure from the empirical cognitive faculty, 2. sensibility from the understanding.)\nPlato took all cognition *a priori* for intellectual. Leibniz too, and thus they did not know the sensible character of space and time. Leibniz explains it also as intellectual, but confused.\n––––––––––\nSynthetic cognition *a priori* is possible only under the principle that all relation of representations to an object and determination of the concept of it is nothing other than the representation of the necessary combination of them in one consciousness. Representations, however, cannot be combined in one consciousness if they are not regarded [themselves] as belonging to one *datum* (as object).\np. II:\n(g Metaphysics of nature and morals.)\n[See image below table for correct format]\n*Metaphysica*\n(g *Generalis*)\n(g *Specialis*)\ngeneral:\nparticular:\n[to] reason and its concepts\nto from reason\nthemselves make up the object.\ndistinct\n(g Transcendental philosophy)\nobjects applied.\nCritique of\nOntology\n(g in the world.)\n(g world itself and\npure\nOf experience\noutside it)\nreason\n(g *physiologia rationalis*\n)\nIdeas\nimmanent\n(g transcendent)\n*Physica rationalis*\n*Psychologia rationalis*\n*Cosmologia*\n*Theologia*\n(g Method: dogmatic or critical. (s Physiological: Locke.))\n(g for the last either: how we arrive at the principles and concepts, or: what they contain, and: how they are possible.)\n(g cognition is called transcendental with regard to its origin, transcendent with regard to the object, which can be encountered in no experience.)\n(g From empirical principles one cannot arrive at transcendent ones, and yet these are the [vorn] real end of metaphysics.)\nWhy not empirical psychology?\nThe *conceptus intellectuales* are *acqvisiti* (g but not from the senses). For [the] we acquire the forms of the bodies that we make out of clay, hence the bricks, although we take the clay from the earth.\nParts: 1. Ontology, cosmology and *theologia rationalis*; these two: 1. *transscendentalis*, 2. *naturalis*. *Cosmologia naturalis* has for its object objects of the senses, outer or inner. and the principles empirical or rational.",["“p.” renders S. (Seite); the table on p. II is given line by line in the transcription's order, which does not follow its printed layout.","“Enthusiasm” renders Schwärmerey; “cognitive faculty” Erkenntnis-Vermögen; “object” renders both obiect and, once, Gegenstände.","[gel], [vorn] are deleted word-beginnings, left untranslated; [themselves] renders the deleted sich."]],"reflm:4852":["Philosophy empirical or rational. The former physics, the latter *metaphysica*. The latter either *metaphysica generalis* (transcendental philosophy) or *specialis*.",[]],"reflm:4853":["Metaphysics treats not of objects, but of cognitions.",[]],"reflm:4854":["*primum principuum conceptuum est ens, iudiciorum est principium contradictionis.*",["Latin: “the first principle of concepts is being, of judgments the principle of contradiction.”","principuum (for principium) stands in the source."]],"reflm:4855":["All philosophy is either *rationalis* (not logic) or *empirica*. The former: whose *principia* are not from experience. The *rationalis* is either *pura* or *applicata*. The former *transscendentalis*, the latter *metaphysica*; or: the former *metaphysica generalis*, the latter *specialis*. The *metaphysica specialis* has for its ground either sensation or feeling. The former the ground (*materia*) of appearance, the latter of the desires. The former metaphysics of nature, the latter of morals. The former either *physiologia rationalis* or *theologia naturalis*, *physica generalis* or *cosmologia specialis*, *psychologia rationalis* or *physica rationalis*.",["“Morals” renders Sitten; “desires” Begierden."]],"reflm:4856":["In every relation in which we want to make something comprehensible, both members must are known. Now the object is [known the] unknown, but sensation known. Thus it cannot be made comprehensible. On the other hand sensation and the feeling of pleasure are both in ourselves, thus it is determined through itself and comprehensible.",["“Must are known” keeps the source's müssen … bekannt sind, where seyn would be expected.","“Comprehensible” renders verständlich; “it cannot be made comprehensible”: sie may refer to sensation or to the relation."]],"reflm:4857":["Pleasure and displeasure alone make up the absolute, because they are life itself.",[]],"reflm:4858":["Transcendental philosophy requires first of all critique (g to distinguish it from the empirical). 2. Discipline. 3. Canon. 4. Architectonic.",[]],"reflm:4859":["Origin of transcendental concepts 1. *per intuitionem mysticam*. 2. (g *influxum*) *sensitivum*.3. *per praeformationem*. 4. *per epigenesin intellectualem*. (g *intellectualia* intuitive or discursive.)\nThe end of metaphysics is God and a future world.\nepicurus nothing *a priori*.",["Latin: “through mystical intuition”, “sensitive (influence)”, “through preformation”, “through intellectual epigenesis”."]],"reflm:4860":["The imputations of Epicurus which represent his doctrines as absurd deserve no belief. The useful aim of philosophical history consists in the prefiguring of good models and the presentation of instructive transgressions, likewise in the cognition of the natural progress of reason from ignorance (not from gross error) to cognition. If someone tells me very absurd opinions of a person whom I have already cognized as acute through a single sample, I do not believe him. his expression has perhaps been incautious or has not been understood, just as I do not believe the slanders of someone in whom I have perceived an unsuspicious illusion of uprightness. What does it help me to know it? It is ugly to act as an accuser.",["“Imputations of Epicurus”: des Epicurs may mean imputations made against Epicurus or by him.","“Illusion” renders Schein, here perhaps rather “appearance” or “show”; “prefiguring” renders Vorbildung."]],"reflm:4861":["Logic considered as propaedeutic is good for beginners, but as architectonic only for masters.",[]],"reflm:4862":["Plato's doctrine of the ideas was supposed to serve to prevent us from seeking from empirical *principia* that which can have its sources and archetype in mere reason, namely true perfection. But from things [which] from which what has served merely as means, to seek out the idea of constitution (not of ends), is giddy, fanatical.",["The second sentence is broken in the source (von Dingen, [welche] davon was blos zum Mittel gedient hat) and is kept so.","“Archetype” renders Urbild; “giddy” renders schwindelicht."]],"reflm:4863":["Organic or spiritual intuiting, the former through the body.\nThat our soul without body, as spirit, intuits other things, i.e. externally, is an overstepping of the limitations of the *dati*. For the soul we cognize only as the object of inner sense and the body as the means of the outer. Our intuiting is physical and not mystical; the physical is not pneumatic, but organic.",["“Intuiting” renders the verbal noun Anschauen; “object” renders Gegenstand."]],"reflm:4864":["It is necessary and certain that every true *paradoxon* find resistance in the beginning, in order to be sifted and tested and thereafter |\nto be all the more durable. just as certain foods must be repugnant in the beginning in order thereafter to please all the longer.",[]],"reflm:4865":["Metaphysics is no *organon*, but canon of reason, a ground not of doctrine, but of discipline, not of dogmatic but of critical cognition, not to augment cognitions but to ward off errors, not of the object but of the rules of the subject, not the mother of religion but its bulwark, not of objective but of subjective use. The important fundamental truths of morality and religion are grounded on the natural use of reason, which is a use according to the analogy of the empirical use and reaches up to the boundary of the world *a priori* and *posteriori*, insofar as it is the boundary, and thus to the bordering, but not beyond it. This natural use is not free from aberrations of speculation, it produces a belief and no knowledge.",["“Doctrine” renders doctrin (Latinate), not Lehre; “bulwark” renders Schutzwehr.","“Insofar as it is the boundary”: sie may refer to the world or to the boundary.","“Bordering” renders Angrenzung."]],"reflm:4866":["M IX:\nLocke a physiologist of reason, origin of concepts. He committed the error that he took the occasion for arriving at these concepts, namely experience, for the source. nevertheless he made use of them beyond the boundary of experience.\nWolff was an artist of reason, he made use of it and did not inquire at all into the sources. dogmatic, not critical.\nLambert analyzed reason, but the critique is still lacking. Crusius (everything that I can think is possible) assumed innate principles (although not Platonic ideas); but since they perhaps |\nM X: |\ncan be only princi|ples\nof the empirical use of the understanding, he was not certain whether he could use them beyond the boundary of experience as well.",["“Artist of reason” renders Vernunftkünstler.","“He made use of it” (ihrer): may refer to reason or to the concepts."]],"reflm:4867":["The use of the understanding is either mystical or logical; the latter metaphysical or physiological. Aristotle and epicurus.",[]],"reflm:4868":["Plato expounded mystical *intellectualia*, Aristotle logical *intellectualia*; the latter erred in this, that he said they lay also in the senses. For the concept of cause never lay in sensible intuition.",[]],"reflm:4869":["Chief rule: that all principles in general are only of empirical use, hence reason has altogether and entirely only rules of use with regard to experience.",[]],"reflm:4870":["Whether the use of reason with regard to the boundary-concepts of the world is transcendent? Whence then do the transcendent *qvaestiones* come?",["“Boundary-concepts” renders Grentzbegriffe."]],"reflm:4871":["Despair is either faint-hearted or foolhardy; in the former there is appeal to sound understanding, either to take metaphysics from it or to throw it away by means of it; in the second |\nappeal to mystical intuition and immediate endowment of the understanding. In skepticism there is no state of rest.",["“Endowment” renders Dotirung.","“Sound understanding” renders gesunden Verstand."]],"reflm:4872":["M XII:\nAll analytic judgments have for their ground the proposition of identity and contradiction. The synthetic have as many *axiomata* as *conceptus synthetici* are given *per intellectus naturam*. These *conceptus synthetici* are the grounds whereby the understanding can attain concepts of experience of things [If], and thus the *principia synthetica* hold only with regard to the laws of experience, hence *a posteriori*, and are practically true. But *in abstracto* they are only subjectively valid.\nM XI:\n(g Judgments of experience)",["*per intellectus naturam*: “through the nature of the understanding”.","[If] renders the deleted Wenn."]],"reflm:4873":["Across the inner margin:\nTranscendental philosophy considers not the objects, but the human mind according to the sources from which cognition *a priori* descends in it, and the boundaries. Hence pure mathematics is not a part of transcendental philosophy, but the sources from which it arises in the mind indeed are.",["“Mind” renders Gemüth; “objects” renders Gegenstände.","“Indeed are” supplies the verb the German leaves elliptical (wohl aber die Qvellen)."]],"reflm:4874":["In the method of philosophy (empirical) 2 pieces as boundaries. |\nUniversal laws and rules and the opposite of them, namely diversities in the singular. Hovers between senses and reason, *a posteriori* and *a priori*.",["“Hovers” has no expressed subject in the source (Schwebt)."]],"reflm:4875":["One cannot demonstrate the existence of God to anyone who is indifferent with regard to morality, and one need not demonstrate it to him who is decided for the moral laws.",["“Morality” renders sittlichkeit.","“Need not” renders darf … nicht, which may also mean “may not”."]],"reflm:4876":["Misological maxims give a logical anarchy.",[]],"reflm:4877":["A science of the understanding which bears on objects in general can speak only of the rules of the use of the same, either of use in general or of the use of the pure understanding. In the latter case it is transcendental logic. As many as there are logical moments, so many transcendental elements are there (categories).",["“Of the same” (derselben) may refer to the understanding or to the objects."]],"reflm:4878":["If this writing needs favor in order to get through, then it may as well be rejected.",[]],"reflm:4879":["Maxim: that there be great manifoldness in the application, i.e. the closer determination, but yet unity of the rule.",["“Manifoldness” renders Mannigfaltigkeit."]],"reflm:4880":["The steps in metaphysics have hitherto been in vain. One has discovered nothing in it. Nevertheless one cannot give it up. subject instead of object.\nOf the incompetence of the understanding with regard to the objects that are above the senses.\nOur pure reason is in itself and naturally dialectical.\nPhilosophy of pure reason. 1. subjective, 2. objective part; the former: transcendental philosophy, consideration of pure reason itself; the latter: cognition of the objects. The last: metaphysics of nature and of morals.",["“Discovered” renders erfunden.","“Object” renders both obiect and Gegenstände."]],"reflm:4881":["Instinct drives the philosopher to systematic treatises, and that has its great use in the extension of cognition. Talent restricts him to single pieces, and that would have great use in correctness, if only there were a means to unite and preserve such. The capacities of philosophers are different in this. The one can consider everything on the large scale and in general or draw out rules, the other better notices the parts and diversities. all want to make systems.",["“Extension” renders Erweiterung."]],"reflm:4882":["The appearance of *coniugation* is a subsumption of a given representation under the universal capacity to dispose sensations. The function of this capacity is the concept of the understanding, and the conditions of the same make the rules, namely of the transition from one representation to another. Thus nothing can be perceived except under the presupposition that it stands under a rule.\nThe concept of ground is no appearance, but a function of the mind, under which everything must be able to be subsumed, consequently under rules. Observation is only [a], that a case is given. Cognition is the subsumption under the function and its rules.\nIf space were nothing subjective: how then should I arrive at cognitions of it *a priori*? how should this cognition also fit objects.\nJust so, were ground nothing subjective: how should I come upon it *a priori*?\nHuman reason progresses with pleasure in the extension of its cognitions over the world of sense and the moral world, but upwards it grows dizzy.",["“Conjugation” (coniugation) is kept in Kant's form; “capacity” renders fähigkeit; “mind” Gemüthes.","“The conditions of the same”: derselben may refer to the function or to the capacity."]],"reflm:4883":["*theologia naturalis*. According to the analogy of the empirical use of reason.",[]],"reflm:4884":["The correctness of moral propositions can be cognized independently of theology and all sciences.",[]],"reflm:4885":["Metaphysics leads to certainty not in the dogmas of reason, but in the maxims of the same.",[]],"reflm:4886":["*dilemma practicum*. The denier of God acts imprudently if he stakes it on the existence of God, or like a villain.",["“If he stakes it on the existence of God” renders wenn ers wagt aufs Daseyn Gottes; the construction is elliptical in the source."]],"reflm:4887":["Bulwark of religion, critique and (g discipline.) canon of cognition and organon of wisdom. Bulwark of religion.",[]],"reflm:4888":["If one asks, not whether yet another proof of the existence of God could be invented in metaphysics, but whether not [the] of beings that are entirely distinct from the world, their nature could not be discovered, then etc etc. Otherwise, if one wants nothing but a proof for the already presupposed existence of God, then it is easily found. Proofs for moral propositions.",["'existence': Daseyn.","The construction 'ob nicht [das] von Wesen ... ihre Natur nicht könne entdeckt werden' is irregular, with a double negative; kept as it stands."]],"reflm:4889":["Metaphysics is the cognition of nature a priori, whose object is at least given through the senses; transcendental philosophy is pure cognition a priori.",["'object': obiect (the Latinate form).","'cognition of nature': Naturerkentnis."]],"reflm:4890":["Cognition a priori is opposed to the empirical; philosophy about the same is transcendental philosophy. Everything that we cognize a priori with regard to the objects of experience lies therein.",["'objects': Gegenstande."]],"reflm:4891":["The more recent philosophers have believed that, because they can make use of their powers quite well without being acquainted with them and their laws, where they come from, e.g. mathematics, so it would also be with regard to the first principles of reason, with regard to quality. (One born blind who has seen a yellow piece of money can represent to himself a hundred, but not yet a white one.)",["'principles': principien (the Latinate word, not Grundsätze).","'quality': qvalitaet.","'being acquainted with': kennen."]],"reflm:4892":["The most important thing is that, before one ventures a doctrine of pure reason, first a critique of it must have been undertaken. But critiques require knowledge of the sources, and reason must know itself. To this investigation one is driven only after long errors.",["'doctrine': doctrin (the Latinate word, not Lehre).","'knowledge': Kentnisse; 'know': kennen."]],"reflm:4893":["Locke an influxionist, at the same time a physiologist of the understanding. Lambert an analyst and architectonic. Wolff a mere dogmatist and mathematical head. Crusius a *praestabilist* of reason. (g denied the subordination of all principles under the *principium contradictionis* and yet wanted to specify an origin, can specify no mark, which then are implanted principles and which are foisted in.) Empiricists of pure reason. Sound understanding. Misologists. Our end of metaphysics. (*In sensibus nihil esse veri.*) Worth.\n1. Distinction of the *phaenomena* and *noumena*.*\n2. Origin of these latter. Innate, mystical; or acquired, logical. Plato, Leibniz. Aristotle, Locke. Abstracted *formulae* from the senses or not.\n*(g distinction of the objects or of the cognition of them.)",["'principles': Grundsätze; 'foisted in': unterschoben.","'Sound understanding': Gesunder Verstand, literally 'healthy understanding'.","*In sensibus nihil esse veri*: 'that there is nothing of truth in the senses'."]],"reflm:4894":["Plato the intellectual philosopher. Mystical.\nEpicurus the empirical philosopher.\nAristotle the [int] bricht ab.\nLocke had the advantage that, since he did not recognize the *intellectualia* as *connata*, he sought the origin.\nInnate intuitions or logical principles, Crusius.\nThe *intellectualia* of Plato were innate, because they are *intuitus*, those of Aristotle were acquired as *conceptus*. But they are not borrowed from the senses, otherwise Locke could not use them beyond the boundary of appearances. Crusius has *conceptus connatos* or *axiomata connata per praestabilitam cognitionem*.",["'bricht ab' (breaks off) is the AA's note, kept as it stands.","'principles': principien.","*conceptus connatos ... cognitionem*: 'innate concepts or innate axioms through a preestablished cognition'."]],"reflm:4895":["Metaphysics is the philosophy of the pure understanding and transcendental is the critique of the pure understanding. Metaphysics proper. *organon*.",["'transscendentalis' has no noun of its own; it may take up 'philosophia' or 'Metaphysica'. It could also be read with the first clause ('of the pure and transcendental understanding'), leaving 'est Critica' without a subject.","'understanding': intellectus."]],"reflm:4896":["transcendental\nAnalytic is where the understanding considers itself.\n—\n—\n—\ndialectic, where it speaks a priori of objects. Illusion.\n(g Logic of truth and of illusion.)",["'where': da, which can also be read as 'since'.","'objects': obiecten; 'illusion': Schein."]],"reflm:4897":["There is no transcendental doctrine; consequently the *organon* of pure reason is a science which shows the use of pure reason with regard to the empirical in general; thus all philosophy of pure reason is either the critique or the *organon* of it. The first is transcendental philosophy, the second metaphysics.",["'doctrine': doctrin."]],"reflm:4898":["Crosswise on the inner margin:\nWe now no longer need to dissemble in metaphysics (g nor conceal anything), we can present the objections of reason against theology |\nboldly and unabashedly, indeed [it] strengthen them, in that we make no partisan distinction between them and the dogmatic speculations. For when we afterwards investigate them back to their sources, we discover the misunderstanding and again unite reason with religion.",["'[it]': the deleted ihr, which may be a dative ('to it') or the start of another word."]],"reflm:4899":["Crosswise on the inner margin:\nIt seems, to be sure, that nothing is more contrary to taste than metaphysics; but the ornaments that glitter on beauty lay at first in dark vaults, at least they pass only through the gloomy workshop of the artist.",[]],"reflm:4900":["I do not occupy myself with the evolution of concepts like Tetens (all actions through which concepts are generated), nor with the analysis like Lambert, but merely with their objective validity. I stand in no competition with these men.",["'evolution': Evolution, an unfolding."]],"reflm:4901":["Tetens investigates the concepts of pure reason merely subjectively (human nature), I objectively. The former analysis is empirical, the latter transcendental.",[]],"reflm:4902":["All cognitions are philosophical, i.e. belonging to philosophy as materials, which are cognitions from concepts. Philo|sophy\nitself, however, as the legislation of human reason, consists of metaphysics and morals. The rest belongs to erudition.",["'erudition': gelehrsamkeit, learning.","'morals': Moral."]],"reflm:4903":["Pure mathematical theorems are methods for determining magnitudes. Here the method is bricht ab.",["'bricht ab' (breaks off) is the AA's note; the sentence is cut off after 'ist'."]],"reflm:4904":["Transcendental freedom is the necessary hypothesis of all rules*, consequently of all use of the understanding. One ought to think thus and so etc etc. consequently this action must be free, i.e. not already determined of itself (subjectively), but have only an objective ground of determination.\n*(g It is the [condition of the] property of beings in whom the consciousness of a rule is the ground of their actions.)",["'property': Eigenschaft (not Beschaffenheit)."]],"reflm:4905":["Necessity, freedom and infinity are the three stumbling blocks of reason, against which three rationalizing principles are, on which the skeptical use of it is grounded.\n[The question of] substance, action and composition;\nmodality, relation and quantity.",["'stumbling blocks': steine des Anstoßes, literally 'stones of offense'.","'against which': wieder welche (= wider); 'are': seyn, possibly subjunctive ('would be').","'rationalizing': vernünftelnde."]],"reflm:4906":["All categories concern objects of the (s inner) intuition, (s of the activity. Repetition) of the understanding, of reason. The first is merely consciousness.",[]],"reflm:4907":["There is no science of pure intuition except mathematics and no use of reason which would be apodictic and at the same time evident, except mathematics with regard to objects and morals with regard to actions. All other inquiry is inquiry into nature.",["'inquiry into nature': Naturforschung."]],"reflm:4908":["Whether sensible representations according to Leibniz are merely confused representations of the understanding. The understanding does not intuit.",[]],"reflm:4909":["Whether through the representation of space we stand in relation with all creatures |\nin general. This is feasible, even if space is something subjective, which however is connected with all our powers and through which all creatures are combined with one another. but then certain creatures, as spirits, will be represented only mediately with regard to space.",["'connected': Verknüpft; 'combined': verbunden.","'This is feasible': Dieses geht an, also 'this is admissible'."]],"reflm:4910":["If the doctrines of the existence of God (g of freedom) and of another world rest on secure principles of the practical use of our reason (*dilemmatis practici Maior*), then metaphysics may well fall away as a dogmatic *principium* of them; it nonetheless serves critically, to hold off pseudometaphysics, and indeed both sensibility from pure concepts of the understanding, and dialectic from pure principles.",["'principles' first renders principien, at the end Grundsatzen.","*dilemmatis practici Maior*: 'the major premise of the practical dilemma'.","'of them': derselben, which may refer to the doctrines or to reason."]],"reflm:4911":["It is a great advantage if one can make science technical, i.e. bring it under functions of the imagination and divide it; e.g. table of categories. The technical is merely mechanical* or architectonic. The former a posteriori, the latter a priori**.Art and science.\n*(s to divide by eye-measure)\n**(s according to a principle)",["'eye-measure': Augenmaas, judgment by eye.","'principle': princip."]],"reflm:4912":["Metaphysics is not for children and youths, but for men. It is a kind of revision of reason. One must already know it in order to be able to estimate its validity. The theologian needs |\nno metaphysics; the simpler, the more by the empirical principles: the more useful. The concept of the divine perfections he can cognize without metaphysics. Only at the conclusion of his studies does he need it. Logic and morals make the beginning. The practical method of teaching morals also comes last. The logician, metaphysician and moralist at once first thereby obtains true importance. — — probably no examination is possible in it.\n(g one will not take this judgment amiss from a man who, in that he is paid to teach metaphysics publicly, wishes to convince himself that he is after all [good for something] good for something.)",["'morals' renders both Moral ('Logic and morals') and Sitten ('teaching morals').","'the simpler, the more by the empirical principles': je einfältiger, je mehr an den empirischen Grundsatzen; einfältiger may also mean 'more simple-minded'.","The deleted [irgend wozu] and the kept zu etwas gut both mean 'good for something'."]],"reflm:4913":["Academy of sciences: board of revision. Encourages through honors all writings that it improves. In it reports are read out.",["'board of revision': revisionscollegium."]],"reflm:4914":[", crosswise on the inner margin.\nM XVII:\nThere are pragmatic laws of reason, namely where one ought to follow no other rule than that according to which it is possible to make use of reason. These are rules of the power of judgment, for making use of speculative reason; |\nM XVIII: |\ne.g. the sight of miracles goes in its probative force beyond all reason, but not the narration. Hence it is a rule for reason, i.e. its use, not to attach belief to the narrations of miracles, because, even if something true has been introduced through miracles, it ought afterwards to be believed not for the sake of this narration, but from itself, because otherwise new narrations could overturn it again.",["'to make use of reason': der Vernunft zu gebrauchen, with the genitive.","'belief': glauben."]],"reflm:4915":["a priori *secundum qvid* or\n— —\n*simpliciter*.",["*secundum qvid*: 'in a certain respect'; *simpliciter*: 'absolutely'."]],"reflm:4916":["Through mere pure concepts of the understanding no object at all can be determinately cognized. Meanwhile these are nonetheless the ways of cognizing the object, not as it appears, but is.",["'determinately cognized': bestimmt erkannt, which may also be read 'cognized as determined'.","'ways': Arten."]],"reflm:4917":["It is not enough to know what the representations contain in themselves, nor to which occasions and conditions they owe their origin, but in which faculties and capacities they have their seat.",["'faculties and capacities': Vermögen und Fähigkeiten.","'to know': wissen."]],"reflm:4918":["Difference between Copernicus and Tycho.",[]],"reflm:4919":["The method makes up everything, not how one has thought, but ought to think. To know philosophizing or to know (g a) philosophy. Philosophy must chiefly consist in the sources of cognition a priori.",["'To know philosophizing': philosophiren wissen, i.e. knowing how to philosophize."]],"reflm:4920":["Mathematics proceeds only with concepts that it can make intuitive a priori. Philosophy can only subsume under pure concepts and never make them intuitive.",["'intuitive': anschauend, literally 'intuiting'."]],"reflm:4921":["Idea is always a concept of reason, according to which the manifold can be ordered. There is thus a *systema naturae* either arranged according to an idea (which, however, is only didactic) or according to a *dessin*.",["*dessin*: French, 'design'."]],"reflm:4922":["That the radius can be carried around in the circle 6 times cannot be derived from the concept of the circle.",[]],"reflm:4923":["That the pure principles are of empirical use with regard to experience and not of transcendent use with regard to ideal objects. They hold in relation to the way in which boundaries for our practical cognition are to be determined by the speculative.",["'by the speculative': von der speculativen, which may also be read 'from the speculative', i.e. boundaries separating practical cognition from speculative."]],"reflm:4924":["Dogmatic transcendent doctrine is rationalizing, namely reason overstepping its |\nlimitations. The disciplinarian of the rationalizers is the moral teacher or even the mocker.",["'rationalizing': Vernünfteley; 'rationalizers': Vernünftler.","'limitations': Schranken; 'disciplinarian': Zuchtmeister."]],"reflm:4925":["Philosophy is the legislation (nomothetic) of human reason. The art of reason is the doctrine of the skill of reason according to rules (not laws).\nThe philosopher is in cognition just as much an ideal as the wise man in the use of his free will. He is the model of all use of reason.",["'art of reason': Vernunftkunst."]],"reflm:4926":["We can appeal to common human understanding when we want to determine the rule and the incentive of our conduct, thus that which actually concerns us. But if we want to ascend further than our duty concerns [then] and to climb too high in theoretical assertions, then we appeal in vain to common human understanding.",["'incentive': Triebfeder.","'climb too high': uns versteigen, to overreach oneself."]],"reflm:4927":["Philosophy is actually nothing other than a practical knowledge of human beings; everything else is the knowledge of nature and an art of reason; but the magisterial dignity over human reason and all powers, insofar as they are subject to it, belongs to philosophy. O! it is to be regretted that we let this significance dwindle. Without such a distinguishing term this knowledge is not set apart from others, and there is no actual doctrine of philosophy.",["'knowledge': Kenntnis/Kentnis.","'magisterial': obrigkeitliche, belonging to an authority or magistrate.","'significance': Bedeutung."]],"reflm:4928":["Crosswise on the inner margin: M XIX:\nAll metaphysical axioms that are not apprehending (exemplar) |\nare called petitions and are hypotheses of pure reason, which have no ground in themselves. For all hypotheses are physiological, namely the *datum* of their possibility is given, but only the assumption of it *in casu* is postulated. Nevertheless I can make transcendent hypotheses, in order to oppose to equally giddy |\nM XX: |\nsomething, so that *petitiones* may hold the balance to the petitions. e.g. Perhaps [is] are the pure spiritual natures the only true thing in the world. All this belongs to the negative means of securing practical propositions of reason against the usurpations of dogmatic speculation; they are retortions or refutations by analogy.\nM XIX, between the lines of the preface:\nsynthetic propositions without proof are either axioms or petitions or hypotheses. With the latter the possibility is known beforehand, only the position arbitrary; with the second the possibility is assumed.",["'apprehending': apprehendent, a doubtful word; '(exemplar)' is as printed.","'petitions': petitionen (postulates); 'petitiones' is the Latin form in the source.","'equally giddy' (ebenso schwindelnden) has no noun after it."]],"reflm:4929":["We can absolutely not comprehend a first beginning from freedom, but just as little a first beginning without freedom, i.e. through chance or necessity of nature (g blind accident and blind fate).\nDialectic is artificial or natural; the thetic of the latter [is] contains the true subjective principles and consequences.",["'comprehend': begreifen.","'blind accident': blindes ohngefehr."]],"reflm:4930":["The illusion from lack of judgment is the semblance (the first illusion): *apparentia*. The illusion that conflicts with judgment and |\ndoes not yield to it is natural illusion: *species*. Of such a kind is transcendental illusion. One will never eradicate it.",["'illusion': Schein; 'semblance': Anschein."]],"reflm:4931":["To transcendent assertions are opposed not refutations, but counter-assertions.\nSeemingness is opposed to probability and remains even with the certainty of the opposite, is from the *vitio subreptionis*.",["'Seemingness': scheinbarkeit, also 'plausibility'.","*vitio subreptionis*: ablative of vitium subreptionis, 'fallacy of subreption'."]],"reflm:4932":["All synthetic principles are either anticipations of appearance or of experience, if they are objective; but if they are not the former, then they are subjective.\nPresumptions of reason are those where I assume that in which my reason can be completely exercised, e.g. unity of forces and causes.",["“the former” renders jenes (singular), presumably “objective”.","“Presumptions” renders Praesumtionen; “in which” renders wobey."]],"reflm:4933":["The soul is unity as object of inner sense, but from this I cannot infer that it is persistent as object of outer intuition.\nConversely, to infer from the synthetic but apodictic propositions of space that it is nothing objective, consequently an object of observation.\nThe empirical anticipations are not apodictic, consequently not axioms. The exponent and adjective predicates.",["“object” renders obiect in the first paragraph and Gegenstand in the second.","“exponent” renders exponirende (“expounding”); “adjective” renders adiective."]],"reflm:4934":["The Analytic contains that which to all transcendental cognition |\nis common, i.e. the mere concepts before all judgment, hence the elements of the same. Consequently there can as yet be no falsehood in them; it is therefore the logic of truth.\nJust as the logical analytic considers only cognition in general and thus gives the guideline of truth.\nThe transcendental concepts are of no hyperphysical use at all except *in subsidium* of the moral.\nEpicurus erred in this, that he made the maxims of reason into axioms, thus the subjective rules into objective ones. e.g. One must seek nothing but mechanical explanations, cite no grounds other than those which [themselves] can be exhibited through the course of nature.\nThe maxims of reason are grounded on the best use of it.\nEpicurus was a philosopher of method. He erected rather the necessary maxims of reason than axioms and theories of it. He wanted to set it within its proper boundaries, but was not dogmatic.",["*in subsidium*: “in aid”; “guideline” renders Richtschnur.","“it” in the last sentence renders sie, which may be reason or the maxims (“them … their”)."]],"reflm:4935":["The entire previous error seems to me to have been this, that one has wanted to proceed in metaphysics from the parts to the whole, indeed even while one has mixed in heterogeneous parts. But here it is only possible to begin from the whole in a completely unmixed cognition, and a single one must carry it out completely.",["“a single one” renders ein einziger (a single person); “it” renders dasselbe, which agrees with das Gantze (“the whole”)."]],"reflm:4936":["The contradictions and the conflict of the systems are still the only thing that has kept the human understanding in recent times, in matters of metaphysics, free from complete decay. Although they are indeed all dogmatic in the highest degree, they nevertheless take the place of the skeptics perfectly for one who views this game as a whole. On this account one can be indebted to a Crusius just as well as to a Wolff that, through the new paths which they struck out on, they at least |\nprevented, so that the understanding did not let its rights lapse in a stupid repose and the germ of a secure knowledge was still always preserved. Analyst and architectonic philosopher. In such a way the course of nature finally leads the beautiful but for the most part enigmatic order of the same itself through destructions to perfection. Even a *system de la Nature* is advantageous to philosophy.",["“prevented, so that … not” renders verhüteten, damit nicht; the double negation is Kant's.","“itself” renders selbst, which may also mean “even” (“even through destructions”); “of the same” renders derselben (nature).","*system de la Nature*: Kant's form of the title of d'Holbach's book; “knowledge” renders Kenntnis."]],"reflm:4937":["Across the inner margins. M XXII:\nIt is of the greatest importance to make a science of reason technical. The *logici* have [in va] attempted it in vain with their syllogistic as a factory. Only with regard to magnitudes have the inventors of the algorithm succeeded in it. Should it not be so also in the critique of pure reason, not for the extension, but the purification of cognitions? |\nM XXI: |\nThrough the technical method one can, in the designation, give each concept its function or rather express the *functiones* themselves in themselves and relative to one another. (Algebra expresses them only relative to one another, perhaps also so in the transcendental algorithm. The mistakes can thereby alone be prevented, and the overlooking.)",["*logici*: “logicians”; “[in va]” renders the broken-off [verg] (vergeblich?), replaced by umsonst.","“mistakes” renders Versehen, “overlooking” Uebersehen; the play on the two words is lost.","“purification” renders Läuterung; “designation” renders Bezeichnung."]],"reflm:4938":["The use of reason in geometry is intuitive with regard to the object, discursive with regard to the form; the form of algebra is not discursive, but technical.\nThe thetic is the dogmatic part, not objective, but the dogmatic grounds of method. Distinguishing of dogmatic illusion, i.e. principle of truth.\nThe mathematicians have believed, when they worked on the objects of pure reason, that they accomplished something excellent. But it is to be regretted that, if they take this cognition objectively, they work an ungrateful field. But in the critique of reason they can create the greatest utility. Holland.",["“illusion” renders Scheins; “principle” renders princip.","“object” renders obiect throughout."]],"reflm:4939":["Critical assertions are something entirely different from dogmatic ones; the latter are objective, the former subjective.",[]],"reflm:4940":["The philosophy of ignorance is very useful, but also difficult, because it must go as far as the sources of cognition.",[]],"reflm:4941":["He who says that there is a God says more than he knows, and he who says the opposite, likewise. No one knows that there is one, but we believe it.",[]],"reflm:4942":["Because metaphysics contains *quaestiones* which are immediately of concern and hang together with the highest ends, so breaks off.",["*quaestiones*: “questions”; “of concern” renders angelegentlich.","“breaks off” (bricht ab) is the AA's note that the Reflexion stops here."]],"reflm:4943":["If someone speaks through synthetic propositions *a priori*, then he speaks only of his own thoughts, e.g. that there is a necessary being etc:, namely that without this presupposition he would not have a complete concept of the existence of things. Or he speaks of conditions of the cognition of experience *a priori*, and then it is objective. e.g. every *compositum reale* has a force of the combination of all parts as its ground. Meanwhile these propositions are actually analytic.",["*compositum reale*: “real composite”; “existence” renders Daseyn; “combination” renders Verbindung.","*a priori* may go with “propositions” or “speaks”, and with “cognition of experience” or “conditions”, as in the German."]],"reflm:4944":["One cannot say that transcendent propositions are undecided. For without the condition for determining them they are not merely undetermined, but have no significance at all, e.g. absolutely necessary. Just as, when the pole makes up the zenith, it is not merely uncertain which quarter of the heavens we have there, but every quarter of the heavens, through which alone we can after all determine our course at sea, ceases.",["“undecided” renders unausgemacht; “significance” renders Bedeutung.","“quarter of the heavens” renders Himmelsgegend (cardinal direction)."]],"reflm:4945":["M XXII:\nThere can be no *quaestiones* of transcendental philosophy whose answer would be unknown to us. For if the predicate is not determined by the subject, then this signifies that the question in itself is nothing, because the predicate in this case has no significance at all, is not affirmative nor its *oppositum contrarium*. just as when I ask [which which] where I, when I am under the pole, should after all look for east. Our cosmological concepts have a significance only |\nM XXIII: |\nin the world, hence none with regard to the boundary or the whole of the world. The possibility of the sensible rests on the condition of that which is given; now neither the boundary nor the whole can be given to the senses.\nWhether the world is 100 million sun-diameters large, I do not know, because experience has not taught it. Whether it is infinite, I do not know, because I can know it through no experience at all.",["*quaestiones*: “questions”; *oppositum contrarium*: “contrary opposite”; “its” renders ihr, which may refer to the question.","“affirmative” renders beiahend; “sun-diameters” renders Sonnendicken (literally “sun-thicknesses”)."]],"reflm:4946":["If I assume that a concept *a priori* has an object, then I must also be able to cognize everything *a priori* of the same for which the concept contains the condition. There is thus nothing uncertain and undecided there, and nonetheless reason contains nothing other than conditions of empirical use, and thus all attempts of it that are transcendent are impossible and futile. The transcendent concepts are not concepts of objects. They are ideas, *ideae*.",["“of the same” renders von demselben, which may refer to the object or to the concept.","“object” renders obiect, “objects” Gegenständen; “undecided” renders unausgemacht."]],"reflm:4947":["Across the outer margin:\nIn metaphysics one must be subtle. For all this cognition is *a priori* and, without derivation from its first sources, uncertain.",[]],"reflm:4948":["Across the inner margin:\nOn the influence of honesty on insight and of the good heart on the spreading of cognition.",["“on” twice renders in die (“into”)."]],"reflm:4949":["If we regard nature as the continent of our cognitions, and our reason consists in the determination of the boundaries of the same, then we can cognize these no otherwise than insofar as we take in along with them that which makes the boundaries, the ocean that bounds it, which however we cognize only by the shore. namely God and the other world, which are necessarily considered as boundaries of nature, although distinguished from it and unknown for us.",["“of the same” renders derselben, which may refer to nature or to our cognitions; “it” after “bounds” renders sie, which may be nature, the cognitions or the boundaries.","“unknown for us” renders vor uns (= für uns) unbekannt."]],"reflm:4950":["One can gather together the whole of metaphysics in very few sheets, insofar as it is extending. The dissection or the elucidating can still be continued henceforth.",["“sheets” renders Bogen (printer's sheets); “extending” renders erweiternd, “elucidating” erläuternde, “dissection” Zergliederung."]],"reflm:4951":["M XXIII:\nIn transcendental cognitions (g or discursive cognitions) of pure reason no more than one proof is possible. Because everything must flow from the concept put forward. e.g. the existence of a necessary being from the concept of necessity.\nNot all concepts can be constructed. Only those of magnitude, because they make up the synthesis of intuition. But the dynamical, through which |\nM XXIV: |\nsomething that does not lie in intuition is found from what is given according to the law of transcendental association, requires empirical condition and cannot be given *a priori*.",["“the dynamical … requires” renders die dynamische … bedarf: the singular verb lets it be read as the dynamical synthesis rather than the dynamical concepts."]],"reflm:4952":["Sophistical dialectic is an art of illusion. Philosophical, a science of the resolution of illusion, and has a paedeutic part, which contains the criterion of truth, and a skeptical one, which indicates the sources of illusion and secures truth against it.",["“illusion” renders Schein; “paedeutic” renders paedevtischen (“instructive”)."]],"reflm:4953":["The belief that there is no God, no other world, is impossible, but not so the opinion (s or doubt and uncertainty).\nAgainst all philosophy of pure reason is misology and empiricism.\nThe empiricism of pure reason. Healthy understanding.\nThe doctrinal concept is either the realism or the formalism of pure reason. The latter permits only principles of the form of the use of our reason *a priori* with regard to experiences. Hence it permits neither to affirm nor to deny dogmatically beyond the boundary of experience. If religion and morality have other sources of cognition than merely speculative ones, then it shows the practical principles of reason as necessarily combined with theoretical *postulatis*. A *postulatum theoreticum*, however, is a necessary hypothesis of the agreement of theoretical and practical cognition. Then metaphysics withstands all objections of pure reason and shows how they are dialectical, i.e. the dialectic of the same.\nIf [it] the *dogmata cardinalia* are secured *in respectu practico*, although not *speculativo*, then metaphysics illustrates and defends.",["“Against” renders Wieder (= wider); “doctrinal concept” renders Lehrbegriff; “but not so the opinion” renders wohl aber die Meinung.","“The latter” renders Dieser (masculine, the formalism); “it” in the next two sentences renders sie (feminine), whose reference is open.","*postulatis*: “postulates”; *postulatum theoreticum*: “theoretical postulate”; *dogmata cardinalia in respectu practico … speculativo*: “cardinal dogmas in a practical … speculative respect”."]],"reflm:4954":["I have read excellent and acute thoughts, but they bring the science not a step further and are extinguished with the novelty. The cause is this, that there is properly as yet no science and the laying of the foundation has not yet taken place. As notions are related to insights, so acute thoughts to the doctrinal edifice.",["“notions” renders einfalle (Einfälle, “sudden ideas”); the clause Sowie sich einfalle Verhalten zu einsichten is ungrammatical as written.","“doctrinal edifice” renders Lehrgebäude."]],"reflm:4955":["Sensibility in human beings is not so very different from reason that both should not go only to the same objects, at least such as are represented in the same way, notwithstanding that the one is valid for the objects with regard to all possible positions of sensibility, the other not.",["“for” renders vor (= für); “positions” renders Stellungen.","“the one … the other” (die eine … die andre) is left open between sensibility and reason."]],"reflm:4956":["Two contradictory propositions whose hypothesis is false can both be false; e.g. that God moves in space in eternity, also not in eternity.",[]],"reflm:4957":["The citing of books is not necessary in a system of transcendental philosophy in the draft, as little as in a geometry. Unanimous judgments of others furnish a ground of proof only where it is not a matter of the rule, but of its application, i.e. the power of judgment, and where not everything must be derived from one idea, but rather the concept from a multitude of compared observations.",["“in the draft” renders beym Entwurf; “ground of proof” renders Beweisgrund."]],"reflm:4958":["We can make metaphysical hypotheses if we can test them on the appearances. e.g. Of diversity of matters, which is original; but not transcendental hypotheses.\nThe unity of the system is in all hypotheses a touchstone, in the dogmatic, however, a *principium* of correctness.\nThe boundary of appearances belongs along [with the] to the appearance, but the thing which makes the boundary is outside them. Consequently we have cause to infer a being as the cause of the world and a future world, but no means of determining them.",["*principium*: “principle”; “touchstone” renders Probirstein, “test” probiren.","“[with the]” renders the struck [der] after mit."]],"reflm:4959":["M XXV:\nNow it is ridiculous to ask: what do you have for an opinion of the community of the soul with the body, the nature of a spirit, the creation in time. I opine nothing at all about this. But: what sort of origin these |\nthoughts have in the human understanding, in that it |\nM XXVI: |\ngoes beyond its boundary. Whence this question is necessary and with regard to the object can only be answered subjectively. That I know, and there I am above all opinion.",["“opinion” renders Meinung, “opine” meine.","“object” renders obiect."]],"reflm:4960":["The opponents of religion are provoked to contest truths which they would have let count as articles of faith if they had not been challenged by the bold presumption of those who pretend to more than they know.",["“articles of faith” renders Glaubenssatze; “presumption” renders Anmaßung."]],"reflm:4961":["Freedom makes us regard much as possible which is not actual; e.g. that the river take another course, because we ourselves can direct it.",["“actual” renders wirklich."]],"reflm:4962":["In transcendental judgments it is not permitted at all to opine, as little as in geometry.",["“opine” renders meynen."]],"reflm:4963":["On the healthy understanding as *qvalitas occulta*. How far its authority reaches; usurped authority. Lazy philosophy.",["*qvalitas occulta*: “occult quality”; “authority” renders Ansehen."]],"reflm:4964":["Through this treatise of mine the worth of my previous metaphysical writings is completely annihilated. I will seek only to save still the correctness of the idea.",[]],"reflm:4965":["To cognize something *a priori* means: to have insight (the principles may also be empirical). To determine something *a priori* (i.e. to give and construct it) means: to comprehend. To comprehend absolutely is without any empirical conditions.",["“to have insight” renders einsehen; “to comprehend” renders begreifen; “absolutely” renders Schlechthin."]],"reflm:4966":["All the difficulty of metaphysics concerns only the rhyming together of empirical principles with ideas. The possibility of the latter is not to be denied, but they cannot become empirically intelligible; the idea is not a *conceptus dabilis* at all, no empirically possible concept.",["“rhyming together” renders zusammenreimen (“reconciling”).","*conceptus dabilis*: “concept that can be given”."]],"reflm:4967":["To the intellectual of magnitude belong totality, the limitations or finitude. Such a concept of magnitude is no mere concept of comparison and relation; it can be understood *absolute* of a thing.\nThe whole, the part.\nBoundary concepts: The first, the last. All.",["“limitations” renders Schranken; *absolute*: “absolutely”.","“concept of comparison and relation” renders Vergleichung und Verhaltnisbegrif."]],"reflm:4968":["If the doctrine of space and time stopped at this, that they are merely affections of the mind, not objective conditions, then it would be a subtle but little considerable consideration. But that one must on that account not extend these concepts beyond the boundaries of sensibility is important. That we actually intuit everything in time proves that this is a condition of all our intuitions of the senses, but not on that account that such also has some significance without this condition.",["'mind' renders Gemüth; 'significance' renders Bedeutung.","'must ... not' renders nicht ... müsse, which can also be read 'need not'."]],"reflm:4969":["All new theories that make a great alteration have had to be introduced by someone else. The inventor has never been able to bring them into currency. It was the confirmation through another's reason which occasioned a good judgment in favor of the innovation.",["'another's reason' renders fremde Vernunft."]],"reflm:4970":["M XXVII:\nThe philosopher treats that which in all human cognitions is the self-subsistent and lies at the ground of everything; hence too the philosophical among all doctrines pleases immediately.\nPhilosophy is the science of the suitability of all cognitions to the vocation of the human being. To this belongs first philodoxy as the culture and instruction of all talents, i.e. the suitability of cognitions to all sorts of ends. Second the suitability of them to the extended use of reason. Third the grounding of the supreme maxims of the speculative as well as practical use of reason; here great learning, indeed even the art of reason of famous men, falls away and is without philosophy. metaphysics and morals, both architectonic, are the two door hinges of philosophy. The philosopher is no misologist, but one versed in the laws of human reason, and the foremost laws are those which restrict the pretensions of reason to the end of humanity. First widespread historical and rational cognition, just as for a legislator first a multiplicity of citizens and of arts. One must, in order to prescribe laws to reason, know it. But only in the maxims of reason is philosophy lodged; the other cognitions are philosophical, i.e. belonging to the former.\nM XXVIII:\nHistory and description must first of all be treated suitably to reason (after they were previously merely adequate to the desire for knowledge). Afterwards their interconnection according to maxims of reason; then their system according to the legislating idea of reason.",["'suitability' renders Angemessenheit; 'rational cognition' renders Vernunfterkentnis; 'art of reason' renders Vernunftkunst.","'then' renders denn, read in its older sense of dann.","'one versed in the laws' renders Gesetzkundiger."]],"reflm:4971":["It has happened to various people that the maxims of reason have been taken by others for axioms of it and the rules of method for principles of the theory (g doctrine), likewise the restriction of judgments has been taken for restriction of the objects. Just as Epicurus's ever-cheerful heart, which can never be attained otherwise than through accord with the universal and essential order of things and thus with everything that one calls morality, has through an unfortunate ambiguity of expression been taken for the voluptuousness of the senses.",["'morality' renders sittlichkeit; 'voluptuousness' renders wollust."]],"reflm:4972":["In the world of sense we follow the principles of empirical cognition, in the world of understanding the principles of pure cognition of the understanding. These latter, however, are those which have no relation to exposition of the appearances, [but] by which we are affected, but merely to that which is given through the understanding (pure use of freedom), or morality. Here now is the necessary presupposition that a God is. A belief.",["The closing 'oder moralitaet haben' can be read 'or [to] morality' or 'or have morality'; the English leaves it open.","'belief' renders Glaube (also 'faith')."]],"reflm:4973":["Metaphysics is something that cannot be learned at all.\nIn hypothetical and disjunctive syllogisms the subsumption is an alteration of the problematic into the assertoric. How is this transition in ontology?",["'syllogisms' renders Vernunftschlüsse."]],"reflm:4974":["The understanding is the faculty of bringing everything that may be given through the senses under universal rules for all powers of representation of the human mind.\n1. The conditions of sensibility under which the concept of the understanding alone (*in concreto*) is possible.\n2. Of the actions *a priori*, to determine the function peculiar to each representation.",["allein ('alone') may qualify the concept or mean 'only'.","'powers' renders Krafte; 'mind' renders Gemüth."]],"reflm:4975":["The faculty of rules is: 1. either to find rules for given representations (g to bring them to rules) or also for possible ones. 2. to bring representations under a given rule.",[]],"reflm:4976":["The transcendental antithesis takes place everywhere where I want to think something without the conditions through which alone it can be given. e.g. There is a first in the series of the contingent.\nAll analytic propositions of pure reason are correct *in thesi*, the synthetic ones only *in hypothesi*; the hypothesis, however, is that they are supposed to pertain to experience as concepts of it, or to the condition of sensibility, and yet without it, or to the completion and the boundaries. If the hypothesis is lacking, then the propositions are arbitrary. If the new condition even contradicts it, then they are false. The universal hypothesis pertains to everything that can be given to us, consequently is an object of the senses. What according to magnitude (g everything possible) exceeds the measure of the senses, or according to quality as spirit, or according to universality as substance, highest being, goes beyond the limitations of synthesis through the understanding. They are thus not axioms but anticipations, if they are put forward without restriction.",["'boundaries' renders Grentzen, 'limitations' Schranken.","'then' (second conditional) renders denn, in its older sense of dann."]],"reflm:4977":["The understanding cognizes objects as they are. This proposition can have two sorts of meaning:\n1. as they would appear if the senses were completely sharp.\n2. What constitutes the origin of appearances: real functions. The being and not-being (g of something) of a thing in general.\n3. (The things according to their absolute quality, which correspond to these appearances.\nThis something *in concreto*. The latter is mystical and chimerical. Spiritual intuiting.)\nOur understanding cognizes [the] even the first sensitive grounds of appearances *a priori*, or rather the elements of experiences and |\ntheir fundamental laws; that is also the cause why it can reduce the appearances.",["'two sorts of meaning' renders zweyerley Bedeutungen, though three points follow.","'fundamental laws' renders Grundgesetze; 'that is' renders daß ist (for das ist)."]],"reflm:4978":["Of the mathematical concept of magnitude, the only one which without any data is capable of a cognition *a priori* to infinity (likewise geometry), because it is nothing but intuition of its arbitrary actions and no quality mixes itself in, [but] also no logical analysis is proceeded with in it, no synthetic principles of reason are encountered in it. By contrast the anticipations of empirical cognition admit a great difference of the subjective and objective conditions, or rather of the relation to experience, in order to understand them, from that to reason, in order to cognize them *a priori*. With quantity it is given to me arbitrarily according to its conditions, with the concepts of synthesis through experience. Those of thesis are accompanying concepts [of the proper cognition] of positing, either absolutely or *a priori*.",["ohne alle data einer erkentnis a priori can also be construed 'without any data of a cognition *a priori*'.","'arbitrary' renders willkührlich; 'absolutely' renders schlechthin."]],"reflm:4979":["M XXX:\nThe proposition: no magnitude is mathematically infinite, is a synthetic proposition; thus it has only a subjective correctness. By contrast: no number is infinite, has an objective one.\nBy contrast [is d] has the proposition:* no magnitude can be divided or diminished to infinity (the angle of contact actually divides every |\nrectilinear one to infinity, but does not on that account give an infinitely small part. For that is not assignable), not even subjective correctness. For where [the infinite] the part is possible only through the limitations of the infinite, there it is not to be assumed as consisting of elements. One must not appeal to the divisibility of unity.\nM XXIX:\n*(g meanwhile the possibility of such a magnitude is not to be comprehended through reason. For that would have to arise through generation of it from its parts. The proposition: the *substantiale* is *stabile* and *accidentia fluunt*, has objective correctness with respect to experience. For only by this stability do I cognize subsistence. Likewise: every *compositum substantiale* requires a communally influencing force. Also: everything contingent has a ground; for I cognize contingency only by this, that it is not at all times, and the ground is the accompanying thing by which I can cognize it again.)",["'assignable' renders angeblich, in the sense of angebbar.","'magnitude' in 'no magnitude can be divided' renders große, as printed (for Größe).","'cognize it again' renders wieder erkennen, which can also be read 'recognize'."]],"reflm:4980":["M XXX:\nWhether there is a single simple action?",[]],"reflm:4981":["Crosswise on the inner margin:\nThat which we have as our aim through the restriction of the [sensible] axioms of sensibility to mere use in this world |\nis not directed at our making room for the cognitions through the pure understanding, but of these we also show that, although the *principia* here are different, the objects nevertheless can also be only of the senses, but undetermined with respect to their kind.",["'their kind' renders der Art derselben; derselben may refer to the objects or to the senses."]],"reflm:4982":["M XXIX. Crosswise on the inner margin:\nThe foremost of all sciences of reason are metaphysics and morals, but the former for the sake of the latter. The utility of the same is dialectical; in that reason opposes something to the dogmatic objections of reason, the grounds of morals become without hindrance.\nM XXXIII. On the lower margin:\ne.g. One makes the empirical propositions into dogmatic ones: that the soul does not think without the concurrence of the body, into the insight that it cannot think; to this one answers in just the same tone that perhaps the body is only the auxiliary means against the hindrance of it. Against this objections and answers are made again; meanwhile reason betakes itself to its natural vocation. The answers must be regarded only as retorsions, but not as dogmatic propositions.\nThe political constitution permits coercive doctrines, because it is all the same with what heart they are accepted, but the moral does not, because sincerity is essential here.",["'of the same' renders derselben, which may refer to metaphysics or to both sciences.","'propositions' at the end renders Setze, as printed (for Sätze).","'retorsions' renders retorsionen (turning an argument back on the opponent)."]],"reflm:4983":["In metaphysics the pure notions, in morals the pure idea.\nThe idea *in concreto* is the archetype in the rational sense. The rule *in concreto* is the example. The *analogon* of the archetype is the symbol (*typus*), that of the example is the simile. The [acceptance as] imitation of the archetype is the copy, the imitation of the rule is the attempt. The semblance of the [archetype] copy is* etc etc. The [prop] concordant sequel of the archetype is the counterimage, e.g. the human being as image of God, the subject obedient to the sovereign (his concordant counterpart). Prototype, *prognosticon*.\n(\n* g The likeness or depiction, spurious image, shadow-image. The idea of nature and of morality; the former is genetic, the latter exemplary. The *principia* for us are rules. Image ([cogn] intuition of the object), archetype, copy, counterimage, symbol, prototype, shadow-image. The counterimage or counterpart corresponds to a principal image. )",["Urbild 'archetype', Nachbild 'copy', Gegenbild 'counterimage', Sinnbild 'symbol', Vorbild 'prototype', Abbild 'likeness', Afterbild 'spurious image', Ebenbild 'image', Bild 'image'.","'in the rational sense' renders in rationalem Verstande (Verstand here 'sense', not 'understanding').","'semblance' renders apparentz; [eige] and [Erkentn] are broken-off deleted words."]],"reflm:4984":["To think in metaphysics and to think about it is a difference; the first all do. Some, instead of thinking about it, rave about it.\nMathematics, physics and metaphysics, although they are rational, contain no *dogmata*. Physics is not apodictic, because the first causes are given only contingently. Mathematics is not from concepts. Metaphysics has no objects that are given *a priori*.",["'rave' renders schwärmen (cf. Schwärmerei, 'enthusiasm').","'objects' renders obiecte."]],"reflm:4985":["The transcendental propositions of the pure understanding are thetic if they are restricted to the condition of appearance, i.e. of the [datorum] conditions of sensibility under which the object is given. |\nBut if they become transcendent, then they are antithetic and give fine material for the skeptical method. The transcendental antithetic is from the nature of reason itself and no contingent or arbitrary one.",["'material' renders stoff; 'object' renders obiect."]],"reflm:4986":["The *organon* of pure reason is a guidance for determining its boundaries and rules. Instead of doctrine (objective) it contains discipline and, as *criterio* and norm, the canon.",["'doctrine' renders doctrin (Lehre is elsewhere also 'doctrine').","*criterio* is kept in the case Kant gives it."]],"reflm:4987":["All philosophy has reason as its object: the maxims, the boundaries and the end. The rest is art of reason.",["'object' renders obiect; 'art of reason' renders Vernunftkunst."]],"reflm:4988":["Upon the logic of pure reason, which is merely critical, follows the *organon*, which is didactic, and through which not merely the judging [ge] is corrected but also the procedure is guided. It contains discipline and canon. Not doctrine, because it contains merely negative doctrines.",["'doctrine' renders doctrin, 'doctrines' Lehren; 'judging' renders Beurtheilung.","[ge] is a broken-off deleted word."]],"reflm:4989":["[The] *organon* is practical logic.\ndiscipline is negative and manly doctrine. (Against error.)\ndoctrine is positive and here children's doctrine.\nThe method of my presentation has a disadvantageous form. It looks scholastic, hence brooding, dry, indeed restricted, and far different from the tone of genius.",["'doctrine' renders both doctrin and Lehre (männliche Lehre, Kinderlehre); Kinderlehre is also the usual word for catechism instruction.","'brooding' renders grüblerisch."]],"reflm:4990":["Reality is the *transcendentaliter addendum*, negation: *subtrahendum*. It serves for transcendental magnitude, e.g. all matter, which contains extension, all limitations: extension (space), time intension.\nReality is the object of sensation *in abstracto*,\nnegation\nthe lack of it.",["The construction of 'all matter, which contains extension, all limitations' is left as loose as the German.","'object' renders obiect."]],"reflm:4991":["It matters little whether some propositions of pure philosophy about the object are true or false; it is more important whether they are thought in the proper method and have their proper place in the whole of cognition, as on the general map.",["'object' renders obiect."]],"reflm:4992":["M XXXI:\nIn the estimation of the writings of others one must choose the *method* of participation in the universal cause of human reason. From the attempt to seek out that which concerns the whole, one finds it worth the examination to offer a helping hand to the author or rather |\nto the common good and to treat the errors as side matters. It is sad for reason as a whole to destroy everything.\nSince it would have been vain to credit myself with such extraordinary luck or exceptional acuity that by the same method I would avoid all pitfalls and [unt] on such confused ground alone hit upon the right path, I have expected a happier outcome than all my predecessors have had merely from the disposition of mind into which I put myself and constantly kept myself; likewise from the length of time through which I held the mind open to every new teaching, which items I doubt that anyone before me had ever observed. I have [on the ground of the] in the territory of the philosophy of pure reason nowhere yet settled myself, I have written no big books in it and put my vanity into the necessity of defending them and of remaining with one and the same opinion.\nM XXXII:\nI have even never looked again at the little essays that I scattered of it, in order not to seem entirely idle, in order not to remain fixed to one and the same spot, and the critique of them [no longer struck me] by others, whose gentleness nevertheless did not take my courage from me breaks off?",["Beurtheilung: estimation; Gebiete: territory; Gemüthsverfassung: disposition of mind (Gemüth = mind).","welche Stüke: which items (pieces), referring back to the disposition and the length of time; the construction is loose in the German.","and put my vanity into the necessity: the German (in die Nothwendigkeit Versetzt) continues the negation of the preceding clause only by implication."]],"reflm:4993":["In mathematics one can well make the division into pure and applied mathematics, for the objects of experience give no *principia mathematica*, but rather these are only applied to them. But philosophy one must divide into the pure and the empirical, because in the latter there even lie certain *principia philosophica* that are undetermined in the first. The universal of the experiences of inner and outer sense, through which they are all possible, is the boundary of pure reason *a posteriori* and thus in the |\napplication. They are namely possibilities that are given *a posteriori*, but serve *a priori* as grounds, to which the rest can be reduced.\nThe qualities [have] according to their relations must often be given anew through the senses, but quantity *qva talis* and its relations are at all times, even in applied mathematics, given *a priori*.",["Grentze: boundary; Verhaltnissen: relations.","Die qvalitaten [haben] ihren Verhaltnissen nach: the deleted haben leaves the construction of the sentence uncertain."]],"reflm:4994":["Many acute and good thoughts have been recorded, but to sheer loss; for they have no place in any system, because one has not yet found the outline for this.",["Abris: outline, sketch."]],"reflm:4995":["The concept of God: is it a dogmatic or a concept of belief?",["Glaubensbegrif: concept of belief or of faith (Glaube = belief; faith)."]],"reflm:4996":["The cognition of God is important only in regard to the practical, but this must be sufficiently certain for itself. For otherwise it would not be obligatory for itself and would not become so through the cognition of God. Thus belief in God must arise from morality, which thereby supports itself. It is good that we do not know, but believe, that there is a God. Method of teaching this. For mere speculation it is an opinion, which oversteps the limitations.",["die sich dadurch selbst unterstützt: which thereby supports itself; die may refer to morality or to belief."]],"reflm:4997":["To proceed according to a guiding thread requires only diligence and attentiveness. But to find the guiding thread itself and the torn-off pieces of it, |\nthere is required the sudden idea, which is the very same in thinking as the lucky chance is in occurrences.",["Leitfaden: guiding thread; Einfall: sudden idea, notion (not Idee); Glüksfall: lucky chance."]],"reflm:4998":["On the inner margin, crosswise. M XXXI:\nIn experience there is appearance and real concept. The experience of our alterations contains the mere form of appearance of time and the concept of being. [The The latter] Both together make up something that in the field of experiences yields a correct ground. But the former, consequently |\nM XXXII: |\nthe concept of existence affected by it, goes no further and has no inner reality, but only the value of a conditioned form of our representations, i.e. it is no thing or its affection in itself, but only the sensible representation of it.",["seyn: being; existentz: existence; realitaet: reality.","Erscheinungsform der Zeit: form of appearance of time, i.e. either the form of appearance that is time or the form in which time appears; the German allows both."]],"reflm:4999":["On the outer margin, crosswise.\nAppearance and illusion are two different things. The former depends on how the object is given, the latter, how it is thought. The apparency in an appearance rests on the judgment about the affection of the sense, and, if this is correct, it nevertheless remains *phaenomenon*.",["Erscheinung: appearance; Schein: illusion; apparentz: apparency, a Latinate word distinct from both.","wenn dies richtig ist: dies may refer to the judgment or to the affection."]],"reflm:5000":["In pure philosophy, metaphysics of nature and of morals, only one proof can be given, because it must be drawn from a single concept.",["einigen: single (= einzigen); Sitten: morals."]],"reflm:5001":["A necessary *hypothesis* of the optional or of the indispensable use (practical *hypothesis*) of the understanding and of reason.\nA necessary theoretical *hypothesis* in regard to the whole of all experiences (existence of God).",["beliebigen: optional, arbitrary; it may qualify Gebrauchs (optional use) or stand as a noun (of the optional); the English keeps the ambiguity.","Daseyn: existence."]],"reflm:5002":["In transcendental cognitions only a single proof is possible, namely from the concept of the subject.",[]],"reflm:5003":["The transcendental analytic is in this still the logic of truth, because it does not judge objectively; but the dialectic judges objectively. In it everything must be taken from the subject and yet judged objectively. Hence the subjective laws here easily count as objective ones, namely those that are grounded on the *conditiones* of comprehensibility, as such that concern the *apprehension* of the object, but these in turn as universal ones, since they are after all restricted by human sensibility.",["Begreiflichkeit: comprehensibility (begreifen = comprehend).","darin: in this; it may refer to the analytic or forward to the reason given."]],"reflm:5004":["Transcendental philosophy in its part on its propositions is dialectical, because, since without critique they carry with them no other touchstone of truth, they must be considered as problematic, consequently also with retention of their opposites, and can become dogmatic only according to agreement with the *canon*.",["The subject shifts from singular (die transscendentale philosophie) to plural (sie ... führen, i.e. the propositions); the English keeps the shift.","Probirstein: touchstone; Satz: proposition."]],"reflm:5005":["Synthetic propositions make up the content of our cognition: what we know; analytic ones only the materials for this cognition. |\nIn regard to the former (g *in concreto*) I know no more than everyone; but I know what the understanding can know of them. I know the rules of the understanding in regard to them.",["Inhalt: content; wissen: know (Wissen = knowledge).","g: Kant's abbreviation, carried over as it stands."]],"reflm:5006":["Of the fundamental maxim of reason, to be difficult in regard to everything that diminishes its use according to universal laws. For reason consists in cognizing something from the universal. Consequently: what restricts the use according to universal laws, likewise the use *a priori*, is contrary to it. [All] Something as innate breaks off.",["schwierig zu seyn: to be difficult, i.e. refractory or unaccommodating; the older sense of schwierig.","vor angebohren: as innate (vor = für)."]],"reflm:5007":["Philosophy is the knowledge of the laws of human reason. The artist of reason needs rules, the teacher of reason laws. *leguleius*.\nIt is the nomothetic of human reason.\nThe science of the rules of human reason is logic.",["Gesetzkunde: knowledge of the laws (Kunde, not Wissenschaft); Vernunftkünstler: artist of reason; Vernunftlehrer: teacher of reason.","*leguleius*: Latin, a pettifogging lawyer, one versed in the letter of the law.","The last two sentences are Latin in the original: Est nomothetica rationis humanae. Scientia regularum Rationis Humanae est Logica."]],"reflm:5008":["The [pra] The theoretical conditions of everything practical are freedom, origin and future. or the inner and the outer principles of all our ends together. These are also the *crux philosophorum*.",["Zwecke: ends; principien: principles (Latinate, distinct from Grundsätze)."]],"reflm:5009":["Nothing more wretched can be found than to make the emergency aids of our presumptuous reason into rules, if it is not still more wretched to regard these presuppositions as divine revelations.",["Nothhülfen: emergency aids, makeshifts; vorwitzig: presumptuous, prying.","wo es nicht noch elender ist: if not, unless it is still more wretched."]],"reflm:5010":["We in fact make use of the skeptical method in many cases, in order to try whether a proposition also has universality; for if something false follows from it, then it proves too much.",[]],"reflm:5011":["It is above all necessary to show whence in mathematics so much, in the philosophy of pure reason nothing at all synthetic can be cognized *a priori*.",["soviel ... so gar nichts synthetisches: synthetic governs both members; the English keeps the compressed construction."]],"reflm:5012":["Transcendental cognition is the only natural dialectic.",[]],"reflm:5013":["At the beginning of the transcendental science.\nMy aim is to investigate how much reason can cognize *a priori* and how far its dependence on the teaching of the senses extends. [It] Which, therefore, are the boundaries beyond which it cannot go without the assistance of the senses. This object is important and great, for it shows the human being his vocation with reason. In order to reach this final end, I find it necessary to isolate reason, but also sensibility, and first to consider everything that can be cognized *a priori*, whether it also belongs to the territory of reason. This separated consideration, this pure philosophy is of great utility.",["Absicht: aim; Belehrung: teaching; Grentzen: boundaries; Gebiethe: territory.","Bestimmung: vocation; also determination."]],"reflm:5014":["The cause why, among the three parts of metaphysics: *Physica* |\n*rationalis*, *Psychologia Rationalis*, *Theologia Naturalis*, the last has the word nature last, is this, because in the former the object is given through the senses and cognized through reason, in this one the object is given through reason and cognized according to the analogy of the senses.",["jenen ... dieser: the former (the first two) ... this one (the last)."]],"reflm:5015":["It took a considerable time before the concepts had so ordered themselves with me that I saw them make up a whole and draw distinctly the boundary of the science that I had in view. Before the *disputation* I already had the idea of the influence of the subjective conditions of cognitions on the objective ones, [but] Afterwards of the distinction of the sensitive and the intellectual. But the latter was with me merely negative.\nOne will not be permitted to believe that everything up to now has been written and thought to sheer loss. [It alter] The dogmatic attempts can always continue, but a critique of them must follow upon them, and they can only be used in order to judge about the illusion |\nthat befalls human reason when it mixes up the subjective with the objective and sensibility with reason.\nTwo *metaphysici*, of whom one proves the *thesis*, the other the *antithesis*, take the place, in the eyes of a third observer, of a skeptical examination. One must do both oneself.\nI believe, to be sure, that this doctrine will be the only one which, when minds have cooled off from the [heat] dogmatic heat, must alone remain and then always endure; but I doubt very much that I will be the one who brings about this alteration. The human mind is of such a kind that besides the grounds that are to enlighten it, time is still required in order to give them force and progress. And when prejudices are contested, it is no wonder that one at first still contests these efforts by just the same prejudices. For it is necessary first to cancel the impressions and the old habit. [One w] I could cite various cases where not the originator of the improvement, but later on those who after long contradictions again found it new to bring such onto the track, have set them going.\nI can still picture to myself the reproach that various things are not elucidated which still should have been said. [It] That is just as much as if one reproached someone that, since he only wanted to write a small book, he has not written a big book. What is lacking in a writing does not make up a fault (failed aim), but indeed the lack that is met with in what one gives out as complete. It requires moderation and power of judgment not to say everything good that one knows, and not to overload one's work with all one's sudden ideas, so that the main aim does not suffer under it. I have said in the *analysis* some things not insignificant etc etc.",["daß ich sie sahe ein gantzes ausmachen und die Grentze ... deutlich zeichnen: zeichnen may depend on ich (I draw) or on sie (the concepts draw); the English keeps both open.","es wiederum neu fanden, solche auf die Bahn zu bringen: the construction is obscure (found it new, or found it fitting anew, to bring such forward); rendered literally.","Gemüth: mind; Einfällen: sudden ideas; Hauptabsicht: main aim; urtheilskraft: power of judgment."]],"reflm:5016":["On the inner margin, crosswise:\nMetaphysical possibilities, e.g. that the mind of the human being is prepared through birth as a school for a greater development of ideas, count not as opinions, but as answers to the objections that such cases could not be thought at all.",["Geist: mind; also spirit.","durch die Geburth als eine schule: through birth as a school; it is open whether birth or what birth leads into is the school."]],"reflm:5017":["On the inner margin, crosswise:\nI must confess it: I have a certain superstition in regard to various expressions that have occurred to great minds. I do not seek the meaning behind them; but when a concept rises up to my thinking [then] and the word strikes me, then, it seems, I feel the inspiration or even the entire sensation that the one had who had the expression with the same concept, with whom I sympathize.",["großen Köpfen: great minds (literally heads); Nachdenken: thinking, meditation; Bedeutung: meaning.","mit dem ich sympathisiere: dem may refer to the one who had the expression or to the concept."]],"reflm:5018":["Logic has no empirical *principia*. It is a science of the objective rules of reason, namely those of correct cognition. Consequently the subjective *principia* are merely practical in regard to the human understanding and belong to the *Organon*. According to *psychologia empirica*.",[]],"reflm:5019":["I have cited no one through whose reading I have learned something. I have found it good to leave out everything [di] foreign and to follow my own idea. I have not disputed against systems etc etc. I have not cited myself, but overturned everything. I do\n|\nnot approve the rule, when in the use of pure reason one has previously proved something, of no longer calling this afterwards into doubt as a firm principle.",["angeführt: cited; Grundsatz: principle.","wie einen festen Grundsatz: as (or like) a firm principle."]],"reflm:5020":["In many places my presentation would have become far more distinct if it had not had to be so distinct.",["Vortrag: presentation, delivery; deutlich: distinct."]],"reflm:5021":["According to Priestley and Locke all cognitions must be empirical, and not a single synthetic one can have true necessity. But this conflicts with the use, which is universal without exception.",["der ohne Ausnahme allgemein ist: which is universal without exception; it is the use, not the cognition, that is said to be universal."]],"reflm:5022":["Transcendental philosophy is the grave of all superstition. Maxims of reason, conditions of intuition and of understanding.",[]],"reflm:5023":["M XXXIII:\nAll the remaining philosophical sciences must gain a great deal if this diversion of our power of reason (which is after all without utility) is stopped up; |\nM XXXIV: |\nthis is the advantage of mathematics, that it represents everything as it can be given in experience, but *philosophia transscendentalis* does not.",["ableitung: diversion, draining off (as of water), here with gestopft (stopped up); not derivation.","Vernunftkraft: power of reason."]],"reflm:5024":["(for the dedication.) you have honored me with your letters. The effort, [to you] at [your] your request to give a concept of the method of pure philosophy, has [my answers from the still obscure in] occasioned a series of considerations, to develop the concept still lying obscurely in me, and, as the prospects widened with the progress, exposed the answers to an incessant postponement. This writing can serve instead [of the fi] of an answer, as regards the speculative part. Since it is to be ascribed to your solicitations and hints, I would wish that it belonged wholly to you through the effort to take it into your working-over. Academies of sciences ought rather to take into consideration the state of the sciences of your age in general than individual elaborations. Yours is the only — — breaks off.\nIt is hardly necessary to mention that this [writing from the] dedication borrows a recommendation from your name and is not there to the end, to it the breaks off.",["“letters” and “dedication” both render Zuschrift(en); the first may also mean “addresses”.","“[of the fi]” renders the broken deletion [des er]."]],"reflm:5025":["My method is not very suited to holding the reader to itself and pleasing him.\nOne must begin one’s estimation from the whole and direct it to the idea of the work together with its ground. The rest belongs to the execution, in which many a thing may have been mistaken and become better.",["“holding the reader to itself” renders den Leser an sich zu halten, which can also be read “holding the reader in check”.","“estimation” renders Beurtheilung."]],"reflm:5026":["On what the plausibility of a metaphysical hypothesis (Swedenborg) rests. On a supposed *intuitus intellectualis* according to the analogy of the sensible.",["“plausibility” renders Scheinbarkeit, which also carries “semblance”.","*intuitus intellectualis*: “intellectual intuition”."]],"reflm:5027":["Dogmatic metaphysics is a *Magia iudiciaria**, *visionaria*. It is not the *organon*, but the *catarcticon* of transcendent reason. Worms of the brain. palliative. relapse. Thorough cure through purgation.\n*(g *Contemplativa*; distinction from *magia empirica*.)",["*Magia iudiciaria*, *visionaria*: “judicial, visionary magic”; *catarcticon*: “cathartic”, “purgative”.","“palliative” renders palliativmittel; “relapse” recidiv; “purgation” abführung."]],"reflm:5028":["Our method also greatly promotes sincerity. Otherwise one had to |\nconceal the difficulties against important articles of faith of philosophy and adduce merely what is advantageous. A paradoxical author could easily be refuted, for he was no longer permitted to answer. He who adhered to the common opinion kept the field alone. Now through the skeptical method a free gate is opened to every thought, and even his objection with the *retorsion* serves to determine the limitations of the understanding better.\nIt is also a *fallacia ignorationis elenchi* to begin from the harmfulness of a proposition when one is supposed to examine its correctness.\nWhen will we once lay aside this great remnant of a barbaric constitution, to prescribe to reason, which is after all the only thing that can guide us, the way imperiously.\nHe always is right in such disputes who has the last word. But he at all times has the last word who asserts the orthodox proposition. For either the [bookseller] publisher is already under fiscal investigation etc etc.",["“articles of faith” renders Glaubenssatze; “limitations” Schranken.","*retorsion*: the turning of an argument back on its author; *fallacia ignorationis elenchi*: “fallacy of ignorance of the refutation”.","“his objection” (sein Einwurf) refers grammatically to “every thought” or to the author."]],"reflm:5029":["The intellectual of the objects of the senses (or of experience) is not that they are given in another way than through senses, but that through which they are thought *a priori*, and how one would think everything through concepts, it might be given as it will. The ancients seemed to want to withdraw from reflecting cognition and believed the understanding to be capable of intuitions of its own.",["“The intellectual” renders Das intellectuale, used as a noun."]],"reflm:5030":["That substance endures through all alterations of nature, we infer from this: we cognize substance only by this enduring. |\nThus the change of substance is for our [conditions] concepts of experience a *contradictio in adiecto*.",["“endures” / “enduring” render fortdaure / Fortdauer; “change” Wechsel; “infer” schließen."]],"reflm:5031":["I have chosen the school method and preferred it[to the free swing] to the free movement of the spirit and of wit, although, since I wanted every reflective head to take part in this investigation, I found that the dryness of this method would deter [the] readers of the kind who look straightaway for the combination with the practical. I would, even if I had been in the greatest possession of wit and of [Ein] an author’s charms, have excluded them from this, for it matters much to me to leave no suspicion that I wanted to captivate and persuade the reader, but so that I would have to expect either [from him] no assent at all from him or merely through the strength of insights.\nThe method too arose for me only through attempts.",["“spirit” renders Geist; “wit” Witz; “combination” Verbindung.","“[Ein]” is a broken deletion and is left as it stands.","“persuade” renders überreden; “assent” beytritt."]],"reflm:5032":["All synthetic propositions which express the condition without which it is impossible in general to cognize an object are objective. Those without which it is impossible to cognize it *a priori* [or] completely are subjective.\nAll our cognition (g *a priori*) always has *correlata*; if the one of them is lacking, we cannot completely cognize the other. All our cognition of magnitude is determinately possible only through limitations, thus we cannot cognize magnitude in general *absolute* completely, because here we are not to posit any limitations.",["“completely” renders complet; “cognition of magnitude” größenerkentnis.","“determinately possible” renders bestimmt … möglich; bestimmt can also be read as “determined”."]],"reflm:5033":["In arbitrary concepts one can very easily become conscious of what one thinks in them. Hence evidence of mathematics.",["“arbitrary” renders willkührlichen; “evidence” Evidentz, i.e. self-evidence."]],"reflm:5034":["The *antithesis* of possibility (g priority) and actuality. No thing is possible without something actual. There is no actuality without under presupposed possibility. The analysis of transcendental concepts is the whole ontological doctrine, but the *synthesis* contains nothing but *antithesis*. There is no proposition without *antithesi*, and why? how do they conflict with one another?",["“without under presupposed possibility” keeps the irregular ohne unter vorausgesetzter Moglichkeit.","“analysis” renders Zergliederung."]],"reflm:5035":["Crosswise on the inner margin. M XXXV:\nWolff did great things in philosophy; but he only went straight ahead and extended cognition, without sifting, altering and reshaping it through a special critique. His works are thus very useful as a magazine of reason, but not as an architectonic |\nM XXXVI:\nof it. Perhaps this, though it was not exactly to be approved in Wolff himself, is yet in the order of nature, that first without correct method the items of knowledge, at least the attempts of the understanding, are multiplied and afterwards brought under rules. Children.",["Kant spells the name Wolf / Wolfen.","“went straight ahead” renders ging … vor sich weg; “items of knowledge” Kenntnisse."]],"reflm:5036":["I have not been able to bring these considerations into this state without at the same time having |\nregard to the remaining influences of pure philosophy, which I have at the same time completed. For I am not of the opinion of an excellent man who recommends [a], once one has convinced oneself of something, afterwards no longer to doubt it. In pure philosophy that does not work. Even the understanding already has a natural aversion to it. One must precisely weigh the propositions in all sorts of applications and even borrow from these a particular proof, try to assume the opposite, and take postponement until the truth is evident from all sides.\nI have at all times had before my eyes that I had to work over only transcendental philosophy, that the boundary of each science must be precisely observed, and that the mixing serves only to make deceptions. but precisely thereby I have lost much that could have served the writing as a recommendation.",["“which” (die) can refer to the influences or to pure philosophy.","“deceptions” renders Blendwerke; “boundary” Grentze.","“Even the understanding” renders Selbst hat der Verstand, which can also be read “The understanding itself”."]],"reflm:5037":["If I only [ver] achieve so much that I convince that one must suspend the working-over of this science until one has settled this point, then this writing has achieved its end.\nI saw this doctrinal system at first as in a twilight. I tried quite seriously to prove propositions and their opposite, not in order to erect a doctrine of doubt, but because I suspected an illusion of the understanding, to discover wherein it lay. The year 69 gave me great light.",["“doctrinal system” renders Lehrbegrif; “doctrine of doubt” Zweifellehre.","“illusion” renders the foreign word illusion, not Schein."]],"reflm:5038":["In *Metaphysicis* *fictio* counts not as *probatoria*, but as *supple|toria*\nor *defensoria*, *replicatoria*. e.g. Of the possibility of a pure spirit, of the spirit-world etc. they are then, as something that one can think, opposed to the objections from what one cannot think, assumed in themselves only problematically.",["*fictio … probatoria … suppletoria … defensoria, replicatoria*: a fiction serving as proof, as supplement, as defense, as rejoinder."]],"reflm:5039":["Division. The [Law] Nomothetic (legislation) of pure reason: 1. negative part, discipline; 2. positive part, Canon. Finally Architectonic.\n1. transcendental philosophy. 2. Metaphysics. Organon.",["“Division” renders Abtheilung; “legislation” Gesetzgebung."]],"reflm:5040":["Even if I, like Hume, had all embellishment in my power, I would yet have scruples about making use of it. It is true that some readers are deterred by dryness. But is it not necessary to deter some, with whom the matter comes into bad hands?",[]],"reflm:5041":["All further explanation through the understanding of space, time and apperception is impossible.",["“through the understanding” (durch den Verstand) can attach to “explanation” or to “of space, time and apperception”."]],"reflm:5042":["M XXXV:\nA concept of reason (world, freedom, necessity) never indicates an object, namely of intuition; consequently the question: whether the world has |\nM XXXVI: |\na beginning, can say only as much as: whether I could expound a pure concept of reason empirically. Here there is really no |\nobjective question. Thus it is also not problematic, but one can at all times answer it with: No, namely such an object cannot be represented empirically. In metaphysics there is no uncertainty.",["“expound” renders exponiren.","“it is also not problematic” (ist sie … nicht problematisch): sie is the question."]],"reflm:5043":["The propositions that are seen immediately according to the principle of contradiction are not seen from it. For this proposition, as to its whole validity, can nevertheless be cognized convincingly only *in concreto*.",["“are seen” renders werden … eingesehen (have insight into).","“principle” and “proposition” both render Satz."]],"reflm:5044":["What was at first held to be a doctrine of pure reason is now its discipline, i.e. its training and *animadversion*. Discipline is a restriction of the powers of the mind or inclinations within their fitting limitations. Discipline is negative. Not dogmatic. The spirit must not only be instructed: *institution*, but disciplined, i.e. its bad habits must be weaned from it.",["“training” renders Zucht; *animadversion*: “censure”, “correction”.","“powers of the mind” renders Gemüthskraften; “spirit” Geist."]],"reflm:5045":["That two *opposita* can at the same time be false, but not at the same time true, because [they] in the one the subject is taken as sensible and the predicate as intellectual, in the second conversely. e.g. There is a first beginning of the world and there is none. In the first, beginning signifies the supreme ground, which is constant; in the second the first member of the series. Now if the first is taken sensitively, then it is false; the second also.",["The first sentence is a “That” clause with no main clause."]],"reflm:5046":["In future one will expect no synthetic objective cognitions of pure philosophy. It is yet good, however, to continue in it always problematically, in order to discover [the subject] the cause of the origin of the axioms and the subreptions which make subjective laws into objective ones.\nThe foremost investigation is this: how do we come to cognitions in general and especially those *a priori*. What is the ground of their correctness and of their reliability?",[]],"reflm:5047":["Mathematics can surely have no use in the invention of objective philosophical propositions, because it cannot judge about the certainty of the *datorum*. But once these are settled, a mathematical head can, just as an *arithmeticam vniversalem*, so invent a transcendental *analysin*.",["“invention” / “invent” render Erfindung / erfinden; *arithmetica universalis*: “universal arithmetic”."]],"reflm:5048":["Mathematics is never empirical, but either *pura* or *applicata*. Philosophy, however, can be *rationalis* or *empirica*. The former is either *pura* or *applicata*. Of magnitudes I can assume no *principia* other than *a priori*. There are no opinions in it.",["“The former” (Die erstere) refers to rational philosophy.","“in it” (darin) can refer to mathematics or to the doctrine of magnitudes."]],"reflm:5049":["[Inner sense] Consciousness is the intuiting of oneself. It [would] would not be consciousness [so] if it were sensation. In it lies all cognition, of whatever it may be. If I abstract from all sensations, I presuppose consciousness. It is the [transcendental] logical personality, not the practical: this is the fac|ulty\nof freedom by virtue of which one can, without being externally determined, be a cause of oneself. Moral personality is the capacity for motives of mere reason, by virtue of which a being is capable of laws and thus also of imputations.",["“[would] would” renders [würde] wäre; “faculty” Vermögen; “capacity” Fähigkeit.","“be a cause of oneself” renders von selbst Ursache seyn, which can also mean “be a cause spontaneously”."]],"reflm:5050":["There are errors that one cannot refute. One must lead the perverse head into [sciences] cognitions that enlighten it; then the error disappears of itself. In vain will one take from someone the blind opinion of sympathy. Let one teach him physics!",["“physics” renders the French physiqve (physique)."]],"reflm:5051":["Crosswise on the inner margin. M XXXVII:\nAll concepts in general, from wherever they may take their material, are reflected representations, i.e. representations brought into the logical relation of manifold validity. But there are concepts whose |\nM XXXVIII: |\nwhole sense is nothing other than one or another reflection to which occurring representations can be subjected; they can be called concepts of reflection (*conceptus reflectentes*), and, because every kind of reflection occurs in judgment, they will contain *absolute* within themselves the mere action of the understanding that is applied in judgment to the relation, as grounds of the possibility of judging.",["“manifold validity” renders Vielgültigkeit.","*absolute* can attach to “contain” or to “the mere action of the understanding”."]],"reflm:5052":["One can, if one wants, derive everything from fundamental powers, only |\nnot the criterion of truth from a sense of the true and false that is made especially for distinguishing them. For then every touchstone of our judgments is lost.",["“fundamental powers” renders Grundkräften.","“distinguishing them” renders dessen Unterscheidung, singular in the German."]],"reflm:5053":["Discipline. How negative cognitions keep off errors.",[]],"reflm:5054":["Dialectic is that which contains the arguments *pro et contra*.",[]],"reflm:5055":["4 titles of the concepts of the understanding, under each 3 categories. And to these various predicables. The predicables that are mixed, e.g. in the *verbis* of action and passion, time and number.",["“passion” renders Leiden, i.e. being acted upon; *verbis*: “verbs”."]],"reflm:5056":["Our faculty of invention has its boundaries; a different kind of intuition than ours cannot be thought, though also not denied.",["“faculty of invention” renders Dichtungsvermögen (the faculty of fiction, of poetic invention)."]],"reflm:5057":["The rules of the [use] formal use of the logical rules, without considering the particular kind of cognition, are dialectical, namely of the play of representations according to logical laws, which is properly only an illusion of truth; for it is not on that account exactly true, because the cognition has this form.\nHere only directions for the power of judgment occur, and there are properly not rules here, but knacks.",["“illusion” renders schein; “knacks” Handgriffe."]],"reflm:5058":["In all [universal] judgments illusion rests on the confusion of the subjective with the objective. Especially in principles of reason, where *a priori* subjective grounds can also be objective.\nIn the transcendental science everything must be taken from the subject, only some of it relates to objects; hence in it the dialectic is something that belongs to the nature of the understanding and of which a science is possible.",["“*a priori*” may attach to “subjective” or to “can … be”; left as ambiguous as the German.","“principles of reason” renders Vernunftgrundsätzen; “objects” renders gegenständen."]],"reflm:5059":["The subreptions of the power of judgment. 1. That we cognize everything only through predicates. [so that] and thus have predicates with an undetermined subject. That we take that which is always the condition of the remaining predicates for the first subject. 2. That we take the conditions of distinction for differences necessary to the things. 3. That of a subject that is thought inwardly (thinking being) we predicate conditions of outer presence, and of the outer, e.g. body, conditions of the inner. Representation.",["“for differences necessary to the things” renders vor den Dingen nothwendige Unterschiede; vor (= für) can also be read “differences necessary before the things”.","The closing “Vorstellung.” stands unattached in the source."]],"reflm:5060":["Illusion and truth both belong to the understanding. We have a concept of things as they are, i.e. as they are represented in accordance with a judgment about |\nthem that agrees with the appearances in accordance with universal laws; what agrees only with the particular conditions of appearance is an illusion.",["“that agrees” (was … zusammenstimmt) may refer to the judgment or to the way the things are represented."]],"reflm:5061":["In regard to rules in general the following errors are committed. 1. Universal rules without particular determination. e.g.[Ub] to prevent indistinctness. 2. Many particular determinations without universal principles. The rules drawn from reason about a common object all have one *principium*, e.g. laws.\nAll consideration of method is the most important thing in a science. The method is either constitutive and scientific or dialectical and a mere manner. Idea.",["“principles” renders principien; “manner” renders Manier, as against Methode.","The deleted [Ub] is a word begun and broken off; left untranslated."]],"reflm:5062":["Other sciences can grow gradually through united effort and addition. The philosophy of pure reason must be sketched out all at once, because here it is a matter of first determining the nature of cognition itself and the universal laws and conditions, and not of trying out one's power of judgment at random.",["“at random” renders auf Gut glük (“on good luck”)."]],"reflm:5063":["Across the inner margin:\nThe transcendental dialectic is the critique of illusion, as the analytic is the doctrine of truth. Among the ancients it was for the most part |\na technique of illusion or sophistical art. The critique of illusion comes after philosophy. The critique of taste is no doctrine; taste [suff] gives rules and does indeed suffer restrictions, but not precepts; one ought not to have given it the name of a science, especially not the one that is borrowed from an old term that has a quite wide sense.",["[suff] renders the deleted leid, the beginning of leidet (“suffers”).","“precepts” renders vorschriften; “term” renders Benennung."]],"reflm:5064":["The analysis of the concepts that we already have does not by a long way suffice for a cognition of things *in concreto*; we must, through a *synthesin*, since we consider the concept in many cases *in concreto*, gather up much that belongs essentially to the concept but does not lie in it. Otherwise such *analyses* are empty (Baumgarten: *perfectio phaenomenon*). Just as when Philolaus said the earth moves, and Copernicus proved it.",["*perfectio phaenomenon*: “perfection [as] phenomenon”, Kant's Latin as written.","*synthesin*, *analyses*: Kant's Latin case forms of synthesis, analysis."]],"reflm:5065":["I have a very great opinion of the genius of the ancients. We act in a corps, which not only makes genius (as also bravery and skill at arms) dispensable, but also hinders it.",["“act in a corps” renders agiren im Corps, a military image (a body of troops)."]],"reflm:5066":["I am very well content if one finds the best of these doctrines here put forward among the ancients. That a judgment of the understanding |\nhas already lain before the sound concept of others is to be supposed and serves to unite the understanding of many. But I understood the passage in Locke *de iudiciis coexistentiae* only afterwards.",["“lain before” renders vorgelegen habe (“been present to”).","*de iudiciis coexistentiae*: “on judgments of coexistence”."]],"reflm:5067":["Those who, following the example of the Newtonians, assumed certain basic actions of the cognition of truth did not consider that a force such as is by itself the first must indeed be assumed with its peculiar effects, as gravity. But that a force originally to cognize the true, [because this] i.e. to cognize objects according to their true constitution, cannot be assumed without showing how either the objects are given by [the c] this basic force or at least their representation through it.",["“basic actions” renders Grundhandlungen; “basic force” renders Grundkraft; “objects” renders the Latinate Obiekte, obiecte.","[the c] renders the deleted der E, a word broken off."]],"reflm:5068":["Belief in God and another world is a consequence of the necessary maxims of theoretical as well as practical reason.",["“consequence” renders Folge."]],"reflm:5069":["The skeptical method gives a proof *a posteriori* for the discipline of pure reason.",[]],"reflm:5070":["Transcendental philosophy is very necessary, for in an empirical one we can be taught about our errors through experience.\nIt is strange that here a critique, hence first attempts at cognitions, must occur before a canon can be erected; for a doctrine it will after all never become.",["“be taught” renders belehrt werden; “attempts at cognitions” renders versuche von Erkentnissen."]],"reflm:5071":["If something intellectual is considered under a sensible condition, then with *praedicata sensitiva* both *opposita* are false; e.g. *Qvod non est in loco, nec est mobile nec immobile*. Yet these predicates are not properly contradictorily opposed, but *contrarie realiter opposita*. If God is not in places, then he is neither in one nor in many places.",["*praedicata sensitiva*: “sensitive predicates”; *opposita*: “opposites”.","*Qvod non est in loco, nec est mobile nec immobile*: “What is not in a place is neither movable nor immovable”; *contrarie realiter opposita*: “contrarily, really opposed”."]],"reflm:5072":["In all systems of reason something has always remained over, and it has successively enlarged itself. Mathematics keeps its acquisition, and its stock grows, in that daily something new is added. In natural science from Aristotle up to now something has always remained over even from false systems, after it was sifted; but metaphysics annihilates itself entirely, in that it makes room for another. The ground has not been investigated. What one took for the ground were the first stones that one laid and that slowly sank into a swampy ground. This necessitates casting suspicion on the methods and investigating the sources in the subject.",["“it has … enlarged itself” (sie hat sich … vergroßert): sie may be reason or the system.","Grund is used throughout as both “ground” (reason) and “ground” (soil, foundation)."]],"reflm:5073":["The critique of pure reason is a preservative against an illness of reason, which has its germ in our nature [namely]. It is the opposite of the inclination that fetters us to our fatherland |\n(homesickness). A longing to lose ourselves outside our circle and to move into other worlds.",["“preservative” renders Präservativ, a preventive remedy.","“move into” renders beziehen (to take up residence in)."]],"reflm:5074":["On the discipline. One cannot in place of the appearances, e.g. in space, posit intellectual concepts, of which the former would be only the confused representations, e.g. *ens obscure repraesentativum*.\nWhether nature is an *automaton*, or whether extramundane influences on it also take place. Providence.\nPreface. To isolate sensibility and understanding.\nTranscendental ideas are concepts from principles of speculation, not of the intellection of appearances, hence about the absolute totality in the synthesis of appearances.",["*ens obscure repraesentativum*: “a being representing obscurely”.","“On the discipline” renders Zur disciplin, which can also mean “For the discipline”; “principles” renders principien; “Providence” renders providentz."]],"reflm:5075":["In the transcendental science it is no longer a matter of going forward, but of going back.",[]],"reflm:5076":["Across the inner margin:\nThe boundary-divide of sensibility. Reason cannot restrict it through objections. It not reason through conditions, or foist something on it. Meanwhile it has a genuine *territorium* in regard to its principles.",["“It not reason through conditions” renders the elliptical Sie die Vernunft nicht durch Bedingungen; the verb is missing, and sie may be sensibility, with die Vernunft as object.","“boundary-divide” renders Grentzscheide; in the last sentence sie may be reason or sensibility."]],"reflm:5077":["Speculative maxims of reason are directed at the appearances in the world and nature, likewise at the boundaries of the world and original being, in order to obtain expansion and unity; they make use of the analogies of experience.",["“expansion” renders Ausbreitung; “original being” renders Urwesen."]],"reflm:5078":["There is a remarkable difference between the mathematical universal formulas and the philosophical ones. The former one cognizes as to their correctness only when one is conscious of their truth *in concreto*. Hence they are never empty. In transcendental philosophy nothing is objectively uncertain, because everything merely to the mere relation to object breaks off.",["“breaks off” renders bricht ab, which is probably the edition's note that the text breaks off rather than Kant's verb."]],"reflm:5079":["One will still be able to pick out much belonging to the *analysi* and the critique from ancients and moderns. But then that will happen which befell the Scholastics: they will be laid aside forever. The cause of metaphysics can no longer be held.",["“they” (sie) may be the ancients and moderns or the Scholastics.","“cause” renders Sache (also “matter”, “affair”)."]],"reflm:5080":["Among the maxims of reason belongs also: that nature everywhere makes a system, although our cognition is seldom such a one.",[]],"reflm:5081":["Baumgarten: The man was sharp-sighted (in the small), but not |\nfar-sighted (in the large)*. Instead of his aesthetic the word fits better: “critique of the beautiful”.\n*(g A Cyclops of a metaphysician, who lacks the one eye, namely critique.)\n(g a good analyst, but not an architectonic philosopher; his outlines of sciences.)",["“outlines” renders Abrisse."]],"reflm:5082":["The metaphysical hindrance of all morals is the denial of freedom, and of all theology the disavowal of necessity.",["“denial” renders Verleugnung, “disavowal” Ableugnung."]],"reflm:5083":["Across the inner margin:\nTranscendental philosophy proves that with our cognition [of the] we can never go out beyond the world of sense. If they seem to be more universal, then they are merely subjective. That even mathematics is the only science that can determine something independently of experience, hence completely *a priori*, That however the qualities must be given empirically.",["“they” (sie) has no antecedent in the passage.","“world of sense” renders Sinnenwelt; “qualities” renders qvalitaeten."]],"reflm:5084":["Across the inner margin:\nIf one leafs back and forth, nothing can seem more pedantic, although it is quite properly aimed at the abolition of everything pedantic in things that concern the nature of the soul, the future, and the origin of all things. But the human understanding errs here through subtlety and must be refuted through it.",["“through it” renders dadurch, which may refer to subtlety or mean “thereby”."]],"reflm:5085":["Across the inner margin: M XXXXIII:\nThat the [crit] transcendental philosophy as critique does not, just |\nas the *catharcticon* of the Pyrrhonians, purge itself out as well, but must necessarily be treated with diligence, just as one must always root out weeds, since the human understanding contains the seed for them, or the vices not, like the pox, |\nM XXXXIV: |\ncan entirely eradicate at once.",["“purge itself out as well” renders sich … selbst mit abführe (abführen, “to purge”, as a laxative does); *catharcticon*: “purgative”.","The subject of “can … eradicate” (kan) is not expressed; it may be the understanding or an implied “one”.","“Pyrrhonians” renders the genitive der pyrrhonischen."]],"reflm:5086":["In the world of understanding the *substratum* is: intelligence, the action and cause: freedom, the community: happiness from freedom, the original being: an intelligence through idea, the form: morality, the *nexus*: a *nexus* of ends. This world of understanding already now lies at the ground of the world of sense and is the true self-subsistent.",["“world of understanding” renders Verstandeswelt; “world of sense” Sinnenwelt; “original being” Urwesen.","“lies at the ground of” renders liegt … zum Grunde; “self-subsistent” renders selbststandige."]],"reflm:5087":["M XXXXII:\n* There are only four sources of cognition: 1. senses, 2. understanding, 3. divine report, 4. mystical illumination and intuition. The last we are not entitled to assume, because it is presumption. They are also not to be convinced. Senses and function of the understanding make up all cognition. For those who are partakers of the inner light this is not written.\nM XXXXI:\n*(g If only they could adduce something that someone had discovered through this intuition, without going the way of abstraction, from natural science; and if they cannot do that, how they can then hope to go even beyond it.)",["“divine report” renders Gottliche Nachricht (also “news”, “tidings”).","“They are also not to be convinced” (Sie sind auch nicht zu überzeugen): sie has no stated antecedent; it can also be read “They also cannot be convincing”."]],"reflm:5088":["The unity of understanding of appearance lies in the analogies of experience. The unity of reason of the principles of these different causes of appearance: where does it lie? and how is it objective? likewise how does it lead to the existence of God and of another world? likewise of the unity of ends?",["“unity of understanding” renders Verstandeseinheit, “unity of reason” Vernunfteinheit.","The genitive der Einheit der Zweke may depend on “existence” or stand loosely."]],"reflm:5089":["M XXXXI:\nDiscipline. We cannot judge synthetically through mere concepts of the understanding. They must always contain [data of the] conditions of appearances and serve only for their exposition. Mere concepts of the understanding can just as little as intuition of the understanding relate to an object. This is always determined through sensibility, for [we ca] our universal concept is only a sign for the concrete ones. Intellectual cosmology is just as well as mystical an *ens rationis*. The usual Scholastic and doctrinal method of metaphysics makes stupid, in that it effects a mechanical thoroughness. It narrows the understanding and makes it incapable of accepting teaching; it is not philosophy. Critique by contrast expands the concepts and |\nM XXXXII: |\nmakes reason free. The school philosophers do as freebooters do, who, as soon as they land on an unoccupied coast, immediately entrench themselves.",["“intuition of the understanding” renders Verstandesanschauung; “object” renders obiect.","*ens rationis*: “thought-entity”; “school philosophers” renders Schulphilosophen."]],"reflm:5090":["We have something in the head and have grasped it only when we can produce it out of ourselves, at least in ourselves. One has the words in one's head only when one can pronounce them within oneself. (To this belongs a consciousness of one's organs.) we have grasped what a line is when we can draw it in thought; and the con|nection\nof things in the world we can cognize only when we can produce it through a universal action, consequently from a *principio* of the inner potency. substance, ground, composition.",["“grasped” renders gefaßt; “connection” renders Verknüpfung; “potency” renders potentz."]],"reflm:5091":["The cognition of pure reason (speculative) can never be applied further than to the field of experiences and has beyond their boundary no significance at all. Mathematics itself contains nothing that cannot be demonstrated in experiences.\nNecessity in the empirical sense and freedom signify [things] that which on the one hand is at all times according to a rule, on the other hand what is not determined through sensibility but subjectively through the *synthesin* of the understanding.\nSulzer in his minor writings hopes in vain for demonstration.\nHere one can survey what and how much can be invented.",["“Mathematics itself” (Die mathematic … selbst) can also be read “Even mathematics”.","“invented” renders erfunden, which can also mean “discovered”."]],"reflm:5092":["To the dialectic of pure reason belongs also the rationalizing against space and time as *realia* or *idealia*, likewise against infinite division.",["*realia* or *idealia*: “real or ideal [things]”; “rationalizing” renders Vernünfteln."]],"reflm:5093":["Principles of pure reason are: that an absolute completeness of the presuppositions of synthesis be assumed. Hence the *principium necessitatis, contingentiae, compositionis et decompositionis*. They contain the condition of the absolute[unity] collective unity (systematic) [for] of cognition in general. In reason there is a *nexus* of prosyllogisms and episyllogisms. The completeness of this synthesis rests on the completeness both of the principles and of the application.",["*principium necessitatis, contingentiae, compositionis et decompositionis*: “principle of necessity, contingency, composition and decomposition”.","“prosyllogisms and episyllogisms” renders prosyllogismen und episyllogismen; “principles” in the last sentence renders Principien."]],"reflm:5094":["The concept of a substance already brings with it the concept of freedom; for if I could not myself act independently of external determination, then my action would be only the action of another, hence I properly the action of another, thus I not substance.",["The last two clauses are elliptical in the German (mithin ich eigentlich eines anderen Handlung seyn, also ich nicht substantz), depending on würde."]],"reflm:5095":["It is impossible that appearances should give something unbounded (g and absolute), whether it be as to expansion or division or as to causality or the condition of existence in general. Hence these negative propositions are objective and dogmatic; on the other hand reason requires the absolute and complete of the synthesis (*principia a priori*). These are objectively practical, just as those are objectively speculative.",["expansion: Ausbreitung, kept apart from Ausdehnung (extension).","These ... those: Die ... iene; the reference of each is left as open as in the German."]],"reflm:5096":["The efforts of the metaphysicians have not all been in vain. Only they have no stability without a system of the critique. They have had this in their thoughts.",["stability: Bestand (also permanence, standing).","They have had this: Sie haben dieses; sie may refer to the metaphysicians or to their efforts."]],"reflm:5097":["One must first make everything difficult for the presumptuous thinkers. They should not boldly appeal to their healthy reason. They are not traveling in a cultivated land.",["presumptuous: vorwitzig (also forward, impertinently curious).","healthy reason: gesunde Vernunft."]],"reflm:5098":["Am I traveling on a plane or a spheroid. The curvature I must measure.",["The question has no question mark in the source; kept so."]],"reflm:5099":["Matter and form.\nLogical and real.\nConcept\nof the predicate.\n* Agreement and opposition.\n[Logical is]\nreality\nnegation\nAbsolute and relative.\nLogical identity and diversity real.\n*(g either among themselves\nor with respect\nto a third; the latter is [or]\nidentity and diversity.)",["The asterisks are Kant's reference signs, carried over, not italics.","Logica et realis: feminine forms; the noun they qualify is not stated.","Agreement: consensus."]],"reflm:5100":["Across the inner margin:\n1. Morals: how I become worthy to be happy, without aim at the means of becoming partaker of happiness. 2. Worldly prudence: how I become happy without regard to my worthiness to be so. 3. Wisdom: how I become happy by becoming worthy to be so. Philosophy is the *organon* of wisdom and must treat of the vocation of my nature, the boundaries and the ends of my faculties. The 1st has nothing but *principia a priori*, the second *a posteriori*, the third from both.",["Morals: Moral; Worldly prudence: Weltklugheit.","vocation: Bestimmung (also determination)."]],"reflm:5101":["Locke thinks to get through with this, that he says: we have just as little distinct concept of body as of spirit.",["spirit: Geist (also mind)."]],"reflm:5102":["Those who limit themselves to what is generally accepted and [everyone] as it were through contract current, and hence seems generally intelligible, have an easy game in showing themselves with their shallowness in an advantageous way; they spread themselves with ease, because they [feel] no difficulties and feel themselves nowhere bound; they seem to extend cognition and acquire esteem, because [they] for them, as for bold persons, everything that they do with confidence also reliably befits them.",["current: gangbar (passable, in circulation).","The last clause is awkward in the German (zuverlaßig ansteht: reliably suits, befits); kept so."]],"reflm:5103":["The intelligible world has laws according to which I fit for every world, not merely for this one or the sensible world, into whatever arrangement of my own and external nature or society I may also come. It has its own *principium constitutivum*: God, and *regulativum*: moral laws. It fits with the rules of prudence, if these are extended; is not an object of intuition; of it no physiology is possible; it is an object of belief as to substance, but of the reflecting understanding as to the universal laws.\nWe also know God only through moral grounds and according to properties connected with morality. To settle the connection of the other world with this one, the transition is grounded on analogical inferences. But the *principia* of inference through analogy and the *argumenta practica κατ' ανθρωπον*, to which the former also refer, constitute the *transitum* (*argumentum ad modulum humanitatis*) *secundum assumta humanae naturae, non hominis singularis*.",["*argumenta practica κατ' ανθρωπον*: practical arguments addressed to the human being; *argumentum ad modulum humanitatis*: argument to the measure of humanity.","*secundum assumta humanae naturae, non hominis singularis*: according to the assumptions of human nature, not of the single human being.","the former: die ersten, presumably the *principia*; left as in the German."]],"reflm:5104":["The maxims of reason consist in this, that one assume constitutive unity in the whole of appearances (if one begins *a priori*) and regulative unity in the parts (if one proceeds analytically from the parts to the whole). That thus no causes be assumed other than those whose law can be found through observation (although of this itself the ground cannot be stated). That thus no spirits, no blind choice etc. etc. But in actions *a priori* freedom. That nothing be derived immediately from God, because one cannot observe his rules of action, although *a priori* God is regarded as the supreme *principium* of unity according to rules, even in the practical. *In summa*: that the understanding preserve its unity as to totality (constitutively) and the unity of the rules. It thus does not limit itself, but it nevertheless demands a first as *principium* of its synthesis.",["cannot be stated: nicht angeblich (not specifiable).","choice: Willkühr.","constitutively: constitutiv, which may qualify the unity or the preserving."]],"reflm:5105":["Dogmatic principles are (g determining) cognitions of reason through concepts. Thus the mathematical ones are not dogmatic, nor the principles of the empirical use of reason from concepts, for these do not determine, still less the principles of pure reason, but only the moral ones.",["principles: Grundsätze and principien both rendered so; principien is the Latinate member."]],"reflm:5106":["Anyone who wants to assess the products of pure reason literarily, i.e. through erudition, undertakes futile labor. He can thereby provide himself with objects of assessment. But because he has not subjected his own understanding to a critique, his critique is nevertheless always idiotic and not philosophical. It makes use of such principles whose examination is properly the end. Philodox instead of philosopher.",["idiotic: idiotisch, in the older sense of lay, unschooled.","erudition: Belesenheit (wide reading)."]],"reflm:5107":["The word »solely, merely, alone, only« against the words »in general, simply, absolutely«. The former are words not of limitations but of the *actus* of restriction. The words »on, through, to« are the [relations] functions of the categories.",["limitations: Schranken; restriction: Einschränkung.","simply, absolutely: schlechthin, schlechterdings.","on, through, to: an, durch, zu."]],"reflm:5108":["The German language is, among the learned living ones, the only one that has a purity which is peculiar to it. All foreign words are forever recognizable in it, whereas English and French can be filled with such without its being noticeable that they had fallen to them from elsewhere. Therefore it repays the trouble to pay attention to this and rather to make use of foreign words in parentheses. This attentiveness makes the language little by little rich and at the same time very significant and determinate.\nLimitations must be set to the new composition of words.\nThe universal of a language and the idiotism.",["learned living ones: unter den gelehrten lebenden, i.e. languages; the noun is not stated.","whereas: an die stelle daß (literally, in place of which).","idiotism: idiotismus (idiom, peculiarity of a language)."]],"reflm:5109":["M XXXXVII:\nThe original as magnitude is unlimited. As thing in general is simple. Original action (g origin itself) is free. Original existence is simply necessary. Everything externally derived is limited; as something that is given in appearance (g *mundus sensibilis*), composite; as originated, conditioned and dependent; as existing, conditionally necessary. The soul is no appearance. In it lies relatively the all of reality of all pos|sible\nappearances. It is with respect to sensations simple (I); with respect to actions [or] free; in regard to all existence of appearances a necessary *substratum*, which is subordinated to no appearance.\nWe have two principles (g *a priori*) of the empirical use of the understanding: principles of the exposition of appearances, i.e. of the determination of concepts from them; 2. of the architectonic use of reason in relation to the practical. The latter are merely intellectual.\nM XXXXVIII:\nIt is a necessary hypothesis of the theoretical and practical use of reason in the whole of our cognition, consequently in relation to all ends and an intelligible world, to assume that an intelligible world lies at the ground of the sensible one, of which the soul as intelligence is the subjective archetype, but an original intelligence the cause; i.e. just as the *noumenon* in us is related to the appearances, so the supreme intelligence is related in regard to the *mundus intelligibilis*; for the soul actually contains the condition of all possible appearances in itself, and in it all could, if only at the beginning the *data* were given, be determined *a priori*.\n(g Reason, *mundus intelligibilis*. Not new synthesis, but completed through boundaries *a priori*.)\nAmong appearances there is no end. The boundary of them and thus the completion of the synthesis is *in mundo intelligibili* according to the analogy of our soul and intelligence.\nThe principles: Everything in the world consists of the abiding and the alterable, which inheres in the former. Everything alterable, every state has a cause. And: everything that flows in is in community. This cognition of the *mundus phaenomenon* is never completed. But *in mundo intelligibili* everything must be completed; consequently it refers |\nto the *ens originarium*, where all this takes place unconditionally. No decomposition is completed.\nM XXXXVII:\nThe [determinability] unity of the (g absolute) whole of our cognition, determinable through reason, is the cause of the synthesis extended beyond the sensible world. i.e. the synthesis of it with the intellectual. From ideas. The idea of intelligence, independent of appearances, and of the *mundus intelligibilis*, which remains, and of the transcendental *principium* [of] the unity of everything.",["As thing in general is simple: the subject is not repeated in the German; kept so.","the abiding and the alterable: dem Bleibenden und Veränderlichen.","flows in: einfließt (exerts influence)."]],"reflm:5110":["Reason is, in regard to freedom and ends in general, determining, and freedom determines appearances. In regard to them there must be a first and single ground of everything.",["them: ihrer, which may refer to the appearances or to freedom and ends."]],"reflm:5111":["1, No absolute completion of the use of reason in sensibility.\n2, Liberation of reason from the restricting conditions of the same. Antinomy of reason: There is a completion of *compositio*, *decompositio*, of generation and of dependence, and there is also none. The completion is only *in mundo intelligibili* and its *principium* of unity: the *ens originarium*.",["of the same: derselben, which may refer to sensibility or to the use of reason."]],"reflm:5112":["The mathematician, the bel esprit, the natural philosopher: what do they accomplish when they practice arrogant mockery of metaphysics. In their inner self lies the call that at all times summons them [themselves] to make an attempt in its field. They cannot, if as human beings they do not seek their final ends in the satisfaction of the aims of this life, help asking: whence am I, whence is the whole. The astronomer is still more summoned to these questions. He cannot refrain from seeking something that would satisfy him herein. With the first judgment that he passes on this he is in the territory of metaphysics. Will he now here, without any guidance, rely merely on the persuasions that can grow up [in] him, although he has no map of the field that he wants to roam through. In this darkness the critique of reason sets up the torch, but illuminates not the regions unknown to us beyond the sensible world, but the dark space of our own understanding.\nMetaphysics is as it were the police of our reason in regard to the public security of morals and religion.",["the bel esprit: der schöne Geist.","persuasions: Überredungen.","The sentence beginning Will he has no question mark in the source; kept so."]],"reflm:5113":["Across the inner margin:\nThe concept of completion either through magnitude (all) or through the boundary or through purity and separation from every attached condition (e.g. possibility, necessity) or also the absolute, which depends on no [possibility] condition.",["concept of completion: Vollendungsbegrif."]],"reflm:5114":["Dialectic:\nNot only that illusion often deceives; but it is also necessary to explain how it comes about that I am deceived, e.g. at the rising of the moon.",[]],"reflm:5115":["It is a strange fate of human understanding when it sees itself, whether through a natural propensity or also through [the importance and] the true interest that drives it, entangled in a science and as it were condemned to it, which after centuries-long efforts with the united force of the most acute minds still cannot be brought a single step further. If one wants to give up the effort (g unwillingly), then [we would partly through a particular propensity] partly the natural motion of our mind pulls us back to it, partly we come up everywhere against questions in regard to our most important concern, in regard to which we cannot be satisfied otherwise than through some insight in this field. Of this kind I know, to the good fortune of human beings, only one single science, namely metaphysics, a theoretical philosophy of pure reason, i.e. of reason free of all sources of experience; it is the stone of Sisyphus, at which one restlessly rolls and without ever bringing it to its abiding place. When I say it has not been brought a single step further, I do not understand by this the analysis of the concepts of reason; for that is nothing other than the greater enlightenment of what we already know, and herein various people have done much in precise determination of the meanings of words. But that is not what one seeks; rather cognitions of objects that cannot be taught to us through any senses, which therefore do not dwell in us, but are to be sought and created: these are the ones in regard to which all labor up to now has been in vain. One can easily convince oneself of this if one but considers that even a single cognition that is a determinate contribution to science must be ac|cepted\nas such by everyone, e.g. air in bodies, whereas these come into vogue only through persuasion in certain places.",["Sisyphus: the source spells sysyphus.","analysis: Zergliederung; enlightenment: Aufklärung.","these: diese, with no clear antecedent in the German; kept so."]],"reflm:5116":["M LI:\nI have not at all times judged so of this science. At first I learned of it what most recommended itself to me. In some pieces I believed I could contribute something of my own to the common treasure, in others I found something to improve, yet at all times with the aim of acquiring dogmatic insights thereby. For the doubt so boldly uttered seemed to me so much to be ignorance with the tone of reason that I gave it no hearing. If one reflects with actual earnestness to find the truth, then one finally no longer spares one's own products, even if it should at the same time seem that they promise us a merit in regard to science. One subjects what one has learned or oneself thought entirely to critique. It lasted long that I in this way found the whole dogmatic theory dialectical. But I sought something certain, if not with regard to the object, yet with regard to the nature and the boundaries of this kind of cognition. I gradually found that many of the propositions which we regard as objective are in fact subjective, i.e. contain the *conditiones* under which alone we gain insight into or comprehend the object. Yet thereby I was indeed made cautious, but not instructed. For since there actually are cognitions *a priori* which are not merely analytic but extend our cognition, I lacked a critique, brought under rules, of the |\npure reason, but above all a canon of it; for I still always believed I would find the method of extending dogmatic cognition through pure reason. For this |\nM LII: |\nI now needed insight into how in general a cognition *a priori* is possible.",["“It lasted long that I ... found” renders Es dauerte lange, daß ich ... fand, which may mean “it was long before I found” or “for a long time I found”.","“gain insight into” renders einsehen, “comprehend” begreifen, “extend” erweitern.","“uttered” renders hingesagte, “said off-handedly”."]],"reflm:5117":["All maxims of pure reason are principles of the unity of reason, both in the empirical and in the transcendent use of it. What disturbs this unity of its use is to be rejected.",["“principles” renders principien, not Grundsätze; “to be rejected” renders verwerflich."]],"reflm:5118":["The world can only have a beginning for sensible intuition. For the beginning is a boundary of appearance *ab antecedenti*. But such an intuition itself presupposes a sensible subject, which itself belongs to the world and thus can never intuit a first beginning. It thus has no beginning. But this boundlessness does not belong to the magnitude of the things that appear, but to their appearance. For the pure understanding it has an origin.",["“It” (Sie) in “It thus has no beginning” may be the world or the intuition.","*ab antecedenti*: “on the side of what precedes”."]],"reflm:5119":["M IL:\nMetaphysics, insofar as it would go further than the pure principles of the understanding with regard to experiences, has no use at all other than negative, both with regard to the nature of the corporeal and the thinking, and in relation to that which is beyond or\n|\nabove nature. The latter cognitions must be cognized according to an analogy of the empirical principles continued beyond the boundary [contin] of the world, consequently as maxims of the universal unity of reason. Then the metaphysical concepts serve [the] polemically against dogmatic doubt, in order to set limitations to its objections.\nA physical utility of metaphysics is also to hold off the dogmatic synthesis *a priori* which can hinder the continuity of cognition according to laws of experience, and serves to that extent for the extension of it. 1. that one not come to ultimate parts; 2. that the different density not |\nM LII: |\npresuppose empty spaces; 3, that there be no material immediate influence without contact; 4. that the world have boundaries. The first and second assertion are principles of reason.\nUtility in (g rational) psychology: 1. that not all substances are material; 2. that experience is not the boundary of all cognition and this world not the world in general.\nIn theology: 1. that the world does not comprehend all things; 2. that not everything is contingent etc etc.\nThe utility is thus throughout negative. 1. To clear away dogmatic negations which restrict the empirical spread of cognition. 2. To restrict dogmatic assertions which would extend reason uselessly beyond the practical use.",["“use” renders Gebrauch, “utility” Nutze(n); “principles” renders Grundsätze, “principles of reason” Vernunftgrundsätze.","The deleted [fortg] is the start of fortgesetzten, “continued”; “limitations” renders Schranken, “boundary” Grenze."]],"reflm:5120":["Relation of the appearances to world-unity. Unity of the system, i.e. of the All.\nWorld-unity is the unity of nature in the whole. The latter is the unity of nature and has principles of the understanding. The other has merely principles of reason, according to which nature in the whole is related to something outside nature as a ground of its possibility. For the connection of nature is contingent, because it rests on many beings. The ground of it must be unity of the cause. Unity of nature is thus |\nderived from the original unity of the original being. The absolute unity is unity of reason. The absolute All is opposed to the hypothetical. The absolute unity of synthesis rests on the conditions of absolute universality.",["“The latter ... The other” render Die letzte ... Die andre; the German does not make clear what each refers to.","“connection of nature” renders Naturverknüpfung; “original being” renders Urwesen; “All” renders das All."]],"reflm:5121":["The only insoluble metaphysical difficulty is that of combining the supreme condition of everything practical with the condition of speculative unity: that is, freedom with nature or the causality of the understanding with regard to appearances. For freedom is the possibility of actions from causes of the understanding. The spontaneity of the understanding in the series of appearances is the riddle. Afterward absolute necessity is the second riddle, which nature does not pose, but the pure understanding. This is the original condition of the possibility of nature. With the first an appearance is not necessary, but contingent under the conditions of appearance. With the second something is necessary without any condition: thus the first absolutely contingent and the first necessary.",["“or the causality” may be a second name for nature or a further term combined with freedom; the German is as open.","“pose” renders aufgiebt, “sets as a task”; “This” (Dieser) is masculine, and so the understanding."]],"reflm:5122":["Whether one could say through nothing but transcendental concepts: everything that exists is in the substance; [has] the contingent has a ground; and: what is found outside one another is in a relation of a possible community. The definitions of these concepts must then turn out differently.",["The question closes with a period in the German; “definitions” renders definitionen."]],"reflm:5123":["If the actuality of something is to be proven whose concept cannot be other than transcendent: e.g. (g the *realissimum*) the simple, freedom, absolute necessity, then the proof can |\nnever be conducted from experience, but merely from transcendental grounds.",[]],"reflm:5124":["The proposition that synthetic propositions *a priori* take place only as principles of the possibility and exposition of experiences is independent of the explanation of the categories. For where else should they then come from?\nAnalytic judgments, because they do not extend, are not on that account to be despised.",["“explanation” renders Erklärung, which may also be read “definition”.","“principles” renders principien; “take place” renders statt finden."]],"reflm:5125":["My author Baumgarten is an excellent man in judgments of clarification, but when he passes over to judgments of extension, which are after all chiefly required in metaphysics, then he is without foundation.",["Erläuterungsurtheile / Erweiterungsurtheile = judgments of clarification / judgments of extension."]],"reflm:5126":["An organon is either according to form or according to content. The organon according to mere form (of a cognition of reason) is the doctrine of method. Thus [we have] the transcendental organon is only the doctrine of method.",["“doctrine of method” renders Methodenlehre."]],"reflm:5127":["1st part of transcendental philosophy is that of immanent |\ncognition *a priori*: analytic; — the second of transcendent: dialectic. Both parts of the critique.",[]],"reflm:5128":["Since we have cognitions *a priori* which, however, go only to experiences, we thereby go to things in general, because those are not borrowed from experiences.",["“go to” renders gehen auf, “bear on”, used twice in the German."]],"reflm:5129":["A science of things in general actually abstracts from all distinction and determinations of things as objects and thus treats merely of pure reason: transcendental philosophy.",[]],"reflm:5130":["At the lower margin:\nTranscendental philosophy has 2 parts: critique of pure reason and ontology.",[]],"reflm:5131":["At the very top of the page:\nOntology is the science of the first cognitions of the pure understanding: 1. of concepts, analytic. 2. of judgments.",[]],"reflm:5132":["On the distinction of concepts, intuitions and sensations. On empirical and pure intuitions and such concepts.",[]],"reflm:5133":["Transcendental philosophy (s which presents the elements of our cognition *a priori*) is a science of the possibility of a synthetic cognition *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5134":["We can indeed hold that to be impossible which conflicts with itself; but not what conflicts with the conditions under which we can only cognize the object. That is not on that account possible which does not contradict itself, but breaks off.",["“conflicts with” renders wiederstreitet, “contradict” wiederspricht.","“breaks off” renders bricht ab, probably the editor's note that the text stops, its angle brackets lost in the transcription.","“only” is placed as in the German and may attach to “we” or to “cognize”."]],"reflm:5135":["Something in the logical sense is an object; in the transcendental it is something or nothing, i.e. possible or impossible.",["“object” renders obiect, not Gegenstand; “sense” renders Verstande."]],"reflm:5136":["In and \nOf a contradictory subject there is no predicate.",["Latin in the original: *subiecti contradictorii nullum est praedicatum*."]],"reflm:5137":["Above and \nThe *principium contradictionis* is the negative universal, but not the only principle.",["*principium contradictionis*: “principle of contradiction”; “principle” renders princip."]],"reflm:5138":["*Principium contradictionis*: the restricting, not constitutive principle of all cognition, *conditio sine qva non*.",[]],"reflm:5139":["The *principium contradictionis* is the restricting, not constitutive principle.",[]],"reflm:5140":["Above and \nObject in general is possible or impossible.",["“Object” renders Obiect, not Gegenstand."]],"reflm:5141":["Impossible is: 1. that whose thought contradicts itself;\n—\n— :2. that to which no object belongs.",["“object” renders Gegenstand; “belongs” renders zukommt."]],"reflm:5142":["Being and non-being is the first; logical: copula, synthetic, analytic; real: possibility, actuality, necessity.",["logisch / real may be read as adverbs, “logically / really”."]],"reflm:5143":["Object either possible or impossible.",["“Object” renders Object, not Gegenstand."]],"reflm:5144":["Logical possibility, actuality, necessity taken for real (of things).",["“taken for” renders Gehalten für."]],"reflm:5145":["On the first four words of M § 13:\nAlthough the thought does not contrad breaks off.",["“contrad” renders the broken-off wieders (for wiederspricht, “contradicts”); “breaks off” renders bricht ab, probably the editor's note that the text stops."]],"reflm:5146":["Under and \nA thing can be undetermined in relation to a concept; posited in itself it is thoroughly determined; posited in itself signifies: relation to the whole possible concept.",["“relation” renders both Verhältnis and Beziehung; “thoroughly” renders durchgängig."]],"reflm:5147":["Because the matter of possibility must be given to us, and we cannot think it beforehand, all thinking concerns the form. An object of thinking in general for the human understanding is *formaliter*: what does not contradict itself, and *realiter*: what is given.",["“matter” renders materie; *formaliter* / *realiter*: “formally / really”."]],"reflm:5148":["Three principles. 1. Of contradiction, 2. of ground, 3. of determination.",["“principles” renders Grundsätze."]],"reflm:5149":["The relation in a judgment is either analytic or synthetic.",[]],"reflm:5150":["The possibility of things according to mere categories cannot be seen into.",["“cannot be seen into” renders läßt sich nicht einsehen."]],"reflm:5151":["The supreme principles are such only according to form, because they go to |\nsomething in general. not: from what, but: according to which one is to judge.",["“according to which” renders nach welchen, plural, referring to the principles; “one is to judge” renders geurtheilt werden soll, impersonal passive."]],"reflm:5152":["Through this, that something is actual, it can become object of intuiting. If, however, one abstracts from all actuality, then it still always remains an object of cognition. This is possibility.",["“intuiting” renders the verbal noun Anschauen, not Anschauung."]],"reflm:5153":["Beside and \n3 principles: 1. of the possibility, 2) of the truth, 3. of the necessity of judgments; the last always says *aut aut*, in order to prove apodictically and apagogically.",["*aut aut*: “either ... or”; “principles” renders Principien."]],"reflm:5154":["The *principium determinationis*. Is distinguished from the *principio omnimodae determinationis*.",["*principium determinationis*: “principle of determination”; *principium omnimodae determinationis*: “principle of thoroughgoing determination”."]],"reflm:5155":["The possibility of the concept (logical) is that the concept subsists in itself; that of the thing (real), that an object corresponds to the concept. Thus the relation to the universal *conditiones* through which something in general is given.",["“thing” renders Sache, not Ding; “object” renders obiect.","“subsists in itself” renders an sich selbst besteht, which may also mean “holds together in itself”."]],"reflm:5156":["Above and \n*Nihil*. 1, *negativum*, 2, *privativum*, 3. *conceptus inanis*, 4. *intuitus mere formalis*.",["*Nihil*: “nothing”; *conceptus inanis*: “empty concept”; *intuitus mere formalis*: “merely formal intuition”."]],"reflm:5157":["At the left margin \nUnfolding and derivation, contradiction and opposition.",["“Unfolding” renders Auswikelung (Auswickelung)."]],"reflm:5158":["At the right margin \nComparison, combination, connection.",["Vergleichung, Verbindung, Verknüpfung."]],"reflm:5159":["Comparison, interconnection, combination.",["Vergleichung, Zusammenhang, Verbindung."]],"reflm:5160":["In the notes to M § 14:\nIf I [the ground] posit something at will and something else is also posited, then the former is the ground.",[]],"reflm:5161":["Which of the two, a or b, which have the relation of ground to consequence, is *hypothesis*? Relation of connection:\n*Categoria*\n*subiecti*,\n— —\n*principii*,\n— —\n*compositi*.",["“consequence” renders Folge; “connection” renders Verknüpfung.","*Categoria subiecti, principii, compositi*: “category of the subject, of the principle, of the composite”."]],"reflm:5162":["*absolutum* is that which is considered in every relation; because we cannot do this *in concreto* [can] (i.e. *in casu vel qvaestione data*), we consider it *interne*, and what holds positively there holds also in every relation, but not the negative.\nLater (φ) written into the Rfl. in different ink:\nThe absolute to the restrictions",["The deletion [kön], a first start on können “can”, stands in the German before the parenthesis, after which the verb follows.","*in casu vel qvaestione data*: “in a given case or question”; *interne*: “internally”.","The last line breaks off."]],"reflm:5163":["What accords with the conditions of a concept in general is possible. What exists itself gives a concept. Mere possibility: what agrees with the conditions of a concept *a priori*.",["zusammenstimmt / stimmt = accords / agrees.","“itself” renders selber, which may attach to “what exists” or mean “of itself”."]],"reflm:5164":["5. E II 1369.\nM 5':\nBetween two *logice oppositis* there is no third, but between *realiter oppositis* (willingly, unwillingly) is the indifferent =0*. Motion and rest are not *contradictorie opposita*. For both do not agree with God and the world-whole. Such *opposita* are both restricted by a condition, as here that of existence in space; if the condition falls away, then there is no contradiction. e.g. The actions of human beings are as appearances |\nM 5: |\nnot free, but as *intellectualia* free. The one proposition has here a condition, the other an opposed one; in this case the *oppositum praedicati* can also be said of the other and thus two apparent *opposita* can at the same time |\nbe true. There is a necessary being *in serie ascendente*, but not *descendente*. In the one case it is a *hypothesis* of reason (g with presupposition of the members); in the other no such *hypothesis* is possible, for the former is conditioned, the latter categorical.\nM 5': |\n*(s The first can be seen into, the 2nd not.)",["The asterisks after =0 and before (s are Kant's reference marks, not italics.","*logice / realiter opposita*: “logically / really opposed”; *oppositum praedicati*: “the opposite of the predicate”; *in serie ascendente / descendente*: “in the ascending / descending series”.","“both do not agree” renders beyde stimmen nicht, which may mean “neither agrees” or “not both agree”."]],"reflm:5165":["In and \nThe synthetic conditions (g *principia*) of possibility are only objective if they contain the conditions of the possibility of experience, not of comprehension.\nA possibility is that which there are *data* to think; no possibility, that for thinking which there are no *data*.",["“comprehension” renders Begreifung."]],"reflm:5166":["In and \nThe logical concept of possibility: *principium contradictionis* (g *analysis*), or the real concept: *principia* of synthesis.",[]],"reflm:5167":["5. E II 505.\nSubjectively representations A and B can follow one another thus or also the reverse way. In order that the sequence hold objectively, i.e. serve for experience, it must be determined, so that I cannot reverse it.\nWhy is the *principium rationis sufficientis* posited next to the *principio contradictionis* as the only one?\nThe principle of |\nM 5: |\ndetermination, derivation and division.",["“sequence” renders folge.","“posited” renders Gesetzt; the reading may also be Gesetz, “as the only law”.","*principium rationis sufficientis*: “principle of sufficient reason”."]],"reflm:5168":["With *ratione* and *rationato* the sequence must necessarily be thought not objectively, but subjectively, namely: that, if I posit the ground, then the *rationatum* is posited.",["“sequence” renders Folge, which may also be read “consequence”.","*ratio / rationatum*: “ground / what is grounded”."]],"reflm:5169":["The connection of the ground with the consequence is synthetic or analytic.",["“connection” renders Verknüpfung; “consequence” renders Folge."]],"reflm:5170":["A cognition must 1. be possible as cognition; 2. true, i.e. grounded.",[]],"reflm:5171":["Every judgment has its ground, but not every thing is sequel.",["“sequel”: Folge."]],"reflm:5172":["The expression *synthesis* is more general than *nexus*.",[]],"reflm:5173":["The ground: *qvo posito*, when I arbitrarily posit it, something else is posited.",["*qvo posito*: “which being posited”."]],"reflm:5174":["Possible, impossible* and empty.\n*(g impossible is a=–a; for a–a=0 is possible.)",[]],"reflm:5175":["We can pass off much not [as] possible, also not as impossible, and yet no rational human being must believe it. Ghosts.",["“as”: vor (für); “mogliche” stands unrevised after the deletion, so the construction is uneven as written.","“must” (muß) may carry the older sense “need”."]],"reflm:5176":["Besides the *principio identitatis et contradictionis* there must still be other *principia* of the *nexus* and of the opposition. For through the former only the *nexus* and *oppositio logica*, but not *realis*, can be seen into. Which then are these *principia synthetica*?",["*principio identitatis et contradictionis*: principle of identity and contradiction; *oppositio logica … realis*: logical … real opposition; *principia synthetica*: synthetic principles.","“seen into”: einsehen."]],"reflm:5177":["5. E II 831.\nLogically impossible is that of which the thought itself is impossible; metaphysically impossible would be that of which the thought is indeed possible, but the thing impossible. But that we cannot assume and reject. Physically possible is what is possible according to the natural laws and conditions. Morally.\nM 5:\nThe metaphysical possibility of a thing in itself, insofar as it also cannot be an object of experience.",["“thing”: Sache; “object”: Gegenstand.","“cannot assume and reject” (nicht annehmen und verwerfen) can be read “can neither assume nor reject” or “cannot assume and [so] reject”.","“natural laws and conditions” (Naturgesetzen und bedingungen): “natural” may govern “conditions” as well."]],"reflm:5178":["In and \nThe possibility of a concept from the principle of *contradictio*, the possibility of the thing not.",["“principle”: Satz.","The location note appears incomplete as transmitted (“In und”)."]],"reflm:5179":["In and \nIn every relation (absolutely) it is possible to be righteous, but not to be always happy: thus to make oneself worthy of happiness, but not a partaker in it.",["“happy”: glücklich; “happiness”: Glükseligkeit; “a partaker in”: theilhaftig.","The location note appears incomplete as transmitted (“In und”)."]],"reflm:5180":["Below and \nThe possibility of things only through intuition or as principle of experience.",["“things”: Sachen; “principle”: princip.","The location note appears incomplete as transmitted (“Unter und”)."]],"reflm:5181":["What is possible only under a certain condition (g presupposition), its possibility is restricted by the condition; what is possible in a certain case, its possibility is extended, until that which is possible in every respect is possible absolutely, i.e. without condition.\nConditional possibility belongs to the *causatis*, absolute possibility to the first cause. Possibility *in abstracto* is merely the non-contradictory; this possibility signifies only the admissibility of the idea. Possibility *in concreto* is alone objective, i.e. that something be possible in the *omnimoda determinatione interna*. What is possible *omni respectu* is necessary, because it is then possible *absqve hypothesi*, |\nand at the same time the ground of everything or the All itself, for it consents with everything.",["“in every respect”: in aller Absicht; “absolutely”: schlechthin; “restricted”, “extended”: restringirt, extendirt.","*causatis*: the things caused; *omnimoda determinatione interna*: thoroughgoing inner determination; *omni respectu*: in every respect; *absqve hypothesi*: without hypothesis.","“the ground” may continue the predicate “is necessary” or the clause “because it is”; the ambiguity is kept."]],"reflm:5182":["*vid.* §. 14. Combined are a and b whose positing makes up one; [or] those whose combination is necessary according to a universal rule, i.e. from one of which one can infer to the other, are connected; those whose combination is contingent, i.e. etc etc., are composed. In all combination there is unity; only [in the] in that of composition it is arbitrary. The necessary transition from a to b is either that from the inner possibility of b, which presupposes a (*accidens*), or of actuality (*rationatum*), or of relation, state (*totum et pars*) (g what is in combination reciprocally according to the determinations). The relation to another makes up the concept of an *accidens*, e.g. of thought, of motion. The former is a *principium essendi*, the second *fiendi*. In the former lets from the given concept to bricht ab.",["“combined”: verbunden (Verbindung); “connected”: verknüpft; “composed”: zusammengesetzt; “positing”: position; “relation”: Verhältnis, and in “The relation to another”, Beziehung.","“which presupposes a” (welche a voraussetzt) can also be read “which a presupposes”.","The last sentence breaks off and lacks “sich” after “läßt”; *principium essendi … fiendi*: principle of being … of becoming."]],"reflm:5183":["Above and to the right of the heading »Connexum«, but nevertheless probably \nThe ground as ground consists in causality; but as thing it can also be posited without sequel.",["The location note appears incomplete as transmitted."]],"reflm:5184":["The analytic criterion of possibility is that there be no contradiction, and is not to be seen into. The synthetic criterion of possibility cannot be seen into from concepts. The synthetic conditions of the possibility of experience are at the same time the conditions |\nof the possibility of the objects of experience. But this is not the possibility of things in themselves.",["“seen into”: einsehen; “objects”: Gegenstande."]],"reflm:5185":["*Possibilitas interna et externa* by itself or in relation is something other than conditioned and unconditioned.",["*Possibilitas interna et externa*: inner and outer possibility; “by itself”: für sich."]],"reflm:5186":["Conditioned possibility (s possibility also *in nexu* is greater) is smaller than absolute, for the latter is not restricted; material possibility *non solum in thesi, sed hypothesi* is greater.",["*in nexu*: in connection; *non solum in thesi, sed hypothesi*: not only in thesis but in hypothesis."]],"reflm:5187":["The inner or relative possibility; the latter is the absolute (in all relation) or restrictive.",[]],"reflm:5188":["*nexus* of subordination: through ground. All connection has a ground.\n*nexus* of coordination: through common ground.",["“connection”: Verknüpfung; “common”: gemeinschaftlichen."]],"reflm:5189":["7. E II 1072.\nThe *principium rationis* as anticipation, hence with the conditions of sensibility, is objective. (g for in time there is always something preceding.*)\nThe *principium rationis* as a *postulatum* of *perspicientz* is subjective.\nThe *principium terminationis* as a demand of comprehension is subjective.\nIf both are made objective, they conflict with one another; but subjective, not.\nM 7:\n*(g What as contingent (arising, *transitorium*) is given only [through] by means of sensation and not *a priori* by [itself] ourselves, has a ground. (s but contingent means precisely that which exists not for itself, but only as *rationatum*.) Free actions are given *a priori*. But occurrences, or what takes place, only through experience and *a posteriori*. The former is the beginning (*terminus*), to be cognized according to a universal law; the second cannot without an *antecedens* be cognized according to universal rules and requires for this a ground.)",["*principium rationis*: principle of ground; *principium terminationis*: principle of termination; *perspicientz*: Kant's Germanized *perspicientia*, insight.","“demand”: Forderung; “conflict”: widerstreiten; “occurrences”: Begebenheiten.","“without an *antecedens*” may attach to “be cognized” or to “according to universal rules”; the ambiguity is kept."]],"reflm:5190":["The principle of ground in the world (g is cosmological, not transcendental) is a principle of cognizing, not of acting.",["The Reflexion is Latin: *principium rationis* (principle of ground), *principium cognoscendi, non agendi*."]],"reflm:5191":["This proposition signifies: nothing happens except according to a universal rule of the imagination. For that it is represented in the *obiect*, i.e. [by] the [Vorstell] determination by us in the object, comes precisely from this.",["*obiect* and “object” (Gegenstand) render two German words; [Vorstell] is an unfinished word (Vorstellung, “representation”).","The construction of the second sentence is uneven as written and is kept so."]],"reflm:5192":["Beside and \nThe true proposition is: *Omne contingens est rationatum*. Only |\nthat whose opposite is in itself possible has a ground, and where the impossibility of the opposite is hidden, there the thing cannot be taken to be true before such has become manifest.\nBut the existence of all things seems to be in itself contingent; at least we do not understand the absolute necessity in existence, and our insight pertains only to the *respective* positing. In this, however, that which is *respective* posited is always posited by something else, although the *nexus* between ground and sequel bricht ab.",["*Omne contingens est rationatum*: everything contingent is something grounded; “thing”: sache; “taken to be true”: vor wahr gehalten.","*respective*: relatively; “positing”: position.","The location note appears incomplete as transmitted (“Neben und”)."]],"reflm:5193":["Everything in the world has a ground means just as much as: it can be cognized *a priori* (s as necessary, either absolutely or conditionally) and stands under a rule of order. *1st synthetic proposition *a priori*. Everything has a ground of cognition.\nEverything must be able to be cognized *a priori*, i.e. from the action of the mind under which a connection is brought.\n(g Conversely I cannot infer securely, as in no affirmative judgment.)\n*(s All *coniugation* presupposes the unity of the action in the mind under which the appearance can be brought, thus insofar as the positing of the one is connected with that of the other according to a rule.)",["“ground of cognition”: Erkentnis Grund; “action of the mind”: Gemüthshandlung; “positing”: position.","*coniugation*: conjugation, joining together."]],"reflm:5194":["Everything that happens, i.e. whereupon follows, must always follow upon it, |\ni.e. stands with regard to what precedes under a rule. The appearances have a rule. 1. That something always precedes. 2. That through this the *conseqventz* is determined (always follows upon it).",["“whereupon follows” (worauf folgt) is elliptical as written: “[what] follows upon [something]”."]],"reflm:5195":["That through which something else is posited determinately is ground; through the sequel a ground is posited indeterminately.",["“determinately”, “indeterminately”: bestimmt, unbestimmt, which can also be read as participles, “posited as determined”."]],"reflm:5196":["In the last third of M § 20:\nEverything is necessary, absolutely or conditionally.\nEverything must flow from the universal as something particular; *a priori* everything can be cognized.",[]],"reflm:5197":["This *principium* is a maxim of the use of reason.",[]],"reflm:5198":["Everything whose inner possibility consists in relation has a ground. This is an identical proposition. All composition. all succession. What happens. everything purposive.",["“relation”: Verhältnisse; “succession”: abfolge."]],"reflm:5199":["Synthetic propositions. Everything is in a substance. 2. Everything contingent has a ground. 3. everything united has a common |\nground. Precisely these propositions applied to sensibility, e.g. What happens has ground.",["“in a substance”: an einer substantz, also “on” or “attached to” a substance."]],"reflm:5200":["In and \nThe concept of ground gives a rule in general. The whole of the grounds either in an aggregate or in a series is complete.",["The location note appears incomplete as transmitted (“In und”)."]],"reflm:5201":["The non-determining ground is the ground of possibility, e.g. when someone has staked in the lottery.",["“has staked”: gesetzt hat (setzen, “posit”, also “stake”)."]],"reflm:5202":["7'. E II 1085. 1084.\nThe *principium rationis* is the principle of the determination of things in the temporal sequence; for it cannot through time, but the latter must be determined through the rule of the existence of appearances in the understanding. *Principium* of the possibility of experience.\nM 7':\nThus it is not possible to determine for things their place in time without presupposition of this principle, through which the course of appearances is first made uniform. Succession in the concept of sequel concerns only the sequel [of that] in positing.",["“temporal sequence”: Zeitfolge; “sequel”: Folge.","“it” (die) and “the latter” (diese) are left as ambiguous as the German; the first verb is not supplied.","“place”: Stelle."]],"reflm:5203":["With everything passive or what is given, the apprehension must not only be met with, but also be necessitated, in order to repre|sent it as given,\ni.e. the single apprehension must be determined through the universal. The universal is the relation to the remaining ones and to the whole of the state. Through its being distinguished from the arbitrary, it is considered as given, and only through its being subsumed under the categories, as something. It must thus be represented according to a rule, so that appearance becomes experience and so that the mind comprehends it as one of its actions of self-consciousness, in which, as in space and time, all *data* are met with. The unity of the mind is the condition of thinking, and the subordination of every particular under the universal the condition of the possibility of associating through an action a given representation with others. Even if the rule does not fall into the senses, one must nevertheless represent the object to oneself as in conformity with a rule, in order to conceive it as that which represents something, i.e. has a certain place and function among the remaining determinations. The beginning cannot be determined according to a subjective rule, consequently not free action, which has its beginning in every point.\n(g Everything that happens, but not as a first beginning (freedom), has its determining ground.)",["“necessitated”: genöthigt; “mind”: Gemüth; “conceive”: concipiren; “place”: Stelle.","“so that the mind comprehends it” (damit es das Gemüth … begreife) can also be read “so that it comprehends the mind”."]],"reflm:5204":["Contingency can be represented empirically or intellectually. In the latter case it is a concept of reason, i.e. *a priori*. Now that everything which *a priori* is in itself undetermined and yet is determined must be through something else is identical.",["“undetermined”, “determined”: undeterminirt, determinirt."]],"reflm:5205":["The proposition: everything contingent has its ground, is quite correct, only that contingency can be cognized only through what happens; consequently the proposition does not hold beyond the boundaries of possible experience.",[]],"reflm:5206":["Ground is that which being posited, something else is posited.",["Latin: *ratio* (ground); *quo posito ponitur aliud*, an ablative absolute, “which being posited, another is posited”."]],"reflm:5207":["The question whether the *principium rationis* is subjective or objective, and in what sense it is objective, is grounded on the exposition of the question: by what do I cognize empirically that something is a ground and something a sequel. Without doubt only by this, that A can never be without B, but B indeed without A. Now that everything is as it were in one series, and indeed where each is combined according to a universal rule of order with an *antecedens*, that is the ground — —",["“sense”: verstande; “exposition”: Erorterung."]],"reflm:5208":["The things that are given to us *a posteriori* must just as well have a relation to the understanding, i.e. a kind of appearance through which it is possible to get a concept of them, as a relation of sensibility, i.e. a kind of impression through which it is possible to get appearance. Hence everything that can become known to us only *a posteriori* (through senses) will stand under the universal conditions of a concept, i.e. be in conformity with the rules through which it is possible to get concepts of things and to connect everything with the concepts of things and to subordinate it to them. Accordingly everything will appear in such a way that there must be a possibility of cognizing it *a priori*. Free actions are already given *a priori*, namely our own.",["“kind”: Art; “relation”: Verhaltnis."]],"reflm:5209":["Since we do not see into the possibility of a real ground: how then will we say *a priori* that there must absolutely be such, except where something according to the rule of identity already relates to a presupposition. Does this *principium* not hold as anticipation, because without rule we would also have no experiences, but this rule consists in the order of time and of space according to universal laws?",["“see into”: einsehen; “real ground”: realgrundes; “absolutely”: durchaus; “identity”: identitaet."]],"reflm:5210":["The ground is that through which, when I posit it, something else is posited according to a rule, [not] not according to an empirical rule, which has no true universality, but according to a true rule.",[]],"reflm:5211":["Above and \nThat everything that appears must appear in relation to the whole is to be seen from space and time. But that it stands under a rule is to be seen from this, because otherwise it would not appear in this whole according to the unity of the relation to it.\nIn the world something is, according to time and circumstances, always a sequel in the appearance. The question is whether it is also a real sequel, i.e. connected according to a universal rule.",["“sequel”: Folge.","The location note appears incomplete as transmitted (“Über und”)."]],"reflm:5212":["The proposition: everything can be cognized through reason. Every assertion must be able to happen *a priori*. Because *a posteriori* only appearances take place, but the things are independent of their appearances. |\nBut all cognition *a priori* goes from the universal to the particular, from the necessary in itself to the contingent.",["“assertion”: assertion; “things”: Sachen."]],"reflm:5213":["Beside and \nSomething can well appear, but never appear completely, without its standing under a rule *a priori*, i.e. in relation (*coniugation*) with the other, which is determinable *a priori*. For since one appearance is connected with the other because the unity of the action connects them, thus all of them among one another, [with] the whole state is connected with a subjective ground, so unity in the manifold must flow from this, without which no real appearance can take place.\nFree actions as appearances are determined by grounds, but as (g *physice*, but not *practice* *) automatic actions they are original, and the subject determines itself according to grounds. The subject is determined, but not passive, and the necessity of its action itself is necessarily unknown to it.\n*(g what is practically determinable must be physically undetermined.)",["*coniugation*: conjugation, joining together; *physice … practice*: physically … practically; “passive”: leidend.","“itself” (selber) may go with “the necessity” or with “its action”.","The location note appears incomplete as transmitted (“Neben und”)."]],"reflm:5214":["All determination in time can take place only in accordance with a principle *a priori*, i.e., so that in accordance with a rule each thing follows upon something preceding.",["“principle” renders princip."]],"reflm:5215":["That which is the condition under which we would posit something in accordance with a rule is the cause. If I let a stork fly in winter, it does not become warm. Thus it is not the cause.",[]],"reflm:5216":["The empirical laws *a priori* contain the conditions of apprehension and conception (together, of intellection). We can combine nothing in appearances and thus give them a real form except by combining them to one another, through one another and with one another, and the appearance determines the mind to this activity. Thus something is possible as object of experience only insofar as it appears in accordance with the laws of apprehension; i.e., if its appearance were whole, it would have to hang together in accordance with the laws of apprehension. Just as nothing can appear except in the universal sum total of space and time, so nothing can become experience except insofar as it is combined with other things in accordance with the universal law of the activities of the mind. Thus nothing happens contingently, i.e., without being subject, with regard to that with which it is combined (whether this now also appears or not), to a universal rule. For the ground of a particular connection we can encounter only in an object insofar as it contains that which, to be connected, can be subsumed under a universal rule. Ground, namely, and sequel are not mere apprehensions, but inferences or universal actions of transition.\nEverything that is given to us through experience must be possible to cognize *a priori*, i.e., its possibility must be able to be cognized from the laws of sensibility or of the understanding, [which vo] in relation to which alone experiences take place. That it can be cognized *a priori* signifies: that it has an object and is not merely subjective modification.",["“and the appearance determines the mind” may continue the clause “except by …” or stand on its own, as in the German.","“sequel” renders Folge; “inferences” renders schlüsse; the second “object” renders obiect, the first Gegenstand.","The struck words “welche vo” break off; “vo” is left as it stands."]],"reflm:5217":["Every object of the senses is in a series in which it is determined *a priori*: the principle of empirical cognition with regard to that which happens; everything that is there has a ground, is a *principium* of cognition *a priori* or of reason. Everything |\nthat has a ground has a first ground: *principium* of comprehension.",["“principle” renders Grundsatz; *principium* is Kant's Latin."]],"reflm:5218":["A complete ground is determining.",[]],"reflm:5219":["Everything contingent in itself happens through a ground; for, were it necessary in itself, then it would happen in itself in accordance with a rule and would be the *principium* of it; since it is not such, it is a *principiatum* of a rule.",["*principiatum*: “that which follows from a principle”."]],"reflm:5220":["The principle of sufficient ground is the *principium* of order in the course of nature, that of freedom of the beginning in the series of things. Because there must be a beginning in the series of sequels, there must be freedom, and even in the course first beginnings are possible. If there is nothing but nature, then the series of the [subordi] connected is continuous. But if there is freedom, then it is interrupted.",["“that of freedom of the beginning” keeps the elliptical “der der freyheit von dem Anfange”; “that” can be the principle of freedom or the *principium* of freedom.","“principle of sufficient ground” renders Satz des zureichenden Grundes; “sequels” renders Folgen.","The struck “subordi” breaks off and is left as it stands."]],"reflm:5221":["Everything that happens happens in accordance with a rule, [lies] is determined in the universal, can be cognized *a priori*. Thereby we distinguish the objective from the subjective play (fiction), truth from illusion. The appearance has an object if it is a predicate of a substance, i.e., is one of the ways [itself] to cognize that which persists; thus appearances belong to the representation of something persistent only insofar as they are connected with one another and have unity through something commonly valid. Something can indeed appear to us without the ground of it appearing to us; but we cannot cog|nize\nit without the cognition presuming a ground, because otherwise it would be no cognition, i.e., objective representation.\nThis is thus a condition of the cognition of the objects, consequently of the objects themselves, for mere appearance does not yet give an object. [Ob] It is indeed no condition of apprehension, for that goes immediately to appearance, without knowing its ground. But the appearance belongs to a whole of time, and in this they can be connected only if they flow from the universal. Through time things are not connected, but in time through the universal of their determinations.\nWe [can ourselves] must, if we apprehended the appearance completely, be able to make ourselves a concept of generation. This, however, is possible only if the contingent that happens, taken in the whole [taken] or in its whole determination (g relation), is necessary. If I did not represent to myself that the occurrence was necessary in consideration of the whole or was a side of that which is constant, then I would hold my representation to be no cognition and thus also not to be something that belongs to an object.",["“object” renders obiect throughout; “representation” renders Vorstellung; “concept of generation” renders Erzeugungsbegrif.","The struck “Ob” is left as it stands: it may be “Ob” (“whether”) or the start of “Obiect”.","“they” in “they can be connected” has no plural antecedent in the sentence, as in the German."]],"reflm:5222":["The *qvo posito ponitur aliud* signifies the position by the understanding and thus the one that I myself perform, consequently not *qvod datur ut phaenomenon*.\n2nd: [according to] what, if I posit it at all, posits something else in accordance with a universal rule. It is thus not the *nexus phaenomenorum*. For these give no universal rule. *Posito rationato* |\n*secundum regulam communem non ponitur ratio*, because the affirmative proposition does not let itself be converted.\n*Nexus* of insition, of subordination, of coordination.",["*qvo posito ponitur aliud*: “which being posited, something else is posited”; *qvod datur ut phaenomenon*: “what is given as phenomenon”.","*Posito rationato secundum regulam communem non ponitur ratio*: “when what is grounded is posited according to a common rule, the ground is not posited”.","“insition” renders insition (Latin insitio, “engrafting”)."]],"reflm:5223":["Every concept is considered as if it stood in a determinate relation to all possible predicates, and the object thus lies in a determinate *positu* toward all possibility and is thoroughly determined.\n*Determinabile* is the transcendental matter.",["“relation” renders Verhaltnis; “thoroughly determined” renders durchgangig bestimmt.","*positu*: “position”; *Determinabile*: “the determinable”."]],"reflm:5224":["All concepts *a priori*, arbitrary, [and] transcendental and moral concepts permit a logical essence,[but the] which is at the same time real essence, because, without the concept preceding, we would have none at all *in concreto*; but the concepts *a posteriori*, which are of empirical origin, permit no cognition of the real essence.",["“essence” renders wesen; “arbitrary” renders Willkührliche."]],"reflm:5225":["Inner determinations are *affectiones*, outer ones *relationes*. The *relationes* of the first [ground] cause are no *affectiones*. The *relationes* of all created beings are *affectiones*; their relation cannot change [without] (variation) without their inner alteration (mutation); that is the property of a thing which in general belongs as a part to the whole and where this partiality is a condition of its existence. Can one not say of this being that all its affections are at the same time relations and an inner alteration cannot be without outer?",["“in general” renders überhaupt, which can attach to “belongs” or to “a thing”.","“alteration” renders Veränderung; “property” renders Eigenschaft.","“this being” renders “diesem Wesen”, with “ihre” (“its”, literally “their”) following, as in the German."]],"reflm:5226":["13. E II 993.\nA law goes to the intention, which is to be good. The rule to the exercise and procedure for any discretionary intention. Hence practical geometry has only rules, not laws, likewise prudence. Yet physics has laws, because it does not contain what ought to be done, but what happens, and thus the agreement with that which is in accordance with a ground not discretionary but actually given. Laws have a universal validity, rules can have a mere common validity. The arithmetical rules are of the first kind; the rules of thrift, of hab|its,\nM 13: |\nof foods, of politeness are only *in subsidium* of the practical understanding *in concreto* and are necessary because we do not place particular trust in its correct use or our own inclination, the former to guide well on average, the latter to bring under constraint. They are the go-cart of weak heads, of the childish.",["“which is to be good” renders the subjunctive “welche gut sey”.","“universal validity” renders allgemeingültigkeit, “common validity” gemeingültigkeit; “discretionary” renders beliebig.","“go-cart” renders Gängelwagen, a child's walking frame; *in subsidium*: “in aid of”."]],"reflm:5227":["Guideline, guiding thread. Through the norm everything is determined down to the individual actions, e.g. spherical trigonometry. One need only go along the thread. e.g. Mechanical construction.",["“guideline” renders Richtschnur (literally a measuring cord); “guiding thread” renders Leitfaden."]],"reflm:5228":["Possibility, actuality and necessity are indeed logical, but not [real] metaphysical predicates, i.e., determinations. Through them we cognize not the (g something in the) things, but the relation of their concepts to the faculty of the mind to posit and to cancel. 1. The |\nrelation to the faculty (possibility), secondly to the activity, thirdly to the activity whose opposite is not in our faculty.\nthetic proposition: modal or pure.",["“things” renders Sachen; “relation” renders Verhaltnis; “cancel” renders aufheben.","“to posit and to cancel” may depend on “faculty” or on “relation”, as in the German."]],"reflm:5229":["If every possible were *qvodammodo indeterminatum*, then some determinations would not be [to] possible.\nHence, because every determination (g also) must belong to mere possibility, but existence does not belong to it, it is no determination.",["*qvodammodo indeterminatum*: “in some way undetermined”."]],"reflm:5230":["everything that exists is thoroughly determined, for it is posited objectively, i.e., absolutely; but it is not therefore determined through its concept. Conversely: what is thoroughly determined through its concept is not therefore existing, precisely because it is posited only respectively to the concept.",["“respectively to” renders “respectiv auf”, i.e. relatively to."]],"reflm:5231":["Synthetic judgment. 1. relative to the concept; 2. also beyond the concept, in itself; 3. thoroughly determined through the concept.\nAll existential propositions synthetic.",["“beyond” renders “über”, which can also mean “about”."]],"reflm:5232":["Of things, matters and beings. The thing is [undetermined with regard to the] without personality, but merely insofar as it is thought; matter, insofar as it is given.",["“things” renders Dingen, “matters” and “matter” render Sachen and Sache (not Materie), “beings” renders Wesen.","“it” in “insofar as it is given” is es, as in the clause before."]],"reflm:5233":["The thing seems to be the object as distinguished from its determinations.\nThat whose determinations contradict one another or are empty concepts is a non-thing.",["“non-thing” renders Unding."]],"reflm:5234":["Skill has rules, prudence maxims, morality laws. The lack of skill (g compliant unskillfulness) is replaced by a norm or guideline (pattern), the [mere animal capacity] (g recalcitrant) needs discipline. [(Examples]",["“guideline” renders Richtschnur; “pattern” renders Muster; “morality” renders Sittlichkeit.","“the … (g recalcitrant)” has no noun after the deletion; presumably “Ungeschiklichkeit” is understood, but the German leaves it open."]],"reflm:5235":["Prudence can be subjected [according to] to rules, which yet can never be so determinate for all cases that a practical understanding would not be specially required to cognize whether a case is under this rule or not. For the rules state only that which, taken on average, would be good to do. Thus there is no rule without exception. But laws are always without exception, although not without restriction, if they contain merely *motiva obligandi* and not *obligantia*.",["“specially” renders besonders, which can also mean “particularly” or “separately”.","*motiva obligandi*: “motives of obligating”; *obligantia*: “what obligates”."]],"reflm:5236":["Morals has laws, practical instruction rules or norms, guideline, only in order not to err in the exercise. One sets oneself rules if one does not trust oneself to conduct oneself in individual cases according to prudence.",["“guideline” renders Richtschnur."]],"reflm:5237":["The rule goes only to the way in which that which one wills is to |\nhappen; the law contains the *imperativus* of that which is to happen (of the end); the maxim is the subjective law, i.e., that which one has resolved for oneself to do universally.\nBecause the rule is the application of a law *in concreto*, for which healthy understanding is required, whose place cannot be replaced by a universal prescription, the rules, because they are abstracted empirically from the most cases and therefore have no true universality, also do not hold punctiliously, and there is thus no rule without exception.\nLaws ought to be without exceptions, likewise axioms; but rules are never without exception, for they are the breaks off.",["“healthy understanding” renders gesunder Verstand.","“breaks off” renders the editor's “bricht ab”: the note ends after “the”."]],"reflm:5238":["Laws [are have a merely] are objective prescriptions, rules prescriptions from a subjective ground. Rules are always practical, but serve to apply a law well *in concreto*. Of this, laws cannot in turn be given, but healthy understanding is required, and in its absence rules, which never guide surely, but only fittingly in most cases; whoever therefore keeps to his rule should know how to make the exceptions according to healthy understanding. They are the go-cart of the stupid. When someone is given rules of politeness, rules of life. What cannot be cognized *a priori*.",["“go-cart” renders Gängelwagen; “rules of life” renders Lebensregeln."]],"reflm:5239":["Rules that one makes for oneself in eating, in play, in social intercourse are often elevated into laws, when one does not trust oneself much good on account of the exceptions, chiefly on account of affect.",[]],"reflm:5240":["The law (g the command, *praeceptum*, if it is the will of another), the statute (g *statutum*), the guideline (norm), the guiding thread (g formula, model, pattern, archetype. Idea).\nLaws of religion contain the canon and the statutes. The former are *principia normalia*, the latter observances (*formalia*). The canon is *principium constitutivum*, the statutes *regulativa* and arbitrary, can thus not burden the conscience.",["“statute” renders Satzung; “guideline” renders Richtschnur; “guiding thread” renders Leitfaden; “pattern” renders Muster; “archetype” renders Urbild."]],"reflm:5241":["Rules are prescriptions of discretionary use; thus all arithmetical, geometrical etc. etc. rules are problematic. Laws are rules of necessary use. Thus they enunciate a necessity. All transcendental rules find laws.",["“find” renders “find” as printed; it may be a misreading of “sind” (“are”).","“discretionary” renders beliebig."]],"reflm:5242":["Nature has laws, skill rules.",[]],"reflm:5243":["In relation to the *data*, e.g. That to a thing possible in itself the conditions of existence are also given, consequently [the Mo] that it is synthetically possible. e.g. A spirit.",["The struck “die Mo” breaks off; “Mo” is left as it stands.","“spirit” renders Geist."]],"reflm:5244":["Order is a condition of distinctness, this of the cognition of truth, but order is not truth.",[]],"reflm:5245":["Perfection is a mathematical concept, expresses totality, *completudinum*, and is only one (the *maximum* is the proper measure for objects of the understanding; for there it must not be arbitrary, but given: perfectly evil, hell). The [form] quality in that which is perfect is the *consensus* to one. That, however, is only the form of perfection. This pleases particularly, the material or end may be whatever it will; just as talents without merits, because this *consensus* can still be steered toward the good or imitated. The children of the world are more prudent etc. etc. Finally a perfection presupposes the end, consequently the first grounds of satisfaction and dissatisfaction, and can only be borrowed from feeling.",["*completudinum*: genitive plural of *completudo*, “of completenesses”, as Kant writes it.","“satisfaction and dissatisfaction” renders Wohlgefallen und Misfallen; “more prudent” renders klüger.","“the material” renders das Materiale."]],"reflm:5246":["Good is what reason cognizes as object of the will. The agreement with an end of reason.",[]],"reflm:5247":["Beside and \nThe idea is unique (*individuum*), self-sufficient and eternal. That is the divine in our soul, that it is capable of ideas. The senses give only after-images or even apparences.",["The editorial location note “Neben und” is cut off in the source.","“apparences” renders apparentzen (Latin apparentia), not Erscheinungen; “after-images” renders Nachbilder."]],"reflm:5248":["Idea is the representation of the whole insofar as it necessarily precedes the determination of the parts. It can never be represented empirically, because in experience one goes from the parts through suc|cessive\n*synthesis* to the whole. It is the archetype of things, because certain objects are possible only through an idea. Transcendental ideas are those where the (g absolute) whole [the] in general determines the parts in the aggregate or series.",["Urbild: archetype.","durch successive synthesis: Kant's Latin *synthesis*, split across the line as in the source."]],"reflm:5249":["The inner necessity of a thing cannot be thought at all; consequently all such necessity must be respective, either in every *respectu* or only in some, i.e. absolutely necessary. The internally breaks off.\nNot every thing is absolutely possible.",["The sentence leaves open whether \"i.e. absolutely necessary\" glosses the first alternative only or both.","Das innerlich bricht ab: \"bricht ab\" (breaks off) is presumably the editor's note that the sentence Das innerlich … is unfinished.","absolut moglich ist nicht iedes Ding: literally \"absolutely possible is not every thing\"."]],"reflm:5250":["Above and \nInternally necessary, i.e. without any relation, nothing can be thought except merely a judgment in which a relation lies, but which itself stands in no relation at all to other judgments. But absolutely necessary, i.e. in every relation, a thing can be, if it contains the *condition* of all possibility. 1. as a *datum originarium* the primitive and universally valid *existence* (matter of possibility); 2. as a |\nsingle thing, because the possibility of many rests on the difference of the *negations*. These, however, are limitations. But the limitations ultimately presuppose the unlimited, and particularity the *omnitudinem*.",["Beziehung: relation; allgültig: universally valid.","einiges Ding: single (one and only) thing.","schranken: limitations; das uneingeschränkte: the unlimited."]],"reflm:5251":["Inner contingency is not yet respective, still much less absolute contingency. Only freedom is an absolutely contingent action *respectu* of the *stimulorum*. [It is free]\n*omnimode* contingent (throughout): of this we cannot see into the existence.",["einsehen: see into (have insight into).","omnimode zufallig (durchaus): contingent in every way; Kant's German gloss durchaus is kept as \"throughout\"."]],"reflm:5252":["The relation of every possible thing to the All, i.e. the thoroughgoing determination, whereby it as it were borders on everything possible and receives its determination merely through this bordering, proves that we represent it as contained in an All. Explanation of this appearance.",["durchgangige bestimmung: thoroughgoing determination.","grentzt … Angrentzung: borders … bordering."]],"reflm:5253":["At the lower margin:\nAbsolute necessity we can indeed think, so that we understand it; but to see into it, to think it *a priori*, is not so easy.\nThe concept of the understanding of the absolute necessity of a proposition is problematic, the concept of reason is [dogmatic or] definitive. The definitive concept is either subjective and a concept of method or objective and dogmatic.",["gedenken: think; verstehen: understand; einsehen: see into.","Verstandesbegrif / Vernunftbegrif: concept of the understanding / concept of reason; methodenbegrif: concept of method."]],"reflm:5254":["At the inner margin, crosswise \nWhat is internally possible is not yet possible in every relation. |\nBut what is possible in every relation can be so only as a ground, and hence it is a universally valid and necessary being.",["Am Innenrand quer: the AA editor's note on where the entry stands.","allgültiges … wesen: universally valid … being (Wesen)."]],"reflm:5255":["*Praedicatum est vel constitutivum vel modale, prius determinatio* (a predicate is either constitutive or modal, the former a determination).\nExistence is no constitutive *praedicat* (*determinatio*), it thus also cannot be found *per analysin* from the concept of a thing as belonging to its content. Thus it cannot be objectively proven from concepts, but can well [as] in relation to everything else as derived concepts be regarded as a necessary *substratum*.",["Daseyn: existence.","als abgeleitete Begriffe is left as ambiguous as the German: it may attach to alles andre (everything else, as derived concepts) or to the way existence is regarded."]],"reflm:5256":["A thing cannot be internally necessary where it is not necessary in every respect; on that account, since its necessity is restricted by no condition, it is regarded as internally considered.",["in aller Absicht: in every respect (Absicht, elsewhere aim or intention).","restringirt: restricted (Latinate)."]],"reflm:5257":["The first hypothesis of everything is necessary in every respect, but the inner necessity cannot be seen into.",["in aller absicht: in every respect.","eingesehen: seen into (einsehen, have insight into)."]],"reflm:5258":["The absolutely necessary can be represented only as necessary condition of the cognition of things in general, and indeed of all things. It is then thought as condition of the possibility of the things, and indeed on account of the derivation of all possible relations from |\nthe relation of each given one to the All of possibility it must consist in the all-sufficiency of the condition.",["schlechthin nothwendige: absolutely necessary (schlechthin, not absolut).","Dinge and Sachen are both rendered \"things\"; Sachen in \"possibility of the things\".","Verhaltnisse: relations; Allgnugsamkeit: all-sufficiency."]],"reflm:5259":["The absolutely necessary is the *substratum* of possibility in general, the *ens realissimum*, namely insofar as all possibility consists in the limitation of it.",["Einschränkung: limitation."]],"reflm:5260":["The first hypothesis of possibility is necessary, that of all possibility is absolutely necessary. Necessary in every respect as hypothesis is only a single one, because everything is only once.",["schlechthin nothwendig: absolutely necessary (schlechthin).","In aller Absicht: in every respect; ein einziges: a single one."]],"reflm:5261":["Absolute necessity is when the opposite conflicts with the [condition] synthetic condition of possibility; *nihil vel logice tale vel transscendentaliter* (nothing either logically such or transcendentally).\n(s Absolute necessity, since it cannot be cognized according to analytic principles of the understanding, will be so from synthetic ones.)",["wiederstreitet: conflicts with (Widerstreit, not Widerspruch).","principien: principles (Latinate, beside Grundsatz)."]],"reflm:5262":["At the upper margin and at the outer margin \nSomething is indeed necessary in every *respectu* (*absolute et sine conditione restrictiva*) if it is necessary in itself (*interne*); but we have no concept of the inner necessity of a thing, |\nbecause positing and cancelling are in themselves equally possible; thus absolute necessity must be grounded on a necessity of a presupposition related to everything in general.\nEither something is necessary under [every cond] a condition (*restrictive*) or in some application (*application*) *extensive*. Under every condition nothing is necessary; for if it already has a condition, then it is not necessary under the opposite.\nAbsolute necessity is the necessity of a presupposition (*hypothesis originaria*) with regard to everything thinkable. Namely the *data* for all *determination*, the all-encompassing sum total of reality. This relates to the whole of sensibility[as well] and of the senses, as well according to form as to matter. Whoever would see into himself entirely with regard to all his sensitivity and form, then [would] we would be able to determine in advance everything that can only be an *object* for us.\nThe *hypothesis originaria* is not the necessity under a *hypothesi*, but the necessity as *hypothesis*.",["setzen und aufheben: positing and cancelling; Allinnbegrif: all-encompassing sum total (Inbegriff).","Empfindsamkeit: sensitivity; obiect: object (Latinate, beside Gegenstand).","Wer … würde, so würden [würde] wir: the construction shifts from \"whoever\" to \"we\"; kept as broken."]],"reflm:5263":["[What contradicts itself is not in accordance with the conditions of understanding.]\nHow the conflict of the subjective conditions, or their presupposition, imitates and substitutes for the truth of the objective. *e.g.* A mathematically infinite is possible, because it does not conflict with the rules of insight; it is impossible, because it conflicts with the conditions of *comprehension*. Everything has its ground, because without this the being of the thing cannot be seen into; Something has no further ground above itself, because it would otherwise conflict with the conditions of comprehending. There is no freedom (transcendental). There is |\none. There is an absolutely necessary being, because without this no comprehending. There is no etc etc. being, because the same conflicts with insight. But if these concepts are taken only according to the conditions of *apprehension*, then they are restricted to the objects of the senses and also have only a restricted significance. *e.g.* Everything that happens has in a certain order a first ground, or rather the *axiomata* are not transcendent, but hold only as anticipations. So that they hold only of the *datis* of the senses, insofar as they can be understood. *e.g.* What happens has a ground, because without a rule, according to which it is given according to laws of sensibility, one would think no object through the representation.\nThe *principium contradictionis* contains the *conditiones* of thinking in general. The *anticipationes*, which affirm the *conditiones* of *apprehension* of the concepts of the understanding (*e.g.* In every substance there is *aliqvid perdurabile*, or a substance endures always), contain the conditions (g *postulata*) of understanding and are thus with regard to the sensible conditions always true.\n(To understand something under its determinations (representations) i.e.: to think or posit an object that corresponds to them.)\n*Apprehension* without the *conditiones* under which something can only be given is a function, but not yet a concept, or rather potential. To understand something is: to cognize, since it is given beforehand; but to see into: to cognize *a priori*; to understand logically is: to cognize something distinctly.",["Verstehen: understanding; Einsicht / einsehen: insight / see into; Begreifen and Begreifung: both comprehending, beside Kant's Latin *comprehension*.","wiederstreitet: conflicts with (Widerstreit); Wesen: being; Obiect: object (Latinate, beside Gegenstand).","da es vorher gegeben ist: da may be causal (since) or temporal (when)."]],"reflm:5264":["If I cancel the light, I retain space with the negative predicate; if I cancel cognition, I retain the subject, if I cancel a part from the whole or a sequel from a cause, I retain the universe or the cause. But if I cancel everything and retain nothing, then this is an empty thought, because no object is left over of which something would be predicated. Accordingly the |\n[void] transcendental void is nothing at all and also cannot be thought.",["aufheben: cancel; folge: sequel.","Leere: void (the noun, beside leer, empty, in leerer gedanke)."]],"reflm:5265":["Another All of things is absolutely impossible, another world is indeed possible in itself, but hypothetically impossible.",[]],"reflm:5266":["At the very top of the upper margin and crosswise at the inner margin:\nOnly the contradictory opposite of the determinations (namely *omni respectu*) proves contingency; but now the *opposita determinatio* at another time is not the *contradictorie oppositum* and thus does not prove contingency. The change of the determinations proves still much less the change of the things, thus also not the contingency of the *subiects*. There is no transition from the *principiis* of appearance to the concepts of reason, thus also not from alteration to contingency.",["Wechsel: change; Veränderung: alteration.","Sachen: things."]],"reflm:5267":["The succeeding opposite is the counterpart.",["Wiederspiel (Widerspiel): counterpart, reverse; succedirende: succeeding (Latinate)."]],"reflm:5268":["The alterable is to that extent not determined through concepts. Thus logically contingent, not with regard to existence.",["Das Veränderliche: the alterable; Daseyn: existence."]],"reflm:5269":["Above, beside and \nIf I think a thing *partim reale, partim negativum* (partly real, partly negative), then I think it first of all [as real] throughout as real and separate some reality from it; for *negare est realitatem tollere* (to negate is to remove reality). Consequently, if I think it in its thoroughgoing determination, I must lay the concept of the *realissimi* at the ground.\nThat the *ens realissimum* be logically possible is no doubt; whether it be *realiter* possible, 1. so that all reality exists in it as subject, 2. or through it as ground, or 3. from it as part of a whole? Of this one can cognize only that the last could not be *ens realissimum*.",["zum Grunde legen: lay at the ground.","durchgängige Bestimmung: thoroughgoing determination."]],"reflm:5270":["42'. E II 1623.\nThe principle of thoroughgoing determination: *qvodlibet existens est omnimode determinatum, i.e. ens qvodlibet per se non nisi ut omnimode determinatum dari potest, sed per conceptum de ipso multimode potest esse indeterminatum* (whatever exists is determined in every way, i.e. any being whatever can of itself be given only as determined in every way, but through the concept of it can be undetermined in many ways).\nThat proposition has in thought the sum total of all possible predicates with their *oppositis* and, since to existence merely reality belongs, the sum total of all realities with their *oppositis*; and, since every thing that does not contain all reality always presupposes an other that contains it: so it also has before its eyes the concept of a thing that contains all reality, as *entis logice originarii*, whose reality or the sequels of it through *limitation* give all things and in relation to which [a th] every thing can alone be determined in distinction from others.\nIf I represent to myself the understanding that thinks reality as light and, insofar as it cancels it, as darkness: then one can think thoroughgoing determination either as a carrying of light here and there into the darkness or the darkness as mere limitation of the universal light, and thus things are distinguished only merely through the shadows, reality lies at the ground and indeed only a single universal one. In the opposite case all things are distinguished only through their light, as if they had been originally lifted out of the darkness. But I can well think a negation if I have reality, but not if no reality is given. Thus reality is *logice* the first, and from this it is inferred that it is also *metaphysice* and objectively the first. |\nM 42': |\nBecause objects of the senses are not given through the understanding (g and in general not *a priori*), here negation is the first and the darkness, out of which the light of experience works out shapes. Thus in accordance with appearances [is] are manifold originally, and unity arises when one abstracts from manifoldness.",["Dunkelheit and Finsternis are both rendered \"darkness\"; Einschrankung and *limitation* both \"limitation\".","geschlossen: inferred (schließen); Inbegrif: sum total; folgen: sequels.","Erscheinungen gemäß … sind Manigfaltige Ursprünglich: the subject of sind is not expressed; kept as in the German."]],"reflm:5271":["In and \nThe *principium omnimodae determinationis* is: Every thing stands, with regard to its entire possibility, under the concept of the *entis realissimi*.",[]],"reflm:5272":["One cannot say: every thing is either thoroughly determined or not, but rather: it is such at all times, but only not [through th] always through the concept of it. except the *ens realissimum*.",["durchgängig determinirt: thoroughly determined (determinirt, Latinate, beside bestimmt)."]],"reflm:5273":["The metaphysical doctrine of the *genus summum*, *species* etc etc. and of thoroughgoing determination is properly the doctrine of the *ente realissimo*, as the sum total of all determination of things, the *substrato* of the All to some and one.",["vom All zu einigem und einem: literally \"from the All to some and one\"; the phrase is obscure and is kept so."]],"reflm:5274":["The *principium determinationis* says that every thing is at all times determined with regard to one of *praedicatis contradictorie oppositis*.\nThe *principium omnimodae determinationis* says that it is determined with regard to all together.\nThe principle is. All negations in things are *limitations*. Consequently the *ens a priori omnimode determinatum* is the *ens realissimum* and lies at the ground of our concepts of the real possibility of things just as the *princip contradictionis* does of the logical. The former is the material principle, and all negations are only the formal of things considered transcendentally.",["Grundsatz and princip are both rendered \"principle\".","*ens a priori omnimode determinatum*: *a priori* may attach to the being or to determinatum."]],"reflm:5275":["Magnitude is a relation of comparison [from], the three remaining ones of connection; the logical relation is also [the] either of comparison: *identity* and *diversity*, or connection: universal and particular.",["Große: magnitude; Verhaltnis: relation; Verknüpfung: connection."]],"reflm:5276":["*Qvantum et mensura* (*Multitudo, unitas*).\n*Compositum et simplex*.\n*Totum et pars*.\n*Substantia et accidens*.",["Quantum and measure (multitude, unity). Composite and simple. Whole and part. Substance and accident."]],"reflm:5277":["Mathematics is the science of the *construction* of concepts; thus it pertains only to intuition as such and has only empirical use.\nPhilosophy pertains only to concepts of being in general, hence to that which corresponds to sensation, and thus cannot make its concepts intuitive. It pertains for that very reason also to objects independently of the conditions of intuition.",["geht auf: pertains to (literally goes to); Seyn: being.","anschauend machen: make intuitive."]],"reflm:5278":["In and \nEverything real exists either merely as *determination* of an other (the way of being of an other) or also merely as *subiect*. The former existence is inherence — In our state — the latter subsistence.",["existentz, inhaerentz, subsistentz: Latinate German forms; Art zu seyn: way of being."]],"reflm:5279":["In and \n*Nexus* of sustentation.\n*Nexus* of subordination: of the series.\n*Nexus* of coordination: aggregates.",["The location heading \"In und\" (\"In and\") is incomplete in the source: the references it joined are missing."]],"reflm:5280":["Dependence and adherence, [necessity] necessity and self-sufficiency.\nThe existence of what is grounded is dependence, of the predicate: inherence.\nWhat does not exist except as part of another is limited (a worldly being); what exists, even if it is not part of another, is the most real being, the world (allness, universality).",["Abhängigkeit: dependence; Anhängigkeit: adherence (hanging-on); Selbständigkeit: self-sufficiency.","\"Die existentia rationati\": German article with Latin; rationatum is rendered \"what is grounded\"."]],"reflm:5281":["Above and \nThe first of things is the absolutely necessary.\nThe first of states is the absolutely contingent.\nNecessity and freedom incomprehensible, because that breaks off.",["The location heading \"Über und\" (\"Above and\") is incomplete in the source.","schlechterdings: absolutely; unbegreiflich: incomprehensible (begreifen).","ienes (\"that\") is singular; what it refers to is left open by the source."]],"reflm:5282":["In and \n1. the accident inheres in the subject. 2. subject is *causa*. 3. accidents make up a whole.",["The location heading \"In und\" (\"In and\") is incomplete in the source: the references it joined are missing.","Latin: accidens inhaeret subiecto; accidentia faciunt totum."]],"reflm:5283":["Predicates are either *determinationes* or *accidentia*. The |\nmagnitude is no *accidens*. Of intellectual relations, e.g. *substantia*, ground, whole.\nWhat is in space or time is its *accidens*. — *antecedens*, *consequens*, *Spatium*.",["Verhältnisse: relations.","dessen (\"its\") is singular: it may refer to space or to time, or to what is in them."]],"reflm:5284":["Categories of the real subject, ground and whole as distinguished from the logical ones. They are real relations.",["Realverhältnisse: real relations."]],"reflm:5285":["Because our understanding cannot think otherwise than through judgments, we also can have no concepts of things except through predicates, [which] which are combined with something constant as characteristic marks of the subject. Hence the concept substance [otherwise] and accident otherwise has no significance.",["Kennzeichen: characteristic marks (not Merkmal).","verbunden seyn: \"are combined\"; the subjunctive may also read \"are to be combined\"."]],"reflm:5286":["The existence of something in a *substratum*.\nThe existence of something through a *principium* in a series.\nThe existence of something in a *composito*. There is not inherence, but still reciprocal connection.",["Daseyn: existence; Verknüpfung: connection.","*composito* is kept in the case Kant gives it (dative of compositum)."]],"reflm:5287":["Combined with the substance, time gives persistence or alteration in acting or suffering. When the substance is altered, that signifies its inner state.\nThat inheres which as a predicate belongs to another and has the ground of existence in it.\nSubstances are things that do not inhere; *accidentia* [are] however are in turn determinations in which nothing inheres. They all inhere in the same subject. Unity.",["Beharrlichkeit: persistence; Veränderung: alteration; leiden: suffering (being acted upon).","in ihm (\"in it\") may refer to the other or to the predicate."]],"reflm:5288":["*entelechia*, the first proper *substratum*, or the ground of all appearances to be cognized through the pure understanding. The rest is *substantia phaenomenon*.",["eigenthümliche: proper, peculiar.","\"to be cognized\" (zu erkennende) may mean \"that can be\" or \"that is to be\" cognized."]],"reflm:5289":["At the very bottom of the page, \n1. *Substratum* (g inherence). 2. Principle of causality (g consequence). a. real ground. or synthetic. The causality of the substance with regard to the accidents is force. primitive, derivative. Action, passion, influence. 3. Community. Reciprocal action. Threefold unity, whose functions are *a priori*, but can be given (constructed) only *a posteriori*.\nSubstance, ground, composite. Appearances. Time is constant, while [determinations] appearances change.",["The location note \"Zuunterst auf der Seite,\" breaks off in the transcription.","Latin: Principium Caussalitatis; ratio realis sive synthetica; Vis; Actio, passio, Influxus; Commercium; Triplex Vnitas; construi.","beständig: constant, permanent."]],"reflm:5290":["Above and \nThe relation of a substance to the accident is merely *actio*. *Vis*. That of the substances among one another can be *actio* and *passio*; when that is *mutua*, then it is *commercium*.\nSince we know a thing only through its predicates, we cannot know the subject by itself alone.",["The location heading \"Über und\" (\"Above and\") is incomplete in the source.","\"when that is *mutua*\": die (feminine) may refer to the *actio* or to the relation (Verhältnis is neuter).","kennen: know."]],"reflm:5291":["Above and \nThe substance is only alterable and does not change. It is altered in that the state changes; i.e. it persists.",["The location heading \"Über und\" (\"Above and\") is incomplete in the source.","veränderlich / verändert: alterable / altered; wechseln: change."]],"reflm:5292":["2. Substance as cause, 3. As part of a whole in community.\nThe *substantiale* is the thing in itself and unknown.",["Ursach: cause; Gemeinschaft: community."]],"reflm:5293":["The real relations are opposed to the ideal ones; these are only relations of the representations, not of the objects. The ideal ones are aesthetic or logical.",["\"objects\": obiecte."]],"reflm:5294":["Bodies are *substantiae comparativae*, *substrata phaenomenorum*. It is ridiculous to want to think the soul as corporeal; for we have the concept of substance only from the soul, and that of body we form for ourselves according to it. The transcendental concepts must not overstep the boundaries of the *intellectualium* and make the sensitive in the same understanding into the intellectual. e.g. substance as *noumenon* or *phaenomenon*; consequently not the proposition: bodies are divisible.\nNecessity cannot occur in experience, likewise substance; hence the intellectual concept does not hold in its entire purity of the sensible.",["*substantiae comparativae*: comparative substances; *substrata phaenomenorum*: substrata of appearances; *intellectualium*: of intellectual things.","\"in the same understanding\" (in demselben Verstande) may also mean \"in the same sense\".","\"of the sensible\" (von dem sinnlichen): \"with respect to\" or \"from\" the sensible."]],"reflm:5295":["At the very bottom of the page:\nWhat is absolutely (in every respect) (g in itself, not logically) subject, the last subject, which does not further as predicate presuppose another subject, is substance. That predicate, [which] to which no |\nfurther other universal concept can be given as subject, is that through which the substance is immediately represented as a something to which this predicate adheres.\nThe mind intuits the substance. [The sustentation] Of external things only the actions, from which substance is inferred, because they are predicates.",["schlechthin: absolutely; in aller Absicht: in every respect.","Gemüth: mind; anschauen: intuit; geschlossen: inferred."]],"reflm:5296":["At the very top of the page:\nThe sustentation seems to designate a relation of one thing to the other, and the inherence is nothing further than the representation of a real predicate, i.e. of an action of the substance, among which the fundamental action designates the substance.",["unter denen (\"among which\") is plural, though \"action\" before it is singular.","Grundhandlung: fundamental action."]],"reflm:5297":["Between substance and *accidens* the logical relation is synthetic. The subject is itself predicate (g for one can think everything only through predicates, except I.), but on that account only that is called a subject which is no further predicate: 1, because no subject is thought for it; 2. because it is the presupposition and *substratum* of the others. This latter can be inferred only from the continued duration, while the other changes. Hence it belongs to the essence of a substance that [it] it be perdurable. If one assumes that the substance ceased, then this ceasing proves that it is no substance, and since therefore for this appearance no *substratum* is thought, then there are predicates without subjects, hence no judgments and no thoughts.",["darum (\"on that account\") may point back to the parenthesis or forward to the reasons 1 and 2.","Fortdauer: continued duration; perdurabel: perdurable.","[it] it: es struck out and replaced by sie."]],"reflm:5298":["All concepts are predicates, and these are substance or *accidens* |\nor relation. Space and time are neither of the two, hence not at all predicates of objects in themselves. Space and time are intuitions *a priori*. For from their concepts the propositions [not synthet] (synthetic) cannot be derived which we have of them *a priori*. How are intuitions *a priori* possible? Not otherwise than that the form of intuiting something through senses, without matter, i.e. a given object of the senses, can be represented by itself. Hence space and time are forms of sensible intuition. Hence we can cognize much *a priori* of space and time and of the objects in them, i.e. as objects of the senses, which for that very reason does not hold of the same objects as things in themselves.",["\"neither of the two\": keines von beyden, though three are named.","\"objects\" renders both obiect and Gegenstand (\"as objects of the senses\")."]],"reflm:5299":["A whole is either of derivation or of composition. The latter is that in which one is not in a certain state without another also being in a similar one. Thus many make up a real unity. e.g. When one part of a body cannot be moved without the other being moved along with it. Here everything with substances comes down to the reciprocal influences of the alterations.\nComposition is the contingent unity of the many. Hence not every whole is composite. e.g. space, because the unity here precedes the multiplicity or the multiplicity presupposes the unity in order to be thought in it. This unity consists in the connection either with a third or among one another. This composition is either ideal or real; the first through taking-together, the second through connection.\nA whole is thus either of continuation or of composition, the former is *qvantum continuum*, the second *discretum*. The former has contingent multiplicity in one, the second contingent unity in many.\nSomething through which much is posited is not for that reason a whole, but that which consists of many (each of which subsists without the thing itself and the thing itself only through each of them). God is thus no whole.",["Vielheit: multiplicity; Vieles: many; Zusammennehmung: taking-together; Verknüpfung: connection.","bestehen: \"consist\" and \"subsist\" render the same verb."]],"reflm:5300":["In and \nThe simple of separation, what is not composed of several of different *species*, e.g. water.",["The location heading \"In und\" (\"In and\") is incomplete in the source: the references it joined are missing.","Scheidung: separation (as in chemical analysis)."]],"reflm:5301":["Conjunction is either of mere composition and is ideal, or of inherence and is real.",[]],"reflm:5302":["Something is simple either according to quantity or also according to quality: *compositum similare*; the first is that which does not consist of any parts; the second, that which does not consist of heterogeneous ones.",["*compositum similare*: a composite of like parts.","\"the second\": Latin qvod non constat heterogeneis."]],"reflm:5303":["On the beginning of § 224 »Ens compositum est«:\nA complex (g of several) of things really determining one another. One of several aggregated things is a complex. [Complex of aggregated things] If the unity [aggrega] of the complex consists in the identity of the complex, it is a [whole] *quantum*. A *quantum* is an ideal composite.",["[aggrega] is a struck-out beginning of a word.","\"One of several aggregated things\": unum plurium aggregatorum, perhaps \"the one of several aggregated things\"."]],"reflm:5304":["A complex of several, insofar as it is thinkable through composition alone, is called a composite, and this precedes the simple, if it is said to be possible in itself only through composition.",["hoc praecedit simplex: \"this precedes the simple\" or \"the simple precedes this\"; the Latin allows both.","The subject of dicatur (\"it is said\") is not stated."]],"reflm:5305":["*Compositum ideale* is that whose parts, without belonging to the whole, are impossible by themselves.",[]],"reflm:5306":["The division of the inseparable is partition and is *compositum ideale*.",["Theilung: division; Eintheilung: partition (the glossary gives \"division\" for both)."]],"reflm:5307":["That composition which is supposed to contain the ground of the possibility of the relation is ideal.",[]],"reflm:5308":["A whole is either quantitative or qualitative.* The latter is a *compositum* or *qvantum discretum*. *Partes qvalitativae* are called members. They are distinguished from the whole according to quality. e.g. rainbow and bow simply.\n*(g The relation of a whole of substances is *commercium*.)",["*Partes qvalitativae*: qualitative parts; glieder: members.","schlechthin: simply, absolutely."]],"reflm:5309":["Magnitude*, whole, composite: how are they distinguished?\n*(g What contains the same many times.)\nThe *correlatum* of magnitude is unity.\nThe *oppositum* of magnitude is the simple, absolute unity.\nThe *correlatum* of the ground is the consequence, the *oppositum* is *sterile*.",["\"consequence\": folge.","einerley: the same."]],"reflm:5310":["Every whole is divisible or has parts which can be distinguished, but is not for that reason composite; for sometimes the parts are possible only through division.",[]],"reflm:5311":["Every body can be considered as composite only after the partition. If one wants to cancel all composition, all partition must first be thought as completed.",["Eintheilung: partition; aufheben: cancel."]],"reflm:5312":["In and \nA *phaenomenon* which is a *substratum* of other *phaenomenis* is not for that reason substance except only *comparative*. In the appearances we cannot cognize something as substance (this is only a concept of apperception), but something appears only as the substratum of the appearance, to which everything in the appearance is ascribed.",["The location heading \"In und\" (\"In and\") is incomplete in the source: the references it joined are missing.","*phaenomenis* is kept in the case Kant gives it; *comparative*: comparatively."]],"reflm:5313":["That space and time are intuitions without things signifies that they must be no objective representations, but subjective ones.",["\"That\": Das, for daß."]],"reflm:5314":["The possibility of a *compositi noumeni* cannot be assumed absolutely; for we would have to think up community for this.",["*compositi noumeni*: genitive, of a composite noumenon; erdenken: think up, devise."]],"reflm:5315":["Space is the form of all relations, which precedes all intuition of external things.",["The verb vorhergeht is singular, so the relative clause belongs to \"form\" rather than \"relations\"."]],"reflm:5316":["That of which the composition must be thought before the parts is ideal.",[]],"reflm:5317":["Time is the form of consciousness, i.e. the condition under which we only become conscious of things.",["nur (\"only\") may qualify the condition (only under it) or the things."]],"reflm:5318":["Space is a mere possibility, but in actual things something actual.",["wirklich: actual."]],"reflm:5319":["Because inner sense alone is infallible and the alteration is perceived through it, time seems to be something absolute; but it is nonetheless for that reason only the form of inner appearance, and we actually have the representation of time, although what lies behind this appearance remains unknown.",["untrüglich: infallible, undeceiving; warnehmen: perceive."]],"reflm:5320":["Time is actual as form of inner sensibility; thereby it is opposed to the *ficto*. For there are actually alterations in us. Bodies are things, even if they were not there. But space is at all times only form.",["*ficto*: dative of fictum, the fictitious.","\"things\" here renders Sachen, not Dinge."]],"reflm:5321":["Of time we have the running (present), the run-out, elapsed (past) and the coming (future) time. Here time flows, the things are standing. But: a thing endures, it has lived out etc etc., presupposes time as at rest. Eternity. Existence either in general or of a thing. A thing puts much time behind it and has much before it. It cannot fix its existence in time, it goes in flight through it, or time flees.",["laufende, verlaufene, verflossene: running, run-out, elapsed.","verlebt: lived out, spent; bevestigen: fix, fasten."]],"reflm:5322":["Space pertains only to objects of the outer senses, but not to that which we posit only through the properties that are borrowed from the inner senses.",["geht auf: pertains to, is directed at; Eigenschaften: properties."]],"reflm:5323":["Space and time are not things, nor their predicates, hence nothing objective.",[]],"reflm:5324":["In and \nSpace does not pertain to something irrespective of all determinations of sense.\nSpace does not pertain to objects of inner sense, that is: it is no intellectual predicate.",["The location heading \"In und\" (\"In and\") is incomplete in the source: the references it joined are missing.","unangesehn: irrespective of."]],"reflm:5325":["Time the form of inner sense. Space the form of outer. Consequently, what we know only as an object of inner sense is not subject to the condition of outer appearance.",["\"condition\": condition, not Bedingung; \"object\": obiect."]],"reflm:5326":["Whence the propositions of space, time and number can be discerned *a priori* before all experience.",["eingesehen: discerned, had insight into.","The source has no question mark."]],"reflm:5327":["If the concept of space, as Leibniz thinks, were taken from the things, then the propositions about it as propositions of experience would have no apodictic certainty.",["Leibniz: Leibnitz in the source; Erfahrungssätze: propositions of experience."]],"reflm:5328":["The physical division is separation of the substance, the mathematical the distinguishing of the parts.",["Theilung: division; Trennung: separation."]],"reflm:5329":["If space were something objective and necessary, whence would we cognize that. From experience we can derive no judgment which would at the same time be cognized as absolutely necessary, and *a priori* it is not cognized and not cognizable. The representation would have to be concreated, but then it would also not be necessary. It must therefore be intuited in God.",["anerschaffen: concreated (created into the mind), innate.","The first sentence has no question mark in the source."]],"reflm:5330":["The first subject. The first ground. The first part.\nThe subject which holds everything in itself. The ground which comprises everything under itself. The whole which comprehends everything.\nThe *totalitas absoluta* of reality. Of the series. Of the coordination.",["*totalitas absoluta*: absolute totality; begreift: comprehends."]],"reflm:5331":["The magnitude of unity is degree. The difference of the magnitude of one and the same object, e.g. light of the very same paper, warmth of the same stove. There it seems not to be the magnitude of the aggregate. Objects do not have degrees, but mere determinations of the same reality. Relations have no degrees; but the causality, the unity has degrees.\nThe extension too has a degree, because according to the meaning of the word it arises from nothing and spreads out. Space has extensive magnitude.",["\"Objects do not have degrees, but mere determinations ...\": either the determinations have the degrees, or the objects have mere determinations.","\"extension\": extension, not Ausdehnung; Wortbedeutung: meaning of the word."]],"reflm:5332":["The infinite, the greatest, the all-sufficient thing.",["allgnugsam: all-sufficient."]],"reflm:5333":["In and \nThe multiplicity that is given simultaneously, insofar as it is unlimited, cannot be determinately cognized at all; for the determination consists in the limitation. That which is not given simultaneously can be cognized not through limitation, but through progression.",["Vielheit = multiplicity; Beschränkung = limitation; fortgang = progression (progressus)."]],"reflm:5334":["In and \nNo *quantum* can be given to us as *infinitum*, for it is not given in itself, but only through the *progressus*, which is never given as *infinitus*. But a *progressus in infinitum* can be given; *indefinitum*: that whose boundary we leave undetermined.",[]],"reflm:5335":["real infinitude is *omnitudo*. what is not limited. what lacks *termini* differs from that which lacks *limites*.",["The Latin distinguishes terminus (end, term) from limes (limit); both are kept in Latin to preserve the distinction."]],"reflm:5336":["The concept of the *maximum* is relative; that of *omnitudo*, to be sure, absolute, but undetermined. That of the *infinitum* subjective.",[]],"reflm:5337":["The *limitatum* of sensibility: *non Infinitum*.\nThe *limitatum* of the understanding: *non Omnitudo*.",[]],"reflm:5338":["Infinite signifies more than not finite or bounded, but also, taken in the whole, great beyond all measure. Hence I can say: the world is not finite, nor is it infinite, for it is a *phaenomenon* and no thing. As *phaenomenon* it can have no determinate boundary and can never be taken whole, but one can only go forth in it.",["\"thing\" here renders Sache, not Ding.","begrentzt = bounded; Grentze = boundary."]],"reflm:5339":["At the very top of the page:\nThe determination of the boundaries takes place in the infinite (space and time),\nThe determination of the limitations in the infinite thing.\nThe former in an infinite appearance, the latter in an infinite reality.",["Grenzen = boundaries; Schranken = limitations.","\"in the infinite thing\": im Unendlichen Dinge, singular dative."]],"reflm:5340":["At the very top of the page:\nThe infinity of the world as to magnitude (time and space) makes no difficulty, but rather the *prorogation*, suspended into the infinite, of the *terminus* of the chain of grounds, [because] since the interest of reason requires a complete comprehension.\nBut if one also concedes the infinity as possible, there still remains the question whether empty time and space cannot bound the world. i.e. whether an empty time and space are the condition of the possibility of things.",["prorogation (deferral, postponement) is Kant's foreign word; ausgesetzte = suspended, deferred.","Begreifung = comprehension (begreifen = comprehend)."]],"reflm:5341":["The possibility of thinking to oneself an empty space and empty time is nothing other than the diminution of the reality in both (in the first, of the impenetrable extension), which, since it can be diminished to infinity, passes over into nothing. If I remove a part of it which is penetrated by another, then a comparatively empty space remains over. Space penetrates everything, i.e. something extended is the basis of the possibility of outer relations according to relations of space. A completely empty space is a mere idea of *decomposition* down to nothing, but not of complete annulment. In space I must be able to distinguish where I am. There must therefore be correlates. The priority of space before the appearances. If an empty space and time (g outside the world) were possible, then an empty space and time in the place of the world would also be possible. I would then have time only in myself and space in the representation of my body; consequently time and space would actually not be empty. Entirely empty is without any intuiting being, consequently nothing at all.",["\"relations ... relations\": Beziehungen and Verhältnissen are both rendered \"relations\".","Aufhebung = annulment; verminderung = diminution."]],"reflm:5342":["Inner difference is no condition of the concept [of the] of distinct things, but rather the difference of places. It is also no condition of appearance; thus the proposition is false, but for |\nreason true; i.e. through reason I can concede distinct things only there where the inner determinations are different. I.e. *a priori* I can [no] not cognize the difference.",["Verschiedenheit (difference, of inner determinations and of places) and Unterschied (the difference) are both rendered \"difference\"; Unterschiedener Dinge = of distinct things.","The deleted [keinen] and the retained nicht overlap: Kant first wrote \"no difference\", then \"not ... the difference\"."]],"reflm:5343":["Opposite the heading »Simultanea«:\nEverything that exists is somewhere and at some time.",["\"exists\": da ist, literally \"is there\"."]],"reflm:5344":["That everything in space is merely appearance, namely nothing but *relation* —, that everything in time (also the inner sense) is appearance, merely representations of outer things and *relationes* [to us] of their representations to one another, to us and our feeling.",["\"(also the inner sense)\": auch dem innern Sinn, dative; it may read \"also in inner sense\" or \"also of inner sense\"."]],"reflm:5345":["The *subject* itself we know only through its relation to the alteration of place; accordingly we do not know that which is present in the place, and the action which it exercises in different places. At the least we do not know all this rationally; but the question is whether our thoughts do not still contain the inner of the world.",["kennen = know; Verhältnis = relation.","\"the inner of the world\": das innere der Welt, a substantivized adjective."]],"reflm:5346":["Matter is the *subject* to which all outer appearances are referred. (The *phaenomenon* to which they refer everything is impenetrability.) That to which everything is referred as *subject* must remain; for without this there is no unity of the accidents. All matter as *substratum* is therefore *constans, stabilis, perdurabilis, permanens*, without which |\nthe *fluxum* and *transitorium* of the states could not be perceived. This proposition is grounded on the possibility of experience as unity of the appearances.",[]],"reflm:5347":["Time is a series in which the order of the *synthesis* is determined, the first, the middle and last. Three points as boundaries.",[]],"reflm:5348":["Why do we not say (g also) of things in different times (g as of those in different spaces) that they are on that account different things: because without the sameness of the things in different times these different times themselves could not be cognized as such. The persistence of the things lies at the ground of temporal sequence. But that is always possible only in space. This persists; but it itself I can perceive only through things in it (which therefore also persist and in which I cognize it (g as) persistent).",["Einerleyheit = sameness; Zeitfolge = temporal sequence.","\"in which\": an denen, also \"by which\"."]],"reflm:5349":["Opposite and \nAll arising and perishing (g of a state as appearance, i.e. everything that happens) happens in time, because between the 0 and reality there is an infinite series of transition.",[]],"reflm:5350":["A worth: for which something else can be given as *aequivalent*. Virtue has no price. Dignity is the inner worth, which therefore has no price.",["Werth = worth; Preis = price; Würde = dignity."]],"reflm:5351":["Objective causes are only in the free will.\nHow these can at the same time be subjective, i.e. *causae efficientes*, is not to be explained. *hypothesis* of freedom.",[]],"reflm:5352":["An action that has its inner worth has no *causa impulsiva*.",["Kant has the accusative causam impulsivam (impelling cause)."]],"reflm:5353":["Of that which is possible only through a cognition, the original cognition is idea. The thing according to the idea is called archetype; if it cannot be brought forth: ideal.",["Urbild = archetype; Ursprüngliche Erkentnis = original cognition.","Daß Ding is read as Das Ding."]],"reflm:5354":["The Idea.* The archetype, copy, imitation, apparency.\n*(g A made representation. It is single, because it pertains to completeness, which is only one, but not to the concept of something insofar as it is given, but rather can only be thought.)",["Urbild = archetype; Nachbild = copy (ectypon); Apparenz = apparency, distinct from Erscheinung (appearance).","einig = single, unitary; geht auf = pertains to, is directed at."]],"reflm:5355":["Besides ontology there is only cosmology and theology.",[]],"reflm:5356":["Metaphysics proper consists of *cosmologia rationalis* and *theologia naturalis*. The former has two parts: nature and freedom, and their laws *a priori*.",["Kant has the ablatives cosmologia rationali and theologia naturali."]],"reflm:5357":["At the very bottom of the page, \nThere is only one time and one space, hence only one world. Is grounded on the presupposition that everything (at least the finite) is in time and in space, and is an example of a *petitio sensitiva*.",["The subject of \"Is grounded\" is unexpressed, as in the German.","Kant has the genitive petitionis sensitivae."]],"reflm:5358":["We do not sense outer substances (only outer effects on us), but rather we only think them in addition. but only in relation to the affections of our mind; thus not what they are in themselves, but rather the *perdurable* in the appearance.",["empfinden = sense (v.), cf. Empfindung = sensation; dazu denken = think in addition.","Gemüth = mind; Verhaltnis = relation."]],"reflm:5359":["At the very top of the page. \nIf someone wanted to have it that the world should have taken its beginning earlier, he would still have to show under which conditions this would take |\nplace and by what one would know it *in concreto*. Were there before Adam some members human beings, one would only say: Adam is later, or those are earlier with respect to him. Thus all [all] priority is merely *relatio*. The world thus cannot arise earlier, but rather one part earlier than the other. If to the boundaries of the world matter were still added, then this would be posited not to space, but to the old whole.",["\"some members human beings\": einige Glieder Menschen, members (links, generations) of a series of human beings; the construction is elliptical.","gesetzt = posited, here with zu: \"set to, added to\"."]],"reflm:5360":["Since empty space and empty time is no object of empirical cognitions, neither of experience immediately, nor through any inference, but also the infinity of the world is just as much beyond experience as those are outside it: so the totality (*absoluta*) of the [world] appearances, i.e. the idea of the world, is a problematic concept. The intellectual possibility of one of these conditions of appearance does not fit here; for they are synthetic propositions, which obtain only as conditions of possible experience.",["Gegenstand = object; schlus = inference.","statt finden = obtain, take place."]],"reflm:5361":["We prove here only that an intellectual series has a first. (g The cause of the series is not in the series, hence no first. Therefore the series can be without a first and yet have a cause which is not again in a series.)\nOf the series in the appearance we do not speak.",[]],"reflm:5362":["Beside and \nThat no single alleged part of the *universum* lies infinitely far distant from the other, but rather that in the universe a progression according to every kind of magnitudes into the infinite is possible.\nWhere reason is cause, there is also found a first: freedom and supreme being. But where it only cognizes the connection, there the *synthesis* is infinite.",["Kant has the genitive universi and the French univers.","Fortschritt = progression; Verknüpfung = connection."]],"reflm:5363":["Under and \nFreedom (g in the world) and, of a free supreme cause, absolute necessity outside the world are dynamical *principia* of reason.",["The construction is obscure as in the German: \"einer freyen obersten Ursache\" may be genitive with \"absolute Nothwendigkeit\" or dative after a lost word."]],"reflm:5364":["Under and \nEvery series of the contingent rests on a necessary. But this does not signify: there is a first of the series.",[]],"reflm:5365":["The beginning belongs to the series, but not the cause of it. A series of contingent things can be without beginning, i.e. in that [at all] no empty time precedes, but rather [before every series] all appearances in one series are determined *a parte priori* and *posteriori*, but can nevertheless have a cause.",[]],"reflm:5366":["There is no blind dependence (g blind chain) (series of the dependent without an independent), no blind transition, no blind [generation] arising, no blind necessity.\nBlind is what is not [posited] assumed in conformity with the laws of the understanding.",["Erzeugung (deleted) = generation; Entstehung = arising."]],"reflm:5367":["In the series of appearances there is no first cause; for that holds only of things in themselves, but there the series of the cause is not subordinated according to time.",[]],"reflm:5368":["On and \nThis means only: we can think no (g absolute) totality according to empirical synthesis, but must nevertheless think one according to pure intellectual, because everything contingent must have a complete cause, not indeed in itself, yet outside itself. We have no image, but nevertheless a concept. For of the infinite no image is possible, and of the finite in the infinite time and space no *substratum* of this image.\nWe cannot think the place of the beginning of the world as determinable in empty space and empty time. How something could begin absolutely in time, still less how time itself could begin.",["\"according to pure intellectual\": nach reiner intellectuellen, with \"synthesis\" understood.","Bild = image; schlechthin = absolutely."]],"reflm:5369":["In and \nBetween nature and chance there is a third, namely freedom.\nAll appearances are in nature, but the cause of the appearances is not contained in the appearance, thus also not nature. Our understanding is such a cause of the actions of choice, which indeed as appearances are nature, but as a whole of the appearances stand under freedom.",["Zufall = chance; Wilkühr = choice."]],"reflm:5370":["Chance is opposed to the purposive (g to that which human reason can take into account in the case of ends.); the haphazard to that which happens according to constant and known laws.\nWe do not see that without a cause acting according to ends nothing purposive can arise and that natural necessity could not just as well bring forth something purposive as the absolute necessity: a rational supreme being; but because experience teaches that there are laws of blind nature, [disting] we see from this the difference of the choosing causes from the physical ones.",["das ungefehr = the haphazard (Ohngefähr); einsehen = see, have insight.","[untersch] is a broken-off word, presumably unterscheiden (distinguish)."]],"reflm:5371":["That chance [alt] must alternate the occurrence with its opposite. That indeed each time the same occurrence that has happened before is possible, and so all following times, but that the same occurrence is not always possible one after another, if it is supposed to stem from chance. Hence dice, when the same side always comes up, are not held to be correct. We also find it probable that, when something which is subject to chance has several times turned out in a similar way, there is now a greater probability that it will fail, than before. Whence comes it that, when we have had good weather for a long time, we find rain more probable than after long rain. Yet one believes that some human beings are especially lucky, some days of play, certain cards. (g Whoever has often lost in the lottery, e.g. the first class, believes he has more hopes.)\nChance has laws, e.g. shipwrecks.\nSomething is called chance for this reason, because its opposite also happens in just the same (generically identical) circumstances. Hence in chance the opposite must *per hypothesin* be the more necessary, the more often something has happened.",["[wech] is a broken-off word, presumably wechseln (alternate).","\"than before\": wie vorher may also be read \"as before\".","Begebenheit = occurrence; Warscheinlichkeit = probability; richtig = correct, fair."]],"reflm:5372":["Insofar as an occurrence does not happen under a particular rule of its causes, it is chance. But there is no rule in the causality of the causes when there are many of them which are not particularly determined for this occurrence. e.g. rain, frost, illnesses are chances in a military campaign. Nature, chance and intention.\nIn human generation many variations happen through chance. Likewise in the fortune of the human being. A chance (fortune) which is attached to one human being is an absurdity. Even under a divine providence the fortune of human beings is very strongly left to chance, i.e. to the influence of many causes, which are not attuned to the person and his merit and which the human being cannot bring under a rule.",["Glück = fortune (also luck, happiness); Unding = absurdity, literally \"non-thing\".","Absicht = intention; gestimmet = attuned."]],"reflm:5373":["(s In the world there is no absolute necessity; this can however be outside it.)\nFate (destiné) and chance (fortune and misfortune) are *qualitates occultae*. We need reason for explaining as well as for acting; the blind haphazard and fate is contrary to both.",["Schicksal = fate; Glük und Unglük = fortune and misfortune; Ohngefehr = haphazard."]],"reflm:5374":["Principles of (g speculative) reason concerning everything that appears.",["Grundsatze = principles."]],"reflm:5375":["Everything stands [either] under a rule, either of necessity or freedom.",[]],"reflm:5376":["Between two states there is a time. In this, if the states are different, the thing is neither in the one nor in the other. Thus there is either an empty time in between (*saltus*) or a time filled with smaller intermediate degrees of the states. Just so there is no transition [from] among the appearances in space through the void. Because this would be appearance without perception.",["“[from]”: the deleted aus.","“the void”: das Leere."]],"reflm:5377":["*Mundus non* * *est in abysso*: no beginning, end and boundary of the world.\n*in mundo non datur hiatus* (g *saltus*. *vacuum*. we fill all space through a kind of sensations.)\n*Nihil accidit per casum. Nihil per fatum.*\n*(g There is no absolute time or space. Pure intuition signifies here not something that is intuited, but the pure formal condition that precedes the appearance. Absolute time is empty intuition.)",["The spaced asterisk after *non* is Kant's reference sign for the addition at the end.","*Mundus non est in abysso*: “the world is not in an abyss”; *in mundo non datur hiatus*: “in the world there is no gap”; *Nihil accidit per casum. Nihil per fatum*: “Nothing happens by chance. Nothing by fate.”"]],"reflm:5378":["The world is no *interruptum*, but a *continuum*. The removal of matter from space is nothing but the infinite diminution.",[]],"reflm:5379":["All alteration or transition happens in time through a continuous approximation.",["“alteration”: Veränderung; “approximation”: Annäherung."]],"reflm:5380":["Physical continuity takes place: if the substances must ascend from the lowest classes to the generation of the greatest; |\nif a mineral must first be plant, then animal, for then it goes through all the intermediate degrees. If animals are properly plants that have gone through a certain degree. Through composition no true continuity arises, but through generation from the small degree to the greatest.",["“continuity”: Kant's Latinate continuitaet, here and below.","“composition”: Zusammenfügung; “generation”: Erzeugung."]],"reflm:5381":["Whether certain oppositions permit a continuity (A grain and a heap). Where a continuous transition is possible, there the difference is only in magnitude. With that which is no vice and vice the difference is in quality. Whether understanding and learning are distinguished only according to magnitude. The transition from one to the other is not a *continuum*.",["“oppositions”, “continuity”, “quality”: Kant's Latinate oppositionen, continuitaet, qvalitaet.","“learning”: Gelehrsamkeit."]],"reflm:5382":["The principle of continuity means only to say: all *diversa* are *remota*, i.e. they are not otherwise in connection than *per intermedia*, between which the difference is still smaller. I.e. no difference [conn] is the smallest, because no transition is elementary and is the smallest, thus always has a magnitude. To transition there belongs a time, consequently an approximation to a new state. The smallest difference would be called a *differentiale*; but because there is no smallest, it is called *fluxion*.\nBecause of the creatures between two genera there is a determinate number, there is no continuity of forms. But there could be one *in potentia*, if matter organized itself from the mineral to the human being; for these forces lie in one unity, and the differences have a magnitude, between which infinitely smaller ones take place.",["“principle”: Satz; “connection”: Verknüpfung. “[conn]” renders the deleted [verk], a word broken off.","*diversa* … *remota* … *per intermedia*: “different things … remote … through intermediates”.","“Because of the creatures … there is a determinate number”: the German construction (der Geschopfe … eine bestimmte Zahl) is kept as loose as it stands."]],"reflm:5383":["Were there no continuity in alteration, then for a time the thing would be unaltered, i.e. at rest.",[]],"reflm:5384":["The immediate combination of the boundaries of one and the same series.\nLaw of the *continui*. Quality of magnitudes.",["“boundaries”: Grenzen; “quality”: Qvalitaet."]],"reflm:5385":["*Lex continui in natura*, the continuity *specierum* or affinity, mediate or immediate, is a mere play of wit. The complete manifoldness gives at the same time unity of progression. For then between two different things A and E still others B and C, whose differences are smaller, must be encountered, if all manifoldness is to take place. For similarities are among all appearances, because otherwise no *commercium* would take place.",["*Lex continui in natura*: “law of continuity in nature”; *specierum*: “of species”.","“manifoldness”: Manigfaltigkeit, Mannigfaltigkeit; “progression”: Fortgang.","“play of wit”: Witzspiel."]],"reflm:5386":["121. E II 1463. 1745.\n M 121':\nThere is no simple *phaenomenon*; for it will be a definite part of every *phaenomenon*, therefore it would occupy a part of space and of time, which again is space and time. |\nM 121: |\ntherefore in running through *phaenomena* from simple to simple there is no transition except through infinite intermediates.\nTwo states as series indeed follow upon one another; but not two simple determinations of space and of time.",["The first two sentences are Kant's Latin; the sigla lines are kept as they stand.","“definite”: definita; “intermediates”: intermedia."]],"reflm:5387":["All motion in a triangle is interrupted, i.e. the body rests at its apex, and no two different states follow upon one another immediately, although two [differ] alterations can follow upon one another (series).",["“[differ]”: the deleted, broken-off [verschi] (verschiedene, “different”)."]],"reflm:5388":["All differences of given representations have a magnitude, e.g. of speed, of extension, of series, and can only be determined thereby. But in the object the [magn] difference is not discrete — breaks off.",["“object”: the Latinate Obiect.","“[magn]”: the deleted, broken-off [groß]."]],"reflm:5389":["Continuity. That all magnitude which begins from 0 arises in a time, and that every difference has a magnitude, consequently none is the smallest. The first is clear from this, because with 0 and a given magnitude, likewise the arising, there is a series, of which 0 and the magnitude are extremes, likewise a degree of the progression.",["“arising”: dem entstehen."]],"reflm:5390":["All appearances stand as representations in time and are determined in time. As a part of a whole appearance it can be determined not in an instant, but in a part of time (be apprehended genetically). A part of time lies between |\ntwo boundaries and thus two instants, is thus itself [determinable in the] a time, consequently every part of the appearance exponible in time; thus just as time itself it does not consist of simple parts.",["“it” in the second sentence (sie) has no expressed antecedent in the singular; kept as it stands.","“exponible”: exponibel."]],"reflm:5391":["Two concepts opposed in themselves cannot be distinguished (g merely) according to magnitude. Vice and virtue.",[]],"reflm:5392":["In and \nEvery alteration in the world is only a continuation of an already present series, and just as much ceases as begins. Hence everything in the world indeed alters; but the all of the series does not alter, because things indeed alter in time, but not time itself.",["The editorial location note “In und” is cut off in the source.","“the all”: das All."]],"reflm:5393":["What appears externally can be united with a *principio repraesentativo*, but not as outer appearance.",["*principio repraesentativo*: “representative principle”, in the ablative."]],"reflm:5394":["Idealism is mystical; egoism Spinozistic, if its defense is dogmatic. If it is skeptical, i.e. merely problematic, then egoism is a [pro] docimastic of reason.",["“docimastic”: docimastic, an assaying or testing; “[pro]” is a deleted, broken-off word."]],"reflm:5395":["The idealist asserts: bodies [become] are only an illusion; the realist: they are an appearance, to which however a particular kind of substances actually corresponds. Illusion is that behind which there is nothing true.",["“illusion”: Schein; “appearance”: Erscheinung.","denen (plural) against eine Erscheinung (singular) is Kant's; kept in “to which”."]],"reflm:5396":["A substantial composite considered intellectually is a *quantum discretum*, which consists of simples; the same as *phaenomenon* is a *quantum continuum*.\nThe *mundus sensibilis* can be considered by the understanding, but only according to principles of appearances. *Mundus intelligibilis* is *pnevmaticus*. If I think the *mundum sensibilem* according to concepts of the *intelligibilis*, then it is *mundus mysticus*. *Mundus sensibilis* has substances, forces and effects, but not the absolute of them [and real], but their appearances, place and alteration of it.",["The first sentence is Kant's Latin: *Compositum substantiale intellectualiter spectatum*.","“principles”: principien; “considered” renders both spectatum and erwogen.","“of it” (desselben, singular) has no clear antecedent; kept as it stands."]],"reflm:5397":["The *mundus intelligibilis* is a *monadatum*, representable not according to the form of outer intuition, but of inner.",["Kant's Latin throughout; “outer intuition”: intuitus externi."]],"reflm:5398":["With regard to the existence of the things of the world — Either idealist or dualist. The idealist either egoist or pluralist.\nWith regard to the grounds of explanation of the form[der] Either materialist or pneumatist.",["“form[der]”: the deleted syllables [der] stand inside the word Form[der] in the source.","“pneumatist”: Pnevmatist."]],"reflm:5399":["The imagination presupposes a sense, of which the former can reproduce the form. Were there no outer sense, then we would also not be able to imagine things outside us as such, consequently according to three dimensions of space.\nWere the cause of the intuition of space in us, then we would be able to become conscious of it as a representation of inner sense, and then we would have to ascribe space and figure to our representations of things just as to the things themselves.\nDreams can represent things to us as outer that are just then not there; but [they] we would also not even be able to dream something as outer if these forms were not given to us through outer things. That one must believe the actuality of outer things if we cannot prove them would not be necessary; for that has no relation to any interest of reason.",["“the former” (jene) is the imagination.","“dimensions of space”: Raumesabmessungen; “actuality”: Wirklichkeit."]],"reflm:5400":["The question whether something is outside me is just as much as [whether] if I asked whether I represented to myself an actual space. For this is something outside me. But this does not signify that something exists in itself, but that such *phaenomena* correspond to objects. For with the *phaenomenon* the talk is never of absolute existence. Dreams are according to the analogy of waking. Apart from the representations of waking that consent with other human beings I have no other marks of the object outside me; thus a *phaenomenon* in space outside me is what can be cognized according to rules of the understanding. How can one indeed ask: whether there actually are outer *phaenomena*? We are indeed not immediately conscious of them, that they are external, i.e. not mere imaginings and dreams, but yet that they are the originals of all possible imaginings, thus themselves no imaginings.",["“objects”, “the object”: Gegenständen, Gegenstande; “existence”: existentz.","“consent with”: consentirenden; “imaginings”: Einbildungen."]],"reflm:5401":["We find in bodies nothing but impenetrability and divisibility, i.e. the possibility of the parts to be without combination with the others. From this it does not now follow that [something there] [the] some of these parts are the ultimate ones, i.e. indivisible, except only by means of the concept substance. But this concept is with regard to [the] the appearance only merely the persistent with regard to the alterations, i.e. the divisions, and does not signify the ultimate subject of all relations. Hence the body is substance. But *in sensu intellectuali* a *corpus mysticum* is no substance, but aggregate of them. The ultimate subject does not appear externally. The *substratum* of outer appearance is merely what persistently acts; the combination or separation is indifferent with regard to it. It inheres further in no other and is no *accidens* of another (g e.g. matter), which could vary. The *substantia noumenon* is not merely what persists in the appearances, but what is the ultimate subject in every respect, consequently cannot itself consist in relations. But this concept, that matter is in every respect the ultimate subject, does not appear; rather the appearance can give no substances at all, but their *phaenomena*, which are all *continua*.",["“ultimate” renders letzte throughout; “subject”: the Latinate subiect.","“in every respect”: in aller absicht.","“of them” (derselben) and “which could vary” (welches) are kept as loose as the German."]],"reflm:5402":["At the top of the page:\nThe difference between substance as outer *phaenomenon* and the inner representation is this, that the former is the subject of inherence without determining the unity, but the latter determines the unity through the I.",[]],"reflm:5403":["The *phaenomenon substantiatum* does not consist of simple *phaenomenis*, rather all *phaenomena* are *quanta continua*.",[]],"reflm:5404":["Beside, below and \nOne cannot argue about appearances further than the conditions of appearance reach. Hence not from grounds of the intellectual concepts [which] about space and time, that they are no *substantiae*, *accidentia*, *relationes*. For from this it follows that it is only the form of appearance. Just so from impenetrability and extension no further than to the divisibility that space permits, of which it is the appearance, not from the concept substance, if it has been abstracted from inner sense.",["The editorial location note “Neben, unter und” is cut off in the source.","“intellectual concepts”: intellectualbegriffe; the verb of the second sentence is left unexpressed, as in the German."]],"reflm:5405":["Essence is the (g logical) inner principle of conceivability, nature (g causal principle) of the causality of all determinations.",["Kant's Latin throughout: *principium internum conceptibilitatis*, *principium caussale*."]],"reflm:5406":["The first [pri] inner (g determining) *principium* of that which belongs to the concept of a thing is essence.\nBut the first [pri] inner (g determining) *principium* of that which belongs (g to) existence is called nature*(s *materialiter*: the sum total of appearances, *formaliter*: the *nexus* of determinations).\n*(s Nature in opposition 1) to essence, 2) to art, 3) to freedom. In the last sense the essence is thought as such a thing as gives itself laws.)",["“[pri]”: a deleted, broken-off word.","“essence”, “the essence”: Wesen; in the last sentence Wesen could also be read as “being”.","*materialiter*, *formaliter*: “materially”, “formally”."]],"reflm:5407":["Nature (of a thing) taken *adiective* in relation to |\nnature (according to totality), *substantive* against the hyperphysical. | Nature and freedom.",["*adiective*, *substantive*: “adjectivally”, “substantively”; “totality”: totalitaet."]],"reflm:5408":["The inner *principium* of the concept of an object is essence. The inner *principium* of the actuality of everything that pertains to an object is nature.",["“object”: Gegenstand, in both places; “actuality”: wirklichkeit."]],"reflm:5409":["All appearances lie in one nature.\nNature is the inner *principium causale* according to constant laws: *Leges stabiles*. Substances have nature.\n*physice necessarium vel practice*.",["*Leges stabiles*: “stable laws”; *physice necessarium vel practice*: “physically necessary or practically”.","“one nature”: einer Natur, which can also be read “a nature”."]],"reflm:5410":["Nature takes its first rules from the understanding. *Commercium*. All effect of substances externally is reciprocal, indeed all motion of bodies is reciprocal.",["“effect”: Wirkung."]],"reflm:5411":["Nature in relation to chance or fate. Opposite: freedom.\nNature in relation to the supernatural. Opposite: the absolute necessity and contingent nature of the things in the world.\nNature of the world in general.",["“chance”: das ohngefehr; “opposite”: Gegensatz."]],"reflm:5412":["The mathematical antinomies go to the essence of appearances, the dynamical to nature. The first to appearances merely through space and time, the second determined through real grounds.",["“real grounds”: realgründe.","“determined” (bestimmt) stands at the end and may attach to both clauses or to the second only."]],"reflm:5413":["That which in the appearance of a rational being begins only relatively and according to time presupposes something else that determines its existence according to a rule has in this as intelligence its ground in that which does not begin and according to time is subordinated to no preceding state. Therein consists the freedom of a rational being as cause through its reason. For this is a faculty of determining itself *a priori*. For were the determining grounds empirical and given subjectively *a posteriori*, then the judgment of reason could not be regarded as *a priori*, consequently as absolutely necessary.\nIn order to judge objectively universally and indeed apodictically, reason must be free of subjectively determining grounds; for if these determined, then the judgment would be only so as it is, contingent, namely according to its subjective causes. Thus reason is conscious of its freedom in objectively necessary judgments *a priori*, namely that only the relation to the object is the ground thereof.",["“judgment of reason”: Vernunfturtheil; “object”: the Latinate obiect.","“in this as intelligence” (in diesem): kept without naming its antecedent.","nur so wie es ist zufallig can be read “contingent only just as it is” or “only, as it is, contingent”; both are left open."]],"reflm:5414":["Empirically one can indeed bring out rules, but not laws; as Kepler in comparison with Newton; for to the latter belongs necessity, consequently, that they be cognized a priori. Yet one always assumes of rules of nature that they are necessary, for that is why it is nature, and that they can be discerned a priori; hence one calls them *anticipando* laws. The understanding is the ground of empirical laws; consequently of an empirical necessity, where the ground of lawfulness can indeed be discerned a priori, e.g. The law of causality, but not the ground of the determinate law. All metaphysical principles of nature are only grounds of lawfulness.",["“discerned”: eingesehen (einsehen), kept apart from verstehen (understand).","“lawfulness”: Gesetzmäßigkeit; “principles”: principien.","*anticipando*: “by anticipation”."]],"reflm:5415":["The harmony of the substances of the world is either *generaliter* or *singulariter stabilita*; the first is necessary, if many substances make up a whole, in which [if a part] the state of a part always also belongs to the state of the whole.",["*generaliter* / *singulariter stabilita*: “established generally” / “established singly”."]],"reflm:5416":["The *harmonia generaliter stabilita* is never *praestabilita*; for the latter is always *singulariter stabilita*. thus: all *harmonia est vel interne vel externe stabilita*; the latter *vel singulariter vel generaliter*, the first *vel occasionaliter vel praestabilita*.",["*harmonia est vel interne vel externe stabilita*: “harmony is established either internally or externally”; *vel … vel*: “either … or”.","*occasionaliter vel praestabilita*: “occasionally (by occasion) or pre-established”."]],"reflm:5417":["The ground of the universal connection of substances is also the ground of space.",[]],"reflm:5418":["We cannot use the *sensitiva* for the explanation of the *intellectualium*, consequently not space for explanation of the *commercii*. Still less tautologically forces.",["*sensitiva* / *intellectualium*: “sensitive things” / “of intellectual things”; *commercii*: genitive of *commercium*, “community”.","“tautologically” (tautologisch) may qualify the use of forces or the forces themselves; the sentence is elliptical."]],"reflm:5419":["The principle of community must be common to all. Therefore a common cause.",["“principle of community”: *principium commercii*; “common cause”: *causa communis*."]],"reflm:5420":["The harmony of substances is either[real or ideal. The first is influence, the other *harmonia externe stabilita*] grounded on a *commercium* of the|same\nand is called influence: *harmonia naturalis*, or [without] on an external determination and is called [*externe stabil*] *artificialis*; this is either per breaks off.",["“influence”: Einflus (Einfluß); *harmonia naturalis* / *artificialis*: natural / artificial harmony.","[*externe stabil*] is a deleted, unfinished phrase; “per” is the last word before the break and is left as it stands.","“breaks off” (bricht ab) is the AA's note that the text ends here."]],"reflm:5421":["Below and \n(s *influxus precarius, postulatus*)\nHyperphysical influence is contradictory; namely it is a harmony [appar] bearing before it the appearance of influence.",["The locus note “Below and” is incomplete in the source.","*influxus precarius, postulatus*: “precarious, postulated influence”; “hyperphysical influence”: *influxus hyperphysicus*.","[appar] is a deleted word-fragment, left as it stands; “appearance” renders *speciem*."]],"reflm:5422":["Physical influence is either naturally original or derivative; the latter is rational.",["“physical influence”: *influxus physicus*; “original”: *originarius*."]],"reflm:5423":["Harmony either without community or from community.",["“community”: *commercium* (*absqve commercio*, *ex commercio*)."]],"reflm:5424":["[influence] Community is either original or derivative, and in this influence either [automatic] physical or hyperphysical. this either automatic or occasionalist.",["“community”: *commercium*; “influence”: *influxus*.","“automatic”: *avtomaticus* (automaticus); “occasionalist”: *occasionalisticus*."]],"reflm:5425":["The nexus of substances is either physical or hyperphysical.",["“nexus”: *nexus substantiarum*."]],"reflm:5426":["If from the determinations of the one substance there cannot be an inference to the other, there is no *nexus realis*, but *idealis*.",["“there cannot be an inference”: kann geschlossen werden (schließen), impersonal passive.","*nexus realis* / *idealis*: real / ideal nexus."]],"reflm:5427":["influence either real (physical) or ideal. the latter is harmony without influence.",["“influence”: *influxus*."]],"reflm:5428":["Community is either original or derivative, the former the cruder physical influence. The derivative from a common cause is either physical or hyperphysical, and this either of pre-established or of occasional harmony. The last is a *commercium* or harmony without nature.",["The first two sentences are Latin: “community” renders *commercium*, “cruder physical influence” *influxus physicus crassior*.","The last sentence is German and keeps Kant's *commercium*."]],"reflm:5429":["Community (*commercium*) (the ground of it, *communitas*, consists in this, that whatever alteration occurs with the one has an effect on all) is twofold: either [according to laws of the] of the real effects or of the ideal, which are merely the *phaenomenon* of the effects. The influences of bodies are merely the *phaenomena* of the effects. For matter effects only relations of space; but these are not something actual or an *accidens* and thus no true relation of one substance to the other, so as to alter it internally. The external relation without internal, however, is nothing true, but a mere appearance. The community of bodies among one another can be understood according to *legibus phaenomenorum*; the community of spirits only according to *legibus intellectualibus*, whose application however requires experience; that of bodies and spirits can not at all be discerned, because the latter are cognized only through inner sense and thus their alterations *realiter*, but the effects of the former and of their forces go only to *phaenomena* of relations in general without their (g inner) determinations.",["“whatever alteration”: was … vor eine Veranderung (vor = für).","“relations”: Verhältnisse; “relation”: relation (Latinate); “a mere appearance”: eine blosse apparentz, not Erscheinung.","“understood”: verstanden; “discerned”: eingesehen; *legibus phaenomenorum* / *intellectualibus*: “by laws of phenomena” / “intellectual laws”."]],"reflm:5430":["Nature and essence.\nNature and art.\nNature and freedom.\nThe nature of a thing (*formaliter*). Nature simply (*materialiter*): the world. Sum total of the objects of the senses according to rules.",["“essence”: Wesen; “simply”: schlechthin."]],"reflm:5431":["The inner *principium caussale* of everything that constitutes the [state a] actuality of a thing is nature of the thing. Of the world.\nThe inner *principium possibilitatum* — *essentia*.",["*principium caussale*: “causal principle”; *principium possibilitatum*: “principle of possibilities”.","“actuality”: Wirklichkeit."]],"reflm:5432":["The nature of a thing is the complete real ground of that which flows necessarily from the constitution of a thing according to universal laws. Thus the natural is 1. to be contradistinguished from the artificial or arbitrary, added, e.g. senses and learnedness. 2. from essence, which contains that which relates necessarily to the concept of the thing, thus not always the whole nature [the]; 3. from that which arises externally: *accessorie*. Contrary to nature, insofar as something resists the laws of the nature of a thing and the possibility, (g according to) universal rules to conclude something from it. (g 4. from freedom.)\nThe universal real ground [wod] of the determinations inhering in the thing is nature; thus that through which, according to a universal law, that which belongs to the predicates of its existence is determined.",["“real ground”: realgrund; “constitution”: Beschaffenheit; “essence”: Wesen.","“arbitrary”: Willkührlichen; *accessorie*: “accessorily”.","[wod] is a deleted word-fragment, left as it stands."]],"reflm:5433":["The word “nature” is taken either *substantive* or *adiective*. *Substantive* it is the aggregate of all objects of the senses, *adiective* it is the (g inner) *principium* of the efficacy of a being.",["*substantive* / *adiective*: “substantively” / “adjectivally”.","“efficacy”: Wirksamkeit; “being”: Wesen."]],"reflm:5434":["Whether we have an experience that we are free?\n(s No! For otherwise we would have to be able to experience of all human beings [say] that they can resist the greatest *stimulo*. On the contrary the moral law says: they ought to resist, consequently they must be able to.)",["*stimulo*: dative of *stimulus*."]],"reflm:5435":["The will is a faculty to act according to the representation of a rule as law. Faculty [according to] of ends. *stimuli* are pleasure that precedes the law. *independentia a stimulis* is where the law precedes the pleasure. (g *arbitrium purum*.) (s Freedom is causality of pure reason in determination of the power of choice.)",["“pleasure”: Lust; “power of choice”: Willkühr.","*independentia a stimulis*: “independence from stimuli”; *arbitrium purum*: “pure power of choice”."]],"reflm:5436":["Freedom is the faculty to be determined only through reason, and not merely mediately, but immediately, thus not through matter, but form of the law. thus moral.",[]],"reflm:5437":["Either the object of the action or the action itself must interest. The good is either the useful or the good in itself.",[]],"reflm:5438":["The faculty to produce the motives of willing simply itself is freedom. This *actus* does not itself rest on willing, but is the spontaneity of the causality of willing. Over this we make ourselves reproaches or approval.",["“motives”: Motiven, not Bewegungsgründe.","schlechthin selbst: “itself” may go with the faculty or with the one who wills; “simply” renders schlechthin."]],"reflm:5439":["Beside and \nExternal freedom, also internal.\nThe faculty of the power of choice to act according to self-given laws.",["The locus note “Beside and” is incomplete in the source.","“power of choice”: Wilkühr."]],"reflm:5440":["Freedom, insofar as it is a concept of reason, is inexplicable (g also not objective); insofar as it is a concept of the activity and causality of reason itself, it can indeed also not be explained as a first principle, but is a self-consciousness a priori.",["“inexplicable” / “explained”: unerklärlich / erklart; “concept of reason”: Vernunftbegrif; “principle”: princip."]],"reflm:5441":["All our and other beings' actions are [nothw] necessitated, only the understanding alone (g and the will, insofar as it can be determined through understanding.) is free and a pure self-activity, which is determined through nothing other than itself. Without this original and |\nunchangeable spontaneity we would cognize nothing a priori; for we would be determined to everything, and our thoughts themselves would stand under empirical laws. The faculty to think and to act a priori is the sole condition of the possibility of the origin of all other appearances. The ought would also have no significance at all.\nFreedom and absolute necessity are the sole pure concepts of reason which are objective, although inexplicable. For by reason one understands the self-activity to go from the universal to the particular, and to do this a priori, consequently with a necessity simply. The absolute necessity: in regard to the determinable, and freedom: of the determining.",["[nothw] is a deleted word-fragment, left as it stands.","“self-activity”: Selbstthätigkeit; “the ought”: Das sollen.","“with a necessity simply”: mit einer Nothwendigkeit schlechthin; “simply” may qualify the necessity or the doing."]],"reflm:5442":["Logical freedom in regard to everything that are contingent predicates.\nAll contingency in the object is objective freedom (to think the opposite); if the thought suffices for the deed: also subjective freedom.\ntranscendental freedom is the complete contingency of actions. It is [transcendental] logical freedom in actions of reason, but not transcendental, namely objective.",["“It is”: Es ist, which can also be read “There is”.","“actions of reason”: Vernunfthandlungen; “suffices”: zureicht."]],"reflm:5443":["The talents give human beings importance, the merits dignity, all morally good properties a worth.",["“properties”: Eigenschaften; “worth”: Werth."]],"reflm:5444":["Simply (unconditionally in every respect) nothing is good except the existence of freely acting beings, and in these not understanding, happiness etc etc., but the disposition, of all that which is mediately good, well to |\navail oneself. Thus the practical disposition of good aims. This is thus the good-naturedness of the will.",["“disposition”: Gesinung (Gesinnung); “aims”: Absichten; “good-naturedness”: Gutartigkeit.","“well to avail oneself”: sich … wohl zu bedienen; the order is kept so that the line break | stands where it does in the source."]],"reflm:5445":["The moral laws do not arise from reason, but are that which contains the conditions through which alone it is possible that free actions can be determined and cognized according to the rules of reason. This happens, however, when we make the universally valid end the ground of actions. So that the particular ends agree with those which one can regard as if through them all things were possible.\nThe morally good requires complete unity of the ground of the action for reason, consequently that it be taken from the *idea archetypa*, which is the end of the whole world.\nWhat is in conformity with the conditions under which [something] everything happens according to rules a priori pleases necessarily. For it brings forth the agreement with the entire nature. and thus a consciousness of the agreement of the actions with themselves and with all others. Only from the whole and the highest ground can that be derived which according to universal laws breaks off.",["“for reason”: vor die Vernunft (vor = für); *idea archetypa*: “archetypal idea”.","“with themselves”: mit sich selbst, which may refer to the actions or to the consciousness.","“breaks off” (bricht ab) is the AA's note that the text ends here."]],"reflm:5446":["Moral laws are those [of the necessity of actions from the], which contain the conditions through which free actions become in agreement with the universally valid end, thus the private will with the original and highest will. Either with the universal end of nature or of freely acting beings. The will is thus [as] considered according to the unity of the ground, insofar namely as all will lies in one will: that which is the cause of nature and every other.",["“become in agreement”: einstimig werden.","“every other” (jeden andern) may mean every other will or every other cause; left as ambiguous as the German."]],"reflm:5447":["The constitution of the morally good will is good conduct (of the human being), is virtue if it happens from inclination, and fear of God if it happens from obedience to God.",["“good conduct”: wohlverhalten; “fear of God”: Gottesfurcht."]],"reflm:5448":["The moral motives ought not merely to have *vim obiective necessitantem* for the [practical] conviction of the understanding, but *vim subiective necessitantem*, i.e. they ought to be *elateres*. The subjective condition under which they can be so [is] is called feeling. Were it an actual feeling (*proprie*), then the necessitation would be pathological; the *causae impulsivae* would not be *motiva*, but *stimuli*; not the goodness, but the *iucundum* would move us. Thus the *sensus moralis* is only *per analogiam* so called and ought to be called not “sense” but “disposition”, according to which the moral motives in the subject necessitate just as *stimuli*. It is thus *in sensu proprio* an absurdity, a mere *analogon sensus* and serves only to express a faculty (not receptivity) for which we have no name.",["*vim obiective / subiective necessitantem*: “an objectively / subjectively necessitating force”; *elateres*: “springs”; *iucundum*: “the agreeable”.","“sense” / “disposition”: Sinn / Gesinnung, a play on words lost in English; “goodness”: bonitaet.","“absurdity”: Unding; *in sensu proprio*: “in the proper sense”; *analogon sensus*: “analogue of sense”."]],"reflm:5449":["Above and beside the end of M § 730 »servitutem — servile«:\nA power of choice is servile in which command over oneself ceases.",["The first line is the AA's locus note; the Latin it quotes is kept.","“power of choice”: *arbitrium*; “command over oneself”: *imperium in semet ipsum*."]],"reflm:5450":["Nature and freedom are two principles of the determination of our concepts. For explanation we can avail ourselves [through] of freedom only *secundum qvid*; for only nature gives the final determining grounds. For this alone contains principles of actual conduct. If I am to give a ground why someone behaves so or so, then the appeal to his free choice is a circle.",["*secundum qvid*: “in a certain respect”; “principles”: principien.","“conduct” / “behaves”: Verhaltens / verhält; “circle”: Cirkel."]],"reflm:5451":["The soul thought according to intellectual determinations:\n1. as to the subject,\n2. the forces,\n3. the state.",[]],"reflm:5452":["The soul as substance.\nintelligence (not pure).\n*simplicitas*. *Vnitas*.\n*libertas*. *Commercium cum corpore*.\n*Sedes*\n      as spirit.\n*immaterialitas* (s *personalitas*)\n       *ortus*, *mors* (s *conscientia*)",["*simplicitas*, *unitas*, *libertas*: simplicity, unity, freedom; *commercium cum corpore*: community with the body; *sedes*: seat.","*immaterialitas*, *personalitas*: immateriality, personality; *ortus*, *mors*, *conscientia*: origin, death, consciousness."]],"reflm:5453":["All inner experience [has] is a judgment in which the predicate is empirical and the subject is I. independent thus of this experience there remains merely the I left over for the *psychologia rationalis*; for the I is the *substratum* of all empirical judgments.",["“for”: vor (= für); *psychologia rationalis*: “rational psychology”."]],"reflm:5454":["Metaphysical predicates: 1. The soul is substance; 2. a substance distinguished from matter, no object of outer senses; 3. Simple, thus immaterial; 4. but whether it persists, does not follow.",["“persists”: beharre."]],"reflm:5455":["The *psychologia empirica* contains no empirical conditions of the propositions about the soul, but only the empirical concept [through which] of the soul itself with everything that belongs to it in order to distinguish it from everything else, belongs thus not to *philosophia pura*, but also not *physiologia*, but metaphysics.",["“propositions”: Satze; “metaphysics”: metaphysic."]],"reflm:5456":["In and \nThinking and willing can not at all be an outer appearance. Now I know myself as soul only according to thinking, feeling and willing. Thus we can not at all represent the soul to ourselves as an object of outer senses. Everything of which we are conscious in it [is] thus indicates an immaterial being. We would have to perceive outer things and ourselves through one and the same sense.",["The locus note “In and” is incomplete in the source.","“know”: kenne (kennen); “one and the same”: einerley."]],"reflm:5457":["In and \nOn the possibility of the *commercium* of what is only an object of inner sense with what is only of outer sense. In matter we know only the outer, in the soul only the inner immediately. The *commercium* (g among) the objects of outer senses we do not know originally and *a priori*, just so not the *commercium* of the inner forces of the soul. But the first *data* of outer cognition already contain concepts of the *commercium*, and those of the inner also. The *phaenomena* are thereby compared as to *identity*. We can therefore have insight *a priori* neither into the natural possibility nor impossibility; consequently all *systemata* about it are in vain, and it is a proposition of experience that inner determinations are grounds of the outer and conversely, and this is an original force.\nOn the seat of the soul: whether it, as a material substance, is determined [externally] in the body with regard to space, or only through the *commercium* with the body with regard to the rest of the world. It has its seat, i.e. the first and immediate connection with the nervous system.\nEverything of which a universal rule can be cognized through experience is natural. Accordingly the *commercium* is natural. Immateriality is a correct problematic concept, which cannot be refuted at all.",["The source doubles the article: \"des commercii des, was\".","\"have insight into\": einsehen; \"know\": kennen; \"force\": Kraft (Krafte, Kraft)."]],"reflm:5458":["The concept of the human soul is entirely independent of the concept of *materiality*. One also cannot at all understand determinations of inner sense as outer *phaenomena*, and conversely. The proof that the soul is immaterial [is] would be this: 1. it is not matter (*materialismus in sensu crassiori*), therefore simple; 2. it is not a simple part of matter, for matter does not consist of simple parts. Animal is animated matter. All animated matter is at the same time ensouled. For the *principium* of life is *absolute internum*, i.e. thinking and willing, i.e. determinations of oneself according to choice.",["*materialismus in sensu crassiori*: materialism in the cruder sense; *absolute internum*: absolutely internal.","\"animated\" / \"ensouled\": belebt / beseelt; \"choice\": willkühr."]],"reflm:5459":["*sedes animae*. What is merely an *object* of inner sense cannot be an *object* of outer. Accordingly the substance of the soul cannot be subjected to the conditions of outer intuition, but merely the *subject* of its influence, and the soul's acting force (outwardly) is a *phaenomenon*. But there everything that is an instrument of sensation is immediately the *subject* of its presence. It can act only by exciting the interaction of the bodily parts, attraction and repulsion of the same.",["*sedes animae*: seat of the soul. *object*, *subject* render Kant's Latinate obiect, subiect.","\"acting force\": wirkende Kraft; \"exciting\": excitirt; \"interaction\": wechselwirkung."]],"reflm:5460":["In and \nThe soul does not move other things by moving it|self.\nIt is not an *object* of the *phaenomena*. It [moves] brings about motions in others which do not [or] have their determining ground in matter, by which, however, the motion of the same is not altered in the sum.",["In the source the line break falls within be|wegt (\"moves\").","\"of the same\" (derselben) may refer to matter or to the others."]],"reflm:5461":["I, as the *correlatum* of all outer intuition, am a human being. The outer intuition to which all the rest in me refer is my body. Therefore I, as a *subject* of outer intuitions, must have a body. The conditions of outer intuition and of inner determine each other reciprocally (*commercium* of the soul and body). The actuality [of a b] of bodies with regard to myself is the interconnection of outer appearances among one another, and with regard to others the agreement in the relations [with] of the outer appearances of that through which others designate their intuition with the designations of my understanding, consequently after all the agreement of all outer appearances among one another. The actuality of bodies is not the actuality of things, but of appearances, which are referred [themselves] to x, y, etc etc. Something that is represented only as an object of appearance and yet is not supposed to be one is nothing for us. Idealism denies more than one knows; realism assumes more than what the question is about.",["\"actuality\": Wirklichkeit; \"interconnection\": Zusammenhang; \"designate\" / \"designations\": bezeichnen / Bezeichnungen.","The deleted \"[eines K]\" is presumably the start of \"eines Korpers\" (of a body).","\"for us\": vor uns, i.e. für uns."]],"reflm:5462":["In epigenesis we must assume that the soul in general belongs to the intelligible world; that it does not have a place in space; that, when an organized body has arisen through generation, it has the |\ncondition in itself to be ensouled straightaway by the intelligible animating principle; and that in the body itself the soul is present not locally but virtually.",["\"straightaway\": so fort (sofort); \"generation\": Zeugung.","\"locally\" / \"virtually\": local / virtual."]],"reflm:5463":["With empirical proofs we do not get out of the world of sense.",["\"world of sense\": Sinnenwelt."]],"reflm:5464":["Empirical proofs are valid neither for nor against, because we can undertake no experience otherwise than in *commercio* with the body. We are harnessed to the body as to a cart. Now its wheels must be in good condition, otherwise we cannot move it and for that reason also not ourselves. Here it is argued only κατ ανθρωπον. Illusion opposed to illusion.",["κατ ανθρωπον: kat' anthrōpon, \"according to the man\", i.e. ad hominem.","\"for\": vor (für); \"illusion\": Schein."]],"reflm:5465":["1. A proof *a priori* from the concept* (I think) of a living being in general. [2. From the cognition of another being *a priori* (of the necessary, of God): *theologia moralis*] 2. Proofs *a posteriori*** neither for nor against the immortality of the soul are possible from *psychologia empirica*, because we can have no experience otherwise than in *commercio* with the body. Cosmologically a proof *a posteriori* can be conducted.\n*(s of the (g immaterial) soul; 2, from the divine properties)\n**(s either from the nature of the soul or from *analogy*)",["The asterisks after \"concept\" and \"a posteriori\" are Kant's reference marks for the two notes that follow.","*theologia moralis*: moral theology; *psychologia empirica*: empirical psychology.","\"against\": wieder (wider)."]],"reflm:5466":["M 314:\n* the argument is either natural or moral**; the former either rational or empirical (s the latter either strictly moral or theological).\n*(s Through the moral one immortality is not proven, also not strictly the universality and eternity.)\n(s The empirical is physical or teleological.)\nM 314':\n**(s The physiological is either a strict or an analogical one; (g an argument,) which is in necessary combination is stringent; what consists in mere comparison touches. strict argument is genetic, the analogical is elective.)",["The first line is Latin: argumentum est vel naturale vel morale; prius vel rationale vel empiricum (posterius vel stricte morale vel theologicum).","\"is stringent\" / \"touches\": stringirt / berührt; \"combination\": Verbindung.","\"genetic\" / \"elective\": genetisch / electiv."]],"reflm:5467":["The destruction of the body removes animal life, not spiritual.",["Latin: Interitus corporis tollit vitam animalem, non spiritualem. \"destruction\": interitus."]],"reflm:5468":["1. That the continuation of the life of the soul does not depend on the *commercio* with the body, because all matter is lifeless.",["\"continuation\": Fortdauer."]],"reflm:5469":["Whether animal life is all life or the condition of the beginning of the spiritual?",[]],"reflm:5470":["The bodily means of thinking can fall away when the hindrances have fallen away.",[]],"reflm:5471":["Only the objections which the *materialist* makes against the immortality of the soul can be refuted metaphysically. The proof itself is only moral.",[]],"reflm:5472":["The proof of immortality is twofold. 1. from grounds of nature; 2. from moral grounds.\nI. a. from the nature of the soul known through experience, b. the nature of a thinking being cognized *a priori*, c. the analogy of nature in general. (g II.) The moral proof: a. theologically from the contingent will of God (g [as author Moral: laws]) (g from moral grounds of the divine will.); b. absolutely morally from the necessary will of God, in that *morality* is regarded as necessary in itself and determines the belief in God and at the same time the concept of his will.",["The deleted \"[als urhebers Moral: Gesetze]\" is so printed; presumably \"as author of moral laws\" (Moralgesetze).","\"belief\": Glaube."]],"reflm:5473":["We have to prove: 1. the persistence (g of the substance) of souls: *incorruptibility*, for which simplicity is not enough;\n2. the continuation of the [person not merely] *intelligence*, not merely as to the faculty but also as to the *actus*. Sleep of the soul;\n3. the continuation of the person and of the consciousness of the *identity* of oneself. Not metempsychosis.",["\"persistence\": Beharrlichkeit; \"continuation\": fortdauer; \"faculty\": Vermögen.","\"Sleep of the soul\": Seelenschlaf."]],"reflm:5474":["1. That the soul is after death,\n2. that it lives as *intelligence*,\n3. that it will be conscious of the *identity* of its person.\n*immortalitas*. 1. from empirical *psychology*,\n2. from the concept of *immateriality*,\n3. the *analogy* of nature.",["In 1. and 2. the verbs stand as bare infinitives (seyn, leben); the auxiliary werde appears only in 3., so a future may be meant throughout.","*immortalitas*: immortality."]],"reflm:5475":["The moral proof does not say that the soul will live in the future, but that the righteous one cannot avoid assuming this and at least regarding it as possible.",["\"the righteous one\": der rechtschaffene."]],"reflm:5476":["If I had to assume the existence of God for the Pythagorean principle, then it would not be a hypothesis, but a *postulate*. Now one must assume the existence of God as incentive for the occurring of good actions, and they are yet as necessary as geometrical propositions.",["\"incentive\": triebfeder; \"existence\": Daseyn.","\"they\" (sie) may refer to the good actions or, less probably, to something else in the sentence."]],"reflm:5477":["The moral *postulata* are evident, their opposite can be brought *ad absurdum morale*. Their ground is that good conduct is the sole condition under which one is worthy to be happy. The ground of their *necessitation* lies not in their offering means to the desire for happiness, but in their prescribing to it the *conditionem sine qva non* by which it is limited. But our longing to be happy is just as necessary and at the same time fair. Now mere worthiness cannot move without hope that it will also make us partake of the end; therefore it is a necessary moral *hypothesis* to assume another world. Whoever does not assume it falls into an *absurdum practicum*.\nThe *dilemma practicum* shows that prudence and honesty can be together in the hope of another world.\nIt is a principle of reason that we should not demand to be happy where we are not worthy of it. It is also another that it not be demanded to conduct oneself worthily if we cannot hope to become it.",["*ad absurdum morale*: to a moral absurdity; *conditionem sine qva non*: the condition without which not; *absurdum practicum*: practical absurdity.","\"worthy of it\": derselben, feminine, apparently referring to Glückseligkeit, which the sentence does not name; \"to become it\" (es zu werden) likewise refers back to being happy.","\"good conduct\": Wohlverhalten; \"prudence\": Klugheit; \"honesty\": Redlichkeit; \"fair\": billig."]],"reflm:5478":["The objections against immortality of the soul are: 1. From experience during life: decline of the mind; 2. From the contingency of generations and the squandering of germs. *praeexistence*. Birth is appearance in the world of sense.\nSeparation of the soul and of the body. Place.",["\"mind\": Geist; \"germs\": Keime; \"generations\": Zeugungen."]],"reflm:5479":["Community with the other world is either mystical or physical. The mystical cannot be *admitted* in this world, because through it laws of experience would be interrupted.",["\"community\": Gemeinschaft; \"admitted\": admittirt."]],"reflm:5480":["Apparition of spirits. Presentiment. Interpretation of dreams. Second sight. Sympathy of the soul.\nHappiness in this world consists in progressing, therefore it can never be completely attained; every moment drives us out beyond the present state.\nMoral perfection also. Therefore the future, at whatever progress it may be. Hence not improvement in dying. Not eternity |\nof rejection or beatification, but unforeseeable extent of the progress.\nM 322':\nThat without which reason could have no known law is contrary to its *maxim*, e.g. appearances, spirit.",["\"Apparition of spirits\": Geistererscheinung; \"Presentiment\": Ahndung; \"unforeseeable extent\": Unabsehlichkeit.","\"Also die Zukunft, auf welchem Fortschritte sie sey\" is elliptical; the reading \"at whatever progress it may be\" is doubtful.","\"appearances, spirit\": Erscheinungen, Geist; possibly meant as apparitions of spirits."]],"reflm:5481":["In and \nWe can extend our concepts of beings *a priori* by leaving out something that is empirical, but not by our adding something non-empirical. The concept of the nonrational souls or of the beings not united with animality.",["\"nonrational\": unvernünftigen; \"animality\": thierheit.","\"not by our adding\": literally \"not however that we add\" (nicht aber, daß wir ... hinzuthun)."]],"reflm:5482":["Possibility can be considered *materialiter* or *formaliter*. Just so necessity. Formal necessity is at all times only the necessity of judgments. Something is *materialiter* necessary, and indeed absolutely unconditioned without a restricting *respectum*. The proposition: there is a necessary thing, is inferred from this, that the *illimitatum* must be presupposed before all *negation*.",["*materialiter* / *formaliter*: materially / formally; *respectum*: respect, relation; *illimitatum*: the unlimited.","\"absolutely\": schlechterdings; \"inferred\": geschlossen."]],"reflm:5483":["If the proof [in s], to assume the necessity in the existence of a supreme being according to laws of the understanding and *maxims* of the will, is also not *apodictic*, it is yet practically *necessitating*.",["\"If ... also not\": Wenn ... auch nicht, i.e. even if ... not.","\"necessitating\": necessitirend."]],"reflm:5484":["Proof *a priori* or *a posteriori*. The latter is physicotheological or moral. The former transcendental or cosmological. The proof *a posteriori* cannot become a demonstrative one, because the ground to believe a God only runs out as onto a necessary hypothesis.",["\"only runs out as onto\": nur als auf ... hinausläuft; the construction is awkward in the German, which may have meant \"amounts only to\"."]],"reflm:5485":["He who contests the proofs of the existence of God, or even sets up proofs of the opposite in order to show that illusion is also on the side of the opponent, conflicts only with the knowledge of the divine existence, but not with the belief, which is the *principium* from principles.",["\"knowledge\" / \"belief\": Wissen / Glauben; \"illusion\": Schein.","\"the *principium* from principles\": das principium aus Grundsätzen; the relative \"which\" (der) refers to the belief."]],"reflm:5486":["The highest being is partly original, partly the being of beings.\nWhether an apodictic or a practical proof of God is possible.\nThe proof is either logically or morally sufficient.\nA propaedeutic or introductory (Wolf), but not an apodictic proof.\nBoundaries of the world of sense *a priori* and *posteriori*.",["The first line is Latin: Ens summum est partim originarium, partim ens entium.","\"sufficient\": hinreichend; \"Boundaries\": Grentzen."]],"reflm:5487":["*deismus*, *atheismus*.\nWithout a determining concept of necessity nothing follows but *deismus*.",["*deismus*, *atheismus*: deism, atheism."]],"reflm:5488":["The proof of the existence of God is either transcendental or [met] natural (physiological); the latter is either metaphysical: from the existence of a world in general, or physicotheological: from the present world.",["The deleted \"[met]\" is presumably the start of \"metaphysisch\"."]],"reflm:5489":["*Conceptus terminator*. the object of all empirical cognition is nature. The *principium* and the end of nature.",["*Conceptus terminator*: terminating (bounding) concept.","\"end\": Ende, not Zweck."]],"reflm:5490":["The being above nature we can cognize not through properties of nature, but only according to an analogy of them. The divine existence is related to all possible duration as time to eternity.",["\"properties of nature\": Natureigenschaften; \"existence\": Daseyn."]],"reflm:5491":["The natural concept.\nSome reality and some not: is a division and presupposes the All of reality (s necessary presupposition in thinking in general). The proof 1. from mere concepts, 2. from experience in general (something exists) or from determinate experience: physicotheological.",["\"division\": Eintheilung; \"determinate\": bestimt."]],"reflm:5492":["The necessary *subjective* condition of the (g inner) possibility of things is itself a (g in itself) necessary presupposition of the understanding. The being of all beings, the highest, the all-sufficient.\nThe necessary presupposition for the concept of the inner possibility of things is a *subjectively* necessary condition of the under|standing.\n(s An absolutely necessary being must exist means: our concept of possibility precedes actuality only in the case of appearances.)",["*subjective* renders Kant's Latinate subiective, subiectiv; \"all-sufficient\": allgnugsame.","\"only in the case of appearances\" (nur bey Erscheinungen) may restrict \"precedes\" or \"our concept of possibility\"."]],"reflm:5493":["The proposition of the ancients: from nothing comes nothing, or: possibility presupposes something actual, refers to this, that our understanding can only order but not produce *data*, but must find them at hand; therefore all possibility is conditioned.",[]],"reflm:5494":["We arrive at the concept of God through pure reason by two ways: 1. in that we are led to the concept of an absolutely necessary one. 2. in that we are led through concepts of the possibilities to the *summam realitatem*. From the latter as a mere *praedicate* one cannot infer to the former. But both can be united.",["\"an absolutely necessary one\": eines absolut nothwendigen, with no noun expressed.","*summam realitatem*: the highest reality."]],"reflm:5495":["M 332.\nHuman beings would no doubt gladly have a theoretical certainty of the existence of God, independent of all grounds of conduct, so that it would remain free to them to direct themselves according to the cognitions, to proceed either in accordance with the moral laws or with the laws of inclination, according as they cognized that there is a God or not. but the moral laws are of such a kind that with them there is no freedom to choose; and [they] there is no moral purity possible if, before the resolutions of the same, cognitions precede which make them unnecessary and reduce everything to prudence. Hence only so much light is given to us as [to] can subsist together with the originality of the moral motives and their impelling power. If with regard to these we were in|different,\nthen the grounds of proof (logical) would not alone convince us. The free belief in God is a merit, apodictic certainty and coerced belief from fear none.\nM 332':\nHe who believes himself objectively free is a freethinker and a practical atheist. He who merely takes him for the author of universal laws of nature without particular government is a naturalist and [also] without religion, as the former without morality.",["“grounds of proof” renders Beweisgründe; “impelling power” renders treibenden Gewalt; “coerced belief” renders Zwangsglaube.","“author” renders Urheber; “him” (ihn) in the last paragraph has no antecedent in the passage."]],"reflm:5496":["The author of nature (original being) is not a *principium* of the government of the world according to the conditions of freedom.",["“original being” renders Urwesen; “government” renders regirung."]],"reflm:5497":["The concept of God (g as supreme cause, necessary being) is a *conceptus terminator*. It belongs to the world and yet is no part of the world; we can according to the first cognize everything in it which relates to the world, for which we need to cognize it as the cause of the same; but we do not cognize what is in it. For with regard to that it also has no significance.",["“It” and “it” render Er and ihm, which may refer either to the concept (Begriff) or to God; the ambiguity is kept.","“For” reads the printed Den as Denn."]],"reflm:5498":["The cognition of the divine existence from practically sufficient principles is the belief in it.",["“principles” renders Kant's Latinate principien."]],"reflm:5499":["The necessity of a being. 1. As (g original being) independent, 2. As first cause, 3. as author, a. of the contingent in general or b. of order.",[]],"reflm:5500":["331'. E II 1646.\nM 331:\n* Absolute necessity is not internal (g I may assume whatever concept (g of the thing) I will, its opposite is |\ninternally possible). It is not external as hypothesis (g everything) of existence. Consequently as hypothesis of all possibility. Now the *substratum* of all possibility *a priori* is the highest reality. Thus the opposite of it is not among the possibilities. For all negations are limitations; consequently with all possibility the unrestricted is presupposed. This is laid at the ground in every respect, hence absolutely necessary: positive concept of necessity. (g Proof of *Carthesius*. Wolf.)\nM 331':\n*(g The necessity of space and of time cannot be proved, but only as a necessary condition of the possibility of things as to form, thus also of the *entis realissimi* as a condition of the possibilities of the same as to matter; meanwhile both grounds are only subjective.)",["“in every respect” renders in aller Absicht; “limitations” renders Schranken, “unrestricted” das uneingeschränkte.","“Proof of *Carthesius*” renders the Latin genitive Carthesii Beweis; Wolf is so spelled in the source."]],"reflm:5501":["Proof from thinking in general, and not from the determinate concept of a thing.\nThe proof of a being which comprehends everything cannot be cognized *a posteriori*, nor otherwise *a priori* than from the conditions of our reason for determining the object of cognition in the All.\nThe concept of absolute necessity also cannot be acquired otherwise. It rests on this, that everything which is to be thought be given in sensation and *negationes* are limitations.",["“comprehends” renders begreift, which can also mean “contains”."]],"reflm:5502":["331'. E II 1628II.\nM 331:\nWe can think non-being determinately only insofar as we think the thing itself, and the non-being of the determinations if these |\ndeterminations are given to us. Thus [if] I cannot think determinately that nothing at all exists, because I would have to say: there does not exist a body, spirit etc etc. But this would not be thought if nothing at all were given.\nIn the concept of the *entis realissimi* alone are the concepts of all things universally (*omnimode*) determinable; thus it is the *substratum* of the thinkability of all things. Absolute necessity is distinguished from conditioned in that the former is a necessary *hypothesis* to a determinate thinkable, the latter to everything.\nM 331':\nwe can indeed have a concept of a (form) shape without anything being there, but not of a reality; for this presupposes sensation, hence empirical intuition. At the ground of all possible real concepts there thus lies a *substratum originarium* as *realissimum*. Our thinking is only a reflecting. The material condition of all thinking (g of an object) is thus that something be given as [object] in intuition. The first formal and real ground of thinkability is necessary. As I represent to myself different possibilities, so I must assume a *substratum*. Every thing I can cancel, but not the condition of its possibility.",["“thinkability” renders Denklichkeit, “thinkable” Denklichen; “cancel” renders aufheben; “[if]” renders the struck wen (wenn).","“shape” renders Gestalt, “(form)” Kant's (Form); “object” renders Gegenstand."]],"reflm:5503":["That is unconditionally necessary which is necessary without restrictive condition, consequently in every respect.",["“in every respect” renders in aller absicht."]],"reflm:5504":["The possibility of a determinate restricted thing does indeed precede actuality; but with regard to all things in general an actuality precedes all possibility. This is the absolute reality.",["“actuality” renders Wirklichkeit, “reality” Realitet."]],"reflm:5505":["The perfect precedes in the idea *a priori* the imperfect, and the latter is determinable only in the former. (g — thoroughgoing determination.)\n(g We would not have a concept of the imperfect at all if we did not think the perfect.)\nThe cosmological proof is from the existence of the contingent, principally from the *primo motore*. The [cause] causality of the cause of the contingent is freedom. But the absolute necessity of such a being cannot be seen into, i.e. the necessity in every respect.\nphysicotheology: author according to analogy.\nMoral necessity of assuming a God. Possibility already Enough — atheism.",["“seen into” renders einsehen; “existence” here renders existentz.","“in every respect” renders in aller Absicht."]],"reflm:5506":["The Cartesian proof is this, that a concept could be so arranged that actuality comes among its predicates, although [it] the concept of the thing in itself distinguishes nothing on account of existence or non-being. That hence the non-being of this thing contradicts itself. But the cancelling of a thing with all predicates is always possible; consequently it shows the falsity of the presuppositions that it is feasible to make such a concept which could not be cancelled at all.",["“of the thing in itself distinguishes” keeps the ambiguity of vom Dinge an sich: either the concept of the thing in itself, or the concept of the thing does not in itself distinguish.","“cancelling” renders Aufhebung."]],"reflm:5507":["Around M § 815:\nIt all comes down to the faulty concept: to take existence from a thing; it should say: to take the thing itself. Existence makes up no *constitutivum* of a concept; internally, then, no concept contains such, and thus absolute necessity is |\nno inner one, but respective, but in every relation, namely of the universal *substrati* to everything possible.",[]],"reflm:5508":["Even if the existence of God does not flow from the conditions on which we ground the concept of possibility, yet enough follows from them for it to be conceded by him who can judge about this *a priori*. The subjective conditions of thinking thus serve very well to convince *cat anthropon*, not apodictically.",["*cat anthropon*: Kant's transliteration of the Greek kat' anthrōpon, “according to the man”.","“from them” renders daraus."]],"reflm:5509":["The supreme *principium* 1. of the possibilities of things; 2. of nature; 3. of [mo] morality.",[]],"reflm:5510":["At the top of the page:\nThe *summum bonum reale* is the universally valid cause.\nThe *summum bonum formale* (g *finale*) (*ideale*) is the free power of choice, which universally valid for every power of choice; hence this is also the form of the causality of the former.",["“power of choice” renders Willkühr.","The relative clause die allgemein gültig vor jede Willkühr has no verb in the source; none is supplied."]],"reflm:5511":["To moral theology it belongs that in the most perfect world moral evil must be possible, because therein there must be freedom and stimulus to evil. Whether it is possible that God does not prevent it is to be seen into only by him who cognizes the relation of the parts to the whole.",["“stimulus” renders reitz; “seen into” renders einzusehen."]],"reflm:5512":["M 335:\n*Entis originarii* (s *omnisufficientis*) (s Necessity, all-sufficiency, unity. Noumenon) 1. *Necessitas*. 2. *substantialitas*.* 3. *im|mutabilitas*.\n4. *impassibilitas*. (g b. *omnisufficientia. infinitudo*.) 5. *Vnicitas*. 6. *simplicitas*. 7. *immaterialitas* (s *supramundanum* (*contra spinozistas*)). 8. *ens extramundanum: extra spatium et tempus* (s Not in *commercio*, but indeed *nexu* with the world.) 9. *aeternitas*. 10. *omnipotentia*. **11. *omnipraesentia*. (s 12. *immensitas. omnitudo*. 13. Analogy as *ens extramundanum contra anthropomorphistas*.) (s Is not an *ens homogeneum* with the world. Is not a member in the sequence of degrees of things.)\nM 335':\n*(g Immutability rests not on necessity, but on this, that the *realissimum* is not determinable *ad oppositum realitatis*.)\nM 335:\n**(s If something exists as conditioned; the possibility of creation in time or from eternity.)",["The single and double asterisks before 3., 11. and the two added notes are the AA's reference marks, not emphasis.","“all-sufficiency” renders Allgnugsamkeit; “sequence of degrees” renders Gradfolge; “Immutability” renders Unveranderlichkeit."]],"reflm:5513":["M 335':\nThree kinds of perfection: 1. *transscendentalis* of causality; 2. *naturalis* as author; 3. of ends (s *summum bonum*). *Ens optimum*. As supreme |\nM 335: |\n*principium morale* (s holy being).",[]],"reflm:5514":["*Theologia rationalis contra* (g *pharisaeismum*) *clericismum* (*plebeii* (g *laici*)).\n*Theologiae communitas contra sophistas* (*idiota*).\n(g *Theologia arrogans* (s or *popularis*).)",["Latin: rational theology against (pharisaism) clericalism (of the plebeian (layman)); the commonality of theology against sophists (the unlearned); arrogant (or popular) theology."]],"reflm:5515":["Speculative theology is only negative, in order to keep errors away. Practical theology is positive.",[]],"reflm:5516":["Moral theology alone requires a determinate concept of a highest being; natural theology does not, but makes the existence intuitive.",["allein may attach to the subject (“moral theology alone”) or to the verb (“requires only”); the English keeps both open as far as it can."]],"reflm:5517":["Beside and \nCosmological proof: *Ens limitatum* can be determined in a twofold way, NB. *respectu* to its concept, but not to existence or non-being. *Ens realissimum* only *vnico modo*, but not with regard to existence.",[]],"reflm:5518":["Around and also perhaps \nAll possibilities presuppose the concept of an *entis realissimi*. and this concept presupposes [pre a] existence, because realities, without their having been given, also cannot be thought in sensation, but what has been given in sensation exists.\nAbsolute necessity rests on the presupposed condition of all possibility, not on the identity of a concept with itself, from which no existence follows.",["“[pre a]” renders the struck “voraus ein”, the separable prefix of voraussetzen and an article, before Kant rewrote the clause as setzt das Daseyn voraus."]],"reflm:5519":["*Necessarium absolutum*. Since diversity can be thought only through restrictions, [thus lies] thus the foundation of the |\npossibilities cannot be in many beings, because these themselves always presuppose something else, but one must contain everything, and the plurality [of the] or manifoldness rests on the restrictions of this unity.\nFor there is in our subject unity of all concepts, because [its] the possibility is only [with] the agreement with the entire inner *datis*.",["“restrictions” renders Einschränkungen; “agreement” renders einstimung; “foundation” renders Grundlage."]],"reflm:5520":["M 336'.\nA well-natured soul is easily* to be convinced to belief (g of God and of a future), but an evil one is not to be helped. His heart's hardness makes him merely keen on speculation, and he fears at most a God, but does not believe him, i.e. does not assume him.\nM 336:\n*(g From the Italian proverb: Deal with your friend as if he could one day be your enemy. God, guard me against my friend; against my enemy I will well guard myself.)",["“belief” renders Glauben; “assume” renders annehmen; “at most” renders allenfals, which can also mean “perhaps”."]],"reflm:5521":["The specific mark of God is not magnitude, but the necessity of his nature; but this is at the same time inseparably combined with the concept of the all as condition of cognition through reason.\nOne can proceed from a smaller to a greater being by degrees, but not from the contingent to the necessary.",["“magnitude” renders Große, which can also mean “greatness”."]],"reflm:5522":["The ground of the transcendental proof lies in this. We can think [no first] possibilities only *derivative*, not *originarie*; |\nconsequently the All given to us is the *substratum* of possibility, where through limitation and through altered relations all our thinking *a priori* rests on it. The subjective ground of all possibility is thus this sum total (subjective) of reality which makes up unity in us; for through this alone can the thoroughgoing relation [what] and agreement, which is the form of possibility, be understood.",["“relation(s)” renders Verhaltnis(se); “agreement” renders Einstimmung; “sum total” renders inbegrif."]],"reflm:5523":["At the top of the page and \n[Not Ma] Necessary in itself is nothing at all, i.e. according to the laws of our understanding; for the non-being of a thing has in itself nothing conflicting. But from this, that something is not cognized as necessary in itself, I cannot conclude that it is contingent, i.e. it could come about that it is or also that it is not, i.e. that its *nonexistentz* is possible as something which follows on the *existentz*, or *existentz* which follows on *nonexistentz*. Although the existence of all things can be cancelled in itself, namely the thought, the thing is not on that account to be cancelled. The proof, therefore, of the necessary from this, that, if something exists, it must be necessary or contingent, Is not valid. It is neither of the two. Its existence has no connection at all with the thought of the thing.",["“existence” renders Daseyn; Kant's Latinate existentz and nonexistentz are kept.","“conflicting” renders wiederstreitendes; “cancelled” renders aufheben; daß Nichtseyn read as das Nichtseyn."]],"reflm:5524":["On M § 826?\nTranscendental theology contains nothing but *praedicata logica Entis realissimi*; the *determinationes* as *praedicata realia synthetica* belong to theism.",[]],"reflm:5525":["1, The inner logical necessity proves no necessary existence: modalities of the determinations are not themselves determinations. |\n(g Absolute necessity from respective predicates with respect to possibility.)\n2, The conditioned is the only possible under given conditions. The all of the conditions of the existing is the only thing possible simply, i.e. in every respect; that which exists in general is in thoroughgoing respect the only possible. The supreme condition must hence be the *principium* of all possibility, hence the *ens realissimum*.\nThe transcendental proof is conducted from the concept of the *entis realissimi* not as *conceptus determinati*, but *principii determinantis summi* (*qvoad existentiam omnem*). The necessary existence is not internal, but respective, as necessary *hypothesis* of all possibility.",["“simply” renders schlechthin; “in every respect” in aller absicht, “in thoroughgoing respect” in durchgangiger absicht.","Latin: not as the concept of something determined, but of the highest determining principle (as regards all existence)."]],"reflm:5526":["Around and \nThe first subjective condition of thinking, i.e. of the representation of objects according to possibility, is that all matter of representation be given in sensation and objectively in perception, secondly the form of combination of the manifold. Hence that which contains the matter and *data* of everything possible is presupposed as an object of perception, i.e. the matter of everything possible exists as necessary presupposition. The manifoldness of objects with respect to this All of reality rests on the form of the limitation of this All. Hence there lies at the ground the absolute unity of the All, in which everything is possible through limitation. Something must therefore exist as a *substratum* of possibility.",["The editorial location note “Um und” is cut off in the source.","“objects” renders Kant’s Latinate obiecte (Object); “limitation” renders Einschränkung."]],"reflm:5527":["Above, beside and \n*Ens necessarium* is a subjectively necessary *hypothesis* of the thinking of all possibility. All possibility of a limited thing is derived in the idea and presupposes a greater, which is limited. All possibility therefore has as its condition the *ens realissimum*.",["The editorial location note “Über, neben und” is cut off in the source.","*ens necessarium*: “necessary being”; *ens realissimum*: “most real being”."]],"reflm:5528":["Beside and also probably \nTranscendental theology has the advantage that in it alone the necessity of a highest being can be cognized. Hence it alone can also determine it thoroughly as unity etc etc.\nIt alone purifies *theologia naturalis* of the influence of empirical predicates, anthropomorphism. The disadvantage is that the ground of proof is only subjective; for existence cannot be cognized completely a priori. But as presupposition it can nevertheless be necessary in the practical use of the understanding.",["The editorial location note “Neben und auch wohl” is cut off in the source.","“ground of proof” renders Beweisgrund; “completely” (völlig) may attach to “a priori” or to “cannot be cognized”."]],"reflm:5529":["*Anthropopathôs*.\nThe deist wants to prevent anthropomorphism.\nWe can represent God to ourselves only according to the analogy with human beings, but not the similarity with them. Anthropomorphism is often more harmful than atheism.\nSpeculative anthropomorphism.\nThe practical.",["*Anthropopathôs*: Greek adverb, “with human feelings”.","“human beings” renders den Menschen."]],"reflm:5530":["(g Presupposed existence of a world.)\nThe Wolffian cosmological proof of the existence of God is really transcendental. (g Something exists. I am.) There must be a necessary being, one or several.[It exists] I am not the necessary being. The necessary being must be unlimited. For the concept of the limited permits many, hence the possibility of the opposite; but the concept of the unlimited does not permit the opposite of a single determination.",["“[It exists]” renders the deleted “Es existirt”.","“being” renders Wesen; “existence” renders Daseyn."]],"reflm:5531":["*Theologia naturalis*. The original being as a cause of nature, hence in relation to it, since the causality is expressed through determinations of nature. The principal concept of causality is freedom as the causality with respect to the contingent, consequently intelligence.",["“original being” renders Urwesen.","“since” renders da, which can also be read “where”."]],"reflm:5532":["Anthropomorphism *crassior*, *subtilior*. Prevention of it *via reductionis*; the purity of the concept of God.",["*crassior*, *subtilior*: “cruder, subtler”; *via reductionis*: “by the way of reduction”."]],"reflm:5533":["The archetype is nowhere in the world, but solely in the Godhead. (g It is an idea, i.e. a cognition which contains the ground of the possibility of the objects and of all estimation of them and cannot be abstracted from them.) There is no creature which is equal to the idea of morality; at least moral perfection can be judged only according to the preceding idea of it. We have an empirical idea of the beauty of a human being and of a horse; otherwise we would not be able to say what each lacks. This idea is single (ideal in the head); everything is determined in it, if it is to have the greatest absolute perfection. That which is there according to the idea is the copy (imitation), and the sensible representation of it *apparentz*. We must study from ideas and not from experience the archetype of the things which are possible only from the understanding. The idea of morals and religion.",["“archetype” renders Urbild, “copy” Nachbild; “imitation” is Kant’s own word.","*apparentz*: Kant’s Latinate word, kept; “single” renders einig, which may also mean “unified”."]],"reflm:5534":["353. E II 1713.\n1. To determine God through psychological predicates, but from ontological grounds of the concept of a *realissimum*;\n2. from physicoteleological grounds.\nin all theology, even if we determine God through psychological or moral predicates, we must nevertheless, in order to infer them in God, derive them from the concept of the |\nM 353: |\n*realissimum*. This: whether as *complexus* or *ratio*.",["*realissimum* stands in the Latin genitive (realissimi) both times in the source.","“infer” renders schließen, which can also be read “include”."]],"reflm:5535":["Whether God through his understanding (g not reason) also contains the ground of the beings and of their possibility.\nThe difficulty is not how God cognizes the future, but how he cognizes at all the actual, when it is present to us. Only through the consciousness of his own will, that it is there; thereby, however, free actions seem to fall away.\nFreedom is absolute spontaneity of actions. It is the question whether that of which God is the cause can have absolute spontaneity. If the power which he imparted is capable of determining itself to act from objective grounds, consequently reason is an efficient cause, then he has imparted absolute spontaneity; the possibility is not to be seen into. It comes down to how God could make a substance.",["“beings” renders Wesen, which can also mean “essences”.","“spontaneity” renders Kant’s Latinate spontaneitaet; “seen into” renders einsehen."]],"reflm:5536":["Whether God cognizes the things and their actions by this, that he produces them?",[]],"reflm:5537":["*cognitio qviescens et operativa*.\nThe former: where God cognizes himself and everything possible.\nThe second: where he [sei] cognizes the products of his will.",["*cognitio qviescens et operativa*: “resting and operative cognition”.","“[sei]” is the deleted German word, left as it stands."]],"reflm:5538":["The possibility of the foreseeing of free actions has its difficulties only from the possibility of freedom itself.",["“foreseeing” renders Voraussehung."]],"reflm:5539":["The difficulty of the divine foreseeing of our free actions lies not in this, that they are future, for before God nothing is present, past or future, but in this, that God at any rate cognizes also present free actions, and thus, how even the *scientia visionis* of them is possible.",["*scientia visionis*: “knowledge of vision”.","“at any rate” renders allenfals, which can also mean “if need be”."]],"reflm:5540":["The effect of God on the human being as [free] free being is the effect of self-sufficient reason on our limited nature. For God is only idea of reason.",["“[free]” renders the deleted inflected form “freyes”, replaced by “frey”.","“self-sufficient” renders Selbständigen."]],"reflm:5541":["(g In nature there is nothing evil, but ill in the parts. In freedom alone is there evil. The whole of nature is nevertheless good, and evil concerns only the part. as each part can have collision.)\nImperfection is, from ill (which is positive) and this from evil, which is an absolute ill and not merely respective, |\nto be distinguished. All ills come from the lack of unity in the world-whole. From this arise conflicts. With perfect unity not only according to substance but also according to forces everything must agree with the nature of the being and thus be good. In the human being evil rests on the animal, which [to the rational] on impressions and inclination, and its conflict against the rational, which is directed to universally valid ends. If we were to consider the human being before development of his reason as a species of animals, then we would find the origin of evil.",["“ill” renders Übel, “evil” das Böse.","“[to the rational]” renders the deleted “dem rationalen”; “respective” renders respectiv."]],"reflm:5542":["The new discoveries in astronomy not only extend but also alter somewhat physicotheology. For if the human race is the whole genus of rational beings and the only one, then one cannot well comprehend how it agrees with the wisdom and goodness of God, since one can certainly think up something more perfect. But if there are millions of other worlds, then this is one level of rational creatures, which together with its deficiencies could not be lacking.",["“could not be lacking” renders nicht fehlen durfte, also “was not allowed to be lacking”."]],"reflm:5543":["Every series has its beginning; but that the whole series has a beginning presupposes that it can be considered as whole.",[]],"reflm:5544":["A first beginning cannot be thought according to laws of sensibility. A sequence without beginning not according to laws of reason.\n*conceptus hybridus*, which constitutes the boundary concept.",["*conceptus hybridus*: “hybrid concept”; “boundary concept” renders Grentzbegrif; “sequence” renders folge."]],"reflm:5545":["We must philosophize about nature as if the world had no beginning, and about God as if it had no succession.\nTo regard God as the highest point of the series is a mixing of kinds.",["“it” renders sie, which may refer to the world or to nature.","“mixing of kinds” renders Vermengung der Arten."]],"reflm:5546":["Beside and on the last three sentences of M § 926, esp. the word »creare« (1909-12):\n*architectus et creator, moderator* (s 1. *Auctor mundi*, not *causa physica*). *actuator substantiae mundi est creator, formae est architectus*.",["The first line is the editors’ location note.","Latin: “architect and creator, governor (… author of the world, not physical cause). The actuator of the substance of the world is creator, of its form architect.”"]],"reflm:5547":["*Theologia applicata*:\nCreation,\nGovernment,\nDirection to ends (vocation, completion).",["*Theologia applicata*: “applied theology”; “direction to ends” renders Abzwekung.","“vocation” renders Bestimung, which can also mean “determination”."]],"reflm:5548":["Above, in and \nThe bordering of the world on an empty time and empty space is impossible. Outside the world there is no possible appearance. Now, however, the world with all its predicates is only the object of appearance.",["The editorial location note “Über, in und” is cut off in the source."]],"reflm:5549":["In and \nWhy is the empty time before the world or the empty space outside the world not filled or are both full or is there no time and space at all outside the world. But if I nevertheless remain in the world, is the time of the measuring of it bounded or unbounded.",["The editorial location note “In und” is cut off in the source.","“of it” renders derselben, which may refer to the world or to time."]],"reflm:5550":["Whether God comprehends everything [creating] in his creation of the world that is possible in itself? and thus also every possible free action? and whether it is then free. How it is possible to create a freely acting being. What chooses from its own discretion according to universal law is free. The moving causes do not determine it; it determines itself. But the grounds of the opposite also become, according to universal laws, only motives to advise against the actions. The connection of the *causarum moventium* in freely acting beings thus consists only in this, that their actions are to be determined according to universal (g objective) laws of the power of choice.\nThe choice according to a universal law of the power of choice is the ground of the appropriation of that which has been chosen at discretion; but the desire which has been determined not from an inner *principio*, but singly as impression, could not be appropriated.\nThe choice according to naturally universal laws would be physically determined, but according to logically universal grounds, according to [reflected] universal principle, in that I have the faculty to compare something with all grounds of my discretion, is discretionary choice, and I am then free from singular determination; all motives can be compared with these universal rules of discretion and have their force, whether I may now be subjectively determined to these actions or not.",["“[creating]” and “[reflected]” render the deleted “Erschaffen” and “reflektirter”.","“choice” renders Wahl, “power of choice” Willkühr; “discretion”/“discretionary” render Belieben/beliebig.","“appropriation” renders Zueignung; *causarum moventium*: “of moving causes”."]],"reflm:5551":["The honor of God properly names no end, but says only that the world is so constituted that it does the greatest honor to a divine author, i.e. one can cognize from it his perfection and also have the sources to acquire it therein, so far as is possible for creatures; but in what this perfection consists is not indicated by this, and it the highest moral and physical finite good. To be happy and worthy of happiness.",["“honor” renders Ehre, which can also mean “glory”.","“and it the highest … good”: the clause has no verb in the source."]],"reflm:5552":["S. I:\nConcepts of reflection (their amphiboly)\n[which lead to paralogisms].\nParalogism is a syllogism that is false as to form, although it is correct as to matter (the premises). — It arises when [a con] the middle concept is taken in different significance in both premises — namely when the logical relation (g in thinking) in one of the premises is taken in the other for a real one (of the objects of intuition):\n1. Identity and difference. 2. Agreement and conflict. 3. The inner and outer. 4. The determinable (matter) and determination (form).\nDifferent relation to the faculty of cognition: to sensibility or to the understanding for difference of things and identity [of the] before the former also such before the latter are bricht ab.\n1. Things identical as to quality and quantity are not different (many) things, but one and the same.\nBefore the understanding indeed two drops of water, one egg like the other, but not in intuition in space as *phaenomena*.\n2. What is not logically opposed to one another is also not opposed to one another in space and time (really) a –a.\n3. [Sub] Outer things (g substances) must have inner determinations, but the determinations of matter consist in nothing but outer relations; hence I cannot infer monads, which have representations, because these are the sole inner.\n4. The matter (the constituent pieces of a thing) go before the form — but in intuition the form, which is given for itself alone, before the matter.\nSomething and Nothing\n1.\nConcept without object;\nthis is nothing. *ens rationis*.\nThought-entity.\n2.\nEmpty object of a concept.\n*nihil privativum*. Shadow.\n3.\nEmpty intuition\nwithout object.\n*ens imaginarium*.\nSpace.\n4.\nEmpty object without concept.\n*nihil negativum*.\nn. 1. and n. 2\nis thought-entity distinguished from\nnon-entity.\naxiom, anticipation, analogy, postulate.\nThe synthetic propositions *a priori* are\nprinciples of possible experience, thus\napply only to objects of the senses.\nConclusion of ontology.\nThe understanding prescribes the law to nature; but none reaching further than that of the form of appearances, which grounds the possibility |\nof experience in general. For with this nature as object of empirical cognition must be in conformity, because otherwise it would not be nature for us, since it would be impossible to find in it an interconnection that would be in conformity with our faculty of bringing the manifold of appearances into an interconnected consciousness, it consequently would not be cognizable.\n(g Empirical intuition and concepts make up experience.)\nWe can have synthetic cognition *a priori* of objects of experience, namely when they contain [on] principles of the possibility of experience in general.\nS. II:\n( g 1. That merely on the ideality of space and time the possibility of the synthetic judgment *a priori* depends. (g That, if we were to cognize the things in themselves, we would perceive them, thus would not cognize them *a priori* as so necessary.) (g because only from this, that our faculty of intuition has this form, can we know *a priori* how the objects will be intuited by us — these forms are the merely subjective of the faculty of representation — and this is objective with respect to things as appearances.) )\n3.\nThat we must lay a schema under all our pure concepts of the understanding, a [relation] way of composing the manifold in space and time. — That this schema is merely in the sensible representation of the subject, we therefore [thereb] 1. cognize only objects of the senses, consequently do not reach out to the supersensible. (Geometry). 2. The concepts, however, can be extended to all objects of thinking in general. But they give no amplification of theoretical cognition. In practical respect, however, where [the] freedom |\nis the condition of their use, [therefore] [propositions] practical-dogmatic cognitions can yet take place — God, freedom, and immortality (spiritual nature).\nIn nature, however, i.e. in space and time, nothing unconditioned can be encountered, and yet reason demands it as the totality of the conditions, because it wants to make the object itself. — Hence in cosmology, where nature is considered as the whole of all objects of the senses, antinomy is encountered; — in theology, where we have to weigh an object only with a practically dogmatic aim, the relation of the supersensible object lying beyond nature [only] to the things of the world can be cognized only according to the analogy with an intelligence of nature, and also only insofar as it is thought in moral respect to human beings.\nThreefold intellectual (g intelligible) (noumenon) contains the unconditioned, and of freedom and its laws one can have cognition and thereby prove the objective reality of humanity as noumenon in the midst of the mechanism of the same as phaenomenon. — God as unconditionally necessary substance. Freedom as unconditioned causality and immortality as personality (spirit) independent of *commercio* with the body (as condition).\nThe categories applied to the intelligible can yet ground practical-dogmatic cognitions, namely when they are directed to freedom and determine the subject of the same only in relation to it; for then we cognize God only according to the analogy of the subsistence [of the same] of a thing in all change of the accidents in time (g duration), freedom according to the analogy of causality in the [sequence] connection of force with the effects in the time-sequence, immortality according to the analogy of the connection of many at all time, consequently [with the] the being-simultaneous of the bricht ab.",["“identity” renders Einerleyheit, “agreement” Einstimmung, “conflict” Wiederstreit; “premises” renders both Vordersätze and Prämissen.","“opposed to one another” renders reflexive sich entgegengesetzt, which can also be read “opposed to itself”; “For with this” keeps dieser ambiguous between the law and the form.","“go before” keeps the plural gehen with singular Materie; “amplification” renders Erweiterung, “extended” ausgedehnt; “bricht ab” (breaks off) is the AA's."]],"reflm:5553":["S. I:\nJust as the senses relate to the understanding, so the understanding to reason. The appearances of the former receive in the second |\nunity of understanding through concepts, and concepts in the third faculty unity of reason through ideas (through prosyllogisms a higher subject is always found, until finally no other can be found any more of which the previous predicate would be; likewise with conditional inferences, but there the prosyllogism proves the *minorem*).\nThe cognitions a priori which go to the objects themselves are exhausted in the transcendental analytic and are the categories of the understanding. Thus reason cannot go to the objects (appearances) themselves, but to the concepts of the understanding of them. How indeed should a concept of reason (if there are such) be objective, since it expresses [the condition] not the unity of (g possible) experience (as that through which all objects are given to us), but that of the cognitions of the understanding. Meanwhile, cognitions of reason which contain entirely a priori and not empirical unity would nonetheless have to be borrowed from the logical form of reason, except that it would have to concern a synthetic unity to which the concepts of the understanding were subject. and thereby mediately went to a particular [unity] determination of the unity of appearances. Because reason seeks to bring cognitions of the understanding (judgments) under higher (universal) conditions, [thus can] so [far as] long as such a cognition can still be regarded as conditioned: so one can say that it is a *principium* of the use of the understanding, [in the series of the] to find for the conditioned the unconditioned. [This unconditioned now (g which contains the rule) in all concepts of relation of the understanding For] The concepts of relation, however, are nothing other than the [connect] unity of the conditioned and its condition, [as] and reason only heightens this relation up to the condition that is itself unconditioned. — Thus all cognitions of reason will be parallel to the three kinds of syllogisms. and more of them will not be possible. The principle of pure reason: all conditioned cognition stands not only under conditions, but finally under such as are themselves unconditioned, may be a mere |\npetition or a postulate (which we do not yet want to decide): still it is at least the ground of all application of reason, which we at least approach. Every syllogism is nothing other than [the cognition] a judgment by means of the subsumption of its condition under a universal rule, which is thus the condition of the condition of the conclusion. The conditional syllogism is so called improperly, for there are more conditions of judgments than merely the ground with regard to the consequence (*conditio conseqventiae*). There is also a condition of inherence etc etc.\nThe proposition: if the conditioned is given, then the whole series of all conditions through which the conditioned is determined is also given, is, [in since] if I abstract from the objects or take them merely intellectually, correct.\n––––––––––\nThe unconditioned can never be given, but must at all times be in thought. Hence ideas. The absolute totality of the conditions is the only unconditioned. Unity of the principle for reason. approximation. Rules of the synthesis of the subordination of empirical concepts.\nS. II:\nSystem of the transcendental ideas. 1. The titles are 3 according to the three kinds of syllogisms; of the dialectical inferences there are 4 according to the 4 categories. In the ideal of pure reason, because there [a Ha] all categories are together in one idea, we do not need to distinguish them; for it is the *principium* of all possibility, through which then the categories themselves are determined.\nOn the threefold kinds of transcendental illusion. There are two ideas and one ideal.* The paralogism of pure reason is properly a transcendental subreption, since our judgment about objects [for] and the unity of consciousness in it is taken for a perception of the unity of the subject.\n*(g The first illusion is that where the unity of apperception, which is subjective, is taken for the unity of the subject as of a thing. The second: where the subjective determination of sensibility and its condition is taken for an object. The third: where the universality of thinking through reason is taken for a thought of an All of the possibilities of things.)\nThere is no deduction of the transcendental ideas except a negative one.\nOn the conclusion of the dialectic: that all dialectical questions are completely answered.\nAll concepts of synthesis require a third: either possible experience or the idea. With regard to the transcendental idea they cannot have objective validity, but can nonetheless be deduced as necessary problems or questions.\n––––––––––\nIn the understanding the concepts conform to possible experience, for reason, however, possible experience to the concepts, just as all virtue in its exercise must conform to concepts and only thereby is possible. although it never reaches the concept. Possible experience in the *regressus* to the conditions conforms to the concepts of reason or transcendental ideas. Precisely therein consists the business of reason, to procure for the use of the understanding in the greatest manifoldness unconditioned unity. That concept of reason which combines the greatest particular unity with this universal one agrees with possible experience and is to that extent a correct rule. But that concept cannot be objectively valid which does not (g be-)stand in (g the) relation to possible experience.\nThe ideal must be thoroughly determined, for the idea is the |\nstandard. It is the unmutilated being of a thing and wherein, as members of the disjunction, all possibilities lie.\n––––––––––\nAll three transcendental ideas hang together through a syllogism. Namely: All objects of the senses are ultimately grounded on an (g existing) *noumenon*.\n––––––––––\nReason serves to give to the understanding [extension in the] necessity and to the sphere of its use domain and unity.\nThe way of regarding the particular as a determination of the universal (of the true universal, which is not drawn as induction from the particular) is a unity a priori which is entirely distinct from the unity of experience.\nS. III:\n(g Lucian's Writings. First Part. Zurich at Gesner's. 1769.)\nThe system of the dialectical syllogisms rests on the unity of them among one another in a syllogism, wherein 1. subjective unity of all representations, i.e. unity of the subject, secondly [collective] synthetic unity of the object or of the appearance, [third] this either of the appearances: collective, or of the thoughts of objects in general: distributive (disjunctive).\nReason [seeks] cognizes first the universal *in abstracto*, then it seeks to find for the particular the universal, in order from this in turn to infer to the particular.\nFirst section: on the [transc:] ideas in general.\n––––––––––\n1. Remark. In syllogisms, if the *major* is an experience-[concept] proposition (All human beings are mortal), then it is nonetheless a universal concept. But since it is so only from induction, it is nonetheless no cognition from concepts. Thus such are nonetheless ultimately required by reason, in order that true universality may arise; for from |\nthis alone does reason want to hold something to be in itself determined and necessary.\n2. The analytic inferences are, to be sure, from concepts; but the *major* is a universally identical judgment, which expresses not unity of the diverse, but of the concordant and identical.\n––––––––––\nThe unconditioned subjective conditions of thinking.\nThe unconditioned (g objective) condition of appearances.\nThe unconditioned objective condition of all objects in general.\n––––––––––\nBy the idea I understand a concept which is sufficiently grounded in reason, but to which no [corres] object can be given in any possible experience.\nTo what then does the idea go and why is it grounded in reason, in order to relate to objects. The idea of the soul is grounded on this, that the understanding must relate all [ex] thoughts and inner perceptions to the I and assume this as the single persisting subject, so that the most complete unity of [cog] self-cognition may come out.\nThe idea of the unconditioned for all conditions of appearance is grounded in reason as a prescription to seek the completeness of all cognition of the understanding in the subordination.\nThe idea of the unconditioned unity of all objects of thinking in an *ens entium* is necessary in order to seek the affinity among everything possible and thereby both thoroughgoing connection and unity of the principle.\nIn right merely concepts of reason are needed, but in morals ideas. To the former a congruent object can be given in experience, to the latter not, because they contain the highest unity of reason of the freedom agreeing with itself and with all ends of its vocation.\nLegislation needs ideas, [whether they] and these can never be completely carried out. But they are not for that reason null and superfluous.\nTranscendental philosophy needs ideas just as necessarily as morals.\nPlato's hyperbolic elevation of the ideas as archetypes are in the highest intelligence, if they are personified, not to be censured; they are the standard of things, which limit one another and do not fulfill their end singly, so that no experience is congruent with it.\nS. IV:\nWhether the inference does not go from the human soul through the world-concepts to God and from him thus back to the intelligible life of the soul?\nBut whence comes the dialectical illusion with the transcendental ideas. That which makes all illusion: namely the confusion of the subjective conditions of our thinking with the objective ones. This we cannot avoid, because we must think an object as unconditioned and have no other way of thinking it than only that which the particular constitution of our subject brings with it. We can, however, nonetheless unravel the rationalizing inferences either *in forma* or *in minore* or *maiori*, because the synthesis goes further than the *data* and the mode of inference permit. The supposition of pure reason is when one presupposes that which one is supposed to prove, and proves it through the consequences. Namely, that which is a priori thoroughly determined is presupposed, in order to represent to oneself thereby the thoroughgoing determination of everything possible.\nIf I assume: I am not an appearance of inner sense, but a thing in itself and *noumenon*, then my inhering *accidentia* must also be *noumena*. *atqvi*: I represent to myself the series of conditions in appearance; thus they must be given in me in themselves. This conclusion, made into the *minor* in the episyllogism, runs: If all appearances are given in one in themselves, then the idea [of the thorough], which determines the things in themselves, is given in some intelligence.\n(g One says that something is a mere idea when one cannot even approach it.)\n1. The unconditioned of inherence (g or aggregate). 2. That of [consequence] dependence or of the series. 3. That of the concurrence of all possibilities into one and of one to all.\n(g Just as syllogisms determine a judgment in the universal through subsumption, so reason determines the objects in the totality of the conditions.)\nIn transcendental psychology the identity, simplicity and modality of the passive existence flow from the one concept of substance.\nThe absolute is as much as the unconditioned, this as the completed, which is thought negatively without restricting condition.\nWe can think the universal only through separation of all restricting conditions. The abstraction from the determinations of the self makes the I seem unconditioned.\n(g How can morals rest on ideas, since one nonetheless demands that the actions ought to be in conformity with them. One can only call them concepts of reason. [The wise man is an idea]. They in their whole purity are ideas. Justice.)\n––––––––––\nWe can call reason the faculty of ideas*. There are ideas of sensibility, also those of pure reason. These are either practical or speculative; the latter are transcendental ideas. These are necessary concepts of reason, to which no object can be given in the senses. As (g pure) concepts, however, they must nonetheless be taken from the categories; as concepts of reason they must However be merely inferred; as necessary concepts of reason they must contain the necessary conditions of the whole use of the understanding, i.e. of its use in its totality, and as transcendent concepts this totality must go so far that it exceeds all sensible intuition; for otherwise they would be immanent. For this the absolute is required etc etc. How does the transcendental syllogism run?\n*(g Reason is a faculty, a to determine to the whole domain of its condition. If I say: Caius is mortal, then I consider first mortality in the [its] whole domain [inso] of the concept under which Caius is contained, i.e. human beings, and subsume the former [further] in this domain, in order to determine him in it.\n1. The unconditioned unity (g of the synthesis) of the subject. 2. The unconditioned unity of the synthesis of the conditions in appearance. 3. The unconditioned unity of the synthesis of thinking in general. All through the four categories, insofar as they indicate unconditioned unity.\nThe transcendental idea can have nothing but the cognitive powers as its object, or representations in general in relation to them. Thus 1. apperception, secondly the apprehension of appearance, 3. the concept of the understanding in general. The first is a concept of reason of the subject, the second of the object, insofar as it can be given, the third of the object of thinking in general.\nThat pure reason has no objective content of its dialectical inferences.)",["'object' renders both Gegenstand and obiect (the Latinate form, as in 'obiecte', 'obiect'); 'subject' likewise renders subiect.","'Plato's hyperbolic elevation of the ideas as archetypes are in the highest intelligence' keeps the German order and the plural verb sind; it may also be read 'as archetypes that are in the highest intelligence'. 'Reason is a faculty, a to determine to the whole domain of its condition' renders the broken ein dem Ganzen umfange seiner Bedingung zu bestimmen; a word seems to be missing after ein.","'syllogism': Vernunftschlus; 'inference': Schlus/Schluß; 'standard': Richtmaas; 'domain': Umfang; 'Das die reine Vernunft' (Das for Daß) is rendered 'That'."]],"reflm:5554":["S. I:\nThe reflective concepts can be taken logically, hence merely [intellectually] analytically, or transcendentally, hence synthetically. Identity and difference intellectually is analytic, but sensibly determined synthetic, and difference of place makes, regardless of the identity of the things in themselves, numerical difference. Agreement and opposition logically rests on the principle of contradiction; in empirical use: two realities which together give a reality or negation. The inner and outer logically is what is a predicate of the thing itself or of another, empirically: what is in another place of space. The determinable and the determined: the substantial and the substance.\nOf this the most famous proposition of subreption is: *principium indiscernibilium*.\nOur three higher powers go to unity, truth and perfection through the understanding, the power of judgment and reason. The last in the transcendental sense brings forth all sorts of confusion.\nOne can regard the whole monadology as the *systema*\n... of Leibniz from the concepts of reflection. The inner and outer taken intellectually gives *monas*, because the *compositum* is a *totum relationum*, and *repraesentativa*, because the *repraesentationes* are inner, place and extension however outer. Space is nothing but the *phaenomenon* of the outer. But what then do the monads represent, if they merely represent one another, each thus the representations of the others? (One also cannot with Maupertuis derive the outer of motion from the inner.) Leibniz. *harmonia praestabilita*. Because the monads do not stand *in commercio* by virtue of coexistence in space.\nS. II:\nNoumenon signifies (g properly) everywhere the same, namely the transcendental object of sensible intuition (g This is however no real object or given thing, but a concept in relation to which appearances have unity.), for to this something must after all correspond, although we know nothing other than the appearance of it. We cannot however say that the (g pure) categories have objects; rather they merely determine the transcendental object in relation to our sensibility through the synthesis of the manifold of intuition. Thus [is] there corresponds to them no *noumenon*.\nThe transcendental object which corresponds to appearances (g or also every object) can only be called *noumenon* insofar as it can be represented through the concept of the understanding. Now this is impossible through the |\ncategory, since the conditions of intuition are lacking, thus we have no concepts for noumena.\nWe can [think] use these concepts according to an analogy with the sensible; but, because they have objective validity only in relation to the synthetic unity of apprehension in time, they refer in themselves to no object at all and under the sensible determination only to *phaenomena*.",["identity and difference: Einerleyheit und Verschiedenheit; agreement and opposition: Einstimung und Widerstreit.","sense: Verstande (im transscendentalen Verstande), not the faculty.","object: both Obiect and obiect in the German; no Gegenstand occurs here."]],"reflm:5555":["χ−θ? (ψ?)\nKant's remark on the back of the undated letter from Glave (X 243 [2 X 260]) in vol. II of the Dorpat collection of letters to Kant, p. 273:\nJust as a pure concept of the understanding arises only through the form of judgments, in that I make them synthetic (g and thereby think an object), so a pure concept of reason arises through the form of a syllogism. This, however, is the subsumption under the universality [of a] of the condition of a judgment; thus the concept is a representation of the totality of the conditions for cognizing an object according to the one or the other relation of a judgment. The logical condition of the judgment is the relation to the subject etc.; the concept of a thing through this logical function is the category. The [totality] universality of the [condition] relation is the logical form of the syllogism; the concept of a thing through the [subsu] representation of the totality of the condition [of the judgment] of the (g application of the) categories is the concept of reason.\nThe concept of the totality of synthesis according to the categories of relation is the pure concept of reason.\nWithout the concept of reason we would indeed have experiences, but the collective unity of experience would be lacking, as that in which after all all [part] empirical cognition must be determinable.\nThe totality of synthesis in a subject, 2. in a series, thirdly in a system. *Suppositum* is the presupposition of that which is to be found, and the conditions of it, in order to find the synthesis |\nof cognitions through which we are to approach it. Idea is such a *suppositum*, which indeed cannot in itself be given, but can nevertheless bring formal unity of reason into our cognition.",["relation: both relation and Verhaltnis (categories des Verhaltnisses); relation is the Latinate member.","syllogism: Vernunftschluß.","[subsu]: a word broken off and struck, kept as it stands."]],"reflm:5556":["Analytic possibility rests on the principle of contradiction and is the possibility of the concept; synthetic possibility, which must be added, i.e. that an object could correspond to the concept, on this, that something be in accordance with the conditions of an object of experience (g in general). (g Only in experience can an object be given.)\nThe actual is opposed to the impossible and at the same time to the merely possible.",["object: Gegenstand here, obiect in the second addition."]],"reflm:5557":["Possibility, actuality and necessity are not determinations, but modality of the position of the thing with its predicates.",["position: position (Latinate), not Setzung."]],"reflm:5558":["The propositions of modality, e.g. A is possible (*realiter*) (g actual etc. etc.), are synthetic propositions. but not to the concept of the thing, rather to thinking in general something is added.",[]],"reflm:5559":["That possibility (g of a thing) from which all possibility must be regarded as derived is simply presupposed, consequently regarded as *positio absoluta* and as an existing thing, and there holds the |\ninference from the *esse* of one thing to the *posse* of all others, hence also the *posse* in general presupposes an *esse*. From the possibility of things in general one can infer a determinate thing as ground.",["simply: schlechthin.","inference: Schlus; infer: schließen."]],"reflm:5560":["Unity of the hypothesis consists (g 1.) in this, that, if I assume something as ground, the manifold can be derived from it. Unity.\n2. That I can assume this [unity] ground or concept and it thus has reality. Truth.\n3. That the given consequences all together agree with the one ground, consequently no *hypothesis subsidiaria* is necessary. Perfection.\nHypothesis of a spiritual nature of the soul:\n1. That from it the *phaenomena* of thinking can be explained, and of independence from the body.\n2. That also such a thing has reality, consequently the synthetic possibility; for the analytic is clear. But since the former would have to be cognized from the analogy of experience, which however it lacks, truth falls away; *ens rationis ratiocinantis*.\n3. That all *phaenomena* together can be derived from it, as many of them as we know, and they thus agree with this ground as the single one. But this fails; for the dependence of the soul on mechanism requires yet another hypothesis, namely the influence of matter on a spirit. Likewise the concept of God.\nIn the distinction of the idea (g *ratiocinatae*) from a chimera, truth comes before [perfection] order, in the difference of experience from dream order before truth and a criterion of the same order of nature.\nFormal or material unity, truth and perfection. The unity of a thing or unities.",["consequences: folgen (Grund and Folge).","a criterion of the same order of nature: either a criterion of it, the order of nature, or a criterion of that same order of nature; the German allows both.","spirit: Geist."]],"reflm:5561":["These concepts are not the categories [but] of things, but logical criteria of agreement with the laws of the understanding.\n1. Unity of the concept, that it be not [yet at] another than this one. or unity of the meaning of the word — clarity.\n2. Truth of the elementary propositions (multiplicity of them) — distinctness.\n3. Completeness of the marks in a definition — exhaustiveness and precision.",["elementary propositions: Elementarsetze, a doubtful form; it may stand for Elementarsätze or Elementargesetze (elementary laws).","exhaustiveness: Ausführlichkeit."]],"reflm:5562":["The transcendental concepts go to the relation of a multitude of representations to an object. 1. Unity of the consciousness of something; 2, combination of the representations among one another in one consciousness; 3. the representation of the object arising from that.\nThe three concepts of the possibility of a thing [as] in general: unity, truth and perfection refer to the three formal principles of all judgments: that of contradiction, of sufficient ground and of the determinability of it in regard to all possible predicates (*judicia categorica, hypothetica et disjunctiva*).",["of it: desselben, which may refer to the ground or to the thing."]],"reflm:5563":["[Logical] criterion of empirical truth. Order of nature or. the order in itself, i.e. combination according to rules, proves the relation to an object and not merely choice.",["choice: wilkühr (also arbitrariness)."]],"reflm:5564":["Whether it be possible that nothing at all exist?",[]],"reflm:5565":["Analytic possibility does not yet prove the synthetic. The non-being of a thing is without contradiction. From this I cannot yet infer that it is *realiter* possible that it not be.\nPossibility and necessity of propositions or of things.",[]],"reflm:5566":["The necessary is (g insofar) determinable in only one single way (*oppositum est impossibile*); the simply necessary is only a single one; otherwise, if there were more, one could cease without all existence being thereby cancelled.",["simply necessary: schlechthin nothwendig.","cancelled: aufgehoben."]],"reflm:5567":["That which is [not] necessary without real condition can nevertheless be necessary as condition, which however is no absolute but restrictive necessity.\nAbsolute dominion can indeed be conditioned, but not restricted.",["restricted: restringirt."]],"reflm:5568":["Necessary is that whose existence can be cognized *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5569":["Necessity is either logical or real. analytic and synthetic. Synthetic necessity [from] either from concepts or intuitions or the relation of concepts in general |\nto intuitions. Necessary existence is either derived or original. *Necessitas originaria vel derivativa*. Necessity as condition is properly hypothetical, but nevertheless not conditioned; it is necessity as presupposition.\nThe necessity of a thing in itself is that of existence, not of the relation of a predicate to the subject or of the conditioned to the condition. To a thing necessary in itself existence does not belong necessarily. For then it lies in the concept; rather it is the condition for positing an existence at all.",["The second sentence has no main verb in the German; kept so."]],"reflm:5570":["Hypothetical necessity is to be distinguished from the necessity of a hypothesis, which is opposed to the postulated necessity, which is absolute. The former is necessity as of a premise, the latter as of a principle of cognition. (g The absolute necessity of a thing is a necessary hypothesis of reason.)\nLogical necessity: that whose opposite is logically impossible from concepts, i.e. contradicts itself. Real necessity: that whose opposite is a non-thing, i.e. whose not-being cannot synthetically subsist together with a concept. The former the necessity of judgments, the latter of things.",["non-thing: Unding.","things: Sachen, not Dinge."]],"reflm:5571":["A thing in general as limited is to that extent in itself undetermined in regard to many predicates; but as unlimited it is thoroughly determined.\nBut as a single one (this thing) it is also thoroughly determined, only not from the concept of a thing in general.",["determined: bestimt in the first sentence, determinirt in the second.","limited ... unlimited: limitirt ... unbeschränkt."]],"reflm:5572":["Bottom margin \nReal necessity is the necessity of the existence of things, logical the necessity of the connection of cognitions, e.g. in judging, likewise the necessity of a hypothesis for the contingent.",[]],"reflm:5573":["At the very top of the page:\nOur concept of possibility is properly derived; namely it presupposes something given which agrees (g with) the conditions of the understanding [ge] as to form. Only one single thing is *originarie* possible, namely the *realissimum*, and this is presupposed.",["[ge]: a struck word-beginning, kept as it stands."]],"reflm:5574":["Above and \nWhether something can be determined in another way or not, we see either from the *principium contradictionis*; and then, if it [not in anoth] can be determined, i.e. does not contradict itself, real contingency does not follow;\nor from alterations: but there not either.",["The heading Über und breaks off in the transcription; kept so."]],"reflm:5575":["*Nulla mutatio est absolute necessaria*, it always has another cause. Thus no *absolute necessarium* can be *mutabile*.",["*Nulla mutatio est absolute necessaria*: no alteration is absolutely necessary; *absolute necessarium*: absolutely necessary thing; *mutabile*: alterable."]],"reflm:5576":["How is alteration in general possible. That cannot be explained, because it is determination in time and presupposes forces, these however *a posteriori*.\n[All] alteration is not an arising or passing away of things as of their determinations, while the thing remains. E.g. motion is alteration of the relation of the body, not of the body itself. Inner motion is alteration of the body.",["of things as of their determinations: der Sachen als ihrer Bestimungen; the reading is doubtful, and als may stand for a contrast (not of things but of their determinations).","The opening question has no question mark in the source."]],"reflm:5577":["Mere form without reality (g thought as thing in itself) is *ens imaginarium*. Space.\nMere negation without reality is *non ens*.",[]],"reflm:5578":["Realities in appearance (experience) can indeed conflict with one another, but not *in noumenis*, because with these the *oppositum* of reality must be thought *a priori*, consequently cannot be other than logical, i.e. negation.",["conflict: wiederstreiten."]],"reflm:5579":["Opposite and \nBecause an *ens partim reale, partim negativum* is always thought as defect (*qvoad entitatem* in general), but this presupposes the concept of the *completum*, the concept of the *realissimum* lies at the ground of all things. The *realissimum* is also represented through its concept as *singulare*, and all its determinations indeed only generically and not specifically breaks off?",["The heading Gegenüber und breaks off in the transcription; kept so.","breaks off?: bricht ab?, the editor's note that the text ends here."]],"reflm:5580":["To every reality a there corresponds its opposite =0 and its counterpart (*contrarium*) –a, i.e. a ground which connected with a becomes =0. Pleasure, pain, indifference; truth, error, ignorance. But light, darkness and deprivation of light through dark bodies, i.e. shadow. Virtue, vice, *adiaphoron*. In general: cause, ineffectiveness (inertia) and hindrance. Utility, harm, being indifferent.",["opposite: Gegentheil; counterpart: Wiederspiel.","connected: verknüpft."]],"reflm:5581":["The quantitative [disposition] decomposition, division of the homogeneous, is to be distinguished from the qualitative (g dissolution) of the heterogeneous or of the mixture (of those different as to matter). Both go to infinity. Water, for example, consists of [infinitely] matters which again consist of others to infinity. No chemical elements.",["division: Zertheilung; dissolution: Zersetzung.","of those different as to matter: der Materie nach verschiedenen; the noun is not stated."]],"reflm:5582":["(g There is no *qvantum discretum*, but rather *qvantitatem discretam*. Number.)\nThe *qvanta* are different with respect to quantity, with respect to quality the same, namely that of decomposition without absolutely first parts. Yet the *qvantum* of absolute position or reality (g sensation) is distinguished from other *qvantis* in this, that the former vanishes into nothing, these, however, nevertheless leave a positive boundary, although no part. Boundaries of space (threefold) and of time (one). The generation of reality has a moment, that of the *qvanti extensivi* an (g as it were) element: differential. (The former is to be regarded like the line which in a time [with accelera] describes a plane.) As a different moment |\ngenerates different degrees of velocity, so difference of the impression different degree of sensation. Intellectual, however, a reality is not [ans] to be regarded as a multitude of different ones, but is* in continuous interconnection with nothing; division of the parts.\n*(g We have 3 *qvanta*. Space, time and sensation (motion, reality). The first has a positive of the boundary, which is a *qvantum*; the second, one which is no *qvantum*, the third nothing positive at all and no boundary, but limitations. Reality taken transcendentally is not of a different kind.)",["Boundary = Grenze, limitations = Schranken; moment = Moment.","intellectual may be read as an adverb (intellectually) or as an adjective with realitaet; the sentence is left as ambiguous as the German.","[Beschleu] and [ans] are deletions broken off in the manuscript."]],"reflm:5583":["Mathematics as synthetic cognition a priori grounds its possibility on this, that it can construct its concepts; for it has to do only with space and time, [which] in which objects of intuition can be given a priori. These, however, are *qvanta*, thus it is a science of *qvantis*. But it also considers quantity by means of number, by means of multitude, which can be constructed in time through counting. (g This science can nevertheless not go further than to the world of sense, because only of this can the intuition a priori be given.)",["objects = obiecte (Latinate).","world of sense = Sinnenwelt."]],"reflm:5584":["Possibility of the construction of concepts, since thus intuition is given through synthesis a priori.",["da may be read as since or as where (in which)."]],"reflm:5585":["*Principium* of the possibility of mathematics as a synthetic cognition a priori. It is the synthesis in the intuition a priori, i.e. space and time. Pure mathematics.\n*Principium* of the mathematical cognition of appearances: All appearance has as intuition its extensive magnitude and as sensation its degree. For (as regards the latter) every sensation arises from not-being, because it is a modification. Thus through alteration. But all alteration passes over from 0 to a through infinitely [small] many small steps.",["alteration = Veränderung; steps = Stufen."]],"reflm:5586":["The magnitude of that which is represented as multitude or as unity.",[]],"reflm:5587":["Some concepts cannot be constructed, such as the concept reality, of cause etc etc. The magnitude of something, insofar as it is represented as multitude, is extensive; as unity intensive. Space and time are extensive. Velocity intensive. Magnitude of the ground.",[]],"reflm:5588":["48'. E II 615.\nM 48:\nThe magnitude of a thing, insofar as it is not intuited as composite*, whose arising and passing away, however, can be regarded as composite (in time), is a degree.\nM 48':\n*(g because its parts are not outside one another in space.\nIntellectual magnitude is that of the ground.)",["whose = deren, referring grammatically to Größe (magnitude)."]],"reflm:5589":["1. Possibility of (g pure) mathematics.\n2. [Application] Possibility of the applied. For all things (g as appearances) have a magnitude: extensive and intensive. (g Thereby mathematics obtains objective reality. It does not go to *entia rationis*.)\n3. All things as objects of the pure understanding also have one, namely a metaphysical, but no transcendental magnitude, because there they are compared as things in general with all others, but here as concept (g of the thing) with the essence of the thing itself.\nThe possibility of natural science rests on the dynamical principles, namely those which concern the existence of things in combination. Those concerned only the (g empirical) intuition and the possibility [according to] of empirical concepts of the objects, but not insofar as they belong to a nature.",["principles = principien; objects = gegenstande (3.) and obiecte (last sentence).","essence = Wesen."]],"reflm:5590":["All properties of things have a degree, but the thing itself (substance) not.\n(g All intensive magnitude must nevertheless in the end be brought to extensive.)\n(g Inner) possibility [in itself] has no degree, but indeed the possibility in connection. The possibility in all connection (absolute) has the highest degree; [ab] on the other hand the conditioned possibility, which takes place only in some connection, has a degree so much smaller as there are many conditions that belong to it. E.g. thus many throws belong to hitting a *terne*.\nConnection is not always condition. Contentment is possible in solitude, in business, in want, in sickness. But these are not its restrictive conditions.\nConnection takes place either with its condition, which, when it is complete, is called complete possibility; or with other grounds, i.e. circumstances.",["terne: a lottery term, three numbers drawn together.","complete renders both complet and vollstandig.","properties = Eigenschaften; connection = Verknüpfung."]],"reflm:5591":["If a magnitude is given as a thing in itself, then the whole precedes the composition, and there I cannot conclude, from the fact that this |\ncomposition can never be completed [k] and thus the *qvantitas* of it can never be wholly cognized, that such an [qua] infinite *qvantum* is impossible. It is only impossible for us, according to our way of measuring magnitudes, to cognize it wholly, because it is immeasurable. From this it does not follow that another understanding could not cognize the *qvantum* as such a whole without measuring.\nJust so with division.",["[k] and [qua] are deletions broken off in the manuscript and are left as they stand."]],"reflm:5592":["In appearances alone can only a first according to time be sought, and in them there is nevertheless no first. But a first of the ground in general one must seek in the intellectual.",[]],"reflm:5593":["Mathematics has the peculiarity that it (g only) goes to such objects as can be represented *coram intuitu*. and thus it always has empirical confirmation.\nIt is, (g as) a synthetic cognition a priori, possible, because there are two *intuitus a priori*: space and time, in which a synthesis of composition a priori is possible. These two objects are *qvanta* and indeed *originaria*, and the mere synthesis of them is quantity. All concepts of *qvantis* can be constructed in them, i.e. given a priori in intuition, likewise all concepts of quantity, i.e. of number, which requires time as well as space. The *universale* is here [weighed] given *in singulari* in intuition and *in singulari* the universal of the synthesis considered. With qualities this does not work. Through discursive cognitions no mathematics can arise. Mathematical cognitions as cognitions of reason a priori are apodictic and as *intuitus* demonstrative, both together evident.\nMathematics is distinguished from philosophy not by the |\nobject (both consider magnitude), but by the *modum cognoscendi*. This, however, determines the difference of the objects. (dynamical principle.)\n(s Philosophy treats of magnitudes so far as one can get on through mere concepts, and mathematics of qualities so far as mere intuition reaches. E.g. the former of the question of the simple parts, the latter of the cause of the gravitation of the moon.)",["the peculiarity = das Besondere.","objects = Gegenstande in the first paragraph, object/objecte (Latinate) in the second.","the lower-case and after intuitu follows the manuscript's punctuation."]],"reflm:5594":["93. E II 892.\nOf the *ente contingente* we have no other concept than that it is such a one whose not-being can be thought. But then everything is logically contingent. *Realiter* contingent would be that whose not-being in itself is impossible, although it can be thought; but of this we have no concept, it is problematic. Substances we represent to ourselves as *entia a se* together with their states. Ends are *entia ab alio*.\nWe have to do only with appearances and not with *entibus per se*. Thus the question is only according to the contingency in appearance, |\nM 93: |\ni.e. that of what happens.",["unmoglich (impossible) is translated as it stands; the sense would seem to call for possible, but the text is not emended.","Ends = Zweke."]],"reflm:5595":["(s The *principium nexus finalis* is: everything is good;\nThe *principium nexus effectivi*: everything has its natural ground.\nThe supreme cause of the *causarum finalium* is God;\nThe supreme cause of the *causarum efficientium*: nature.)\nAn effect whose concept can through reason be cause (*causa efficiens*) [of] of it is the end, and the *nexus* of such effects or [of the] efficient causes is *nexus finalis*.\nThe end is objectively, the effect (g of an action) subjectively, the cause of it.",["Vernunft ursache may also be read as one word, Vernunftursache (cause through reason, rational cause).","The last sentence may also be read: The end is objective, the effect (of an action) subjectively the cause of it."]],"reflm:5596":["Principles of the understanding are principles of the exposition of experience. Principles of reason are those according to which experience itself (through the understanding) is given a priori (freedom and necessary being). Rationalizing principles: where the subjective conditions of reason are taken for objective ones. Principles of reason are transcendental maxims (g of speculation). Rationalizing ones are transcendental paralogisms.\nTranscendental ideas of reason determining itself a priori.\nThe sophistical principle of reason is: what does not stand (g wholly) under the conditions of empirical determination is false. Thus all synthesis of magnitudes which is without end is impossible; all dynamical synthesis which is not mediately determined (freedom and necessity), impossible. On the other hand the possibility of the idea of reason is nevertheless also not to be had insight into.",["principles = principien throughout; principle in the last paragraph = principium.","Rationalizing = Vernünftelnde; not to be had insight into = nicht einzusehen.","Nothwendig Wesen: necessary being, Wesen also essence."]],"reflm:5597":["The concepts of the world, of the simple, of freedom and of the supreme cause are nothing but concepts of reason, because they cannot be shown *in concreto* in experience. Their principles are therefore not principles of the empirical*, but of the transcendent use of rea|son;\nnevertheless they lie in the progression of reason toward the completion of the synthesis.\nDistributive or collective unity of experiences in general. All experience is not the all of experience, and the whole of possible experience is no object of experience. But here there nevertheless occurs that [whereby] which is the condition of the possibility of a whole.\n*(g not to expound experiences, but [a pri] to determine them in an idea through limitation of the synthetic whole. 1. Progression to the synthetic unity of the parts. Beginning from the synthetic whole.)",["principles = Grundsatze; progression = Fortgang.","[a pri] is a deletion broken off in the manuscript."]],"reflm:5598":["*Principium commercii*.\nAll combination of things, insofar as they are at the same time, is a *commercium*. For insofar as they are at the same time, i.e. I can pass over from A to B just as well as from B to A and each can be *conseqvens* in the synthesis of the other, they determine one another reciprocally, i.e. are *in commercio*. On the other hand, if [only] I could only determine B through A and not A through B, then B follows A.\nEach thing has with respect to what coexists a relation of community. In the sequence of time nothing can be reciprocally determined in the same significance.",["at the same time = zugleich; sequence of time = Zeitfolge."]],"reflm:5599":["At the top of the page. At the end of the Ontology?\nThe principles of the possibility of experience are independent of experience. The connection of the principles a priori (necessary) with experience is either this, that they depend on experiences, or these on them.",["The first line is the edition's locus note; Ontologiε is so transcribed, with a Greek epsilon for e.","principles = principien in the first sentence, Grundsatze in the second."]],"reflm:5600":["Next to and \nWe call nature the object of possible experience. Thus all our cognition a priori nevertheless goes only to nature.\nPrinciples of the understanding are rules a priori which contain the conditions of the synthetic unity of possible experience.",["The first line is the edition's locus note, broken off in the transcription after und.","object = obiect; principles = Grundsatze."]],"reflm:5601":["Up to here we have spoken of nothing but concepts whose objects can be given to us in the senses, except in the concepts of the *entis realissimi* and of the simple (*monas*).\nNow we come to concepts of the unconditioned totality of the synthesis (world-concept).\nThe [whole] composite of substances as absolute whole (*est vel mundus sensibilis vel intelligibilis*).",["est vel mundus sensibilis vel intelligibilis: is either a sensible or an intelligible world.","totality = totalitaet; world-concept = Weltbegriff."]],"reflm:5602":["The use of the concepts of the understanding was immanent, that of the ideas as concepts of objects is transcendent; but as regulative principles of completion and at the same time of the determination of the limitations of our cognition they are critically immanent.",["determination of the limitations = Schrankenbestimung (one compound)."]],"reflm:5603":["In the ontology we have spoken of concepts of the understanding whose use is possible in experience, because they themselves make experience possible.",[]],"reflm:5604":["Cosmological ideas rest on the thought of the absolute totality of the conditions in a whole, which precisely thereby is unconditioned.",[]],"reflm:5605":["Opposite and \nThe doctrine of monads must not pass over into physics.\nAre bodies then substances or [not] *accidentia* in themselves? They are neither the one nor the other; for they are not things at all or objective reality, but are appearances, of which the doctrine of monads does not hold. Something substantial may lie at their ground.",["The first line is the edition's locus note, broken off in the transcription after und.","muß nicht may mean must not or need not."]],"reflm:5606":["Atomistics, although it is very arbitrary, is nevertheless more appropriate to philosophy than monadistics. The former gives mechanical explanations from figure and laws of motion, the latter metaphysical ones from merely intellectual concepts, which cannot be *principia* of the *phaenomenorum*.\nThe *principia physica* are either mechanical or dynamical.",[]],"reflm:5607":["All possible objects of experience have their nature, partly their particular one, partly the one in common with other things. Nature taken substantively signifies: the sum total of all objects of experience. [The] Nature rests on forces (fundamental forces) and is in general the lawfulness of appearances.\nNature is to chance — —\nto freedom — —\nto fate }\n}\n}\nopposed.\nThings are not in themselves appearances, but merely because there are beings that have senses; just so they belong to a nature, |\nbecause we have understanding. For the word nature also does not signify something in things in themselves, but only the [unity] order of their appearances through the unity of the concepts of the understanding or the unity of consciousness, in which they can be combined.\nWe do not have understanding because there is a nature; for we could never know its rules (g laws) from experience; [they det] their necessity consists precisely in their being cognized a priori.\nPrecisely for this reason we can have a priori cognitions both of appearances and of the nature in which they are connected, because the form [of the] of our sensibility underlies the former and the form of our understanding the second as principle of possibility.\nTo say: “we can determine a priori the constitution of things” and yet at the same time: “these things have such constitution independently of our faculty to determine them” is a contradiction; for where then do we take our cognition from?",["“chance” renders das Ohngefähr; “lawfulness” renders Gesetzmäßigkeit.","[The] renders the deleted [Das], whose neuter article does not agree with Natur; [they det] renders the broken-off deletion [sie best].","“principle” in the last paragraph but one renders Princip, not Grundsatz."]],"reflm:5608":["Things are represented as appearances because there are beings that have senses. The same beings, however, also have understanding, under whose laws the appearances stand, insofar as their possible consciousness must necessarily agree with a universally valid consciousness, i.e., they have a nature.\nAppearances stand to one another in the relation of the manifold [of the] in a pure sensible intuition, and their consciousness in the relation to a common apperception, both a priori and necessarily.\n1. Nature taken generally and *formaliter*:\nnature of a thing; [the] corporeal and thinking nature.\n2. *Materialiter*: as sum total of appearances in opposition to the intelligible world.\nNature is opposed to blind chance (accident) and to blind necessity (fate).\nTo the latter, if I do not assume a cause outside the world, belong miracles.\n(*non datur abyssus, saltus, casus, hiatus.*)\nNatural necessity opposed to the supernatural.\nNature is not opposed to freedom, but distinguished from it.\nFreedom is the independence of causality from the conditions of space and of time, hence [from] the causality of the thing as thing in itself. Mechanism of nature and freedom do not conflict with one another, because causality is not taken in one sense.\n*Casus* is absolute contingency. *Fatum* the unconditioned necessity in the world.\n(g All laws cognized through experience belong to heteronomy, but those through which experience in general is possible, to autonomy.)\nM 143':\nThe infinity or finitude of composition or division must be assumed on both sides in the sensible world. Nevertheless these ideas of absolute totality belong to the concept of things in themselves; hence they are both false. On the other hand the concept of natural necessity and that of freedom: of these the first belongs to the sensible world, the second to the intelligible, and the idea of totality belongs to the things themselves, which the idea of freedom, if it were counted as belonging to the sensible world, might contradict; but now, as belonging to the intelligible world, both can be true.\nThe causality of a being, to think itself independent of determining grounds of the sensible world in regard to appearances, is no contradiction, if [one] the being is given only under a concept of a thing in itself. Now a rational being as intelligence is given as such a one; consequently freedom can be thought in it. On the other hand no causality can be thought of the intelligible of bodies, for their appearances betray no intelligence; |\nhence no freedom can be thought of their *substrato intelligibili* either, and we know it through not a single predicate.\nWe need freedom only for the objectively practical from grounds a priori.\nThe faculty to begin an occurrence absolutely (without the causality itself beginning).\nIn bodies we do have a representation of the intelligible, but do not know it as cause. In intelligences the intelligible is presupposed according to causality; the question here is how something can be a cause.\nAntinomy.",["“chance (accident)” renders Ohngefähr (Zufall); “contingency” renders Zufälligkeit.","“thing(s)” renders both Ding and Sache (“Dinges an sich selbst”, “Sachen an sich selbst”, “Sache an sich selbst”).","“which the idea of freedom … might contradict”: the relative welcher is dative and most naturally refers to “the idea of totality”."]],"reflm:5609":["*Maxima realitas phaenomenon* is *mundus perfectissimus*: *compositum realissimum*.\nThe principles of the possibility of experience hold only in the world and do not extend to the world-whole. Hence this can be thought neither as finite nor as infinite.",["*Maxima realitas phaenomenon*: “the greatest reality [as] phenomenon”; *phaenomenon* may stand in apposition or as predicate. *mundus perfectissimus*: “most perfect world”; *compositum realissimum*: “most real composite”.","“the world-whole” renders das Weltganze."]],"reflm:5610":["Opposite and \n*In Mundo non datur abyssus*, i.e., there is nothing unconditioned in the sensible world, for this belongs to the intelligible; but the senses [lo], when they take space and time for determinations of things, here into the infinitely empty or full.\n*In mundo non datur saltus*, i.e., all appearances are generated through degrees of all things from 0 to something.\nWere time and space conditions of the existence of things in themselves, then one would not be able to say in the mathematical antinomy: both opposites are false, for one would have to be true; nor [both can be] |\nin the dynamical: both can be true, for one would have to be false. Now the contradiction is encountered only because space and time as things in themselves are nevertheless supposed to contain something unconditioned.",["The editorial location note “Gegenüber und” is cut off in the source.","[lo] renders the broken-off deletion [verlie] (presumably verlieren, “lose”); the sentence has no finite verb after it, and is left so.","*In mundo non datur abyssus* / *saltus*: “In the world there is no abyss” / “no leap”. “determinations of things” renders Sachbestimmungen."]],"reflm:5611":["Reason is not in the chain of appearances and is in regard to all of them free in regard to its own causality (the actions of reason themselves are also not appearances, but only their effects are). Were everything determined through reason, then everything would be necessary, but also good. Were it determined through sensibility, then there would be nothing evil or good, nothing practical at all. Now actions are for the most part occasioned through sensibility, but not wholly determined; for reason must give a *complement* of sufficiency. Reason draws sensibility gradually in the *habitus*, arouses incentives and therefore forms a character, which however is itself to be ascribed to freedom and is not sufficiently grounded even in the appearances. For that reason all actions cohere well with laws of sensibility, but not with omissions of the understanding, whose opposite is always possible according to all grounds of sensibility. If one now asks whether the understanding itself, in itself as well as in regard to what it does or omits, is not determined, then we must say: not a single possible experience, because it would after all always be only appearance, can give this. The understanding is itself no object of sensible intuition. Beyond experience, however, determinability through other causes does not hold, because beyond this boundary the understanding can be represented only as cause (highest understanding) and the concept of cause serves only for the *principio* of the synthesis of appearances, but not of the actions of the understanding.",["“draws … in the *habitus*” renders zieht … im habitus; ziehen may also mean “trains” or “cultivates”.","“incentives” renders Triebfedern; “sufficiency” renders Zulänglichkeit; “omissions” renders Unterlassungen."]],"reflm:5612":["277. E II 1552.\nM 277':\nWe explain committed free actions according to laws of the nature of the human being, but we do not thereby cognize them as determined; otherwise we would not regard them as contingent and demand that they ought and must have happened otherwise. In free actions reason flows in not merely as a comprehending but as an effecting and impelling *principium*. How it does not merely rationalize and judge, but takes the place of a natural cause, we have no insight into, much less how it is itself determined through impulses to act or to omit. (How the representation of the good in general, which abstracts from my state, is nevertheless efficacious upon my state, and how this reflection, which itself contains no *affection*, can be contained in the series of natural appearance. For the good is a relation of pure reason to object. We must therefore regard future actions as undetermined through everything that belongs to *phaenomenis*. Reason makes use of the natural constitution according to its laws as incentives (honor, peace of mind), but is not determined thereby.\nThe resolution of this is. The interconnection of reason with the *phaenomenis*, with which it is supposed to stand [not at all] *in commercio*, cannot at all |\nM 277: |\nbe understood (g they are *heterogenea*). The true activity of reason and its *effect* belongs to the *mundo intelligibili*. Hence we also do not know in what measure we ought to impute. Nevertheless we know so much of the inflowing power of reason, that it is determined and necessitated through no *phaenomena*, but is free, and we judge the action merely according to rational laws (in *imputation*). The actions here in the world are mere schemata of the intelligible; meanwhile these appearances (this word already signifies schema) do cohere according to empirical laws, if one regards reason itself according to its manifestations as a *phaenomenon* (of character). But what the cause of this is, we do not find in *phaenomenis*. Insofar as one cognizes one's own character only from the *phaenomenis*, one imputes these to oneself, although they be determined in themselves through |\nexternal causes. If one knew it in itself, then all good and evil would be to be ascribed not to external causes, but only to the subject alone, together with the good and disadvantageous consequences. In the intelligible world nothing happens or alters, and there the rule of causal connection falls away.",["The parenthesis opened at “(How the representation” is not closed in the source.","“we have no insight into” renders sehen wir nicht ein; “power” renders Gewalt; “manifestations” renders Äußerungen.","“one imputes these to oneself”: diese may refer to the actions or to the *phaenomena*; the ambiguity is kept."]],"reflm:5613":["Every action as appearance has its determining ground in another positive or negative action of mine, this again in another, and so on to infinity. There is therefore no complete ground at all among appearances, hence always only a necessity conditioned under my own discretion (to apply or to turn away this or that attention). This condition, however, because it always remains [so] and because it is the condition of one's own self-activity, so it is never an externally conditioned necessity. The same action, however, is as *noumenon*[determined through my good or evil will] not under the rule of that which happens (g in me) and must be represented in connection through [the suffi] another determining ground, except in the case of a good will; there the objective necessity (which is a freedom from physical causes) is at the same time a subjective one. On the other hand in the case of an evil will, because it is after all a will and not nature, all its actions are objectively impossible and subjectively contingent. For this contingency is the condition under which an objective law can be thought, in regard to which an action can be represented as evil. An [evil] action evil in itself, which one ought to omit, is evil precisely because we do it without an objectively sufficient ground; and the will is evil because it is subjectively not determined through precisely the same rule.\nFreedom is the determinability of force through mere reason. Reason, however, is not a cognition that contains the way in which one is affected by objects; consequently the use of reason itself is freedom.",["[the suffi] renders the broken-off deletion [den Zurei] (presumably zureichenden, “sufficient”).","“discretion” renders Belieben; “evil” renders böse throughout.","“This condition, however, because … so it is”: the anacoluthon of the German is kept."]],"reflm:5614":["As appearance not only an action but also its |\nopposite would be able to have its sufficient ground in the *regressu* ([within] between two states), to infinity (through *decomposition*). But this series of grounds can never be complete and cannot be composed a priori from the smallest elements of the ground on.",[]],"reflm:5615":["The pleasure in the action either precedes the law or follows from it. In the latter case it is respect.",["“pleasure” renders Lust; “respect” renders Achtung."]],"reflm:5616":["279'. E II 1551. 1513.\nM 278':\nEverything that happens is sufficiently determined, but not from appearances, but rather according to laws of appearance. For in freely acting beings there is a constant influence of intellectual grounds, since the opposite is possible as appearance. But the action or its opposite will be so grounded among the appearances that only the moment of determination is intellectual. But this cannot be used in the empirical explanation, because it is not perceived. For from the intellectual up to the determinate action there is an infinite intermediate series of incentives, whose interconnection with the given state can be cognized only according to universal laws of possibility. For example: |\nM 279': |\nSomeone entices me to drink, this enticement leads me astray [But before this] and can thus be explained according to laws of the senses. The leading astray would also be necessary if I were merely animal. Meanwhile it is possible that the intellectual power of choice intervenes, which is exempted from the law of dependence on senses; this determines only another course of sensibility. This can also [be explained and] be connected with the first given state according to laws of nature, but only through an infinite intermediate series of appearances. Thus vice as well as virtue happens according to laws of nature and must be explained accordingly. (Honor, health, reward.) Even the morally good actions from the above incentives, upbringing and temperament. The explanation also has its ground; only the first direction |\nof these causes, the moment of determining them, [is unknown and] is not encountered among the appearances, but also cannot be missed there, because we cannot observe the appearances up to the moment of their beginning.\nThe law of cause and effect (*caussalitatis*) rests on the condition of the possibility of a unity of experience. This unity cannot fully obtain in free beings, except when they are fully intellectual.\nThe higher power of choice is the faculty of making use of the incentives or sensible stimuli according to their laws, but yet always in accordance with the representation of the understanding (in relation to the last and universal ends of sensibility). *A posteriori*, therefore, we will have cause to find the ground of the action, namely its ground of explanation, but not its determining ground, in sensibility; but *a priori*, when the action is represented as future (*antecedenter*), we will feel ourselves undetermined toward it and capable of making a first beginning of the series of appearances. If there is free power of choice, then the appearances of rational beings make no *continuum*, except with firm principles of the understanding. Thus merely the actions governed partly by understanding, partly by senses can be explained according to no rule of the one or the other. Before the action we place ourselves merely in the standpoint of the understanding. Now because the understanding is not properly affected, but can affect sensibility: so its action is not predetermined, but determining *spontaneo*; and the opposite of what happens without understanding could always have happened. Thus the action is contingent only in a conditioned way (under conditions of the understanding), insofar as it is irrational.",["Willkühr: power of choice; Triebfedern: incentives; Erklärungsgrund / Bestimungsgrund: ground of explanation / determining ground.","Moment: moment, either the decisive factor or the instant; the German allows both.","blos: merely; it may qualify the actions (only these actions) or the government (governed merely partly ... partly)."]],"reflm:5617":["Among the appearances everything must be determined, but either according to [laws] pathological or moral laws; if it is the first, then the opposite is nevertheless possible according to laws of reason, thus the human being free; if it is the latter, then the subject is also free. The agreement with laws of reason does not belong among appearances, and the transition from |\nsensibility to reason *in regressu* also cannot be observed; but rather, as far as is observed, everything happens by means of sensible incentives, through which the action is determined.",["Vernunftgesetzen: laws of reason; triebfedern: incentives."]],"reflm:5618":["Pure freedom acts according to laws of inwardly determining grounds, but they do not fall into the senses. The animal power of choice proceeds according to sensibly determinable laws. The mixed human power of choice (*libertas hybrida*) also acts according to laws, but whose grounds do not [occur] wholly occur in the appearance; hence with the same appearances the same human being can act otherwise. Here one must first wait for a character, and then one has a law for explaining the appearances, but never for determining them.",["Willkühr: power of choice.","sie fallen nicht in die Sinne: they do not fall into (come within) the senses; sie may refer to the grounds or to the laws."]],"reflm:5619":["The difficulty regarding freedom is: how a (g absolutely) first action is possible that is not determined by a preceding one. For the latter is required for the unity of appearances, insofar as it is to give a rule of experience. But if we do not count the actions of reason among the appearances (principle of reason) and the determination through it to action by means of the incentives according to laws of sensibility (*association*, habit): then everything is necessary *qvoad sensum* and can be explained according to laws of appearance. But it cannot be predetermined, because reason is a *principium* which does not appear, thus is not given among the appearances. Hence the causes and their relation to action can well be cognized *a posteriori* according to laws of sensibility, but not their determination to the *actu*. This interconnection of the actions according to laws of appearance without determinateness through them is a necessary presupposition of practical rules of reason, which are in themselves the cause of a regularity of the appearances, because |\nthey pass over to actions only by means of sensibility. In the appearances there is no *hiatus* for the understanding, but these also cannot be determined *a priori*, i.e. from the absolutely first on.\nIn general the difficulty here concerns not the lack of the sufficient ground, but only of it among the appearances. If the action is determined in the higher powers, their omissions or perfections: then (g in that case) it is not [any longer] the [talk] question of the ground of what happens, but rather [it shows] of what is there at all times, namely the rational will, from which the opposite of the evil was always possible.\nReason determines itself in regard to its concepts, sensibility is determined by the object. Hence the former is also not grounded on conditions of *apprehension* and *apperception*, but determines the *synthesis a priori*.\nOne cannot say that the opposite of all our actions must be subjectively possible in order that one be free (good actions), but only of those arising from sensibility. But even in this case they are determined under sensibility, although, taken in general, still undetermined. Sensibility and reason do not determine each other, but each works according to its laws; but they direct each other (harmony).\nThe causality of reason is freedom. The determining causality of sensibility: animality.",["The clause und die Bestimung ... (association, gewonheit) has no verb of its own in the German; the English keeps it so.","Kräften: powers; Bestimtheit: determinateness; Zusammenhang: interconnection.","causalitaet / caussalitaet: both causality; Kant's two spellings."]],"reflm:5620":["Just as reason judges not through the senses, but yet in relation to them according to the universal conditions of a cognition in general: so also the very same not through feeling, but yet in relation to it according to the conditions of the [possib] universal validity of the judgment about satisfaction and dissatisfaction. It is the appetition made universal, as the former is the apprehension made universal. No feeling distinguishes right from wrong, but rather reason projects the conditions under which alone a rule takes place for judging about this. The satisfaction in regularity is proper|ly\na satisfaction in the ground of constancy and security in regard to everything agreeable that is intended through the use of freedom. It is also a gratification about the use of reason and the happiness wrested from the blind power of choice.",["Wohlgefallen / Misfallen: satisfaction / dissatisfaction; Vergnügen: gratification; Willkühr: power of choice.","jene: the former, i.e. reason in its first use as just described."]],"reflm:5621":["We must indeed (by virtue of time) place the soul in the sensible world, but we cannot place it in the corporeal world, but rather only place the latter in its representation. Hence the soul as such has no place in the human body, and its place is the place of the body in the world. Hence no *sedes animae in communi sensorio*. No departure from the body. Virtual.",["*sedes animae in communi sensorio*: seat of the soul in the common sensorium.","Virtuel: virtual; whether adjective or adverb (virtually) is not marked."]],"reflm:5622":["Of existence after death. 1. *Spes vitae futurae*. 2. *Immortalitas*: a. not universal, not necessarily eternal; b. /either from empirical or rational grounds. The last: 1. from the principle of the immateriality of that which lives, and of the body as hindrance of life. Then it has lived before the human being. 2. From simplicity — 3. From the analogy of nature.\n*Status post mortem*.\n*Personalitas*. Sleep of the soul. Existence as pure intelligence or vehicle.",["*Spes vitae futurae*: hope of a future life; *Immortalitas*: immortality; *Status post mortem*: state after death; *Personalitas*: personality.","Denn hat sie: denn read as dann (then); it could also be for (then it has lived)."]],"reflm:5623":["The anticritique is either skeptical or dogmatic. The first re|jects\nall [philosophical] extension of cognition through pure concepts and at the same time the investigation of this reason itself. The second asserts the former and rejects merely the latter. The first is misology, the second *ignava ratio*.",["*ignava ratio*: lazy reason."]],"reflm:5624":["On theology: every presupposition that can be proved neither *a priori* nor *a posteriori*, but rather is made only for the sake of our insight of reason, is hypothesis, if the [Fol der] propositions for the sake of which the presupposition is made are contingent: e.g. contingent perfection in the world; but it is postulate if they are necessary *a priori*: e.g. moral propositions and their moving force. Thus the presupposition of the existence of God for the sake of morals is postulate. A hypothesis does not exclude that there should not also be other grounds of explanation. e.g. that something other than the most perfect being could also be cause of the perfection perceived by us. A postulate determines the ground as the only possible one. Likewise, if I found order and perfection everywhere in the essence of things, the existence of God would be a postulate, and the original being would have to be presupposed as all-sufficient ground of the possibility of things in all teleological considerations.",["[Fol der]: a deleted beginning (Fol- perhaps for Folgen, consequences) carried over untranslated.","schließt nicht aus, daß nicht: the double negative of the German is kept.","Vernunfteinsicht: insight of reason; Moral: morals; Urwesen: original being; Algnugsam: all-sufficient."]],"reflm:5625":["(s No empirical theology.) Theology is either rational or revealed. The former either transcendental or natural or moral. The natural either cosmotheology or physicotheology.",["From the second sentence on the original is Latin: Theologia est vel rationalis vel revelata. Prior vel transscendentalis vel naturalis vel moralis. Naturalis vel cosmotheologia vel Physicotheologia."]],"reflm:5626":["*Entis originarii possibilitas originaria*. *Ens realissimum*.",["*Entis originarii possibilitas originaria*: the original possibility of the original being; *Ens realissimum*: the most real being."]],"reflm:5627":["Of [different] things that belong under a certain universal concept we can think different ones without contradicting ourselves; but of God only a single one can be thought that does not contradict itself (*conceptus singularis*, not *communis*). All transcendental theology rests on determining this concept; but from the fact that all concepts except a single one (g [of]) cannot belong to a divinity without contradiction, it cannot be concluded that its object is itself necessary.",["verschiedene ... nur ein einziger: different ones ... only a single one; whether concepts or things are meant is left open, as in the German.","desselben: its, i.e. of that single concept."]],"reflm:5628":["The cosmological proof (g If something exists, then something must also be necessary) fails in this, that, because according to the (g universal) concept *entis limitati* many different things, but according to that *realissimi* only one thing, consequently the opposite of a given thing not, can be thought, the former can be only contingent, but this alone only necessary; but a thing which is determined otherwise than a given one is indeed determined through opposed predicates, but is not the opposite of the previous one, on that account (g precisely) because it is another thing. The opposite is the *determinatio opposita* of the same thing.\n(g The consequence is false: limited things cannot be necessary, because the concept of a thing can be determined in them *ad oppositum*.)\nIt would sound better if I said: all *limitata* have *possibilitatem logice derivativam*, but the *ens necessarium* is supposed to be *originarium*, thus it cannot be *limitatum*.\n(s Of analogy, the use of empirical predicates *per reductionem et eminentiam*. *Anthropopathos Theosophia*.)",["*entis limitati*: of a limited being; *realissimi*: of a most real (being); *possibilitatem logice derivativam*: logically derivative possibility.","Folge: consequence (here the inference drawn), not sequel.","*Anthropopathos Theosophia*: anthropopathic theosophy, i.e. ascribing human affections to God; Greek-derived terms."]],"reflm:5629":["Above, beside and \nUnbelieving is he who concedes no other acceptance of rational [truths] propositions than those which have logical certainty.\nPractically unbelieving, he who does not want to be righteous except insofar as he knows certainly that it also has its reward (not out of self-interest), thus not upon or in belief; or also he who ascribes to the moral disposition so much force as to abstract from all consequences and to content himself merely with acting rightly.\nThe former is the unbelief of mistrust.\nThe latter is the unbelief of self-conceit.",["Über, neben und: the beginning of the AA's note on the position of the entry, cut off in the transcription.","Vernunft [warheiten] Satze: the deleted warheiten makes Vernunftwahrheiten (truths of reason), replaced by Vernunftsätze (propositions of reason).","Glauben: belief; Gesinung: disposition; Eigendünkel: self-conceit."]],"reflm:5630":["If we were to have complete insight into the nature of things, then nature and freedom, natural determination and determination of ends would be entirely one and the same. So it is with God; hence all ends in the world at the same time follow from the essence of things and are one and the same with his nature in an original being.",["Zweksbestimmung: determination of (by) ends; naturbestimung: natural determination.","mit seiner Natur: seiner may refer to God or to the original being."]],"reflm:5631":["On the divine blessedness. Self-sufficiency.\nDifference from happiness, which rests on external things. Analogue of self-contentment, to need nothing external.\nWe have considered the properties of God [as] [according to] physicotheologically. Now the existence of such a being is still to be proved according to physical principles.\nInference of physicotheology.\nThis consists (after the concept of God has been determined physiologically) in this, that from the experience of the constitution of this world the existence of God be proved. Thus not the cosmological proof from the existence of any world whatever, but from the constitution of this world. Herein are the following difficulties.\n1. [We] Only as much as we know of this world-perfection can we prove of God. Thus the concept of God is [likewise] entirely inconstant and alterable. When one regarded the earth merely as the stage of the godhead, many gods had to arise, which worked [very] against one another, and even the supreme godhead had to |\nhave human failings. Now, since we regard it only as an infinitely small part of the whole, we can at least ascribe to God more magnitude [than] and goodness than shines forth in his work. — One must have infinite cognition in order to cognize something as a work that proves an infinite one.\n2. [Even if we the Un] We can indeed, if we otherwise already cognize the existence of the *entis realissimi*, explain the imperfections in the world as mere illusion, but not infer from this imperfect to the highest perfection.\n3. Thus physicotheology is a ladder without highest rung; it presupposes a pure rational theology.",["\"Inference\" renders Schluß, which can also mean \"conclusion\" (the closing part of physicotheology).","\"Blessedness\" renders Seeligkeit, \"happiness\" Glükseeligkeit; \"magnitude\" renders Größe, which here may also be read as \"greatness\".","\"Rational theology\" renders Vernunfttheologie; \"illusion\" renders Schein."]],"reflm:5632":["M 394e:\nProvidence (provision): founding of the laws: concerns the arrangement of the world. (s[in the world-arr] wisdom laid into the beginning for the future.)\nGovernment (*gubernation*): preservation of the whole (g [in the] course of the world) according to laws.\nSteering (s disposition) (*directio*): the determination of each part to agreement (g of individual occurrences) with the laws. (s is government with regard to an individual occurrence.)\n(s Dispensation, (*praedestinatio*, fatality): is a predetermination of an occurrence in which the free will has no share.)\nProvidence is either* the universal, according to universal laws of nature, or particular (specific), which is referred to individual beings.\n*(s Particular providence is distinct from particular direction. The former [according to th] by means of the order of nature. The possibility (g of a particular provision) according to an order of nature |\nis incomprehensible to us, because we cognize nature only as universal lawfulness and not as a whole.)\nAll things (g and occurrences) stand under a universal and particular providence; but not everything that happens happens through a particular providence, for which an entirely particular arrangement, directed at individual cases, would have to be sought**. For not everything that agrees with the end of God is his end, and does not always contain the motive of his arrangement. *providentia legislatoria* or *generalis* is to be distinguished from *providentia decretoria* or *speciali*. The *actus* of a *providentia specialis* [are] belong not to the ordinary but to extraordinary providence, and are distinguished from the *directione speciali* only by this, that the former are thought as already included in the creation. The divine counsel is an *actus* and concerns the whole, and from the idea of it all particular arrangements must be derived: it is not composed out of parts; then an aggregate of ends and no system would arise.\nM 394:\nThe *providentia specialis* is at all times a miracle (*miraculum praestabilitum*) and can only be conceded as such. This the *providentia generalis* of God has particular to it, that here the welfare of the individual is not sacrificed to the art in the whole. No individual occurrence can take place that does not also belong to the best of the whole; all occurrences happen as if they were preestablished through divine particular providence. The cause of this is that even the possibility of things rests on the dependence of their essences on the highest wisdom, and the parts in the whole are always immediately determined through wisdom; but according to our way of explaining we cannot express this so, because thereby the derivation from universal laws of nature is hindered and *ignava ratio* introduced.\nM 394e:\n**(s The (g wisdom in) supplementing of the lack of the purposive|ness\nof the freedom of the world-beings through divine [wisdom] influence is the *concursus*.)",["\"Providence\" renders both Vorsehung and the Latinate providenz/providentz; \"provision\" renders Vorsorge.","\"Steering\" renders Lenkung, \"disposition\" Fügung, \"dispensation\" Schikung, \"fatality\" Verhängniß; \"government\" renders Regirung.","\"Preestablished\" renders the Latinate praestabilirt; \"an *actus*\" may also be read \"one *actus*\" (ein)."]],"reflm:5633":["On the use of the cognition of God granted to human reason. 1. Not to pursue and investigate the concept of him in a speculative aim otherwise than merely in order [as well] to regard it as a necessary boundary-concept of all natural philosophy and the *non plus ultra* of human reason. Thus not to want to investigate it in itself. Also not to base explanations of nature on him as cause, and thus to set aside the maxims of the use of reason that alone pertains to us. 2. To regard it as a concept belonging to morals, but not in order to make them possible, but in order to give [them] to them [pra] the force of the incentive. 3. To use it, through combination with morals, for a religion, in which, however, morals and not theology prescribes the rule, so that we do not, in thinking anthropomorphistically, thereby pervert [our] even the moral principles. With regard to religion, to follow the [maxims] concepts of reason according to (g universal) laws of nature, in that one considers these themselves as divine institution. To derive the divine punishments and rewards as from the universal and at the same time moral interconnection of nature and freedom. [The fut] To regard the fates of this life as the progression of nature to better ends. The future life as an effect according to laws of nature, the future weal or misery as something that we naturally expect from ourselves and our conduct; in a word: to seek God in the order of nature and so to venerate him. For how he may be in himself we cannot grasp; and if we depart from the order of nature, we pervert our vocation.",["The German ihn may refer either to God or to the concept (Begrif); \"it\" and \"him\" in the English choose between them where the German does not.","\"Morals\" renders Moral (feminine, hence \"them\" for sie/ihr); \"incentive\" renders Triebfeder; \"vocation\" renders Bestimmung.","\"Boundary-concept\" renders Grentzbegrif; \"interconnection\" renders Zusammenhang."]],"reflm:5634":["The cognition of God is not to serve to alter our use of reason according to the order of nature and of morals, but |\nto give it completeness, so that we connect the former with ends and the latter with physical laws of the course of nature.",["\"Morals\" renders Sitten; \"connect\" renders verknüpfen."]],"reflm:5635":["Revelation is either symbolic or [intellectual] intuitive.\n[The] Revelation is to be considered either with regard to him to whom it has happened, or to those who are to believe the report of it.\nRevelation can also have as its sole aim to bring into currency a doctrine that needs no revelation in order to maintain itself once it is there, because it has the proof in universal human reason. Then the revelation is only of local and temporal necessity.\nRevelation of such things as the human being does not cognize as duty through reason, consequently not as duty necessary in itself, [can not] is in itself contingent.\nAll (g merely) revealed theology (g which cannot be cognized through reason) and religion [we] gives in time a merely learned religion. A merely natural religion can also have been brought into currency through revelation; with it it is indeed not necessary, but still useful, that the learned one also go in pairs with it.",["The first sentence is Latin in the source: Revelatio est vel symbolica vel [intellectualis] intuitiva.","\"Temporal\" renders tempereller, presumably for temporeller.","\"Go in pairs with it\" renders zu Paaren gehe literally; \"the learned one\" renders die gelehrte (sc. religion)."]],"reflm:5636":["S. I:\n*Quaestio facti* is in what way one has first put oneself in possession of a concept;\n*qvaestio iuris*, with what right one possesses [this] it and uses it.\nThe universality and necessity in the use of the pure concepts of the understanding betrays their origin, and that it must be either entirely inadmissible and false or not empirical.\nIn pure sensibility, pure imagination and pure apperception lies the ground of the possibility of all empirical cognition *a priori* and of the synthesis according to concepts which has objective reality. For it concerns only appearances (which are in themselves contingent and without [comb] unity), so that one properly cognizes only oneself as the thinking subject, but everything else as in this One. Heautognosy.\n––––––––––\nAll representations, let them come from where they will, are yet in the end, as [modif] representations, modifications of inner sense, and from this point of view must their unity be regarded. To the |\nreceptivity of them corresponds a spontaneity of synthesis. Either of apprehension as sensations or of reproduction as imaginings or of recognition as concepts.\n(g transcendental principles of mathematics (not mathematical principles), namely that all intuitions and sensations are magnitudes and that the mathematical propositions about magnitudes have reality, although only as about appearances.)\nS. II:\nNo appearance can ever prove an empty space nor an empty time. Because appearances in themselves are nothing, namely not objects subsisting for themselves, the empty space is a perception of an extension without matter of the appearance.\nEvery magnitude has a quality, i.e. continuity. Every quality has a magnitude, i.e. intensity (degree). The boundaries of extensive magnitude are not at the same time limitations of the intensive, but the latter can notwithstanding grow to infinity. The [boundaries] limitations of intensive magnitude, e.g. weight, are for that reason not yet the boundaries of the extensive (or if the latter are equal, the former are also equal), but this can grow to infinity. Against atoms and the void.\n––––––––––\nSince the objects of our senses are not things in themselves but only appearances, i.e. representations whose objective reality consists only in the constancy and unity of the interconnection of their manifold, it is not the objects that give the concepts, but the concepts make it that we have in them objects of cognition; since they also, as representations, are modifications of inner sense, their possibility rests on the synthesis of appearances in time.\n––––––––––\nIt is a very important question whether the categories are merely of empirical or also of transc: use. for the schematistic.",["*A priori* may attach to \"cognition\" or to \"the ground of the possibility\", as in the German; \"it\" (er) may be the use or the origin; \"it\" (sie) may be the synthesis or the cognition.","\"Objects\" renders both Gegenstande and the Latinate obiecte (the latter in \"not objects subsisting for themselves\", \"give the concepts\" and \"objects of cognition\").","\"Against\" renders Wieder, for wider; \"Heautognosy\" renders Heavtognosie; \"for the schematistic\" renders zur Schematistick."]],"reflm:5637":["S. I:\n(g Key — through the nature of synthetic judgments *a priori*.)\nIf no space were given *a priori* in our subject as the form of its (g sensible) intuition, and objects outside us given merely in this form, then no synthetic propositions that at the same time hold of actual outer objects would be possible *a priori*. For should we take the representations from the objects as they are given in themselves, then everything would rest merely on experience, and no synthesis *a priori* together with the necessity of the judgments would hold, at least not objectively. If time were not given subjectively and hence *a priori* as the form of inner sense (g and no understanding to compare it), then apperception would not cognize *a priori* the relation in the existence of the manifold, for in itself time is no object of perception; it would also, to be sure, determine the succession and coexistence of the representations, but not the position of the objects in time, hence would not be able to make any experience, if it did not have rules of the [time determined] time determinable in the object; but these it cannot take from the object.\nI ask everyone whence he will take the mathematical and necessary (g synthetic) propositions of things in space, if space were not already in us *a priori* the condition of the possibility of the em|pirical\nrepresentation of the objects, through which these can be given to us. We determine *a priori* the manifold of appearance as to existence through the categories. I ask: whence is this synthesis to be taken, if time, the condition of the possibility of all perception, did not lie *a priori* at the ground, and thus the rule of the determination of existence in this time and through it among one another did not flow, even before all perception, from the subjective constitution of our [intui] sensibility, on which all objective rests.\n[Nevertheless restricts] From this it follows, however, that our synthetic propositions *a priori* can hold only of appearances, but not of things in themselves. That the former are given only through the synthesis and as far as it reaches, that therefore the concepts do not, of them as things in themselves, reach beyond all possible synthesis, through which then the misunderstandings arise, of progressing from the relative totality, which alone takes place in all empirical synthesis, to the absolute.\nReason, which will not put up with this restriction, supposes that our experiences and also our cognition *a priori* go immediately to objects and not in the first instance to the subjective conditions of sensibility and of apperception and by means of these to unknown objects, which are represented through those alone. It therefore strikes out on various ways. 1. The empirical way and universality through induction. 2. The fanatical one of intuition through the understanding. 3. That of predetermination through innate concepts. 4. The *qvalitas occulta* of sound understanding, which gives no account at all. |\nS. III: |\nIf one concedes these, then they cancel all critique of pure reason and open a wide field to all fabrications. Hence it belongs to the discipline of pure reason to investigate them and, as circumstances warrant, to stop up such ways.\nS. IV:\nOn 66. [Against this cheerful man can] To be healthy, equally active until late old age, and useful to the world through insights, is a good fortune that is to be begrudged to no one. If only this same worthy |\nmathematical man would renounce all judgment about this (g his) sphere or [could renounce. Perhaps sees himself Once however one must after all in this and] (g with that *de gente hircosa centurionum*) say (g could): *qvod sapio, satis est mihi*. But since these problems are essential to human reason and can never be dismissed, at least one will after all have to fast over them to the point of being ill, so that after this everyone may be healthy and yet satisfy [his the inquiring] reason.\n––––––––––\nNot only does reason overlook the ideality of the objects of the senses, but it also resists it as it does everything that restricts its sphere of action. Hence it is necessary to investigate the ways that it takes. The first is empiricism. But since not only does mathematical knowledge *a priori* refute the falsity of this alleged origin of our cognitions, but also the concepts that occur in experience contain a necessity (cause) that experience could not teach, then afterwards Locke, who thereby acquired almost too much honor, after Leibniz had already refuted him, falls away. Thus there remain the epigenesis, the mystical intuition, the involution. Finally there comes in addition the *qvalitas occulta* of common reason.\nThat our sensibility is nothing other than a confused intellectual representation is false. Incorrect concept of the sensible.\n––––––––––\nAll our cognition is grounded on the subjective, insofar as it represents an object through the synthetic unity of the manifold of intuition.\n––––––––––\nNo *dogmata*.\n––––––––––\n(g On the canon: the end of the whole of metaphysics is God and the future, and the end of these our conduct, not whether we should arrange it in accordance with morality, but whether it is without consequences.)\n[The Be] Transcendental propositions are either those where there is indeed an object, but no concept, but only the problem of it (g paralogism). or those where there is indeed a concept, but no object (reality of it). Both come from this, that reason reaches further than understanding.\n(g We have no permission to appeal to God. contingency of mathematics and possibility of explaining all order from natural laws. God. we also cannot prove synthetically the necessary generation through understanding.)\nDiscipline of the opponent. He too speaks more synthetically than he can assert.\nWhether there are pure hypotheses of reason. Permission for them.\nLastly on the speculative interest of reason. Even if one has realized those ideas of reason, one must nevertheless explain nature as if there were no beginning, no simple, no freedom, no absolute contingency, and as if there were no cause located outside the world. For nature is our problem, the text of our interpretations. Who knows what Epicurus thought of this. and what his gibberish explanations of nature are also supposed to be.\nS. II:\nThere is in all our thoughts not the slightest trace of (g the intuition) of other objects than that of the senses, and no thoughts that went to anything other than the exposition of the appearances. An intellectual intuition of the objects outside us that do not exist through us also seems to be impossible.\n(g If one assumes intellectual intuitions, then this gives no cognition of the understanding through concepts and hence no thinking and also no cognition to be communicated.)\n––––––––––\nSuppose: we had everything *a posteriori* through experience and immediate perception of the objects, hence also space and time, then we would know nothing of them other than contingent truths. *A priori* we can cognize nothing of them synthetically except if these intuitions are given to us *a priori*, consequently not through objects but through the subject, but the relation to the objects, because these are given as appearances through those subjective conditions.\nEmpty space of 1 cm.\nReason now abhors principles that are not its own work. It is its maxim to assume everything as explicable. Consequently no sensible primitive intuition.\nAt the very bottom of S. II:\nThe logical system of the cognitions of the understanding is now either the empirical or the transcendental. The former Aristotle and Locke, the second either that of epigenesis or of involution, acquired or innate. The so-called sound understanding is *asylum ignorantiae*.\nS. III. At the very top, under Obiecte als (27421):\nCognitions do not need to be originally inspired; we see it in the synthetic cognitions *a priori* of mathematics.\n––––––––––\nThat one must not assume a *mundus intelligibilis*.\nAbout 6 cm lower:\nA (g sense-) world in general is the object of an intelligence. But it would not be so were it not the work of an intelligence. For without rules of order there is no nature. But these are contingent, consequently an arrangement.\nAt the bottom of the page under 27228:\nEvery explanation of something purposive according to universal natural laws would otherwise have to sadden. e.g. The flattening of the earth. It for|bids\nitself, no doubt, of its own accord, to explain the *organisatio* mechanically; but if it were feasible, one could always aim at it. Who then makes the existence of a most perfect being comprehensible. Contingency after all grows with perfection.",["“objects” renders both Gegenstände and the Latinate Obiecte, obiect; “position” renders stelle; “ways” renders Wege; “knowledge” renders Kentnis; “on which all objective rests” renders darauf alle obiective beruht, with no noun after obiective.","[Against this cheerful man can] renders Wieder diesen frohlichen Man kan, where Man may be Mann (“man”) or man (“one”); “about this (g his) sphere” renders über diese (g seine) Sphäre, über being also “beyond”. *de gente hircosa centurionum*: “of the goatish tribe of centurions”; *qvod sapio, satis est mihi*: “what I know is enough for me”.","“afterwards” renders nachdem in so fellt nachdem Loke … weg, which may be a slip for nachher or for an unfinished clause; *qvalitas occulta*: “occult quality”; *asylum ignorantiae*: “asylum of ignorance”; *mundus intelligibilis*: “intelligible world”."]],"reflm:5638":["S. I:\nOur concept of the world is for the empirical synthesis not too large and not too small, if by it only the [to no] totality of appearances limited by no restrictive condition is understood, hence a problematic concept that serves merely as a rule.\nIf one says: the conditions are [to a given] given along with it by this, that the conditioned is given, this signifies: that the rule is given according to which [they] each of them can be found; but thereby the totality of the series is not given: the collective all. The synthesis (g of the *regressus*) is unlimited (*indefinita*), this seems as much as: the series is infinite (*infinita*); the *synthesis inversa*[of the] *in conseqventia* has a beginning, this seems: the series has a beginning. We have an outermost of the senses (relative) and also a first of our use of reason; from there we begin.",["“restrictive” renders restringirende, “limited” eingeschränkte, “unlimited” unbeschränkt.","*synthesis inversa … in conseqventia*: “inverse synthesis … in consequence”, i.e. toward the consequences; *regressus*: “regress”."]],"reflm:5639":["S. I:\nThe proposition: the concept of an absolute *totality* of the series of conditions must be either too large or too small, signifies: that no such concept at all is possible. For absolute time would have to be determined either thereby, that [some] the *synthesis* congruated with a part [o] of it or with the whole time. But we have a concept of the magnitude of time only by means of the appearances. Our world-concepts are transcendent, and it is said through such a principle that they can altogether be immanent and thereby alone adequate to the ob|ject.\nThat, however, there can be no *totality* in the (g empirical) *synthesis* of appearances signifies that it is [through] with regard to the empirical *indefinita*, but not given as infinite, because it is given only through the *synthesis*, which is at all times finite.\nIt is a remarkable rule or maxim of reason, which belongs to its discipline, that one must prove no [proposition of] transcendental proposition of reason from concepts apagogically, since thereby it is often only shown that our concept is faulty on both sides. E.g. that there can be no absolute freedom of religion, and on the other side: that there must be an absolutely complete one. One has a faulty concept of religion or of freedom. [and through the] But [through] such antinomy [is a] does serve for a skeptical method, to test the correctness of our concepts and presuppositions. One shows the hindrances and [Sch] contradictions from both sides and is thereby kept from judging dogmatically on one or the other, thus driven merely to criticize one's judgment.\n[The unlimited synthesis is not] The infinity of the *synthesis* in a series is not the infinity of the manifold of the members considered as given, for this [member] manifoldness is given only through the *synthesis*. It is [in the pr] as in the *progressu* merely potential.\n––––––––––\nBecause the series of conditions cannot be given; but indeed the concept, one must rather say: the series is too large for the concept, than: the concept too small for the series, for the series is fitted to the concept and not conversely.\nS. II:\nWhether, when I say: the world [must] is too large for our thoughts, it signifies just as much as: our thoughts are too small for the world. That which is given is the world. and not the thoughts. Where then does the fault lie: in the world or in thinking. In thinking lies the fault, because we think further than that which is empirically given; for a world is not empirically given, but everything given and what we can think belongs — in the world.\nIt should read: the thought of the world must for the neither too large nor too small, hence be exactly adequate to the world as a sum total of all [given] appearances. The world, however, is a mere *synthesis* of appearances, wherein the ground of the *synthesis* can always be determined only internally and not outside the appearances. The *synthesis* according to empirical laws and as *indefinita* is adequate to the world.\n––––––––––\nBecause the concept of appearances is given not before the *synthesis*, but only through it, the *synthesis* is in itself undetermined with regard to the appearances, consequently [infinite altho] it goes into the infinite, although on that account the appearance is not given as infinite. It is thus at all times finite, and every given world is finite reckoning from the point *a priori*. On the other hand it is *potentialiter* [of the appearance] infinite according to the illusion, if namely one considers the *synthesis* as given through the object. In such a way the thought or the concept according to which we are to think the world is neither too large nor too small for it, but is entirely adequate to this problematic concept or to the problem that is stuck in the concept, i.e. to the possibility of all empirical cognition in the field of appearances.\n(g In the senses there is no completed *synthesis* and nothing complete and unconditioned.)\nThe world must be neither too large nor too small for our thoughts, means as much as: one must think it so that its concept agrees with the conditions of the thoroughgoing empirical *synthesis* and its rule. Or conversely: the concept of the world must itself arise from this. Now this is a *synthesis* progressing without end from the conditioned to conditions and a *Progressio indefinita*, [through] in which time itself is determined through the *synthesis* of the appearances and thus [neither] time neither determines the appearance nor limits itself with regard to the appearances. For then the world is an idea, [which only] whose object is given only through this *synthesis* and its rule, but never as an absolute whole for itself and all possible *synthesis* in a collective unity.",["The line break after Gegen|stande falls before angemessen seyn können in the German; the English has to finish the clause before it.","der Gedanke von der Welt muß vor die weder zu groß noch zu klein: vor die (for the) has no noun; kept as broken.","dem Scheine nach: according to the illusion (Schein), i.e. seemingly; angemessen: adequate; Weltbegriffe: world-concepts."]],"reflm:5640":["[Also: XXIII 483,12-485,6]\nA reason that was sensibly determined would not be reason.\nOn the inherited and yet imputed wickedness.\nIn short, the key is this: freedom signifies the [possibility] relation of an action as appearance [partly] (g on the one side) to causes in the appearance [partly] and on the other side to an intelligible faculty of the same, whereby they themselves are the cause of the appearances and with regard to which the empirical conditions are not determining.",["derselben (of the same) and sie (they) are left as open as the German: they may refer to the causes or to the actions.","Bosheit: wickedness; zugerechnet: imputed; Vermögen: faculty."]],"reflm:5641":["On the back of C. Spener's letter to Kant of 28 Apr. 1781 (X 248 [2 X 265]):\n*Qvant:*\n*Qval. Relat:*\n*Modal*",["Abbreviations of quantity, quality, relation, modality; left abbreviated as in the source."]],"reflm:5642":["S. I. At the upper margin:\nMy apparent idealism is the limitation of sensible intuition to mere experience and prevention, that we do not with them stray beyond its boundary to things in themselves.* It is merely the prevention of the transcendental *vitii subreptionis*, where one makes one's representations into things. I once named this doctrine transcendental idealism, because one has no name for it.\n*(g It would not have been worth it, for the sake of the objects of experience, to set one's principles so high. One may have the cog|nition\n*a priori* from wherever one will, they are confirmed through experience and to that extent reliable in use. Nevertheless it would be pleasant there too for the sake of the scientific. But where the use goes further than experience, where there is danger of fabrication, where mighty and apparent conflict is found, there it is necessary etc etc.)\nS. II:\nFor judging concepts are required, and for concepts [judgments] intuitions. The concepts, insofar as they pertain to intuitions *a priori*, cannot arise through the single empirical consciousness of the manifold, otherwise they would not be concepts of the connection of intuition; but they [on] are possible only through [the relation of the] the combination of it in one apperception by means of the unity of its *synthesis*. And in this consist the concepts *a priori*.\n––––––––––\nDialectic. Up to here we have had to do only with appearances, in whose exposition, their principles and use there is nothing but truth, and there was no idealism. For truth consists merely in the interconnection of the representations thoroughly according to laws of the understanding. In this consists all difference from the dream. Not in this, that the images exist thus for themselves separated from the mind. But now there first arises an illusion, and indeed a natural and unavoidable illusion, where our judgments say something of objects that is not contained in our concepts of them, i.e. of possible experience, and there our theory is the refutation of idealism.\nFirst the illusion consists in this: that in the field of experience we represent to ourselves according to mere laws of experience a *Progressus* that is not an empirical *Progressus*, but a mere idea, which can be no experience. We remain in the world of sense [but] and are guided by nothing but the principles of the [law] understanding that we use in ex|perience,\nbut we make our possible *Progressus* into an object in itself, in that we regard the possibility of experience as something actual in the object of experience.\nHere antinomy shows itself. All ideas that make up the ground of this dialectic are contained herein: psychological as well as theological, but only insofar as they belong in the series of possible experience, which is to bound itself. Here the ideas are only to complete the *Progressus* and are cosmological. But there shows itself yet another illusion, where they do not belong to the series and by themselves are to add [such a] something to experience, and there the ideas are partly psychological, partly theological. The hypothetical syllogisms are the guidance: to the first the categorical and disjunctive to the last. The first lay at the ground the subjective connection of all representations in one subject, the last the objective in one idea. Of the first subject there is then no concept, of the second object there is only an ideal.\nIf we require for truth something more than the thoroughgoing interconnection of intuitions according to laws of the understanding, wherein would we then posit it, if this were not at the same time the representation of a determinate object. If it is besides this to be in agreement with something else that does not lie in our representations, [s] how will we compare it with that. All objects (g are determined only through the representations in me; what they may otherwise be in themselves is unknown to me) are at the same time in us; an object outside us is transcendent, i.e. entirely unknown to us and useless for the criterion of truth. dialectic.\nIdealism assumes no other than thinking beings. That we never do, but only that we do not regard our representations as their properties. |\nContinuation at the upper margin: |\nIn the senses there is indeed neither truth nor error, because they do not judge at all, [but] hence all appearances are to that extent without possible error and no illusion.\nThe idealist concedes that actually extension and bodies outside us could be, but not actually be, thus merely a dream in us. |\nWe assert that these are nothing but representations and can be only in us, but their objects may nevertheless be outside us, but we know nothing of what they may be in themselves.\nBetween 2807-14:\nSynthetic unity of apperception *a priori* is the *synthesis* of the manifold according to a rule *a priori*. The logical function is the action of uniting one and the same consciousness with many representations, i.e. of thinking a rule in general. The unity of intuition *a priori* is possible only through the combination of the manifold in one apperception, which thus must also take place *a priori*, consequently also the unity of the *synthesis* of all empirical intuitions, because they are to be encountered in space and time.\nWhat then would one demand that I should assert, so that I would not be an idealist?\nIdealism is a metaphysical whim [merely in order], which goes further than is necessary to awaken to thinking. It goes with the philosophers as with the theologians. Syncretists. Semipelagians.",["sachen: things (Sachen); obiect and Gegenstand are both rendered \"object\"; Verknüpfung: connection, Verbindung: combination, Zusammenhang: interconnection.","zum ersten die categorische und disjunctive zum letzten: the distribution of the two kinds of syllogism to the first and the last is left as compressed as the German.","zum Denken aufzuweken: may mean to awaken (someone) to thinking or to awaken for the sake of thinking."]],"reflm:5643":["S. I:\nIn all synthetic cognition *a priori*, first intuition *a priori*, second a concept of the synthesis of the manifold *a priori* will have to be thought. On this are grounded the principles of synthetic cognition in general. For these contain nothing but the conditions under which alone certain intuitions are brought under concepts of the synthesis of them. The latter are called categories.\n(g We can sense something without thinking, [wir] the question is whether we cannot also think without sensing. (*a priori*.) But we can also intuit without sensing. Now if we are to think without sensing, then this must relate itself to that intuition.)\nIntuition is immediate relation of the power of representation to a single object. Concept the representation of it through a mark, |\nwhich it has in common with others. Intuition belongs to the senses, concept for the understanding.\nConcepts of the synthesis of the manifold of possible intuitions are nothing other than the combination that the representations have in one consciousness (g can, insofar as they are necessary in regard to an intuition), but only thought synthetically, i.e., that to a [jeden] (g to the concept of the object) something else added produces the consciousness of the representation of an object.\nIn the representation of an object, which is to contain the manifold of the intuition of it, the synthetic unity of the latter is necessary. The representation of this necessary unity, under which everything manifold of the intuition must stand, is, if it is to become cognition of an object of intuition, the principle of synthetic cognitions in general, and it itself must take place *a priori*.\nS. II:\nSomething that is determined in regard to the functions of the [Größe] judgments is the object, and this determination is determination of the object, and so in the others. The categories are thus concepts for the determination of the objects of our cognition in general, insofar as the intuition for it has been given. Thus principles for making experience out of appearance, which is purely objective, i.e., universally valid empirical cognition, since thus the synthesis must be determined *a priori*, because otherwise it would not be necessary and universal. For we know an object only as a something in general, to which the given intuitions are only predicates. Now how these can be the predicates of a third cannot be cognized through their comparison, but through the way in which in[de] one consciousness the consciousness of the manifold in general could be regarded as necessarily combined. [Der Begrif] In the representation of an object the manifold is united. All intuitions are only representations; the object [daß] to which they are related lies in the understanding.\nA synthesis can never be cog|nized as necessary and thus *a priori*\nfrom the representations that are to be combined synthetically, but from the relationship of them to a third concept, in which [die Verkn] and in relation to which this combination is necessary. This third concept is that of an object in general, which is thought precisely through this synthetic necessary unity and which is determined in regard to the logical functions of such unity. For through this the manifold of the representations first becomes objective, i.e., cognition, and appearance empirical cognition.\nThat something is objectively determined I cannot cognize *a posteriori* without determining it objectively according to a rule *a priori*; for everything that is objectively determined must be able to be determined *a priori* from the concept of the object, not indeed according to the matter, but according to the form of the connection. Through the very same representations through which the concept of an object is determined, there becomes a concept which conversely determines those representations objectively.",["\"object\" renders Kant's Latinate obiect/Object throughout (Gegenstand does not occur here); \"principle(s)\" renders both Principien and Grundsatz; \"relation\" is Beziehung, \"relationship\" Verhältniß.","The interlinear \"können\" construes with \"haben\": \"the combination that the representations can have in one consciousness\".","The last sentence seems incomplete: \"wird ein Begrif, der ...\" may mean \"becomes a concept which ...\" or lack a participle; rendered as it stands."]],"reflm:5644":["19. S. I, II.\nS. I:\n(s Nature does not satisfy us, neither in regard to the efficient causes nor ends.)\n(s It is something different: science of pure reason, and: science of the principles of pure reason in general. Possibility of such a one is transcendental philosophy.)\n(s Metaphysics. Either [pura oder] *vniversalis* — *ontologia*, or *specialis* and *applicata*: 1. cosmology, which contains objects of experience —, 2. theology.\n*Metaphysica pura*. *Ontologia* is the system of the pure principles *a priori*. Logic is a science of the principles of all reason, itself indeed pure, but does not treat of the principles.)\nA science is not sufficiently determined by the fact that it contains the first principles of human cognition, but rather: that this cognition is of a particular kind, *a priori*. [Logic auch] Cognition from principles *a priori* is cognition of pure reason and either pure mathematics or pure philosophy. Metaphysics: the system of pure |\nphilosophy (g of speculative or practical philosophy (of nature or of morals). All rational cognition from concepts has its metaphysics.) Mathematics is rational cognition through construction of the concepts in intuition, for the reason that it goes only to objects of the senses. Philosophy, rational cognition from concepts, thus also goes to things that are not objects of the senses. (g Even possibility of mathematics.)\nThings can only be given through the senses, but we can nevertheless cognize much of them *a priori*. Things that are not given to the senses, if they are to be cognized *a priori*, (s that we become aware of them only on the occasion of experience proves nothing.) make up the *metaphysicam puram*.\n(s Metaphysics proper has 2 parts: the 1st goes to things as objects of experience: psychology and general natural science, the second to objects as ideas: world and God, which nevertheless belong necessarily to reason. Thus 1. all elements and principles of pure thinking with abstraction from all objects (g ontology) — 2. application to objects: a. of the senses, b. of pure reason.)\nThus *metaphysica* is either *pura* or *applicata*.\n(g philosophical is a rational cognition from concepts,\nmetaphysical: pure rational cognition from concepts.)\nBefore metaphysics goes transcendental philosophy, which, like logic, does not treat of objects, but of the possibility, the [Umfange] sum total and the boundaries of all cognition of pure reason (also of pure mathematics). It is the logic of pure rational cognition.\nBefore transcendental philosophy critique of reason in general.\nS. II:\n(g As long as I only analyze concepts that have use in experience or set forth principles of it, it still goes; but if I go away from it and further, there the difficulty begins. I will first set everything forth critically, then my own propositions.)\nThus first metaphysics itself is the object, and there that which investigates its possibility is critique.\n(s Ontology is the sum total of all concepts and principles of pure thinking.)\nSecond in (g proper) metaphysics the system of all pure concepts *a priori* that we relate to objects*: transcendental philosophy** (s pure reason entire), ontology (s *conceptus dabiles*), cosmology and theology (s *ideales*). (s Explain hyperphysically. Hyperphysical. metaphysical. physical.)\n*(g if concepts are *a priori*, consequently are not given through objects, then they can only be taken from the understanding itself in relation to objects in general. 1. To things in general: ontology; 2. connection in a whole; 3. dependence [dieses] on a ground.)\n**(g what logic is in regard to all cognition, that is transcendental philosophy in regard to pure cognition *a priori*.)\nThird these principles applied to objects of outer and inner experience in general. *Physica rationalis*, *Psychologia rationalis*.\nNot *psychologia empirica*.\n(g 1. part occupies itself with things;\n2. part with ideas (in themselves).)\n(g All philosophical cognition has its metaphysics, which determines the principles *a priori*.)\nWe have many cognitions *a priori* of which we are in need in regard to experience. e.g. Everything has a cause. But in regard to these we would need no metaphysics.\nThe objects of experience do not fully satisfy reason itself according to these principles. Hence reason goes out beyond experience, and in regard to that metaphysics is necessary: God and another world. In metaphysics there is a part that merely analyzes the use of reason in regard to experience. This is\n|\nvery useful for culture, but dispensable for science. Hence still another part: synthetic, through which concepts are made transcendent.",["\"object\" renders both Gegenstand and Kant's Latinate obiect; \"principle(s)\" both Principien and Grundsätze; the asterisks after \"objects\" and \"transcendental philosophy\" are Kant's reference signs for the two additions that follow.","\"denn eigene Sätze\": \"denn\" read as \"dann\" (then).","\"deren wir uns ... benöthigt sind\" is irregular (a blend of \"bedürfen\" and \"benötigt sein\"); rendered \"of which we are in need\". \"deren\" in both following \"in Ansehung deren\" is ambiguous in reference."]],"reflm:5645":["36. Sheet 1. 2.\nSheet 1. p. I:\nIn order to find out the need of our age with respect to the danger, of getting through happily between the two cliffs of dogmatism and skepticism, and [in doing so] at the same time to determine both concepts appropriately to this need, we must first of all establish its character with respect to the way of thinking that makes that caution necessary.\nExtensive knowledge and possession of a great multitude of sciences does not yet constitute the character of the way of thinking [and because]; for this depends on the quality and specific constitution of the power of judgment (g and on the principles that determine), what use one intends to make of the former. Whether our age has come far in knowledge [be there] and whether its cognition is to be called great, about that one can judge only comparatively; our descendants will perhaps find it still small. But a [skill] faculty can nevertheless already now have attained its maturity, so that the later world has no need to add anything further to it (because here it depends not on the quantity but on the quality [of the manifold] in the use of our cognitive powers), and this is the faculty of the power of judgment (*iudicium discretiuum*).\nOur age is the age of critique, i.e., of a (g sharp) estimation of the foundation of all assertions, to which the |\nexperience of long ages, perhaps also the cautious investigation of nature through observation and experiment set going by the famous Bacon of Verulam, has brought us not only in the assertions of natural science but, according to the analogy, also in all the rest, of which the ancients knew nothing and so were accustomed to wavering opinions. In this a future age can hardly surpass us, even if out of negligence we often do not make [all] (g as we should) use of these principles of critique. Certainly no past (g age) surpasses us in this, and this can therefore be called the (g scientific) character of ours.\nIn all science [is], if we abstract from the multitude of knowledge, the essential aim is that it distinguish itself from mere opinion, hence certainty. The method that one employs in it is merely the means of attaining this. Certainty is the unalterability of taking to be true. But taking to be true is unalterable either objectively: when we cognize that no preponderant ground for the opposite is possible in itself, or subjectively: when we are convinced that neither we nor any human being could ever get hold of greater grounds for the opposite.\nThe (g with consciousness) unalterable taking to be true is knowledge, the subjectively unalterable taking to be true belief. The [to be sure] taking to be true with the consciousness of its alterability is opining.\nExample in history. The word \"belief\" can signify either [with respect to] the source of our cognition or [the kind and] the degree of the taking to be true of it. In the former meaning no cognition [through] of history can arise otherwise than through a trust in the testimonies of others, i.e., by our believing others. In the second meaning a knowledge of history can certainly be a knowledge and |\np. II: |\nit has no need, according to the degree of taking to be true, to be distinguished from one's own experience, to which they do not dispute the name of knowledge, [distinguished to] by the term \"belief.\" Thus one knows that a Louis XIV lived just as surely as if he had seen him himself, and so a good part of history is true science; the taking to be true is objectively unalterable in it. It is impossible [that ever] to concede sufficient grounds for the opposite of the|\nsame. On the other hand I take the history of the 7 first kings of Rome to be true, although only on insufficient grounds, yet on such as are of the kind on which all history rests, and [of] moreover I am convinced that no one will ever prove the opposite, because all documents for it are lacking, i.e., I believe it. But that there was perhaps, even before the building of Rome, a cultivated people and a flourishing city on the same site, from which [the] perhaps the Cloaca Maxima still derives, is an opinion that is worth hearing; |\nbut it is not impossible that one might yet find a proof of the opposite, if perhaps this underground work should be cleared out and inscriptions by its builder (perhaps Servius Tullius) should be found; I therefore do not cognize unalterability in my taking to be true.\nAll knowledge is either empirical [ode], i.e., derived from experience, or rational: arising from reason, hence possible *a priori* and self-sufficient. To the former are counted one's own experience and history (i.e., reliable report, hence a knowledge, of the experience of others). The second certainty is independent of all experience.\nAll empirical certainty is combined with the consciousness of the contingency of the truth; for experience teaches indeed that something is constituted [se] or has happened thus or otherwise, but never that it could not at all have been constituted or have happened otherwise.\nRational certainty is inseparably combined with consciousness of the necessity of what is taken to be true. All cognition which [with the] according to its essential constitution is combined with the consciousness [of its] of its necessity is apodictic. Thus [is] [all] every proposition of reason must be apodictically certain, and in [propositions] assertions of reason (*assertionibus*) neither opining nor believing takes place.\nBut all apodictic propositions are twofold: cognized either from mere concepts or (g only) through the construction of concepts. The former are called *dogmata*, the second *mathemata*. If it [could be proved], as Leibniz held, could be proved from the mere concept of a body as a composite substance that it consists of simple parts, then this would be a *dogma*; but if through the geometrical presentation of a space that a body occupies, and the equally geometrical division of this space, its divisibility to infinity is proved, then this proposition is a *mathema*. Philosophy alone |\np. III: |\ncan therefore con|tain *dogmata*,\nbecause it is distinguished from mathematics, which, just as (g well) as the former, is [a] rational cognition, precisely in this, that it judges merely from concepts, but the latter not otherwise than through construction of concepts.\nPhilosophy is thus indeed capable of apodictic certainty, but not of the intuitive [son] by means of intuition *a priori*, such as mathematics can provide, but only of the discursive from concepts. Hence the former contains principles, to be sure, the latter alone axioms, the former proofs (*probationes*), the latter alone demonstrations; the former [delivers] provides conviction, the latter at the same time self-evidence (*evidentiam*).\nAddition in the margin: |\n(g Critique. Art of distinguishing opining from knowledge.)\n[All cognition, even if it were not even rational cognition but only historical, nevertheless needs certain principles which contain within themselves the criteria of truth. Thus there are certain principles according to which one assesses the probability of a history, not merely those that [flow] from the nature of the things narrated and are thus taken from reason, but also those which determine the way in which experience of ancient times could be brought (g with reliability) to us.]\nOne can [oppose] set historical certainty alongside rational certainty, by which one understands the empirical certainty that does not rest on our own experience (hence on reports of others' experience). In the same way there also takes place a rational belief, which is analogous to historical belief, although it rests on other grounds. In certainty (g of) both kinds objective unalterability of the judgment is thought, in [second] belief of both kinds only subjective unalterability. That [there in] the planets contain rational inhabitants can be believed according to reason; for as many grounds of proof of it as one can reasonably expect, given our distance from them, [make] furnish a (g so) great analogy between them and the earth as is necessary for this consequence, and one is moreover certain that no one will ever know anything more of them in order to prove the opposite.\nRational belief is called moral certainty when it is sufficient [according] for the |\nobligation to lay it [in] at the ground of his actions [as principle]. For because the moral laws, which contain the grounds of obligation, (g also rest on reason and thus) are objectively unalterable, to that extent [Vernunftglaub Vernunft] certainty can take place. But this cannot be called rational certainty (*certitudo logica*), because not [merely] from theoretical [principles of reason follows but as] principles, but merely for the sake of practical maxims, it must necessarily [as true] be assumed, and [may], because these maxims rest on moral laws, which have objective unalterability, one may not call such a rational belief moral belief, but one can call it moral certainty. Thus the propositions: There is a God, there is a future life, would for speculative cognition yield only a rational belief and nevertheless [as] count as morally certain[ange]; for because only through them are all [well-founded hindrances] (g objections against) the obligation according to moral laws removed (namely those which are taken from the consideration that observance or transgression of the latter could, according to the mere course of nature, have no effect proportioned to them), [but these objective certainty] so [just as objectively] their acceptance is [eben s] on account of this necessity of theirs [and objective unalterability] likewise [an unalterable] practically necessary, and it is impossible that there could be grounds for the opposite, because otherwise there could also be grounds for the opposite of morality and this would thus itself be doubtful and uncertain.\np. IV:\nBy *dogmata* one understands apodictic propositions from concepts. Hence mathematics contains apodictic propositions, to be sure, but no *dogmata*, because they are possible not from concepts but only through their construction. The propositions that between two points only one straight line is possible, or that in every triangle the sum of all the angles is equal to two right ones, of which the first is an axiom, the second a demonstrated theorem, are *mathemata*, i.e., cognitions [which] whose (g insight and) certainty is intuitive (g but at the same time nevertheless apodictic); but that [everything\n|\n(g which) exists contingently is a proposition must have a cause is indeed also an apodictic but only discursive proposition from concepts hence] every statement must be truthful and that the lie is reprehensible, is an apodictic but discursive proposition from concepts and can be called a *dogma*.\nAll [sciences] apodictic certainty is [therefore] contained only in two sciences, which comprise the cognition of reason *a priori*: in mathematics or philosophy. *Dogmata* can be met with only in the latter. But philosophy is either theoretical, where reason has to do merely with itself, or practical: where it has to do with the laws of the will. The former part, insofar as it contains apodictic, i.e., pure rational cognitions, is called metaphysics, the second, which contains precisely such practical laws, pure morals.\n––––––––––\nSummary concept.\n[Dogmatism is the way of thinking]\nBetween dogmatism and skepticism the middle and solely lawful way of thinking is criticism.\nThis is the maxim of never accepting anything as true otherwise than after complete examination of the principles.\nAll principles are [either] (g now on the one side) *a posteriori*, i.e., taken empirically, and these (g again either) one's own experience or testimonies (g of) the experience of others, hence experience (*in sensu stricto*) or history.\nAll principles are on the other side *a priori* and taken from reason, but these (g either) from reason insofar as it judges merely according to concepts, hence philosophical principles, or insofar as it judges merely in accordance with the construction of concepts, i.e., their presentation *a priori* in intuition.\nOne's own experience and mathematics need [not], the latter not at all, the former only seldom, a critique, so that illusion is not taken for perception. But all the more do history of distant ages among the empirical cognitions and speculative philosophy among the rational cognitions need critique, i.e., examination of the principles.\np. V:\nDogmatism is the way of thinking of adhering to assertions without [Prüfun] critique (i.e., examination |\nof the principles). The most natural propensity of the human being with respect to cognitions is toward dogmatism: 1. on account of laziness, because going back to principles is harder than the progression to the application of principles once accepted and put into circulation. 2. Because through critique cognition is not extended but only secured. 3. From fear of exposing the poverty of our knowledge to our own and others' eyes.\nSkepticism is a chosen principle of doing damage to dogmatism, yet not [by means of the Crit] with the intention [the truth] of introducing true conviction [to] against it, but only in order to overthrow the persuasion of others. [For this] The inclination to this is not natural but artificial and can arise only from displeasure at the usurpation of dogmatism. The occasion for it is given to it [in historical] (g 1st in) empirical cognition by the polymathy [of the] of all, chiefly of ancient history, and its system-building, 2nd in rational cognition [Meta] by speculative philosophy, namely metaphysics and universal morals with their [questions] assertions about the highest good. It is thus, to be sure, a malice, in that it (g seeks) to cause nothing but harm, namely seeks to rob human reason of all hope in the most important questions of reason: but not an unprovoked malice, for dogmatism as a (g an assent) usurping self-conceit cannot at least complain of the resistance of the misologist as of an injustice. Yet it is (g to say the least) folly; for it transforms all taking to be true into illusion, which it opposes to truth, must therefore after all concede criteria of their difference, and nevertheless it denies these entirely.\nDanger is the possibility of a greater ill. There is therefore danger both in dogmatism and in skepticism, in the former of the scraping together of a heap of errors among a small number of truths and of contempt for the latter themselves on account of their kinship with the former; in the latter, of the disavowal of our duty to make use of our reason everywhere, and of a laziness of the former glossed over by a specious pretext.\nThis danger can be removed only through the greatest critical diligence, on the empirical side to trace the sources (g of history) and the derivation |\nof them to us, and on the side of reason the nature and the faculty of human reason as [far] well in its speculative use in metaphysics as in the practical in morals, and to establish convincingly its boundaries, likewise its domain and the necessary principles of the latter.",["Vorwarhalten (= Fürwahrhalten) is \"taking to be true\" throughout; Glauben is \"belief\" and, as a verb (\"daß wir anderen Glauben\", \"ich Glaube sie\"), \"believe\"; in \"ich Glaube sie\" the \"sie\" may refer to die Geschichte (\"it\") or to the kings (\"them\").","Broken-off deletions ([ode], [se], [son], [Vernunftglaub Vernunft], [eben s], [Prüfun], [Meta], [Crit], gewis[ange]) are left in Kant's German; \"des|selben\" and \"ent|halten\" are split words, rendered \"the|same\" and \"con|tain\".","\"nicht ungewitzte Bosheit\": ungewitzt (unschooled, unprovoked) is a doubtful sense; Augenscheinlichkeit (evidentia) is \"self-evidence\"; Erfahrenheit (not Erfahrung) is \"experience\" in \"experience of long ages\"."]],"reflm:5646":["S. I:\nThe I.\nAccording to the appearances of inner (g as well as outer) sense one can never regard oneself as the identical self, even according to the sensible character. Merely in regard to morality, which [Int] is the pure consciousness of ourselves independent of determination in space and time, is there always the same subject of free actions under the same laws [as long as] in everything wherein we are conscious of ourselves, and there the whole of our actions is regarded as unity, and we cannot, because we once improve ourselves, believe that we on that account already have another personality and could not be punished on account of the previous one, as [almost] almost all human beings believe. Of course this cannot happen from human beings (s as judges). In the same way one reproaches oneself for the evil of one's childhood years (Rousseau: ribbon story) or also for what we have done in drink. Only the improvement is nevertheless an experience that in us the character is not so entirely evil.",["Moralitaet = morality; Personlichkeit = personality; Character = character (sensibelen Character: sensible character).","Bandgeschichte: Rousseau's story of the stolen ribbon (Band) in the Confessions; rendered literally as ribbon story.","von Menschen (s als Richtern) geschehen: the von may mean by human beings or on the part of human beings; left as from."]],"reflm:5647":["S. IV:\nReason has no other than analytic use. The synthetic, through which it makes principles, is dialectical. Hence the |\nprinciples of understanding are either immanent or transcendent. The objective principles of reason are dialectical, the subjective dogmatic.\nThe principles are either anticipating or ...., the latter are dialectical. The anticipating principles either of transcendental apprehension or association, both of recognition; the breaks off.",["Grundsatz = principle throughout; Verstandesgrundsatzen = principles of understanding.","die bricht ab: the text stops after die (the), and bricht ab (breaks off) is presumably the editor's notice that the note breaks off there.","The gap .... is in the source; anticipirend = anticipating."]],"reflm:5648":["Kant's remark on the blank reverse of the letter from G. Hufeland of 11 October 1785 (X 388f. [2 X 412]) in vol. I of the Dorpat collection of letters to Kant p. 206:\nOne must have thoughts to give up.\nOne cannot get away from cognitions that concern the first beginning.\nThe decrying of hypotheses.",["Preis zu geben: preisgeben = to give up, abandon, or expose; which sense is meant is left open.","Verschreyung = decrying, disparagement."]],"reflm:5649":["S. I:\nTiedemann.\nMetaphysics can take three paths: 1. That universal cognitions (g of nature) are possible merely through experience (also their concepts). 2. That universal cognitions are also possible merely from concepts a priori and through reason. 3. That [Erfa] universal cognitions do indeed take place a priori and prior to all experience, but only insofar as they are grounds of the possibility of experience. The first path is empirical, |\nthe second dogmatic, the third critical, because the critique (g and resolution) of experiences alone can direct it. For the last path it is required that to the (g empirical) intuition [in de] pure sensible intuition a priori, and to the empirical concepts pure concepts a priori, which relate merely to the unity (g of consciousness) of these intuitions, lie at the ground.\nThat, however, besides sensibility and understanding (both a priori) the faculty of reason still contains principles of the use of both, and indeed to restrict the concepts of understanding to experience and those of sensibility also to the same, so that their conditions are not extended to beings in themselves, God and spirit. At the same time, therefore, to give reason freedom still to think something beyond experience, which, however, is indeed necessary for the completion of our use of the understanding, but can never be thought through theoretical concepts otherwise than negatively, through moral ones alone positively, and contains the totality of the conditions for everything.\nS. II:\nHow much one wishes that, if one wants to raise oneself to supersensible objects, that one were rid of the restricting concepts of space and time, likewise, if one wants to arrive at an absolute whole of all conditions of understanding, that one were of the conditions always continuing in the understanding [rid], which make totality impossible, although they belong to every experience, in a word: that one were rid of that which properly belongs only to the possibility of experience and goes no further.\nThe pure concepts of reason God (g freedom) and other world are properly of moral origin.\n––––––––––\nWith space and time one can take only two paths: 1. that they are [mere] concepts, 2. mere intuitions. In the first case they are a. empirical or b. concepts a priori. In the second they are [ans] 1. intuitions of things in themselves through observation and yet nec|essary,\n2. [anschau] formal intuition a priori, i.e. consciousness of the way [we] in which objects are represented to us to the senses.",["Wege = paths; Critik = critique; Auflösung = resolution (also solution, dissolution).","zum Grunde liegen = lie at the ground; the two datives (der empirischen Anschauung, dem empirischen Begriffe) are kept in their order.","Daß aber ... and Zugleich also ... zu geben are incomplete clauses in the source and are left so; Wesen an sich selbst = beings in themselves."]],"reflm:5650":["S. I:\n(g If the concept of space and time were not borrowed from the form of our sensibility, then one would have to have taken the synthetic propositions of them from the things themselves, thus space and time would have objective reality for themselves either as substances or accidents or relations; [these] both, however, precede the things, thus they would have to be mere concepts, but from these no synthetic propositions can be taken.)\nMetaphysics.\nSubstance is the ultimate subject of reality. Its relation to the [of this] existence of this is called force, and this is it alone through which the existence of the substance is designated and wherein its existence itself also consists. Now because every force has a degree, there can be many subjects for much smaller degrees of the same kind, and a whole be composed out of them, if they stand externally in community, or they can also [in] be so combined that they make up only one force, in which no external community of different subjects is encountered, i.e. unity of the subject [with] of much [degrees] great degree of force is just as much as multiplicity of the subjects of smaller degrees, indeed |\nthe one can be transformed into the other. For if out of a drop of water a drop of quicksilver came to be (as far as weight is concerned), then the amount of the parts would not be increased, thus not of the subjects, and yet the same could arise if 14 drops of water were swallowed up into one. The simple subject is thus not on that account indivisible, for it is not divisible as composed of many subjects, but as [a single] (g unity of the) subject, but of a magnitude of force, alterable into many subjects of smaller. The soul is simple means as much as: it does not consist of many subjects in space. This also cannot be, for we cognize it through no forces that can appear in space; but from this it does not follow that the absolute unity of the subject should not be transformed into a multiplicity of them without alteration of the degree of reality in the whole.\nThe constitution of something as absolute subject, which does not inhere in others, signifies a force [whose magnitude] which does not consist of a multiplicity of mutually determining forces, but [unity and] consists in degree.\nThat a being could exist as unity of the substance and nevertheless be dissolved into a multiplicity of them involves no contradiction. For it is not necessary[ity] that multiplicity of the subjects must already have been before, and consequently that the substance must already before the dissolution be thought as composite, i.e. as containing that multiplicity of the subjects as parts which are possible in it through the dissolution. For if this dissolution is merely the effect of the setting-outside-one-another of that which previously inhered in one, then the inherence is transformed into a subsistence and the accident into an absolute subject. This always happens in the dissolution of degree. Here we never have the concept of multiplicity of the subject, because it is considered internally and not [in] its external relation (extensively) as to magnitude. Nevertheless the intensive magnitude can always be regarded as potential multiplicity of the subjects, which can be separated; for instead of all forces ceasing according to degree (whereby then also the |\nwhole subject would cease), [all] these degrees (g of all forces) and with them also the concept of a subject in which they inhere (for this in the one is not at all distinguished from that in the other) can exist separated from one another. The resisting force of a body could decrease in that degrees of it were entirely lost or also in that what it lost, (g without a part which is outside the other being taken away, but rather each remained, though with diminished, degree.) existed as a particular subject.\nS. II:\nThat we [through the] from immediate perception, and indeed validly for all thinking beings universally, could infer that they must be simple [nature] beings would be impossible, for perception, without a contradiction being encountered [could] in the opposite, can furnish no necessity: if here the thinking subject were not merely considered as object of its own inner sense, since only the subject of all its own thoughts can be its object, consequently, since it must be distinguished from all objects and their manifoldness, can be represented through nothing but the unity of apperception, but for that reason also represents nothing at all but merely a something without further predicates (as subject). [If] Thus thinking can take place only with the unity of apperception. Outside me I can perceive no thought, consequently also not a thinking subject as such. A thinking being can thus [from] through the united representation of different (g outside one another) thinking subjects represent its thoughts to itself as having arisen.\nOn existence in subjects in general.\nWe can think only through judgments, because we have concepts only through the (g necessary) unity of the consciousness of the manifold of representations. In judging, everything that we think of the object is predicate, and the object which we think is in a further *respectu* |\nitself predicate. In what exists we must nevertheless think subjects in themselves which are not predicates, but our concept of their reality is none other than that of reality and its inherence, consequently force and that of certain degrees. But the subject remains only a something. Every degree, however, can be represented as composed of smaller ones, and each as belonging to one subject, then it is a *compositum*, or all to one subject, then it is *simplex*. Now whether that which has existed as unity of the subject, i.e. a great degree of force, could exist as multiplicity of the subjects, and on what it depends that something which existed (g as) unity of the subject exists as multiplicity, can be neither explained nor refuted at all.\nOn metaphysics.\nI have proved that human reason in its speculative use [does not get further] can extend to no other objects than to objects of a possible experience, and of [them] these also to nothing more than can be given in some possible experience, that consequently, because metaphysics does not place its importance in those cognitions that are found [through] on the path of experience [confirm] or at least can be confirmed through it, but rather in that which goes out beyond the boundary of all possible experience, all dogmatic utility of it [completely] falls away, indeed that even its existence (g as useless) would have to fall away, if [the] cognitions which we actually have a priori and without the help of experience did not let us believe that their use, since those are independent of experience, could well reach further than experience, and from that, again, through false but deceptive judgments [Einwü] attacks and difficulties could arise for important cognitions. Now it came down to how cognitions a priori, consequently without being [gebor] derived from experience, could nevertheless go everywhere to objects of experience, indeed even to nothing else. This I accomplished in such a way that I showed intuitions a priori and also concepts a priori, of which the first presented nothing but the form of appearances, the latter the form of the concepts of things in general that appear, whose use, although they |\nare representations a priori, (g itself) extends merely to experience. [That] Here everything that is to be accomplished was comprised in one problem: how are synthetic cognitions a priori possible?",["Kraft = force; Grad = degree; Vielheit = multiplicity; Auflösung = dissolution; Schweere = heaviness, weight.","nothwendig[keit]: Kant first wrote Nothwendigkeit (necessity), then deleted -keit; [Einwü] and [gebor] are broken-off deleted words, left in German.","wichtige Erkentnisse ... entstehen könnten: the accusative/dative of wichtige Erkentnisse is unclear; read as (for) important cognitions, but it may be the subject (important cognitions could arise)."]],"reflm:5651":["S. II:\nA root is a quotient that is at the same time the *divisor* of a given magnitude. In every division two magnitudes must be given: the *dividendus* and the *divisor*, and the quotient is to be found, i.e. the number that comes from unity just as the *dividendus* from the *divisoren*. Thus the relation of the *dividendi* to the *divisore* is given, and (g now) the relation of the *divisoris* to unity is to be found, which (g is equal to the previous one and which) the quotient expresses. But is extraction of the root: is only the *dividendus* given* as (g the) 2nd power of an unknown magnitude x, which is to be found, namely a:x\n= x:1, thus here –9:x\n= x:1. so that x2 = –9. Now let x be +x or –x, then x2 is always =9, [and] but not =–9.\n1:3 = –3:–9 | √–9 = (√9) × (–√9) [not (√9)× √–9] = 3 × -3.\n*(g the *divisor*, which is at the same time quotient, is to be found, thus the middle geometric proportional number between the given one and unity. Unity must here at all times be positive, otherwise –9 cannot [with it only] the product breaks off.)\n[The magn] The [un]conditioned connection (g in an unconditioned totality) is expressed by the sign –, the breaks off.\nIf for a *Substantia phaenomenon* the substantial, for a *Caussa phaenomenon* the (g sensibly) unconditioned cause, for a *Commercio* the unconditioned unity of different substances in their inter|action\nis to be found, or the ground of it: then this unconditioned is -a. But this multiplied with a gives [du] in the whole –, i.e. that the whole lies outside sensibility and for magnitude of this kind no root can be found. [but] But indeed the root as category designated only with + and – at once, which in the former case is a[s] *phaenomenon* (g but not whole) and in the second a [phaen] *noumenon*, which would indeed be positive, but cannot be given, hence it signifies only the root with the sign –, insofar as it multiplied with itself without sign (as category) something [ab]: the concept [of an abso] of a whole with the sign – of an absolute whole, which is yet impossible, signifies. The regulative principle is lodged here in the removal of all boundaries from the sensible, i.e. the progress to infinity and the assumption of a supersensible √–a, which however cannot be given.\nThe categories of quantity come close to addition, those of quality to subtraction, those of relation to multiplication, those of modality to division, and, if all these are to be regarded as sensibly unconditioned compositions, then the latter are impossible roots.",["“But is extraction of the root: is only the *dividendus* given”: the German (Allein ist Ausziehung der Wurzel: ist nur der dividendus gegeben) is elliptical; the second ist may be read as “if only … is given”.","“[s]” keeps the deleted letter of Kant's ein[s]; “[du]”, “[ab]”, “[phaen]” are deleted, broken-off words kept as they stand. “breaks off” renders the editorial bricht ab, twice.","“connection”: Verknüpfung; “interaction”: Wechsel|wirkung; “progress”: Fortschritt; “is lodged”: stekt."]],"reflm:5652":["S. I:\nThe categories' similarity with *species Arithmetices*.\n1. Those of magnitude through addition, 2. of quality through subtraction of the parts from the whole (which is given), 3. of causality through multiplication are cognized, for there a state of the world comes from a preceding one just as this from the preceding one. They are actually generated thus, and in the series of causes there is true multiplication of appearances. 4. Of modality through division, since the cause is to be found |\nwhich arises from the first in the way that the given effects each from their causes.\nIn all this it is to be noted that the categories are here applied only to appearances. Hence, because quality in appearance is the object of sensation (the empirical in space), it is given as unity and only divisibility can be thought in it. — Of empty space and time with quality and the proof that it cannot be experienced, but also never proved and, since also its opposite cannot be proved, one is necessitated always to assume such small degrees of filling as are needful for the explanation of appearances.\nThe ideal of the universal original being is to be compared with the irrational magnitude. For to seek the square root means: to seek that number for a given one from which this comes in just the way that that unknown one comes from unity. Thus to seek that cause which is necessary through itself. It is an unnamable root, for [it] it can come from no alleged unity [in itself] in the way that the world comes from it, i.e. the necessity of the effect from a cause can be completely specified, but the necessity of the cause that is not effect requires a generating concept that is not encountered in our whole reason, but yet is not contradictory.\n––––––––––\nIn is the intuition as magnitude, A the reality, –A the *remotion* of it. A–A the objective negation =0. [A] d × 0 (that is, a determination which cancels itself) is impossible. Substance determines the quantity of time [Alteration and], causality the succession and position in time of the subordinated, *commercium* simultaneity. Substance is =ant, accident at. Cause adt, the caused dta.\n––––––––––\nThe general refutation of the empirical origin of time and space is that both give synthetic propositions *a priori*, and from this follows also their ideality as intuitions.",["*species Arithmetices*: “the species (operations) of arithmetic”. From “d × 0” to “dta” the source is Kant's Latin; the letter groups =ant, at, adt, dta are kept as they stand.","“In is the intuition”: the source reads In ist; a symbol after In appears to be lost. “alleged” renders angeblichen, which may stand for angebbaren, “assignable”.","“original being”: Urwesen; “unnamable”: unnennbare; “filling”: Erfüllung; “object”: Gegenstand."]],"reflm:5653":["P. I:\nAgainst (g material) idealism.\nIt is grounded on this, that we are immediately conscious of our own existence, but of external things only through an inference from the immediate consciousness of mere representations of [something] things outside us to the existence of the same, which inference, however, is not evident in its consequence, as the [to us] known property of our imagination proves, which is a faculty of representing objects to us intuitively even without their presence.\nAgainst this argument it is sufficient to adduce only this, that the transcendental consciousness of ourselves, which accompanies the spontaneity of all our actions of the understanding, but which consists in the mere I without the determination of my existence in time, is indeed immediate, but the empirical consciousness of myself, [i.e. d] which constitutes inner sense (as the former the form of the intellectuality of my subject), by no means takes place immediately, and that the consciousness of other things outside me [and] (which also must be presupposed as intellectual and insofar is not a representation of them in space, but can be called intellectual intuition, through which we have no cognition of things) and the determination of their existence in space must be simultaneous with the determination of my existence in time, I thus of my own empirically determined existence no more than of that of the things (which I do not know, as to what they are in themselves) outside me.\nFor in space alone do we posit the persistent, in time there is unceasing change. Now, however, the determination of the existence of a thing in time, i.e. in such a change, is impossible without also combining the persistent with its intuition. This must therefore be [given] intuited outside us as object of outer sense. But since I [insofar as I] am at the same time determining my existence and thus insofar am not empirically conscious of myself, I also cannot insofar be empirically [conscious] of the persistent outside me, i.e. as given in space, conscious, but only of my determination of the representation of them, insofar as I am merely affected by them according to the form of space, [which] in that I draw it and thereby at the same time become conscious of my own existence in time.\nThe intuition of a thing as outside me presupposes the consciousness of a determinability of my subject, in which I am not myself determining, which therefore does not belong to spontaneity, because the determining is not in me. And in fact I can think no space as in me*. Thus the possibility of representing [a] things in space [as] in intuition is [merely] grounded on the consciousness of a determination by other things, which signifies nothing further than the original passivity of me, in which I am not at all active. That the dream produces [just such] deception of existences outside me proves nothing against this; for external perceptions must always have preceded. Originally to obtain a representation of something as outside me, without in fact being passive, is impossible. —\n*(s and through space the representation of an object as outside me (in intuition) [becomes] first obtains reality. Conversely I would through space also obtain the concept of the existence of something outside me. if the concept of a relation that belongs to the *commercio*, and indeed as given in perception, did not lie at the ground. This concept, however, is that of mere passivity in a sta|te\nof the representations. That this is not inferred, because we do not perceive the cause of the existence of a representation in us, but [in th] is an immediate perception, must be proved. — If we were affected merely by ourselves, without however noticing this spontaneity, then only the form of time would be encountered in our intuition: and we would be able to represent no space to ourselves (an existence outside us). The empirical consciousness as determination of my existence in time would thus go around in a circle and presuppose itself — but above all be impossible, since even the representation of the persistent would be lacking, in which there is no continuous synthesis as in time.)\nP. II:\nIn the margin:\n(g That this is the only possible ground of proof.)\nAbove and below 30830-3098:\n(g That we must always carry out space- and time-determination at the same time, but may thereby determine just as little our existence in space as the existence of the things of space in time.\nPersistence attaches inwardly to the representation of space, as Newton says. The persistence of the form in our mind is not the same (for the form of time is just as persistent), but as representation of something [persistent] outside us, which we lay under all time-determination and therefore represent (g as) persistent, consequently also cannot regard it as spontaneity of self-determination. — The proposition is: [our] the empirical consciousness of our existence in time is necessarily combined with the empirical consciousness of a relation of something outside us, and the one is just as little a deception from a fallacious inference, also (g at all) just as little an inference, as the other.)\nThe representation of space lies at the ground of time-determination on account of persistence (likewise only in it can one, through a line that I draw, in that I am conscious of my synthesis merely in the subject, |\nobtain a representation of time as magnitude). Now the persistent cannot [a determination] be merely thought in the determination of time and belong to the spontaneity of self-determination, for then it would not lie at the ground of time-determination. Consequently it must be represented in relation to the mere receptivity of the mind, i.e. in relation to something affecting that is distinct from me, and this representation cannot be inferred, but must be original.\n––––––––––\nNot everything that is in time is at the same time in space, e.g. my representations: but everything that is in space is in time. In time I namely represent only myself, both by myself alone and also in community, and indeed not through inferences, but immediately, i.e. a *correlatum* to my state, without, however, cognizing it, and the sensible, but real representation of this external relation is space, but this representation itself, consequently also everything that is represented in space, is in time.\nThat, when I make myself into an object, space is not in me, but (g yet) in the formal subjective condition of the empirical consciousness of myself, i.e. in time, proves that something outside me, i.e. what I must represent in another way than myself, is combined with the empirical consciousness of myself and this at the same time a consciousness of an external relation (g is), without which I could not empirically determine my own existence.\nIt comes down to this, that I can become conscious of myself in an external relation through a particular sense, which, however, is required for the (g time-)determination of inner sense. Space proves a representation that is not related to the subject (g as object); for otherwise it would be the representation of time. That it is now related not to this, but immediately to something distinct from the subject as existing, that (g is) the consciousness [of the same] of the object as thing outside me. Thus that we have an outer sense and that even imagination can impress images on us only in relation to it, that is the proof of *dualismus*.\nAll objects of the senses are in time; but not everything that is in time (i.e. all objects), are in space. But now if all representations of things outside us were only objects of inner sense and representations of ourselves, then [I would have to all things] the [representations] objects of inner sense would at the same time be all objects, [also] and space would itself be time.\nThe proof of dualism is grounded on this, that the determination of our existence in time [unver] by means of the representation of space contradicts itself, if one did not regard this as the consciousness of a wholly different relationship than that of the representations in us to the subject, namely as the perception of the relationship of our subject to other things, and space as the mere form of this intuition. For were the space-[representation]perception grounded merely on ourselves without an object outside us, then it would at least be possible to become conscious [of the ground] of these representations as [inne] merely containing a relationship to the subject. But since in the latter way only the intuition of time ever comes out, the object that we represent to ourselves as spatial must rest on a representation of something other than our subject. But that we can be conscious of an external relationship without being able [to] ever to cognize the object itself, but only the form of this relation of our self to the presence of it, makes no difficulty. Nor that in dreams and lively fantasies it is possible, without actuality of the object, Yet the subjective of this intuition, makes no objection. For [the rel] without an outer sense, [which we however] whose representations we only repeat and put together in another way (as also happens with inner sense in fantasizing), we would be able to have no dreams at all.\nCrosswise on the left half of the page:\nOn idealism.\nIn the margin (phase θ3-4):\nPreface.\nIf we, (g in order) to explain the purposiveness of the things of the world, adduce a cause which is the cause according to the analogy of an understanding, then that is a tautological explanation, for end signifies that which has such a form as if the representation of the thing were at the same time the cause of it. But if we apply this very causality (g to) the world, insofar as it is a moral whole, and to the reality of its laws through their outcome, namely the highest good, then it reads otherwise. For there it is not tautologically explained *idem per idem*, but an effect brought out that would not have followed from the world alone.\nNow it still comes down to whether one could say God is the cause of the substance without at the same time having determined all its actions. We have, to be sure, not the least concept of the possibility of such a causality nor an example of the reality of it. But if this is assumed, then it would after all concern only the intelligible, in regard to which the concept of freedom is already of itself necessarily combined with the concept of a substance; because substance must be the ultimate subject of its actions and cannot itself be the manner of acting of another. How the substance of the world may be constituted in itself, we do not know. But (g it in) its empirically cognizable character, which concerns merely appearances, is no thing nor object of creation; but in it everything is always inwardly determined in the world of sense, which does no damage to the freedom of the intelligible. If one now said: in the intelligible character everything is good (because time vanishes there), only the causality through |\nfreedom is not to be comprehended at all. But the possibility of being cause of a substance, still less. The difficulties thus drawn from this against freedom are null, because one can combine no concept with the *hypothesi*.",["\"object\" renders both Gegenstand and the Latinate Object/Obiect/object/objecte; \"relation\" renders Relation and Beziehung (\"related\" bezogen), \"relationship\" Verhältnis; \"persistent\"/\"persistence\" das Beharrliche/Beharrlichkeit.","The clause \"I thus of my own empirically determined existence no more than of that of the things\" has no verb in the manuscript (presumably bewust bin); none is supplied. \"Zelt\" is read as Zeit, \"Ziehe\" as ziehe (draw); the sentence \"Nor that in dreams ... makes no objection\" is broken in the source and kept so.","\"its intuition\" (ihrer Anschauung) may refer to the determination or to time; the AA's location headings (S. I, Am Rande, etc.) are given in English and the deleted word-fragments ([d.i. d], [in de], [unver], [inne], [der Bezi]) are rendered or left as fragments."]],"reflm:5654":["1788). L Bl. D. 7. S. I, II. R I 200-202.\nS. I:\nAgainst idealism.\nIf there were no outer objects of our senses, hence no sense at all, but only imagination, then it would yet at least become possible to become conscious of this its action as a spontaneity; but then this representation would belong only to inner sense and contain nothing persistent that could lie at the ground of the determination of our existence in empirical consciousness. The mind must therefore be (g itself) immediately conscious of a representation of outer sense as such a one, i.e. not through an inference from the representation as effect to something outer as cause, which, because it is valid only [hyp] as hypothesis, contains no certainty.\nBut how [is] can the consciousness of a representation of the senses [yet not] (g as) merely [as] passive determination take place and become conscious of the object of the same as external, but at the same time also of it or its appearance as persistent?\nHere it is now to be noted that every object signifies something distinguished from the representation, which however is only in the understanding, hence inner sense itself, which makes us ourselves into the object of our representations, refers (g itself to) something different from our self (as [ob] transcendental object of apperception). If we therefore did not refer the representations to something different from ourselves, they would never yield cognition of objects; for, as concerns inner sense, it consists only in the relation of the representations, whether they signify something or nothing, to the subject.\n––––––––––\nThe above proof means to say this much: were there not an outer |\nsense, i.e. a faculty of becoming conscious of something[outside] as outside us immediately (without syllogism) and, on the other hand, of ourselves in relation, then not even the representation of outer things as such would be possible in us as belonging to intuition, i.e. not even space. For inner sense can contain nothing but [the sequence] the temporal relation of our representations.\n––––––––––\nOne can indeed posit time in oneself, but not oneself in time and determine oneself therein, and yet empirical self-consciousness consists in that. In order thus to determine one's existence in time, one must intuit oneself in outer relation with something else, [in] which precisely for that reason must be considered as persistent.\nSince time cannot be perceived externally in things, [so] as it is only a determination of inner sense, we can [represent] determine ourselves in time only insofar as we stand in relation with things outside us and consider ourselves therein, and being outside us brings in an existence, [which no] insofar as it is subject to no change, i.e. being persistent.\nThe existence of a thing in time can be determined not through the relation of its representation [to] in the imagination to other representations of the same, but as of a representation of sense to that which [in A] in the objects of the same is persistent.\nS. II:\nI [am still working until] climb myself through difficult subtleties to the summit of the principles, not so much as if the healthy understanding would be able to arrive there without this detour, but in order to take away the force entirely from all sophistical subtleties that rise up against it.",["“persistent” renders beharrlich, “spontaneity” Spontaneitaet; “object” renders both obiect and Gegenstand.","“not so much as if ... would be able to arrive there” keeps the German as it stands (nicht so wohl als würde ... gelangen können), without a negation.","“healthy understanding” renders der Gesunde Verstand; “detour” Umschweif."]],"reflm:5655":["S. I:\nTrichotomy. All relation of representations through concepts has a threefold dimension: 1. the relation of one representation to consciousness, 2. of an (g other) representation to consciousness, 3. the connection of both together in one consciousness. Thereby the connection of the representations among one another first becomes possible (*connexa uni tertio sunt connexa inter se*).\n––––––––––\nI cannot say (g through inner sense): [I who am in space but] space (g or spatial relation) is in me, but time or the temporal relation is in me. On the other hand, time is in me, and I am in time. That [I as] the thinking being [through] in the representation of inner sense is to itself merely appearance means nothing further than when I say: I, in whom the temporal relation alone is to be encountered, am in time. The *continens* is at the same time *contentum*.\nThe proposition: I am to (g of) myself [an] (g as) object [of the] of a sense (g conscious), means just as much as: I cognize myself in appearance before, namely as my existence is given to me [before] for thinking. It means also as much as: I am in time, but the temporal relations are merely in me (they are not possible to be represented (g as) outside me, like those of space, although the representation of the latter is also in me).\nIt is not possible that I the temporal relation [merely] as merely intuitive in me [whereas myself] and myself (g self) nevertheless also in this time as object of intuition [think] represent, [without myself] if this consciousness concerned me as thing in itself.\nI intuit myself in time, but not in space; but time is (g a relation) in me, space however is [something] a relation outside me.\nThat I am in time, which is yet a mere relation in me, consequently the *continens* a *contentum* and I am in myself, that already indicates that [that I, when I posit myself in time, myself as] I think myself in twofold significance [posit].\nS. II:\n† Just so, when I pay attention to the representations of inner sense |\nI dissolve everything into nothing but temporal relations, and the absolute for the understanding is lacking. — [For if we except the feeling of pleasure, which however is no cognition, then is] Everything (g is) in us representation, [in (the] posited in temporal relations, and if we ask what it then represents, then [we have] it is either [outer things] the outer [in space], of which we have just seen that it refers to nothing but spatial relations, for which the thing in itself is uncognizable for us, or the inner relation of these representations in time to one another, where the pure synthesis which the concepts of the understanding express is again nothing other than connection of these representations [is now] with respect to the unity of time, where the feeling of pleasure and the faculty of desire (g connected with it) yield only the mere relation of those representations to the subject without cognition or to the object through the determination of the causality of the subject, hence also no cognition of the thing in itself, and of this nothing remains but the idea of something [which] that (g my) self-consciousness independent of all these temporal conditions [my] indicates as an object [determined by its nature and kno], but furnishes nothing [as a G] as to what it would be cognizable as in itself and without relation to the causality of my self in the world of sense.\nThat which [in empirical consc] speaks decisively for the (g empirical) consciousness of myself as an appearance, not as the thing itself, is the mere manner of determination of my existence in this consciousness. Time is [as] as a sum total of relations in me [and nevertheless I am] (not of the relations outside me) [and yet I say I am in time], i.e. I must presuppose my existence (g time is determination of my existence) in order to be able to think time (g as determination [of the same] of this my existence and of that of all things outside me). Nevertheless I yet also say: I am in time, i.e. (g I am determination of time) I must presuppose time in order to be able to [cognize] determine (g it through) my existence (empirically). Were my existence here now (g to be) understood in the same significance, then there would be herein |\na contradiction. Therefore my existence, which I presuppose, must be taken in another significance than just the same, when I consider it only as determination of time. Now, however, before all determination it is merely the existence of a thing, but nevertheless, although not determined in time, yet as existence (in itself) thoroughly determined, although uncognized by me as such; therefore it is, insofar as time must be presupposed in order to determine me and my existence, mere appearance. Nothing is taken from the experiential cognition of myself by this, only this is not extended to the whole [cog], although to the whole merely possible cognition, and so the supersensible is left over, but at the same time every attempt to determine it theoretically is declared extravagant.\nThe first signifies: all things outside me are appearances, for the [subjective] condition [them] of determining their existence is in me. — The second: I myself am appearance, and time, which is merely in me, can serve as condition only to myself, insofar as I distinguish my pure I from it.",["*connexa uni tertio sunt connexa inter se*: “things connected with one third thing are connected among themselves”; *continens*, *contentum*: “that which contains”, “that which is contained”.","“I am to (g of) myself ... (g conscious)” keeps both stages: first ich bin mir selbst ein Gegenstand des Sinnes, revised to ich bin mir meiner selbst als Gegenstand eines Sinnes bewust; the vor in “in appearance before” is doubtful as it stands.","“consider it” renders sie (feminine), which agrees with no noun nearby; “extravagant” renders überschwenglich."]],"reflm:5656":["1788).\nRemark of Kant's on the address side of the letter from Ch. Fr. Heilsberg of 23 Nov. 1788 (2 X 554) in the III Dorpat volume of letters, sheet 11':\nThat an object corresponds to the categories (g of magnitude) can be shown only in a sensible intuition. If one departs from this even in only a single case, then the whole beautiful [dep] precision of the system, since one also has insight into the ground whence all cognition *a priori* comes, is lost.\nConcepts of number are those which first determine the concept of time as a *quantum*. They do not presuppose the concept of time, but only the sensible form of the same, and first determine the concept of time as a *quantum*. Thereby, however, neither an object in time (such as space, whose parts are all at the same time), nor my [e] own existence in time is determined, but merely the syn|thetic\nunity of the manifold, whereby a magnitude is possible, is given, thus reality is procured for the concept of magnitude without thereby touching any quality of it (because it can be given altogether only in time).\nImputation is the estimation of an action (of the free one according to laws) with respect to its origin from freedom. This origin, however, can be [through the] thought only insofar as it is under moral laws, for that is causality from freedom.",["“as a *quantum*”: the Latin is in the genitive, als eines quanti.","“estimation” renders Beurtheilung; “has insight into” renders einsieht."]],"reflm:5657":["S. I:\nNB. Of transcendent ideas. I cannot even lay the categories under the concepts of the original being and in general of the supersensible. But when I call it cause, I understand thereby only a being from whose concept I could derive [the or] my acquaintance with the order of the world (the physical and moral) just as I derive these from things in the world which I know through their effects. Thereby I now properly attribute nothing to this being, but only add in thought a principle unknown to me for my theoretical, chiefly practical use of reason as of a being in the world wherein I am to consider and act, and indeed in conformity with a determination not restricted merely to the sensible. The taking to be true of my way of thinking in conformity with this principle, namely that it also harmonizes with the world in wider [interconnection] domain and duration than I may have insight into, is the belief in such a being. The theoretical determination of the same consists of nothing but words which, apart from the relation of such a concept as principle of the practical (where it has an absolutely necessary interest), have no significance.",["“acquaintance” renders Kenntnis, “know” kenne; “these” renders diese, which may refer to the acquaintance or to the effects.","“add in thought” renders denke ... zu; “determination” renders Bestimmung, which can also be read “vocation”.","“original being” renders Urwesen; “way of thinking” Denkungsart."]],"reflm:5658":["Meiners: Historia doctrinae de vero deo omnium rerum auctove atque rectore (Lemgo. 1780) Pars I p. 63\nUpper margin above the beginning of Sectio II, which treats of the Phoenicians:\nIf the concept of God precedes morals, then it is framed very immorally.\nThe Trinity proves an ancient moral concept.",["The last sentence is Latin: *Trinitas probat conceptum antiquum moralem*.","“morals” renders die Moral."]],"reflm:5659":["In the same place, I 131, in the sentence: The Indians »*tam bonos quam malos genios vel Deos vel Debos vel etiam Dewtas romano verbo nominare solent ... loca porro taetra et formidolosa, in quibus scelesti quondam et nefarii homines ob impia facta omni tormentorum genere cruciantur, Gehennae nomine indigitant*« Kant underlined in lead the words spaced by me and set two exclamation marks in the margin beside the last word, beside the words »*romano verbo*«:\ncattle!",["The quotation from Meiners is left in Latin, since the editor's remarks refer to its words: “are accustomed to call good as well as evil genii either Gods or Debos or even, with a Roman word, Dewtas ... further, the foul and dreadful places in which once wicked and nefarious human beings are tormented with every kind of torment for impious deeds, they designate by the name of Gehenna”.","“cattle!” renders Kant's Latin *pecus*!; the spacing that marked the underlined words is not reproduced."]],"reflm:5660":["In the same place, II 263, in the right margin beside lines 7-8 from the top, which treat of the Ionian natural philosophers:\nThales physicist, Pherecydes theologian.",["Latin: *Physicus*, *Theologus*."]],"reflm:5661":["-Sch. XI 1 S. 261-3. Hb. IV S. 499f. Ki. L S. 193f.\nAnswer to the question, is it an experience that we think?\nAn empirical representation of which I am conscious is perception; that which I think in addition to the representation of the imagination by means of the apprehension and comprehension (*comprehensio aesthetica*) of the manifold of perception is the empirical cognition of the object, and the judgment which expresses an empirical cognition is experience.\nIf I think a square to myself *a priori*, then I cannot say |\nthis thought is experience; but this can well be said if I apprehend an already drawn figure in perception, and think the comprehension of its manifold by means of the imagination under the concept of a square. In experience and through it I am taught by means of the senses; but if I think an object of the senses to myself merely arbitrarily, then I am not taught by it and in my representation depend in nothing on the object, but am entirely the author of it.\nBut also the consciousness of having such a thought is no experience; precisely because the thought is no experience, but consciousness in itself is nothing empirical. Nevertheless this thought brings forth an object of experience or a determination of the mind which can be observed, insofar namely as it is affected by the faculty of thinking; I can therefore say I have experienced what belongs to grasping in thought a figure of four equal sides and right angles in such a way that I can demonstrate its properties from it. This is the empirical consciousness of the determination of my state in time through thinking; thinking itself, although it also happens in time, takes no account at all of time when the properties of a figure are to be thought. But experience is impossible without combining time-determination with it, because in it I am passive and feel myself affected according to the formal condition of inner sense.\nThe consciousness, when I undertake an experience, is representation of my existence insofar as it is empirically determined, i.e. in time. Now if this consciousness were in turn itself empirical, then the same time-determination would in turn have to be represented as contained under the conditions of the time-determination of my state. There would therefore have to be thought yet another time, under which (not in which) the time that constitutes the formal condition of my inner experience would be contained. Thus there would be a time in which and with which simultaneously a given time elapsed, which is absurd. But the consciousness of undertaking an experience, or also of thinking in general, is a transcendental consciousness, not experience.\nRemarks on this essay.\nThe action of the imagination of giving an intuition to a concept is *exhibitio*. The action of the imagination of making a concept out of an empirical intuition is *comprehensio*.\nApprehension of the imagination, *apprehensio aesthetica*. Comprehension of the same, *comprehensio aesthetica* (aesthetic comprehending), I comprehend the manifold together into a whole representation and so it gets a certain form.",["The first line is the editors' list of earlier printings (Schubert, Hartenstein, Kirchmann), kept as it stands.","Auffassung = apprehension; Zusammenfassung / zusammenfassen = comprehension / comprehend, as is Begreifen; Kant glosses both with comprehensio aesthetica.","Object and Gegenstand are both \"object\"; Dasein = existence; willkührlich = arbitrarily."]],"reflm:5662":["-Sch. XI 1 S. 263-5. Hb. IV S. 500-2. Ki. L S. 194-6.\nOn miracles.\nNeither through a miracle nor through a spiritual being in the world can a motion be brought forth without effecting just as much motion in the opposite direction, consequently according to laws of the action and reaction of matter, for otherwise a motion of the *universum* in empty space would arise.\nBut also no alteration in the world (thus no beginning of that motion) can arise without being determined through causes in the world according to laws of nature in general, thus not through freedom or miracles proper; for since it is not time that determines the order of occurrences, but conversely the occurrences, i.e. the appearances according to the law of nature (of causality), determine time, an occurrence which happened in time independently of this, or were determined, would presuppose a change in empty time, consequently the world itself would be determined in absolute time as to its state.\nRemarks.\nl. One can divide miracles into outer and inner, i.e. into alterations of appearance for outer sense and into those for inner sense. The former happen in space, the latter in time. If miracles in space were possible, then it would be possible that appearances happen in which action and reaction are not equally great. For all alterations in space are motion. But a motion which is supposed to be brought forth through a miracle, its cause is not supposed to be sought in the appearances. But the law of action and |\nreaction rests on this, that cause and effect belong to the world of sense (to the appearances), i.e. are represented in relative space; now since this does not hold of the cause in the case of miracles in space, they will also not stand under the law of action and reaction. Now if a motion is effected through a miracle, then, since it does not stand under the law of action and reaction, the *centrum gravitatis* of the world will be altered through it, i.e. in other words, the world would move in empty space; but a motion in empty space is a contradiction, for it would namely be the relation of a thing to a nothing, for empty space is a mere idea.\nIn a similar way it is proved that there can be no miracles with respect to appearances in time. For an appearance in time is a miracle if its cause cannot be given in time, does not stand under its conditions. But since only through this, that both cause and effect belong to the appearances, can the latter be determined in relative time, this will not be able to happen with an effect which is brought forth through a miracle, because its cause does not belong to the appearances. Thus a supernatural occurrence will be determined not in the relative, but in the absolute (empty) time. But a determination in empty time is a contradiction, because for each relation two *correlata* must be given.\n2. Miracle is an occurrence whose ground is not to be found in nature. It is either *miraculum rigorosum*, which has its ground in a thing outside the world (thus not in nature); or *miraculum comparativum*, which indeed has its ground in a nature, but in one whose laws we do not know; of the latter kind are the things which one ascribes to spirits. *Miraculum rigorosum* is either *materiale*, where the force which brought forth the miracle is also outside the world, or *formale*, where the force is indeed in the world, but its determination is found outside the world, e.g. if one took the drying up of the Red Sea at the passage of the children of Israel for a miracle, then it is a *miraculum materiale* if one passes it off as an immediate effect of the deity; on the other hand |\na *miraculum formale* if one lets it dry up through a wind which, however, was sent by the deity.\nFurther, the *miraculum* is either *occasionale* or *praestabilitum*. In the first case one assumes that the deity has stepped in immediately; but in the other one lets the occurrence be brought forth through a series of causes and effects which are all there for the sake of this single occurrence. —",["The first line is the editors' list of earlier printings, kept as it stands; \"l.\" at the first remark stands so in the text, for 1.","Wirkung und Gegenwirkung = action and reaction; Wirkung elsewhere = effect; Kraft = force.","\"or were determined\": oder bestimmt wäre, a second subjunctive alongside geschähe, kept as loose as in the German."]],"reflm:5663":["-Sch. XI 1 S. 271f. Hb. IV S. 507. Ki. L S. 202.\n    On formal and material significance of some words.\nThere are several words which, used in the *singulari*, have another sense than when one uses them in the *plurali*; they are then to be taken in the *singulari* in formal, in the *plurali* in material significance: these are unity, perfection, truth, possibility*. Unity used in the *singulari* is qualitative, used in the *plurali* quantitative. Qualitative unity is to be considered as the ground of the whole, quantitative as a part of the whole. Thus one cannot say e.g. that warmth consists of lukewarmnesses; one thus determines its magnitude not according to the parts which it contains, but according to the effects which it brings forth, e.g. that it extends bodies, and one can therefore attribute to it not a proper magnitude, but a degree; the unity which is found in it is thus qualitative unity. — The unities of which discrete magnitudes (numbers) consist are quantitative unities.\nPerfection (used *formaliter*) of a thing is the agreement of its realities to an idea; perfections (used *materialiter*) are these realities**.\nTruth in the *singulari* (used *formaliter* and *qualitative*) is the agreement of our cognition of an object with it; truths in the *plurali* (used *materialiter* and *quantitative*) are true propositions.\nPossibility of an object (used *formaliter* and *qualitative*); possibilities (used *materialiter* and *quantitative*), objects insofar as they are possible.\n*(g One sees that this is grounded on the titles of the categories: quantity, quality, relation and modality.)\n**(g Thus one speaks of the perfection of a clock insofar as there is found in it what one can expect of a good clock. Perfections of a clock are properties of it which agree with the concept of a good clock. — But one must also still distinguish quantitative and qualitative perfection from perfection.)",["The first line is the editors' list of earlier printings, kept as it stands.","Bedeutung = significance; Sinn = sense; Lauigkeiten = lukewarmnesses.","qualitative and quantitative after formaliter and materialiter are read as Latin adverbs."]],"reflm:5664":["Science is a whole 1. as *aggregate*; 2. as series. Science of the first principles.\n( s subord: *utrinque* terminated  coordinated *in infinitum* )",["principien = principles; utrinqve = on both sides; subord: abbreviates subordinated."]],"reflm:5665":["Logic treats of thinking without *object*. Physics of the cognition of [obj] things from experience. Metaphysics of their cognition prior to all experience. The origin is twofold: 1. how we have come to it .. psychology; 2. how the cognitions *a priori* are possible. transcendental philosophy.",["[Geg] is a broken-off word, presumably Gegenstände (objects)."]],"reflm:5666":["What precedes goes before either according to kind: principles *a priori*, or according to origin, i.e. historically.",["Art = kind; principien = principles."]],"reflm:5667":["Metaphysics is the system [of the principles] of all cognition *a priori* (g from concepts) in general.\nThe science of the possibility, domain etc etc. of cognition *a priori* is transcendental philosophy. Sum total of metaphysics. To draw transcendental philosophy out of the pure reason that is at hand, and boundaries: is critique of pure reason.",["Umfang = domain; Inbegrif = sum total; Grenzen = boundaries.","\"and boundaries\": und Grenzen, syntactically loose; it may be a second object of ziehen (draw the boundaries)."]],"reflm:5668":["M I:\nSome cognitions are *secundum quid a priori*, others *simpliciter*, where there is nothing empirical.\nM II:\nThe distinguishing mark of cognition *a priori* is necessity.",["Kennzeichen = distinguishing mark (not Merkmal)."]],"reflm:5669":["In cognition one can look to connection with grounds and consequences and to the generation.",["Folgen = consequences; Verknüpfung = connection; Erzeugung = generation."]],"reflm:5670":["Every cognition which is *respective a priori* is called principle.",["princip = principle; respective = relatively, a Latin adverb."]],"reflm:5671":["All our cognitions are: 1. *a priori* or *posteriori* connected with others; or 2. absolutely *a priori*, which cannot be drawn from experience (properly not from the senses), or absolutely *a posteriori*.",["\"connected\": connex, Kant's Latinate word; schlechthin = absolutely."]],"reflm:5672":["All cognition has its origin either *a posteriori*: from experience, or *a priori*. Metaphysics is a science of the cognitions *a priori*.\nThe *principia* of philosophy are either *a posteriori*: [physiol:] physical, or *priori*: metaphysical.",["[physiol:] abbreviates physiologisch (physiological)."]],"reflm:5673":["Our cognition consists for the understanding in an interconnection of grounds and consequences, the derived and the original.\nWe distinguish cognition:\n1. according to the connection,\n2. according to its kind, origin.\nTwofold connection: subordination and coordination; in the latter there are no boundaries.",["\"for the understanding\": vor dem Verstande; Kant's vor often stands for für, but \"before the understanding\" is also possible.","Zusammenhang = interconnection; Verknüpfung = connection; Grenzen = boundaries."]],"reflm:5674":["Metaphysics is science of the principles of all cognition *a priori* and of all cognition which follows from these principles. Mathematics contains such *principia*, but is not science of the possibility of these principles.",["Principien and principia are both \"principles\"; the Latin form is kept where Kant uses it."]],"reflm:5675":["Metaphysics can be not the foundation, but surely the bulwark of religion, and indeed as such it is indispensable. For the opponent possesses a dialectical metaphysics, to which we must oppose the critical one; and this opponent lies in every natural human reason.",["Grundveste = foundation; Schutzwehr = bulwark."]],"reflm:5676":["From faintheartedness one becomes an empiricist, the other a mystic; the first asserts that raw understanding better than cultivated judgments.",["The verb of the last clause is missing in the German, and is not supplied."]],"reflm:5677":["One can either attain to science in metaphysics, or not: skeptical. Reason can be studied through completely.",[]],"reflm:5678":["The two cardinal propositions: God and a future world.",[]],"reflm:5679":["Metaphysics treats either of objects of pure reason or of objects of experience through pure reason, not according to empirical, but rational principles.",[]],"reflm:5680":["1. Of principles of pure reason: transcendental philosophy 2. *Metaphysica applicata* to *objects*: nature and freedom.",[]],"reflm:5681":["In all philosophy the properly philosophical is the metaphysics of the science. All sciences in which reason is used have their metaphysics.",[]],"reflm:5682":["Instead of the distinction of *synthesis* and *analysis*: *synthesis regressiva* and *progressiva*.",[]],"reflm:5683":["The mere possibility of the proposition that a God is, is sufficient for religion, and this possibility is apodictically certain.",["das ein Gott sey is read as daß ein Gott sey; hinreichend = sufficient."]],"reflm:5684":["Whence is the supreme analytic principle expressed negatively?\nThe *principium summum* is expressed negatively in order to designate necessity. Principle of negative judgments.",["Grundsatz and Princip are both \"principle\"."]],"reflm:5685":["In and \nThe principle of the determination of the undetermined: Every thing is in itself thoroughly determined, but not always through its concept; the latter is: *ens realissimum*.",["Grundsatz = principle; durchgängig = thoroughly."]],"reflm:5686":["*Principium Contradictionis* is the principle of all logical possibility, i.e. of concepts, insofar as such can be cognized *a priori*, but not of things.",["\"such can be\": solche ... kan, a plural pronoun with a singular verb, as in the German."]],"reflm:5687":["The possibility of analytic combination can be seen into *a priori*, but not that of synthetic.",["Verbindung = combination; einsehen = see into, have insight into."]],"reflm:5688":["The possibility of a concept rests on this, that it does not contradict itself; the possibility of a thing, that the concept have *objective* reality, that an example of it can be given, i.e. that an *object* correspond to it, e.g. *ens absolute necessarium*.",["*ens absolute necessarium*: absolutely necessary being. *objective*, *object* render obiective, obiect."]],"reflm:5689":["Dogmatic metaphysics is that which proceeds without critical investigation of the main question: how is synthetic cognition *a priori* possible? So it is also with *history* without critique.",["\"history\": historie."]],"reflm:5690":["Everything impossible through itself is also [hypothetically] absolutely impossible; not everything possible in itself is absolutely possible, but only under some hypothesis.",["Latin throughout: Omne per se impossibile est etiam [hypothetice] absolute impossibile; non omne in se possibile est absolute possibile, sed tantum sub hypothesi aliqua."]],"reflm:5691":["Possibility is either inner or outer. The latter is either *restrictive* outer possibility under certain conditions or *absolute* in every respect.",["\"in every respect\": in aller Absicht."]],"reflm:5692":["»Only under a condition« or: »also possible under it«, is *opposed* to the inner: either under a *restrictive* condition |\n(g as sequel) *hypothetice* or [in nexu] under an *extensive* one as ground. Finally absolutely possible without any *restriction*, and the greatest *extensive* possibility.",["*hypothetice*: hypothetically; the deleted *in nexu*: in connection.","\"sequel\": Folge; \"is opposed\": wird ... opponirt."]],"reflm:5693":["Hypothetical possibility can be considered *restrictively* and also *extensively*. The latter, if it is complete, is absolute possibility. For in the more respects and outer relations something is possible, the more it approaches absolute possibility.",["\"respects\": Absicht; \"relations\": Beziehung."]],"reflm:5694":["What contradicts itself is impossible in every respect, i.e. in itself nothing, because it is internally impossible. but this does not hold conversely; what, considered in every respect, is not possible, is impossible in itself.\nAbsolutely impossible is something other than in every respect. The former refers to *restrictive*, the latter to *extensive* conditions.",["The clause after the semicolon states the converse that does not hold; the German gives no sign of this beyond the semicolon.","\"Absolutely\": Schlechterdings; \"in every respect\": in aller Absicht."]],"reflm:5695":["* Possible *in thesi* (s according to the concept: logically), but not *in hypothesi*. (s no real possibility.)\n*(s »in hypothesi« is extensive, »sub hypothesi« restrictive.)",["*in thesi*: in the thesis, i.e. considered by itself; *in hypothesi*: in a hypothesis; *sub hypothesi*: under a hypothesis."]],"reflm:5696":["In general possible *in thesi*, or universally *in hypothesi*; this is wider.",["\"this\": diese, feminine, presumably the possibility *in hypothesi*."]],"reflm:5697":["At the very top of the page:\nIt is remarkable that the categories of *quantity* and *quality* have no *opposita* and *correlata*, as do those of *relation* and *modality*. (although those of *quality* contain an *oppositum* as gradation, namely *negation*.) The ground of this seems to be this, that the first merely contain the *apprehension* of intuition and produce it synthetically, but the second are concepts of relation either of the *objects* among one another or to the faculty of cognition.",["\"concepts of relation\": Verhaltnis-Begriffe, whereas the category name is the Latinate relation.","\"the first\" / \"the second\": die ersten / die zweyten, i.e. the first two and the second two categories.","The opening line is the editor's note on the place of the Reflexion."]],"reflm:5698":["Above and \nSomething is hypothetically possible either *sub conditione restrictiva* or *constitutiva*. The first is the condition of agreement, which must not be conflicted with; the second the condition of derivation. What is impossible under no *conditione restrictiva* is absolutely possible, i.e. possible under every *hypothesi*.",["*sub conditione restrictiva* / *constitutiva*: under a restrictive / constitutive condition.","\"agreement\": Einstimmung; \"must not be conflicted with\": nicht wiederstritten werden muß."]],"reflm:5699":["The *principium rationis* holds only of experience; for we can have no concept of a real ground except only through experience, namely where, when something is posited, something else follows according to a rule (consequently is determined). Therefore we also cannot say anything from mere concepts about the relation of things to real grounds.",["*principium rationis*: principle of (sufficient) ground. \"relation\": Beziehung."]],"reflm:5700":["Every thing (s existing) is in itself thoroughly determined, but through its concept undetermined with regard to many possible *predicates*. Only a single thing is thoroughly determined through its concept: *ens realissimum*.",["\"determined\" / \"undetermined\": determinirt / undeterminirt; \"thoroughly\": durchgängig.","*ens realissimum*: most real being."]],"reflm:5701":["The sequel does not determine the ground, but indeed the ground the sequel.\nAll *predicates* are determinations of an object in general.\nA logical predicate can be analytic;\ndetermination is a synthetic predicate.",["\"sequel\": Folge.","Latin in the source: Praedicatum logicum (kan analytisch seyn); determinatio est praedicatum syntheticum."]],"reflm:5702":["My concept determines a thing or is determined in a certain way; synthetically no thing is determined through its concept, therefore only in relation to another cognition, e.g. intuition or experience.",["\"determine\": determiniren throughout, not bestimmen."]],"reflm:5703":["All determination is *synthesis*.",[]],"reflm:5704":["That which being posited another is posited: by it this is not on that account determined. If it is determined by that, that is the ground.\nTo determine logically means either to affirm or to negate a *predicate* of a thing (copula in a judgment) without regard to the content; to determine metaphysically means to attribute to a concept a *predicate* which in itself contains a being or excludes such a one. |\nWhat in itself excludes a being is *negation*, and only in the opposite can the real be encountered.",["The first two sentences are Latin: Qvo posito ponitur aliud, illo hoc non ideo determinatur. Si illo determinatur, illud est ratio.","\"a being\": ein Seyn (the verbal noun), not Wesen.","\"to determine\": bestimmen."]],"reflm:5705":["The first inner ground of that which belongs to the possibility of a thing is essence; of that which belongs to actuality: nature.",["\"essence\": Wesen; \"actuality\": Wirklichkeit."]],"reflm:5706":["The first logical ground of all [determinations which the] *predicates* of a thing is the logical essence,\nthe first (g inner) real ground of everything that belongs to the existence of the thing, nature.\nSubsequently (in phase ψ3) deletions were made and words added, so that the first sentence now has the following form: |\nThe first inner ground of everything that belongs necessarily to the concept of a thing is the logical essence. |\nIn addition, above the first two words of the Rfl. was added: |\nlogical, identity, |\nand at the end: |\nThe analytically first ground is the logical, the synthetically first ground the real essence. Nature. On the complaint that the essence of things remains hidden from us.",["The third, fifth and seventh lines are the editor's report on Kant's later revisions, not Kant's text.","\"essence\": Wesen; \"real essence\": realwesen; \"existence\": Daseyn."]],"reflm:5707":["*Subordination* is analytic or synthetic, the latter carried up to the first inner real ground: essence.",["\"essence\": Wesen."]],"reflm:5708":["On the rule in general.\nIt is the *objective* unity of the consciousness of the manifold of representations (consequently that which holds universally). The rule is either empirical, if the condition of unity lies in mere perceptions. It can therefore not be *objective* otherwise than in relation to possible experience as cognition of the *objects* of perception. The possibility of experience is therefore the ground of the [necessity] *objective* |\nvalidity of the rules of perception, and this possibility of experience is grounded on the necessary unity of the consciousness of representations, insofar as cognition (of the *objects*) is to come from it. All representations must be represented in relation to one consciousness and therefore as universally subject to the [cond] unity of consciousness (of this relation to consciousness we are not always conscious, and then the representation is obscure, but nevertheless always compared with this consciousness).",["\"either\" (entweder) has no matching \"or\" in the passage.","\"relation\": Beziehung (to possible experience), Verhaltnis (to one consciousness); \"obscure\": dunkel.","\"[cond]\" renders the deleted \"[Bed]\", presumably the start of Bedingung."]],"reflm:5709":["On the existence\nof outer things.\nIdealism is the opinion that we experience only our own existence immediately, but only infer that of outer things (which inference from effect to cause is in fact uncertain). But we can experience our own existence only insofar as we determine it in time, to which the persistent belongs, which representation has no object in us. [If he himself] On the mere imagination of a persistent outside us [Gr] this representation also cannot be grounded, for an imagination is impossible to which no corresponding object can be given. It is that which gives the object in intuition, and our representation, insofar as it belongs merely to the consciousness of ourselves, has no such object.",["\"imagination\": Einbildung, not Einbildungskraft; \"the persistent\": das Beharrliche.","\"It\" (Sie) may refer to the imagination or to the representation.","\"[Gr]\" is presumably the start of Gründen, written again after it."]],"reflm:5710":["Everything that exists is thoroughly determined; but this thoroughgoing determination does not make up the concept of existence, but rather that a thing is posited absolutely and not merely in relation to its concept.\nI cognize existence through experience, but not the thoroughgoing determination; this happens through reason.",["\"determined\" / \"determination\": determinirt / determination; \"existence\": Existenz, existentz."]],"reflm:5711":["The proposition: »a thing A is actual« is synthetic.",[]],"reflm:5712":["*Possibile* etc etc. are not synthetic *predicates*.\nSomething is either merely thought (*a priori*) or given (also to be represented *a posteriori*) or given through this, that something in general is merely thought (or that it itself is merely thought): necessary (hypothetically or absolutely).",["*Possibile*: the possible."]],"reflm:5713":["Everything that exists is thoroughly determined, but not conversely.",["\"thoroughly determined\": durchgängig bestimt."]],"reflm:5714":["Being is then taken only problematically when it is supposed to consider judgments in the relation alone; but assertorically always, when it is considered in itself. But if the problematic *position* is at the same time analytic, then it does not differ from the absolute one and is apodictic.",["\"when it is supposed to consider judgments\": wenn es Urtheile ... betrachten soll; the subject es is doubtful, and the clause may mean \"when one is to consider judgments\".","\"position\": position, i.e. positing (Setzung)."]],"reflm:5715":["God contains *attributes* from each of which all the rest can be inferred. Such a thing cannot belong under a species, but is possible only as a single one: *conceptus singularis*.",["\"species\": Art; *conceptus singularis*: singular concept."]],"reflm:5716":["Beside and \nIn existence there is more than in possibility, but not in the existing thing.\nexistence is not determination of the thing, but of the understanding through the *object*.",["\"existence\": Existenz, then existentz."]],"reflm:5717":["That between possibility and actuality in the things in themselves there is a difference, namely where something is possible (g *omni respectu* and in |\nits thoroughgoing determination) although it is not actual, we cannot have insight into, for the reason that we would have to cognize the thoroughgoing determination of actual things.\nThat whose opposite would be impossible in its thoroughgoing determination would be absolutely necessary, i.e. *absolute*, not *respectively* necessary with regard to a concept. In such a way perhaps everything would be absolutely necessary, although, considered merely *respectively*, contingent (in a certain *respectu*).",["*omni respectu*: in every respect; \"respectively\": respectiv, i.e. relatively.","\"have insight into\": einsehen; \"determination\": determination, Bestimung."]],"reflm:5718":["The cause of our distinction of the possible from the actual is this: because we do not cognize things through intuitions of the understanding, therefore consider them first *respectively* to our faculty of thinking, afterwards to intuition.",["\"intuitions of the understanding\": Verstandesanschauungen; \"faculty of thinking\": Denkungsvermögen."]],"reflm:5719":["Something is actual for the reason that it is possible, because, if it is possible only as *rationatum*, if the ground is actual, it must also be actual. But nothing is actual for the reason that it is merely possible, although everything that is possible may be actual. For possibility is a mere relation to our understanding, actuality either a connection with our fundamental experience space and time, which contain an original actuality, or a connection with an infinite understanding; here, however, an existence is always presupposed. But that we can cancel every being (of the real) proves not the possibility of non-being, but the dependence of our concept of being on the connection with experience.",["*rationatum*: what is grounded.","\"connection\": Verknüpfung; \"fundamental experience\": Grunderfahrung; \"being\" / \"non-being\": Seyn / Nichtseyn."]],"reflm:5720":["The possibility of the thing is distinct from the possibility of existence, and the possibility of the [non] opposite of the thing from the possibility of non-being; e.g. *possibilitas oppositi* in the finite, which for that reason does not prove contingency.",["*possibilitas oppositi*: possibility of the opposite. \"existence\": Daseyn."]],"reflm:5721":["How can the possibility of a thing (not merely of the concept) be cognized *a priori*. A triangle is possible from three sides, two of which together are greater than the third. A circle is possible. I can give them in pure intuition. What can be given in experience in general, therefore is in accordance with the categories, is *objectively* possible; but the opposite is not on that account impossible.",[]],"reflm:5722":["We can assume no *object* as possible except that which we can exhibit in intuition, consequently set forth its actuality; otherwise, if the representation does not contradict itself, the thought is indeed possible, but not the thing.\nThe logical (g inner) possibility (g according to the principle of contradiction) without real (g to which no intuition corresponds): empty concept.\nImpossibility we can cognize only according to the principle of contradiction when the talk is of *objects* to which no intuition can be given.",["\"exhibit\" / \"set forth\": darstellen / darlegen; \"thing\" here renders Sache, not Ding.","\"principle of contradiction\": Satz des Wiederspruchs."]],"reflm:5723":["The distinction between possibility and actuality we posit in the connection with space and time, which we regard as necessary in themselves, consequently as the foundations of all actuality. Now if we consider things merely according to the form of space and time, but not connected with it, then they are merely possible. This distinction must therefore fall away when the talk is of the thing in itself. The second distinction of the two concepts is merely logical: namely the undetermined is merely possible; in thoroughgoing determination only that is possible which is actual. For the former contains a mere relation of the object to the understanding, the latter with my existence. What is possible in thoroughgoing determination is, if it is possible as ground in every respect (consequently as *independens*), necessary. If it is possible only as sequel, then, if the possibility is thoroughgoing, there must be a ground for it. The concept of thoroughgoing determination is a relation to omniscience.",["\"with it\": damit, singular, after \"space and time\".","\"sequel\": Folge; \"in every respect\": in Aller Absicht; *independens*: independent.","\"relation\": Verhaltnis; \"existence\": Daseyn."]],"reflm:5724":["*non ens* [is ent]: non-thing;\nImpossible, empty\nconcept. or *ens rationis*: thought-entity*.\n[",["*non ens*: non-being; \"non-thing\": Unding; \"thought-entity\": Gedankending.","The asterisk has no note in the passage, and the final bracket is unclosed; the passage appears to break off."]],"reflm:5725":["an impossible, an empty, a negative concept; a mere form of representing things, which is not a thing itself: *ens imaginarium*; that whose concept itself is impossible is logically impossible, A non A =0: no concept. [If] The object that is impossible although its concept is possible is A × 0: *nihil privativum*, *non ens*.",["*ens imaginarium*: imaginary being; *nihil privativum*: privative nothing; *non ens*: non-being."]],"reflm:5726":["17. E II 639. 597.\nSpace and time.\nFrom the beginning according to the distinction of analytic and synthetic cognition and the principle of contradiction. Every object of thinking is either something or nothing. Either insofar as no thought corresponds to the object, i.e., the thinking itself is nothing, i.e., contradicts itself, or no object ((g none) in intuition) corresponds to the thought (which does not contradict itself). Thinking, considered subjectively as representation, before it is analyzed, always has an object; but if thinking contradicts itself, then [it] the thought and therefore the object is nothing, and both are struck out. Where the thought remains, there the object is, after the *analysis*, problematic.\nSomething can now be either [posited] merely posited or repeatedly (*iterative*) posited, in order to bring about the representation of the object; in the latter case it is plurality, in the [second] first [unity] one. All plurality is thus homogeneous, and the repeated positing is addition. [This] The object whose representation arises through the plurality of given is a *quantum*; and the representation of it as an object that contains much in itself is that of magnitude. In all magnitude there is composition. That [which] out of which something is composed is called unity and is comparatively (i.e., out of such unity) not composite, hence simple. Thus unities are relatively simple, but in themselves they can in turn be composite, i.e., be magnitudes.\nAn object of a certain kind is uniform in the same kind, e.g., space. As plurality, however, the unity can still be heterogeneous with the *quantum*, and the unity can be a *quantum* that is in turn composed of unities of an entirely different kind. Accordingly not every plurality is a magnitude, |\nM 17: |\nbut that which is homogeneous with the object in which the magnitude is considered. What multitude, what counting, what number, what infinite magnitude is: given (is contradictory) or only infinite progression, through which the magnitude is only negatively determined. Space and time are *quanta a priori*. *Quantum continuum et discretum*. Measure. The measure of magnitude in itself is totality, of the comparative, given unity (in intuition). We know only comparative magnitudes. Boundaries: space and time alone have them; magnitudes of pure thinking: limitations. [That which] in thinking there are limitations, in intuition boundaries.\nWhen the following category is at an end, it must be considered in combination with the preceding one. After the categories immediately the concepts of comparison. identity and diversity; similarity, equality, con|gruence.\n*oppositum et contrarium*. and after that the categories themselves compared. Of division in general.",["“unity” renders Einheit throughout, which can also mean “unit”; “plurality” Vielheit, “multitude” Menge, “addition” Hinzuthuung.","“the plurality of given”: Vielheit gegebener, with no noun expressed; “after the *analysis*”: nach der analysi, “after” or “according to”.","“boundaries” renders Grenzen, “limitations” Schranken; “the comparative, given unity”: der Comparativen gegebene Einheit, the construction is unclear."]],"reflm:5727":["(g The) magnitude [is] of a thing is [the] the unity that can be generated through the mere repetition of one and the same. The repeated positing of just the same is the multitude; what thus has a magnitude (*quantum*) contains a multitude [deren E]. Magnitude is the united multitude or unity that is the same as a given multitude. The determinate concept [of a] of the magnitude of a thing rests on the unity's being given. All continuous magnitudes can be cognized as to their magnitude only relatively. Divided magnitude can be cognized absolutely. namely through absolute unity and number.\nWe can construct the concept of magnitude, i.e., exhibit it *a priori* in intuition. But we can make ourselves a concept of a magnitude only *in concreto* if we bring about the [Begr] empirical concept according to the rule of construction, e.g., by measuring and counting and thus becoming conscious that we could also have thought and determined such a magnitude *a priori*.\nDynamical magnitude (ground), mathematical (magnitude).",["[deren E] (“whose u…”) and [Begr] are deleted, unfinished words, left as they stand.","“multitude” renders Menge; “unity” Einheit, which can also mean “unit”."]],"reflm:5728":["All magnitude is composite because it is divisible, and not conversely; the [compositions] parts are not prior to the continuous magnitude.",[]],"reflm:5729":["How large something is can be cognized only relatively. Absolute unity there is none except the *ens realissimum*; thus the unity is not always that which, repeated several times, makes up the magnitude; for the *ens realissimum* is unity, but cannot be posited repeatedly.",[]],"reflm:5730":["The inner ground of distinction of things, insofar as they are [not] considered as |\n*quanta*, is the quantity, insofar as they are not considered (intuited or judged) according to their quantity, is the quality. The figure is quality. Through it even equal spaces can still be distinguished.",["The two clauses share one sentence in the source and are kept so."]],"reflm:5731":["That something is a *quantum* one can cognize from the thing itself; but the quantity not otherwise than through something else assumed. E.g., one cannot cognize the magnitude of the earth through German miles, if one has nothing of these other than the concept of the 15th part of a degree, but has not measured the degree through rods (thus *alio assumto*).",["Ruthe (“rod”) is a unit of length; *alio assumto*: “with something else assumed”."]],"reflm:5732":["The measure of the *quantum* must always itself be a *quantum* of the same kind. For firstly it must be homogeneous, because, *aliqvoties sumtum*, it is to produce the former. Secondly also itself a *quantum*, because as *simplex* it would be heterogeneous from the *composito*. A *quantum* that as such consisted of simple parts would *continuum* ...",["*aliqvoties sumtum*: “taken some number of times”; *simplex*: “simple”; *composito*: “the composite”.","The last sentence breaks off in the source."]],"reflm:5733":["The unity of a thing (qualitative) and of a unity (quantitative, mathematical).",["einer Einheit may be read as “of a unity” or “of a unit”."]],"reflm:5734":["Above, beside and \nUnity, truth and completeness (transcendental perfec|tion)\nare the *reqvisita* of every cognition [the] respectively in relation to understanding, power of judgment and reason (for the last, apodictic certainty is required, i.e., complete truth).\nEverything derived from one.\nEverything combined in one.\nThe one derived from everything.\nUnity of the subject, of the ground and of the whole.\nPossibility\nActuality\nNecessity\nThere are three transcendental criteria of the possibility of things in general. 3-fold formal unity.",["The location note “Über, neben und” is cut off in the source.","“completeness” renders Vollständigkeit, “perfection” Vollkommenheit."]],"reflm:5735":["In and \nUnity of the rule (universality); truth in the subsumption under rules, for these alone make something objectively valid, i.e., true; finally perfection, interconnection of all predicates in one principle. The essence is the unity, the essential pieces the truth, all attributes together the perfection.",["The location note “In und” is cut off in the source.","“essential pieces”: Wesentliche Stücke; “principle” renders Princip."]],"reflm:5736":["Unity of the object and unity of the manifold in the object. The former quantitative, the latter qualitative. 1. Unity out of many, 2. unity of the many among one another, 3. many out of one.",[]],"reflm:5737":["Formal unity is philosophical, material mathematical.\nUnity of a thing\nA unity\nMany agree with one, many agree among one another, one with many.",["“many”: Vieles (singular, “much”); “agree with”: stimmt zu."]],"reflm:5738":["Determination of a thing with respect to its essence (g as thing) is transcendental.",[]],"reflm:5739":["*consensus unius ad varia, variorum inter se, variorum ad unum.*\nThe three criteria of the inner possibility of a* thing.\n*(g thought-entity, e.g., hypothesis: unum, possibility; verum, actuality; bonum, necessity.)",["The asterisks after “a” and before “(g” are Kant's reference mark, carried over; unum, verum, bonum are Latin, left unitalicized here so as not to confuse them with the mark.","Latin line: “agreement of one with various, of various among themselves, of various with one”; “thought-entity” renders Gedankending."]],"reflm:5740":["In the relation of a representation to an object in general, unity of the object must be thought through a concept. Plurality of the predicates that flow from the concept. Totality of the determinations (g as ground,) to the whole concept. totality.",["“Totality” renders Allheit, the final “totality” totalitaet."]],"reflm:5741":["transcendental properties of things are those that are essentially combined with the concept of a thing in general.",["“properties”: Eigenschaften; “combined”: verbunden."]],"reflm:5742":["In hypotheses unity of concordance: possibility, of connection: actuality, of derivation: necessity.\nThe three *requisita* of a definition besides the formal one, namely distinctness, because it is logical.",["“concordance” renders Zusammenstimmung, “connection” Verknüpfung."]],"reflm:5743":["For the cognition of a thing there is required. 1. Unity of the concept (object), that the concept not represent [several] still others than the one object. *Conceptus vagus*. Distinction from all the rest. 2. [Unity] Plurality as determination of this unity connected in one consciousness, or truth. 3. Totality as plurality [bestim] determined through the unity, or perfection.",["[bestim] is a deleted, unfinished bestimmt, left as it stands.","*Conceptus vagus*: “wandering (indeterminate) concept”."]],"reflm:5744":["1. The object as determinable. 2. The determination of it in re|spect\nof the distinction from others. 3. The thoroughgoing determination through a concept.",["“thoroughgoing”: durchgängig."]],"reflm:5745":["1. Unity of the object [to that] to which the manifold is related, i.e., concept. Unity of consciousness.\n2. Concordance of the manifold with the object according to rules, i.e., truth.\n3. Interconnection of all rules out of one concept, i.e., out of principles, i.e., perfection.\nThis is nothing but the distinguishing of an *ens rationis* from the *ente reali*.",["“concordance”: Zusammenstimmung; “interconnection”: Zusammenhang; “principles”: Principien."]],"reflm:5746":["1. The determinable something is one in respect of all sorts of *praedicatorum oppositorum*.\n2. The determination of it and its truth.\n3. The possible thoroughgoing determination in respect of what belongs to its essence, i.e., perfection.",["*praedicatorum oppositorum*: “opposed predicates”; deren Warheit (“its truth”) may refer to the determination or to the something."]],"reflm:5747":["I assume, e.g., through hypothesis [the] an evil principle: 1. it lacks unity, for there can be no completely evil one. etc etc.",["“one” supplies the noun understood with kein vollstandig böses (referring to Principium)."]],"reflm:5748":["Of the concepts that can be used either merely as predicate (g *adiective*) or also as subject. *formaliter* or *materialiter*. In the last case they permit a plural. e.g. unity, [perfection] truth, perfection, possibility, reality.",["“a plural”: Latin pluralem in the source; *adiective*: “adjectivally”."]],"reflm:5749":["1. Unity of the determinable object.\n2, Plurality of the determinations and their concordance among themselves according to laws of the understanding: formal truth, logic.\n3. Totality of the determinations in the [Be] complete concept of an object.\nThrough this we distinguish the thing from the non-thing.\nThese propositions are [subjective] merely logical and not metaphysical, i.e., objective as determination of things.",["[Be] is a deleted, unfinished word (Begriffe?), left as it stands.","“non-thing”: Unding."]],"reflm:5750":["[The unity] The relation of the many among one another, insofar as they are [together] contained in one, is the combination. The combination according to a rule: order.\nIn the mind all order is in time [either] and indeed one after another. what can be intuited reciprocally one after another is simultaneous. Harmony and melody.\nRule is: the universality of the condition in the determination of the manifold.\nOr it is the unity of the condition under which something is determined universally validly. or the determination of a concept insofar as it is at the same time universally valid.",["“combination”: Verbindung; “mind”: Gemüth; “simultaneous”: zugleich."]],"reflm:5751":["Unity of the condition under which something is posited universally is rule.",[]],"reflm:5752":["The concept of perfection belongs to the concept of totality or of totality, the accomplished (g completed) (*perfectum*) in oppo|sition\nto the begun (*affectum opus*). The perfection of a thing does not yet make up a perfection that arises through my positing the thing, hence not [in Ans] in respect of things in general. All completion contains a relation to satisfaction 1. with respect to a problematic end, and there the satisfaction rests on the unity of the manifold and on the subjective ground of comprehensibility; or 2. to a categorical end, and there it is called a perfection. Perfections in the metaphysical sense are realities [da]. The good is the object of choice; hence a thing is not good because it is perfect in its kind; but the *reqvisita* belonging to its perfection are good relatively to it. Perfections in the human being are means to [se] all sorts of possible ends of his existence in general, thus talents, not well-being and health.",["“totality or of totality”: Allheit oder der totalitaet; “the accomplished”: das Verrichtete; “completion”: Vollendung; “satisfaction”: Wohlgefallen.","[in Ans], [da] and [se] are deleted, unfinished words, left as they stand.","*affectum opus*: “a work begun”."]],"reflm:5753":["Perfection can either (g in the theoretical sense) signify mere completeness, and there it is, as the perfection proper to the essence of each thing in relation to the thing itself, transcendental; as completeness of the entity or reality[under] of all things in general it is the metaphysical perfection. The first is the perfection of a thing, the second a perfection, of which there are many.\nOr it signifies in the practical sense the good, and there either that which is good in itself (good will), or what is good as means to all ends, the aptitude for all ends, e.g., understanding.",["“completeness”: Vollständigkeit; “entity”: entitaet; “aptitude”: Tauglichkeit."]],"reflm:5754":["On *Caput* II. In the categories of modality being is considered, and indeed the possibility not so much of the thing as of its existence, whereby it is considered not merely in itself but also in relation with all requisite [things] conditions. If it is connected with them, then it is actual. if these conditions are [not merely contingently] already given in the concept of it, then it is necessary.",["“being”: Seyn; “existence”: Daseyn."]],"reflm:5755":["[The necessity] The existence that can be cognized completely *a priori* is [that of the] absolutely necessary; that only under a condition [is], hence *secundum qvid a priori*, is hypothetically necessary. The concept of an *absolute necessarii* is problematic, i.e., the possibility [de] of such an object cannot be comprehended. The human understanding can cognize no existence *a priori* from the mere concept of a thing. It completes the hypothetical necessity.",["“that only under a condition”: daß in the source, read as das.","*secundum qvid*: “in a certain respect”; *absolute necessarii*: “of an absolutely necessary [being]”; [de] is a deleted, unfinished word."]],"reflm:5756":["The opposite is either logically impossible or *realiter*. The latter either absolutely without condition, neither logical nor real, and is mere idea; or under the condition of a possible experience, e.g. freedom [it its opposite] is *realiter* impossible in experience.",["*realiter*: “really”, “in reality”."]],"reflm:5757":["Agreement with the conditions of an experience in general: possibility.\nConnection of a thing with experience in general: actuality.\nThis connection, insofar as it can be cognized *a priori*, i.e., independently of experience, is necessity.",["“agreement”: Uebereinstimmung; “connection”: Verknüpfung."]],"reflm:5758":["From mere concepts the existence of a thing cannot be |\ncognized, hence not *simpliciter a priori*. But also not [through] under presupposition of pure intuitions. Since beyond concepts and intuitions *a priori* there is nothing further than experience, then for [Noth] necessity experience must, which goes before the necessary, i.e., go before from the condition of actuality.",["The last sentence is syntactically broken in the source and is kept so; [Noth] is a deleted, unfinished Nothwendigkeit.","*simpliciter*: “absolutely”, “without qualification”."]],"reflm:5759":["Everything that exists is thoroughly determined. The absolutely necessary thing is supposed to be regarded through its concept as existing, consequently also as thoroughly determined. *Deus est conceptus singularis*; that every one of its predicates necessarily belongs to it and it is determinable *vnico modo* does not prove the necessity of its existence.",["“determined” renders determinirt here; ihm and es (“it”) may refer to God or to the concept.","*Deus est conceptus singularis*: “God is a singular concept”; *vnico modo*: “in a single way”."]],"reflm:5760":["I can say: an *ens necessarium* we can think only as *realissimum*, because this concept alone is thoroughly determining. But not conversely: an *ens realissimum* I must think as *ens necessarium*, for there I would go beyond the concept, in that I posited the object outside it. *omnimoda determinatio* is not *definitio existentiae*.",["*ens necessarium*: “necessary being”; *ens realissimum*: “most real being”.","“thoroughly determining” renders durchgangig determinirend.","*omnimoda determinatio*: “thoroughgoing determination”; *definitio existentiae*: “definition of existence”."]],"reflm:5761":["E II 332:\nA necessary being is that whose nonexistence is impossible. Absolutely, such a thing does not involve contradiction, but transcends the human concept. For not-being in itself involves no contradiction for this reason, because being, as synthetic predicate, is not identical with the concept of the thing. For in a contradiction precisely the same thing that was affirmed in a concept, thus lay in it, must also be denied. If space and time were objects outside thoughts, or their |\nproperties, then they would be *entia necessaria* or properties of the same. For their concept is already a being which contains the ground of the possibility of things.",["The first two sentences are Latin in the source: Necessarium ens est, cuius nonexistentia est impossibilis. Absolute tale non involvit contradictionem, sed transscendit conceptum humanum. “Such a thing” (tale) may refer to the necessary being or to its nonexistence.","“not-being” renders Nichtseyn; “being” in “being, as synthetic predicate” and in “a being which contains” renders Seyn (not Wesen).","“of the same” renders the singular desselben after the plural entia necessaria; “their properties” may be the properties of thoughts or of objects."]],"reflm:5762":["Of the relation of the real ground to the consequence, and that it cannot be cognized and distinguished otherwise [than] *in concreto* which is the ground and which the consequence, than that, when [A] I posit A, B constantly follows upon it, and not conversely.",["“consequence” renders Folge (glossary: sequel); “real ground” renders Realgrund.","The construction “otherwise [than] … than that” is as awkward in the German (nicht anders [als] … als daß)."]],"reflm:5763":["Possible is what is determined with regard to time in general, thus with regard to the concept of time, wherein therefore the conditions of determination in time lie; e.g. the real possibility of the attractive force. (g it is grounded in general.) This possibility we cognize only through experience. Secondly, [also possible] actual is what is determined in relative time, consequently is at some time, i.e. is determined through and with regard to other things given in time. Finally, a necessary being is what is determined with regard to absolute time, on the presupposition of which therefore all relative time-determination rests.",["“actual” renders wirklich; “time-determination” renders Zeitbestimmung.","“on the presupposition of which” may refer to the necessary being or to absolute time (auf dessen Voraussetzung)."]],"reflm:5764":["29'. E II 1092.\n M 29:\nThe difference between that something is thought or is given is this, that it is an object of undetermined or of thoroughly determined thinking. In itself there is here no difference between things, but only in relation to our cognition *a priori*. What *a priori* can be thought only as thoroughly determined is necessary; what is thought only as undetermined is to that extent contingent. Although our concept is never thoroughly determined, in that it does not contain everything, yet the understanding represents |\nthe object as thoroughly determined in itself, |\nM 29': |\nand thinking and apperceiving are one and the same, consequently possibility and existence. It also cannot be distinguished in things themselves, but only in their relation to the understanding, with which apperception lies at the ground. Consequently I myself am determined in only a single way in relation to everything.",["The first sentence is as awkward in the German: zwischen dem, das etwas gedacht oder gegeben ist, ist der, da es … (da for daß).","“in itself” (an sich selbst) may attach to “the object” or to “represents”.","“existence” renders Daseyn; “apperceiving” renders appercipiren."]],"reflm:5765":["The conditionally necessary, as hypothesis, is (g it) not on that account in itself.",["The sentence is elliptical in the German (ist (g es) darum nicht an sich selbst); no predicate is supplied."]],"reflm:5766":["In and \nWe have no example of hypothetically necessary things except only with regard to the form of them; e.g. [water] origination of ice, of salts from mixture.",["The editorial location note “In und” is cut off in the source."]],"reflm:5767":["A judgment about the existence of an object is always synthetic. Necessary judgments do not on that account represent the object as necessary.",["“object” renders obiect in the first sentence and Gegenstand in the second."]],"reflm:5768":["There is indeed a difference between absolute, i.e. inner, and hypothetical necessity, if the latter is thought only under a possible hypothesis. But if it is carried out to its supreme actual ground, then, as far as the degree of necessity is concerned, the difference vanishes. For the necessary consequences of a necessary cause are inseparable from its absolute necessity. But an absolutely necessary being can have only a single complete effect of its existence; and if all possible existence is grounded on |\nthe same, then there can also be only a single complete possibility, namely the entire actual.",["“existence” renders existentz throughout this passage.","“complete” renders both Vollstandig and complete."]],"reflm:5769":["Possibility seems to be distinguished from actuality only with regard to the appearance of things. The opposite of the predicates of a thing can well be possible, but not the opposite of its existence in the thoroughgoing determination.",["“existence” renders Daseyn; “thoroughgoing determination” renders durchgängige Bestimung."]],"reflm:5770":["Contingency and necessity are not differences in the existence of things, nor of the determination of things in themselves, but merely of their concepts, thus always relative to this concept, thus never in themselves. The possibility of alteration can be explained as belonging to the thing itself.",["“thing itself” renders Sache selbst, elsewhere “thing” renders Ding; “alteration” renders Veranderung.","“in themselves” in “things in themselves” may attach to “things” or to “determination”."]],"reflm:5771":["Everything necessary must be cognized *a priori* as existing, thus from the mere possibility and the concept. But to cognize this hypothetically means to consider this concept of something merely possible as attaching, according to its condition, to something actual. If therefore the thing is posited which contains the condition, then from the mere possibility of the same the conditioned follows. e.g. »Glass must break through the fall« *a priori* from brittleness in general. This brittleness attaches to glass, and thus the breaking also exists as necessary. To infer actuality from mere possibility.",["“this” in “to cognize this hypothetically” renders the feminine diese, whose referent is unclear.","“attaching” renders anhängend; “to infer” renders schließen."]],"reflm:5772":["The possibility of things is distinguished from the possibility, actuality or necessity of their existence. The former consists merely in this, that their concept has nothing in itself that conflicts; it is, so to speak, the agreement of their archetype with the understanding that thinks it. The |\npossibility of existence, on the other hand, signifies the agreement of the position of such an object outside the understanding. Now if thinking itself is not the cause of this, then the material and the combining ground of the same must be outside the same. Existence does not belong at all to the idea of a thing, and the possibility of the same, if it is complete, cannot be distinguished from actuality and necessity.",["“archetype” renders Urbild; “position” renders the Latinate position.","“the agreement of the position … outside the understanding” is elliptical in the German: what the position agrees with is not stated.","“material” renders Stoff; “combining ground” renders Verbindende Grund."]],"reflm:5773":["Above and \nContingency, i.e. possibility of the opposite, cannot be cognized through alteration, but only from this, that all alteration is a *caussatum*, and thus in itself, and without there being a cause for it, is contingent. *A priori* it can be cognized by nothing.",["The editorial location note “Über und” is cut off in the source.","*caussatum*: “something caused”."]],"reflm:5774":["The necessary can, with regard to the predicate in respect of which it is determined, be determined in only a single way. But existence does not belong under this.",["“determined” renders both bestimt and the Latinate determinirt."]],"reflm:5775":["Absolutely necessary is either a thing or a concept of a thing. The concept of the *realissimum* is necessary in every respect with regard to concepts of things in their thoroughgoing determination. Its cancellation leaves a complete void for the understanding.",["“cancellation” renders Aufhebung; “void” renders leere."]],"reflm:5776":["*vnico modo determinabile* the author calls a thing that is thoroughly determined through its concept; of this kind is only the *ens realissimum*; for the *mere negativum* is no thing, and the *partim reale partim negativum* is thoroughly determined through no concept. Now Leibniz had attempted, following Descartes' precedent, [from] to prove that a thing which is thoroughly determined through its concept also necessarily exists; but the inference is found invalid. Thereupon he or Wolff attempts to convert the proposition and to say: Every necessary thing is thoroughly determined through its concept (consequently what is not thoroughly determined through its concept is no necessary being. Now since some necessary being or other must be, so too a being thoroughly determined through its concept, i.e. an (g such as) *ens realissimum* must exist), hence an *ens realissimum*. But then the [opposite] (g *per accidens*) converted proposition would also have to be true, which however, because only a single thing can be *ens realissimum*; and indeed precisely for this reason, because [it] the concept of the same is a |\n*conceptus non communis*, but *singularis* [because zw] and the combination of convertibility precisely [thereby] for this reason must make up a *conversionem simpliciter talem*, and indeed from mere concepts, necessity would have to follow from the concept of the *realissimum* just as that concept from this, which however is false.",["*vnico modo determinabile*: “determinable in a single way”; *mere negativum*: “merely negative”; *partim reale partim negativum*: “partly real, partly negative”; *conceptus non communis*, *singularis*: “not a common but a singular concept”.","*per accidens* and *conversio simpliciter talis* are the logicians' terms for conversion with change of quantity and simple conversion; “converted” renders umgekehrt, “convertibility” convertibilitaet.","The clause “which however, because …” breaks off in the German; “[because zw]” keeps the deleted fragment, whose second word is cut off."]],"reflm:5777":["Through no single concept of a limited thing is this thoroughly determined; for *a priori* no thing can be thoroughly determined except only through the idea of the *maximum*.",["“limited” renders beschränkt; *maximi* (genitive) is given as *maximum*."]],"reflm:5778":["The necessary is determined in only a single way. The concept of a thing which according to this concept [in] is determinable in only a single way is *conceptus entis realissimi*, and hence it is inferred: only *ens realissimum* can be *absolute necessarium*; that is, however, only *necessitas logica*. The thing itself to which this concept belongs can be determined otherwise than in accordance with this concept.",["*conceptus entis realissimi*: “concept of the most real being”; *absolute necessarium*: “absolutely necessary”; *necessitas logica*: “logical necessity”.","“determined” renders both bestimt and the Latinate determinirt; “determinable” renders bestimbar."]],"reflm:5779":["Only the *omnitudo* is absolute unity and *individuum rationale*.\nThat the necessary must be determinable in only a single way is *conditio sine qva non*, but not on that account yet the sufficient ground of its necessity, any more than that it contains no contradiction.\nAnd then all this is only logical, because the possibility is grounded merely on |\nthe lack of contradiction and not on the possible unity of all this reality in one thing.",["*omnitudo*: “allness”; *individuum rationale*: “individual of reason”; *conditio sine qva non*: “indispensable condition”."]],"reflm:5780":["Even if the concept of a thing can be determined in only a single way, then these predicates indeed belong to it necessarily, but not existence; and an existence could be necessary, although not from the concept which we have, or any other; for from the concept of no thing can an existence be inferred.",["“existence” renders Daseyn."]],"reflm:5781":["That a necessary being cannot be altered does indeed follow, if time is mere form of sensibility and [be] determines the existence of no things in itself, but never if it belongs to determinations of the thing in itself. For then, if it is actually altered, this is not the contradictory opposite of its determinations in existence.",["“in itself” in “the existence of no things in itself” may attach to “existence” or to “determines” (keiner Dinge Daseyn an sich bestimmt).","“thing in itself” here renders Sache an sich; “existence” renders Daseyn."]],"reflm:5782":["In and \nThe necessary must be determined in a single way, because it is supposed to be cognized *a priori* as existing. Now the *ens realissimum* is thoroughly determined through its concept, thus only this can be an *ens necessarium*. If we therefore want to assume one, then we must think it thus; but from the concept of the highest reality existence cannot be cognized *a priori*, and yet even then everything is only relative to the human understanding.",["The editorial location note “In und” is cut off in the source."]],"reflm:5783":["The *ens necessarium* is that whose opposite is absolutely impossible. But the human understanding cannot have insight into this impossibility |\nwithout through this, that not-being contradicts its concept. Now the not-being of a thing never contradicts the concept of the thing in itself; thus the concept of the *ens necessarium* is unattainable for human reason, but yet necessary to assume, because otherwise the series of the conditionally necessary is never ended.\nThat it is determinable in only a single way follows from this, because its existence is supposed to follow from the mere concept. Now everything that exists is thoroughly determined, and existence can thus only [be inferred there] be inferred from a concept which thoroughly determines [or]; otherwise it does not follow from the concept at all, but from another assumed existence.\nThere is a difference: to assume the necessary being for the sake of another, and: to cognize a being through its concept as necessary.\nWe cannot say that a being exists contingently because it is altered, but rather: that we could not then cognize its existence from its mere concept; for then it would have to be determinable in only a single way. But we can cognize the existence of no thing at all from its mere concept, although, if we could cognize it, the thoroughgoing determination would at the same time be contained in it.\nThe concept of the *realissimum* alone suits the concept of the *ens necessarium*.",["“without through this, that” keeps Kant's construction ohne dadurch, daß (“otherwise than by”).","“the concept of the thing in itself” may also be read “the concept of the thing, in itself” (dem Begriffe des Dinges an sich selbst).","“which thoroughly determines” renders der durchgängig bestimmt, which may also be an ellipsis for “which is thoroughly determined”; “existence” renders both Existenz and Daseyn."]],"reflm:5784":["* I posit something either as object of a partial or of the thoroughgoing determination. In the first case I posit through the concept of a thing the universal and thus what is of many kinds. In the second case the individual [and its], which cannot be of many kinds. The *substratum* of thoroughgoing determination is what contains the matter for all determinations of a thing in general; hence I cannot say: if I assume such a thing, but rather reason presupposes such a thing with all possibility. With regard to thoroughgoing determination, therefore, possibility and actuality are one and the same, and its thoroughgoing determination is not, say, thought as universal, but at the same time as individual.\n*(s We cannot prove that, if a concept is of such a kind that it at the same time includes in itself the thoroughgoing determination of the object, this necessarily exists, but only: that, if we could derive existence from mere concepts, these would at the same time have to contain the thoroughgoing determination. That there cannot be more than one *necessarium* cannot be had insight into at all from concepts.)",["“what is of many kinds” and “of many kinds” render vielerley; “individual” renders einzelne; “universal” renders allgemein.","“these” in “these would … have to contain” renders the plural diese, which may refer to the concepts or to the existence derived.","“existence” renders Existenz; *necessarium*: “necessary [thing]”."]],"reflm:5785":["Our concept of absolute necessity signifies not the inner necessity from the concept of a thing, but the presupposition, necessary externally in relation to everything possible, and condition of its possibility (*ens realissimum*), just as time is regarded as absolutely necessary because everything must be in it.",["“its possibility” renders ihrer Moglichkeit, whose feminine or plural referent is unclear."]],"reflm:5786":["The proposition: the necessary being is determinable in only a single way, signifies: all determinations belong to it necessarily. But this does not prevent there being still other and otherwise determined necessary beings. But if the proposition is to say: all determinations must be given through a single concept, then this indicates only that we could otherwise have no concept *a priori* of a necessary being according to which at the same time the thoroughgoing determination would be given; thus admittedly only the concept of the *realissimum* is fit for this, if we already know that a being is necessary; but from this one cannot infer even the possibility, let alone the necessity, of such a being, but only that only in this way [the] could we thoroughly determine it from a mere concept as *ens singulare*.",["“*a priori*” may attach to “concept” or to “necessary being” (von einem nothwendigen Wesen a priori sonst keinen Begrif).","*ens singulare*: “singular being”; “determinable” renders determinabel."]],"reflm:5787":["If something can [is to] be cognized through concepts [as a] as a necessary being, then this concept can be no other than that through which the thing is at the same time thoroughly determined, consequently is determinable in only a single way; for otherwise it would be thought not as a (necessarily) |\nexisting, but only as a merely possible being. But the former is impossible [thus also].",["“thoroughly determined”: durchgangig bestimt; “being”: Wesen.","The deletions [als ei] and [soll] are rendered beside the words that replaced them."]],"reflm:5788":["The existence of the *contradictorie oppositorum*, which is not contradictory. When a predicate conflicts not with the thing, but with another predicate.\nAlteration goes merely to existence.",["*contradictorie oppositorum*: “of things contradictorily opposed”; “conflicts”: wiederstreitet; “alteration”: Veränderung."]],"reflm:5789":["That the concept of time is presupposed in the concept of the connection of the *contradictorie oppositorum*; *contradictorie opposita* in different times do not contradict one another.",["“concept of time”: Zeitbegrif; *contradictorie opposita*: “things contradictorily opposed”."]],"reflm:5790":["Something is called unalterable either insofar as it does not exist in time at all (God), or insofar as it is not considered according to its existence at all (the essences of things). That is the nominal unalterability and must not be used at all. The real unalterability is merely in appearances. In them, however, merely relative, not absolute.",["“unalterable”: Unveranderlich; “essences”: Wesen."]],"reflm:5791":["Alteration is the connection of the determinations with their opposite in the existence of a thing *post se invicem*. The thing remains, but the determinations arise and pass away.",["*post se invicem*: “after one another”."]],"reflm:5792":["The possibility of alteration, i.e. [how] to think a thing (g as) in a twofold opposed way in its existence as determined, is not possible a priori, from mere concepts, but only in time, and also in this the cause of it only in experience.",["“to think … as determined”: sich als bestimmt zu denken, reflexive (“to think to oneself”).","“from mere concepts”: aus lauter Begriffen."]],"reflm:5793":["What is alterable does not on that account exist contingently, and the |\nnecessary being is on that account not exactly unalterable. But if we take the necessity comprehensible and graspable by us, namely existence from concepts, then, because in the concept of a thing in itself time falls away, every [that which the] opposite of the predicates must also be contradictory in regard to the thing (its concept); for in the existence of an alterable thing predicates are opposed only to predicates and can be after one another without contradiction, because none of these predicates is determined through the concept of the thing.\nIf I want to think the concept of an unalterable being, then I must be able to specify a concept which would at the same time contain the existence of the thing, consequently the thoroughgoing determination through a concept and not merely that, but also the objective validity of it.",["“comprehensible and graspable”: begreifliche und faßliche.","“every … opposite”: alles … Gegentheil; “thoroughgoing determination”: durchgangige Bestimmung."]],"reflm:5794":["All alterability proves the contingency of the thing according to mere concepts of the understanding (g as thing in itself), because through the concept it is not, as to existence, consequently thoroughly, determined; but not the contingency as appearance.",["“determined”: determinirt; “as to existence”: dem Daseyn nach."]],"reflm:5795":["The logical possibility of the opposite does not prove the real one in the connection.",[]],"reflm:5796":["In and \nEverything alterable is through its concept undetermined in regard to the *praedicatorum oppositorum*, thus logically contingent.",["The locus note “In and” is incomplete in the source.","*praedicatorum oppositorum*: “of opposed predicates”."]],"reflm:5797":["In and \nContingency is inferred from alteration only so, because the thing is then in time. This, however, can also be empty. everything in time is contingent.",["The locus note “In and” is incomplete in the source.","“inferred”: geschlossen; “This” (Diese) may be time."]],"reflm:5798":["The alterable is hypothetically necessary, but the alteration with all ground necessary. Everything alterable is contingent, i.e. not absolutely necessary. For it is in time, consequently appearance; hence it is nothing absolutely necessary; absolutely necessary [can] is that whose opposite can take place not at all, not even after one another.",["“with all ground” (mit allem Grunde) may mean “with its whole ground” or “with every right”; left literal."]],"reflm:5799":["The alteration itself can be absolutely necessary, and the alterable is contingent.",[]],"reflm:5800":["Lower margin, \nThe possibility of alteration, that a state contains the ground of its opposite in the sequel, cannot be comprehended; for comprehension of the temporal sequence in experience, however, it is necessary.",["“comprehended” / “comprehension”: begreifen / Begreifung; “sequel”: Folge; “temporal sequence”: Zeitfolge."]],"reflm:5801":["The following determination is in itself contingent, but not the sequence and the series.",["“sequence”: Folge; “series”: Reihe."]],"reflm:5802":["The possibility of alteration cannot be cognized a priori.",[]],"reflm:5803":["In and \nContingent is that whose opposite is possible in its place.",["The locus note “In and” is incomplete in the source."]],"reflm:5804":["In and \nAlteration of the relation is not always alteration of the thing; e.g. since fire makes hard or soft.",["The locus note “In and” is incomplete in the source.","“since”: da, which can also be read “where” or “when”."]],"reflm:5805":["Above and \nAlteration is the connection of contradictorily mutually opposed determinations in the existence of a thing (which yet do not contradict the con|cept\nof the thing, but only *praedicatum praedicato*, not *subiecto oppositum*).\nWhat makes that possible which according to the mere concept of a thing is impossible? Time. (g *determinationes oppositae* can merely succeed one another.) Thus time does not belong to the concepts of things in themselves, but to the way in which we intuit them.",["The locus note “Above and” is incomplete in the source.","*praedicatum praedicato*, not *subiecto oppositum*: “a predicate opposed to a predicate, not to the subject”; the “not” is German (nicht).","*determinationes oppositae*: “opposed determinations”; “way”: Art; “intuit”: anschauen."]],"reflm:5806":["The alteration of that which belongs to the essence of a thing is merely an alteration in determination of the concepts, [not] thus logical, and not alteration of things and their existence.",["“essence”: Wesen."]],"reflm:5807":["On the inner margin \n*contrarie* mutually opposed determinations [have] are *realitas ut ratio opposita realitati*.",["*contrarie*: “contrarily”; *realitas ut ratio opposita realitati*: “reality as a ground opposed to reality”."]],"reflm:5808":["From alteration we can indeed infer that a state is [necessary] contingent, but not that a thing is contingent. The first signifies that the state has a cause; but that does not hold of the thing, which contains the cause of it.",[]],"reflm:5809":["Alteration first makes possible the concept of contingency in existence; hence, to be sure, everything contingent is alterable, and everything that is not alterable, necessary. Alteration makes only the inference to a cause necessary.",["“inference”: Schluß."]],"reflm:5810":["The logical unalterability in regard to the concept is commonly taken for the real unalterability in regard to existence, which many infer from it as persistence of things in regard to certain properties.",["“persistence”: Beharrlichkeit; “properties”: Eigenschaften."]],"reflm:5811":["In themselves things can neither by themselves (g alone) nor in community determine themselves *ad oppositum*; for *nulli subiecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum*, and *ratio*[cont] &lt;8*A est etiam ratio* >8 *Non A*. thus time, in which alone alteration can be thought, is no [representation of the] determination of things in themselves; consequently all alterations are merely determinations of appearances, and time itself, in which existence is determined alterably, is not something that attaches to the existence of things in themselves, but merely to our way of sensible representation (not of the understanding). Unalterable, therefore, is not merely the (g in itself) necessary being, but also everything intellectual. [In] In the objects of the senses, however, every [alteration necessary] determination that makes up the alteration is necessary, but only ever hypothetically to infinity. The principle of contradiction, insofar as it is restricted to that which is in time, goes not to concepts of things, but [to] of mere appearances; and it does not contradict itself that to those both predicates *opposita* (s only not at the same time) belong as sequels in the appearance and neither of them to them as things in themselves.\nI as object of sensible intuition am alterable and cannot bring the concept of myself to transcendental predicates of things in themselves, e.g. the simple or composite etc etc., but only speak of my intuition. I, however, as subject of thinking and merely as object of reason, can in turn cognize no predicates through which such an object is given to me *in concreto*, no extension, persistence in time, but also not their opposite. Thus all psychology as doctrine goes only to the human being as thinking being, not to the mere I in general; but psychology as critique prevents that what holds of me insofar as I am a human being is not taken for the only thing that holds for me as thinking being in general.",["*ad oppositum*: “to the opposite”; *nulli subiecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum*: “to no subject belongs a predicate opposed to it”; *ratio A est etiam ratio Non A*: “the ground of A is also the ground of not-A”.","“&lt;8” stands as it does in the source, an encoded opening angle bracket; [cont] is a deleted word-fragment.","“principle of contradiction”: Satz des Wiederspruchs; “sequels”: Folgen; the double negative after “prevents that” is the German's."]],"reflm:5812":["Beside and \nThe concept of the *entis realissimi* is the ontological place for all |\npossible things, insofar as they are distinguished from one another as things in general. Their difference then consists merely in the limitation of the concept of the *omnitudo realitatis*. Instead of thinking each thing as determinable according to the *principio exclusi medii* in regard to the real of all predicates, or rather for the sake of this determinability, in order to represent it analytically, one takes a thoroughly determined one as the ground of all.",["*entis realissimi*: “of the most real being”; *omnitudo realitatis*: “totality of reality”; *principio exclusi medii*: “principle of excluded middle”.","“it” (sie) may be the determinability or the things; “place”: Ort."]],"reflm:5813":["A merely possible thing cannot to that extent be altered, because alteration is the existence of the *oppositorum* in time.\nThe sole contingent (of nature) is that which is considered merely as actual through freedom.",["*oppositorum*: “of opposites”; “to that extent”: so fern."]],"reflm:5814":["*Realitas phaenomenon* is what corresponds to sensation. Full and empty space.\nWe do not always need true reality, in which no negation at all (*non esse*) is thought; but initially we also have to do with *realitatibus phaenomenis*.",["*realitas phaenomenon* / *realitatibus phaenomenis*: “reality as phenomenon” / “phenomenal realities”; *non esse*: “non-being”.","“need”: brauchen, which can also mean “use”."]],"reflm:5815":["On M § 135:\nNegation is not lack of things, but of the determination of things. — *Realitas, negatio, limitatio*; every *negatio* is either merely *limitatio*, i.e. *oppositum* of quantity, or *negatio repugnantiae* and an *oppositum* of quality. What holds of a *quanto* holds also of the *limite quanti*; for the quality remains.",["*negatio repugnantiae*: “negation of conflict”; *quanto* / *limite quanti*: “a quantum” / “the limit of a quantum”."]],"reflm:5816":["Beside and \nThe negation [as] it is either according to quantity or quality. In the first case it is to be regarded as a vanishing *quantum* and |\nnothing but merely limitation and not contradictorily opposed to reality, [for] so that one of the two would take place, but *disparatum* or *defectum*; in the second case it is *negatio oppositionis*, as when I say: a spirit is not composite, consequently simple. On the other hand I cannot infer: a spirit [is not in] does not occupy a space, consequently it is present in a point. For here the same quality of presence is assumed, and the point is a vanishing space. God does not move, consequently he rests.",["The locus note “Beside and” is incomplete in the source.","*disparatum* / *defectum*: “something disparate” / “a defect”; *negatio oppositionis*: “negation of opposition”."]],"reflm:5817":["39'. E II 1387.\nM 39:\nIn the *phaenomenis* negation is nothing other than restriction; in the pure understanding it is remotion, thus the qualitative *oppositum*. Limitation is either merely of determination or of counteraction. The former is logical cancellation, the latter real. Motion is transformed into rest either merely as *defectus* through lack of determining grounds or through reaction.\nM 39':\nIn the mathematical antinomy one proposition is the other's quantitative opposite, but they have the same condition and are therefore both false. In the dynamical propositions one is the other's qualitative opposite, but the condition is different; hence both can be true.",["“restriction”: Einschrankung; “limitation”: Limitation; “remotion”: remotion (Latinate, “removal”).","“counteraction”: Gegenwirkung; “cancellation”: aufhebung; “determining grounds”: Bestimmungsgründe."]],"reflm:5818":["At the very top of the page. \nThe counterpart is what can be the positive ground of the opposite of a reality.",["“counterpart”: Wiederspiel; “opposite”: Gegentheil."]],"reflm:5819":["The contrary of a reality (respectively such) is the ground of cancelling the reality, e.g. descent with respect to ascent. A reality absolutely such is not [*habet contr*] contrarily opposed to a reality equally absolute.",["“the contrary of a reality (respectively such)”: *contrarium realitatis (respective talis)*; “ground of cancelling”: *ratio tollendi*.","[*habet contr*] (“has contr…”) is a deleted, unfinished phrase, left as it stands."]],"reflm:5820":["Negation is either of a deficient or of a cancelling ground (really oppo|sed).\nWe do not know whether the negations arise from lack or real conflict.",["The first sentence is Latin: “of a deficient or of a cancelling ground” renders *rationis deficientis vel tollentis*, “really opposed” *realiter oppositum*.","“conflict”: Wiederstreit."]],"reflm:5821":["Limitation has degrees down to 0, thus also reality.",[]],"reflm:5822":["In and \nThe real is also opposed to the merely formal in intuition. Hence empty space, since no being of something is represented.",["The locus note “In and” is incomplete in the source.","“being”: Seyn; “since”: da, which can also be read “where”."]],"reflm:5823":["In and \nOnly reality in appearance can be opposed to a reality and negation agree with a reality.",["The locus note “In and” is incomplete in the source.","“agree”: zusammenstimmen."]],"reflm:5824":["Beside and \nNegation is not specifically distinguished from reality. It is the middle between two opposed realities [and the Ub], which is common to both. Between *contradictorie oppositis* there is no middle, but indeed between *realiter oppositis*.",["“middle”: Mittel; [Ub] is a deleted word-fragment, left as it stands.","*contradictorie oppositis* / *realiter oppositis*: “things contradictorily opposed” / “things really opposed”."]],"reflm:5825":["Below and \nThe possibility of alteration rests on the contrariety of certain *realitatum phaenomenorum*.",["The locus note “Below and” is incomplete in the source.","*realitatum phaenomenorum*: “phenomenal realities”."]],"reflm:5826":["41. E II 676. 677. 1640.\nM 41':\nOne can consider negation either as opposed to reality or as opposed to affirmation; in the latter case it is the logical *oppositum*, in the first [the *evanescens*] the limitation. [Extension in] »A spirit is not extended« does not signify as much as: »its extension is vanishing«, but: »it cannot have it at all«. On the other hand the point is not extended as a vanishing space. Spirits therefore cannot be regarded as points.\nIf I say the non-being of a predicate, then by that |\nthe mere vanishing of it must not straightaway be thought, and I may not count the subject in the same kind of things, but often I must reckon it to another kind.\nAbsolute reality is what, in regard to a thing in general, belongs to being. Comparative, what belongs to the being of a certain kind of [E] perceptions; thus pain is comparative reality, but not on that account absolute. *Negatio absoluta* is the mere restriction of the existence of an object as thing in general. *Realitas evanescens est defectus in sensu absoluto*. [Ein D] The existence in which all [existence] possible existence is contained is without limitations. But it cannot be composed of much restricted existence. Thus the existence without limitations is that of an *entis realissimi*, considered at the same time as ground. From this every other thing's possible existence is considered as derived, because it, as thing in general, in regard to its negations can be represented merely as (g a) restricted *ens realissimum* or its restricted product [am]. We can no longer distinguish possibility from actuality as soon as we have come to the first *substrato* of the determination of all concepts. |\nM 41: |\nIf non-being is considered merely as limitation, then all realities are assumed as of one and the same kind.",["*Realitas evanescens est defectus in sensu absoluto*: “vanishing reality is a defect in the absolute sense”; *evanescens*: “the vanishing”.","“non-being” / “being”: Nichtseyn / Seyn; “existence”: Daseyn; “limitations”: Schranken; “restricted”: eingeschränkt.","[E], [Ein D] and [am] are deleted fragments, left as they stand."]],"reflm:5827":["To a thing belongs neither a predicate nor [the] its non-being, insofar as this can be regarded merely as a diminution of the predicate. For that is not the contradictory opposite.",["“this” (dieses) may be the non-being or the predicate."]],"reflm:5828":["Above and \nNon-being (g of B) as a contradiction of the predicate B with the concept A cannot at all subsist together with the concept A and is its contradictory opposite. Non-being, however, which [the concept not] contains no contradiction with the concept, is the privative opposite or *defectus*. E.g. »The soul is not extended, because it is not determined at all as regards space or because [to it] exten|sion\n[against] contradicts its concept«, would also contain the proposition: »the soul is not present as a point in space«. For the point is after all nothing but vanishing extension, and the concept of it does not contradict the [extension] concept of extension, but is only a lack of the extended.",["\"[against]\": the deleted word is \"wieder\", which in Kant's spelling may stand for \"wider\" (against) or be \"wieder\" (again).","\"contradictory opposite\", \"privative opposite\": contradictorisches Gegentheil, privative Gegentheil; *defectus* (lack) is Kant's Latin."]],"reflm:5829":["Above and \n*opposita logice* under an inadmissible condition, e.g. of extension, are both false. e.g. the soul is either extended in space or unextended in it, i.e. a point. *opposita* under two different conditions are not contradictorily opposed; the opposition is inadmissible, and both propositions can be true. e.g. the will as appearance is under natural necessity, and as intellectual it is free. Both conditions are to be thought in all beings, but only in the will do we notice the latter.",["*opposita logice*: logical opposites.","\"inadmissible\": unstatthaft, used both of the condition and of the opposition."]],"reflm:5830":["When I say: god is not alterable, because nothing breaks off.",["\"breaks off\" (bricht ab) appears to record that the note breaks off after \"nothing\"."]],"reflm:5831":["The *oppositum* can be a qualitative or quantitative *oppositum*. The first is contradiction, the second *quantum* =0 or restriction; hence in a formula rest can be a motion =0, [extension in space] simplicity an extension =0, unaltered duration an alteration =0.",["\"restriction\": Einschränkung."]],"reflm:5832":["A barrel of apples is indeed a *compositum*, but not a *qvantum*. [In the case of the latter the can] The latter is [whose un] a *compositum* whose unity, assumed at will, brings out the *compositum* through frequent repetition.",["\"[whose un]\": the deleted \"dessen Ei\" breaks off, presumably in \"Einheit\".","\"unity\": Einheit, here in the sense of a unit of measure; the German word is the same."]],"reflm:5833":["Space is *qvantum*, matter *compositum*, body *totum*. *Mundus*, however, *totum absolute tale*.",["*Mundus*: the world; *totum absolute tale*: a whole absolutely as such."]],"reflm:5834":["A *compositum* is (g one) the same as several conjoined. if [sev] the conjunction of several has been made a number of times, the positing of the same is a *qvantum*.\nA *compositum*, insofar as it is not a part, is a *totum*.",["Latin in the original throughout; \"(g one)\" is *unum*, \"[sev]\" the deleted \"pl\" (*plurium*).","\"the positing of the same\": *positio eiusdem*."]],"reflm:5835":["A *Qvantum* is one, the same as many (s homogeneous) (taken together). This as *totum* contains *omnitudinem*.",["\"one, the same as many\": *unum idem cum multis*.","*omnitudinem*: allness (accusative of *omnitudo*)."]],"reflm:5836":["A *compositum* is what is represented through the conjunction of several. If these several are homogeneous, it is a *quantum*; if the allness of several, it is a *totum*; thus *pars* belongs to the *compositum*, *quantum* and *totum*.",["Latin except the last clause; \"allness\": *omnitudo*. Kant gives the three terms in the dative, *composito, quanto et toto*."]],"reflm:5837":["Cognate concepts, which represent several posited in one: *compositum*, *qvantum*, *totum*; each has *partes*. In the first I begin from them as unity, in the second I determine another unity through its repetition.",["\"unity\": Einheit, which can also be read as \"unit\"."]],"reflm:5838":["Totality (*totalitas*) is the greatest, not infinity. The [abs] unity (as homogeneous part of a magnitude) is to that extent the smallest and absolute unity the absolutely smallest. The simple has no magnitude at all, and is also no homogeneous part of a *quantum*.",["\"Totality\": Allheit; \"absolutely\": schlechthin."]],"reflm:5839":["Not every *qvantum* can be regarded as a *totum*.",[]],"reflm:5840":["Unity of multitude is *omnitudo*. *Totalitas* is synthetic *omnitudo*. (collective).\nIt is the category of totality in the synthesis of magnitude or of aggregation.\nOn the absolute all.",["First two sentences Latin: *Unitas multitudinis est omnitudo. Totalitas est omnitudo synthetica. (collectiva).*","\"totality\": Allheit; *totalitas* is Kant's Latin."]],"reflm:5841":["If I do not look to the homogeneity of the many, then *simplex*, *compositum*, *totum* are in opposition to the *qvantum*. *Unum*, *pluralitas* and *omnitudo*.",[]],"reflm:5842":["In and  M 45:\nif several are homogeneous, it is a quantum*; a real *totum* (in which there is not an ideal but a real *nexus*) is a *compositum*. One arisen through repetition of the same is a *qvantum*, through addition or conjunction of several it is a *compositum*.\nM 45':\n*(g Every *quantum* is thought as a *compositum*, not conversely; for the parts can be heterogeneous.)",["The text on M 45 is Latin; the addition on M 45' is German."]],"reflm:5843":["A thing can be discerned as a *compositum* (in a series), but without totality (of the aggregate). Thus the concept of the *compositum* is not yet that of a *totum*; with the *compositum* homogeneity is not required, but certainly with a *quantum*. The *totum* is always considered as a *quantum* according to a certain concept.\nTotality belongs to the concept of a *compositum* as *homogeneum*, i.e. as *quantum*.",["\"discerned\": eingesehen.","\"totality\": totalitaet here, not Allheit."]],"reflm:5844":["A *qvantum* through whose concept of quantity the multitude of |\nparts is determined is *discretum*; through whose concept of quantity the multitude of parts is undetermined in itself, is *continuum*.",["\"multitude\": Menge."]],"reflm:5845":["[Space] A *qvantum* all of whose possible parts are *qvanta* is *continuum*. therefore what does not consist of simples. Therefore in which, how many parts there are, is undetermined in itself. Therefore any *phaenomenon* whatever.",["Latin in the original; \"[Space]\" is the deleted *Spatium*."]],"reflm:5846":["Opposite and \nThe *qvantum* in which alone all quantity can be determined is undetermined with regard to the multitude of parts and *continuum*. Space and time.",["\"alone\" (allein) may attach to \"in which\" or to \"can be determined\"."]],"reflm:5847":["Every *Qvantum* is a *compositum*,[which] whose parts are all homogeneous with it; consequently it is *continuum* and does not consist of simple parts. consequently not indeed every *compositum*, but yet every *qvantum* is a *continuum*.",["\"homogeneous\": gleichartig."]],"reflm:5848":["Every *qvantum* has a quality (*continuum*) and every *qvale* a quantity (degree).",[]],"reflm:5849":["Every *quantum* (g *continuum*) as such is that through which a multitude [of the] of homogeneous parts is posited; consequently it necessarily precedes composition. The composition which precedes the multitude gives *quantum discretum*.",[]],"reflm:5850":["Since the measure of magnitude is itself magnitude, all magnitude can be determined only relatively and is, in the case of *phaenomenis*, also only a [representation] concept of the way in which representations can be composed through common unity. There is no concept of abso|lute\nmagnitude, except where the all is already determined through the concept [itself] of the thing; only metaphysical perfection is absolute magnitude.",["\"common unity\": gemeinschaftliche Einheit."]],"reflm:5851":["extensive magnitude, the multitude of the homogeneous in an *obiect* or of that which is posited through the *obiect*, thus the magnitude of a whole and that of a ground; that something is a ground is quality.\nUnity, possibility, truth has *in plurali* extensive magnitude, *in singulari* intensive.",["*obiect*: Kant's Latinate Object, as against Gegenstand.","*in plurali*, *in singulari*: in the plural, in the singular."]],"reflm:5852":["The magnitude of a *Qvanti* as multitude (given) is extensive. The magnitude of unity is intensive, i.e. degree. (s magnitude of the aggregate, magnitude of the ground.)",["*Qvanti*: genitive of *qvantum*."]],"reflm:5853":["All *objecte* as objects of intuition have extensive magnitude, as grounds of sensation intensive.",["\"*objecte*\" is Objecte, \"objects\" Gegenstände."]],"reflm:5854":["Threefold way in which things determine existence. Thus (g real) relation firstly of the subject, 2ndly of the cause, 3. of the union in a whole of substances.\nThe category of relation (of the unity of consciousness) is the foremost among all. For unity really concerns only relation; thus this makes up the content of judgments in general and alone can be thought as determined a priori.\nOn the section on *substantia* (g the relation of realities). A category is the concept through which an *obiect* in general is regarded as determined with re|gard\nto a logical function of judgments in general (i.e. of the objective unity [of] in the consciousness of the manifold), i.e. that I must think the manifold of its intuition through one of these moments of the understanding.\nThere are therefore [only] three logical functions [of] under a certain title, hence also three categories: Because [the] two of them show the unity [of consciousness [of the] of two *oppositorum*] of consciousness in two *oppositis*, but the third again combines the consciousness of both sides. More kinds of unity of consciousness cannot be thought. For let a be a consciousness which connects a manifold, b another, which [the sa] connects in an opposite way: then c is the connection of a and b.\n*Judicia infinita*. »*Anima est non mortalis*« signifies not merely that A belongs under the *sphaeram non B*, but under the sphere C outside B, which restricts and bounds B, thus signifies limitation of the proposition: »a est b«.",["\"relation\": Verhältnis; \"combines\": verbindet; \"connects\", \"connection\": verknüpft, Verknüpfung.","\"[the sa]\": the deleted \"dasse\" breaks off, presumably in \"dasselbe\".","*Judicia infinita*: infinite judgments; *Anima est non mortalis*: the soul is not-mortal."]],"reflm:5855":["The *per se esse* of a substance is not an *a se esse*.",["*per se esse*: being through itself; *a se esse*: being from itself."]],"reflm:5856":["In and \nThat whose existence is never a determination of another thing: substance. That whose concept is a determination [of] of other things is logical. subject or predicate.",[]],"reflm:5857":["A reality whose *esse* is *inesse* is [subst] *accidens*; whose *esse* is *subsistere*: *substantia*.",["Latin in the original; *esse*: being; *inesse*: being-in, inhering; *subsistere*: to subsist."]],"reflm:5858":["In and \nEverything can be thought as subject or predicate, but not everything can exist as subject.",[]],"reflm:5859":["Relation and modality belong to the consideration of the nature of beings, quantity and quality to the doctrine of beings.",["\"beings\": Wesen in both places; Wesenlehre can also be read as \"doctrine of essence\"."]],"reflm:5860":["In substance there is. 1. The relation of inherence (*accidens*); 2. Of causality (force); 3. Of *commercii* (influence).",["*commercii*: genitive of *commercium*, community; \"influence\": Einfluß."]],"reflm:5861":["The sensible concept of sustentation (the bearer) is misunderstanding. *accidentien* are only the way of existing of substance according to the positive.",["\"sustentation\": sustentation, the supporting of accidents; \"the bearer\": der Träger."]],"reflm:5862":["We cannot think up any fundamental force. or cognize its possibility a priori, e.g. impenetrability.",["\"think up\": erdenken; \"fundamental force\": Grundkraft."]],"reflm:5863":["*De commercio*.\nIt lies in the concept of space that a substance of the world cannot flow into another without suffering from it.",["*De commercio*: on community; \"flow into\": einfließen (cf. Einfluß, influence); \"suffering\": leiden, being acted upon."]],"reflm:5864":["Logic, which expounds objective rules of the use of the faculty of cognition, and ethics, which does this with regard to the faculty of desire (the ought), presupposes for both only faculties |\nof the mind. Psychology, which explains what happens, does not prescribe what ought to happen, is occupied with powers of the mind.",["\"mind\": Gemüth; \"powers of the mind\": Gemüthskräfte.","The verb \"setzt voraus\" is singular in the German."]],"reflm:5865":["*Unum plurium coniunctorum* is, for the sake of the combination, *compositum* and, insofar as it is not a part of a larger *compositum*, *totum*. Thus we can think something as *compositum* without on that account considering it as *totum*, e.g. when we assume no bounds at all or determinate magnitude of it.",["*Unum plurium coniunctorum*: one of several conjoined things.","\"combination\": Verbindung."]],"reflm:5866":["*Complexus*, taking-together is not *compositum obiective tale*.",["\"taking-together\": Zusammenfassung; *compositum obiective tale*: a composite objectively as such."]],"reflm:5867":["Dividing up is not dividing.",["Eintheilen (division into members, as of a concept) as against theilen (division into parts)."]],"reflm:5868":["Among all *coniunctis* only that is to be called a *compositum* which is thought through the combination of the manifold of which one is possible without the other.\nA one of several mutually determining one another is a *compositum*.\nA one of several mutually influencing one another is a *compositum substantiale*.",["The last two sentences are Latin: *Vnum plurium se mutuo determinantium* / *in se mutuo influentium*."]],"reflm:5869":["*compositum ideale* is any *qvantum* whatever whose parts do not precede the possibility of the *compositum* (s but whose parts already presuppose the whole as given), e.g. space.\n*reale*, whose parts are in *commercio*. Therefore the relation of the cause to what is caused is not composition.\nThe *compositum* of parts which can exist even outside the combination with others is *reale*.\nWe cannot call the *accidentia* parts. For their *nexus* is inherence in a third, not *aggregatum* to one another.",["The first two paragraphs are Latin, the last two German."]],"reflm:5870":["On the possibility of an alteration.\nA thing arises and perishes, a state commences and ceases. A thing whose state commences and ceases is altered; and what is altered, that remains.",["\"arises and perishes\": entsteht und vergeht; \"commences and ceases\": hebt an und hört auf."]],"reflm:5871":["We can notice change only in that which persists. If everything flows, then flowing itself cannot be perceived. The experience of arising and perishing is thus possible only through that which persists. Thus [Subst] there is something in nature which remains (neither arises nor perishes), and this is substance. only the *accidentia* change. *principium* of the possibility of experiences. Place designates the substance. In different places there are different substances; [the accide] what adheres to the persisting in a place and is distinguished from the persisting is *accidens*.",["\"change\": wechseln (noun and verb); \"persists\", \"the persisting\": beharrt, das Beharrliche."]],"reflm:5872":["Nothing in the world arises out of nothing.\nNothing in the world is annihilated.\nNo experience is possible about this.\n(s Propositions of the persistence of substance.)",["The first two lines are Latin: *Nihil in mundo oritur ex nihilo. Nihil in mundo annihilatur.*"]],"reflm:5873":["We can cognize things only through predicates, thus arising and perishing only as predicates; now one can have no concept of something as predicate without subject, thus the subject must remain in everything that perishes; otherwise we cannot cognize perishing itself through experience.",[]],"reflm:5874":["The parts of a *compositi realis* are in *commercio*.\nThreefold relation: of the *accidens* to the substance, of the effect to the cause and of the parts in a composite among one another.",["The first line is Latin: *Partes compositi realis sunt in commercio*, the parts of a real composite are in community."]],"reflm:5875":["Above, beside and \nThe only thing that is cognized a posteriori in appearances is the matter or the real of them, namely what corresponds to sensation. The formal of appearances, space and time, is cognized a priori and is pure intuition. To experience belongs, besides [appearance] intuition, also the real in general and unity of the manifold of intuition.",[]],"reflm:5876":["69. E II 414. 355.\n M 69':\nOn space and time.\nIt is so far from the case that the sensible intuitions of space and time should be confused representations, that they rather provide the most distinct cognitions among all, namely the mathematical ones. And [they] that they are the forms of sensible intuition, this makes it comprehensible how mathematical cognitions of [appearances a priori] things a priori are possible; which 1. would not take place if the objects of the senses were things in themselves, 2. Also not if appearances were nothing other than indistinct intuitions of things; for then our cognition of appearances would always be taken only a posteriori, because their form would be not in our senses but in the things.\nM 69:\nThe mathematical properties of matter, e.g. infinite divisibility, proves that space and time belong not to the properties of things but of the representations of things in sensible intuition; for since the essential of these representations is composition, then, if I cancel it, nothing (hence also nothing simple) remains.\nSpace is not a concept a priori, but intuition, which precedes the concept. For whence then should synthetic judgments a priori come? and what sort of *obiect* should then be represented through it, since space does not yet contain any *obiect*?\nSpace is itself a *synthesis a priori*.",["\"cognitions of things a priori\": \"a priori\" may attach to \"things\" or to \"possible\", as in the German.","\"proves\": beweiset is singular in the German.","\"properties\": Eigenschaften; \"cancel\": aufhebe."]],"reflm:5877":["A *compositum* is either formal, which contains nothing but the form of composition, e.g. of sensitive intuition. [this] or real, and this either real *phaenomenon* or real *noumenon*.",["Latin in the original; *intuitus sensitivi* can be genitive singular (of sensitive intuition) or nominative plural (sensitive intuitions)."]],"reflm:5878":["Between M § 230 and 231:\n*Phaenomena*, insofar as they are outside one another simultaneously, are in *commercio*.",["Latin: *qvatenus sunt extra se simul*; *simul* may go with \"outside one another\" or with \"are\"."]],"reflm:5879":["The (concept of) space is itself only a form of composition; thus, if this is canceled, everything is canceled and nothing remains.",["\"canceled\": aufgehoben."]],"reflm:5880":["In and \nThat of which, if the thought is canceled (*compositio*), the *obiect* itself is canceled, is mere appearance.",[]],"reflm:5881":["In and \n*Continuum* — *discretum per se*.",[]],"reflm:5882":["A composite is such either originarily or derivatively. The former, that of which, all composition being removed, nothing remains, whose division precedes composition; but that whose composition precedes all division is derivative. That whose every division is quantitative, i.e., into what is homogeneous, |\nis a continuum; that whose division is heterogeneous into simple parts (not *quanta*), is qualitative.",["andecedit stands in the source for antecedit.","Prius ('the former') has no verb of its own; the sentence is left as incomplete as the Latin."]],"reflm:5883":["Space, time and sensation.",[]],"reflm:5884":["*respectus* of *coordination* in that which is at the same time, of *subordination*: what is one after another, both in time. In the first the *respectus* of *extraposition*.",["respectus: relation, respect."]],"reflm:5885":["Space and time are *composita idealia*, neither of substances nor accidents, but of relations, which go before things.",["composita idealia: ideal composites."]],"reflm:5886":["In and \nSpace contains the form of all *coordination* in intuition, time of all *subordination*.",["'In und' (In and) stands broken off in the source."]],"reflm:5887":["The magnitude of multitude is extensive.",["Große is Größe, magnitude; Menge, multitude."]],"reflm:5888":["Everything that occupies a part of time or of space has extensive magnitude, extension and alteration. Every reality has its degree between a and 0 infinitely smaller breaks off.",["'bricht ab' (breaks off) appears to be the editor's note that the text stops here.","Veränderung: alteration."]],"reflm:5889":["All *division* is either quantitative or qualitative. The latter is separation; both are either *divisio immediata*: dismemberment, or *mediata*: decomposition, *decompositio* into mediate parts. All division is either [logical] ideal or real. The ideal logical or mathematical. The former either of the *sphaera* or of the content. The real of a composite substance.",["'division' renders both the Latinate division and Theilung.","scheidung: separation; zerfällung: dismemberment; Zersetzung: decomposition."]],"reflm:5890":["A given *quantum* (g in space and time), which would nevertheless be *illimitatum*, is impossible. Space is infinite *progressive*, but not *collective*.",["illimitatum: unlimited.","progressive, collective: Latin adverbs, progressively and collectively."]],"reflm:5891":["The quantity of an aggregate is extensive, of a ground intensive: degree.",["rationis may be 'of a ground' or 'of reason'."]],"reflm:5892":["Totality as a collective concept cannot be thought of space and time. For there the plurality is only successive *addition* and the *completudo* of it never possible. On the other hand indeed of a thing which is thought through pure concepts of reason: *illimitatum*.",["completudo: completeness; illimitatum: unlimited.","Vielheit: plurality."]],"reflm:5893":["Beside and \nThe greatest and unlimited is unique (s not (it does not comprehend Everything in itself)). The absolute *totality* is that which is unlimited in a certain respect.\nThe progression in the *construction* of a magnitude is either finite or infinite. Both concern not the magnitude of the thing, but of the measurement, and hold only of appearances. The infinite is never given, but only the condition of the possibility of the *progressus in infinitum* or *indefinitum*.\n(s *infinitudo* is not the idea of *omnitudo*, also not of the *maximum*, also not of *totality*.)\nThe infinity of the possible or actual addition.",["'Neben und' (Beside and) stands broken off in the source.","einmalig: unique, occurring once; the reading is doubtful.","in gewisser Absicht: in a certain respect (Absicht)."]],"reflm:5894":["Negation of lack =0 can be regarded as an infinitely small degree, but not negation of the contradiction of concepts (*opposition* of *quality*).",[]],"reflm:5895":["In and \nThe infinite in a thing in itself is not impossible, but indeed the infinite of a given appearance, because, if here the representation is not wholly given, the thing is also not given. For the object is here only representation.",["'In und' (In and) stands broken off in the source.","'thing' renders Sache here.","'but indeed the infinite of a given appearance': the German, too, leaves 'is impossible' to be understood."]],"reflm:5896":["The *totality* of that which can be thought only through a *progressus* to infinity is impossible. What is thought merely through a concept of the understanding as *quantum* can also be represented as infinite given; for it is given before the *progressus*.",["als unendlich gegeben: 'as given as infinite' or 'as infinitely given'; left open."]],"reflm:5897":["What is given only through *composition* is [therefore] always finite, although the *composition* goes to infinity.",[]],"reflm:5898":["Space and time are both nothing but compositions of sensible impressions. This composition goes to infinity, but is never infinite. The magnitude of space presupposes the magnitude of time.\nInfinity of division. In infinite time infinitely small.",["Zusammensetzung: composition; Theilung: division."]],"reflm:5899":["*indefinitum* is a universal concept which is undetermined with respect to magnitude. A *quantum singulare* is not *indefinitum*.",[]],"reflm:5900":["*Quantum infinitum* in itself may be possible, but not as *phaenomenon*, because here the *progressus in infinitum* is thought as completely given. Thus no infinite whole and no infinite multitude of parts of a [whole] given whole.",[]],"reflm:5901":["If, because the line ab can be divided to infinity, it also actually consists of infinitely many parts next to one another, then the line af too must, because infinitely many parts can be laid off on it, actually consist of infinitely many parts, and if the first does not follow, the second not follow. Since also the line ab can be traversed in a finite time, and to each little part of time a velocity can correspond with which the space ae or ef would be traversed, then, if that infinite multitude of parts were actual, the traversal of an infinite space in a finite time would also have to be possible.\nIf all parts of the possible division (g at the same time) were actual, then there would be an absolute magnitude of space, which would be determinable from the multitude of all parts. But since to an infinite multitude there belongs an infinite time, in order to cognize it wholly, an infinite time would be able to be given wholly.",["Getragen: read as aufgetragen, laid off.","The letters refer to a figure not reproduced here."]],"reflm:5902":["A thing in itself does not depend on our representations, can thus be much greater than our representations reach. But appearances are themselves only representations, and their magnitude, i.e., the idea of their generation through the *progressus*, cannot be greater than this *progressus*; and since this is never given as infinite, but is only possible to infinity, the magnitude of the world as appearance is also not infinite, but the *progressus* in it goes to infinity.",[]],"reflm:5903":["If space and time were properties of things in themselves, then from the fact that they are mathematically infinite, i.e., that the *progressus* in them, insofar as they are given as infinite whole, is greater than all number, it would not follow that they are impossible, but that they are incomprehensible for us. But now space and time are not things in themselves, and their magnitude is not given in itself, but only through the *progressus*. Now since a *progressus in infinitum* that has been given wholly is a contradiction, |\nan *infinitum mathematicum datum* is impossible, but a *quantum in infinitum dabile* possible. From this, however, it also does not follow that space and time have bounds in themselves, for that is also impossible, but only that they are not things in themselves at all, but always have only the bounds where our thoughts and representations come to a stop.",["als unendlich gantz gegeben: 'given as an infinite whole' or 'given as infinitely whole'.","infinitum mathematicum datum: a given mathematical infinite; quantum in infinitum dabile: a quantum that can be given to infinity.","Grentzen: bounds."]],"reflm:5904":["Just so nature is not the order of things in themselves, but that which the understanding posits for appearances; of things in themselves I can say that they are not subject to the order of things, and of our understanding, that it, according to the order of nature, but not determined through it, makes the appearances possible.",["'der Ordnung der Dinge' (the order of things) may be a slip for 'der Natur'; the text is kept as it stands.","setzen: posit."]],"reflm:5905":["In the *noumenon* I can think *quantity* only through the relation to the *illimitatum*, i.e., to that which contains *omnitudo*, as the standard, and indeed through mere negations. The *ens realissimum* is a determinate *quantum*, but not the *infinitum*, because the *quantum* in itself is not determined, as to how great it is, by the impossibility of measuring it out wholly. The *maximum* is conversely for that reason not also *infinitum*. But it is still a *totum absolute tale*. *In phaenomenis* there is no absolute *totality*.\nThe *infinitum reale* (of things (g of existence) in general), in which everything is, is Spinoza's concept. There is the *limitation*, where in space the bounding. There being goes first, afterward the determination of it.",["totum absolute tale: a whole absolutely as such.","Limitation and Begrentzung: limitation and bounding."]],"reflm:5906":["*Argumentum* On the objective *reality* of time.\nIt was objected that the unknown something x, which at one |\ntime produces the appearance of the egg, produces in me at another time that of the chick; thus something must have altered in the *object*, because it could not contain the ground of two opposed determinations at the same time. I answer: It is the same *object* which produces the ground of the appearance of two opposed states as successively existing, and thus the appearance of an alteration. This is no more difficult to explain than how [appearance] alteration is possible, i.e., where a thing or a multitude of things is supposed to contain the ground of two opposites.",["'object' renders Obiect here.","da: 'where' or 'since'; left open."]],"reflm:5907":["Leibniz's proposition is that *numerica diversitas* must also be *specifica diversitas* and conversely *specifica identitas totalis* must also be *numerica*. This latter proposition holds of an *object* of pure reason, e.g. *ens realissimum* is with another *realissimum specifice totaliter idem*. If it were in space and time, then it would not follow from this *numerica identitas*, namely that it is one single being.\nAppearances are different things merely through difference of places, for the difference of places precedes *a priori* and [lets] makes, regardless of the *quality* of the things, numerical difference.\nOn the other hand the (g specific) difference of the things in things in themselves or the difference of the *qualities* always makes different things. But in the case of *phaenomena* it can be one and the same thing, and must also be, which is determined otherwise at different times; thus here it means: *numero idem potest esse specifice diversum*, if one understands by this every determination through a different *quality*.",["numerica diversitas: numerical diversity; specifice totaliter idem: specifically, totally the same.","numero idem potest esse specifice diversum: what is numerically the same can be specifically different.","Verschiedenheit: difference."]],"reflm:5908":["The difference of places makes difference of things, because places in space, with all inner *identity*, are still different as found outside one another.",[]],"reflm:5909":["The parts of time do not determine one another reciprocally, but |\nthe preceding is only determining. Hence it has only one *dimension*.\nEverything which determines one another mathematically in space and time does not for that reason determine one another dynamically.",[]],"reflm:5910":["The proposition of *contingency* means to say that everything is necessary either in itself or as *causatum alterius* (for the *causatum* is also necessary). This proposition, however, holds only [so] of this: that I can cognize *a priori* the existence of a thing [never otherwise], if I can think its non-being, only by means of a cause. e.g. a motion which is known through no experience. — But the genuinely contingent is cognized not so much [in de] in alteration, but in the form of purposiveness. This in matter (because it has no life) must have a cause that is distinct from it.",["causatum alterius: what is caused by another.","Zweckmaßigkeit: purposiveness."]],"reflm:5911":["One knows the things in the world only through the relation which they have to a possible occurrence. Thus as causes and according to their forces.",["kennt: knows (kennen); Begebenheit: occurrence."]],"reflm:5912":["Our concept of a contingent thing is the concept of a thing which is possible only as *causatum alterius*, not: whose non-being is impossible, i.e., unthinkable. Thus the proposition: *quodlibet contingens est causatum alterius* is tautological; e.g. We call the existence of organized beings contingent. We can namely cognize real possibility only by the object's being given in some way. If we want to cognize it *a priori*, then it must be cognized not through intuition (empirical), but as existence through something else which determines *a priori*, i.e., cause.",["quodlibet contingens est causatum alterius: whatever is contingent is caused by another.","'whose non-being is impossible' stands so in the source, though the sense may want 'possible'."]],"reflm:5913":["If something is contingent in itself, then it is to that extent represented merely through the understanding and must have a cause. But if something is given merely in a contingent way (in appearance as something that alters), then the thing itself is not for that reason contingent.\n(s To infer from one's concept of a thing to the necessity or contingency of its existence means: to leap out of oneself.)",[]],"reflm:5914":["93. E II 893.\nThe *principium contingentiae* signifies that properly nothing exists as absolutely contingent, i.e., that its existence must be cognized objectively as determined if it is subjectively determined in perception. — (g Everything is necessary, either absolutely or hypothetically; |\nM 93: |\njust so nothing is absolutely necessary either, but only in relation to the possibility of the objects of experience.)\nM 93':\n(g *Contingens* is either in the logical sense that whose non-being can be thought, or: whose non-being is possible; the latter we cannot cognize. it requires |\nM 93:|\nacquaintance with the necessary.)",["schlechterdings: absolutely.","Kentnis: acquaintance."]],"reflm:5915":["That something exists cannot be cognized through reason if it is contingent. [If it] Through experience it also cannot be cognized as existing (objectively), except insofar as [it] according to empirical laws the perception of it is necessary. In a word: the objectively valid and the necessarily valid are one and the same. What I am to say of the object must be necessary. For if it is contingent, then it holds only in the subject, but not of the object.",["'object' renders Obiect; 'subject', Subiect."]],"reflm:5916":["Laws pertain to existing things, likewise mutability.",["existentia: read as neuter plural, existing things."]],"reflm:5917":["Every thing considered according to its existence in itself (not as appearance) is an *object* of the understanding and not of mere experience, must thus be determined according to principles of the understanding as absolutely or hypothetically necessary. that whose existence is cognized only through the understanding is cognized *a priori*, thus as necessary.",["Verstandesprincipien: principles of the understanding (Principien, not Grundsätze)."]],"reflm:5918":["In and \nThe contingent as such cannot be cognized *a priori*. But every *object* must be able to be cognized *a priori* as such.\nWhat is given in experience is just thereby thought under laws, consequently as necessary.",["'In und' (In and) stands broken off in the source."]],"reflm:5919":["The existence of a thing can be cognized only relatively to a concept that I have of it, never absolutely, as contingent or necessary.",[]],"reflm:5920":["Everything that exists is necessary, either in itself or through cause.",["was da ist: what is there, what exists."]],"reflm:5921":["In and \nThat something is contingent with respect to a certain concept I can well cognize *a priori*, but not that it is contingent *in singulari* and *individuo*.",["'In und' (In and) stands broken off in the source.","in singulari and individuo: in the singular and in the individual."]],"reflm:5922":["We can cognize the contingency neither of things nor of the state, not *a priori*, also not *a posteriori*. For the non-being which follows upon existence does not prove that it could have taken place in the place of existence. But the principle of contingency holds only of the empirically contingent. What is constant is empirically necessary;\n|\nwhat arises or perishes, empirically contingent. Thus every state has its cause, but not the substance, and this proposition holds only in the series of appearances, but not of these as things in themselves in relation to something outside the series.",["bestandig: constant, lasting (not beharrlich).","Grundsatz: principle."]],"reflm:5923":["95'. E II 983.\nDeduction of the pure cognitions a priori.\nIn experience alone can our concepts be given completely *in concreto*, hence their objective reality be completely exhibited. Concepts whose nature it is contrary to, [so] to be exhibited in experience, are mere ideas. Hence the objective reality of all concepts [to seek], i.e., their significance, is to be sought in the relation to possible experience. Others, namely those that are mere ideas, can indeed be accepted as hypotheses, but not count as demonstrable.\nNow we can, when it is a matter of the possibility of pure cognition a priori, transform the question into this one: whether experience contains (g nothing but) cognition that is given only a posteriori, or something is encountered in it that is not empirical and yet contains the ground of the possibility of experience.\nFirst there belongs to all experience [immediately] representation of the senses. Second consciousness; this, when it is immediately combined with the former, is called empirical consciousness, and the representation [the] (g of the senses), combined with empirical consciousness, is called perception. Were experience nothing further than an accumulation of perceptions, then [except] nothing would be encountered in it that was not of empirical origin.\nBut the consciousness of perceptions relates all representation only to ourselves as modifications of our state; they are then separated among themselves, and above all they are not cognitions of any thing and relate to no object. They are therefore not yet experience, which must indeed contain empirical representation, but at the same time as cognition of the objects of the senses.\nM 95':\nIf we question logic concerning what can be called cognition in general, then concept is [that which] a representation (g or sum total of them) that has been related to an object and designates it; and in that we connect (separate) one concept with another in a judgment, we think something of the object that has been designated through a given concept, i.e., we cognize it, in that we judge about it. All cognition, hence also that of experience, accordingly consists of judgments; and even concepts are representations that are prepared for possible judgments, in that they represent something in general that has been given as cognizable through a predicate.\nTherefore [are] is experience possible only through judgments, in which perceptions indeed make up the empirical materials, but the [form of the] relation of them however to an object however and cognition of it through perceptions cannot depend on empirical consciousness alone.\nThe form, however, of every judgment consists in the objective unity of consciousness of the given concepts, i.e., in the consciousness that these must belong to one another, and thereby designate an object in whose (g complete) representation they are always encountered together.\nThis necessity of connection, however, is not a repre|sentation\nof empirical origin [and nevertheless], but presupposes a rule that must [is] be given a priori, i.e., unity of consciousness that takes place a priori. This unity of consciousness is contained in the moments of the understanding in judging, and only that is object in relation to which unity of consciousness [a prio] of the manifold representations is thought a priori.",["[to seek] renders the broken-off deletion [zu such].","“exhibited” renders dargestellt; “designates” renders bezeichnet; “demonstrable” renders erweislich.","“however … however” keeps the doubled aber of the German."]],"reflm:5924":["The universal proposition is: synthetic cognitions a priori from mere concepts are impossible, but indeed 1. from the construction of concepts, 2. from rules that contain the possibility of experience and through which perceptions become objective cognitions. For in experience alone do pure cognitions a priori obtain their significance and use.",[]],"reflm:5925":["NB. synthetic (g pure) cognitions from concepts that cannot serve for the determination of the objects of experience in general, such as the transcendental ideas are, have the significance that they determine the boundary of all cognition of experience, i.e., show that this is never sufficient to itself and complete; consequently that in every respect something that underlies experience must be assumed; but that we can cognize this only so far as it underlies experience, and are entitled to think hypothetically so far as practical propositions, which go beyond grounds of experience, require them.\nThe ground on account of which we can cognize something a priori is that objects of [experi] perception are appearances; the cause why we cannot cognize them completely is that we would then not take them as objects of experience, since this is nev|er\ncomplete, and if we take them, we always contradict ourselves.",["“sufficient” renders zulänglich; “cognition of experience” renders Erfahrungserkenntnis.","“require them”: sie may refer to the ideas or to the hypothetical thoughts; the ambiguity is kept.","[experi] renders the broken-off deletion [Erfahr]."]],"reflm:5926":["Space and time are themselves nothing other than forms of the composition of the objects of sensation; hence also, if one cancels all composition there, nothing remains. Now the unity of consciousness in this composition, insofar as that (g as) is considered universally, is the concept of the understanding, and that unity belongs to experience as objective cognition, hence concepts of the understanding a priori are also required for the possibility of experience. Something must therefore precede even experience*, through which it itself becomes possible; but in it all cognition a priori must alone have its reality.\n*(g For the logical form of the understanding in judging must after all precede, and the appearances [must, as determined] (as mere representations) must be regarded as determined in regard to each of these forms, otherwise no experience can arise from them. We can also put the word experience in the place of the word objects of the senses. For the things in themselves we do not cognize; we can know nothing of them except all experience of them possible for us, and indeed insofar as this is determined a priori from the form of sensibility and that of the understanding.)",["“insofar as that (g as) is considered”: jene may refer to the unity or to the composition.","“things in themselves” here renders Sachen an sich."]],"reflm:5927":["It is absolutely impossible to cognize anything synthetically a priori of things in themselves, but merely of appearances, because synthetic judgments require intuitions, either pure or empirical, but synthetic judgments a priori pure ones. This [are] is, however, possible only as a form of our sensibility [because we] and holds only of appearances, not of things in themselves.\nCategory is the representation of the relation of the manifold of intuition to a universal consciousness (to the universality of consciousness, which is properly objective). The relation of representations to the universality of consciousness, consequently the (g transformation of the empirical and particular) unity of [particular consc empirical] consciousness, which is merely subjective, into a consciousness that is |\nuniversal and objective, belongs to logic. This unity of consciousness, insofar as it is universal and can be represented a priori, is the pure concept of the understanding. This can therefore be nothing other than the universal of the unity of consciousness, which constitutes the objective validity of a judgment.\nThe manifold, insofar as it is represented as necessarily belonging to one consciousness (or also to the unity of consciousness in general), is [as belonging] thought through the concept of an object: the object is always a something in general. The determination of it rests merely on the unity of the manifold of its intuition, and indeed on the universally valid unity of consciousness of it.\nTwo pieces of cognition take place a priori. 1. Intuitions, 2. unity of consciousness of the manifold of intuitions (even of the empirical ones). This unity of consciousness constitutes the form of experience as objective empirical cognition.",["“This [are] is”: the singular ist after the deleted plural sind leaves it open whether Diese refers to pure intuition or to the intuitions.","“one consciousness” renders einem Bewußtsein, which may also be read “a consciousness”.","[particular consc empirical] renders the deletion [besonderen Bewus empirischen], broken off in the middle word."]],"reflm:5928":["Synthetic propositions through concepts are always a priori and impossible; but through the construction of concepts (in the sensible formal intuition in general) or the combination of universality with the empirical synthesis in general they are not only possible, but also necessary. For experience is nothing other than synthetic [cognition] connection of perceptions in one consciousness (as necessary, hence contained universally).",["“combination” renders Verbindung; “connection” renders Verknüpfung."]],"reflm:5929":["If I think something through the understanding as (g in itself) contingent, then I cannot think precisely the same through the understanding as existing without a cause. (but why must I think it existing through the understanding, why not rather through experience?) (g what designates contingency?)\nIf I want to think a thing through the understanding, then something must be thought thereby as ultimate subject, to which everything else as predicate |\npertains (but what designates the ultimate subject to me?). If I represent a thing as [plurality] sum total of the many homogeneous, then I must think it as magnitude; but what is given to me before the plurality that is thought in it as magnitude? Every thing has reality; but how is its difference from 0 possible to think.\nAll this indicates that our understanding indeed has laws for itself to think something, but that we can give these thoughts no application and significance except through sensible intuition, which we bring under the condition of the unity of consciousness of the manifold, and that finally only in experience lies the reality of the concepts of experience, and indeed of experience in general as one which without those concepts would be merely a sum total of perceptions, whereas those without this would be a mere manifold mode of the thoroughgoing consciousness of itself in the manifold consciousness of representations.",["“before the plurality”: vor may be read in the older sense “for” (“what is given to me for the plurality … as magnitude?”).","“plurality” renders Vielheit; “homogeneous” renders gleichartig; “thoroughgoing” renders durchgängig.","“of itself”: seiner selbst may also be read “of oneself”."]],"reflm:5930":["The objective unity of consciousness of the manifold of representations is the connection of it either with one and the same concept, e.g. All human beings [or] (in a word: a universally valid combination (g of the concepts) in one consciousness), and then the unity is called logical; or this logical unity of consciousness is regarded as determined in the concept of a thing and constitutes its concept: that is the synthetic or transcendental unity of consciousness. There the unity is [distributively] represented that concerns merely the relation of the [representations] concepts; here that which itself constitutes a concept of the thing through the unification of its manifold in one consciousness. e.g. [All] Many [human beings] things are outside one another, and on the other hand: space is One, which comprehends much outside one another. The former is the quantity [of a] of the concept of a judgment, the latter the concept [of the] of a thing as *Qvanti*.",["“comprehends” renders begreift.","*Qvanti*: genitive of *quantum*, “of a quantum”, in Kant's spelling."]],"reflm:5931":["Category is the (g necessary) unity of consciousness (g in the composition) of the manifold of representations (g intuition), insofar as it makes possible the concept of an object in general (for |\ndistinction from the [sub] merely subjective unity of consciousness of perceptions). This unity in the categories must be necessary. e.g. Logically a concept can be subject or predicate. But an object, considered transcendentally, presupposes something that is necessarily merely subject and the other merely predicate. — Ground — Logically something can be *reciproce* ground and consequence; but *realiter* everything, if it stands in community of time, must stand in community. In a magnitude there is necessarily unity of the many.\nTo experience belongs: 1. intuition (the manifold); 2. connection of the manifold in one (g empirical) consciousness; 3. unity of the connection of the manifold, which is universally valid. Intuition is given a priori (*progressus* in it also finite, also the unity of connection).",["*reciproce*: “reciprocally”; *realiter*: “really”.","“consequence” renders Folge, in the pairing Grund und Folge."]],"reflm:5932":["Through the category I represent to myself an object (g in general) as determined in regard to the logical functions of judgments: of the subject (not predicate), of the consequence as ground, of the plurality in its representation. But why must I represent every object as determined in regard not only to one but to all logical functions in judgments? Because only thereby alone is objective unity of consciousness, i.e., universally valid connection of perceptions, hence experience as the sole reality of cognitions possible.\nThis unity of consciousness (g of the connection of our representations) is given in us a priori as the foundation of all concepts just as well as form of appearance as the foundation of intuitions. But both hold only of human cognition and also have objective significance only in regard to it, indeed the |\ncategory can have no significance a priori if there were not intuitions a priori.\nTherefore category is the concept of an object in general, insofar as it is determined a priori in itself in regard to a logical function of judgments (that one [it through no other] must think through this function the combination of the manifold in its representation).\nAll objects that we are to think must be determined in regard to all logical functions of the understanding; for through these alone can we think, and through the fact that something [is represented as determined in regard to these functions] determines thinking in general (how it is to be thought), it is an object, i.e., something to which a particular thought, which is distinguished from others, corresponds.\nAll principles[of the from] of the pure understanding go to sensibility and indicate the conditions under which the representation of sensibility belongs under a category. they therefore determine the rule of judgments in general in regard to appearances and are principles of possible experience; for without the categories our representations could not relate to objects, because they alone determine thinking in general [to] in relation to something in general.",["“consequence” renders Conseqventz.","“principles” renders both Grundsätze (“All principles of the pure understanding”) and principien (“principles of possible experience”).","“for through these alone”: dadurch may refer to the logical functions or to their being determined."]],"reflm:5933":["The unity of consciousness [of the representations in] of the manifold in the representation of an object in general is the judgment. The representation of an object in general, insofar as it is determined with regard to this objective unity of consciousness [determined] (the logical unity), is category.\nThe unity of consciousness is either empirical: in the perception of the manifold, combined through imagination. Or it is logical: the unity in the representation of the object. The former is contingent and merely subjective, the second necessary and objective. The former is required for concepts, the second for judgments and their possibility in general. The schematism indicates the conditions under which |\nan appearance is determined with regard to the logical function and thus stands under a category; the transcendental principles indicate the categories under which the schemata of sensibility stand.",["“object” renders Kant's Latinate obiect throughout; “combined” renders Verbunden.","“and their possibility in general” (und deren Moglichkeit überhaupt): deren may refer to the judgments or to concepts and judgments both; left open."]],"reflm:5934":["100. E II 984. 408.\n M 100':\nExperience is cognition (g *a posteriori*, i.e.) of that which is [in the] object of sensation (*a posteriori*). Sensations yield no cognition at all; thus something (g *a priori*) must be added beyond them, [so f] if [it] experience is to become possible. Beyond the representation *a posteriori* only that *a priori* from concepts can be added, and this can only be the connection (*synthesis*) [se a priori], insofar as it is determined *a priori* (for the mere comparison of sensations yields nothing but sensation and no object).\nThe universal (g formal) principle of possible experience is thus: *all perceptions [stand] are determined *a priori* with regard to their connection in one consciousness (for consciousness is unity, in which alone the connection of all perceptions is possible, and, if it is to be cognition of the object, must be determined *a priori*). The objective unity in consciousness of different representations is the form of judgment. Thus all perceptions, insofar as they are to make up experience, stand under the formal conditions of judgments in general, and the [Ver] determination of them through this function is the concept of the understanding. [Experience thus] All experiences as possible perceptions stand *a priori* under concepts of the understanding, through which alone they can become empirical cognition, i.e. representation of the objects (*a posteriori*).\nM 100:\n*(g All appearances are determinable *a priori* with regard to their connection [through those with regard to the] in accordance with the unity of consciousness in all judgments in general, i.e. they stand under categories.\nSpace and time are the forms of combination in intuition and serve to apply the categories *in concreto*.)",["The asterisks before “all perceptions” and “(g All appearances” are Kant's reference marks, kept.","“and, if it is to be cognition of the object, must be determined *a priori*”: the subject (sie) may be the unity or the connection; left open.","Verknüpfung = connection; Verbindung = combination; Verstandesbegrif = concept of the understanding; [Ver] is a deleted, unfinished word."]],"reflm:5935":["All synthetic cognitions from mere concepts pertain only to things as appearances, never to things in themselves, and to the former merely insofar as they are intuitions (mathematics), or as belonging to a possible experience.\nWe can represent nothing to ourselves *a priori* except that of which we ourselves contain the grounds in our power of representation, either in sensibility or in the understanding: In the latter either, where the understanding merely determines the unity of consciousness *a priori* (theoretical), or where reason, merely in accordance with the understanding, directs actions toward objects (practical).",["“pertain to” renders gehen auf; Vorstellungskraft = power of representation.","da (“where”) may also be read as causal (“since”); “merely in accordance with the understanding”: blos may attach to gemäß or to the verb; left open."]],"reflm:5936":["Ontology is science of things in general, i.e. of [our] the possibility of our cognition of things *a priori*, i.e. independently of experience. It can now teach us nothing of things in themselves, but only of the conditions *a priori* under which we can cognize things [which to us] in experience in general. i.e. principles of the possibility of experience.",[]],"reflm:5937":["Besides ontology there also belong to metaphysics the cosmological antinomy (among nothing but ideas of pure reason), the psychological paralogisms and the theological ideal. The last two are transcendent, but without contradiction. The cosmological ideas, however, conflict with one another. The psychological take the intellectual as empirically given, the theological the intellectual as given *a priori*. Existence from mere concepts.",["“among nothing but ideas” renders unter lauter Ideen; unter may also mean “under”."]],"reflm:5938":["The transcendental ideas serve to restrict the principles of experience, so that they are not extended to things in themselves, and to show that what is no object of possible experience at all is not on that account a non-thing, and that [there is outside the] experience is not sufficient to itself and to reason, but always refers further and thus away from itself.",["Erfahrungsgrundsätze = principles of experience; Unding = non-thing.","“refers ... away” renders abweise (abweisen: to turn away, to reject); the construction is elliptical in the German."]],"reflm:5939":["Ideas. Through the cosmological ideas we want to cognize the absolute whole of the series of conditions in appearances. Through the psychological the absolute constitution of an object of experience (g not in relation to senses) as thing in itself. Through the theological the existence of things from mere concepts (g without any experience.), i.e. we [want a] make ourselves an idea which at the same time inseparably carries existence *a priori* with it.\nTo want to cognize the soul according to what it is in itself from inner sense is futile.",["Gantze = whole; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Daseyn = existence.","“*a priori*” may attach to “existence” or to “carries”; left open."]],"reflm:5940":["The world is a *totum finitorum*, because one part is *complementum* of the other to the whole, consequently none is infinite; secondly: *contingentium*, because one part depends on the other.",["*totum finitorum*: whole of finite things; *complementum*: complement; *contingentium*: of contingent things."]],"reflm:5941":["The intelligible world is the absolute whole of substances *per se*; the [intelli] sensible world is the whole of phenomena.",["Latin throughout: mundus intelligibilis, mundus sensibilis, totum absolutum, phaenomena.","*per se* (“through themselves”) may attach to “substances” or to “absolute whole”; left open. [intelli] is a deleted, unfinished intelligibilis."]],"reflm:5942":["In and \nThe absolute whole (which is not a part) of substances is the world in the transcendental sense. World of sense and of understanding.",["The editorial location note “In und” is cut off in the source.","The first sentence is Latin: totum absolutum (qvod non est pars) substantiarum est mundus in sensu transscendentali. Sinnen und Verstandes-Welt = world of sense and of understanding."]],"reflm:5943":["*Mundus noumenon* (*intelligibilis*) is the idea of a whole of substances that is in turn no part. *Mundus phaenomenon* is the application of this idea to appearances.\nOf the *mundus noumenon* one can think several worlds [to] outside one another; in the *phaenomenon* there is only one single one through space and time. The *commercium* of the substances of the first is possible only through *harmoniam praestabilitam*, 1. because many substances can be in community only through a common cause; 2. because this harmony would be no nature were it not already laid into the creation of the things of the world and thus pre-established.\nIn the *mundo sensibili* *influxus physicus* holds. In the *mundo noumeno* all substances are intellectual, at least we cannot think them through other predicates than of representations. In the *sensibili* they are extended. Motion. The influence of the first and of the second is actually nothing, for the alterations of the second are only *phaenomena*. Bodies are not substances and motion not an *accidens* of them, but only *phaenomena* of the intelligible.\n(g intelligible or intellectual.)",["*commercium*: interaction; *harmoniam praestabilitam*: pre-established harmony; *influxus physicus*: physical influence; Gemeinschaft = community.","“than of representations” (als der Vorstellungen): elliptical in the German, kept so.","intelligibel and intellectuel are distinct words in the German, rendered “intelligible” and “intellectual”."]],"reflm:5944":["Above, beside and \nOne cannot say *corpus* [ph] *noumenon*, but *substantia composita noumenon* or *phaenomenon*.",["The editorial location note “Über, neben und” is cut off in the source; [ph] is a deleted, unfinished phaenomenon.","*corpus*: body; *substantia composita*: composite substance."]],"reflm:5945":["Above and \n*Harmonia absqve commercio* is that which does not happen according to a universal rule of nature and thus without the cause lying in the substances themselves.",["The editorial location note “Über und” is cut off in the source.","*Harmonia absqve commercio*: harmony without interaction; Naturregel = rule of nature."]],"reflm:5946":["Beside and \nWhether the state of the world does not always remain the same, while that of the things changes.",["The editorial location note “Neben und” is cut off in the source.","Weltzustand = state of the world."]],"reflm:5947":["The question of the unicity of the world [is] concerns either the copulative plurality or the disjunctive.",["“unicity” renders Kant's Latinate unicitaet (unicitas, oneness)."]],"reflm:5948":["*Contingentia mundi*. Everything contingent at one time was not (for that is the mark by which contingency is cognized), thus it is a sequel of something preceding, thus it has a cause. Only that is taken to be contingent which is in a series that presupposes another.",["*Contingentia mundi*: contingency of the world.","Folge = sequel; Merkmal = mark; vor Zufallig gehalten = taken to be contingent."]],"reflm:5949":["The 4th antinomy. We need an absolute necessity, but we can reach none in the *regressus* of the world. For the causality must always begin, and such a cause is contingent. This antinomy is unavoidable if we regard time as determination of things in themselves and appearances as things. If this is not so, then in the time-series there is nothing necessary; but that which contains the ground of appearances is not in the time-series and can therefore have causality without beginning and be necessary.",["“things” renders both Dinge (things in themselves) and Sachen (appearances as things).","*regressus*: regress; caussalitaet = causality."]],"reflm:5950":["Whoever asserts the *regressus in infinitum* does not on that account yet assert an *infinitam seriem rationatorum datam*, if they are *phaenomena*; for these are given only in the *regressus*.",["*regressus in infinitum*: regress to infinity; *infinitam seriem rationatorum datam*: a given infinite series of things grounded (rationata)."]],"reflm:5951":["Because the *regressus* goes *in infinitum*, the absolute totality of the world of sense is not to be thought.",["“totality” renders totalitaet; Sinnenwelt = world of sense."]],"reflm:5952":["In and \nThe magnitude of the world-whole as of an aggregate or series (the latter in time or the series causes). The magnitude of the series of appearances is not distinguished from the magnitude of the *progressus in infinitum*.",["The editorial location note “In und” is cut off in the source.","“the series causes” (der reihe Ursachen) is irregular as it stands (probably for “the series of causes”); rendered literally. diese may refer to the series or to the aggregate and series both."]],"reflm:5953":["Opposite and \n*Regressus in infinitum absqve caussa prima* is impossible among *noumenis*, but among *phaenomenis*, which are not things but themselves subsist only in the *regressus*, necessary.",["The editorial location note “Gegenüber und” is cut off in the source.","*Regressus in infinitum absqve caussa prima*: regress to infinity without a first cause; bestehen = subsist."]],"reflm:5954":["*In mundo* [non] (*vniverso*) *non datur* (s *infinitum*) *abyssus* either of things, or also of empty time and space. One of the two is necessary if *phaenomena* are to be represented in their absolute totality, i.e. given in themselves.\nHere there is no completion, neither in the infinite nor of the boundary; but all sensibility has a cause outside itself, for it is not a thing in itself.",["*In mundo (vniverso) non datur (infinitum) abyssus*: in the world (universe) there is no (infinite) abyss.","“nor of the boundary” (noch der Grenze): der Grenze may be genitive or dative (“nor in the boundary”); left open."]],"reflm:5955":["The proposition: In the world everything is nature, i.e. *non datur hiatus*, where something happens against nature and is torn away from its chain. We can explain occurrences only according to determinate laws of nature.",["*non datur hiatus*: there is no gap; Begebenheiten = occurrences.","The first sentence has no main verb after the colon in the German; kept so."]],"reflm:5956":["In and \n*Casus* in the theoretical or teleological sense. If space and time adhere to things in themselves, then the causality |\nof them and of their cause must also be determined in time, consequently be naturally necessary, thus nothing unconditioned.",["The editorial location note “In und” is cut off in the source.","*Casus*: chance; anhängt is singular after two subjects in the German."]],"reflm:5957":["*non datur hiatus*, because otherwise we would have to perceive time and space in themselves. *vacuum metaphysicum non datur*.",["*non datur hiatus*: there is no gap; *vacuum metaphysicum non datur*: there is no metaphysical void."]],"reflm:5958":["No absolutely empty space (for then there would be no outer relations). No empty time; for nothing can begin absolutely. No leap, no chance, no fate.",["Verhaltnisse = relations; Sprung = leap; Zufall = chance; Schicksal = fate."]],"reflm:5959":["123. E II 1393. 1438.\nM 123':\n*Abyssus*, *Saltus*, *Casus*, *Fatum* (s *Hiatus*: *vacuum intermedium*; *circumfusum*) are all together concepts of the unconditioned, which can be thought in a *mundo* [*phaenomeno*] *noumeno* or at least in connection with it, but which do not fit the *mundus phaenomenon*.\nThe absolute totality of composition must be thought in the *mundo noumeno*; in the *phaenomeno* the world-whole (g as) according to space and time (g given) is *abyssus*, and the like cannot be given. In the *noumeno* the absolute totality of division, in the *phaenomeno* that would yield the *saltus*. In the first, where the condition of time does not adhere to existence and there is no alteration that needs a cause, freedom can be thought; in the second it would be *Casus*. |\nIn the *noumen* a being necessary for itself can be thought as cause of the world. In this I can, according to its laws, think no other relation [ders] of the cause of the world to the world than in time, and there the causality is always contingent, consequently the *causa* itself.\n(g In the world of sense all (g *synthesis*) unity is only that of the *regressus*. Without boundaries nothing is given therein. Thus also [not] |\nM 123: |\nin space and time, which are unbounded, nothing with an absolute totality of synthesis, nothing unconditioned is given.)",["*Abyssus, Saltus, Casus, Fatum*: abyss, leap, chance, fate; *Hiatus: vacuum intermedium; circumfusum*: gap: intervening void; surrounding void.","Zeitbedingung = condition of time; Daseyn = existence; für sich nothwendig wesen = being necessary for itself.","“In this” (In dieser) most naturally refers to the world, possibly to its cause; left open."]],"reflm:5960":["The totality of composition in the (g world of sense) world and of division are both false and must be so[s], because it is taken in space and time.\nBut the totality of cause and effect*, likewise of necessity, can both be true, because the one can be considered in the intelligible world and the other entirely outside all world in the intelligible, consequently outside all dependence, i.e. contingency.\n*(g for these concepts do not necessarily belong to objects of the senses, if namely one wants thereby to cognize nothing, but only to have the possibility of a thought of it.)",["[s] stands after es (“so”) in the source.","“it is taken” (wird) is singular after the two totalities in the German; gegenstanden = objects.","The asterisks are Kant's reference marks, kept."]],"reflm:5961":["123'. E II 1401.\nM 123:\nThe whole dialectic comes down to this. One absolutely wants the (g sense-)world to be held to be a thing in itself, although it can be thought only in space and time. Now as thing in itself there must cer|tainly\nbe absolute totality of conditions in it. But this is possible in time and space neither [in] according to composition nor decomposition nor origination. — But if everything is merely *phaenomena*, i.e. representations, which at any time can posit something only under conditions of time, then |\nM 123': |\nneither the world nor anything in the world is given in itself, but everything given only as far as the *progressus* reaches, and one contradicts oneself if one thinks a *totum* [abso], which is supposed to be given in itself, and yet posits it in space and time.",["composition, decomposition: Kant's Latinate words; Entstehung = origination; Zeitbedingungen = conditions of time.","*totum*: whole; [abso] is a deleted, unfinished absolutum."]],"reflm:5962":["132'. 133'. 133. E II 376. 1386.\nM 131':\nIf space and time were considered as conditions of the existence of the world in itself and *mundus Noumenon* not distinguished from the *phaenomenon*: then the causality of God with regard to the world would also be determined in time, consequently [the W] God would belong along with the world, [its] his causality would through time belong in [the] a series of causes and effects. God therefore, like everything that belongs with the world to one whole, be contingent.\nThe possibility of counting the very same subject which belongs to the world of sense, with regard to the same [connection through] categories (of cause and of existence in general), among intelligible beings, thus either in the intelligible world or outside all world, is properly the possibility of using the categories not merely with regard to the objects of the senses but for things in general, but only as for something which we do not otherwise cognize, except only that it is not appearance; which is entirely possible, because it signifies only the possibility of thinking, not of determining what is thought [to] through predicates of the world of sense.\n1. The same being which as member of the world of sense [under the] has causality that is always [according to] conditioned under rules of the determinations of time, can, as unconditioned with regard to time, with respect to the same *Caussatum* in the world of sense, be free cause of it, i.e., it |\ndoes not stand under the condition of the determination of time and yet belongs to [the s] one world with the intelligible substratum of the *mundi noumeni*.\n2. Everything that [with the] belongs in time belongs to one (g and the same) world, even if it is assumed as the cause of the things in it, and exists contingently, because time does not necessarily require the existence of things, and in general substances that were necessary belong to no whole at all and to no world. But it is possible [it] to think its existence outside time, thus also without counting it in a world, consequently without robbing it of necessity; and the category of existence is at least a concept that still always remains left over, notwithstanding that it cannot be cognized determinately, e.g., as duration without time. — In a word: if space and time are regarded as properties of things, then it is not possible to get out of the contradictions.\nM 132':\nThe cause on account of which the first two antinomies are both false is that I had to lay at the ground of both of them a contradictory concept, namely of a whole in space and time which was nevertheless supposed to be an absolute whole, consequently a thing in itself. For the conflict concerns the composition and division of an intuition, which [at all times] must necessarily happen in space and time, because [this the] these themselves make the concept of it as magnitude possible. On the other hand causality and modality of existence — or better — Those antinomies concern the [possibility] inner possibility of the appearances themselves (as *quanta*); the other however the possibility of the same through something else, thus the dependence or independence of their state or of their existence itself (i.e., the conditioned or unconditioned possibility [of their] of existence). In order to answer the first questions, I had to seek [with] the conditions |\namong the appearances alone, which however are never complete here; in order to answer the second, I could seek the unconditioned (because it can be something other than *correlatum*) outside appearance in the non-sensible, and there both propositions could be true. — Thereby the ideality of space and time is indirectly proven, because contradictions with themselves follow from the opposite. But I have also proven it directly, and indeed from this, that synthetic cognitions *a priori* are, but that these are impossible without intuition *a priori* (pure); that finally pure intuition, where its form is not given in the subject before the *obiect*, would be impossible, consequently that we can anticipate only appearances, hence all objects of the senses are nothing but appearances.\nFor the rest, the cognition of God remains entrusted to the same grounds of healthy understanding as before; only the enthusiastic audacity of deciding about it through speculation out of ignorance of the faculty of pure reason, or even of a presumption [of the] to infer so high up according to the analogy of the principles of experience, is taken away, and the determination entrusted entirely to morals.\nM 133:\nThe opposite of the proposition: »the world is infinite as to space and time« is: »it is not infinite (given)«; and then of the two propositions the latter is true. But if one asks: »what is it then, if it is not infinite in space or time?«, then one can answer in two ways; either: »it is finite in space and time«, and then one runs into nothing but absurdities, or: »as a given whole it is not in space and in time at all«; for in both an absolute whole cannot be given, because it is a whole of *phaenomena*, i.e., of representations of the senses, which are given only in [experience] perception, not in themselves (although the ground of the possible perceptions, the thing in itself, is given without perception), and which, since the *progressus* of perceptions never has a [through] boundary possible in perception, permit a *progressus* into |\ninfinity, which thus never makes up an absolute whole. This same holds of division. These two propositions can both be false, because the one contains more than is required for contradiction. [They are a] That is the logical resolution of the antinomy. But they are also both false, because they contain with it an impossible condition, namely: that the world is given whole in space and time (precisely (g as) a *compositum* given whole (g i.e. as world)), and nevertheless is given in space and time. For the first proposition is grounded on the presupposition that a whole of appearances is given in itself and outside the representations, which is contradictory. And that is the transcendental resolution of the antinomy.\nSecondly. The opposite of the proposition: »all occurrences (g things) in the (g sense-)world stand under the mechanism of natural causality«, is: »they do not stand under it«. Here both propositions cannot be false; but they could both be true, because the second contains less in itself than is required for contradictory opposition. For then it would have to be added that they as things of the world of sense do not stand under the mechanism mentioned. But because it can still take place that the things of the world of sense [also as], which are the causes of the occurrences in it, could also, considered as [things of an] intelligible beings, be causes (for that can be thought without contradiction and, without the concept: cause being cognition), they could insofar be exempted from the mechanism of natural necessity, without yet contradicting that they as things of the world of sense belonged to it.\nThe other proposition: that all things are contingent, because [it] they are determined in space and time as dependent on one another and alterable as to their existence, can be granted, and the counter-proposition does not conflict |\nwith it: that there could be some necessary being; for this is then outside space and time, consequently all dependence on the world, rather everything is to be posited as dependent on it.\nM 133:\nLeibniz's pre-established harmony is perhaps only the idea of an intelligible world without space and time, in which the divine universal presence is the principle of the real *nexus*, as of an intelligent cause, whereby the relations in which finite beings intuit themselves (as of the form of appearance) are pre-established [to agreement] in agreement with the rules already in the creation for thoroughgoing harmony with the intelligible world, in which alone the immediate truth is.",["“God therefore ... be contingent”: the German omits the finite verb (würde ... seyn), and the English keeps the ellipsis.","“that synthetic cognitions a priori are” (daß synthetische Erkentnisse a priori sind) can be read either as “that there are synthetic cognitions a priori” or as “that synthetic cognitions are a priori”; “consequently all dependence”: ausser (“outside”) governs both “space and time” and “all dependence”.","“healthy understanding” = gesunder Verstand; “determination” = Bestimmung (also “vocation”); “without the concept: cause being cognition” (ohne das der Begrif: Ursache Erkentnis ist) renders a doubtful construction; “intuit themselves” (sich ... anschauen) may also be “intuit one another”."]],"reflm:5963":["God is either at rest in space or moved or both at once (like bodies on the earth) or neither of the two. Now the last is true; thus all the others, consequently the disjunctive proposition made of the first two, are false. The truth of the last is properly the explanation of the falsity of both the first, for God is not in space at all.\nMatter (g in itself) consists either of infinitely many parts or of a determinate multitude of parts. Neither of the two is true, for it presupposes the totality of the division of space and this the absolute reality of the same.",[]],"reflm:5964":["125. E II 1382.\nM 125':\nWe can consider a disjunctive proposition as such beforehand problematically, namely as if its disjunction were not yet settled; and then it reads: each thing is either A or B, or: both A and B, or: neither A nor B.[Everything] Something in the world is either nature or chance, or both nature and chance, or neither |\nnature nor chance. If both can be true, then the opposition is not correct; if both can be false, the division is not complete.* The world either has a beginning (in time) or is from eternity. [Both cannot (g together) be true, thus the opposition is correct; but the division is incomplete, because the condition of a] Because here a condition attaches to the world, namely time, which contradicts completeness, the division is not complete, and it is neither eternal nor begun in time, because it is not total; for as appearance no totality takes place. But if I say: the human soul is either in its actions under the mechanism of nature (g\n|\nM 125: |\nif I add \"merely,\" then this is an exponible proposition) or it is free or both together or neither of the two, then the soul is taken in different *respectu*, partly as *phaenomenon*, partly as *noumenon*, since I am conscious of my absolute self not merely as appearance *a priori*. (I am: a proposition that is not empirical). and there both propositions can be true, the opposition is not correct, and totality does not take place in it as *phaenomenon*, but indeed as *noumenon* with regard to the *phaenomenon*. The flower is either red or blue or neither of the two: yellowish, or both: violet.\nM 125:\n*(g The opposition is correct, but the division is not complete. The actions of the human being are not merely nature, but by no means therefore chance; rather they are free, i.e., also to be regarded as if they did not stand in the series of the determining grounds of appearance at all, but are determined *a priori*.\nFreedom is a faculty of determining oneself *a priori* to act, not through empirical causes. Nature and chance both put the action under conditions *a posteriori*, namely the [determining] members or world-alterations that in time determine every occurrence according to its *positus*.)",["“chance” = Zufall; “division” = Eintheilung (also division in “the division is not complete”).","“merely” (blos) is the word mentioned, hence the quotation marks.","“since I am conscious of my absolute self not merely as appearance a priori”: the German leaves open whether a priori goes with “appearance” or with “conscious”."]],"reflm:5965":["In the division into nature and freedom the ground of the |\naction is taken either in the series of appearances [or], and so there is nature everywhere; or outside the series, and indeed [from the series itself] either from each member of the series separately, and then there is no nature, but nothing but chance; or from each member together with the series, and then nature and freedom are possible together, and indeed in the same subject, taken partly as *phaenomenon*, partly as *noumenon*. For the soul can posit objects for itself *a priori*, of which reason itself can also be cause *a priori*.",["“chance” = Zufall."]],"reflm:5966":["The correctness of the disjunctive proposition is settled by this, that both copulative propositions of the disjunction: the affirmative and the negative, are canceled.",["“canceled” = aufgehoben."]],"reflm:5967":["The vocation of our reason with regard to the transcendental (g cosmological) ideas is 1. [to] to regard everything in the world of sense as empirically conditioned, hence always to go on in the series of conditions, and indeed within the world (not to leap to an unconditioned outside it), and thereby to procure no ease for one's reason. Rule of immanent use.\n2. To procure the conviction that all objects of experience are nothing but appearances and that things in themselves must lie at their ground, consequently to prevent the transcendent use of the empirical. Through the latter all antinomy is removed, which rests merely on this, that appearances are considered as things in themselves.",["“vocation” = Bestimmung (also “determination”); “ease” = Gemächlichkeit."]],"reflm:5968":["That in the world of sense, in any kind of connection, nowhere an |\nabsolutely first, also no infinity as given whole can be represented, consequently no absolute totality, proves that the absolute must be thought outside it and that it itself consists only in the relation to our senses.\nIf the first can be taken according to mere conditions of sensibility, which happens in the composition and division of appearances in space and time, then the completeness of the *regressus* is impossible and there is always a conflict between the assertion of completeness through an infinite series and that through a bounded series.\nBut if the first is not necessarily a determination in space and time (dynamical idea), [then is] but can also belong to the intelligible world, which lies at the ground of the sensible one as *substratum*, then a first, consequently totality, is possible in the same world as *noumenon*, and on the other hand infinity without totality as *phaenomenon* at the same time possible.",[]],"reflm:5969":["The infinity of the *regressus* and totality [consequently the absolute] cannot be united. The bounding in the *regressus* of appearances cannot be united with space and time, i.e., sensibility.",["“bounding” = Begrenzung."]],"reflm:5970":["115. E II 1397.\nM 115':\n*In mundo non datur abyssus, saltus, casus, fatum*. For the totality of the connection of the conditioned with the condition, i.e. the absolute whole of the series of connection, is either that of the mathematical: of the composition of appearance, or of dynamical connection: of the derivation of existence. Both 1. of the way in which the series (g total) is given; 2. how a series given as a whole is resolved; mathematically: of *composition* or *decomposition*; dynamically: the first arising or the unconditioned existence in general.\n*non datur abyssus*: namely the totality through* an infinite, filled space and time as given, or through a finite series that has been given as absolutely whole in an infinite empty space and time.\n*(g the world is given neither as infinite nor as finite in an infinite space and time.)\n*non datur saltus*. The leap in the metaphysical sense is the transition from a *quantum* in appearance to the simple =0, as a part, both in the concept of appearance and its possibility and in the arising or perishing of it, i.e. of alteration. Everything in appearance is *quantum*, both insofar as it contains time or space (*extensive tale*), or fills them, i.e. is contained in time or space (*intensive tale*: reality in sensation).\n*non datur casus*. Occurrence without determining cause (in the world). The totality through determining causes in the series of appearances and in general in time is impossible. Arising of itself (*casus*) is also impossible; but the action of these same efficient subjects as things in themselves, insofar as they determine the appearances, but (since they themselves do not belong in the series of *phaenomena*) are not determined by them, is freedom. Therefore |\nM 115: |\nconnection of occurrences according to natural laws through freedom is possible, e.g. actions from intellectual principles, not sensible impulses.\n*Non datur fatum*, i.e. absolute necessity in appearance and the arising of it, but indeed of the intellectual cause, which is no part of the sensible world, nor any substratum.\n(g *Saltus** is the immediate connection of *oppositorum* in the same subject one after another. All appearances are *quanta*, and also therefore all parts of them; for were something existing simple, it could only be generated or perish *per saltum*. But the possibility of generating in time makes the possibility of the appearances.)\n*(g *est progressus immediatus a determinatione aliqva ad eius oppositum, ergo a mera negatione ad qvantum vel viceversa*.)",["*In mundo non datur abyssus, saltus, casus, fatum*: “in the world there is no abyss, leap, chance, fate”; *extensive tale* / *intensive tale*: “such extensively” / “such intensively”; *oppositorum*: “of opposites”.","The closing Latin: “is the immediate progression from some determination to its opposite, hence from mere negation to a quantum or vice versa”.","“but indeed of the intellectual cause” renders the elliptical aber wohl der intellectuellen Ursach; the genitive has no governing noun stated."]],"reflm:5971":["The immediate connection of the opposed in the same |\nsubject is not possible, except insofar as I think the 0 as the infinitely small of the quality of A. e.g. The arising of pain from indifference is only possible if this is regarded as an infinitely small pain and so intensified.",[]],"reflm:5972":["Freedom is causality without outer condition. In the *mundo phaenomeno* it does not take place. It is, however, possible that natural necessity be in this, and in the *noumeno* freedom. Just so absolute necessity in existence, not in *phaenomenis*, which exist in space and time in a certain position, which is always contingent.",["*mundo phaenomeno*, *noumeno*, *phaenomenis*: Kant's Latin case forms (“phenomenal world”, “noumenon”, “phenomena”).","“which is always contingent” (die immer zufallig ist) may refer to “position” (Stelle) or to “necessity” (Nothwendigkeit)."]],"reflm:5973":["116. E II 1394.\nM 116':\n*Non datur saltus*. Every difference (in appearance) is a *qvantum*. The *quantum*, however, must be possible through *repetitam positionem eiusdem*; therefore 0 must be regarded as homogeneous with A, but only as vanishing or infinitely small; therefore there is no *progressus* in the determination of a thing to another state except through intensification of the same quality from the infinitely small on.\nAll appearance is *quantum*, and indeed *continuum*. Therefore also the appearance of opposed determinations of the same thing, i.e. alteration, is a *continuum*.\n*Non datur casus*. No occurrence happens of itself, but is always determined through natural causes. In this way, however, no absolute totality is to be expected. But if I say: a being is a priori and therefore of itself cause of an occurrence according to the order of nature, i.e. the way in which [it] it combines such with other occurrences happens according to an order of nature, then it is freedom in one relation, namely to the subject as thing in itself, and natural necessity in another relation, as to a subject as a part in the series of appearances. — *Non datur hiatus*.\n*Non datur fatum*. All necessity is natural necessity of occurrences, i.e. always determined through other grounds in the same series. In this way, however, there is no totality in the series. But if |\nI relate the series as a whole to something outside it*, [whose] which is no *phaenomenon*, then there can be absolute necessity of the whole series, but not in it, [but] in that I go from the parts to the whole, but in that everything in the series depends on that which [is given] is thought outside it.\n(s *Lex continui*.)\n*(s The connection of the conditioned with the condition is always series; in the dynamical field, however, totality is possible through something outside the series, i.e. what is not *phaenomenon*.\nM 116: |\n*Non datur hiatus*. There is no empty space or empty time in the world; for neither is an object of possible experience. To conclude, through an inference from the difference of specific gravity, indeed also from the figure of a cavity, the possibility of enclosed empty space, presupposes after all that it is for itself an object of perception. If, however, it cut the world through into two parts, then after all a *saltus* from the one to the other would be necessary in order to connect them, and which could be inferred from nothing.)",["*repetitam positionem eiusdem*: “repeated positing of the same”; *Lex continui*: “law of continuity”; *Non datur hiatus*: “there is no gap”.","In “[it] it combines”, the deleted sie (feminine) and its replacement es (neuter) differ in gender, so they may have had different referents.","“object” renders Gegenstand, and in “an object of perception” the Latinate obiect."]],"reflm:5974":["It seems that no absolute totality is to be expected, and it is not one either, as in appearance.",["“as in appearance” (als in der Erscheinung) can be read “qua in appearance” or “except in appearance”."]],"reflm:5975":["*Non datur casus*. In the world everything happens according to the mechanism of nature, namely as consequence from that which itself happens, insofar as the world is a *phaenomenon*; except insofar as in the subject the cause of this mechanism itself is, i.e. insofar as it can be considered as *noumenon*, which [not] determines itself of itself, independently of *phaenomenis*, i.e. a reason as principle [of a] of spontaneity. Then everything happens indeed also according to the mechanism of nature in the sensible world; this combination itself, however, is grounded on the [subject] ground of the appearances in general.\nThe necessity of occurrences in nature is not the necessity of the things themselves, [this as] i.e. of the existence of nature. |\nThis, if it is attributed to the appearances, is *fatum*. Consequently there is in the existence of nature itself no inner necessity, [hence] because it is no absolute whole, hence entirely outside it.\nThe faculty to represent things in themselves and through this representation to be cause of the appearances is a faculty, according to the mechanism of nature [to act] and yet of itself to act, i.e. to effect occurrences without itself being dependent on and determined by an occurrence, i.e. freedom.\nThe faculty to give laws to oneself originally is freedom. (g The faculty, *independenter* of the mechanism of nature, to determine this mechanism itself, is freedom.)\nThe things of the sensible world can be considered in a twofold way: 1. as appearances, and then everything happens according to mechanism in general, but they are the subjects in which something happens; 2. as subjects [whose represe] which represent appearances to themselves; and then [to themselves] they do represent to themselves what happens, but nothing happens in them, rather there is in them the ground of the representations that something happen.\nIf among the things of the world we also encounter ones active according to reason, then these themselves are [not app] to that extent not appearances; for reason as cause is no object of appearance, nor determined by it, consequently to that extent free from the mechanism of nature; but yet, as far as the appearance of their effects is concerned, effective according to the mechanism of nature.",["“mechanism” renders both Mechanismus and Kant's shortened Mechanism; “of itself” renders von selbst throughout; *independenter*: “independently”.","“consequence” renders Folge; “principle” renders princip."]],"reflm:5976":["The proposition: everything happens either according to the mechanism of nature or through blind chance, permits a third, namely: through freedom, namely a [Su] sufficient ground in the world, but not as *phaenomenon*, but *noumenon*.",["“[Su]” stands for the deleted false start Zu of Zureichenden (“sufficient”)."]],"reflm:5977":["I cannot explain freedom: that it has in common with other fundamental|forces.\nI cannot, however, prove it empirically either; for it is a mere idea of something that does not belong in experience at all.",["“fundamental|forces” renders Grund|kräften (Grundkräfte)."]],"reflm:5978":["The sensible is grounded in the intellectual, but not in the same way as it is grounded in other conditions of sensibility, therefore not according to the mechanism of nature. I.e. Freedom from mechanism can subsist with [Ub]conformity to it in one subject in different significance.\nI call mechanism of nature where the causality (g of the cause) of an occurrence is itself in turn occurrence; and so it is with everything that happens, insofar as the cause is appearance; insofar, however, as the cause is thing in itself, the causality is not itself occurrence, for it does not originate in time. The objective grounds of reason and this faculty, [according to] through them to determine, remain, and herein nothing happens, and nothing alters.\n*in mundo non datur nec casus nec fatum*. For it is a connection of the sensible with the intelligible: either as *substrato* of the sensible world or as cause of the appearances [but], which is distinct from the *substrato*.\nIn the first [sense] case it is reason itself as being in the world, insofar as it can act from objective grounds; in the second a being that can be cognized only through reason, which contains the ground of the *substratorum* of the sensible world. In neither of the two cases does the cause make up a part of the series and is therefore not subject to the mechanism of nature, but determines the mechanism itself.",["*in mundo non datur nec casus nec fatum*: “in the world there is neither chance nor fate”; *substrato*, *substratorum*: Latin case forms of substratum.","“this faculty” renders dieser Vermögen, which can also be read as a genitive plural: “the objective grounds of reason and of these faculties”.","“a being that” renders ein Wesen, daß, with daß for das."]],"reflm:5979":["The world is 1. as to space and time (g no absolute totality.) not infinite nor finite (but a *progressus*, nothing further, because we can speak only of possible experience.) *non datur* |\n*abyssus*. (g *Mundus intelligibilis* is totality without relation to time and number and *progressus*.)\n2. As to division consisting neither of infinitely many nor of a finite number of parts (because we also speak only of possible experience in *decomposition*). Hence no monads. But *mundus intelligibilis* consists [of] not of *substantiis phaenomenis*, therefore of monads. These have in their outer relation nothing of space, therefore only inner forces, i.e. *vires repraesentativas*.\n*non datur saltus*. There is nothing simple in time and space, no simple alterations.\n3. In the world as sensible world everything is nature and therefore an infinite *regressus* in the series of causes without *causa simpliciter tali*; for nothing can begin absolutely and empty time, which determines nothing, precede.\nBut at the same time as intelligible world (because here I think not the connection of space and time, but of causes and effects, which are mere categories) freedom can be thought, namely, without being determined through *phaenomena*, to determine them. But freedom only where *Caussalitas intellectualis* is, i.e. in intelligences that are causes through reason.\n4. In the sensible world everything contingent, because everything is *rationatum*.",["*Mundus intelligibilis*: “intelligible world”; *substantiis phaenomenis*: “phenomenal substances”; *vires repraesentativas*: “representative forces”.","*causa simpliciter tali*: “a cause such without qualification”; *Caussalitas intellectualis*: “intellectual causality”; *rationatum*: “what is grounded”.","“absolutely” renders schlechthin."]],"reflm:5980":["The egoist or pluralist with regard to the unity or plurality of substances. The pluralist is either idealist or materialist or dualist.",[]],"reflm:5981":["One should not properly say: God has created the appearances, but: things which we do not know, to which, however, [in conformity] a sensibility in us correspondingly arranged. The things in themselves we can represent to ourselves only as thinking beings; for otherwise we have no determinations that would be distinct from appearance. Therefore |\nhe has made the actions of the thinking of these beings harmonious and interconnected outside themselves through an organization of sensibility. *Harmonia praestabilita*. It is universal and arrangement of nature. Bodies do not flow into spirits and conversely, but the former, as appearances of beings that are also spiritual, are only proportional to the latter's inner determinations. *Composita* consist of monads, but bodies not; they are *phaenomena substantiata*. *Causae occasionales* would indicate two kinds of substances, of which one would be only *phaenomenon substantiatum*, or continuous harmony of the appearances with things in themselves established. If I speak of appearances as substances [then I must] (while I nevertheless judge: they are only appearances), then I must think [pos] a pre-established harmony among them all and can yet in so doing think only the *nexum realem* among the *substantiis noumenis*.",["*Harmonia praestabilita*: “pre-established harmony”; *phaenomena substantiata*: “substantiated phenomena”; *Causae occasionales*: “occasional causes”; *nexum realem*: “real nexus”; *substantiis noumenis*: “noumenal substances”.","The clause “to which, however, ... correspondingly arranged” has no finite verb in the German; “or continuous harmony ... established” is likewise elliptical.","“[pos]” renders the deleted false start set (setzen, “posit”); “arranged” / “arrangement” render angeordnet / Naturanordnung, “organization” Einrichtung."]],"reflm:5982":["In the *mundo phaenomeno* nothing but relations are given to us in outer objects.",["*mundo phaenomeno*: “phenomenal world”."]],"reflm:5983":["The best is always the [greatest] object that is the greatest objective ground of the world; the most perfect is the [greatest] object of the greatest unity of cognition.",["“object” renders Gegenstand in the first clause and the Latinate obiect in the second."]],"reflm:5984":["146'. E II 1080. 1197.\nHow is alteration possible (hence causality)? How is *commercium* possible? among *phaenomena*, indeed.\nM 146':\n(g The *commercium* of the soul with matter as *phaenomenon* cannot be thought at all; for that would have to be in space. The soul, however, is no object of outer intuition, therefore it must think itself as well as matter as *noumenon*. Consequently also not its existence in time. If, however, it is to [think] determine this, then it must have something outside itself and in community with it, but not given in space, but whose intelligible representation it must determine (g as) in space, in order to determine its own existence in time.\nWe must determine something as in space outside us, in order to determine our own existence in time. That outside us is therefore represented before this determination as *noumenon*.)",["*commercium*: “commerce”, the scholastic term for the interaction of substances.","“indeed” renders wohl, which can also mean “presumably”.","“before this determination” renders vor dieser Bestimmung; vor may be Kant's spelling of für, “for this determination”."]],"reflm:5985":["The *commercium* of substances as phenomena in space presents no difficulty — the other is transcendent.",[]],"reflm:5986":["A nexus of substances is either real or ideal; the former is *commercium*, the latter harmony without *commercium*.",["*commercium* (community, interaction) is kept in Latin as Kant's technical term."]],"reflm:5987":["That occasionalism and praestabilism are an idealism.",["praestabilismus: the system of pre-established harmony."]],"reflm:5988":["These are not three *systemata* for explaining the *commercium*, but rather the harmony of the *substantiae* either *per commercium* or *absqve commercio*. The former is the *influxus physicus*. In the sensible world there is, by virtue of space, already a condition of the *commercium*, and the outer causality (of influence) is no harder to comprehend than the inner causality of the *actiones immanentes*. Causality cannot be comprehended at all. But if we assume substances as *noumena* (without |\nspace and time), then they are all isolated; consequently instead of space a third substance must be thought, in which they can all stand *in commercio* with one another *per influxum physicum*.",["Sinnenwelt = sensible world; begreifen = comprehend.","Einfluß = influence (cf. *influxus physicus*)."]],"reflm:5989":["The existence of things in appearance is determined according to universal laws. The sum total of all appearances is called nature in general.",["Daseyn = existence; allgemein = universal."]],"reflm:5990":["World already signifies as much as nature taken *substantive*. Without an inner principle of its alterations [would] according to laws it would not be a world as a being distinct from God.",["*substantive*: Latin adverb, i.e. taken as a noun.","The doubled würde (one deleted, one not) is kept as the AA prints it; the sentence remains ungrammatical.","Veränderung = alteration; Wesen = being."]],"reflm:5991":["Above, beside and \nThe all of nature logically [*collective*] is to be distinguished from the whole of natural things, i.e. the totality of composition; the former is thought in distinction from the intellectual, the latter in distinction from a (g mere) part of the sensible.\nThe question is: whether God has wanted to make an arrangement of nature or an arrangement of art.",["The opening words (Über, neben und) are the AA's note of where the Reflexion stands; the reference that should follow them is missing in the source.","Das All = the all; Natureinrichtung / Kunsteinrichtung = arrangement of nature / arrangement of art."]],"reflm:5992":["Above and \nThe all of nature is not the whole. The first concept is negative, namely: without exception each one; the second positive. and the concept of a *quantum* as *completum*.",["Über und: the AA's note of position; the reference that should follow is missing in the source.","quanti als completi: genitive in the source."]],"reflm:5993":["All (g philosophical) derivation of what is given in our cognition or can be given is either physical or metaphysical or hyperphysical. The first from empirical principles of nature cognized through experience; the second from the [faculty] prin|ciples\nof the possibility of our cognition *a priori* in general, independent of the (g empirically cognized) nature of things; the third from [de] the representation of objects beyond nature. The last kind brings our cognition entirely outside the conditions of the use [the] of our reason *in concreto*. The metaphysical mode of explanation is objective when it rests on the universal conditions under which alone we cognize objects as such that can be given to us. It does not exclude the supernatural, but merely limits our reason to the natural.",["*a priori* may attach to cognition or to possibility, as in the German.","objects: Gegenstände in the third clause, obiecte in the fifth sentence.","Erklärungsart = mode of explanation; schränkt ein = limits."]],"reflm:5994":["The order of nature, i.e. its form according to rules, not merely as to occurrences but as to the original predispositions, is as to its origin either automatic or organic. The first is grounded on the inner constitution of the individual, from which the interconnection in general arises; the second is grounded on an idea which makes use of the individual as an instrument for an arrangement that would not have arisen from the individual natural things according to universal laws. The first order comes about through inner forces by means of physical influence; the second through a force distinct from nature according to a pre-established harmony. The order of natural things according to pre-established harmony is not an order of original nature, but of supernatural art. E.g. figure of the heavenly bodies, proportion of their magnitudes and distances, plants. Even if we grant nature an origin, and indeed from a being in which the idea contains the ground of causality, but also at the same time of the essence of things, the order is nevertheless physical and not arbitrary.",["des einzelnen = of the individual (neuter: the individual thing); Anlagen = predispositions.","im Allgemeinen may also be read as in the universal.","Wesen = being (a being in which...) and essence (the essence of things)."]],"reflm:5995":["If in nature I think, besides the material (not merely formal) mechanism, also life, [then] i.e. an activity of natural things according to laws of the faculty of desire, then there arises the concept\n|\nof needs and of an organism, whether it be automatic or grounded on pre-established harmony. Since the things outside every living being do not act for themselves, but necessarily must originally be arranged for the need of the preservation of living natures, [it cannot suitably] the order of nature here can hardly be grounded on anything other than on the idea of an author [s], hence be organism.\nThe causality of these living beings, i.e. the determination of their faculty of desire, is either autonomy or heteronomy; in the latter case grounded through instinct as *organon* of the ends in the idea of another, in the second through freedom in the beings' own idea. In the first case there is always only a formal mechanism of nature according to physical laws, in the second a spontaneity according to practical ones, and their nature is not (g merely) organically [but] and physically, but also morally determinable. Insofar these beings do not direct themselves merely according to their natural needs, i.e. a foreign and impressed idea, but according to their own, which can arise *a priori* from themselves, and [it] their causality is freedom.\nThus all causality is either [mere] material mechanism or instinct or freedom.",["Begehrungsvermögen = faculty of desire; Urheber = author.","muß is singular in the source though its subject (die Dinge) is plural.","Kant writes im letzten Falle (the latter case) and then im Zweyten (the second) of the same alternatives; kept as written."]],"reflm:5996":["Order of nature is for experience, it is also for the recognition of the world as a particular substance, necessary.",["nothig stands only at the end and governs both clauses; the German word order is kept.","Anerkennung = recognition, acknowledgment."]],"reflm:5997":["Neither through a miracle nor through a spiritual being in the world can a motion be produced without effecting just as much motion in the opposite direction, consequently according to laws of the action and reaction of matter. For otherwise a motion of the *universum* in empty space would arise.\nBut neither can any alteration in the world (thus no beginning of that motion) arise without being determined through causes in the world according to laws of nature in general, thus not through freedom or |\noccasional miracle. For since it is not time that determines the order of occurrences, but conversely the occurrences, i.e. the appearances, determine time according to the law of nature (of causality), an occurrence that happened or were determined in time independently of this would [the] presuppose a change of empty time, consequently the world itself would be determined as to its state in absolute time.\nMotions can thus not begin of themselves, nor through anything which was not itself [moved] previously moved; and freedom is not to be met with in phenomena, nor any miracles in them that would be occasional, but at most *miracula praestabilita* in a world which itself had no beginning.",["Wirkung und Gegenwirkung = action and reaction (literally effect and counter-effect).","universi: genitive in the source; Wechsel = change; Begebenheit = occurrence."]],"reflm:5998":["The being that represents to itself alone time and itself in time cannot cognize itself as persistent; rather only that being can which is outside it.",["daß (for das) in the source.","sich selbst allein is ambiguous: to itself alone, or only itself.","es (can it) refers back to cognizing as persistent; left implicit as in the German."]],"reflm:5999":["Immortality rests on this, that the soul in itself is a substance, an intelligence and a person.\nIf immortality is to be proven, then the spiritual and not the animal must be proven from transcendental and not psychological grounds.",["immortality renders both Unsterblichkeit and the Latinate immortalität.","intelligence: intelligentz."]],"reflm:6000":["practical freedom is the consciousness of absolute self-activity,\nfreedom the faculty of the causality of pure reason.\nWhether the soul is a particular substance?",["Selbstthätigkeit = self-activity; besondere = particular."]],"reflm:6001":["The soul is in transcendental apperception *substantia Nou|menon*;\nhence no persistence of it in time; and this can only be in objects in space.",["an Gegenständen: in or on objects."]],"reflm:6002":["The first substances which lie at the ground of matter must also be simple, but give no other than composite appearances. Thus perhaps the soul too.",[]],"reflm:6003":["The difficulty of the *commercium* rests on the concept of immateriality, not on the influence of thinking on motions.",[]],"reflm:6004":["If the question is whether the intelligible *substratum* of thinking is not one and the same as that of bodies, when these are regarded as *monadatum*: then the immateriality of the soul is not proven.",["monadatum: so the source, singular against the plural diese (perhaps for monadata); doubtful reading."]],"reflm:6005":["One cannot say that soul and body are not *heterogenea* because I cannot compare them through one sense, namely the soul not through the outer, body not through the inner.",["einen Sinn: one sense or a sense."]],"reflm:6006":["Leibniz has presumably aimed his *harmonia praestabilita* at this, that 1. all alterations in the corporeal world must happen according to laws of mechanism, because otherwise, if a spirit moved them, the *centrum gravitatis vniversi* would move and, if this spirit nevertheless produced the motions according to the law of action and reaction, an alteration in the world, yet not according to the law of causality in the corporeal world, would happen; and in the spirit itself something would begin as it were in empty time, because time can only be determined through the relation to the things in space.",["Geist = spirit; Korperwelt = corporeal world.","The source has a 1. with no 2. following."]],"reflm:6007":["We have no need to prove the actuality of freedom; for it lies, as psychological, in the moral law.\nNor the possibility, [for] but only to show that there is no contradiction in it.",["Wirklichkeit = actuality.","die liegt als psychologische: the feminine may refer to freedom or to its actuality."]],"reflm:6008":["Question. 1. on the hope of a future life, 2. on immortality; as to the latter, on the perdurability of the substance as well as of the personality.",[]],"reflm:6009":["The hope of the future life can be grounded on resurrection without existence of the soul.",["Seelen Existenz: soul-existence, the existence of the soul."]],"reflm:6010":["On the *commune sensorium*.",[]],"reflm:6011":["argument either empirical or rational, either according to laws or the analogy of the laws of nature, 2. either metaphysical or moral. The proposition is transcendent.",["The source has a 2. with no preceding 1."]],"reflm:6012":["Immortality is the necessary continuance of personality.",["Fortdauer = continuance."]],"reflm:6013":["It is necessary to prove that the future life is universal and also perpetual with the identity of the person.\nImmortality (g impossibility of the end of life, of the animal indeed, but not the spiritual) of the soul is continuance of the person.",["immerwährend = perpetual; allgemein = universal."]],"reflm:6014":["(s perduration of the substance, of the personality. Separation of the soul from the body.)\nFuture life either animal or spiritual. The former either with the body of this life* (or a little body) or with another body** (metempsychosis). If it be spiritual: whether in this life we can already become conscious of this commercium.\n*(s resurrection)\n**(g Palingenesis (s either of resurrection or [in reno substitutionis] of metamorphosis; metamorphosis either of evolution or of migration.))\nState, either of the dreaming soul, or of the awakening one. The Lethean cup.\nPlace in heaven or hell.\nProgress either (g continuous) to infinity, either[per] natural or [in inf] by a leap.\n(s Great objection against immortality from the epigenesis of the soul. Moral teleology.)\n(s The separation of the soul from the body.)\nFreedom of any intelligence whatever: causality through reason or the practical faculty [p] of pure reason.\nIt cannot be proven a posteriori nor comprehended a priori.\nindependence from causes of the mundus phaenomenon determining the will.\n(s Freedom is the independence of causality from all determining causes in nature (the sensible world).)",["The asterisks here are Kant's reference marks, so no italics are used in this passage.","The deleted [in reno substitutionis] is carried over untranslated; its reading is doubtful.","corpusculum = little body; progressus = progress; facultas practica = practical faculty."]],"reflm:6015":["Above and \nThe separation of the soul from the body is the cessation of sensibility |\nand the beginning of the spiritual and intellectual life. The other world is the present one, either through other senses or spiritually intuited.",["Über und: the AA's note of position; the reference that should follow is missing in the source.","angeschaut = intuited."]],"reflm:6016":["theology is either rational or historical,\ndoctrine of reason or doctrine of faith. The latter presupposes the former; the first breaks off.",["Vernunftlehre = doctrine of reason (also the usual title for logic).","die erstere bricht ab: the first, i.e. the doctrine of reason, or possibly a remark on the text itself; ambiguous."]],"reflm:6017":["Natural Theology.\n1. The concept of the *ens* [*necessarium*] *originarium* as the being of all beings (quantity); that there are not two kinds of *entia originaria*: God and matter.\n2. Of the highest of reality — that he is not merely the ground of all reality, but its content.\n3. Of the highest of causality — cosmological proof — not Spinozism, inherence.\n4. Absolute necessity.\nTo prove the latter not merely *per hypothesin*, but to have synthetic insight into it, transcendental theology must do from the concept of an *ens originarium* [either] as content of all possibility (but then Spinozism comes of it).\na. The existence of no thing can be [prov] derived from concepts; for it is a synthetic proposition. (ontological proof.)\nb. Absolute necessity from no effect, as for instance from the contingent existence of something; and even if it must be assumed in some thing or other, it gives no properties to cognize [because from no] from which absolute necessity would follow; thus it gives only a deistic proof.\nc. Not from the constitution of the actual world. For this would require that we surveyed all possible worlds.\nd. All not physical-theoretical, not teleological, hence merely as practical presupposition, and also determination of the concept of it for the latter purpose. (s *Immutabilitas, Libertas, praescientia, determinismus, voluntas antecedens et conseqvens*.)",["entis originarii etc.: genitives in the source. Der höchsten der realitat: the noun governed by höchsten is not expressed.","Inhalt = content; Wesen aller Wesen = being of all beings.","Alle nicht physisch-theoretisch: elliptical in the source; kept so."]],"reflm:6018":["Subsistence distinct from [causality] the independence of the being *a se*. Spinoza.\nOmnipresence. Not as soul of the world, not local.\nEternity.",["independentia entis a se: *a se* may go with *ens* (the being from itself) or with independence."]],"reflm:6019":["(g The logical possibility lies in concepts, the real in God.)\nPhysicotheology. 1. The original being as cause of the world is a free cause (g because otherwise things caused would be in it): intelligent being. According to analogy: understanding. Beatitude. Will.\nPrinciple of possibility, i.e. of essences, just as the concept of the most real being is such for us.\nKnowledge of simple intelligence, free, middle.\nCreation of the world, not emanation, nor as architect. Subtle atheism. In time Conservation. Concurrence toward human freedom moral. (s End of creation.)\nProvidence through nature, immediate direction.\nDecrees. predestination. absolutism with respect to freedom.\n(s The system of cosmological apotheosis, where God is the world according to Spinozism, or the world God: Pantheism, or God the soul of the world: Zootheism.)",["Scientia simplicis intelligentiae, libera, media: the scholastic knowledge of simple intelligence, free knowledge and middle knowledge.","Concursus ad libertatem humanam moralis: moralis may qualify concursus; the Latin order is kept.","intellectus = understanding; ens realissimum = most real being."]],"reflm:6020":["The ground why we can think no absolute necessity of a |\nthing is because we can represent nothing to ourselves without thinking, can cognize nothing through mere intuition. Hence we make a distinction between possibility and actuality. Since the latter is supposed to be something that is posited over and above the thought in itself. If we could cognize through intuition, then we would find no distinction between possibility and actuality (if the former is not thought through concepts). Only what is actual is possible.",["Wirklichkeit / wirklich = actuality / actual.","\"über dem Gedanken an sich gesetzt\": \"an sich\" may go with \"Gedanken\" (the thought in itself) or with \"gesetzt\" (posited in itself); left ambiguous.","\"Da das letztere ...\" stands in the German as a sentence on its own."]],"reflm:6021":["The immutability of God must be inferred only from this, that his concept is a *conceptus Noumeni* and his existence cannot be represented as in time, consequently alteration is not a predicate of it; for otherwise from alteration there is no inferring at all to the contingency of the thing and of its alteration.",["Unveränderlichkeit / Veränderung = immutability / alteration; geschlossen, schließen = inferred, infer.","\"desselben\" (of it) may refer to God or to his existence (Daseyn)."]],"reflm:6022":["God 1. as original being, 2. as author, 3. as supreme head of the *mundi intelligibilis*.\n*causa* —\n*conditor* —\n*summus imperans, monarcha, legislator*.\nWorld in general, world of sense — intelligible world.",["Urwesen = original being; Urheber = author; Oberhaupt = supreme head.","Latin: cause, founder, supreme ruler, monarch, legislator; *mundi intelligibilis* = of the intelligible world (genitive)."]],"reflm:6023":["*ens originarium* in the logical sense is: that whose concept is derived from the concept of no thing, which therefore must be thoroughly determined a priori (from concepts). It is *omnitudo realitatis*. [Log] Its existence is *originaria* if it is necessary in itself, not as presupposition.",["\"der also ... bestimt seyn muß\": the relative \"der\" grammatically refers to the concept (Begrif).","\"existentz\" = Existenz, existence; durchgängig = thoroughly."]],"reflm:6024":["We have only through contradiction a concept of impossibility.\nOnly the possibility in appearances is thought before the actuality, because sensibility precedes the object as condition; logically the concept precedes the predicate of the thing. The concept of existence is subaltern.",["Daseyn = existence; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Gegenstand = object."]],"reflm:6025":["In the logical use of the understanding the concept precedes the thing, in the transcendental the thing precedes the concept.",[]],"reflm:6026":["The world is either *ens a se* or *causatum*. *Causa mundi est Deus*.",["Latin: a being from itself, or something caused. The cause of the world is God."]],"reflm:6027":["332'. E II 1590.\nM 332:\nThe ontological proof would have to run thus: There must exist a highest perfection in relation to possibility in general, but not: a being which we think as most highly perfect must on that account exist. For this latter does not follow.\nM 332':\nThe proposition that a being (under whichever [I its] concept I may even only think it) exists is a synthetic proposition and cannot be proved *per analysin*.",["Wesen = being; Satz = proposition; \"hochst Vollkomen\" = most highly perfect.","*per analysin* = by analysis."]],"reflm:6028":["We can originally think no possibility other than merely the form of it.\nAs regards content the real thing must be given to us.",[]],"reflm:6029":["Whether the highest perfection alone constitutes the concept of an original being.",[]],"reflm:6030":["Transcendental theology has merely negative utility.",["Nutzen = utility."]],"reflm:6031":["Concept of God an idea. *Vnicitas* — ideal. Yet only regulative.",["*Vnicitas* (= unicitas) = uniqueness, spelled as in the AA."]],"reflm:6032":["Whether the ground of the possibilities, *essentiarum*?",["*essentiarum* = of essences (genitive plural); the question is fragmentary as it stands."]],"reflm:6033":["Something exists etc etc. *derivative*; therefore something must exist *originarie*, this must be necessary.",["*derivative* / *originarie* = derivatively / originally (Latin adverbs)."]],"reflm:6034":["If the *ens originarium* is assumed as dynamically *realissimum* (*omnisufficiens* as ground), i.e. that it does not contain within itself all reality as determination, for which [it] it is the sufficient ground (e.g. not precisely understanding, although the original ground of all beings possessing understanding: Hume, or those who assume a world-creator proceeding from the original being. Tibet.): then this is entirely different from the concept where it |\nis assumed as mathematically *realissimum* and all reality as homogeneous in a single one.\n(g It depends on whether certain realities descend from others of a different kind or are only *sui generis*, i.e. can rest only on homogeneous causes. Understanding on the highest understanding.)\nThe derivation of the possibilities of things through limitation of the *Realissimi* seems to contradict itself; for reality cannot be limited in a necessary being. But the limitation of the sequel or effect to manifoldness is possible.\nThat in a *composito* all reality should be encountered is impossible, because precisely the combination in one substance is the form of the highest being. Absolute necessity is also not in the *composito*, but *simplici*.",["\"determination\" renders the loanword determination, not Bestimmung; Einschränkung = limitation; Folge = sequel; Verbindung = combination.","\"Verstandigen Wesen\" = beings possessing understanding (verständig); \"in einem einigen\" = in a single one.","*composito* / *simplici* = in a composite / in a simple (Latin ablative/dative forms kept)."]],"reflm:6035":["The *infinitudo* must not be regarded as *mathematica*, hence as *commensurabilis* with the reality of creatures. It is *ens illimitatum*.",["Latin: infinity; mathematical; commensurable; an unlimited being."]],"reflm:6036":["God is immutable because he is not in time at all. He therefore does not begin an action or cease with one. If in the human being something begins which is ascribed immediately to God, then the human being must be the cause of the altered relation. He becomes a better human being, and God becomes gracious. He is more diligent, and God stands by him. In God there is always the same principle and *actus*.\nThat God renders extraordinary assistance signifies only that besides the divine order which we know, a far more extensive one, which we do not know, is to be encountered, although both according to an order of nature from a single counsel and principle. If God were diverted by world-occurrences even in his very counsel before the world, then he would not have the immutability of an original being. From this one should conclude that no miracle is possible through a single original being. |\nWhoever denies the possibility of miracles would be able to say: there is indeed force for it, but no ground that would divert the exercise of it according to a principle from which the order of nature springs; for this ground would have to lie in nature and yet make a modification of nature necessary.",["\"princip\" / \"Princip\" (Kant's spellings) both = principle; Rathschlus = counsel; Weltbegebenheiten = world-occurrences.","\"auch selbst in seinem Rathschlusse vor der Welt\": \"selbst\" may intensify the counsel or God himself; \"vor der Welt\" may mean before the world existed or in regard to the world.","Veränderung = alteration; Abänderung = modification; Kraft = force."]],"reflm:6037":["The beginning of the world cannot be thought otherwise than as an existence before which there precedes a time in which the thing is not. An absolute beginning of the world can be thought neither in the world alone nor in dependence on God; for otherwise there must have been a state in him in which the causality of the world was not. In the course of the world God can be the cause of an alteration without altering himself. For the altered state of things brings forth altered relations to the divine activity (which is always the same). But before the existence of the world nothing could alter that had worked toward the coming-into-being, except God himself.",["\"die caussalitaet der Welt\" may be the causality of the world or the causality (of God) with respect to the world; left ambiguous.","Daseyn = existence; Zustand = state; Verhältnisse = relations."]],"reflm:6038":["It is a necessary hypothesis of [unity] reason, as of a *principii* of the unity of all our cognitions, to assume a single universal original being as *principium* of everything, to assume this being as possessing understanding, because only by its being through understanding the cause of everything is the world ordered according to rules, whereby it becomes an object for our understanding, finally as a cause through rational choice, so that it may be for us a *principium* of a rational will and of the universal unity of all our free actions. Theism is therefore not a dogmatic assertion, but a necessary hypothesis of the thoroughly concordant use of reason, chiefly of its self-sufficiency.",["\"als eines principii\": Latin genitive kept, agreeing with \"der Vernunft\".","\"obiect\" = Object, object (the Latinate term); verständig = possessing understanding; Willkür = choice.","\"Behauptung\" = assertion; \"Selbstgnugsamkeit\" = self-sufficiency."]],"reflm:6039":["Only a deity can cognize the world as one that is worthy of a deity; for this would have to contain [all mo] the highest possible perfection of a world in general, and to this belongs the cognition of all possible worlds.\nThus physicotheology has indeed this advantage, that it thinks the concept of God through [pra] *realitates in concreto* (understanding, will), but it can give no determinate concept in regard to the degree and the number of the [Urs] authors.",["The struck fragments [mo], [pra], [Urs] are left as they stand.","\"diese\" (this) may refer to the deity or to the world; *realitates in concreto* = realities in the concrete."]],"reflm:6040":["From the existence of the world one cannot prove that a God is, but that he is intelligence.\nThe highest understanding, the highest self-sufficiency, the highest will.",["\"intelligentz\" = Intelligenz, intelligence."]],"reflm:6041":["The divine understanding is called the highest and pure [unbe] understanding, which cognizes things simply, as they are in themselves. It is not sensibly conditioned. It is no receptivity, but absolute spontaneity. It is *intellectus originarius*, not *derivativus*. Its cognitions are intuitions, not concepts, but not sensible intuitions, rather ideas, which do not presuppose the things but make them possible. *intellectus archetypus*. Not thinking. Not reason by a detour. One thinks it with regard to the world *secundum analogiam*, but only insofar as its concept is supposed to be a regulative *principium*; but *per eminentiam* without any analogy, when the talk is of it *absolute*.\nM 352':\nGod cognizes all things a priori, consequently his understanding is a pure understanding. In this there must be subjective conditions of the possibility of things (but not of their appearances, for his cognition is not sensible), consequently subjective conditions of the possibility of the things in themselves.",["schlechthin = simply; \"receptivitaet\", \"spontaneitaet\" = receptivity, spontaneity; the struck [unbe] is left as it stands.","\"Man denkt ihn\": \"ihn\" may be the understanding or God.","\"Dinge\" and, in the last clause, \"Sachen\" are both rendered \"things\"."]],"reflm:6042":["On the heading »Intellectus Dei«:\nAccording to psychological concepts *in concreto*.\nThe living God.",[]],"reflm:6043":["Understanding and will, because these are the only properties that have absolute spontaneity.\nNot divine reason.",["Eigenschaften = properties."]],"reflm:6044":["On the heading »Intellectus Dei« and M § 863:\nThe highest intelligence. Author of the world. *causa libera*. (g *theologia naturalis qvoad conceptum* breaks off?)\nPhysical teleology indeed leads to an *ens intelligens*, but does not give the determinate concept of an *entis summi*.",["Latin: free cause; natural theology as regards the concept; an intelligent being; of a highest being."]],"reflm:6045":["Physicotheology needs the transcendental: 1. to supplement it, 2. to cleanse or purify it; analogy.\nThe necessity of an author distinct from the world is inferred from the contingency of the order, not of the existence. Hume: doubt that the divine perfection might be just as contingent.",["\"suppliren\", \"reinigen oder purificiren\" = supplement, cleanse or purify (the second verb Latinate).","Urheber = author; Zufälligkeit = contingency."]],"reflm:6046":["A [moral theology] theological morals is not fitting, because there morals makes the existence and the will of God into the *principio* (g of morality); but a moral theology is good, because morals is made into the *principio* of faith in a God.\nIn the first case morals is derived from the existence and will of God, in the second (g faith in) the existence of God and his will from morals.",["\"zum principio\": Latin dative after German \"zum\", kept.","Moral = morals; Sittlichkeit = morality; Glaube = faith."]],"reflm:6047":["Physicotheol. Cognitive faculty. Feel. of pleas. a. displ. Faculty of des.\nthe alone wise — blessed\n— holy\nin theoretical relation; as the highest good the order is reversed, and wisdom is derived from blessedness (kindness) and holiness.",["The abbreviations are kept: Gef. d. Lust u. Unl. = feeling of pleasure and displeasure; Begehr. Vermogen = faculty of desire.","Seeligkeit = blessedness; Gütigkeit = kindness."]],"reflm:6048":["It is hard to comprehend how another intuitive understanding should take place than the divine. For this cognizes in itself, as original ground (and *archetypo*), all things' possibility; but finite beings cannot cognize other things out of themselves, because they are not their authors, unless it be mere appearances, which they can cognize a priori*. Hence we can cognize things in themselves only in God.\n*(s But one supposes that all cognition a priori is cognition of things in themselves; it is however precisely the opposite, but always only of things as appearances, i.e. as objects of experience.)",["\"als Urgrunde (und archetypo) aller Dinge Moglichkeit\": \"aller Dinge\" may go with \"Urgrunde\" (original ground of all things) or with \"Moglichkeit\" (the possibility of all things).","begreifen = comprehend; Gegenstände = objects."]],"reflm:6049":["The divine cognition is not empirical, not pure sensible a priori. Nevertheless he cognizes our sensibility.",["\"reine sinnliche a priori\": the construction is elliptical, as in the German."]],"reflm:6050":["355. E II 236. 306.\nOn philosophical enthusiasm.*\nPlato remarked correctly that through experience we do not know things as they are in themselves, but only learn to connect their appearances [according to determin] in conformity with law. (Further he saw that to cognize things according to what they are in themselves [an intuition a cognition would require which not] would also require an intuition of things in themselves, i.e., pure intellectual intuition, of which we are not capable.) He remarked that, in order that our representation agree with the object, it must be thought either as produced by the object or (g as) producing the object. The latter would be the original representation (*idea archetypa*), of which we human beings, if it is to be original in all respects, are not capable. Thus the ideas can be encountered only in the original being. [Now he believed all] The ideas of this original understanding, however, can be not concepts, but only intuitions, but intellectual ones. Now he believed all cognitions a priori to be cognitions of things in themselves**, and since we partake of the former, so we also partake of the latter, and among these he counted mathematics. From ourselves, however, we cannot come to partake of them, consequently only through communication of the divine ideas. [Now] But since we are conscious of them not as merely historically imparted and transferred, but as immediately seen into: so they must be not implanted |\nconcepts, which are believed, but immediate intuitions, which we have of the archetypes in the divine understanding. [Thus they are intu] We can, however, develop these only with effort. Thus they are mere recollections of the old ideas from the community with God. Now this would not yet be enthusiasm, but merely a way of explaining the possibility of cognitions a priori. But now comes a conjecture of partaking even now of this community with God and of the immediate intuition of these ideas (mystical intuition), and also of finding therein the [obj] immediate object of all of one's inclinations, which only from misunderstanding have hitherto turned to appearances as the types of it (mystical love of God). But since it is [geni] probable that there is between us and God a great [ser] ladder of creatures, which extend from us up to him (g *genii*), astral spirits, aeons, one could first attain to community with these and to the prelude of intellectual original intuitions. But since the original ideas are the cause of the actuality of their objects, one could thereby still hope to exercise a supremacy over nature. and so the Neoplatonic school, which called itself eclectic in that it professed to find its wisdom in all the ancients, because it put [such] its reveries into them, was finished with all the raving enthusiasm with which it has visited the world. (g finally Spinozism. (Theosophy through intuiting.)) The Aristotelian philosophy displaced this delusion. One began from concepts at which we arrive on the occasion of experience (*nihil est in intellectu* —). But now |\nM 355: |\none arrived at cognitions a priori without investigating how this is possible according to the above principle. These expanded, and, because everything that remains within the sensible world is always conditioned, reason drove the principles valid there higher up and out beyond the sensible world, in the confidence that they will yield [always] an acquisition just as secure as they had up to then given explanation of what is present. Now the subjective conditions |\nof reason with regard to comprehensibility began to be taken for objective conditions of the things in themselves, and, since the former is not content until it has encompassed the whole, conquests to be made in the supersensible world. Now because no boundaries are to be specified where one could stop here, one finally had to, since their individual and separate possibility of existing had been taken from all things, also take from them separate existence and leave them only inherence in one subject. Spinozism is the true conclusion of dogmatizing metaphysics. Critique of the propositions accomplishes nothing here; for the distinction of the subjective from the objective with regard to their validity [direct] cannot be called to mind there, because those subjective ones that are at the same time objective are not distinguished beforehand. The necessity of assuming them is once there, and one does not notice that it is merely subjective. That experience is possible only through principles a priori occurs to no one. Only critique of reason itself can accomplish something here. Meanwhile men of a reason bright and surveying the great domain of the use and misuse of opinions still hold up this decline into enthusiasm for a time. — If one will not take the path of critique, then one must let enthusiasm go its way and laugh at it with Shaftesbury.\n*(g The great distinction of intellectual from empirical cognition misled the ancients into this.)\n(g Sect in China: I am the highest being.\nSect in Tibet: God is the assembly of all the saints.)\n**(g And it is precisely the opposite. For only of things as appearances can there be synthetic cognitions a priori. For the form of sensibility with regard to intuition can be cognized prior to all objects, for it is given in the subject. Space and time. In this intuition, however, many propositions a priori can be given synthetically, which then hold of all objects of possible experience, but also of none further, although the concepts of them as objects in general are merely intellectual, but are only monograms, which give nothing to cognize *in concreto*, but only cognition *in abstracto*.)\n(g analytic cognitions one has, to be sure, a priori, when the concept of the object is given, whether it be an empirical or a rational one. But synthetic judgments a priori would not be possible without pure intellectual intuitions, which are encountered only in God. What human beings are to cognize synthetically, and indeed a priori, must have an object of sensible intuition as its object.)",["\"Enthusiasm\" renders Schwärmerey throughout; \"object\" renders both obiect (Latinate) and Gegenstand, e.g. in the last sentence, \"ein obiect sinnlicher Anschauung zum Gegenstande\".","\"was finished with\" renders \"fertig mit\", which can also mean \"ready with\"; \"conquests to be made\" keeps the German's elliptical infinitive (\"Eroberungen ... gemacht zu werden\"), governed by \"began\".","\"the types of it\": derselben may refer to the immediate object or to the inclinations."]],"reflm:6051":["354. E II 237. 238.\n M 354':\n* The origin of all philosophical enthusiasm lies in Plato's original divine intuitions of all possible objects, i.e., the ideas, since we intuit them only through their appearances, thus only passively. Now on this is grounded first [the conjecture] Plato's opinion that all our cognition a priori [chiefly] (mathematics), chiefly that of perfections, derives from the recollection of these former intuitions and that we must now seek to develop these (g only) ever further; from this, however, arises the second step of mysticism, to intuit everything even now in God, which then makes all investigation of synthetic cognition a priori unnecessary, in that we read it in God; third, since other beings may be nearer to God, that we, so to speak, must perhaps first come to know those |\nideas through reflection, consequently associate with spiritual natures, etc.\n*(s Even before Plato one distinguished intellectual cognitions from the [sen] empirical [which one] and understood the latter when one named sensible ones, and even made a distinction between intelligible and sensible things. One held all cognition a priori to be [sensi] intellectual, thus also mathematics; and since various |\nM 354: |\nsensitive things and really only this can be cognized a priori, one had examples of a supposed intellectual cognition. But to find this distinction important, a need of reason was necessary to go out beyond the empirical, because this is always conditioned and therefore cannot be a thing in itself, which must at all times have its complete conditions.\nThe necessity of the hypothesis of such a something one held to be insight into the necessity of these things.)\nM 354':\nThe highest degree of enthusiasm is that we ourselves are in God and [ourselves] feel or intuit our existence in him. The second: that we intuit all things according to their true nature only in God as their cause and in his ideas as archetypes. The third: that we do not intuit them at all, but yet derive them only from the concept of him and thus infer from our existence and our concepts of reason of things straight to the existence of God, in whom alone they can have objective reality. Now back from the lowest degree to the highest: Spinoza.",["\"since various ... sensitive things and really only this can be cognized a priori\": the German (\"da verschiednes ... sensitive und eigentlich nur dieses\") is obscure across the page break; \"things\" is not in the text.","\"since we intuit them only through their appearances\": the German order (\"da wir nur sie\") lets \"only\" attach to \"them\" as well."]],"reflm:6052":["The cause of enthusiasm is the lack of critique of reason itself; for if I by my own powers attain to the descent of all things from One and also to the qualities of this One, how do I come to this? 1. Through analysis of my concepts? Then the concept would have to be originally inspired in me. 2. Through the synthesis of the laws of experience? But there I remain in the world. 3. Through |\nprinciples that I take from neither of the two? These must then be secret intuition of the supersensible.",["\"analysis\" renders Zergliederung; \"descent\" renders Abstammung."]],"reflm:6053":["Superstitious religion is grounded on a principle of the subjection of reason to the delusion of perceptions.\nThe human being raises himself in enthusiasm above humanity.",["\"delusion\" renders Wahn; \"humanity\" renders Menschheit."]],"reflm:6054":["Delusion is the persuasion of the dispensability of the testing of one's judgments according to universal laws of nature.",[]],"reflm:6055":["On Plato's ideas as self-subsistent archetypes. The idea of humanity as the ground of the existence of the world. To these ideas we must make our judgment conformable.\n*Theosophia* and *Theurgia*. *Theologia Mystica*.",[]],"reflm:6056":["Anthropomorphism thought regulatively (g is the relation of an unknown cause [to th] according to laws of sensibility) is nothing but [the sche] the conditions of sensibility applied to divine actions as a schema of the application of them in the use of experience. Anthropomorphism constitutive is the absolute representation of the divine properties according to laws of sensibility. The latter gives the opponents openings; without the former we are deists.",["\"gives the opponents openings\" renders \"giebt den Gegnern Blößen\" (exposes weak points to them).","\"of them\" (derselben) may refer to the conditions of sensibility or to the divine actions."]],"reflm:6057":["God has given the human being not independence from him (God) himself, but from the incentives of sensibility, i.e., [moral] practical freedom. The actions of it are appearances and to that extent under merely inner conditions of humanity. To them be|long\nalso punishments and rewards. What God did is all good, but does not lie in the sensible world as a mere schema of the intelligible. Thus space is nothing in itself and no thing as divine work, but lies in us and can also take place only in us. Likewise the agreeable and its distinction from the good. Appearances are really not creatures, thus also not the human being; rather he is merely the appearance of a divine creature. His state of acting and suffering is appearance and rests on him as bodies on space. The human being is *principium originarium* of appearances.",["\"The actions of it\": derselben may refer to freedom or to the human being; \"incentives\" renders Triebfedern."]],"reflm:6058":["Because the understanding *originario* is the faculty of considering itself in relation to objects, the causality of an *entis originarii*, or what determines it, can rest on it alone.",["*originario* (ablative) stands without a clear construction; it may qualify the understanding (\"originally\") or be a slip for *originarius*.","\"it\" in \"what determines it\" (sie) is the causality; \"on it alone\" (auf ihm) is the understanding."]],"reflm:6059":["The question is: what determines a self-sufficient being to causality outside it. It is content for itself.",["\"self-sufficient\" renders \"sich selbst gnugsam\"; \"outside it\" (außer ihm) could also be \"outside him\"."]],"reflm:6060":["The highest understanding of God, his highest self-sufficiency, this as all-sufficiency combined with the cognition of all other possible things outside him, the causality with regard to the greatest whole of them, consequently the (g *summum*) *bonum originarium* the origin of the *summi boni derivativi*.",["\"self-sufficiency\" renders Selbstgenugsamkeit, \"all-sufficiency\" Allgnügsamkeit.","*bonum originarium*: original good; *summi boni derivativi*: of the derivative highest good. The sentence has no main verb."]],"reflm:6061":["The will is nothing other than causality through the understanding. This is, by means of the self-contentment in its all-sufficiency, |\nseen into. — Whether a physicotheology without a cause, without understanding and will, is possible?",["\"seen into\" renders eingesehen (has insight into); \"self-contentment\" renders Selbstzufriedenheit; \"its\" (seiner) may be \"his\"."]],"reflm:6062":["*Intellectus* (understanding) — *actiones immauentes* (immanent actions)\n*Voluntas* (will) —\n*transeuntes* (transient).",["*immauentes* is the transcription's reading, presumably for *immanentes*."]],"reflm:6063":["The highest being is conscious of its all-sufficiency through understanding and has in this (in itself) the highest satisfaction. In this, now, consists the causality of it.",["\"satisfaction\" renders Wohlgefallen; \"in itself\" renders \"an sich selbst\", here reflexive (in itself, i.e., in the being), not the technical \"an sich\"."]],"reflm:6064":["Under and \nThe consciousness of its existence as an (g in itself) sufficient ground of everything else is self-sufficiency.\nThe causality of representations with regard to the existence of the objects is desire, the relation of the [objects] representations to the subject [and its], to determine the power of it or causality, is pleasure.",["\"Under and\" (Unter und) is a fragment standing alone in the transcription.","\"desire\" renders Begierde, \"pleasure\" Lust; \"its existence\" (seines Daseyns) may also be \"his\"."]],"reflm:6065":["A will without incentives from the object. Satisfaction, not stimulus or need.",["\"object\" renders obiect; \"stimulus\" renders Anreitz."]],"reflm:6066":["Divine feeling. Self-sufficiency with regard to the feeling of pleasure in its existence is blessedness.",["\"blessedness\" renders Seeligkeit; \"its\" (seinem) may be \"his\"."]],"reflm:6067":["The understanding determines in him immediately the will, not through the mediation of a pleasure or displeasure; the will of God is not a faculty dependent on the feeling of the object.",["\"object\" renders obiect."]],"reflm:6068":["Feeling of pleasure and displeasure in God is self-contentment; how is a willing of objects outside him possible with this?",[]],"reflm:6069":["We can here make out no interest that God would take in the existence of things.",[]],"reflm:6070":["How does the actuality of things outside him interest God? What motive has he to create them. He has indeed an aim, but no motive. Omnisufficiency.",["\"motive\" renders Bewegursache (= Bewegungsgrund), \"aim\" absicht; \"Omnisufficiency\" renders the hybrid Omnisufficientz."]],"reflm:6071":["The divine understanding is a pure understanding, not affected by sensibility, thus also the will a pure will.\nThe divine will desires nothing.\nThe pleasure in the existence of the object does not precede. The pleasure in its all-sufficiency.\nA will that, without mediation of a feeling of pleasure in the object, is determined immediately through the understanding is free.",["\"object\" renders Gegenstand in the third line and obiect in the last; \"its\" (seiner) may be \"his\"."]],"reflm:6072":["A manifold cannot agree with ends (which are concepts) unless either this manifold must be the cause of the understanding or of thinking and of the representation of the ends, i.e., it must be able to think, or [d] a thinking being must be the cause of the manifold and of its purposive combination.",["\"unless either\" renders \"als entweder\"; \"ends\" renders Zweke/Zwecke."]],"reflm:6073":["Cause of the world through freedom, i.e. author. Not, however, on account of stimuli through intellectual appetition, but by [merely] merely objective reasons determined through the understanding, subjectively through acquiescence in itself alone.",["“determined” renders *determinatus*, which agrees with *causa* / *autor*.","“understanding” renders *intellectus*; “reasons” renders *rationes*."]],"reflm:6074":["Opposite and also probably \nThe causality of the divine understanding with regard to the objects, which stand in the greatest connection with it and among one another, [is] is the will itself. And the consciousness of this all-sufficient will the highest pleasure, which therefore is not immediately in the object, but in it|\nself: just as in a moral action the pleasure not in the object, but its own good will brings forth the deed.\nNot cause of possibility, also not limited by external impossibility.",["The editorial location note “Gegenüber und auch wohl” is cut off in the source.","“object” renders both obiect and Gegenstand (“am Gegenstande”).","The clause after “just as” is elliptical in the German (“die Lust nicht am obiect, sondern seinem eigenen Guten Willen die that hervorbringt”) and is left so."]],"reflm:6075":["External effects of God we can, as things contingent in themselves, ascribe only to freedom, internal actions (as the cognition of God) only to natural necessity. With the former the idea of the best must lie at the ground, with the second the evil as well as the good is the object.",["“the cognition of God” (die Erkentnis Gottes) is ambiguous, as in the German: God's cognition, or cognition of God."]],"reflm:6076":["Opposite and \nThe negative concept of freedom is independence,\nThe positive concept: autonomy through reason.",["The editorial location note “Gegenüber und” is cut off in the source."]],"reflm:6077":["Opposite and \nTranscendental freedom (of substance in general) is absolute spontaneity to act (as distinguished from *spontaneitas secundum qvid*, where the subject is yet determined *aliunde* by *causas physice influentes*). Practical freedom is the faculty to act from mere reason.\nThe causality in the case of freedom is *originaria*, although the *caussa* is *ens derivativum*.",["The editorial location note “Gegenüber und” is cut off in the source.","*spontaneitas secundum qvid*: “spontaneity in a certain respect”; *aliunde*: “from elsewhere”; *causas physice influentes*: “physically influencing causes”.","*originaria*: “original”; *caussa*: “cause”; *ens derivativum*: “derivative being”."]],"reflm:6078":["The [divine] freedom of the divine will does not consist in its having been able to choose also something other than the best; for not even human freedom consists in that, but in that [it] the idea of the best necessarily determined it, which the human being lacks and therefore also limits his freedom.",["“it” renders er, which may refer to the will (der Wille) or to God."]],"reflm:6079":["M § 898 end:\nThis *fatalismus theologicus* would take place if God had created the world in |\ntime; for one would have to posit for it a fate of the determination of his power.",["*fatalismus theologicus*: “theological fatalism”.","“it” renders ihr (the world); “determination” renders Bestimmung."]],"reflm:6080":["Divine blessedness.\nDifficulty concerning the freedom of the human being under the divine *decreto*.\nPhysicotheological proof from the ends of nature does not alone suffice, but is sufficient with a practical aim for the sound understanding.",["“blessedness” takes Seelichkeit as Seligkeit; the spelling could also suggest “soul-ness” (from Seele).","*decreto*: “decree” (ablative).","“does not suffice” renders reicht nicht zu; “sufficient” renders hinreichend."]],"reflm:6081":["The divine will is with regard to the end either manifest or hidden. Presumption, to impute our connection in willing to the godhead. Unsearchable are his ways. Even in the general of nature.",["“to impute our connection in willing to the godhead” could also be read “to impute our connection to the willing of the godhead” (der Gottheit as genitive or dative).","“manifest” renders offenbar, “hidden” verborgen.","“the general of nature” renders im allgemeinen der Natur, which has no noun after allgemeinen."]],"reflm:6082":["The will in the use of the means with regard to a presupposed end is indeed often manifest, but with regard to the end itself hidden; e.g. why the human being is formed thus or thus is manifest, but hidden, to what end in general the human race exists.\nIf once a human being is to exist, then it is manifest that he must be upright, wise, etc. etc. But that a human being (g and why he) must exist is hidden. what always remains hidden is unsearchable.",[]],"reflm:6083":["We can think in the proper psychological sense no end of God, because this indicates dependence of contentment, therefore only an *analogon* of the ends in the world. The divine end is a satisfaction without interest, where therefore the existence would be indifferent, which contradicts itself.",["“sense” renders Verstande (im eigentlichen psychologischen Verstande).","“contentment” renders Zufriedenheit, “satisfaction” Wohlgefallen."]],"reflm:6084":["The perfection, order and purposiveness of the world makes the relation to an original will, hence physicotheology, necessary, which for its part extends the knowledge of the world as a system of ends.\nBut the incomprehensibility of the divine will, if one separates off all anthropomorphism [and], makes it that one can derive this perfection also as from the essence of God as the origin of all beings and [which itself] thus from the essence of things themselves, because the will is only the self-contentment of God with himself as a being of all beings.\nBut the unsearchableness of this will limits our physicotheology in the determination of the divine ends to nature. It is soothing.",["“essence” and “being(s)” both render Wesen.","“knowledge” renders Kentnis."]],"reflm:6085":["We have brought the psychological properties of the divine will to an end. It is incomprehensible. The ontological ones are thereby understood *tacite* — Physicotheology is possible.",["*tacite*: “tacitly”."]],"reflm:6086":["At the very top of the page:\nOn moral theology.\nWhether the concept of God is a merely speculative or moral concept? How it arises from morals as a cognition of reason *a priori* (not for the sake of experiences), and how it makes the concept of God also ontologically determinate, which physicotheology does not do.",["“morals” renders Moral; “cognition of reason” renders Vernunfterkenntnis."]],"reflm:6087":["The reader feels a certain shy [fear] apprehension about engaging in the considerations and objections of Hume, and sees in it the expression of a presumption. On the other hand, something noble, sincere and unhypocritical does also shine forth from it, to venture upon judging without slavish anxiety, like Job, not in order to condemn God's ways |\nbut to confess one's scruples openly to oneself [and], without letting oneself be led, through apprehension that one will thereby become irreverent, into suppression of them and into flattering eulogies, like Job's friends. The government of God is not despotic, but paternal. It does not say: do not reason, but obey, but rather: reason diligently, so that from your own conviction you can freely and unintimidated show God the veneration which [nothing] would be of no worth at all if it were extorted. With the slavishly believing, and just for that reason also tyrannically moving others to this belief, nothing can be done. Whoever has love of peace does not start with them on grounds of reason.",["“reason” in “do not reason … reason diligently” renders räsonnirt / raisonnirt, not Vernunft; “grounds of reason” renders Vernunftgründe.","“it” renders darin / daraus, which may refer to the engaging or to Hume's considerations and objections."]],"reflm:6088":["The lawgiver must be benevolent; otherwise his command makes no obligation to obedience (He must also be omnipotent); but his law must, if he is wise, be holy. As lawgiver God is thus holy, as regent benevolent, and, since the latter is possible only under the condition of agreement with the former, for the sake of wisdom, he is as judge just.",["“benevolent” renders gütig; “obligation” renders Verbindlichkeit."]],"reflm:6089":["Benevolence under the limiting condition of the worthiness of the *subditi* is justice. It thus presupposes an *imperium*. God as ruler is benevolent, but under limiting conditions of his holiness. Legislative holiness is the perfection of the will, not merely of the legality of actions, but also of morality. The divine will is holy; it demands morality.",["*subditi*: “subject” (genitive), i.e. one ruled; *imperium*: “rule, command”.","“benevolence” renders Gütigkeit."]],"reflm:6090":["Benevolence, insofar as it is limited to conditions of lawfulness, is justice.\n(s Is the wisdom of God in the limitation of his benevolence |\nto the condition of the agreement of rational beings with his holy law.)",["The parenthesis lacks a subject (“Ist die Weisheit Gottes …”); it is left so."]],"reflm:6091":["370. E I 680.\nWe are not in a position to cognize the wisdom and benevolence of God in this world *in concreto* and at the same time in the whole interconnection, because we know rational beings as [objects] subjects of all ends only on the earth. The new astronomy has in this created great utility for theology. For if we had to regard our earth as the whole stage of divine wisdom, great scruples would arise.\nOn our earth the arrangement has been made that we should bring forth all good, both in ourselves and in our external state, even the knowledge of the good and the pleasure and satisfaction in it, out of ourselves. Then it was impossible that we should be guided by agreeableness; for then we would have had to be able to foresee the good and also to have pleasure in it (we were, however, to bring ourselves to our vocation through culture). Thus activity had to be the course of our vocation, the goad of activity, however, pain. Here, however, it could also not happen that each individual [its] reached the vocation of its existence [fulfilled]; thus deficiencies always remained. Only the genus was to reach it. Elsewhere it may be otherwise; also in the future; belief.\nThe disproportion between our natural predisposition and its development in each *individuo* gives ground for belief in a future. We need not assume God beforehand as benevolent, but only conclude according to the analogy of nature even without God, then a guidance toward highest wisdom will always be encountered here, and a theology that flows from the firm resolution of progress toward perfection.\nM 370:\nHuman beings were even to bring forth out of themselves a system of the public good with a general inclination to refer everything to private|\ninclination, from which nothing but strife and violence arises. They were to need from their own species a master or at least a supreme authority, and many apart from one another. They were to invent universal peace.\nThe role of a human being is perhaps among all of this planetary system the most artful, the most arduous, but also in its outcome the most glorious. Happiness we have here too, but of course according to our concept of happiness not *in abstracto*, but *in concreto*; for we cannot think one up for ourselves, without in the striving to work our way through hindrances, in work dangers, in a word: in the reward of our power to bring it out of ill.",["“vocation” renders Bestimmung throughout; “[fulfilled]” (erfüllete) is the deleted word standing before erreichte (“reached”), moved to the end here.","“knowledge” renders Kentnis; “belief” renders Glauben; “genus” renders Gattung.","The last sentence breaks off after ohne (“without”) with no verb; “it” renders sie, which may refer to happiness or to our power."]],"reflm:6092":["We speak of happiness as contentment, but [we] as of an idea which we have *in abstracto*, whose reality, however, we can exhibit through no determination *in concreto*, not even in fiction. If it is to be a complete satisfaction of all desires: why then desire, i.e. lack, in order to acquire it? For this does signify that one was not happy before. (Moreover, the satisfaction of desire increases the longing for more and thereby makes contentment entirely impossible.) If, on the other hand, we are to be in possession of well-being without desiring anything, then this state seems to make the being entirely useless, because there is no activity in it; moreover, persistence in the same state in a being that drags its existence through time, [only] and yet with complete contentment, is impossible, because a part of its existence always lies ahead of it, with regard to which it expects something different from the previous state.\nReligion can be moral only insofar as we venerate God in a threefold person. [Thus] As lawgiver he cannot be benevolent, i.e. his law cannot be subordinated to the aim at the welfare of the creature or mixed with it. It is directed at freedom and not at happiness: that the creatures become worthy of happiness, not that they |\nbecome happy; otherwise we represent his law to ourselves as indulgent, suited to our weaknesses and yielding, but not as holy. As benevolent [has he the The] and from no other ground he wills the existence of the creature; but as holy he wills, if they exist, that they must conduct themselves in order to become partakers of benevolence. As just he does presuppose benevolence, but with limitation through the holiness of the law; justice, however, is not benevolent, it is also not (g merely) holy, but benevolence in conformity with the holiness of the law is distributive justice. As benevolent he would not punish, as holy he will not reward (for everything is due), for he demands the deed regardless of the happiness of the state. Thus justice is the third personality.\nIn human beings the three personalities are distributed in three *individuis*, in God it is a threefold personality. This, however, is only in the concept of God with a practical, not speculative aim: an idea of the relation to human morality and freedom.",["“satisfaction” renders Befriedigung; “contentment” Zufriedenheit; “well-being” wohlbefinden; “welfare” Wohl.","“everything is due” renders alles ist schuldigkeit.","“that they must conduct themselves in order to become” follows the German, which has no “so” or “how” (daß sie sich verhalten müssen, um …)."]],"reflm:6093":["That is the holy symbol of moral theology, the monogram of his mysterious being, but in order to prevent theosophy and theurgy.\nThe threefold function in relation must presuppose in God a threefold original principle of his activity; but this we cannot have insight into.\nNumerical identity is the unity of the *individui*: of that which in different relations was considered as many.\nThe [specula] consideration of God in this threefold personality is not theoretical, but moral. theology as theosophy is presumption and enthusiasm.",["“his” renders seines, which may refer to God or to the symbol.","[specula] is a deleted, unfinished word (perhaps for Speculation) and is left as it stands.","“enthusiasm” renders Schwarmerey; *individui*: “of the individual”."]],"reflm:6094":["Theology is no theosophy, to cognize God according to his nature in itself, but only in relation to us and the morality of our will. Likewise religion is no theurgy, to have an [immediate] influence on God and his will through formulas, through exertions of the spirit, purifications, penances, but to direct the cognition of the same to the improvement of our selves. The question is |\nonly: what makes us better human beings? The moral concept of God and of the threefold personality relative to our practical maxims, not with regard to his own nature. Through good conduct of life one does not attempt to act upon God, but only to make oneself receptive to divine benevolence.",["The first sentence is construed as in the German: the infinitive (“to cognize”) may state what theosophy does or what theology does.","“of the same” renders desselben, which may refer to God or to his will."]],"reflm:6095":["The judge is in himself indifferent, he does not pronounce his judgment out of love, is impartial; he cannot be moved by entreaty, he can only [be] pronounce according to laws. This becomes very confused if we assume benevolence and justice in one person. But also if we take holiness and justice in one. For then he does not pronounce from the standpoint of a human being. Who can stand against him? Job. He has, besides the laws, benevolence in advance and is ready to bestow good fortune.",["[be] is a deleted, unfinished prefix and is left as it stands; “pronounce” renders sprechen throughout.","“has … in advance” renders voraus hat, which can also mean “has as an advantage”."]],"reflm:6096":["3fold personality, which I think to myself in the highest good. 1. I desire to be happy from inclination and relate [my] this wish to an author of happiness. 2. I cognize myself as subject to the moral law through reason and relate this obligation to the will of a highest being, which can obligate me, because it has my fate wholly in its power. 3. The reason in me resolves upon happiness only insofar as I act in conformity with my duty. And this I relate to a third person, whose place conscience takes. [Al] A benevolent person thus represents my inclination, the other my reason, the third my conscience.",["“personality” renders Personlichkeit; “obligation” Verbindlichkeit, “obligate” verbinden (Kant's capitalized Verbinden).","“resolves upon” renders beschließt, which can also mean “concludes”.","“benevolent” renders Gütige; [Al] is a deleted beginning and is not translated."]],"reflm:6097":["Subjectively considered my own person is the first. I would gladly wish that my happiness were limited by no conditions at all. But objectively I must represent my happiness through another person, and reason is the first person, which prescribes the con|ditions\nof happiness. Subjectively (g from inclination) I would gladly like the application of the moral law to me to take place according to goodness [appli]; but reason teaches that it take place not merely according to the holy law (g also not according to goodness alone), but [from] according to goodness combined with holiness, i.e. according to justice.",["“goodness” renders Gütigkeit throughout; “holiness” Heiligkeit; “justice” Gerechtigkeit.","“limited” renders eingeschrankt (einschränken)."]],"reflm:6098":["The question is: whether the concept of God is a concept necessary with a physiological or a moral aim. since (g in that case) nature must be regarded as contingent, the inner necessity cannot be concluded from it, but is only a necessary hypothesis (namely among the properties that are to make up the concept of a deity), in order to state [the] from the phenomena the highest [sufficient] ground adequate for them (not for all other questions of reason). For the perfection and magnitude of the world do not require the presupposition of an [perf highest] all-sufficient being. Thus it is not determined as a physiological basic concept and its existence only hypothesis.\nWere morality grounded on physiological conditions, then the very same would follow. We would have to presuppose the existence of god only with regard to our need and aim at happiness. But all morals is necessary from the essence of things, and that without which it would have no objective reality is necessary a priori, but only in practical relation. Also with regard to it God must first be represented as the highest good, thereupon, so that he can be it, also as the highest being.",["“with a … aim” renders in … Absicht; “physiological” keeps Kant's physiologisch (pertaining to nature).","“sufficient” renders the deleted zureichenden; “adequate” renders hinreichenden; “all-sufficient” allgnugsamen. [voll hochsten] is rendered as the deleted fragment “perf highest”.","“it” in “Thus it is not determined” renders er, which may refer to Gott or to der Begrif; the English “it” keeps the second reading, “he” in the last sentence the first."]],"reflm:6099":["M 372':\n(g If it were possible to know that there is a God, would there then still remain morality in the human being.)\nIn moral theology\nGod is represented as the head in a necessary system of ends, not of a contingent system, which we assume hypothetically as universal and believe, but which we know, |\nnot through choice among the contingent and a will that sets ends at its pleasure, but whose will is one and the same with that which in the order of ends among rational beings is absolutely necessary, consequently not as despot, but as the highest good itself through the nature of things in general. God as *summum bonum originarium*. The moral properties are not his [subordination] supreme dominion over beings according to laws of morality, but the self-sufficiency of these laws themselves. Here we lay at the ground what we know with certainty, namely the necessary system of all ends, to which all others must be subordinated as their *conditio sine qva non*. Hence the errors of moral theology are properly pernicious, above all anthropomorphism, which however can be more easily prevented, in that we regard God not as author of the laws but of the obligation according to these laws.\nM 373':\nThereby moral theology becomes at the same time ontotheology. It is nonetheless a merely practical and subjective certainty, which is grounded on an interest, but on an interest objectively necessary and inseparable from the essence of reason, subordinated with no aim. Were morals only a pragmatic system of prudence, then belief in God would be merely hypothesis; now it is a postulate.\nThe highest wisdom in physicotheology is merely relative. and concerns adequacy with regard to the system of all ends; but in moral theology it is self-sufficiently the source of the possibility of things themselves. Thereby we are led to derive everything from the order of things as necessary in itself and yet at the same time from God as the self-sufficient principle of all order, not to turn to God as to the being that gives laws which are contingent in themselves and from which it can dispense, but to expect what is necessary in the nature of things according to laws of morality also from nature according to physiological laws.\nAll moral properties of God are here to be determined only objectively according to our concepts, not subjectively — for in the latter case we have two *correlata*, whose connection we could represent to ourselves anthropomor|phically:\nthe moral order and God, who wills it. but his will is this moral order itself. He is benevolent and indeed infinitely, because from him in himself all possible good flows, but also holy, because it flows only according to the moral order, and just, because this goodness does not establish the moral order, but is determined according to it.",["*summum bonum originarium*: “original highest good”; *conditio sine qva non*: “indispensable condition”.","“self-sufficiency”, “self-sufficient(ly)” render selbstandigkeit, selbstandig; “adequacy” renders Zulanglichkeit.","“in himself” renders an sich, which may attach to ihm or to fließt."]],"reflm:6100":["Justice is limitation of goodness through holiness. It is thus properly not rewarding (s for were the reward merely in conformity with justice, we would be able to expect little), but only goodness rewards. For where [holiness] justice does not limit goodness according to principles of holiness, there goodness acts.\nProperly we ourselves limit the divine goodness through our guilt, and punishments may not be regarded as positive.",["“goodness” renders Gütigkeit; “guilt” renders Schuld, which also means “fault” or “debt”."]],"reflm:6101":["How evil is possible under a good God rests on the question how freedom is possible in beings that are yet wholly and entirely dependent as to their existence, hence how morality in general is possible in them; for were everything nature, then everything would also be, though only physically, good and no morality at all. The possibility of freedom we cannot comprehend; but we must yet presuppose it, for rational beings can yet act only according to the idea of it. Thus it is practically certain. The question how freedom [is possible] is possible in created beings is one and the same with that how substance, which yet exists only *derivative*, is possible.\nWith regard to God, where the progression into the infinite, which for the creature is never completed, is only an *intuitus*, there is no evil. This is only in the parts because they are not the whole. Evil seems to belong merely to appearances; in itself it is only a manifoldness of the good as to degrees. Yet for us appearances are the things themselves. We also really do not depend on ap|pearances\n(g in acting) in ourselves and are thus free. Nevertheless we necessarily represent ourselves as on them with regard to happiness [act] in the world of sense we depend on it; hence we are yet dependent on it as to the object of the will.\nWhat holiness: goodness: and justice are.",["*derivative*: Latin adverb, “derivatively”; *intuitus*: “intuition”.","The sentence beginning “Nevertheless” breaks off after “as on them” and resumes with a new construction; the German is left as incoherent as it stands. “it” in “on it” renders davon and ihr, whose referent (Sinnenwelt or Glükseeligkeit) is open.","“things themselves” renders Sachen selbst."]],"reflm:6102":["(g Without astronomy these objections would be important.)\nObjection against the holiness of God: that evil (vice) is in the world.\nObjection against the goodness of God: that ill is in the world.\nObjection against the justice of God: that [both] happiness and ill are not distributed proportionately to the worthiness of rational beings. *Vide pag.* 283.\n* We are to expect everything from nature, both our own and that which surrounds us in this world of sense, so that we bring forth the good and happiness out of ourselves and do not exist as foster children. The conflict of animality with the principle of reason in us must be overcome through gradual culture; one generation forms the other. Falsehood, laziness, cowardice are vices; but they are in the whole yet as it were corruptions that serve for the generation of the good. The whole lies in the intelligible world, this is only appearance.\nIll only according to our idea of happiness is cause of discontent, but it is a goad to activity. We are not to want to become happy here, we must die.\nLack of justice. Were it such as we wish, then all morality would turn into self-interest.\n*(g Evil arises from our looking only at one part of the world and not at the whole of the world's course, and is also only the imperfection that attaches to the part. It rests on the [human] human being's making up, in each generation, only one part of the whole race.)",["“evil” renders Böses (das Bose), “ill” Übel.","*Vide pag.*: “see page”.","“this” in “this is only appearance” renders dieses, whose referent is not stated."]],"reflm:6103":["The good in the world of sense is progression toward, not possession of perfection; so happiness is progression, not possession of contentment.",[]],"reflm:6104":["Beside the first half of M § 915:\nHow God is the cause of evil is just the question as breaks off.",["“breaks off” renders the editor's note bricht ab."]],"reflm:6105":["The removal, i.e. the cancelling of the actual evil in the world through the progression toward perfection is the *perfectio phaenomenon*. although evil is not a means to the good.",["*perfectio phaenomenon*: “perfection as phenomenon”.","“cancelling” renders Aufhebung; “actual” wirklichen."]],"reflm:6106":["In all (g moral) properties of God that carry an obligation with them, i.e. a necessity [itself] of being subject oneself to the moral laws, the properties are considered only [according to as] *respectiv* to us. *e.g. iustitia non commutativa, sed distributiva*.",["*respectiv*: “relatively”, in Kant's Latinate form.","*iustitia non commutativa, sed distributiva*: “not commutative but distributive justice”."]],"reflm:6107":["M 376:\n1. Morals subsists for itself (as to its principle) without presupposition of a deity.\n2. The highest good is not the determining ground, but the object of a will determined through the moral law, and the* possibility of it, consequently also of the existence of God, is practically necessary.\n3. To assume the existence of God is the consequence of the moral law and of the disposition according to it; for only through this does the highest good become object of the will.**\n*(g[The] Nature cannot without a supreme author with a moral will agree of itself with morality; for that would have to be a nature in which the good will of the creature were also sufficient efficient cause of the happiness harmonious with it; this, however, is not completely in its power, but indeed the disposition is.)\nM 376':\n**(g The subjective ground of taking to be true is here grounded on an objective practical law, thus objective, but sufficient not with a logical but with a practical aim.)",["“disposition” renders Gesinnung; “determining ground” Bestimungsgrund; “consequence” Folge.","“sufficient” renders hinreichend; “efficient cause” wirkende Ursache.","“this” in the first addition renders die, most likely the happiness; “its” renders seiner, the creature's."]],"reflm:6108":["(g On theoretical belief [and its alter] as well as practical rational belief and its unalterability.)\nSufficient taking to be true from subjective grounds [which] must [at the same time rest on objective grounds] be so constituted that the subjective ground is at the same time objective or necessary from an objective ground, i.e. that the moral law, which commands promoting the highest good, is at the same time maxim. Whoever has this is sufficiently convinced with a practical aim of God and a future world; but he cannot convince any others of it, because he cannot presuppose the maxim in others. It is thus a conviction with a practical aim, a rational belief, not a historical one, which would be merely an opining and would be combined with alterability.\nHow knowledge is harmful here. *Minimum. Qvantum est, qvod nescimus. qvantum est, cujus scientia non indigemus.*",["“rational belief” renders Vernunftglaube; “sufficient(ly)” zureichend; “opining” Meynen.","Latin: “Least. How much there is that we do not know; how much there is of which we need no knowledge.”"]],"reflm:6109":["M 377':\nIf we wanted to say: it is highly probable that there is a God, then this would be a judgment that would not at all be in conformity with the constitution of the cognition and also, if one admitted it, would say much too little. For probability can only be thought in a kind of connections |\nwhose possibility is otherwise certain, e.g. that planets too are inhabited. But the possibility of a causal combination can be valid only through experience, hence of causes in the world; but of a cause outside it and not [the] one in nature, but of nature itself, we have no ground for inferring according to the analogy of nature. Probability is only a judgment pending closer acquaintance.\nM 377:\nBut it can be necessary to assume something, and indeed from subjective causes of the use of the understanding in general, although we do not know the object, e.g. that weather and other occurrences, e.g. of whole states, take place according to natural laws and from natural causes, because otherwise we could not make use of our understanding. That is a postulate of universal human reason. Here lies a practical postulate: we are to think occurrences in such a way that we can use our understanding on them. But this practical postulate always has only conditioned necessity, namely if we will that our understanding agree with itself according to principles. But there are absolute practical postulates of willing, and those are the moral ones. We are to be faithful and honest without conditions of an arbitrary end. Whatever with regard to certain objects must necessarily be assumed in conformity with this postulate is also a practical postulate. I must think it thus, and it is not merely hypothesis.\nHere the ground is objective, but not theoretical, rather practical, completely a priori; but the conviction is also only relative to the practical, not probability, but firm belief.",["“causal combination” renders caussalverbindung; “connections” Verknüpfungen.","“acquaintance” renders Kentnis; “know” in “do not know the object” renders kennen.","“arbitrary” renders beliebigen (“at one's discretion”)."]],"reflm:6110":["M 377':\nWe cannot say: we cognize with certainty, indeed not even: with probability, that a God exists, from objective grounds. For probability (g is approximation to that of which certainty is possible) requires laws that are subject to those of nature which we know, and according to which we would here have to assume that the origin of nature itself would also have to be explained; we therefore pass over from the law of nature to an entirely different order (μεταβασισ εἰσ αλλο γενοσ) |\nand want the same rule to hold for things outside a certain genus that may hold for things of this genus, not to mention that [no on] no probability can justify an inference from a limited effect to an unlimited cause, and that, if one cannot prove a highest being, to prove the existence of any other is without use.\nBut we have subjective grounds, of the [the] speculative as well as the practical use of our reason, to presuppose such an existence, because without that we find no satisfaction for our reason possible, nor any |\nM 377: |\nthoroughgoing unity of it. Above all the practical laws, which are yet objectively necessary, have no subjective force without that presupposition. Thus we have enough for rational belief, indeed even for it as a necessary presupposition; for in speculative philosophy it is not necessary that I attempt to explain the origin of nature (g There is no objective necessity to speculate, but indeed to obey the moral law); but moral laws I must acknowledge, hence also presuppose as an unavoidable hypothesis that without which moral laws would have no binding force for rational beings.",["[no on] renders the struck kein auf; [the] is a struck, cut-off beginning, left as it stands.","Glauben = belief; Verbindende Kraft = binding force (verbinden in the sense of obligating, not Verbindung = combination).","In the German the second (g ...) addition stands between zu erklären and versuche."]],"reflm:6111":["The pragmatic interest from the principle of happiness as a subjectively universal end makes belief in God only into a hypothesis; the moral one from the principle of the system of all ends as the [ver] objectively necessary final end of rational beings makes of it a postulate, i.e., an absolutely necessary presupposition of pure reason. God as the highest [Go] self-sufficient good, in which morality as the highest formal good alone can have reality, i.e., can become a ground of the existence of an intelligible world, i.e., [of the] of a kingdom of ends.\nHappiness as a consequence of morality belongs to it [as a *corollarium de*] and to its transcendental truth; but as a ground of it, it cancels it.\nWere the cognition of God a knowledge and not merely a belief, would morality then indeed remain left over. To believe in God is already moral and has a worth. Whether one could say: devils also believe? They fear that there is one; they do not need him, they do not want there to be one.",["The second sentence has no main verb; haben (plural, with moralitaet) and werden kan are both carried by the relative clause.","aber als ein Grund derselben hebt er sie auf: er is masculine though Glückseeligkeit is feminine; aufheben = cancel.","Folge = consequence; Wissen = knowledge; Glauben = belief."]],"reflm:6112":["If I am justified only with a moral (g and indeed one fixed a priori) aim in assuming something distinct from nature, such as God, freedom and immortality of the soul, can establish nothing of this through insight, nor may take it as ground in any other respect, then hereby the field of hypotheses of reason is closed and all enthusiasm is deprived of its evasion.",["in moralischer Absicht = with a moral aim (Absicht = aim).","Schwärmerey = enthusiasm; Ausflucht = evasion, way out."]],"reflm:6113":["(g Of the world as the object of the most perfect will as of the *summi boni originarii*.)\nThe divine end is not the mere happiness of the creature outside him, not the satisfaction of an inclination to honor in him, but that which is the necessary consequence of the highest original good, namely: the highest derived good. The best world — i.e., happiness under the conditions under which each is worthy of happiness. The former, as the benevolent will, is in itself unlimited and therefore infinite; the latter, as the holy will, ties it to conditions. Thus the product of the most benevolent and holiest will is the highest good. Justice is the consequence of it. But in this consists the practical perfection of the highest being. The world as the *ectypon*, i.e., the effect which bears witness to the cause, thus has its greatest perfection in this, that it bears witness to a (g practically) most perfect, not merely benevolent author, and its perfection is thus the honor of God, not that the creature honor him [but that] through high praise, but through action and deed. And thus be happy in the highest degree.",["summi boni originarii (genitive, of the original highest good) kept as Kant wrote it.","Ehre / ehren = honor throughout (die Ehre Gottes: the honor, or glory, of God); gütig = benevolent; Urheber = author.","The last sentence has no subject in the German."]],"reflm:6114":["[With] Under a good principle evil must always be in vanishing, and this diminution and progress in the good is the perfection of the sensible world. Evil is not substantial, it cancels itself.",["im verschwinden seyn = be in (the process of) vanishing; hebt sich selbst auf = cancels itself (aufheben)."]],"reflm:6115":["*Prosperitas summa*, the highest welfare is happiness; the highest self-contentment is blessedness.",["Wohlfarth = welfare; Glükseeligkeit = happiness; Selbstzufriedenheit = self-contentment; Seeligkeit = blessedness."]],"reflm:6116":["Pleasure in one's state is welfare.\nPleasure in oneself is self-contentment.",[]],"reflm:6117":["Contentment with our whole existence is happiness; this requires in the human being physical causes too, i.e., welfare. Happiness independent of physical causes is blessedness. Thus only from self-contentment. God is thus the alone-blessed.",["der Allein-seelige = the alone-blessed (the only blessed one); Seeligkeit = blessedness."]],"reflm:6118":["Of the author of the world we have 3 points to consider:\n1. creation, 2. preservation, 3. government.\na. The causality either per emanationem* or *libertatem*; *caussa libera est autor*. In the latter case God is either *creator* or *architect*. 1. *Creatio*. Whether from eternity or in time? b. *conservatio* 1. *omnipraesentia*, 2. (g The preservation of the form) government. The wisdom [as] with regard to all particular occurrences in the course of the |\nworld is either placed in the** beginning of the world and is called providence, or is exercised in the duration and progression of the world and is called (g *concursus*) gubernation. As *concursus* it is called direction, and indeed above all with regard to freedom. The *directio* is either *ordinaria* or *extraordinaria*.\nThe *decretum* of providence is either *absolutum* (predestination) or hypotheticum.***\n*(s The absolute necessity of existence as from its nature; it can never be a ground of the existence of other things, for this natural necessity is isolated and can have only *rationata immanentia*.)\n**(s or causality with regard to individual ends: *concursus*)\n***(s Difficulty, how God can be *auctor* of freely acting beings and yet not of their free actions. The latter have difficulty only if these actions were not appearances.)",["The asterisks after per emanationem, in the and hypotheticum are Kant's note signs, not italics; those two Latin words are left in roman to keep the signs legible.","Erhaltung = preservation (conservatio); Regirung = government; providentz, gubernation, direction kept as Kant's Latinate words.","Als Aus ihrer Natur in the first note is defective as it stands (Aus capitalized, no noun); rendered as from its nature."]],"reflm:6119":["System 1. (g of natural causality or only of the phenomenon, to intuit all things in God) of inherence or [of emanation] of causality (Spinoza, the ancients); 2. of free causality (s or of emanation, but this either of the architect or of the creator) either of the architect or of the creator; 3. of influx either through *commercium* (world-soul) or providence.",["The note is entirely in Latin; anima mundi = world-soul; commercium (community) kept in Latin.","inhaerentiae vel caussalitatis may also be read as one alternative, of inherence or of causality, standing under 1."]],"reflm:6120":["*Creatio et conservatio* pertain to the substance. (s *providentia et*) *Gubernatio et concursus* (s to the freedom of the beings of the world. But as such they are not free) to the state. *providentia* is the divine government, placed in creation.",["gehen auf = pertain to, are directed at; Weltwesen = beings of the world; Regirung = government."]],"reflm:6121":["creation; preservation; government; concurrence, not toward nature, but freedom. providence or [through] and direction ordinary and extraordinary; absolute decree.",["Latin: creatio, conservatio, gubernatio, concursus, providentia, directio ordinaria et extraordinaria, absolutum decretum.","vel [per] et: vel (or) stands uncorrected beside the struck per and the following et (and)."]],"reflm:6122":["Categories of magnitude. Unity, plurality, το παν. (pantheism.) 2. Reality. Substratum of all reality. or limited (g infinity). Categories of modality: possibility, actuality, necessity.",["Große read as Größe = magnitude; το παν = the all (Greek)."]],"reflm:6123":["1. God as cause of the world: *nexus effectivus*,\n2. as author of the world: *nexus finalis*,\n3. as ruler of the world: *nexus moralis*.\npantheism does not signify: everything is God, but: the all is God.",["alles ist Gott / das All ist Gott: alles = everything, das All = the all (the universe as a whole)."]],"reflm:6124":["System either of inherence or of causality; the former Spinoza's, the latter Deism's.",["Latin throughout: prius spinozae, posterius Deismi."]],"reflm:6125":["The absolute necessity of nature is only the ground of immanent, but not of transient actions; to these belong representation of the object and freedom.",["dazu = to these (the transient actions) or to that; Gegenstand = object."]],"reflm:6126":["*Caussalitas Dei respectu mundi* 1: with regard to breaks off.",["Caussalitas Dei respectu mundi = the causality of God with respect to the world.","bricht ab (breaks off) records that the note ends here."]],"reflm:6127":["Divine causality *per libertatem*, i.e. *auctor*.",["per libertatem = through freedom; auctor = author."]],"reflm:6128":["how God could bring forth something outside himself is not to be proven; Spinoza.",[]],"reflm:6129":["either *per emanationem*: necessity of nature, or *per libertatem*: through freedom (s *autor*). This either as *creator* or *architect*. The architect is not complete author (s *demiurgus*); matter also bears the blame and merit.",["Urheber = author; Schuld = blame, guilt."]],"reflm:6130":["In and \nWere matter not a creature, then it would be a sum total of necessary beings. These can therefore not be *in commercio*. Moreover God would be limited by the nature of the subjects subsisting for themselves.\nOne cannot say that the essential imperfection of the creature limits God.",["In und breaks off at the start of the note.","Geschöpf = creature; Inbegrif = sum total; für sich bestehend = subsisting for themselves."]],"reflm:6131":["[In] Of God we can represent to ourselves no *actus successivos* with regard to the world, but indeed successive effects of one and the same *actus* of creation and preservation; e.g. that, when we better ourselves, God from an angered God becomes a gracious God. But if this is to happen, that the different effects of God on the world must be able to be developed out of a single *actus*, then that means as much as: they must be able to be explained from the *actus* of creation, hence from its effect, i.e. nature. For nature is nothing other than the single *actus* of the divine power, from which everything else in the world can be explained according to universal laws.\nHence no successive creation can be thought, because this is not as occurrences developing themselves successively out of the single *actus* of creation according to the order of nature.\nMiracle would be something which indeed according to an order of nature, which however is possible only through the cognition of the nature of a world-whole as such, can be cognized according to its laws. Thus only insofar as the intelligible ground of appearances were known to us, which is always moral.\n(g Creation does not belong to occurrences in time. The world with its existence must be thought as possible through it alone.)",["weil diese nicht als ... entwickelnde Begebenheiten sey: the clause is defective as it stands (singular sey, no predicate noun) and is rendered literally.","der einige actus = the single actus (einig for einzig); Kraft = power; Erhaltung = preservation (conservatio).","der immer moralisch ist (which is always moral) refers to der intelligibele Grund; the English order follows the German."]],"reflm:6132":["Moral theology has as its object God as the highest self-sufficient good, as author of the world, which for that reason is the highest created good.\nThe principle of moral theology is: that the (g positive) idea of freedom as the ground of all morals is taken from the idea of the highest good, which alone constitutes the system of all ends, in which we think ourselves as members and are to act from this point of view, because it is to be possible through us and our freedom. Now this system of all ends from freedom is nothing other than a world in which (g highest) happiness stands in proportion with the worthiness to be happy. Thus morals includes this idea in itself as principle. But such a world is not possible merely through our freedom, rather nature must contain [this] the agreement with this law. But it can contain this only if there is a God.",["Moral = morals; selbständig = self-sufficient; Würdigkeit = worthiness."]],"reflm:6133":["God according to his holiness willed the moral worth of ra|tional\nbeings as the condition of happiness. This, however, consists in this, that he can impute all good to himself. The less predisposition God therefore gave for it, so that the human being could become *autor* of it out of freedom, the greater the moral worth. Great predispositions to the good would have diminished the imputation of the good. Now evil arose precisely from the conditions under which alone the greatest moral worth could arise: namely a moral character acquired through one's own effort. Ills serve to put the strength of morality to the test through resistance and to exercise it.\nThe not prompt execution of divine justice in this world is also the best means to present morality purely. Thus all apparent objections are rather consequences of the necessary spontaneity, energy and purity of the moral character, which could be obtained through this alone and to which God has directed the highest aim in this life, as the highest condition of the good.",["Das Böse = evil; die Übel = ills; Anlage = predisposition; Zurechnung = imputation.","er in daß er alles Gute sich selbst zurechnen könne can grammatically be God or the rational being; left as he, as ambiguous as the German.","Scheinbare Einwürfe = apparent (or specious) objections."]],"reflm:6134":["We assume in organized beings that everything is purposive, although we do not see it. Why not this very thing in all things of the world.\nWithout a best world there is no morals.\nThe sensible world is as sensible world* on the whole the best.\n*(g perfect namely as transition to the intelligible, since morality will govern nature and be in its place. This is both in this, that ills befall the virtuous too, and also that through vice there is progress toward the good.)",["zwekmäßig = purposive; Moral = morals; durchgang = transition, passage; Fortgang = progress."]],"reflm:6135":["In an animate world corporeal nature as means and conversely thinking nature as end must agree with each other; otherwise it is not a nature, because it does not preserve itself. Here ends make up the principle of existence.",["belebt = animate, living; Daseyn = existence."]],"reflm:6136":["Teleology. The principle that everything has its end and is good either in itself or for something is the supreme natural principle of natural science, which has not been derived from the hypothesis of a rational author, but because we would otherwise have no standard for our idea of the good, namely what it is as means, if it were not nature. Anatomy, all things that hang together in a system. From this need arises that of the hypothesis of a rational cause — for the things in nature outside one another cannot be represented as acting on one another purposively of themselves. Hume.",["Richtmaas = standard, guiding measure; Naturprincipium = natural principle; Naturwissenschaft = natural science.","wenn es nicht die Natur wäre: es may refer to the principle or to what is good as means; as ambiguous as the German."]],"reflm:6137":["Through experience it cannot be proven that this world is the best, hence also not inferred from this to the highest perfection of the author. One must take the latter as ground in order to infer the former; but then this proposition can indeed be justified through experience, above all teleology. For the ancients, who held the earth to be the only end, it was harder. Hume's objection, taken from the ills of human beings; they are culpably incurred. The guilt is itself an *effect* of our freedom and limitations, and the limitations a particular arrangement of the progress toward perfection.",["verschuldet / Schuld = culpably incurred / guilt; Schranken = limitations.","effect is Kant's own foreign word, not Wirkung."]],"reflm:6138":["End of creation. By this is understood either the whole of what is created or the *actus* of creation, thus either the effect or the determination of the cause to the effect. The end of the former is the whole of all possible ends in the *universo* (of the members\n|\nof it), consequently the free will in a system and through it the happiness in a system. That is the objective ground of the divine will. The subjective one or the incentive cannot be distinguished in God from the objective one. We can indicate it only negatively in the self-sufficiency of a being as the supreme source of all good and in the possession of its infinite causality.",["Zwek: end; Triebfeder: incentive; Bestimung: determination.","Selbstgnugsamkeit: self-sufficiency; Caussalität: causality."]],"reflm:6139":["The free will as agreeing together into a whole of all possible ends or from the *princip* of the possibility of a universal system of ends.",["zusammenstimend zu: agreeing together into; *princip* is Kant's Latinate spelling of Prinzip, principle.","The passage has no main verb."]],"reflm:6140":["The objective end of God is (s the absolutely (unconditionally) good of the world together with what attaches to it as sequel), that the world the greatest (g created) good in itself itself, not: that it be good for something.\nIt is, however, the greatest good in itself insofar as rational beings [gl] in it are happy and worthy of this happiness.\nAs a system of ends through freedom, and then also through nature. (s Honor: no subjective end.)",["The clause daß die Welt das großte ... Gut an sich sich selbst has no verb, and an sich sich selbst is doubled as written; the English keeps both.","[gl]: a deleted fragment, presumably the beginning of glüklich, left in the German.","Ehre: honor."]],"reflm:6141":["God has no subjective moving cause, i.e. *interesse*, of creation.",["Bewegursache: moving cause."]],"reflm:6142":["The concept of God is not a speculative but a moral concept; not in relation to nature, but the interest, and indeed the moral one of human beings, |\nM 386': |\nhe is through this also determined as a perfect being.",["The sentence changes construction across the page break as in the German."]],"reflm:6143":["(g *mundus optimus*, because otherwise a better will would be possible; the highest happiness is not the supreme end, for it is only conditionally good; nor morality, for it is not the whole end.)\n*Summum bonum (finitum) derivativum*. That rational beings in the world happy, insofar as they be not unworthy of this happiness.\nBelief in God through *moralitaet*. This gives the interest. The moral disposition is impossible without this, although the estimation.\nHow is it possible that God have an end outside him? Not as incentive, but this is in him and in the consciousness (of his highest fruitfulness) as *summi boni*.",["*mundus optimus*: best world; *Summum bonum (finitum) derivativum*: the derived (finite) highest good; *summi boni*: of the highest good.","Daß Vernünftige Wesen in der Welt glüklich ... seyn: the single verb seyn stands at the end; obzwar die Beurtheilung is elliptical in the German.","Gesinung: disposition; Beurtheilung: estimation; die ist in ihm: die may refer to Triebfeder."]],"reflm:6144":["Desire requires the existence of a thing; will goes only to action which pleases in itself, because it brings forth something good.",["Begierde: desire; gefällt: pleases."]],"reflm:6145":["Let the world be as good as one will, still we ask: what interest has God in creating it. Its existence can indeed be indifferent to him. Was he weary of solitude? Did he seek honor. Moral interest.",[]],"reflm:6146":["Above, in and \nNot so that God would be an object of our love, but of our veneration, i.e. as holy lawgiver to the morally good, and only through this to the physical, did he create the world.",["The editorial location note Über, in und is cut off in the source.","Verehrung: veneration."]],"reflm:6147":["In and \nThe concept of the honor of God is yet better than the concept of the happiness of the creatures; for the former goes to perfection of the world, but it is yet ambiguous.",["The location note In und is cut off in the source.","Ehre Gottes: honor of God (also glory of God); zweydeutig: ambiguous."]],"reflm:6148":["In and \nEvery will has an object, but not always a moving cause. The highest good and its existence has for a most perfect will no moving cause, hence also no end distinct from the object.",["The location note In und is cut off in the source.","Bewegursache: moving cause; obiect: object."]],"reflm:6149":["1. God as world cause. 2. As world creator and preserver, author. 3. As world ruler, and the world not only as a realm of nature, but also as a realm of ends. Moral theology. Existence. Belief.",["Reich: realm; Erhalter: preserver; Urheber: author; Glaube: belief."]],"reflm:6150":["End considered according to its objective constitution is the highest good; according to its subjective ground there is none at all, but *analogon*. The former is immediate end.",["ist gar keiner: either there is no end at all, or it is no end at all; the German allows both."]],"reflm:6151":["The will of God has merely objective moving causes, no subjective ones, thus no incentives.",["Bewegursachen: moving causes; Triebfedern: incentives."]],"reflm:6152":["Attributes, first, moral; in aid, transcendental and natural theology. Evidence: |\nM 386': |\nthe greatest practical conviction. World as *summum bonum derivativum*.",["The Latin Attributa 1mo moralia; in subsidium theologia transscendentalis et naturalis is translated; theologia is nominative.","Evidentz: evidence; Überzeugung: conviction; *summum bonum derivativum*: derived highest good."]],"reflm:6153":["God has created the world for his honor, not for the sake of his honor. i.e. the objective, not subjective end; honor is objective, what in itself determines the rational judgment about [the worth] all worth of the person. God has thus created the world for the sake of rational beings, and indeed insofar as they were to be capable of the estimation of themselves and of their true worth.",["zu seiner Ehre / um seiner Ehre willen: for his honor / for the sake of his honor.","die Ehre ist obiectiv, was ...: obiectiv may be predicate adjective or adverb (objectively what ...).","Werth: worth; Schatzung: estimation."]],"reflm:6154":["The question still remains: 1. whether God has at all a subjective moving cause of the creation of the world, i.e. has an interest in its existence, as the ground of the desiring that it be there; [or] 2ndly which it |\nbe. If the first is denied, then the second falls away. The will is twofold: a, that which arises from the [Bed] own need of the subject, [and that] where this for its complete contentment requires the existence of other things; or b, from all-sufficiency, where the subject has a satisfaction in itself [as a] and its adequacy to all good outside it. The self-contentment with oneself as a ground, as a source of the possibility of all other things is at the same time the causality with regard to the existence of the greatest [and with] effect, which according to form agrees with the form of its self-contentment. Thus a distinction between an *appetitio originaria*, which rests on no subjective moving cause or incentive, and a *derivativa*.\nThe (g objective) honor of God refers to rational beings and not to the moving cause which these receive in the world to love him, but to honor. (g\n|\nM 387: |\nGod is the highest good, insofar as [he] his will is a morally perfect and holy will and under this condition alone a beneficent one.) |\nM 387': |\nThis rests on his beneficence, [ver] limited and determined by his holiness, i.e. [on] on this, that the world be a [Geg] stage of moral good conduct for them, in order to make themselves worthy of the beneficence of God. Through this alone is he honored; and, because the good that is brought about through this alone does not [se] flow to him, but is immanent in the world, so through this God is indeed served, i.e. we act as in the service of God for the best of the world, but God is not waited upon, i.e. through some cult some end [outside] or effect outside the world and in God intended. Thus there is no immediate service of God, but only [through] through this, that we carry out his commands in the world and thereby ourselves become 1. good, 2. worthy of the [ge] goodness of God.",["*appetitio originaria* / *derivativa*: original / derivative desire. [Bed], [ver], [Geg], [se] and [ge] are deleted fragments, left in the German.","Allgenugsamkeit: all-sufficiency; Wohlgefallen: satisfaction; Gütigkeit, gütig: beneficence, beneficent; Güte: goodness; Wohlverhalten: good conduct.","gedient / bedient: served / waited upon; Gottesdienst: service of God; Weltbesten: best of the world."]],"reflm:6155":["Above, beside and \nWhether God created the world from eternity or in time. I.e. whether creation be a beginning of the world or a [world] causality with regard to the substance of the world without any relation to time. In this relation only the existence of the world appears to us. This appearance is only a mode of representation for us, not a constitution |\nof the things in themselves which appear to us so. To this extent the world is neither in itself from eternity, nor does it have a beginning, but only the duration in time which our *regressus* in it contains, which goes to infinity without a first in it, but no series as e.g. of generations as actually infinite, but as in itself not determinable must be regarded.",["The location note Über, neben und is cut off in the source.","Vorstellungsart: mode of representation; Beschaffenheit: constitution; Zeitdauer: duration in time.","The last clause (aber keine Reihe ... angesehen werden muß) keeps the German order; keine ... muß may mean no series must be, or must not be, so regarded."]],"reflm:6156":["Creation refers either to a beginning of the world or to the existence of the world without reference to time as the form of sensibility.",[]],"reflm:6157":["To the end of God belongs that the rational creature be completely adequate to the will of the same (through holiness); but no creature attains that. Thus it is the *progressus* to it into infinity, where, if the disposition is such that it lets this be firmly hoped, this is in accordance with the divine end.",["derselben (fem. or pl.) agrees with no preceding noun as written; the English keeps “of the same”.","Gesinung: disposition."]],"reflm:6158":["Respect for an unconditioned (personal) good is veneration, that for the highest *adoratio*.",["Achtung: respect; Verehrung: veneration; *adoratio*: adoration."]],"reflm:6159":["The world is a whole of ends (g kingdom of God); God: creator as architect;\n[for] as rational beings (g containing) also a realm of ends; God: governor (g *progressus* to the highest good).\nInsofar as they themselves are to cooperate toward the highest end. *Civitas Dei*. Judge.\n(g 1. as realm of nature,\n2. as realm of grace,\n3. as kingdom of heaven, where [everything] nature wholly under the condition of agreement with the moral in the highest breaks off.)",["Reich: realm; Reich Gottes and Himmelreich are given as kingdom of God and kingdom of heaven; *Civitas Dei*: city of God.","als vernünftige Wesen (g enthaltend): the insertion makes it as containing rational beings.","Baumeister: architect; Regirer: governor."]],"reflm:6160":["*actuatio initii* — *durationis*.\n*Creatio et conservatio* (g the opinion of the *conservation* of form, because it could not be preserved through the nature of things) go to the substance. *Providentia* and *gubernatio* to the state of the world. (The former is the arrangement of the agreement of the world with the divine ends, insofar as it is already laid into creation, the second insofar as it belongs to preservation, thus also in the sequence of time.) *gubernatio* is either *directio* or *concursus*. The first [concerns] is the supplementation of the imperfection of things with regard to form, the second with regard to force.\n(g The determinations either of the forces of natural things or the supplementation of these as means to particular ends.)",["*actuatio initii — durationis*: actualization of the beginning — of duration; *creatio et conservatio*: creation and conservation; *providentia*, *gubernatio*: providence, government.","Erhaltung: preservation; Veranstaltung: arrangement; Ergänzung: supplementation.","The Latin gubernatio est vel directio vel concursus is translated, its terms kept."]],"reflm:6161":["The principle of the estimation of all *actuum* of the *Gubernation* is: that they all belong to one *actus* of creation, hence also of preservation.",["*actuum*: of the acts; Gubernation: Germanized Latin, government.","Beurtheilung: estimation; Erhaltung: preservation."]],"reflm:6162":["*directio* is the determination of the forces of nature [through th] as means to the divine aim, *concursus* the supplementation of them through divine force.",["[through th] renders the deleted fragment durch d, which breaks off.","Absicht: aim; Kraft: force."]],"reflm:6163":["The action of God by which he has laid the *nexum finalem* into creation is *providentz*; that by which he brings it forth in preservation is *gubernatio*.\nOnly one *actus*.\n*Concursus*. *cooperatio*.",["*nexum finalem*: final nexus; *providentz*: Germanized Latin, providence.","Erhaltung: preservation."]],"reflm:6164":["(g Conservation is not a particular operation, but only the respect of things differs.)\nVirtual omnipresence — perpetual, intimate;\nnot *commercium* as *anima mundi*,\nnot as a sole substance,\nbut as *ens extramundanum*.",["The Latin passage is translated; *commercium*: community; *anima mundi*: soul of the world; *ens extramundanum*: extramundane being.","respectus: respect, i.e. relation; tantum (only) may attach to differt or to the whole clause."]],"reflm:6165":["That the evils in the world (on our earth) do not refute the divine wisdom and goodness, but leave it possible [them in] to regard their permission as not unworthy of a deity: this manner of judging is very much justified by the astronomical discovery of so many worlds. There are so many infinite levels (though in such a way that every rational creature also reaches the higher ones).\nBut this very same discovery in turn so strikes down all claims of the human being to provision and eternity and so annihilates him in his own eyes that he does not trust himself with importance enough to be an end of creation.",["Ubel: evils; Zulassung: permission; Beurtheilungsart: manner of judging.","Vorsorge: provision, care taken in advance; Stufen: levels."]],"reflm:6166":["*Concursus* to nature or to freedom.\nGod is the *autor* of all determinations of nature, whether they happen according to the order of nature or not according to it. (g Of moral ones he cannot be *autor*, neither of the good nor of the evil.)\n*Concursus* properly means accession, as distinguished from the *causa principali*.\nIn the moral, so that it can be said of God that he concur, he must not be *autor*, i.e. *causa solitaria* of the good. He concurs to freedom.",["concurrire, concurrirt: Germanized Latin verb, rendered concur.","Beytritt: accession, joining; *causa principalis*: principal cause; *causa solitaria*: sole cause."]],"reflm:6167":["The *nexus* of a *caussae remotae* with the *causa proxima* to a |\n*caussato* is not the *concursus*, but of *causarum immediatarum*, where one makes up the *complementum* to the insufficiency of the other. Now one cannot think that God concur to natural effects as such (g for he is not *concausa* of nature, but *causa solitaria*), i.e. to the insufficiency of his own causality in creation. Thus God cannot concur otherwise than to the causality of freely acting beings toward his moral ends in the world, for of the free actions of these he must not be regarded as *causa*. That which gives to the free actions the *complementum ad sufficientiam* toward divine moral ends (of holiness) is called the spirit of God. These, however, if the actions are nonetheless to remain [themselves the] imputable, must also not be *causata* of the holy spirit, but only removal of the hindrances of freedom. The spirit of God is that which gives moving force to the moral laws, thus an inner moral life, which is not at all possible according to natural laws, works in us. All morally good in us is effect of the spirit of God, and it is imputed to us that we allow this room.",["*caussae remotae*, *causa proxima*, *caussato*: of a remote cause, proximate cause, effect; *causarum immediatarum*: of immediate causes; *complementum ad sufficientiam*: complement to sufficiency.","insufficientz, caussalitaet: insufficiency, causality; concurriren: concur.","Diese aber (These, however) may refer to the free actions or to the moral ends; the German allows both."]],"reflm:6168":["We can say: all morally good and evil comes from ourselves and is to be imputed to us, and at the same time: all [comes] good comes from God. In order to prevent the *concursus* and mysticism. The good comes according to the order of nature from God, the evil from freedom.",["zuzurechnen: to be imputed."]],"reflm:6169":["1. The *conservation* is no *concursus*, for this is the *nexus* of the *concausae* with a *caussa*; [so f] thus the *caussa solitaria* does not concur. 2. The *concursus* to the state of substances is either to nature in itself or to freedom in the world. To the first [is] the divine effect is also not *concursus*, e.g. God and the physician. 3. Thus only to freedom: to the worthiness to be happy and to happiness. For here God is with regard to the sensible world not *causa solitaria*. 4. Also with regard to this either *per providentiam* or *occasionalismum*; the *occasionalismus in nexu finali* is hyperphysical: thus *concursus per naturam*.",["caussa solitaria non concurrit is translated; [so f] is a deleted fragment, left in the German.","*per providentiam*, *occasionalismum*: through providence, occasionalism; *in nexu finali*: in the final nexus; *per naturam*: through nature.","Wirdigkeit: worthiness; Sinnenwelt: sensible world."]],"reflm:6170":["Of subordinate concauses the superior (nor the inferior) does not concur, but is *causa solitaria*. If the actions are regarded as natural effects, then God is to be regarded as *causa solitaria*: he does not concur.\nBut of actions from freedom God is not *causa*, and there he can concur toward the highest good: 1. insofar as it contains happiness. 2. insofar as the freedom of the human being is under hindrances and God can remove these, [hence] not insofar as he gives us powers hyperphysically, for then he would be *causa solitaria*. Lead us not into temptation.",["The Latin sentence and non concurrit are translated; (nec inferior) is literally nor the inferior, as written.","Krafte: powers; Hindernissen: hindrances."]],"reflm:6171":["The *concursus* to nature presupposes that nature has a causality independent for itself; for, if God is the cause of it, then it is said: *causa solitaria non est iuvans*. God thus concurs [to] only to freedom; for nature is his own work, to that he cannot concur. But he concurs only to freedom insofar as it has the highest good as its end: either as [worthi] subjective condition of it or as objective sequels.\n(g The actuation of the highest good in the successive execution is called providence.)\nThis *concursus* takes place either in the creation; there it is called providence and can, because it aims at freedom and its use as condition, only be general. All providence is either with a physical or with a moral aim; the latter is concurrence. Or in the conservation, and then it is called gubernation, guidance. The direction [is], dispensation, is an *actus extraordinarius* of gubernation, [not] which is directed at a single aim.\nIt is not necessary to assume any gubernation, but only providence and extraordinary direction. The ordinary direction is providence, considered insofar as it agrees with all ends in particular cases, although it has been derived from the end in the whole.",["\"providence\" renders Kant's Latinate *Providentz* here; Fügung is \"dispensation\", Führung \"guidance\".","[worthi]: a deleted fragment, Würdigke, of Würdigkeit (worthiness).","\"sequels\": Folgen."]],"reflm:6172":["The general providence already includes the particular within itself, and the latter thus cannot be disputed if the former is conceded. But whether providence goes from the general to the particular, (g i.e.) from the whole to the parts, or conversely, is the question. We can think no lawfulness and harmony of the particular ends according to determinate laws except thus, that it begins from that which is [general] common to all, and from the whole.\nMany ends cannot harmonize if they have not previously been compared with the whole of all ends. This idea of the whole contains, according to our concept, *generalia*, in regard to which special ends have been excluded. To preserve the species and more species animals, the remainder of nature had to be sacrificed.\nProvidence is not merely *generalis*, but also *specialissima*, but not to be thought after the manner of our provision. For there a system of them cannot arise out of many single ends, and the whole we can determine only through the single; or, if the idea of the whole goes first, then we must set aside aims at the parts, as in a state various estates must do without freedom and well-being, so that in the whole [but not] there be happiness, but not an absolute whole of all happiness suited to each *individuum* and his merit. We must thus, after our manner, attribute to God merely provision for species, but not *individua*. Not everything is end, but secondary sequel.",["besondere Vorsehung is rendered \"particular providence\" here to keep besonder = \"particular\" through the passage; Latinate *speciell* is \"special\". Vorsorge is \"provision\", Vorsehung \"providence\".","\"To preserve the species and more species animals\": the German (Die Art zu erhalten und mehr arten thiere) is syntactically incomplete; \"the remainder\" renders die Übrige.","\"secondary sequel\": Nebenfolge."]],"reflm:6173":["b.\nThe actuation of the beginning (g of the world itself in existence) of the substance is creation. Of the continuance, conservation. With respect to the state [is the conser] of things in the world, the actuation of their purposiveness in the continuance of the world is: 1. laid into the beginning: providence; 2. in the progression either as gubernation, insofar as the forces themselves are not encountered in [nature] the world, or direction, insofar as they are indeed in the world, but not the determining ground of the purposiveness.\nBoth, gubernation and direction together, are *concursus*.\n*concursus* is distinguished from providence.\n(s *Creatio, conservatio, gubernatio*.\nEvery extraordinary providence is a miracle. Direction a formal, *concursus* a material miracle.\nGod is the author of the perfection of the world either through the order of nature or against it. The first is the ordinary, the second, as *miraculum praestabilitum*, the extraordinary providence.\nThrough the order of nature, according to our concepts of it, the final end is not attained.)\nM 394b:\n(s Matter as an aggregate of substances can [not] no being necessary for itself exist. For necessary beings cannot be *in commercio*, hence make up no composite.)\nM 394aI:\n(s Moral proof. We find ends in the world; these give us [us] for our insight an indication of a being which, according to the analogy of an intelligent one, would be cause of the world. Through this, however, its concept is not determined either for theoretical or for practical principles of our use of reason: because in respect of the former it explains nothing, in respect of the second determines nothing at all.\nBut reason, through the moral law, sets us a |\nfinal end. This cannot be attained through our forces, and yet we ought to have it as our aim. It can only be effected in the world, consequently insofar as nature agrees with it. But a nature which harmonized with the moral final end would be a morally acting cause. Thus we must assume a being outside nature as its author, which as a moral being, which as (g with) endowed with understanding and will is cause of the world. That now is theism.\nWhat we must merely assume from subjective grounds of reason (relatively to the determination of our will), but cannot prove, is a matter of faith. Thus the existence of God is a matter of faith of reason, and through this proof alone is a determinate concept of the original being given. — With this the concepts of nature, omniscience etc etc., are then combined. Creation and difference from conservation concerns not God, but merely determination of the world. To assume creations in the continuance of the world is contrary to the principle of the possibility of experience.)",["\"Matter as an aggregate … exist\": the sentence's construction is irregular in the German (kan [nicht] kein … Wesen existiren) and is kept so.","\"an intelligent one\": eines Verständigen; \"matter of faith\": Glaubenssache (Glaube = belief; faith).","\"concepts of nature\": Naturbegriffe, which could also be read as \"natural concepts\"."]],"reflm:6174":["With God the general signifies the all, which contains everything else within itself.\nWith human beings the general signifies the little that is contained in everything else.\nThe divine providence, if it is general, then it must also be special; it is not so with human provision. For the general with God is the cognition of the whole, with the human being the cognition of the genus. Now that which is contained in the single is not contained in the concept of the genus, but indeed in the cognition of the whole, which, however, it is impossible for us human beings to grasp. The cognition of God determines each part in the whole, that of human beings the whole through the parts.\nThus with God the particular providence is de|rived from the general.\nThrough this it is also possible that, since genera and species can subsist only through *individua* being preserved, with God this as well as all other ends can be preserved according to an order of nature which finds the ground of possibility itself in him.\nParticular [provision] dispensation (direction) is that where a (g single) occurrence is regarded as end and the causes as means. It is thus not considered as possible according to the order of nature, because the latter goes from the general to the particular, but the former lets the general [to] follow (g from) the particular.\nThe cause for assuming such a dispensation is that one cannot represent how otherwise all single occurrences in the world could turn out purposively, since we can form no concept of how God goes in the ends from the whole [to the pa] and its end to the parts.\nThe *concursus* of God to freedom (g insofar as it is morally determined), in order to make nature accordant with it and its moral law, can also be considered as lying in the order of nature and must be so considered.\nPresumption of the judgments with respect to the determination of occurrences as ends (g and of causes as means) and of the particular direction of the divine will to the plans of human wisdom.",["\"general\" renders both allgemein and the Latinate General, generale; \"special\" is speciell, \"particular\" besonder.","erhalten is rendered \"preserved\" both times; with the ends it could also mean \"obtained\".","Vorsorge is \"provision\", Vorsehung and Providentz \"providence\"."]],"reflm:6175":["The agreement of divine providence with the perfection of the world according to laws of nature is called a *concursus* to freedom and to morality. It imparts faculties, opportunities and incentives to the good. The extraordinary one with respect to single good or evil actions is possible; but we can never make it out. The *concursus* of God to the *progressus* [in the] of the good in the world is the essential thing here. God is not *auctor mali* because the human being is free and God gives no incentives to evil; but he can be regarded as *auctor* of the moral [law] good, because he is *auctor* of the obligation according to this law.",["\"incentives\": Triebfedern; \"faculties\": Vermögen."]],"reflm:6176":["Whether the divine providence be merely general\nor also particular providence?\nThe highest good is to be regarded not [an] (g as) aggregate of single aims, but as a system of ends derived from one idea. For without deriving the former from the latter, no unity of aim is possible, e.g. clock. In a system of ends the agreement with the end is possible only through this, that that through which they agree with the aim is derived from the idea of the final end, but that in which they do not contribute to the aim is only restricted to this, that it not hinder the aim. Thus not every part can be aim.\nOne can indeed say: *qvicqvid valet in genere* (g *omnibus*), *valet etiam in specie*, but not: *qvicqvid valet de genere* (g *toto*) etc etc. [For] What belongs to everything belonging to a genus etc etc., but not: what belongs to the genus in the whole etc etc. All human beings die, but the genus does not die. The particular providence would be that where in the creation every occurrence would already be predetermined with aim as means or end. Then one would have to assume either that the [general] order of nature according to general laws necessarily led to such results as [also], even if one considered them outside this *nexus*, would yet for themselves already have been necessary in the *nexus* of ends. But then one would also not have needed to assume as supreme cause an intelligent being, but only a *causam brutam*. Thus, if the order of nature cannot present every occurrence as end or means, then the special providence is possible only through supernatural effects and, since they already lie in the creation, *miraculum praestabilitum*.\nEverything also stands under the particular providence, but not everything happens through it.",["\"either\" (entweder) has no matching \"or\" in the German.","\"clock\": Uhr, which may also be a watch.","\"special providence\": specielle providentz, beside besondere Vorsehung, \"particular providence\"."]],"reflm:6177":["If providence is regarded as the most special of all in aim, i.e. that all occurrences happen through the divine particular providence, then it cannot be regarded as *generalis* (but indeed as *universalis*); i.e. [the occurrences] It cannot be directed first and preeminently at the whole and be determined only in relation to it. For this is possible only through an order of nature (according to our concepts). |\nThe occurrences would all be supernatural, and [it] the world would be no *totum reale*, but merely *ideale*.",["\"most special of all\": allerspecielleste.","*ideale*: German Ideale, capitalized, which may be the Latin word paired with *totum reale* or the German adjective \"ideal\"."]],"reflm:6178":["Intuitive generality is to be distinguished from discursive. In the latter the particular is contained not in the general, but under it, but indeed in the former. The general providence contains the particular within itself according to general laws of nature. The particular direction is distinguished from the providence which is contained in the creation. It asserts that something is not contained in the end according to general laws of nature. But that is possible, because God is a moral author.",["Allgemeinheit and allgemein are rendered \"generality\" and \"general\" throughout; \"universality\" and \"universal\" would serve equally."]],"reflm:6179":["How it is possible, through the general dependence of beings on God, that they, according to general laws, are nevertheless in conformity with his ends. e.g. tree with spreading of its branches. Twilight. Snow for plants. Snow-light in long nights. The moon is, in its waxing part, visible to the polar regions for half a month.\nIf rational beings were supposed to become subaltern authors of the good in the world, then they had to have nothing before them but the order of nature, so that the good could be ascribed to their freedom.",[]],"reflm:6180":["*Directio* (g steering) is properly *concursus formalis*; the *concursus materialis* is a particular *actus* of creation, a material miracle.\nOrdinary or extraordinary direction. Everything stands under it, but does not happen through it.",["\"steering\": Lenkung."]],"reflm:6181":["*Concursus vel ad Naturam vel libertatem*; the latter does not increase the moral good in the world, because it is not to be imputed to us.",[]],"reflm:6182":["*Gubernatio*: Because the world continues, we also distinguish in God a continuance of actions.\nDirection is *concursus formalis*;\nopposed to it is *concursus materialis*: cooperation.",[]],"reflm:6183":["Particular providence is that where a single occurrence is laid into nature as end and the cause as means.",[]],"reflm:6184":["To [that which in] the perfection of the world as a moral whole, it seems, God must concur. But it means: the concept of rational beings of God, i.e. religion, must concur to it, in order to do good in faith; which faith we can have only through presupposing God and his providence.",["\"it means\": es heißt, which can also be read \"it is said\".","\"the concept of rational beings of God\": Der Begrif der Vernünftigen Wesen von Gott, i.e. the concept that rational beings have of God."]],"reflm:6185":["The extraordinary divine direction is not necessary with respect to the ends of nature, nor with respect to the ends of God with respect to our freedom (for through that no moral worth would accrue to these); now it seems to be necessary merely for the sake of the harmony of freedom with nature, for rewards and punishments, because, as it seems, freedom is a kind of lawlessness which displaces the divine aim.\nWere everything in the world to reward virtue and punish vice, then the moral worth would fall away and God's end would not be attained. Only in eternity can it happen, i.e. in the infinite.\nIt is contrary to the moral warrant to determine extraordinary divine direction here or there.\nIt is contrary to the logical vocation of our reason. For to assume a miracle means: to deny entirely the maxim of our reason, which suffers wholly through a single exception. Reason, where it is to judge of itself, thus according to its own nature, can cognize nothing as supernatural; for that would contradict itself. That a miracle is possible in itself may be conceded; but if it happens, then the conviction of it must be effected through this very miracle.",["\"logical vocation\": logischen Bestimung; Bestimmung can also be \"determination\".","\"warrant\": Befugnis; \"displaces\": verrükt.","\"through that … to these\": durch jene würde diesen; the referents are left as open as in the German."]],"reflm:6186":["Whether God as lord or (g lawgiver) also as owner of human beings [to himself] could sacrifice one to the other as means to an end.\nWhether, if the latter is not the case, God is limited by laws, consequently not *dominus plenus*?\nHe has the right to bind morally; but this presupposes freedom.",["*dominus plenus*: “full lord (owner)”.","“bind” renders verbinden, the verb of Verbindlichkeit (“obligation”)."]],"reflm:6187":["The necessitation of a rational being through the mere cognition of the law is obligation. But the law can necessitate a rational being, and this cognize itself as obligated, only if it cognizes its universal validity for every will, thus also the necessary agreement of its will thereto. Now the rational being can agree to no end where it would not itself at the same time be used as end, but merely as means. Thus he also cannot cognize himself as bound to it.\nThe authorization to dispose [et] of an object without its agreement to the law (g *disponiren*) as one will, is property. God is thus lord, but not owner; sovereign (*autocrator*), but not despot, who would dispose of subjects as his property, consequently would dispose as of things according to a will which does not have the happiness of these as its end.",["Nöthigung = “necessitation”; Verbindlichkeit = “obligation”; verbindlich, verbunden = “obligated”, “bound”; Befugnis = “authorization”.","“its universal validity” leaves open, as the German does, whether the law's or the being's; Kant shifts from es to er (“he”) in “Thus he also”.","“would dispose” renders both verfügte and disponirte; “da” (“where”) may also be read “since”."]],"reflm:6188":["Despot is he who regards his subjects as his property. Now he can be kind, but without consulting the will of the people as to the way in which they can will it to be. He thus regards them not (tyrant is a cruel despot) as ends in themselves, out of whose will at the same time alone he may give laws, but merely as tools of his ends. (E.g. to sacrifice one as an example to others.)",["“will it to be” renders “wollen können es zu seyn”, where es seems to take up gütig (“kind”).","allein may go with “out of whose will” (“only out of whose will”) or with er (“he alone”); the English keeps both readings."]],"reflm:6189":["It is very agreeable to observe divine provision according to universal laws of nature; for this gives the understanding unity according to rules. But it is something absurd to allege special providence for individuals, because these are nothing but interruptions of the laws and, without making a reference to freedom onto individual laws of providence, subjects everything to our pleasure. But hope in special providence, but without the presumption of wanting to determine it, is in turn a law both of nature and of good conduct.\nWith regard to the history of human freedom we refer not to a divine predetermination[nei], i.e. providence, neither according to universal laws through ordinary nor through special ordinances through extraordinary arrangement, but to divine *direction*; and this too it is profitable for reason and morals to observe in history. It is the gradual development of the natural predispositions toward the vocation of humanity.",["Vorsorge = “provision”; Vorsehung = “providence”; Vorherbestimmung = “predetermination”; Bestimmung = “vocation”.","“without making a reference to freedom onto individual laws of providence” follows the obscure German construction as it stands.","[nei]: the deleted wede, a start of weder (“neither”)."]],"reflm:6190":["The doctrine of predestination is grounded either on the concept of the [nature] necessity of actions in opposition to freedom, or on the concept of moral incapacity for the good, since it can never arise from ourselves, but only be effected by God.\nHere the rational being is merely a machine for divine aims. Damnation is no punishment and blessedness no reward for good conduct.\nEnthusiastic concept of the elect. To be merely passive with regard to the divine decrees, both externally with regard to death and life and internally in the improvement of oneself.\nReluctance to ascribe the good to oneself. false humility.",["“Enthusiastic” renders schwärmerisch.","“Reluctance” renders Scheu (also “shyness”, “dread”)."]],"reflm:6191":["In predestination the concept of freedom is violated. Since this is incomprehensible, those who assert it are to be excused.",[]],"reflm:6192":["The making known of that which was hidden is revelation.\nGod has hidden himself from us. Through morality he first gives the impetus to seek him in nature. Enlargement. Yet he wants merely faith (practical), not to investigate speculatively.",["Offenbarung = “revelation”; Glaube is rendered “faith” throughout these notes on religion (also “belief”).","“Enlargement” renders Erweiterung."]],"reflm:6193":["God wants to be hidden from our speculative inquisitiveness with regard to [their] his decrees, still more from our intuition. — Nature is a mirror which has its own color, anamorphotic.",["“inquisitiveness” renders Vorwitz (presumptuous curiosity).","Rathschlüsse = “decrees”."]],"reflm:6194":["All revelation goes to practical cognition.",["Revelation here renders the Latinate Revelation, elsewhere also Offenbarung."]],"reflm:6195":["All religion, even natural, is a matter of faith. For it is doctrine objectively and disposition subjectively.\nTo regard all duties as divine commands and the whole object of them, the highest good, as possible merely under a divine legislation and government, consequently to expect this result merely in faith. Whether there is a duty to believe in God, or whether not rather (g historical faith without rational faith does not improve) the cognition of our duty produces faith in God?\nThe divine will must first be cognized through reason, for otherwise one cannot test any revelation. But through this I cognize the will of God only from the moral side. With regard to [our] the pragmatic laws as sanctions it must be revealed. But the moral can alone be universal human duty. Revelation, as discovery of that which is necessary merely for knowledge, and through which no one becomes a better human being, cannot be for all human beings. But with regard to the moral it is not therefore unnecessary because human beings can also see into it through reason.\nwhat belongs to the introduction of a religion is not itself a precept of religion for all times.",["Gesinnung = “disposition”; Vernunftglaube = “rational faith”; wissen = “knowledge”.","The second “Revelation” renders “relevation”, so spelled in the transcription.","The last sentence of the third paragraph can also be read “it is not unnecessary on the ground that human beings can also see into it through reason”."]],"reflm:6196":["revelation contains *credenda* and *agenda*, but the former as belonging to religion insofar as they are directed to *agenda* as moral ideas. Sanctions produce observances, only laws universal human duties.\nThere can be no obligation to faith; for faith is a consolation, not coercion; one holds to it gladly, coercion one hates.",["*credenda* and *agenda*: “things to be believed” and “things to be done”.","“obligation” here renders Verpflichtung, elsewhere Verbindlichkeit."]],"reflm:6197":["(g An unbeliever is only he who does not) morally [un] believe, thus who will not accept that which alone can strengthen morality.",["The added beginning and the original “Moralisch [un] glaubt” do not join grammatically in the German either."]],"reflm:6198":["Unbelieving is he who, what is not certain to him through his own experience, [to that] will not take to be true otherwise than through certainty *a priori*. Thus neither as hypothesis for the comprehensibility (g or the practical use) of other propositions certain *a priori*, nor on account of the experience of others. Incredulous.",["“Unbelieving” renders ungläubig; the closing “Incredulous” renders Ungläubisch, a distinct and rarer form.","“take to be true” renders vor wahr halten (cf. Fürwahrhalten)."]],"reflm:6199":["Faith in revelation is moral if it is duty; but only historical if it is not duty but merely means to duties.",[]],"reflm:6200":["Whether God reveals himself to us (g announces) through pure reason (the practical, not speculative) or through nature (he opens himself, but discloses himself to us) or in a supernatural way through outer or inner experience?",["“reveals” renders offenbahre; “announces” Ankündige; “opens himself” eröfnet sich; “discloses himself” entdeckt sich."]],"reflm:6201":["Whether God could reveal himself through appearance?\nWhether through [immediat] speech, so that we know that it is God who speaks?\nWhether, if this is so, he communicates his will, which cannot be cognized by us through reason, or only the confirmation of the moral (g natural) will in our souls. Effects of grace.\nWhether he reveals himself only to the one to whom he speaks, through the [production] conviction at the same time effected in him, or also to others just as certainly through [this] the latter's narration, which as historical requires testing according to criteria of history.",["“speech” renders Sprache (also “language”); [immediat] renders the deleted, broken-off unmittelb.","Erscheinung = “appearance”; Überzeugung = “conviction”.","“the latter's narration” renders “jener ihre Erzählung”."]],"reflm:6202":["*athei*. (g *diagoras*.) Strato. Epicurus. Anaximander.\nDiagoras.*Practici*.\nNatural theology is necessary:\n1. because according to its concepts we judge the genuineness of the revealed,\n2. because it is needed to preserve the latter in its purity.\nIts origin can be through revelation, but once it is there, it must at all times be able to be seen into through reason by everyone.\nIn the lecture to look chiefly to the moral properties of God.\nTo seek to prevent superstition, enthusiasm and unbelief.\nIn a word: empty rationalizing about God.",["*athei*: “atheists”; *Practici*: “practical ones”, perhaps practical atheists.","Schwärmerei = “enthusiasm”; Vernünfteln = “rationalizing”; Vortrag = “lecture” (also “presentation”)."]],"reflm:6203":["The believing way of thinking is either receptivity or spontaneity of faith. The latter is practical and consists in the principle of doing good in faith. With regard to the truths, with regard to the knowledge of which we must be merely passive, there he counts this passive conduct not as obligation, but only as authorization to accept it, and neither denies what he does not know enough, nor presumes himself for its truth.",["Kentnis = “knowledge”; Verbindlichkeit = “obligation”; Befugnis = “authorization”.","“he” has no antecedent in the German either.","“presumes himself for its truth” renders “vermißt er sich für dessen Warheit” (presumes to vouch for)."]],"reflm:6204":["Sound understanding is, like beef and mutton, a dish for burghers and peasants etc etc.\n(g The human being who adopts nature as principle, but allows himself as many exceptions as he wants, or thinks yet another nature that is not experience, is unenlightened.)\nTo be enlightened means: to think for oneself, to seek the (g supreme) touchstone of truth* in oneself. i.e. in principles.\n*(g of my judgment. the ground of taking to be true; for I must answer for it.)\nEnthusing: in (g spirit-) sensations*, which pretend to the feeling of truth. Panegyrists of common human understanding.\n*(g these I do not have from myself. Only reason sets itself criteria.)\nSuperstition: in *factis* [alone]; for these still have a touchstone above them.\nUnbelief (g The blameworthy lack of faith or resolve not to believe): in theoretical reason alone. (g For practical reason needs for an incentive also *hypothesis*.)\nNaturalist: how many meanings. Whether there are atheists. Free spirit in the historical meaning is a freethinker. Who subjects himself to no statutes.\nThe morally unbelieving one is a free spirit.",["“Sound understanding” renders Gesunder Verstand; “Enthusing” renders Schwärmen; Probirstein = “touchstone”; Triebfeder = “incentive”.","“Free spirit” renders Freygeist, “freethinker” Freydenker.","*factis*: “facts” (Latin dative/ablative plural)."]],"reflm:6205":["The delusion: 1. of the lazy trust in passive reason: mysticism; 2. of mistrust in all reason; 3. of the self-conceit of the speculative.",["Wahn = “delusion”; Eigendünkel = “self-conceit”."]],"reflm:6206":["Th II:\nHuman reason has the particular property, [not only the Mo] besides what belongs to making for itself, for a certain aim, a concept of a thing, further not only to complete this concept itself by means of everything that constitutes it, but also to complete the object of the concept in the kind of things to which it belongs. We do not content ourselves with what would be sufficient for the common use of words, to know distinctly the concept of a body, of a human being, of a plant; we seek [it] to become conscious of it in all its marks, and from this, when the law of parsimony is added, comes the definition. But we seek moreover, when we have counted the *obiect* among a certain kind of things, |\nto think it to ourselves completely with respect to this kind. Body belongs to matter, and what in its extension is not matter is empty space; hence we make for ourselves the concept of a perfectly dense body. It has cohesion; we think to ourselves a perfectly hard one, without concerning ourselves about whether such a thing is actual or even (g only) possible. Thus the completeness of a thing of a certain kind serves us only as the standard of all the remaining concepts [things] that we could make of it, insofar as they are distinguished from one another merely according to magnitude. These magnitudes are variable; one must compare them with one that is invariable, i.e. that of a thing which contains everything that can be contained in the concept of it in relation to its kind.\nSome of these concepts of completion we can determine, in that we [all] have a determinate concept, and one [at least negatively to be specified in experience], of that which belongs to its completion (e.g. among all chords in the circle the diameter); others are so constituted that we only think the completion, but cannot ourselves complete the concept. The concept of well-being is empirical, but there can still be much [lacking] lacking in contentment with one's state. Here, now, a concept is necessary which lacks nothing in content, i.e. the greatest and [best] everlasting well-being, i.e. happiness (g\n|\nTh I: |\nwhich we can nevertheless never think determinately). If this also does not depend on contingent external causes, but springs from ourselves: blessedness. Now we can extend this concept of the satisfaction of a rational being still further, in that to what it enjoys we [do] add what it does, i.e. contentment with its person and the morally good. The complete morally good is the highest virtue. If this is itself free of all inclination to evil: holiness. Holiness, then, of the will and blessedness of the state together make the idea of heaven. On the other side, because that [which not] whose concept consists in the relation of cause and effect has a twofold opposite: a negative =0 and a privative = –, one can think a state that leaves no contentment at all remaining: misfortune, and insofar as the being contains the cause in itself: misery, likewise also a will that acts contrary to all moral laws with |\nintention, and so arises the idea of hell. Hence\nheavenly joys and hellish torments heavenly virtue and hellish vices\n}\nIdeas.\nTh III:\nThese ideas are entirely necessary, even if [the] no object corresponded to them. Nor does it matter whether such a thing exists, but only: how we can better compare our concepts of that which is with |\none another, in that we hold them together with a third, although it is merely idea, as a common measure. (The Julian period goes back 532 years before the beginning of the world.)\nIf we now think to ourselves an individual according to these ideas, then it is the good or evil original being; for both are considered not according to what it contains in itself, but according to that of which it is the cause. (Whether in the world, as much good as there is on the one side, there is also as much evil on the other, and whether, just as motion cannot be imparted in one direction without producing or taking away just as much on the opposite side, *lex isonomiae*, then the principle would indeed be good, but the work of it would necessarily contain just as much evil as good). Both would contain everything that is required for activity, but the one always the ground of the reaction against the [activ] effect of the other. If the evil being is derived as to its existence, but nevertheless, although the restricted, cause of all ill: then it is the ill or the devil, a not *praecise* idea, of which one does not know how far its influence and effect go.\nIf we think to ourselves a completed being not according to determinate concepts of good and evil, but as thing in general: then this is the transcendental idea of a highest being, which is also necessary in order to think to ourselves the differences of all possible things in thoroughgoing determination with respect to the something and nothing in general. First everything is thought in One, in order afterwards to think more in one thing, less in another, through mere restriction of the original image. I always retain the same *subiect*: a thing in general, and through various restriction of the being that contains everything positive I get out all things insofar as they are something, and the differences rest, as it were, on the composition of the something with the nothing in infinite space. The evil original being is also thought as infinite reality, but in its effect as the counterpart of all *caussalitaet* of the former, whereupon the world then contains indeed no contradictions, but nothing but conflict of aims.\nBesides the necessity of the idea of a most perfect being for |\nthe concepts of all others, reason [counts] judges it [also] as necessary for the existence of things. These, considered merely as things, can be distinguished only through [reali] negations, hence only through limitations of a highest reality. Now the existence of restricted things seems to be just as derivative as their concept, and on the contrary the existence of a being that has all reality alone to be able to be original. All shadows are only remnants of the infinite nothing, namely of the night, which without the all-illuminating sun would fill space. It thus seems natural that it is not light mixed with shadow that is there first, but that it arises only through greater or lesser restriction of sunlight, which must precede. The highest reality can restrict its own effect, whereby it reveals its existence externally; but restricted reality has in itself no sufficient principle of its possibility; consequently the actuality of it is also to be regarded as dependent. Further, from the principle of thoroughgoing determination there arises a thoroughgoing community of descent, hence affinity of everything possible, because it is possible only in one concept, because all negations are possible only through the bounding of the highest reality.",["\"limitation\" renders Schranke(n); \"restriction\", \"restrict(ed)\" render Einschränkung, einschränken, eingeschränkt; \"bounding\" renders Begrenzung.","\"ill\" renders Übel, \"evil\" böse/das Böse; \"satisfaction\" renders Wohlgefallen, \"contentment\" Zufriedenheit; \"original being\" renders Urwesen, \"original image\" Urbild.","\"(g\" and the closing \"}\" are carried over as they stand in the AA text; \"lex isonomiae\" and \"praecise\" are Kant's Latin."]],"reflm:6207":["The *principium* [Der du] *exclusi medii*, i.e. of determination, contains: that if the concept is compared with one of two *oppositorum*, one of the two must belong to it. The principle of thoroughgoing determination says that the concept of a thing in general, in order to constitute the representation of a single one, must be compared with all possible *praedicatis oppositis*, so that, when it has been determined with respect to one, it must in this determination be compared with other *praedicatis oppositis*, and thus it can be thought, as thing in general, as determined solely through the relation to the *ente realissimo*. Through this it happens that a universal concept determines itself thoroughly and becomes a concept of a single being.",["The deleted \"[Der du]\" is left in German: it is a broken-off start, perhaps of \"Der durchgängigen\" (\"of the thoroughgoing\"), and cannot be read with certainty.","\"principle\" renders Kant's \"princip\"; the Latin \"principium\" is kept."]],"reflm:6208":["The principle of thoroughgoing determination is a synthetic proposition; for it demands that the concept of every thing stand in community with the con|cepts\nof everything possible, and thus that to every determination there belong still more which do not belong analytically in the concept of the thing, hence that the universal concept be regarded only as a part of the concept that contains the predicates to all possible ones.",["\"the predicates to all possible ones\" keeps the German \"die praedicate zu allen moglichen\", which leaves open what \"possible ones\" refers to."]],"reflm:6209":["The *principium exclusi medii* says only that of two *oppositis* not both can belong to a thing or be removed from it, but not that it must necessarily be compared with all possible predicates [or also], i.e. exist only in relation to them and the determination according to the *principio exclusi medii*. This is the principle of thoroughgoing determination. The first is logical and signifies only the nature of disjunctive judgments, which contain the *oppositionem contradictionis*; the second is metaphysical and designates the derivation of the concept of every thing from the sum total of all possibility, namely the highest reality.\nThe human understanding requires for a determinate concept of a thing (namely not of that which is common to many things, but of a single one) that it can thereby be distinguished from everything possible, because it judges only through universal concepts. It must therefore compare it with all possible predicates in the idea, and thinks the thoroughgoing determination, which yet it is possible for no understanding to carry out. It therefore presupposes: in order to cognize a thing entirely, one must know not only what it contains, but moreover everything that it lacks, so that one also cognizes it in *relation*.\nThe *principium* of determination is that *exclusi medii*. The principle of thoroughgoing determination runs: every single thing (i.e. insofar as it is distinguished from all others; for [the] a thing, considered universally as genus and species, is distinguished only from those that do not stand under this concept) is to be distinguished with respect to everything possible through its relation to the sum total of all possible predicates.",["\"removed\" renders \"removirt\" (Latin removere); \"relation\" in the second paragraph is Kant's foreign word, beside German \"Verhaltnis\", also rendered \"relation\".","\"it\" in \"compare it\" (ihn) and in \"that it can thereby be distinguished\" (es) is left as unresolved as in the German."]],"reflm:6210":["(g Theology. We can exhaust this object of cognition; for it is not given to us in itself.)\nThe reason of the human being needs a threefold completeness. 1. The completeness of the determination of a *subiect* with respect to all possible predicates. 2. The completeness of the derivation of its existence from the existence of others. 3. The completeness of the derivation of the existence of all from one existence. I.e. of the community of all in one single cause. Three ideas.\n1. The completeness of the determination of a thing relative to a certain concept. e.g. the concept of figure, as of the rectilinear, if they are regular (as equilateral triangle, quadrangle), or of the curvilinear: circle. The determination from the principle of the end with respect to all ends: morality, or also of an end that conflicts with all. Completeness of friendship.\n2. The completeness of the derivation. The independent existence, the first beginning, the cause that is not further *caussatum*. The idea of freedom as a kind of causality that is not externally conditioned. The first human being. The beginning of the world or of chaos (beginning of order), finally the first existence in general.\n3. Completeness of community. I.e. thoroughgoing determination of the many among one another through the dependence of their existence on One. e.g. commonwealth under one lawgiver. Unity of the church under one head. The *caussalitaet* of many causes, insofar as it is thoroughly determined through their dependence on one. The world and God.\nWe have 1. Only one concept of a thing in general through which this is thoroughly determined: *ens realissimum*; 2. [Only one concept which] Insofar as this concept is the only one that needs no other for its determination, also an existence which is no consequence from other existence, thus of an original being; 3. insofar as all other existence is |\nderived and none but that one is an original one, community of origin of all things, consequently complete concept of their combination among one another. Best world. — The all-sufficient being: 1. as the highest being as *subiect*; 2. the original being as cause; 3. the being of all beings as cause of everything. Theology.",["\"We can exhaust\" follows the text as printed (können), without a negation.","\"consequence\" here renders Folge; \"original being\" renders Urwesen, \"commonwealth\" gemeines Wesen."]],"reflm:6211":["How can the *maximum* be a guiding measure of our judgment, since the idea of it exceeds all our given concepts? Because the restrictions are positive, all of which I omit in the *maximo*. Thus because it is the pure idea, just as the concept of totality arises from plurality without restriction.\n[Just so]\nSo the divine understanding is a pure understanding, the divine will a pure will, the divine presence pure, eternity etc etc.\nThe idea of the *maximi* is that which is most determined and hence also determining.\nThe idea of the *maximi* alone is *praecisa*, e.g. perfect justice and morality.",["\"guiding measure\" renders Richtmaas; \"Because\" renders \"Darum well\", where \"well\" stands for weil."]],"reflm:6212":["The three problems (g of metaphysics): God, freedom and immortality fit the three latter antinomies, where (if one takes this series in reverse) simplicity, (g absolute) causality, necessity can all be applied to the intelligible. That of the magnitude of the world in space and time does not come into account here, because it is entirely restricted to sensible conditions. Yet through its insufficiency for the unconditioned in general it can lead to the intelligible (the first beginner of the series).",["\"problems\" renders Aufgaben; \"beginner\" renders Anfänger (one who begins)."]],"reflm:6213":["From the above grounds of the need natural to reason, in |\nregard to the concepts that arise from it itself, to ascend as far as the [completeness] idea that would completely determine that concept, there now arises the concept of a most perfect being. 1. As of a thing in general, 2. as a perfect nature (s according to psychological concepts), 3. as the most perfect principle of the system of all ends. The transcendental, the natural and the moral perfection. The highest being [as] now, considered as the most perfect nature and at the same time as the highest good, is God.\n(g greatest transcendental, greatest natural perfection, greatest practical.)\nThe system of our cognition of God is now a science and is called theology.\nQuestions. 1. How does theology as a possible science relate to the faculty of our reason? Can one bring it to completeness, or does it remain, like others, always capable of an extension? Answer. The object by far exceeds the human understanding, and what we could cognize of it, if it pleased it to reveal itself to us, reaches into the infinite. But what we can cognize of it through the light of reason can be [completely] exactly measured, and the natural theology possible for the human being completely presented, without an extension of this insight ever being able to be hoped for.\n2. Of what kind will the cognitions be (g extending or correcting) that such a science can furnish us. As cognition of the common understanding (s as hypothesis, in order to complete the [nat] chain of natural causes.) it will contain various positive things, which however also lie before everyone's eyes without science. As science, however, its business is merely negative: a. to ward off errors, consequently to prevent that we do not contradict ourselves in the concept of the highest perfection. b. in that a healthy critique distinctly shows the limitations of our reason, to moderate the outrage of the conceited wiseacres, who by groundless presumption pretend to be able to penetrate so deeply into the nature of things that they think themselves able to refute the rational belief [as a] in such a being.\n3. what interest does reason have in this science? No speculative, but merely a practical interest, and indeed of this only the moral. This cognition is to make us not more learned, but better human beings, or give emphasis and persistence to our already previously good dispositions. It is to arise from morality and work back upon it again. (s It belongs not to physics, but to morals.)\n4. With this aim, will common understanding suffice for the cognition of God that interests everyone, or will erudition and deep speculation be required? Common understanding, for the cognition must be able to be demanded of everyone.\n5. What is the minimum of theology? That it be at least possible that a God is, and that no one could know so much as to refute us if we believe him.\n6. How far does knowledge go on this point? Merely that our concept agree with itself and not conflict with its own aim. The object is a mere idea, thus cannot be given according to concepts of experience, and our positive cognition cannot here be knowledge.",["The opening is grammatically defective in the German (der der Vernunft natürlichen Bedürfnis); it is rendered as it stands.","“if it pleased it to reveal itself”: ihm … sich can equally be read “if it pleased him to reveal himself”; “believe him” renders ihn glauben, which may also be read “believe it” or “believe in him”.","“to prevent that we do not contradict ourselves” keeps the pleonastic negative of the German; “knowledge” at the end renders wissen (lower case, used as a noun)."]],"reflm:6214":["S. I:\n(s *theologia vel rationalis vel revelata*.)\n(s *Physicotheologia a primo motore*.)\nThe concept must be thoroughly determined *a priori*. This is only that of the *ente realissimo*. (g Hence this theology the foundation of the rest.)\n1. *Ens originarium* (s *Cuius nec conceptus nec existentia est derivativa*) as *ens summum*: *Theologia transscendentalis*. (s This com|prehends\nontotheology and cosmotheology.) (s Utility of transcendental theology.)\n2. *Ens originarium* as *summa intelligentia* (s living): *Theologia naturalis*.\n3. *Ens originarium* as *summum bonum*: *Theologia moralis*.\n(s The concept of God: 1. as* [isolated concept] sibi sufficiens conceptus, 2. as boundary concept, 3. as architectonic concept.)\n*(s Possibility from concepts, 2. necessity as ground of the series, 3. of the system.)\nHe who assumes merely the first theology asserts merely world-cause.\n(s Anselm:\nOntotheology.\nWolff:\nCosmotheology.)\nHe who also assumes the second theology asserts it as world-author.\n(s Physicotheology.)\nHe who also assumes the third theology asserts it as world-ruler (s lawgiver).\n(s Concept — nature — will of God)\n(s speculation or moral theology. Moral theology is not theological morals. In the latter, morals is derived from the existence of God, in the former the existence and the concept of God are determined through morals.)\nDeism, theism. Relig: *Theismus Moralis*.\nThe world as a realm of nature, 2. of ends. 3. of grace.\nThe principle for proving the existence:\n1. From mere concepts or experience in general;\n2. From the object of particular experience, knowledge of the world;\n3. From our ends.\n(s *Ens realissimum* as ground is the principle of all beings and of their [material] real possibility.)\nThe certainty is apodictic or physical (s hypothetical) or moral.\nKnowing, conjecturing and believing.\n(s Utility of transcendental theology: negative.)\nProperties. 1. transcendental theology: Nothing but ontological predicates.\n1. *Possibilitas* (g *omnimoda*).(g *Essentia* (g *originaria*)) (g *Ens summum* (*entium*) as aggregate or as ground. I only limit the consequences.) (s *contra Atheistas dogmaticos*) 2. *Existentia* (g *necessaria*) *contra Atheistas*. 3. *Necessitas contra scepticos*. 4. *Substantia* (g *non unica*) *contra Spinozam* (g *mundus non aliud*). 5. *Immaterialitas contra Anthropomorphismum*. 6. *Vnicitas contra Polytheismum* (g *Manichaeism*). 7. *Extra Mundum contra Stoicorum Animam Mundi*. (s *Omnisufficientia contra independentiam materiae*.) 8. *Infinitum* (s *incommensurabile*) *contra Anthropomorphismum subtiliorem*. (s We |\nrepresent to ourselves the possibility of all *finitorum* as derivative.) 9. *summa perfectio* (g *formalis* as *consensus* with all reality outside it, thus not as *destructor*, but *conditor realitatis*) *contra Manichaeismum*. 10. *Aeternitas, Omnipraesentia, Omnipotentia* (*Omnisufficientia*) (g *non solum formalis, sed materialis*). (s *attributa operativa*.) (s *Causa, qvae non habet concausam, e.g. Materiam causam mali*.) *Immutabilitas*. *Impassibilis*.\n(s Whether the concept of God is a physically or a morally necessary concept, necessary in order better to rationalize or to act? First determination of its idea as supreme principle of all existence. Anthropomorphism. Either from the concept of the highest being the concept of the original being, or conversely. From the order of nature contingency is inferred. The understanding of the author does not prove his necessity.)\n(s transcendental theology for the sake of the purity of the concept of God.)\nS. II:\n(g Moral proof of the existence of God. as of a necessary hypothesis for the speculative and practical interest.)\nSceptical atheism is godlessness, the dogmatic: denial of God. The first disputes only the possibility of making oneself a concept of it, the other the possibility of the being itself.\n(g All concepts except that of the *entis realissimi* are undetermined in regard to the remaining predicates.)\nThe oneness follows from the *omnitudo realitalis*, because in the concept of the *entis realissimi* as *Noumeni* space and time do not occur; otherwise two beings with the same properties could be in different places.\n(s The subjective necessity of reason to assume God holds for us objectively.)\n(g from concepts or from *factis*)\nThe ontological proof is one and the same with the pure cosmological, only that there from the concept of the entis realissimi the necessity, here from the necessity of some being or other (as independent existence or supreme ground of the actual) the concept of the realissimi is inferred. The first errs in that it regards the proposition: ens realissimum exists, as analytic, since it is nevertheless synthetic and as such cannot be proved. The second: in that it takes the concept of the relative necessity of the determination (of a thing (g in general) in regard to the (g opposed) predicates that can belong to a thing in general) for a concept of the absolute necessity of the thing itself*; namely, that ens realissimum is thoroughly determined in regard to all its predicates and these belong to it necessarily is supposed to be just as much as: it exists necessarily. The analytic necessity again confused with the synthetic.\n*(g The error is really this, that one believes [the real nec] to infer from the logical necessity of the predicates of an entis realissimi, since namely only this concept is throughout necessarily determined, to the real necessity. We should only say: we have thereby a necessary concept of a being, not: a concept of a necessary being.)\n(g Contingent is that which exists only in a conditioned way. Thus only the unconditioned is necessary. We can know this only through its being the condition of everything else. Time and space are contingent forms of our own intuition.)\n(g To the moral proof anthropomorphism is most of all opposed.)\nThe theistic concept is a merely relative or regulative, not absolute and constitutive anthropomorphism. The concept of God as boundary concept of the cognition of nature according to speculative laws of the whole of reason.",["The asterisks after “as”, before “(s Possibility”, after “thing itself” and before “(g The error” are Kant's reference marks; the Latin on those lines is left without italic asterisks so as not to be confused with them.","“godlessness” renders Ohngötterey, “denial of God” Gottesläugnung; “oneness” renders Einigkeit (as against Einheit, “unity”).","*omnitudo realitalis* is printed so; probably a slip for *realitatis* (“totality of reality”)."]],"reflm:6215":["Th 2':\nWe live in an age which has not yet had its equal in the history of the human understanding. To be sure, the human mind has [to be sure] perhaps already exhausted all possible folly and delusion of an erring reason, and for every new nonsense one will no doubt always find an example in the old time; but that all kinds of absurdities and delusion show themselves at the same time and indeed publicly, while [the true investigation its] reason pursues its business publicly and calmly: this is [the] to be regarded as an unavoidable misuse of the freedom to think, now first beginning to sprout, which, just as in states that have thrown off despotism, must first produce anarchy and civil disruption, but finally nonetheless a lawful civil state. The [civil] magisterial authority does not meddle in any disputes that concern merely (g public (written)) thinking, except solely insofar as this concerns religion, because this is actually a mighty support of morality and by means of it of civil [peace] security and combination |\nis. States that have an inner firmness let these disputes, to be sure, run their free course, so far as they remain among scholars; but as soon as separations and factions begin to be generated from them, which in matters of religion commonly produce either partiality for some and hatred against others: so they too become concerned about the outcome, for the equilibrium is hard to maintain. [Would let] Best, though, is: they let these things go and favor reason and science; for in this way alone can lawful freedom of thought (instead of anarchy) and supremacy of reason (instead of despotism of orthodoxy) arise.\n(g Nothing is more harmful than stupidly brazen ignorance.)\nThe nonsense rests for the most part on the chatter about religion without preceding determination of theology, and indeed first of all of that which has its origin in reason alone and which must also be laid at the ground of every other, be it grounded on history or on immediate inspiration, |\nas criterion of its correctness. — It is therefore, chiefly in our times, of the greatest importance to expound a well thought-through [theol] and, in its whole domain, completely executed theology of mere reason [to bring about], which latter can also be done, since what is demanded is not to know everything that belongs to the object, but what human reason can cognize of God. When theology and religion are mixed together, there arises a confusion of concepts, in which one regards theology as a necessary consequence and duty of religion and therefore already proceeds partially. In regard to the former, [all] speculative reason must first be left alone and free.\nIn order that we may first make out the way of thinking and consequently the subjective causes of the discord of human opinions [here] and, when we sift and examine these, at the same time discover the source from which the absurdities arise, and thus be able to divide the history of the aberrations determinately, we must first of all oppose to the genuine use of reason the manifold delusion in regard to the use of reason in general, and consider this preferentially.\nDelusion is the [maxim] opinion of a freedom in thinking without [the consid] regard to the laws of reason. Thus it has imagina|tion\nor illusion of the senses as its ground. Imitation. Presumptuous ignorance.\nTo delusion the experiential use of reason is opposed. Not that it must take its grounds precisely from experience, but [to] it must not think further than [that] so far as it can be applied [according to laws of the] to experience according to laws according to which this itself is possible.\nTh 3':\nWhat is the experiential use of our reason?\nWe can give to all our concepts significance, and to our judgments reality, only insofar as [that we them] so far as (g they) agree with the principles of an experience possible in accordance with them (otherwise they are paraphysical). All principles for explaining things of experience which are not in conformity with the rules of a possible experience, but [indep] transcendent breaks off.",["“experiential use” renders Erfahrungsgebrauch; “delusion” renders Wahn, “illusion” Schein; “combination” renders Verbindung (here also “union”).","“magisterial authority” renders obrigkeitliche Ansehen; “factions” renders Rotten.","“breaks off” renders bricht ab, the edition's note that the text stops here."]],"reflm:6216":["Someone accepts (g [as]) [principles of experience the ... a] experiences, with which no reason at all can be used, and is superstitious; or as a principle of reason, with which [all reliability] no connection of experience is possible, and is enthusiastic.\nOr someone rejects everything that is not provable either through reason or experience, and he is unbelieving. He, on the other hand, who also concedes something as a necessary hypothesis [to comp], insofar as it is indispensable to the completion of the theoretical or practical use of reason, is believing.\nThe believer does indeed accept something which can be no object of experience at all breaks off.",["“superstitious”: abergläubisch; “enthusiastic”: schwärmerisch; “unbelieving” / “believing”: ungläubig / gläubig.","“accepts ... as”: annehmen zum; the interlinear “zum” and the struck-out words leave the first construction broken, as in the German.","“breaks off”: the AA's “bricht ab”, an editorial note that the text stops here."]],"reflm:6217":["He, therefore, who accepts an idea (g of reason) with which no laws of experience can agree is enthusiastic; he who [appear] accepts laws of experience with which no use of reason can coexist is superstitious. He who indeed cognizes the reciprocal agreement of reason and experience in [one ob] the estimation [o] of an object as necessary, but at the same time will concede no |\nother object than one whose existence is sufficiently provable through reason or experience, is unbelieving. (Cash.)\nRational belief is [the necess] the principle (g maxim) of reason to accept that which is unavoidably necessary [in the] for the completion of the series of grounds of a reason agreeing completely with the experiential use. Now the first of this series can be proved neither through reason nor by way of inferences from experience [proved] as a determinate object. Thus rational belief is indispensably necessary for the complete experiential use (by which I understand the derivation of the maxims of reason exercised on objects of experience). Enthusiasm is madness. Superstition stupidity and unbelief folly, i.e., frivolous abandonment of the principal end (namely of the completeness of the grounds for that which can in general be the end of speculation or practice). Inquiring use of reason, thus the one [se] which concludes with a rational belief completing that one, is wisdom.",["“object”: obiect (Latinate spelling) here, Gegenstand later in the passage; “experiential use”: Erfahrungsgebrauch; “rational belief”: Vernunftglaube.","“(Cash.)”: “(Baar.)”, i.e. ready money; the reading and its connection are as obscure as in the German.","“completing that one”: “jenen vollendenden”; “jenen” may refer back to the use of reason or to the principal end."]],"reflm:6218":["The way of thinking according to ideas of which one can make no experiential use (g is enthusiasm). God, freedom, immortality are ideas of which one can make an experiential use. But secret forces, to pervert nature, spiritual intuitions are ideas of which, precisely because they make experience impossible, one can make no experiential use.\nThe way of thinking of principles from *factis* of which one can make no experiential use is superstition; e.g., presentiments, significant dreams, appearances of the deceased.\nUnbelief is the principle of denying everything that cannot be an object of experience. For there are always still subjective grounds of taking to be true when objectively sufficient ones are lacking. The subjective ground, however, with regard to the supersensible is reason, never sufficiently satisfying itself through the sensible, in the theoretical and moral.",["“way of thinking”: Denkungsart; “experiential use”: Erfahrungsgebrauch.","“secret forces, to pervert nature”: the comma leaves open whether the infinitive qualifies the forces or is a further item.","“appearances”: Erscheinungen, here of the dead (apparitions)."]],"reflm:6219":["The speculative concept of God is (g it) first of all most highly necessary to |\ncorrect; but its worth cannot be rated so high that the moral one would not have to give it its foremost determination. Even the unity of this being, if one does not at the same time ascribe to it a holy will, can yield no secure religion.\nThe way of thinking [of the] of a reason separating itself entirely from the experiential use is fantasticism. (Whim-catching.) (g Chimera. Reverie.)\nThis sentence has subsequently been altered as follows by words or letters written above it, without the words that no longer fit the new context having been struck out:\nThe way of thinking of a reason conflicting with the principles of the experiential use is delusion.\n* Fantasticism is: 1. Enthusiasm, i.e., a [through] fantasticism that seeks to raise itself through higher reason (g by means of pure intuition) above the reason attaching solely to the experiential use (restricted to it). 2. Superstition: a fantasticism that by means of (g empirical intuition) experience (his own or others') wants to raise itself above (the experiential use of) reason. (g To this experience are counted narratives of miraculous effects (which conflict with all laws of experience) or wondrous occurrences united by fantasy as cause and effect.)\nThe former is insanity (g madness), the latter is stupidity. The first is twofold: either of the delusion of immediate illumination or of extravagant speculation, e.g., of the astral nature-spirits. The supposed derangement.\n*(g The transcendent use of reason is the one that ventures to judge without any [possibility] relation of it to possible experience. To this is *diametraliter* opposed the denial of reason in that which deviates entirely from experience. Consequently denial of the experiential as well as of the transcendent use of reason.)\nUnbelief is the maxim of conceding no other experiential use of reason (g hence no use of reason at all) than with regard to an object of experience. Thus he must either hold everything that is |\nnot an object of the senses to be impossible (this he can only do if he regards the senses as the kind of cognition through which alone objects are represented immediately as they are) or, contrary to the experiential use of reason and thus to the maxims of its use in general, at least as dispensable and entirely groundless, to accept such a thing, e.g., to regard God [as], who is no object of experience, therefore as nothing or yet (his presupposition) as entirely dispensable and unnecessary to reason. The former unbelief is the empiricist, the second the sophistical or rationalist, which [believes] supposes to explain everything [to be able] or also to be able to provide all [moral] practical laws with sufficiently moving force [thus an influence of the if] and asserts an experiential use of reason, namely its force to produce good actions, although he merely accepts objects of experience and no other actual things, but merely makes use of reason.\nBelieving* is he who concedes to his reason (g to accept) what is indispensably necessary to its completeness, be it in the theoretical or practical experiential use, although it cannot prove it it.\n*(g The small number of believers who are capable of a rational belief out of moral interest. They want to have cash: either in cognitions or the advantages of life.)\nThe belief without which it is impossible to make even the experiential use of reason, be it in the theoretical or practical, [complete] self-satisfying, is a pure rational belief.\nWithout a pure rational belief (g the use of reason becomes) either know-it-all-ism (pansophy) or misology, [self-] suicide of reason.\nTo become superstitious: for this human beings have a* natural propensity; they personify natural causes, do not yet know the lawfulness of nature, nor the importance of the use of reason solely under [the] presupposition of that lawfulness. They are thus easily brought to the point, when something is unusual or they vehemently desire some|thing\n(or when they dream), of imagining something that they do not see, which knows their wishes and could be moved or appeased by them. Superstition without determinate representations of their objects.\n*(g Enthusiasm is grandiloquent (presumptuous) ignorance and a convulsive state that is contagious through sympathy.)\nWe can make no other applied use of reason, i.e., one in which the object is given (g immanent use.), than by our being able to produce in thought, according to laws of experience, that which we think.",["“fantasticism”: Phantasterey; “whim-catching”: Grillenfängerey; “insanity” / “madness”: Wahnwitz / Tollheit; “delusion”: Wahn.","The paragraph “This sentence has subsequently been altered ...” is the AA editor's note, standing in the source text; the “or ... to accept such a thing” clause is syntactically broken in the German and left so.","“it it”: the German repeats “es es”; “[self-]”: the struck-out “Selbstver” is a fragment whose completion is unknown."]],"reflm:6220":["The delusion of honor on account of posthumous fame after death.\nThe delusion of honor on account of the high esteem of that which has no inner worth.\nMoral perfection, although it cannot be entirely attained, is nevertheless no delusion.\nEnthusiastic and superstitious delusion. God is nevertheless no delusion.\nThe experiential use of reason is the use of reason that [according to the rules] (g its reality) can prove its truth through presentation of its (concepts and) principles in experience, even if one accepts something that is not an object of experience. e.g. soul as incorporeal being or pure morality. If one also merely accepts something of the sort without being able to prove it, then even the acceptance of it is nevertheless in conformity with the experiential use of reason with regard to the form [of the re] of its application and the principles, just as we proceed in general with regard to hypotheses.\nEvery use of reason that does not agree with the principles of the experiential use is delusion; e.g., to sense heavenly influences, to have influence on the realm of spirits.\nAll use of [pure] reason is either an experiential use, whose application to experience is possible, or a [super] chimerical use of it. The maxim of the latter is delusion.\n[To the] To the former [is] is either merely an object of experience laid at the ground or also an object of mere reason, but the application to objects of experience according to rules of it. The first is the physical, the second the [pure] experiential use of pure reason.\nHe who denies the possibility of the last is unbelieving.",["“principles”: Grundsätze, but Principien in “the principles of the experiential use”; “presentation”: Darstellung.","“its (concepts and) principles”: “ihrer” may refer to reason or to truth; “rules of it”: “derselben” may refer to experience or to the objects.","“[super]”: the struck-out “übers”, presumably the start of a word beginning “über-”."]],"reflm:6221":["Th 5':\n(g The need of reason to think to itself, beyond the things of experience, still more, which cannot be experienced and on account of ends has reason, is the first cause for accepting gods.)\nHow did human beings first come upon the opinion of the existence of invisible forces, which the usual course of experience could not teach them.\nEither 1. by the way of reason, or 2. of imagination; (g For objects which neither experience nor reason has given are possible merely through imagination;) on the first: a. through rational proof, b. through rational belief; on the second: a. through enthusiasm (g rational intuition), or b. superstition.\nThe first way is not the one which human beings took at the beginning.\nOn the second way, guidance by enthusiasm is also not the first, for it presupposes attempts, also some beginnings of rational knowledge, which however do not satisfy (g reason) imagination. Thus it is imagination without the guiding thread of reason, hence supported by seeming experience, without knowing (g demanding) its (g constant) lawfulness (as that in which the experiential use of reason consists), i.e., superstition, which first brought invisible forces or also powers onto the scene.\n(g Rational belief is earlier than rational proof. Both are grounded on the striving of reason toward completeness.)\nThese could have been merely forces of visible things (g objects of experience), which however did not stand under the rule of experience: fetish, Manitou, talisman or shades of the dead (g objects of past experiences), or even invisibly ruling beings. The latter took place only in the state of civil constitution under chiefs, and thus arose the proper concept of gods. Whether they first represented these as fearsome, because misfortune and dangers make one superstitious, to which longing hopes also belong, or the provision of nature as their work represented them as kindly, cannot be the question.\nThat these gods were even the authors of nature could not occur to them; they held them themselves to be natural things (g or former human beings.), which only had more power, but all natural things to be necessary in themselves.\nThese gods had to be persons, otherwise they could not direct their superstitious wishes to them, but mighty without morality. Alone they could think no god, precisely because being alone seems a misfortune, thus polytheism. Finally it comes to moral concepts: a law and a lawgiver. Unity of God, and now all perfection. — Up to here sheer superstition and now reason. — One was not content with the influence on morals. — Now enthusiasm arose. Neoplatonic sects last as long as rational proofs alone are supposed to hold. — Only rational belief with consciousness of its ignorance can keep off enthusiasm.\nTh 5:\nRational cognition is either subjective, i.e., cognition of one's own reason, or objective: cognition of an (g distinct from us) object through reason. The necessary presup|position\nof a first ground in the series of things subordinated to one another is not a cognition of the necessity of this first in itself. This would be cognition of its absolute necessity, of which we are entirely incapable. It is also not the cognized hypothetical necessity of the thing, but the cognition of the subjective necessity of the hypothesis according to principles of reason or rather of the acceptance of an absolutely necessary thing. This necessity of a thing determined through concepts that do not lie merely in pure reason, for the explanation of appearances or practical rules, is not knowing, not opining (hypothetical), but believing.",["“imagination”: Einbildung (not Einbildungskraft); “rational knowledge”: Vernunftkenntnis; “rational proof”: Vernunftbeweis.","The first interlinear sentence is obscure in the German (“was nicht erfahren werden kan und wegen der Zweke Vernunft hat”), and is left so.","“objects”: obiecte (Latinate spelling) in the two interlinear additions, Gegenstand elsewhere."]],"reflm:6222":["God signifies to crude human beings a being that has reason and has more power than all forces of nature known in experience that act voluntarily. Yet sometimes lifeless things such as fetish and Manitou have been taken for it. Deceased human beings.",["“act voluntarily”: willkührlich wirkende; the phrase may also be read as “acting arbitrarily”."]],"reflm:6223":["In and beside, perhaps also on Th § 4:\nReason needs very much in order to satisfy itself in the concept of a supreme ground of things, chiefly not merely in pure use, but in the applied to all experience as well as morals. Nature and freedom.",["\"Morals\": *Sitten*.","\"in the applied\": *dem Angewandten*, elliptical (sc. use), left elliptical."]],"reflm:6224":["On Th § 2:\n[The] religion is the consideration of moral laws as divine commands.\nMorals teaches how we become good human beings, [theology] religion: how we become human beings well-pleasing to God.\nMorals teaches how we can become worthy of the highest good, religion teaches how we can become partakers of it.",["\"Morals\": *Moral*."]],"reflm:6225":["On Th § 2:\nTheology, insofar as it has influence on morals, is (moral) religion; insofar as it [itself a] contains a particular object of morals, is *cultus*. This would presuppose religion.\nFor religion it is enough to believe; for the *cultus* one must know, otherwise it is hypocrisy.",["\"morals\": *Sitten*."]],"reflm:6226":["On Th § 2:\nThe moral religion is that which makes better human beings.\nThe *cultus* is the religion which, if it is to be genuine, already presupposes good human beings, because they are to take to heart the duty toward God himself.\nThe mere possibility of the divine existence is already sufficient for moral religion, yet not so much as belief.\nBelief is absolutely necessary for the *cultus*, indeed scarcely sufficient.\nFor divine service proper knowledge is required; otherwise one only worships in order to keep oneself safe in all cases, not out of conviction.",["In the second sentence the German passes from *die Religion, die* to *wenn er* (masc., the *cultus*); the shift is not reproducible in English.","\"the duty toward God himself\": *die Pflicht gegen Gott selbst*; *selbst* may go with *Gott* or with *die Pflicht* (\"the duty toward God itself\").","\"sufficient\" renders both *zureichend* and *hinreichend*; \"divine service\": *Gottesdienst*."]],"reflm:6227":["On Th § 2, end:\nReligion is the moral disposition insofar as it has the cognition of God as its ground (not the pragmatic). There is indeed no religion without cognition of God; but this need not at all be knowing; it can be merely a pure idea of God that is morally correct (although as speculation full of errors), and secondly contain only the conviction that it is after all possible that there is a God, or moreover a firm belief. For the former morality is not required; but if it is there, then it can with that problematic judgment [tog] in combination yield religion. For the last morally good disposition is already required.\nWhich is the *minimum* of theology that is requisite and sufficient for religion? (s I. (g With regard to existence:) the possibility of assuming one. 2. With regard to the concept: the moral and the metaphysical combined with it. (The ancients had a *cultus* without religion (g moral), consequently also without theology from reason, but tradition.) Philosophical theology cannot with regard to the positive about God expand that *minimum*, but can nevertheless bring it to science through the negative, through which the errors that are unavoidable for common theology are kept off. — Philosophical (g rational) theology is no divinity; the *revelata*, as grounded on ancient documents and ancient languages, is alone divinity.)",["\"need not at all be knowing\": *darf gar nicht wissen seyn*, with *wissen* lower-case, verbal; *darf nicht* in the older sense \"need not\".","\"disposition\": *Gesinnung*; \"divinity\": *Gottesgelahrtheit* (learnedness in God), as distinct from *Theologie*.","\"[tog]\": the deleted *[zus]*, presumably the start of *zusammen*."]],"reflm:6228":["On Th § 3:\nDoes our cognition of God have dignity and can we occupy ourselves with nothing else more worthily. The object has the highest dignity, but our acquaintance with it is very paltry: it consists for the most part only of restrictions of that which we know, namely insofar as it is only creature. Thus our calling is not to investigate behind the divine nature, and this cognition is of no worth; but rather: 1. To conclude and to crown our cognition of nature and of morals with it, in that we consider everything as belonging to the infinite cause, hence in the highest nexus, likewise represent our will as contained under the universal legislation in the realm of ends. 2. Not to investigate what God is, but: what we ought to be in relation to him, i.e. what use the idea of him can be to us.",["The first sentence has question word order but ends with a period, as in the source.","\"acquaintance\": *Kentnis*; \"know\": *kennen*; \"restrictions\": *Einschränkungen*; \"calling\": *Beruf*."]],"reflm:6229":["On the heading of Th § 3:\nSpeculative truth (logical) consists here merely in the possibility of this concept. practical in the necessity of the presupposition of this being with regard to all moral laws.",[]],"reflm:6230":["On Th § 3 »the theologian proper«:\nAre there also theologians of natural religion?\nAs concerns knowing, the philosopher here knows merely his ignorance, i.e. he is acquainted with the bounds of his reason; but to this science also belongs. The bounding of nature in every respect makes precisely the concept of God necessary for us, but, since it goes beyond the concepts of nature, also unattainable as speculation.",["\"theologian\": *Gottesgelehrte* (one learned in God).","\"knows\": *weiß*; \"is acquainted with\": *kennt*; \"bounding\": *Begrenzung*."]],"reflm:6231":["In Th § 3 no. 1 Kant inserts after *haben*:\nthat I think something in it and can give them in examples.",["\"them\": *sie*, which may also be read as singular (\"it\")."]],"reflm:6232":["Under Th § 3:\nAmong the Greeks: Thales *physicus*, Anaxagoras *theologus*, Anaximander (*physicus*), Xenophanes (*theologus*).",[]],"reflm:6233":["On »errors« in the heading of Th § 4:\nThose that are not practical are to that extent also pardonable.",[]],"reflm:6234":["On Th § 5, beginning:\nThe real difficulty consists in this, that the concept is transcendent and no property of it at all can be given in any possible experience, consequently no example can be added to the concepts.\nWe [must] are to leave out everything sensible from it; but then nothing remains but concept without corresponding intuition, thus without examples and application *in concreto*, except only according to analogy and *symbola*.\nOn Th § 5 no. 1:\nThe limitations do not hinder us so much as the inclination to overstep them and the self-conceit of reason and presumption to introduce false judgments and to pass off incomprehensibility for impossibility.",["\"limitations\": *Schranken*; \"presumption\": *vermessenheit*."]],"reflm:6235":["Moral theology has as its principle: If the laws of duty stand firm a priori, consequently flow necessarily from practical reason, but nevertheless without presupposition of a rational moral perfect being as author of the whole of nature have no force to move the will: then this presupposition is inseparable from practical reason, and the idea of God must be arranged not in accordance with the observation of nature, but with the need of morality: In this I am now relieved of all the circuitousness of speculation. I can declare it entirely incapable for this end and demand only that it after all cannot prove that there is no God, and have enough in belief. The proposition has no difficulty, but rather the speculative attainment of it.",["\"a rational moral perfect being\": *eines Vernünftigen moralischen Vollkommenen Wesens*; may be read \"a rational, morally perfect being\".","The last sentence is elliptical in the German and left so."]],"reflm:6236":["In moral theology [can] it is enough to presuppose that it is after all possible that there is a God, and that no one could ever prove his non-being; hence we would then be entitled to lay this existence by hypothesis at the ground of a practical and indeed for the sake of necessary laws [in order]. For these laws are absolutely necessary, but cannot subjectively become practical without that presupposition.\nThe practical indifferentism of theology makes speculative theology the only possible one, and, if the critique of reason is not favorable to it, it leads to skeptical atheism. Moral theology, however, permits a theism that at the same time can be critical with regard to speculative theology.\nTheology as principle of virtue serves only to remove from morality the hindrances that could be taken from the objection of an empty ideality. As principle of religion it is, through duty toward a higher being, itself a motive to virtue. As principle of a divine service it is the ground of actions whose effect is directed immediately at God. — A subjectively necessary hypothesis is when I see no other ground of explanation; an objectively necessary one, when I have insight that no other is possible for human reason.",["\"of a practical\": *einer practischen*, feminine dative with no noun following; the construction is incomplete, perhaps through the deletion [um].","\"principle\" renders *Princip* here, as it renders *Grundsatz* elsewhere."]],"reflm:6237":["The atheism of doubt: skeptical (g which denies all cognition and conviction of God).\nThe dogmatic atheism of assertion (g which denies the existence of God).\nThe critical (with regard to the faculty of pure and speculative reason) deism is moral.\nThe physicotheological deism is in itself undetermined and a source |\nof all theological errors contrary to religion, polytheisms and anthropomorphisms.\nIn physicotheology I infer from the contingent nexus of ends to the existence of God, and this concept therefore cannot be determined.",["\"contrary to religion\": *Religionswiedriger*."]],"reflm:6238":["Even if neither reason nor nature proved to me the existence of the most perfect, reason would nevertheless need a concept of it, in order merely [to] to estimate the derived according to it.",[]],"reflm:6239":["The supreme concept, (g which) lies at the ground of all things.\n1. as complete concept of a thing in general,\n2. as complete concept for nature as ground of explanation,\n3. as complete concept for the ends of rational beings.\n1. God as the highest being.\n2. God as the supreme universal cause.\n3. God as the highest good.\na) For pure speculative reason,\nb) for the empirical use of reason with regard to nature,\nc. for the practical.\nAlways the unconditioned and at the same time complete.",["\"supreme\": *oberste*; \"highest\": *höchste*."]],"reflm:6240":["The belief of a thing and [its] of a being is distinct from belief in this being. The latter is the presupposition of its existence as supreme practical ground, thus in which I take a moral interest.",["\"belief of a thing\": *Der Glaube eines Dinges*, genitive, as against *Glauben an* (\"belief in\")."]],"reflm:6241":["First the need of (g pure) reason with regard to (g the concept of) a complete thing in general: in order to serve all others as foundation and measure. Second the need of reason with regard to an existence: in order to serve as ground for all remaining existence. Third the need of reason with regard to an |\nexistence that is possible only according to concepts, namely of the actually present ends (of nature) or of the merely possible and as such nevertheless at the same time necessary ends (of freedom).\nFor all of this reason needs unity. 1. of [determi] thoroughgoing determination (g of each thing for itself), 2. of connection (g thoroughgoing) in one world, 3. of purposiveness.\nThis Refl. was subsequently, from the first *dienen* on, heavily altered by additions and deletions, so that it now reads as follows:\nand with regard to an existence: in order to serve as ground for all remaining existence. Second the need of reason with regard to an existence that is possible only according to concepts through nature, namely of the actually present ends (of nature). Third: for that which is possible only according to concepts through [fr] our freedom, to serve as principle* (necessary ends (of freedom)).\n(* g A.\nNeed of a (g first) thing with regard to those (g the first of the things) which are possible only as *causata*. *Primus Motor*. B.\nNeed of a (g first) thing with regard to (g the first) those which are possible only (g as *caussata*) [through] according to concepts (purposiveness). (g the first of causality according to concepts) C.\nNeed of a (g first) thing with regard to that which is possible outside nature, thus of freedom only [through] according to concepts, but in harmony with nature. )",["\"need\": *Bedürfnis*, used both feminine and neuter in the passage.","\"purposiveness\": *Zwekmäßigkeit*; \"connection\": *Verknüpfung*.","*causata* and *caussata*: both spellings are Kant's."]],"reflm:6242":["God is a concept (idea) of the first of all beings, which is the supreme cause of everything else.",["\"which\": *welches*, neuter, refers to the first being (*Wesen*), not to the concept."]],"reflm:6243":["The final end is moral theology. Thus concept of God must be sufficiently (g determined) for it.",["\"final end\": *letzte Zwek*; \"sufficiently\": *hinlänglich*."]],"reflm:6244":["Minimum of rational theology for the sake of morals, since it makes better human beings.\nIt is possible that there is a God, is sufficient for religion, but not for the *cultus*; for that presupposes not merely belief but knowledge. The proposition: one ought to believe, presupposes that he who says it knows what (g he) wants to be believed.\n(g The *minimum* of rational theology is a concept of God well in agreement with itself and with the need of reason in principles, chiefly the practical ones, and the possibility of his existence, consequently the entitlement to assume it (g opining). The *maximum* is knowledge, i.e. the (g complete) taking to be true, insofar as it is grounded on proofs. The middle, appropriate to the least capacity [appropriate] and to the best will, is believing, which is the acknowledgment of the necessity of such a hypothesis either for one's own theoretical or practical use (theoretical and practical belief).)\n(g For virtue [belongs] the *minimum* of theology is (g sufficient for virtue), namely mere opinion; for fear of God (g religion) *majus*, namely belief; for the *cultus* (divine service) the *maximum* of cognition, namely knowledge.)",["\"rational theology\": *Vernunfttheologie* (theology of reason).","\"what (g he) wants to be believed\": *was (g er) geglaubt werden will*; without the addition, \"what is meant to be believed\".","\"entitlement\": *Befugnis*; \"opining\": *Meynen*; \"believing\": *das Glauben*."]],"reflm:6245":["Because the concept of the original being is entirely negative, namely that as regards its possibility and existence it descends from no other ([and] is not derivative), through which, however, one does not know what it is, the concept of the original is here to lead to a concept thoroughly determined a priori.\nBut the concept of an original being is entirely arbitrary if one does not presuppose that something exists; in the latter case, however, it is necessary to assume such a one. But because then some existence or other is presupposed, the proof is not ontological, but cosmological, yet nevertheless transcendental.\nIf the existence of the original being is to be carried through by ontological grounds of proof alone, then existence must be concluded from sheer concepts and not go ahead a posteriori. In the ontological way, therefore, from mere concepts of possible beings that one must be found which includes existence in itself, and this [is the] seems to be the concept of the *entis realissimi*.\nThis has the peculiarity about it that through a universal concept the object is at the same time thoroughly determined, and to that extent similar to the concept of an existing one. But since besides the concept in its thoroughgoing determination something else must still come in addition, namely no new determination, but the position of the thing in itself outside the concept, i.e. existence, the thoroughgoing determination cannot yet include existence, and the proposition: *ens realissimum* exists, is not an analytic but a synthetic proposition; hence it cannot be found through mere *analysis*.",["\"original being\": *Urwesen*; \"the original\": *des Ursprünglichen*.","\"the position of the thing in itself\": *die position des Dinges an sich selbst*; *an sich selbst* may go with *position* (\"the position, in itself, of the thing\") or with *Dinges*.","\"carried through\": *geführt*, the verb used of conducting a proof."]],"reflm:6246":["But why, if the all of reality is to be laid at the ground of the concepts of all things, precisely all reality in one being and not in many? Because the multiplicity of things as things in general takes place only through the different limitation of unity, consequently of the all of reality, consequently each of these beings is always possible only through a greater [alone], the greatest thus alone can contain original (not derivative) possibility. So many beings that have shadows presuppose a foreign light by which they are illuminated; only that which is [no] light without any possible shadow, i.e., the original light, presupposes no foreign one.",["“the all of reality” = das All der Realität; “multiplicity” = Vielheit."]],"reflm:6247":["The question is whether for the concept of the *entis originarii* the abso|lute\nnecessity of its existence is also required. Can one not perhaps say: it is neither necessary in itself nor contingent in itself? For both pertain only to respective position and to the existence of the inhering, i.e., of an incomplete being that is itself no thing; the subsisting can perhaps indeed be thought as to its non-existence, but not-being can never be something that can befall it once it is regarded as existing, indeed not its arising either.\nAll proofs of the existence of God and of the determination of his concept rest in the end on the transcendental proposition: That the most perfect being is an *ens originarium* (s *possibilitas originaria* and *existentia originaria*) or: that conversely the *ens originarium* is an all-most-perfect being, consequently there could not be more of the same, also everything else is derived from it. The first of these two propositions is the ground of ontotheology, and there existence is inferred from the concept of its properties. The second is the content of cosmotheology (s *theologia naturalis*), and there conversely its concept is concluded [to the] from the existence of something in general. [The Physi] Thus all theology is properly built upon the transcendental concept of the *entis realissimi*. Physicotheology makes use of the contingency of the |\nthings of this world (chiefly on the ground of their purposiveness) in order to infer a being distinct from the world, but afterwards pursues the transcendental path in order to conclude from it the concept and the existence of the most perfect being; insofar it has no advantage over transcendental theology. But now it infers from the particular properties of this world the (g psychological) properties of the highest being as a highest intelligence and transforms deism into a theism. Moral theology adds further to the realm of ends as a realm of nature the concept of a supreme head in the realm of morals and makes of the highest being the highest good.\nIf ontotheology does not succeed, then cosmotheology does not succeed either; if both, i.e., transcendental theology, do not succeed, then neither does physicotheology, consequently knowledge nowhere. So then belief still remains. Cosmotheology gives God as first cause of the contingent and has therein an advantage over ontotheology, because it gives indication of a free will (*summa intelligentia*). Physicotheology gives indication of a will governing the world: *summus imperans*.",["“all-most-perfect” = allervollkommenstes; “not-being” = Nichtseyn; “existence” = Daseyn.","“infer” = schließen, “conclude” = folgern; “knowledge” = Wissen, “belief” = Glauben; “morals” = Sitten.","“consequently knowledge nowhere” (folglich überall nicht das Wissen): the verb “succeed” is understood from the preceding clauses, as in the German."]],"reflm:6248":["The transcendental concept of God is no cognition, because one can posit no object corresponding to it, e.g. There exists a most real being, through nothing but logical concepts, but not categories, because these require intuitions to which those can be referred.\nThe *omnitudo* in the concept of the *realissimi* is not the same as logical universality (one in all); for the latter is distributive, but the former collective (much that makes up one), namely synthetic unity. Whether objective reality now belongs to this concept I cannot guess from my thoughts; I must adduce intuition, e.g. circle. But this is always sensible and is not supposed to be applied to the *ens realissimum*, and other intuition I do not have; thus I cannot procure objective reality for it, i.e., my thoughts cannot be called cognitions of the object. Reality is that whose representation in itself contains a being;[whether such] what that is supposed to mean I do not even rightly understand, although I understand the logical being in the relation of concepts. I want an example, that is sensation (of the senses); but this cannot serve as example for God; consequently I cannot give it objective reality.",["“object” = Obiect throughout; “objective” = obiectiv.","“a being” and “the logical being” render ein Seyn and das logische Seyn (the infinitive “to be”), not Wesen."]],"reflm:6249":["The existence of the unconditioned [is] to assume, reason has an objective ground of cognition. This cognition is objectively certain. But |\nhow is it to be determined. All concepts of possibility outside the *Realissimo* are conditioned; for everything is either *realissimum* or *limitatum*, and with us the concept of the *limitati* is at all times derivative with regard to the *realissimi*. This alone is *conceptus originarius*, namely a *principium cognoscendi non derivativum*, and, what is remarkable, reason, in order to represent to itself a possibility according to thoroughgoing *determination*, thus as complete, must conceive for itself the principle of all possibility in the *realissimo*; but on that account the *ens originarium* is not straightaway *principium essendi* of all things.\nOf the *ente originario* [shall b] it is thought that it is not merely no *derivativum* but itself *principium* (*non principiatum*). That is its [negative] general concept as of the unconditioned principle. In order to determine it, I must think it as *realissimum*, i.e., a subjective ground, i.e., need of human reason and necessary hypothesis, merely in order to make the possibility of things thinkable. But when the practical need, by which I am necessitated to think (g it), is added to that, then a belief arises from it, since theoretical and practical reason agree and nevertheless there is no insight.\nThus 1. the subjective condition of thinking possibilities is taken for the objective condition of the possibility of the things themselves as dependent on an *ente realissimo*.\n2. The concept of the *realissimi* as all-sufficient ground or as being [be] containing all reality in itself.\n3. The determination of this concept through predicates of intuition (because this is sensible and not adequate to it) is impossible. Thus I can think an *ens realissimum* through logical functions, but through theoretical reason cannot bring it under categories, because these refer merely to intuition.\nThe proposition that the unconditioned must be the condition not only of one |\nkind of cognition but at the same time of all follows from this, because otherwise it would itself be limited and thus its possibility again conditioned.",["The opening sentence is left incomplete by the deletion of “ist” (“The existence of the unconditioned is to be assumed”); the English keeps the resulting construction.","“ground of cognition” = ErkentnisGrund; “belief” = Glaube; “insight” = Einsicht."]],"reflm:6250":["ZuTh § 9:\nWhether it is an invention to ascend to the concept of a most perfect being.\nWhether a determination belongs to the concept of the most real being, one can never know with reliability. Is it extended or not? There we can only say that extension does not belong to it, because it comprises limitations, being and not-being, in itself, consequently not-being belongs to its [Re] determination. In this, however, we know nothing real. Where we believe we cognize something merely real, e.g. cognition, there it is |\nuncertain whether it does not stand in real conflict with another real which is still more fruitful, i.e., the grounds of it do not cancel each other in the same subject or reciprocally annihilate the consequences.",["“limitations” = Einschränkungen; “consequences” = Folgen; “real conflict” = realer Wiederstreit.","“In this” (An dieser) may refer to “determination” or to “extension”."]],"reflm:6251":["ZuTh § 9 Überschrift:\n(g independent:) The original being,\n(g perfect:) highest being,*\n(g all-sufficient:) being of all beings.\nThe word \"infinite\" can only then follow.\n*(g This concept is of such a kind that it determines its object thoroughly, and indeed with regard to that which lies at the ground in all *praedicatis oppositis*, namely the real.\n*Ens summum est igitur ens entium*, for all manifoldness is that of limitations.\n*ideoque ens summum non est derivativum, sed originarium*. An idea as *prototypon*.)",["*Ens summum est igitur ens entium*: “The highest being is therefore the being of beings”; *ideoque ... originarium*: “and therefore the highest being is not derivative but original”.","“limitations” = Schranken; “original being” = Urwesen."]],"reflm:6252":["ÜberTh § 9:\nEither we begin from the *realissimo* or *necessario* and infer from the former concept to the latter or conversely.",[]],"reflm:6253":["The concept of a negation is a derived concept, for it presupposes that of reality. But it is also not by itself the concept of a thing (because every *ens* must be something real and the negation thus only the determination of a real). It is thus only the limitation, and, since that of a thing already thus *partim* negative presupposes just such limitation, the original concept on which all thoroughgoing determination |\n(g of a thing as thing in general) rests is that of an *entis realissimi*. Thoroughgoing determination is an idea which cannot be carried out *in concreto* (g as *limitati*), but of a thing as thing in general it would be that of a thing as *realissimi*. For the complete is the ground of the possibility of determining everything incomplete[as thing].",["“original concept” = Urbegrif; *partim*: “partly”."]],"reflm:6254":["The concept of an *entis perfectissimi* does not contain as much (g and is also not as determinate) as the concept of the all-sufficient being (*omnisufficientis*); for even if no being were sufficient for everything possible, one could nevertheless be a sufficient ground for most of what is possible. Some moral concepts already contain the relation to this *omnitudinem* without containing a *superlativum*. These are the only wise, holy and blessed, because all three contain [the high] relation to the highest good for cognition, for the [feel] will as legislative, and for the same as good.",["“for cognition, for the will ..., and for the same”: the German has bare datives (dem Erkentnis, dem Willen, demselbigen), whose relation to “relation to the highest good” is left open."]],"reflm:6255":["A most perfect being, of whatever kind it may be, is thoroughly determined through this concept*, and if it is thought only according to metaphysical perfection, then it is also thoroughly determined through this concept (although the concepts of place and time cannot be encountered therein). Now we call something, insofar as it is still undetermined through its concept with regard to many predicates, insofar merely possible; for the actual is thoroughly determined, although not on that account actual. For although I can say: everything actual is thoroughly determined, I nevertheless cannot say: everything thoroughly determined is actual.\n*(g Hence Plato's ideas are represented as individual beings which, unalterable and eternal, are the self-subsistent archetypes of things.\nAs thing in general (*metaphysice*) only one being is thoroughly |\n[determ] determined through its concept. All other things can be considered as if they give all possibilities through the omission of some determinations.)",["“for the actual is thoroughly determined, although not on that account actual” renders the German as it stands (das wirkliche ist durchgangig bestimmt, obgleich darum nicht wirklich); the reading looks doubtful.","“actual” = wirklich; “archetypes” = Urbilder."]],"reflm:6256":["It is impossible to determine a concept thoroughly; but it is subjectively necessary to represent to oneself the thoroughgoing determination of it as the idea of the completeness of the concept, i.e., to represent the object to oneself without this subjective limitation, and so we think to ourselves every object insofar as it is posited in itself, namely in the completeness of the determination of the concept of an object in general. If this concept is at the same time that of a real object, i.e., of a thing (g consequently the concept of an object insofar already more closely determined), then the complete determination of it is the concept of the *entis realissimi*. No other concept furnishes a rule for determining the object with regard to every given predicate. Thus it is the condition of the application of the principle of thoroughgoing determination to an object thought *a priori*.",["“object” = obiect throughout; “limitation” = Einschränkung."]],"reflm:6257":["ZuTh § 10:\nWhether one can actually think objectively many realities or one without limitations?\nThe greatest reality as ground is at the same time combined with all others. Whoever posits one property of God posits all.",[]],"reflm:6258":["ZuTh § 10:\nWe seek to cognize true reality (g not phenomenal) only by this, that we try whether it could belong to God.",[]],"reflm:6259":["ZuTh § 10:\n*realitas phaenomenon* is (g not) pure reality and does not belong to the *enti realissimo*.\nThe *prototypon ideale* [all] as *logicum*. All the positive needs the determinations of the understanding of things. We take namely merely that from the real of our (g possible) experience which belongs to the concept of the understanding.\nDependence alone constitutes the sure sign of a *realitatis phaenomeni*. Now the question is whether we can ascribe to it even a single one apart from the transcendental ones, which are properly only the titles for concepts, or all that we can give *in concreto* only *per analogiam*.",["“All the positive needs ...” (Bedarf alles positive der Verstandesbestimungen der Dinge): the sentence can also be read with an unstated subject, “[It] needs all the positive of the determinations of the understanding of things”.","“sign” = Kenzeichen (not Merkmal)."]],"reflm:6260":["ZuTh § 10 Anmerkung, Satz 3: »Ein endlicher — Grössen«:\nwere that so, then we would not be able to use the concept of our understanding, through elevation to completeness, in order to represent to ourselves the divine one.",["“the divine one” (den Göttlichen) is masculine, agreeing with Verstand."]],"reflm:6261":["ZuTh § 11, Satz 2: »widerspreche«:\nbut indeed: that it cannot subsist with it in one subject, [without] namely follow from a common ground.",["Whether the negation “nicht” also governs the second clause (“namely [not] follow from a common ground”) is left open, as in the German."]],"reflm:6262":["ZuTh § 11:\nAll predicates of God are derived either from his concept as *entis originarii* (g his independence) or *realissimi*. The first, which are at the same time those of absolute necessity, are the surest.* (g e.g. *Simplex* — immaterial.) Unity from the concept of the *realissimi*.\n*(g for that the *ens originarium* is a *realissimum* is itself only a hypothesis, to infer from subjective conditions of reason to objective ones.)\nOneness says that [all R] the highest reality cannot be distributed among many things, nor belong wholly to many things. The first [is] follows from the simplicity of the *originarii*.\nWe cannot indeed see into the possibility [of God of the concept of] of God as sum total of all reality [but] (for the possibility of the concept, since realities do not contradict one another, is not enough); [for] but we can nevertheless be sure that no one could make even the opposite comprehensible; for realities that conflict with one another in one being must absolutely be *realitates phaenomena*, and those are not in God.",["“Unity” = Einheit, “Oneness” = Einigkeit; “see into” = einsehen; “sum total” = Inbegrif."]],"reflm:6263":["On Th § 11? On Th § 12?\nIt is a great difference whether one regards the *ens realissimum* with respect to all reality as its *subiect* or as ground. According to the latter idea, understanding beings could come from it without its itself having understanding. For the cause does not need to have the quality of the effect. The expression: \"God is the highest reality\" is the *genus* under which those two concepts stand as *species*.",["\"understanding beings\" renders \"verstandige Wesen\"."]],"reflm:6264":["On Th § 12, beginning:\nThe highest reality: 1. as content of the thing, 2. as ground.\nAll reality and the All of reality [In ei] as one single being.\nThe All is the standard of mere reason, which cognizes no restricted unity otherwise.",["The deleted \"[In ei]\" is a broken-off start (perhaps \"In einem\", \"in one\") and is left in German.","\"restricted\" renders \"beschrankte\"; \"standard\" renders Maasstab."]],"reflm:6265":["On Th § 12, beginning:\nWhether I should not rather say: a reality in its highest degree?",[]],"reflm:6266":["On Th § 12, Remark 1, sentence 3:\n[The] Infinity is a mathematical concept.\nOf the given mathematically infinite magnitude is also determined through this concept, but only relative to the human understanding. It is that which in relation to the unit as measure is greater than all number.\nThe metaphysically infinite is what contains everything, hence the entire possible magnitude of a certain reality.",["The second sentence is grammatically broken in the German (\"Des gegebenen mathematisch unendlichen Große ist ...\": a genitive with no governing noun); it is rendered as it stands.","\"unit\" renders Einheit."]],"reflm:6267":["On Th § 12, Remark 3, final sentence: \"determinable — finitam\":\n*definitam*",["Kant's correction replaces Baumgarten's \"finitam\" (finite) with \"definitam\" (definite, delimited)."]],"reflm:6268":["On Th § 13:\ninner infinity: extensive, intensive infinity.\nInfinity in the real relation: just as before.",[]],"reflm:6269":["On Th § 13:\nGod as *ens summum*. Deism.\nThe concept of the *entis realissimi* as *entis summi* determines itself thoroughly (g with respect to the (g ontological) predicates), but not that of the *infiniti*.\nBecause all negations are here regarded as limitations, the *ens realissimum* is *ens illimitatum*.\nWhether not every reality, insofar as it is represented (in relation to all possible reality) as absolutely complete, could furnish a central concept of the *entis realissimi*, and the highest understanding, the highest power, the Eternal, the All-blessed not each in particular [the Go] constitute the complete concept of God.\nIf its existence can be seen into a priori, then the *ens summum* is a necessary being.\nIf a determinate concept of the absolute necessity of a being is possible, then through this the existence of it is also proved.",["\"limitations\" renders Schranken; \"seen into\" renders eingesehen (einsehen, have insight)."]],"reflm:6270":["Probably with reference to Th § 13, Remark 2, no. 4:\nPossibility and actuality are not distinguished in things in themselves; rather [through] in relation to the concept the thing is merely possible, in relation to completely determined intuition actual. — If I say: a thing is possible, that means: it can be (but not: because it can be, therefore it is). (g Our concept of possibility pertains |\nmerely to relations; the absolute permits no concept of possibility that would be distinguished from actuality.)\nWe can ask of every thing why it is, because we can derive its existence from no concept; but it is not therefore contingent.\n[Possibility] Necessity and contingency are thus only relations of things to the concept. In themselves things are neither necessary nor contingent; they [are] exist, and their non-being can very well be united with their concept alone; but under the condition of connection with another existence [it] the non-being of them is impossible, i.e. they can be regarded as necessary in a conditioned way and at the same time as internally contingent according to concepts. In themselves, however, no such separation from the conditions takes place, hence neither conditioned nor unconditioned necessity. For possibility is distinguished from actuality only in relation to concepts.\nOnly with respect to the idea of freedom is possibility distinguished from actuality. Actions as appearances are determined in space and time (the determining ground of actuality), and there actuality is one and the same with possibility, i.e. necessity. On the other hand the very same actions are, *respectiv* to the intellectual *subiect*, which determines itself merely according to concepts, free, because in themselves they are nothing and are possible only *respectiv* to the activity according to concepts, as ways of representing this activity sensibly; thus the actions as [appearan] determination of the intellectual *subiect* are neither [necess] sensibly neces|sary\nnor contingent, but not sensible at all, and insofar free from natural necessity.\nThe absolute possibility of a thing we cannot comprehend, i.e. cognize and have insight into a priori, for that is already itself the insight into possibility without anything being given; but possibility is supposed to be cognized without actuality and before it, and it is supposed to be synthetic. Synthetic impossibility can never be comprehended, e.g. of a figure, where synthesis in our sensible intuition [i.e. a] is not first laid at the ground, whereby the *obiect* is given a priori *respective* to our sensibility. Hypothetical possibility can be comprehended if something has already been given as actual, and the law of effects and causes. The absolute necessity of a thing, however, is entirely beyond our concept; yet we have insight that possibility precedes actuality only *respectiv* to our concept, and that in itself the one is not separated from the other.",["\"Synthetic impossibility\" follows the text as printed (\"Die synthetische Unmoglichkeit\"), though the context speaks of possibility.","\"comprehend\" renders begreifen; \"have insight into\" renders einsehen; \"one and the same\" renders einerley."]],"reflm:6271":["On Th § 14:\nEverything that belongs to its determinations belongs to its essence, for it is thoroughly *determinirt* through its concept.\nIts possibility is not distinguished from actuality, hence also the essence not from its nature.\nYet here the talk is not yet of existence, but only of the concept of the *entis realissimi*.",["\"essence\" renders Wesen; \"determinirt\" is Kant's Latinate verb beside German bestimmen."]],"reflm:6272":["On Th § 14, sentences 3-5:\nThe transcendental concepts are pure only when one omits the conditions of their empirical use and in general all conditions of sensibility, e.g. presence, or cause, upon which something follows.",[]],"reflm:6273":["On Th § 14, sentence 3: \"attribute without hesitation\":\nFor through this we determine only a thing in general, without restricting it in particular and distinguishing it from the most real.",[]],"reflm:6274":["On Th § 14:\nFirst it comes down to showing what lies in the concept of the *entis realissimi* with respect to the ontological predicates, [as] insofar as it is considered according to content or as ground. Afterwards existence.\n1, Analytic predicates. Ontological apart from existence.\n2. Synthetic: first* the absolute, 2nd relative as cause of a given world.\n*(g of its absolute or relative existence [of the] in relation to a world. In Spinozism God has no relative existence at all.)",[]],"reflm:6275":["On Th § 14:\n(g Atheist *statuirt* no existence: the dogmatic one (denier of God) denies the possibility, the skeptical one (Ohngötter) all proof of the actuality. The former the concept, the latter the ground of proof.)\nThe atheist must nevertheless concede the analytic predicates of God (g for the possibility of such a being one cannot dispute).\nThe deist concedes also the synthetic ones, but purely transcendental ones, not the physiological ones, out of fear of anthropomorphism. He thus also has no moral use of the cognition of God.\nSpinoza was no atheist in the transcendental sense, also no deist, for he denied only cosmotheology.\n[He] His error arose, however, from a false ontology, in that he set up the concept of a substance so that there would be only a single one.\n(g One could say with much semblance in favor of Spinozism: if all forces and faculties of a substance created and preserved by God are merely divine actions, if apart from these nothing can be thought by us in them, then one could not at all have insight into how the *subiect* of them is then to be posited outside God. Against this, however, if we perceive in ourselves effect and with respect to other things counter-effect, then again it is not to be seen into how we should be *accidentia*, which can never be *subiecte* of acting and suffering.)\nThe [is] deist is one often from modesty, the theist is one (g often) from arrogance.\nThe [reality] relative existence of God as the creator of the world, not as the world-soul.\nThe synthetic predicate of absolute existence,\nof relative existence.\nUnity. *conceptus singularis*.\nMany gods would, as necessary beings, not stand *in commercio*. Manicheans.\nCosmological argument. We can infer from alteration not |\nto contingency, but, if one does not admit the infinite series, only to the necessity of a first, but also cannot make its first action comprehensible.\nOntological argument. From the concept of the *realissimi* the necessity of existence,\nCosmological argument. From the concept of a necessary being to infer its quality as highest reality.\nIn the cosmological proof I infer either from the concept of the dependence of everything that is alteration to a first, which is without alteration (*primus motor*), or from that of contingency to something necessary, and then I ask: what properties does a necessary being have. The necessity of existence, however, can be derived from no properties at all and is absolutely incomprehensible.",["\"Ohngötter\" (literally \"without-gods\") is Kant's coinage beside \"Gottesleugner\" (denier of God) and is left in German; \"statuirt\" (Latin statuere, posit) is kept.","\"semblance\" renders Schein; \"substance\" renders \"Substan§\", a slip in the transcription.","\"suffering\" renders Leiden (passio, being acted upon)."]],"reflm:6276":["Through the predicate of existence I add nothing to the thing, but rather the thing itself to the concept. In an existential proposition I thus go beyond the concept, not to another predicate than what was thought in the concept, but to the thing itself [to] with precisely the same predicates, no more, no fewer, only that [it as add] the absolute position is thought in addition over and above the relative (*complementum possibilitatis*). The ground of the illusion lies in this, that the concept of the *entis realissimi* contains the *omnimodam determinationem*, but all others leave the *obiect* undetermined in many ways.",["\"existential proposition\" renders \"existentialsatz\"; \"illusion\" renders Schein."]],"reflm:6277":["At the lower margin under Th § 15, but probably still on Th § 14:\nThe whole difficulty of transcendental theology rests on this, that it is not possible to determine the concept of the absolute necessity of a thing, i.e. to say on what its thinkability rests. |\nNecessity of propositions is absolute only when it is analytic; but the absolute necessity of things is a synthetic proposition.*\nThe deduction of this concept yields: that it is a necessary hypothesis.\n*(g one can cite no case of it; everything is logical necessity. The opposite of no thing contradicts itself.)",[]],"reflm:6278":["According to Mendelssohn God [after all] cognizes the contingency of all things outside himself even in relation to his nature, thus the thoroughgoing natural or theoretical [necessity] contingency. But at the same time the practical necessity of them through his will as the best, and so the contingency of existing things is a proof of the existence of an understanding cause, without which they could not exist. His own necessity he cognizes absolutely (without our being able to comprehend it). But since we have no other concept of the way in which possible things can become actual otherwise than through nature than through a will, we lay this concept, which is taken from experience and has only subjective validity, at the ground of things in themselves. If we substitute for the concept of contingency the concept that we can think the opposite, not the conditioned but the unconditioned, then the argument infers thus: what we cannot think otherwise than so, not on account of contradiction, say, but because otherwise no rule of thinking is given to us, that is necessarily so; so we see that all are merely subjective presuppositions.\nIn the cosmological *argumento* (*a contingentia mundi**) one would have had to infer the contingency of the things of the world not from their alterations but from their restrictions; but then one would have had to presuppose [prove] what was to be proved, namely that the *ens realissimum* alone exists necessarily.\nBut if someone assumes that time and all alteration is not a determination of things but only a particular form of their sensible intuition, then the world could nevertheless be necessary.\n*(g This argument can [only], if it is conducted from the alterations of the world, serve only to show that the world could not be the *ens necessarium*. Otherwise it can be conducted thus: If something exists, then a necessary being exists. *Atqvi*. *E*. [The Ne] But no restricted being can be necessary (because it is not thoroughly determined through its universal concept, consequently it is contingent whether it is restricted so far and not less or more); thus the necessary being is unrestricted.\nThe alterations in the world nevertheless lead in the end only to contingency and a first as necessary being. Thus it comes down only to this: what sort of properties belong to it in order to cognize from the concept of such a being at the same time its necessary existence. Or is the possibility of an absolutely necessary being to be derived from some other concept? (It is a synthetic proposition and thus can by no means be derived from mere concepts.) Absolute necessity is a boundary concept, at which we, as everywhere, must necessarily arrive at the first, [without] and which can only be assumed [can] for the sake of the consequences, but is not in itself seen into or comprehended.\nTo infer from effects to the existence of the *entis realissimi* as cause proves this only as *realissimum tanqvam caussa*; but from possibilities of things which are regarded only as determinations of a single universal possibility, namely of the highest being, proves the existence of the *realissimi* as sum total, consequently also, if understanding [is] is reality, that it is understanding.\nAll error consists in this, that we take our way of determining or deriving or dividing concepts for conditions of things in themselves. — One can use Spinozism in order to overthrow dogmatism. The critical and practical philosopher fears nothing from such enthusiasms.)",["\"things\" renders both Dinge and Sachen (\"determination of things\", \"conditions of things in themselves\" are Sachen).","\"understanding\" as adjective renders verstandig/verstendig; \"Atqvi. E.\" (Latin: but; therefore) is kept as Kant's syllogistic shorthand.","\"enthusiasms\" renders schwarmereyen; \"boundary concept\" renders Grenzbegrif."]],"reflm:6279":["If we want to represent to ourselves a thing, whichever it may be, according to a certain concept, and to represent this concept to ourselves completely [so] with everything that belongs to the thing also outside the understanding, then we lay thoroughgoing determination at the ground as the schema of every complete concept. The concept of a thing in general as determinable according to it, or as the mere subject of determination, is merely possible, as congruent with this schema outside the understanding is actual, and, if it could also be considered through the mere concept as posited outside the understanding [through], necessary. Everything in a thing is called contingent with regard to which this is undetermined through its concept. Hence [are called the determin we call] that seems to us necessary which is thoroughly determined through its concept.",["durchgängige Bestimmung = thoroughgoing determination; bestimbar = determinable; zufällig = contingent.","\"ist blos möglich, als mit diesem Schema congruent ausser dem Verstande ist wirklich\": the German has no subject or comma before the second \"ist\"; the construction is kept as it stands."]],"reflm:6280":["On Th § 15? Th 21':\nThe inference *per metabasin εισ αλλο γενοσ* is not permitted in logic, since I use what holds of one kind of things or of cognition as a |\nprinciple for another kind of things or cognitions; but the transition to another kind of inferring can nevertheless be permitted, indeed necessary, namely from objective principles of reason to subjective ones: 1. for the sake of the completion of the grounds of reason in their derivation; 2. for the sake of the separation of the grounds of reason from all determining grounds of sensibility and on account of their self-subsistence, for a procedure that is supposed to determine concepts fully a priori, as in the moral. In such a *metabasis* no probability holds, but also no knowledge, but instead of the former there is necessity of the idea, instead of the second: hypothesis, which indeed has analogy with theoretical presuppositions, since I must nevertheless necessarily presuppose something which [I not] neither I know nor anything similar of the kind, nor can represent determinately to myself, in order to obtain for my use of reason completeness or even only security of the empirical use. I do not, properly speaking, step over to another thing, but to another kind of use of reason, and the necessity of this, insofar as it is supposed to be practical, justifies the theoretical. Without morality the hypothesis would always be ungrounded and [a] the purposiveness in the universe would lead at the very most to a Spinozism or emanation. But morality has without such a presupposition no prospect of the connection of the objective principle of the will (good) with the subjective (of happiness). To the system of reason (g and freedom) there corresponds no system of nature, and so the moral concept would concern a mere *ens rationis*, which would dissolve into nothing.\nThe existence of a merely happy being without morality has some value indeed for this being, but not for a mere spectator. The existence of a merely moral being without happiness has indeed the greatest value for a spectator, but not for the subject itself. The value of existence, however, must be objectively as well as subjectively determined |\nand certain. For I can well say: I must be truthful, even should fortune deny me all favor; but this holds only insofar as I am there and live, I exist as a good being. But I do not know why I am supposed to be there merely for acting. The very same will (in the idea) which commands this of me, why has it commanded my existence. I cannot agree to that. Thus morality is necessary in a conditioned way, but the condition (my existence) is then not merely con|tingent,\nTh 22': |\nbut impossible for my wish. The will thus does not agree as to matter with that in regard to form. — A necessary hypothesis of reason, which however in transcendental theology is itself only hypothetically necessary, namely under the subjective condition that I want to explain. Morals gives objective condition.",["*per metabasin εισ αλλο γενοσ* = by a transition into another genus; Vernunftgründe = grounds of reason; Selbständigkeit = self-subsistence; Wissen = knowledge.","sittlich and moralisch are both rendered \"moral\"; Moral = morals; Glükseeligkeit = happiness; Zweckmäßigkeit = purposiveness.","\"so fern ich da bin und lebe, existire ich wie ein gutes Wesen\": the clause order is the German's; \"wie\" may mean \"as\" or \"like\"."]],"reflm:6281":["Morality is clear for itself if the rational being exists; but its existence itself has no value for him if good conduct is not rewarded.",["\"für ihn\" is masculine, although \"Wesen\" is neuter; \"him\" keeps the discrepancy."]],"reflm:6282":["The concept of the unconditionally necessary being is the most unavoidable and yet most unattainable concept of human speculative reason.\nThe concept of the *realissimi* was only subjectively necessary, that of unconditioned existence is objective. To cognize this through reason means: to [vorst] think it as necessary. — The necessity of the hypothesis is distinct from the necessity of insight.\nOne can distinguish the necessity of the thing thus: 1. where the opposite of being*, or 2. where the possibility of all other things would be cancelled by it.\n*(g The possibility of the same thing; to have insight into the possibility of an *entis necessarii* means: to prove its actuality.)",["The struck fragment [vorst] (presumably vorstellen, represent) is left as it stands.","Sache = thing; Einsicht / einsehen = insight / have insight into; aufgehoben = cancelled.","Clause 1 breaks off after \"des Seyns\" as in the German."]],"reflm:6283":["[Die Exi] The concept of an absolutely necessary being is a necessary hypothesis of our reason: first in the series from the dependent to the independent (negative concept), second in the series of the parts to the complete (*ens realissimum*), in order to derive everything limited from the unlimited. The latter is only a greater ease, but not subjective necessity. On the other hand in morals this hypothesis is necessary for the agreement of nature with freedom, because otherwise this is a transcendent concept and thus its laws could also be transcendent, thus subjectively not practical.\nThe difficulty of comprehending the absolutely necessary, and nevertheless the (g subjective) necessity of assuming it, is common to the theologian |\nwith the atheist; hence the concept of the *entis realissimi*, as the one which is least opposed to it, is after all a permitted hypothesis. — From this follows immutability, not so much in the whole of time (because he then remains the same), as that he is not in time: eternity. (Duration) without time. *impassibilitas*. *Solitudo*. Nothing but concepts from absolute necessity. Now follow those from the highest reality. In transcendental theology there is no anthropomorphism, and it is therefore not necessary to ascribe anything to God [secund] *per analogiam*.\n(g The warrant, indeed even the subjective necessity of reason, to assume an *ens realissimum* rests on the presupposition, unavoidable even for the atheist, of an *entis necessarii* as independent ground of that which exists. Now such a being must after all have certain properties, and indeed such as cohere with its complete independence and at the same time with its sufficiency for the supreme ground of the contingent. We cannot, to be sure, state this concept objectively validly. But in our reason there is a single concept which alone for it, with regard to the thoroughgoing determination of a thing, is original and cannot be derived. This is the concept of the *realissimi*. It is moreover *conceptus singularis* and is suited to complete derivation and at the same time thereby to the affinity of all possibilities. Thus we have [subi] a subjectively sufficient ground to assume such an existence. This is a rational belief, which objectively is neither certainty nor probability. Rational unbelief has nothing to say against this assumption as hypothesis. Even the advantage of the unity of reason condemns its maxim.)",["The struck fragments [Die Exi], [secund], [subi] are left as they stand.","\"diese\" (this) in the first paragraph may be nature or freedom; \"ihm entgegen\" (opposed to it) may refer to the absolutely necessary or to the atheist.","Vernunftglaube / Vernunftunglaube = rational belief / rational unbelief; *impassibilitas*, *Solitudo* = impassibility, solitude."]],"reflm:6284":["The relative predicates of God (g with regard to the existence of other |\nthings) are not at all in Spinozism, if by relation external relation is understood. But if by it inner *respectus* is also meant, and indeed of the accidents to a substance and conversely, then relative predicates are also ascribed to God there. Thus relative predicates of God with the world: either 1. Of inherence: Spinozism. 2. Of dependence or causality according to the system of creation. 3. Of *commercii* according to the theory of the world-soul. The system of causality (g as to substance) is either according to emanation or according to creation. That of causality as to form either [des Ursp] of its coming into being or of mere modification of it (thus mixed cause: matter and God. *fatum*).\nThe relative predicates of God with regard to the state of other things. 1. Conservation. 2. Omnipresence. 3. Concurrence.",["\"Relation\" and \"Verhältnis\" are both rendered \"relation\" (the first Latinate).","*commercii* = of community (genitive); Mitwirkung = concurrence; the struck fragment [des Ursp] is left as it stands."]],"reflm:6285":["On Th § 17 end:\nThe things in space are all bound a priori to the condition of space. If this were something in itself, then it would also be necessary and God also bound to existence in it. God must |\nbe present to the things in themselves and so also to thinking beings, and in them thereby make the idea of space the necessary condition of their outer intuition.",["\"an sich selbst\" = in itself / in themselves throughout."]],"reflm:6286":["On Th § 19:\nThe deist ascribes to the *enti summo* only all reality *in abstracto*, but none *in concreto*. how now is the theist to proceed in order to ascribe it *in concreto* to god?\nWe proceed in the choice of realities *via* (g *tam*) *negationis quam eminentiae*, but in the way in which we ascribe the realities *in concreto* to the highest being, *secundum analogiam*.\n*per analogiam*.\nRealities cannot be thought *in concreto* through mere understanding, but they are always affected with conditions of sensibility; first, therefore, I will *via reductionis* free reality, as far as possible, from what belongs to it as *phaenomenon* (*adhaerentibus sensitivis*), for otherwise anthropomorphisms come out. But thereupon [the] raise it infinitely *per eminentiam* as *realitas noumenon* (even should all particular determinations *in* |\n*concreto* fall away). (Before the reduction the way of *eminentia* must not be taken; for human perfection too could grow to infinity without being different as to species.) But since the cancellation of everything sensitive also cancels the concept *in concreto*, which would transform all theism into a mere deism, there remains the way of application according to analogy, according to which I confess not to know how the divine properties are constituted in themselves, but only that they are thought in relation to the world just as human properties are to their products.",["*via tam negationis quam eminentiae* = by the way both of negation and of eminence; *via reductionis* = by the way of reduction; *adhaerentibus sensitivis* = the sensitive things adhering (to it).","\"Darauf aber [das] sie ... erhöhen\": the infinitive depends on the earlier \"werde ich\"; the ellipsis is kept.","Eigenschaften = properties; beschaffen = constituted; Verhältnis = relation."]],"reflm:6287":["On Th § 20:\n1. Possibility (g against dogmatic atheism) of the *entis rea|lissimi*:\nthat all reality could be contained in one common ground, i.e. the real possibility we do not have insight into, but merely the logical.*\nAgainst skeptical atheism this is not directed, for it only doubts proofs of actuality.\n*(g whether all perfections can be grafted onto one stock and can spring from an inner principle of the very same thing, we do not have insight into (although not into the opposite either), for in human beings different perfections can after all hardly be united, e.g. great activity, zeal with cautious examination and investigation etc etc.\nAgainst dogmatic atheism it is sufficient that we show that the impossibility of a highest being cannot be proved, because that would have to arise through the contradiction of this concept with itself; but we do not on that account have insight into its possibility.\nAgainst skeptical atheism it is enough to show that not yet on that account is every way of arriving at conviction cut off, |\nbecause that through speculation does not procure it (for so the skeptical atheist infers) (Ohngötter). Because, even if speculative conviction does not take place, moral conviction is nevertheless possible.\nThe speculative doubter is he who asserts that a thing must not be assumed because its existence cannot be proved. This holds, to be sure, in all speculative cognition. But this very same one, if he is a doubter in every respect with respect to the existence of God, is a skeptical atheist; to this, however, he is by no means entitled, unless he must hold all moral laws to be empty imagination, for then he also does not need to assume a God in a practical respect. He who asserts that virtue rewards itself does not need to assume a God.)",["\"Ohngötter\" is left in German: a coinage, literally \"without-gods\" or \"un-gods\".","Überzeugung = conviction; einsehen = have insight into; Einbildung = imagination (fancy); sittliche Gesetze = moral laws.","\"in aller Absicht ... in Absicht auf ... in practischer absicht\": Absicht rendered \"respect\" in each."]],"reflm:6288":["On Th § 21:\n*Ens realissimum est ens originarium*, if it can be proved that all possibility takes place only in and through it.\nExistence from mere concepts.",["Latin: the most real being is the original being."]],"reflm:6289":["On Th § 21:\nOne could infer in the same way that the most perfect of every being in its kind exists, because [it necessarily] existence is also perfection.",["\"eines jeden Wesens\" may be \"of every being\" or \"of every essence\"; \"in seiner Art\" = in its kind (or species)."]],"reflm:6290":["On Th § 21: Th 30':\nThat something is [possible] actual because it is possible according to a universal concept does not follow. But that something is actual because through its concept it is thoroughly determined among everything possible and is distinguished from everything possible as one, signifies as much as: it is not [more] merely a universal concept, but [the concept] the representation of an individual thing thoroughly determined through concepts in relation to everything possible. This relation to everything possible according to the principle of thoroughgoing determination is precisely the same according to concepts of reason as the somewhere or somewhen is according to conditions of sensible intuition.* For space and time determine not merely the intuition of a thing, but at the same time its individuality through the relation of the place and of the point of time, because |\n(g in) space and time possibility cannot be distinguished from actuality, for the reason that both together contain all possibility in appearance [hence all] in themselves as substrata, which must be given beforehand.\nFrom this it follows only that the *ens realissimum* must be given beforehand for the real concept of all possibility, hence just as space cannot be thought beforehand as possible, but as given; but not as [something] an object actual in itself, but a mere sensible form, in which alone objects can be intuited, consequently also the *ens realissimum* not as object, but as the mere form of reason, in its thoroughgoing determination, of thinking for itself the difference of everything possible, consequently as idea which is actual (subjectively) before anything is yet thought as possible; from which, however, it does not at all follow that the object of this idea is actual in itself.\nNevertheless one sees that in relation to the nature of the human understanding (g and its concepts) a highest being is just as necessary as space and time in relation to the nature of our sensibility and its intuition.\nTh 30:\n*(g Something whose relation (g to everything possible) is determined in absolute space and time is actual. Likewise that whose relation to everything possible is determined in the absolute representation of a thing in general is actual. Both belong to the thoroughgoing external determination with regard to possibility in general and [thus] thereby also make up the thoroughgoing internal determination of an *individui*.)",["“principle” renders princip; “thoroughgoing determination” durchgängige Bestimmung; “somewhere or somewhen” das Irgendwo oder Irgendwenn.","*ens realissimum*: “most real being”; *individui*: genitive of *individuum*, “individual”.","“in its thoroughgoing determination” may go with “reason” or with “to think”; the German is equally open."]],"reflm:6291":["Formal ground of possibility — *principium contradictionis*.\nMaterial ground of possibility — *principium omnimodae determinationis*. As sum total.\nThe first concept of the absolute necessity of a being is (g logical, i.e.) this: that [being] its non-being contradicts itself.\nThe 2nd concept of the absolute necessity of a being is metaphysical: that its non-being would cancel the possibility of all things.\nProof of a common ground even of the possibilities of things from essential purposiveness.",["*principium contradictionis*: “principle of contradiction”; *principium omnimodae determinationis*: “principle of thoroughgoing determination”.","“cancel” renders aufheben; “purposiveness” Zweckmäßigkeit."]],"reflm:6292":["Th 30':\nContingent agreement with ends can perhaps, as the atheist would have it, also be explained by mere chance. But necessary agreement with them cannot be thought at all if I assume the essences of things as given independently for themselves; for then it is impossible that they should necessarily agree together toward unity of end (e.g. to plot geometrical proportions). For this one must necessarily lay at the ground unity of the being from which all possibility is derived, in which possibility and actuality are not distinguished; for only then do all agree necessarily with one another toward unity, because they descend from such a one.*\nTh 30:\n*(g One must thus infer not from the concept of the *entis realissimi* to its necessity (inner impossibility of non-being), but from that of absolute necessity (as of a being whose cancellation cancels everything possible, hence of the possibility of an *entis summi* as highest ground, *entis entium*) to complete reality.)",["“essences” and “being” both render Wesen.","“contingent” renders zufällig, “chance” Zufall.","*ens realissimum*, *ens summum*, *ens entium*: “most real being”, “highest being”, “being of beings” (genitives in the source)."]],"reflm:6293":["On Th § 22:\nEven if through this proof the objective necessity of the highest being is not seen into, yet the subjective necessity of a hypothesis of it as *substrati* of all possibility (of the |\nthoroughgoing determination of every thing in general) in our reason, even in its speculative use, is seen into, although this use is not exactly necessary in itself.",["“seen into” renders eingesehen (einsehen, “have insight into”).","*substrati*: genitive of *substratum*.","“even in its speculative use” renders “selbst bey dem speculativen Gebrauch”; selbst may also go with “in our reason itself”."]],"reflm:6294":["On Th § 23:\nIf from the concept of a necessary being its highest |\nreality could be derived, then among all possible beings one would be determined as absolutely necessary, and I would here not have needed to lay experience at the ground.",[]],"reflm:6295":["On the heading of Th § 23:\nCosmological",["Cosmologischer: a masculine adjective standing alone, its noun not given."]],"reflm:6296":["If it is impossible to prove from the concept of the *realissimi* the *necessarium*, then it is still much less possible to prove from the concept of the *necessarii* its properties (realities).",["*realissimi*, *necessarium*, *necessarii*: “the most real (being)”, “the necessary (being)”, in the cases of the source."]],"reflm:6297":["On Th § 23. Th 34':\nProof from the indication of existing things to an *ens originarium*, and from this the inference that an *ens originarium* is also *ens realissimum*.\nThis proof assumes that an *ens originarium* is an *ens* (g *absolute*) *necessarium*, and infers that the *absolute necessarium* can be no other than *realissimum*. All this happens through our first applying the concepts of contingency and necessity to the existence of all things (not merely the conditioned) and therefore thinking an *ens originarium*, whose concept is merely negative, through a positive predicate, namely that of the absolute necessity of its existence.\nIf we separate possibility from actuality, this is a sign that we have not yet thoroughly determined our concept of the thing; for then the object is thought only as something that can be determined in one way or another among all [poss] predicates, with regard to [that] which it must nevertheless be thoroughly determined if it is to exist. The difference of possibility [is al] and actuality is thus no difference of things, but of concepts. insofar, the one which is regarded in all sorts of respects as still undetermined makes up the concept of a merely possible thing, but the one which is regarded objectively as determined, that of an actual thing. [The *ens origin*] Conditioned is the existence which cannot be cognized *a priori* otherwise than under the presupposition of a cause; unconditioned: that which [to be sure] either can be cognized *a priori* without yet (g to) being *rationatum*, or, although it may not be cognized *a priori* at all, consequently also not as *caussatum alterius*, nevertheless must itself be presupposed as cause.\nThe cosmological proof presupposes the ontological. For if from the fact that something is an *ens necessarium* it follows that it is also *realissimum* (because this can be only a single being and the concept of the *absolute necessarii* must be thoroughly determining, hence many sorts of beings are not to be understood under it), then it must also be possible to infer conversely that the [*absol: necessarium*] *ens realissimum* is an *ens necessarium*, for they are reciprocal concepts. I cannot convert the proposition: all triangles are figures, because the predicate fits more things than triangles. But if I find that the predicate solely and alone to this subject |\nTh 34: |\nis suited, e.g.: is enclosed in three sides, then I must be able to convert it. [Now the infer goes] and since the proposition is certain *a priori*, its converse must also be certain *a priori* for itself, i.e. in our case from the concept of the |\n[*abs: nec:*] *realissimi* the necessity must flow. Chiefly, however, the inference goes from the *necessario* to the *realissimo* through this, that the *necessarium* is not determinable in more than one way, consequently only a single one, and this single one must already through its concept be distinguished from all contingent beings.",["*ens originarium*: “original being”; *rationatum*: “consequence (of a ground)”; *caussatum alterius*: “something caused by another”.","“with regard to [that] which”: the deleted dessen is singular, the replacing deren plural; “(g to)” stands for the added infinitive particle zu.","“insofar, the one which …” follows the German, which opens with a lowercase “so fern,” after a full stop and leaves Begrif (“concept”) to be understood."]],"reflm:6298":["On Th § 23:\nWe can ask: why anything at all is actual; for we here presuppose possibility as independent of existence. But is the concept of possibility then also independent of all actuality and experience. The logical in possibility, the principle of contradiction, is it, namely the possibility of the concept. But the possibility of the things themselves requires more, namely synthesis. At the ground of this lies the actual nature of our sensibility and of our understanding, and it goes no further than to objects of possible experience. But even there the possibility, e.g. of a building, where none is there, is nothing but the determination of a concept through all sorts of predicates, which however is not yet extended to thoroughgoing determination, hence also with regard to the relations. Complete possibility we can never see into, [but] because we cannot determine a concept thoroughly and *in concreto* exhaustively. For if we could do this, there would belong to it predicates that cannot be taken otherwise than from experience, and whose meaning also consists only in the relation of the cognitive powers to possible experience.",["“things” renders Sachen here; elsewhere “thing” renders Ding.","“principle of contradiction” renders Satz des Wiederspruchs; “see into” renders einsehen.","The question “But is the concept … experience.” ends with a full stop in the German."]],"reflm:6299":["On Th § 23:\nThe cosmological proof: If something exists, then something |\nexists absolutely necessarily, i.e. it must be impossible that it not exist. If this signifies as much as: it must contradict itself, then no being is absolutely necessary. If it signifies as much as: I must necessarily (not as necessary) presuppose its existence according to laws of reason, then it is necessity of hypothesis. Synthetic propositions *a priori* that refer to no object [de] of possible experience can never have objective necessity. But the subjective, of the presupposition of reason of a *realissimo* as *substrato* of all possibility [at] in the principle of thoroughgoing determination, they can well have, and that is sufficient for us.\nReason always connects one existence to another and can posit nothing for itself; it is thus only the faculty of connections *a priori*, not to cognize absolute necessity in existence. Nevertheless absolute necessity is an unavoidable presupposition of reason for its totality. Thus it is subjectively, not dogmatically necessary.",["[de]: a deleted, broken-off word, left as it stands.","“the subjective” renders Subiective, with Nothwendigkeit (“necessity”) to be understood from the sentence before.","“not to cognize absolute necessity” keeps the German's loose construction; the infinitive may govern “connections” as well."]],"reflm:6300":["On Th § 24:\n*Theologia Naturalis*: God thought as cause of nature according to the analogy with natural causes, i.e. psychological predicates.\nPhysicotheology. Spinoza takes two properties of the deity, extension and thinking: the modification of the former is motion. But he will not have motion come from thinking, but immediately from the nature of the being, and then God is no being distinct from the world.\nTo infer from cosmotheology to the intellectuality of the original being would go thus. The world is a series of states following upon one another. The first beginning a being can make only through freedom; but freedom presupposes understanding, consequently the *ens originarium* is *summa intelligentia*. *Primus Motor*. This proof is better than the one that *intellectus* belongs to the highest reality. — Prevention of anthropomorphisms in theism — *secundum Analogiam* and *via reductionis*. Incomprehensible. — Of him no example, nor anything similar.\nLiving God. *Summa intelligentia*. *summa sibisufficientia* (g blessedness). *summum Numen* (g will). First only of the physicotheological concept, then of the existence.",["*Theologia Naturalis*: “natural theology”; *summa intelligentia*: “highest intelligence”; *Primus Motor*: “first mover”; *intellectus*: “understanding”.","*secundum Analogiam*, *via reductionis*: “according to analogy”, “by way of reduction”; *summa sibisufficientia*: “highest self-sufficiency”; *summum Numen*: “highest divinity”.","Urwesen = “original being”; Seeligkeit = “blessedness”."]],"reflm:6301":["On Th § 24:\nThe makeshift, in the case of the highest and in itself necessary being, which can be cognized through nothing given in experience (as cognition through appearances), of nevertheless making for oneself some concepts of nature which can have their examples *in concreto*, rests not on its unfathomability but on its incomprehensibility. The first it has in common with all things, the latter is proper to all its properties. We consider its relational properties only according to the analogy, but do not carry the absolute over onto it. These relations are thought through |\npure categories; but one cannot say: the *analogon* of these world-properties belongs to it, for this would prove a similarity in the constitution of the thing; we want, however, to say only that according to our way of representing to ourselves the possibility of things we must think its properties according to the same relations as we represent to ourselves those in the world.",["“makeshift” renders Nothhülfe; “unfathomability” Unergründlichkeit; “incomprehensibility” Unbegreiflichkeit.","“constitution” renders Beschaffenheit; “its” throughout may also be read “his”."]],"reflm:6302":["On Th § 35:\nThe preservation of species can either be regarded as entirely natural or requires a supernatural influence. In the first case the origin of species too might well have to be regarded as natural: for every generation is to be regarded as a new origin insofar as there are so many foreign causes which can modify and alter the formative force that, if there were not a counteracting principle according to universal laws, the regularity in propagation could not be explained from a predisposition once created. Especially if one assumes epigenesis.",["Arten = “species”; bildende Kraft = “formative force”; Anlage = “predisposition”; princip = “principle”."]],"reflm:6303":["Of moral theology. Here morality is laid at the ground of all\ntheology.\nConscience is 1. the faculty of becoming conscious of the rightfulness or unrightfulness of one's own actions. 2. The inner authority of this judging faculty as a judge, to call us to account on account of the authorization of our actions.\nThe supreme principle of conscience is: that nothing is permitted to be done of which the agent is not entirely certain that it is (in general) permitted to be done. We can undertake nothing at the risk of acting wrongly.\nA hypothesis whose contestation brings with it the risk of doing wrong, (g through) whose acceptance (g we) however can never do wrong, is morally certain, and [ein Geg] the presupposition of the same with the aim of the strengthening of morality is moral belief. Moral belief therefore is taken not from the agreement of our judgment with the object, but with our conscience.\nMatters of belief are those in which the morality of the taking to be true is the essential thing. The existence of God and of the future world are matters of belief of mere reason. With regard to speculation they are of no great importance, and also cannot be proved apodictically. But if morality is laid at the ground, they are indispensable hypotheses for putting them into effect.\nThe guideline of conscience here, with a morally good intention, is not to contrive more conviction than we are capable of, so that we are sure not to do wrong in this cognition.",["“taking to be true” renders Vorwarhalten (Fürwahrhalten); “authorization” renders Befugnis; “authority” renders Ansehen.","“[ein Geg]” keeps the deleted fragment, whose second word is cut off.","“putting them into effect” renders sie ins Werk zu richten; sie may refer to the hypotheses or to morality."]],"reflm:6304":["On Th § 49 note 2 »Spinoza«:\n*εν και παν*.",["*εν και παν*: “one and all”."]],"reflm:6305":["On Th § 71:\nOf the human-like in the expressions, which must nevertheless be understood *Θεοπρεπωσ*.\nOf the God-like in the idea of a perfect human being.",["*Θεοπρεπωσ*: “in a manner befitting God”.","“human-like” renders Menschenähnlichen; “God-like” renders Gottähnlichen."]],"reflm:6306":["On Th § 71 note 1, sentence 2 »as means«:\nnever merely",["The lemma is German in the source: »als Mittel«."]],"reflm:6307":["On Th § 71:\nOf God as the cause of the world. The ideal of humanity in its entire perfection is his first-born Son, the reflection of his glory, in him and through him all things are made [he himself is from |\neternity]. Hence he is also called the self-subsistent or original Word* (the ground of becoming). In him merely he loves (g alone) the world, and in relation to him the creator of the world is also called Father of human beings in his Son, i.e. as his *ectypo*. To bring the human being to this agreement with the ideal of the Son, there is the sanctifying Spirit from eternity in him, who unites the deficient creature with the holiest will through the striving to become similar to the ideal of the Son, and through supplementation of what is deficient in righteousness.** He is the judge in us, who holds the holy law before us, judges according to it, but also supplements what we lack in righteousness through the ideal of humanity, if we are on the way to coming ever nearer to it, and brings us in infinite, uninterrupted progression nearer to it and at the same time to blessedness.\nIn the [image] consciousness of the first Adam, in whom we have all sinned, and [with] the humble consciousness of the frailty of every (g human) virtue, which through contingent tempting circumstances, however great it may be, can be overthrown, and in faith in a second Adam, i.e. a human being who withstands all such temptations, even the hardest through disgrace and suffering, and thereby proves the practicability of what the holy law demands, hence that it is not an empty but a practical ideal: in the always progressing [Besse] approach to this example of perfection in this spirit of humility and at the same time of hope we can become the objects of the love of God in his Son by becoming similar to him and justified with him. Here there are no feelings and super|natural\ninfluences nor historical sources of cognition, but mere human reason.\n*(g He is the uncreated, but yet the archetype of the most glorious that can be encountered in creation; the human being who is similar to him as far as the limitations to which he as creature is unavoidably subject permit, is united with him in one person.)\n**(g He proceeds from the Father and is sent by the Son. For because no creature is equal to the ideal of humanity, since it can be holy not through concreation but through freedom alone: so the holy law has as its aim the influence of this law (g in) itself on the will of creatures, but the goodness of the Father with regard to the Son the will to take up the creature through him into his sonship. Thus a morally [erh] animating and sustaining power of God is necessary, to bring the human being [with] nearer to the ideal of humanity, to direct him inwardly according to it, and to ground and to strengthen faith in it toward the striving of an ever continuing approach to it. This Spirit is thus the means to bring the object of creation, humanity, [in the] pleasing to God, to actuality, namely the founding [of the community] of a kingdom of God under laws, a benevolent administration and a just court, and indeed under the only possible paternal one through sonship of the subjects.)",["*ectypo*: “copy” (ablative of ectypon); “archetype” renders Urbild; “concreation” renders Anerschaffung; “righteousness” renders Gerechtigkeit (also “justice”).","“[Besse]” and “[erh]” keep deleted fragments whose words are cut off.","“one” in “the only possible paternal one” stands for the bare adjective Vaterlichen, whose noun the German leaves unstated."]],"reflm:6308":["On Th § 73:\nIn religion the requirement is objectively orthodoxy (g *orthodoxie*), but subjectively conscientiousness, i.e. [pure] tested sincerity in the profession of what is taught as orthodox.\nIf someone according to his best capacity [that as] what now seems to him orthodox [to] chooses, then he can have religion with complete conscientiousness, and in fact there is religion only with pure conscientiousness. Now where there is no freedom of [choice is] public investigation, where either the forestalling with impressed prejudices or compulsion hinders the investigation, there is unconscientious religion, i.e. priestcraft [or the submissiveness power de], slavish [of God] or hypocritical submissiveness under the pressure of pious observances. One should not call that religion: it is priestcraft. For re|ligion\nmust be conscientious, and to conscience belongs freedom. One cannot say that he [who] who even accepts a true religion under compulsion proceeds conscientiously; for he must know and himself have insight that something is his duty, and cannot rely on the assurance of others. What he does herein according to moral laws of reason: the effort to establish himself firmly in integrity etc etc., that alone can be imputed to him for good; the rest is lost work and moreover hazarded, hence impure and mere solicitation of favor. Priestcraft is everywhere one and the same: Catholicism and Protestantism are essentially different. So there are arch-Catholic Protestants and indeed also Protestant Catholics. If one once assumes [the least] that among what we can do to please God there is something more than the good conduct of life, then there are no bounds.\nIf in one respect the character of the people is corrupted, then this extends also to several. Certain observances still seem to be necessary for religious [exercise no] -formation, and in this there must be a mechanism not easily to be altered; but [that it with that which] they must be so constituted that, even if they were unnecessary or grounded on false histories or pretended revelation, they nevertheless at least [would be] are morally indifferent. But confessions of faith in their truth are [the] a burden for the conscience. What I merely believe, [need] I cannot [to] (g as true) swear to, and to swear to it as believed by me is indeed for [the] now a guarantee, but I cannot swear that I will always believe it, [wei] consequently cannot pledge myself to it, and a sovereign who from his servants |\nyet not for this reason, because they do not want to act against conscience, takes the office (unless it be that of a teacher), always does wrong.",["“priestcraft” renders Pfaffenthum; “conscientiousness” Gewissenhaftigkeit; “sincerity” Aufrichtigkeit; “integrity” rechtschaffenheit; “solicitation of favor” Gunstbewerbung.","“[wei]”, “de” in “[or the submissiveness power de]” and “no” in “[exercise no]” keep fragments of struck words that are cut off.","The last sentence is kept as the German has it (der seinen Dienern doch nicht darum, weil …, das Amt nimmt … thut jederzeit unrecht); the placing of nicht makes its sense doubtful."]],"reflm:6309":["On Th § 73:\nOf sincerity, uprightness and integrity.\n(g The opposite of sincerity is impurity, in a higher degree falsity, in the highest unscrupulousness, which is distinguished from wickedness.)\n(g External sincerity (g in utterances toward others) is truthfulness, internal is uprightness, both together, insofar as they [on] tested [are], are secure, integrity.)\n*Integer vitae scelerisqve purus*. This seems to be the easiest and that which is in every human being's [power might] capacity. For it signifies nothing other than: never with consciousness that it is wrong, or with (g the consciousness) of an overcomable ignorance whether it is right, to do something. Nevertheless this is the real impurity rooted in our nature, which is laid aside latest, and everyone seeks to deceive himself and others. Truthfulness is what is required first. What is true is often hard to make out; but truthful one can be in all cases, if only one earnestly wills. The propensity to illusion, which the servants of form nourish, is the effect and in turn the cause of this corruption of character. Formality is very necessary in serious actions, but the end (g and disposition) must be truthful. At most it can (g then) become pedantry. But to set formalities as a surrogate for dispositions corrupts minds in civil dealings, in religion, even |\nin social intercourse. Honest someone can be, but he is not on that account also upright, i.e. honesty from principles (toward others); integrity is still more, namely sincerity with regard to oneself in the sharpest self-examination. Job. The harm which springs from it to the sciences, if one is not upright, to religion, if one is not of integrity. What is truth?, is a logical question in religion, and there orthodoxy is various. What is truthfulness and integrity?, is a practical (moral) question, and there one can easily have insight that each must follow his reason and conscience. The material of religion is: to be a better human being, the formal: to adopt the manner and way of a disposition devoted to God through actions in which in themselves there is no moral content. Thus formalities as final end or as essence of religion.\nOaths are formalities to awaken conscience; otherwise, as asseverations, they are, as Christ indicates, absurd. But they do great harm, like the signatures of the English clergy to the 39 Articles. They make the lie permitted if only no oath has been sworn. The first part of education.\nThe *argumenta a tuto* (nothing is safe but what is conscientious), *ab vtili*, used as proofs of truth, corrupt the character. How many a one conceals his own uncertainty and speaks loudly as decided, because he thinks he is being of use. To lay a sense into Scripture of which one is not certain that it is in it, if this sense is only moral, means: to ascribe a good intention to it, which we are always obligated to do as long as the opposite is not proved, and which is also the most eminent thing. Those who always ask: what God does to make them blessed, not: what they must do to become partakers of blessedness, always believe that by professing and highly praising the former they are doing everything on their side to become partakers of it, without being worthy of it, thus to acquire heavenly favor through ingratiation, and they corrupt the character.\n(g Solemnities in matters of religion are very dubious means. They are formalities as business. Formalities as method are good in the |\nadministration of justice and religion. But they must not displace the realities, are honestly meant.)\nLook of a human being who is not sincere (he squints, staring look).\nFreedom in thinking alone can make sincere human beings, compulsion in religion makes either hypocrites or at least such as, in that they investigate nothing, since (g it) does not help them anyway, in that they have no choice, are unconscientious. The very same freedom does indeed also make windbags and frivolous people.",["“sincerity” renders Aufrichtigkeit, “uprightness” Redlichkeit, “integrity” Rechtschaffenheit, “truthfulness” Warhaftigkeit, “honesty” Ehrlichkeit, “impurity” Unlauterkeit, “disposition” Gesinnung.","*Integer vitae scelerisqve purus*: “whole of life and pure of crime” (Horace, Odes I.22); *argumenta a tuto*, *ab vtili*: “arguments from safety, from utility”.","“formality” renders Förmlichkeit, “formalities” both Förmlichkeiten and the Latinate Formalien; “realities” renders Realien."]],"reflm:6310":["Of the Son of God. Every creature has duties. For all these it must have a model.\nThis it can find in no creature (g for that is entirely adequate to no duty, because before it a temptation can still be found which overthrows its good will), nor in the creator, for he has no duty, |\nthus only in that which proceeds from God, serves humanity as archetype, insofar therefore is human, but yet can exist only in God, thus [to] is combined with his essence as the Son of God, i.e. in the idea of humanity in the divine understanding. (No one is good but the one God.) that is thus the God-man, and whoever is in progress toward this archetype [is] is taken up by him.\nBut if the creature also has this model: whence does it take the capacity to be adequate to it? from himself he must take it, for otherwise it could not be imputed to him; but since no creature can at any point of time be completely adequate to it, there must be something which can serve it as guidance and give courage with regard to this progress into the infinite [i.e. the God-man]. This confidence the creature also cannot draw from itself. Thus only in that he comforts himself with the holy but at the same time also benevolent providence, in the measure that he makes use of his powers, also to be equipped in the progression with new (g greater) capacity for the future or also, in the contrary case, to be abandoned by it. That is the sanctifying Spirit, the source of moral life, which at the same time judges.",["“it” and “he” follow the German's shift between es (Geschöpf) and er; “model” renders Muster, “archetype” Urbild, “God-man” Gottmensch, “essence” Wesen.","“progress” renders Fortschritt, “progression” Fortgang; “before it” (vor ihm) may also be read “ahead of it”.","The sentence beginning “Thus only in that he comforts himself” has no main verb in the German."]],"reflm:6311":["-Sch. XI 1 S. 265-7. Hb. IV S. 502f. Ki. L S. 197f.\nRefutation of problematic idealism.\nOne divides idealism into the problematic (that of Cartesius) and into the dogmatic (that of Berkeley). The latter denies the existence of all things outside that of the one asserting, the former on the other hand says merely that one cannot prove the same. We will here restrict ourselves merely to problematic idealism.\nThe problematic idealist concedes that we perceive alterations through our inner sense, but he denies that one can on that account infer the existence of outer objects in space, because the inference from an effect to a determinate cause is not valid. — Alteration of inner sense or inner experience is thus conceded by the idealist, and if one therefore wants to refute him, then this cannot happen otherwise than by showing him that this inner experience, or what is the same, the empirical consciousness of my existence, presupposes outer perception.\nOne must here distinguish well the transcendental and empirical consciousness; the former is the consciousness I think and precedes all experience, in that it first makes it possible. This transcendental consciousness, however, furnishes us no cognition of our self; for cognition of our self is the determination of our existence in |\ntime and if this is to happen, then I must affect my inner sense. I reflect e.g. on the Godhead and combine with these thoughts the transcendental consciousness (for otherwise I would not be able to think), yet without thereby representing myself to myself in time, which would have to happen if I were conscious of this representation through my inner sense. If impressions happen on my inner sense, then this presupposes that I affect myself (although it is inexplicable to us how this comes about) and so thus the empirical consciousness presupposes the transcendental.\nIn our inner sense our existence in time is determined and thus presupposes the representation of time itself; in time, however, the representation of change is contained; change presupposes something persistent, in which it changes and which brings it about that the change is perceived. Time itself is indeed persistent, but it cannot be perceived alone, consequently there must be something persistent in which one can perceive the change in time. This persistent thing we ourselves cannot be, for we are precisely as object of inner sense determined through time; thus the persistent can be posited merely in that which is given through outer sense. So thus possibility of inner experience presupposes reality of outer sense. For suppose one wanted to say that the representation of the persistent given through outer sense is also merely perception given through inner sense, which is only represented through imagination as given through outer sense, then it would still in general (even if not for us) have to be possible to become conscious of it as belonging to inner sense; but then the representation of space would be transformed into a representation of time, i.e. it would be possible to represent space to oneself as a time (according to one dimension), |\nwhich contradicts itself. Thus outer sense has reality, because without it inner sense is not possible. — From this it seems to follow that we always cognize our existence in time only in *commercio*.",["The first line gives the AA's references to other editions; kept as it stands.","Veränderung = alteration; Wechsel = change; Beharrliches = something persistent; Daseyn = existence; schließen = infer.","“in which it changes” (woran es wechselt): es may be impersonal (“in which there is change”); *commercio*: interaction, community."]],"reflm:6312":["S. II:\nBy what do we cognize the simultaneity of things, since our representations nevertheless follow one another in apprehension? By the fact that we can apprehend the manifold there forward and backward. Now because in inner sense everything is successive, consequently nothing can be taken backward, the ground of the possibility of the latter must lie in the relation of the representations to [einen S] something outside us, and indeed to what is not in turn itself mere inner representation, i.e. form of appearance, consequently is thing in itself. The possibility of this cannot be explained. — The representation of the persistent must also pertain to that which contains the ground of time-determination, but not with regard to succession, for therein is no persistence; consequently only in that [simultaneous] which is simultaneous must the persistent lie, or in the intelligible, which contains the ground of appearances.\nThat even the empirical determination of one's own existence in time is not possible without the [his] consciousness of one's relation to things outside us constitutes the ground why this is the only possible refutation of idealism.\n––––––––––\nWhether the objects (g outside us) or their representations affect us (of which the first the reality of the [Materi] outer sense, the second the mere —), [under] one can distinguish by this. We need space in order to construct time, and thus determine the latter by means of the former. Space, which represents the outer, thus precedes the possibility of time-determination. Now since with regard to time we are affected only by representations, not by outer things, |\nnothing remains but that in the representation of space we must be conscious of ourselves as affected by outer things. This we cognize not through an inference, but it lies in the way in which we affect ourselves in order to construct time as mere form of the representation of our inner state, whereby yet something else, but not belonging to this inner state, must always still be given to us (i.e. something outer, [in] of which the construction[that time s] at the same time contains the intuition of time and lies at its ground).\n––––––––––\nIn order that something can appear to be outside us, something must actually be outside us, although not constituted in the way in which we have the representation of it, since other kinds of sense could furnish other kinds of representation of the same thing. For the representation of something outside us could otherwise never come into our thoughts, because we are conscious only of our representations as inner determinations and have for the object of them the inner sense, which, however, we carefully distinguish from the outer.",["Zugleich seyn = simultaneity; Auffassung / auffassen = apprehension / apprehend; “thing in itself” here renders sache an sich; Zeitbestimung = time-determination.","[einen S] and [Materi] are deleted, unfinished words, kept as they stand; “daran” (“by this”) has no expressed antecedent; the parenthesis breaks off at “the mere —”.","“lies at its ground” (ihr zum Grunde liegt): ihr may refer to the construction or to the intuition of time; left open."]],"reflm:6313":["S. I:\nAgainst idealism.\nIt can be refuted 1. by showing that the representation of outer things must not lie in imagination, but in an outer sense, because the form of representation in time, without taking that in space along with it, would make possible no empirical consciousness of one's own existence in time, consequently no inner experience.\n2ndly by this, that the matter of the representations in space could not possibly take place in the mind without an outer sense. For imagination can furnish a representation of the outer only by affecting outer sense (in the interior of its organ), and there would be no material for outer representations in imagination were there not an outer sense. Now, however, it is also not demanded that we be able to give a sure universal mark of every object of outer senses and of its actu|ality\nbut it is sufficient to have established that there is an outer sense.\n3. Because imagination (g and its product) is itself only object of inner sense, the empirical consciousness (g *apprehensio*) of this state can contain only succession [the time-cond]. But this can itself not be [determined] represented otherwise than through the persistent, with which that successive is simultaneous. This persistent, with which the successive is simultaneous, i.e. space, can now not in turn be representation of mere imagination, but must be representation of sense, because otherwise that abiding would not be in sensibility at all.\nNB. I. The simultaneity [of the representation] of A and B cannot be represented at all without something persistent. For properly all apprehension is successive. But insofar as the succession can happen not merely forward from A to B, but also (g as often as I will) backward from B to A, it is necessary that A endure. The representations of the senses A and B must thus have another ground than that in inner sense, [mith] but still in some sense, consequently in outer sense; consequently there must be objects of the outer senses (and as concerns the dream, this object, which brings about the deception of the presence of several outer objects, is the body itself).\nNB. II. Because we thus could not even perceive the succession in us, consequently could undertake no inner experience, if we could not [simultaneously] also become empirically conscious of simultaneity, [but this is possible only through the persistent in the object of the representations] but this latter is possible only through an apprehension undertaken forward and backward, which does not take place with regard to the objects of inner sense, inner experience itself can be thought only by means of the relation of our sense to objects outside us. (g Inner sense would otherwise have to represent us ourselves as outside us etc etc.)\nIf our cognition of outer objects had to be a cognition of them (g and of space) as things in themselves, then we would |\nnever be able to prove their actuality from our sense-representation of them (g as outside us). For only representations are given to us; the cause of them can now be (g either) in us or also outside us, about which sense decides nothing. But if the representations of inner sense as well as of outer are merely representations of things in appearance and if even the [inner senses] determination of our consciousness for inner sense is possible only through representations outside us in space breaks off.\nS. II:\nverte. But if the soul is to itself only appearance, consequently its empirical intuition is only the sensible form of the way in which its own subject is affected through the apprehension of the manifold of a given intuition, then this latter must be an intuition other than inner, i.e. an outer one, so that precisely this alone is immediate.\nIn the distinction of idealism and dualism one must distinguish the transcendental consciousness of my existence in general. 2. of my existence in time, consequently only in relation to my own representations, insofar as I determine myself through them. This is the empirical consciousness of myself. 3. The cognition of myself as a being determined in time. This is the empirical cognition. — That the latter can only be [as] the [Dase] of myself as a being existing in a world, and indeed for the sake of empirical consciousness and its possibility, insofar as [it] I am to cognize myself as object, is proven in the following way. — I cannot cognize time as determined beforehand in order to determine my existence in it (thus only insofar as I [the] connect my alterations simultaneously according to the law of causality). Now in order to determine the former [an de] empirically, something persistent must be given, in the apprehension of which I can cognize the succession of my representations and through which alone the simultaneity of a series, in which each part passes away while the other arises, can become a whole. in which I posit my existence.",["Zugleichseyn = simultaneity; Beharrliche = persistent; Bleibende = abiding; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Stoff = material; Täuschung = deception.","[mith] (mithin), [Dase] (Daseyn) and [an de] are deleted, unfinished words, kept as they stand; “breaks off” renders bricht ab, the AA's note.","“through them” renders durch dieselbe (singular); “simultaneously” (zugleich) may attach to “connect” or mean “at the same time”; left open."]],"reflm:6314":["S. I:\nOn Idealism.\nWe can represent to ourselves no number [without] except through successive enumeration in time and then the taking together of this plurality into the unity of a number. This latter, however, cannot happen otherwise than by my setting them next to one another in space; for they must be thought as given simultaneously, i.e. as taken together into one representation, otherwise this many makes up no magnitude (number); but being-simultaneous can be cognized by nothing other than that, besides my action of composing, I can apprehend (not merely think) the plurality forward and backward as given. Thus an intuition in which the manifold is represented outside one another and [represented simultaneously] next to one another, i.e. the intuition [of space], which makes the representation of space possible, must be (g in perception) given, in order to determine [the existence] my own existence in time, i.e. an existence outside me lies at the ground of the determination of my own existence, i.e. of the empirical consciousness of myself. Thus [it is just so] I must, just as well as I am conscious of (g my) existence in time, also become conscious of the existence of outer things, although only as appearances, yet as actual things. No one can have inner sense alone, and indeed for the purpose of the cognition of his inner state, and yet idealism asserts this.\nComposition is in the representation of the composite always merely our own work. How can we now say that the object agrees with it. This agreement can surely (g not consist in this, that) the quality of the composition [not] similar to the composite, but that one of the two is the ground or consequence of the other (it is the latter if the object is merely appearance). That (g in the representation) which is related to the object of the senses in itself [belongs] is sensation; but there the representation is related merely to the subject (as to its quality), the object is a mere something in general. If I leave out this something (g sensation) and at the same time the composition, then there remains the form of intuition and the object |\nas appearance. (The sensations, related to the object, make illusion.)\nS. II:\nCritical philosophy brings it about that we need not ask of what properties God is in order to know what we have to do in the world, but need only consult in ourselves the voice of reason, which teaches us immediately what we have to do, and[the will] assumes in the supreme cause of our existence such a will, of which those laws of reason are the commands, wherewith then also are all the promises for our wishes which the same reason in us represents as agreeing with such a conduct.\nWe shall not learn his will first from [the Be] his revelation, in his works as well as in Scripture; for these can be interpreted in many ways, and only that sense which we put into them by virtue of our moral vocation is undoubtedly morally the correct one, since then those revelations serve to strengthen this in us.\nWe shall not have need to engage in combat with the [doub] theoretical doubts against those theoretical dogmas of the divine nature and his aims or of the doctrine of immortality. For because we can convince the opponent that he, in order to pronounce negatively on this, understands just as little of these objects as (g others) affirmatively, we shall stand firm in our place without wavering; for [our] the law of our conduct, the hope of being able to accomplish it because we ought, the disinterested conjecture of a state of things which as a whole harmonizes with morality, which before reason is the [impor] highest condition of the possibility of a world according to rules of wisdom, will raise [a] us to the idea of a [in] deity governing the world wisely and at the same time furnish this with the properties that strengthen and uphold our morality.",["Vielheit = plurality; Zugleichseyn = being-simultaneous; Erscheinung = appearance; Schein = illusion.","The sentence beginning This agreement is left as broken as the German, where the interlinear insertion and the deleted nicht do not fit together.","an sich may attach to the object or to the senses; Bestimmung = vocation; Absichten = aims."]],"reflm:6315":["S. I:\nOn Idealism.\nExperience is cognition of the objects that are present to the senses. Imagining is intuition even without presence of the object, and [this intuit] the object is then called a *phantasma*, which can be a production (fiction) or reproduction (recollection) of a previously had intuition. — The assertion that we can never be certain whether all our supposed outer experience is not mere imagining is idealism. It is thus not exactly an assertion that it is so, but only that we can bring forward no proof of it, consequently [always] (g the reality [of the] of an outer experience taken for such always) still can doubt.\nThe idealist thus assumes it to be possible that we have [actu] no outer sense, but in regard to outer intuitions only imagination. — But now the Critique proves that this is impossible. For the form of the intuition of inner sense is time, which contains only one dimension of sensible intuition. [All outer objects thus would thus therewith] So in order that my intuition have three dimensions (g as space contains them in itself), we would have to think this our (g inner) representation as situated outside us, which contradicts itself. — To take imagining of outer objects for perception (to dream) is indeed possible, but only under presupposition of an outer sense, i.e. that [it actually] our outer intuition is related to objects actually situated outside us, because otherwise all these intuitions, as at bottom merely inner, would have the form (g and dimension) of time and not (g that) of space, and this form is not thought but intuited, i.e. immediately related to an object, even though we do not know what this is in itself, but only how it |\nappears to us. If this were not so, we would also have no imaginings; for these are only sense-intuitions of outer objects reproduced as to form, which can indeed be fictions, but not in regard to their having no outer objects at all. We are beforehand an object of outer sense to ourselves, for otherwise we could not perceive our place in the world and intuit ourselves in relation with other things. — Hence the soul as object of inner sense cannot perceive its place in the body, but it is in the place where the human being is. — Leibniz's *harmonia praestabilita* necessarily carries idealism with it: because since each of the two subjects is by itself in the play of alterations without the other's influence, one of them is entirely unnecessary for the determination of the existence and the state of the other. — But also the inner alterations cannot be comprehended as to their possibility without something outer which contains the ground.\nS. II:\n# vid.S. 2. C. below.\nNevertheless this doubt, which, when it is propounded dogmatically, is called idealism, could, besides its being a stumbling block for metaphysics, of which one expects that it, which promises so many still unopened prospects, could yet have an objection against a matter laid so near to us: so it can also well furnish a hindrance to that which makes up the final end of metaphysics, to progress to the supersensible, if everything sensible is posited merely in us.\nWe have two kinds of intuition: sense-intuition [and which de], for which the object must be represented as present, and imagining as intuition without presence of the object. Imagining, if one is conscious of it as such [and], can also be considered as [intuit] (g inner) sense-intuition.\nNow the problem is: whether [and how] sense-intuition can be distinguished from imagining (g of outer objects); the idealist denies |\n[the former consequently also and so falls the] this, without doubt on the ground that we immediately perceive our representations (g as inner determinations of the mind) only through inner sense, but not the cause of them, which we only infer, but the inference from an effect to [the] a determinate cause is not certain, since for one and the same effect more than one cause, as here (g either) the outer object or the subject itself the cause, can be, which latter intuition would then be imagining. Of the latter the example is the dream or also madness, from which therefore the [sense] outer sense-representation as such could not be distinguished.\nNow I say: outer sense-intuition distinguishes itself with complete certainty from the inner through the mere form of the former, space, in which we posit outer objects, in distinction from the other, whose object we only in time breaks off.\nIf we cannot distinguish them immediately, then it will also not happen mediately through inferences to their cause, for [if w]. I am myself an object of my outer intuition in space and without that could not know my position in the world. Hence the soul cannot know its place in the body, because it would have to perceive itself through outer [place] sense, thus as outside itself.\nMy representations cannot be outside me and an outer object of the representations not in me, for that would be a contradiction. But, although the representation is in me, the object of it can well be without contradiction outside me, or also [the object] the representation together with the object in me. According to idealism it is asserted that it is not possible to distinguish whether [not] with the representation [of a] at the same time the object of it is in me, even if this is represented (g in intuition) as existing outside me. — Against this the realist of outer intuition asserts that this is possible, and indeed rightly, on the following ground. [That my representation cannot exist outside me (g the subject) What I Th] What I represent to myself as spa|tial\ncannot be counted as a representation of inner sense, for its form is time, which has only one dimension. Likewise what is mere representation I cannot make into an object of outer sense, for its form is space. — Now the question arises: whether [the whose f] that intuition which has the form of outer sense, such as imagining is (in dreams or in fever), is not so much one and the same with that which also has an object of outer sense that the two cannot be distinguished from each other. The answer is: that in this state of imagining they certainly cannot be distinguished, for this is a deception of the power of judgment; but the question is properly: whether they cannot be distinguished at all, i.e. whether one could not become conscious that the one is a sense-intuition, the other indeed sensible intuition, but only in imagining, for which the object outside the representation is not present. The answer is: consciousness can accompany all representations, hence also those of imagining, which, and whose play, is itself an object of inner sense, and of which it must be possible to become conscious of it as such, because we actually distinguish such as inner representations, hence existing in time, from sense-intuition.",["imagining renders Einbildung, kept apart from imagination (Einbildungskraft); Sinnenanschauung = sense-intuition; Dichtung = fiction.","bricht ab (breaks off) stands in the source after an unfinished clause; the paragraph beginning Nevertheless is an anacoluthon in the German and is kept as one.","Ort = place, Stelle = position; Object and Gegenstand are both object."]],"reflm:6316":["S. I:\nAgainst idealism.\n1.) That the ideality of space and of time, which is merely formal, does not contain the real idealism which alleges that to the perception of things in space no object at all outside the representation is given. but that to this object [because] or to these outer objects (which remains undecided) only the same form of space does not belong in itself under which we intuit it or them, because it belongs merely to the |\nsubjective way of our faculty of representation in perception, which can be concluded [from the] from this, because space contains nothing in itself that could be representation of a thing or of the relation (g of different ones) of them to one another in itself, and, if it is considered as such a determination, [to the *entibus*] as *ens imaginarium* is a *non ens*.\n2.) That (g the representation) of the object outside us in space is not illusion, i.e. merely seems to be something outside us, but is outer, not merely inner perception, but that (g it) seems to us to contain this form of space in itself, because we cannot cognize otherwise than through inferences that it is merely appearance.\n3.) That this form of things in appearance is sufficiently distinguished from every other, which contains that which adheres not to the objects outside us but merely to our way of representation, by this, that through it we can determine the appearances a priori, which we cannot say in the case of a tone as distinguished from the sound, in the case of warmth as distinguished from the perception [of the] of a fluid [the] penetrating all other matter and extending it, hence that the form of appearance concerns the (g outer) sense in general and not certain particular ways of sensing and (g immediately) perceiving.\n4.) That thus with respect to this universal, sense can (g and also must) be distinguished from imagination through a correct inference, [because] unless the objection, taken from the possibility of this confusion in particular representations of the imagination in madness or in the dream, is to count as a proof that we might perhaps exchange even these faculties (of sense and of imagination) themselves, and indeed without means of getting out of this ambiguity: that in the realism of outer sense nothing else is asserted than that not even any imagining could make us [say] represent things in general as objects of the senses outside us as such, were there not actually such a sense, hence that this, as a faculty different from imagination, we, not indeed through sensation alone, but through a sure inference |\ndistinguish, and that something outside us itself lies at the ground of the imagining (although (g it) is not [the] now contained in the given (g supposed) perception).\nIn the margin next to No. 4:\nIf we cognize a law a priori, then we prescribe this law to the object. to nature, if it is a law of nature, to freedom, i.e. to ourselves, if it is a moral law, but not arbitrarily, but as necessary.",["\"object\" renders both Gegenstand and the Latinate Object (objecten in 3., Objecte in the marginal note).","\"imagination\" is Einbildungskraft; \"imagining\" is Einbildung.","\"tone … sound\": Ton … Schall; \"illusion\": Schein; \"fluid\": Flüßigkeit."]],"reflm:6317":["S. I:\nN. I. |\nOn the critique with respect\nto theology.\nIn order to prove that it is unavoidable for reason to assume an existence of God, and indeed according to a concept that is sufficient for the theoretical as well as the practical use of our reason, insofar as it proceeds to the ultimate principles *a priori*, I had to prove that speculative reason can neither give his concept consistently with itself nor [prove] such an existence or even only establish the reality of this concept. — For had I conceded the latter, then I would have had (g either) to come to the use of reason with respect to the objects of experience, and, since I would have had to hold these to be things in themselves, I would first have run into antinomies, on which all speculative reason was wrecked, and finally I would have sensified and anthropomorphized the divine being; or I would have had to hold everything to be appearances and to seek only the deity among the things in themselves through pure ontological concepts, where then no cognition at all would have remained to me. I thus had to establish the inability of the |\nmerely theoretical use of reason herein, whereby there still remained, however, that this did not contradict the concept of God and his existence, instead of which otherwise entirely false concepts of God and in the end the impossibility of thinking such a being would have come out.\nOn the possibility of cognition *a priori*.\nThe question is whether, if the causal combination were not given to me in experience, I would have to have insight into it *a priori*? When a manifold existence follows one upon another, then the time in which it follows must also be perceived. But this is not possible otherwise than that something is there with which all this manifold, which succeeds among itself, is nonetheless simultaneous.\nOn the manner of proof in relation to possible experience.\nIf the forms of things according to their universal properties are, as condition of the possibility of these things, [given] given *a priori*, then I can cognize immediately what these things themselves are with respect to the categories, e.g., of magnitude. But if I want to cognize things in general, not merely their form, through categories, then I can cognize this not otherwise than only in relation to possible experience [beweis] *a priori*. e.g. That all things have a magnitude can be proved only in such a way that it is established that they can become objects of experience only through their being given in space and time, but under this condition their apprehension is always a magnitude, thus also the object.\nLaw of continuity.\nBetween a and –a (attraction and repulsion, e.g., in the magnetized rod) there is a point where the predicate of the thing vanishes, becomes =0. Thus an acute as well as an obtuse angle can be taken for a right one which is only infinitely little smaller or larger; but an acute one cannot count as an obtuse one. Usefulness can be regarded as an indifferent matter when it is very |\nsmall, but not as harmfulness. Hence it is impossible that virtue be the mean between two (according to degree opposed) vices. For the vanishing vice is innocence.\nOn the occasion of the critique.\nWe have cognitions *a priori* which we extend without experience: the question is whether we can also extend them beyond experience (g and its objects). If we attempt the latter, then we cannot discover our errors through experience, [because] and possibilities of thinking something always remain also without reality. Further: even in the propositions that seem necessary we cannot distinguish the objective necessity of the propositions from the subjective necessity of thinking the objects, according to the particular nature of our cognitive powers, thus and not otherwise with respect to given consequences, without a critique of reason. — But in all this nothing is at stake; for if we have erred, then that is merely the failure of an aim at speculative cognition, through which we wanted to extend ourselves beyond the boundary of possible experience. We can at most err when we say: the objects are actually so, e.g., the supreme original ground of things an intelligent being, because according to the constitution of our cognitive faculty we cannot make the *phaenomena* comprehensible to ourselves otherwise; but that is of no disadvantageous consequences. — But there is in us a property, or reason attributes it to us as practical beings, which is entirely distinct from the natural properties, and its laws entirely distinct from natural laws (g are), indeed even are contradicted by these: freedom and under this concept the law of morality with [the] our independence of nature. Here we now have two laws certain *a priori* which contradict each other in the same subject if I represent the subject to myself [in] according to the theoretical principles in the same significance. Through this I am necessitated to think my own subject, as object of the senses and (g at the same time) of reason, as existing in two different ways: 1. as object of the senses, 2. As a being [which] insofar as it is not at all object of the senses. This necessitates me to the critique of sensibility. But that too would make no difference, were empiricism and predeterminism not contrary to all morality. |\nThus morals runs danger through speculative reason without critique. But even here the power of the moral disposition would still be able to outweigh speculation. But the same practical reason necessitates us to assume those laws as divine commands, because otherwise they would be without the lawful effect, and the course of nature as principle of practical laws, insofar as they aimed at one's own happiness, would not at all correspond to the course of nature in relation to moral ones. Thus I must think a God and assume him, but I cannot prove his existence and cannot comprehend him.\nS. II:\nNow it becomes of interest not to make the conditions of the cognition of things possible for us into conditions of the possibility of the matters; for if we do this, then freedom is abolished (g and) immortality, and we can get no concepts of God other than contradictory ones. This now necessitates determining precisely the possibility, the domain and the boundaries of our speculative cognitive faculty, so that Epicurean philosophy does not take possession of the whole field of reason and ruin morals and religion. or at least not make human beings inconsistent.\nMoreover space and time are such necessary determinations *a priori* of the existence of things that they would have to be made not only, together with all the consequences attaching to them [of limitedness], conditions of the [God] existence of the deity, but, on account of their infinity, absolute necessity and necessity, even into divine properties, were they determinations of things in themselves. For once one has made them that, there is no ground why one should restrict them merely to finite beings. Theology, so that it not contradict itself, sees itself necessitated to make both only into the form of our sensibility, and to lay under all things that can be cognized by us, as *phaenomena*, *noumena*, which we do not know, but with respect to which alone the unconditioned takes place. Since now the dispute between the principles of the unconditioned in the synthesis and the principles of the conditioned in space and time [schle], hence the antinomy of\n|\nreason, can absolutely not be settled without making this distinction among the objects and their representations, theology leads to the aesthetic critique.\nWith respect to the theoretical cognition of the objects of possible experience the critique nonetheless has the utility that it resolves the antinomy between the principle of the unconditioned according to mere concepts and of the always conditioned according to conditions of intuition, in that it shows that the latter, however pure it may be, is always only sensible and represents the object not as thing in itself but merely in appearance [moreover], e.g. antinomy of the beginning of the world, of the world-whole with respect to space, of the absolute and unconditioned causality and of the unconditioned necessity [oder Zuf] of things. Meanwhile it is nonetheless necessary against skepticism, which aims precisely, through contradictions between the clearest convictions of reason, at robbing the trust in itself — idealism is indeed not so dangerous, but it nonetheless restricts the field of experience very much and gives to a belief a claim even upon our empirical cognitions. — But if it is shown that the determination of our own existence in time presupposes the representation of a space, in order also to be able to represent to oneself the relation of the determinations of inner intuition to the abiding object, and we the space, which is merely a form of intuition, yet cannot be the form of inner intuition, precisely because the inner is not space but time, then (g the) reality (as things in themselves) can be secured to outer objects precisely by not assuming their intuition as that of a thing in itself; for were it this, and the form of space the form of a thing, (g which) even without the particular constitution of our subject adhered to it in itself, then it would be possible that we had the representation of such a thing even without its existing. But if it is a particular kind of intuition in us, which cannot represent that which is in us, hence what exists in the change of time, because then as mere representation it can be thought only in |\nrelations of time, then such an intuition must consist in actual relation to an object outside us, and space actually signifies something which, represented in this form of intuition, is possible only through relation to an actual thing outside us. — Thus refutation of skepticism. Idealism. Spinozism. likewise also of materialism, predeterminism.\n|––––––––––\n[Since] Some judge that it is difficult to make [die Enda] the content of the critique, and by means of it also of metaphysics (g together with its final aim), graspable from a single point of view; moreover [probably] also probably of those who (g are fair enough,) do not (g to) mistake its good aim, hold it to be an entirely useless chicanery with the [previous] merely speculative proofs of reason [of God] of the most sublime [belief] ideas, on which the tranquility of human beings rests, since in the end the critique nevertheless comes out at precisely the same, although only as matters of belief of pure reason, whereby still that which one apostle says to the other, namely that therein things [are] are encountered which are too high for many and confuse the simple. [To this end] As regards the first, precisely the ease of surveying the whole plan is a strong recommendation of this system and a confirmation of the unity of its principle, and, as regards the confusion, such a critique is not composed in order to be presented to the simple, but to the subtlest rationalizers, who believed no matter to be too high [dacht] for them. Since then this critique is precisely to prove that they are to them [wirkl] and to everyone [für die S] much too high for speculative insight, and to confuse them in the delusion of the latter (since it is their own fault to persist therein), [is the preparation] in order to let themselves down to the same low level of which all human beings are capable, for which |\nthose matters of belief must be just as accessible as to the subtlest investigation.\nThere are three supersensible objects with which human reason has been occupied incessantly and at all times and will remain so: God, immortality and freedom. Of the last alone we have an immediate conviction of its actuality, without, however, [geh] being able to have insight into it. It is natural to start from it, in order to assess our possible cognition of the others accordingly.",["Doubtful or broken constructions kept as they stand: “that this did not contradict” (daß der … nicht wiedersprach, der without clear referent); “and we the space … yet cannot be”; “it is nonetheless necessary against skepticism” and “whereby still that which one apostle says” have no main verb; “of those who … hold” (von denen, partitive).","[beweis], [schle], [oder Zuf], [die Enda], [dacht], [wirkl], [für die S] and [geh] are deleted, unfinished words, left as they stand; “matters” renders Sachen (also in “things in themselves”, Sachen an sich), “things” Dinge.","“causal combination”: Caussalverbindung; “mean”: Mittel; “abiding”: bleibend; “inability”: Unvermögen; “rationalizers”: Vernünftler; “its laws”: deren Gesetze, which may also be read “their laws” (of the natural properties)."]],"reflm:6318":["On psychology. We cannot cognize the nature of the soul as a spirit, hence also not immortality, because we can never free it from bodily influence. — We cannot cognize God as *realissimum*, because we can attribute to him neither understanding nor will without transferring our limitation onto him.\nThe concept of the *realissimum* is *conceptus originarius*, because the negations presuppose reality and are removals of it, thus derivative. These negations can also be represented only as limitations of the *realissimum*. Hence the representation of the highest reality as the |\nmatter of all things, so that all ills are supposed to derive only from the form, as that which consists in negations. — But from the necessity of presupposing that concept it does not follow that a *realissimum* is possible.",["“limitation” renders Kant's limitation (not Schranke); “removals”: remotionen; “ills”: Übel.","*conceptus originarius*: “original concept”."]],"reflm:6319":["R II 254f\nS. I:\nIdealism.\nA specifically different imagination must have another sense lying at its ground, for the imagination is only an inner determination of the sense to the same intuition that it has as sense.\nOn the critique of pure reason.\nThe two difficulties in it consist in this, that it be shown that it does not contradict itself: I. The soul cognizes itself theoretically only as *phaenomenon*, hence it cognizes itself, but only as appearance. — The solution is this: it cognizes itself not through concepts, which are merely the simple actions of synthesis which belong to cognition in general, namely not through the consciousness of these concepts, for that would be a contradiction, because it is to cognize itself as object, but only by means of the application of them to inner intuition. But it cannot determine time in itself without representation of space and the product in it through the imagination. Space, however, lies in its outer sense, which the imagination must affect in a certain way, and thereby inner sense too is affected with regard to the inherence of this representation, even the feeling of pleasure etc etc. But also the empirical consciousness of the representations of reason or also of the categories and of thinking in general still always belongs to appearance, because it is occurrence, and nothing intellectual remains but the I — practically, however, freedom together with its law as cognition.\nII. How we can speak of the intelligible, e.g. God, through categories, notwithstanding that these hold only for *phaenomena*, in order to yield cognition, thus of a being that cannot at all be represented as *phaenomenon*.\nS. II:\nOf the soul in the birth, the life and the death of the human being. Of which we have no experience, thus would have either to infer from experience or to prove it a priori from the mere faculty of thinking in life or from freedom as presupposition for the practical [conduct] use of reason. But since the first would always be inferred from the sensible and the latter merely from the supersensible, which is given to us breaks off?\nThe identity of the person concerns the intelligible subject in all the diversity of empirical consciousness. The latter can be very much altered. But insofar as it remains connected, it is the cognition of oneself as the same person and is required for imputation.",["\"On the critique of pure reason\": *Zur Critik der reinen Vernunft*; may be the title of the work or the enterprise.","\"time in itself\": *die Zeit ... in sich*; *in sich* may go with *Zeit* or with *bestimmen* (\"determine within itself\").","\"breaks off?\" is the editor's note, not Kant's text."]],"reflm:6320":["S. I:\nOn the cosmological proof.\nIt is grounded on this, that (g the concept of) a necessary being is a singular concept, just as that of the *entis realissimi*; hence these concepts meet and must also meet, if it is true that the concept of a necessary being is not the concept of a species of things but of a single one and thoroughly determined. The proof is supposed to be this: that, if there were several, then [they on] one necessary being would be determined in a certain way, another in another, i.e. opposite way, thus the first could also, without detriment to its necessity, be deter|mined\nin an opposite way, i.e. the opposite of it would be possible, thus the thing contingent and yet necessary, which contradicts itself. — But from the [other way] possibility of being determined in another way a contradiction with the concept of the necessary would only follow if [it is presupposed the thoroughgoing determination must follow from the concept or the necessary existence (which at the same time contains the thoroughgoing determination) is an existence which can already be concluded from the concept of a thing. But no such concept is possible from which existence can be concluded (hence the concept of a necessary being is also a merely problematic concept)] the very same being were thought as determinable in another way. For that one necessary being is determined in a certain way, another necessary one in another way, proves only that the necessary being [be] is through this its concept not at all determined as to what it is, for existence is no particular determination of it.\nS. II:\n1.\nïï [If] The most perfect being of all must necessarily (g i.e.) for the sake of this concept exist; for if it did not exist, a perfection, namely existence, would be lacking to it.\n2.\nïï The necessary being must* contain all perfection. For if it did not contain them all (already through its concept), then through this concept it would be undetermined with regard to one or another predicate, hence that it is (g as) such a one [as] and not (g as) another would be contingent, i.e. it would not be necessary.\n*(g already through this mere concept be cognized as the most perfect; |\nS. III: |\nfor if it were not as being in general determined through all reality, [then it would be in re] but also only undetermined with regard to one, then [it be opposite], just as it is, its opposite would be possible, and so it would also not be necessary. But that does not follow, for a necessary being with a and also one with non a would be possible; i.e. not the opposite of the existence of this being, but of its predicates, without detriment to the existence of the subject, would be possible. — In the Cartesian proof one made existence into a |\nparticular predicate, — in the cosmological one makes existence into the subject. In both the concept of an all of reality is always laid at the ground as of the sole concept which a thing[in its] as thoroughly determined a priori, and hence of the sole non-derivative concept.)\nS. II:\nEven if the best thinker hears this proof, he can indeed, as long as he has it in his thoughts, suppose himself to feel [a] conviction; but if he has only forgotten the words, then he is quite empty, as if he had heard nothing. He must orient himself by healthy reason.\nBoth must be analytic judgments. although the first is evidently synthetic. But if the second were analytic, then the all of perfection would be derived from the concept of necessary existence, but since this, as mere being, contains no concept of the subject of the judgment, consequently, just as [the] in the first proposition the synthetic predicate is lacking to the subject, so here the concept of a subject as a thing of particular quality would be lacking to the predicate and merely being be taken in its place.\nS. IV:\nCosmological proof.\nThe proposition that the necessary being must have all reality is conducted synthetically, although it can only be conducted analytically. For it does not say: a necessary being has in its concept all reality, but: if we want to make ourselves a concept of it, then we must think it under the conditions under which only a single being can stand. But now the first (to make oneself a concept of it) is impossible; for that could be nothing other than that a being of certain determinations were thought in such a way that according to these its non-being would be impossible, i.e. would be self-contradictory.",["\"which a thing[in its] as thoroughly determined a priori\": *der ein Ding[in sein] als durchgängig bestimt a priori*; the clause has no verb in the German.","\"healthy reason\": *Gesunde Vernunft* (sound common reason); \"being\" for *Seyn* and for *Wesen* (as in \"necessary being\").","\"conducted\": *geführt*, of a proof; \"ïï\" is a sign in the manuscript, kept as printed."]],"reflm:6321":["*Ens necessarium*.\n(g Its *conceptus* is not generic, but *singularis*.)\nIt is said: The concept of an *entis necessarii* is only *conceptus singularis** (because this must be thought through its concept as thoroughly determined). But now it does not follow that, if a thing also exists necessarily, this existence must also be able to be derived from its concept (rather, from the *omnitudine realitatis* existence can never be concluded), and it would thereby also not become contingent if besides it still several things *partim realia, partim negativa* were assumed as necessarily existing.\n*(g i.e. there cannot be necessary beings internally distinguished from one another.)\nA being that is already thoroughly determined through its concept (g identical proposition) (can exist only in one way, if it exists) contains all reality (as aggregate). Granted. But not: give me such a concept, which carries absolute necessity with it. You can only name one of which it is problematic whether such a thing is possible at all.",["\"generic\": *generisch*; \"this\" in \"because this must be thought\": *dieses*, neuter, which may refer to the *ens* or to the concept."]],"reflm:6322":["S. I:\n*a contingentia transscendentali* — in opposition to the *naturali*.\nThat an absolutely necessary being must contain all reality would have to be an analytic judgment, because it has been drawn from the concept of the absolutely necessary. Thus the predicate is identical with the concept of the subject, but not conversely; for otherwise they would have to be *conceptus reciproci*. [Now are] Thus it is not the concept of all predicates that belong to the *necessario*. Thus the concept of the object was not such a one as would contain the thoroughgoing *determination* [because existence was not among it. — Were the concepts not entirely identical, then some most real beings would not be necessary beings — Thus there would be more than one *realissimum*], which was nevertheless demanded in the proposition [contained] that the necessary being could be determined in only one way; for the concept of the whole of reality contains, according to this ad|mission\nnot the thoroughgoing *determinatio*. Thus the proposition that [a being] from absolute necessity an inference can indeed be drawn to thoroughgoing reality, but not conversely from the latter, is a *contradictio in adiecto*.\nRather one must say: the concept of the *realissimo* is a transcendental original concept, i.e. one can [according to] with transcendental concepts invent no other things than by laying the *realissimum* at the ground, [since] which as it were contains the material of all things, and now positing the manifoldness only in the form, consequently thinking all other concepts as derived, but, be it well understood, through mere ontological concepts.\nThe analytic *oppositum* of a is =0, the synthetic\n=a–a=0; to designate these two kinds of *remotion* is the aim.\n––––––––––\nThe proposition: an existing being must at the same time be represented as a thoroughly determined being, i.e. it is thoroughly determined, is an analytic proposition. — But the proposition: (g some) existing being must be able to be thoroughly determined [as] through the concept of its existence, is not only a synthetic proposition, because it goes [still] beyond the concept of existence, which contains the thoroughgoing existence, and says that the thoroughgoing determination of it as of a single being is possible through the mere concept of its (necessary) existence, but also a false proposition; for from the (g mere) concept of [mere] existence this concept of a thing cannot be determined at all, even if I assume it as necessary: rather the proposition means to say only so much: if I [cognize] should be able to cognize its absolute necessity, I would have to cognize (g it) from the mere concept of it, which [abs] latter is absolutely impossible. — The second proposition reads thus: [if I] I can cognize the thoroughgoing determination through the concept of it as of an All of reality. [But that is already ref] But thereby I do not cognize the existence of this thing. [For] That is already refuted: for could I do this, I would need no other proof than the Cartesian.\nThe proposition: an existing being must be represented through the concept of its |\nexistence as thoroughly determined; but from this it does not follow that, even if it is thought as necessarily existing, it could be thoroughly determined through this concept; rather the concept of existence [is] leaves the determination of the thing, i.e. with which predicates it exists, entirely undetermined, except only that something real is in it, because otherwise it would be no thing.\nThe concept of an *ente realissimo* is a *conceptus singularis*: it [is] contains not something that can be common to different things.\nThe cosmological proof runs thus:\nThe necessary being must be a most real thing. For (g as necessary being it must be determined through its concept in regard to existence, consequently as most real) were it not such a one, then it would not be thoroughly determined through [the] its concept [of a thing] [(because the concept of the most real is the only one that at the same time contains the thoroughgoing determination in itself)], thus not thought as existing. This inference is false; for through the concept of existence I think to myself the thoroughgoing determination, but not that through this concept the thing (g must) be able to be thoroughly determined (as the concept of the *realissimi* carries it with it). For the concept of the thing is not determined at all through the (g concept of) existence [even if it] (except only that something real is thought in it), but only that it is, outside my concept, [posited] given.\n(g The proposition that the absolutely necessary being has all reality is just as much synthetic. For the concept says only that its non-being is impossible, whatever determination it may have. Thus the proposition goes beyond this concept, in that it determines it.)\nIf from the concept of a necessary being [at the same time] its *omnitudo realitatis* followed, then I would not be permitted [to prove] to ground the existence of a necessary thing on experience, |\nbut among everything possible there is also an absolutely necessary one, i.e. such a one exists.\nThe common proof runs thus: If [it not] a necessary being were not the most real, then [it through] its concept would be alterable, consequently this being itself alterable, thus contingent. But this does not follow.\nS. II:\nAccording to the Wolffian philosophy one holds that, even if all our actions, indeed every use of reason, were predetermined in time, nevertheless, if through [the latter] reason the concepts according to which we determine ourselves to action were distinct, they would nevertheless be free. The critique has this peculiarity, that it does not do that.\nPerpendicular to the preceding:\nOn the divine causality in regard to human beings.\nHow a *Caussatum alterius* could nevertheless be a free being, capable of imputation, since it is nevertheless thoroughly determined by another in regard to its own causality, cannot be comprehended. Thus also not how God himself could know the present free actions. — The ground of this incomprehensibility lies in this, that we have no concept for a substance's being able nevertheless at the same time to exist and endure only as *Caussatum* of another being. For persistence for itself is the character of all substance as phenomenon.",["“thoroughgoing” / “thoroughly” render durchgängig; “determination” renders both Bestimmung and the Latin *determination*, *determinatio*; “invent” renders dichten.","“which contains the thoroughgoing existence” renders welcher die durchgängige Existenz enthalt as printed; welcher can refer to “concept” or to “proposition”.","“Perpendicular to the preceding” renders the edition's location note Senkrecht zum Vorhergehenden; “persistence” renders Beharrlichkeit."]],"reflm:6323":["S. I:\nCosmological proof of the existence of God.\nThe proposition reads thus: if [a nec] the necessity of the existence of a being consists in this, that [its] it is thoroughly determined through a single concept, then it has all reality. —\n*idem aliter*. If the necessity of the existence of a being can be cognized from concepts, then it must be cognized as the most real being. — But the necessity [through] of a being can never be cognized through concepts of it.\nOr conversely: If the most real being is to be cognized as a necessary being, then its existence must be cognized from concepts. Now the latter is false, thus also the former. — For if in the *antecedens* breaks off.\nOr if a [be] necessary being [is to be cognized as such] is, then [its] its thoroughgoing determination must [cogn] follow from a concept of it (but not to infer conversely). Here it must be remarked that this thoroughgoing determination would follow from the concept of a necessary being and not from other concepts, which is false.\nïï Or If a being is absolutely necessary, then it must be thoroughly determined through its concept. — [The consequence is not evident. But if one wanted to say it must be thoroughly determined (although not through its concept), then it would not have to be the most real.] Should read thus: if it is to be cognized as such a one. For even if it is [as] necessary, but [as] this absolute necessity permits no cognition of the being as such a one, then one can have no concept of it that would determine [it] that problematic [concept].\nIf one admits that from (g the concept of a being of) the highest reality it cannot be inferred that it [necessarily] therefore (from so concepts) exists, but that nevertheless, if a necessary being is assumed (which is already a kind of contradiction), the highest reality can be inferred, then the concept of a *realissimi* must be a wider concept, which contains [in] under itself not merely the concept of the *necessarii* but still more things. But then through the concept of |\nnecessity the being is not thoroughly determined in regard to its constitution (as to reality), which nevertheless was supposed to have happened in the concept of reality.\n(g The concept of a necessary (g modality) being is completely indifferent toward every possible determination that only does not contradict itself.)\nA necessary being must have all reality; for if it did not have [in its concept] all reality, then it would not through its concept be thoroughly determined [be], consequently be no being necessarily so constituted as it is.\nSince we now can find no concept from which the necessity of its object could be cognized, the *omnitudo realitatis* will be only the *conditio sine qua non* of the concept of a necessary being, without which a thing indeed cannot [be] be, but through which it does not become all that it is, i.e. through which we cannot cognize its existence, although we thereby think everything that it is.\nS. II, to the left of Motherby's communication, crosswise to it:\nThe real is opposed to the\nnegative\nalso— — —\nto the ideal\n— — — — —\nto the formal.\nThe merely formal ideality of the objects of the senses is proved in the transcendental aesthetic.\nThe material ideality of the objects of outer senses, namely that corresponding to them no outer object at all exists, is there|by\nrefuted, because otherwise we ourselves would have no inner sense and our empirical consciousness in time, since time as magnitude can be cognized only in outer [alterations] objects.\nOnly space and time have a formality of which the properties can be stated synthetically *a priori* (not so with the colors).\nOn the supposed necessity of stating something real (pleasure or displeasure lying at the ground) as the incentive of the will in the moral law.\n––––––––––\nIdealism.\nThe impossibility of determining one's existence in the succession of time through the succession of representations in us, and yet the actuality of this determination of one's existence, is an immediate consciousness of something outside me which corresponds to these representations*, and this intuition cannot be illusion. — The possibility of this consciousness of an object as lying outside us lies in the simultaneity of the manifold of intuition, because I can undertake the successive taking-together forwards and backwards, which cannot happen in the representation of the manifold in time without spatial limitation.\n*(g and which exists not merely in my representation (g but (g as thing) in itself), because otherwise no time-determination of my existence would be possible from this representation itself.\nThe thought of which can indeed be possible, e.g. a relation of things outside us according to (g more than) three dimensions, but it is not yet therefore clear that such an object is also possible; at least it would not be space. Now one says here: that thought lacks the proof of its objective reality, although logically considered it is possible.)\nTo the right of the letter, crosswise to it:\nOn God as the greatest aggregate of reality: *ens maximum*, or the highest ground: *ens summum, ens entium*.\nComplaints about the mischief that metaphysics wreaks in matters of the state |\nand of the church. To introduce anarchy into both. To limit the supreme power so much that in the end none at all is valid any more, or to change things in religion so long and often until none is left any more. Importance that one thereby gives to metaphysics.\n1. reality of the empirical way of thinking against the ideality of the rational. Glossing over of the [last by] first by the last.\n2) reality as to matter, in distinction from the mere form, like filled space, and space not as property of things, but merely of the way of representing.",["“breaks off” renders bricht ab, the edition's note that the sentence stops; “ïï” stands so in the source and is left as it is.","“(from so concepts)” renders aus so Begriffen as printed, perhaps for aus solchen Begriffen (“from such concepts”).","The asterisks after “representations” and before “(g and which” are Kant's reference marks. “incentive” renders Triebfeder; “Glossing over” renders Beschönigung."]],"reflm:6324":["S. I:\nCosmological proof.\nIf someone asked whether we can prove from concepts *a priori* (g of some being or other) that it is necessary, we would have to admit that this does not work, because, the concept may contain much or little, if only it does not contain alteration in itself, it would be just as easy to cancel it (g without contradiction), [it may] if it contained all reality. — But if someone said: experience teaches that something is, and reason, that something or other is necessary (s One could thus perhaps artfully extract from being necessary the concept of such a being, e.g., all reality), then the latter would also have to teach that everything is necessary; for it is necessary either for itself or through a supreme cause. Thus one has given no proof of any being that would on that account have something special, but rather everything that is, is necessary.\nIf that alone is necessary whose concept can be determined in only a single way, then nothing is necessary. For a thing can be determined as the most real of all or as the less real etc etc. But if it is said that, if a thing is the most real of all, then it can as such (not as a determinable concept of a thing in general) be determined in only a |\nsingle way, then one must assume what is supposed to be proved: that the necessary being is the most real of all. — On orienting.\nIf I cannot prove from the mere concept of a thing that such a thing exists, then I can never cognize the [necessary] existence of it as necessary; for precisely cognition *a priori* proves [that] the necessity.\n(s Proposition. A necessary being must have all reality. For if it does not have it, then it would lack one that could yet belong to it, thus the opposite of its determinations would be possible; it would be possible that a being with the given determinations not be, i.e., it would be contingent. — But once I have assumed it as a necessarily existing being just as it is, then I cannot say that a reality that could be in it is lacking to it, but rather that a certain reality is necessarily lacking in it (or that one that is in it could not exist). But had I not added this, then I also could not say that it is contingent, because it cannot exist otherwise than with this lack. — But from my concept of an existing being in general it cannot be thought as thoroughly determined otherwise than through that of the *realissimi*. But from this thoroughgoing determinability existence does not follow at all, and in general I cannot, if something exists, determine through the mere concept of its existence [whether and ho] how much reality it has.)\nIf one draws from the Critique of Pure Reason the (g theoretical) propositions to which it leads, and compares them (g by themselves) alone with their dogmatically [deriv] assumed opposites that are in vogue, without first setting out the derivation of the former from their grounds, then one can, as Rector Ludwig of Schlottheim supposed, set out conspicuous absurdities.\nS. II:\nBeing and non-being are the simplest concepts, when they express the logical relation of the subject to the predicate in a categorical judgment. But the concept of a thing which contains in itself the representation of a being, and the opposite: the concept of that which in itself is [only] the representation of non-being, are the simplest representations of things.\n1.) *Realitas* (g as the material) in the object as distinguished from the mere form, which represents only the way of composition and separation. e.g., since one thinks [oneself] the determining ground in the moral law as lying merely in the possibility of serving as law, and in the form of the law, — others, however, in some object of choice as incentive.\n2. *Realitas* in opposition to ideality. For even the form can lie in the object itself or merely in the representation of the subject.\n3. *Realitas*: In opposition with the nullity of a thought which can well be thought, but has no object (g for cognition).\n4. *Realitas* [in opposition] as ground, although its consequence is a ne|gation.\nSo –a is for itself something real, but [only a ground] in combination with a, a–a is a negation =0.\n5. To regard the real as the good (metaphysical perfection) and the evil merely as the formal. Consequently, by taking together all reality in general in the idea of one being, to make the concepts of all possible things metaphysically for oneself merely through limitation. [De] Thus not to make oneself a God out of what we [as real] cognize as real through moral incentives, but only to seek evidence for the concept of reality. Existence of God inferred from that. (g That which is *realiter* opposed. — The aggregate of all reality as sum total: God, or as ground.)\nAccording to Aristippus pleasure and displeasure alone are true realities, everything else is only representations of relations.\nNow follows a preliminary draft for the Common Saying. At the very bottom of the page stands the following further s-addition, from the same time (ω2) as the earlier ones:\n(s *Realitas*: that whose concept already contains a being, signifies (in opposition to negation as *aliqvid oppositum nihilo*) that whose concept is existence, and there I can distinguish the possibility, actuality and necessity of existence. In objects of the senses *realitas* (*phaenomenon*) is that which corresponds to sensation. *sensibile qvoad materiam* (*qvoad formam* is space and time, which are indeed something actual, but nothing existing). All appearances, i.e., objects of possible experience, contain space, time and the sensable in general within them. — One could also call the latter *apprehensibile*, the graspable, -able of sensible intuition, which would otherwise be empty.)",["“object”: Gegenstand, but Object in nos. 2–3 and objecte in the last addition; “consequence”: Folge; “sensable”: das Empfindbare; “-able”: “-baare”, an alternative ending (Ergreifbare) for “Ergreifliche”.","“Now follows a preliminary draft for the Common Saying ...”: the AA editor's note, standing in the source text; “Common Saying”: Gemeinspruch, the title word of the essay on theory and practice.","“On orienting”: “Vom Orientiren”; “Rector Ludwig of Schlottheim”: “Rektor von Schlottheim Ludwig”."]],"reflm:6325":["S. I.:\nA necessary being must have all reality.\nFor if one were lacking to it, then it could yet have this (g as a thing in general); it was thus not necessary, such as it is. — We can obtain from the concept *a priori* of no thing in general the existence and from the [presup] *a priori* given [concept of the] existence no concept of the thing. Concept (thoroughly determined) gives no existence and existence given *a priori* gives no concept.",["“it could yet have”: the German has the masculine “er”, though “Wesen” is neuter."]],"reflm:6326":["S. IV:\n(Ontotheology.)The thoroughgoing determination of a thing in general through a concept is to assume it either as thoroughly negatively or really determined. The first gives no thing, thus only the second. The most real of all is the concept of a single thing, not of a genus of things which could still be A or non A with regard to some predicate or other.",["“really determined”: “real-bestimt”; “single”: “einzelnen”, i.e. an individual."]],"reflm:6327":["If I say: a [resting] body (g which is at rest) is moved, then the predicate moved can go to rest or also to body. It is thus designated thereby that it is meant here as synthetic predicate, and indeed with regard to the concept of the body. But if it is said: a resting body is moved, then that means: as well, insofar as I think it as at rest, it is moved, and the judgment would be analytic and false.\nThe two propositions: a body which is at rest is not at rest,\nand that: a resting body is not at rest,\nare indeed logically, i.e., *formaliter* (according to the relation) the same, but not *materialiter* (according to the content). — For in the latter [case] it is a relation according to concepts, in the former a relation [to] in time that is expressed. A body which exists as at rest (in [the] one time) exists in another as not at rest. Rest is here put in relation not with the concept of the body but with the existence of it, which is indicated by the separation of the predicate from the subject, where the first judgment is posited in the relation of two judgments: A body is at rest and the very same body is not at rest, as assertoric judgment; if thus the body is not at rest, while the body remains, then it can yet also be at rest, but at another time.",["“as well”: “so wohl” (sowohl), which has no counterpart in the sentence.","“while the body remains”: “indes das der Korper bleibt”; “das” may stand for “daß”, or be a demonstrative (“while that remains the body”)."]],"reflm:6328":["S. I:\nThat a thing can be another than a certain given thing, although it is internally determined in just the same way — is contrary to the *principium identitatis indiscernibilium*.\nBut that a thing can be just the same, although it is internally determined in an entirely opposite way, is contrary to the *principium diuersitatis discernibilium*. — According to the latter the proposition: Caius, who is (was) unlearned, is (now) learned, is a proposition that expresses the identity of the subject with (g mutually) opposed predicates of it in time, hence a proposition of the possibility of alteration, where thus the predicate (learned) holds not of the thing nor of the concept of it, but according to the state in which I posit it, which (g in itself) is no concept of the thing.",["“is contrary to”: “ist wieder”, read as “wider” (against); it could also be read “is again”.","“posit”: the German has “Sätze”, presumably for “setze”."]],"reflm:6329":["In the proposition: Caius, who is learned, is unlearned, there is no contradiction, because the »is« is only the *copula* in the relation of two representations and I connect the »unlearned« not with the [Caius] learned one, but with the [who is learned] Caius. But if I said: the learned Caius is unlearned, then [the em] the subject makes up only one concept, and there the predicate conflicts with it. In the first case the »est« signifies an existence in time, yet such that the time itself remains undetermined, whether it is the past or the present.\nIf I thus say: Caius, who is learned, (g is) unlearned, then these are two opposed predicates, combined in one thing (g namely Caius, as alterable according to his existence), but not in one concept; but if I say: the learned Caius is unlearned, then I combine both in one concept, and there they contradict each other. — This has only the use of showing*, how little we have insight into the possibility of objects, e.g., of alterations: a and non a in one thing.\n*(g That time can absolutely not be brought to concepts of the understanding; Because (in it) *conjunctio praedicatorum oppositorum in eodem subjecto*, in the concept of alteration, would occur, of which the possibility can be thought only under presupposition of time.)\n(g In the proposition:) A man who is learned is unlearned, the concept of the learned is not a partial concept of the subject; for the »is« signifies here not the *copula* in a judgment, but merely the existence of the man, represented in a determinate way.",["“combined”: verbunden; “connect”: verknüpfe; “objects”: objecte (Latinate spelling).","“partial concept”: Theilbegrif."]],"reflm:6330":["1793-Jan. 1794). L Bl. F 19. S. I. R II 357.\nThe infinite divisibility of matter proves that it is merely appearance. For according to concepts of the understanding a whole cannot contain the ground of the possibility of the parts, but conversely; the first ground of it can thus lie only in the simple.",["The first line is the AA's dating and sigla header, carried over as it stands (its opening is cut off)."]],"reflm:6331":["1793-Jan. 1794). L Bl. C 1. S. I-III. R I 121-124.\nS. I:\nCausality as ground of existence\nCan also be used of the supersensible. like all categories, because it pertains merely to synthetic unity (here of the relation), but does not determine the way in which such a one is given in experience, e.g., not as that upon which something else necessarily follows. Whether such a thing is possible or not is not settled theoretically, but only assumed practically. — Now to consider God not as sum total, but as ground of all reality guards again against the otherwise unavoidable anthropomorphism, if we want to lay examples under this.\nIn the right margin:\nThat metaphysics be recommended to all through the supersensible.\nWhether there is probability in metaphysics.\nAbove this marginal note Kant has noted two book titles:\nRayd. F. F. C. de aeqvitate etc etc. Lingae apud F.A. Julicher.\nWild: de rotatione annuli Saturni. 4. Hannover. apud fratres Hahn.\nS. II:\nAlteration.\nContingency is supposed to be cognized from the alterability of a thing, thus also [if it] its necessity from unalterability, which would be encountered only in the concept of the highest reality. That, however, is only the logical unalterability of a concept with regard to its determinations in thinking, but not the real one of the thing itself.\nAlteration in general.\nThat a *realissimum* be necessary, [no objective] i.e., [from] in its concept existence does not lie; so also, that a *necessarium* be an *ens realissimum*, cannot lie in the concept of the *necessarii*. For in the first case the concept of the *realissimi* would be a *conceptus latior* than that of a necessary being, the latter thus *angustior*; in the second case the former would be |\n*angustior* than that of a necessary being and contained under it. They must be *conceptus reciproci*, because they pertain to the thoroughgoing determination, which is identical on both sides.\nS. III:\nAlthough one can make for oneself no concept at all of a necessary being, i.e., although no concept of a being suffices in order to be able to infer from it that such a being must necessarily exist, yet one believes that, once [one] the necessity of some thing or other, of which one has no concept at all, has been assumed, [the constit] the concept of what constitution this thing has could well be developed from it. Now the concept of a necessary existence is [quite simply] a concept of no object at all, but merely of the existence of it and of the impossibility of not being. Existence, however, and not being, likewise possibility and impossibility, are concepts of mere modality, which leave the object itself entirely undetermined as to content. Thus it is in vain to hope to find the properties of a necessary being in this way. It is as with a point, which can be represented only as the boundary of a line.\nThe proposition: a sick human being is healthy, is a logical judgment, because the »is« signifies merely the copula and by means of it contains a contradiction. The proposition: a human being who is sick is healthy, would say just the same, hence contain a contradiction, if the »is«, which stands twice, the one time belonging to the subject, the other time to the copula, were regarded in both cases as copula. But the first time it is not the copula (of the categorical judgment), but determination (synthetic) of the concept of the subject, and the judgment is regarded as synthetic: namely that I, when I have affirmed something of the subject, deny not the very same, but something else of it. Namely he is [healthy] sick at one time and healthy at |\nanother. This is expressed thus: a sick human being becomes healthy. This »becomes« is, however, not the mere copula, but a representation of the existence of an object in time.",["\"guards again against\" renders \"bewahrt wieder den ... Anthropomorphism\", which lacks the usual \"vor\"; read literally, \"bewahrt\" would mean \"preserves\".","The sentence \"That a *realissimum* be necessary ... does not lie\" has no main clause. *conceptus latior/angustior*: wider/narrower concept; *conceptus reciproci*: reciprocal concepts.","\"existence\" renders Daseyn (\"nothwendig daseyn\": \"necessarily exist\"); \"alteration\" renders Veränderung."]],"reflm:6332":["Astrology, Cabbala, alchemy, treasure-digging, animal (g magnetism) electricity, incantation formulas.",[]],"reflm:6333":["The consciousness of the presence of an object is perception. — The subjective of perception is sensation, the objective, i.e., the concept of what is sensed, is reality.",["\"object\" renders Gegenstand; \"reality\" renders Realitaet."]],"reflm:6334":["But not merely this negative ground of the deception with insights supposedly soaring beyond all boundaries of sensibility, but the great fortune that reason makes [a priori] in the expansion of its cognition a priori, chiefly in mathematics, is [it] that which instills in metaphysics [hope makes courage also with] the courage, on the wings of mere [reas underst] concepts, without needing the construction of concepts [which th to be permitted] that gives mathematics its whole firmness, to venture on its part (g it on) an adventure [of the same] of similar kind. For if mathematics, without borrowing anything from experience, yet gives the rule a priori to experience itself, why should this also not [succeed] for philosophy, in the part of it that is called metaphysics, herewith not succeed just as well? and it really does succeed for it in various principles belonging to natural science. But if it succeeds |\nfor it independently of experience: why should it not succeed for it out beyond all boundaries of possible experience (within which mathematics still keeps itself) up to the attainment of that El Dorado (g i.e.) of the supersensible. — Before the appearance of the Crit. of p. R. everyone thought so, hence also so many metaphysical systems and in fact as — the Organon breaks off.\n|––––––––––\nIt seems that, if one grants that the soul is substance, persistence must also be granted to it, as to bodies. We can, however, cognize [in] in the soul nothing persistent at all, as, e.g., in body weight or impenetrability. — Thus the concept of the soul as substance is only the concept of a mere category of the subject as distinguished from the inhering accident, a concept [whose] of an object whose possibility is not cognizable a priori at all.",["The German word order across the deletions is rearranged only as far as English requires; \"the courage\" (den Muth) is the object of \"instills\" (einflößt).","The double negative \"why should this also not ... not succeed\" is the German's (\"warum sollte dies auch nicht ... nicht eben so gut glücken\").","\"the Organon breaks off\": the sense of \"das Organon bricht ab\" after the dash is unclear; the text may be cut off."]],"reflm:6335":["S. I:\nOutline of metaphysics. The investigation of the laws of nature, which constitutes the beginning of all philosophy, could not take place without one's becoming conscious, in the case of many of them, besides their actuality and empirical universality, at the same time of their necessity, without, however, separating these, as cognitions a priori, especially carefully from the others, which are of empirical origin, and setting them up arranged together in a system of their own. For what would this have been necessary, if one, as still happens in the investigation of nature, could make do with those laws *in concreto* without precisely investigating and appraising them in their abstract form. So Newton proceeded with the proposition of the equality of action and reaction — similar to the orators, who long followed the grammatical rules before they thought of a grammar, who with their practice taught the gram|marians\nrather than learned their art of oratory from them. — Also such a knowledge of nature, united with mathematics, could make great progress with consciousness of the necessity of its propositions, without rationalizing about the possibility of this its progress, because its certainty laid itself immediately before their own eyes. — What then may well have moved an Aristotle to separate concepts that are in constant use in common life and in experience, and just such principles, from the judgments of experience, and where possible as it were to measure out all our cognition [which] of which we are master a priori, if he had not intended something other than natural science, namely something that reaches out beyond it. This science, reaching out beyond nature, resting on mere concepts a priori and therefore called metaphysics, is as it were the India that promises the human being far greater and more splendid possessions than the meager sensible fatherland, in which he here, despotized by nature, finally after [long futile labor to] constant deception [with] through feigned attainment of a final end sees nothing before him but death.\nFrom the interior of human reason there now develops a need of a final end that no nature grants, indeed not even promises, because it lies outside the field of sensibility, and toward this land he looks out.\nS. II:\nThe addition (to the, [everything is] there is no freedom, and everything is natural necessity) makes it that in the opposition actually less is said than is required for contradictory opposition. For natural necessity signifies [a] the causality of a thing as a sensible being. This can now always take place, because freedom is the causality of a being of understanding.",["\"knowledge of nature\" renders Naturkunde, \"natural science\" Naturwissenschaft, \"investigation of nature\" Naturforschung; \"final end\" renders Endzwek.","\"opposition\" renders both Gegensatz and the Latinate opposition; \"being of understanding\" renders Verstandeswesen."]],"reflm:6336":["(2nd half of June–beginning of August 1795) L Bl. F 4. S. I. R II 281.\nAffinity according to laws of vitality (g of attraction and aversion, in which, however, I fear Doctor Akakia.), not according to chemical ones, makes hybrids. — Laws of animal affinity in the generations of hybrids prove the sentient principle to be an organized or self-organizing *fluidum*, for otherwise they could not interpenetrate.",["\"hybrids\" renders Bastarte; \"affinity\" renders both Verwandtschaft and affinitaet.","\"they could not interpenetrate\": the referent of sie is not specified."]],"reflm:6337":["One could not have hit upon the [dyna] mathematical antinomies if one had not taken the things in space and time for things in themselves instead of appearances. For how could one assume a world-whole whose parts as conditions were yet supposed always to be [un]conditioned. But in space it is so. In appearances, however, there is to be sure nothing unconditioned, because they are mere representations. (Moreover, in the case of a given whole one would also have to let go of the unconditioned, which would be infinite.) — But if we assume a boundary of the world, then we have a place of the world in empty space, i.e., a relation and alteration of place without objects to which it is the *relatum*. Both are false, because space is not a thing in itself, but form of intuition, which signifies [Gr G] space only insofar as we draw it, thus merely as *processus* in our representation. — Likewise infinite elapsed or beginning time. = Infinite division is also possible only under the presupposition of the ideality of space, in that bodies or alterations in time are only appearances, consequently in themselves undetermined with regard to the multitude of parts, the *regressus* thus goes to infinity. — They are, however, both false, because they contain not [cont] logically opposed things, but *realiter opposita*. *contraria* thus say more than etc etc.\nThat everything that happens in the world stands under the law of predetermination is true, because they are *phaenomena*, thus objects of possible experience, which without those laws, however, they could not be. — Just this, however, also proves that an intelligible ground of them, insofar as human beings could be regarded as *noumena* (which the moral law must afterwards show), can be thought at the same time, which determine the very same occurrences, although they are not [not de] dependent on the chain of causes and effects in the sensible world and this thus does not predetermine them. Thus both can be true, because they contain less than is required for opposition. — Just this holds of the possibility of a necessary being, although all *phaenomena* connected [de] with the world as cause and effect are contingent. — For the dynamical categories permit that the conditions are of another kind than the conditioned; not so the mathematical ones, which assume merely the homogeneous as condition.\n(g The I that composes and separates. — 2. I as the composite of inner intuition.)\nWe can have a (g dynamical) cognition of a *noumenon*, but only in a practical respect, if we cognize a practical law, which lays a supersensible condition of it at the ground, [of it] for human beings.\n|––––––––––\nThe proposition: all [effects] occurrences in space and time stand under the law of natural necessity, is true. — The other: they do not stand under this law, because the ground of them can also be supersensible, since in the former case the relation of the ground to the consequences is only a sensible one and [...] pertains to possibility of experience. — Likewise: there is no necessary being in the sensible world, but it can yet be in the intelligible one.",["\"things in themselves\" renders \"Sachen an sich\" here, \"thing in itself\" \"sache an sich\"; elsewhere \"thing\" renders Ding.","*realiter opposita*: really opposed; \"which determine\" (welche ... bestimmen) is plural in the German, its referent unclear.","\"object\" renders both Gegenstand and the Latinate objecte."]],"reflm:6338":["S. II:\nA proof of the ideality of space is that it is a magnitude that always only as a part of a still greater one can be |\nrepresented (i.e., is infinite). — A second is this, that, however small we may represent a space to ourselves, I can think all given things in a still smaller space, without the things in the one needing, considered internally, to be distinguished in the least from those in the other. — It is thus not the sum total of things in themselves. — Likewise in time.",[]],"reflm:6339":["A great ground for assuming the distinction of objects as *noumena* and *phaenomena* [as] also as a necessary hypothesis is that without this freedom cannot be defended at all, but without presupposing this, no morality.",["\"without presupposing this\": diese may refer to freedom or to the distinction."]],"reflm:6340":["S. II:\nOn the utility of the principle of making every progress of cognition through going back to principles, as it were as if they were now first to be invented, because thereby one-sidedness is |\navoided (*fallacia systematis*), of taking an assumed system itself to be free of error on account of the plausibility of the consequences from it. If one investigates every proposition as if the center of the system had not yet been found out, then one can often discover errors in it which make necessary a new examination of the system and often its overthrow.\nS. I:\nDeutschland, 7th number, 1796. Berlin. p. 10: »über den Begrif des Republicanismus« by Friederich Schlegel.",["\"principle\" renders Princip, \"principles\" Grundsatzen; *fallacia systematis*: fallacy of the system.","The article title is left in German (\"on the concept of republicanism\")."]],"reflm:6341":["g-addition in the margin at upper right beside ll. 15-21:\n(g On the identity of the person in manifold alterations. The I as that which does not arise, pass away, or even is merely persistent.)\nWhether, if a thing (a simple substance or also a composite as of such), if it has ceased to be, could then be called the very same (*per palingenesiam*), if a perfectly equal and similar one followed upon it? Or whether, like the *cyclus* of the Platonic year, the previous state must always be the cause of the following one and, should this *causalnexus* once be broken off, the restoration (*apocatastasis*) would in fact be an entirely new generation, and so also with the future resurrection. — There lies hidden here in the question itself a contradiction. For alteration of the very same is thought, where only exchange with another should be thought.\n––––––––––\nHe who philosophizes not in a school-like but in a genius-like way keeps house from abundance, which then lets a near bankruptcy be foretold |\n(*noris quam sit tibi curta supellex*). The critical philosophy is that [which] economy of reason which first examines its state of assets, in order to know how far it can go in expenditures, and looks like a simpleton beside the witty head who [so] like a certain minister boasts of his state administration: the more debts he makes, the richer he becomes.",["ob gleich dem cyclus: gleich may be read with dem cyclus (like the cycle) or as obgleich (although); the English takes the first.","*per palingenesiam*: through rebirth; *noris quam sit tibi curta supellex*: may you know how scant your household stock is; Veränderung: alteration; beharrlich: persistent.","schulmäßig / geniemäßig: school-like / genius-like; wirthschaftet aus dem Vollen: keeps house from abundance; Pinsel: simpleton."]],"reflm:6342":["We could not cognize things *a priori*, [were] did not the subjective of our power of representation, hence the way in which they appear to us, lie *a priori* at the ground for us as the condition under which alone they can occur to us thus and not otherwise.\nWe can cognize things, as they are in themselves, only through perception. But then w — breaks off. If space and time were the forms of things in themselves, then we would cognize them only through perception, thus not as necessary.\nWe can cognize things according to what they are in themselves (*noumena*), and in general *a priori*, only insofar as we make them ourselves.",["Vorstellungskraft: power of representation; Warnehmung: perception.","überhaupt: in general, or at all; it may go with a priori or with the whole clause."]],"reflm:6343":["S. I.:\nThe final aim of all metaphysics is to ascend from the cognition of the sensible to that of the supersensible. The *Critique of P. R.* now proves that this can never be accomplished in a theoretical, but indeed in a moral-practical a|im\nby means of the transcendental concept of freedom, which with [aim] regard to the theoretical faculty of cognition is [completely] transcendent and [completely] absolutely inexplicable and indemonstrable, but with respect to the pure practical (through [mer] pure reason alone determinable) faculty, however, has through the categorical imperative [completely] undoubted reality. — The reality of the concept of freedom, however, unavoidably draws after it the doctrine of the ideality of objects [insofar as they] as objects[their] of intuition in space and time. For were these intuitions not merely subjective forms of sensibility, but of the objects in themselves, then the practical use of them, i.e. the actions would [the] depend utterly only on the mechanism of nature, and freedom together with (g its sequel) morality would be annihilated.\nS. II:\nAccording to the letter, not to the supposed spirit.\nIn reverse direction:\nwhat the very same man says in context.\nAn uprising of the dogmatists en masse.",["Gegenstände and Objecte are both “objects”; the second “objects” in “objects as objects of intuition” is Objecte.","Absicht, Rücksicht, Hinsicht: aim, regard, respect; Endabsicht: final aim. [mer] renders the deleted fragment blo, which breaks off.","The sentence on the practical use (der practische Gebrauch derselben, d.i. die Handlungen würden ...) changes construction as in the German."]],"reflm:6344":["S. III:\nAll objects are: 1. the *sensibile*, 2. the *aspectabile*, 3. the *intelligibile*.\nIn the margin:\n(g On the manner of writing: Niemand, ein Mal, Muse, concludiert.)\nThere are 2 cardinal principles of the whole of metaphysics: the ideality of space and of time and the reality of the concept of freedom. If one does not concede the former, then there are no synthetic propositions *a priori* for theoretical cognition; if the second is not, then there are no such unconditionally practical ones, i.e. no laws of duty. But if there are none of the latter, then there is no ground there to think the concepts of God, freedom and immortality as [concept] ideas of the supersensible. — Mathematical and dynamical potencies. — Between both that of the power of judgment of purposiveness in the objects, which is [merely] subjective and thereby objective principle.\nA *quantum*, against which every other indicable one (*dabile*) can be thought only as a part of a still greater *quanti*, is infinite. But the *quantum* which in comparison with every other assignable *quanto* can be considered only as a part is infinitely small. That all extended beings in the world can be brought into a drop of water or into an infinitely still smaller space proves the ideality of space, if everything is always considered as relatively, never absolutely, large or small.\nS. IV:\nIn order also still others, who have allied themselves in the same aim, |\nI set here too the notice for them that the one whom I regard as the one who understands me best etc etc.",["Niemand, ein Mal, Muse, concludiert are words mentioned for their spelling and are left in German.","*sensibile*, *aspectabile*, *intelligibile*: the sensible, the visible, the intelligible; angebliche (*dabile*): indicable, that can be given; Potenzen: potencies; Zwekmäßigkeit: purposiveness.","Um auch noch andere ... has no verb of its own; the sentence is incomplete in the German."]],"reflm:6345":["S. I [also: XXIII 357,3-8 and XXIII 359,8-11 and 25-27]:\nNature and freedom. With both cognition *a priori*. Mathematical and dynamical faculty of reason in the sensible and supersensible.\n(Against idealism). Whether we could well think things as existing simultaneously if they represented merely that which [is] in us and is in our mind. The thoughts in me are not simultaneous.\n(Schletwein and Hufeland.)\nS. II:\nHow are synthetic propositions* of the supersensible possible? As regulative principles of practical reason. Those of the sensible as constitutive concepts of the theoretical.\n*(g possible *a priori*, the former as theoretical, objectively determining the objects as appearances. The second as practical, merely subjectively determining the objects as things in themselves.)",["The lone asterisks after propositions and before (g are Kant's reference mark, not italics.","Gemüth: mind; zugleich: simultaneously.","die erstere ... Die Zweyte: as written, the first applies to the sensible, though it is named second in the main text."]],"reflm:6346":["Two times are not simultaneous and two spaces not successive. But since there are nevertheless two different series (g of existence) in one time and likewise different sums total in one space, one can consider both not as properties inhering in the objects of the senses, but only as forms of the composition of the manifold in a sensible intuition.\nSince the conditions of space and time, which lie at the ground of all representations of experience, carry necessity with them, hence lie *a priori* in the faculty of representation of the senses, this can happen not otherwise [possible] than in such a way that they lie in the subject and its sensible form of intuition; for this [goes] alone is given before all experience.\nSuppose we cognized the objects of the senses as they are in themselves when we are immediately conscious of them, then this would still be no cognition *a priori*, but mere perception, which carries no necessity with it, but would contain only that it is so, not however that it necessarily must be so.\nThis ideality of intuitions *a priori* (g in the representation of the senses), the (g form of the) composition of the manifold of intuition in one apperception [and a] through the understanding together with the schematism of the power of judgment, — finally that through reason in the practical.\nThe infinity of actual space proves that it is merely the form of appearance. For infinite is that [Geg] which [wholly given] given, but which cannot exist otherwise than as a part of a whole. Now the *quantum* which can exist only as part of another *quanti* is not a thing that is objectively given.\nFurther, however large the given world may be, it can [in] still be contained in the space of a drop of water, a pin's head etc etc.; because of the infinite divisibility. Thus it cannot at all be composed of things in themselves.",["Beschaffenheiten: properties; Inbegriffe: sums total; Zusammensetzung: composition; Vorstellungsvermögen: faculty of representation.","[Geg] is a deleted fragment (Gegen-), left in the German; was gegeben has no verb as written.","The paragraph beginning Diese Idealität has no main verb in the German."]],"reflm:6347":["Of the something and nothing, things and non-things, — members of the division, the concept of the object is the divided. — I cannot experience my death, for that is contradiction, but indeed the death of another.",["Undinge: non-things; Eintheilung: division; erleben: experience, live through (not Erfahrung)."]],"reflm:6348":["(Summer 1797.) L Bl. E 21. S. I. R II 89f.\nThe form of intuition (not the form of thinking) of objects in space and time, because it is represented *a priori* and as necessary, proves its subjectivity, namely that they do not belong to the constitution of the objects, but must lie *a priori* in the sensibility of the subject, with which one can of course know *a priori* [how] in which form they will be intuited by us and how they will thus appear to us: But at the same time there also, that there a kind of representations, namely that of how the things are, at least must be thought.\nSecondly, freedom is a property of which we are not immediately conscious through inner sense, but it is apodictically proved through the concept of duty (namely negatively). This property represents to us a being such as the human being, not as he [as] appears, but as he is. — This principle of cognition *a priori* is, however, merely practical.\nBoth together are the *cardines* of the critical philosophy, and all metaphysics has them as its end. These together with the doctrine of taste make the whole of the principles [of the] breaks off?\nThe forms which we cognize through experience cannot be cognized *a priori*, because otherwise the character of necessity would be lacking to them.\nTranscendental concepts can be called sense-free. Transcendental philosophy also sense-free. Everything in which there is nothing empirical, but yet something universally valid. [Transcendent] e.g. the categories. Transcendent: what is no piece of cognition at all.",["Anschauungsform / Denkungsform: form of intuition / form of thinking; Beschaffenheit: constitution; Eigenschaft: property; *cardines*: hinges, cardinal points.","Aber zugleich da doch auch, daß es eine Art der Vorstellungen ... Gedacht werden müsse is broken in construction; the English keeps it so. muß/müsse: the subject of müsse may be die Anschauungsform or the objects.","sinnenfreye: sense-free; allgemeingeltendes: universally valid; Erkenntnisstük: piece of cognition."]],"reflm:6349":["S. I:\nIf the appearances of the senses were the things in themselves, then they would all have necessity in the chain of effects and causes, and there would be no freedom, hence also no categorical imperative, which yet through reason commands unconditionally necessarily.\nWere the object of inner sense, the soul, not mere appearance of itself, but a pure consciousness of its thinking, then we could not have the being-together of representations in time, consequently in a form that is thought *a priori* [in us] synthetically, in |\nus, thus have no (g experiential) cognition of ourselves. For what contains representations combined in relations of space and time is merely appearance.\nOn Aenesidemus and the diallelus. — To answer Tieftrunk. — How can a subject [itself] intuited by itself yet cognize itself merely as appearance?\nOne can [oneself] have no cognition at all of an object through the mere category: 1. that many together make up one; 2. that reality has a degree; 3. that something is so constituted that something else is the necessary sequel of it; 4. that the existence of a thing is absolutely necessary for itself.\nLikewise one can also have no cognition through intuitions alone and, if they are empirical, no cognition *a priori*, if the rule does not already precede.\nThe ideality of space and time (consequently the concept of all objects of experience as appearances) and the [R] practical reality of the concept of reason of freedom are the two cardinal points of metaphysics.\nOn the spectacle that is made with the proposition that one is to oneself in inner intuition only appearance, not the thing itself. It signifies only as much as: »inner experience is nothing more than [cogn] empirical cognition of oneself only according to the subjective of the way of intuition of ourselves in time (the apprehension of inner representations), as we represent ourselves inwardly, not as the subject in itself may be; as it is given to us inwardly, not as it is thought by us.« The difficulty concerns only how the subject in itself can institute experience. It must not merely perceive sensations in itself |\nbut excite them and connect them synthetically, hence affect itself. Thus it is not a thinking but an intuiting of itself.\nTo Herr Collegiate Councillor and Director Euler at the Russian Imperial Academy of Sciences at St. Petersburg.\nThat space and time yield synthetic propositions *a priori*. That this is not possible otherwise than merely if they are subjective forms of sensibility; for otherwise they would be synthetic empirical propositions about the object.\nS. II:\nThat synthetic propositions *a priori* are actual, consequently also possible, is proved by mathematics. That these [possibility] however are not possible through perception of the objects of intuition as things in themselves is to be seen from this, that otherwise they would be empirical and would [be] contain no necessity, which is proper only to cognitions *a priori*. That [thus] they thus only [through] indicate the subjective constitution of our sensibility, which still before everything empirical, (g thus) *a priori*, provides the form of intuition, hence can contain the cognition (g objects) of the senses merely as appearances, follows from this inevitably. That it must also be so, however, follows if we proceed from the practical-supersensible of freedom. [For the categorical imperative could not be valid if actions were determined by natural causes, and no freedom would be possible if the determining nature represented things in themselves. The human being (g as object in) himself (g himself merely) would think himself as determinable (g in accordance with) natural laws. He must, insofar as he is to be efficacious in himself, think himself, considered as phenomenon, solely under natural laws, for thus he can think himself as noumenon independent of the condition of time, determining himself:] Conversely, if our actions are free, then the [sense] inner sense can give us only appearances, not cognition of our substance as thing in itself. For were the latter the case, then* all |\ninner determinations of the soul would be physically necessary, like everything that happens according to the law of cause and effect, and there would be no freedom.\n*(g The consciousness of all determinations of the soul (of inner sense) be empirical, thus no practical principle of them *a priori*, as that on which all unconditioned necessity, thus also that of actions of duty, rests, be possible.)\nThat the concept of freedom and its reality is a sufficient ground to assume the existence of God and a future life. — The moral imperative contains an ought and just for that reason a faculty, in conflict with the whole of nature on account of its causality, yet to overcome it (to resist it). This faculty is necessarily related to the faculty of a being to make these [decree] decrees of practical reason also in agreement with nature or conversely. Such a faculty, however, is in God.",["“diallelus” renders Dialele, the circular argument (Greek diallelos).","“object” renders both Object and Gegenstand; “perceive” and “perception” render warnehmen / warnehmung.","The footnote continues the main sentence's würden (“would”): “would the consciousness … be empirical … would no practical principle … be possible”; the German is left without its finite verb."]],"reflm:6350":["S. I:\nOnly under presupposition of the [exter] subjective form of inner and outer intuition are synthetic propositions *a priori* possible, for the objects must be in accordance with this. This is the principle of the ideality of space and time.\nIf the subject [in the] affects itself to representations of inner sense, then these are only [appear] ways in which the subject appears to itself, and experience is only the composition, ordered according to the categories, of the representations of inner sense.\nMyinda, blind man's buff, is merrier than [the c] the brawl with blindfolded eyes *andabatarum*.\nThings [in themselves], as they are in themselves, not considered as appearances, qualify for no theoretical cognition, [but] for they are merely ideas. But to conduct oneself in a manner adequate to these ideas [goes] |\nis a real concept of practical reason. *Myinda metaphysica* or anticritique of pure reason.\nOne can never have an experience of a supersensible nature: God, freedom, spiritual being; for between the two an action and a reaction would have to take place.\nWhat is object? That whose representation is a sum total of several predicates belonging to it. The plate is round, warm, of tin etc. etc. Warm, round, of tin etc. etc. is no object, but indeed the warmth, the tin etc. etc.\nObject is that in whose representation various others (g than) can be thought as synthetically combined.\nIn every judgment there is subject and predicate [and and object which]. The subject of the judgment, insofar as it can contain various [pre] possible predicates, is the object. The predicates all depend on the subject, as warm on warmth.\nWarm, quadrangular, deep are predicates. — The warmth, the quadrangle, the depth are objects. — Likewise rational and reason. The determinable in a judgment, the logical subject, is at the same time the [object] real object.\nWhat is virtuous I can explain analytically; but virtue itself is afterwards [thereby] composed synthetically from it.\n(g Remark. Principle of synthetic universality, insofar as it flows from the analytic. — 2 parallel lines, cut by a third breaks off?)\nS. II:\nThe subject of a judgment whose representation contains the ground of the synthetic unity of a manifoldness of predicates is object.\nThat which contains the synthetic (real) universality of a concept is the object. — What contains merely the analytic [contains is] (logical) is the logical (subject). Reinhold.\nThe concept which contains the synthetic unity of apperception of the mani|fold\n(whatever might be added to it) is the concept of an object. It is also the subject of a judgment that has many predicates.\n[The represented in] The thought in a judgment is the object (g matter); the thinking of it through the predicate is the form, how I think it. [A quadrangle] Thus the concept [virtuous happiness] wise is the predicate in a judgment, e.g. [This] The virtuous one is wise.\nAt the bottom of the page, separated by a space of about 1 cm from what immediately precedes:\nTo the Society of Artists for the Mechanical Reproduction of Paintings in Duisburg.\nOn the words “ein Mahl”, “Niemand”, “Jemandt”.",["*Myinda*: Greek name of the game of blind man's buff (Blindekuhspiel); *andabatarum*: “of the andabatae”, gladiators who fought blindfolded.","“object” renders both Object (objekt) and Gegenstand (in “The thought in a judgment is the object”).","The words mentioned at the end are left in German: ein Mahl (“once”), Niemand (“nobody”), Jemandt (“somebody”)."]],"reflm:6351":["1797). L Bl. Berliner Staatsbibliothek. Nr. 21. S. I, II.\nS. I:\nBerl. Zeitung, 30th September 1797.\n»The Royal Academy of Sciences is not of the opinion of those who regard it as mathematically proved that there are pure subjective representations; it is rather convinced that there are essential counter-grounds to which up to now no satisfactory answer has yet been given, and that there is no lack of strong grounds for the universal empirical origin of all our cognition, which only up to now have perhaps not yet been placed in their full light.«\nNB. The prize question has been set by the Philosophical Class of the Academy of Sciences until 1 June 1799. The director is Herr Selle.\nSuch a ground of proof is a contradiction in itself. For, |\nif it is to be universally valid, then it must also contain unconditioned necessity, consequently be able to be conducted *a priori* [condu], hence also independently of everything empirical. For (g only) by necessity does one cognize universality *stricte*.\nS. II:\nThere is no doubt that if a physician had [had] had the commission from the Academy to draft the formula of this problem, (g he) could have adduced not only strong but also incontestable grounds for the universal [orig] empirical origin of all his (medical) cognition. — But how such a problem could then have passed over [into the philosophical] into the philosophical department and not into the medical is not to be comprehended.\nOf synthetic practical cognition *a priori* it is also said that it is merely subjective; (of the will) freedom is the first — and the transcendental concepts of God and immortality refer only to the principles of my actions. I ought to act as if there were a God and a future life.\nHow are synthetic propositions of the supersensible possible? As regulative principles of the practical, not as constitutive ones of theoretical cognition.\n(g The supersensible that is given is the concept of freedom; consequently no synthetic-theoretical proposition, hence one that would be objectively transcendent, is possible.)\nOf objects as things in themselves. That there must be such in our reason is to be seen from the opposite of the sensible in general.",["The opening line is the editors' header for the loose sheet and is left as it stands.","[condu] renders the deleted, unfinished gefüh (for geführt, “conducted”); *stricte*: “strictly”.","In the German the deleted [gehabt] stands after “zu entwerfen” (“to draft”); English word order moves it forward."]],"reflm:6352":["S. I:\nOn the analogy between the parallels and the principle of universal empiricism of Selle.",["“of Selle” (von Selle) may attach to “the principle of universal empiricism” or to “the analogy”."]],"reflm:6353":["S. I:\nWhat if Herr Bek began from the categories, which have no significance for themselves but are yet concepts *a priori*, then progressed to intuitions *a priori* which correspond to them, and so came to space, time and reality.\nThe system of the Critique of Pure Reason turns on 2 cardinal points: as system of nature and of freedom, of which the one leads to the necessity of the other. — The ideality of space and time and the reality of the concept of freedom, from one of which one is unavoidably led analytically to the other. According to the one to synthetic-theoretical cognition *a priori*, according to the other to synthetic practical, likewise completely *a priori*. The nature of the human being in himself cannot be determined *a priori* without this determination (to actions) presupposing freedom; for otherwise this determination would not happen *a priori*. Something supersensible must therefore be assumed, under which the sensible can be considered as determinable through it, and conversely something sensible *a priori*, [under] according to which the supersensible [empi to activity] determines sensibility in deeds. The (g\n1st) principle: all objects of our cognition, if they are to [become] yield experience, can be considered only as appearances. The 2nd principle is the categorical imperative: all actions from freedom can be thought only according to the principle |\nof agreement with the universal validity of their maxims for universal legislation.",["“Bek” is Kant's spelling of the name.","“principle” renders Grundsatz in “The 1st principle”, “The 2nd principle”, and Princip in “according to the principle”."]],"reflm:6354":["S. I:\nInner sense is not yet [the consciousness wher] cognition of myself, but [only representation of the inner appear] first we must have appearances through it, only afterwards through reflection on them make for ourselves a concept of ourselves, which then has as its sequel empirical cognition of myself, i.e. [exper] inner experience. — But that even this experience yet again lets us judge ourselves in another relation merely as appearance, not as we are absolutely in ourselves, comes down to this, that the form of inner sense-intuition may also derive wholly and entirely from the specific constitution of the subject, because we must yet be affected by something [outs], at any rate through attention by ourselves, and thus all inner intuition is passive. Such a form we also encounter in our sense-intuition (the inner), namely time, which could never *a priori* (as yet actually happens) and thus as necessarily proper to the subject be [represented be] thought. This representation of time also cannot be derived from concepts, but merely as to the appearance of the subject with regard to myself. This now makes up a double I, but not of consciousness (I appear to myself, I am to myself also in this empirical consciousness yet the observed and at the same time observer, who breaks off.",["“breaks off” renders the editors' bricht ab; “who” renders Kant's last word, der.","The sentence on time (“which could never … be thought”) seems to lack a condition; it is left as it stands.","[outs] renders the deleted, unfinished auss; “reflection” renders Reflexion."]],"reflm:6355":["S. II:\nThat synthetic [propositions] propositions a priori are possible only through the subjective form |\nof sensibility, consequently can represent their objects only as appearances, is to be seen from this, because they carry necessity with them, but not from the concept through analysis. — For supposing we could perceive the things in themselves, then such propositions would lack necessity and universality. But if they are merely appearances, then we can know a priori how they must appear to us; for they can have no other intuitions than the subjective constitution of our sense permits. — But this has no kinship with the fact that, as far as color is concerned, each of us may have his own kind of sense. For this is sensation, consequently not objective, but merely subjective, and has no universality with it [for everyone] and necessity.",["“objects” renders Objecte (Latinate).","“they can have no other intuitions” keeps the German sie, which may refer to the propositions or to the appearances; “this” in “For this is sensation” renders diese, most likely die Sinnesart.","“kind of sense” renders Sinnesart."]],"reflm:6356":["S. III:\n1. How are synthetic propositions a priori about things that can be object of experience possible. 2. How are synthetic propositions a priori about things that cannot be objects of experience, [possible] i.e. about [mere ideal beings] objects of [mer] pure reason possible? — The former only in that the objects (outer as well as the inner object of inner sense) can be cognized merely as appearances in theoretical cognition: the latter merely as objects that [determine] correspond to the final end of reason for (g pure) practical cognition [in us s] of ourselves.",["“object(s)” renders Gegenstand, Gegenstände; “final end” renders Endzwek.","“cognition” renders Erkentnis, here neuter (das Erkentnis) as often in Kant."]],"reflm:6357":["S. III:\nSince merely the subjectivity of the form of sensible intuition can make synthetic propositions a priori (as going merely to objects in appearance.) |\npossible, it can also be comprehended why they are contradictory to the principles of the pure understanding and to the concepts made according to them. e.g. that space consists neither of infinitely many parts nor of a finite number of them. Why the given space nor the elapsed time is neither finite nor infinite; because namely here not the object in itself, but the making of it through the composition of the manifold in appearance [to] underlies the concept or is laid under it.\nIs it one and the same to say: that space is divisible into the infinite, or to say: that it consists of infinitely many parts. Likewise in what the infinity of space and of time, which are represented as given.\n––––––––––\n[Auch:XXIII 462,18-19]\nOnly through and for the moral law do the theoretical ideas of God and immortality obtain their (practical) reality.\nS. IV:\nHow are synthetic propositions a priori possible with regard to the faculty of cognition, of the feeling of pleasure and displeasure and the faculty of desire, with regard to the sensible objectively, with regard to the supersensible subjectively? — on the centering of the power of representation, through the [Subj] objective being thought on the one hand as appearance, on the other as in itself.",["“they are contradictory” keeps sie, which may refer to the propositions or to the objects in appearance; “object(s)” renders Obiecte, Obiect (Latinate).","“Why the given space nor the elapsed time” keeps the German's doubled negative (noch … weder … noch); “Likewise in what …” is incomplete in the source.","“composition” renders Zusammensetzen; [Auch:XXIII 462,18-19] is the editor's cross-reference and is left untranslated."]],"reflm:6358":["S. II:\nThe whole of the critical philosophy.\nFirst the highest division according to the categories.\na. Mathematical and dynamical potencies. Constitutive and regulative principles: the former of the faculty of cognition, the latter of the faculty of desire. — A. All [in] representations given in intuition as appearances, |\nB. as things in themselves. (Appearances not empirical, e.g. those of colors, but representations of intuition given a priori). — The sensible as such considered universally points toward a supersensible.\nPrinciple: Synthetic propositions a priori (g the theoretical ones) represent all objects to me only as appearances. — The things in themselves thought a priori relate to the supersensible.\nb. dynamical potencies. l. Freedom: basis of the constitutive principle of the supersensible. 2. Necessity: basis of the regulative. Theoretically transcendent — practically immanent. α. God, β. immortality of the soul. — Freedom: ideality as to the subject and reality with regard to its causality. Concepts of reason and ideas. — Apparent antinomy of reason with regard to the totality of conditions, which are all contingent and conditioned in comparison with the unconditioned, which is merely in pure reason, i.e. is in the idea. Practical idea as regulative principle, to act as if there were a God and another world. — The scholastic systematic (*simplex et vnum*), the popular fragmentary*.\nS. III:\n* I do not see why one should not begin with Mr. Bek from the categories, yet in such a way that at the same time it would be admitted that these pure concepts of the understanding, without laying sensibility under them as *Materia circa quam*, can produce no cognition at all. E.g. quantity (how one in many), quality (how many in one) is thought as contained; relation: how, when something is given in perception, something really distinct from it is also (g a priori) given as sequel, modality: how the form of the connection of all perceptions (in one experience) a priori makes the actuality of this manifold in one experience [det] cognizable. — Here |\nMr. Beck will only remark that these categories lack objective reality, namely one does not see whether such a thing is possible or not. Now he would apply the latter to appearances in general in space and time as intuitions and so follow the synthetic method, which attempt would yet have another standpoint in it.\nS. IV:\nTo empirical cognition (experience) belongs: (g 1.) intuition, i.e. representation through which an object is given, 2. concept, through which this is thought, 3. composition of the manifold of intuition, 4. unity in the consciousness of it.\nThe empirical in the consciousness of an intuition is called perception (*animadversio*) and has a degree, i.e. intensive magnitude; pure intuition merely extensive magnitude (space and time). Space and time in which nothing is perceived (no empirical representation of the object is met with) are empty.\nTo the composition of the manifold of intuition in conformity with the rule that is contained in the concept of it belongs the synthesis of the manifold of intuition into a concept (category) and then that of perceptions into the possibility of an experience.\n(g From the moral-practical to ascend to ideas and indeed of the theoretical: God and immortality, and so again downward to the sensibly practical: religion, happiness of the state and private happiness. Here too the subjective of practical reason is determining ground of the objective in appearance, on which synthetic-practical propositions a priori can be grounded.)\nOn the cognition of the sensible and the supersensible.\nSynthetic propositions a priori can give us to cognize objects only as appearances, not according to what they are in themselves. — For since through such a synthetic proposition I say more of the object than is contained in my representation of the object, I assert something of it that does not belong to it, i.e. it is without truth. (Spontaneity of an empty judgment.). — It is not possible to go beyond my given concept a priori otherwise than only through a judgment that contains the relation of the object to the constitution |\nof the subject and of its faculty of representation [sub] or rather first of its receptivity for being affected by the object in a certain way, hence says only how it appears to me, not how it is. [With] With this limitation I can say a priori how the object will and must appear to me.\nS. III:\nOne can think no kind of representation as limited with regard to a certain principle without opposing [it] to it another that is universal with regard to it. I.e. if I distinguish a cognition by its being limited to the sensibility of the subject, then I must think to myself a cognition of the supersensible in opposition and can afterwards investigate whether and how (of theoretical or practical use) reality could be procured for it.\nTo explore the supersensible [is] is thus an unavoidable problem of reason, what outside me or in me [necessarily] must be assumed as necessarily existing, if not in theoretical, yet in moral-practical respect. God and immortality, which through the law of freedom (morality). All philosophy aims at these 3 points, namely to strive from the sensible to the supersensible (metaphysics).",["*simplex et vnum*: “simple and one”; *Materia circa quam*: “matter about which”; *animadversio*: “perception”. “Bek” is Kant's spelling of Beck.","“Object(s)” and “Gegenstand” are both “object”; “really” renders Realiter; “the latter” renders die letztere, whose referent is unclear in the German.","“God and immortality, which through the law of freedom (morality)” is incomplete in the source and left so."]],"reflm:6359":["and 11 Dec. 1797). L Bl. C 2. S. I, II. R I 128-131.\nS. I:\n1. Note which (to the treatise on the false subtlety of the 4 syllogistic figures) would state in all brevity the essential difference of the |\nprocedure of the understanding in its logical function from that in its transcendental function; 2. a note which (to the treatise on the use of teleological principles in philosophy — at the end) would state briefly the difference between exposition and deduction of the categories, whereby it could at the same time be remarked how the proposition in the Critique of Pure Reason p. 177 is to be understood: that through the transcendental time-determination (because it is homogeneous with the appearances and the categories) the application of the categories to appearances is mediated and made possible. The difficulty seems to be, because the transcendental time-determination is itself already a product of apperception in relation to the form of intuition and thus itself also raises the inquiry how the application of the category to the form of intuition is possible, since categories and form of intuition are heterogeneous. In general the schematism is one of the most difficult points. — Even Mr. Beck cannot find his way in it. — I hold this chapter to be one of the most important.\nNB. The intuition of time is not homogeneous with the categories, but the time-determination, but the unity of representations in the synthesis (composition) of the given intuition breaks off?\n(g The desire to rule is as a rule with the faculty of law.)\nNB. 1.) That the categories in themselves and for themselves alone have no object at all (g or sense) (because they are mere forms of thought) and the possibility e.g. of a composite (since many together make up one) cannot be explained.\nNB. 2) When with the philosophical principles of cognition a priori it comes to metaphysics, then one can no longer judge fragmentarily, |\nbut must judge systematically; a critical dogmatic philosophy is an absurdity.\nS. II:\nThe categories [are] as functions (subjective possibility) of the composition of the manifold, insofar as the many in the representation of something (*entis*) makes up one. a) Of magnitude in (g pure) intuition, b) of sensation, quality of the empirical or of perception [c.)] of the homogeneous, c) of the real ground and the sequel of sensations, i.e. of causality, d) of experience, empirical cognition, consciousness of the existence of an object of given perception.\nThe composite [can] as such can not be given (g be) in intuition, but only through the composing of the manifold, thus through the concept of synthesis, be cognized as composite.\nThe merely subjective in intuition [is] as representation of an object is appearance. The form of an appearance, insofar as it is representation a priori, not empirical, is called pure intuition. Thus [light and] representations of colored objects are not pure intuitions.\nThe (g re)finding of what has been thought is (g selective) memory. The holding together of it in looking back on what has been thought and what is still foreseen is the power of apprehension. The power of judgment (namely the negative) lasts longest in growing old. If this dwindles, then age makes one childish.\nN.B. That time is expressed through a line (which is yet a space) and space through a time (an hour's walking) is a schematism of the concepts of the understanding. *Compositio*.",["“breaks off?” renders the editor's bricht ab?; “absurdity” renders Unding; “inquiry” renders Nachfrage.","*entis*: “(of) a being”; *Compositio*: “composition”. “(g re)finding” and “(g selective) memory” render (g wieder) Auffinden and (g Wahl)Gedächtnis.","“power of apprehension” renders Fassungskraft; “foreseen” renders prospicirte; “this” in “If this dwindles” renders dieses, whose referent is not clear."]],"reflm:6360":["—\nS. I:\n1.) The doctrine of the objects of the senses, both of the outer and of the inner, as appearances. 2. The doctrine of the supersensible [as] (unconditioned) as regulative principle, not constitutive, and so as practical matter in itself, namely not beginning from the concepts of God and of immortality toward morals, but to step from morals to those concepts.\nA *Qvantum* of which every magnitude to be specified (g can) always be thought only as part of another homogeneous one, is infinite. — That an object, however, can always exist only as part proves that it is no true thing, but only a thought-entity. That we do not intuit it as composite, but are conscious only [der Z uns] of the act of our composition according to a certain form of intuition. What is infinitely small. — Through categories alone, because they contain merely thinking, we cognize no object (matter). [they] One cannot even have insight into the possibility of an object according to them, e.g. how many homogeneous things together make up one, or how something positive can yet with regard to the same predicate also be determined negatively, or how, if something actual is given, another actual thing must necessarily follow upon it, or finally how a thing's existence can be absolutely necessary. Joined to the real (to what can be sensed), space and time intuition alone gives us cognitions, which in the end (even pure mathematics) [th] can prove the reality of their concepts merely through their agreement with the possibility of experience, whereby, however, the intuitions can supply cognitions a priori only when they must be assumed not as things in themselves, but always only as appearances, i.e. as the subjective form of our sensibility, because one can know this for itself alone, |\nlet the objects be what they will, as to its constitution, consequently those are given a priori.\n––––––––––––––––––\nThat a human being can be represented to himself also from one side also as appearance is no harder to see than that it takes place with outer objects; if one lays [the] the possibility of representing oneself as object, which cannot be further proven, at the ground. For he is to himself partly object of his sense, or of his faculty of thinking. In the former case he is conscious how he affects himself empirically, and places these impressions in the form of time. In the second he is conscious how he affects the subject and is [himself] to that extent in the act of spontaneity.\nOne cannot become conscious of the intuition of a composite as such immediately, but only of the composition (*synthesis*), i.e. of the self-activity of the composition. Hence the categories.\nOf pressure, pull and both together at the beginning of interaction: impact.\nS. III:\nPhilosophy (as doctrine of wisdom) is the doctrine of the vocation of the human being with regard to [his] the final end proceeding from his own reason.\nA. To this belongs, as science of the object (g theoretical) of this vocation in general, to find through reason the highest condition for the conditioned in general (the totality of the conditions) and, since this, if the representation of the objects is sensible, always stands in contradiction (antinomy) with itself, but nevertheless an unconditioned must yet be assumed, to arrive at the conclusion: that the cognition of things through senses never gives anything more than mere appearance: never the things in themselves.\nB. That practical cognition from reason can never command unconditionally (conse|quently\nonly through the concept of duty), which grounds the concept of freedom, which can be cognized only through this imperative. — With this now the doctrine of right (and the doctrine of state right and of the right of nations to be met with in it) and the doctrine of virtue — both as pure rational cognition from concepts — is grounded.\nC. That from this non-sensible a step to the supersensible is still open, namely to the concepts of God and immortality, which, taught dogmatically, contain only transcendent attempts, but critically, [with] after preceding critique of his own faculty of reason, do not give those objects to be cognized [but] (as they are unattainable for us), but whose idea yet provides a regulative principle (not constitutive) to act as if those ideas at the same time had objective theoretical reality.\nNow still the doctrine of taste as belonging to the power of judgment according to principles. — —",["“[der Z uns]” is a deleted fragment, left in German.","“can never command unconditionally” renders nie unbedingt ... gebieten könne as printed; the reading sits awkwardly with the parenthesis that follows.","“object” renders both Gegenstand and the Latinate Object; “principle” renders Princip."]],"reflm:6361":["S. I:\nHow are synthetic cognitions (judgments) a priori possible according to the three categories of modality, of the possibility, actuality and necessity of judgments about the object. (of the problematic, categorical and apodictic.) the faculty of cognition, feeling of pleasure and displeasure and faculty of desire. Understanding, power of judgment and reason. Logic, aesthetic and morals. — Cognition a priori indicates necessity. Necessity *simpliciter* or *secundum quid*. Skill, prudence, wisdom. Philosophical or historical history of philosophy. — Ascending from the sensible of intuition through concepts of reflection (of the end) to the supersensible.",["“the faculty of cognition, feeling of pleasure and displeasure and faculty of desire” stand in the dative in the German (dem Erkentnis Vermogen ...), with no governing word.","*simpliciter* or *secundum quid*: “without qualification” or “in a certain respect”; “object” renders the Latinate object."]],"reflm:6362":["S. I:\nXenophanes. Empedocles, Parmenides, Pythagoras. Thales: whether they were the first is uncertain.\nNB. The composite as such I cannot (g im|mediately)\nintuit, but only become conscious of the composition in [space and] time [consequently] and so get a concept of the intuited object, so that inner sense to that extent also contains the consciousness of outer intuition.",[]],"reflm:6363":["S. II:\nOn the Metaph. (g Foundations) of the doctrine of nature. The (g empirical) progress in space goes to infinity, without my therefore being permitted to assume an infinite space: [for] Because I can experience no boundary; for that would have to happen through perception of empty space, which is impossible. Where, however, [I] there is no boundary for me, there my progress goes to infinity.",["“Foundations” renders Anfangs Grunde (Anfangsgründe); “progress” renders Fortschritt."]],"reflm:6364":["S. I:\nAllgemeine Geographische Ephemeriden March 1799 p. 276. where the difference between theoretical and practical astronomy is spoken of and it is said: »what then would astronomy be without observation? Perhaps a Kantian astronomy a priori? Theory and practice must go hand in hand« (but which goes first in the mathematical?) — In the review of the theoretical astronomy by Friedrich Theodor Schubert, member of the Russian Imperial Academy of Sciences at St. Petersburg.",[]],"reflm:6365":["11. S. II R.-Sch. XI 2 S. 164.\nOf the method of teaching that treats the way of thinking of human beings like an *ars veterinaria* (g cattle-medicine-science), e.g. through frightening with purgatory and hell, and in general to work on senses, not on reason. — One can also treat mathematics philosoph|ically,\nbut not conversely: namely in order to determine the boundaries of its possible use and of its end.",["*ars veterinaria*: “veterinary art”; Kant's gloss is Vieh-Arzney-Kunde."]],"reflm:6366":["S. III:\nBetween philosophical cognition and philosophy there is a difference. Being able to philosophize and being a philosopher.",[]],"reflm:6367":["1800). L Bl. K 7.\nS. II:\nThe consciousness of oneself as a being residing in the body, touching the matter on which it acts, which perceives itself therein, is contradictory; for it would then be an atomistic principle that acted thus, which, however, is thoroughly canceled by the dynamical.",["“canceled” renders aufgehoben."]],"reflm:6368":["1800-1801). L Bl. Kemke 2. S. II.\nS. IIa:\nWhat are the *reagentia* as trial experiments in morals and what the mixtures that cannot be further separated, in chemistry?\nMathematics is the most reliable of all sciences, because it itself makes everything that it offers for cognizing, and presents pure intuitions in space and time.\nS. IIb:\nTo make the subjective in our way of representing objective.\nS. IIc:\nTheologians and jurists are the 2 scholars of reason on whom the highest principles, i.e. those of the use of reason with regard to morality, rest.",["*reagentia*: “reagents”; “scholars of reason” renders Vernunftgelehrte."]],"reflm:6369":["Remarks of Kant's in his copy, interleaved with octavo sheets, of vol. II of G. Chr. Lichtenberg's Vermischte Schriften, ed. L. Chr. Lichtenberg and Fr. Kries (1801; preface dated Aug. 1800; announced as completed in the catalogue of the Michaelmas fair of 1800).\nOn p. 13 »It always seems to me as if the concept *being* were something borrowed from our thinking, and if there are no longer any sensing and thinking creatures, then there is also nothing any more«, Kant remarks on p. 13':\npure idealism.\np. 28/9. »In the drives of insects there is many a thing that must make us believe that they are guided more by the future than by the past.« On »insects« p. 28':\nWhich make their bed for the winter sleep, since they did not know beforehand that they would rise again in future.\np. 32/3. Beside the beginning of the sentence: »I believe from the bottom of my soul and after the most mature reflection that the doctrine of Christ, cleansed of priestly scribbling, and understood duly according to our way of expressing ourselves, is the most perfect system that I at least can think of for promoting peace and happiness in the world most quickly, most powerfully, most surely and most universally« stand, written by Kant in red crayon, the words:\nI too.\np. 33: »Christ at the same time accommodated himself to the material, and this compels admiration even from the atheist. (In what sense I here take the word atheist every thinker will feel.)« On the underlined word »atheist« p. 33':\nGodlessness and denying God.\np. 74: »Caring for the future must, for creatures that do not know the future, suffer peculiar restrictions«. Opposite on p. 74':\nCaring for the future (being worried) is commonly that which does not let the intended happiness arise.\np. 76 below:\n*verte*\np. 92: »it is impossible that a being can receive the influences of another without the main effect appearing mixed«. After »can receive« Kant makes in the margin the addition:\nwithout taking them up on its part.\np. 137: »To make the human being such as religion would have him resembles the undertaking of the Stoics; it is only another degree of the impossible«. »religion would have« underlined in red crayon, in addition a stroke in red crayon beside the whole sentence in the margin, and beside the beginning (up to »undertaking«) a large question mark (also red crayon).\np. 234: »The saddest thing that the French Revolution has brought about for us is indisputably this, that every reasonable demand, one to be required by God and by right, will be regarded as a germ of rebellion.« Beside the last words in red crayon:\nNot happened!\np. 269: »I have never been able to find a kind that had so much in common with the tinker as the people who, under the pretence of plying a useful trade, travel about in order to deceive and rob people«. Beside it in red crayon:\nLavater.\np. 415: »About superstition something very good could certainly be written, namely in its defense. Everyone is superstitious. I with my lights; I do not believe in these things, but it is still agreeable to me if they do not turn out adverse.« Beside the words »namely — lights« in the margin a stroke and question mark (red crayon).\nOn the last blank leaf stand, written in red crayon, the following words, of which the last is quite uncertain and of all of which it is doubtful whether they come from Kant:\nPatria Fr. II Patri.",["“Godlessness and denying God” renders Ohngötterey und Gottesläugnen, two German renderings of “atheism”, the first literally “without-gods-ery”.","*verte*: “turn over”; “red crayon” renders Rothstift; the underlined words on p. 137 are die Religion haben.","*Patria Fr. II Patri*: Latin, perhaps “fatherland, to Frederick II, to the father”; the last word is marked as quite uncertain."]],"reflm:6370":["Above and\nThe hypothetical possibility is problematic. If certain conditions are there, e.g. the equality of the angles of a triangle. The possibility *in hypothesi* is categorical. The conditions are.",["The editorial location note “Über und” is cut off in the source.","*in hypothesi*: “in the hypothesis”."]],"reflm:6371":["*Conditio est, qvo non posito non ponitur aliud*. It is therefore not the ground. The *hypothesis* can be negative.",["*Conditio est, qvo non posito non ponitur aliud*: “A condition is that which, not being posited, another is not posited”."]],"reflm:6372":["Something is possible *in Hypothesi*: signifies more than internally possible.\n—\n—\nonly in it\n—\nWhat is possible only under a *hypothesi* is not absolute.",["*in Hypothesi*, *hypothesi*: Latin case forms, “in the hypothesis”, “a hypothesis”."]],"reflm:6373":["Something can be possible *in thesi* and yet is not so *in hypothesi*. The hypothetical possibility is always restrictive *sub conditione suspensiva*.",["*in thesi* / *in hypothesi*: “in the thesis” / “in the hypothesis”; *sub conditione suspensiva*: “under a suspensive condition”."]],"reflm:6374":["I cannot say: it is absolutely possible (i.e. without any restric|tive\ncondition) that the square of one side of the triangle be equal to the squares of the others; but in itself it is yet possible.",[]],"reflm:6375":["The absolute is opposed not to the hypothetical, but to the restrictive and conditioned.",[]],"reflm:6376":["One can say: something is not merely in itself (internally), but also hypothetically possible. But also: something is only hypothetically, not absolutely possible. What is possible in every *hypothesi* is actual; for it is absolutely possible.",["“in every *hypothesi*” renders in aller hypothesi."]],"reflm:6377":["According to the *principio rationis* the same state is always accompanied by the same sequel; because we can determine [through] the state only through the sequel, the state determines the sequel. We cannot have insight into how something must follow upon something else; but that the same *antecedens* must have the same *conseqvens* flows from this, because the state itself is determined through the sequel and otherwise there would be no *continuum* of the *phaenomenorum* without interconnection of the members, and they are also represented in a series through this alone.",["*principio rationis*: “principle of ground”; *phaenomenorum*: “of the phenomena”.","“sequel” renders Folge; *antecedens* / *conseqvens*: “antecedent” / “consequent”."]],"reflm:6378":["The sequel is posited, as to time, after the ground, although this |\ntime is vanishing. If, however, there is a series of sequels, and indeed one that is infinite, then the succession becomes noticeable. The immediate ground is the starting point of a series of sequels.",["“succession” renders Kant's succession, not Abfolge."]],"reflm:6379":["In and\nThat everything contingent or what arises has its ground flows from this, because without *prius* there would be no continuity of the *phaenomenorum* and without rule no identity of them.",["*prius*: “something prior”; *phaenomenorum*: “of the phenomena”."]],"reflm:6380":["In, below and\nThe ground determines the sequel, but this not conversely.",["The editorial location note is cut off in the source."]],"reflm:6381":["14. E II 1662.\nExistence contains at the same time the concept of a thoroughgoing determination in itself; but through it (g alone) the concept of the thing is not determined at all. Now we have a concept which at the same time contains the thoroughgoing determination of a thing: the concept of the *realissimi*; and these determinations (it seems to us) we can also specify *in concreto* [giv], e.g. wisdom, power etc etc. Only the existence of the thing (because this is no determination) is not given thereby, as following necessarily from this concept. But even if the necessary existence of some thing is assumed, I cannot say that the thoroughgoing determination with regard to its quality is through the concept of necessity, but it is given only through that of existence, where the predicates remain undetermined. There could be several necessary beings distinct from one another; but of course then none of them would be [existence] determinable from the concept of necessity (how it is constituted). One could only say: |\nM 14: |\nwe have no concept for its thoroughgoing determination other than that of the *realissimi*, but not: this concept is con|tained in that of its necessity.\nIt is only a subjective [necess] logical necessity of the condition, to make ourselves a concept of its determinations; but that if from the concept of it (of a *non realissimi*) this idea it is determinable *duplici modo*, then that signifies only: we can think many kinds of beings as necessary, not: the same being could also be determined otherwise, for here we have only concepts.",["\"determination\", \"determined\": both the Latinate determination, determinirt and Bestimmung, bestimmt.","*realissimi*: genitive of *realissimum*, the most real; *duplici modo*: in a twofold way.","The clause \"but that if ... determinable *duplici modo*\" is broken in the German and is translated as it stands."]],"reflm:6382":["Through existence no predicate is posited in addition to the thing, but the thing with all its predicates is posited outside the concept (for intuition) [in addition].",["\"posited in addition\": hinzu gesetzt; the deleted \"[hinzu]\" stands before \"gesetzt\" at the end of the German sentence."]],"reflm:6383":["If I posit something only relatively to my concept, then it is in many ways undetermined; but if it is posited absolutely, then according to the *principio exclusi medii* it is thoroughly determined; but I cannot infer conversely.",["*principio exclusi medii*: principle of the excluded middle.","\"thoroughly\": durchgängig."]],"reflm:6384":["In and \nI can indeed say: everything actual is thoroughly determined, but not: everything thoroughly determined is actual. — It is only a thought.",[]],"reflm:6385":["The objective reality of the concept of a *quanti*, namely that many make up one, is not clear for itself, but only in space and time, since namely not many times can exist as it were separated from one another (nor many moments), but only so that they make up one time. Just so many spaces. — Thereby the concept of the |\n*qvanti* becomes a principle of possible experience, namely of perceptions so connected that many together represent one (homogeneous) object, which is one not in many, but represents many as contained in one. *Praedicabilia quantitatis*.",["\"principle\": princip, not Grundsatz.","\"which is one not in many, but represents many as contained in one\": the construction of the German relative clause is irregular.","*Praedicabilia quantitatis*: predicables of quantity."]],"reflm:6386":["The transcendental propositions a priori are all analytic and drawn from the concept of a thing in general, the essential pieces and the thoroughgoing determination belonging to its existence: the unity of the concept; the truth in the combination of the manifold that pertains to it, i.e. that everything belonging to it be grounded either sufficiently for existence or at least for its possibility, and because every common ground of the manifold [combined] composite is really a ground [is according to] for me to compose it, this composition stands, as to its kind, under a rule according to which they are coordinated (*essentialia*) or subordinated ([*attribu*] *affectiones*); — perfection is the [comp] adequacy of this manifold to its concept — *totalitas*. *Vnum, plura conjuncta, totum*.",["\"pieces\": Stücke.","\"[comp]\": the deleted \"Zus\" breaks off, presumably in \"Zusammen-\"; \"adequacy\": Angemessenheit.","*Vnum, plura conjuncta, totum*: one, several conjoined, whole."]],"reflm:6387":["Transcendental perfection consists in this, that all the pieces belonging to the essence together make up the whole essence.",["\"essence\": Wesen, which can also be read as \"being\"; \"pieces\": Stücke."]],"reflm:6388":["That all combination as composition must be not perceived as (g outside me), but thought as having come about through me.",[]],"reflm:6389":["26. 25'. E II 1670II. 1669.\nM 26':\nAssume a necessary being. Then you represent to yourselves a being whose non-being is impossible. But of such a one you have no concept at all; for you can cognize impossibility only through a contradiction; but the non-being of a thing never contradicts itself, because that a thing is, is not an analytic proposition following from its concept, but a synthetic one. — Now you can well say that, because everything existing must be thoroughly determined, if you are to give any concept of the necessary being [from which its existence] which determines it thoroughly a priori, it can only be the concept of the *realissimi*. — But I cannot say that, if I did not think the [*realissimum*] *necessarium* under the concept of the *realissimi* (g we never represent substances as necessary as regards their existence, but only the *accidentia*), then a contradiction with the concept of the *necessarii* would arise, since after all, if I presuppose a *realissimum*, the non-existing of this thing |\nM 26: |\ncontains no contradiction (such a relation of concepts is possible only if the concept of the *realissimi* contains only a partial concept of the *necessarii*; for then |\nI can indeed infer from the *necessario* to the *realissimum*, but not from the latter also to that necessity; but the concept of the *realissimi* itself, according to the presupposition, makes up the whole concept of the *necessarii*, because it is assumed [on that account necessary] precisely for this, in order to represent the *necessarium* in its thoroughgoing determination); for necessity can just as little be inferred from a concept of a being determinable in two ways (g its contingency) as from that which is determinable in only one way, because the existential proposition is always synthetic and from concepts, be it that which contains a thoroughgoing determination, or that which leaves it undetermined in many pieces, it cannot be inferred whether it can be or not be, hence its objective reality can be neither affirmed nor denied.\nIt is not that the necessary being is determinable in only one single way, for it could be that, even if a non A were alongside the A among its determinations, but, if the existence of a thing is to be cognized from its concept, then this concept must already contain the thoroughgoing determination in itself; e.g. [If] from the concept of the *realissimi* alone this could happen, if the existence of a thing could be cognized from its concept at all; for this is the only concept which [all the] represents an *ens singulare*.\nBut from the concept of no thing can the absolutely necessary [being] existence be derived; thus [is it also] if it is thought by us as a necessary being, which is the modality of its *position*, then with regard to this concept it is entirely undetermined which predicates it contains.\nThere is no ground why the *partim negativum* should not exist just as necessarily.\nM 25': |\nThe concept of a necessary being is (g 1.) the concept of a thing which is undetermined with regard to none of all possible predicates A or non A, i.e. of an *individui*; but through this con|cept\nit is 2.) undetermined; consequently it is logically contingent whether I attribute to it a or non A. 3. from the necessity of its existence it does not follow that it is thoroughly determined through its concept, i.e. is *ens realissimum*.\nThe proof of the (g necessary) existence of a *realissimi* is this: if it did not [all perfection ha] exist, then it would not have all perfection, for existence (as thingly perfection) would be lacking to it. — The proof of the highest reality as predicate of the necessary being is: If this did not have all perfection, then it would be (g in itself) (through its concept) undetermined whether it has or does not have a certain perfection, consequently, however it may exist, possible that it nevertheless did not exist as such, hence [a contingent being] its existence (the thoroughgoing determination) contingent.\nOne thinks here that, since the first proposition would have to be synthetic and a priori, the second is merely analytic: for there I go from possibility (but is this also given?) to actuality, consequently beyond the concept — but here from actuality, which [but its] is at the same time determined as necessary, to the [sing] concept which alone contains thoroughgoing determination (which must always be thought in existence) and seems to be identical with the previous concept, hence through an analytic proposition not beyond the concept, but to what is contained in it. — But the proposition is nevertheless synthetic; for I go beyond every concept that I may have of a necessary being as such, if I make the concept which for me is the only one that contains thoroughgoing determination into the condition of the possibility of the thoroughgoing determination of the object itself (in intuition) or infer to it; for in intuition it can, without prejudice to its necessity, [contain] contain many negative predicates in its thoroughgoing determination, only that I cannot derive them from a single concept or comprehend them together in it.",["\"determination\": both the Latinate determination and Bestimmung; \"pieces\": Stücke; \"thingly\": dinglich.","*realissimi*, *necessarii*, *necessario*: inflected forms of *realissimum* (the most real) and *necessarium* (the necessary); *partim negativum*: the partly negative.","\"[all perfection ha]\": the deleted words break off, presumably in \"haben\" or \"hätte\"; \"[sing]\": the deleted \"einzi\" breaks off, presumably in \"einzigen\"."]],"reflm:6390":["»A necessary being can exist only [with] as one that is already thoroughly determined through its concept; for were it not determined through the same, then it could (g *salvo hoc conceptu*) be A, but also non A, i.e. its opposite would be (g objectively, i.e. in itself) possible, i.e. it would be contingent.« — That is the *argumentum cosmologicum* for proving the existence of an *entis realissimi*. For this concept is the only one which at the same time contains in itself the thoroughgoing determination of a thing as a thing in general. But through the concept of a necessary being [this] no thing at all is determined, what it is, [for if] i.e. it remains entirely undetermined whether with regard to any synthetic predicate it is A or non A, without on that account being contingent. For for the concept of a body it is contingent [to be warm or] to be heavy or [to be cold] not; but from this it does not follow that what that concept leaves undetermined does not belong necessarily to the body itself.",["*salvo hoc conceptu*: this concept being preserved; *entis realissimi*: genitive of *ens realissimum*.","\"determined\": determinirt in the quotation, bestimmt afterwards."]],"reflm:6391":["We cannot think any existing thing as contingent in every respect. (For then it would be something as object of experience, but for reason nothing.)\nBut in relation to our concept of a thing all things are contingent.\nWe must think at least one thing as necessary, but we can make ourselves no concept of this thing as such; i.e. relative to our concept everything is contingent.",["\"in every respect\": in aller Absicht."]],"reflm:6392":["Everything determined is necessary, the undetermined contingent.",[]],"reflm:6393":["Above, beside and \n*Absolute Necessarium Ens*. That something is possible we cannot cognize through the mere non-contradicting of the thought, but indeed that something is impossible, from the contradiction.\nThat a thing is necessary, however, we can have insight into only through the contradiction of the opposite (either since the concept contradicts itself, or the existence contradicts other given laws); where, therefore, such a thing cannot be demanded, e.g. that the non-being of a thing contradict itself, there we cannot have insight into the impossibility of non-being, hence the necessity. If we cognized the possibility of all things intuitively, then we would cognize the impossibility of something even without contradiction, by not encountering the thing at all in the field of possibility. Absolute necessity would be nothing but the intuition of a thing a priori.\nWe are necessitated to assume all things as necessary (as absolutely or hypothetically). The ground is, because we must at the same time represent everything true logically as necessary (*principium exclusi medii*), in that we determine things only through judgments, and judgments a posteriori, without being confirmed through such a priori, are not objective.",["*Absolute Necessarium Ens*: the absolutely necessary being; \"have insight into\": einsehen.","The source has the slips \"st\" (ist) and \"zuglelch\" (zugleich), read as \"is\" and \"at the same time\".","\"through such a priori\": \"a priori\" may qualify \"such\" (judgments) or \"confirmed\"."]],"reflm:6394":["From the concept of an object I can never cognize the necessity or contingency of its existence.",["\"object\": Object, not Gegenstand."]],"reflm:6395":["If there is no ground for a thing, then it is contingent for me.",[]],"reflm:6396":["We can think an occurrence which is no alteration of a thing, e.g. arising or perishing of substance. Of such a one I do not say that it has a cause, but that such an arising is not an occurrence at all, i.e. cannot take place.",["\"occurrence\": Begebenheit; \"arising or perishing\": Entstehen oder Vergehen."]],"reflm:6397":["(g *Qvantum*) An absolute minimum would not be a *quantum*; therefore it is im|possible.\nAn absolute maximum would not be givable as regards quantity; therefore as object of intuition it is impossible.",["Latin in the original; \"givable\": *dabile*; \"object of intuition\": *obiectum intuitus*."]],"reflm:6398":["All magnitude is in appearances merely comparative, in things in themselves absolute. The whole solar system could be contained in a drop of water, and there are thus in neither of the two a determinate number of parts. But in a *mundo noumeno* there must be a determinate multitude of parts. One drop of water is not to be distinguished from the other in itself by anything, but only by place. But in the *substantia noumenon* the difference is actual. Just so the successive Platonic year-revolutions. In the soul one minute can contain what a century does in another. But in itself there must nevertheless be more reality in the one than in the other. This proves *animam vt phaenomenon*.",["*mundo noumeno*: ablative of *mundus noumenon*, the world as noumenon; *animam vt phaenomenon*: the soul as phenomenon.","\"year-revolutions\": Jahrrevolutionen.","\"in another\" (in einer andern): feminine, so either another soul or another minute."]],"reflm:6399":["The magnitude of a thing as aggregate is extensive, as ground is intensive.",["The source has the slip \"instensiv\"."]],"reflm:6400":["Magnitude of space and degree of extension.",[]],"reflm:6401":["Contingency is either (g merely) inner or (g also) outer, the latter is either in a particular or in every *respectu* possibility of the opposite; we represent everything that is actual also as necessary.",["*respectu*: respect (ablative of *respectus*).","\"inner\", \"outer\": innerlich, äußerlich."]],"reflm:6402":["Possibility not merely *in thesi*, but also *in hypothesi*, is greater; but that *sub hypothesi* smaller.",["*in thesi*: in thesis, absolutely; *in hypothesi*, *sub hypothesi*: in, under a hypothesis."]],"reflm:6403":["In every alteration the substance persists, because alteration is the succession of the determinations of one and the same thing. This is a merely logical proposition according to the rule of identity. But it does not say that the substance in general does not arise or perish, but only that it remains during the alteration.\nThat proposition holds (g however) also only of (g corporeal) substances in space. For there arising or perishing is always an alteration of the non-substantial, namely of space, which remains, but is no object of experience at all, consequently that alteration would also be no object of experience, hence no alteration. The persistence of matter in all change of relations [is] is proven by this, that matter itself is a mere sum total of relations, which cannot drop away without all other relations also dropping away, hence all possible outer experience.",["Veränderung = alteration; Wechsel = change; beharren / Beharrlichkeit = persist / persistence.","[ist] wird ... bewiesen: the struck ist and its replacement wird both come out as is in English."]],"reflm:6404":["Because the reality that is encountered partially in all things is thought together in God, there arises from this Spinozism: that all things inhere in God, because their essence is possible only as part of his essence. *Non indiget existentia alterius.*\nInherence, dependence, and real whole. In so many kinds of way do realities belong to one another as regards existence.",["Non indiget existentia alterius = it does not need the existence of another; the subject is not stated.","realem Ganzen stands in the dative, unconnected to the list; rendered real whole."]],"reflm:6405":["Spinoza's proposition, that that whose idea needs no other is substance, is one and the same with this, that the *realissimum* alone is substance, hence alone is the necessary being, inasmuch as everything else inheres in it.",["realissimum = the most real (being); Wesen = being."]],"reflm:6406":["Over and \nAll alteration has its cause; substances do not arise and perish.",["Über und breaks off at the start of the note."]],"reflm:6407":["*Principia*:\nl. *Inhaerentiae*. No substance perishes.\n2. *Causalitatis*, of alteration.\n3. *Commercii*.",["Principia: Inhaerentiae, Causalitatis, Commercii = principles: of inherence, of causality, of community.","l. is as printed in the transcription, evidently for 1."]],"reflm:6408":["Contingent is that which is possible only in a conditioned way (hypothetically) (whose non-being is thus possible in itself).\nOne cannot begin from this, that the opposite is possible, i.e. that it does not exist. For since we cognize impossibility only through a contradiction, but the non-being of a thing never contradicts itself, everything would be contingent. Everything that happens is contingent, i.e. possible only in a conditioned way: *principium caussalitatis*. Hence substances which persist are not to be regarded as contingent, although their state is contingent, hence the alterations of substances do not prove their contingency.",["bedingter Weise = in a conditioned way; zufällig / Zufälligkeit = contingent / contingency; Nichtseyn = non-being."]],"reflm:6409":["What happens is possible only in a conditioned way. For before it there precedes a time, which contains the condition of the existence of the occurrence.",["Begebenheit = occurrence."]],"reflm:6410":["Contingent is not that whose non-being does not contradict itself, but which is ungrounded.\nNow everything that exists is grounded; consequently nothing existing is contingent. Being grounded pertains here to experience, not to objective principles.",["gegründet / ungegründet = grounded / ungrounded; geht auf = pertains to."]],"reflm:6411":["Necessity and contingency are mere concepts of reason, thus of course they can be cognized in no object.",[]],"reflm:6412":["On the contingency that is ascribed to a connection on account of its purposiveness without an end.",["Zwekmäßigkeit ohne Zwek = purposiveness without an end (Zweck = end); Verknüpfung = connection."]],"reflm:6413":["94. 95'. E II 1089.\nEvery synthetic predicate, i.e. *determination*, has its ground, namely something else, through which it is combined a priori with the concept of a thing. For otherwise the determination would not be objective. [Thus] But the reality of an *entis realissimi* is no determination. But the existence of a thing is a synthetic predicate of our representation of things, and one cannot say: it has a ground [but] (*rationem existendi*), but only: *rationem cognoscendi*, and this not merely a posteriori, but also a priori.\nM 94:\nThe concept of cause holds only of objects of experience; for that something exists can be cognized only by means of experience, and of that which is not an object of experience, be thought only [according to the] insofar as it is represented according to the analogy of experience. But that something is contingent in such objects cannot be cognized from concepts and the possibility of the opposite, i.e. the agreement of the opposite with concepts; for contingency here concerns existence as the predicate of a synthetic judgment. Thus only the opposite of that which belongs to existence is possible, which is determined according to [the] (g no) law of experience and according to no analogy of it. Hence nothing is contingent but (g what) happens, |\nbut without cause or respectively without external cause. Motion inheres in a body *accidentaliter* [or]. It arises *accidentaliter*. It is a *contingency*, i.e. event, but not according to concepts, i.e. absolute |\nM 94': |\npossibility of the opposite. For everything that is determinable through experience is necessary, because otherwise the combination of perceptions would not be objective. Logical contingency is yet physical necessity.\nM 95':\n*Omne contingens, h.e. qvodcunque contingit, est rationatum alterius*, every event is [an] effect of a cause: can be understood in two ways, either that by it merely the determination of a thing, or the existence of it as substance is understood. In the first meaning it is a possible concept of experience, and the proposition a principle of nature; in the second it is a problematic concept, which is entirely empty, since namely the existence of the subject itself is to be an event and existence itself is to follow upon non-existence — empty —. This contingency of the thing can as little be seen into as the necessity. *Contingentia absoluta* is just as unknowable as *necessitas absoluta*.",["Allein das Daseyn eines Dinges: Allein may be But or Only (only the existence of a thing); the English keeps But, and the ambiguity is the German's.","Determination (Latinate) and Bestimmung both = determination; contingentz kept as Kant's foreign word; Eräugnis (Ereignis) = event.","Omne contingens ... est rationatum alterius = everything contingent, i.e. whatever happens, is the consequent of something else; eingesehen werden = be seen into (einsehen)."]],"reflm:6414":["Metaphysics proper is the application of transcendental philosophy to [that which] concepts given in reason (which are necessary to it), to which, however, [in the] no corresponding objects can be given in experience (consequently to the supersensible).\nThis can thus only be the unconditioned, for that is the only theoretical idea of reason. Thus metaphysics pertains 1. to that of which only |\nthe whole is to be represented as absolutely unconditioned; 2. to things, insofar as they are in themselves sensibly unconditioned.\nThe first part is thus cosmology, the second rational doctrine of the soul [and] as pneumatology and theology.",["Eigentliche Metaphisik = metaphysics proper; Seelenlehre = doctrine of the soul.","sinnlich unbedingt = sensibly unconditioned (unconditioned as regards the senses)."]],"reflm:6415":["Metaphysics proper: the application of transcendental philosophy to things that cannot become objects of experience, either to a whole of the objects of the senses or to supersensible things.",[]],"reflm:6416":["Cosmology. Is transcendent. For it pertains to the whole of all conditions, which is unconditioned.",["The transcription runs Cosmologie.Ist together without a space."]],"reflm:6417":["The sensible world is a mere *totum ideale*, because space is mere form of intuition; thus the synthesis is never total; time does not make a measure of the magnitude of the world, but the whole of its states.",["totum ideale = ideal whole.","ihrer Zustände: its may refer to the world (Weltgröße) or to time; as ambiguous as the German."]],"reflm:6418":["All antinomies come from this, that one seeks the unconditioned in the sensible world.",[]],"reflm:6419":["That both *opposita* in the 2 first antinomies can be false comes from this, that they stand not *contradictorie*, but also *contrarie* |\nopposed to each other. The world has a beginning — It has not — It is infinite.",["opposita = opposites; contradictorie / contrarie = contradictorily / contrarily.","The | falls inside the verb entgegen stehen (stand opposed), whose second half begins the next line."]],"reflm:6420":["Were space and time [as infinite] something given in itself, then they would have to be considered as infinite magnitudes. Now they are nothing but forms, to be enlarged or diminished to infinity.",["Größen = magnitudes; ins unendliche = to infinity."]],"reflm:6421":["In the mathematical antinomies both propositions are false, because the unconditioned is supposed to be a part of appearances and [it] yet as such can never be unconditioned. In the dynamical ones both can be true, because the unconditioned is taken as the ground of appearances, but is not a part of them, and the one proposition holds of things in appearance, the other of their relation to the intelligible ground.\nThe two first antinomies are grounded on the unconditioned totality of conditions — the two others on the unconditioned ground of the existence of the conditioned. Hence the 2 first are false; the others can be true.",["den Erscheinungen zum Grunde gelegt = taken as the ground of appearances; Satz = proposition."]],"reflm:6422":["Freedom is a (g sensibly unconditioned) faculty of a substance to determine itself to act.",["Vermögen = faculty."]],"reflm:6423":["*Non datur fatum.* Everything in the world is composite, hence contingent.",["Non datur fatum = there is no fate."]],"reflm:6424":["Idea. A pure concept of reason must be a [through] concept subjectively necessary to reason, and this is that of the unconditioned, consequently of the absolute totality of conditions (because reason is a faculty of deriving the particular from the universal). Now the world as *qvantum* is composite, and the possibility of a *qvanti* which is itself still part is always still conditioned; thus only the concept |\nof the absolute totality of the whole of substances is in accordance with the concept of reason of a world. Now, however, contradictions arise, whether one says: the sensible world as absolute whole of things is infinite (given), or also: it is given as finite. That comes about because it is a contradiction that a world in space and time is [a] given wholly in itself, because it is no thing in itself. The sensible world is infinite, means [only] not: it is actual as such a one. For, because it exists merely in representation, not in itself, as one thinks of it: it is given only as far as the *progressus* reaches, hence never wholly; i.e. the last goes to infinity.",["Vernunftbegrif = concept of reason; würklich = actual; Ganze = whole.","der letzte (the last, masculine) has no stated noun; Progressus is the nearest masculine noun.","so wie man sie sich denkt may attach to existirt or to the negated an sich selbst; as ambiguous as the German."]],"reflm:6425":["One can say: matter is divisible to infinity, but not: it consists of infinitely many parts: somewhat just as Euclid: two parallel lines [become], drawn to infinity, cannot meet, is not one and the same with the proposition: they meet at an infinite distance. For the latter would give a triangle whose angles contained more than 2 right ones. It is just the same with the proposition: the (g world) space can be pursued to infinity, and that: it is infinite (the latter we can say of pure space, but only as idea). One can also carry out a proof of this, that, if [the] (g a given) space consists of infinitely many parts, world-space is infinite.",["The Euclid clause has no verb of saying; it is rendered as it stands.","Weltraum = world-space."]],"reflm:6426":["Nature of the things of the world is either under laws of the determination of their existence in space or merely in time: corporeal and thinking nature.",[]],"reflm:6427":["Beside and \nBecause good conduct in life makes (g him) worthy of an enlargement of his natural predispositions, progress in adequacy to his duty must also bring the human being in the future to a greater level of his natural rank. But because this in turn brings greater duties in its train, progress toward fulfillment of these will also continue, and so into eternity.",["Neben und breaks off at the start of the note.","Fortschreiten and Fortschritt both = progress; Naturanlagen = natural predispositions; Stufe = level, stage.","diese (this) may refer to the greater level or to the progress; as ambiguous as the German."]],"reflm:6428":["The concept of freedom is a concept of reason whose reality cannot be cognized theoretically-dogmatically; but also the only one which is not only capable of a practical-dogmatic [not] proof, but also is necessarily combined with it. — But from it also flow God and immortality. Thus these too are merely practically dogmatic.\n(s κατ' ανθρωπον to prove a priori means: to prove sufficiently for the subject, but only with a practical aim.)",["κατ' ανθρωπον = according to the human being (ad hominem).","in practischer Absicht = with a practical aim; Verbunden = combined."]],"reflm:6429":["If space were the form of outer relations in itself, then there could be more *entia realissima*.",["entia realissima = most real beings."]],"reflm:6430":["*immensum*, [*infinit*] *incommensurabile*, What analogy is without similarity.\nThat there is no probability of the existence of God.\nThe divine nature is unattainable for our reason, transcendent. The cognition of God as a moral being is immanent. The categories in the moral concept are concepts that are left |\nover when I omit all theoretical relation of them — of a thing in general.\n*natura divina est imperscrutabilis*.",["immensum, incommensurabile = immeasurable, incommensurable; the struck infinit is cut off.","natura divina est imperscrutabilis = the divine nature is inscrutable.","The | falls inside übrig bleiben (be left over)."]],"reflm:6431":["2) God not merely as first cause, but also as author.\nProof of a living God:\n1. as *primus motor*,\n2 — architect,\n3 — creator.",["primus motor = first mover; Urheber = author."]],"reflm:6432":["On God and immortality.\nIf the moral law, in order to bind us, needed God and a future life, then it would be absurd to ground on such a need the belief in the actuality of that which can satisfy it.\nNow, however, the moral law stands firm for itself independently of all theoretical presupposition; indeed it also has, instead of this, something supersensible, which surpasses all our insight, freedom, as its ground (which itself lies beyond nature in another field). This law as principle of wisdom leads our reason in its practical use to the final end of a highest wisdom: the greatest happiness combined with virtue as the final end of all things, which, however, as far as we have insight, makes necessary an eternity [in] of our existence and a moral author of the world for the effecting of the constitution of the world [belo] required for it.\nEven if one could neither [assume] believe immortality nor the existence of God, the moral law and the end of a will in accordance with it (the highest good) would on that account have just as much their mighty influence on the will; we would yet not take up such a scandal into our principles with a practical aim, but rather as (g permitted) hypothesis promote [our] the contentment with our existence and the world that we have in prospect.\nThe *argumentum a tuto* is good for actions, but very bad (g even) for the [determining gr] (g inner) confession of belief.",["verbinden = bind (obligate); Glauben = belief; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Weltverfassung = constitution of the world.","[belo] renders the struck, cut-off geho (gehörigen); [determining gr] the struck, cut-off Bestimmungsgr(und).","argumentum a tuto = argument from safety; böse = bad (also evil); scandal is Kant's foreign word."]],"reflm:6433":["340. E II 1676. 873.\nAll rational theology is either that of deism (g transcendental) or of theism (g psychologically determined). The first either ontotheology or cosmotheology. — That of theism either physico- or ethicotheology.\nThe cosmotheological proof wants to infer from [the] a given, absolutely necessary existence (without being able to derive this itself from any [determinate] concept of a thing, but merely because it is unconditioned) the [concept] highest reality of this being. For, it is said, if we do not think it as *realissimum*, then we have no concept through which it would at the same time be cognized [in] according to its thoroughgoing determination. consequently [they] we would be able to think its concept through a or also non a, i.e., the concept that we made of it would be only contingent; thus the thing itself contingent.\nM 340:\nBut is the concept of a necessary or contingent substance thinkable at all? At least alterations of substances [are] do not prove their contingency. These concepts aim only to indicate the possibility of the cognition of the existence of things completely *a priori* or the impossibility of it, and thus hold not of objects but of the relation of our concepts of them to our faculty of cognition. The former is impossible. Instead of these concepts one should make use of those of unconditionally [cognizable] possible existence.",["Daseyn is \"existence\", Wesen \"being\", Veränderungen \"alterations\"; \"durchgängige Bestimmung\" is \"thoroughgoing determination\"."]],"reflm:6434":["Irrational concepts (g *conceptus surdi*) are to be distinguished from ideas; they are namely such concepts of the understanding from which one nevertheless withdraws everything that is required for example and application *in concreto*, which thus can have no significance, although they are without contradiction. |\ne.g. (g 1.) God is eternal, i.e., his existence has a magnitude, but not of time. But now we can think no magnitude of existence, i.e., a duration, otherwise than in time. 2. His presence has a magnitude with respect to the all of things outside one another (in space). But it is nevertheless not to be taken as in space, for then God would be outside himself. 3. He is cause of substance. But the way of being a cause must be different from that which we know; for there nothing can be the cause of a substance.",["*conceptus surdi*: literally \"deaf\" concepts, the term used of irrational (\"surd\") numbers.","\"das All der Dinge\" is rendered \"the all of things\"."]],"reflm:6435":["Concepts of irrational relations are those which cannot be exhausted by any approximation; but they are transcendent when in this relation an object has no measure.",[]],"reflm:6436":["1. A (g most perfect of all) being that contains all reality (be it as ground or as aggregate) is (g necessarily) actual. For were it not actual, then it would lack a perfection (metaphysical), namely existence.\n2. A being that is there necessarily must contain all reality (i.e., if existence can be inferred from its concept, for only by this can one cognize necessity). For if it did not contain all reality, then it would not be thoroughly determined through its concept, which thoroughgoing determination must be [noth] attributed to every existing thing. But one can indeed think a necessary being, but cognize it by nothing at all, because every thing can be canceled in thought, even all reality, unless one had to take existence [al] for a particular reality, which contradicts the first argument.",["\"existence\" renders both Existenz (in 1.) and Daseyn; \"is there necessarily\" renders \"nothwendig da ist\"; wirklich is \"actual\".","[noth] (for nothwendig) and [al] (for als) are broken-off deletions, left in the German."]],"reflm:6437":["That no [theoretical] cognition (g with speculative aim) of God is possible for the human being, because we must thus separate from our concepts of things everything that makes the things cognizable to us, hence nothing is left for us to give them reality. The categories\n|\nare concepts of things whose objective reality is problematic. Through them we can think a being, but not cognize it.",["The first sentence, opening with \"Daß\", has no main clause in the German; it is left so."]],"reflm:6438":["Belief in the existence of God is not probability. The cognition of God in practical regard is merely representation of him according to the analogy in theoretical, whereby he is thought only according to the analogy with an object of the senses. Anthropomorphism.\nUnderstanding — but not human in thinking — feeling of pleasure and displeasure, but not humanlike — faculty of desire or will, but not human. Yet according to the analogy.",["\"in theoretischer\": the noun (presumably Rüksicht, \"regard\") is elided in the German, and is not supplied."]],"reflm:6439":["In and\nThe psychological concept of God as *summa intelligentia* is that of a living God with understanding and will. The proof of such a one is teleology, not the concept of reality. But both ontotheology and physicotheology give no cognition of God without anthropomorphisms.",["\"In und\" stands alone and breaks off in the source."]],"reflm:6440":["From the existence of the world one can indeed infer according to laws of causality to supreme causes, but not their absolute necessity, unity and infinity.\nInference according to the analogy of nature from contingent effect to free cause.",[]],"reflm:6441":["NB. Inference according to the analogy of the cause of alteration, of which the first is always free action.",[]],"reflm:6442":["One has cause to represent God according to the analogy with an understanding, |\ni.e., that just as a clock, a ship etc etc. is related to its cause (an intelligent being), so the world to the unknown, which we call an infinite understanding, although it has nothing in itself similar to it. — But we cannot infer from the causality of things through understanding to a world-cause (g of the same kind, i.e.) that has understanding, just as little as from the artworks of bees that they have understanding; because, just as with these the causality is of a far lesser species, so there it can be of a far higher kind than that which we indicate by understanding.\nIt is also not, say, probable that there is such a cause. For everything probable must lie on the way to certainty, insofar as this is reached through the mere supplementing of what is lacking in the progress. But here the data are of such a kind that they do not lead there at all.",["\"species\" renders Species, \"kind\" Art.","\"although it has nothing in itself similar to it\": the German \"er ... damit\" leaves open which of the two terms each pronoun refers to."]],"reflm:6443":["That [the] our idea of a highest being as an intelligent being has objective reality can be concluded only from the (g objective) reality of the concept of freedom, which makes it necessary for us to direct our actions toward [the] a highest [G] good in the world possible through our cooperation, because in doing so we assume the possibility of it, at least must proceed as if we assumed it (in that we must not only, as is required for a best world, act in agreement with it, but also make it our incentive), from which it then follows that we, since our faculty alone does not suffice for it, must assume a highest original good, consequently the concept of the latter has objectively practical reality, although in theoretical relation it is transcendent, hence only an object of belief.\nThe mere categories (of substance, cause, community) give mere ways of making for ourselves concepts of things in general, i.e., of the synthetic unity of a manifold that may be given to us, in one consciousness, which must be in us *a priori* for this reason, because a *compositum* as such must be, not perceived by us, but always made, and the action of making such a thing, i.e., of giving to the |\nmanifold unity of consciousness in one representation, must be inherent in us *a priori*. But these categories have for themselves alone no objective reality, because they do not contain the way in which a manifold may be given to us. Since this now cannot be given to them before the theoretical faculty of cognition, i.e., as belonging (g to) a cognition of the nature of the objects: there remains only the practical reality of them with respect to the concept of freedom (which presupposes merely the form of unity, not the intuition itself) as immanent for our reason, in order to think the highest being according to the analogy with those that we cognize in nature according to their spontaneity.",["\"vor dem theoretischen Erkentnisvermögen\": vor may be the period spelling of für (\"for\") as well as \"before\".","\"objectiv practische Realität\" is rendered \"objectively practical reality\"; the German leaves open whether objectiv qualifies practisch or Realität.","Triebfeder is \"incentive\", verständig \"intelligent\", gefolgert \"concluded\"."]],"reflm:6444":["Physicotheology is the inference, from the experience [in] of this world to infer an intelligent supreme cause:\n1. From the motion of matter as something lifeless to a first mover as a living one.\n2. From the art in this world to a [first] master craftsman who has understanding.\nMoral theology:\n1. From moral wisdom as a [rule] legislation in rational beings for this world, although what is not in conformity with this wisdom can be shown in experience, to infer an intelligible world in which this is.\n2. From the idea of such a [to a] as the highest good possible only through a moral author to the existence of such a one.",["\"obgleich dieser Weisheit nicht gemäßes in der Erfahrung gewiesen werden kan\" can also be read \"although what is in conformity with this wisdom cannot be shown in experience\".","Werkmeister is \"master craftsman\", Urheber \"author\"."]],"reflm:6445":["We can indeed unite divine freedom with reason, but not the arising of the effect from this freedom.\nThe transition from the intellectual as cause to that which is merely appearance cannot be comprehended. Thus, even if we wanted to cancel the freedom of the human being, we could nevertheless not resolve the difficulty of how the determining causes of the will could first have begun.",[]],"reflm:6446":["Human actions cannot be considered merely as *phaenomena* [according to] and hence as determined according to empirical laws; they are at the same time *noumena* and have a relation to the causality of the (g pure) understanding and of pure [understanding] reason. What arises from this as from a cause is good. The possibility of acting from reason, i.e., from the motive of the good in itself, is freedom. This possibility is (g to be found) in all actions, because the incentives of sensibility never determine. not even the evil actions, and no action is empirically certain according to laws of the *phaenomenorum*. The higher power of choice is therefore at all times free (*a stimulis*), and between its motives and the empirically determined power of choice there is a connection which can be represented only according to the analogy of the connection of appearances and which constitutes no series. Thus it remains incomprehensible how the very same reason that produces certain actions could have produced others in their stead. With respect to these actions as *phaenomenorum*, for the human understanding, which does not comprehend the transition from the intellectual to the sensible and the first ground of arising, freedom is a necessary hypothesis. But among the *noumenis* the *effectus* of freedom are not according to what happens in time, but in themselves; in the soul it is to be assessed otherwise, and that which was evil there can be good here in the whole.",["Willkühr is \"power of choice\", Bewegungsgrund \"motive\", Triebfeder \"incentive\", Verknüpfung \"connection\".","\"vor den Menschlichen Verstand\": vor in the period sense of für (\"for\").","Kant's Latin case forms (*phaenomenorum*, *noumenis*) are kept as he wrote them."]],"reflm:6447":["The theodicy or justification of God on account of ills is easy, because they can be a partial good; but on account of evil (the moral), because it simply ought not to be (and also cannot belong to the negations |\nas necessary limitations of creatures), neither an accusation nor a justification can take place, because all evil in the world takes place in it only as *phaenomenon* and, as far as the actual world as *noumenon* is concerned, we cannot at all have insight into its possibility in conformity with and contrary to the moral laws.",["\"ills\" renders Übel, \"evil\" das Böse; Schranken is \"limitations\"."]],"reflm:6448":["The transcendental ideas of reason pertain to the totality of the series of conditions *a parte priori*, consequently to the absolutely unconditioned in the synthesis. The absolutely whole [time] elapsed time (beginning of the world), the absolutely whole division (the simple), the absolutely whole series of determining causes (freedom) and the postulate of what is presupposed (g or absolute presupposition.) (necessity).\nAccording to the schema of sensibility this is impossible (because its synthesis can be not whole, nor empirically bounded); but intellectually it is possible. These transcendental ideas cannot be used for the explanation of appearances, but they have their significance only in the pure system of reason.",["\"absolutely\" renders both schlechthin and absolut."]],"reflm:6449":["M 381':\n*Ens necessarium*.\nHe who does not know how to derive from the concept of a thing its existence (g as necessary) also does not know what determinations a thing must have in order to be [from it] absolutely necessary, if he assumes such an existence.* For for human beings this would have to happen from concepts. Now, if existence is not to be derived from concepts, nothing further can be cognized of it than that its concept is thoroughly determined, without cognizing what these determinations consist in; thus he would only be able to say that it is represented as also |\nposited outside the understanding, for (g only) for that reason is a thing called thoroughly determined; thus he has said nothing but breaks off.\n*(s The cosmological proof demands that the thoroughgoing determination of an *entis necessarii* must lie in its concept, but such is only that of the highest reality; consequently the properties of the *entis necessarii* can be derived from the mere necessity of existence. But it nevertheless admits that [from] conversely from no concept can the existence of the thing be derived. It would therefore have to |\nM 381:\n[say] not say: the *ens necessarium* has by virtue of its concept the highest reality, but rather: we cannot determine it from concepts otherwise than through sheer reality (for as (g necessarily) existing it must be represented *a priori* as thoroughly determined). But if the inference is to hold of the object itself, then the concepts of necessity and of the *realissimi* must also be *conceptus reciproci*; for here we have to do only with concepts, not with intuitions, in which more can be contained than in the concept of them.)",["\"breaks off\" renders the AA's \"bricht ab\", which stands unbracketed in the source.","\"thoroughly determined\" renders both \"durchgängig bestimmt\" and the Latinate \"durchgängig determinirt\"."]],"reflm:6450":["On creation in time or from eternity.\nAn existence upon which the whole duration of a given thing follows (the beginning of the same) is quite well thinkable, and of this kind is the birth of an animal. But an existence upon which the whole of time in general follows, (g as definition of the absolutely first beginning) is a contradiction; for time can only follow insofar as something precedes (which thus was not in it). [Thus the beginning belongs to the Ver] The preceding, however, itself presupposes a time. Thus not all time can follow upon a given existence; for that upon which a certain whole time (which contains the beginning of the thing) follows is a preceding time.\nThe definition of the absolutely first beginning is the existence before which a time preceded, when as yet no thing at all was.\nIf we assume that a beginning can be thought before which no time at all precedes, then an end will also (g be able to) be thought upon which no time at all follows. But then one would have to say that a world has been and is now no more, which, since it nevertheless assumes a time that follows upon the duration of the world, would be a contradiction.",["'absolutely first beginning' renders both 'absolut ersten Anfangs' and 'schlechthin ersten Anfangs'.","The deleted '[Also gehört der Anfang zur Ver]' breaks off at 'Ver'; the stub is left as it stands.","The insertion '(g lassen)' turns 'wird sich ... denken' (will be thought) into 'will be able to be thought'; its English position is approximate."]],"reflm:6451":["On moral theology.\nThe final end of all things one can know, because it is moral, namely the highest (g contingent) good. This gives the concept of a highest original good, i.e., of a moral author of the world, and this concept determines the same as the most perfect of all, whereas the physico-theologian could prove only a being of great perfection. Properly, morality gives objectively no proof, but only subjectively a morally necessary assumption of the conditions under which the object of morality, the highest good, on the side on which it is not in our power (namely of happiness), is alone possible.\nFirst the representation of the world as a system [of the caus] of the *nexus finalis physici* (*causarum finalium physicarum*, among which the human being must also be). Thus an intelligent original being, but not yet God, because for that the concept of the perfection of the world from experience does not suffice. Now the representation of the world as a *systematis caussarum finalium moralium* toward the highest good. For the human being, who is a member in the *nexu finali physico*, but finds in himself a principle of a higher *nexus finalis*, will relate [himself] his existence also in regard to this to [an Ve] the same intelligent author; but the concept of the same is as of a being as author of the highest good, because this alone is adequate to the relation to ends of the moral human being. Thereby the assumption of a living God as a moral being and herewith also as an all-sufficient being is assumed.\nThis is not an objectively sufficient theoretical proof, but a subjectively-practically sufficient moral proof. The assumption of God is not necessary in order to fulfill a duty in individual actions, |\nbut in order to work toward the object of the extended moral disposition, the highest good, without which the moral disposition has, to be sure, incentives of action (from the form of morality), but no final end (in regard to the matter, i.e., the objects of rational choice). Artistic wisdom we see in corporeal things; moral wisdom we find in us as a rule.",["'final end' = Endzwek; 'author of the world' = Welturheber; 'original being' = Urwesen; 'morality' = die Moral (first paragraph) and Sittlichkeit ('form of morality').","'disposition' = Gesinnung; 'incentives' = Triebfedern; 'choice' = Willkühr; 'artistic wisdom' = Kunstweisheit.","'sufficient' renders both zureichen and hinreichend; '[einen Ve]' breaks off and the stub is left as it stands."]],"reflm:6452":["The end is (g either) the object of the will or the incentive of the action to the production of this object. The final end is the [selb] immediate end.\nThe highest good is the final end, but not as an incentive found outside God.\nThe honor of God consists in respect for his command. This, however, is the supreme condition of the highest good, consequently the final end.",["'end' = Zwek, 'final end' = Endzwek; 'incentive' = Triebfeder.","The deleted '[selb]' is a word broken off and is left as it stands.","'honor' = Ehre, which can also be rendered 'glory'."]],"reflm:6453":["If the world were regarded only as created for the end of human beings, their own happiness, then it would be created a proof of God as a being which one could love; that, however, is only a relative perfection with respect to the wish of human beings. Only the absolute, when it is encountered in the world, brings it about that we can honor the author, namely in that one cognizes him not merely as benevolent, but as wise. Wisdom, however, is the benevolence which subordinates its exercise to the condition of the objects, of being worthy of it. Thus God has created the world for his honor, not in order to be loved.",["The construction 'so würde sie ein Beweis von Gott ... geschaffen seyn' is irregular as it stands; it is rendered literally, not repaired.","'benevolent' / 'benevolence' = gütig / Gütigkeit.","'of being worthy of it' (ihrer Würdig zu seyn): 'ihrer' may refer to the benevolence or to its exercise."]],"reflm:6454":["Moral theology.\nThe moral need to assume a highest moral good is not a (g pathologically) conditioned one, in order [on account of] for the good actions which the moral law commands still to have incentives of self-|love;\nfor it is a moral need to assume even a just judge, thus not a being from whose goodness we hope, but whose holiness we must fear. Even the thought of it is, given the uncertainty of the purity of his actions, more fearsome than [einschm] enticing. — But for our legislative reason, if we represent ourselves not even as standing under laws, but as projecting according to moral laws the highest good for the world, everyone will will that virtue be happy and vice be punished. This wish alone is purely moral, not in the least selfish and unavoidable for the rational human being, and it makes it a necessity to assume a living God as moral author of the world and ruler, insofar as we relate our idea of the world not to theory but to our practical vocation. Hence the wavering, when one weighs this matter theoretically and then again looks back to the satisfaction of our practical incentives.\nIt is not a passive interest, but an active one in the idea of a merely rational being which regards itself as morally legislative.\n1. On the end of creation,\n2. the best world,\n3. the end of God with this object.",["'nicht einmal' may be read 'not even' or 'for once not'; 'sonder' is the source's spelling of 'sondern'. The deleted '[einschm]' breaks off and is left as it stands.","'his actions' (seiner Handlungen) is as unspecified as the German; 'vocation' = Bestimmung.","'goodness' = Güte; 'holiness' = Heiligkeit; 'ruler' = regirer."]],"reflm:6455":["The *potestas legislatoria diuina* rests on benevolence. For only he can obligate whose will agrees with that which is the essential property of the will of others. This is only the desire of a finite being for happiness. Thus he must be benevolent, but also omnipotent, omniscient etc etc. Only benevolence is the moral condition, but under the supreme one of his holy will. Benevolence is a consequence of blessedness. Wisdom, because it is merely limiting [in Anseh] for benevolence, is not rewarding; thus it is justice.",["'benevolence' / 'benevolent' = Gütigkeit / gütig; 'blessedness' = Seeligkeit; 'consequence' = Folge.","The deleted '[in Anseh]' (in Ansehung, 'in regard') breaks off and is left as it stands."]]};
